        <!-- ==================== PART 1 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part1">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟢 Part 1 · Foundations</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Think Like Kubernetes</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 1–4 · Beginner · <em>"What is all this, and why?"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 4 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~8 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Foundational — pre-exam concepts</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Before you can <em>administer</em> a cluster, you have to be able to <strong>see it in your head</strong>. Part 1 builds that picture from first principles: why Kubernetes exists, what a container actually is, the machines and brains behind a cluster, and the handful of objects you'll declare every single day on the job. Every abstraction is anchored on a real app — <strong>easypj</strong> — so nothing here stays theoretical.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you'll be able to do by the end:</strong> explain the control-plane / worker split from memory, trace how a single <code>kubectl apply</code> turns YAML into a running easypj Pod, and name what every field in a manifest is for — all before you've installed a single tool.</div>
            <div class="analogy-box">
                <h5>🏢 Real-World Analogy: Running an Apartment Portfolio</h5>
                <p>Think of a Kubernetes cluster as a property-management company that runs dozens of apartment buildings. You, the administrator, don't personally paint walls or fix leaks — you write down the rules every unit must meet ("working water, heat, two smoke detectors") and the management system enforces them around the clock. If a unit fails inspection, nobody calls you at 3 AM: the system already knows the rule, finds the gap, and fixes it or moves the tenant to a working unit on its own. That is the entire idea behind Kubernetes in one sentence — you declare the desired state, and the system relentlessly makes reality match it, without you standing over its shoulder.</p>
<pre>  REAL-ESTATE PORTFOLIO                       KUBERNETES CLUSTER
  ───────────────────────                     ──────────────────
  🏢 Property-management office     ──────▶   Control plane
  🏗️  Apartment buildings           ──────▶   Worker nodes
  🚪 Individual units               ──────▶   Pods
  📋 The lease / building code      ──────▶   Desired state (YAML manifest)
  🔧 Auto-dispatched maintenance    ──────▶   Controllers (reconciliation loop)
  📞 "Call the manager at 3 AM"?    ──────▶   Doesn't happen — self-healing</pre>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-title myth-table-title">🧭 Before You Start: 3 Things Beginners Get Wrong</div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>❌ What Beginners Think</th>
                            <th>✅ What's Actually True</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"Kubernetes is just a fancier way to run Docker."</td>
                            <td>Docker (or any runtime) builds and runs one container. Kubernetes is the layer above it — deciding where containers run, restarting them when they die, and keeping a whole fleet matching a rule you wrote, across many machines at once.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"A Pod is basically a lightweight virtual machine."</td>
                            <td>A Pod has no OS of its own and no fixed identity. It's a thin, disposable wrapper around one or more containers. Kubernetes expects Pods to die and be replaced — you don't patch them in place the way you'd patch a VM.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>"When something's broken, exec into the Pod (or SSH the server) and fix it directly."</td>
                            <td>That fix vanishes the moment the Pod restarts or reschedules. The Kubernetes way: fix the YAML (the desired state) and let the cluster re-apply it everywhere — automatically, and permanently.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — the Part 1 mental model</div>
<pre>
   Ch 1 — WHY            Ch 2 — HOW                 Ch 3 — WHERE
   the problem    ─────▶ the unit K8s runs   ─────▶ the machines &amp; brains
   (orchestration)       (containers/images)        (control plane + nodes)
        │                                                  │
        └──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                   ▼
                       Ch 4 — WHAT  (the objects you declare)
              📦 Pod · 🎛️ ReplicaSet · 🚀 Deployment · 🌐 Service · 🏷️ Namespace
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: take easypj from "an app that runs on my laptop"
                     to "an app described as desired state a cluster keeps alive."
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 1 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch1">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 1 · What Is Kubernetes &amp; Why Administrators Need It</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">
                <strong>easypj</strong> runs fine on your laptop with <code>python manage.py runserver</code>.
                The moment real users arrive, it must survive a 3 AM crash, a traffic spike, a bad deploy,
                and a dead server — <strong>automatically</strong>, without you waking up. That automation
                <em>is</em> Kubernetes, and building &amp; operating the platform that provides it is the CKA's job.
                This chapter installs the mental model the rest of the guide is built on.
            </p>

            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Concepts &amp; Foundation</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 easypj / jobpost</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Define <strong>container orchestration</strong> in one sentence</li>
                    <li>✅ Give the one-line definition of Kubernetes + the <strong>desired vs actual state</strong> model</li>
                    <li>✅ Distinguish <strong>CKA</strong> (administrator) from <strong>CKAD</strong> (developer) work</li>
                    <li>✅ Name &amp; place the <strong>5 core concepts</strong> — cluster, control plane, nodes, pod, desired state</li>
                    <li>✅ Identify the major <strong>ecosystem</strong> tools (CNCF, runtimes, CNI, Helm, GitOps)</li>
                    <li>✅ Describe what a Kubernetes administrator actually <strong>does day to day</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Foundation <span class="cka-weight">ALL DOMAINS</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 Why "Why" Matters on the CKA</h5>
                <p>The CKA is a <strong>performance-based exam</strong> — 2 hours, 15–20 hands-on tasks in a live terminal, 66% to pass. Chapter 1 isn't a graded task by itself, but every <strong>Architecture (25%)</strong> and <strong>Troubleshooting (30%)</strong> question assumes this mental model. Get it wrong and nothing else lands. Internalize three ideas now:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Desired vs Actual State</strong> — controllers constantly reconcile the two. This is <em>why</em> the control plane and etcd exist.</li>
                    <li><strong>Self-Healing</strong> — a node dies, K8s reschedules its pods. The exam tests your ability to manage this.</li>
                    <li><strong>The administrator owns everything below the app</strong> — install, certs, etcd, upgrades, nodes, networking, storage.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — the Chapter 1 Learning Arc</h4>
            <p>This chapter isn't a random list of topics — it follows a deliberate arc that takes you from <em>"why does this even exist?"</em> to a complete mental model. We start with the <strong>pain</strong> (so the solution makes sense), <strong>name</strong> the solution, clarify <strong>your role</strong> in it, build the <strong>mental model</strong>, show what <strong>plugs in</strong>, and finish by grounding it in <strong>real-world practice</strong>. Each stop answers one question and feeds the next:</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chapter 1 Roadmap — six stops, one mental model</div>
<pre>
   ▶ YOU ARE HERE
   "I have an app (easypj). How do I run it for real, 24/7, without me?"
        │
