            <!-- ─────────── §1.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.1 The Problem Before Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>You've built <strong>easypj</strong> — a Django <code>jobpost</code> app where employers list jobs filtered by Skills, Location, and Author, backed by PostgreSQL. It works perfectly on your laptop. But how do you run it in production so thousands of users can reach <code>/jobs/</code> reliably, 24/7, while you sleep?</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Container Orchestration</h5>
                    <p><strong>Container orchestration</strong> is the automated management of containerized applications across many machines — placement, scaling, networking, healing, and rollout — done by <em>software, not humans</em>. Kubernetes is the industry-standard orchestrator, and as a CKA administrator you build and maintain the orchestration platform itself.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Evolution of Deployment</h4>
                <p>Deployment has moved through four eras. Each fixed the previous one's pain — and introduced new challenges:</p>
                <div class="evolution-strip">
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🖥️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2000s</div>
                        <h5>Bare Metal</h5>
                        <p>One app per physical server. No isolation, massive waste. easypj would need its own dedicated box.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status bad">❌ Wasteful &amp; rigid</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">💻</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2010s</div>
                        <h5>Virtual Machines</h5>
                        <p>Many VMs per server. Better isolation, but each runs a full guest OS — heavy and slow to provision.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status ok">⚠️ Heavy overhead</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">📦</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2015+</div>
                        <h5>Containers</h5>
                        <p>Lightweight, fast, shared kernel. But managing hundreds by hand across servers is a nightmare.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status ok">🟡 Almost there…</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item active">
                        <div class="evo-icon">☸️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">2018+</div>
                        <h5>Kubernetes</h5>
                        <p>Orchestrated containers: auto-heal, auto-scale, zero-downtime deploys. The admin builds &amp; maintains this platform.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Production-ready</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Without Kubernetes vs With Kubernetes — the easypj story</div>
<pre>
  ❌ WITHOUT KUBERNETES — one box, all your eggs in it
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ 🖥️  ONE server   (you hand-installed the whole stack)                      │
  │     🐍 easypj-web :8000      🐘 postgres :5432      🌐 nginx :80            │
  │                                                                          │
  │   💀 the process crashes   →  the WHOLE site is down until you log in      │
  │   📈 traffic doubles       →  buy &amp; configure another server, by hand      │
  │   🚀 ship a new version    →  scp files, restart, pray; no clean rollback  │
  │   🌙 3 AM page             →  YOU wake up and fix it                        │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                              │
                          ────▼────   the SAME app, orchestrated:
  ✅ WITH KUBERNETES — a self-healing, self-scaling fleet
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              🌐 users ──▶ Service / Ingress   (one stable address)         │
  │                                │ load-balances across replicas           │
  │              ┌─────────────────┼─────────────────┐                       │
  │              ▼                 ▼                 ▼                        │
  │         📦 easypj-web      📦 easypj-web      📦 easypj-web    :8000         │
  │         └── Node 1 ──┘     └── Node 1 ──┘     └── Node 2 ──┘                │
  │         🐘 postgres :5432   ── mounts ──▶  💾 PVC   (data survives)          │
  │                                                                          │
  │   🔄 a pod crashes  →  recreated in seconds      📈 load up  →  autoscaled  │
  │   🚀 new version    →  rolling update, 0 downtime    😴 3 AM  →  you sleep   │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Containers Alone Aren't Enough</h4>
                <p>Docker gives you <em>a</em> container. Kubernetes gives you an <em>army</em> of containers that manage themselves. Compare the two on-call realities:</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>😰 Running easypj WITHOUT Kubernetes</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>🔴 SSH in at 3 AM when the process crashes</li>
                            <li>🔴 Restart Django by hand every time it dies</li>
                            <li>🔴 No load balancing — one box serves all <code>/jobs/</code> traffic</li>
                            <li>🔴 Deploy = SCP files + pray nothing breaks</li>
                            <li>🔴 Scale = buy a server, install everything from scratch</li>
                            <li>🔴 Rollback = restore from backup (if you made one)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>🎉 Running easypj WITH Kubernetes</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Pod crashes → restarted in seconds; you stay asleep</li>
                            <li>✅ Traffic spikes → HPA adds pods automatically</li>
                            <li>✅ New version → rolling update, zero downtime</li>
                            <li>✅ Bad deploy → <code>kubectl rollout undo</code>, instant</li>
                            <li>✅ Node fails → pods migrate to healthy nodes</li>
                            <li>✅ Config change → update a ConfigMap, pods pick it up</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Make It Concrete — Anatomy of a 3 AM Outage</h4>
                <p>Abstract benefits are easy to nod along to. Here's the same incident — easypj's process dies at 3:00 AM — played out second-by-second in both worlds. This is <em>why</em> teams adopt Kubernetes:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Same crash, two timelines</div>
<pre>
  ❌ WITHOUT KUBERNETES                        ✅ WITH KUBERNETES
  ───────────────────────────────────────     ───────────────────────────────────────
  03:00  easypj process crashes 💥             03:00  easypj pod crashes 💥
  03:00  users get 502s, /jobs/ is DOWN        03:00  kubelet's liveness probe fails
  03:12  monitoring finally pages you 📟        03:01  K8s restarts the container (10s) 🔄
  03:18  you wake up, find your laptop 😴       03:01  readiness probe passes → back in
  03:25  VPN in, SSH to the box                       the Service's endpoints
  03:32  read logs, guess the cause             03:01  users never noticed ✅
  03:40  restart by hand, watch it              03:00  YOU ARE ASLEEP 😴 the whole time
  03:45  service restored — 45 MIN DOWN ⛔
  next day: write the post-mortem 📝            next day: nothing to write 🎉

  Downtime:  ~45 minutes, 1 very tired human    Downtime:  ~10 seconds, 0 humans
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 What just happened (preview of mechanisms)</h5>
                    <p>In the "with" column, three Kubernetes features cooperated automatically: a <strong>liveness probe</strong> detected the dead container, a <strong>controller</strong> restarted it to honor "I want 1 running," and a <strong>readiness probe + Service</strong> kept traffic away until it was healthy again. You'll build all three on easypj in Part 4 — for now, just notice that <em>detection, recovery, and traffic-routing</em> all happened with no human in the loop.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy</h5>
                    <p>A lone server is a <strong>solo musician</strong> — if they stop, the music stops. Kubernetes is the <strong>conductor of an orchestra</strong>: it decides who plays what and instantly covers anyone who drops out, so the performance never stops.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What an Orchestrator Actually Does — the 6 Hard Problems</h4>
                <p>"Orchestration" is an abstract word. Concretely, Kubernetes automates <strong>six</strong> jobs you would otherwise do by hand. Every feature in this entire guide is one of these six in disguise — so it's worth memorizing the list:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>#</th><th>Problem</th><th>By hand (without K8s)</th><th>Kubernetes does it via…</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Scheduling</strong> — where does each container run?</td><td>You pick a server and SSH in</td><td>kube-scheduler places pods by resources/affinity</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Self-healing</strong> — what if it dies?</td><td>You get paged, restart it manually</td><td>Controllers recreate pods; probes restart sick ones</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Scaling</strong> — handle more/less load</td><td>Buy + configure servers; edit configs</td><td><code>kubectl scale</code> / HPA / Cluster Autoscaler</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Service discovery + load balancing</strong></td><td>Hard-code IPs; configure nginx upstreams</td><td>Services + CoreDNS give a stable name &amp; VIP</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Rollouts &amp; rollbacks</strong></td><td>Copy files, restart, hope; restore backups</td><td>Deployments: rolling update + <code>rollout undo</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Config &amp; secret management</strong></td><td>Edit files on each box; secrets in plaintext</td><td>ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets injected at runtime</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>"Why Can't I Just Use Docker + a Restart Script?"</h4>
                <p>This is <em>the</em> beginner question — and answering it precisely is what makes the rest of the guide click. A bash script wrapping <code>docker run</code> in a <code>while true; do … done</code> loop really can keep a container alive… <strong>on one machine</strong>. The catch is that every serious production need is a <em>distributed-systems</em> problem, and a single-machine script is structurally blind to the rest of the fleet:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>What you actually need</th><th>Why a single-box script <em>structurally can't</em> do it</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Place each container on the machine with free CPU/RAM</td><td>The script only sees its own box — it has no view of the whole fleet's spare capacity</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Keep running when a machine dies</td><td>If the box dies, the script dies with it — nothing is left to react or move the work</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>One stable address for N moving replicas</td><td>Replicas come and go with changing IPs; a script can't track them or load-balance across them</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Zero-downtime version upgrades</td><td>Needs <em>coordinated, health-gated</em> replacement across replicas — not a blind local restart</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Identical config &amp; secrets everywhere</td><td>A script edits one box at a time; configuration drift creeps in across the fleet</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Kubernetes is the program that holds the <strong>whole-cluster view</strong> and solves all of these continuously. That's the real leap: from <em>"keep this process up on this box"</em> to <em>"keep this <strong>service</strong> healthy across the entire fleet."</em> Containers gave us a portable <em>unit</em>; Kubernetes gives us a brain that places, heals, connects, and upgrades thousands of those units without a human deciding <em>where</em> or <em>when</em>.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The blind spot — a script sees one box; Kubernetes sees the cluster</div>
<pre>
   🔧 DOCKER + RESTART SCRIPT                ☸️  KUBERNETES (the orchestrator)
   ┌──────────────────────────┐             ┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🖥️ node-1  (all it sees)  │             │  sees ALL nodes + their free capacity   │
   │   while true:             │             │  ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐       │
   │     docker run easypj     │             │  │ node-1 │ │ node-2 │ │ node-3 │       │
   │   ↳ restarts here ONLY    │             │  │ 60% used│ │ 20% used│ │ 90% used│      │
   │   ✗ node dies? script too │             │  └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘       │
   │   ✗ no idea node-2 exists │             │   ↳ "place the new easypj pod on node-2 │
   │   ✗ can't load-balance    │             │      (most free), and if node-1 dies,   │
   │   ✗ can't roll out safely │             │      reschedule its pods elsewhere"      │
   └──────────────────────────┘             └───────────────────────────────────────┘
        one machine's view                          the whole-cluster view
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🐮 Mental Model: Pets vs Cattle</h5>
                    <p>The single most important shift Kubernetes asks of you:</p>
                    <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <div class="card-icon-lg">🐶</div>
                            <h5>Pets (the old way)</h5>
                            <p>Servers have names, are hand-fed, and you nurse them back to health when sick. One dies → crisis. <em>"server-prod-01 is acting up again."</em></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="info-card highlight">
                            <div class="card-icon-lg">🐄</div>
                            <h5>Cattle (the K8s way)</h5>
                            <p>Pods are numbered, identical, and disposable. One dies → it's replaced automatically and you never notice. You manage the <em>herd</em>, not the individual.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <p class="mb-0">This is why pods are <strong>ephemeral</strong> (they come and go), why you never SSH into one to "fix" it, and why state lives <em>outside</em> the pod (in a database or a PersistentVolume). Internalize this now — it explains 80% of Kubernetes design decisions.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Admin Perspective</h5>
                    <p>Your job isn't just deploying apps — it's <strong>building and maintaining the platform</strong> that makes all of the "with Kubernetes" column possible: installing the cluster, managing certificates, upgrading versions, backing up etcd, and troubleshooting at the infrastructure level.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.2 What Is Kubernetes? (The Simple Answer)</h3>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Official Definition</h5>
                    <p><strong>"An open-source system for automating deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications."</strong></p>
                    <p>In plain English: Kubernetes is an <em>operating system for your containers</em>. You tell it <strong>what</strong> you want — "3 copies of easypj:v2, always running" — and it figures out <strong>how</strong> to make that true across your cluster, and keeps it true.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🚢 Analogy: the Shipping Port (why the logo is a ship's wheel)</h5>
                    <p>The name comes from the Greek <em>κυβερνήτης</em> — "<strong>helmsman</strong>," the person who steers a ship. The ☸️ logo is a ship's wheel. The analogy runs deep:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Shipping port 🚢</th><th>Kubernetes ☸️</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>A standard <strong>shipping container</strong> — any cargo, any ship, any crane</td><td>A <strong>container image</strong> — runs the same anywhere</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>ship</strong> that carries many containers</td><td>A <strong>node</strong> running many pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>harbor master</strong> deciding which ship docks where</td><td>The <strong>scheduler</strong> deciding which node runs each pod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>manifest</strong> — the document listing what's aboard</td><td>Your YAML <strong>manifest</strong> — what should be running</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>The <strong>helmsman</strong> steering it all to the goal</td><td><strong>Kubernetes</strong> steering actual state toward desired</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">You don't tell the helmsman every rudder movement — you give the destination and trust the steering. That's declarative control in one image.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Heart of It: Declarative Control</h4>
                <p>You describe the <strong>end state</strong> you want; Kubernetes continuously makes reality match it — restarting, rescheduling, and scaling on its own. You never say "start a container on node 2."</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Declarative — you DECLARE, Kubernetes DELIVERS</div>
<pre>
  📝 YOU WRITE (deploy.yaml)                ⚙️ KUBERNETES CONTINUOUSLY GUARANTEES
  ┌───────────────────────────┐            ┌───────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ apiVersion: apps/v1       │            │ ✅ exactly 3 easypj pods are RUNNING     │
  │ kind: Deployment          │   apply    │ ✅ every pod runs image easypj:v2        │
  │ spec:                     │  ───────▶  │ ✅ each pod capped at 256Mi memory       │
  │   replicas: 3             │            │ ✅ a pod crashes  → restarted in seconds │
  │   image: easypj:v2        │            │ ✅ a node dies    → pods rescheduled     │
  │   resources: 256Mi        │            │ ✅ new version    → rolled out, 0 downtime│
  └───────────────────────────┘            └───────────────────────────────────────┘
        "DESIRED state"                              "ACTUAL state"
        (you declare it; stored in etcd)             (what's really running on nodes)
              │                                              ▲
              │                  🔄 THE RECONCILE LOOP        │
              └──▶ observe ACTUAL ──▶ compare to DESIRED ──▶ act to close the gap ──┘
                   (runs forever — this is essentially the ONLY thing Kubernetes does)

  ┌─ what "act to close the gap" actually means ─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   actual 2  &lt; desired 3   →  create 1 pod         pod is unhealthy   →  restart it  │
  │   actual 4  &gt; desired 3   →  delete 1 pod         image v1 ≠ v2      →  roll out v2  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  💡 You never list the STEPS — only the DESTINATION. The loop figures out the rest.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Why "Declarative + Reconciliation" Is Such a Big Deal</h4>
                <p>It's easy to read "you declare desired state" and shrug. But this single design choice is <em>why</em> Kubernetes won — it hands you three superpowers for free, none of which a step-by-step (imperative) system can offer:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">♻️</div>
                        <h5>1 · Idempotency</h5>
                        <p>Apply the same manifest once or a hundred times — same result. If reality already matches, the loop finds no gap and does nothing. No "already exists" errors, no half-applied state.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>2 · Self-healing</h5>
                        <p>Because the loop never stops checking, a crash or a dead node is just a <em>new gap</em> to close. Recovery isn't a special code path — it's the <em>same</em> reconciliation running again.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>3 · Git as source of truth</h5>
                        <p>Desired state is a file, so it can live in Git — reviewed, versioned, diffed, rolled back. That's the entire basis of <strong>GitOps</strong> (Chapter 39).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>Contrast that with an imperative script ("create this, then start that, then…"). If it fails halfway, you're left in an unknown in-between state, and re-running it may double-create or error out. The declarative model sidesteps all of that: <strong>you never describe the steps, only the destination</strong> — so there's no fragile sequence to get wrong, and the cluster is free to reach the goal whatever way is currently safe. Hold onto this; literally every feature ahead (Deployments, HPA, rollouts, operators) is just a controller closing a gap.</p>

                <h4>Quick Facts</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☸️</div>
                        <h5>Name Origin</h5>
                        <p><strong>K8s</strong> = K + <em>8 letters</em> + s. Greek <em>κυβερνήτης</em> = "helmsman" — steering the ship.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏛️</div>
                        <h5>Born at Google</h5>
                        <p>Evolved from Google's internal <strong>Borg</strong>. Open-sourced 2014, donated to the <strong>CNCF</strong>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔤</div>
                        <h5>Written in Go</h5>
                        <p>Built in Google's Go for high concurrency, compiled performance, and clean APIs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>Apache 2.0</h5>
                        <p>Fully open-source — free to use, modify, and run commercially.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes Lineage — from Borg to K8s</div>
<pre>
    2003 ───────────────▶ 2013 ───────────────▶ 2014 ───────────────▶ 2018 ─────▶ today
  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐
  │ 🏛️ Google BORG    │─▶│ 🔬 Google OMEGA   │─▶│ ☸️  KUBERNETES     │─▶│ 🎓 CNCF GRADUATE  │
  │ internal cluster │  │ next-gen         │  │ Borg's ideas,    │  │ 1st CNCF project │
  │ manager; ran     │  │ scheduler        │  │ rebuilt &amp; OPEN-  │  │ to graduate;     │
  │ Gmail, Search,   │  │ research:        │  │ SOURCED, donated │  │ now among the    │
  │ YouTube, Maps    │  │ flexible queues, │  │ to the CNCF.     │  │ largest open-    │
  │ for 15+ years    │  │ multi-scheduler  │  │ Written in Go.   │  │ source projects  │
  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘  └──────────────────┘
   "run everything       lessons learned        "give everyone        the de-facto
    in containers"        → cleaner design        Borg, for free"       standard ☸️
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kubernetes by the Numbers</h4>
                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">CNCF survey — organizations using/evaluating K8s</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">96%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:96%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Fortune 100 companies running Kubernetes</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">77%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:77%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">GitHub stars — top-tier OSS project</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">112K+</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:88%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Active contributors</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">3,500+</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:70%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Declarative vs Imperative — See the Difference</h4>
                <p>This distinction is the heart of working with Kubernetes (and a recurring exam theme). <strong>Imperative</strong> = you give step-by-step commands ("do this, now"). <strong>Declarative</strong> = you describe the end state in a file and let K8s reconcile it. Same result, very different philosophy:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">imperative — fast, one-off</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Tell K8s exactly what to do, step by step</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj \
  --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=5
kubectl set image deployment/easypj \
  easypj=easypj:v3
<span class="comment"># Great for speed on the exam,</span>
<span class="comment"># but nothing records your intent.</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">
                            <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">declarative — the K8s way</span>
                        </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Describe the end state in a file…</span>
<span class="comment"># deploy.yaml:  replicas: 5, image: easypj:v3</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
<span class="comment"># Run it again? No harm — already matches.</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml   <span class="comment"># idempotent</span>
<span class="comment"># The file is the source of truth → Git,</span>
<span class="comment"># code review, and GitOps all work.</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Best of both on the exam</h5>
                    <p>Generate YAML <em>imperatively</em>, then apply it <em>declaratively</em>: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml</code>, tweak, then <code>kubectl apply -f d.yaml</code>. You get imperative speed <em>and</em> a declarative artifact.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Kubernetes Is <em>NOT</em></h4>
                <p>Understanding the boundaries is as important as understanding the features — it stops you from expecting things K8s doesn't do:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div>
                        <h5>Not a build tool</h5>
                        <p>It does <strong>not</strong> build container images. You build with Docker/BuildKit/Kaniko in CI; K8s only <em>runs</em> the resulting images.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔁</div>
                        <h5>Not a CI/CD system</h5>
                        <p>It doesn't run your test pipeline. Tools like GitHub Actions, Jenkins, or Argo/Flux sit <em>around</em> K8s to deliver into it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">☁️</div>
                        <h5>Not a PaaS</h5>
                        <p>Unlike Heroku, it won't "just run my code." It orchestrates <em>containers</em> — you still package the app and declare how it runs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗄️</div>
                        <h5>Not a database (and won't manage your data)</h5>
                        <p>etcd stores <em>cluster</em> state, not your app data. Persisting easypj's PostgreSQL data is <em>your</em> job (PersistentVolumes, backups).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Remember the equation</h5>
                    <p><strong>Desired State → [control loops] → Actual State</strong>. Every Kubernetes feature you'll learn — Deployments, self-healing, autoscaling, rollouts — is just a controller closing the gap between what you declared and what's actually running.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.3 The CKA Perspective: Administrator vs Developer</h3>
                <p>There are two Kubernetes certifications from the Linux Foundation, and they test fundamentally different skills. Knowing the boundary tells you exactly what to study.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👩‍💻</div>
                        <h5>CKAD — Developer</h5>
                        <p>"I deploy &amp; configure <strong>apps</strong> on a cluster someone else runs."</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Create Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps</li>
                            <li>Configure probes &amp; resource limits</li>
                            <li>Build multi-container pods</li>
                            <li>Manage the application lifecycle</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>CKA — Administrator ← YOU</h5>
                        <p>"I <strong>build, operate &amp; troubleshoot the cluster itself</strong>."</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Install clusters with kubeadm</li>
                            <li>Manage certificates, PKI &amp; RBAC</li>
                            <li>Back up &amp; restore etcd</li>
                            <li>Upgrade versions, drain &amp; patch nodes</li>
                            <li>Configure networking (CNI, CoreDNS)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The administrator owns EVERYTHING below the app</div>
<pre>
          🌐 users ───▶ GET /jobs/        (the easypj app, serving traffic)
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   👩‍💻 DEVELOPER (CKAD) — owns the APP layer
        📦 Pods · Deployments · Services · ConfigMaps · Secrets · health probes
  ───────────────────────────── ⬇ the dividing line ⬇ ───────────────────────────
   🔧 ADMINISTRATOR (CKA ← YOU) — owns EVERYTHING below the app:
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────┐
   │ 🛡️  Access &amp; security    RBAC · ServiceAccounts · PodSecurity       │ Arch 25% │
   │ 🌐 Networking            CNI · CoreDNS · kube-proxy · Ingress       │ Net  20% │
   │ 💾 Storage               PV · PVC · StorageClasses · CSI            │ Stor 10% │
   │ 🧠 Control plane         apiserver · etcd · scheduler · ctrl-mgr    │ Arch 25% │
   │ 🔐 Cluster lifecycle     kubeadm install · upgrades · certs / PKI   │ Arch 25% │
   │ 🖥️  Nodes &amp; runtime       kubelet · containerd · drain / cordon      │ Arch/Tbl │
   │ 🩺 Keeping it alive      troubleshoot · etcd backup/restore · HA    │ Tbl  30% │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴──────────┘
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
          🖧 infrastructure   (VMs · bare metal · cloud instances)
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CKA Exam Domain Weights</h4>
                <p>The CKA tests five domains. Know exactly where each topic fits — and that <strong>troubleshooting is the single biggest slice</strong>:</p>
                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">25%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:25%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Workloads &amp; Scheduling</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">15%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:15%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Services &amp; Networking</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">20%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:20%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Storage</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">10%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width:10%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Troubleshooting</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">30%</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width:30%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Troubleshooting is 30% of the CKA</h5>
                    <p>Unlike the CKAD (which is about building apps), the CKA dedicates <strong>30%</strong> to fixing broken clusters — failed nodes, crashed control-plane components, networking and DNS issues — under time pressure. Part 9 of this guide is built entirely around a repeatable diagnostic method for exactly this.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Five Domains, Explained — What Each One Actually Tests</h4>
                <p>The percentages above are just bars until you know what hides behind each. Here's the plain-English meaning of every domain, the kind of task you'll be handed, and where easypj meets it in this guide — so the exam blueprint becomes a concrete study plan instead of five numbers:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 The CKA 2025 blueprint, decoded</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏗️</span> Cluster Architecture <strong>25%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Build &amp; configure the cluster itself: install with <code>kubeadm</code>, manage RBAC, set up Helm/Kustomize, understand the control plane. <em>Typical task:</em> "create a ServiceAccount + Role + RoleBinding so easypj can read its ConfigMap." → Parts 1, 3, 7, 11.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚙️</span> Workloads &amp; Scheduling <strong>15%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Run apps correctly: Deployments, rolling updates/rollbacks, scaling, scheduling (affinity, taints), resource limits, config/secrets. <em>Typical task:</em> "roll easypj to v2 with zero downtime, then roll it back." → Part 4.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> Services &amp; Networking <strong>20%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Connect &amp; expose: Services, DNS/CoreDNS, Ingress, the Gateway API, NetworkPolicies. <em>Typical task:</em> "expose easypj on a NodePort and restrict who may reach the DB." → Part 5.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Storage <strong>10%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Persist data: PersistentVolumes, PVCs, StorageClasses, dynamic provisioning. <em>Typical task:</em> "give easypj's PostgreSQL a 5Gi PVC that survives a pod restart." → Part 6.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🩺</span> Troubleshooting <strong>30%</strong></div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Diagnose &amp; fix broken things: NotReady nodes, crashed control-plane pods, failing Services/DNS, stuck workloads. <em>Typical task:</em> "easypj pods are Pending / the node is NotReady — find and fix it." → Part 9 (the biggest, most point-dense domain).</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 How to read these weights as a strategy</h5>
                    <p>Troubleshooting (30%) + Architecture (25%) = <strong>over half the exam</strong>, and both reward <em>method</em> over memorization. Storage is only 10% but its tasks are quick, high-certainty points — never skip them. The smart play: bank the fast Storage/Workloads tasks first, then spend your remaining time on the heavier Troubleshooting and Architecture problems.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>CKA vs CKAD — Who Owns What</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>CKAD (Developer)</th><th>CKA (Administrator) ← YOU</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Focus</strong></td><td>App deployment &amp; config</td><td>Cluster lifecycle &amp; infrastructure</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Format</strong></td><td>2 h · ~15–20 hands-on tasks</td><td>2 h · ~15–20 hands-on tasks</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Passing score</strong></td><td>66%</td><td>66%</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>Backup, restore, snapshot</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubeadm</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>Install, upgrade, configure</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Certificates</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>CSR approval, TLS, rotation</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Node management</strong></td><td>Not tested</td><td>Drain, cordon, troubleshoot</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Networking</strong></td><td>Services, Ingress basics</td><td>CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>CKA Exam Logistics — Quick Reference</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Exam Format</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Duration:</strong> 2 hours</li>
                            <li><strong>Tasks:</strong> 15–20 performance-based</li>
                            <li><strong>Passing:</strong> 66%</li>
                            <li><strong>Environment:</strong> live Linux terminal (browser)</li>
                            <li><strong>Version:</strong> latest stable K8s</li>
                            <li><strong>Retake:</strong> 1 free retake included</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📚</div>
                        <h5>Allowed Resources</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code></li>
                            <li>✅ <code>kubernetes.io/blog</code></li>
                            <li>✅ <code>github.com/kubernetes</code></li>
                            <li>❌ No other sites, notes, or AI tools</li>
                            <li>💡 Practice navigating the docs <em>fast</em></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Kubernetes Release Cycle — What an Admin Must Track</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes ships <strong>three minor versions a year</strong> (~every 4 months: v1.31, v1.32, v1.33…). As the admin, keeping the cluster inside the supported window — and upgrading it safely — is <em>your</em> recurring job, and a guaranteed exam task. Each release has a life:</p>
                <div class="evolution-strip">
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🆕</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">Month 0</div>
                        <h5>New Release</h5>
                        <p>e.g. v1.33 ships — new features, deprecations, and bug fixes land.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Latest</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item active">
                        <div class="evo-icon">🛡️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">~0–14 months</div>
                        <h5>Supported</h5>
                        <p>Gets patch releases (security &amp; bug fixes). The CKA tests within this window.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status good">✅ Patched</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="evo-item">
                        <div class="evo-icon">⚠️</div>
                        <div class="evo-era">14+ months</div>
                        <h5>End of Life</h5>
                        <p>No more patches. Running EOL = a growing security risk → you must upgrade.</p>
                        <div class="evo-status bad">❌ Unsupported</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The Golden Upgrade Rule: one minor version at a time</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only supports upgrading <strong>one minor version at a step</strong> — <code>1.31 → 1.32 → 1.33</code>. You <strong>cannot skip</strong> (no 1.31 → 1.33 jump). There's also a <strong>version-skew</strong> rule: the kubelet may lag the API server by up to 3 minors but never run ahead of it. The CKA puts you in front of a real cluster and asks you to upgrade it with <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (control plane first, then workers) — you'll do exactly this in Chapter 28.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>What Exam Day Actually Looks Like</h4>
                <p>Knowing the format removes half the stress. The CKA is taken <strong>remotely, in your browser</strong>, proctored live through the PSI platform — here's the flow end-to-end:</p>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Check-in</h5><p>Show photo ID, do a 360° room scan via webcam. Desk must be clear; you're watched the whole time.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>The terminal</h5><p>A real Linux terminal in the browser, with multiple clusters. You SSH/context-switch between them per task.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>15–20 tasks</h5><p>Each is weighted and independent. A score bar shows weights — do the cheap, high-value ones first.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Docs allowed</h5><p>One extra tab to <code>kubernetes.io/docs</code> (+ blog, GitHub). Nothing else. Practice copy-paste from the docs.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Flag &amp; move on</h5><p>Stuck &gt; a few minutes? Flag it, move on, return later. Never leave a task blank — partial credit is real.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Results &amp; retake</h5><p>Score by email in ~24 h. 66% to pass. One <strong>free retake</strong> included; the cert is valid <strong>3 years</strong>.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 The #1 exam-day habit: set context first, every task</h5>
                    <p>Each task may use a <em>different</em> cluster/namespace. The most common way to lose points is doing correct work in the <strong>wrong place</strong>. Start every single task with the context switch the question gives you:</p>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">muscle memory for the exam</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config use-context &lt;cluster-from-the-question&gt;
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;   <span class="comment"># then work</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <p>Free, realistic practice: the <strong>killer.sh</strong> CKA simulator (2 sessions included with your exam registration) mirrors the real environment closely — Chapter 45 builds a full timed simulator on easypj.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three Kubernetes Certifications</h4>
                <p>The Linux Foundation offers three hands-on Kubernetes certs. They build on each other — and the CKA (this guide) is the foundation the others assume:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👩‍💻</div>
                        <h5>CKAD — Developer</h5>
                        <p><strong>Focus:</strong> deploying &amp; configuring apps. <br><strong>Owns:</strong> Pods, Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, probes, multi-container patterns.</p>
                        <p><span class="badge">2 h · 66% to pass</span></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>CKA — Administrator ← this guide</h5>
                        <p><strong>Focus:</strong> building &amp; operating the cluster. <br><strong>Owns:</strong> install, etcd, certs, upgrades, nodes, RBAC, networking, storage, troubleshooting.</p>
                        <p><span class="badge badge-exam">2 h · 66% to pass</span></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>CKS — Security Specialist</h5>
                        <p><strong>Focus:</strong> hardening &amp; securing clusters. <br><strong>Owns:</strong> Pod Security, supply-chain, runtime security, network policy, audit. <em>Requires a valid CKA first.</em></p>
                        <p><span class="badge">2 h · 67% to pass</span></p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Which order?</h5>
                    <p>CKA is the natural starting point: it's the platform layer the other two stand on. CKAD overlaps with CKA on workloads; CKS literally <em>requires</em> you to already hold the CKA. Master this guide and you're well-positioned for all three.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🧠 Analogy</h5>
                    <p>CKAD <strong>furnishes the rooms</strong>; CKA <strong>builds &amp; maintains the house</strong> — foundations, plumbing, and wiring included; CKS <strong>installs the locks, alarms, and security cameras</strong>. This guide is the house.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.4 Kubernetes Core Concepts — The Big Picture</h3>
                <p>Everything in Kubernetes is built from five ideas. Lock these in now — they're the mental model you'll use for the entire CKA journey.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-5">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🗂️</div>
                        <h5>1 · Cluster</h5>
                        <p>A set of machines (nodes) Kubernetes manages as a single unit.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧠</div>
                        <h5>2 · Control Plane</h5>
                        <p>The "brain" — makes all scheduling &amp; orchestration decisions.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💪</div>
                        <h5>3 · Worker Nodes</h5>
                        <p>The "muscle" — actually run your containerized apps.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>4 · Pod</h5>
                        <p>Smallest deployable unit — 1+ containers sharing IP &amp; storage.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>5 · Desired State</h5>
                        <p>You declare <em>what</em> you want; K8s continuously keeps it that way.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Words You'll Keep Hearing — a Beginner's Glossary</h4>
                <p>Before the diagrams, here's a plain-English decoder for the terms that appear on every page from here on. Bookmark this — you won't feel lost again:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📖 Core vocabulary</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> container</div><div class="anatomy-value">A packaged, isolated process — your app + its dependencies. The thing that actually runs easypj.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🖼️</span> image</div><div class="anatomy-value">The read-only template a container is started from, e.g. <code>easypj:v2</code>. Built once, run anywhere.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> pod</div><div class="anatomy-value">The smallest thing K8s schedules — one (or a few) containers that share an IP &amp; storage.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💪</span> node</div><div class="anatomy-value">A machine (VM or physical) that runs pods. The cluster is a set of nodes.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗂️</span> cluster</div><div class="anatomy-value">All your nodes + the control plane, managed as one system.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏷️</span> namespace</div><div class="anatomy-value">A virtual folder that partitions a cluster (e.g. <code>easypj</code>, <code>kube-system</code>) for isolation &amp; quotas.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📄</span> manifest</div><div class="anatomy-value">A YAML file describing a desired object (a Deployment, Service…). The "what you want."</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🧩</span> object / resource</div><div class="anatomy-value">Any thing K8s tracks in etcd — Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Node. Everything is an object.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> controller</div><div class="anatomy-value">A loop that watches objects and drives actual → desired (e.g. the Deployment controller).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⌨️</span> kubectl</div><div class="anatomy-value">The command-line tool you use to talk to the API server ("koob-cuddle" / "koob-control").</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>How the Objects Nest — the Workload Hierarchy</h4>
                <p>Beginners often blur Pod, ReplicaSet, and Deployment. They're <strong>layers</strong>, each wrapping the one below and adding a capability. You almost always create the <em>outermost</em> one and let it manage the rest:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod → Container (inside a Namespace)</div>
<pre>
  🏷️ Namespace: easypj   (a virtual folder that isolates these objects)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ 🚀 Deployment  "easypj-web"                       ← you create THIS     │
  │    adds ▶ rolling updates · rollback · revision history                │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  "easypj-web-6d8f9"   (one per pod-template version) │ │
  │  │    adds ▶ "keep exactly N pods alive"  → self-healing              │ │
  │  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐   N = 3      │ │
  │  │  │ 📦 Pod         │  │ 📦 Pod         │  │ 📦 Pod         │            │ │
  │  │  │  …-6d8f9-x7k2 │  │  …-6d8f9-m9p4 │  │  …-6d8f9-q2j7 │            │ │
  │  │  │ ┌──────────┐ │  │ ┌──────────┐ │  │ ┌──────────┐ │            │ │
  │  │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │  │ │🐋 web :8000│ │ ← container│ │
  │  │  │ └──────────┘ │  │ └──────────┘ │  │ └──────────┘ │            │ │
  │  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘            │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │  🕓 old ReplicaSet "easypj-web-5a1c" → scaled to 0, kept for rollback   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   naming chain ▶  Deployment ──(+ pod-template-hash)──▶ ReplicaSet ──(+ random)──▶ Pod
   rule of thumb ▶  you create the Deployment; it makes the ReplicaSet; the RS makes the
                    Pods; each Pod runs your container(s). You almost never make a bare Pod.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why not just create Pods directly?</h5>
                    <p>A bare Pod is a <em>pet</em> — if it dies, it's gone for good (nothing recreates it). Wrap it in a ReplicaSet and you get self-healing; wrap that in a Deployment and you also get rolling updates &amp; rollback. That's why in real life you create <strong>Deployments</strong>, almost never naked Pods.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Architecture — Bird's-Eye View</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">One easypj cluster at a glance</div>
<pre>
                          👩‍💻 admin / CI
                               │  kubectl   (HTTPS, port 6443)
                               ▼
   ╔══════════════════════ 🧠 CONTROL PLANE — "the brain" (decides) ══════════════════════╗
   ║                                                                                       ║
   ║   ┌─────────────────────┐      watch       ┌──────────────────────────┐               ║
   ║   │ 📋 kube-scheduler     │ ───────────────▶ │                          │               ║
   ║   │  "which node fits?"   │                  │   📡 kube-apiserver        │               ║
   ║   └─────────────────────┘                  │   the ONE front door     │               ║
   ║   ┌─────────────────────┐      watch       │   authn → authz →        │   read /      ║
   ║   │ 🔄 controller-manager │ ───────────────▶ │   admission → validate   │ ◀═══════════▶ ║
   ║   │  "keep 3 replicas up" │                  │                          │   write       ║
   ║   └─────────────────────┘                  └──────────────────────────┘     ▲         ║
   ║                                                                              ║         ║
   ║                                                            ┌─────────────────╨──────┐  ║
   ║   ⚠️ ONLY the API server touches etcd. Every other          │ 🗄️  etcd  (key-value DB) │  ║
   ║      component watches/acts THROUGH the API server.        │     = ALL cluster state │  ║
   ║                                                            └────────────────────────┘  ║
   ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
                  │  scheduler binds pod → node ; the node's kubelet then pulls & runs it
   ═══════════════╪═══════════════════ 🌐 flat pod network (CNI: Calico / Cilium) ═══════════════
                  ▼
   ┌──────────── 💪 WORKER NODE 1 ────────────┐     ┌──────────── 💪 WORKER NODE 2 ────────────┐
   │ 🤖 kubelet  ·  🔀 kube-proxy  ·  🐋 containerd │     │ 🤖 kubelet  ·  🔀 kube-proxy  ·  🐋 containerd │
   │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐ │     │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
   │ │ 📦 easypj-web   :8000   ip 10.244.1.5     │ │     │ │ 🐘 postgres     :5432   ip 10.244.2.4    │ │
   │ │ 📦 easypj-web   :8000   ip 10.244.1.6     │ │     │ │    └─ mounts 💾 PVC  (durable disk)       │ │
   │ │ 📦 easypj-web   :8000   ip 10.244.1.7     │ │     │ │ 🤖 kubelet ── reports status ──▶ API svr │ │
   │ └────────────────────────────────────────┘ │     │ └────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────┘     └───────────────────────────────────────────┘

   DESIRED (declared, stored in etcd):  3 × easypj-web  +  1 × postgres
        ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶  reconciled, forever
   ACTUAL (actually running on the nodes):  exactly that  ✅

   Legend   ──▶ request / data flow      ◀── watch = a component observes desired state via the API
            📡 API server = the hub (only it reads/writes 🗄️ etcd)   🌐 CNI = the network all pods share
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Preview — the components (full deep-dive in Chapter 3)</h5>
                    <p>You'll meet each component properly in <strong>Chapter 3: Kubernetes Architecture</strong>. For now, just learn the one-liners — they make every later chapter click.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🧠 Control Plane Components — the admin must know these (Architecture = 25%)</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📡</span> kube-apiserver</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>front door</strong> — ALL communication goes through it (kubectl, node heartbeats, controllers). It's the <em>only</em> component that talks to etcd. If it's down, the cluster is blind.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗄️</span> etcd</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The cluster's <strong>memory</strong> — a distributed key-value store holding ALL cluster state. <strong>CKA tests backup &amp; restore.</strong> Lose etcd with no backup = lose the whole cluster.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> kube-scheduler</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>matchmaker</strong> — watches for unscheduled Pods and picks the best node by resources, affinity, and taints/tolerations.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> kube-controller-manager</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>watchdog</strong> — runs control loops that compare desired vs actual and act ("only 2 of 3 replicas → create 1 more").</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">☁️</span> cloud-controller-manager</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Cloud glue</strong> (optional) — integrates with AWS/Azure/GCP for load balancers, disks, and node lifecycle. Absent on bare-metal.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What Happens When Each Component Goes Down?</h4>
                <p>Memorizing what each component <em>does</em> is half the battle; the deeper understanding — and what the 30% troubleshooting domain really tests — is knowing what <strong>breaks</strong> when each one fails. This table is worth more than any definition, because it turns "the scheduler schedules pods" into "if pods are stuck <code>Pending</code>, look at the scheduler":</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>If this is down…</th><th>What you observe</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td><code>kubectl</code> fails; nothing can be changed. <em>Running pods keep running.</em></td><td>It's the only door to the cluster — no reads/writes, no scheduling, no controller actions. The cluster "freezes" but doesn't crash.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>API server can't serve → whole control plane unusable. Lost with no backup = cluster gone.</td><td>It holds <em>all</em> state. No etcd, no source of truth to read or write.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>New pods stay <code>Pending</code> forever. Existing pods are unaffected.</td><td>Nothing is left to assign pods to nodes — they sit unscheduled.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>No self-healing: a crashed pod isn't replaced, scaling/rollouts stall, dead nodes aren't acted on.</td><td>The reconciliation loops live here — without them, desired ≠ actual is never corrected.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong> (on a node)</td><td>That node goes <code>NotReady</code>; after a grace period its pods are rescheduled elsewhere.</td><td>No agent to run/report pods on that node, so the control plane writes it off.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The one insight that makes troubleshooting click</h5>
                    <p>The control plane is the <strong>brain</strong>; running pods are the <strong>body</strong>. Knock out the brain (API server / etcd) and the body keeps moving on reflex — your apps stay up, you just can't <em>change</em> anything. This is why an etcd or apiserver outage is silent to users but paralyzing to admins, and why "my app still works but <code>kubectl</code> is dead" points straight at the control plane, not the workload. Match the <em>symptom</em> to the <em>component</em> and you've solved most of the exam's troubleshooting tasks before typing a command.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>💪 Worker Node Components</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🤖</span> kubelet</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>node agent</strong> — takes pod specs from the API server, makes the containers run &amp; stay healthy, and reports node status back.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔀</span> kube-proxy</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>network plumber</strong> — programs the node's dataplane rules so Services route to the right pods.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> container runtime</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>engine</strong> that actually runs containers — containerd (default) or CRI-O via the CRI. Docker-shim was removed in v1.24.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Desired vs Actual State — the Declarative Magic</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Reconciliation Loop</div>
<pre>
                 ┌────────────────── 🔄 ONE CONTROLLER, RUNNING FOREVER ──────────────────┐
                 │                                                                        │
     ┌───────────▼───────────┐      ┌───────────────────┐      ┌─────────────────────────┐
     │ 1. 👁️ OBSERVE           │ ───▶ │ 2. 🧮 DIFF          │ ───▶ │ 3. 🛠️ ACT                 │
     │ read ACTUAL (cluster)   │      │ does ACTUAL match  │      │ create / delete / update │
     │ + DESIRED (from etcd)   │      │ DESIRED? a gap?    │      │ objects to close the gap │
     └─────────────────────────┘      └───────────────────┘      └────────────┬────────────┘
                 ▲                                                             │
                 └──────────────────────────── repeat ◀─────────────────────────┘

  easypj tick-by-tick — the Deployment controller, desired replicas = 3:
    t0   observe 0 pods    · diff: need 3    · act: create 3            → 3 running
    t1   observe 3 pods    · diff: none       · act: nothing  (idempotent — no churn)
    t2   a pod crashes 💥   · observe 2 pods  · diff: need 1   · act: create 1  → 3 running
    t3   you set image v2  · diff: v1 ≠ v2    · act: replace pods one-by-one  → all v2

  💡 Recovery is NOT a special code path — it's the SAME loop noticing a NEW gap.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>How a Pod Gets Created — the Full Journey</h4>
                <p>Here's the single most clarifying flow in all of Kubernetes: what <em>actually</em> happens between you typing <code>kubectl apply</code> and easypj serving traffic. Watch how every component plays its part. (Chapter 3 dissects each step; this is the map.)</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl apply → a running easypj pod</div>
<pre>
  👩‍💻 kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
        │  (1) HTTPS request
        ▼
  📡 kube-apiserver ──(2) authn → authz → admission → validate──┐
        │                                                       │
        │  (3) write DESIRED object                             │
        ▼                                                       │
  🗄️ etcd  "Deployment easypj wants 3 pods"  ◀─────────────────┘
        │
        │  (4) controller-manager sees Deployment → makes ReplicaSet → 3 Pod objects
        │      (Pods exist but have NO node yet → "Pending")
        ▼
  📋 kube-scheduler  (5) "which node fits?"  → binds Pod → Node 2 (writes to API→etcd)
        │
        ▼
  🤖 kubelet on Node 2  (6) "a pod was assigned to me!"
        │   ├─ 🐋 tells containerd to PULL easypj:v2 and START the container
        │   └─ 🌐 CNI plugin gives the pod its IP
        ▼
  📦 easypj pod RUNNING :8000   (7) kubelet reports status ──▶ API server ──▶ etcd
        │
        ▼
  🔄 controllers keep watching forever: actual=3 = desired=3  ✅  (reconciled)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Submit</h5><p>kubectl sends your YAML to the API server over TLS.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Admit</h5><p>API server authenticates, authorizes (RBAC), runs admission, validates.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Persist</h5><p>Desired state is written to etcd — the single source of truth.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Expand</h5><p>Controllers turn the Deployment into a ReplicaSet into Pod objects (Pending).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>Schedule</h5><p>The scheduler picks the best node and binds each pod to it.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>Run</h5><p>That node's kubelet pulls the image, starts the container, CNI assigns an IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>Report</h5><p>kubelet reports status up; controllers reconcile forever.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Why this matters for troubleshooting</h5>
                    <p>Every "my pod won't run" problem lives at <em>one</em> of these steps. <strong>Pending</strong> = stuck at step 5 (scheduler can't place it). <strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> = step 6 (kubelet can't pull). <strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> = step 6 (container starts then dies). Knowing the journey tells you exactly <em>where</em> to look — the core skill behind the 30% troubleshooting domain.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why declarative matters for the CKA</h5>
                    <p>The exam rewards working declaratively: declare desired state, <code>kubectl apply -f</code>, and trust the controllers. It's also the key to troubleshooting — when something breaks, you compare <strong>desired state</strong> (in etcd) against <strong>actual state</strong> (on the nodes) and find the gap.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.5 The Kubernetes Ecosystem</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes is a <strong>core that everything plugs into</strong>. It sits at the center of a large ecosystem of runtimes, plugins, and platforms — all governed by the <strong>Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)</strong>. As an admin you'll touch many of these daily.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The ecosystem orbit — a core everything plugs into</div>
<pre>
                  📦 PACKAGING                          🔄 GitOps / DELIVERY
               Helm · Kustomize                          Argo CD · Flux
                        ╲                                    ╱
                         ╲                                  ╱
   🐋 RUNTIME (CRI)    ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐    🌐 NETWORK (CNI)
   containerd · CRI-O ─┤                                        ├─ Calico · Cilium · Flannel
                       │        ☸️   KUBERNETES  CORE             │
   💾 STORAGE (CSI)    │   the orchestration engine:            │    📊 OBSERVABILITY
   EBS · PD · Ceph ───┤   schedule · heal · scale · reconcile   ├─ Prometheus · Grafana
   NFS                 │        (drives desired → actual)        │    Loki · OpenTelemetry
                       └────────────────────────────────────────┘
                         ╱                                  ╲
                        ╱                                    ╲
                  🕸️ SERVICE MESH                       🛡️ SECURITY / POLICY
               Istio · Linkerd                          Kyverno · OPA Gatekeeper

   ☁️  Everything around the core is governed by the CNCF (Cloud Native Computing Foundation).
   💡 CRI · CNI · CSI are the three "sockets" Kubernetes DEFINES — vendors just supply the plugs.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Key Ecosystem Categories for the CKA</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>Container Runtimes (CRI)</h5>
                        <p><strong>containerd</strong> (default since v1.24), <strong>CRI-O</strong>. K8s talks to them via the Container Runtime Interface; the Docker shim was removed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CNI Plugins (networking)</h5>
                        <p><strong>Calico</strong> &amp; <strong>Cilium</strong> (with NetworkPolicy), <strong>Flannel</strong> (simple overlay), <strong>Weave</strong>. You install a CNI during cluster setup — no CNI = NotReady nodes.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Packaging</h5>
                        <p><strong>Helm</strong> (templated charts — the "apt for K8s") and <strong>Kustomize</strong> (template-free overlays). Both are 2025 CKA topics.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div>
                        <h5>GitOps</h5>
                        <p><strong>ArgoCD</strong>, <strong>FluxCD</strong> — Git is the source of truth; a controller syncs the cluster to match and self-heals drift.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🕸️</div>
                        <h5>Service Mesh</h5>
                        <p><strong>Istio</strong>, <strong>Linkerd</strong> — observability, traffic management, and mTLS between services without touching app code.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📊</div>
                        <h5>Observability</h5>
                        <p><strong>Prometheus</strong> (metrics), <strong>Grafana</strong> (dashboards), <strong>Loki</strong> (logs), <strong>Jaeger</strong> / <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> (traces). The CKA expects <code>kubectl top</code> + metrics-server.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CNCF — who governs Kubernetes?</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>Cloud Native Computing Foundation</strong> (part of the Linux Foundation) stewards Kubernetes and 170+ related projects through three maturity levels — <strong>Sandbox → Incubating → Graduated</strong>. Kubernetes was the first to graduate (2018). The CNCF also runs the CKA &amp; CKAD certifications.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="arch-layers">
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-app">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🎓</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Graduated</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Production-proven, widely adopted</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Kubernetes</span><span>Prometheus</span><span>containerd</span><span>CoreDNS</span><span>etcd</span><span>Helm</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-k8s">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🧪</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Incubating</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Growing adoption, maturing features</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Argo</span><span>Cilium</span><span>Knative</span><span>Kyverno</span><span>Backstage</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-infra">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🌱</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Sandbox</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Early-stage, experimental</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>Headlamp</span><span>OpenCost</span><span>Inspektor Gadget</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What "Cloud Native" Actually Means</h4>
                <p>"Cloud native" is the phrase behind the CNCF's name and gets thrown around constantly — but it's rarely defined. It's not "runs in the cloud." It's a <strong>way of building software</strong> so it can be operated by software (like Kubernetes). Five properties define it, and easypj will gain each one as we go:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Containerized</h5><p>Each piece is packaged with its dependencies into an image — portable and isolated. (easypj → a container in Ch 2.)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧩</div><h5>Loosely coupled</h5><p>Independent services talk over the network (web ↔ DB), so each can scale, fail, and deploy on its own.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🤖</div><h5>Dynamically orchestrated</h5><p>A platform schedules, scales, and heals it automatically — no human placing processes on machines.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Declarative</h5><p>Desired state lives in version-controlled manifests, not in someone's head or a runbook.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>Resilient &amp; observable</h5><p>It expects failure and recovers; it exposes metrics, logs, and traces so you can see what it's doing.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why the term matters to you</h5>
                    <p>Every tool in the orbit above exists to deliver one of these five properties. So "cloud native" isn't marketing — it's the <em>checklist</em> the whole ecosystem is built around. When you finish this guide, easypj will satisfy all five, and you'll be the administrator who keeps the platform that makes that possible running.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three "Sockets" — How Kubernetes Stays Pluggable (CRI · CNI · CSI)</h4>
                <p>Why does such a huge ecosystem exist? Because Kubernetes itself doesn't run containers, wire up networks, or attach disks. Instead it defines three <strong>standard interfaces</strong> — think of them as universal sockets — and lets vendors plug in. This is <em>the</em> reason Kubernetes runs identically on your laptop, on bare metal, and on every cloud.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes defines the socket; you choose the plug</div>
<pre>
                       ☸️  KUBERNETES  (defines 3 standard interfaces)
                                       │
        ┌──────────────────────┬───────┴───────────────┬──────────────────────┐
        ▼                      ▼                        ▼
   🔌 CRI                  🔌 CNI                    🔌 CSI
   Container Runtime IF    Container Network IF      Container Storage IF
   kubelet asks:           K8s asks:                 K8s asks:
   "run / stop this        "give this pod            "attach this disk
    container"              an IP &amp; connect it"        to the pod"
        │                      │                         │
   any plug speaking ▼     any plug speaking ▼       any plug speaking ▼
   🐋 containerd · CRI-O   🌐 Calico · Cilium ·       💾 AWS EBS · GCP PD ·
                             Flannel                    Ceph · NFS
        │                      │                         │
        ▼                      ▼                         ▼
   starts the easypj       easypj pod 10.244.1.5     postgres data lives on a
   container on the node   reaches any pod, no NAT   real disk, survives restarts

   💡 Swap any plug (e.g. containerd → CRI-O) and Kubernetes ITSELF doesn't change.
      THAT is why one set of easypj manifests runs on a laptop, bare metal &amp; every cloud.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>CRI — Runtime</h5>
                        <p>How the kubelet asks something to <strong>run a container</strong>. Default: <strong>containerd</strong>. (You'll configure this in Chapter 9 when installing the cluster.)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>CNI — Network</h5>
                        <p>How pods get an <strong>IP and reach each other</strong>. You install a CNI (Calico/Cilium) right after <code>kubeadm init</code> — without one, nodes stay NotReady (Chapter 17).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>CSI — Storage</h5>
                        <p>How a <strong>real disk</strong> gets attached to a pod for durable data — exactly how easypj's PostgreSQL keeps its files (Chapter 22).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The big idea</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes is a <strong>platform for building platforms</strong>: it owns the orchestration logic and delegates the "hardware-touching" parts to pluggable drivers. Whenever you see "install a CNI" or "pick a StorageClass," you're choosing a plug for one of these sockets. Keep this model in your head — it makes Parts 5 (networking) and 6 (storage) click instantly.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §1.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.6 How Companies Use Kubernetes (a day in an admin's life)</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes isn't just for tech giants. Companies of every size run it — scale changes the <em>job</em>, not the <em>fundamentals</em>.</p>

                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Company Size</th><th>K8s Setup</th><th>Admin Tasks</th><th>Team</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>🏠 <strong>Startup</strong> (5–20)<br>e.g. easypj</td>
                            <td>1 cluster, few nodes. Minikube → managed cloud (EKS/AKS/GKE).</td>
                            <td>Deploy, monitor, basic troubleshooting, simple CI/CD.</td>
                            <td>1 admin does it all</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>🏢 <strong>Mid-size</strong> (50–200)</td>
                            <td>2–5 clusters. Separate dev / staging / prod; multi-team RBAC.</td>
                            <td>CI/CD, RBAC, upgrades, monitoring, cost control, policies.</td>
                            <td>2–4 admins</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>🏙️ <strong>Enterprise</strong> (500+)</td>
                            <td>Many HA, multi-region clusters; service mesh; custom networking.</td>
                            <td>HA &amp; DR, compliance, GitOps, multi-cluster, platform engineering.</td>
                            <td>Dedicated platform team</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>A Day in the Life of a CKA Administrator</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Morning Check</h5>
                        <p>Review dashboards; check node health, pod restarts, and overnight alerts.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Troubleshoot</h5>
                        <p>Investigate failed pods/nodes: <code>describe</code>, <code>logs</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Ship Changes</h5>
                        <p>Apply upgrades/RBAC/config via GitOps or PRs — staging first, then prod.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Secure &amp; Audit</h5>
                        <p>Review audit logs, rotate certificates that are due, verify NetworkPolicies, and check RBAC for over-permissioned accounts.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Maintain</h5>
                        <p>Drain → patch → uncordon a node; verify the latest etcd backup restores.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">6</div>
                        <h5>Capacity Plan</h5>
                        <p>Watch resource trends; add nodes or right-size requests/limits.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Real Companies Running Kubernetes at Scale</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎵</div><h5>Spotify</h5><p>Migrated 150+ services to K8s; deployments dropped from hours to minutes.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛒</div><h5>Shopify</h5><p>Survives Black Friday by auto-scaling clusters to ~10× normal load.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎮</div><h5>Pokémon GO</h5><p>Launched on GKE; scaled from 1× to ~50× expected traffic in days.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏦</div><h5>Capital One</h5><p>Runs regulated banking workloads on K8s — proof it works under compliance.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Organizations Actually Adopt Kubernetes (and What It Costs)</h4>
                <p>Spotify and Capital One didn't adopt Kubernetes for the logo — they adopted it because it pays back three concrete dividends. Understanding these tells you <em>when</em> K8s is the right call (and, honestly, when it isn't):</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚀</div>
                        <h5>1 · Velocity</h5>
                        <p>Ship more often, safely. Rolling updates + instant rollback + a declarative pipeline turn risky, hours-long deploys into routine, minutes-long ones — so teams release many times a day instead of monthly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>2 · Resilience</h5>
                        <p>Self-healing + rescheduling + HA mean a crashed pod or a dead node is a non-event, not a 3 AM page. Uptime stops depending on a human being awake.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💰</div>
                        <h5>3 · Efficiency</h5>
                        <p>Bin-packing many containers onto shared nodes (and autoscaling them) drives up utilization and drives down the cloud bill — versus one fat VM per app sitting mostly idle.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p>There's a fourth, quieter dividend: <strong>portability</strong>. Because workloads target the Kubernetes API (not a specific cloud), the same easypj manifests run on your laptop, on bare metal, and on any cloud — which is real leverage against lock-in.</p>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚖️ The honest trade-off — it isn't free</h5>
                    <p>Those dividends are bought with <strong>operational complexity</strong>: a control plane to run (or pay a cloud to run), certificates that expire, upgrades to perform, networking and storage to wire up, and a real learning curve. That complexity <em>is the administrator's job</em> — which is precisely why the CKA exists and why this skill is valuable. The rule of thumb: Kubernetes pays off when you have <em>multiple services, real scale/uptime needs, and frequent releases</em>; for a single low-traffic app, it's usually more machine than the job requires (see the "when NOT to use it" box below).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Managed vs Self-Managed — Who Runs the Control Plane?</h4>
                <p>There are two ways to get a cluster, and the difference decides what's <em>your</em> job. The CKA deliberately tests the <strong>self-managed</strong> path (you run everything), because once you understand that, managed clusters are easy.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>🛠️ Self-managed (kubeadm) — what the CKA tests</th><th>☁️ Managed (EKS / GKE / AKS)</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Control plane</strong></td><td>You install &amp; run it (API server, etcd, scheduler…)</td><td>The cloud runs &amp; upgrades it for you</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd backups</strong></td><td>Your responsibility (Chapter 29)</td><td>Handled by the provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Upgrades</strong></td><td>You run <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (Chapter 28)</td><td>Click/CLI; you still upgrade node pools</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Nodes</strong></td><td>You join &amp; maintain them</td><td>Managed node pools / autoscaling</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>You still own</strong></td><td colspan="2" class="tac">Workloads · RBAC · NetworkPolicy · storage · ingress — <strong>everything in this guide transfers to both</strong></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why learn the hard (self-managed) way?</h5>
                    <p>Because it makes you <em>unbreakable</em>. When a managed cluster misbehaves, the engineers who understand etcd, certificates, and the kubelet are the ones who fix it. Chapter 36 maps every skill here onto EKS/GKE/AKS — same concepts, the provider just runs the control plane.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚫 Honest Take: When NOT to Use Kubernetes</h5>
                    <p>Maturity means knowing the limits. Kubernetes is <em>overkill</em> when:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>A single small app, low traffic</strong> — a managed PaaS (Heroku, Render, Fly.io) or one VM is simpler and cheaper.</li>
                        <li><strong>No one to operate it</strong> — K8s adds real operational complexity; without an admin (you!) it becomes a liability.</li>
                        <li><strong>Static workloads that never scale</strong> — if you'll never need scaling, healing, or rolling updates, you're paying complexity for nothing.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">Use it when you have <strong>multiple services</strong>, need <strong>resilience &amp; scale</strong>, ship <strong>frequently</strong>, or want a <strong>portable</strong> platform across clouds — which is exactly where easypj is headed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Where you fit in — the easypj journey</h5>
                    <p>This guide follows <strong>easypj</strong> — our Django <code>jobpost</code> board — from a single-server app to a production-grade cluster. You'll containerize it, deploy it, scale it, secure it, break it, fix it, and ship it. By the end you'll pass the CKA <em>and</em> be able to run real clusters.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 1.7 OBJECT MODEL & API — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s1-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">1.7 The Object Model &amp; the Kubernetes API</h3>
                <p>Everything you will ever create is a <strong>Kubernetes object</strong> — a persisted record of intent the cluster works to make real. Four ideas make the whole API click: the <em>shape</em> of every object, how you <em>name</em> it, how the API is <em>organised &amp; versioned</em>, and the <em>ways you apply</em> it. Master these and manifests stop being magic.</p>

                <h4>① The four required fields — in every manifest, no exceptions</h4>
                <p>Every object you write carries the same four top-level fields. Omit one — or mis-case <code>kind</code> — and the apiserver rejects the manifest before it ever reaches etcd.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it is</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>apiVersion</code></td><td>Which API group + version defines this object (so the server knows its schema)</td><td><code>apps/v1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kind</code></td><td>The object type — what you're creating</td><td><code>Deployment</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>metadata</code></td><td>Identity: <code>name</code> (required), <code>namespace</code>, <code>labels</code>, <code>annotations</code>, and the server-set <code>uid</code></td><td><code>name: easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>spec</code></td><td>Your <strong>desired state</strong> — the shape varies per <code>kind</code></td><td><code>replicas: 3</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② <code>spec</code> vs <code>status</code> — desired vs actual, inside the object itself</h4>
                <p>Almost every object has two halves: a <code>spec</code> you write (the goal) and a <code>status</code> the cluster writes (reality). The reconciliation loop from §1.4 is just "keep <code>status</code> matching <code>spec</code>." You never edit <code>status</code> — <em>reading</em> it is how you troubleshoot.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o yaml (trimmed)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:                 <span class="comment"># 👈 YOU declare this — desired state</span>
  replicas: 3
status:               <span class="comment"># 👈 KUBERNETES writes this — actual state (read-only)</span>
  replicas: 3
  readyReplicas: 2    <span class="comment"># only 2 are Ready right now → a controller is closing the gap</span>
  conditions:
  - type: Available
    status: "False"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>③ Naming rules — why <code>easypj_web</code> is rejected</h4>
                <p>Object names aren't free-form. Most follow the <strong>DNS Subdomain</strong> rule; a few (pods, nodes, namespaces) follow the stricter <strong>DNS Label</strong> rule. Break them and you get a validation error, not a running object.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Used by</th><th>Max</th><th>Allowed</th><th>✅ valid · ❌ invalid</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Subdomain</strong></td><td>most objects: Deployments, ConfigMaps, Secrets…</td><td>253</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 - .</code>; start/end alphanumeric (may start with a digit)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code>, <code>app.prod</code> · ❌ <code>easypj_web</code>, <code>EasyPJ</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label</strong></td><td>namespaces, nodes &amp; many short names</td><td>63</td><td>lowercase <code>a–z 0–9 -</code> (<strong>no dots</strong>); <strong>start with a letter</strong>, end alphanumeric</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web-1</code> · ❌ <code>my.pod</code>, <code>POD-1</code>, <code>9web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>DNS Label (RFC 1035)</strong></td><td>Service names</td><td>63</td><td>same, but <strong>must start with a letter</strong> (a feature gate relaxes this)</td><td>✅ <code>easypj-web</code> · ❌ <code>9web</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>UID</strong></td><td>every object (set by the server)</td><td>—</td><td>cluster-unique, auto-generated, never user-supplied</td><td>tells apart two objects that reused a name over time</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>④ API groups, versions &amp; stability levels</h4>
                <p>The API is split into <strong>groups</strong>, each <strong>versioned independently</strong>. The <code>apiVersion</code> you write picks the group + version; the stability ladder tells you what's safe to depend on.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how apiVersion resolves to an API path</div>
<pre>
   apiVersion: v1          → CORE group    → /api/v1           Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Namespace · Node
   apiVersion: apps/v1     → "apps" group  → /apis/apps/v1     Deployment · ReplicaSet · StatefulSet · DaemonSet
   apiVersion: batch/v1    → "batch" group → /apis/batch/v1    Job · CronJob
   apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1                    Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole
   └─ kubectl api-resources   lists every kind → its group, version &amp; whether it's namespaced
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Means</th><th>On by default?</th><th>In a name</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Alpha</strong></td><td>experimental — may change or vanish, may have bugs</td><td>no (needs a feature gate)</td><td><code>v1alpha1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Beta</strong></td><td>well-tested; details may still change before GA</td><td>varies (newer betas off)</td><td><code>v1beta1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Stable / GA</strong></td><td>production-ready, backward-compatible</td><td>yes</td><td><code>v1</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0"><strong>Deprecation policy:</strong> a <strong>GA</strong> (stable) API version is <strong>never removed within a major version</strong> — a <code>v1</code> API lasts for all of Kubernetes 1.x; a deprecated <strong>beta</strong> version is dropped after <strong>9 months or 3 releases</strong> (whichever is longer); <strong>alpha</strong> can change or vanish in any release. There's <strong>no LTS</strong>: each minor release is maintained ~14 months (≈12 months support + a ~2-month maintenance window) — read the release notes and upgrade before EOL.</p></div>

                <h4>⑤ Three ways to manage objects — and why the exam loves <code>apply</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Command</th><th>Idempotent?</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Imperative command</td><td><code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v2</code></td><td>no — errors if it exists</td><td>exam speed &amp; quick fixes; keeps no file</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Imperative object config</td><td><code>kubectl create -f f.yaml</code> / <code>replace -f</code></td><td>no</td><td>rarely — forces delete-then-recreate</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Declarative</strong></td><td><code>kubectl apply -f f.yaml</code></td><td><strong>yes</strong> — run 100×, same result</td><td>production &amp; GitOps; the default to reach for</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip"><p class="mb-0">💡 <strong>Server-Side Apply</strong> (<code>kubectl apply --server-side</code>) moves the merge into the apiserver and tracks <em>field ownership</em>, so several controllers can co-own one object without clobbering each other. ⚡ <strong>Exam speed:</strong> <code>kubectl create deploy … --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; d.yaml</code> scaffolds a manifest you then edit &amp; <code>apply</code>.</p></div>

                <h4>⑥ Add-ons — what a fresh cluster still needs</h4>
                <p>A brand-new cluster can schedule pods but is missing pieces you'll assume are there. These ship as <strong>add-ons</strong> (themselves pods), not part of the core binaries.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>🧭 CoreDNS</h5><p>In-cluster DNS — every pod resolves <code>name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> here. Normally installed; no DNS → no name-based service discovery.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>📊 metrics-server</h5><p>Feeds CPU/memory to the API. <strong>Required</strong> for <code>kubectl top</code> and the HorizontalPodAutoscaler. Not installed by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🌍 Ingress controller</h5><p>Terminates &amp; routes external HTTP(S) to Services (e.g. ingress-nginx). You must pick &amp; install one — none ships by default.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🖥️ Dashboard</h5><p>Optional web UI. Handy, never required for admin work — and one more thing to secure if you deploy it.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — the reconciliation loop that powers everything</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Self-healing isn't magic — it's a <strong>closed control loop</strong> running forever inside the controller-manager. Each controller does three things, over and over:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌──────────── CONTROL LOOP (runs continuously) ────────────┐
   │                                                          │
   │   👁️ OBSERVE  ──▶  🧮 DIFF  ──▶  🛠️ ACT  ──┐             │
   │   read actual     desired vs     make a change           │
   │   state from      actual          (create/delete/        │
   │   the API          (a gap?)        update an object)     │
   │        ▲                                    │            │
   │        └────────────────────────────────────┘            │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj example: desired replicas=3, actual=2  →  create 1 pod  →  repeat
                    </pre>

                    <h5>🗺️ The Full Trace — Deploying easypj with 3 Replicas, Step by Step</h5>
                    <p>Here is the loop playing out for real. Notice that <strong>no single component drives the whole thing</strong> — each controller independently reconciles its own slice, and the result emerges:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
 1. USER declares desired state (deploy.yaml):
    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ apiVersion: apps/v1                        │
    │ kind: Deployment                           │
    │ spec:                                      │
    │   replicas: 3                              │
    │   template:                                │
    │     spec:                                  │
    │       containers:                          │
    │       - name: web                          │
    │         image: easypj:v2                   │
    └───────────────────────────────────────────┘

 2. kubectl apply → API server → persisted in etcd
    /registry/deployments/easypj/easypj-web = {replicas:3, image:v2}

 3. Deployment controller OBSERVES: desired replicas=3, image=v2
                            ACTUAL: no ReplicaSet, no Pods   → DIFF: everything missing
 4. Deployment controller ACTS:  creates ReplicaSet easypj-web-7d8f9

 5. ReplicaSet controller OBSERVES: want 3 pods (app=easypj-web)
                             ACTUAL: 0 pods                  → DIFF: 3 needed
 6. ReplicaSet controller ACTS:  creates 3 Pod objects (nodeName empty → Pending)

 7. Scheduler OBSERVES: 3 Pods with no node
                ACTS:  filters + scores nodes, binds each Pod → a Node

 8. kubelet (on each chosen node) OBSERVES: a Pod is assigned to me
                                     ACTS:  CRI pull image → create → start container
                                            CNI assigns the pod an IP

 9. easypj pods RUNNING :8000  → kubelet reports status up to the API server → etcd

10. Controllers keep watching forever: actual=3 = desired=3 ✅
    ── crash a pod? ReplicaSet makes a new one.  kill a node? Scheduler re-places.
    ── At NO step did anyone say "start these pods on these nodes." It EMERGED.
                    </pre>

                    <h5>🔬 Level-Triggered vs Edge-Triggered — Why Controllers Never "Miss" an Event</h5>
                    <p>The deep reason self-healing is reliable: Kubernetes is <strong>level-triggered</strong>. Controllers don't depend on catching every event — they periodically re-read the <em>whole</em> state. A dropped watch event (network blip) is simply corrected on the next resync.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Edge-triggered (imperative systems)</th><th>Level-triggered (Kubernetes)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Relies on receiving every event</td><td>Periodically re-reads the full state</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Missed event → permanently inconsistent</td><td>Missed event → caught on next resync</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>"Start Apache" — if it crashes, nobody restarts it</td><td>"3 replicas should exist" — a crash is auto-replaced</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Needs complex error handling</td><td>Self-healing by design</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <div class="uth-callout">
                        <strong>🔑 CKA Insight.</strong> When you <code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code> you are NOT telling Kubernetes "do these steps" — you're declaring "I want 3 easypj:v2 replicas." The controllers figure out the steps. That's <em>why</em> <code>apply</code> is idempotent: run it 100 times, same result as once. No state machine, no step sequence — just a declared end state and loops that converge on it.
                    </div>
                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> almost <em>everything</em> in Kubernetes is an API object stored in etcd and reconciled by a controller — Deployments, Services, Nodes, even certificates and namespaces. Master this one pattern and the whole system becomes predictable: to understand any feature, ask "what object, and which controller reconciles it?" This is what makes Kubernetes a universal <em>control plane</em>, not just a container runner.</p>

                    <h5>① How a controller <em>really</em> OBSERVES — list-watch, informers &amp; the work queue</h5>
                    <p>The loop above says "observe actual state," but <em>how</em>? Naively you'd poll — "is it 3 yet? is it 3 yet?" — which would hammer the API server with thousands of controllers asking constantly. Real Kubernetes never polls. It uses the <strong>list-watch</strong> pattern behind an <strong>informer</strong>: LIST the full state once, then WATCH a live stream of changes, keeping a local cache in sync.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🗄️ etcd ──change──▶ 📡 apiserver (watch cache)
                            │  streams events:  ADDED · MODIFIED · DELETED
                            ▼
                 ┌────────────────────────────┐
                 │ 🧠 INFORMER (in the controller)│
                 │  • LIST once → fill a local  │   the cache = a live, in-memory
                 │    cache (the "store")       │   mirror of cluster state, so the
                 │  • WATCH → apply deltas      │   controller reads it WITHOUT
                 │  • fire event handlers       │   touching etcd every time
                 └───────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │ enqueue the changed object's KEY  (namespace/name)
                                 ▼
                 ┌────────────────────────────┐
                 │ 📥 WORK QUEUE                │   dedups duplicates · rate-limits ·
                 │                              │   retries failures with backoff
                 └───────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │ a worker pops one key
                                 ▼
   🔄 Reconcile(key):  read DESIRED + ACTUAL from the cache  →  diff  →  act
                       (this is the OBSERVE → DIFF → ACT loop, in code)</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>❌ Naive polling</th><th>✅ list-watch + informer (real K8s)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>API-server load</td><td>constant, heavy (every controller, every second)</td><td>one LIST + a single long-lived WATCH stream</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Latency to react</td><td>up to the poll interval</td><td>near-instant — changes are pushed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Reads of "actual state"</td><td>hit the API/etcd each time</td><td>served from the informer's in-memory cache</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Missed events</td><td>silently lost</td><td>a periodic <strong>resync</strong> re-LISTs and re-reconciles everything (this <em>is</em> level-triggering in practice)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Failures</td><td>DIY retry logic</td><td>work queue dedups, rate-limits &amp; retries with backoff</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 So "observe actual state" is cheap</strong> because the controller reads its own <em>informer cache</em>, not etcd — and the cache is kept fresh by one shared WATCH. The periodic resync is the safety net that makes missed events impossible to matter.</div>

                    <h5>② Inside the API server — what <code>kubectl apply</code> passes through before it's saved</h5>
                    <p>"The API server validates and persists" hides a precise 6-stage pipeline. Knowing it tells you <em>exactly</em> which stage rejected a request from the error message alone:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml ─▶ 📡 kube-apiserver
        │
        ├─ 1. 🔐 Authentication      WHO are you?        cert / token / OIDC
        ├─ 2. 🛂 Authorization       MAY you do this?    RBAC (verb + resource)
        ├─ 3. 🧬 Mutating admission   change the object   webhooks: inject sidecars, set defaults
        ├─ 4. 📋 Schema validation    is it well-formed?  OpenAPI schema check
        ├─ 5. ✅ Validating admission policy gate         PodSecurity, ValidatingWebhooks, quota
        └─ 6. 💾 persist to etcd      write + bump resourceVersion  ──▶ controllers WATCH it</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Typical rejection you'd see</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Who is calling?</td><td><code>Unauthorized</code> (401) — bad/expired cert or token</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Authorization (RBAC)</td><td>Are they allowed?</td><td><code>Forbidden</code> (403) — "User cannot create deployments…"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Mutating admission</td><td>—</td><td>(usually silent — it <em>edits</em> the object, e.g. adds a sidecar)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Schema validation</td><td>Is the YAML valid?</td><td><code>error validating data: unknown field…</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Validating admission</td><td>Does it meet policy?</td><td><code>admission webhook denied the request</code> / PodSecurity violation</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Persist to etcd</td><td>—</td><td><code>Conflict</code> (409) if resourceVersion is stale (see ③)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Read the error, name the stage.</strong> "Forbidden" = stage 2 (fix RBAC). "unknown field" = stage 4 (fix the YAML). "admission webhook denied" = stage 5 (a policy). This single mapping shortcuts a huge share of troubleshooting.</div>

                    <h5>③ etcd under the hood — Raft consensus, MVCC &amp; optimistic concurrency</h5>
                    <p>Stage 6 above ("persist to etcd") is where state actually lives. etcd is a distributed key-value store that stays <em>consistent across replicas</em> via the <strong>Raft</strong> algorithm and versions <em>every</em> change via <strong>MVCC</strong>. Every Kubernetes object is just a key:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   Every object is ONE key in etcd (the "/registry" tree):
     /registry/deployments/easypj/easypj-web        = { …spec…, resourceVersion: 4821 }
     /registry/replicasets/easypj/easypj-web-6d8f9  = { …spec…, …status… }
     /registry/pods/easypj/easypj-web-6d8f9-x7k2    = { …spec…, …status… }
     /registry/services/easypj/easypj-web           = { … }

   Writes go through RAFT — a MAJORITY (quorum) must agree before a commit:
     ┌─────────┐   append entry   ┌─────────┐   replicate   ┌─────────┐
     │ etcd-1  │ ───────────────▶ │ etcd-2  │ ────────────▶ │ etcd-3  │
     │(leader) │ ◀─────ack─────── │(follower)│ ◀────ack───── │(follower)│
     └─────────┘                  └─────────┘               └─────────┘
        committed only once a MAJORITY acks  →  3 members tolerate 1 down (quorum = 2)
        ⚠️ lose quorum (2 of 3 down) → etcd goes READ-ONLY → the cluster "freezes"</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it is</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>MVCC + revision</td><td>every write bumps a global <em>revision</em>; old versions are kept until compaction</td><td>lets a WATCH replay changes from a point in time → reliable informers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resourceVersion</code></td><td>the per-object version stamped on every read/write</td><td>the basis of optimistic concurrency (below)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Optimistic concurrency</td><td>an update carries the rv you read; if it changed meanwhile → <code>409 Conflict</code></td><td>two controllers can't clobber each other — <strong>no locks needed</strong></td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Raft quorum</td><td>a majority of members must agree to commit</td><td>use ODD counts; losing quorum makes the API read-only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Compaction / defrag</td><td>prune old revisions &amp; reclaim disk</td><td>keeps etcd healthy — a real Day-2 admin task (Ch 29)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Optimistic concurrency is the quiet hero that lets the level-triggered loop stay correct even when many controllers touch the same object at once — <em>no locks, just retry-on-conflict</em>:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   controller A reads easypj-web   (resourceVersion 4821)
   controller B reads easypj-web   (resourceVersion 4821)
   A writes its change            → accepted → object is now rv 4822
   B writes with rv 4821          → ❌ 409 CONFLICT  (someone moved first)
   B re-reads (gets rv 4822), recomputes the diff, writes again → ✅ accepted
                   └──────────────── this is just the loop running again ────────────────┘</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 It's all one machine.</strong> The reconcile loop (OBSERVE→DIFF→ACT), the informer/watch that feeds OBSERVE, the API-server pipeline that guards every write, and etcd's revisions + optimistic concurrency are not separate features — they're one coherent design. That's why the right question for <em>any</em> Kubernetes behaviour is always: "which object, which controller, what's the gap?"</div>

                    <h5>🔬 Deeper still — why the loop scales &amp; survives failures</h5>
                    <p>Four production mechanisms keep that loop fast, cheap and reliable across thousands of objects and flaky networks:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it does</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Shared informer</strong></td><td>one <code>LIST</code>+<code>WATCH</code> per <em>resource type</em>, cached in memory and shared by every controller that needs it</td><td>API-server load is <strong>O(resource types)</strong>, not O(controllers) — a 50th controller adds no new watch on etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Work queue + rate limiter</strong></td><td>a failed reconcile is re-queued with <strong>exponential back-off</strong> (~5ms, doubling, capped ~1000s), deduped, and reset on success</td><td>a broken resource is retried <em>calmly</em>, not hammered thousands of times a second</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>WATCH bookmarks</strong></td><td>the apiserver streams periodic <code>BOOKMARK</code> events ("still here, at <code>resourceVersion</code> X") even when nothing changed</td><td>a client can tell "quiet" from "stalled" and resume from the last rv without a full re-LIST</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>410 Gone</strong></td><td>watching from a <code>resourceVersion</code> older than the apiserver's history window returns <strong>HTTP 410 Gone</strong></td><td>forces a fresh <code>LIST</code> + resync so caches can't silently drift — and is why aggressive etcd compaction (Ch 29) shrinks that window</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The pattern, named:</strong> read → compute → write <em>with</em> the <code>resourceVersion</code> you read → on <code>409 Conflict</code>, re-read &amp; retry. That's <strong>lock-free optimistic concurrency</strong>: two controllers touching one object never deadlock — the loser just retries and still makes progress.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Orchestration automates operations</strong> — placement, scaling, healing, networking, and rollout, done by software instead of a human at 3 AM.</li>
                    <li><strong>Declarative, not imperative</strong> — you declare desired state; control loops drive actual state toward it, forever.</li>
                    <li><strong>The administrator owns everything below the app</strong> — install, certs/PKI, etcd, upgrades, nodes, networking, storage, and troubleshooting.</li>
                    <li><strong>The API server is the only path to etcd</strong> — every component and every <code>kubectl</code> goes through it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Troubleshooting is 30% of the exam</strong> — the biggest single domain; method beats memorization.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>"Kubernetes replaces Docker."</strong> No — Kubernetes <em>orchestrates</em> containers; a runtime (containerd/CRI-O) still runs them. Only the Docker <em>shim</em> was removed (v1.24), not container images.</li>
                    <li><strong>Thinking imperatively</strong> ("start a container on node 2"). Wrong mental model — you declare <em>what</em> (3 replicas) and let K8s decide <em>where/how</em>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing CKA with CKAD.</strong> CKAD builds apps; CKA builds and operates the <em>cluster</em>. etcd, kubeadm, certs, and node management are CKA-only.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Control plane" = "master node."</strong> Modern Kubernetes dropped the word <em>master</em> — the exam says <strong>control plane node</strong>. Know its four components: API server, scheduler, controller-manager, etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>"A Pod is a container."</strong> A Pod can hold several containers sharing one network namespace &amp; storage (sidecar / init-container patterns). The Pod, not the container, is the unit K8s schedules.</li>
                    <li><strong>Desired ≠ current state.</strong> The exam constantly probes whether you grasp the reconciliation loop — controllers continuously compare the two and self-heal the gap.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Think in desired state.</strong> When something's wrong, ask "what's declared vs what's actually running?" — that's the whole troubleshooting model.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl explain</code> is your in-exam manual.</strong> <code>kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive</code> beats hunting the docs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Read events first.</strong> <code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> and <code>describe</code> usually name the cause before you guess.</li>
                    <li><strong>Architecture quick-recall mnemonic:</strong> <code>kube-apiserver</code> → front door · <code>etcd</code> → brain · <code>kube-scheduler</code> → which-node · <code>kube-controller-manager</code> → reconciliation. All four run as <strong>static pods</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — edit a file there and the kubelet recreates the pod.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 1)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">orient yourself in any cluster</span>
                </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Cluster endpoints (API server + CoreDNS URLs)</span>
kubectl cluster-info

<span class="comment"># Nodes, roles, versions, internal IPs</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide

<span class="comment"># Control-plane health (older clusters)</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses        <span class="comment"># a.k.a. cs</span>

<span class="comment"># Every API resource type + its short name + apiVersion</span>
kubectl api-resources

<span class="comment"># Client + server (cluster) versions</span>
kubectl version</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p>In <strong>5 minutes, from memory</strong>, sketch one cluster and label every box: the control-plane components (API server, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager), the worker-node components (kubelet, kube-proxy, runtime), and 3 easypj pods spread across 2 nodes. Then draw an arrow from "<code>replicas: 3</code>" to "3 pods running" and label it <em>reconciliation</em>.</p>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
<pre class="uth-diagram">
   🗂️ CLUSTER
   ┌──────────────── 🧠 CONTROL PLANE ────────────────┐
   │ 📡 API server · 🗄️ etcd · 📋 scheduler · 🔄 controller-manager │
   └──────────────────────┬───────────────────────────┘
                          │  (API server is the only one that talks to etcd)
   ┌────── 💪 NODE 1 ──────┐        ┌────── 💪 NODE 2 ──────┐
   │ 🤖 kubelet · 🔀 kube-proxy │     │ 🤖 kubelet · 🔀 kube-proxy │
   │ 🐋 runtime (containerd)│       │ 🐋 runtime (containerd)│
   │ 📦 easypj  📦 easypj    │       │ 📦 easypj             │
   └──────────────────────┘        └──────────────────────┘
       "replicas: 3"  ──reconciliation──▶  3 pods always running ✅
</pre>
                        <p><strong>Check yourself:</strong> control-plane = API server · etcd · scheduler · controller-manager (the "brain"); each node = kubelet · kube-proxy · container runtime (the "muscle"); the 3 easypj pods are spread across the 2 nodes; and the arrow is the control loop closing desired (3) → actual (3). If you drew all of that, you've got Chapter 1's mental model cold.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 1 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Define "container orchestration" in one sentence.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The automated deployment, scaling, healing, and networking of containerized applications across many machines — performed by software, not humans.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Containers solved "works on my machine"; orchestration solves "works across a fleet, reliably, unattended." Kubernetes is the industry-standard orchestrator, and the CKA is about <em>running the orchestrator itself</em>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">easypj's pod crashes at 3 AM. What does Kubernetes do, and <em>why</em> does it work even if a controller missed the crash event?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A controller observes that <strong>actual ≠ desired</strong> (2 pods running, 3 wanted) and recreates the missing pod automatically — no human needed.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>It's <strong>level-triggered</strong>: the loop reacts to the current <em>state</em>, not to the crash <em>event</em>. Even if the event was missed or the controller restarted, the next iteration still sees the gap and fixes it — which is why self-healing is reliable.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Give the one-line definition of Kubernetes.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>"An open-source system for automating deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications."</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The operative word is <strong>automating</strong> — you declare intent, K8s carries it out and keeps it true. Born from Google's Borg, open-sourced 2014, CNCF-governed, written in Go.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain desired-vs-actual state using a <code>replicas: 3</code> example.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Desired</strong> = what you declared (<code>replicas: 3</code>, stored in etcd). <strong>Actual</strong> = what's really running on the nodes. A controller continuously diffs them: if actual is 2, it creates one pod; if actual is 4, it deletes one.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This gap-closing loop is the core mental model for the whole exam. Troubleshooting = "find where actual diverged from desired, and why the controller can't close the gap."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List three tasks that belong to the CKA but NOT the CKAD.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Any three of: install a cluster with <strong>kubeadm</strong>; <strong>etcd</strong> backup &amp; restore; approve/rotate <strong>certificates</strong> (PKI); <strong>cluster upgrades</strong>; <strong>node management</strong> (drain/cordon); configure <strong>CNI/CoreDNS</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>All are <em>below the app</em>. CKAD stays at the workload layer (Pods/Deployments/Services/ConfigMaps); CKA owns the infrastructure those run on.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the four control-plane components and the one that stores all cluster state.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>kube-apiserver</strong>, <strong>etcd</strong>, <strong>kube-scheduler</strong>, <strong>kube-controller-manager</strong> (plus optional cloud-controller-manager). State lives in <strong>etcd</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Only the API server talks to etcd — everything else goes through the API server. That's why an etcd backup <em>is</em> a cluster backup, and why losing etcd without a backup loses the cluster.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a Pod, and what do containers inside one share?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A Pod is the smallest deployable unit — one or more containers scheduled together. Containers in a pod share the <strong>network namespace</strong> (one IP, reach each other on <code>localhost</code>) and can share <strong>storage volumes</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is why a sidecar (e.g. a log shipper) can read the app's files and talk to it on localhost. easypj-web runs one container; a sidecar pattern would add a second to the same pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is <code>kubectl apply</code> idempotent — running it twice causes no harm?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because it's <strong>declarative</strong>: it sets the desired state, it doesn't issue step-by-step commands. If the cluster already matches, the reconciliation loop finds no gap and does nothing.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Contrast with imperative <code>kubectl create</code>, which errors if the object already exists. Declarative + reconciliation is what makes GitOps ("re-apply the repo continuously") safe.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Match each tool to its job: Calico · Helm · ArgoCD · Prometheus · containerd.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Calico</td><td>CNI — pod networking + NetworkPolicy</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Helm</td><td>Packaging — templated charts</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>ArgoCD</td><td>GitOps — sync cluster to Git</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Prometheus</td><td>Observability — metrics</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>containerd</td><td>Container runtime (CRI)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each plugs into a Kubernetes extension point: CNI (networking), runtime (CRI), plus higher-level tooling for packaging, delivery, and monitoring. Knowing the category matters more than the brand.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Sort "admin-owns vs dev-owns": etcd backup · a Deployment · RBAC · a ConfigMap · a node upgrade.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>🔧 CKA admin owns</th><th>👩‍💻 CKAD dev owns</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>etcd backup</td><td>a Deployment</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>RBAC</td><td>a ConfigMap</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>a node upgrade</td><td>&nbsp;</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>RBAC is borderline — devs may <em>request</em> roles, but defining and granting cluster access is an admin responsibility. The rule of thumb: if it's <em>below the app</em>, it's CKA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <span class="ch-jump" id="ch1-scenario"></span>
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 1 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — 10 drills that mirror how the CKA actually asks: a broken state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A freshly built cluster has its control plane up, but worker nodes show <code>NotReady</code>. You SSH to a worker and find the node agent isn't running. Which component is it, and how do you recover?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>kubelet</strong> — the per-node agent that (1) registers the node with the API server and (2) starts/monitors the pods assigned to it. No kubelet ⇒ the node can't report in ⇒ <code>NotReady</code>.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Why is it down? Read the logs first.</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -30

<span class="comment"># Start it and make it survive reboots</span>
sudo systemctl enable --now kubelet

<span class="comment"># Verify from the control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the CKA, <strong>NotReady → always check the kubelet first</strong>: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>. Common root causes are a stopped service, a bad <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, swap enabled, a missing CNI, or a down container runtime (Chapter 32 drills all of these).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the four control-plane components and each one's primary job. Where do they run on a kubeadm cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td>Front door — REST API, authn/authz/admission; the <em>only</em> component that talks to etcd</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>etcd</code></td><td>Distributed key-value store — the single source of truth for all cluster state</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-scheduler</code></td><td>Assigns unscheduled pods to nodes (resources, affinity, taints/tolerations)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>kube-controller-manager</code></td><td>Runs reconciliation loops (Deployment, ReplicaSet, Node… controllers)</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On a kubeadm cluster all four run as <strong>static pods</strong> defined in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> on the control-plane node — edit a manifest there and the kubelet recreates the pod. The topology is a <strong>star</strong>: every other component (scheduler, controller-manager, kubelet) talks <em>only</em> through the API server, never directly to etcd.</p>
                            <p class="mb-0">There's an optional <strong>fifth</strong> component — the <code>cloud-controller-manager</code> — on managed/cloud clusters; it runs the cloud-specific loops (provision LoadBalancers, manage node lifecycle &amp; routes) so the core four stay cloud-agnostic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment specifies <code>replicas: 3</code>. You manually delete one of its pods. What happens, and which component is responsible?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>ReplicaSet controller</strong> (inside kube-controller-manager) sees current=2 ≠ desired=3 and immediately creates a replacement — count returns to 3.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">watch self-healing happen</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 --replicas=3
kubectl get pods                 <span class="comment"># 3 running</span>
kubectl delete pod easypj-xxxxx-yyyyy
kubectl get pods                 <span class="comment"># still 3 — a new one was created!</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The declarative model in action: you declared "3," and the control loop (Observe → Diff → Act) continuously enforces it. Controllers never stop watching — this self-healing is heavily tested. (To remove the pod permanently you must change the <em>desired</em> state — scale down or delete the Deployment.)</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between a control-plane node and a worker node? Which components run on each — and why does the kubelet run on <em>both</em>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Control-plane node:</strong> the management components — <code>kube-apiserver</code>, <code>etcd</code>, <code>kube-scheduler</code>, <code>kube-controller-manager</code> (as static pods).</p>
                        <p><strong>Worker node:</strong> runs your workloads — needs <code>kubelet</code>, <code>kube-proxy</code>, and a <strong>container runtime</strong> (containerd/CRI-O).</p>
                        <p><strong>Both:</strong> kubelet and kube-proxy run on control-plane nodes too — because the control-plane components <em>are</em> static pods, and only the kubelet can run static pods.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Say "control-plane node," not "master." By default control-plane nodes carry the taint <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code> so ordinary workloads (like easypj) don't land there. That taint — and how to tolerate/remove it — is exactly the kind of thing the scheduling chapter (14) covers.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code>. Trace the request through the architecture — which components act, and in what order?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <ol>
                            <li><strong>kubectl</strong> → HTTPS request to the API server</li>
                            <li><strong>kube-apiserver</strong> → authenticate → authorize (RBAC) → admission → validate</li>
                            <li><strong>etcd</strong> → API server persists the Deployment object</li>
                            <li><strong>Deployment controller</strong> → creates a ReplicaSet</li>
                            <li><strong>ReplicaSet controller</strong> → creates Pod objects (<code>nodeName</code> empty)</li>
                            <li><strong>kube-scheduler</strong> → binds each pod to a node</li>
                            <li><strong>kubelet</strong> (on the node) → pulls image via the runtime, starts the container; CNI assigns an IP</li>
                        </ol>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>It's <strong>asynchronous and event-driven</strong> — no single orchestrator. Each component watches the API server for changes relevant to it and acts independently. That loose coupling is what makes Kubernetes resilient: if the scheduler is briefly down, pods just queue as <code>Pending</code> and get placed when it returns. This flow is the backbone of every troubleshooting scenario (Part 9).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>etcd</code>, why is backing it up critical, and what command takes a snapshot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>etcd</code> is a distributed, consistent key-value store holding <strong>all</strong> cluster state — every Pod, Service, Secret, ConfigMap, RBAC rule, and CRD. Lose it without a backup and the whole cluster is gone (an etcd snapshot effectively <em>is</em> a cluster backup).</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">guaranteed exam task</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Snapshot (flags come from the etcd static-pod manifest)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /tmp/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify it's readable</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /tmp/etcd-backup.db --write-table</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three things the exam checks: (1) use <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code>; (2) supply the three TLS flags — read the exact paths from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>; (3) the endpoint is usually <code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code>. Full backup <em>and restore</em> is drilled end-to-end in Chapter 29.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What does <code>kube-proxy</code> do? If it stops on a node, what breaks — and what still works?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kube-proxy</code> programs each node's dataplane (iptables / IPVS) so traffic to a <strong>Service</strong> VIP is load-balanced to the backing pods. It watches the API server for Service &amp; EndpointSlice objects.</p>
                        <p><strong>If it's down on a node:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>❌ Service (ClusterIP / NodePort) traffic stops reaching pods</li>
                            <li>❌ DNS via CoreDNS may fail (CoreDNS is reached <em>through</em> a Service)</li>
                            <li>✅ Pod-to-pod by pod IP still works (that's the CNI, not kube-proxy)</li>
                            <li>✅ <code>kubectl exec</code> / <code>logs</code> still work (API server → kubelet directly)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Key distinction for troubleshooting: <strong>kube-proxy = Service networking</strong>; the <strong>CNI plugin = pod-to-pod networking</strong>. So if pods can ping each other by IP but Services are unreachable, suspect kube-proxy (Chapter 34 makes this a 4-layer method).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Container Runtime Interface (CRI)? Name two CRI runtimes. Why was Docker's direct support removed in v1.24 — and do my images still work?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>CRI</strong> is the standard interface the kubelet uses to talk to <em>any</em> container runtime without runtime-specific code. Two runtimes: <strong>containerd</strong> (most common, extracted from Docker) and <strong>CRI-O</strong> (purpose-built, common on OpenShift).</p>
                        <p>Docker didn't implement CRI directly, so K8s shipped a <code>dockershim</code> translation layer — extra complexity for no gain (Docker uses containerd internally anyway). v1.24 removed the shim. <strong>Your images still work</strong> — they're OCI images, a universal format; only the <em>runtime plumbing</em> changed.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node-level: use crictl, not docker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps                 <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>After v1.24 you debug containers on a node with <code>crictl</code> (talks to containerd via CRI), not <code>docker</code>. This matters in Chapter 32 when the API server is down and you must diagnose from the node itself.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Imperative vs declarative management — what's the difference, and what's the recommended CKA workflow?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Imperative</strong> = tell K8s the steps (<code>kubectl create/run/expose</code>) — fast, one-off, no YAML. <strong>Declarative</strong> = describe the end state in YAML + <code>kubectl apply -f</code> — idempotent, version-controllable, repeatable.</p>
                        <p><strong>CKA gold standard:</strong> generate YAML imperatively, then apply it declaratively:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative generation → declarative apply</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Scaffold YAML instantly — don't write it from scratch</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v2 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; easypj.yaml

<span class="comment"># Edit in vim (add probes, resources…), then apply</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Speed wins the exam. <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> scaffolds correct YAML in seconds; you tweak the bits a one-liner can't express (multi-container, volumes, probes). Pure <code>kubectl create</code> is fine for trivial objects, but the hybrid is the habit to build.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You're asked to verify overall cluster health under exam time pressure. Write the command sequence and say what each reveals.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">top-down health check</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get nodes                       <span class="comment"># 1. all nodes Ready?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system         <span class="comment"># 2. control-plane + CNI + CoreDNS Running?</span>
kubectl cluster-info                    <span class="comment"># 3. API + CoreDNS endpoints reachable</span>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20   <span class="comment"># 4. recent failures</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running   <span class="comment"># 5. anything CrashLoop/Error/Pending?</span>
kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox:1.28 --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup kubernetes               <span class="comment"># 6. DNS resolving?</span>
<span class="comment"># 7. if a node is NotReady: ssh in → systemctl status kubelet → journalctl -u kubelet</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A systematic <strong>top-down diagnosis</strong>: nodes → control-plane pods → events → DNS → node logs. Look for all nodes <code>Ready</code>, all kube-system pods <code>Running</code>, no <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>, and DNS resolving <code>kubernetes</code>. If a static-pod component is down, check its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and that node's kubelet logs — the exact method Part 9 turns into reflex.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 1 Visual Summary — from your YAML to a running easypj pod</div>
<pre>
  👩‍💻 YOU write          🧠 CONTROL PLANE ("the brain")           💪 WORKER NODES ("muscle")
  ┌──────────────┐       ┌──────────────────────────────┐       ┌───────────────────────────┐
  │ deploy.yaml  │ ①apply│ 📡 apiserver — the ONE door    │       │ 🤖 kubelet · 🔀 kube-proxy   │
  │ replicas: 3  │ ─────▶ │   ② writes DESIRED ─▶ 🗄️ etcd   │ ④bind │ 🐋 containerd               │
  │ image:       │       │   ③ 📋 scheduler picks a node   │ ─────▶│  ┌─────────────────────┐  │
  │  easypj:v2   │       │   ④ 🔄 controllers reconcile    │       │  │📦 easypj-web :8000    │  │
  │ resources:   │       │      desired vs actual,        │       │  │📦 easypj-web :8000    │  │
  │  256Mi       │       │      FOREVER                   │ ⑥stat │  │📦 easypj-web :8000    │  │
  └──────────────┘       └──────────────────────────────┘ ◀───── │  └─────────────────────┘  │
                         ⑤ kubelet pulls image · 🌐 CNI gives each pod an IP · starts it      │
                                                                  └───────────────────────────┘
   ① declare desired   ② persist to etcd   ③ schedule to a node   ④ reconcile (keep actual = desired)
   ⑤ kubelet + CNI run it   ⑥ status reported back ─▶ etcd   ……and the loop never stops.
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   You declared "3 easypj-web on v2." Kubernetes GUARANTEES 3 are always up — it heals, scales &amp;
   rolls out on its own. THAT is Kubernetes ☸️ — and operating this whole machine is the CKA's job.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> You write a <code>deploy.yaml</code> that <em>describes</em> the end result you want (3 replicas of <code>easypj-web</code> on image <code>easypj:v2</code>) and <code>apply</code> it. You never tell Kubernetes <em>how</em> — only <em>what</em>.</p>
                    <p>The control plane is the brain: the <strong>apiserver</strong> is the single front door, it persists your desired state to <strong>etcd</strong>, the <strong>scheduler</strong> picks a node for each pod, and <strong>controllers</strong> keep comparing <em>desired vs actual</em> and acting on the gap — forever. The worker nodes are the muscle: the <strong>kubelet</strong> pulls the image and starts the containers, <strong>CNI</strong> gives each pod an IP, and status flows back to etcd.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> this never-ending "make actual match desired" loop is <em>reconciliation</em> — the core idea of the whole platform. Because of it, a crashed pod is replaced, a scale-up just works, and a new version rolls out automatically. Operating this machine is exactly what the CKA tests.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 1 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 2 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch2">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 2 · Containers &amp; Docker — The Unit Kubernetes Runs</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">
                Kubernetes schedules, heals, and scales one thing: <strong>containers</strong>. Before you can operate a
                cluster, you have to understand the unit it manages — what a container actually <em>is</em>, how an image
                is built from cached layers, and which runtime runs it on the node. This is also where troubleshooting
                begins: when an easypj pod won't start, the answer is almost always in the image or the runtime.
            </p>

            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Hands-On</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐳 Docker · containerd · CRI</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 easypj / jobpost</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Why containers solve the <strong>"works on my machine"</strong> problem</li>
                    <li>✅ Containers vs Virtual Machines — how each isolates, and when to use which</li>
                    <li>✅ Docker basics: <strong>Dockerfile → image → container → registry</strong>, and how layers cache</li>
                    <li>✅ Build a production, multi-stage, non-root image for <strong>easypj</strong></li>
                    <li>✅ The essential <code>docker</code> / <code>crictl</code> commands — and which to use on a node</li>
                    <li>✅ The <strong>CRI</strong>, containerd vs CRI-O, and why Docker was removed in v1.24</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🐳 Container-runtime knowledge on the CKA</h5>
                <p>The CKA doesn't ask you to write Dockerfiles, but it <strong>assumes</strong> you understand container runtimes — because they show up everywhere you'll be graded:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Cluster install</strong> — you configure <code>containerd</code> (and its cgroup driver) during kubeadm setup.</li>
                    <li><strong>Troubleshooting</strong> — when a pod fails, you inspect the runtime with <code>crictl</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>NotReady nodes</strong> — a dead container runtime takes the node down with it.</li>
                    <li><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> — understanding images &amp; layers is how you diagnose failed pulls.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — the Chapter 2 Learning Arc</h4>
            <p>This chapter walks the lifecycle of the unit Kubernetes runs: from <strong>why</strong> containers exist, to <strong>what</strong> they are vs VMs, to <strong>how</strong> you build and run one for easypj, to the <strong>runtime</strong> the cluster actually uses.</p>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chapter 2 Roadmap — from "works on my machine" to a running pod</div>
<pre>
   ▶ YOU ARE HERE
   "easypj runs on my laptop but crashes in prod. How do I package it once, run it anywhere?"
        │
        ▼
   2.1 😩 WHY CONTAINERS ─────▶ 2.2 ⚖️ CONTAINERS vs VMs ─────▶ 2.3 🐳 DOCKER BASICS
   the "works on my machine"    shared kernel = MB &amp; ms          Dockerfile → image →
   problem, solved by packaging vs a full guest OS per app       container → registry; layers
        │                                                              │
        ▼                                                              ▼
   2.6 🔌 RUNTIMES &amp; THE CRI ◀───── 2.5 🛠️ docker / crictl ◀───── 2.4 📦 BUILD easypj's IMAGE
   containerd / CRI-O; why          the commands (and which        a multi-stage, non-root
   Docker left the node in v1.24    one works on a real node)      production Dockerfile
        │
        ▼
   ✅ READY — you understand the artifact (image) and the engine (runtime) Kubernetes drives.
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Answers the question…</th><th>One-line takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-1"><strong>2.1</strong> Why Containers</a></td><td>Why does this exist?</td><td>Package the app + everything it needs → runs identically everywhere.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-2"><strong>2.2</strong> Containers vs VMs</a></td><td>What is a container, really?</td><td>A process sharing the host kernel — MB &amp; milliseconds, not GB &amp; minutes.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-3"><strong>2.3</strong> Docker Basics</a></td><td>How is an image made &amp; shipped?</td><td>Dockerfile → build → image (cached layers) → registry → run.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-4"><strong>2.4</strong> Build easypj</a></td><td>How do I package easypj?</td><td>A multi-stage, non-root Dockerfile = small, fast, safe.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-5"><strong>2.5</strong> Commands</a></td><td>How do I inspect containers?</td><td><code>docker</code> on your laptop; <code>crictl</code> on a cluster node.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-6"><strong>2.6</strong> Runtimes &amp; CRI</a></td><td>What runs it in the cluster?</td><td>containerd/CRI-O via the CRI — Docker's daemon left the node in v1.24.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s2-7"><strong>2.7</strong> Images in K8s</a></td><td>How does K8s pull &amp; manage images?</td><td>Tag/digest, imagePullPolicy defaults, pull secrets, ImagePullBackOff, image GC.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s2-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.1 Why Containers? The "Works on My Machine" Problem</h3>
                <p>Every developer has lived this nightmare: easypj runs perfectly on your laptop, then explodes the moment it hits a server. A container ends the argument by packaging <strong>everything</strong> the app needs — code <em>and</em> its entire environment — into one immutable, portable unit.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The "works on my machine" problem — and the fix</div>
<pre>
   👩‍💻 your laptop                          🖥️ production server
   ┌────────────────────────┐              ┌────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ easypj runs ✅          │   deploy →   │ 💀 ModuleNotFoundError: 'psycopg2'   │
   │ Python 3.13 · Django   │              │    (different Python · no psycopg2 · │
   │ psycopg2 · env vars set │              │     missing env vars · different OS) │
   └────────────────────────┘              └────────────────────────────────────┘

   THE FIX — pack EVERYTHING into one image 📦, ship the box, not the recipe:
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 📦 easypj:v1                                                   │
   │   • Python 3.13            • Django 5.2.5                      │
   │   • gunicorn · psycopg2    • easypj source + settings          │
   │   • env / config baked in  • a slim Debian userland            │
   │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────   │
   │  runs IDENTICALLY:   laptop ✅   staging ✅   production ✅      │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — Container</h5>
                    <p>A <strong>container</strong> is a lightweight, standalone, executable package that bundles everything needed to run a piece of software: code, runtime, system tools, libraries, and settings. Crucially, containers <strong>share the host's OS kernel</strong> — which is what makes them far lighter and faster than virtual machines.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🚢 Analogy — the shipping container</h5>
                    <p>Before standardized shipping containers, every cargo was loaded by hand and broke in transit. The steel box changed the world because <em>the same sealed unit</em> rides any ship, truck, or crane without anyone caring what's inside. A software container is the same idea: a standard box the cluster can pick up and run anywhere, without caring whether it holds easypj, nginx, or PostgreSQL.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Four Properties That Matter</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Portable</h5>
                        <p>The same image runs on Linux, Mac, Windows, cloud, or bare metal — identical behavior everywhere.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>Isolated</h5>
                        <p>Each container gets its own filesystem, network, and process space — no dependency conflicts between apps.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>Fast</h5>
                        <p>Starts in seconds (not minutes like a VM) — it shares the host kernel, so there's no full OS to boot.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📐</div>
                        <h5>Lightweight</h5>
                        <p>Megabytes, not gigabytes — run dozens (even hundreds) of containers on a single machine.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>What's Inside a Container — and What Is <em>Not</em></h4>
                <p>This is the misconception that trips up almost everyone, and clearing it up makes the rest of the chapter click: a container image does <strong>not</strong> contain an operating system. It bundles the app's <em>userland</em> — your code, language runtime, and shared libraries — but it borrows the <strong>host's kernel</strong>. That single fact is why containers are MB not GB, and why a Linux container needs a Linux kernel underneath.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>✅ IN the image (userland — shipped)</th><th>❌ NOT in the image (shared from the host)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Your code (easypj) + settings</td><td>The Linux <strong>kernel</strong> itself</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Python 3.13 runtime + pip packages</td><td>Kernel modules &amp; device drivers</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>System libs (<code>libc</code>, <code>libpq</code>) from a slim Debian</td><td>The CPU / memory scheduler</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The default command (<code>gunicorn …</code>)</td><td>Namespaces &amp; cgroups (these <em>are</em> kernel features)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">An image is userland on top of the host's kernel</div>
<pre>
   📦 easypj image  (everything you ship)        ── all containers share ──▶  one kernel
   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ easypj code · gunicorn         │            ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ Python 3.13 · psycopg2         │  ───runs──▶ │ 🐧 HOST LINUX KERNEL                   │
   │ libc · libpq (slim Debian)     │   on top of │   scheduling · namespaces · cgroups ·  │
   │ (NO kernel in here!)           │            │   drivers · filesystems                 │
   └──────────────────────────────┘            └──────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 So how do containers run on a Mac or Windows?</h5>
                    <p>They don't, directly — those OSes have no Linux kernel. Docker Desktop quietly runs a <strong>tiny Linux VM</strong> and starts your containers inside it. On a real Kubernetes <em>node</em> (which is Linux), there's no such trick — the container runs natively on the node's kernel. This is also why a Linux image can't run on a Windows kernel and vice-versa: the userland must match the kernel it's calling.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why this is the foundation of everything ahead</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes never runs your <em>code</em> — it runs your <em>image</em>. Every later topic (pods, deployments, rollouts, probes) is just the cluster choreographing copies of an image like <code>easypj:v1</code>. Get the image right here, and the rest of the guide is choreography.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.2 Containers vs Virtual Machines</h3>
                <p>Both VMs and containers give you isolation — but at fundamentally different levels, and that difference explains every "containers are light and fast" claim. A <strong>VM virtualizes hardware</strong> (a full guest OS per app); a <strong>container virtualizes the OS</strong> (all containers share the host kernel).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">VM architecture vs container architecture</div>
<pre>
   VIRTUAL MACHINES                          CONTAINERS
   ┌───────────┐ ┌───────────┐              ┌───────────┐ ┌───────────┐
   │  App A     │ │  App B     │             │ 🐍 easypj  │ │ 🌐 nginx   │
   │  Libs      │ │  Libs      │             │  Libs      │ │  Libs      │
   │ ┌────────┐ │ │ ┌────────┐ │             └─────┬─────┘ └─────┬─────┘
   │ │Guest OS│ │ │ │Guest OS│ │  ← a WHOLE         │   shared    │
   │ └────────┘ │ │ └────────┘ │    OS per app      ▼             ▼
   └───────────┘ └───────────┘             ┌────────────────────────┐
   ┌───────────────────────────┐           │ 🐋 container runtime     │ (containerd)
   │       Hypervisor           │           ├────────────────────────┤
   ├───────────────────────────┤           │   Host kernel  (SHARED) │ ← the key difference
   │         Host OS            │           ├────────────────────────┤
   ├───────────────────────────┤           │        Host OS          │
   │         Hardware           │           ├────────────────────────┤
   └───────────────────────────┘           │        Hardware         │
     heavy → GIGABYTES · MINUTES            └────────────────────────┘
                                              light → MEGABYTES · SECONDS
</pre>
                </div>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>🖥️ Virtual Machine</th><th>📦 Container</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Size</strong></td><td>Gigabytes (a full OS per VM)</td><td>Megabytes (shares the kernel)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Startup</strong></td><td>Minutes (OS must boot)</td><td>Seconds / ms (just a process start)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>OS</strong></td><td>Full guest OS per VM</td><td>Shares the host kernel</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Isolation</strong></td><td>Strong (hardware-level)</td><td>Process-level (namespaces + cgroups)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Performance</strong></td><td>Near-native (hypervisor overhead)</td><td>Native (direct kernel access)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Density</strong></td><td>~10–20 per server</td><td>~100–1000 per server</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>A different OS, or strong isolation</td><td>Same OS, many microservices</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA reality: nodes ARE VMs (usually)</h5>
                    <p>In most real clusters, Kubernetes worker nodes are themselves <strong>virtual machines</strong> in a cloud (EC2, Azure VM, GCE). So you actually use <em>both</em> layers: VMs as the host infrastructure, and containers for the workloads on top. The administrator manages both — VMs for the cluster machines, containers for what runs on them.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The flip side of a shared kernel</h5>
                    <p>Sharing the host kernel is what makes containers light — but it also means weaker isolation than a VM. A kernel exploit or a privileged container can potentially affect the host. That's <em>why</em> later chapters lock containers down: non-root users, dropped capabilities, seccomp, and Pod Security Standards (Ch 27). Light ≠ automatically safe.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.3 Docker Basics for Kubernetes Admins</h3>
                <p>Docker created the container ecosystem. Even though Kubernetes no longer uses Docker as a <em>runtime</em>, Docker's concepts and tooling are still how you build and reason about images — so the vocabulary is essential.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Docker workflow — recipe to running container to registry</div>
<pre>
   📝 Dockerfile  ──docker build──▶  📀 Image  ──docker run──▶  📦 Container
   (the recipe)                      (read-only               (a running
                                      template)                instance)
                                         │
                                         │ docker push
                                         ▼
                                   🏪 Registry   ──docker pull──▶  🤖 a K8s node
                                   (Docker Hub / ECR / GCR)        pulls &amp; runs it
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>Dockerfile</h5>
                        <p>A text recipe that defines how to build an image: the base OS, dependencies, your code, and the startup command.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📀</div>
                        <h5>Image</h5>
                        <p>The built, read-only template. Like a <em>class</em> in code — it defines the container but isn't running yet.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Container</h5>
                        <p>A running instance of an image. Like an <em>object</em> created from a class — live and executing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏪</div>
                        <h5>Registry</h5>
                        <p>Where images are stored &amp; distributed: Docker Hub (public), ECR (AWS), GCR (GCP), ACR (Azure).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Layers — How Docker Images Are Built (and Cached)</h4>
                <p>An image isn't one blob — it's a <strong>stack of read-only layers</strong>, one per Dockerfile instruction. Each layer is content-addressed and cached, which is the single most important thing to understand for fast builds:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj image — layer stack (bottom = base, top = your code)</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Container layer (read-WRITE)                 │ ← runtime changes, ephemeral
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │  L5  CMD ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi…"]         │ ← startup command
   │  L4  COPY . .            (easypj source)      │ ← changes MOST → put it LAST
   │  L3  RUN pip install -r requirements.txt      │ ← Python deps (slow; cache this!)
   │  L2  COPY requirements.txt .                  │ ← the deps list
   │  L1  FROM python:3.13-slim   (base OS+Python) │ ← shared base, pulled once
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   💡 Layers are cached. Edit L4 (your code) → only L4+L5 rebuild; L1–L3 are reused.
      THAT is why you COPY requirements.txt and pip install BEFORE COPY . .
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why layer order matters for the CKA</h5>
                    <p>When you debug an <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, knowing images are layers helps you reason about <em>where</em> it broke — a missing base image, a build failure, or a registry-auth problem. As an admin you also configure <code>imagePullSecrets</code> so nodes can pull from private registries (Ch 23/25).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Dockerfile Instructions You'll Actually Use</h4>
                <p>You don't need all 18 Dockerfile instructions — about ten do 99% of the work. Here's the full working set, each with what it does and the gotcha that bites people:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Instruction</th><th>What it does</th><th>Note / gotcha</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>FROM</code></td><td>The base image to build on</td><td>Pin it (<code>python:3.13-slim</code>), never <code>:latest</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>WORKDIR</code></td><td>Sets (and creates) the working dir</td><td>Use it instead of <code>RUN cd …</code> (cd doesn't persist)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>COPY</code></td><td>Copies files from build context → image</td><td>Order matters for caching; respects <code>.dockerignore</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ADD</code></td><td>Like COPY but also untars / fetches URLs</td><td>Prefer <code>COPY</code> — ADD's magic causes surprises</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>RUN</code></td><td>Executes a command at BUILD time → new layer</td><td>Chain with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> to keep layers small</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ENV</code></td><td>Sets an env var (persists into the running container)</td><td>Available at build AND run time</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ARG</code></td><td>A build-time-only variable</td><td>Gone at runtime; <strong>don't</strong> use for secrets</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>EXPOSE</code></td><td>Documents the port the app listens on</td><td>Documentation only — doesn't publish the port</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>USER</code></td><td>Switches the user for later steps + runtime</td><td>Set a non-root user before <code>CMD</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>CMD</code> / <code>ENTRYPOINT</code></td><td>The default process the container runs</td><td>See the breakdown below — the #1 confusion</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>CMD vs ENTRYPOINT — the Classic Confusion</h4>
                <p>Both define what runs when the container starts, but they combine in a specific way, and Kubernetes overrides them with its <em>own</em> fields. Get this straight once:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ENTRYPOINT = the program · CMD = its default arguments</div>
<pre>
   ENTRYPOINT ["gunicorn"]          ← the fixed executable (hard to override)
   CMD ["easypj.wsgi","-b","0.0.0.0:8000"]   ← default args (easy to override)

   container runs:  gunicorn easypj.wsgi -b 0.0.0.0:8000
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   only CMD, no ENTRYPOINT:   CMD ["gunicorn","easypj.wsgi"]
        → runs that, but `docker run img python manage.py shell` REPLACES it entirely
   ENTRYPOINT + CMD:          `docker run img --workers 5`  → gunicorn easypj.wsgi --workers 5
                              (CMD's defaults are swapped, ENTRYPOINT stays)
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>In the Dockerfile</th><th>In Kubernetes (pod spec)</th><th>Overrides…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>ENTRYPOINT</code></td><td><code>command:</code></td><td>the executable</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>CMD</code></td><td><code>args:</code></td><td>the arguments</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The K8s payoff</h5>
                    <p>When a pod sets <code>command:</code> / <code>args:</code>, it's directly overriding the image's <code>ENTRYPOINT</code> / <code>CMD</code>. That's how you run a one-off (e.g. <code>python manage.py migrate</code>) from the <em>same</em> easypj image without rebuilding it — exactly what Jobs do in Chapter 16. Use the <strong>exec form</strong> (<code>["gunicorn","…"]</code>), not the shell form, so signals (SIGTERM on pod stop) reach your process for clean shutdown.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Layers also speed up pod startup</h5>
                    <p>Because layers are cached <em>on the node</em>, once one easypj pod has pulled the base + deps layers, the next pod on that node reuses them and starts almost instantly. Shared base images across your apps = less to pull, faster scaling.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.4 Building the easypj Image</h3>
                <p>Now let's package easypj into the image Kubernetes will pull and run as pods. We'll go straight to the <strong>production-grade, multi-stage, non-root</strong> pattern — the way you'd actually ship it.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Dockerfile.production — multi-stage</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-docker"><span class="comment"># ── Stage 1: builder — compile/install deps with build tools ──</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
ENV PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
RUN apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends gcc libpq-dev \
    &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
COPY requirements.txt .                       <span class="comment"># copy deps list FIRST (cache layer)</span>
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt

<span class="comment"># ── Stage 2: runtime — slim final image, NO build tools ──</span>
FROM python:3.13-slim
WORKDIR /app
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
COPY --from=builder /install /usr/local       <span class="comment"># just the installed packages</span>
COPY . .                                      <span class="comment"># easypj source (changes most → last)</span>
RUN python manage.py collectstatic --noinput
RUN useradd -m app                            <span class="comment"># create a non-root user…</span>
USER app                                      <span class="comment"># …and run as it (security!)</span>
EXPOSE 8000
CMD ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi:application", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000", "--workers", "3"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">.dockerignore — keep junk &amp; secrets out of the image</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Don't copy these into the build context / image</span>
.git/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.env                <span class="comment"># never bake secrets into an image!</span>
db.sqlite3
venv/
env/
node_modules/
*.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Always run as non-root</h5>
                    <p>Notice the <code>useradd app</code> + <code>USER app</code> lines. Running a container as root is a real risk — if an attacker breaks out, they land as root on the node. The image declares the intent; in Kubernetes you <em>enforce</em> it with a <code>securityContext</code> (<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>) and Pod Security Standards (Ch 27).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Why Multi-Stage? Size &amp; Safety</h4>
                <p>The builder stage has compilers and <code>-dev</code> headers; the runtime stage copies only the <em>installed result</em> and throws the builder away. Smaller image = faster pulls, less attack surface, fewer CVEs.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Multi-stage build — discard the toolchain, keep the app</div>
<pre>
   ❌ single stage:  python:3.13 + gcc + libpq-dev + build tools + app   →  ~900 MB 😬
   ✅ multi-stage:   python:3.13-slim + installed deps + app (no tools)  →  ~150 MB ✅

   ┌─────────────────────────┐   COPY --from=builder   ┌─────────────────────────┐
   │ Stage 1: BUILDER        │   only the /install     │ Stage 2: RUNTIME        │
   │  gcc, libpq-dev, headers │  ─────────────────────▶ │  slim base + deps + app │
   │  pip install …           │   (build tools are      │  runs as non-root        │
   │  💥 DISCARDED after build │    left behind)         │  ← THE FINAL IMAGE       │
   └─────────────────────────┘                         └─────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Build &amp; Test Workflow</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — build, run, verify, push</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Build the image (tag it — never rely on :latest)</span>
docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .

<span class="comment"># Run it locally and map the port</span>
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 --name easypj-app easypj:v1

<span class="comment"># Verify it serves /jobs/</span>
curl http://localhost:8000/jobs/

<span class="comment"># Inspect logs if it misbehaves</span>
docker logs easypj-app

<span class="comment"># Tag &amp; push to a registry so Kubernetes can pull it later</span>
docker tag easypj:v1 myregistry.io/easypj:v1
docker push myregistry.io/easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Security Checklist</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>Do ✅</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Use slim / distroless base images</li>
                            <li>Run as non-root (<code>USER app</code>)</li>
                            <li>Pin versions (<code>python:3.13-slim</code>, not <code>:latest</code>)</li>
                            <li>Scan images (Trivy / Grype) in CI</li>
                            <li>Use <code>.dockerignore</code> to exclude secrets</li>
                            <li>Set <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> in K8s</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div>
                        <h5>Avoid ❌</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Running as root inside the container</li>
                            <li><code>:latest</code> (unpredictable, non-reproducible)</li>
                            <li>Baking secrets / <code>.env</code> into the image</li>
                            <li>Full-OS base images (Ubuntu 1 GB+)</li>
                            <li>Leaving build tools in the final image</li>
                            <li>Skipping vulnerability scans</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>The Build Context — What <code>docker build .</code> Actually Sends</h4>
                <p>That trailing <code>.</code> in <code>docker build -t easypj:v1 .</code> is not "the Dockerfile" — it's the <strong>build context</strong>: the whole directory is tarred up and shipped to the build engine, which is why a fat context (a <code>.git</code> folder, a venv, node_modules) makes builds slow and can leak secrets into the image. The <code>.dockerignore</code> is what trims it.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">docker build . — the context is the directory, not just the Dockerfile</div>
<pre>
   your project dir (the "context")            🔨 build engine (BuildKit)
   ┌───────────────────────────┐              ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ Dockerfile                 │  ──tar the── │ reads Dockerfile, runs each   │
   │ requirements.txt           │   WHOLE dir  │ instruction → cached layers   │
   │ easypj/  manage.py         │  ──────────▶ │ (.dockerignore'd files never  │
   │ .git/  venv/  .env  ✂️       │   (minus     │  enter the context)           │
   │   ^ excluded by .dockerignore│  ignored)   └──────────────────────────────┘
   └───────────────────────────┘
   💡 A 5 MB context builds fast; a 2 GB context (venv/.git) crawls and risks leaking .env.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>ENV vs ARG — Build-Time vs Run-Time Variables</h4>
                <p>A frequent source of broken builds and leaked secrets. They look similar but live in different worlds:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th><code>ARG</code></th><th><code>ENV</code></th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Available at…</td><td>build time only</td><td>build time AND run time</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>In the running container?</td><td>❌ gone</td><td>✅ present (e.g. <code>DEBUG=False</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Set via</td><td><code>docker build --build-arg VER=1.2</code></td><td>baked in, or overridden by K8s env</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secrets?</td><td>❌ no — visible in image history</td><td>❌ no — use Secrets/ConfigMaps in K8s</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Never bake secrets into an image (any layer)</h5>
                    <p>Both <code>ARG</code> and <code>ENV</code> values are visible in the image's layer history (<code>docker history</code>) — and anyone who can pull the image can read them. easypj's <code>SECRET_KEY</code> and DB password belong in Kubernetes <strong>Secrets</strong>, injected at runtime (Ch 23), never in the Dockerfile.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Going Smaller Still — Distroless &amp; Scratch</h4>
                <p>Slim is good; <strong>distroless</strong> is the next level — an image with <em>only</em> your app and its runtime: no shell, no package manager, almost no OS userland. Tiny attack surface, tiny size.</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5><code>-slim</code></h5><p>Minimal Debian + your runtime. Has a shell for debugging. The pragmatic default (what we used for easypj).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div><h5>distroless</h5><p><code>gcr.io/distroless/python3</code> — just Python + your app. No shell, no apt. Hardest to attack; debug via ephemeral containers (Ch 31).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚛️</div><h5><code>scratch</code></h5><p>Literally empty. Only for static binaries (Go/Rust). Not for Python, which needs a userland.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                    <p>Build <code>easypj:v1</code> from the multi-stage Dockerfile, run it with <code>-p 8000:8000</code>, and <code>curl /jobs/</code>. Then edit a source file and rebuild — watch the <code>pip install</code> layer stay <em>cached</em> while only the code layer rebuilds. Finally run <code>docker history easypj:v1</code> to see the layers and prove no secret leaked in.</p>
                    <details class="drill">
                        <summary>Show solution</summary>
                        <div class="drill-solution">
                            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) build &amp; run</span>
docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 --name easypj easypj:v1
curl http://localhost:8000/jobs/          <span class="comment"># → HTML/JSON from easypj ✅</span>

<span class="comment"># 2) edit a .py file, rebuild — note the cache</span>
docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .
<span class="comment"># =&gt; ... RUN pip install ...   ---&gt; Using cache   ✅  (deps layer reused)</span>
<span class="comment"># =&gt; COPY . .                  ---&gt; running        (only your code layer rebuilds)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3) inspect layers; confirm no secret baked in</span>
docker history easypj:v1                   <span class="comment"># each line = one layer; no .env / SECRET_KEY</span></code></pre>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>What you should observe:</strong> the second build reuses the <code>pip install</code> layer (<em>Using cache</em>) and only re-runs <code>COPY . .</code> — proof that ordering deps before source pays off. <code>docker history</code> shows the layer stack with no secret values, because <code>.env</code> is excluded by <code>.dockerignore</code> and config comes from K8s at runtime.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.5 Essential Container Commands</h3>
                <p>You'll <strong>build</strong> images with <code>docker</code> on your laptop, but on a real cluster node Docker often isn't installed — there you use <code>crictl</code> (the CRI CLI) to inspect what the kubelet is running. Know both, and know <em>which</em> to reach for.</p>

                <h4>docker — on your build host</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th><th>CKA relevance</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>docker build -t easypj:v1 .</code></td><td>Build an image from a Dockerfile</td><td>Prep images for K8s</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker run -p 8000:8000 easypj:v1</code></td><td>Run a container, expose a port</td><td>Test before deploying</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker ps</code></td><td>List running containers</td><td>Debug outside K8s</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>View a container's logs</td><td>Mirrors <code>kubectl logs</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker exec -it &lt;id&gt; sh</code></td><td>Shell into a container</td><td>Mirrors <code>kubectl exec</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker images</code></td><td>List local images</td><td>Check what's cached</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker push &lt;registry&gt;/easypj:v1</code></td><td>Push an image to a registry</td><td>Required so K8s can pull it</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker pull &lt;image&gt;</code></td><td>Pull an image from a registry</td><td>Pre-cache on nodes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker stop &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td>Stop a running container</td><td>Graceful shutdown</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>docker system prune</code></td><td>Clean up unused images/containers</td><td>Reclaim node disk</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>docker ↔ crictl — the same moves, on a node</h4>
                <p>On a node, <code>crictl</code> talks straight to the CRI (containerd/CRI-O). The verbs are nearly identical to Docker, so the muscle memory transfers:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>🐳 docker (build host)</th><th>🔧 crictl / ctr (on the node)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>List containers</td><td><code>docker ps</code></td><td><code>crictl ps</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View logs</td><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Shell in</td><td><code>docker exec -it &lt;id&gt; sh</code></td><td><code>crictl exec -it &lt;id&gt; sh</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Inspect</td><td><code>docker inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List images</td><td><code>docker images</code></td><td><code>crictl images</code> / <code>ctr images ls</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Runtime info</td><td><code>docker info</code></td><td><code>crictl info</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                    <h5>⚡ crictl is your lifeline</h5>
                    <p>When the <strong>apiserver or kubelet is down</strong>, <code>kubectl</code> is useless — but the containers are still running on the node. <code>crictl ps</code> / <code>crictl logs</code> let you see and debug them <em>directly</em> via the runtime. This is exactly the move the Troubleshooting domain (30%) wants from you (Ch 32).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 <code>ctr</code> vs <code>crictl</code> — don't mix them up</h5>
                    <p><code>crictl</code> is the <strong>Kubernetes-aware</strong> CRI client — it sees pods/containers the way the kubelet does. <code>ctr</code> is containerd's <em>low-level</em> tool and is namespace-scoped (use <code>-n k8s.io</code> to see K8s containers). For troubleshooting cluster workloads, reach for <code>crictl</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Three containerd CLIs — Which One, When</h4>
                <p>On a containerd node you'll meet three tools. They overlap, so people get lost — here's the one-line rule for each:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>crictl</code></td><td>The CRI debug CLI (K8s view: pods + containers)</td><td><strong>Troubleshooting cluster pods</strong> on a node — your default</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>nerdctl</code></td><td>A <strong>Docker-compatible</strong> CLI for containerd</td><td>Building/running containers by hand with familiar <code>docker</code>-style commands (<code>nerdctl run</code>, <code>nerdctl build</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ctr</code></td><td>containerd's raw, low-level client</td><td>Deep containerd internals; namespace-scoped (<code>-n k8s.io</code>) — rarely needed</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Mental model</h5>
                    <p><code>nerdctl</code> ≈ "docker, but for containerd" (great when you miss Docker's ergonomics on a node). <code>crictl</code> ≈ "what the kubelet sees" (great for debugging actual pods). <code>ctr</code> ≈ "the engine's bare-metal wrench." For the CKA, <code>crictl</code> is the one that earns points.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>A Container's Lifecycle — the States You'll See</h4>
                <p>Whether via <code>docker ps</code>, <code>crictl ps</code>, or <code>kubectl describe pod</code>, a container moves through a small set of states. Recognizing them tells you instantly whether something is healthy, stuck, or dead:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Container lifecycle states</div>
<pre>
   📥 created ──start──▶ ▶️ running ──stop (SIGTERM→SIGKILL)──▶ ⏹️ stopped ──rm──▶ 🗑️ removed
                          │   ▲
                   pause  │   │ unpause
                          ▼   │
                       ⏸️ paused                  💥 exited (code ≠ 0)  ← crashed; K8s → CrashLoopBackOff
                                                  ✅ exited (code 0)    ← finished cleanly (e.g. a Job)
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   In Kubernetes you mostly see:  Running · Terminated(reason: Completed | Error | OOMKilled) · Waiting
   (Waiting covers ContainerCreating, ImagePullBackOff, CrashLoopBackOff — the kubelet is retrying.)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §2.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.6 Container Runtimes: Beyond Docker</h3>
                <p>Since Kubernetes <strong>v1.24</strong>, Docker is no longer a supported runtime. The kubelet now talks to runtimes directly through the <strong>Container Runtime Interface (CRI)</strong> — and as the admin, you install &amp; configure that runtime on every node.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Term — CRI (Container Runtime Interface)</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>CRI</strong> is the standard gRPC "socket" the kubelet uses to tell a runtime "run / stop / list this container." Any CRI-compliant runtime plugs in. This is one of the three extension interfaces from Chapter 1 (CRI · CNI · CSI) — the runtime is swappable, so Kubernetes itself never has to care which one you picked.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Container runtime evolution — Docker out of the data path (v1.24)</div>
<pre>
   BEFORE v1.24                          AFTER v1.24
   🤖 kubelet                            🤖 kubelet
      │                                     │  CRI (gRPC)
   dockershim  ❌ (removed)              ┌──┴───────┐
      │                                  ▼          ▼
   🐳 Docker daemon                  🐋 containerd   🔴 CRI-O
      │                                  │          │
   containerd                          📦          📦   containers
      │                              (direct — no Docker daemon, no shim)
   📦 containers
   💡 Docker IMAGES still work everywhere (they're OCI). Only the node-side
      runtime changed. You still BUILD with Docker; nodes RUN via containerd.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div>
                        <h5>containerd</h5>
                        <p>The industry standard, extracted from Docker itself. Default for kubeadm; used by GKE, EKS, AKS. Lightweight, stable, CNCF-graduated.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Default runtime for kubeadm clusters</li>
                            <li>CLIs: <code>ctr</code> (low-level), <code>crictl</code> (K8s-native)</li>
                            <li>Config: <code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div>
                        <h5>CRI-O</h5>
                        <p>Purpose-built for Kubernetes — nothing more. Preferred by Red Hat / OpenShift. Minimal footprint, releases version-aligned to K8s.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Used by OpenShift &amp; Fedora CoreOS</li>
                            <li>Version-locked to Kubernetes versions</li>
                            <li>Config: <code>/etc/crio/crio.conf</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The #1 runtime gotcha: cgroup-driver mismatch</h5>
                    <p>The kubelet and the container runtime must use the <strong>same cgroup driver</strong> — on modern systemd hosts that means <code>systemd</code>. If containerd is left on the <code>cgroupfs</code> default while the kubelet expects <code>systemd</code>, the <strong>kubelet won't start and the node stays NotReady</strong>. The fix is to set <code>SystemdCgroup = true</code> in <code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code> and restart containerd (you'll do this in Ch 9).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Runtime Troubleshooting — Start Here on a NotReady Node</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — on the node</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Is the runtime even running?</span>
systemctl status containerd

<span class="comment"># Did the kubelet complain about the CRI?</span>
journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i "container runtime"

<span class="comment"># List containers + check the CRI endpoint, via crictl</span>
crictl ps
crictl info | grep -i runtime

<span class="comment"># Restart the runtime if it's wedged</span>
sudo systemctl restart containerd</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Full Call Chain — Who Actually Starts the Container</h4>
                <p>"containerd runs the container" is true but vague. There are several layers between the kubelet's decision and a process appearing on the node, and knowing them tells you <em>exactly</em> which component to suspect when a container won't start:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">From kubelet to a running process — the runtime stack</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet            "start this pod's containers"
      │  CRI (gRPC over a unix socket: /run/containerd/containerd.sock)
      ▼
   🐋 containerd         high-level runtime: pulls image, unpacks layers (snapshots),
      │                  manages container lifecycle &amp; the image store
      ▼
   🔧 containerd-shim    one per container; keeps the container alive &amp; reparents it
      │                  if containerd restarts (so a runtime upgrade ≠ killing pods)
      ▼
   ⚙️ runc               low-level OCI runtime: the program that actually creates it —
      │                  clone() with namespace flags + cgroups + pivot_root, then exec
      ▼
   📦 your process       gunicorn (PID 1 inside the container) — easypj is now running
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Job</th><th>If it's broken you'd see…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td>Decides &amp; calls the CRI</td><td>Node NotReady; nothing starts</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CRI socket</strong></td><td>The gRPC endpoint</td><td><code>crictl</code> "connect: no such file or directory"</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>containerd</strong></td><td>Pull/unpack image, manage lifecycle</td><td>ImagePull errors; runtime down → NotReady</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>containerd-shim</strong></td><td>Babysit each container</td><td>Rare; containers die when containerd restarts</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>runc</strong></td><td>clone()+cgroups+pivot_root, exec</td><td>"failed to create container" / permission errors</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why the shim matters (a real-world win)</h5>
                    <p>Because each container has its own <strong>shim</strong> holding it, you can <em>restart or upgrade containerd itself without killing running pods</em> — the shims keep the containers alive and re-attach. That's the kind of detail that separates "knows the commands" from "understands the system," and it's why a runtime hiccup doesn't automatically take your workloads down.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Building Images Without a Docker Daemon</h4>
                <p>If nodes no longer run Docker, how does CI build the easypj image — especially when the build itself runs <em>as a pod inside the cluster</em>? You don't need the Docker daemon at all. Several tools build OCI images without it (and without root), which matters because giving CI access to a Docker socket is a serious security hole:</p>
                <div class="card-grid">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🧰</div>
                        <h5>BuildKit</h5>
                        <p>The modern build engine (now Docker's default too). Faster, parallel, better caching. Runs standalone as <code>buildkitd</code> — no full Docker needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🪄</div>
                        <h5>kaniko</h5>
                        <p>Builds from a Dockerfile <strong>inside a container/pod</strong>, no daemon, no privileged access. The go-to for building images in a K8s-based CI pipeline.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔨</div>
                        <h5>buildah</h5>
                        <p>Red Hat's daemonless builder (pairs with <code>podman</code>). Scriptable, rootless, OCI-native. Common on OpenShift.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Why this matters for security</h5>
                    <p>The old pattern — mounting <code>/var/run/docker.sock</code> into a CI container so it can build — effectively grants <strong>root on the node</strong> (the Docker daemon runs as root). Daemonless, rootless builders (kaniko/buildah) exist precisely to kill that risk. You'll wire one into the easypj CI/CD pipeline in Chapter 39.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Node Image Garbage Collection — Keeping Disk From Filling</h4>
                <p>Every image a node pulls is cached on its disk (the <strong>imagefs</strong>). Over time that fills up — and a full disk triggers <code>DiskPressure</code>, which evicts pods. So the <strong>kubelet automatically garbage-collects</strong> unused images; as the admin, you tune the thresholds:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubelet image garbage collection — high/low watermarks</div>
<pre>
   node disk usage (imagefs) ▲
   100% ┤
        │   imageGCHighThresholdPercent (default 85%) ─────────── ▶ GC TRIGGERS
    85% ┤■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■
        │   kubelet deletes UNUSED images, oldest-first, until…
    80% ┤■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■  ◀── imageGCLowThresholdPercent (default 80%)
        │   (images in use by running pods are NEVER removed)
     0% ┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶ time
   ⚠️ cross 85% and never recover → DiskPressure taint → pods evicted (Ch 30/32).
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Admin takeaways on image GC</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>It's automatic, but the <strong>thresholds are yours to set</strong> in the kubelet config (<code>imageGCHighThresholdPercent</code> / <code>LowThresholdPercent</code>).</li>
                        <li>Manual cleanup on a node: <code>crictl rmi --prune</code> removes unused images now.</li>
                        <li>This is the K8s automation of <code>docker system prune</code> — and a frequent root cause when a node mysteriously goes <code>NotReady</code> with <code>DiskPressure</code>.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Where this leads</h5>
                    <p>You now know the <em>artifact</em> (an OCI image of cached layers), <em>how it's built</em> (with or without Docker), and the <em>engine</em> that runs it (the full kubelet → CRI → containerd → shim → runc stack). Chapter 3 zooms out to the whole control plane that decides <em>where</em> these containers run; Chapter 9 has you install &amp; configure the runtime as part of <code>kubeadm</code>; and Chapter 32 returns here when a node goes NotReady.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 2.7 IMAGES IN KUBERNETES — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s2-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">2.7 Images in Kubernetes — Pull Policy, Registries &amp; Failures</h3>
                <p>You built <code>easypj:v1</code> in §2.4. Now the <em>cluster</em> side: how Kubernetes <strong>names</strong>, <strong>pulls</strong>, <strong>authenticates</strong>, and <strong>garbage-collects</strong> images — and the pull errors you'll diagnose on the exam.</p>

                <h4>① Image reference syntax &amp; defaults</h4>
                <p>A full reference is <code>[REGISTRY[:PORT]/]NAME[:TAG][@DIGEST]</code>. Leave parts off and Kubernetes fills in surprising defaults:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>It resolves to</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>easypj</code></td><td><code>docker.io/library/easypj:latest</code> — default registry <em>and</em> default tag</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>easypj:v1</code></td><td><code>docker.io/library/easypj:v1</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>registry.example.com:5000/easypj:v1</code></td><td>your private registry on port 5000</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>easypj@sha256:9f2a…</code></td><td>pinned by <strong>digest</strong> — exact bytes, ignores tags</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning"><p class="mb-0">⚠️ A bare image with no registry comes from <strong>Docker Hub</strong> (<code>docker.io</code>), not your private registry — a classic "why is it pulling the wrong thing?" surprise.</p></div>

                <h4>② Tags vs digests — mutable name vs immutable bytes</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>🏷️ Tag (<code>:v1</code>)</h5><p>Human-friendly but <strong>mutable</strong> — someone can re-push <code>:v1</code> to different bytes, so two nodes may run different builds of "the same" tag.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>🔒 Digest (<code>@sha256:…</code>)</h5><p><strong>Immutable</strong> &amp; tamper-evident — the same digest is byte-identical everywhere, forever. Pin production with <code>easypj:v1@sha256:…</code>.</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>③ <code>imagePullPolicy</code> — the default depends on your tag (exam favourite)</h4>
                <p>If you don't set <code>imagePullPolicy</code>, Kubernetes <em>infers</em> it from the tag — the single most-missed image fact on the exam:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Image tag</th><th>Default policy</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>:latest</code> or no tag</td><td><code>Always</code></td><td>pulls from the registry on every pod start</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>a fixed tag (<code>:v1</code>)</td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>pulls only if the node lacks it (cached otherwise)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>@sha256:…</code> digest</td><td><code>IfNotPresent</code></td><td>cached; the digest already guarantees the bytes</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">Override explicitly with <code>Always</code> / <code>IfNotPresent</code> / <code>Never</code> (<code>Never</code> = it must already be on the node). <strong>Gotcha:</strong> running <code>:latest</code> in production means an unannounced re-pull can change your app under you — always deploy a fixed tag or a digest.</p>

                <h4>④ Private registries — <code>imagePullSecrets</code></h4>
                <p>To pull from a private registry, create a docker-registry Secret and reference it — on the Pod, or (cleaner) on the ServiceAccount so every pod inherits it.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — create the registry secret</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com --docker-username=ci --docker-password=$PASS</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod spec — reference it</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  imagePullSecrets:
  - name: regcred          <span class="comment"># ← pod-level: this pod only</span>
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: registry.example.com/easypj:v1</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><p class="mb-0">💡 Attach <code>imagePullSecrets</code> to the namespace's ServiceAccount and <em>every</em> pod using it pulls privately with no per-pod config. A missing/wrong secret shows up as <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> with an <em>"unauthorized"</em> event.</p></div>

                <h4>⑤ Pull failure states — read the event, fix the cause</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Means</th><th>Usual cause → fix</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>ErrImagePull</code></td><td>a pull attempt just failed</td><td>transient / registry hiccup → kubelet retries</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ImagePullBackOff</code></td><td>repeated failures, backing off (up to ~5 min)</td><td>wrong name/tag · missing <code>imagePullSecrets</code> · registry unreachable</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>InvalidImageName</code></td><td>the reference is malformed</td><td>fix the image string (typo / bad characters)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">Diagnose with <code>kubectl describe pod easypj-web</code> → the <strong>Events</strong> section names the exact error.</p>

                <h4>⑥ Why a pod sits in <code>ContainerCreating</code> — serial pulls</h4>
                <p class="mb-0">By default the kubelet pulls images <strong>one at a time per node</strong> (to avoid hammering the registry). Several big images on a fresh node = pods wait in <code>ContainerCreating</code> for minutes. Pre-pulling images onto nodes — or smaller images (§2.4) — makes pods start faster. Tunable via the kubelet's <code>serializeImagePulls</code> / <code>maxParallelImagePulls</code>.</p>

                <h4>⑦ Image garbage collection — the disk watermarks</h4>
                <p class="mb-0">The kubelet auto-deletes unused images when node disk crosses <code>imageGCHighThresholdPercent</code> (default <strong>85%</strong>), oldest first, until it drops to <code>imageGCLowThresholdPercent</code> (default <strong>80%</strong>). Images in use by running pods are never removed. If disk stays high the node gets a <strong>DiskPressure</strong> condition and starts evicting pods (Ch 3 §node health / Ch 30). Manual sweep: <code>crictl rmi --prune</code>.</p>

                <h4>⑧ Beyond — RuntimeClass (choosing a stronger sandbox)</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>What it is</h5><p>A <code>RuntimeClass</code> object maps a name → a runtime <em>handler</em> the node supports; a pod opts in with <code>spec.runtimeClassName</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>Why you'd use it</h5><p>Run untrusted / multi-tenant workloads in a stronger sandbox — <strong>gVisor</strong> (user-space kernel) or <strong>Kata</strong> (lightweight VM) — instead of default <code>runc</code>. Adds per-pod overhead.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — what a container REALLY is: namespaces, cgroups &amp; union filesystems</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>A container is <strong>not</strong> a tiny VM. It's an ordinary Linux <strong>process</strong> with three kernel features applied: <strong>namespaces</strong> (what it can <em>see</em>), <strong>cgroups</strong> (what it can <em>use</em>), and a <strong>union filesystem</strong> (what it can <em>read/write</em>). That's the whole trick — and understanding it is what turns "OOMKilled" and "Exit 137" from mysteries into one-glance diagnoses.</p>

                    <h5>🗺️ The three pillars of isolation</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌──────────────────────── HOST LINUX KERNEL (shared by all containers) ────────────────────┐
   │   🪟 NAMESPACES (visibility)     📊 CGROUPS (limits)        💾 UNION FS (layers)            │
   │   PID · NET · MNT · UTS          CPU · Memory · IO          overlay2 (lowerdir = image,    │
   │   IPC · USER · CGROUP · TIME     PIDs · HugeTLB             upperdir = writable top)        │
   └───────────────┬──────────────────────┬────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────┘
                   │                       │                        │
                   ▼                       ▼                        ▼
   ┌──────────────────────────────── 📦 the easypj container = a PROCESS ──────────────────────┐
   │  PID ns   → sees itself as PID 1            (host sees PID 48291)                          │
   │  NET ns   → its own eth0 (veth → host bridge); the whole POD shares this ns               │
   │  MNT ns   → sees /app as root              (host: /var/lib/containerd/…)                   │
   │  UTS ns   → hostname "easypj-web-6d8f9-x7k2"                                               │
   │  USER ns  → sees UID 0, mapped to UID 1000 on the host (rootless)                          │
   │  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────   │
   │  CPU cgroup    → limited to 500m (0.5 core) → over-use is THROTTLED (slowed)               │
   │  Memory cgroup → limited to 512Mi          → over-use is OOM-KILLED (Exit 137)             │
   │  PIDs cgroup   → max N processes            → fork() fails with EAGAIN                       │
   │  rootfs        → overlay2: read-only image layers + one writable upper layer               │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>

                    <h5>🪟 The 8 namespaces — how a container gets its own view of the world</h5>
                    <p>When containerd starts easypj, it calls <code>clone()</code>/<code>unshare()</code> with namespace flags. Each namespace isolates one dimension:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>Isolates</th><th>Why it matters in K8s</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>PID</strong></td><td>Process IDs — container sees its own PID 1</td><td><code>kubectl exec</code> PIDs ≠ host PIDs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NET</strong></td><td>Interfaces, routes, iptables</td><td>Each <em>pod</em> gets one NET ns, shared by its containers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>MNT</strong></td><td>Mount points / root filesystem</td><td>emptyDir, hostPath, PVC all mount here</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>UTS</strong></td><td>Hostname &amp; domain</td><td>The pod's hostname</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>IPC</strong></td><td>Shared memory, semaphores</td><td>Containers in a pod share IPC</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>USER</strong></td><td>UID/GID mappings</td><td><code>runAsUser</code>, rootless containers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CGROUP</strong></td><td>The cgroup root it can see</td><td>Hides the host's cgroup tree</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>TIME</strong></td><td>Clock offsets</td><td>Newer kernels (5.6+)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>💾 Union filesystem — how image layers become a writable rootfs</h5>
                    <p>overlay2 stacks the read-only image layers under a single writable layer, presenting one merged view:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ upperdir (READ-WRITE)  → all runtime writes land here          │ ← EPHEMERAL: gone when
   │   …/&lt;container-id&gt;/upper                                        │   the container dies!
   ├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │            ▼  merged, overlaid view the process sees           │
   ├───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ lowerdir (READ-ONLY image layers, newest on top):             │
   │   L3  sha256:jkl…   easypj source code                        │
   │   L2  sha256:ghi…   pip packages                              │
   │   L1  sha256:def…   python:3.13-slim base                     │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   read:  check upper → L3 → L2 → L1 → ENOENT      write: copy-up from lower, then modify
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA insight — Exit Code 137 = OOMKilled.</strong> 137 = 128 + SIGKILL(9). The container blew past its <code>resources.limits.memory</code> and the kernel's OOM killer terminated it. Confirm with <code>kubectl describe pod easypj-web-…</code> → <code>State: Terminated, Reason: OOMKilled</code>. The fix: raise the memory limit or cut the app's usage. And because <code>upperdir</code> is ephemeral, <em>any</em> data a restarted container wrote is gone — which is the whole reason Volumes (Ch 21) exist.</div>

                    <h5>📊 cgroups — how a memory/CPU limit is actually enforced (v1 vs v2)</h5>
                    <p>"cgroups limit resources" is where most explanations stop. Here's the real mechanism, because it directly explains throttling, OOMKills, and the kubelet's QoS classes. A <strong>cgroup</strong> is a kernel-managed group of processes with accounting + limits; your easypj container is placed in one, and the kubelet writes its limits into special files:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Pod spec field</th><th>cgroup setting (v2)</th><th>What the kernel does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.requests.cpu: 250m</code></td><td><code>cpu.weight</code> (relative share)</td><td>Under contention, you get a <em>proportional</em> slice — a guarantee, not a cap</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.limits.cpu: 500m</code></td><td><code>cpu.max</code> (quota per period)</td><td>Exceed it → process is <strong>THROTTLED</strong> (slowed), never killed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.limits.memory: 512Mi</code></td><td><code>memory.max</code></td><td>Exceed it → the <strong>OOM killer</strong> terminates it → <code>Exit 137</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources.requests.memory</code></td><td>(scheduling + <code>memory.low</code>)</td><td>Used to place the pod; soft-protects that much memory</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th></th><th>cgroups v1 (older)</th><th>cgroups v2 (modern default)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Hierarchy</td><td>One tree <em>per controller</em> (cpu, memory, … separate)</td><td>One <strong>unified</strong> tree for all controllers</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Controllers could disagree / be mounted differently</td><td>Cleaner, consistent limits + pressure metrics (PSI)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Kubernetes</td><td><strong>Deprecated (v1.35)</strong> — kubelet refuses to start on a v1 node by default (override: <code>failCgroupV1: false</code>)</td><td>Recommended &amp; the default; required for some features (PSI-based eviction, MemoryQoS)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 This is exactly why CPU and memory behave differently.</strong> CPU is <em>compressible</em> → over-limit = throttle (your app just runs slower). Memory is <em>incompressible</em> → over-limit = OOMKill (you can't "slow down" RAM). That asymmetry drives every right-sizing decision in Chapter 15.</div>

                    <h5>📥 How an image pull actually works (manifest → config → layer blobs)</h5>
                    <p>"It pulls the image" hides a precise OCI flow — and understanding it explains layer caching, multi-arch images, and most ImagePull failures:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   crictl/kubelet asks containerd for  easypj@sha256:abc…
        │
        ▼  1. GET the MANIFEST (or manifest LIST for multi-arch)
   🏪 registry  ──▶  manifest list:  amd64 → sha256:111…   arm64 → sha256:222…
        │            (containerd picks the node's architecture)
        ▼  2. GET the IMAGE CONFIG (json): env, CMD/ENTRYPOINT, user, the layer order
        ▼  3. GET each LAYER BLOB by digest (only the ones not already cached!)
   📦 layers:   sha256:def… (base)   sha256:ghi… (deps)   sha256:jkl… (easypj code)
        │
        ▼  4. containerd UNPACKS each blob into a snapshot, then overlay2 stacks them
   🗂️ ready rootfs  ──▶  runc creates the container from it
   💡 Step 3 is content-addressed: if another image already pulled sha256:def…, it's
      REUSED from the node — that's why shared base images make pods start fast.
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 ImagePull failures, decoded.</strong> Fails at step 1 (manifest) → wrong name/tag or "not found". Fails with "unauthorized" → private registry, no <code>imagePullSecret</code>. Fails at step 3 (a blob) → network/registry flake or disk-full on the node. Knowing <em>which</em> step broke turns a vague <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> into a targeted fix.</div>

                    <h5>🧩 Which namespaces are shared <em>inside</em> a pod</h5>
                    <p>A pod's containers don't each get 8 fresh namespaces — they <strong>share some</strong> (that's what makes them a pod) and isolate the rest:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Namespace</th><th>Isolates</th><th>Shared across a pod's containers?</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>NET</strong></td><td>interfaces, IP, ports</td><td>✅ yes — one pod IP; that's why containers reach each other on <code>localhost</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>IPC</strong></td><td>shared memory, semaphores</td><td>✅ yes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>PID</strong></td><td>process IDs</td><td>optional — only with <code>shareProcessNamespace: true</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>MNT · UTS · USER · CGROUP</strong></td><td>mounts · hostname · UID maps · cgroup view</td><td>❌ no — each container is isolated</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The pause container</strong> holds the shared NET + IPC namespaces open, so app containers join them — which is why the pod keeps its IP even as individual containers restart.</div>

                    <h5>📂 overlayfs — how the writable layer really behaves (copy-on-write)</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   READ  /app/manage.py    → search top-down: upper? no → L3? no → L2? no → L1 base? YES   (no copy)
   WRITE /app/cache.txt    → the file is COPIED UP into the writable upper layer, then changed there
   DELETE /usr/bin/oldtool → can't touch the read-only image, so overlayfs writes a "whiteout"
                             marker that simply HIDES the file from the container's view
   ⚠️ the upper layer is the container's ephemeral R/W scratch — gone when the container is removed
      (exactly why durable pod data needs a Volume / PVC — Ch 6)
</pre>

                    <h5>🚪 Exit codes — decode them at a glance</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Exit</th><th>Means</th><th>First move</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>clean exit</td><td>fine (Job done; <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> still restarts it)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>1</code>–<code>128</code></td><td>app error / crash</td><td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>137</code></td><td>128 + 9 (SIGKILL) → usually <strong>OOMKilled</strong></td><td>raise the memory limit / fix a leak (<code>describe</code> shows <code>OOMKilled</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>143</code></td><td>128 + 15 (SIGTERM)</td><td>graceful stop — or shutdown ran past the grace period</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>139</code></td><td>128 + 11 (SIGSEGV)</td><td>app/runtime bug (segfault)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> images and runtimes follow the <strong>OCI</strong> (Open Container Initiative) image &amp; runtime specs, which is why a Docker-built image runs under containerd/CRI-O unchanged. Layers are content-addressed by <code>@sha256:</code> digest (pin these for true reproducibility). Every pod also has a tiny <strong>pause/sandbox</strong> container that <em>holds</em> the shared network + IPC namespaces so app containers can join them. <code>privileged: true</code> effectively switches namespace isolation OFF — which is why restricted Pod Security forbids it. For stronger isolation without a full VM, sandboxed runtimes like <strong>gVisor</strong> (user-space kernel) and <strong>Kata</strong> (lightweight microVM) plug in via <code>RuntimeClass</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Containers share the host kernel</strong> — they're processes with namespaces (visibility) + cgroups (limits) + union FS (layers), so they're MB &amp; milliseconds, not GB &amp; minutes.</li>
                    <li><strong>An image is a stack of cached layers.</strong> Order the Dockerfile cheap→expensive (deps before source) so edits don't bust the <code>pip install</code> cache.</li>
                    <li><strong>Multi-stage + non-root</strong> = small, fast-pulling, lower-CVE production images.</li>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes runs containers via the CRI</strong> (containerd / CRI-O), not Docker — the dockershim was removed in v1.24. You still build with Docker.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>crictl</code> is your node-level lifeline</strong> when kubectl/kubelet is down.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>cgroup-driver mismatch.</strong> containerd default (<code>cgroupfs</code>) vs kubelet's <code>systemd</code> → kubelet won't start, node stays NotReady. Set <code>SystemdCgroup = true</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>:latest</code> is a trap.</strong> It's non-reproducible and defaults <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to <code>Always</code> (tagged images default to <code>IfNotPresent</code>) — surprise pulls and surprise versions.</li>
                    <li><strong>Running as root.</strong> A container breakout as root = root on the node. Always <code>USER</code> + <code>runAsNonRoot</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>"Kubernetes removed Docker, so my images break."</strong> No — images are OCI; only the node runtime changed.</li>
                    <li><strong>Using <code>docker</code> on a node.</strong> It may not be installed; use <code>crictl</code> (and <code>ctr -n k8s.io</code> for containerd internals).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Debug image issues fast:</strong> <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; | grep -A8 Events</code> — it names ImagePullBackOff vs OOMKilled vs CrashLoop instantly.</li>
                    <li><strong>Order layers for cache hits</strong> — COPY requirements + <code>pip install</code> BEFORE <code>COPY . .</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pin digests</strong> (<code>image@sha256:…</code>) for reproducible, tamper-evident deploys.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reach for <code>crictl ps</code> / <code>crictl logs</code></strong> the moment <code>kubectl</code> stops responding.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 2)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">build host (docker) + node (crictl)</span>
                </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># build host</span>
docker build -t easypj:v1 .          <span class="comment"># build image from Dockerfile</span>
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 easypj:v1 <span class="comment"># run + expose</span>
docker push myregistry.io/easypj:v1  <span class="comment"># push so K8s can pull</span>

<span class="comment"># on a node (Docker may be absent) — talk to the CRI directly</span>
crictl ps                            <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;            <span class="comment"># view logs</span>
crictl images                        <span class="comment"># images cached on this node</span>
crictl info | grep -i runtime        <span class="comment"># which runtime + endpoint</span>
systemctl status containerd          <span class="comment"># is the runtime up?</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Containerize easypj &amp; Inspect It Like an Admin</h5>
                <p>A chapter capstone that exercises the whole pipeline — try each step before peeking:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Build the multi-stage <code>easypj:v1</code> and confirm it's slim.</li>
                    <li>Run it and hit <code>/jobs/</code>.</li>
                    <li>On a cluster node, use <code>crictl</code> (not <code>docker</code>) to find the running container, read its logs, and identify the runtime.</li>
                    <li>Verify the node's cgroup driver is <code>systemd</code>.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">1–2 · build &amp; run (build host)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">docker build -f Dockerfile.production -t easypj:v1 .
docker images easypj                 <span class="comment"># ~150MB (slim, multi-stage) ✅ not ~900MB</span>
docker run -d -p 8000:8000 --name easypj easypj:v1
curl http://localhost:8000/jobs/     <span class="comment"># easypj responds ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">3–4 · inspect on a cluster node (no docker, no kubectl)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps | grep easypj              <span class="comment"># find the container ID</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;            <span class="comment"># its stdout/stderr (like kubectl logs)</span>
crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt; | grep -i image   <span class="comment"># which image it runs</span>
crictl info | grep -i runtime        <span class="comment"># runtime = containerd, + the CRI endpoint</span>

<span class="comment"># 4) confirm cgroup driver matches the kubelet (must be systemd)</span>
grep -i SystemdCgroup /etc/containerd/config.toml   <span class="comment"># → SystemdCgroup = true ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>What you proved:</strong> easypj ships as a small OCI image; the node runs it via <strong>containerd</strong> (Docker is nowhere on the data path); <code>crictl</code> gives you full visibility <em>without</em> kubectl; and the cgroup driver lines up so the kubelet stays healthy. That's the entire Chapter 2 mental model exercised end-to-end.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A — CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 2 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the mental model — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">State two concrete differences between a VM and a container.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) A VM runs a <strong>full guest OS</strong> on a hypervisor (gigabytes, boots in minutes); a container <strong>shares the host kernel</strong> (megabytes, starts in milliseconds). (2) VM isolation is hardware-level/strong; container isolation is process-level via namespaces + cgroups.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>🖥️ Virtual Machine</th><th>📦 Container</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Kernel</td><td>its own full guest OS on a hypervisor</td><td>shares the host kernel</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Size · start</td><td>GBs · boots in minutes</td><td>MBs · starts in milliseconds</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Isolation</td><td>hardware-level (strong)</td><td>process-level — namespaces + cgroups</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The shared kernel is the root cause of every "light &amp; fast" property — and of the weaker isolation that later security chapters harden.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. Give three likely causes and how to confirm.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Likely: (1) wrong image name/tag, (2) private registry with no <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, (3) registry unreachable / rate-limited. Confirm with <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> and read the <strong>Events</strong> — it states the exact pull error.</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Likely cause</th><th>Event says…</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>wrong image name/tag</td><td><code>not found</code> / <code>manifest unknown</code></td><td>correct the image reference</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>private registry, no creds</td><td><code>unauthorized</code></td><td>add an <code>imagePullSecrets</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>registry unreachable / throttled</td><td><code>timeout</code> / <code>429</code></td><td>fix networking · mirror the image</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Events is always the first stop. "unauthorized" → credentials; "not found" → name/tag typo; "timeout" → network/registry.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a multi-stage build produce a smaller, safer image?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>builder</strong> stage holds compilers and <code>-dev</code> headers; the <strong>runtime</strong> stage copies only the installed result and discards the builder. Less in the final image = smaller size, faster pulls, and a smaller attack surface / fewer CVEs.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>easypj's image drops from ~900 MB to ~150 MB this way, and nothing exploitable (gcc, build deps) ships to production.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question"><code>kubectl</code> is unavailable on a node. How do you list and log the containers?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <strong><code>crictl</code></strong>, which talks to the CRI directly:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the node — talk to the CRI directly</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">crictl ps                  <span class="comment"># list running containers</span>
crictl logs &lt;id&gt;           <span class="comment"># view a container's logs</span>
crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;        <span class="comment"># full details: mounts, env, state</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>When the apiserver/kubelet is down, the containers still run — crictl is the node-level lifeline (Ch 32).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the CRI, and what replaced dockershim in v1.24?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>Container Runtime Interface</strong> is the standard gRPC API the kubelet uses to drive a runtime. v1.24 removed the <code>dockershim</code> translation layer; nodes now run containers via a CRI-native runtime — <strong>containerd</strong> (default) or <strong>CRI-O</strong>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Docker-built images still work (they're OCI) — only the node-side runtime path changed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Reorder a Dockerfile so <code>pip install</code> stays cached across code edits.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Copy the deps list and install <em>before</em> copying the source, so editing code only busts the last layer:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">Dockerfile — deps before source</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-docker">COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt   <span class="comment"># cached unless deps change</span>
COPY . .                                             <span class="comment"># only THIS layer busts on a code edit</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Layers cache top-down by content; put what changes <em>least</em> (deps) low, what changes <em>most</em> (your code) high.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name three ways to shrink the easypj image.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) Multi-stage build (drop build tools); (2) a slim/distroless base (<code>python:3.13-slim</code> not full <code>python</code>); (3) a <code>.dockerignore</code> to exclude <code>.git</code>, caches, venvs. (Bonus: combine <code>RUN</code> layers, <code>--no-cache-dir</code> for pip.)</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Smaller images pull faster (quicker pod starts/scaling) and expose fewer packages to scan/exploit.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why run as non-root, and how do you do it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>If an attacker escapes a root container, they're root on the node. Declare a user in the image <em>and</em> enforce it in the pod:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">image (Dockerfile) + pod (securityContext)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"># Dockerfile
RUN useradd app
USER app
---
# Pod spec
securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Defense in depth — the image declares intent; the cluster enforces it cluster-wide via Pod Security Standards, so a bad image can't slip through (Ch 27).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is <code>:latest</code> dangerous, and what should you use instead?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>:latest</code> is a moving pointer — two nodes can run different builds, and it defaults <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to <code>Always</code>. Use <strong>pinned tags</strong> (<code>easypj:v1</code>) or, best, an immutable <strong>digest</strong> (<code>easypj@sha256:…</code>).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Reproducibility &amp; rollback depend on knowing <em>exactly</em> which bytes ran. Digests are tamper-evident too.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node's kubelet won't start, citing cgroups. What's the fix?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The runtime and kubelet disagree on the cgroup driver. Set the runtime to <code>systemd</code>, then restart both:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/containerd/config.toml → systemd driver</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">[plugins."io.containerd.grpc.v1.cri".containerd.runtimes.runc.options]
  SystemdCgroup = true

sudo systemctl restart containerd kubelet    <span class="comment"># node should go Ready</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On systemd hosts both must use the <code>systemd</code> driver; a mismatch leaves the node NotReady (Ch 9).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch2-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 2 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken state, and you produce the fix with real commands.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. You run <code>kubectl describe pod easypj-web</code> and see the event: <em>"Failed to pull image 'myregistry.io/app:v2': rpc error: code = Unknown desc = Error response from daemon: unauthorized"</em>. What is the most likely cause and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The image is stored in a <strong>private registry</strong> and the pod doesn't have credentials configured. Fix by creating an <code>imagePullSecret</code> and referencing it in the pod spec:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">fix the pull auth</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create the secret with registry credentials</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=myregistry.io \
  --docker-username=myuser \
  --docker-password=mypass

<span class="comment"># Add to pod spec under spec.imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment"># imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - name: regcred</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> means the kubelet failed to pull the container image. The "unauthorized" error specifically indicates authentication failure. Common causes: (1) Private registry without <code>imagePullSecrets</code>, (2) Expired/wrong credentials, (3) Wrong registry URL. On the CKA exam, always check the <strong>Events</strong> section of <code>kubectl describe pod</code> — it tells you exactly why the pull failed. Other ImagePullBackOff causes include typos in the image name or tag not existing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>crictl</code> and <code>docker</code> commands? On a Kubernetes node running containerd, which tool should you use to list running containers and why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Use <strong><code>crictl</code></strong>. It communicates directly with the CRI (Container Runtime Interface) and works with any CRI-compliant runtime (containerd, CRI-O).</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th>Task</th><th>crictl Command</th><th>Docker Equivalent</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>List containers</td><td><code>crictl ps</code></td><td><code>docker ps</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>List images</td><td><code>crictl images</code></td><td><code>docker images</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>View logs</td><td><code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>docker logs &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Inspect container</td><td><code>crictl inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td><td><code>docker inspect &lt;id&gt;</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Since Kubernetes v1.24 removed <code>dockershim</code>, Docker is no longer a supported runtime. Most clusters now use <code>containerd</code> directly. The <code>crictl</code> tool is the standard debugging tool for CRI runtimes. On the CKA exam, if you SSH to a node and need to inspect containers at the runtime level, <code>crictl</code> is the expected tool. Configure it via <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code> to point at the runtime socket (e.g., <code>unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code>).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the difference between the three <code>imagePullPolicy</code> values: <code>Always</code>, <code>IfNotPresent</code>, and <code>Never</code>. What is the default policy when you use the <code>:latest</code> tag versus a specific tag like <code>:v1.2</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong><code>Always</code></strong> — kubelet always contacts the registry to check for newer image. Re-pulls if digest changed.</li>
                            <li><strong><code>IfNotPresent</code></strong> — kubelet uses the local cached image if available; pulls only if not cached.</li>
                            <li><strong><code>Never</code></strong> — kubelet never pulls; uses local image only. Fails if image isn't cached.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p><strong>Defaults:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>:latest</code> tag → defaults to <code>Always</code></li>
                            <li>Specific tag (e.g., <code>:v1.2</code>) → defaults to <code>IfNotPresent</code></li>
                            <li>No tag specified → treated as <code>:latest</code> → defaults to <code>Always</code></li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is a common trap on the CKA. If you specify <code>image: nginx</code> without a tag, Kubernetes treats it as <code>nginx:latest</code> with <code>imagePullPolicy: Always</code>. This means every pod restart will contact the registry. In air-gapped or exam environments, this can cause unexpected <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> errors. Best practice: always use specific tags and explicitly set <code>imagePullPolicy</code> to avoid surprises.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a <strong>container image layer</strong>? Why are layers important for both storage efficiency and pod startup speed in Kubernetes?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A container image is built from <strong>read-only layers</strong>. Each layer represents a filesystem change from a Dockerfile instruction (<code>FROM</code>, <code>RUN</code>, <code>COPY</code>, etc.). Layers are:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Cached and shared</strong> — If two images share a base layer (e.g., <code>python:3.11-slim</code>), the layer is stored and downloaded only once</li>
                            <li><strong>Content-addressable</strong> — Each layer is identified by its SHA256 digest, enabling deduplication</li>
                            <li><strong>Immutable</strong> — once built, a layer never changes; containers add a thin writable layer on top</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>For Kubernetes: shared layers mean that if node already has the base image cached, pulling a new version only downloads changed layers, dramatically speeding up pod startup.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>While the CKA doesn't test image building, understanding layers helps diagnose issues: (1) Large images cause slow pulls and startup — use slim/alpine bases. (2) <code>imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</code> leverages the layer cache. (3) If a node's disk fills up with cached images, use <code>crictl rmi --prune</code> to clean unused images. The kubelet also has garbage collection settings (<code>imageGCHighThresholdPercent</code>) that automatically clean old images when disk pressure is detected.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod spec references a <strong>multi-container pod</strong> with an init container and a sidecar container. Explain the difference between init containers and regular sidecar containers in terms of execution order and lifecycle.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Init containers:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run <strong>sequentially before</strong> any app containers start</li>
                            <li>Must complete successfully (exit 0) before the next init container or app containers start</li>
                            <li>If an init container fails, the pod restarts according to its <code>restartPolicy</code></li>
                            <li>Common uses: wait for a dependency, clone a git repo, populate a shared volume</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p><strong>Sidecar containers:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run <strong>alongside</strong> the main app container for the entire pod lifetime</li>
                            <li>Share the pod's network namespace (communicate via <code>localhost</code>) and volumes</li>
                            <li>Common uses: log shipping, service mesh proxy, monitoring agent</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  initContainers:            <span class="comment"># Run first, sequentially</span>
    - name: init-db-check
      image: busybox
      command: ['sh', '-c', 'until nslookup db-service; do sleep 2; done']
  containers:                <span class="comment"># Run together after init completes</span>
    - name: easypj-web       <span class="comment"># Main container</span>
      image: easypj:v1
    - name: log-shipper      <span class="comment"># Sidecar</span>
      image: fluentd:latest</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKA frequently tests multi-container pod patterns. Key points: (1) Init containers share volumes with app containers but NOT the same array — they're under <code>initContainers:</code>. (2) As of Kubernetes v1.28+, there's a native <code>restartPolicy: Always</code> for sidecar containers declared in <code>initContainers</code> that makes them true sidecars. (3) All containers in a pod share the same IP address and port space. A sidecar on port 8080 conflicts with the app on 8080.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to troubleshoot a pod on a worker node. The <code>kubectl</code> command is not available on the node. What tools can you use to inspect containers, view logs, and check images directly on the node?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all running containers on the node</span>
sudo crictl ps

<span class="comment"># List all containers (including stopped/failed)</span>
sudo crictl ps -a

<span class="comment"># View logs of a specific container</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
sudo crictl logs --tail 50 &lt;container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># Inspect container details (config, state, mounts)</span>
sudo crictl inspect &lt;container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># List all pods on this node</span>
sudo crictl pods

<span class="comment"># List cached images</span>
sudo crictl images

<span class="comment"># Check kubelet status and logs</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>On the CKA exam, you will encounter scenarios where you must SSH to a node and troubleshoot without <code>kubectl</code>. The two essential tools are: (1) <strong><code>crictl</code></strong> — for container-level inspection, and (2) <strong><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code></strong> — for kubelet logs showing why pods fail to start. Remember that <code>crictl</code> talks to the container runtime (containerd) via the CRI socket, while <code>kubectl</code> talks to the API server. They show different views of the same containers.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the OCI (Open Container Initiative) and why does it matter that Docker images are OCI-compliant? Can you use Docker-built images on a Kubernetes cluster running containerd?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Yes, absolutely.</strong> Docker-built images work perfectly on containerd or CRI-O clusters because they conform to the <strong>OCI Image Specification</strong>.</p>
                        <p>The OCI defines two standards:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>OCI Image Spec</strong> — how container images are structured (layers, manifests, configs)</li>
                            <li><strong>OCI Runtime Spec</strong> — how containers are executed (runc, crun)</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Docker, containerd, CRI-O, and Podman all produce and consume OCI-compliant images. The tool you use to <em>build</em> the image is irrelevant — what matters is the resulting image format.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A common misconception is that removing Docker from Kubernetes means Docker images no longer work. This is false. The removal of <code>dockershim</code> only affects the <em>runtime layer</em>, not the <em>image format</em>. You can build with Docker, Buildah, Kaniko, or any OCI-compliant tool and run the images on any CRI-compliant runtime. The CKA may test this conceptual understanding in a scenario where someone incorrectly believes images need to be "converted" for containerd.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> state. What does this status mean, and what are the systematic steps to diagnose the root cause?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> means the container starts, crashes, and Kubernetes keeps restarting it with exponentially increasing delays (10s, 20s, 40s... up to 5 minutes).</p>
                        <p><strong>Diagnostic steps:</strong></p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pod status and restart count</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -o wide

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check events for clues</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web | grep -A 20 "Events:"

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check container logs (current attempt)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check previous crash logs</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web --previous

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check if it's an OOMKill</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web | grep -i "OOMKilled"

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check if command/args are wrong</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -o yaml | grep -A 5 "command\|args"</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> is one of the most common CKA troubleshooting scenarios. Common root causes: (1) <strong>Application error</strong> — app crashes on startup (check <code>kubectl logs --previous</code>), (2) <strong>Wrong command/args</strong> — container entrypoint fails, (3) <strong>Missing ConfigMap/Secret</strong> — referenced env vars don't exist, (4) <strong>OOMKilled</strong> — container exceeds memory limit, (5) <strong>Liveness probe failure</strong> — probe kills the container before it's ready. The <code>--previous</code> flag is crucial because it shows logs from the <em>last crashed</em> instance, not the currently starting one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a <strong>container registry</strong>? Explain the full image reference format <code>registry/repository:tag@sha256:digest</code> and what each part means.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A container registry is a storage and distribution system for container images. The full reference format:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">anatomy</span></div>
<pre><code>registry.example.com/namespace/image-name:tag@sha256:abc123...

│                    │          │          │    │
├─ Registry host     │          │          │    └─ Digest (immutable hash)
│  (docker.io if     │          │          └─ Tag (mutable label)
│   omitted)         │          └─ Image name
│                    └─ Namespace/org
└─ Full reference</code></pre></div>
                        <p><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>nginx</code> → <code>docker.io/library/nginx:latest</code></li>
                            <li><code>mycompany/app:v2</code> → <code>docker.io/mycompany/app:v2</code></li>
                            <li><code>gcr.io/my-project/app:v1</code> → explicit registry</li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Understanding image references is critical for troubleshooting <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> errors on the CKA. Common mistakes: (1) Omitting the registry for private images, (2) Using <code>:latest</code> when a specific tag is needed, (3) Not specifying <code>imagePullSecrets</code> for private registries. The <strong>digest</strong> (<code>@sha256:...</code>) is the most reliable way to pin an image — tags are mutable and can be overwritten, but digests are content-addressable and immutable.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You SSH to a Kubernetes worker node and run <code>crictl ps</code> but get the error: <em>"connect: no such file or directory"</em>. What is wrong and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <code>crictl</code> tool cannot find the container runtime socket. Fix by configuring the runtime endpoint:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option 1: Set environment variable</span>
export CONTAINER_RUNTIME_ENDPOINT=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Create/edit crictl config file</span>
sudo cat &gt; /etc/crictl.yaml &lt;&lt;EOF
runtime-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
image-endpoint: unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock
timeout: 10
debug: false
EOF

<span class="comment"># Now try again</span>
sudo crictl ps</code></pre></div>
                        <p><strong>Common socket paths:</strong></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>containerd: <code>unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code></li>
                            <li>CRI-O: <code>unix:///var/run/crio/crio.sock</code></li>
                        </ul>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The CKA exam may present a node where <code>crictl</code> isn't configured. The tool needs to know which socket to connect to — this is the CRI endpoint. If <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code> doesn't exist or has the wrong socket path, <code>crictl</code> will fail. To find the correct socket, check what runtime is installed: <code>systemctl status containerd</code> or <code>systemctl status crio</code>. Then set the endpoint accordingly. This is a quick fix that can save you from being stuck on a troubleshooting question.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 2 Visual Summary — easypj source → image → running pod</div>
<pre>
   👩‍💻 your code            🔨 BUILD (laptop / CI)              🏪 REGISTRY            ☸️ KUBERNETES NODE
   ┌──────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────┐         ┌────────────┐        ┌──────────────────────┐
   │ easypj source │ ─────▶ │ Dockerfile (multi-    │ ──push─▶ │ easypj:v1   │ ─pull─▶ │ 🤖 kubelet → CRI       │
   │ + requirements│  docker│ stage, non-root)     │         │ (cached     │        │ 🐋 containerd starts   │
   │               │  build │ → 📀 image = layers   │         │  layers)    │        │ 📦 the container       │
   └──────────────┘        └──────────────────────┘         └────────────┘        │   namespaces + cgroups │
                                                                                    │   + overlay2 rootfs    │
   ① build once  ──────────  ② push to a registry  ──────────  ③ nodes pull &amp; run ──┘  :8000  ✅
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One immutable image, built once, runs identically everywhere — and the cluster runs it via the CRI,
   not Docker. That artifact is the unit every later chapter schedules, scales, heals, and secures.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> You take the <code>easypj</code> source plus its dependencies and a <strong>Dockerfile</strong> (multi-stage, non-root) and <code>docker build</code> it into an <strong>image</strong> — a stack of read-only layers. You build it <em>once</em>, then <strong>push</strong> it to a registry where the layers are cached and shareable.</p>
                    <p>When a node needs to run it, the <strong>kubelet</strong> asks the container runtime (containerd) — through the <strong>CRI</strong>, not Docker — to <strong>pull</strong> the image and start a container. That container is just a normal Linux process that the kernel isolates with <strong>namespaces</strong> (what it can see), <strong>cgroups</strong> (what it can use), and an <strong>overlay</strong> root filesystem (its files).</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the image is one immutable artifact that runs identically on every node — that reproducibility is why containers exist. From here on, this image is the <em>unit</em> every later chapter schedules, scales, heals, and secures.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 2 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 3 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch3">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 3 · Kubernetes Architecture — The Admin's Blueprint</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every troubleshooting question is really one question: <strong>"which component failed?"</strong> — and you can't fix what you can't name. This chapter is the map: every part of the control plane and every part of a worker node, what each does, what breaks when it's gone, and the exact end-to-end journey from <code>kubectl apply</code> to a running easypj pod. It is the foundation the entire rest of the guide stands on.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Architecture</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 CKA Core (25%)</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 easypj / jobpost</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The whole cluster at a glance — <strong>control plane</strong> (brain) + <strong>worker nodes</strong> (muscle)</li>
                    <li>✅ Every control-plane component: <strong>API server · etcd · scheduler · controller-manager</strong> (+ cloud-controller-manager)</li>
                    <li>✅ Every worker-node component: <strong>kubelet · kube-proxy · container runtime</strong></li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>How a Pod is born</strong> — the full 8-step journey, grounded on easypj</li>
                    <li>✅ The three network layers (pod / service / external) and what routes each</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Static pods</strong> — the admin-only concept that runs the control plane itself</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🏗️ Architecture knowledge is CKA gold — it's the foundation for ~55% of the exam</h5>
                <p>Every troubleshooting task assumes you know how the components interact. Match the symptom to the component and you've half-solved it:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Node NotReady?</strong> → check the <code>kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pods stuck Pending?</strong> → check the <code>scheduler</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>State lost?</strong> → restore <code>etcd</code> (backup/restore is tested directly).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl</code> hangs?</strong> → the <code>apiserver</code> is down; debug from the node with <code>crictl</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — the Chapter 3 Learning Arc</h4>
            <p>We zoom from the whole cluster, into each half (control plane, then nodes), then trace one pod's entire life across them, and finish with the network and the static-pod trick that bootstraps it all.</p>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chapter 3 Roadmap — name every part, then watch it work</div>
<pre>
   3.1 🗺️ THE WHOLE CLUSTER ──▶ 3.2 🧠 CONTROL PLANE ──▶ 3.3 💪 WORKER NODES
   brain + muscle, one map      apiserver·etcd·scheduler   kubelet·kube-proxy·
        │                       ·controller-manager        container runtime
        ▼                                                        │
   3.6 📌 STATIC PODS  ◀──── 3.5 🌐 NETWORKING ◀──── 3.4 🐣 HOW A POD IS BORN
   the control plane runs    pod · service · external    the 8-step journey,
   AS static pods            (3 layers)                  kubectl → Running
        │
        ▼
   ✅ READY — you can name any component, say what breaks without it, and trace a pod end-to-end.
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Answers…</th><th>One-line takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-1"><strong>3.1</strong> Overview</a></td><td>What are the two halves?</td><td>Control plane decides; worker nodes run. You manage both.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-2"><strong>3.2</strong> Control plane</a></td><td>What's the "brain"?</td><td>apiserver (door) · etcd (state) · scheduler (placement) · controllers (reconcile).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-3"><strong>3.3</strong> Worker nodes</a></td><td>What's the "muscle"?</td><td>kubelet (agent) · kube-proxy (service routing) · runtime (runs containers).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-4"><strong>3.4</strong> Pod is born</a></td><td>How does a pod actually start?</td><td>kubectl → apiserver → etcd → controllers → scheduler → kubelet → Running.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-5"><strong>3.5</strong> Networking</a></td><td>How do they all connect?</td><td>Flat pod network + Service VIPs + external entry — 3 layers.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-6"><strong>3.6</strong> Static pods</a></td><td>What runs the control plane?</td><td>The kubelet runs YAML in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> directly.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s3-7"><strong>3.7</strong> Node health &amp; comms</a></td><td>How does the CP know a node is alive?</td><td>Conditions + Lease heartbeat + 40s/5min eviction; secure apiserver hub.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s3-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.1 Cluster Architecture Overview</h3>
                <p>A Kubernetes cluster is two layers: a <strong>control plane</strong> (the brain — makes every decision) and one or more <strong>worker nodes</strong> (the muscle — actually run the workloads). As a CKA admin you install, configure, and repair <em>both</em>. Everything else in this chapter is a zoom-in on one of these two halves.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The complete cluster — brain + muscle</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ───────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  🧠 CONTROL PLANE  (decides)                                                            │
   │   ┌──────────────┐ ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────────┐    │
   │   │ 📡 apiserver  │ │ 🗄️ etcd     │ │ 📋 scheduler    │ │ 🔄 controller-manager       │    │
   │   │ the ONLY door │ │ ALL state  │ │ places pods    │ │ reconcile loops             │    │
   │   │ :6443         │ │ :2379/2380 │ │ filter→score→  │ │ (node · ReplicaSet ·        │    │
   │   │ authN/Z/admit │ │ (Raft)     │ │ bind           │ │  endpoint · serviceaccount) │    │
   │   └──────┬───────┘ └────────────┘ └────────────────┘ └────────────────────────────┘    │
   │          │   ⚠️ ONLY the apiserver talks to etcd; every component &amp; kubectl goes        │
   │          │      THROUGH the apiserver (the single choke point).                          │
   │  ────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │
   │  💪 WORKER NODES  (run)                                                                  │
   │   ┌──────▼──────────────────┐                  ┌─────────────────────────┐              │
   │   │ Node 1                   │                  │ Node 2                   │             │
   │   │ 🤖 kubelet  🌐 kube-proxy │                  │ 🤖 kubelet  🌐 kube-proxy │             │
   │   │ 🐋 containerd            │                  │ 🐋 containerd            │             │
   │   │ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐   │                  │ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐   │             │
   │   │ │📦 easypj│ │📦 easypj│   │                  │ │📦 easypj│ │🐘 postgres│ │             │
   │   │ │  web    │ │  web    │   │                  │ │  web    │ │  :5432   │ │             │
   │   │ └────────┘ └────────┘   │                  │ └────────┘ └────────┘   │             │
   │   └─────────────────────────┘                  └─────────────────────────┘              │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="arch-layers">
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-app">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🧠</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Control Plane</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Makes every orchestration decision</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>kube-apiserver</span><span>etcd</span><span>kube-scheduler</span><span>controller-manager</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-k8s">
                        <span class="layer-icon">💪</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Worker Nodes</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Run the actual workloads</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>kubelet</span><span>kube-proxy</span><span>containerd/CRI-O</span><span>Pods</span></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="arch-layer layer-infra">
                        <span class="layer-icon">🌐</span>
                        <span class="layer-name">Networking Layer</span>
                        <span class="layer-desc">Connects everything together</span>
                        <div class="layer-examples"><span>CNI plugin</span><span>CoreDNS</span><span>Service network</span><span>Pod network</span></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The mental shortcut</h5>
                    <p>For the rest of your CKA life, sort every problem into one bucket: is it a <strong>decision</strong> problem (control plane — won't schedule, won't reconcile, can't reach the API) or a <strong>run</strong> problem (a node — container won't start, NotReady, networking)? That single split routes you to the right component before you type a command.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.2 Control-Plane Components — Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>These are the components you maintain, troubleshoot, and restore. For each, learn three things: its <strong>job</strong>, its <strong>config location</strong>, and — most exam-relevant — <strong>what breaks when it's gone</strong>.</p>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🍽️ In plain English — the cluster as a restaurant</h5>
                    <p>If the components feel abstract, map them to a busy restaurant — it makes every role (and every failure) intuitive:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Restaurant</th><th>Kubernetes</th><th>So if it's gone…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>🛎️ the <strong>front desk</strong> — every order/request goes through it</td><td>📡 <strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>nobody can place or change orders (no kubectl)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>📖 the <strong>order ledger</strong> — the single written record of every order</td><td>🗄️ <strong>etcd</strong></td><td>lose the ledger = lose the whole restaurant's state</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🪑 the <strong>host</strong> who seats each party at the best table</td><td>📋 <strong>scheduler</strong></td><td>new parties wait at the door (pods Pending)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🧑‍💼 the <strong>floor manager</strong> watching everything &amp; fixing gaps</td><td>🔄 <strong>controller-manager</strong></td><td>no one refills empty tables (no self-healing)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>👨‍🍳 the <strong>line cook</strong> at each station who actually makes the food</td><td>🤖 <strong>kubelet</strong> (on each node)</td><td>that station stops cooking (node NotReady)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>🏃 the <strong>runner</strong> delivering plates to the right table</td><td>🌐 <strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td>food's made but never reaches diners (Services break)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="mb-0">Notice the front desk (apiserver) is the only one who reads/writes the ledger (etcd) — exactly like the real cluster. Keep this picture; the rest of §3.2 just adds detail to each role.</p>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2a">a) 📡 kube-apiserver — the front door</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📡 kube-apiserver</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">The <strong>only</strong> entry point. Every <code>kubectl</code> call, every kubelet, every controller talks to it — and it's the only component that reads/writes etcd.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔐</span> Pipeline</div><div class="anatomy-value">Each request: <strong>authentication</strong> (certs/tokens) → <strong>authorization</strong> (RBAC) → <strong>admission</strong> (validate/mutate) → persist to etcd.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📂</span> Config</div><div class="anatomy-value">Static-pod manifest: <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Port</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>6443</code> (secure HTTPS)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">No <code>kubectl</code>, no new pods, nodes can't report status. <strong>Existing pods keep running</strong> — the kubelet is independent.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2b">b) 🗄️ etcd — the cluster database</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🗄️ etcd — the brain's memory</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">A distributed key-value store holding <strong>ALL</strong> cluster state under <code>/registry/…</code> — pods, services, secrets, RBAC, everything.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Consensus</div><div class="anatomy-value">Uses <strong>Raft</strong> — a quorum (majority) must agree on every write. That's why HA clusters run 3 or 5 etcd members.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Ports</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>2379</code> (client) · <code>2380</code> (peer)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> CKA</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Backup &amp; restore is directly tested</strong> — know <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> / <code>restore</code> cold.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 etcd lost = cluster lost</h5>
                    <p>Lose etcd without a backup and your <em>entire</em> cluster — every pod, service, secret, and RBAC rule — is gone. This is precisely why "back up etcd" is the #1 admin task and a guaranteed exam item (Ch 29).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd backup (CKA skill)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># snapshot (cert paths come from /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># verify it's readable</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2c">c) 📋 kube-scheduler — the matchmaker</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 kube-scheduler</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches for Pods with no node assigned, evaluates every node, and binds the pod to the best fit.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> Process</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Filter</strong> (which nodes <em>can</em> run it?) → <strong>Score</strong> (which is best?) → <strong>Bind</strong> (assign the winner).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📊</span> Considers</div><div class="anatomy-value">CPU/mem requests, nodeSelector, affinity/anti-affinity, taints/tolerations, PV topology, spread constraints.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">New pods stay <code>Pending</code> (existing pods unaffected). Spot it: <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code>.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>Watch</h5><p>See a Pod with empty <code>nodeName</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>Filter</h5><p>Drop nodes that can't fit (resources, taints, affinity)</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Score</h5><p>Rank the survivors (spread, balance, preferences)</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Bind</h5><p>Assign the top node → write to etcd → kubelet takes over</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2d">d) 🔄 kube-controller-manager — the watchdog</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔄 kube-controller-manager (many controllers, one process)</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🖥️</span> Node ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches node heartbeats; marks a node <code>NotReady</code> after ~40s of silence, evicts its pods after ~5 min.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> ReplicaSet ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Keeps the pod count right — spec says 3, only 2 running → creates 1 more.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> Endpoint ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Wires Services to Pods by maintaining the EndpointSlice (the list of ready pod IPs).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> ServiceAccount ctrl</div><div class="anatomy-value">Creates the default ServiceAccount for each new namespace.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> If it fails</div><div class="anatomy-value">No self-healing: crashed pods aren't replaced, scaling/endpoints stall, dead nodes aren't acted on.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">A controller loop — the heart of Kubernetes</div>
<pre>
   ┌─────────── CONTROLLER LOOP (runs forever) ───────────┐
   │  👁️ OBSERVE  ──▶  🧮 COMPARE  ──▶  🛠️ ACT  ──┐         │
   │  actual state    desired vs       reconcile          │
   │  "2 pods"        actual "want 3"  "create 1 pod"      │
   │      ▲                                    │           │
   │      └────────────────────────────────────┘           │
   └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj ReplicaSet: spec replicas=3, see 2 → create 1 → repeat
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s3-2e">e) ☁️ cloud-controller-manager — the cloud glue (optional)</h4>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Only on cloud clusters</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>cloud-controller-manager</strong> integrates a cloud provider: provisioning LoadBalancers, managing routes, and handling node lifecycle (detecting deleted VMs). Bare-metal/kubeadm clusters don't run it. It exists so the core controllers stay cloud-agnostic — another example of Kubernetes keeping vendor specifics behind a plug.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.3 Worker-Node Components — Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>Every node — worker <em>and</em> control-plane — runs three things that actually make containers happen: the <strong>kubelet</strong> (agent), <strong>kube-proxy</strong> (service routing), and a <strong>container runtime</strong>. These are where "run" problems live.</p>

                <h4>a) 🤖 kubelet — the node agent</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🤖 kubelet</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Receives PodSpecs from the API server, drives the runtime to start/stop containers, runs probes, and reports status back.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚡</span> Key fact</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>The kubelet is NOT a pod</strong> — it's a <code>systemd</code> service on the node. Check it with <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📂</span> Config</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔌</span> Port</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>10250</code> (HTTPS API)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> Troubleshoot</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> — most common causes: expired cert, runtime down, swap on, or cgroup-driver mismatch.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>b) 🌐 kube-proxy — the network plumber</h4>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🌐 kube-proxy</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> Role</div><div class="anatomy-value">Programs each node's dataplane so a <strong>Service VIP</strong> load-balances to the right backing Pod IPs.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⚙️</span> Modes</div><div class="anatomy-value"><strong>iptables</strong> (default), <strong>IPVS</strong> (faster at 1000+ services), <strong>nftables</strong> (newer).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> Runs as</div><div class="anatomy-value">A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> (one pod per node) in <code>kube-system</code> — unlike the kubelet, it <em>is</em> a pod.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💡</span> Remember</div><div class="anatomy-value">kube-proxy handles <em>Service</em> networking; the <strong>CNI</strong> handles <em>pod-to-pod</em> networking. Pods ping by IP but Services fail → suspect kube-proxy.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>c) 🐋 container runtime</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐋</div><h5>containerd</h5><p>The default for kubeadm, EKS, AKS, GKE. CNCF-graduated. Drives the actual container lifecycle via the CRI.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔴</div><h5>CRI-O</h5><p>Kubernetes-native, minimal. Used by OpenShift; versioned in lockstep with K8s.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA triage: node shows NotReady? Check in this order</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — is the agent running?</li>
                        <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> — what's the error? (cert / swap / CNI / cgroup)</li>
                        <li><code>systemctl status containerd</code> — is the runtime up?</li>
                        <li><code>crictl ps</code> — can the runtime even list containers?</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Everything the kubelet Actually Does</h4>
                <p>"The kubelet runs containers" undersells it — it's the busiest agent in the cluster and the prime suspect in most node problems. Knowing its full job list tells you exactly what can break:</p>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🤖 the kubelet's responsibilities</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> Register</div><div class="anatomy-value">Registers the node with the apiserver on startup (creates the Node object, advertises capacity/labels).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💓</span> Heartbeat</div><div class="anatomy-value">Renews a <strong>node Lease</strong> (in <code>kube-node-lease</code>) every few seconds so the control plane knows it's alive — stop renewing → NotReady.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👁️</span> Watch</div><div class="anatomy-value">Watches the apiserver for Pods bound to <em>its</em> node (and the static-pod dir on disk).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🐋</span> Run</div><div class="anatomy-value">Drives the CRI to pull images and start/stop containers; sets up the pod sandbox.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Mount</div><div class="anatomy-value">Attaches/mounts volumes via the CSI before starting containers (emptyDir, PVC, configMap/secret).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🩺</span> Probe</div><div class="anatomy-value">Runs liveness/readiness/startup probes → restarts dead containers, gates traffic on readiness.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📊</span> Report</div><div class="anatomy-value">Reports pod &amp; node status back to the apiserver; exposes stats (cAdvisor) for <code>kubectl top</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🧹</span> Evict</div><div class="anatomy-value">Under node pressure (disk/memory) it evicts pods and applies the matching taint (e.g. DiskPressure).</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why this matters: the kubelet is "trusted" but standalone</h5>
                    <p>Because the kubelet reads its work from the apiserver but acts <em>independently</em>, two important things follow: (1) if the <strong>apiserver dies, running pods keep running</strong> — the kubelet doesn't need it to keep containers alive; and (2) the kubelet can run <strong>static pods</strong> with no control plane at all, which is exactly how kubeadm bootstraps the cluster (§3.6). It's the one component that bridges "declared in the API" and "actually running on Linux."</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.4 How a Pod Is Born — The Full Journey</h3>
                <p>This is the single most important sequence in Kubernetes — every troubleshooting task assumes it. Watch what happens after <code>kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml</code>, and notice that <strong>no single component runs the show</strong>: each one reconciles its own slice and the pod emerges.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>kubectl → apiserver</h5><p>HTTPS request to <code>:6443</code> with a client cert.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>authN → authZ → admit</h5><p>Who are you? (cert) · Allowed? (RBAC) · Valid? (admission).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>apiserver → etcd</h5><p>The Deployment object is persisted — desired state recorded.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>controllers</h5><p>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod objects (still nodeless).</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>scheduler</h5><p>Sees a nodeless Pod → filter → score → binds to a node.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">6</div><h5>kubelet</h5><p>On that node: pulls <code>easypj:v2</code>, starts the container, CNI gives it an IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">7</div><h5>kube-proxy</h5><p>Programs Service routing to the new Pod IP.</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">8</div><h5>Running ✅</h5><p>kubelet reports status → apiserver → etcd; the loops keep watching.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml → a Running pod</div>
<pre>
   👩‍💻 kubectl apply
        │ ① HTTPS :6443 + client cert
        ▼
   📡 apiserver ──② authN → authZ(RBAC) → admission──┐
        │ ③ persist Deployment                       │
        ▼                                            │
   🗄️ etcd  (desired state)  ◀───────────────────────┘
        │ ④ controller-manager: Deployment → ReplicaSet → 3 Pod objects (Pending)
        ▼
   📋 scheduler ── ⑤ filter → score → BIND each Pod → a node
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet (on the node) ── ⑥ 🐋 pull easypj:v2 · start container · 🌐 CNI assigns IP
        ▼
   🌐 kube-proxy ── ⑦ program Service routing to the new Pod IP
        ▼
   📦 easypj Running :8000 ── ⑧ status ─▶ apiserver ─▶ etcd     (loops keep reconciling)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The troubleshooting map — match the symptom to the broken step</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Connection refused / kubectl hangs</strong> → step 1–3 (apiserver/etcd down)</li>
                        <li><strong>Forbidden / Unauthorized</strong> → step 2 (RBAC or certs)</li>
                        <li><strong>Pod stuck Pending</strong> → step 5 (scheduler can't place it)</li>
                        <li><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> → step 6 (kubelet can't pull the image)</li>
                        <li><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> → step 6 (container starts, then dies)</li>
                        <li><strong>Service unreachable</strong> → step 7 (kube-proxy / endpoints)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🍽️ In plain English — the same journey, as a restaurant order</h5>
                    <p>Tie it back to §3.2's restaurant: <em>"You hand your order to the <strong>front desk</strong> (apiserver), which checks you're allowed and writes it in the <strong>ledger</strong> (etcd). The <strong>floor manager</strong> (controllers) turns 'a table of 3' into three seat requests; the <strong>host</strong> (scheduler) seats each at a table (node); the <strong>line cook</strong> there (kubelet) actually makes the dish (starts the container); and the <strong>runner</strong> (kube-proxy) makes sure diners can find it (Service routing)."</em></p>
                    <p class="mb-0">Same eight steps, zero jargon — and the failure map still works: nobody seated = the host (scheduler) is out; food never delivered = the runner (kube-proxy) is out.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.5 Cluster Networking Overview</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes networking has one founding rule: <strong>every Pod gets its own unique IP</strong>, and all Pods can reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong> — one flat network. A CNI plugin makes that real. Keep the layers straight and networking stops being scary (full depth in Part 5).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The four network layers — from the wire up to the user</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🌍 EXTERNAL traffic (users, internet)                          │
   │    enters via: NodePort · LoadBalancer · Ingress · Gateway     │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🔗 SERVICE network  (stable virtual IPs, e.g. 10.96.0.0/12)    │
   │    kube-proxy DNATs a Service VIP → a ready Pod IP             │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 📦 POD network  (real pod IPs, e.g. 10.244.0.0/16)            │
   │    assigned by the CNI · flat · every pod reaches every pod     │
   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🖧 NODE network  (the machines/VMs, e.g. 192.168.1.0/24)       │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   easypj-web pod 10.244.1.5  ──reaches──▶  easypj-db 10.244.2.4 (no NAT)
   a client hits the Service VIP → kube-proxy picks a ready easypj-web pod
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>CNI Plugins — Your Choice as Admin</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐯</div><h5>Calico</h5><p>Most popular. Full NetworkPolicy, BGP routing. Common in CKA environments.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐝</div><h5>Cilium</h5><p>eBPF-based, deep observability (Hubble), high performance. NetworkPolicy +.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🟦</div><h5>Flannel</h5><p>Simple VXLAN overlay, easy setup — but <strong>no NetworkPolicy</strong>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🕸️</div><h5>Weave</h5><p>Encrypted mesh, auto-discovery, NetworkPolicy support.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CKA task: install a CNI right after <code>kubeadm init</code></h5>
                    <p>A fresh cluster's pods (including CoreDNS) sit <code>Pending</code> and nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until a CNI is installed — e.g. <code>kubectl apply -f calico.yaml</code>. "No CNI" is a classic NotReady cause: the fix is install a CNI, not restart the kubelet.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Why it's all pluggable — the three "sockets" (CRI · CNI · CSI)</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet   ──CRI──▶  🐋 containerd / CRI-O          RUN containers
   pod network ──CNI──▶  🌐 Calico / Cilium / Flannel   WIRE pods (give IPs)
   volumes     ──CSI──▶  🧱 EBS / PD / Ceph / NFS driver ATTACH storage
   📖 CRI = runtime · CNI = network · CSI = storage — swap the plug, K8s is unchanged.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Rules of the Kubernetes Network Model</h4>
                <p>Every CNI must honour the same contract — four rules that make the "flat network" promise concrete. Memorize these; they're the foundation of all Part 5 troubleshooting:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Rule</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>Every Pod gets its <strong>own unique IP</strong></td><td>No port-mapping juggling — each pod is a "real host" on the network</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td>All Pods reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong></td><td>The destination sees the <em>real</em> source pod IP (flat L3 network)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td>Nodes reach all Pods <strong>without NAT</strong></td><td>The kubelet &amp; health checks talk to pods directly</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td>A Pod sees <strong>its own IP</strong> as others see it</td><td>No surprising address translation inside the pod</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 CoreDNS — names instead of IPs</h5>
                    <p>Pods come and go with changing IPs, so apps address each other by <em>name</em>. <strong>CoreDNS</strong> (a Deployment in <code>kube-system</code>, fronted by the <code>kube-dns</code> Service, usually <code>10.96.0.10</code>) resolves Service names to ClusterIPs — e.g. <code>easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local</code>. Every pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points at it. If name resolution breaks cluster-wide, suspect CoreDNS first (full chapter: Ch 19).</p>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ kube-proxy vs CNI — keep them separate when debugging</h5>
                    <p>The <strong>CNI</strong> implements rules 1–4 (pod-to-pod). <strong>kube-proxy</strong> implements the <em>Service</em> abstraction on top (VIP → pod). So: pods can ping each other by IP but a Service is unreachable → <strong>kube-proxy/endpoints</strong> problem; pods can't reach each other by IP at all → <strong>CNI</strong> problem. That one split routes most networking tickets.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §3.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.6 Static Pods — an Admin-Only Concept</h3>
                <p>Static pods are managed <strong>directly by the kubelet</strong> on one node — not by the scheduler or API server. They're how kubeadm runs the control plane itself, and they're tested directly on the CKA.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Regular pods</h5><p>Created via the API server (kubectl) → stored in etcd → scheduled by the scheduler → managed by controllers.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📌</div><h5>Static pods</h5><p>Created by the kubelet from YAML in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. The kubelet watches that dir and owns the lifecycle. Drop a file → pod appears; remove it → pod gone.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The control plane IS static pods</h5>
                    <p>On a kubeadm cluster, the apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager, and etcd all run as <strong>static pods</strong> — which is why you configure them by editing YAML in the manifests dir (the kubelet reloads automatically):</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — the control-plane static pods</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml  kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep controlplane
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane                       1/1  Running   ← note the -&lt;nodename&gt; suffix</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-controlplane             1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-controlplane    1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-controlplane             1/1  Running</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Creating a Custom Static Pod</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-easypj.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: static-easypj
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Static-pod facts the exam loves</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>You <strong>cannot</strong> <code>kubectl delete</code> a static pod — the kubelet instantly recreates it. Remove the <em>manifest file</em> instead.</li>
                        <li>The kubelet creates a read-only <strong>"mirror pod"</strong> in the API so you can see it; that's why the name carries a <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix.</li>
                        <li>The watched directory is set by <code>staticPodPath</code> in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>.</li>
                        <li>Static pods (kubelet-managed) ≠ DaemonSets (API-managed) — both put one pod per node, but only DaemonSets are API objects.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>etcd Restore — the Companion Procedure</h4>
                <p>You saw <em>backup</em> in §3.2; restore is the other half (and a classic exam task). Always restore to a <strong>new</strong> data dir, then repoint the etcd static pod at it:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd restore</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1) restore the snapshot into a NEW directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># 2) edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml → point the data hostPath</span>
<span class="comment">#    volume from /var/lib/etcd  →  /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># 3) the kubelet sees the manifest change &amp; recreates etcd automatically</span>
<span class="comment"># 4) verify</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Restore gotcha</h5>
                    <p><code>--data-dir</code> must be a <strong>new</strong> path (e.g. <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>), then repoint the manifest. Restoring over the live dir while etcd runs can corrupt it. Full DR drill in Chapter 29.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Component Quick Reference — Ports · Config · Logs</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Port</th><th>Config / manifest</th><th>Logs</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td><code>6443</code></td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td><code>2379/2380</code></td><td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system etcd-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td><code>10259</code></td><td><code>…/kube-scheduler.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>controller-manager</strong></td><td><code>10257</code></td><td><code>…/kube-controller-manager.yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-controller-manager-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td><code>10250</code></td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td><td><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td><code>10256</code></td><td>ConfigMap <code>kube-proxy</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-proxy-*</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>containerd</strong></td><td>—</td><td><code>/etc/containerd/config.toml</code></td><td><code>journalctl -u containerd</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CoreDNS</strong></td><td><code>53</code></td><td>ConfigMap <code>coredns</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system coredns-*</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key file paths to memorize</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — static-pod manifests (control plane)</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> — all cluster certs (CA, server, client)</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/admin.conf</code> — the admin kubeconfig</li>
                        <li><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> — kubelet config (incl. <code>staticPodPath</code>)</li>
                        <li><code>/var/lib/etcd/</code> — etcd data directory</li>
                        <li><code>/etc/cni/net.d/</code> &amp; <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code> — CNI config &amp; binaries</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 3.7 NODE HEALTH & CP↔NODE COMMS — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s3-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">3.7 Node Health &amp; Control-Plane ↔ Node Communication</h3>
                <p>§3.3 named the node's components; this is how the control plane <em>knows a node is alive</em>, <em>how much it can schedule onto it</em>, and <em>how the two halves talk securely</em>. It's the backbone of "node NotReady" troubleshooting (30% domain).</p>

                <h4>① Node status conditions — the health report</h4>
                <p>Each Node object carries a set of <strong>conditions</strong> (see them with <code>kubectl describe node</code>). <code>Ready=True</code> means healthy &amp; schedulable; the four "pressure" conditions are early-warning flags.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Problem state</th><th>Typical cause</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Ready</code></td><td><code>True</code> = healthy · <code>False</code>/<code>Unknown</code> = problem</td><td>kubelet down, lost heartbeat, runtime/CNI broken</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>MemoryPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = low memory</td><td>node RAM nearly exhausted → starts evicting pods</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DiskPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = low disk</td><td>full disk / image cache → eviction + image GC</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>PIDPressure</code></td><td><code>True</code> = too many processes</td><td>fork bomb / runaway workload</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>NetworkUnavailable</code></td><td><code>True</code> = node network not configured</td><td>CNI not installed / route missing</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② Heartbeats, the node Lease &amp; the eviction timeline</h4>
                <p>A node proves it's alive two ways: the kubelet renews a lightweight <strong>Lease</strong> in the <code>kube-node-lease</code> namespace (one per node, ~every 10s — the cheap, frequent heartbeat) <strong>and</strong> posts a fuller <code>.status</code> update whenever something changes (or periodically). When heartbeats stop, the node controller runs a fixed clock:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what happens when a node goes silent</div>
<pre>
   t = 0s     kubelet stops renewing its Lease / status   (node crashed, network cut)
   t ≈ 50s    node controller marks the node  Ready = Unknown   (node-monitor-grace-period)
              └─ adds taint  node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute   (or …/not-ready)
   t ≈ 5min   pods without a matching toleration are EVICTED &amp; rescheduled elsewhere
              └─ eviction is rate-limited so a big outage doesn't stampede the cluster
   kubectl get lease -n kube-node-lease     ← see each node's heartbeat
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">🔑 This is why "I rebooted a node and my pods vanished a few minutes later" is <em>expected</em> — the ~5-minute eviction timer fired. A pod's <code>tolerations</code> for <code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code> / <code>not-ready</code> (with <code>tolerationSeconds</code>) control how quickly it's moved.</p></div>

                <h4>③ Capacity vs Allocatable — what the scheduler actually sees</h4>
                <p>A node's <strong>Capacity</strong> is its total hardware; <strong>Allocatable</strong> is what's left for your pods after reservations for the kubelet, the OS, and an eviction buffer. The scheduler only ever fills <em>Allocatable</em> — which is why a pod can sit <code>Pending</code> even though the node "looks" half-empty.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box">
   NODE CAPACITY   (e.g. 4 CPU / 16Gi)
   ├─ kube-reserved       kubelet, container runtime
   ├─ system-reserved     OS, sshd, systemd
   ├─ eviction-threshold  safety buffer (e.g. memory.available &lt; 100Mi)
   └─ ALLOCATABLE   ◀── the only slice the scheduler can place pods into
   kubectl describe node node-1     → shows Capacity and Allocatable side by side
</pre>

                <h4>④ Control-plane ↔ node communication — the trust asymmetry</h4>
                <p>Traffic from a node <em>into</em> the control plane is secured by default; traffic from the control plane <em>out</em> to nodes is the leg you must harden.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Path</th><th>Security</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Node → control plane</strong></td><td>kubelet / kube-proxy → apiserver <code>:6443</code></td><td>✅ TLS + auth + RBAC by default — the one hub everything dials</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CP → kubelet</strong></td><td>apiserver → kubelet <code>:10250</code> (logs, <code>exec</code>, <code>port-forward</code>, metrics)</td><td>⚠️ HTTPS, but the apiserver won't verify the kubelet's cert unless you set <code>--kubelet-certificate-authority</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CP → nodes / pods / services</strong></td><td>apiserver → cluster IPs</td><td>⚠️ plain HTTP by default — unsafe over untrusted networks; use the <strong>Konnectivity</strong> service for a secured tunnel</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><p class="mb-0">💡 Mental model: <strong>everything dials the apiserver</strong> (secure, single front door); the apiserver dialing back out to nodes is the weaker leg — <strong>Konnectivity</strong> (the konnectivity-agent/server pair) wraps it in a managed tunnel for production.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how the control plane stays consistent &amp; highly available</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The components in §3.2 are simple to name; the <em>machinery</em> that makes several copies of them cooperate without conflict is where real understanding lives. Four mechanisms do the heavy lifting.</p>

                    <h5>① Leader election via Lease — how scheduler &amp; controller-manager run HA</h5>
                    <p>You can run 3 schedulers and 3 controller-managers for high availability, but if all of them acted at once they'd fight (three controllers each creating the "missing" pod). So they're <strong>active-passive</strong>: only one is the leader at a time, chosen by a <strong>Lease</strong> object the others watch.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   3 kube-scheduler replicas         🗄️ a Lease object in kube-system
   ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ sched-1  │ │ sched-2  │ │ sched-3  │ ────▶ │ Lease "kube-scheduler"    │
   │ LEADER ✅ │ │ standby  │ │ standby  │      │ holder: sched-1           │
   └────┬─────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘      │ renewTime: …(every ~2s)   │
        │ only the leader schedules            └──────────────────────────┘
        └─ stops renewing (crash) → a standby grabs the Lease → becomes leader
   kubectl get lease -n kube-system   ← see who holds it
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why it matters:</strong> "I have 3 control-plane nodes but only one scheduler seems to work" is <em>correct</em> — the other two are hot standbys. Check <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code> to see the current holder.</div>

                    <h5>② The Binding subresource — how the scheduler actually places a pod</h5>
                    <p>The scheduler doesn't "move" a pod. It computes the best node, then makes a tiny API call — a <strong>Binding</strong> — that sets the pod's <code>nodeName</code>. The kubelet on that node then notices "a pod is assigned to me" and runs it. Scheduling is just one field being written.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   scheduler picks node2  ──POST /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods/easypj-web-x/binding──▶ 📡 apiserver
   apiserver sets pod.spec.nodeName = "node2"  ──▶ 🗄️ etcd
   🤖 kubelet(node2) is watching pods where nodeName == its own → sees it → starts it
   💡 you can hand-schedule by setting nodeName yourself (bypasses the scheduler entirely)
</pre>

                    <h5>③ API Priority &amp; Fairness (APF) — keeping the apiserver alive under load</h5>
                    <p>Because <em>everything</em> goes through the apiserver, a flood of requests (a buggy controller, a stampede of kubelets) could starve it. <strong>APF</strong> classifies incoming requests into priority levels and fair-queues them, so critical traffic (leader election, node heartbeats) isn't drowned out by bulk list calls.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>FlowSchema</td><td>Matches requests (by user/verb/resource) → assigns them to a priority level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>PriorityLevelConfiguration</td><td>Reserves concurrency shares + a fair queue per level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Effect</td><td>A misbehaving client is throttled (HTTP 429) instead of taking down the API for everyone</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>④ The aggregation layer — extending the API itself</h5>
                    <p>Beyond CRDs, you can register a <strong>second API server</strong> behind the main one via an <code>APIService</code>. The apiserver proxies a slice of the API path to it. That's how <code>metrics-server</code> serves <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> (powering <code>kubectl top</code>) — it's not a CRD, it's an aggregated apiserver.</p>
                    <h5>⑤ REST + watch — the API machinery everything is built on</h5>
                    <p>The apiserver is a <strong>RESTful</strong> server: every object is a URL, and the usual HTTP verbs act on it. The magic extra is <strong>WATCH</strong> — a long-lived stream of changes that lets components react instantly instead of polling. That single mechanism is how the scheduler, controllers, and kubelets all stay in sync.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   REST verbs on a resource:
     GET    /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments/easypj-web   read one
     LIST   /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments              read all
     POST / PUT / PATCH / DELETE                                     create / replace / modify / remove

   WATCH (the key to reactivity):
     GET …/deployments?watch=true&amp;resourceVersion=4821
        ──▶ keeps the connection OPEN and streams events as they happen:
            ADDED {…}   MODIFIED {…rv:4822}   MODIFIED {…rv:4830}   DELETED {…}
     each event carries the new resourceVersion → a client can resume the stream
     after a disconnect from exactly where it left off (no missed &amp; no duplicate work)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>LIST then WATCH</td><td>Get the full state once, then only the deltas — the pattern every informer uses</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resourceVersion</code></td><td>A cursor: "stream changes since here" + the basis of optimistic concurrency</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>watch cache</td><td>The apiserver serves watches from memory, so thousands of watchers don't hammer etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubectl get -w</code></td><td>Is literally a watch stream you can see in your terminal</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this is the whole design:</strong> components never call <em>each other</em> — they all watch the apiserver and react to changes. That's the loose coupling that makes Kubernetes resilient: a component that was briefly down just re-LISTs and resumes watching, catching up to current state automatically.</div>

                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Tie it together:</strong> the apiserver isn't just a CRUD endpoint — it's a REST + watch hub (the API machinery), a consistency &amp; fairness hub (APF), a placement coordinator (Binding), an HA arbiter (Leases), and an extensible surface (aggregation). Master these and "the control plane" stops being a black box.</div>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> reads can be served from the apiserver's <em>watch cache</em> (fast, slightly behind) or forced consistent; every object carries a <code>resourceVersion</code> for optimistic concurrency (Ch 1 UTH); controllers are built on <strong>informers</strong> (shared list-watch + cache) feeding <strong>workqueues</strong> with rate-limiting and retries; and the node controller's NotReady (~40s) → eviction (~5min) timers are why a briefly-down node doesn't instantly lose its pods.</p>

                    <h5>⑥ The full pod-creation trace — what happens <em>inside</em> §3.4's 8 steps</h5>
                    <p>§3.4 showed the <em>journey</em>; here is the <em>internal trace</em> — the named admission plugins, the scheduler's filter/score plugins, and the exact <strong>CRI</strong> calls the kubelet makes. This is the depth CKA troubleshooting questions live at.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
kubectl run easypj-web --image=easypj:v1
   │
   ▼  STAGE 1 — apiserver: authN → authZ → admission → etcd
 📡 kube-apiserver
   ├─ a. AuthN      validate the bearer token (who are you?) → user: easypj-admin
   ├─ b. AuthZ      RBAC: may easypj-admin CREATE pods in ns easypj? → yes
   ├─ c. Admission chain (mutate, then validate):
   │       MutatingAdmissionWebhook   → inject sidecars / defaults
   │       LimitRanger                → apply default requests &amp; limits
   │       PodSecurity                → enforce baseline / restricted
   │       ValidatingAdmissionWebhook → reject if a policy is violated
   ├─ d. WRITE      /registry/pods/easypj/easypj-web  → 🗄️ etcd (quorum write)
   └─ e. RETURN     201 Created → "pod/easypj-web created"
   │
   ▼  STAGE 2 — scheduler: watch → filter → score → bind
 📋 kube-scheduler  (watches pods where .spec.nodeName == "")
   ├─ FILTER (predicates) — drop nodes that CAN'T run it:
   │     NodeReady? · CPU/mem fits requests? · nodeSelector/affinity? · taints tolerated? · hostPort free?
   ├─ SCORE  (priorities) — rank the survivors 0–100:
   │     NodeResourcesFit(LeastAllocated) · NodeResourcesBalancedAllocation · ImageLocality · NodeAffinity
   └─ BIND: POST …/pods/easypj-web/binding → sets .spec.nodeName = "node-1" → 🗄️ etcd
   │
   ▼  STAGE 3 — kubelet → CRI → containerd → runc
 🤖 kubelet(node-1)  (watches pods assigned to it)
   └─ calls the CRI (gRPC), in order:
        RunPodSandbox   → create the pause container + network namespace (CNI assigns the IP)
        PullImage       → fetch easypj:v1 layers from the registry
        CreateContainer → set up namespaces + cgroups + overlay rootfs
        StartContainer  → exec the easypj process
      containerd builds the OCI bundle → runc create → runc start
        runc: clone() with namespace flags · write cgroups · pivot_root · exec
   └─ kubelet reports status → Running → Ready (once probes pass) → 🗄️ etcd
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Map a failure to its stage:</strong> hangs at <code>kubectl</code> → Stage 1 (apiserver unreachable); stuck <code>Pending</code> → Stage 2 (nothing survived filtering — taints / resources / selector); stuck <code>ContainerCreating</code> → Stage 3 <code>RunPodSandbox</code>/<code>PullImage</code> (CNI, or image/registry); <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> → Stage 3 <code>StartContainer</code> (the app itself — read <code>kubectl logs</code>).</div>

                    <h5>⑦ Static pods — the bootstrap paradox solved</h5>
                    <p>The control plane (apiserver, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager) runs <em>as pods</em> — which is a chicken-and-egg problem: how do you run pods before the apiserver that schedules them even exists? The answer is the <strong>static pod</strong> — the kubelet reads manifests straight from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and runs them itself, with no apiserver and no scheduler involved.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   boot order on a control-plane node
   ① kubelet starts             (a systemd service — it does NOT need the API)
   ② reads /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
   ③ runs the apiserver static pod    (CRI → containerd → runc)
   ④ the apiserver is now up
   ⑤ kubelet registers a read-only MIRROR pod for it in the API
        → visible as kube-apiserver-&lt;nodename&gt;   (that's where the suffix comes from)
   ⑥ kubelet runs the remaining manifests: etcd, scheduler, controller-manager
   ⑦ control plane operational → normal, API-driven scheduling begins
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔁 Restarting a static pod (you can't <code>kubectl delete</code> it):</strong> move the manifest out and back —
                    <code>sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/ &amp;&amp; sleep 20 &amp;&amp; sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet sees the file vanish (stops the pod), then reappear (recreates it). Editing the manifest in place also triggers a restart.</div>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 CKA insight:</strong> if you SSH to a control-plane node and <code>kubectl</code> won't respond, the apiserver static pod may be down — there's no API to ask. Check it directly: <code>sudo crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver</code> (is the container running?) and <code>sudo ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (is the manifest present?). A missing or corrupt manifest takes the entire control plane down.</div>

                    <h5>⑧ A few more control-plane internals worth knowing</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Lease fields</strong></td><td>a leader-election Lease carries <code>holderIdentity</code> (who holds it), <code>leaseDurationSeconds</code> (TTL — ~15s for scheduler/controller-manager) and <code>renewTime</code> (the leader rewrites it every couple of seconds). If a renewal is missed past the TTL, a standby claims the Lease — so failover takes up to about one lease duration.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>APF: 429 vs 503</strong></td><td><strong>429 Too Many Requests</strong> = API Priority &amp; Fairness fair-queued you and the queue/wait was exceeded → <em>back off &amp; retry</em>. <strong>503</strong> = a priority level with no queueing, or the server is genuinely overloaded → <em>investigate</em>. <code>exempt</code> flows (e.g. <code>system:masters</code>, health checks) bypass APF so a misconfig can't lock you out of the API.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Admission <code>failurePolicy</code></strong></td><td>if a webhook errors or times out: <code>Fail</code> (default) rejects the request — safe for policy/security; <code>Ignore</code> lets it through — for optional webhooks. <strong>Mutating runs before validating</strong> by design, so validators judge the <em>final</em> object (after sidecars/defaults are injected).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Control plane decides, worker nodes run</strong> — sort every problem into "decision" vs "run" and you've found the half to look in.</li>
                    <li><strong>Only the apiserver talks to etcd</strong> — and everything else talks only to the apiserver (the single choke point).</li>
                    <li><strong>The kubelet is a systemd service, not a pod</strong> — debug it with <code>systemctl</code> / <code>journalctl</code>, never <code>kubectl</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>The control plane runs AS static pods</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — edit the YAML, the kubelet reloads.</li>
                    <li><strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong> are the three plug points (runtime · network · storage).</li>
                    <li><strong>Match symptom → component:</strong> NotReady=kubelet · Pending=scheduler · lost state=etcd · kubectl dead=apiserver.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubelet ≠ pod.</strong> It's systemd — <code>kubectl get pods</code> won't show it; <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> will.</li>
                    <li><strong>You can't <code>kubectl delete</code> a static pod</strong> — the kubelet recreates it. Remove the manifest file from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>apiserver is the single choke point.</strong> If it's down, <code>kubectl</code> is useless — switch to <code>crictl</code> / <code>journalctl</code> on the node.</li>
                    <li><strong>Static pod vs DaemonSet.</strong> Both = one pod per node, but static = kubelet-managed (mirror pod, <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix), DaemonSet = API object.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scheduler vs controller-manager.</strong> Scheduler only <em>places</em> pods; the controller-manager handles replication, nodes, endpoints. Don't conflate.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>apiserver down?</strong> Debug from the node: <code>crictl ps -a</code>, <code>crictl logs &lt;apiserver-id&gt;</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pods Pending?</strong> Suspect the scheduler — <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code> then check the scheduler pod/logs.</li>
                    <li><strong>Editing a control-plane component?</strong> Just edit its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet restarts it for you.</li>
                    <li><strong>Find the HA leader:</strong> <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 3)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect the cluster's anatomy</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -n kube-system            <span class="comment"># control-plane + CNI + CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses             <span class="comment"># cs — control-plane health (older clusters)</span>
kubectl get lease -n kube-system          <span class="comment"># who holds leader election</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/             <span class="comment"># the static-pod manifests</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide                 <span class="comment"># node roles, versions, IPs</span>

<span class="comment"># node-level (when the apiserver is down)</span>
crictl ps -a                              <span class="comment"># containers via the CRI</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
journalctl -u kubelet -f                  <span class="comment"># live kubelet logs</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Map the Control Plane</h5>
                <p>On a control-plane node — predict each output before running it:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>List the static-pod manifests.</li>
                    <li>List the control-plane pods and note the <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> suffix.</li>
                    <li>Identify the leader of the scheduler/controller-manager.</li>
                    <li>Tail the apiserver logs.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/              <span class="comment"># etcd · kube-apiserver · kube-scheduler · kube-controller-manager</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep controlplane   <span class="comment"># all 4 Running, -controlplane suffix (mirror pods)</span>
kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-scheduler kube-controller-manager   <span class="comment"># HOLDER column = leader</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-controlplane | tail -20        <span class="comment"># apiserver logs</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>What you proved:</strong> the control plane is just static pods the kubelet runs; their mirror pods carry the node-name suffix; HA components elect one leader via a Lease; and you can read any control-plane component's logs with <code>kubectl logs</code> (or <code>crictl logs</code> if the apiserver itself is down).</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A — CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 3 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the architecture — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which component is the ONLY one that reads/writes etcd?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>kube-apiserver</strong>. Every other component (scheduler, controller-manager, kubelet) reaches state <em>through</em> the apiserver, never etcd directly.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This star topology is why an etcd backup = a cluster backup, and why securing/operating the apiserver is so critical.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node shows <code>NotReady</code>. Which agent do you check first, and how?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The <strong>kubelet</strong> (it's a systemd service, not a pod), then the runtime and CNI:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node NotReady — first checks (on the node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">systemctl status kubelet        <span class="comment"># is the agent even up?</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -f        <span class="comment"># why did it fail?</span>
systemctl status containerd     <span class="comment"># runtime alive?</span>
ls /etc/cni/net.d/              <span class="comment"># CNI configured?</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>NotReady = the node can't report healthy. Usual causes: kubelet down, swap on, expired cert, missing CNI, cgroup-driver mismatch.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Trace what happens after <code>kubectl apply -f easypj-deploy.yaml</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Each actor reconciles its own slice — no central script:</p>
                        <div class="diagram-container">
                            <div class="diagram-title">what kubectl apply sets in motion</div>
<pre>
 kubectl apply ─▶ 📡 apiserver (authN → authZ → admission) ─▶ 🗄️ etcd   (persist desired state)
                       │
                       ├─ 🔄 controller-manager:  Deployment ─▶ ReplicaSet ─▶ Pods
                       └─ 📋 scheduler:  filter → score → BIND (sets nodeName)
                              ▼
                    🤖 kubelet:  pull image → start container → 🌐 CNI assigns the pod IP
                              ▼
                    🔀 kube-proxy programs Service routing ─────▶ Running ✅
</pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>No central script — each actor reconciles its own slice. Knowing the order lets you map any symptom to a step (§3.4).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The scheduler is down. What symptom appears, and on which objects?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>New pods stay <code>Pending</code></strong> (no node gets assigned). Existing pods keep running. Spot it: <code>kubectl get pods -A | grep Pending</code>, then check the scheduler pod/logs.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The scheduler only sets <code>nodeName</code>; with it down, pods are created but never bound.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a static pod on a node, then delete it. Which files/commands?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The kubelet watches a directory; creating/deleting the <em>file</em> creates/deletes the pod:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">static pod — create &amp; delete (on the node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># create: drop a manifest where the kubelet watches</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-easypj.yaml   <span class="comment"># path = staticPodPath in /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># delete: remove the FILE — `kubectl delete` just gets recreated</span>
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/manifests/static-easypj.yaml</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Static pods are owned by the kubelet, not the API; the API only shows a read-only mirror pod.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the network layers and what routes each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Three layers, each programmed by a different actor (all riding on the node/infra network):</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Addresses</th><th>Who programs it</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>Pod network</strong></td><td>real pod IPs (flat, no NAT)</td><td>the <strong>CNI</strong> plugin</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>Service network</strong></td><td>stable virtual IPs (ClusterIP)</td><td>apiserver assigns the IP · EndpointSlice controller tracks Ready pods · <strong>kube-proxy</strong> programs the routing</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>External</strong></td><td>NodePort · LoadBalancer · Ingress/Gateway</td><td>the cluster edge</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Pods can fail to reach each other (CNI) independently of Services failing (kube-proxy) — keep the layers separate when debugging.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which ports does etcd listen on, and for what?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Two ports, two jobs:</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>2379</code></td><td>client traffic — the apiserver (and <code>etcdctl</code>) connect here</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>2380</code></td><td>peer traffic — etcd member ↔ member for Raft; only matters in multi-member HA</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>etcdctl uses <code>--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379</code>; 2380 only matters in multi-member HA.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why is the apiserver the cluster's single choke point?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Every read/write of state, every component's watch, and every kubectl call flows through it — and it's the only thing that touches etcd. Down apiserver → no scheduling, no reconciliation, no kubectl (though running pods continue).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>That's also why it gets HA replicas behind a load balancer and APF to stay alive under load (UTH).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain a controller's reconcile loop using the ReplicaSet example.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>OBSERVE → COMPARE → ACT, forever — a deleted pod is just the next gap to close:</p>
                        <pre class="diagram-box">
        ┌──────────── reconcile loop (runs forever) ───────────┐
        ▼                                                      │
   OBSERVE actual ──▶ COMPARE to desired ──▶ ACT to close gap ─┘
   (2 pods now)        (spec replicas: 3)     (create 1 pod)
   delete a pod → next pass sees 2 ≠ 3 again → recreates it
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Level-triggered: it reacts to current state, not events, so it self-heals even if an event is missed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a "mirror pod" and why does its name end in <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code>?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>When the kubelet runs a static pod, it creates a read-only <strong>mirror pod</strong> in the API so you can <code>kubectl get</code> it. The kubelet appends its node name (e.g. <code>etcd-controlplane</code>) to show which node owns it — and to keep names unique across nodes.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>You can view but not control a mirror pod via the API; the real lifecycle lives with the kubelet and the manifest file.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch3-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 3 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — a broken state or a "trace it" question, with the real answer.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You SSH to the control plane node and find that <code>kubectl get nodes</code> returns <em>"The connection to the server was refused"</em>. The kubelet is running. What is the most likely problem and how do you diagnose it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The <strong>kube-apiserver</strong> is down. Since it's a static pod, diagnose as follows:</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if the api server container is running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if the API server container is running</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver

<span class="comment"># 2. If not running, check the static pod manifest</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet logs for manifest errors</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i "error\|apiserver" | tail -20

<span class="comment"># 4. Common fixes:</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix YAML syntax errors in the manifest</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix wrong certificate paths (--tls-cert-file, --tls-private-key-file)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix wrong etcd endpoints (--etcd-servers)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Fix port conflicts (--secure-port)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a <strong>classic CKA troubleshooting scenario</strong>. The kube-apiserver runs as a static pod managed by the kubelet. If the manifest file in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> has errors, the kubelet cannot start the pod. Key diagnostic path: <code>crictl ps</code> → <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → check manifest → fix → kubelet automatically restarts the static pod. No <code>kubectl apply</code> needed — static pods are managed by the kubelet directly from filesystem watching.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>static pods</strong>? Where are they defined, which component manages them, and how are they different from regular pods created via the API server?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Static pods</strong> are pods managed directly by the <strong>kubelet</strong> on a specific node, without going through the API server.</p>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th>Static Pods</th>
                                            <th>Regular Pods</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Managed by</td>
                                            <td>kubelet (filesystem watcher)</td>
                                            <td>API server → scheduler → kubelet</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Defined in</td>
                                            <td><code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></td>
                                            <td>etcd (via kubectl/API)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Visible in API</td>
                                            <td>Yes, as a <em>mirror pod</em> (read-only)</td>
                                            <td>Yes, fully managed</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Can be deleted via kubectl</td>
                                            <td>No — delete the file to remove</td>
                                            <td>Yes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods are how the control plane bootstraps itself — <code>kube-apiserver</code>, <code>etcd</code>, <code>kube-scheduler</code>, and <code>kube-controller-manager</code> all run as static pods. The kubelet watches the <code>staticPodPath</code> (configured in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>) and automatically creates/restarts pods from YAML files found there. On the CKA, you may be asked to create a static pod: simply place a valid pod manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and the kubelet will start it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the <strong>kube-scheduler's</strong> decision process. When a new pod is created without a <code>nodeName</code>, what steps does the scheduler take to place it on a node?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The scheduler follows a <strong>two-phase process</strong>:</p>
                                <p><strong>Phase 1: Filtering</strong> — Eliminates nodes that can't run the pod:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Insufficient CPU/memory resources</li>
                                    <li>Taints that the pod doesn't tolerate</li>
                                    <li>Node affinity rules not satisfied</li>
                                    <li>PodAntiAffinity constraints</li>
                                    <li>Unschedulable nodes (<code>cordon</code>ed)</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p><strong>Phase 2: Scoring</strong> — Ranks remaining nodes by preference:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Resource balance (spread workloads evenly)</li>
                                    <li>Node affinity preferences (soft rules)</li>
                                    <li>Pod topology spread constraints</li>
                                    <li>Image locality (node already has the image cached)</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p>The highest-scoring node wins. The scheduler then sets <code>spec.nodeName</code> via the API server.</p>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA tests scheduling concepts heavily (15% of the exam). Key scenarios: (1) Pod stuck in <code>Pending</code> — all nodes filtered out (check taints, resources, node selectors). (2) Pod scheduled to wrong node — scoring preferences not configured correctly. (3) Manually bypass the scheduler by setting <code>spec.nodeName</code> directly in the pod spec. If a pod is Pending, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events will show <em>"0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had taint..."</em> — this tells you exactly which filter failed.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The <code>kube-controller-manager</code> runs many controllers. Name at least 5 built-in controllers and describe what each one is responsible for.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Controller</th>
                                            <th>Responsibility</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Node Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Monitors node heartbeats (every 10s), marks NotReady after 40s, evicts pods after 5min</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>ReplicaSet Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Ensures the correct number of pod replicas are running at all times</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Deployment Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Manages ReplicaSets for rolling updates, rollbacks, and scaling</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Endpoint Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Populates Endpoints objects (maps Services to pod IPs)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>ServiceAccount Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Creates default ServiceAccount + token for every new namespace</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Job Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Tracks Job completions and creates new pods until the desired completion count is reached</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Namespace Controller</strong></td>
                                            <td>Cleans up resources when a namespace is deleted (cascading deletion)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>All these controllers run as goroutines inside a single <code>kube-controller-manager</code> binary/pod. Each controller follows the same <strong>Observe → Diff → Act</strong> pattern. On the CKA exam, understanding controllers helps diagnose issues: if replicas aren't being created, the controller-manager might be down. If nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> for too long without pod eviction, check the node controller's <code>--pod-eviction-timeout</code> flag.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How does the <code>kubelet</code> know which static pod manifests to watch? What configuration file controls the <code>staticPodPath</code>, and how would you find it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find kubelet's config file</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Find kubelet's config file</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -- --config
<span class="comment"># Typically: --config=/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the staticPodPath in kubelet config</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># Output: staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># List static pod manifests</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml  kube-scheduler.yaml</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA exam, you may need to create a static pod on a specific node. The steps are: (1) SSH to the target node, (2) Find the <code>staticPodPath</code> from the kubelet config, (3) Place your pod YAML file in that directory, (4) The kubelet automatically detects and creates the pod. To find the kubelet config: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> shows the systemd unit file, which contains the <code>--config</code> flag pointing to the config file. Default path is almost always <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the role of <strong>CoreDNS</strong> in a Kubernetes cluster? If DNS resolution fails inside a pod, what troubleshooting steps would you take?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>CoreDNS</strong> provides cluster-internal DNS resolution. It runs as a Deployment in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace and resolves:</p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li><code>service-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code> → ClusterIP</li>
                                    <li><code>pod-ip-dashed.namespace.pod.cluster.local</code> → Pod IP</li>
                                </ul>
                                <p><strong>Troubleshooting DNS failure:</strong></p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if coredns pods are running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if CoreDNS pods are running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 2. Check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># 3. Verify the kube-dns Service exists</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># 4. Test DNS from inside a pod</span>
kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup kubernetes.default

<span class="comment"># 5. Check pod's /etc/resolv.conf</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># Should point to kube-dns ClusterIP (usually 10.96.0.10)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>DNS is critical infrastructure in Kubernetes. Every pod gets a <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> pointing to the CoreDNS service's ClusterIP. If DNS fails, almost everything breaks — service discovery, external API calls, etc. Common causes: (1) CoreDNS pods not running, (2) kube-dns Service ClusterIP changed, (3) Network policy blocking DNS traffic on port 53, (4) CoreDNS ConfigMap misconfigured, (5) kube-proxy not programming iptables rules for the kube-dns service. This is a high-priority CKA troubleshooting topic.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the difference between the <code>kube-apiserver</code>'s <strong>authentication</strong>, <strong>authorization</strong>, and <strong>admission control</strong> stages. What happens at each stage when a request arrives?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>Every API request passes through three stages in order:</p>
                                <ol>
                                    <li><strong>Authentication</strong> — <em>"Who are you?"</em> — Verifies identity via client certificates, bearer tokens, or service account tokens. If authentication fails → <code>401 Unauthorized</code></li>
                                    <li><strong>Authorization</strong> — <em>"Are you allowed to do this?"</em> — Checks RBAC rules (Role/ClusterRole + RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding). If denied → <code>403 Forbidden</code></li>
                                    <li><strong>Admission Control</strong> — <em>"Should we modify or reject this?"</em> — Webhooks and built-in plugins that can mutate (change defaults) or validate (reject invalid) requests. Examples: <code>LimitRanger</code> (set default resource limits), <code>NamespaceLifecycle</code> (reject requests to terminating namespaces)</li>
                                </ol>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash">Request → [Authentication] → [Authorization] → [Admission] → etcd
              401?                  403?              Reject/Mutate</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this pipeline is critical for CKA RBAC and security questions. When you get a "forbidden" error, it's authorization (RBAC). When you get "unauthorized", it's authentication (bad/expired cert or token). Admission controllers can silently modify your objects — for example, <code>DefaultStorageClass</code> adds a storage class to PVCs that don't specify one. The CKA tests RBAC extensively (creating Roles, ClusterRoles, Bindings). Remember: Roles are namespace-scoped, ClusterRoles are cluster-wide.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A Kubernetes node's kubelet is configured with <code>--pod-cidr=10.244.1.0/24</code>. What does this mean, and how does the CNI plugin use this information to assign IP addresses to pods?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The <code>--pod-cidr</code> defines the <strong>subnet of IP addresses</strong> allocated to pods on that specific node. Each node gets a unique pod CIDR from the cluster's overall pod network CIDR (e.g., <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>).</p>
                                <p><strong>How it works:</strong></p>
                                <ul>
                                    <li>Controller-manager assigns each node a unique <code>/24</code> subnet from the cluster CIDR</li>
                                    <li>The CNI plugin (Flannel, Calico, Cilium) reads the node's pod CIDR</li>
                                    <li>When a pod is created, the CNI assigns an IP from that node's subnet</li>
                                    <li>Node 1: <code>10.244.0.0/24</code> → pods get <code>10.244.0.2</code>, <code>10.244.0.3</code>, etc.</li>
                                    <li>Node 2: <code>10.244.1.0/24</code> → pods get <code>10.244.1.2</code>, <code>10.244.1.3</code>, etc.</li>
                                </ul>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding pod networking is essential for the CKA's "Services & Networking" domain (20%). The pod CIDR is configured during cluster initialization (<code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code>). Each node gets a unique subnet. The CNI plugin handles the actual IP assignment and inter-node routing. If pods can't communicate across nodes, check: (1) CNI plugin is installed, (2) pod CIDRs don't overlap with node IPs, (3) firewall rules allow inter-node pod traffic.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code> and see that <code>scheduler</code> shows <code>Unhealthy</code>. What could cause this, and how would you fix it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if the scheduler pod is running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check if the scheduler pod is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep scheduler

<span class="comment"># 2. If not running, check the static pod manifest</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet logs for scheduler errors</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet | grep scheduler

<span class="comment"># 4. Check scheduler logs directly</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-controlplane

<span class="comment"># 5. Common issues:</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Wrong kubeconfig path (--kubeconfig flag)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Certificate expired or wrong path</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Port conflict (--secure-port)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - YAML syntax error in manifest file</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><strong>Note:</strong> <code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code> (or <code>cs</code>) is <strong>deprecated</strong> since v1.19, but may still appear on the CKA. It checks health endpoints of control plane components. If the scheduler is unhealthy: (1) The scheduler static pod may have crashed — check its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, (2) The scheduler's health endpoint may be unreachable. Symptoms of a dead scheduler: new pods stay in <code>Pending</code> state because nothing assigns them to nodes. Existing pods continue running — only new scheduling decisions are blocked.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Draw or describe the complete communication flow between all Kubernetes components when a user runs <code>kubectl delete pod easypj-web</code>. Which components are involved and what happens at each step?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Pod deletion flow:</strong></p>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl delete pod easypj-web
        │
        ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│  kube-apiserver  │ ← Receives DELETE request
│  1. Authenticate │
│  2. Authorize    │
│  3. Set deletion │
│     timestamp    │
└────────┬────────┘
         │ Updates etcd: pod.deletionTimestamp = now
         │ pod.deletionGracePeriodSeconds = 30 (default)
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│    kubelet       │ ← Watches API, sees deletionTimestamp
│  1. Send SIGTERM │
│  2. Wait 30s     │    to containers
│  3. Send SIGKILL │    (if still running)
│  4. Report back  │
└────────┬────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│  kube-apiserver  │ ← kubelet confirms termination
│  Remove from     │
│  etcd            │
└─────────────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌─────────────────┐
│ Endpoint ctrl    │ ← Removes pod IP from Service endpoints
└─────────────────┘</code></pre>
</div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Key points for the CKA: (1) Deletion is <strong>graceful</strong> by default — containers get SIGTERM and 30 seconds to shut down. (2) The <code>--grace-period=0 --force</code> flags skip the grace period (useful for stuck pods). (3) The Endpoint controller removes the pod from Service endpoints <em>concurrently</em> with the grace period, so in-flight requests may still reach a terminating pod. (4) <code>preStop</code> hooks run before SIGTERM — use them for graceful shutdown logic. Understanding pod lifecycle is critical for troubleshooting stuck deletions and graceful application shutdown.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 3 Visual Summary — the whole cluster, annotated</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ───────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🧠 CONTROL PLANE (decides)        every request &amp; watch flows through apiserver │
   │   📡 apiserver :6443  ── the ONLY door, the ONLY etcd client                    │
   │        │  authN → authZ(RBAC) → admission                                       │
   │        ├── 🗄️ etcd :2379/2380   ALL state (Raft quorum)                          │
   │        ├── 📋 scheduler          filter → score → BIND (sets nodeName)           │
   │        └── 🔄 controller-mgr     reconcile loops (node·RS·endpoint·SA)           │
   │  ───────────────────────────── apiserver ⇅ nodes (watch) ──────────────────────│
   │ 💪 WORKER NODE                                                                   │
   │   🤖 kubelet (systemd, not a pod) · 🌐 kube-proxy (Service routing) · 🐋 runtime  │
   │   🌐 CNI gives each 📦 pod a flat IP   ·   💾 CSI attaches volumes                │
   │   ┌────────────┐ ┌────────────┐                                                 │
   │   │📦 easypj-web│ │🐘 postgres  │   the control plane itself = static pods in     │
   │   │  :8000      │ │  :5432      │   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ (kubelet-run)      │
   │   └────────────┘ └────────────┘                                                 │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   Decide (control plane) vs Run (nodes) · only apiserver→etcd · symptom→component = the exam.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> A cluster splits cleanly into two halves. The <strong>control plane decides</strong>; the <strong>worker nodes run</strong>. The <strong>apiserver</strong> (:6443) is the only way in and the <em>only</em> component that talks to <strong>etcd</strong> — every request first passes authentication → authorization (RBAC) → admission. etcd (:2379) holds <em>all</em> cluster state, kept consistent by Raft quorum.</p>
                    <p>Behind the apiserver, the <strong>scheduler</strong> filters and scores nodes then <em>binds</em> each pod (sets <code>nodeName</code>), and the <strong>controller-manager</strong> runs the reconcile loops (node, ReplicaSet, endpoint, service-account…). On each worker node, the <strong>kubelet</strong> runs the containers (it's a systemd service, not a pod), <strong>kube-proxy</strong> programs Service routing, the <strong>runtime</strong> executes containers, <strong>CNI</strong> gives each pod a flat IP, and <strong>CSI</strong> attaches storage. The control plane itself runs as <em>static pods</em> the kubelet starts from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> hold the "decide vs run" split and "only the apiserver touches etcd" in your head, and troubleshooting becomes a lookup — match the <em>symptom</em> to the responsible <em>component</em>. That mapping is the heart of the exam's Architecture and Troubleshooting domains.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <!-- ::CH3-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 3 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 4 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch4">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 4 · Core Kubernetes Objects — The Admin's Toolkit</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">These six objects are the <strong>nouns</strong> of every exam task and every easypj manifest: <strong>Pod, ReplicaSet, Deployment, Service, Namespace</strong>, and the <strong>labels</strong> that wire them together. Chapter 3 showed you the machines; this chapter shows you the things you actually create on them. Master how they nest and reference each other and you can read any manifest at a glance.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 Core Objects</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Workloads 15% · Services 20%</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 easypj / jobpost</span>
            </div>
            </div><!-- /chapter-intro -->

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Pods</strong> — the smallest unit; single vs multi-container (sidecar)</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>ReplicaSets</strong> — self-healing by keeping a desired pod count</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Deployments</strong> — rolling updates, rollback, the ownership chain</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Services</strong> — stable networking for ephemeral pods (4 types)</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Namespaces</strong> — what they isolate, and what they don't</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Labels · Selectors · Annotations</strong> — the glue that connects everything</li>
                    <li>✅ The full <strong>object relationship map</strong> — the one picture to memorize</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🧩 Object mastery = exam speed</h5>
                <p>Almost every CKA task is "create object X, wire it to Y, fix why Z isn't working." You'll create Deployments, expose them with Services, and chase down empty endpoints. Learn each object <strong>imperatively</strong> (fast <code>kubectl create … $do</code>) <em>and</em> <strong>declaratively</strong> (the YAML), and watch the <strong>labels</strong> — they're where most wiring bugs hide.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <h4>🧭 Section Map — the Chapter 4 Learning Arc</h4>
            <p>We climb the workload ladder (Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment), add stable networking (Service) and isolation (Namespace), learn the glue (labels), then assemble everything into one relationship map.</p>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">Chapter 4 Roadmap — the objects, and how they connect</div>
<pre>
   4.1 📦 POD ──wrapped by──▶ 4.2 🎛️ REPLICASET ──wrapped by──▶ 4.3 🚀 DEPLOYMENT
   smallest unit             keeps N alive (self-heal)         rolling updates + rollback
        │                                                              │
        ▼                                                              ▼
   4.4 🔌 SERVICE ──────▶ 4.5 🏷️ NAMESPACE ──────▶ 4.6 🔖 LABELS / SELECTORS
   stable IP + DNS         virtual cluster (isolation)         the glue that wires it all
        │
        ▼
   4.7 🗺️ THE RELATIONSHIP MAP — Deployment→RS→Pods · Service finds Pods by label ·
        ConfigMap/Secret feed env · Ingress→Service · PVC→PV→StorageClass · RBAC governs
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ✅ READY — read any easypj manifest and say exactly what each object does &amp; how it links.
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Object</th><th>One-line takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-1"><strong>4.1</strong> Pods</a></td><td>📦 Pod</td><td>1+ containers sharing one IP &amp; storage; ephemeral — replaced, never repaired.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-2"><strong>4.2</strong> ReplicaSets</a></td><td>🎛️ ReplicaSet</td><td>Keeps a desired count alive via a label selector (self-healing).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-3"><strong>4.3</strong> Deployments</a></td><td>🚀 Deployment</td><td>Manages ReplicaSets; adds rolling updates &amp; rollback.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-4"><strong>4.4</strong> Services</a></td><td>🔌 Service</td><td>Stable VIP + DNS load-balancing to pods by selector.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-5"><strong>4.5</strong> Namespaces</a></td><td>🏷️ Namespace</td><td>Virtual partitions of one cluster; don't isolate nodes/PVs.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-6"><strong>4.6</strong> Labels</a></td><td>🔖 Labels</td><td>Key/value tags that select &amp; wire objects; annotations don't select.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-7"><strong>4.7</strong> Relationship map</a></td><td>🗺️ All</td><td>How every object connects — the picture to memorize.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-8"><strong>4.8</strong> Pod lifecycle</a></td><td>⏱️ Pod states</td><td>Phases, container states, restartPolicy, conditions, termination, probes.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s4-9"><strong>4.9</strong> Rollouts &amp; scope</a></td><td>🎚️ Details</td><td>maxSurge/maxUnavailable 25%, port mapping, label grammar, namespaced vs cluster.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s4-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.1 Pods — The Smallest Unit</h3>
                <p>A Pod is the <strong>smallest deployable unit</strong> — one or more containers that share a network namespace (one IP, reachable over <code>localhost</code>) and storage volumes. You rarely <em>create</em> pods directly (you use Deployments), but you <em>debug</em> them constantly.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod anatomy — single vs multi-container (sidecar)</div>
<pre>
   SINGLE-CONTAINER POD (most common)        MULTI-CONTAINER POD (sidecar pattern)
   ┌──────────────────────────┐             ┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ 📦 POD   IP 10.244.1.5     │             │ 📦 POD   IP 10.244.1.8  (shared net)   │
   │  ┌────────────────────┐   │             │  ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐    │
   │  │ 🐍 easypj-web        │   │             │  │ 🐍 easypj      │ │ 🌐 nginx       │    │
   │  │ Django gunicorn:8000│   │             │  │ Django :8000 │ │ static :80   │    │
   │  └────────────────────┘   │             │  └──────┬───────┘ └──────┬───────┘    │
   │                            │             │         │   localhost     │            │
   │                            │             │         └────────┬────────┘            │
   │                            │             │              ┌───▼────┐ shared 💾       │
   │                            │             │              │ volume │ (emptyDir)      │
   │                            │             │              └────────┘                │
   └──────────────────────────┘             └──────────────────────────────────────┘
   one container = the norm                   localhost:8000 = Django · localhost:80 = nginx
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">💡</div><h5>Key pod facts</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Every container in a pod shares the <strong>same IP</strong></li>
                            <li>They talk to each other over <code>localhost</code></li>
                            <li>Pods are <strong>ephemeral</strong> — replaced, never repaired</li>
                            <li>Never rely on a pod's name or IP — both change</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5>Essential pod commands</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- sh</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-pod.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-pod
  labels:
    app: easypj
    tier: web                <span class="comment"># labels = how other objects find this pod</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000
    resources:
      requests:
        memory: "128Mi"        <span class="comment"># scheduling floor (guaranteed)</span>
        cpu: "250m"
      limits:
        memory: "256Mi"        <span class="comment"># hard ceiling (OOMKilled if exceeded)</span>
        cpu: "500m"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why "ephemeral" is the whole point</h5>
                    <p>A bare Pod is a <em>pet</em>: delete it and it's gone for good — nothing recreates it, and its IP vanishes. That's exactly why you wrap pods in controllers (next) and why apps reach each other through <strong>Services</strong> (§4.4), never by pod IP. Treat pods as disposable cattle and Kubernetes' design suddenly makes sense.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Init Containers, Sidecars &amp; the Pause Container</h4>
                <p>A pod can hold more than one container, and they don't all behave the same way. There are three roles worth knowing — and one hidden container you never write but always have:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>When it runs</th><th>easypj example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>init container</strong></td><td>BEFORE app containers, <em>to completion</em>, in order — the next won't start until it exits 0</td><td>wait for <code>easypj-db</code> to accept connections, or run <code>manage.py migrate</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>app container</strong></td><td>The long-running main process(es)</td><td><code>gunicorn easypj.wsgi</code> on :8000</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>sidecar</strong></td><td>ALONGSIDE the app for its whole life (a special init container with <code>restartPolicy: Always</code>)</td><td>an nginx static-file server, or a log shipper</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>🔒 pause</strong></td><td>The hidden "sandbox" — created first, <em>holds</em> the pod's network + IPC namespaces</td><td>auto-created; app/sidecar containers join its namespaces</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod startup order — init runs to completion, then the rest share the sandbox</div>
<pre>
   pod created
      │
      ▼  🔒 pause container starts → reserves the pod IP + network namespace
      ▼  ⏳ initContainer "wait-for-db"   runs → exits 0
      ▼  ⏳ initContainer "migrate"       runs → exits 0   (ordered, must succeed)
      ▼  ▶️ app: 🐍 easypj  +  sidecar: 🌐 nginx   start together, share the sandbox
   Running ✅
   ── if an init container fails → pod stuck in Init:0/2 → it never reaches Running
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why init containers are the clean answer to "wait for X"</h5>
                    <p>Instead of baking retry/wait logic into easypj's startup, an init container does the waiting (e.g. until <code>easypj-db:5432</code> answers) and the app container starts <em>only</em> once the dependency is ready. And the <strong>pause container</strong> is why all containers in a pod share one IP: they all join <em>its</em> network namespace — so a crashing app container can restart without the pod losing its IP. You'll use init containers for real in Chapter 16 (Jobs/migrations).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.2 ReplicaSets — Maintaining the Count</h3>
                <p>A ReplicaSet keeps a <strong>desired number of identical pods</strong> running, using a label <strong>selector</strong> to know which pods are "its." If one dies, it creates a replacement — that's self-healing. You rarely create a ReplicaSet directly; a Deployment manages it for you.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ReplicaSet self-healing — always 3 replicas</div>
<pre>
   ReplicaSet easypj-web-rs   (replicas: 3, selector: app=easypj,tier=web)

   HEALTHY                        POD 2 CRASHES                  SELF-HEALED
   ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐          ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐          ┌────┐ ┌────┐ ┌────┐
   │📦 1│ │📦 2│ │📦 3│          │📦 1│ │💀 2│ │📦 3│          │📦 1│ │🆕 4│ │📦 3│
   └────┘ └────┘ └────┘          └────┘ └────┘ └────┘          └────┘ └────┘ └────┘
   3/3 Running ✅                 2/3 ⚠️                          3/3 Running ✅
                                     │ RS: "want 3, see 2"          ▲ new pod created
                                     └──────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 ReplicaSet vs Deployment</h5>
                    <p>A ReplicaSet only ensures <em>count</em>. A <strong>Deployment</strong> adds rolling updates, rollback history, and version tracking on top — and creates/owns the ReplicaSet for you. In practice you always use Deployments; ReplicaSets are the layer doing the actual self-healing underneath.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.3 Deployments — Managing the Application Lifecycle</h3>
                <p>The Deployment is the <strong>standard way to run a stateless app</strong>. You declare desired state (image, replicas, resources); the controller makes it real and adds rolling updates, rollbacks, and revision history — by managing a chain of ReplicaSets.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Deployment ownership chain (old ReplicaSets kept for rollback)</div>
<pre>
   🚀 Deployment: easypj-web
   ├── manages ─▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  easypj-web-7f8b4  (current, v3)
   │                ├── owns ─▶ 📦 easypj-web-7f8b4-abc1
   │                ├── owns ─▶ 📦 easypj-web-7f8b4-abc2
   │                └── owns ─▶ 📦 easypj-web-7f8b4-abc3
   ├── keeps ──▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  easypj-web-6d5a2  (old v2, scaled to 0)
   └── keeps ──▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet  easypj-web-5c3f1  (old v1, scaled to 0)
   💡 old ReplicaSets at 0 replicas = your instant rollback history.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="analogy-box">
                    <h5>🪆 In plain English — the wrapping (nesting) model</h5>
                    <p>Pod, ReplicaSet, and Deployment aren't competing things — each <strong>wraps</strong> the one below and adds a single capability. Think Russian dolls, smallest inside:</p>
<pre class="diagram-box">
   🚀 Deployment        "give me rolling updates + rollback + history"
     └─ 🎛️ ReplicaSet    "keep exactly N copies alive"   (self-healing)
          └─ 📦 Pod      "schedule these container(s) together, one IP"
               └─ 🐋 Container   "run my easypj process"
</pre>
                    <p class="mb-0">You create the <strong>outermost</strong> doll (the Deployment) and it builds the rest. That's why you almost never make a bare Pod or a lone ReplicaSet — you'd give up the capabilities the outer wrappers add. Need a one-off task instead of a long-running app? Swap the Deployment for a <strong>Job</strong> (Ch 16): same Pod inside, different outer doll.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-deployment.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                  <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the template labels below</span>
      tier: web
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                   <span class="comment"># at most 1 EXTRA pod while rolling</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0             <span class="comment"># zero-downtime: never dip below desired</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                 <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the selector above</span>
        tier: web
        version: v3
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: easypj:v3
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: "128Mi"         <span class="comment"># scheduling floor (guaranteed)</span>
            cpu: "250m"
          limits:
            memory: "256Mi"         <span class="comment"># hard ceiling (OOMKilled if exceeded)</span>
            cpu: "500m"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod Lifecycle — the Phases &amp; Statuses You'll See</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod phases + the STATUS column you read in kubectl get pods</div>
<pre>
   ┌─────────┐    ┌─────────┐    ┌───────────┐
   │ Pending │──▶ │ Running │──▶ │ Succeeded │ (exit 0 — Jobs)
   └────┬────┘    └────┬────┘    └───────────┘
        │              └──▶ ┌────────┐
        └──────────────────▶│ Failed │ (exit ≠ 0)
                            └────────┘
   STATUS column (what kubectl actually shows):
   ContainerCreating  image pulling / volumes mounting
   Running            at least one container up
   Completed          all containers exited 0
   CrashLoopBackOff   container keeps crashing (growing backoff)
   ImagePullBackOff   can't pull the image
   Pending            not yet scheduled to a node
   Terminating        being gracefully deleted
   Init:N/M           init containers still running
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Reading those statuses for the exam</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Pending</strong> → scheduler can't place it (no fit), ResourceQuota, or unbound PVC</li>
                        <li><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> → wrong image/tag, private registry without <code>imagePullSecrets</code></li>
                        <li><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> → <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> for the crash reason</li>
                        <li><strong>Init:0/1</strong> → an init container is stuck — <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;init&gt;</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Deployment Operations (CKA Speed)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — the rollout toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3   <span class="comment"># fast create</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5                      <span class="comment"># scale</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web easypj=easypj:v4              <span class="comment"># rolling update</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web                         <span class="comment"># watch it</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web                        <span class="comment"># revisions</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web                           <span class="comment"># roll back 1</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web --to-revision=2           <span class="comment"># to a specific rev</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 The strategy knobs</h5>
                    <p><code>maxSurge: 1</code> allows one extra pod during the update; <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> means never drop below the desired count → <strong>zero downtime</strong>. Deep dive (incl. canary/blue-green) in Chapter 13.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.4 Services — Stable Network Endpoints</h3>
                <p>Pods get random IPs that change on every restart — so you can never point a client at a pod IP. A <strong>Service</strong> gives a <strong>stable virtual IP + DNS name</strong> and load-balances across the pods its selector matches (its "endpoints").</p>

                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Reachable from</th><th>Use case</th><th>Port</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>ClusterIP</code></td><td>Inside the cluster only</td><td>Internal (the default)</td><td>any</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>NodePort</code></td><td>Outside, via <code>&lt;nodeIP&gt;:port</code></td><td>Dev / testing</td><td>30000–32767</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>LoadBalancer</code></td><td>Outside, via a cloud LB</td><td>Production (cloud)</td><td>any</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ExternalName</code></td><td>DNS alias (CNAME)</td><td>Point at an external host</td><td>n/a</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service types — who can reach each</div>
<pre>
   🔒 ClusterIP (default)        🚪 NodePort                  ☁️ LoadBalancer
   ┌──────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────┐        ┌──────────────────┐
   │ in-cluster only   │         │ &lt;nodeIP&gt;:30080    │        │ cloud LB          │
   │ VIP 10.96.0.10    │         │      │            │        │ 203.0.113.50      │
   │      │            │         │      ▼            │        │      │            │
   │      ▼            │         │ VIP 10.96.0.11    │        │      ▼            │
   │ 📦 easypj  📦 easypj│         │      │            │        │ VIP 10.96.0.12    │
   └──────────────────┘         │      ▼            │        │      │            │
                                │ 📦 easypj 📦 easypj│        │      ▼            │
                                └──────────────────┘        │ 📦 easypj 📦 easypj│
                                                             └──────────────────┘
   all three pick a READY pod by label selector (app=easypj) and load-balance to it
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-service.yaml — ClusterIP + NodePort</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># internal Service (default) — stable VIP inside the cluster</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-svc
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: easypj           <span class="comment"># matches the pod labels</span>
    tier: web
  ports:
  - port: 80              <span class="comment"># the Service port (what clients call)</span>
    targetPort: 8000      <span class="comment"># the container port (gunicorn listens here)</span>
---
<span class="comment"># external dev access — same pods, reachable from outside the cluster</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-nodeport
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8000
    nodePort: 30080       <span class="comment"># reachable at &lt;nodeIP&gt;:30080 (range 30000–32767)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — expose fast (CKA)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000          <span class="comment"># ClusterIP</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 \
  --type=NodePort --name=easypj-nodeport
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc      <span class="comment"># the pod IPs this Service routes to (empty = bug!)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Service DNS — How Pods Find Each Other by Name</h4>
                <p>Every Service gets an automatic DNS name from CoreDNS, so easypj-web can reach easypj-db without ever knowing its IP:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service DNS resolution &amp; cross-namespace access</div>
<pre>
   from a pod in the easypj namespace, all of these reach the DB Service:
     easypj-db                              ← same namespace (short)
     easypj-db.easypj                       ← name.namespace
     easypj-db.easypj.svc.cluster.local     ← the full FQDN
   pattern:  &lt;service&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local

   cross-namespace:  easypj-web (ns: easypj)  ──curl postgres.data.svc.cluster.local──▶
                     🐘 postgres Service (ns: data)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Debugging DNS &amp; endpoints</h5>
                    <p>Service unreachable by name? Check CoreDNS (<code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code>) and test from a pod: <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- nslookup easypj-db</code>. Unreachable by VIP? Check <code>kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc</code> — empty endpoints = the selector doesn't match any ready pod (the #1 Service bug; full depth in Part 5).</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Two More Service Behaviours Worth Knowing</h4>
                <p>Beyond the four types, two knobs come up constantly — especially the moment you hit stateful apps like easypj's database.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>Headless Service (<code>clusterIP: None</code>)</h5>
                        <p>No VIP, no load-balancing. DNS returns the <strong>individual pod IPs</strong> directly. This is what <strong>StatefulSets</strong> use so clients can address a <em>specific</em> replica by a stable name like <code>postgres-0.easypj-db…</code> (Ch 16).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📌</div><h5>sessionAffinity: ClientIP</h5>
                        <p>Pins a client to the <em>same</em> backend pod for a timeout window instead of load-balancing every request — useful for sticky sessions. Default is <code>None</code> (spread freely).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 How kube-proxy actually routes (iptables vs IPVS)</h5>
                    <p>A Service VIP isn't a real interface — it's a <strong>rule</strong>. kube-proxy programs the node so traffic to the VIP is DNAT'd to a ready pod IP:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>iptables</strong> (default): a chain of rules with random probability per endpoint — simple, but rule lookup is O(n) at thousands of services.</li>
                        <li><strong>IPVS</strong>: a kernel load-balancer with hash tables — O(1), better at large scale, more LB algorithms (round-robin, least-conn).</li>
                        <li><strong>nftables</strong>: the newer successor to iptables mode.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="mb-0">Check the mode: <code>kubectl -n kube-system logs ds/kube-proxy | grep -i proxier</code>. This is why "Services are slow at scale" → consider IPVS (Ch 18/34).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.5 Namespaces — Virtual Clusters</h3>
                <p>Namespaces are <strong>logical partitions</strong> of one cluster — used to isolate teams and environments. Each can carry its own ResourceQuota, RBAC, and NetworkPolicy, so <code>easypj-dev</code> and <code>easypj-prod</code> live side-by-side without colliding.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Namespace isolation — the easypj example</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────────────────────────── ☸️ CLUSTER ────────────────────────────────┐
   │  ┌─────────────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────┐                     │
   │  │ 🟢 easypj-dev         │        │ 🔴 easypj-prod        │                    │
   │  │  1 replica · DEBUG=on │        │  3 replicas · DEBUG=off│                   │
   │  │  low limits           │        │  high limits           │                   │
   │  └─────────────────────┘        └─────────────────────┘                     │
   │  ┌─────────────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────┐                     │
   │  │ 🔧 kube-system        │        │ 📊 monitoring         │                    │
   │  │  CoreDNS · kube-proxy │        │  Prometheus · Grafana  │                   │
   │  └─────────────────────┘        └─────────────────────┘                     │
   │  each namespace can have its OWN: ResourceQuota · RBAC Roles · NetworkPolicy │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>The Four Built-in Namespaces</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>default</h5><p>Where objects land with no namespace set. Avoid it in production.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5>kube-system</h5><p>Control-plane add-ons: CoreDNS, kube-proxy, metrics-server. Don't deploy apps here.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌍</div><h5>kube-public</h5><p>Readable by all (even unauthenticated). Holds the cluster-info ConfigMap.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">💓</div><h5>kube-node-lease</h5><p>Node heartbeat Lease objects — used for fast node-health detection.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — namespace commands</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl get namespaces
kubectl get pods -n easypj-dev
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># make it the default</span>
kubectl get pods -A                                            <span class="comment"># across ALL namespaces</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ The #1 exam trap: wrong namespace = zero marks</h5>
                    <p>Every CKA task names a namespace at the top. Create the resource in the wrong one and you score nothing even if it's perfect. Always pass <code>-n &lt;ns&gt;</code> or switch context first. Also know what namespaces <strong>don't</strong> isolate: <strong>nodes</strong> and <strong>cluster-scoped objects</strong> (PersistentVolumes, Nodes, StorageClasses, ClusterRoles) live outside any namespace.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>ResourceQuota — Capping a Namespace</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-quota.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj-prod
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: 8Gi
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 16Gi
    pods: "20"
    services: "10"
    persistentvolumeclaims: "5"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 ResourceQuota vs LimitRange</h5>
                    <p><strong>ResourceQuota</strong> caps the <em>namespace total</em> (all pods combined). <strong>LimitRange</strong> sets per-pod/container defaults &amp; maxes. Use both: Quota bounds the team, LimitRange stops any single pod from being greedy (full depth in Ch 15/41).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.6 Labels, Selectors &amp; Annotations</h3>
                <p>Labels are the <strong>glue of Kubernetes</strong> — key/value tags that <em>select</em> objects. They wire Services→Pods, Deployments→Pods, NetworkPolicies and RBAC. Annotations look similar but are non-identifying metadata that <em>never</em> select anything.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔖</div><h5>Labels — for selection</h5>
                        <p>Used by selectors to match &amp; group. Keep them meaningful.</p>
                        <ul><li><code>app: easypj</code></li><li><code>tier: web | db</code></li><li><code>env: prod</code></li><li><code>version: v3</code></li></ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>Annotations — for metadata</h5>
                        <p>For tools &amp; humans. Never used for selection.</p>
                        <ul><li><code>description: "easypj job board"</code></li><li><code>owner: "team-alpha"</code></li><li><code>kubernetes.io/change-cause: "v3"</code></li><li><code>prometheus.io/scrape: "true"</code></li></ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How labels connect objects (the Service selector)</div>
<pre>
   🔌 Service easypj-svc  (selector: app=easypj, tier=web)
        │ matches pods whose labels include BOTH
        ▼
   ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐
   │ 📦 Pod        │  │ 📦 Pod        │  │ 📦 Pod        │
   │ app: easypj  │  │ app: easypj  │  │ app: easypj  │
   │ tier: web    │  │ tier: web    │  │ tier: db     │  ← tier mismatch
   │ ✅ MATCHED    │  │ ✅ MATCHED    │  │ ❌ NOT matched│
   └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘
   💡 change a label and you change what's selected — the source of most wiring bugs.
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — label commands</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label pod easypj-pod tier=web          <span class="comment"># add a label to a live object</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels                 <span class="comment"># see all labels</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj                 <span class="comment"># filter by selector</span>
kubectl get pods -l 'app=easypj,tier=web'      <span class="comment"># AND</span>
kubectl get pods -l 'app in (easypj, other)'   <span class="comment"># set-based</span>
kubectl label pod easypj-pod tier-             <span class="comment"># remove a label (trailing dash)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 The easypj label convention</h5>
                    <p>Throughout this guide easypj uses <code>app=easypj</code> · <code>tier=web|db</code> · <code>version=vN</code>. Consistent labels are what make Services, Deployments, NetworkPolicies, and <code>kubectl -l</code> filters all "just work."</p>
                </div>

                <h4>The Recommended Labels (a Real Convention)</h4>
                <p>Kubernetes defines a standard set of <code>app.kubernetes.io/*</code> labels so tools (dashboards, Helm, meshes) can understand <em>any</em> app the same way. Our short <code>app=easypj</code> is fine for learning; the well-known set is what you'll meet in production:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Recommended label</th><th>Meaning</th><th>easypj value</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/name</code></td><td>The application's name</td><td><code>easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/component</code></td><td>The role within the app</td><td><code>web</code> / <code>db</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/instance</code></td><td>This specific install</td><td><code>easypj-prod</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/version</code></td><td>The app version</td><td><code>v2</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/managed-by</code></td><td>What deploys it</td><td><code>Helm</code> / <code>kubectl</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>Two Kinds of Selector</h4>
                <p>Selectors come in two flavours — know both, because Services/ReplicaSets use the first and you'll use the second on the command line:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🟰</div><h5>Equality-based</h5>
                        <p>Simple key=value (AND of all). What <code>matchLabels</code> in a Service/Deployment uses.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -l app=easypj,tier=web</code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔢</div><h5>Set-based</h5>
                        <p><code>in</code>, <code>notin</code>, <code>exists</code>. More expressive; used in <code>matchExpressions</code> and rich queries.</p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -l 'tier in (web,db),version!=v1'
kubectl get pods -l 'app'      <span class="comment"># has the label at all</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 matchLabels vs matchExpressions</h5>
                    <p>In a Deployment/Service spec, <code>selector.matchLabels</code> is the easy equality form; <code>selector.matchExpressions</code> unlocks set-based rules (e.g. "tier in (web,db)"). Both compile to the same selection — use matchLabels unless you genuinely need the extra power.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §4.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.7 The Object Relationship Map</h3>
                <p>This is the one picture to memorize — how every object you've met wires together into a running easypj. If you can redraw this from memory, you can read (and debug) almost any manifest.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How easypj wires together — the relationship map</div>
<pre>
   🌍 Ingress / 🚪 Gateway
        │ routes host/path
        ▼
   🔌 Service ──selector(app=easypj)──▶ 📦 Pods
        ▲                                  ▲ owned by
   🚀 Deployment ──manages──▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ─┘ creates
        │ the pod template references:
        ├─ ⚙️ ConfigMap   (envFrom — non-secret config)
        ├─ 🔒 Secret      (envFrom — credentials)
        ├─ 💾 PVC ──▶ PV ──▶ 🧱 StorageClass   (durable storage)
        └─ 🛡️ ServiceAccount  ◀── RBAC (Role + Binding) controls access
   🗂️ Namespace contains ALL of the above   ·   🧯 NetworkPolicy firewalls pod traffic
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   💡 LABELS are the glue: a Service finds Pods ONLY by matching labels — not by name or IP.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 How to use this map for troubleshooting</h5>
                    <p>Almost every "it's not working" follows an edge of this graph: <em>Service has no endpoints</em> → the selector↔label edge is broken; <em>pod can't read config</em> → the ConfigMap/Secret edge; <em>forbidden</em> → the ServiceAccount/RBAC edge; <em>data lost on restart</em> → the PVC edge. Find the broken edge, fix that object.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 4.8 POD LIFECYCLE & HEALTH — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.8 Pod Lifecycle &amp; Health Probes</h3>
                <p>A pod isn't simply "up" or "down" — it moves through <strong>phases</strong>, its containers have their own <strong>states</strong>, and probes decide whether it's <strong>Ready</strong> for traffic. This is the vocabulary behind every "why is my pod stuck?" task.</p>

                <h4>① Pod phases — <code>.status.phase</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Pending</code></td><td>accepted, not running yet — being scheduled, or pulling images</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Running</code></td><td>bound to a node; ≥1 container running / starting / restarting</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Succeeded</code></td><td>all containers exited 0 and won't restart (Jobs / one-offs)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Failed</code></td><td>all containers terminated; at least one exited non-zero</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Unknown</code></td><td>the node's kubelet can't be reached (usually a comms problem)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>② Container states — <code>.status.containerStatuses[].state</code></h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning · what to read</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Waiting</code></td><td>not yet running — the <code>reason</code> says why (<code>ContainerCreating</code>, <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Running</code></td><td>started — has a <code>startedAt</code> timestamp</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Terminated</code></td><td>finished — records <code>exitCode</code> (137 = OOM/SIGKILL, 0 = clean), <code>reason</code>, <code>finishedAt</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>③ <code>restartPolicy</code> — Always · OnFailure · Never</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Restarts when…</th><th>Used by</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Always</code> (default)</td><td>the container exits for <em>any</em> reason</td><td>Deployments / long-running services</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>OnFailure</code></td><td>only on a non-zero exit</td><td>batch Jobs that may retry</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Never</code></td><td>never — pod goes Succeeded/Failed</td><td>one-shot tasks</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">🔁 Restarts use <strong>exponential back-off</strong> (10s, 20s, 40s … capped at 5 min). A container that keeps crashing shows <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> — that's the back-off <em>waiting</em>, not an error in itself; read <code>kubectl logs</code> for the real cause.</p></div>

                <h4>④ Pod conditions — the path to <code>Ready</code> (and to a Service endpoint)</h4>
                <p>A pod's <code>.status.conditions</code> flip True in order. Only a <code>Ready</code> pod is added to its Service's endpoints — this is the answer to "pod is Running but gets no traffic."</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">conditions → endpoint membership</div>
<pre>
   PodScheduled ─▶ Initialized ─▶ ContainersReady ─▶ Ready ──▶ added to Service endpoints
   (got a node)    (init done)    (all probes pass)   (serving)
      ✗ stuck = no node (taints/resources)        ✗ Ready=False ⇒ removed from endpoints
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>⑤ Termination — the graceful shutdown sequence</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what happens on a pod delete</div>
<pre>
   1. apiserver sets metadata.deletionTimestamp         (pod → Terminating)
   2. pod removed from Service endpoints                 (stops new traffic)
   3. kubelet sends SIGTERM to each container            (app should drain &amp; exit)
   4. grace period: spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds   (default 30s)
   5. still alive? → SIGKILL (forced)                    then cleanup
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip mb-0"><p class="mb-0">💡 Set <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> to match how long easypj needs to finish in-flight requests, and handle SIGTERM in the app for zero-downtime rollouts. <code>kubectl delete pod --grace-period=0 --force</code> skips the wait — only for genuinely stuck pods.</p></div>

                <h4>⑥ Health probes — startup, liveness, readiness</h4>
                <p>Probes let Kubernetes act on app health (deep config is in Part 4; the concepts belong here):</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Probe</th><th>Question</th><th>On failure</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>startup</strong></td><td>has the app finished booting?</td><td>holds off liveness/readiness; kills it if it never starts (good for slow JVMs/DBs)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>liveness</strong></td><td>is it still alive (not deadlocked)?</td><td><strong>restarts</strong> the container</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>readiness</strong></td><td>can it serve traffic right now?</td><td><strong>removes</strong> the pod from Service endpoints (no restart)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">Each probe uses one of four methods: <code>httpGet</code> (HTTP 2xx/3xx), <code>tcpSocket</code> (port opens), <code>exec</code> (command exits 0), or <code>grpc</code> (gRPC health check, stable since v1.27); tuned with <code>initialDelaySeconds</code>, <code>periodSeconds</code>, <code>failureThreshold</code>.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 4.9 ROLLOUTS · PORTS · LABELS · SCOPE — official-docs enrichment ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s4-9">
                <h3 class="section-h">4.9 Rollouts, Service Ports, Label Rules &amp; Resource Scope</h3>
                <p>The exam-critical details that make the §4.3–4.6 objects actually behave: how a rollout paces itself, how Service ports map, the exact label grammar, and which resources live inside a namespace.</p>

                <h4>① Deployment rollout mechanics</h4>
                <p>A Deployment's <code>strategy</code> is <code>RollingUpdate</code> by default (the alternative, <code>Recreate</code>, kills all old pods first = downtime). The roll is paced by two knobs, plus a history limit for rollback:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>maxSurge</code></td><td><strong>25%</strong></td><td>extra pods allowed <em>above</em> desired during the roll (higher = faster)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>maxUnavailable</code></td><td><strong>25%</strong></td><td>pods allowed <em>down</em> during the roll (0 = zero-downtime, needs surge)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>revisionHistoryLimit</code></td><td><strong>10</strong></td><td>old ReplicaSets kept at 0 replicas for instant rollback</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">manage a rollout</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web easypj=easypj:v2   <span class="comment"># trigger a rolling update</span>
kubectl rollout status  deploy/easypj-web             <span class="comment"># watch it progress</span>
kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web             <span class="comment"># list revisions</span>
kubectl rollout undo    deploy/easypj-web             <span class="comment"># roll back to previous</span>
kubectl rollout undo    deploy/easypj-web --to-revision=3
kubectl rollout pause|resume deploy/easypj-web        <span class="comment"># batch edits into one roll</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note mb-0"><p class="mb-0">⚠️ The Deployment default is <strong>25% / 25%</strong>. A <strong>DaemonSet</strong> differs: <code>maxUnavailable</code> defaults to <code>1</code>, no surge by default. And a Deployment's <code>.spec.selector</code> is <strong>immutable</strong> after creation.</p></div>

                <h4>② Service ports — <code>port</code> vs <code>targetPort</code> vs <code>nodePort</code></h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the three ports of a Service</div>
<pre>
   client ──▶ Service:PORT ──▶ TARGETPORT on the pod ──▶ app listening there
              (e.g. 80)         (e.g. 8000)               easypj on :8000

   NodePort type also opens:  &lt;anyNodeIP&gt;:NODEPORT ──▶ Service:port ──▶ targetPort
                              nodePort range 30000–32767  (auto-assigned if unset)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning mb-0"><p class="mb-0">⚠️ <code>targetPort</code> must match the container's real port (easypj = <code>8000</code>) or you get "connection refused" from a healthy-looking Service. <code>port</code> defaults to <code>targetPort</code> if omitted; a <code>nodePort</code> outside 30000–32767 is rejected.</p></div>

                <h4>③ Cross-namespace DNS — short name vs FQDN</h4>
                <p class="mb-0">Within one namespace, a pod reaches a Service by its <strong>short name</strong> (<code>easypj-db</code>). Across namespaces the short name fails — use the <strong>FQDN</strong> <code>service.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>. Example: easypj-web in <code>staging</code> calling the DB in <code>data</code> → <code>easypj-db.data.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>

                <h4>④ Label grammar &amp; set-based selectors</h4>
                <p>A label key is <code>[prefix/]name</code>: an optional DNS-subdomain prefix, then a name ≤ 63 chars from <code>a–z A–Z 0–9 - _ .</code> starting/ending alphanumeric. Values follow the same ≤ 63-char rule (or empty). Selectors come in two forms:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>equality (AND)</td><td><code>app=easypj,tier=web</code></td><td><code>matchLabels</code>, <code>kubectl -l</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>set — <code>in</code></td><td><code>tier in (web,db)</code></td><td><code>matchExpressions</code>, <code>-l</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>set — <code>notin</code></td><td><code>tier notin (cache)</code></td><td><code>matchExpressions</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>set — <code>exists</code></td><td><code>tier</code> (has it) · <code>!tier</code> (lacks it)</td><td><code>matchExpressions</code> / <code>-l</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>⑤ Namespaced vs cluster-scoped resources</h4>
                <p>Some objects live <em>inside</em> a namespace; others are cluster-wide. Deleting a namespace deletes everything namespaced in it — cluster-scoped objects survive.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Namespaced</th><th>Cluster-scoped</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Pod · Deployment · ReplicaSet · Service · ConfigMap · Secret · PVC · Role · RoleBinding · Ingress · NetworkPolicy</td><td>Node · PersistentVolume · StorageClass · Namespace · ClusterRole · ClusterRoleBinding · CustomResourceDefinition</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p class="mb-0">See which is which: <code>kubectl api-resources --namespaced=true</code> and <code>kubectl api-resources --namespaced=false</code>.</p>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — ownerReferences, cascading deletion &amp; how a Service finds pods</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>The relationship map isn't a metaphor — those edges are real fields in etcd. Understanding the actual links explains cascading deletes, "why did my pods come back," and empty endpoints.</p>

                    <h5>① ownerReferences — the real parent-child links (and cascading deletion)</h5>
                    <p>Each Pod a ReplicaSet creates carries an <code>ownerReferences</code> field pointing at that ReplicaSet; the ReplicaSet points at the Deployment. That chain is what makes "delete the Deployment" also remove its ReplicaSets and Pods — <strong>garbage collection</strong> follows the owner links downward.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🚀 Deployment easypj-web
        ▲ ownerReference
   🎛️ ReplicaSet easypj-web-7f8b4
        ▲ ownerReference
   📦 Pod easypj-web-7f8b4-abc1     (metadata.ownerReferences → the ReplicaSet)

   delete the Deployment → GC deletes the RS → GC deletes the Pods   (cascades down)
   delete a Pod only     → the RS sees "want 3, have 2" → makes a new one (why it "comes back")
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>GC / delete mode</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Background</strong> (default)</td><td>Owner deleted immediately; children cleaned up in the background</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Foreground</strong></td><td>Children deleted first, then the owner (owner shows "deleting" until done)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Orphan</strong></td><td><code>--cascade=orphan</code> — delete only the owner, leave children running (adoptable later)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>② The EndpointSlice controller — how a Service actually reaches pods</h5>
                    <p>A Service doesn't hold pod IPs. The <strong>EndpointSlice controller</strong> watches pods matching the Service's selector and writes the list of <em>ready</em> pod IPs into EndpointSlice objects; kube-proxy programs those into the dataplane. That's the whole "selector → endpoints" mechanism — and why a label typo yields empty endpoints.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   🔌 Service easypj-svc (selector app=easypj,tier=web)
        │ EndpointSlice controller watches matching + READY pods
        ▼
   🗂️ EndpointSlice easypj-svc-x9k2 → [10.244.1.5:8000, 10.244.1.6:8000, 10.244.1.7:8000]
        │ kube-proxy programs iptables/IPVS
        ▼
   client → Service VIP → DNAT → one of those pod IPs
   ⚠️ selector ≠ pod labels, OR no pod passes readiness → EndpointSlice empty → 503/timeout
</pre>

                    <h5>③ GVK / GVR &amp; the RESTMapper — how <code>kubectl</code> resolves a "kind"</h5>
                    <p>When you type <code>kubectl get deploy</code>, the client maps the short name → a <strong>GVK</strong> (Group/Version/Kind: <code>apps/v1, Deployment</code>) → a <strong>GVR</strong> (Group/Version/Resource: <code>apps/v1/deployments</code>) → the REST path <code>/apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments</code>. That's why every manifest needs <code>apiVersion</code> + <code>kind</code>, and how CRDs add brand-new kinds.</p>

                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 spec vs status.</strong> Every object splits into <code>spec</code> (your desired state — you write it) and <code>status</code> (the controller's report — you read it). Controllers reconcile spec→reality and write status back. <code>kubectl get -o yaml</code> shows both; you almost never edit status by hand.</div>

                    <h5>④ Finalizers — why a "deleted" object gets stuck Terminating</h5>
                    <p>Deletion isn't always instant. If an object has a <strong>finalizer</strong> (a string in <code>metadata.finalizers</code>), a <code>delete</code> doesn't remove it — it just sets <code>deletionTimestamp</code> and the object sits in <strong>Terminating</strong> until some controller finishes cleanup and removes its finalizer. Only when the list is empty does the API actually delete it.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl delete pvc easypj-data
        │
        ▼  API sets metadata.deletionTimestamp  (object now "Terminating")
        │  metadata.finalizers: [kubernetes.io/pvc-protection]   ← still present
        ▼  the responsible controller does cleanup (detach the volume…)
        ▼  controller REMOVES its finalizer from the list
        ▼  finalizers empty → API garbage-collects the object for real ✅
   ⚠️ controller gone/broken → finalizer never removed → object STUCK Terminating forever
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The classic "stuck namespace."</strong> A Namespace stuck <code>Terminating</code> almost always means something inside it still has a finalizer (or an aggregated API is down). You diagnose with <code>kubectl get ns &lt;n&gt; -o yaml</code> (look at <code>status</code> + <code>spec.finalizers</code>) and fix the root cause — force-removing a finalizer is a last resort, since it skips the cleanup it was protecting.</div>

                    <h5>🧹 Control the cascade — and the cross-namespace ownership rule</h5>
                    <p>You can override the default deletion behaviour with <code>--cascade</code>, and ownership has one hard constraint:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl delete deploy easypj-web                       # --cascade=background (default): owner gone now, GC sweeps children
   kubectl delete deploy easypj-web --cascade=foreground  # children deleted FIRST; owner stays Terminating until they're gone
   kubectl delete deploy easypj-web --cascade=orphan      # delete the Deployment; LEAVE the ReplicaSet + Pods running
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 ownerReferences can't cross namespaces.</strong> A namespaced owner and its dependent must live in the <em>same</em> namespace (only a cluster-scoped object — e.g. a Node — may own across them). That rule is why deleting a namespace cleanly garbage-collects everything inside it.</div>

                    <h5>🔗 EndpointSlice vs the legacy Endpoints object</h5>
                    <p>The old <code>Endpoints</code> API packed every backend IP into one object — which became huge and churn-heavy for large Services. <strong>EndpointSlices</strong> shard them into pieces of <strong>≤ 100 endpoints</strong> (so a 1,000-pod Service ≈ 10 small slices that update independently). EndpointSlices are the default; the legacy <code>Endpoints</code> API is now deprecated. Only <strong>Ready</strong> pods land in a slice — the exact mechanism behind "Running but receiving no traffic."</p>

                    <p><strong>🔬 Deeper (exam + beyond):</strong> <strong>server-side apply</strong> tracks field ownership in <code>managedFields</code> so multiple actors (you, a controller, a GitOps tool) can co-own one object without clobbering each other; <code>generateName</code> lets the API mint unique names (how RS pods get their random suffix); and the spec/status split is itself a <em>subresource</em> (<code>/status</code>, <code>/scale</code>) with its own RBAC — which is how <code>kubectl scale</code> works without granting full edit.</p>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pod &lt; ReplicaSet &lt; Deployment</strong> — you create the Deployment; it owns the RS; the RS owns the Pods (via ownerReferences).</li>
                    <li><strong>Pods are ephemeral</strong> — replaced not repaired; never rely on a pod name or IP.</li>
                    <li><strong>Services give stable networking</strong> — a VIP + DNS name that load-balances to pods by <em>label selector</em>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Namespaces isolate</strong> teams/envs — but NOT nodes or cluster-scoped objects (PV/Node/StorageClass).</li>
                    <li><strong>Labels are the glue</strong> — Service↔Pod, Deployment↔Pod, NetworkPolicy, RBAC all wire by labels; annotations never select.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Selector ≠ template labels.</strong> A Deployment's <code>selector.matchLabels</code> must match its pod-template labels, or it won't manage them; a Service's selector must match pod labels or endpoints are <strong>empty</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deleting a Pod under a Deployment</strong> just respawns it — change the <em>desired</em> state (scale/delete the Deployment) instead.</li>
                    <li><strong>Annotations aren't selectable.</strong> Put anything you need to <em>match</em> in labels, not annotations.</li>
                    <li><strong>Wrong namespace = 0 marks.</strong> Always set <code>-n</code> / context first.</li>
                    <li><strong>port vs targetPort.</strong> <code>port</code> is the Service's; <code>targetPort</code> is the container's (8000 for easypj). Mismatch = connection refused.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Generate, don't hand-write:</strong> <code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; d.yaml</code> then edit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Expose fast:</strong> <code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>First Service check:</strong> <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — empty = selector/readiness problem.</li>
                    <li><strong>Change live objects:</strong> <code>kubectl label</code> / <code>kubectl annotate</code>; filter with <code>-l</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 4)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the core-object toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get all -n easypj                 <span class="comment"># deploys, rs, pods, svc at a glance</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -o wide    <span class="comment"># filter by label</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3
kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc          <span class="comment"># does the Service find pods?</span>
kubectl label pod easypj-pod tier=web     <span class="comment"># label / annotate live objects</span>
kubectl explain deployment.spec --recursive   <span class="comment"># in-exam field reference</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Wire It Together &amp; Watch It Self-Heal</h5>
                <p>Build a self-healing workload end to end — predict each result first:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Create the <code>easypj</code> namespace + a Deployment (3 replicas).</li>
                    <li>Expose it as a Service and confirm it has 3 endpoints.</li>
                    <li>Delete one pod and watch the ReplicaSet replace it.</li>
                    <li>Break the Service selector and watch endpoints go empty, then fix it.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj
kubectl -n easypj create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3
kubectl -n easypj expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000
kubectl -n easypj get endpoints easypj-web        <span class="comment"># 3 pod IPs ✅</span>

kubectl -n easypj delete pod -l app=easypj-web --field-selector status.phase=Running | head -1
kubectl -n easypj get pods -w                      <span class="comment"># a new pod appears → back to 3/3</span>

<span class="comment"># break it: point the Service selector at a label no pod has</span>
kubectl -n easypj patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"nope"}}}'
kubectl -n easypj get endpoints easypj-web         <span class="comment"># &lt;none&gt; — empty! 💀</span>
kubectl -n easypj patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"easypj-web"}}}'   <span class="comment"># fixed ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>What you proved:</strong> the ReplicaSet self-heals to the desired count (owner links + reconcile loop), and a Service's endpoints are <em>entirely</em> driven by the selector↔label match — break that edge and traffic stops, even though the pods are perfectly healthy.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A — CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 4 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the core objects — 10 questions, each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Pod vs Deployment — when would you create each?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Create a bare <strong>Pod</strong> only for a quick one-off/debug; for anything real, create a <strong>Deployment</strong>:</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>📦 bare Pod</th><th>🚀 Deployment</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Self-heals?</td><td>no — gone for good if it dies</td><td>yes (via its ReplicaSet)</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Scale · roll out · roll back?</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Use for</td><td>a quick one-off / debug pod</td><td>anything real</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A bare pod is a pet; a Deployment manages cattle. In practice you almost never create naked pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Service has 0 endpoints. Give the two most common causes.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) The Service <strong>selector doesn't match</strong> any pod's labels. (2) Pods match but <strong>none is Ready</strong> (readiness probe failing). Confirm: <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> + compare selector to pod labels.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Only ready, label-matching pods land in the EndpointSlice — both conditions must hold.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do Namespaces isolate — and what do they NOT isolate?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>They scope <em>namespaced</em> objects and can carry RBAC/Quota/NetworkPolicy — but cluster-scoped objects live outside any namespace:</p>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>✅ Namespaces DO scope</th><th>❌ Namespaces do NOT scope</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Pods · Services · Deployments · ConfigMaps · Secrets</td><td>Nodes</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Roles · RoleBindings · ResourceQuota · NetworkPolicy</td><td>PersistentVolumes · StorageClasses</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><em>(namespaced)</em></td><td>ClusterRoles · ClusterRoleBindings · CRDs <em>(cluster-scoped)</em></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Namespaces are a logical, not a hard security/physical, boundary — that's what NetworkPolicy + RBAC add.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Add the label <code>tier=web</code> to a running Deployment.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">label a live object</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label deployment easypj-web tier=web
kubectl label deployment easypj-web tier=web --overwrite   <span class="comment"># change an existing value</span>
<span class="comment"># to label the PODS, edit the pod template — selectors match pod labels, not the Deployment's</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Labeling the Deployment object ≠ labeling its pods — the pod template's labels are what selectors match.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Label vs annotation — the difference and a use for each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th></th><th>🔖 Label</th><th>📝 Annotation</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>identify &amp; <strong>select</strong></td><td>non-identifying metadata</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Selectable?</td><td>yes — <code>-l</code>, <code>matchLabels</code></td><td>never</td></tr>
                                <tr><td>Example</td><td><code>app=easypj</code></td><td><code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> · <code>prometheus.io/scrape</code></td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If you need to <em>match</em> on it, it's a label. If it's just info, it's an annotation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the ownership chain for a pod named <code>easypj-web-7d4-x9k2</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The name literally encodes the chain; each layer links up via <code>ownerReferences</code>:</p>
                        <pre class="diagram-box">
   🚀 Deployment   easypj-web
        │ owns (ownerReference)
        ▼
   🎛️ ReplicaSet   easypj-web-7d4            ← Deployment name + pod-template-hash
        │ owns
        ▼
   📦 Pod          easypj-web-7d4-x9k2       ← ReplicaSet name + random suffix
</pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The name literally encodes the chain — and that chain drives cascading deletion.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why are Pods "ephemeral," and what's the implication for IPs?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A pod is never repaired — when it dies it's replaced by a <em>new</em> pod with a new name and a new IP. So you must never hard-code a pod IP; reach pods through a <strong>Service</strong> (stable VIP + DNS) instead.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the entire reason Services exist — stable networking over a moving set of pods.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List the 4 Service types and who can reach each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Reachable by</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong> <em>(default)</em></td><td>in-cluster only</td><td>internal service-to-service</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td><code>&lt;nodeIP&gt;:30000–32767</code></td><td>dev / bare-metal external access</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td><td>a cloud load balancer</td><td>cloud production</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><strong>ExternalName</strong></td><td>DNS CNAME (no proxying)</td><td>alias an external host</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ClusterIP for internal, NodePort for dev/bare-metal, LoadBalancer for cloud prod, ExternalName to alias something outside.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You delete a Pod managed by a Deployment. What happens and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The ReplicaSet immediately creates a <strong>replacement</strong> — its reconcile loop sees "want 3, have 2" and acts. To actually remove it, lower the desired state (<code>kubectl scale</code>) or delete the Deployment.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>You changed actual, not desired; the controller just closes the gap again.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">From the relationship map: how does a ConfigMap reach a container?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The pod template references it three ways; the kubelet injects it when the container starts:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">3 ways a ConfigMap reaches a container</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ① all keys → env vars</span>
envFrom:
- configMapRef:
    name: easypj-config
<span class="comment"># ② one key → one env var (optionally renamed)</span>
env:
- name: DB_HOST
  valueFrom:
    configMapKeyRef:
      name: easypj-config
      key: db_host
<span class="comment"># ③ keys → files in a mounted volume (can hot-reload)</span>
volumes:
- name: cfg
  configMap:
    name: easypj-config</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Env values are fixed at start; file mounts can hot-reload. Full detail in Chapter 23.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch4-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 4 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style and hands-on — produce the object or the fix with real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a pod named <code>web-pod</code> in namespace <code>production</code> using the image <code>nginx:1.25-alpine</code> with the label <code>app=web</code> and a resource request of 100m CPU and 128Mi memory. Write the imperative command and the equivalent YAML.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative approach (generate yaml</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Imperative approach (generate YAML, then add resources)</span>
kubectl run web-pod --image=nginx:1.25-alpine \
  --labels=app=web \
  -n production \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > web-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Then edit to add resources, or use the full YAML:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: web-pod
  namespace: production
  labels:
    app: web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web-pod
    image: nginx:1.25-alpine
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: "100m"
        memory: "128Mi"

<span class="comment"># Apply it</span>
kubectl apply -f web-pod.yaml</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a fundamental CKA task. The hybrid approach — imperative generation + declarative editing — is the fastest strategy. Key points: (1) <code>kubectl run</code> only creates pods (not deployments since v1.18+). (2) <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> generates YAML without creating the resource. (3) Resource requests affect scheduling — the scheduler only places the pod on nodes with at least 100m CPU and 128Mi memory available. (4) Always create the namespace first if it doesn't exist: <code>kubectl create namespace production</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the relationship between a <strong>Deployment</strong>, a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>, and a <strong>Pod</strong>? If you create a Deployment with 3 replicas, how many of each object exist?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The hierarchy is: <strong>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods</strong></p>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">Deployment (nginx-deploy)
  └── ReplicaSet (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8)     ← 1 ReplicaSet
        ├── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-abc12)  ← Pod 1
        ├── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-def34)  ← Pod 2
        └── Pod (nginx-deploy-7d4f6b9c8-ghi56)  ← Pod 3

Total: 1 Deployment + 1 ReplicaSet + 3 Pods = 5 objects</code></pre>
</div>
                                <p><strong>Ownership chain:</strong> Each object has an <code>ownerReference</code> pointing to its parent. Deleting the Deployment cascades down — deletes the ReplicaSet, which deletes the pods.</p>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this hierarchy is critical for CKA troubleshooting. Key points: (1) You should <strong>never</strong> create ReplicaSets directly — always use Deployments. (2) During rolling updates, the Deployment creates a <em>new</em> ReplicaSet and scales it up while scaling the old one down. (3) Old ReplicaSets are kept (with 0 replicas) for rollback — controlled by <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code> (default: 10). (4) If you see orphaned pods not managed by a Deployment, check for missing ownerReferences.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the three Service types: <code>ClusterIP</code>, <code>NodePort</code>, and <code>LoadBalancer</code>. For each type, describe who can access the service and provide an example command to create one.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Type</th>
                                            <th>Accessible From</th>
                                            <th>Port Range</th>
                                            <th>Use Case</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>ClusterIP</code></td>
                                            <td>Inside cluster only</td>
                                            <td>Any</td>
                                            <td>Internal services (DB, cache)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>NodePort</code></td>
                                            <td>External via node IP</td>
                                            <td>30000-32767</td>
                                            <td>Dev/test external access</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>LoadBalancer</code></td>
                                            <td>External via cloud LB</td>
                                            <td>Any</td>
                                            <td>Production external access</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">clusterip (default)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ClusterIP (default)</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># NodePort</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --type=NodePort --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># LoadBalancer</span>
kubectl expose deployment nginx --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --target-port=80</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Services provide stable networking for ephemeral pods. Each Service gets a permanent <strong>ClusterIP</strong> and DNS name. Key CKA concepts: (1) <code>NodePort</code> automatically creates a <code>ClusterIP</code> underneath — it's an extension. (2) <code>LoadBalancer</code> creates both <code>ClusterIP</code> and <code>NodePort</code> underneath. (3) The <code>selector</code> in the Service spec must match labels on the pods — if they don't match, the service has no endpoints. (4) <code>kubectl expose</code> is the fastest way to create a service on the CKA exam.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You create a Deployment with <code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3</code>. Now you need to update the image to <code>easypj:v2</code>. What command do you use, and what happens internally during the rolling update?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">update the image</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update the image</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web easypj-web=easypj:v2

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># What happens internally:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Deployment controller creates a NEW ReplicaSet (easypj-web-v2-xxxxx)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Scales new RS up: 0 → 1</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Scales old RS down: 3 → 2</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Repeats until new RS has 3 and old RS has 0</span>
<span class="comment"># (controlled by maxSurge and maxUnavailable)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check rollout history</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Rollback if v2 is broken</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web

<span class="comment"># Rollback to a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web --to-revision=1</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Rolling updates are a core CKA task. The Deployment's <code>strategy.rollingUpdate</code> settings control the pace: <code>maxSurge</code> (how many extra pods above desired count) and <code>maxUnavailable</code> (how many pods can be down during the update). Default is <code>25%</code> for both. The old ReplicaSet is kept with 0 replicas so you can <code>rollout undo</code>. Pro tip: to record the change cause, add <code>--record</code> (deprecated but still works) or annotate the deployment with <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>Labels</strong> and <strong>Selectors</strong> in Kubernetes? How do they connect Services to Pods? Give an example where a Service fails to route traffic because of a label mismatch.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p><strong>Labels</strong> are key-value pairs attached to objects. <strong>Selectors</strong> are queries that match labels to connect objects together.</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pod has label: app=web</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod has label: app=web</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: web-pod
  labels:
    app: web        # ← Label
    version: v1

<span class="comment"># Service selects pods with app=web</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-svc
spec:
  selector:
    app: web        # ← Must EXACTLY match pod label
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8080

<span class="comment"># ❌ BROKEN: Service selector doesn't match pod labels</span>
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend   # ← "frontend" ≠ "web" → NO endpoints!</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose: check if service has endpoints</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Diagnose: check if service has endpoints</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># If ENDPOINTS shows &lt;none&gt;, selector doesn't match any pods</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Label/selector mismatches are one of the <strong>most common CKA troubleshooting scenarios</strong>. If traffic doesn't reach pods: (1) Check <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — empty means no matching pods. (2) Compare <code>kubectl get svc &lt;svc&gt; -o yaml | grep -A 5 selector</code> with <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code>. (3) Labels must match <strong>exactly</strong> — <code>app: web</code> ≠ <code>App: web</code> (case sensitive). ReplicaSets and Deployments also use selectors to find their pods — same matching rules apply.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a Namespace called <code>exam-prep</code>, then create a Deployment named <code>api-server</code> in that namespace with 2 replicas of <code>httpd:2.4</code>. Finally, expose it as a NodePort service on port 80. Write all the imperative commands.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create namespace</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create deployment</span>
kubectl create deployment api-server \
  --image=httpd:2.4 \
  --replicas=2 \
  -n exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Expose as NodePort</span>
kubectl expose deployment api-server \
  --type=NodePort \
  --port=80 \
  --target-port=80 \
  -n exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Verify everything</span>
kubectl get all -n exam-prep

<span class="comment"># Access the service</span>
kubectl get svc api-server -n exam-prep
<span class="comment"># Note the NodePort (e.g., 31234)</span>
<span class="comment"># Access via: http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:31234</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This 3-command sequence is a <strong>bread-and-butter CKA task</strong>. Timing tip: this should take under 60 seconds. Key points: (1) Always specify <code>-n &lt;namespace&gt;</code> or use <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=exam-prep</code> to avoid mistakes. (2) <code>--port</code> is the service port, <code>--target-port</code> is the container port. (3) Kubernetes auto-assigns a NodePort from 30000-32767 unless you specify <code>--node-port=XXXXX</code>. (4) <code>kubectl get all</code> shows deployments, replicasets, pods, and services in one view.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl create</code> and <code>kubectl apply</code>? When would you use each one on the CKA exam?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th><code>kubectl create</code></th>
                                            <th><code>kubectl apply</code></th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Type</td>
                                            <td>Imperative</td>
                                            <td>Declarative</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>If exists</td>
                                            <td>Error: "already exists"</td>
                                            <td>Updates (merges changes)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Idempotent</td>
                                            <td>No</td>
                                            <td>Yes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Best for</td>
                                            <td>First-time creation, speed</td>
                                            <td>Updates, CI/CD, repeatability</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cka strategy: use create for speed, apply for edits</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># CKA strategy: use create for speed, apply for edits</span>
kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > web.yaml
vim web.yaml  <span class="comment"># add resources, probes, volumes</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml

<span class="comment"># Later, edit and re-apply</span>
vim web.yaml  <span class="comment"># change replicas, image</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml  <span class="comment"># applies only the diff</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA, use <code>create</code> with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate quick scaffolds, then <code>apply</code> to submit them. If a question says "create," either works. If a question says "update" or "modify," you need <code>apply</code> or <code>kubectl edit</code> or <code>kubectl patch</code>. Pro tip: <code>kubectl apply</code> stores the last-applied configuration as an annotation, enabling three-way merge diffs on subsequent applies.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>Pending</code> state. When you describe it, events show: <em>"0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu"</em>. What does this mean and what are your options to fix it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The scheduler cannot find any node with enough free CPU to satisfy the pod's <code>resources.requests.cpu</code>. Options to fix:</p>
                                <ol>
                                    <li><strong>Reduce the pod's CPU request:</strong>
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">or edit the deployment</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl edit pod &lt;name&gt;  <span class="comment"># or edit the deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># Change resources.requests.cpu to a lower value</span></code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                    <li><strong>Free up resources</strong> by deleting/scaling down other workloads:
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -10
kubectl scale deployment heavy-app --replicas=1</code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                    <li><strong>Add more nodes</strong> to the cluster to provide additional capacity</li>
                                    <li><strong>Check ResourceQuotas</strong> — a namespace quota may be artificially limiting available resources:
                                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                        <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get resourcequota -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></pre>
                                        </div>
                                    </li>
                                </ol>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The "Insufficient" message is from the scheduler's <strong>filtering phase</strong>. It checked all 3 nodes and none had enough allocatable CPU. On the CKA, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> events section is your best friend for diagnosing Pending pods. Other common Pending causes: node taints without matching tolerations, node selector/affinity not matching, PVC unbound (waiting for PV). Always read the specific message — it tells you exactly which filter failed and on how many nodes.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are <strong>Annotations</strong> in Kubernetes? How do they differ from Labels, and when would you use annotations instead of labels?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Feature</th>
                                            <th>Labels</th>
                                            <th>Annotations</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Purpose</td>
                                            <td>Identify and select objects</td>
                                            <td>Attach non-identifying metadata</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Used in selectors</td>
                                            <td>Yes (<code>selector: app=web</code>)</td>
                                            <td>No</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Size limit</td>
                                            <td>63 chars value</td>
                                            <td>256KB total</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Example</td>
                                            <td><code>app: web, tier: frontend</code></td>
                                            <td><code>description: "Main web app"</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">add annotation</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add annotation</span>
kubectl annotate deployment web kubernetes.io/change-cause="Updated to v2"

<span class="comment"># Add label</span>
kubectl label pod web-pod tier=frontend

<span class="comment"># Filter by label (annotations can't be used in selectors)</span>
kubectl get pods -l tier=frontend</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Labels are for <strong>identification and grouping</strong>. Annotations are for <strong>metadata storage</strong>. Common annotations you'll see on the CKA: (1) <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> — records why a rollout happened, (2) <code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code> — stored by <code>kubectl apply</code>, (3) Ingress controller annotations (e.g., <code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target</code>). The CKA may ask you to annotate objects or add labels for selection — know the syntax for both.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to scale a Deployment from 3 replicas to 5, then verify the rollout completed. What are three different ways to accomplish this on the CKA exam?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl scale (fastest)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl scale (fastest)</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl edit (in-place edit)</span>
kubectl edit deployment easypj-web
<span class="comment"># Change spec.replicas from 3 to 5, save and exit</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl patch (JSON patch)</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify (all methods)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment easypj-web
kubectl get deployment easypj-web
<span class="comment"># Ready: 5/5</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl scale</code> is the fastest method for the CKA exam. However, <code>kubectl edit</code> is useful when you need to change multiple fields at once. <code>kubectl patch</code> is scriptable and good for automation. Key insight: <strong>scaling does NOT trigger a rollout</strong> — it only changes the replica count on the <em>current</em> ReplicaSet. Only image changes, env changes, or other spec updates trigger new rollouts. Verify scaling with <code>kubectl get deployment</code> — check that READY shows <code>5/5</code>, not <code>3/5</code> (which would indicate pods are still starting). You can also use <code>kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web</code> to see all 5 pods.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 4 Visual Summary — the objects, wired together</div>
<pre>
   🌍 Ingress/Gateway ─routes─▶ 🔌 Service ─selector(app=easypj)─▶ 📦 Pods
                                    ▲                                ▲ owned by
                               🚀 Deployment ─manages─▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ┘ creates (self-heal)
                                    │ pod template pulls in:
                                    ├─ ⚙️ ConfigMap / 🔒 Secret (envFrom)
                                    ├─ 💾 PVC ─▶ PV ─▶ 🧱 StorageClass
                                    └─ 🛡️ ServiceAccount ◀─ RBAC governs access
   🗂️ Namespace contains it all   ·   🧯 NetworkPolicy firewalls it   ·   🔖 LABELS are the glue
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   You create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods; a Service finds those Pods
   by LABEL and gives them a stable address. That sentence is 80% of every CKA workload task.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> The objects nest. You create a <strong>Deployment</strong>; it <em>manages</em> a <strong>ReplicaSet</strong>; the ReplicaSet <em>creates and self-heals</em> the <strong>Pods</strong>. You never make the lower layers by hand — you describe the top one and ownership cascades down.</p>
                    <p>A pod's template pulls in everything it needs: <strong>ConfigMap/Secret</strong> for configuration (<code>envFrom</code>), a <strong>PVC → PV → StorageClass</strong> chain for storage, and a <strong>ServiceAccount</strong> (governed by RBAC) for API access. Out front, a <strong>Service</strong> uses a <em>label selector</em> (<code>app=easypj</code>) to find the ever-changing pods and give them one stable address; <strong>Ingress/Gateway</strong> routes external traffic to that Service. Around it all, a <strong>Namespace</strong> contains the objects and a <strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> firewalls them.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> <em>labels are the glue</em> — nothing is wired by name, everything is matched by label. "Create a Deployment → it owns a ReplicaSet → which owns Pods → a Service selects them by label" is the single sentence behind roughly 80% of every CKA workload task.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <!-- ::CH4-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 4 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 1 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part1-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟢 Part 1 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Foundations — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You started unable to picture a cluster; now you can name every component, explain what breaks without each, trace a pod from <code>kubectl apply</code> to Running, and read any easypj manifest. That mental model is the platform the rest of the guide builds on.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Part 1 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Kubernetes automates</strong> deploy / scale / heal via <em>desired-state reconciliation</em> — you declare, control loops deliver (Ch 1).</li>
                    <li><strong>Containers share the host kernel</strong> (namespaces + cgroups + union FS); K8s runs them via the <strong>CRI</strong>, not Docker (Ch 2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Control plane = apiserver + etcd + scheduler + controller-manager; nodes = kubelet + kube-proxy + runtime.</strong> Only the apiserver talks to etcd (Ch 3).</li>
                    <li><strong>Core objects:</strong> Pod &lt; ReplicaSet &lt; Deployment; Service for stable networking; Namespace for isolation; <strong>labels</strong> wire it all together (Ch 4).</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> "which component?" thinking powers the whole 25% Architecture + 30% Troubleshooting domains.</li>
                    <li>🔑 <code>cluster-info</code> · <code>get nodes -o wide</code> · <code>api-resources</code> · <code>explain</code> · <code>crictl</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 1 Visual Summary — easypj source → image → desired state → running pods</div>
<pre>
   🐍 easypj source ─build─▶ 📀 easypj:v1 ─push─▶ 🏪 registry        (Ch 2: the unit)
                                                     │ pull
   📝 deploy.yaml ──kubectl apply──▶ 📡 apiserver ──▶ 🗄️ etcd          (Ch 1: declare desired)
   (replicas: 3)                       │ authN/Z/admit  (desired state)
                                       ├─ 🔄 controllers: Deployment→ReplicaSet→Pods
                                       └─ 📋 scheduler: bind each Pod → a node       (Ch 3: the brain)
                                              ▼
                            🤖 kubelet ─🐋 run─▶ 📦📦📦 easypj pods ─🌐 CNI gives IPs   (Ch 3: the muscle)
                                              ▼
                            🔌 Service (selector app=easypj) ─load-balances─▶ the pods   (Ch 4: stable net)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   one app, described as desired state, that the cluster keeps alive — heal, scale, roll out, repeat. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> You build the <code>easypj</code> app into an image (<code>easypj:v1</code>) and push it to a registry — that image is the portable <em>unit of deployment</em> (Ch 2). You then <strong>declare</strong> what you want in <code>deploy.yaml</code> (e.g. 3 replicas) and run <code>kubectl apply</code>: the <strong>apiserver</strong> authenticates, authorizes, and admits the request, then writes that desired state into <strong>etcd</strong> — the single source of truth (Ch 1).</p>
                    <p>From there the cluster's <strong>brain</strong> takes over (Ch 3): <strong>controllers</strong> expand <code>Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods</code>, and the <strong>scheduler</strong> picks a node for each Pod. On that node the <strong>muscle</strong> acts — the <strong>kubelet</strong> tells the container runtime (via the CRI) to start the containers, and the <strong>CNI</strong> hands every Pod an IP. A <strong>Service</strong> with selector <code>app=easypj</code> then gives those churning Pods one stable address and load-balances across them (Ch 4).</p>
                    <p><strong>The one idea to keep:</strong> nothing here is a one-off command — it's all <em>desired state</em> the cluster reconciles toward, continuously. That's why it can heal a dead pod, scale on demand, and roll out a new version on its own. Master this loop and the other 13 parts are just variations on it.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END PART 1 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 2 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part2">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟢 Part 2 · Hands-On Basics</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Drive the Cluster</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 5–8 · Beginner → Intermediate · <em>"Make it run"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner → Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 4 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~7.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🛠️ Hands-On Basics</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Part 1 gave you the mental model; Part 2 puts your hands on the controls. You'll stand up a real, local cluster, master <strong>kubectl</strong> as your remote control, learn the <strong>YAML</strong> Kubernetes speaks, and deploy <strong>easypj</strong> for the first time — watching every Part 1 concept turn into a live, running object.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you'll build by the end:</strong> Minikube + kubectl installed, contexts under control, and easypj deployed end-to-end — Namespace → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service — reachable in your browser at <code>/jobs/</code>.</div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — Part 2</div>
<pre>
   Ch 5 — ENVIRONMENT   ─▶   Ch 6 — kubectl    ─▶   Ch 7 — YAML        ─▶   Ch 8 — DEPLOY easypj
   a cluster to use          the remote control      the language             put it all together
   Minikube + kubeconfig     verbs · explain         manifests · dry-run      CM · Secret · Deploy · Svc
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: from "I have a cluster" to "easypj is live and reachable at /jobs/".
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 5 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch5">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 5 · Setting Up Your Environment</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Before you can <em>administer</em> a cluster, you need one to practice on. This chapter installs the tools, connects you to a cluster, and drills the one skill every exam question opens with — <strong>switching context</strong> — all anchored on deploying <strong>easypj</strong>.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🛠️ Setup</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">💻 Hands-On</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Choose the right local Kubernetes tool — Minikube, Kind, k3s, kubeadm, Docker Desktop</li>
                    <li>✅ Install &amp; start Minikube with the optimal settings for CKA practice</li>
                    <li>✅ Install kubectl and confirm it connects to your cluster</li>
                    <li>✅ Read a <strong>kubeconfig</strong> — the file that controls every cluster connection</li>
                    <li>✅ Switch between clusters, contexts &amp; namespaces (used in <em>every</em> exam question)</li>
                    <li>✅ Verify cluster health and enable the essential add-ons</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">kubeconfig <span class="cka-weight">EVERY QUESTION</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 The CKA exam environment — context first, always</h5>
                <p>The exam gives you <strong>multiple pre-built clusters</strong>. <em>Every</em> question opens with "Use context <code>k8s-cluster-X</code>" — run <code>kubectl config use-context …</code> <strong>before you touch anything</strong>, or you'll do perfect work on the wrong cluster and score zero.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Context switching</strong> is the first action of nearly every task — make it muscle memory.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl is pre-installed</strong>, but your own <code>alias k=kubectl</code> + shell completion saves real minutes.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubernetes.io/docs</strong> is the one reference allowed in-exam — know how to navigate it fast.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 5 Roadmap — from a laptop to a verified, ready cluster</div>
<pre>
   5.1 PICK a tool ──▶ 5.2 INSTALL Minikube ──▶ 5.3 INSTALL kubectl ──▶ 5.4 kubeconfig
   Minikube / kubeadm    a multi-node lab          the one CLI            clusters · users · contexts
        │                                                                       │
        └─────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┘
                                           ▼
   5.5 SWITCH context/ns ──▶ 5.6 VERIFY health ──▶ 5.7 ENABLE add-ons
   use-context · set ns       cluster-info · nodes   metrics · ingress · storage
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s5-1"><strong>5.1</strong> Choosing a tool</a></td><td>What do I run K8s on locally?</td><td>Minikube to learn fast; kubeadm because the exam tests it.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s5-2"><strong>5.2</strong> Install Minikube</a></td><td>How do I get a cluster in minutes?</td><td><code>minikube start</code> + a multi-node flag for scheduling practice.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s5-3"><strong>5.3</strong> Install kubectl</a></td><td>How do I talk to any cluster?</td><td>One CLI for all clusters; it reads <code>~/.kube/config</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s5-4"><strong>5.4</strong> kubeconfig</a></td><td>How does kubectl know where to connect?</td><td>clusters (where) + users (who) + contexts (the combo).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s5-5"><strong>5.5</strong> Contexts</a></td><td>How do I move between clusters?</td><td><code>use-context</code> + a default namespace — do it first, every time.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s5-6"><strong>5.6</strong> Verify</a></td><td>Is the cluster actually healthy?</td><td><code>cluster-info</code>, all nodes Ready, kube-system Running.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s5-7"><strong>5.7</strong> Add-ons</a></td><td>What's still missing on a fresh cluster?</td><td>metrics-server, ingress, storage-provisioner — enable as needed.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s5-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.1 Choosing a Local Kubernetes Tool</h3>
                <p>Several tools run Kubernetes locally; the right one depends on what you're practising for. For the CKA the path is simple — <strong>learn on Minikube, then build with kubeadm</strong> (Ch 9).</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best for</th><th>Nodes</th><th>Footprint</th><th>CKA prep?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Minikube</strong></td><td>learning, interactive</td><td>1 → multi</td><td>medium</td><td>✅ start here</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Kind</strong></td><td>CI/CD, testing</td><td>multi</td><td>low</td><td>✅ good (K8s-in-Docker)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>k3s</strong></td><td>edge, lightweight</td><td>multi</td><td>very low</td><td>⚠️ differs from full K8s</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubeadm</strong></td><td>real clusters</td><td>multi</td><td>high</td><td>✅ MUST learn (tested)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Docker Desktop</strong></td><td>Win/Mac dev</td><td>1</td><td>medium</td><td>⚠️ limited</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 CKA learning path</h5><p>Use <strong>Minikube</strong> for Chapters 5–8 (instant, great for learning), then switch to <strong>kubeadm</strong> in Part 3 to build a cluster from scratch — that build is <em>directly tested</em> on the exam.</p></div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Which local tool? — a 10-second decision</div>
<pre>
   What are you doing right now?
   ├─ Brand new, just learning ............▶ 🐣 Minikube        one command · addons · dashboard
   ├─ Practising the CKA cluster build ....▶ 🔧 kubeadm         real certs · systemd kubelet · multi-node
   ├─ CI / automated test pipelines .......▶ 📦 Kind            K8s-in-Docker · throwaway · fast
   ├─ Tiny / edge / Raspberry-Pi ..........▶ 🪶 k3s             single binary · very low memory
   └─ Quick dev on a Win/Mac desktop ......▶ 🐳 Docker Desktop  one click · single node
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🖥️ What the CKA exam environment actually provides</h4>
                <p>Practising in realistic conditions starts with knowing what's on the other side of the screen:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Pre-installed tools</h5><ul><li><code>kubectl</code> + bash completion</li><li><code>jq</code> · <code>curl</code>/<code>wget</code></li><li><code>tmux</code> · <code>vim</code>/<code>nano</code></li><li><code>systemctl</code> on nodes</li></ul></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📚</div><h5>Allowed references</h5><ul><li><strong>kubernetes.io/docs</strong> (+ /blog)</li><li><strong>github.com/kubernetes</strong></li><li>one browser tab, docs only</li><li>no personal notes/bookmarks</li></ul></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚙️</div><h5>Exam clusters</h5><ul><li>~6 pre-built clusters</li><li>single- &amp; multi-node mix</li><li><strong>switch context per question</strong></li><li>SSH + <code>sudo</code> on nodes</li></ul></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⏱️</div><h5>Format</h5><ul><li><strong>2 hours</strong>, 15–20 tasks</li><li><strong>66%</strong> to pass</li><li>performance-based (live terminal)</li><li>flag &amp; return; one free retake</li></ul></div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Your CKA tool progression</div>
<pre>
   Stage 1 — NOW            Stage 2 — Part 3          Stage 3 — EXAM
   ┌──────────────┐        ┌──────────────┐         ┌───────────────┐
   │ 🐣 Minikube   │ ─────▶ │ 🔧 kubeadm    │ ──────▶ │ 🎯 Exam env    │
   │ one command   │        │ full install  │         │ pre-built      │
   │ learn basics  │        │ real skills   │         │ context-switch │
   └──────────────┘        └──────────────┘         └───────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.2 Installing Minikube</h3>
                <p>Minikube spins up a Kubernetes cluster inside a VM or container on your laptop — one command and you have a working control plane + node.</p>
                <h4>Install by OS</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🪟</div><h5>Windows</h5><p><code>choco install minikube</code><br>or <code>winget install minikube</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🍎</div><h5>macOS</h5><p><code>brew install minikube</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐧</div><h5>Linux</h5><p><code>curl -LO …/minikube-linux-amd64</code> → <code>sudo install … /usr/local/bin/minikube</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐳</div><h5>Driver</h5><p>needs Docker / VirtualBox / Hyper-V — <strong>Docker</strong> recommended</p></div>
                </div>
                <h4>Start your first cluster</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">minikube start --driver=docker --cpus=2 --memory=4096   <span class="comment"># single-node, sane defaults</span>
<span class="comment"># 😄  minikube v1.34.0 on Darwin 14.0</span>
<span class="comment"># ✨  Using the docker driver based on user configuration</span>
<span class="comment"># 📦  Preparing Kubernetes v1.34.0 on Docker 26.0.1 ...</span>
<span class="comment"># 🏄  Done! kubectl is now configured to use "minikube" cluster</span>

minikube status
<span class="comment"># host: Running    kubelet: Running    apiserver: Running    kubeconfig: Configured</span>

<span class="comment"># multi-node lab → practise drain/cordon/scheduling (CKA-relevant)</span>
minikube start --nodes=3 --driver=docker --cpus=2 --memory=4096</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why <code>--nodes=3</code></h5><p>A multi-node cluster lets you practise <code>cordon</code>/<code>drain</code>/<code>uncordon</code>, taints, and affinity — all heavily tested. Single-node is fine for the basics in this part.</p></div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Minikube architecture — control plane + worker on ONE node</div>
<pre>
   Your machine (host OS)
   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  Docker / hypervisor                                         │
   │  ┌──────────── Minikube node (VM or container) ───────────┐  │
   │  │ CONTROL PLANE: 📡 apiserver · 🗄️ etcd · 📋 scheduler     │  │
   │  │                🔄 controller-manager · 🧭 CoreDNS         │  │
   │  │ WORKER:        🤖 kubelet · 🌐 kube-proxy · 🐋 containerd │  │
   │  │ 📦 Pod   📦 Pod   📦 Pod    ← your easypj workloads       │  │
   │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
   │  kubectl (laptop) ──HTTPS──▶ apiserver :8443                 │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   💡 Minikube runs BOTH halves on one node; real clusters split them.
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🔧 Troubleshooting common Minikube issues</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>DRV_NOT_DETECTED</code></td><td>Docker/Hyper-V not running</td><td>start Docker Desktop, retry</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>insufficient memory</code></td><td>too much RAM requested</td><td><code>minikube start --memory=2048</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Unable to connect to the server</code></td><td>cluster crashed / IP changed</td><td><code>minikube delete &amp;&amp; minikube start</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>apiserver not responding</code></td><td>stale state</td><td><code>minikube stop &amp;&amp; minikube start</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>slow first start (&gt;5 min)</td><td>first-time image pull (~1 GB)</td><td>normal — wait it out</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">minikube lifecycle</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">minikube start | stop | delete   <span class="comment"># create · stop (keep state) · destroy</span>
minikube pause | unpause         <span class="comment"># free CPU but keep state</span>
minikube ssh                     <span class="comment"># shell into the node</span>
minikube ip                      <span class="comment"># node IP</span>
minikube logs                    <span class="comment"># system logs for debugging</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.3 Installing kubectl</h3>
                <p><code>kubectl</code> is the CLI for <em>any</em> cluster — local or production. It's your cluster's remote control, and it reads <code>~/.kube/config</code> to know where to point and who you are.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">install kubectl (per OS)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">choco install kubernetes-cli            <span class="comment"># Windows</span>
brew install kubectl                     <span class="comment"># macOS</span>
<span class="comment"># Linux — fetch the stable build, then install it onto PATH</span>
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
kubectl version --client                <span class="comment"># confirm it's installed</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">How kubectl reaches your cluster</div>
<pre>
   👤 YOU                          ~/.kube/config tells kubectl        ☸️ CLUSTER
   ┌──────────────┐               WHERE + WHO                         ┌──────────────┐
   │ $ kubectl    │  1 read kubeconfig → 2 find server URL            │ 📡 apiserver  │
   │   get pods   │  3 authenticate (cert/token) → 4 REST over TLS ──▶│   :6443       │
   │ ┌──────────┐ │                                                   │      │        │
   │ │ kubectl  │ │ ◀── 6 format &amp; display ── 5 JSON response ───────│      ▼        │
   │ └──────────┘ │                                                   │ 🗄️ etcd       │
   └──────────────┘                                                   └──────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>⚡ Set up aliases &amp; completion FIRST (exam speed)</h4>
                <p>Do this as the very first thing in any practice or exam shell — it pays for itself within minutes:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~/.bashrc</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ── the aliases that save real minutes (set these FIRST on the exam) ──</span>
alias k=kubectl
alias kn='kubectl config set-context --current --namespace'   <span class="comment"># kn easypj-dev → switch ns</span>
alias kgp='kubectl get pods'
alias kgn='kubectl get nodes'
alias kga='kubectl get all'
alias kd='kubectl describe'
alias kaf='kubectl apply -f'
<span class="comment"># ── env-var shortcuts you'll type constantly ──</span>
export do='--dry-run=client -o yaml'    <span class="comment"># $do = generate a manifest, don't create</span>
export now='--force --grace-period=0'   <span class="comment"># $now = delete immediately</span>
<span class="comment"># ── completion (and make it work for the `k` alias) ──</span>
source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)
complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k
<span class="comment"># verify:  k run nginx --image=nginx $do &gt; pod.yaml   ← should print YAML, not create</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🔌 kubectl plugins worth knowing (via <code>krew</code> — not on the exam)</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>ctx</strong> (kubectx)</td><td>fast interactive context switching</td><td><code>kubectl ctx dev</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ns</strong> (kubens)</td><td>fast namespace switching</td><td><code>kubectl ns easypj-dev</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>neat</strong></td><td>strips clutter from <code>-o yaml</code></td><td><code>kubectl get pod web -o yaml | kubectl neat</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>tree</strong></td><td>shows ownership hierarchy</td><td><code>kubectl tree deploy easypj-web</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.4 Understanding kubeconfig</h3>
                <p>The <code>~/.kube/config</code> file is the single most important file for <code>kubectl</code>: it says <strong>where</strong> to connect (cluster), <strong>who</strong> you are (user), and <strong>which namespace</strong> is the default — bundled into a <strong>context</strong>.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubeconfig — three building blocks → one context</div>
<pre>
   ~/.kube/config
   ├─ clusters:  WHERE   minikube · easypj-staging · easypj-prod   (server URL + CA)
   ├─ users:     WHO     minikube-user (cert) · dev-user (token) · admin-user (cert)
   └─ contexts:  COMBO   minikube = minikube + minikube-user
                         dev      = easypj-staging + dev-user  + ns:easypj-dev
                         prod     = easypj-prod    + admin-user + ns:easypj-prod
   current-context: minikube     ◀── kubectl acts HERE

         🖥️ Cluster (WHERE)  +  👤 User (WHO)  +  📂 Namespace (SCOPE)  =  📋 Context
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~/.kube/config (trimmed)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
current-context: minikube          <span class="comment"># ← the active context</span>
clusters:
- cluster:
    certificate-authority: ~/.minikube/ca.crt
    server: https://192.168.49.2:8443   <span class="comment"># WHERE</span>
  name: minikube
users:
- name: minikube-user                    <span class="comment"># WHO</span>
  user:
    client-certificate: ~/.minikube/profiles/minikube/client.crt
    client-key: ~/.minikube/profiles/minikube/client.key
contexts:
- context:
    cluster: minikube
    user: minikube-user
    namespace: default                   <span class="comment"># default ns for this context</span>
  name: minikube</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Always check your context first</h5><p>Every exam question names a context. Acting in the wrong one = right work on the wrong cluster = <strong>zero points</strong>. Run <code>kubectl config current-context</code> before you answer, every time.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.5 Managing Clusters &amp; Contexts</h3>
                <p>In real life — and on the exam — you juggle several clusters. These four commands are your lifeline; the first one is the opening move of nearly every task.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">context management</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List every context (★ = current)</span>
kubectl config get-contexts
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME       CLUSTER    AUTHINFO        NAMESPACE</span>
<span class="comment"># *         minikube   minikube   minikube-user   default</span>
<span class="comment">#           dev        staging    dev-user        easypj-dev</span>
<span class="comment">#           prod       prod       admin-user      easypj-prod</span>

<span class="comment"># Which context am I on right now?</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># minikube</span>

<span class="comment"># SWITCH context — the opening move of nearly every exam task</span>
kubectl config use-context dev
<span class="comment"># Switched to context "dev".</span>

<span class="comment"># Set the default namespace for the CURRENT context (stop typing -n)</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># Context "dev" modified.</span>

<span class="comment"># Create a brand-new context from existing cluster + user</span>
kubectl config set-context easypj-staging \
  --cluster=staging --user=dev-user --namespace=easypj-staging

<span class="comment"># Merge several kubeconfig files into one merged view</span>
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/.kube/cfg-staging:~/.kube/cfg-prod
kubectl config get-contexts        <span class="comment"># now lists all merged contexts</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
   📋 read the question  ─▶  🔄 use-context  ─▶  ✅ current-context (verify)  ─▶  ⌨️ answer
        the 4-beat rhythm of EVERY exam task — never skip the verify step
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Practise the exact exam workflow</h5><p>Make 3 throwaway contexts on Minikube, then drill "switch → verify → act" until it's reflex:</p></div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">context drill (all point at minikube)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Setup — 3 throwaway contexts, all pointing at the one minikube cluster</span>
kubectl config set-context cka1 --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=default
kubectl config set-context cka2 --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=kube-system
kubectl config set-context cka3 --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># ── Simulated exam Q1: "Use context cka1. List pods in default." ──</span>
kubectl config use-context cka1
kubectl config current-context        <span class="comment"># verify → cka1</span>
kubectl get pods

<span class="comment"># ── Simulated exam Q2: "Use context cka2. Find the kube-proxy pods." ──</span>
kubectl config use-context cka2
kubectl config current-context        <span class="comment"># verify → cka2</span>
kubectl get pods | grep kube-proxy

<span class="comment"># ── Simulated exam Q3: "Use context cka3. Scale deploy easypj to 3." ──</span>
kubectl config use-context cka3
kubectl config current-context        <span class="comment"># verify → cka3</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># Cleanup the practice contexts</span>
kubectl config delete-context cka1
kubectl config delete-context cka2
kubectl config delete-context cka3
kubectl config use-context minikube</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.6 Verifying Your Cluster</h3>
                <p>After setup — and on every fresh exam cluster — orient yourself with these five checks before doing anything else.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">orient yourself on any cluster</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Cluster info — where is the API server &amp; CoreDNS?</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://192.168.49.2:8443</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS is running at https://192.168.49.2:8443/api/v1/.../kube-dns:dns</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Nodes — all Ready? (status, version, IP, OS)</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION   INTERNAL-IP    OS-IMAGE</span>
<span class="comment"># minikube   Ready    control-plane   5m    v1.34.0   192.168.49.2   Ubuntu 22.04</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Control-plane pods — every one Running?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-7db6d8ff4d-xxxxx           1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-minikube                      1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-minikube            1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-minikube   1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-xxxxx                   1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-minikube            1/1     Running   0          5m</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. API health — the apiserver's own view (each check → ok)</span>
kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'
kubectl get --raw=/healthz           <span class="comment"># → ok</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Namespaces — the 4 built-ins, plus any app namespace</span>
kubectl get namespaces
<span class="comment"># NAME              STATUS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># default           Active   5m</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-node-lease   Active   5m</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-public       Active   5m</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system       Active   5m</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>cluster-info</h5><p>API server URL reachable</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>get nodes</h5><p>every node STATUS: Ready</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>kube-system</h5><p>control-plane pods Running</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>readyz</h5><p>API returns ok</p></div>
                </div>
                <h4>🔍 Deeper check — does in-cluster DNS actually resolve?</h4>
                <p>Nodes <code>Ready</code> and pods <code>Running</code> still doesn't prove the cluster <em>works</em> — the first thing every real workload needs is name resolution. A throwaway pod is the fastest proof that CoreDNS + the CNI are healthy:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">DNS smoke test</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it --restart=Never -- \
  nslookup kubernetes.default        <span class="comment"># → resolves to the API ClusterIP (10.96.0.1)</span>

<span class="comment"># the all-in-one readiness one-liner you can paste on any fresh cluster:</span>
kubectl get nodes &amp;&amp; kubectl get pods -n kube-system &amp;&amp; kubectl top nodes</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Why a DNS test is the highest-value 10-second check</h5><p>If <code>nslookup kubernetes.default</code> fails, <strong>CoreDNS or the CNI is broken</strong> — and then <em>every</em> Service name in the cluster fails to resolve, even though nodes look Ready. It catches a whole class of "the cluster is up but nothing can talk" failures that <code>get nodes</code> alone hides (full troubleshooting in Ch 19).</p></div>

                <h4>🚨 Node shows <code>NotReady</code>? Diagnose <em>and fix</em> fast</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Check</th><th>Common cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Node <code>NotReady</code></td><td><code>kubectl describe node &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>kubelet down · CNI not installed</td><td><code>systemctl enable --now kubelet</code> · apply a CNI manifest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pods stuck <code>Pending</code></td><td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;n&gt;</code></td><td>no schedulable node · resources</td><td>free CPU/mem · <code>uncordon</code> / remove a blocking taint</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>kube-system <code>CrashLoop</code></td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system &lt;p&gt;</code></td><td>etcd disk full · bad config</td><td>free etcd disk · fix the static-pod manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>API unreachable</td><td><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> (on node)</td><td>kubelet service not running</td><td><code>sudo systemctl restart kubelet</code>; check <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 5.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s5-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">5.7 Enabling Essential Add-ons</h3>
                <p>A fresh cluster can schedule pods but is missing pieces you'll assume are there (Ch 1 §1.7). Minikube ships them as toggleable add-ons:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Add-on</th><th>Enables</th><th>CKA?</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>metrics-server</strong></td><td><code>kubectl top</code> + HPA</td><td>✅</td><td><code>minikube addons enable metrics-server</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ingress</strong></td><td>NGINX ingress controller</td><td>✅</td><td><code>minikube addons enable ingress</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>storage-provisioner</strong></td><td>dynamic PVCs</td><td>✅</td><td>on by default</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>dashboard</strong></td><td>web UI</td><td>⚠️ not tested</td><td><code>minikube addons enable dashboard</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">enable &amp; verify add-ons</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">minikube addons enable metrics-server
minikube addons enable ingress
minikube addons list | grep enabled        <span class="comment"># confirm what's on</span>
<span class="comment"># | ingress             | minikube | enabled ✅ |</span>
<span class="comment"># | metrics-server      | minikube | enabled ✅ |</span>
<span class="comment"># | storage-provisioner | minikube | enabled ✅ |</span>

kubectl top nodes                          <span class="comment"># wait ~60s after enabling metrics-server</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># minikube   250m         12%    1024Mi          25%</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>📋 One-shot environment readiness check</h4>
                <p>Paste this on any fresh cluster to verify the whole stack at once — kubectl, API reachability, nodes, control-plane pods, DNS, and metrics:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">readiness-check.sh</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
echo "── CKA environment readiness check ──"
echo -n "kubectl : "; kubectl version --client -o yaml &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;&amp; echo OK || echo "❌ not installed"
echo -n "cluster : "; kubectl cluster-info 2&gt;/dev/null | head -1 || echo "❌ unreachable"
echo    "nodes   :"; kubectl get nodes -o wide 2&gt;/dev/null || echo "  ❌ cannot list nodes"
echo    "control plane:"
kubectl get pods -n kube-system \
  -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,STATUS:.status.phase 2&gt;/dev/null \
  | grep -E "etcd|apiserver|scheduler|controller"
echo -n "CoreDNS : "; kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns --no-headers 2&gt;/dev/null | awk '{print $3}'
echo -n "metrics : "; kubectl top nodes 2&gt;/dev/null | head -1 || echo "⚠️ enable metrics-server"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Minikube convenience ≠ real cluster</h5><p>Two timing/default traps when you graduate to kubeadm (Part 3): <strong>(1)</strong> <code>metrics-server</code> needs <strong>~60 s</strong> after enabling before <code>kubectl top</code> works — it has to scrape a window of metrics first, so an immediate <code>top</code> errors with "metrics not available." <strong>(2)</strong> <code>storage-provisioner</code> is <em>on by default in Minikube</em> but <strong>absent on kubeadm</strong> — there a <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code> stays <code>Pending</code> forever until you install a provisioner/StorageClass yourself (Ch 22). The convenience that makes Minikube great for learning is exactly what the exam's bare clusters won't give you.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>✅ Chapter 5 done — your environment is ready</h5><p>You have a working cluster, kubectl with completion + aliases, contexts under control, and the add-ons enabled. A one-line readiness check: <code>kubectl version --client &amp;&amp; kubectl get nodes &amp;&amp; kubectl get pods -n kube-system &amp;&amp; kubectl top nodes</code>. Next (Ch 6): the kubectl fluency that multiplies your exam score.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how kubectl finds, merges &amp; authenticates with a cluster</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>"kubectl just connects" hides a precise resolution order. Knowing it turns "why am I on the wrong cluster?" into a two-second fix.</p>
                    <h5>① Which kubeconfig wins — strict precedence</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   1. --kubeconfig=FILE       ← explicit flag (highest priority)
   2. $KUBECONFIG=a:b:c       ← colon-separated list → MERGED (first file wins a clash)
   3. ~/.kube/config         ← the default
   chosen file → read clusters/users/contexts → act on current-context (or --context=X)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Merging:</strong> with <code>$KUBECONFIG=a:b:c</code> every context is visible at once and you switch with <code>use-context</code> — no single giant file needed. On a name clash the <em>first</em> file wins.</div>
                    <h5>② Identity &amp; auth tricks worth knowing</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>exec credential plugin</strong></td><td>kubeconfig calls an external binary for a short-lived token — how cloud auth works (<code>aws eks get-token</code>, <code>gke-gcloud-auth-plugin</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>impersonation</strong></td><td><code>--as=jane --as-group=dev</code> acts as another identity (needs RBAC) — the fastest way to test "can this user do X?"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>insecure-skip-tls-verify</code></td><td>skips the API server cert check — convenient in labs, a real risk in prod</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectx / kubens</strong></td><td>community tools for instant context/namespace switching (krew) — handy off-exam</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <h5>③ How kubectl actually authenticates — the mutual-TLS handshake</h5>
                    <p>Connecting isn't just "open a socket." kubectl and the apiserver run a <strong>mutual TLS</strong> handshake: the client verifies the server <em>and</em> the server verifies the client — and the client's certificate <em>is</em> its identity.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl (client)                                   📡 apiserver :6443
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ① TCP + ClientHello ────────────────────────────▶
   ② ◀─ server Certificate (CN=kube-apiserver,          client verifies it against
        SANs: kubernetes · kubernetes.default ·         certificate-authority(-data) from
        10.96.0.1 · the node IP)                        kubeconfig ✅ (mismatch → conn refused)
   ③ client Certificate ───────────────────────────▶   apiserver verifies it was
        (CN=kubernetes-admin, O=system:masters)          signed by the cluster CA ✅
   ④ encrypted session ──── kubectl get pods ───────▶   authN done → authZ (RBAC) → respond
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   🔑 the cert's  CN → your USERNAME  ·  O → your GROUP(s)
      RBAC RoleBindings then grant that username/group its permissions (Ch 24)
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Auth method</th><th>Identity comes from</th><th>Used by</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>TLS client certificate</strong></td><td>cert <code>CN</code> → username · <code>O</code> → group</td><td>kubeadm admin (<code>kubernetes-admin</code> / <code>system:masters</code>), control-plane components</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Bearer token (JWT)</strong></td><td>token subject &amp; claims</td><td>ServiceAccounts (pods), OIDC users, CI, managed clusters</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The insight cka_1 only hints at:</strong> Kubernetes has <em>no</em> "User" objects — your identity is whatever the apiserver <em>extracts</em> from your cert or token at handshake time. That's why "creating a user" (Ch 26) really means <em>issuing a client cert with the right <code>CN</code>/<code>O</code></em>, and why <code>insecure-skip-tls-verify: true</code> is dangerous — it disables step ②, so a spoofed apiserver would be trusted blindly.</div>

                    <h5>④ Two facts that make it all click: switching context is local, auth is per-request</h5>
                    <p>Two mechanics trip up almost everyone — and the moment they click, kubeconfig stops feeling like magic:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   SWITCHING CONTEXT = editing ONE local field — zero network, nothing in the cluster changes
     kubectl config use-context prod
        └─ just sets  current-context: prod  in ~/.kube/config
           no connection is opened; the NEXT command simply reads the new cluster + user + namespace

   EVERY API CALL re-authenticates from scratch — HTTP is stateless, there is NO login/session
     kubectl get pods   → TLS handshake → present cert/token → authN → authZ → response
     kubectl get svc    → TLS handshake → present cert/token → authN → authZ → response   (all over again)
        no cookie, no "logged-in" state — each request independently carries and proves its identity
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters:</strong> because there is no session, a rotated or expired credential breaks the <em>very next</em> command (not "after you log out") — a classic "it worked five minutes ago" exam panic. And because switching context is purely local, you flip between the exam's ~6 clusters instantly with zero latency. An <em>exec credential plugin</em> (cloud auth) is also invoked per request, but it caches the token until expiry — which is why the first call after a token expires pauses briefly while it refreshes, then is fast again.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Minikube to learn, kubeadm to pass</strong> — the exam builds clusters with kubeadm (Part 3).</li>
                    <li><strong>kubeconfig = clusters (where) + users (who) + contexts (the combo)</strong>; <code>current-context</code> is active.</li>
                    <li><strong>Switch context FIRST, verify, then act</strong> — the 4-beat rhythm of every exam task.</li>
                    <li><strong>Set a default namespace</strong> with <code>set-context --current --namespace=…</code> so you stop typing <code>-n</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify any cluster</strong> with <code>cluster-info</code> · <code>get nodes</code> · <code>get pods -n kube-system</code>; add-ons unlock <code>top</code>/ingress.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Wrong context = 0 marks.</strong> Every question names one — <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code> before you touch anything.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgotten namespace.</strong> Miss <code>-n</code> (or a default ns) and your object lands in <code>default</code> — graded as missing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Completion not loaded.</strong> If <code>kubectl &lt;TAB&gt;</code> is dead: <code>source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)</code> (+ the <code>complete … k</code> line for your alias).</li>
                    <li><strong>Minikube ≠ kubeadm.</strong> The exam's kubeadm clusters have real systemd kubelets, real certs, and real multi-node topology — practise on kubeadm too.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>First three lines of any shell:</strong> <code>alias k=kubectl</code> · <code>export do='--dry-run=client -o yaml'</code> · <code>source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>One-key namespace:</strong> <code>alias kns='kubectl config set-context --current --namespace'</code> → <code>kns easypj-dev</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Open every task</strong> with <code>use-context</code> then <code>current-context</code> — muscle memory beats memory.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 5)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">environment &amp; context essentials</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">minikube start --nodes=3 --driver=docker     <span class="comment"># a multi-node practice cluster</span>
minikube addons enable metrics-server ingress
kubectl config get-contexts | current-context | use-context &lt;name&gt;
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev
kubectl cluster-info ; kubectl get nodes -o wide ; kubectl get pods -n kube-system
kubectl top nodes                            <span class="comment"># needs metrics-server</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Own your contexts in under 3 minutes</h5>
                <p>Do it before peeking:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>From Minikube, create two contexts <code>dev</code> and <code>ops</code> (both → minikube), with namespaces <code>easypj-dev</code> and <code>kube-system</code>.</li>
                    <li>Switch to <code>dev</code>, <strong>verify</strong> it's current, and confirm <code>kubectl get pods</code> defaults to <code>easypj-dev</code>.</li>
                    <li>Switch to <code>ops</code> and list the control-plane pods without typing <code>-n</code>.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config set-context dev --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=easypj-dev
kubectl config set-context ops --cluster=minikube --user=minikube --namespace=kube-system
kubectl config use-context dev &amp;&amp; kubectl config current-context   <span class="comment"># → dev</span>
kubectl get pods                          <span class="comment"># scoped to easypj-dev</span>
kubectl config use-context ops
kubectl get pods                          <span class="comment"># now lists kube-system control-plane pods</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Why:</strong> the namespace is baked into the context, so switching context also switches your default scope — exactly the exam workflow.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 5 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of environment &amp; context skills — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the three sections of a kubeconfig and what each holds.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table>
                            <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr><td><code>clusters</code></td><td>server URL + CA cert</td><td>WHERE to connect</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>users</code></td><td>client cert/key or token</td><td>WHO you are</td></tr>
                                <tr><td><code>contexts</code></td><td>cluster + user + namespace</td><td>the COMBO you switch to</td></tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>current-context</code> names the active one; <code>kubectl config view</code> prints the whole file.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Switch to the context named <code>prod</code> (command).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">switch context</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config use-context prod
kubectl config current-context   <span class="comment"># verify → prod</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is the first action of nearly every exam task; always verify after switching.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set the current context's default namespace to <code>easypj</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">set namespace</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--current</code> edits the active context, so you stop appending <code>-n easypj</code> to every command.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Use two kubeconfig files in one kubectl session.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list contexts</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/.kube/cfg-prod
kubectl config get-contexts            <span class="comment"># all merged contexts now visible</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Colon-separated list (semicolon on Windows). On a name clash, the <em>first</em> file wins.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Verify a freshly-built cluster is healthy (three commands).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">cluster health</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl cluster-info             <span class="comment"># API reachable?</span>
kubectl get nodes                <span class="comment"># all Ready?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system  <span class="comment"># control plane Running?</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Same three you run to orient yourself on any unfamiliar exam cluster; add <code>get --raw='/readyz?verbose'</code> for the API's own view.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What <em>exactly</em> is a "context"?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A named triple — <strong>cluster + user + namespace</strong> — that tells kubectl where to connect, who to be, and the default scope. Switching context switches all three at once.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>It's pure client-side config; switching context changes nothing in the cluster, only which cluster/identity your kubectl uses.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable metrics-server and prove <code>kubectl top</code> works.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">enable addon</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">minikube addons enable metrics-server
sleep 60                          <span class="comment"># give it time to collect</span>
kubectl top nodes                 <span class="comment"># CPU/memory per node</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>kubectl top</code> and the HorizontalPodAutoscaler both depend on metrics-server; it's not installed by default.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Start a 3-node local cluster (command).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">start cluster</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">minikube start --nodes=3 --driver=docker --cpus=2 --memory=4096</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Multiple nodes unlock realistic practice for drain/cordon, taints, and affinity.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Print the current context in one command.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify context</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config current-context</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Run it reflexively before answering — it's the cheapest insurance against working on the wrong cluster.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">KUBECONFIG precedence — flag vs env var vs <code>~/.kube/config</code>: which wins?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary>
                        <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>1.</strong> <code>--kubeconfig=FILE</code> (flag) → <strong>2.</strong> <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> (env, merged list) → <strong>3.</strong> <code>~/.kube/config</code> (default). Highest present wins.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>So a stray <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> in your shell silently overrides the default file — a classic "why can't I see my cluster?" gotcha.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch5-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 5 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style "do it / fix it" tasks with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You've just started the CKA exam and need to connect to the cluster named <code>cluster-prod</code>. The exam question specifies namespace <code>finance</code>. Write the exact commands to switch context and set the default namespace.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">switch to the correct cluster context</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Switch to the correct cluster context</span>
kubectl config use-context cluster-prod

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Set the default namespace for this context</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=finance

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl config current-context    <span class="comment"># Should show: cluster-prod</span>
kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace  <span class="comment"># Should show: finance</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is the <strong>FIRST thing you do for every CKA question</strong>. The exam provides ~6 different cluster contexts. If you forget to switch, you'll create resources on the wrong cluster and get zero marks. The <code>--current</code> flag modifies the currently active context. Setting the namespace means you don't have to type <code>-n finance</code> on every subsequent command. Always verify with <code>kubectl config current-context</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain the structure of a <code>kubeconfig</code> file. What are the three main sections and how do they relate to each other?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>A kubeconfig file has three main sections:</p>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Section</th>
                                            <th>Contains</th>
                                            <th>Purpose</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>clusters</code></td>
                                            <td>Server URL + CA certificate</td>
                                            <td>Where to connect</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>users</code></td>
                                            <td>Client cert + key, or token</td>
                                            <td>Who you are</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>contexts</code></td>
                                            <td>cluster + user + namespace</td>
                                            <td>Links cluster to user</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">view kubeconfig</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View kubeconfig</span>
kubectl config view

<span class="comment"># Default location</span>
cat ~/.kube/config

<span class="comment"># Use a custom kubeconfig</span>
kubectl --kubeconfig=/path/to/config get pods
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
export KUBECONFIG=/path/to/config</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>A <strong>context</strong> is a triple: (cluster, user, namespace). This is how kubectl knows which cluster to talk to, which credentials to present, and which namespace to use by default. The CKA expects you to understand this structure because you'll work with multiple contexts. You can merge multiple kubeconfig files: <code>export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:/path/to/other-config</code>. The default kubeconfig is at <code>~/.kube/config</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Your cluster shows 3 nodes when you run <code>kubectl get nodes</code>, but one node shows <code>NotReady</code>. You need to SSH to that node and diagnose the issue. What are the first three things you check?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">from control plane: identify the notready node</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From control plane: identify the NotReady node</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
kubectl describe node &lt;node-name&gt; | grep -A 10 Conditions

<span class="comment"># SSH to the node</span>
ssh &lt;node-name&gt;

<span class="comment"># Check 1: Is kubelet running?</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># If not: sudo systemctl start kubelet && sudo systemctl enable kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 2: Are there kubelet errors?</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -30

<span class="comment"># Check 3: Is the container runtime running?</span>
sudo systemctl status containerd
<span class="comment"># If not: sudo systemctl start containerd</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>A node shows <code>NotReady</code> when its kubelet hasn't sent a heartbeat to the API server within the timeout (default: 40 seconds). The three most common causes: (1) <strong>kubelet stopped</strong> — start it with systemctl, (2) <strong>kubelet config error</strong> — wrong certificate paths, wrong API server address in kubelet config, (3) <strong>container runtime down</strong> — containerd crashed or is not installed. On the CKA, always check <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> — the logs tell you exactly what's wrong.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>Minikube</code>, <code>Kind</code>, <code>k3s</code>, and <code>kubeadm</code>? Which one does the CKA exam use and why does that matter for your practice?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Tool</th>
                                            <th>Nodes</th>
                                            <th>Best For</th>
                                            <th>CKA?</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Minikube</td>
                                            <td>Single-node VM</td>
                                            <td>Local dev, learning basics</td>
                                            <td>❌</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Kind</td>
                                            <td>Docker containers</td>
                                            <td>CI/CD testing, multi-node</td>
                                            <td>❌</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>k3s</td>
                                            <td>Lightweight VMs</td>
                                            <td>Edge, IoT, resource-constrained</td>
                                            <td>❌</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>kubeadm</strong></td>
                                            <td>Real multi-node</td>
                                            <td>Production clusters</td>
                                            <td><strong>✅ YES</strong></td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA exam uses <strong>kubeadm-bootstrapped clusters</strong> with real VMs. This matters because kubeadm clusters have: (1) Real systemd services (kubelet), (2) Real TLS certificates in <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code>, (3) Static pods in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, (4) Real multi-node networking. Minikube hides all of this complexity. If you only practice on Minikube, you won't be prepared for node-level troubleshooting. Use <code>kubeadm</code> with Vagrant or cloud VMs for realistic practice.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Set up the essential bash aliases and environment variables for maximum CKA exam speed. Write the commands you would run at the start of the exam.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">essential aliases (add to ~/.bashrc)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Essential aliases (add to ~/.bashrc)</span>
alias k=kubectl
alias kn='kubectl config set-context --current --namespace'
alias kgp='kubectl get pods'
alias kgn='kubectl get nodes'
alias kga='kubectl get all'
alias kd='kubectl describe'
alias kaf='kubectl apply -f'

<span class="comment"># Environment variables for speed</span>
export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"
export now="--force --grace-period=0"

<span class="comment"># Enable autocompletion for alias</span>
complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k

<span class="comment"># Verify setup</span>
k get nodes        <span class="comment"># Should work like kubectl get nodes</span>
k run test --image=nginx $do   <span class="comment"># Should output YAML</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA exam usually pre-configures <code>alias k=kubectl</code> and bash completion, but <strong>always verify</strong>. The <code>$do</code> variable saves massive time: <code>k run nginx --image=nginx $do > pod.yaml</code> generates YAML without creating the resource. The <code>$now</code> variable force-deletes pods instantly: <code>k delete pod stuck-pod $now</code>. These shortcuts can save 5-10 minutes over a 2-hour exam. Practice using them so they're muscle memory on exam day.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You have two kubeconfig files: <code>/tmp/admin.conf</code> (for cluster-A) and <code>~/.kube/config</code> (for cluster-B). How do you merge them and switch between clusters?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">merge via kubeconfig env variable</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option 1: Merge via KUBECONFIG env variable</span>
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:/tmp/admin.conf

<span class="comment"># View merged config — both clusters visible</span>
kubectl config get-contexts

<span class="comment"># Switch between clusters</span>
kubectl config use-context cluster-A
kubectl config use-context cluster-B

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Permanently merge into one file</span>
KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:/tmp/admin.conf kubectl config view --flatten > ~/.kube/config-merged
mv ~/.kube/config-merged ~/.kube/config

<span class="comment"># Option 3: Use --kubeconfig for one-off commands</span>
kubectl --kubeconfig=/tmp/admin.conf get nodes</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA exam, the kubeconfig is pre-configured with all exam contexts, but you may need to work with custom kubeconfig files in troubleshooting questions. The <code>KUBECONFIG</code> environment variable accepts a colon-separated list of files (semicolon on Windows). kubectl merges them at runtime. The <code>--flatten</code> flag resolves all relative paths and embedded certificates into a single self-contained file. Always back up before merging: <code>cp ~/.kube/config ~/.kube/config.bak</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">After setting up a local Kubernetes cluster, you run <code>kubectl get nodes</code> and the output shows <code>STATUS: NotReady</code> for all nodes. What is the most likely cause for a brand new cluster?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The most likely cause is that a <strong>CNI (Container Network Interface) plugin is not installed</strong>.</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify: check node conditions</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify: check node conditions</span>
kubectl describe node &lt;node-name&gt; | grep -i "ready\|network"
<span class="comment"># Look for: "NetworkPluginNotReady message:Network plugin returns error: cni plugin not initialized"</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: install a CNI plugin (e.g., Calico)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Or Flannel</span>
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flannel-io/flannel/master/Documentation/kube-flannel.yml

<span class="comment"># Wait for pods to come up</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E "calico|flannel"

<span class="comment"># Check nodes again</span>
kubectl get nodes   <span class="comment"># Should now show Ready</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>After <code>kubeadm init</code>, the cluster is initialized but nodes remain <code>NotReady</code> until a CNI plugin is installed. The CNI plugin is responsible for pod-to-pod networking. Without it, the kubelet reports the node as not ready because the network subsystem isn't functional. On the CKA, you may need to install a CNI plugin as part of a cluster setup question. Common choices: Calico (most feature-rich), Flannel (simplest), Cilium (eBPF-based). The pod network CIDR must match what you specified in <code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What Minikube addons are useful for CKA practice? Enable the <code>metrics-server</code>, <code>ingress</code>, and <code>dashboard</code> addons and explain what each provides.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">enable addons</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Enable addons</span>
minikube addons enable metrics-server
minikube addons enable ingress
minikube addons enable dashboard

<span class="comment"># Verify addons</span>
minikube addons list | grep enabled

<span class="comment"># What each provides:</span>
<span class="comment"># metrics-server → enables: kubectl top pods, kubectl top nodes</span>
<span class="comment">#   (resource monitoring — needed for HPA and debugging)</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress → NGINX ingress controller</span>
<span class="comment">#   (HTTP routing by hostname/path — CKA networking topic)</span>
<span class="comment"># dashboard → Web UI for cluster visualization</span>
<span class="comment">#   (not used on exam but helpful for learning)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>metrics-server</code> is essential — without it, <code>kubectl top</code> commands fail, and HorizontalPodAutoscalers can't get CPU metrics. On the CKA exam, metrics-server is pre-installed. The <code>ingress</code> addon installs an NGINX Ingress Controller, which is required for Ingress resource questions. Note: on kubeadm clusters (like the exam), you install these as regular manifests/Helm charts, not Minikube addons. Practice both approaches.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that your cluster is healthy and all essential components are running? Write the commands for a complete health check.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes ready?</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. All nodes Ready?</span>
kubectl get nodes

<span class="comment"># 2. Control plane pods running?</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># 3. CoreDNS functioning?</span>
kubectl run dns-check --image=busybox --rm -it --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Should resolve to the API server ClusterIP</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Cluster info</span>
kubectl cluster-info

<span class="comment"># 5. Can you create resources?</span>
kubectl run health-check --image=nginx --restart=Never
kubectl get pod health-check
kubectl delete pod health-check

<span class="comment"># 6. Metrics working? (if metrics-server installed)</span>
kubectl top nodes
kubectl top pods -A</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Perform this health check after every cluster setup or modification. On the CKA, if something seems broken, start here. The DNS check is particularly important — many services depend on DNS resolution. If <code>nslookup kubernetes.default</code> fails from inside a pod, check: (1) CoreDNS pods in kube-system, (2) kube-dns Service exists, (3) kube-proxy is running (services need iptables rules). A quick <code>kubectl run</code> + <code>kubectl delete</code> verifies the entire pipeline: API server → scheduler → kubelet → runtime.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">On the CKA exam, you switch to a context but realize you're working in the <code>default</code> namespace instead of <code>kube-system</code>. You've already created a pod in the wrong namespace. How do you fix this?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">delete the pod from the wrong namespace</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Delete the pod from the wrong namespace</span>
kubectl delete pod my-pod -n default

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Set the correct namespace</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=kube-system

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Recreate the pod in the correct namespace</span>
kubectl run my-pod --image=nginx -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># OR if using your saved YAML:</span>
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Verify it's in the right namespace</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Pro tip: you can also move resources by exporting and re-creating</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -n default -o yaml | \
  sed 's/namespace: default/namespace: kube-system/' | \
  kubectl apply -f -
kubectl delete pod my-pod -n default</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>You <strong>cannot move</strong> a pod between namespaces — you must delete and recreate. This is a common CKA mistake that costs marks. Prevention strategies: (1) Always set the namespace at the start of each question: <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=X</code>. (2) Always verify with <code>kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace</code>. (3) Include <code>namespace:</code> in the metadata section of every YAML manifest. Namespaces are a fundamental isolation boundary — resources in different namespaces are independent.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 5 Visual Summary — one laptop, many clusters, one context at a time</div>
<pre>
   💻 your laptop
      └─ kubectl ──reads──▶ ~/.kube/config   (or a $KUBECONFIG merge)
                             ├─ clusters (WHERE)  ├─ users (WHO)  └─ contexts (the COMBO)
                             current-context ─┐
        ┌─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┐
        ▼                                      ▼                                  ▼
   ☸️ minikube                       ☸️ easypj-staging                  ☸️ easypj-prod
   use-context minikube              use-context dev (ns:easypj-dev)    use-context prod (ns:easypj-prod)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the rhythm:  use-context ▶ verify (current-context) ▶ act.  Switch FIRST — every time.  ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> your laptop's <code>kubectl</code> reads one <strong>kubeconfig</strong> (or a merged set), which holds <em>clusters</em> (where), <em>users</em> (who) and <em>contexts</em> (the combo). The <code>current-context</code> decides which cluster, identity and namespace every command targets — so you point kubectl at a different cluster simply by switching context, no reconnect needed.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> on the exam you'll hop between ~6 clusters; the discipline that saves you is the same three beats every time — <strong>use-context → verify → act</strong>, with a default namespace set so nothing lands in <code>default</code> by accident.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH5-INSERT:: -->
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 6 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch6">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 6 · kubectl — The Administrator's Command Center</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">kubectl is the one tool behind <em>every</em> exam task and every admin day. This chapter drills the command anatomy, the daily verb set, your in-exam manual (<code>explain</code>), the imperative⇄declarative split, output extraction, and live-pod debugging — the fluency that decides whether you finish in time.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⌨️ CLI · SPEED chapter</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 used in 100% of tasks</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The kubectl anatomy — <strong>verb + resource + name + flags</strong> (and the short names)</li>
                    <li>✅ The essential admin commands, organised by intent (get · inspect · create · debug)</li>
                    <li>✅ <code>kubectl explain</code> — your built-in API manual during the exam</li>
                    <li>✅ Imperative vs declarative — when to use each, and the <strong>dry-run YAML generator</strong></li>
                    <li>✅ Output formatting — JSONPath, custom-columns, label &amp; field selectors</li>
                    <li>✅ Debugging live pods — <code>logs</code>, <code>exec</code>, and <strong>ephemeral containers</strong> via <code>kubectl debug</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">All Domains <span class="cka-weight">100%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Speed <span class="cka-weight">PASS/FAIL</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>⚡ kubectl speed = CKA pass/fail</h5>
                <p>2 hours, 15–20 tasks — every second counts. Candidates who type full resource names run out of time; candidates who live in <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> finish early. This is the single highest-leverage chapter for the exam clock.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Alias</strong> <code>k=kubectl</code> — saves ~8 chars on every command.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl explain</code></strong> — faster than opening the docs tab.</li>
                    <li><strong>Imperative + dry-run</strong> — scaffold a manifest in seconds, then edit.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 6 Roadmap — from one command to total fluency</div>
<pre>
   6.1 SYNTAX ──▶ 6.2 COMMANDS ──▶ 6.3 explain ──▶ 6.4 imperative ⇄ declarative
   verb·resource    by intent          in-exam manual    create fast ⇄ apply -f
   ·name·flags      get/desc/create…   (any field's YAML) (+ dry-run = YAML generator)
        └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘
                                        ▼
   6.5 OUTPUT ──────────▶ 6.6 DEBUG
   jsonpath · columns ·    logs · exec · ephemeral containers (kubectl debug)
   label/field selectors
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s6-1"><strong>6.1</strong> Syntax</a></td><td>How is any command built?</td><td>verb + resource + name + flags — learn it once, build anything.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s6-2"><strong>6.2</strong> Commands</a></td><td>What do I run daily?</td><td>get · describe · create · edit · delete · logs · exec — by intent.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s6-3"><strong>6.3</strong> explain</a></td><td>What's the YAML for this field?</td><td><code>kubectl explain</code> is the manual you're allowed to "memorise."</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s6-4"><strong>6.4</strong> Imperative⇄Declarative</a></td><td>Type a command or write YAML?</td><td>generate with <code>$do</code>, then <code>apply -f</code> — the exam power move.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s6-5"><strong>6.5</strong> Output</a></td><td>How do I extract one value fast?</td><td>jsonpath / custom-columns / <code>-l</code> / <code>--field-selector</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s6-6"><strong>6.6</strong> Debug</a></td><td>How do I get inside a broken pod?</td><td><code>logs --previous</code>, <code>exec</code>, and <code>kubectl debug</code> for distroless/crashed.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 6.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s6-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.1 The kubectl Syntax Pattern</h3>
                <p>Every command is the same shape. Learn it once and you can construct any command from memory — even ones you've never used.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubectl command anatomy</div>
<pre>
   kubectl    get      pods     easypj-web-x9k     -n easypj-dev   -o wide
   ───────   ─────   ───────   ───────────────   ─────────────   ───────
   binary    VERB    RESOURCE       NAME              FLAGS        (output)

   VERB     get · describe · create · apply · delete · edit · logs · exec ·
            scale · rollout · label · annotate · taint · drain · explain · top
   RESOURCE pods(po) · deploy · svc · no · ns · cm · secret · ing · pv · pvc · sa
   FLAGS    -n ns · -A (all-ns) · -o wide|yaml|json · -l labels · -w (watch) · -f file
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Short names save real seconds</h5><p><code>po</code>=pods · <code>deploy</code>=deployments · <code>svc</code>=services · <code>no</code>=nodes · <code>ns</code>=namespaces · <code>cm</code>=configmaps · <code>ing</code>=ingress · <code>pv</code>/<code>pvc</code> · <code>sa</code>=serviceaccounts · <code>netpol</code>=networkpolicies. See them all with <code>kubectl api-resources</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 6.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.2 Essential Admin Commands — by Intent</h3>
                <p>The commands you'll reach for every day, grouped by what you're trying to do:</p>
                <h4>📋 Get information &amp; 🔍 inspect</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">look around</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get nodes -o wide                       <span class="comment"># IPs, OS, runtime</span>
kubectl get pods -A                             <span class="comment"># all pods, all namespaces</span>
kubectl get pods -o yaml                        <span class="comment"># the full YAML of each pod</span>
kubectl get pods --sort-by=.status.startTime    <span class="comment"># oldest → newest</span>
kubectl get all -n easypj-dev                   <span class="comment"># deploy/rs/pod/svc at a glance</span>
kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -n easypj-dev
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-x9k -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># events, conditions, volumes</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1                  <span class="comment"># capacity, allocated, taints, conditions</span>
kubectl top nodes ; kubectl top pods -n easypj-dev <span class="comment"># needs metrics-server</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>✏️ Create &amp; modify · 🗑️ delete</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">change things</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml ; kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl edit deployment easypj-web -n easypj-dev
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}' -n easypj-dev
kubectl label node worker-1 disk=ssd ; kubectl taint node worker-1 gpu=true:NoSchedule
kubectl delete pod easypj-web-x9k -n easypj-dev
kubectl delete -f deploy.yaml                    <span class="comment"># delete exactly what a manifest created</span>
kubectl delete pods --field-selector status.phase=Failed -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># bulk cleanup</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>🐛 Debug &amp; troubleshoot</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">when something's broken</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl logs easypj-web-x9k -n easypj-dev
kubectl logs easypj-web-x9k --previous -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># last crashed container</span>
kubectl exec -it easypj-web-x9k -n easypj-dev -- /bin/sh
kubectl port-forward pod/easypj-web-x9k 8000:8000 -n easypj-dev
kubectl debug node/worker-1 --image=busybox            <span class="comment"># ephemeral node-debug pod</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>get</h5><p>list resources; <code>-o wide</code> for detail</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div><h5>describe</h5><p>events, conditions, volumes — the deep dive</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>logs</h5><p>container output; <code>--previous</code> for a crash</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐚</div><h5>exec -it</h5><p>shell into a running container</p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>🏷️ Labels &amp; selectors — the glue of Kubernetes</h4>
                <p>Services find pods, Deployments manage pods, and Ingress targets a Service — all by matching <strong>labels</strong>. Master the selector syntax and most "it's not connected" bugs become obvious.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">labels &amp; selectors</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add a label to a pod</span>
kubectl label pod easypj-web-x9k app=easypj -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># Show every pod's labels</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME             READY   STATUS    LABELS</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-x9k   1/1     Running   app=easypj,tier=web,version=v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Filter — equality</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj
kubectl get pods -l 'app=easypj,tier=web'        <span class="comment"># AND (comma)</span>

<span class="comment"># Filter — set-based</span>
kubectl get pods -l 'app in (easypj,nginx)'      <span class="comment"># in the set</span>
kubectl get pods -l 'tier notin (cache)'         <span class="comment"># not in the set</span>
kubectl get pods -l 'environment'                <span class="comment"># HAS the label (any value)</span>
kubectl get pods -l '!environment'               <span class="comment"># does NOT have the label</span>

<span class="comment"># Remove a label (trailing minus) · overwrite an existing one</span>
kubectl label pod easypj-web-x9k version- -n easypj-dev
kubectl label pod easypj-web-x9k version=v2 --overwrite -n easypj-dev</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how labels wire everything together</div>
<pre>
   🚀 Deployment           🔌 Service            🌍 Ingress
   selector.matchLabels    selector              backend → easypj-svc
     app: easypj             app: easypj
         │                     │
         └──────────┬──────────┘   labels are the GLUE
                    ▼
              📦 Pod  labels: { app: easypj, version: v1 }  ◀── must match
   ⚠️ labels don't match → Service has 0 endpoints → app unreachable
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Equality vs set-based selectors — which to use</h5><p><strong>Equality</strong> (<code>app=easypj</code>, <code>tier!=cache</code>) is the everyday case and the <em>only</em> kind a Service's <code>selector:</code> field understands. <strong>Set-based</strong> (<code>in</code>, <code>notin</code>, <code>exists</code>) is richer and used by <code>kubectl -l</code>, plus Deployment/ReplicaSet/NetworkPolicy <code>matchExpressions</code>. Two traps worth memorising: <code>tier notin (cache)</code> <em>also</em> matches pods that have <strong>no</strong> <code>tier</code> label at all; and a plain Service <strong>cannot</strong> use set-based selectors — only controllers/policies can, via <code>matchExpressions</code>.</p></div>

                <h4>⚡ Top 15 CKA must-know commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Task</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>Switch context</td><td><code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td>Set default namespace</td><td><code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td>Generate YAML</td><td><code>kubectl run web --image=easypj:v1 --dry-run=client -o yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td>Create deployment</td><td><code>kubectl create deploy &lt;name&gt; --image=&lt;img&gt; --replicas=&lt;n&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>5</td><td>Expose as service</td><td><code>kubectl expose deploy &lt;name&gt; --port=80 --target-port=8000</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>6</td><td>Scale</td><td><code>kubectl scale deploy &lt;name&gt; --replicas=5</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>7</td><td>Pod logs</td><td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; [-c ctr] [--previous]</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>8</td><td>Shell into pod</td><td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- sh</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>9</td><td>Everything in a namespace</td><td><code>kubectl get all -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>10</td><td>Describe</td><td><code>kubectl describe &lt;res&gt; &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>11</td><td>Find field paths</td><td><code>kubectl explain &lt;res&gt;.spec --recursive</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>12</td><td>Drain a node</td><td><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>13</td><td>Cordon / uncordon</td><td><code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code> · <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>14</td><td>Rollout status / undo</td><td><code>kubectl rollout status|undo deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>15</td><td>Apply a manifest</td><td><code>kubectl apply -f &lt;file.yaml&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 6.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.3 kubectl explain — Your Built-in API Manual</h3>
                <p><code>kubectl explain</code> prints the exact fields for any resource, straight from the API server — usually faster than the docs tab and the perfect cure for "what's that field called again?"</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">explain — drill into any field</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Top-level fields of any resource — shows KIND, VERSION, and the field list</span>
kubectl explain pod
<span class="comment"># KIND:     Pod</span>
<span class="comment"># VERSION:  v1</span>
<span class="comment"># FIELDS:   apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec, status</span>

<span class="comment"># Drill down ONE level at a time — each dotted segment goes deeper</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources.limits

<span class="comment"># --recursive dumps the WHOLE subtree of field names (the fastest field-finder)</span>
kubectl explain deployment.spec.strategy --recursive
<span class="comment"># strategy              &lt;Object&gt;</span>
<span class="comment">#   rollingUpdate       &lt;Object&gt;</span>
<span class="comment">#     maxSurge          &lt;string&gt;</span>
<span class="comment">#     maxUnavailable    &lt;string&gt;</span>
<span class="comment">#   type                &lt;string&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Forgot the exact field name? recurse, then grep for it</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers --recursive | grep -i probe
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.livenessProbe --recursive

<span class="comment"># Pin a specific API version's schema (output differs between versions)</span>
kubectl explain deployment --api-version=apps/v1</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">explain — drill-down navigation</div>
<pre>
   kubectl explain pod
        └─ spec ─▶ kubectl explain pod.spec
                    └─ containers ─▶ kubectl explain pod.spec.containers
                                      ├─ image · ports · env
                                      ├─ resources ─▶ .resources.limits / .requests
                                      ├─ livenessProbe / readinessProbe / startupProbe
                                      └─ volumeMounts
   💡 add --recursive at any level to dump the whole subtree of field names
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 explain + dry-run = speed</h5><p>Forgot the path for resource limits? <code>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources --recursive</code> (2 s), then scaffold with <code>kubectl run web --image=easypj:v1 $do</code> and drop the field in. Faster than any doc search.</p></div>
                <h4>📖 Reading <code>explain</code>'s type annotations — the part most people skip</h4>
                <p>The <code>&lt;…&gt;</code> tag after every field name tells you <em>exactly</em> how to shape it in YAML. Learn these five and you'll stop guessing whether something is a list, a map, or a plain value:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>You see in <code>explain</code></th><th>It means</th><th>So you write…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>&lt;string&gt;</code> · <code>&lt;integer&gt;</code> · <code>&lt;boolean&gt;</code></td><td>a leaf scalar value — stop here</td><td><code>replicas: 3</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>&lt;Object&gt;</code></td><td>nest deeper — it's a map of sub-fields</td><td><code>strategy:</code> then indented children</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>&lt;[]Object&gt;</code></td><td>a YAML <em>list</em> of maps (dash items)</td><td><code>containers:</code> then <code>- name: …</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>&lt;map[string]string&gt;</code></td><td>arbitrary key/value pairs</td><td><code>labels:</code> → <code>{app: easypj}</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>-required-</code> (in the description)</td><td>the field cannot be omitted</td><td>the apiserver rejects the object without it</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>🧭 Worked drill-down</h5><p>To find where <code>maxSurge</code> lives: <code>explain deployment.spec</code> shows <code>strategy &lt;Object&gt;</code> → go deeper with <code>explain deployment.spec.strategy</code> → that shows <code>rollingUpdate &lt;Object&gt;</code> → <code>explain deployment.spec.strategy.rollingUpdate</code> finally shows <code>maxSurge &lt;string&gt;</code> (a leaf). Each <code>&lt;Object&gt;</code> is a signpost to add one more dotted segment.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 6.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.4 Imperative vs Declarative — When to Use Each</h3>
                <p>Two ways to manage objects. Knowing which to reach for is worth real exam minutes — and real production safety.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">imperative vs declarative</div>
<pre>
   🏃 IMPERATIVE — "do this NOW"            📝 DECLARATIVE — "make it look like THIS"
   ┌────────────────────────────────┐      ┌────────────────────────────────┐
   │ kubectl create deploy easypj-web│      │ kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml    │
   │   --image=easypj:v1             │      │                                 │
   │ kubectl scale … --replicas=5    │      │ the file IS the desired state;  │
   │ kubectl expose … --port=80      │      │ K8s computes what to change     │
   │                                 │      │                                 │
   │ ✅ fast for one-offs            │      │ ✅ repeatable · version-control │
   │ ✅ exam speed                   │      │ ✅ GitOps · production standard │
   │ ❌ no record of what you did    │      │ ❌ slower for quick tests       │
   └────────────────────────────────┘      └────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>When</th><th>Exam</th><th>Production</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Imperative</strong></td><td>quick tasks · generate YAML</td><td>✅ for speed</td><td>❌ avoid</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Declarative</strong></td><td>real, repeatable changes</td><td>✅ for complex tasks</td><td>✅ always</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <h4>🎯 The CKA power move: imperative + dry-run = a YAML generator</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">$do = --dry-run=client -o yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl run easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --port=8000 $do &gt; pod.yaml                     <span class="comment"># Pod</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 $do &gt; deploy.yaml   <span class="comment"># Deployment</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 $do &gt; svc.yaml        <span class="comment"># Service</span>
kubectl create namespace easypj-dev $do &gt; ns.yaml                                       <span class="comment"># Namespace</span>
kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DEBUG=False $do &gt; cm.yaml         <span class="comment"># ConfigMap</span>
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret --from-literal=SECRET_KEY=x $do &gt; secret.yaml <span class="comment"># Secret</span>
<span class="comment"># then edit the generated file and apply it:</span>
vim deploy.yaml          <span class="comment"># add resources, env, probes…</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>--dry-run=client</code> vs <code>--dry-run=server</code></h5><p><strong>client</strong> validates locally (no API call) — use it to <em>generate</em> YAML. <strong>server</strong> runs the request through the apiserver (admission + "does it already exist?") without persisting — use it to <em>validate</em> before applying for real.</p></div>
                <h4>🎯 Real exam workflow — generate → edit → validate → apply → verify</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~47 s vs 3+ min hand-writing YAML</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># STEP 1 — generate the skeleton (≈5s)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 $do &gt; web.yaml

<span class="comment"># STEP 2 — edit to add the requirements (≈30s)</span>
vim web.yaml                                <span class="comment"># resource limits · labels · nodeSelector · env…</span>

<span class="comment"># STEP 3 — validate against the REAL cluster before applying (≈5s)</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml --dry-run=server
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web created (server dry run)</span>

<span class="comment"># STEP 4 — apply for real (≈2s)</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml

<span class="comment"># STEP 5 — verify (≈5s)</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o wide
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj-web
<span class="comment"># Total ≈ 47 seconds — vs 3+ minutes hand-writing YAML from scratch</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
   ⚡ Generate  ─▶  ✏️ Edit  ─▶  ✅ Validate (server dry-run)  ─▶  🚀 Apply  ─▶  🔍 Verify
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 6.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.5 Output Formatting &amp; Filtering</h3>
                <p>The exam constantly asks you to extract one value — a pod's node, a node's IP, every image — and often to <em>save it to a file</em>. Output flags are what separate fast solvers from slow ones.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>-o wide</code></td><td>extra columns (IP, node)</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>-o yaml</code>/<code>json</code></td><td>full object</td><td><code>kubectl get pod web -o yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>-o jsonpath</code></td><td>extract a field</td><td><code>… -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>-o custom-columns</code></td><td>custom table</td><td><code>… -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--sort-by</code></td><td>sort by field</td><td><code>… --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>-l</code></td><td>label filter</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -l app=easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--field-selector</code></td><td>field filter</td><td><code>… --field-selector status.phase=Running</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <h4>JSONPath — the CKA favourite</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">jsonpath examples</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># All pod names</span>
kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}'

<span class="comment"># Pod name + its node, one per line (a range/end loop with tab &amp; newline)</span>
kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.nodeName}{"\n"}{end}'

<span class="comment"># Node internal IPs (a filter expression: only addresses of type InternalIP)</span>
kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}'

<span class="comment"># Every container image across all namespaces, de-duplicated</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].image}' | tr ' ' '\n' | sort -u

<span class="comment"># custom-columns — a clean table of exactly the fields you want</span>
kubectl get pods -o custom-columns=\
NAME:.metadata.name,\
STATUS:.status.phase,\
NODE:.spec.nodeName,\
IP:.status.podIP</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 JSONPath cheat-sheet</h5><p><code>{.items[*]}</code> all items · <code>{.metadata.name}</code> a field · <code>{range …}{end}</code> loop · <code>{"\n"}</code>/<code>{"\t"}</code> newline/tab · <code>[?(@.type=="InternalIP")]</code> filter. Always <strong>single-quote</strong> the expression (double quotes break in bash).</p></div>
                <h4>🎯 Common extraction patterns (muscle memory)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">patterns that show up every exam</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ── Pattern 1: pod names sorted by creation time ──</span>
kubectl get pods --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp \
  -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\n"}{end}'

<span class="comment"># ── Pattern 2: which node each pod runs on ──</span>
kubectl get pods -o custom-columns=POD:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName

<span class="comment"># ── Pattern 3: the internal IP of every node ──</span>
kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}'

<span class="comment"># ── Pattern 4: every container image in the cluster (de-duped) ──</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.spec.containers[*].image}{"\n"}{end}' | sort -u

<span class="comment"># ── Pattern 5: pods NOT in Running state (triage) ──</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running

<span class="comment"># ── Pattern 6: count pods per namespace ──</span>
kubectl get pods -A --no-headers | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

<span class="comment"># ── Pattern 7: SAVE the answer to a file (the exam often requires this) ──</span>
kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}' &gt; /opt/answers/pod-names.txt</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📤</div><h5>Output to file</h5><p>Many tasks say "save to <code>/opt/answers/x.txt</code>." Use <code>&gt;</code>, then <code>cat</code> to double-check you actually wrote it.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div><h5>Pipe power</h5><p>Combine with <code>grep</code>/<code>awk</code>/<code>sort</code>/<code>wc -l</code> — e.g. <code>kubectl get po -A | grep -c Running</code>.</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 6.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s6-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">6.6 Debugging Pods — logs, exec &amp; ephemeral containers</h3>
                <p>When a pod misbehaves you climb a ladder: read its <strong>logs</strong>, then <strong>shell in</strong>, and — if the image has no shell or the container has already crashed — attach an <strong>ephemeral debug container</strong> with <code>kubectl debug</code>.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Rung</th><th>Use when…</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>logs</strong></td><td>the container ran (even if it crashed)</td><td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; [--previous]</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>exec</strong></td><td>it's running <em>and</em> the image has a shell</td><td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- sh</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>debug</strong></td><td>distroless (no shell) or crashed</td><td><code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;ctr&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">logs &amp; exec</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl logs easypj-web-x9k -c web --since=1h -f   <span class="comment"># container · last hour · follow</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-x9k --previous             <span class="comment"># the LAST crashed instance</span>
kubectl exec -it easypj-web-x9k -- sh              <span class="comment"># interactive shell (needs a shell in the image)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>Ephemeral containers — <code>kubectl debug</code></h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">get a shell where there isn't one</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl debug -it easypj-web-x9k --image=busybox --target=web   <span class="comment"># attach to a running pod</span>
kubectl debug easypj-web-x9k -it --image=busybox --copy-to=dbg --share-processes  <span class="comment"># debug a crashed pod via a copy</span>
kubectl debug node/worker-1 -it --image=busybox                 <span class="comment"># node shell (host fs at /host)</span>
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/easypj-web-x9k --timeout=60s   <span class="comment"># block in scripts</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 What an ephemeral container is</h5><p>A temporary debug container injected into a running pod — the <em>only</em> way to "get a shell" into a <strong>distroless</strong> image or a <strong>crashed</strong> pod. It shares the pod (optionally its PID namespace with <code>--share-processes</code>), but it <strong>can't be removed or restarted</strong> and takes no ports/probes/resources. It's not part of <code>pod.spec</code> — it's added through a special subresource.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how a kubectl command becomes an HTTP API call</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>kubectl is just a REST client for the apiserver. Every command becomes one or more HTTP calls — and knowing the four stages explains "connection refused", RBAC "forbidden", stale-cache surprises, and why <code>-v</code> is your sharpest debugger.</p>
                    <h5>🗺️ From a kubectl command to an HTTP request — the translation pipeline</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl get pods -n easypj -l app=web -o yaml
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ① PARSE — split the command into parts
        verb=get   resource=pods   -n easypj (namespace)   -l app=web (selector)   -o yaml (format)

   ② DISCOVER — find the URL path  (kubectl GETs /api &amp; /apis once, then caches the resource→path map)
        core group   /api/v1 .............. pods → /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods
                                            services · configmaps · secrets · pvcs …
        named groups /apis/&lt;grp&gt;/&lt;ver&gt; ..... deployments → /apis/apps/v1/namespaces/easypj/deployments
                                            ingresses   → /apis/networking.k8s.io/v1/…
                                            cronjobs    → /apis/batch/v1/…
        cache: ~/.kube/cache/discovery/    (stale after a CRD/version change → `kubectl api-resources`
                                            to refresh, or --cache-dir=/tmp to dodge a bad cache)

   ③ BUILD — construct the HTTP request
        GET /api/v1/namespaces/easypj/pods?labelSelector=app%3Dweb
        Host: 10.0.1.100:6443                 Accept: application/yaml
        Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGci…       User-Agent: kubectl/v1.34
        (token OR client cert — whichever your kubeconfig user holds)

   ④ APISERVER — process the request
        a. authN     validate the token/cert → username + groups
        b. authZ     RBAC: may this user LIST pods in easypj?      ◀── a 403 stops here
        c. admission GET needs none (mutating + validating run for writes)
        d. etcd      read /registry/pods/easypj/  (usually served from the watch cache)
        e. format    convert the stored object → the version &amp; format you asked for (YAML)
        f. respond   200 OK + YAML body  →  kubectl prints it
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 See the raw calls:</strong> <code>kubectl get pods -v=8</code> prints every request &amp; response (URL, headers, body) — the fastest way to debug auth, discovery, or "why did that 403?" problems. Ladder: <code>-v=6</code> one line/call · <code>-v=8</code> full request/response · <code>-v=9</code> adds the equivalent <code>curl</code>.</div>

                    <h5>Every verb is an HTTP method on a resource URL</h5>
                    <p>kubectl verbs map straight onto REST — which is why <code>-v=8</code> shows real HTTP, and why a "forbidden" error names the exact verb RBAC blocked.</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>kubectl</th><th>HTTP</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>get</code> / <code>describe</code></td><td>GET</td><td>read one/all (describe = a GET + related GETs &amp; events)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>create</code></td><td>POST</td><td>create a new object</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apply</code></td><td>PATCH</td><td>declarative merge (last-applied, or server-side)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>edit</code></td><td>GET → PATCH</td><td>fetch, you edit in $EDITOR, patch back</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>replace</code></td><td>PUT</td><td>overwrite the whole object</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>delete</code></td><td>DELETE</td><td>remove it</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>logs</code></td><td>GET …/log</td><td>a pod <em>subresource</em></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>exec</code> / <code>attach</code> / <code>port-forward</code></td><td>POST …/exec (WebSocket/SPDY)</td><td>streaming subresources</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>get -w</code></td><td>GET <code>?watch=true</code></td><td>a long-lived stream of change events</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why RBAC is verb-based:</strong> because the API is. "cannot get pods" literally means "you may not issue <code>GET</code> on <code>…/pods</code>." <code>kubectl auth can-i get pods</code> asks the apiserver that exact question — the fastest way to confirm a permission before (or after) a 403.</div>

                    <h5>How <code>--dry-run</code> really works — client vs server</h5>
                    <p>Same flag, two completely different machines do the work — which is exactly why a <em>server</em> dry-run catches errors a <em>client</em> one never can:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   --dry-run=client          (kubectl ONLY — no API call leaves your laptop)
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      build the object in memory  →  serialize to YAML/JSON  →  print it.   that's all.
      ✅ generate a manifest fast, even offline
      ❌ checks NOTHING server-side: no schema, no admission, no quota, no "does it already exist?"

   --dry-run=server          (a REAL request the apiserver runs but does NOT persist)
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      kubectl → apiserver runs the FULL write pipeline:
         authN ✅   authZ/RBAC ✅   mutating + validating admission ✅   schema validation ✅   defaulting ✅
      …then returns the object it WOULD have stored — and skips the etcd write.
      ✅ catches RBAC denials, admission/webhook rejects, quota breaches, unknown fields, type errors
      ⚠️ requires a live cluster connection (unlike client dry-run)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The exam workflow:</strong> <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to <em>generate</em> a manifest (no server needed) → edit it → <code>--dry-run=server</code> to <em>validate</em> it against the real cluster → <code>apply</code>. And <code>kubectl explain &lt;res&gt; --recursive</code> is the offline manual that always matches the cluster's exact API version — faster than the docs tab.</div>

                    <h5>One more layer — <code>get -w</code>, <code>--watch</code> and how big lists are paged</h5>
                    <p>A plain <code>get</code> is a single request/response, but two behaviours go deeper than that and explain things you'll actually see:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   WATCH — a long-lived stream, not polling
     kubectl get pods -w
       1. GET …/pods?resourceVersion=0      → the full current list (a snapshot + its resourceVersion)
       2. GET …/pods?watch=true&amp;resourceVersion=N  → connection stays OPEN
            apiserver pushes ADDED / MODIFIED / DELETED events as they happen (chunked HTTP)
       3. periodic BOOKMARK events advance your resourceVersion cheaply (no data)
       • if the stream falls too far behind → 410 Gone → kubectl re-LISTs and resumes
       this is exactly how every controller &amp; the kubelet keep up to date — efficiently, without polling

   PAGINATION — huge lists arrive in chunks
     kubectl get pods -A                    (10 000 pods)
       apiserver returns ~500 at a time with a `continue` token; kubectl fetches the next page
       automatically — which is why a big get streams in rather than hanging on one giant reply
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why it matters:</strong> <code>resourceVersion</code> is the cluster's optimistic-concurrency clock — every object carries one, watches resume from one, and <code>apply</code>/<code>replace</code> use it to reject a write if someone changed the object since you read it ("the object has been modified"). Understanding watch also demystifies controllers: they don't poll the apiserver, they <em>watch</em> it — the whole system is event-driven on top of these streams.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>verb + resource + name + flags</strong> builds any command; the short names save real seconds.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl explain</code></strong> is the manual you're allowed to "memorise" — drill with <code>--recursive</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Generate, don't hand-write</strong>: <code>kubectl create … $do &gt; f.yaml</code> → edit → <code>apply -f</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Extract anything</strong> with jsonpath / custom-columns / selectors; save with <code>&gt;</code> when asked.</li>
                    <li><strong>Debug ladder:</strong> <code>logs --previous</code> → <code>exec</code> → <code>kubectl debug</code> (distroless/crashed).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong><code>apply</code> vs <code>create</code>.</strong> <code>create</code> fails if the object exists; <code>apply</code> creates-or-updates. Use <code>apply</code> for files, <code>create</code> for imperative one-liners.</li>
                    <li><strong>jsonpath quoting.</strong> Single-quote the expression: <code>-o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}'</code> — double quotes break in bash.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>-A</code> = <code>--all-namespaces</code></strong>, but cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs, clusterroles) ignore it.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgot <code>-n</code>.</strong> Without a default namespace your object lands in <code>default</code> — set one per context (Ch 5).</li>
                    <li><strong>Editing the <em>live object</em> instead of the manifest.</strong> A <code>kubectl edit</code> / <code>kubectl scale</code> / <code>kubectl patch</code> change is invisible to your YAML file — the next <code>apply -f</code> (or a GitOps sync) silently reverts it. Fine for a quick exam fix; in production change the file and re-apply, or your edit is "configuration drift" waiting to be undone.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>alias k=kubectl</code> · <code>export do='--dry-run=client -o yaml'</code> · <code>export now='--force --grace-period=0'</code> · bash completion.</li>
                    <li><code>k get po -o wide</code> to see the node · <code>--sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code> for age · <code>-l app=easypj</code> to filter.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl explain &lt;res&gt; --recursive</code> for unknown fields · <code>kubectl diff -f f.yaml</code> to preview before apply.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── BEYOND THE BASICS ─────────── -->
            <h4 class="section-h">🔬 Beyond the basics — power-user kubectl worth knowing</h4>
            <p>These aren't day-one commands, but each removes a reason to ever leave the terminal — from scripting waits to debugging the API itself:</p>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>-o go-template</code></td><td>format output with a Go template — more powerful than jsonpath for conditionals/loops</td><td><code>k get po -o go-template='{{range .items}}{{.metadata.name}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}'</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>-o custom-columns</code></td><td>a quick table of exactly the fields you want</td><td><code>k get po -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>kubectl wait</code></td><td>block in a script until a condition is met — no <code>sleep</code> guessing</td><td><code>k wait --for=condition=Ready pod -l app=easypj --timeout=120s</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--raw</code></td><td>hit a raw API path directly (health, metrics, custom endpoints)</td><td><code>k get --raw='/readyz?verbose'</code> · <code>k get --raw='/metrics'</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>-v=6 … -v=9</code></td><td>verbose logging — see the exact HTTP requests kubectl makes (debug auth/RBAC/TLS)</td><td><code>k get po -v=8</code> (shows URL, headers, response)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>krew</strong> plugins</td><td>the kubectl plugin manager — <code>ctx</code>/<code>ns</code>/<code>neat</code>/<code>tree</code> (great for practice; not on the exam)</td><td><code>kubectl krew install ctx ns</code> → <code>kubectl ctx</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 6)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the kubectl power set</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">k get|describe|logs|exec|apply|create|delete|edit|scale|rollout   <span class="comment"># the verbs</span>
k explain &lt;res&gt;.spec --recursive          <span class="comment"># the in-exam manual</span>
k create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; d.yaml   <span class="comment"># generate</span>
k get po -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.podIP}'            <span class="comment"># extract</span>
k get po -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName
k debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;ctr&gt;           <span class="comment"># ephemeral shell</span>
k get po -v=8                              <span class="comment"># see the raw API calls</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Generate, apply, extract (the exam loop)</h5>
                <p>Time yourself; aim for under two minutes before peeking:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Generate a Deployment manifest for <code>easypj-web</code> (image <code>easypj:v1</code>, 3 replicas) with <code>$do</code> — don't create it yet.</li>
                    <li>Edit it to add a container port <code>8000</code>, then <code>apply</code> it.</li>
                    <li>Extract the names of all its pods, one per line, into <code>/opt/answers/pods.txt</code>, and confirm with <code>cat</code>.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 $do &gt; d.yaml
vim d.yaml          <span class="comment"># under containers: add  ports: [{containerPort: 8000}]</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl get po -l app=easypj-web \
  -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\n"}{end}' &gt; /opt/answers/pods.txt
cat /opt/answers/pods.txt   <span class="comment"># always verify the file landed</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Why:</strong> generate → edit → apply → extract → verify is the loop behind most exam tasks; the <code>$do</code> alias is what makes it fast.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 6 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Fast kubectl recall — the commands you must produce without thinking.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Generate a Pod manifest <em>without</em> creating it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">export manifest</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl run easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; pod.yaml</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--dry-run=client</code> renders locally (no API call); redirect to a file to edit and <code>apply</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Show the YAML path for a container's liveness probe.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">field reference</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.livenessProbe --recursive</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>explain --recursive</code> dumps the whole field subtree — no doc search needed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Extract every pod's IP with jsonpath.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.podIP}'</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>{.items[*]}</code> iterates the list; single-quote the whole expression.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List pods sorted by start time.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--sort-by</code> takes a JSONPath field; great for "which pod is newest/oldest?"</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Show only pods labelled <code>app=easypj</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -l app=easypj</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>-l</code> is an equality selector; combine with commas for AND (<code>-l app=easypj,tier=web</code>).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Show only <code>Running</code> pods (field selector).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods --field-selector status.phase=Running</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Field selectors filter on a few server-supported fields (status.phase, spec.nodeName, metadata.name).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Get logs from a container that already crashed.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">read logs</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous   <span class="comment"># -p</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--previous</code> reads the last terminated container — essential for CrashLoopBackOff.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Open a shell inside a running easypj pod.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">exec into pod</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl exec -it easypj-web-x9k -- sh</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Needs a shell in the image; if it's distroless, use <code>kubectl debug</code> instead.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Port-forward the easypj Service to <code>localhost:8000</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">access locally</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl port-forward svc/easypj-web 8000:80   <span class="comment"># local 8000 → service port 80</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Forwards through the Service to a backing pod — handy to test without exposing externally.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Print NAME + node + IP as custom columns.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">inspect</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,NODE:.spec.nodeName,IP:.status.podIP</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>custom-columns build a tidy table from any JSONPath fields — perfect for "report X for all pods."</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch6-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 6 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style "produce the command / fix it" tasks with real answers.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a single <code>kubectl</code> command to list all pods across all namespaces, showing their node assignment, IP address, and status. Then filter to show only pods that are NOT in <code>Running</code> state.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all pods with node and ip info</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all pods with node and IP info</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o wide

<span class="comment"># Filter for non-Running pods</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running

<span class="comment"># Alternative: use field selector for specific statuses</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running

<span class="comment"># JSONPath for custom columns</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o custom-columns=\
NAME:.metadata.name,\
NAMESPACE:.metadata.namespace,\
NODE:.spec.nodeName,\
STATUS:.status.phase,\
IP:.status.podIP</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>-A</code> (or <code>--all-namespaces</code>) is essential for getting a cluster-wide view. <code>-o wide</code> adds NODE and IP columns. The <code>grep -v Running</code> approach is the fastest on the exam. Field selectors work at the API server level (more efficient but limited to certain fields). Custom columns with <code>-o custom-columns</code> let you show exactly the fields you need — useful for complex output formatting questions on the CKA.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Use <code>kubectl</code> to find the exact YAML field path for a pod's restart policy, resource limits, and liveness probe. Which command acts as an in-cluster documentation system?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the kubectl explain command — your in-cluster docs</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The kubectl explain command — your in-cluster docs</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.restartPolicy
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources.limits
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.livenessProbe

<span class="comment"># Recursive view to see ALL fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive | head -80

<span class="comment"># Search for specific fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive | grep -i "liveness\|readiness"

<span class="comment"># Drill into nested fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.livenessProbe.httpGet</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl explain</code> is your <strong>most valuable tool on the CKA exam</strong>. It's faster than the official docs browser and works offline. The <code>--recursive</code> flag shows ALL field paths, and piping to <code>grep</code> helps you find exact field names. On the exam, if you can't remember whether it's <code>livenessProbe</code> or <code>liveness-probe</code>, just run <code>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers --recursive | grep -i probe</code>. Combine with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate the scaffold, then use <code>explain</code> to add advanced fields.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Extract the image names of all containers running in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace using JSONPath output. Show two approaches: JSONPath and custom-columns.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">jsonpath</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: JSONPath</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.containers[*].image}{"\n"}{end}'

<span class="comment"># Method 2: custom-columns</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system \
  -o custom-columns='POD:.metadata.name,IMAGES:.spec.containers[*].image'

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Quick grep approach (fastest on exam)</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o yaml | grep "image:"

<span class="comment"># Method 4: go-template</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system \
  -o go-template='{{range .items}}{{.metadata.name}}: {{range .spec.containers}}{{.image}} {{end}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}'</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA sometimes asks you to extract specific fields from resources. JSONPath and custom-columns are the two main approaches. JSONPath is more powerful but tricky syntax — practice the <code>{range .items[*]}</code> pattern. Custom-columns is simpler for tabular output. In practice, the <code>grep</code> approach is fastest when you just need to see values quickly. Pro tip: <code>-o json | jq '.items[].spec.containers[].image'</code> also works if <code>jq</code> is available (it usually is on the CKA).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl edit</code>, <code>kubectl patch</code>, and <code>kubectl replace</code>? When would you use each on the CKA exam?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Command</th>
                                            <th>How</th>
                                            <th>Best For</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>kubectl edit</code></td>
                                            <td>Opens resource in vim for editing</td>
                                            <td>Interactive changes, exploring spec</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>kubectl patch</code></td>
                                            <td>JSON/YAML merge via CLI</td>
                                            <td>Quick single-field changes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>kubectl replace</code></td>
                                            <td>Replaces entire resource from file</td>
                                            <td>Full resource replacement</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">edit — interactive</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># edit — interactive</span>
kubectl edit deployment nginx

<span class="comment"># patch — one-liner</span>
kubectl patch deployment nginx -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'

<span class="comment"># replace — from file (must include complete resource)</span>
kubectl get deployment nginx -o yaml > nginx.yaml
vim nginx.yaml  <span class="comment"># make changes</span>
kubectl replace -f nginx.yaml</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA exam: use <code>edit</code> when you need to see the full resource and change multiple fields. Use <code>patch</code> for quick changes to a single field. Use <code>replace</code> when modifying immutable fields (like pod spec) — you may need to <code>delete</code> + <code>replace --force</code>. Note: some fields can't be changed on existing pods (e.g., container image in a standalone pod). In those cases, export, modify, delete the old pod, and create the new one.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Sort all pods in the <code>default</code> namespace by their restart count (highest first). Then sort all events by timestamp. Show the <code>kubectl</code> commands.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">sort pods by restart count (highest first)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Sort pods by restart count (highest first)</span>
kubectl get pods --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'

<span class="comment"># Sort events by timestamp (most recent first)</span>
kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'

<span class="comment"># Sort events across all namespaces</span>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'

<span class="comment"># Sort pods by creation time</span>
kubectl get pods --sort-by='.metadata.creationTimestamp'

<span class="comment"># Sort nodes by CPU capacity</span>
kubectl get nodes --sort-by='.status.capacity.cpu'</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The <code>--sort-by</code> flag accepts a JSONPath expression. This is frequently tested on the CKA for diagnostic questions like "find the pod with the most restarts" or "show the most recent events." Key gotcha: <code>--sort-by</code> sorts in ascending order. To get descending, pipe to <code>tail</code> or <code>tac</code>: <code>kubectl get pods --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount' | tail -5</code>. For events, the timestamp sort is essential when debugging cascading failures.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Use <code>kubectl</code> to find all API resources that support the <code>list</code> verb. Then find the short name and API group for <code>NetworkPolicy</code>.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all api resources with their verbs</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all API resources with their verbs</span>
kubectl api-resources -o wide

<span class="comment"># Filter for resources that support 'list'</span>
kubectl api-resources --verbs=list

<span class="comment"># Find NetworkPolicy details</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i networkpol
<span class="comment"># NAME              SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION             NAMESPACED   KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># networkpolicies   netpol       networking.k8s.io/v1   true         NetworkPolicy</span>

<span class="comment"># Short name: netpol</span>
<span class="comment"># API group: networking.k8s.io</span>
<span class="comment"># Namespaced: true</span>

<span class="comment"># Use the short name</span>
kubectl get netpol -A</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl api-resources</code> is your cheat sheet for finding resource names, short names, API versions, and whether they're namespaced. On the CKA, if you can't remember the exact resource name or API version, this command gives it to you instantly. Short names save typing: <code>po</code> (pods), <code>svc</code> (services), <code>deploy</code> (deployments), <code>rs</code> (replicasets), <code>ns</code> (namespaces), <code>netpol</code> (networkpolicies), <code>pv</code>/<code>pvc</code> (persistentvolumes/claims), <code>sa</code> (serviceaccounts).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Execute a command inside a running pod named <code>web-app</code> to: (1) check the default gateway, (2) test DNS resolution, (3) view environment variables. Write the <code>kubectl exec</code> commands.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">interactive shell</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Interactive shell</span>
kubectl exec -it web-app -- /bin/sh

<span class="comment"># One-off commands (non-interactive)</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Check default gateway</span>
kubectl exec web-app -- ip route
kubectl exec web-app -- cat /proc/net/route

<span class="comment"># 2. Test DNS resolution</span>
kubectl exec web-app -- nslookup kubernetes.default
kubectl exec web-app -- cat /etc/resolv.conf

<span class="comment"># 3. View environment variables</span>
kubectl exec web-app -- env
kubectl exec web-app -- printenv

<span class="comment"># Multi-container pod: specify container</span>
kubectl exec web-app -c sidecar -- env

<span class="comment"># Debugging with a temporary container</span>
kubectl debug web-app -it --image=busybox -- sh</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl exec</code> is invaluable for CKA troubleshooting. Key syntax: <code>--</code> separates kubectl args from the command to run inside the container. For multi-container pods, use <code>-c &lt;container-name&gt;</code>. If the container has no shell (distroless images), use <code>kubectl debug</code> to attach an ephemeral debug container with tools like busybox. Common CKA patterns: exec into a pod to test network connectivity, verify mounted volumes (<code>ls /data</code>), or check if ConfigMap/Secret values are properly injected as env vars.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a <code>kubectl</code> command to get the logs of a pod named <code>api-pod</code> that has crashed. The current container has just started (so its logs are empty). How do you see the logs from the previous crash?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">view logs of the previous (crashed) container…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View logs of the previous (crashed) container instance</span>
kubectl logs api-pod --previous

<span class="comment"># View only the last 100 lines</span>
kubectl logs api-pod --previous --tail=100

<span class="comment"># Stream logs in real-time (for current container)</span>
kubectl logs api-pod -f

<span class="comment"># Multi-container: specify which container's logs</span>
kubectl logs api-pod -c main-app --previous

<span class="comment"># All containers in the pod</span>
kubectl logs api-pod --all-containers=true

<span class="comment"># Logs from all pods matching a label</span>
kubectl logs -l app=api --previous</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The <code>--previous</code> flag is <strong>critical for CKA troubleshooting</strong>. When a container is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>, the current instance may have empty logs or only startup messages. The <code>--previous</code> flag shows logs from the <em>last terminated</em> container instance — this is where the actual error that caused the crash is recorded. On the exam, always try <code>--previous</code> if the current logs don't show the error. Note: Kubernetes only stores the last terminated container's logs — if it has crashed multiple times, you only see the most recent crash.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Use <code>kubectl</code> to generate a Deployment YAML file for an application named <code>backend</code> with image <code>node:18-alpine</code>, 3 replicas, in namespace <code>staging</code> — without creating the resource. Then explain the dry-run flag.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate yaml without creating anything</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Generate YAML without creating anything</span>
kubectl create deployment backend \
  --image=node:18-alpine \
  --replicas=3 \
  -n staging \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > backend-deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Two types of dry-run:</span>
<span class="comment"># --dry-run=client  →  generates YAML locally (no API server contact)</span>
<span class="comment"># --dry-run=server  →  sends to API server for validation but doesn't persist</span>

<span class="comment"># Edit the generated YAML to add more fields</span>
vim backend-deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Apply when ready</span>
kubectl apply -f backend-deploy.yaml</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> is the <strong>CKA exam's most used flag combination</strong>. It generates valid YAML for any resource without creating it. <code>client</code> mode doesn't contact the API server (works even if API server is down). <code>server</code> mode validates against the actual API server (catches invalid field names, missing namespaces, quota violations). Strategy: always start with <code>--dry-run=client</code> to generate the scaffold, add advanced fields (resources, probes, volumes), then <code>kubectl apply</code>. This is 10x faster than writing YAML from memory.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Permanently delete a stuck pod named <code>zombie-pod</code> that has been in <code>Terminating</code> state for 10 minutes. The normal <code>kubectl delete</code> doesn't work. What do you do?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">force delete with zero grace period</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Force delete with zero grace period</span>
kubectl delete pod zombie-pod --grace-period=0 --force

<span class="comment"># If the pod had a finalizer preventing deletion</span>
kubectl patch pod zombie-pod -p '{"metadata":{"finalizers":null}}'
kubectl delete pod zombie-pod

<span class="comment"># If it's a pod managed by a deployment, delete won't permanently remove it</span>
<span class="comment"># The replicaset will recreate it. Instead:</span>
kubectl scale deployment &lt;deploy-name&gt; --replicas=0
<span class="comment"># OR delete the deployment</span>
kubectl delete deployment &lt;deploy-name&gt;</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Stuck <code>Terminating</code> pods occur when: (1) The kubelet on the node is down and can't confirm termination, (2) The container's process ignores SIGTERM, (3) finalizers prevent deletion. The <code>--grace-period=0 --force</code> combination tells the API server to remove the pod object immediately from etcd, regardless of whether the kubelet has confirmed container shutdown. On the CKA, this is essential for cleanup when a node is unreachable. Caution: force-deleting doesn't stop the actual container — if the node comes back, the container may still be running (orphaned).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 6 Visual Summary — one command, four parts, total fluency</div>
<pre>
   kubectl   VERB   RESOURCE   NAME   FLAGS
       │
       ├─ 🔎 explain &lt;res&gt;.field --recursive            ← what's the YAML? (the in-exam manual)
       ├─ ⚡ create … $do &gt; f.yaml → edit → apply -f     ← generate, don't hand-write
       ├─ 📤 -o jsonpath / custom-columns / -l / --field-selector  → &gt; file   ← extract
       └─ 🐛 logs --previous → exec → kubectl debug      ← climb the debug ladder
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   it's all REST: kubectl ─▶ apiserver (authN → authZ → admission) ─▶ etcd.  `-v=8` reveals it. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every kubectl command is the same four parts — <em>verb · resource · name · flags</em>. From there, four moves cover almost everything: <code>explain</code> tells you a field's YAML, <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> generates a manifest to edit and <code>apply</code>, the output flags (jsonpath / custom-columns / selectors) extract any value (and <code>&gt;</code> saves it), and the debug ladder (logs → exec → <code>kubectl debug</code>) gets you inside a broken pod.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> kubectl is a REST client — it parses your command, discovers the API path, authenticates, and renders the JSON back. Speed comes from aliases + completion + <code>$do</code>, and when anything is "forbidden" or "refused," <code>kubectl … -v=8</code> shows you the exact call to fix.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH6-INSERT:: -->
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 7 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch7">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 7 · YAML &amp; Kubernetes Manifests</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Every Kubernetes object is described in YAML, and the #1 cause of failed exam tasks is a stray indent — not missing knowledge. This chapter makes YAML automatic: the syntax that bites, the four required fields, finding the right <code>apiVersion</code>, multi-doc files, validation, and how <code>apply</code> actually manages objects.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📝 YAML</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 used in 100% of tasks</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ YAML syntax — key/value, lists, nested maps, and the rules that trip people up</li>
                    <li>✅ The <strong>4 required fields</strong> every manifest must have (and selector == template labels)</li>
                    <li>✅ How to find the right <code>apiVersion</code> for any resource</li>
                    <li>✅ Multi-resource files with <code>---</code>, and applying a whole directory</li>
                    <li>✅ Validating with <code>--dry-run</code> and <code>kubectl diff</code> before you apply</li>
                    <li>✅ Object management (imperative vs declarative) &amp; <strong>server-side apply</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">All Domains <span class="cka-weight">100%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">YAML correctness <span class="cka-weight">PASS/FAIL</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>⚠️ YAML mistakes = failed tasks</h5>
                <p>A single wrong indent breaks the whole manifest — silently. Build the habit of generating with <code>$do</code> and validating with <code>--dry-run=server</code> before you apply.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Spaces only</strong> — a tab breaks YAML with a cryptic error.</li>
                    <li><strong>2-space indents</strong> — the Kubernetes convention.</li>
                    <li><strong>Colon + space</strong> — <code>key: value</code>, never <code>key:value</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 7 Roadmap — read, write &amp; validate any manifest</div>
<pre>
   7.1 SYNTAX ──▶ 7.2 4 REQUIRED ──▶ 7.3 apiVersion ──▶ 7.4 MULTI-DOC
   maps·lists·     apiVersion·kind·     api-resources       --- separator ·
   indent rules    metadata·spec         /api-versions       apply -f dir/
        └──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘
                                    ▼
   7.5 VALIDATE ──────────▶ 7.6 OBJECT MANAGEMENT
   client vs server dry-run   imperative ⇄ declarative · server-side apply
   · kubectl diff             · last-applied · managedFields
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s7-1"><strong>7.1</strong> Syntax</a></td><td>How do I write valid YAML?</td><td>spaces (not tabs) · 2-space indent · colon+space · <code>- item</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s7-2"><strong>7.2</strong> 4 fields</a></td><td>What must every manifest have?</td><td><code>apiVersion·kind·metadata·spec</code>; selector == template labels.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s7-3"><strong>7.3</strong> apiVersion</a></td><td>Which group/version for this kind?</td><td><code>kubectl api-resources</code> / <code>api-versions</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s7-4"><strong>7.4</strong> Multi-doc</a></td><td>Many objects in one file?</td><td><code>---</code> between docs; <code>apply -f dir/</code> for a folder.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s7-5"><strong>7.5</strong> Validate</a></td><td>Is my manifest correct?</td><td><code>--dry-run=server</code> + <code>kubectl diff</code> before applying.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s7-6"><strong>7.6</strong> Object mgmt</a></td><td>How does <code>apply</code> track changes?</td><td>3-way merge / last-applied; server-side apply + managedFields.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 7.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s7-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.1 YAML Basics for Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>YAML ("YAML Ain't Markup Language") is how you describe <em>desired state</em>. Three building blocks — and a handful of rules that silently break manifests:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">YAML building blocks &amp; the rules that bite</div>
<pre>
   ① KEY–VALUE (scalar)        ② LIST (sequence)         ③ NESTED MAP (indent = depth)
   name: easypj                ports:                    metadata:        ◀ parent
   replicas: 3                 - 8000   ◀ item           &nbsp;&nbsp;name: easypj   ◀ child (2 spaces)
   debug: true                 - 8080                    &nbsp;&nbsp;labels:
   note: "a string"            - 443                     &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;app: easypj  ◀ grandchild

   ⚠️ RULES:  ✅ spaces only (2/level)   ❌ NO tabs (breaks silently)
              ✅ colon + space  key: value     ❌ key:value
              ✅ dash + space   - item         ❌ -item
              ✅ ---  separates documents in one file
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml syntax in one glance</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">name: easypj                 <span class="comment"># scalar — string</span>
replicas: 3                  <span class="comment"># scalar — int</span>
enabled: true                <span class="comment"># scalar — bool</span>

skills:                      <span class="comment"># a list of strings</span>
- Python
- Django
- Kubernetes

containers:                  <span class="comment"># a list of OBJECTS (maps)</span>
- name: web
  image: easypj:v1
  ports:
  - containerPort: 8000

args: ["runserver", "0.0.0.0:8000"]   <span class="comment"># inline (flow) list — shorthand for the above</span>

description: |               <span class="comment"># block scalar: |  PRESERVES newlines</span>
  easypj is a Django job board.
  It filters jobs by Skill, Location, and Author.

summary: &gt;                   <span class="comment"># folded scalar: &gt;  JOINS lines into one paragraph</span>
  This becomes a single line
  even though it spans several.

metadata:                    <span class="comment"># a nested map</span>
  name: easypj
  labels: { app: easypj, tier: web }   <span class="comment"># inline (flow) map</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Indentation matters — same words, different meaning</div>
<pre>
   ✅ CORRECT — ports is a field OF the container        ❌ WRONG — ports is a sibling, not a child
   containers:                                          containers:
   - name: web                                          - name: web
     ports:            ← indented under the list item   ports:              ← NOT indented = separate field!
     - containerPort: 8000                              - containerPort: 8000

   ✅ CORRECT — labels nested under metadata            ❌ WRONG — labels at the same level as metadata
   metadata:                                            metadata:
     name: easypj                                         name: easypj
     labels:                                            labels:               ← NOT nested = top-level field!
       app: easypj                                        app: easypj
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>🔄 Why YAML, not JSON</h5><p>YAML is a friendlier superset of JSON — it allows <strong>comments</strong>, drops the braces/quotes clutter, and diffs cleanly in Git. Kubernetes accepts both, but every manifest you'll meet is YAML.</p></div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div><h5>YAML — what you write</h5>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod definition</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                        <p class="mb-0">✅ comments · ✅ readable · ✅ less typing</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">{ }</div><h5>JSON — what K8s stores</h5>
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "apiVersion": "v1",
  "kind": "Pod",
  "metadata": {"name": "easypj"},
  "spec": {
    "containers": [{
      "name": "web",
      "image": "easypj:v1"
    }]}}</code></pre>
                        <p class="mb-0">❌ no comments · ❌ verbose · ✅ precise</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Both are valid for kubectl</h5><p><code>kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code> and <code>kubectl apply -f pod.json</code> both work — the apiserver speaks JSON and kubectl converts YAML to it. Always <strong>write</strong> YAML (readable, comments); reach for JSON only with <code>-o json</code> when extracting data programmatically.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>📏 <code>|</code> vs <code>&gt;</code> — the two block scalars people mix up</h5><p><code>|</code> (<strong>literal</strong>) keeps every newline exactly — use it for multi-line scripts, certs, or config files inside a ConfigMap. <code>&gt;</code> (<strong>folded</strong>) replaces single newlines with spaces, collapsing wrapped lines into one paragraph — use it for long prose. Add a <code>-</code> (<code>|-</code> / <code>&gt;-</code>) to strip the trailing newline.</p></div>

                <h4>📋 The five YAML mistakes that break manifests</h4>
                <p>Every one of these is a real "<code>error converting YAML</code>" or a silent misparse. This is the reference to scan when a manifest won't apply:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What you wrote</th><th>What Kubernetes sees</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Tab character</td><td><code>&#9;name: easypj</code> (leading tab)</td><td>parse error — the whole file fails to load</td><td>spaces only, 2 per level</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>No space after colon</td><td><code>name:easypj</code></td><td><code>name:easypj</code> read as a single string</td><td><code>name: easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Wrong indent level</td><td><code>labels:</code> at the root</td><td>a top-level field instead of nested under <code>metadata</code></td><td>indent 2 spaces under its parent</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>String vs number</td><td><code>replicas: "3"</code></td><td>a string where an integer is required → type error</td><td><code>replicas: 3</code> (no quotes)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Missing dash for a list</td><td><code>containers:</code> → <code>name: web</code></td><td>"expected a list, got a mapping"</td><td><code>containers:</code> → <code>- name: web</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>

                <h4>🧪 The same snippet — broken vs fixed</h4>
                <p>Each error below is a real "<code>did not find expected key</code>" or a silent misparse. Train your eye on the pair:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>❌ Broken</h5>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
&#9;containers:          <span class="comment"># a TAB → breaks silently</span>
  - name:web           <span class="comment"># no space after colon</span>
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    -containerPort: 8000 <span class="comment"># no space after dash</span>
    env:
    - name: DEBUG
      value: yes         <span class="comment"># unquoted → becomes boolean true!</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>✅ Fixed</h5>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  containers:          <span class="comment"># 2 spaces, never a tab</span>
  - name: web          <span class="comment"># space after the colon</span>
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000 <span class="comment"># space after the dash</span>
    env:
    - name: DEBUG
      value: "yes"       <span class="comment"># quoted → stays the string "yes"</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🔍 Catch it before the apiserver does</h5><p><code>cat -A file.yaml</code> shows tabs as <code>^I</code> and line-ends as <code>$</code>; in vim, <code>:set list</code> reveals hidden whitespace and <code>gg=G</code> re-indents the whole file. On the exam, <code>--dry-run=server</code> (§7.5) is your final gate.</p></div>

                <h4>🔬 Beyond the basics — anchors, null &amp; quoting traps</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Feature / trap</th><th>What to know (so a doc is never needed)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Anchors &amp; aliases</strong> <code>&amp;name</code> / <code>*name</code></td><td>define a block once and reuse it: <code>defaults: &amp;def {cpu: 100m}</code> … then <code>resources: *def</code>. Pure-YAML DRY — valid in manifests, though Kustomize/Helm usually do reuse better. You'll see it in CI configs and the odd manifest.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Null forms</strong> <code>null</code> · <code>~</code> · empty</td><td><code>key:</code> (nothing after), <code>key: null</code>, and <code>key: ~</code> are all the <em>same</em> null = "unset". That's different from <code>key: ""</code> (an empty <em>string</em>) and from omitting the key entirely — a distinction that matters when a patch <em>clears</em> a field vs leaves it alone.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>The "Norway problem"</strong></td><td>unquoted <code>yes/no/on/off/true/false</code> become booleans; <code>NO</code> (Norway's country code) silently becomes <code>false</code>. Quote any country code, "true/false" config value, or version string.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Numbers that aren't numbers</strong></td><td><code>08000</code> may error (looks like invalid octal) and <code>1.20</code> drops its trailing zero. Quote ports, versions, and zip codes you want preserved as strings.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 7.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.2 The 4 Required Fields — Every Manifest</h3>
                <p>Every object — Pod, Deployment, Service, ConfigMap, Secret — needs exactly these four top-level fields. Burn them in:</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div><h5>apiVersion</h5><p>which API group/version — <code>v1</code>, <code>apps/v1</code>, <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>kind</h5><p>the object type — <code>Pod</code>, <code>Deployment</code>, <code>Service</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div><h5>metadata</h5><p>identity — <code>name</code>, <code>namespace</code>, <code>labels</code>, <code>annotations</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚙️</div><h5>spec</h5><p>desired state — THE MEAT; different shape per kind</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-pod.yaml — the 4 fields</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1                    <span class="comment"># 1️⃣ which API group/version</span>
kind: Pod                         <span class="comment"># 2️⃣ the object type</span>
metadata:                         <span class="comment"># 3️⃣ identity</span>
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-dev
  labels:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
spec:                             <span class="comment"># 4️⃣ desired state (the meat)</span>
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000
    env:
    - name: DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE
      value: "easypj.settings"
    resources:
      requests:
        cpu: "100m"
        memory: "128Mi"
      limits:
        cpu: "500m"
        memory: "256Mi"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Deployment structure — and the rule that breaks rollouts</div>
<pre>
   apiVersion: apps/v1
   kind: Deployment
   metadata: { name: easypj-web, namespace: easypj-dev }
   spec:
     replicas: 3
     selector:
       matchLabels: { app: easypj }   ───────────┐  these TWO
     template:                                    │  MUST be
       metadata:                                  │  identical
         labels: { app: easypj }   ◀──────────────┘
       spec:
         containers: [{ name: web, image: easypj:v1, ports: [{containerPort: 8000}] }]
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>selector.matchLabels</code> must equal <code>template.metadata.labels</code></h5><p>If they differ, the Deployment can't find the pods it created — the rollout errors or the Service ends up with 0 endpoints. This is the single most common manifest bug.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 7.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.3 Finding the Right apiVersion</h3>
                <p>A wrong <code>apiVersion</code> = manifest rejected. Here's the reference plus the two commands that look it up live on any cluster.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>apiVersion</th><th>Short</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Secret · Namespace · PV · PVC · ServiceAccount</td><td><code>v1</code> (core)</td><td>po·svc·cm·ns·pv·pvc·sa</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deployment · ReplicaSet · DaemonSet · StatefulSet</td><td><code>apps/v1</code></td><td>deploy·rs·ds·sts</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Job · CronJob</td><td><code>batch/v1</code></td><td>—·cj</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Ingress · NetworkPolicy · IngressClass</td><td><code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code></td><td>ing·netpol</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole · ClusterRoleBinding</td><td><code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>StorageClass · CSIDriver</td><td><code>storage.k8s.io/v1</code></td><td>sc</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>PodDisruptionBudget</td><td><code>policy/v1</code></td><td>pdb</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">look it up — never guess</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List ALL resources with their apiVersion, short name, and scope</span>
kubectl api-resources
<span class="comment"># NAME          SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION             NAMESPACED   KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># pods          po           v1                     true         Pod</span>
<span class="comment"># services      svc          v1                     true         Service</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy       apps/v1                true         Deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># ingresses     ing          networking.k8s.io/v1   true         Ingress</span>

<span class="comment"># List every served API group/version</span>
kubectl api-versions
<span class="comment"># apps/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># networking.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick lookup: what apiVersion does Ingress use?</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i ingress
<span class="comment"># ingresses   ing   networking.k8s.io/v1   true   Ingress</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Memory trick</h5><p><strong>Core = <code>v1</code></strong> (Pod/Service/ConfigMap/Secret/NS/PV/PVC/SA) · <strong>Workloads = <code>apps/v1</code></strong> (Deployment/RS/DS/STS) · <strong>Networking = <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code></strong> · <strong>RBAC = <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</code></strong> · <strong>Batch = <code>batch/v1</code></strong>.</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">API group map — where each kind lives</div>
<pre>
   Kubernetes API
   ├─ core  /api/v1 ............ Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Secret · Namespace · PV · PVC · SA · Node
   ├─ apps/v1 ................. Deployment · ReplicaSet · DaemonSet · StatefulSet
   ├─ batch/v1 ................ Job · CronJob
   ├─ networking.k8s.io/v1 .... Ingress · NetworkPolicy · IngressClass
   ├─ rbac.authorization…/v1 .. Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole · ClusterRoleBinding
   ├─ storage.k8s.io/v1 ....... StorageClass · CSIDriver · CSINode
   └─ policy/v1 .............. PodDisruptionBudget
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 7.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.4 Multi-Resource YAML Files</h3>
                <p>Bundle several objects in one file with <code>---</code> between them — handy for shipping an app and its Service together.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-complete.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ── 1. Namespace — created first so everything below has a home ──</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: easypj-dev
---
<span class="comment"># ── 2. ConfigMap — non-secret config ──</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj-dev
data:
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"
  DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE: "easypj.settings"
---
<span class="comment"># ── 3. Deployment — the workload ──</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                  <span class="comment"># ← must match template labels</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                <span class="comment"># ← must match selector</span>
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        envFrom:
        - configMapRef:
            name: easypj-config
---
<span class="comment"># ── 4. Service — stable address in front of the pods ──</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  selector:
    app: easypj                    <span class="comment"># ← selects the Deployment's pods</span>
  ports:
  - port: 80                       <span class="comment"># Service port → </span>
    targetPort: 8000               <span class="comment"># → container port</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">apply strategies</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f easypj-complete.yaml   <span class="comment"># one multi-doc file</span>
kubectl apply -f k8s/                    <span class="comment"># every YAML in a directory</span>
kubectl apply -k overlays/prod/          <span class="comment"># a Kustomize overlay (Ch 37)</span>
kubectl delete -f easypj-complete.yaml   <span class="comment"># tear it all down</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">file organization — two approaches</div>
<pre>
   Approach A — ONE multi-doc file            Approach B — a DIRECTORY of files
   ┌──────────────────────────┐              ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ easypj-complete.yaml      │              │ k8s/                      │
   │  ├─ Namespace             │              │  ├─ 01-namespace.yaml     │
   │  ├─ ---                   │              │  ├─ 02-configmap.yaml     │
   │  ├─ ConfigMap             │              │  ├─ 03-secret.yaml        │
   │  ├─ ---                   │              │  ├─ 04-deployment.yaml    │
   │  ├─ Secret                │              │  └─ 05-service.yaml       │
   │  ├─ ---                   │              │                           │
   │  ├─ Deployment            │              │  kubectl apply -f k8s/    │
   │  ├─ ---                   │              │  (applies every file)     │
   │  └─ Service               │              └──────────────────────────┘
   │  kubectl apply -f easypj-complete.yaml                               │
   └──────────────────────────┘
   ✅ simple, one command                     ✅ better organization
   ❌ unwieldy for large apps                  ✅ each file = one concern
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Ordering with <code>-f dir/</code></h5><p>A directory is applied in <strong>alphabetical</strong> order — if a Deployment references a ConfigMap that's applied later, it can fail. Number files (<code>01-namespace.yaml</code>, <code>02-configmap.yaml</code>, …) <em>or</em> use one multi-doc file (created top-to-bottom). <code>apply</code> is idempotent, so re-running fixes most ordering hiccups anyway.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 7.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.5 Validation &amp; Dry Run</h3>
                <p>Never apply blind. Validate locally, then against the server, then preview the diff — then apply.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the validation workflow</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1 — client-side validation (no API call): is the YAML well-formed?</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml --dry-run=client
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web created (dry run)   ← YAML is valid</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2 — server-side validation: does the apiserver accept it? (admission, no persist)</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml --dry-run=server
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web created (server dry run)   ← API accepted it</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3 — preview the exact change (like git diff)</span>
kubectl diff -f deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 4 — apply for real</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web created</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>client dry-run</h5><p>local syntax/schema check</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>server dry-run</h5><p>full admission, nothing saved</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>diff</h5><p>preview the live-vs-new delta</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>apply</h5><p>commit the change</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🩺 Decode the two errors you'll actually hit</h5><p><code>error converting YAML to JSON: did not find expected key</code> → a bad indent. <code>error validating data: unknown field "X"</code> → a typo'd/mis-placed field — confirm the path with <code>kubectl explain</code>.</p></div>
                <h4>🚨 The six errors you'll meet — message → fix</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">common manifest errors &amp; fixes</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ERROR — wrong apiVersion</span>
error: resource mapping not found for name: "easypj"  kind: Deployment  apiVersion: v1
<span class="comment"># FIX: a Deployment is apps/v1, not v1</span>

<span class="comment"># ERROR — missing required field</span>
error: error validating: ValidationError(Deployment.spec): missing required field "selector"
<span class="comment"># FIX: add spec.selector.matchLabels</span>

<span class="comment"># ERROR — selector ≠ template labels</span>
The Deployment "easypj" is invalid: spec.selector does not match template.metadata.labels
<span class="comment"># FIX: make selector.matchLabels == template.metadata.labels</span>

<span class="comment"># ERROR — invalid YAML (indent/tab)</span>
error: error parsing deploy.yaml: yaml: line 12: did not find expected key
<span class="comment"># FIX: check indentation at line 12 (a tab, or wrong nesting)</span>

<span class="comment"># ERROR — unknown field (typo)</span>
error: error validating data: ValidationError(Deployment.spec): unknown field "replica"
<span class="comment"># FIX: it's "replicas" — confirm with kubectl explain deployment.spec</span>

<span class="comment"># ERROR — namespace doesn't exist</span>
Error from server (NotFound): namespaces "easypj-dev" not found
<span class="comment"># FIX: create it first — kubectl create namespace easypj-dev</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🛠️ Editing YAML in vim — the exam's default editor</h4>
                <p>The exam gives you <strong>vim</strong>, and YAML is whitespace-sensitive — so the very first thing to do in a fresh exam shell is fix vim's indent behaviour. These settings prevent the indent disasters that eat exam minutes:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~/.vimrc — set this FIRST</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add to ~/.vimrc the moment the exam starts:</span>
set tabstop=2       <span class="comment">" a tab shows as 2 spaces</span>
set shiftwidth=2    <span class="comment">" one indent level = 2 spaces</span>
set expandtab       <span class="comment">" the Tab key inserts spaces, never a real tab</span>
set autoindent      <span class="comment">" keep the indent level on the next line</span>
set number          <span class="comment">" line numbers — error messages cite them</span>

<span class="comment"># One-liner to set it all at once:</span>
echo 'set ts=2 sw=2 et ai nu' &gt;&gt; ~/.vimrc</code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>vim command</th><th>What it does</th><th>When to use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>:set paste</code></td><td>turn OFF auto-indent so a paste isn't re-indented</td><td><strong>before</strong> pasting YAML from the docs tab</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>:set nopaste</code></td><td>turn auto-indent back on</td><td>after pasting, to resume normal editing</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>gg=G</code></td><td>re-indent the whole file</td><td>when indentation got mangled</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Shift+V</code> then <code>&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;</code></td><td>visual-select lines, indent right / left</td><td>push a YAML block deeper or shallower</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>/text</code></td><td>search</td><td>jump to a field in a big manifest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>dd</code> / <code>yy</code> / <code>p</code></td><td>delete / yank / paste a line</td><td>move or duplicate list items</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>:set list</code></td><td>reveal hidden whitespace (tabs as <code>^I</code>)</td><td>hunting an invisible tab</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>u</code> / <code>Ctrl-r</code></td><td>undo / redo</td><td>revert a mistake instantly</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ The #1 paste disaster</h5><p>Pasting indented YAML into vim <em>without</em> <code>:set paste</code> makes auto-indent compound every line's indentation into a staircase. Habit: <code>:set paste</code> → paste → <code>:set nopaste</code>. (With <code>expandtab</code> set, the Tab key is also safe — it inserts spaces.)</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 7.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s7-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">7.6 Object Management &amp; Server-Side Apply</h3>
                <p>There are three ways to manage objects — don't mix them on the same object — and it pays to know how <code>apply</code> figures out what changed.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Command</th><th>Idempotent?</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>imperative command</td><td><code>kubectl create/run/expose/scale/label</code></td><td>no (errors if it exists)</td><td>exam speed, quick fixes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>imperative config</td><td><code>kubectl create -f</code> / <code>replace -f</code></td><td>no</td><td>rarely (whole-object replace)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>declarative</strong></td><td><code>kubectl apply -f</code> / <code>-k</code></td><td><strong>yes</strong></td><td>production &amp; GitOps — the default</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how <code>apply</code> knows what changed — the 3-way merge</div>
<pre>
   ① last-applied-configuration     ② live object (cluster)     ③ your new file
   (annotation from last apply)        (may have drifted)          (what you want)
            └────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┘
                                          ▼
              apply SETS fields you set · CLEARS fields you removed ·
              LEAVES fields others wrote (e.g. a replica count from `kubectl scale`)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Server-Side Apply (<code>--server-side</code>)</h5><p>The apiserver does the merge and records <strong>field ownership</strong> in <code>metadata.managedFields</code>. Two managers writing the same field → a conflict (resolve with <code>--force-conflicts</code>). It's the modern choice for controllers, GitOps, and any object with several writers.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Don't mix</h5><p>Editing an apply-managed object with <code>kubectl edit</code>/<code>scale</code> can be reverted by the next <code>apply</code>. Pick one workflow per object.</p></div>

                <h4>🧮 The 3-way merge, with real numbers</h4>
                <p>Suppose you first applied <code>replicas: 2</code>, someone then ran <code>kubectl scale --replicas=4</code>, and now your file says <code>replicas: 3</code> with <code>image: easypj:v2</code>. What does <code>apply</code> produce?</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th><code>replicas</code></th><th><code>image</code></th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>① last-applied annotation (your previous apply)</td><td>2</td><td>easypj:v1</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>② live object (after <code>scale</code> drifted it)</td><td>4</td><td>easypj:v1</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>③ your new file</td><td>3</td><td>easypj:v2</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>→ result of <code>apply</code></strong></td><td><strong>3</strong></td><td><strong>easypj:v2</strong></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <p>Your file <em>sets</em> both fields, so <strong>your values win</strong> (3 overrides the drifted 4; v2 overrides v1). The two edge cases that surprise people:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>You remove <code>replicas</code> from the file</strong> (and it was in last-applied) → apply <em>clears</em> it, so it reverts to the default <code>1</code>. Pruning is why "the field I deleted came back to default."</li>
                    <li><strong>A field you never managed</strong> (not in your file, not in last-applied — e.g. <code>replicas</code> owned by an HPA) → apply <em>leaves it alone</em>. This is exactly how an HPA and your manifest coexist without fighting.</li>
                </ul>

                <h4>🔧 Targeted edits: strategic-merge vs JSON patch</h4>
                <p>When you don't want to re-apply a whole file, <code>kubectl patch</code> edits one path. Three patch grammars, each for a different job:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Patch type</th><th>Flag</th><th>Best for</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>strategic merge</strong> (default)</td><td><code>--type=strategic</code></td><td>K8s-aware — merges lists <em>by key</em> (patch one container by <code>name</code> without replacing the rest)</td><td><code>kubectl patch deploy easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>JSON merge</strong> (RFC 7386)</td><td><code>--type=merge</code></td><td>simple object overwrite; <em>replaces</em> whole lists — also what CRDs use (no strategic metadata)</td><td><code>… --type=merge -p '{"metadata":{"labels":{"tier":"web"}}}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>JSON patch</strong> (RFC 6902)</td><td><code>--type=json</code></td><td>precise op-by-op edits at an explicit path (add / remove / replace)</td><td><code>… --type=json -p '[{"op":"replace","path":"/spec/replicas","value":5}]'</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Validate offline with kubeconform</h5><p><code>kubeconform</code> (the maintained successor to <code>kubeval</code>) checks manifests against the Kubernetes JSON schemas <strong>without a cluster</strong> — perfect for a CI gate before anything reaches the apiserver: <code>kubeconform -strict -summary k8s/</code>. It can't see RBAC/admission/quota (only <code>--dry-run=server</code> can), but it catches shape and type errors in a pipeline where no live cluster exists.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — what the apiserver does to your manifest before it's stored</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p><code>apply</code> isn't a file save — your YAML runs a gauntlet. Knowing it explains why <em>server</em> dry-run catches errors <em>client</em> dry-run can't.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl apply -f x.yaml
     ① DECODE              YAML → JSON   (a tab / bad indent dies HERE — client-side)
     ② AuthN → AuthZ       who are you? may you? (RBAC)
     ③ MUTATING admission  inject defaults (LimitRanger) · sidecars (webhooks)
     ④ SCHEMA validation   unknown field? wrong type? (needs the server's schema)
     ⑤ VALIDATING admission PodSecurity · ResourceQuota · policy webhooks
     ⑥ PERSIST → etcd      resourceVersion assigned
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why server dry-run is stronger:</strong> client dry-run stops at ① (local shape). Server dry-run runs ②–⑤ <em>without</em> ⑥ — so it catches RBAC, admission, quota, and unknown-field errors a client check never sees.</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>defaulting</strong></td><td>the server fills omitted fields (e.g. a Service's <code>type: ClusterIP</code>) — why <code>get -o yaml</code> shows more than you wrote</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>pruning</strong></td><td><code>apply</code> removes fields you deleted from the file (via last-applied) — declarative means the file is the source of truth</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>structural schema</strong></td><td>CRDs validate against their OpenAPI schema too — a bad custom-resource field is rejected just like a built-in</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>② Defaulting, made concrete — what the server adds to a minimal manifest.</strong> This is why <code>kubectl get deploy -o yaml</code> always shows far more than you wrote. You submit the left; the apiserver stores the right:</p>
                    <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                        <div class="info-card"><h5>You write</h5>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>The server stores (defaults added)</h5>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">spec:
  replicas: 1                 <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate       <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 25%           <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
      maxUnavailable: 25%     <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
  revisionHistoryLimit: 10    <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1
        resources: {}                       <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
        terminationMessagePath: /dev/termination-log  <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent       <span class="comment"># ← default (tag ≠ :latest)</span>
      restartPolicy: Always   <span class="comment"># ← default</span>
      dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst <span class="comment"># ← default</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why it matters:</strong> the <code>LimitRanger</code> admission plugin can <em>also</em> inject defaults here (e.g. a default CPU/memory request if your namespace has a LimitRange), and a mutating webhook like Istio injects a sidecar container — all before the object is stored. Stage 5 then writes the fully-defaulted object to etcd at key <code>/registry/deployments/easypj/easypj-web</code> (protobuf-encoded) and fires a <strong>watch event</strong> the Deployment controller is waiting on.</div>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>③ apiVersion resolution &amp; the storage version — why what you <em>GET</em> isn't always what you <em>PUT</em>.</strong> A single <code>kind</code> (say <code>Deployment</code>) can be <em>served</em> at several versions at once. The apiserver never stores your YAML verbatim — it converts every object through an internal "hub" form, persists it as the one <strong>storage version</strong>, and converts again on read to whatever version you asked for. That is why you can submit an old beta object and read it back as <code>apps/v1</code>.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   WRITE PATH                                    READ PATH
   you submit  apps/v1beta1                       you ask for  apps/v1
        │  decode + convert                            ▲  convert from storage
        ▼                                              │
   internal "hub" version  ──────────────►  internal "hub" version
        │  convert to storage version                  ▲
        ▼                                              │  read from etcd
   STORAGE VERSION (apps/v1)  ──►  etcd  ──────────────┘
   key: /registry/deployments/&lt;ns&gt;/&lt;name&gt;   value: protobuf (or JSON)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Core idea:</strong> conversion between versions of the same kind is required to be <em>lossless</em> — round-tripping <code>v1 → v1beta1 → v1</code> must give back the identical object. Exactly one served version is flagged <code>storage: true</code>; everything in etcd is written in that version. When the team bumps the storage version, the apiserver rewrites objects lazily (on next write) or via a one-off migration.</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>What's really happening</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>get -o yaml</code> shows a different <code>apiVersion</code> than you applied</td><td>you read back the <strong>preferred</strong> served version, converted from the storage version — not the literal text you submitted</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>a deprecation <em>warning</em> on apply (object still created)</td><td>you used a still-served-but-deprecated version; the apiserver returns a <code>Warning:</code> header — fix the manifest before the version is removed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>no matches for kind … in version …</code> (hard error)</td><td>that version is <strong>removed</strong>, not just deprecated — migrate the manifest (e.g. <code>extensions/v1beta1</code> Ingress → <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>a <strong>CRD</strong> with two versions returning the same data in both</td><td>the CRD declares a <code>conversion</code> strategy — <code>None</code> (identical schema, relabel only) or <code>Webhook</code> (you run a conversion server); one version is <code>storage: true</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Practical levers: <code>kubectl api-versions</code> lists every served group/version; <code>kubectl explain &lt;kind&gt; --api-version=apps/v1</code> pins the schema you're validating against; and the <code>kubectl-convert</code> plugin rewrites a manifest from a removed version to a current one. The deprecation lifecycle is fixed and worth memorising: GA/stable versions are supported for a long window, beta for a few releases after a successor ships, and alpha can break or vanish at any release — never run alpha in anything you care about.</p>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>④ Field validation — why a typo is sometimes a warning and sometimes a hard error.</strong> Since v1.27 the apiserver validates fields <em>server-side</em> against the OpenAPI schema, and you choose how strict via <code>--validate</code>:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th><code>--validate=</code></th><th>An unknown/duplicate field…</th><th>When you'd use it</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>strict</code> (the default)</td><td>is a hard <strong>error</strong> — the request is rejected</td><td>always, on the exam — catches <code>replicas</code>→<code>replica</code> typos</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>warn</code></td><td>is accepted but returns a <code>Warning:</code> header</td><td>migrating old manifests you can't fix yet</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>false</code> / <code>ignore</code></td><td>is silently dropped</td><td>almost never — it hides real mistakes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Field ownership (Server-Side Apply) goes one level deeper:</strong> the apiserver records <em>which manager wrote each field</em> in <code>metadata.managedFields</code>. When two managers (say your <code>apply</code> and an HPA) both try to own <code>spec.replicas</code>, you get a <strong>conflict</strong> — resolve it with <code>--force-conflicts</code> (you take ownership) or by removing the field from your manifest (you cede it). This is how a Deployment manifest and an HPA coexist on the same object without endlessly overwriting each other — they own <em>different</em> fields, and the apiserver enforces the boundary. Inspect it with <code>kubectl get deploy easypj-web -o yaml --show-managed-fields</code>.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>4 required fields</strong> in every manifest: <code>apiVersion · kind · metadata · spec</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Spaces, not tabs</strong>; 2-space indent; colon+space — indentation is the #1 failure cause.</li>
                    <li><strong>selector == template labels</strong>, or the controller loses its pods.</li>
                    <li><strong>Find apiVersion</strong> with <code>kubectl api-resources</code> — never guess.</li>
                    <li><strong>Generate then validate</strong>: <code>$do</code> → <code>--dry-run=server</code> → <code>diff</code> → <code>apply</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Tabs.</strong> A single tab breaks the file with a cryptic "did not find expected key." Set your editor to spaces.</li>
                    <li><strong>Missing colon-space.</strong> <code>key:value</code> parses as one string — always <code>key: value</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>selector ≠ template labels.</strong> Rollout errors or 0 endpoints; make them identical.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deprecated apiVersion.</strong> <code>extensions/v1beta1</code> is long gone — Deployments are <code>apps/v1</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Unquoted "yes"/numbers.</strong> <code>enabled: yes</code> becomes <code>true</code>; a port like <code>"08000"</code> needs quotes to stay a string.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Never hand-write</strong> — scaffold with <code>kubectl create … $do &gt; f.yaml</code>, then edit.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl explain &lt;res&gt;.spec --recursive</code> when you forget a field path.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl diff -f f.yaml</code> before every <code>apply</code> in a real cluster.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 7)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">manifests &amp; validation</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl api-resources ; kubectl api-versions       <span class="comment"># find apiVersion / kind / scope</span>
kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; d.yaml   <span class="comment"># generate</span>
kubectl explain deployment.spec --recursive        <span class="comment"># field reference</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml --dry-run=server           <span class="comment"># validate</span>
kubectl diff -f d.yaml                              <span class="comment"># preview</span>
kubectl apply -f k8s/                               <span class="comment"># apply a directory</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — catch a YAML bug before it costs you</h5>
                <p>Do it before peeking:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Generate a Deployment YAML for <code>easypj-web</code> with <code>$do</code>.</li>
                    <li>Break it on purpose: change one indent (or set <code>selector.matchLabels</code> to <code>app: wrong</code>).</li>
                    <li>Catch the error with a server dry-run, fix it, and confirm with <code>diff</code> before applying.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; d.yaml
vim d.yaml                                  <span class="comment"># introduce the bug</span>
kubectl apply -f d.yaml --dry-run=server    <span class="comment"># → error (selector mismatch / parse)</span>
vim d.yaml                                  <span class="comment"># fix: selector == template labels</span>
kubectl diff -f d.yaml &amp;&amp; kubectl apply -f d.yaml</code></pre>
</div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Why:</strong> server dry-run runs admission + schema validation, so it catches the selector/label mismatch that a client check would pass.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 7 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Read/write/validate-YAML recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A manifest won't parse — what are the two most likely YAML causes?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>(1) A <strong>tab</strong> instead of spaces. (2) A missing <strong>space after a colon</strong> (<code>key:value</code>). Both throw "did not find expected key."</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Set the editor to 2-space indents; <code>cat -A file.yaml</code> reveals hidden tabs (shown as <code>^I</code>).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the four required top-level fields of any manifest.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>apiVersion</code> · <code>kind</code> · <code>metadata</code> · <code>spec</code>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Omit one and the apiserver rejects it; <code>metadata.name</code> is itself required.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Find the apiVersion for an Ingress (command).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">discover API</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl api-resources | grep -i ingress   <span class="comment"># → networking.k8s.io/v1</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Always look it up rather than guess — versions drift between releases.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Turn <code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1</code> into a YAML file.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate manifest</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; deploy.yaml</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The dry-run YAML generator — then edit and <code>apply</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Put a Deployment + Service in one file and apply it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Separate the two objects with a <code>---</code> line in one file, then <code>kubectl apply -f app.yaml</code>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Multi-doc files are created top-to-bottom, so the Deployment's deps can precede it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Validate a manifest against the apiserver WITHOUT creating it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">server validate</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl apply -f app.yaml --dry-run=server</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Runs admission + schema validation but doesn't persist — catches more than client dry-run.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Preview exactly what an <code>apply</code> would change.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">preview changes</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl diff -f app.yaml</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Shows the live-vs-new delta (like <code>git diff</code>) using a server dry-run under the hood.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why does a label typo leave a Service with no endpoints?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>The Service's <code>selector</code> no longer matches any pod's labels, so the EndpointSlice is empty and traffic has nowhere to go.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Check with <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> and compare selector to pod labels (<code>--show-labels</code>).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You get "expected map, got list" (or vice-versa). What's wrong?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A field that should be a map has a <code>-</code> list item under it (or a list field lacks <code>-</code>). Fix the shape to match <code>kubectl explain</code>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>e.g. <code>env:</code> is a <em>list</em> (<code>- name: …</code>), while <code>resources:</code> is a <em>map</em>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why quote <code>"true"</code> or a port like <code>"8080"</code> in some fields?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>YAML auto-types <code>true</code>→boolean and <code>8080</code>→integer. If a field needs a <em>string</em> (e.g. a ConfigMap value, an annotation), quote it so it isn't coerced.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>ConfigMap <code>data:</code> values must be strings — <code>DEBUG: "False"</code>, not <code>DEBUG: False</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch7-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 7 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Manifest "build it / fix it" tasks with the real answers.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are the four mandatory top-level fields in every Kubernetes YAML manifest? Write a minimal valid Pod manifest with all four fields.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">api version</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1          <span class="comment"># 1. API version</span>
kind: Pod               <span class="comment"># 2. Resource type</span>
metadata:               <span class="comment"># 3. Metadata (name, namespace, labels)</span>
  name: minimal-pod
spec:                   <span class="comment"># 4. Specification (desired state)</span>
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx:1.25</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <p><strong>The four required fields:</strong></p>
                                <ol>
                                    <li><code>apiVersion</code> — which API group/version (v1, apps/v1, networking.k8s.io/v1)</li>
                                    <li><code>kind</code> — the resource type (Pod, Deployment, Service, etc.)</li>
                                    <li><code>metadata</code> — at minimum, <code>name</code> is required</li>
                                    <li><code>spec</code> — the desired state definition (varies per resource type)</li>
                                </ol>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Every K8s manifest follows this structure. The most common CKA mistake is getting <code>apiVersion</code> wrong. Quick reference: Pods/Services/ConfigMaps/Secrets → <code>v1</code>. Deployments/ReplicaSets/DaemonSets → <code>apps/v1</code>. Ingress → <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code>. RBAC → <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</code>. If unsure: <code>kubectl api-resources | grep &lt;kind&gt;</code> shows the correct API version.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You paste a YAML manifest into vim on the CKA exam and it comes out garbled — indentation is wrong everywhere. What happened and how do you prevent it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the problem: vim's auto-indent added extra…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The problem: vim's auto-indent added extra indentation to pasted text</span>

<span class="comment"># Prevention: Enter paste mode BEFORE pasting</span>
:set paste       <span class="comment"># Disable auto-indent for pasting</span>
<span class="comment"># Now paste your YAML</span>

<span class="comment"># After pasting, re-enable normal mode</span>
:set nopaste     <span class="comment"># Re-enable auto-indent</span>

<span class="comment"># Better approach: use vim's paste toggle</span>
<span class="comment"># Add to ~/.vimrc on the exam:</span>
set pastetoggle=&lt;F2&gt;
<span class="comment"># Then press F2 before pasting, F2 after</span>

<span class="comment"># Essential vim settings for YAML editing</span>
:set tabstop=2 shiftwidth=2 expandtab
<span class="comment"># This ensures tabs are converted to 2 spaces</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>YAML is <strong>whitespace-sensitive</strong> — incorrect indentation means invalid syntax. Vim's auto-indent feature adds extra spaces when you paste, corrupting the YAML. On the CKA exam, this is a common time-waster. Always <code>:set paste</code> before pasting, <code>:set nopaste</code> after. Also useful: <code>:set number</code> (show line numbers), <code>gg=G</code> (re-indent entire file), and <code>:set list</code> (show hidden whitespace). Practice vim YAML editing before the exam — it saves enormous time.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the correct <code>apiVersion</code> for each of these resources: Pod, Deployment, Service, Ingress, NetworkPolicy, PersistentVolumeClaim, Role, ClusterRoleBinding? How do you find the right apiVersion if you can't remember?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Resource</th>
                                            <th>apiVersion</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Secret, PVC, SA</td>
                                            <td><code>v1</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Deployment, ReplicaSet, DaemonSet, StatefulSet</td>
                                            <td><code>apps/v1</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Ingress, NetworkPolicy</td>
                                            <td><code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding</td>
                                            <td><code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>StorageClass</td>
                                            <td><code>storage.k8s.io/v1</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">can't remember? use this command</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Can't remember? Use this command:</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i deployment
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy   apps/v1   true   Deployment</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Getting <code>apiVersion</code> wrong causes "no matches for kind" errors. The pattern is: core resources (pods, services) use <code>v1</code>. Workload resources use <code>apps/v1</code>. Networking resources use <code>networking.k8s.io/v1</code>. RBAC uses <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</code>. On the CKA, <code>kubectl api-resources</code> is the definitive answer — it shows the exact API group and version for every resource. Alternatively, <code>kubectl explain &lt;resource&gt;</code> shows the VERSION at the top of its output.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a YAML manifest that defines BOTH a Deployment and a Service in the same file. How do you separate multiple resources in one YAML file?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">multi-resource yaml — separated by ---</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Multi-resource YAML — separated by ---</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web-app
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: nginx:1.25
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: web-svc
spec:
  selector:
    app: web
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
  type: ClusterIP</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">apply both at once</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply both at once</span>
kubectl apply -f multi-resource.yaml

<span class="comment"># Or apply all files in a directory</span>
kubectl apply -f ./manifests/</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The <code>---</code> document separator allows multiple resources in one file. Each document is an independent YAML document. On the CKA exam, it's generally faster to use one file per resource for easier editing and debugging. However, multi-document files are common in production. Key gotcha: make sure the <code>---</code> is at column 0 with no spaces. Also critical: the Deployment's <code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code> MUST match <code>spec.template.metadata.labels</code> — and the Service's <code>spec.selector</code> must match the pod's labels.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You apply a YAML manifest and get the error: <em>"error validating data: ValidationError(Deployment.spec): unknown field 'replicas' in io.k8s.api.apps.v1.DeploymentSpec"</em>. What is wrong with your YAML?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <p>The <code>replicas</code> field is at the <strong>wrong indentation level</strong>. It should be under <code>spec:</code> of the Deployment, not nested under another field.</p>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ❌ WRONG — replicas indented under template
spec:
  template:
    replicas: 3    # ← This is wrong! replicas belongs under spec, not template
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web

<span class="comment"># ✅ CORRECT — replicas at the right level</span>
spec:
  replicas: 3      # ← Correct! Direct child of spec
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>YAML indentation errors are the #1 cause of failed applies on the CKA. The Deployment spec has three main sections at the same level: <code>replicas</code>, <code>selector</code>, and <code>template</code>. The <code>template</code> section is a PodTemplateSpec — it has its own <code>metadata</code> and <code>spec</code>. This nested <code>spec</code> (pod spec) is different from the outer <code>spec</code> (deployment spec). Use <code>kubectl explain deployment.spec</code> to see which fields are direct children of the deployment spec. Common trap: putting pod-level fields (like <code>containers</code>) at the deployment level.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you validate a YAML manifest BEFORE applying it? Show three different validation methods available on the CKA exam.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">client-side dry run (no api server needed)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Client-side dry run (no API server needed)</span>
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --dry-run=client
<span class="comment"># Validates YAML syntax and basic structure</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Server-side dry run (validates against API server)</span>
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --dry-run=server
<span class="comment"># Validates: schema, RBAC, admission controllers, quotas</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl diff (shows what would change)</span>
kubectl diff -f deployment.yaml
<span class="comment"># Shows the diff between current state and proposed changes</span>

<span class="comment"># Bonus: validate only (no apply)</span>
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --validate=true --dry-run=client

<span class="comment"># Quick YAML syntax check with python</span>
python3 -c "import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open('deployment.yaml'))"</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>--dry-run=server</code> is the most thorough validation — it sends the request through the entire API pipeline (authentication, authorization, admission control, schema validation) but doesn't persist to etcd. <code>--dry-run=client</code> only validates locally — it won't catch missing namespaces or quota violations. <code>kubectl diff</code> is great for seeing exactly what will change. On the CKA, always use server-side dry-run before applying complex manifests to catch errors without wasting time on failed deployments.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Explain YAML data types: strings, integers, booleans, lists, and maps. What happens if you forget to quote the string <code>"true"</code> in a ConfigMap value?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml type coercion gotchas</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># YAML type coercion gotchas</span>
data:
  debug: true           # ❌ Boolean! Not string "true"
  debug: "true"         # ✅ String "true"
  port: 8080            # ❌ Integer! Not string "8080"
  port: "8080"          # ✅ String "8080"
  version: 1.0          # ❌ Float! Not string "1.0"
  version: "1.0"        # ✅ String "1.0"

<span class="comment"># ConfigMap data values MUST be strings</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: app-config
data:                    <span class="comment"># All values must be strings</span>
  debug: "true"          # ← Quoted
  port: "8080"           # ← Quoted
  message: "Hello World" # ← Quoted

<span class="comment"># Lists (arrays) use - prefix</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app          # ← List item 1
    image: nginx
  - name: sidecar      # ← List item 2
    image: fluentd</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>YAML automatically interprets unquoted values: <code>true</code>/<code>false</code> become booleans, numbers become integers/floats. ConfigMap <code>data:</code> requires string values — unquoted <code>true</code> causes validation errors. This catches many people on the CKA. Also tricky: <code>yes</code>/<code>no</code> are booleans in YAML! And <code>0666</code> is octal. Always quote values in ConfigMaps and anywhere you mean a literal string. For binary data, ConfigMap provides <code>binaryData:</code> with base64 encoding.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the Deployment <code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code> field and why is it required? What error do you get if it doesn't match <code>spec.template.metadata.labels</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the selector must match the template labels</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The selector MUST match the template labels</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web          # ← Must match template labels
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web        # ← Must match selector
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: nginx

<span class="comment"># ❌ Mismatch causes this error:</span>
<span class="comment"># "selector does not match template labels"</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The <code>selector.matchLabels</code> tells the Deployment which pods it owns. If the selector doesn't match the template labels, the Deployment can never find its own pods. This is a <strong>hard requirement</strong> since <code>apps/v1</code> — the API server rejects the manifest immediately. Unlike in older API versions, you cannot omit the selector. On the CKA, when generating with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code>, the selector is automatically set correctly. But if you edit YAML manually, make sure these match. The selector is also immutable — once created, you can't change it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Convert this imperative command into a complete YAML manifest: <code>kubectl create deployment redis --image=redis:7 --replicas=2 --port=6379</code></div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate the yaml</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate the YAML</span>
kubectl create deployment redis --image=redis:7 --replicas=2 --port=6379 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Result:</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  labels:
    app: redis
  name: redis
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: redis
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: redis
    spec:
      containers:
      - image: redis:7
        name: redis
        ports:
        - containerPort: 6379</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding the mapping between imperative commands and YAML is essential for the CKA. <code>--image</code> → <code>spec.template.spec.containers[0].image</code>. <code>--replicas</code> → <code>spec.replicas</code>. <code>--port</code> → <code>spec.template.spec.containers[0].ports[0].containerPort</code>. The generated YAML is always a good starting point — add fields like <code>resources</code>, <code>livenessProbe</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>volumeMounts</code> by editing. Remember: <code>kubectl run</code> creates Pods (not Deployments), <code>kubectl create deployment</code> creates Deployments.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You have a running Deployment and need to add a <code>livenessProbe</code> to it. You don't have the original YAML. How do you extract the current YAML, modify it, and re-apply it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">export and edit (safest)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Export and edit (safest)</span>
kubectl get deployment easypj-web -o yaml > easypj-web-deploy.yaml
vim easypj-web-deploy.yaml
<span class="comment"># Add the livenessProbe under spec.template.spec.containers[0]:</span>
<span class="comment">#   livenessProbe:</span>
<span class="comment">#     httpGet:</span>
<span class="comment">#       path: /healthz</span>
<span class="comment">#       port: 8080</span>
<span class="comment">#     initialDelaySeconds: 15</span>
<span class="comment">#     periodSeconds: 10</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-web-deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl edit (faster, in-place)</span>
kubectl edit deployment easypj-web
<span class="comment"># vim opens — add the livenessProbe section, save and exit</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl patch (one-liner, no editor)</span>
kubectl patch deployment easypj-web --type=json \
  -p='[{"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/livenessProbe","value":{"httpGet":{"path":"/healthz","port":8080},"initialDelaySeconds":15,"periodSeconds":10}}]'</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA, you'll often need to modify running resources. <code>kubectl edit</code> is the fastest for adding a few lines. <code>kubectl get -o yaml > file.yaml</code> is safer as you have a backup. When extracting YAML from a running resource, remove server-generated fields (<code>resourceVersion</code>, <code>uid</code>, <code>creationTimestamp</code>, <code>status</code>) before re-applying — otherwise you may get "resourceVersion conflict" errors. The <code>livenessProbe</code> tells the kubelet how to check if the container is alive. If it fails, the container is killed and restarted.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 7 Visual Summary — the manifest skeleton, colour-coded by the 4 fields</div>
<pre>
   apiVersion: apps/v1        ◀ ① which API group/version   (kubectl api-resources)
   kind: Deployment           ◀ ② what object
   metadata:                  ◀ ③ identity
     name: easypj-web
     namespace: easypj-dev
     labels: { app: easypj }
   spec:                      ◀ ④ desired state
     replicas: 3
     selector: { matchLabels: { app: easypj } } ─┐  MUST
     template:                                    │  match
       metadata: { labels: { app: easypj } } ◀────┘
       spec: { containers: [{ name: web, image: easypj:v1, ports: [{containerPort: 8000}] }] }
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   write it → $do generates it → --dry-run=server validates it → diff previews → apply. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every object is the same four-field skeleton — <code>apiVersion</code> (which API), <code>kind</code> (what), <code>metadata</code> (identity) and <code>spec</code> (desired state). YAML is whitespace-significant: spaces only, 2-per-level, colon+space. The one rule that breaks rollouts is <em>selector must equal the template labels</em>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> don't hand-write — <strong>generate</strong> with <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code>, <strong>validate</strong> with <code>--dry-run=server</code> (it runs admission + schema, unlike client), <strong>preview</strong> with <code>diff</code>, then <strong>apply</strong>. <code>apply</code> tracks your last-applied config to add/clear/leave fields — that's declarative management.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH7-INSERT:: -->
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 8 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch8">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 8 · Deploying easypj — Your First Kubernetes Application</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">Everything from Parts 1–2 fuses here into a running system you can open in a browser. You'll deploy the <strong>easypj</strong> Django web tier step by step — Namespace → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service — then port-forward to <code>/jobs/</code> and understand every piece. (PostgreSQL joins in Part 6.)</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟢 Beginner</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🚀 Deploy</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🐍 Django · easypj</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Plan a deployment — which objects you need, and in what order</li>
                    <li>✅ Isolate the app in a <strong>Namespace</strong></li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>ConfigMap</strong> for plain config, <strong>Secret</strong> for credentials</li>
                    <li>✅ Write a complete <strong>Deployment</strong> — env from CM+Secret, resources, probes</li>
                    <li>✅ Expose it with a <strong>Service</strong> and reach it via <code>port-forward</code></li>
                    <li>✅ Verify &amp; troubleshoot end-to-end; then read how ConfigMaps/Secrets really work</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 This is the pattern for every deployment task</h5>
                <p>The exam asks you to create Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets again and again. This exact workflow <em>is</em> the answer — drill it to muscle memory.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Namespace → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service</strong> — this order avoids reference errors.</li>
                    <li><strong>Generate with <code>$do</code></strong>, edit for the specifics, then <code>apply</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always verify</strong> with <code>kubectl get all -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> and check the Service has endpoints.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 8 Roadmap — the deploy pipeline for easypj</div>
<pre>
   8.1 PLAN ─▶ 8.2 ns ─▶ 8.3 ConfigMap ─▶ 8.4 Secret ─▶ 8.5 Deployment ─▶ 8.6 Service
   target arch   isolate   plain config      credentials   pods + envFrom    stable VIP
                                                                                  │
                              ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                              ▼
   8.7 ACCESS ─▶ 8.8 VERIFY ─▶ 8.9 WHAT HAPPENED ─▶ 8.10 CM/Secrets deep
   port-forward   get all/ep      apply→…→Running        consume · limits · types
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Step</th><th>Object / takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-1"><strong>8.1</strong> Overview</a></td><td>plan</td><td>target architecture for easypj-dev</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-2"><strong>8.2</strong> Namespace</a></td><td>1</td><td>isolate everything in <code>easypj-dev</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-3"><strong>8.3</strong> ConfigMap</a></td><td>2</td><td>non-secret config (DEBUG, ALLOWED_HOSTS…)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-4"><strong>8.4</strong> Secret</a></td><td>3</td><td>SECRET_KEY, DB_PASSWORD (base64)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-5"><strong>8.5</strong> Deployment</a></td><td>4</td><td>pods + envFrom CM/Secret + probes</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-6"><strong>8.6</strong> Service</a></td><td>5</td><td>ClusterIP 80 → targetPort 8000</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-7"><strong>8.7</strong> Access</a></td><td>6</td><td><code>port-forward</code> → <code>/jobs/</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-8"><strong>8.8</strong> Verify</a></td><td>7</td><td><code>get all</code>, endpoints, logs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-9"><strong>8.9</strong> What happened</a></td><td>—</td><td>apply → apiserver → … → Running</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s8-10"><strong>8.10</strong> CM/Secrets</a></td><td>deep</td><td>consume · live-update · limits · Secret types</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s8-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.1 The easypj Application Overview</h3>
                <p>We're deploying the <strong>easypj</strong> Django project (the <code>jobpost</code> app). Knowing the app shapes the Kubernetes objects we create.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐍</div><h5>Django project: easypj</h5><p>Job board. Models: Skills · Author · Location · JobPost · User. Endpoints <code>/jobs/</code>, <code>/jobs/&lt;id&gt;/</code>, <code>/skills/</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Image: easypj:v1</h5><p>Containerised Django on port <strong>8000</strong>; needs env vars for settings, allowed hosts, DB host, and a secret key.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Target architecture — easypj-dev (web tier)</div>
<pre>
   NAMESPACE: easypj-dev
   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │  🚀 Deployment easypj-web (replicas: 1→3)                      │
   │     ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐                        │
   │     │📦 easypj-web  │  │📦 easypj-web  │  image easypj:v1 :8000 │
   │     └──────┬───────┘  └──────┬───────┘                        │
   │            └────────┬─────────┘                                │
   │            🔌 Service easypj-web (ClusterIP)  80 ─▶ 8000        │
   │   ⚙️ ConfigMap easypj-config        🔒 Secret easypj-secret     │
   │      DEBUG·ALLOWED_HOSTS·DB_HOST       SECRET_KEY·DB_PASSWORD   │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   💻 you ──port-forward──▶ Service ──▶ Pod ──▶ Django /jobs/
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-box">
   📂 Namespace ─▶ ⚙️ ConfigMap ─▶ 🔐 Secret ─▶ 🚀 Deployment ─▶ 🌐 Service ─▶ ✅ Verify
   create in THIS order so nothing references an object that doesn't exist yet
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.2 Step 1 — Create the Namespace</h3>
                <p>Isolate the app in its own namespace — tidy, and trivial to tear down later.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">namespace + make it your default</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># stop typing -n</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Or declaratively</h5><p>A 3-line manifest works too: <code>apiVersion: v1 / kind: Namespace / metadata: {name: easypj-dev}</code>. Every object below sets <code>namespace: easypj-dev</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.3 Step 2 — ConfigMap (non-secret config)</h3>
                <p>Plain, non-sensitive settings go in a ConfigMap so config lives outside the image.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-config.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj-dev
data:                          <span class="comment"># every value MUST be a string (quote it)</span>
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"
  DB_HOST: "easypj-db"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE: "easypj.settings"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">…or generate it imperatively</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create configmap easypj-config -n easypj-dev \
  --from-literal=DEBUG=False --from-literal=ALLOWED_HOSTS='*' \
  --from-literal=DB_HOST=easypj-db --from-literal=DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=easypj.settings</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Values are strings</h5><p>ConfigMap <code>data:</code> values must be strings — quote <code>"False"</code> so YAML doesn't coerce it to a boolean (Ch 7 §7.5).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.4 Step 3 — Secret (sensitive config)</h3>
                <p>Credentials go in a Secret. Use <code>stringData</code> so you write plaintext and Kubernetes base64-encodes it for you.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-secret.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secret
  namespace: easypj-dev
type: Opaque                  <span class="comment"># arbitrary key/value (the default Secret type)</span>
stringData:                   <span class="comment"># plaintext IN → apiserver base64-encodes it at rest</span>
  SECRET_KEY: "change-me-in-prod"
  DB_PASSWORD: "supersecret"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">…or imperatively</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret -n easypj-dev \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY='change-me-in-prod' --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD='supersecret'</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 base64 ≠ encryption</h5><p>Secrets are only <strong>base64-encoded</strong> and sit <strong>unencrypted in etcd</strong> by default. Never commit real Secrets to Git; protect them with <strong>encryption-at-rest</strong> + <strong>RBAC</strong> (Ch 11 / Ch 24). <code>data:</code> holds base64; <code>stringData:</code> takes plaintext and encodes it.</p></div>
                <h4>🔑 Creating the typed Secrets (not just generic)</h4>
                <p>Beyond <code>generic</code> Secrets, two typed ones come up constantly — registry pull creds and TLS certs. Memorise these imperative forms; they're far faster than hand-encoding the JSON/PEM:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">typed secret creation</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># docker-registry: private-image pull creds → reference via imagePullSecrets</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com \
  --docker-username=admin --docker-password='s3cret' \
  --docker-email=admin@example.com -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># tls: a cert + key (for Ingress HTTPS / webhooks)</span>
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \
  --cert=path/to/tls.crt --key=path/to/tls.key -n easypj-dev</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.5 Step 4 — The Deployment</h3>
                <p>The heart of it: pods from <code>easypj:v1</code>, configuration pulled from the ConfigMap + Secret via <code>envFrom</code>, resource bounds, and probes so traffic only flows when the app is ready.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-deploy.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-dev
  labels:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                       <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the template labels below</span>
      tier: web
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                       <span class="comment"># at most 1 EXTRA pod during a rollout</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0                 <span class="comment"># never drop below desired while rolling</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                     <span class="comment"># ← MUST match the selector above</span>
        tier: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
          name: http
        envFrom:                        <span class="comment"># inject ALL keys as env vars…</span>
        - configMapRef:
            name: easypj-config         <span class="comment"># ← every ConfigMap key</span>
        - secretRef:
            name: easypj-secret         <span class="comment"># ← every Secret key</span>
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"                 <span class="comment"># ← scheduling floor (0.1 core)</span>
            memory: "128Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"                 <span class="comment"># ← hard ceiling (0.5 core)</span>
            memory: "256Mi"
        readinessProbe:                 <span class="comment"># gates traffic — only Ready pods get requests</span>
          httpGet:
            path: /jobs/
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 10
          periodSeconds: 5
        livenessProbe:                  <span class="comment"># restarts a wedged container</span>
          httpGet:
            path: /jobs/
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 30
          periodSeconds: 10</code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>🔬 The deployment manifest — field by field</h4>
                <p>Every line above earns its place. Here's what each field does and why it's there:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it does</th><th>Get it wrong and…</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>replicas: 3</code></td><td>run 3 identical pods; the ReplicaSet maintains this count, recreating any that die</td><td>too few = no resilience; omitted = defaults to 1</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>selector.matchLabels</code></td><td>the query that says "these pods are mine" — links the Deployment to its pods</td><td>≠ template labels → apiserver rejects the manifest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>strategy: RollingUpdate</code></td><td>replace pods gradually; <code>maxSurge:1</code> adds one new pod before removing an old, <code>maxUnavailable:0</code> keeps full capacity</td><td><code>Recreate</code> instead = brief full downtime during updates</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>template.metadata.labels</code></td><td>stamped on every pod; what the selector (and the Service) matches on</td><td>≠ selector → rollout error; ≠ Service selector → 0 endpoints</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>image: easypj:v1</code></td><td>the container image; a tag change here is what triggers a rollout</td><td>wrong tag/registry → <code>ImagePullBackOff</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>envFrom</code></td><td>injects <em>every</em> key of the ConfigMap + Secret as env vars at container start</td><td>missing CM/Secret → <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>resources.requests</code></td><td>the guaranteed floor the scheduler reserves a node for</td><td>too high → pod stuck <code>Pending</code> (no node fits)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>resources.limits</code></td><td>the hard ceiling; CPU is throttled, memory over-limit is <code>OOMKilled</code></td><td>too low memory → container killed &amp; restarted in a loop</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>readinessProbe</code></td><td>"can it serve traffic <em>now</em>?" — controls EndpointSlice membership</td><td>fails → pod silently dropped from the Service (no traffic)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>livenessProbe</code></td><td>"is it still alive?" — kubelet restarts the container on failure</td><td>too aggressive → restart loop on a slow-starting app</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔢</div><h5>replicas + selector</h5><p>how many pods, and which pods are "mine" (by label)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📥</div><h5>envFrom</h5><p>inject every key of the ConfigMap &amp; Secret as env vars</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">⚖️</div><h5>resources</h5><p>requests = scheduling floor · limits = hard ceiling (Ch 15)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">❤️</div><h5>probes</h5><p>readiness gates traffic; liveness restarts a dead container</p></div>
                </div>
                <h4>❤️ Readiness vs liveness — same endpoint, opposite jobs</h4>
                <p>Both probes can hit <code>/jobs/</code>, yet they react in completely different ways — and confusing them is one of the most common troubleshooting traps on the exam:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>readinessProbe</th><th>livenessProbe</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Question it asks</strong></td><td>"can it serve traffic <em>right now</em>?"</td><td>"is it alive, or wedged?"</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>On failure</strong></td><td>pod is <strong>removed from the Service endpoints</strong> — traffic stops, the container keeps running</td><td>the kubelet <strong>restarts the container</strong></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Use it for</strong></td><td>warm-up, temporary overload, waiting on a dependency (DB not up yet)</td><td>deadlocks / hangs that only a restart can clear</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>easypj example</strong></td><td>app slow under load → drops out of rotation, recovers, rejoins — no restart storm</td><td>gunicorn worker deadlocks but the process stays up → restart to recover</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what each probe failure does</div>
<pre>
   readiness FAILS ─▶ ❌ removed from EndpointSlice ─▶ Service stops routing ─▶ (container still running, may recover)
   liveness  FAILS ─▶ 🔁 kubelet kills + restarts the container ─▶ restartCount++ ─▶ back to readiness gate
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ The classic restart-loop trap</h5><p>A liveness probe that's too aggressive (tiny <code>initialDelaySeconds</code>/<code>timeoutSeconds</code>) on a slow-booting app becomes a <strong>restart loop</strong> — the app never finishes starting before liveness kills it. The fix: a <strong>startupProbe</strong> for slow boots (it suspends liveness until the app is up), generous <strong>readiness</strong> for warm-up, and a forgiving <strong>liveness</strong> reserved only for true hangs.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>⚡ Scaffold then edit</h5><p><code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 -n easypj-dev $do &gt; d.yaml</code>, then add <code>envFrom</code>, <code>resources</code>, and probes — far faster than hand-writing.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.6 Step 5 — The Service</h3>
                <p>Pods come and go with new IPs; a Service gives easypj a <strong>stable VIP + DNS name</strong> and load-balances across the pods its selector matches.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">easypj-svc.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-dev
spec:
  type: ClusterIP             <span class="comment"># default — in-cluster stable VIP (Part 5 covers NodePort/LB/Ingress)</span>
  selector:                   <span class="comment"># MUST match the pod labels, or 0 endpoints</span>
    app: easypj
    tier: web
  ports:
  - name: http
    port: 80                  <span class="comment"># the Service port (what clients call)</span>
    targetPort: 8000          <span class="comment"># the container port (where gunicorn listens)</span>
    protocol: TCP</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">…or imperatively, then check endpoints</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj-dev
kubectl get svc,endpoints easypj-web -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># endpoints should list pod IPs</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service → Pod traffic flow</div>
<pre>
   client request
        │
        ▼
   ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🔌 Service: easypj-web        │
   │    ClusterIP: 10.96.45.123    │
   │    port: 80                   │
   └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                  │ selector: app=easypj, tier=web   (matches every Ready pod)
          ┌───────┴────────┐
          ▼                ▼
   ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐
   │ 📦 Pod 1     │ │ 📦 Pod 2     │   each listening on :8000
   │   :8000      │ │   :8000      │   labels: {app: easypj, tier: web}
   └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘
   kube-proxy load-balances (round-robin / random) across the EndpointSlice
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ 0 endpoints?</h5><p>Selector doesn't match pod labels, or no pod is Ready. Compare <code>spec.selector</code> with the pod labels, and check the readiness probe. (Types/ports in depth: Ch 4 §4.9 &amp; Part 5.)</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.7 Step 6 — Access It</h3>
                <p>For a quick local test, forward a port straight to the Service — no external exposure needed.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">port-forward → browser</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl -n easypj-dev port-forward svc/easypj-web 8000:80
<span class="comment"># now open  http://localhost:8000/jobs/   ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">port-forward — how it works</div>
<pre>
   your laptop                         Kubernetes cluster
   ┌─────────────────┐                ┌──────────────────────────────┐
   │ 🌐 browser       │                │                              │
   │ localhost:8000  │──kubectl tunnel─│─▶ 🔌 Service:80 ─▶ 📦 Pod:8000│
   │                 │                │      easypj-web      gunicorn │
   └─────────────────┘                └──────────────────────────────┘
   traffic path:  browser → kubectl → apiserver → pod   (a private, session-scoped tunnel)
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Reachable from</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>port-forward</code></td><td>your laptop only (kubectl tunnel)</td><td>quick local test / debug — what we did here</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong> (default)</td><td>inside the cluster only</td><td>pod-to-pod / service-to-service traffic</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td><code>&lt;anyNodeIP&gt;:30000–32767</code></td><td>simple external access without a cloud LB</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td><td>a cloud-provisioned external IP</td><td>production on a cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ExternalName</strong></td><td>no proxying — returns a DNS <code>CNAME</code> to an external host</td><td>alias an in-cluster name to an outside service (e.g. a managed DB)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Ingress</strong></td><td>one external IP, HTTP routing by host/path</td><td>many services behind one entrypoint (easypj graduates here, Part 5)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Port-forward is for testing only</h5><p>It tunnels through your kubectl session. Real external access uses <strong>NodePort</strong>, <strong>LoadBalancer</strong>, or <strong>Ingress</strong> (Part 5) — easypj graduates to Ingress there.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.8 Step 7 — Verify Everything</h3>
                <p>Confirm the whole stack before you call it done.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the verification sweep</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. See EVERYTHING in the namespace at once</span>
kubectl get all -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME                              READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-web-6d7f8c9b5d-abc12   1/1     Running   0          2m</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-web-6d7f8c9b5d-def34   1/1     Running   0          2m</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                 TYPE        CLUSTER-IP     PORT(S)   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># service/easypj-web   ClusterIP   10.96.45.123   80/TCP    1m</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                         READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web   2/2     2            2           2m</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                                    DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># replicaset.apps/easypj-web-6d7f8c9b5d   2         2         2       2m</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Endpoints — the proof the Service found Ready pods</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME         ENDPOINTS                         AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web   10.244.1.5:8000,10.244.2.3:8000   1m</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Detailed deployment state (events at the bottom)</span>
kubectl describe deployment easypj-web -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># 4. App logs — did Django actually start?</span>
kubectl logs deployment/easypj-web -n easypj-dev

<span class="comment"># 5. Confirm the config landed as env vars inside the container</span>
kubectl exec deployment/easypj-web -n easypj-dev -- env | grep -E 'DEBUG|SECRET'
<span class="comment"># DEBUG=False</span>
<span class="comment"># SECRET_KEY=change-me</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. ConfigMap + Secret present?</span>
kubectl get configmap,secret -n easypj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME                       DATA   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># configmap/easypj-config    2      3m</span>
<span class="comment"># secret/easypj-secret       Opaque   1    3m</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><h5>✅ Pod</h5><p><code>Running</code> &amp; <code>READY 1/1</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>✅ Service</h5><p>has endpoints (pod IPs)</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>✅ App</h5><p><code>/jobs/</code> returns 200</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><h5>✅ Config</h5><p>env vars present in the pod</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">resource dependency chain — and one-command cleanup</div>
<pre>
   🗂️ Namespace easypj-dev
      ├─ ⚙️ ConfigMap easypj-config ─┐
      ├─ 🔒 Secret easypj-secret    ─┤ envFrom
      ├─ 🚀 Deployment easypj-web ──▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ──▶ 📦 Pods
      └─ 🔌 Service easypj-web ──selector──▶ the Pods
   🧹 cleanup:  kubectl delete namespace easypj-dev   ← cascades, deletes ALL of the above
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>🩺 Pod not Running? — the troubleshooting decision tree</h4>
                <p>Start at <code>kubectl get pods -n easypj-dev</code>, read the STATUS, and follow it down to the exact fix. This single tree resolves the vast majority of "my pod won't start" exam tasks:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">pod STATUS → cause → fix</div>
<pre>
   kubectl get pods -n easypj-dev
     │
     ├─ Pending ──────────────▶ kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;   (read the Events)
     │     ├─ "Insufficient cpu/memory"   → lower requests, or add / free a node
     │     ├─ "node(s) didn't match …"    → check nodeSelector · affinity · taints/tolerations
     │     └─ node Unschedulable           → kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;
     │
     ├─ ImagePullBackOff / ErrImagePull ─▶ wrong image:tag, or the registry needs auth
     │     └─ fix the image name/tag, or add an imagePullSecret (Ch 2)
     │
     ├─ CreateContainerConfigError ──────▶ a referenced ConfigMap/Secret key is missing
     │     └─ create/fix it, then kubectl rollout restart deploy/easypj-web
     │
     ├─ CrashLoopBackOff ────▶ kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous   (last crashed container)
     │     ├─ app error          → fix the code / config
     │     ├─ missing env var      → check ConfigMap/Secret refs
     │     └─ permission denied    → check securityContext
     │
     ├─ Running, READY 0/1 ──▶ readiness probe failing → check probe path/port; needs more warm-up?
     │
     └─ Running, READY 1/1 ──▶ ✅ healthy
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>✅ Verify after each step (so you never debug the whole stack at once)</h5><p>After <strong>Namespace</strong> → <code>kubectl get ns</code>. After <strong>ConfigMap/Secret</strong> → <code>kubectl get cm,secret -n easypj-dev</code>. After <strong>Deployment</strong> → <code>kubectl get pods -n easypj-dev</code>. After <strong>Service</strong> → <code>kubectl get ep easypj-web -n easypj-dev</code> (endpoints = the proof it found pods). Catching a failure at its own step is far faster than untangling it at the end.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.9 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-9">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.9 What Just Happened? — The Full Flow</h3>
                <p>One <code>apply</code> set the whole reconciliation machine in motion. Here's the trace from Chapter 3, grounded on easypj — with where each step can fail.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">apply → running easypj, step by step</div>
<pre>
   kubectl apply -f k8s/
     ─▶ 📡 apiserver (authN → authZ → admission)
            ─▶ 🗄️ etcd            persist desired state
            ─▶ 🔄 controller-mgr  Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pods
            ─▶ 📋 scheduler       filter → score → BIND each pod to a node
                   ─▶ 🤖 kubelet  pull easypj:v1 → start container → 🌐 CNI gives a pod IP
                          ─▶ ❤️ readiness probe passes → pod joins endpoints
                          ─▶ 🔀 kube-proxy programs Service routing
     ─▶ ✅ easypj LIVE — reachable via the Service
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   where it breaks:  image not built/pushed → ImagePullBackOff  ·  targetPort≠8000 → refused
                     secret missing → env error  ·  selector≠labels → 0 endpoints
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Debug in flow order</h5><p>Pod not <code>Running</code>? → <code>describe pod</code> (image/scheduling). Running but no traffic? → <code>get endpoints</code> + readiness. Endpoints empty? → selector vs labels. Each symptom maps to one step above.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 8.10 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s8-10">
                <h3 class="section-h">8.10 ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets — the Full Picture</h3>
                <p>You used both above; here's everything the exam (and real ops) expects you to know about how they're consumed and how they behave.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>ConfigMap</th><th>Secret</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Purpose</strong></td><td>non-sensitive config (URLs, flags)</td><td>sensitive data (passwords, tokens, keys)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Stored as</strong></td><td>plain text</td><td>base64 (encoding, not encryption)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Size limit</strong></td><td>1 MiB</td><td>1 MiB</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Consume as</strong></td><td>env vars or volume files</td><td>env vars or volume files (kept in tmpfs RAM)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td>less restricted by default</td><td>should be tightly RBAC-restricted</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Create with</strong></td><td><code>kubectl create configmap</code></td><td><code>kubectl create secret generic</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <h4>🔌 How each is consumed</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Consume via</th><th>How</th><th>Live-updates?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>one key → env</td><td><code>valueFrom.configMapKeyRef</code> / <code>secretKeyRef</code></td><td>❌ fixed at pod start</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>all keys → env</td><td><code>envFrom: [configMapRef|secretRef]</code></td><td>❌ fixed at pod start</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>keys → files (volume)</td><td>mount the CM/Secret as a volume</td><td>✅ auto-updates (~kubelet sync)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>one file (<code>subPath</code>)</td><td>mount a single key with <code>subPath</code></td><td>❌ no live update</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>into <code>command</code>/<code>args</code></td><td>reference an injected env var as <code>$(VAR)</code> in the container's command/args</td><td>❌ fixed at pod start</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <h4>🎚️ envFrom vs env — inject everything, or pick &amp; rename</h4>
                <p><code>envFrom</code> injects <em>every</em> key as an env var (the simplest path — what §8.5 used). <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code> lets you inject <em>individual</em> keys and <strong>rename</strong> them to whatever the app expects:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">env-vs-envFrom.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># envFrom: inject ALL keys from a ConfigMap/Secret</span>
envFrom:
- configMapRef:
    name: easypj-config
- secretRef:
    name: easypj-secret

<span class="comment"># env: inject INDIVIDUAL keys — and optionally rename them</span>
env:
- name: DJANGO_SECRET            <span class="comment"># ← the name the container sees</span>
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef: { name: easypj-secret, key: SECRET_KEY }            <span class="comment"># ← key in the Secret</span>
- name: SETTINGS_MODULE          <span class="comment"># ← renamed from the ConfigMap key</span>
  valueFrom:
    configMapKeyRef: { name: easypj-config, key: DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE }</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 env-over-envFrom precedence</h5><p>If the same variable name arrives from several sources, a <em>later</em> <code>envFrom</code> source overrides an earlier one, and an explicit <code>env</code> entry overrides <strong>any</strong> <code>envFrom</code> value. Pattern: set bulk defaults with <code>envFrom</code>, then override one specific key with <code>env</code>.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Limits &amp; immutability</h5><p>Each ConfigMap/Secret is capped at <strong>1 MiB</strong>. Set <code>immutable: true</code> (v1.19+) to forbid edits and cut apiserver/etcd watch load — change it by delete-and-recreate. <strong>When to use immutable:</strong> a config referenced by thousands of pods (every pod watches every mutable CM/Secret; marking it immutable removes that watch load) and to guarantee no accidental mid-rollout config change.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>📝 <code>data</code> vs <code>stringData</code> — stop hand-encoding base64</h5><p>In a Secret manifest, <code>data:</code> values must be <strong>base64-encoded</strong>; <code>stringData:</code> lets you write <strong>plaintext</strong> and the apiserver encodes it for you (write-only — it never shows up on read). Prefer <code>stringData</code> for hand-written manifests. If a key appears in <em>both</em>, <code>stringData</code> wins. <code>kubectl create secret … --from-literal</code> handles the encoding either way.</p></div>
                <h4>🔒 Built-in Secret types</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>For</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>Opaque</code></td><td>arbitrary key/value (the default)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code></td><td>private-registry pull creds — modern <code>~/.docker/config.json</code> (<code>imagePullSecrets</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/dockercfg</code></td><td>legacy registry creds — the older <code>~/.dockercfg</code> format</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/tls</code></td><td>a TLS cert + key (Ingress, webhooks)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/basic-auth</code></td><td>basic-auth credentials (<code>username</code>/<code>password</code> keys)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/ssh-auth</code></td><td>an SSH private key (<code>ssh-privatekey</code> key)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code></td><td>a ServiceAccount token (Ch 25)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>bootstrap.kubernetes.io/token</code></td><td>a node-join bootstrap token (kubeadm, Ch 9)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Remember</h5><p>base64 is encoding, not encryption — Secrets sit unencrypted in etcd by default. Lock them down with <strong>encryption-at-rest</strong> (apiserver <code>--encryption-provider-config</code>, Ch 11) + <strong>RBAC</strong> (limit who can <code>get</code>/<code>patch secrets</code> in the namespace, Ch 24), and never commit real values.</p></div>
                <div class="analogy-box"><h5>🧠 In plain English</h5><p>A <strong>ConfigMap</strong> is the app's settings file; a <strong>Secret</strong> is the same idea for passwords (just base64'd, not locked). Inject as <em>env vars</em> and the values are printed once at startup (fixed); mount as a <em>volume</em> and it's a live noticeboard the app can re-read.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how config reaches the container &amp; how readiness gates traffic</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Two mechanisms make this deploy "just work": <strong>envFrom injection</strong> at start, and the <strong>readiness gate</strong> on endpoints.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   CONFIG INJECTION (once, at container start)
     ⚙️ ConfigMap + 🔒 Secret ──envFrom──▶ kubelet builds the container's env block ──▶ Django reads os.environ
     precedence: a later envFrom source, or an explicit `env:` entry, OVERRIDES an earlier key

   READINESS GATE (continuous)
     readinessProbe GET /jobs/ → 200 → pod Ready → EndpointSlice includes its IP → 🔀 Service routes to it
     probe fails → pod DROPPED from endpoints (no restart) → traffic stops, the pod keeps running
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 env is fixed at start:</strong> edit a ConfigMap/Secret and the running pods don't see it until you <code>kubectl rollout restart deploy/easypj-web</code>. A <em>volume-mounted</em> ConfigMap updates live (Ch 8 §8.10).</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>What it's for</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>initContainers</strong></td><td>run <code>manage.py migrate</code> to completion <em>before</em> the web container starts (Ch 16)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>downward API</strong></td><td>expose pod metadata (name, namespace, IP) to the app as env/files</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>minReadySeconds</code></td><td>a new pod must stay Ready this long before the rollout proceeds — guards against flapping</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Recreate vs Rolling</strong></td><td>schema-incompatible DB migrations may need <code>strategy: Recreate</code> to avoid two versions at once</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>③ One <code>apply</code>, a whole controller chain — what really happens when easypj goes live.</strong> Your single file is split on <code>---</code> into separate API requests, each written to etcd. Nothing is "deployed" yet — a chain of independent controllers, each watching for work, then turns those records into running containers. Every link only knows its own job:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl apply -f easypj-all.yaml   (Namespace → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service)
        │  5 objects POSTed to etcd, in file order
        ▼
   Deployment Controller   sees Deployment, no ReplicaSet  ─▶ creates ReplicaSet  (adds pod-template-hash)
        ▼
   ReplicaSet Controller   sees RS wants 2, has 0 pods     ─▶ creates 2 Pods      (ownerReference → RS)
        ▼
   Scheduler               sees Pods with nodeName=""       ─▶ filter+score+bind   (PATCH spec.nodeName)
        ▼
   kubelet (on that node)  sees Pod bound to me             ─▶ pull image · CRI create/start · mount CM+Secret · probes ─▶ Ready
        ▼
   Endpoints Controller    sees Service + Ready pods        ─▶ writes EndpointSlice (pod IPs)
        ▼
   kube-proxy (every node) sees EndpointSlice change        ─▶ programs iptables/IPVS  ─▶ Service IP now routes
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Each controller is blind to the others:</strong> the Deployment Controller never touches a Pod — it only makes a ReplicaSet and watches its status. This is why a stuck rollout is debugged top-down: <code>describe deploy</code> → <code>describe rs</code> → <code>describe pod</code>, following the <code>ownerReferences</code> chain down to whichever link is failing.</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Created</th><th>Depends on</th><th>If it's out of order…</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Namespace</strong></td><td>nothing</td><td>every later create fails with "namespace not found"</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>ConfigMap / Secret</strong></td><td>Namespace</td><td>Deployment still creates, but pods sit in <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code> until the CM/Secret exists</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Deployment</strong></td><td>Namespace, CM, Secret</td><td>works; if config is added later, the broken pods need a restart to pick it up</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Service</strong></td><td>Namespace only</td><td>fine <em>anytime</em> — the Endpoints Controller adds pod IPs dynamically as they turn Ready</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>Two details worth locking in. The <strong><code>pod-template-hash</code></strong> the Deployment Controller stamps onto each ReplicaSet (and its pods) is how one Deployment can own several ReplicaSets during a rollout without their selectors colliding — the hash is computed from the pod template, so a template change makes a new hash, hence a new ReplicaSet. And <strong><code>CreateContainerConfigError</code></strong> is specifically the kubelet failing step "mount CM/Secret" — the pod was scheduled fine; it's a missing/renamed ConfigMap or Secret key. Fix the config, and because the Deployment owns the pods, you can just <code>kubectl rollout restart</code> (or delete a pod) and the chain re-runs from the kubelet down.</p>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>④ What actually drives the chain — the reconciliation loop, and how readiness gates more than traffic.</strong> Each controller in the chain runs the same loop, and it's <em>level-triggered</em> (it acts on current state, not on events) — which is why the system is so self-healing:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   THE LOOP every controller runs (Deployment, ReplicaSet, Endpoints…)
     watch → an event lands in a per-controller WORK QUEUE → reconcile(key):
        read DESIRED (spec)  vs  ACTUAL (status/world)  →  make ONE change toward desired  →  return
     • success → drop the key.   failure → RE-QUEUE with exponential backoff (think 1s,2s,4s…)
     • level-triggered: even if an event is missed or the controller restarts, the next
       reconcile still sees the gap and fixes it — events are hints, state is the truth

   READINESS gates TWO things, not one
     ① traffic — only Ready pods are written into the EndpointSlice (you saw this in §8.5)
     ② rollout — during a Deployment update, a NEW pod must report Ready before the rollout
        removes an OLD one; maxUnavailable/maxSurge bound how many can be down/extra meanwhile
        → a readiness probe that never passes STALLS the rollout (old pods stay up, new ones never count)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 The debugging payoff:</strong> "my rollout is stuck at 1/3 updated" almost always means the new pods aren't passing readiness — not a Deployment bug. Check <code>kubectl rollout status</code>, then <code>describe</code> a new pod's readiness probe. And because reconcile is level-triggered with backoff, a transient failure (image registry briefly down) self-heals once the cause clears — you'll see <code>RESTARTS</code>/age climb, then the pod settle, with no manual intervention. (Rollout strategy mechanics in full: Ch 13.)</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The order:</strong> Namespace → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service → port-forward.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>envFrom</code></strong> injects every CM/Secret key as env vars (fixed at pod start).</li>
                    <li><strong>Readiness gates traffic</strong> — only Ready pods appear in the Service's endpoints.</li>
                    <li><strong>Verify</strong> with <code>get all</code> + endpoints; a Service with 0 endpoints = selector/label or readiness problem.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cleanup is one command:</strong> <code>kubectl delete namespace easypj-dev</code> cascades.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ First-Deployment Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Image not built/pushed.</strong> Pod stuck <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> — build &amp; push <code>easypj:v1</code> (Ch 2), or check the registry/secret.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>targetPort</code> ≠ containerPort.</strong> Service says 80→8000 but the app listens elsewhere → "connection refused."</li>
                    <li><strong>Secret values not base64.</strong> Use <code>stringData</code> (plaintext) or pre-encode for <code>data</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>selector ≠ pod labels.</strong> Service ends up with 0 endpoints — match them exactly.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Scaffold the Deployment</strong>: <code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 -n easypj-dev $do</code>, then add envFrom/probes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Make the Service fast</strong>: <code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj-dev</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>First check after deploying</strong>: <code>kubectl get endpoints easypj-web -n easypj-dev</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 8)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the deploy toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DEBUG=False -n easypj-dev
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret --from-literal=SECRET_KEY=x -n easypj-dev
kubectl apply -f k8s/                                 <span class="comment"># deploy + service</span>
kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj-dev
kubectl get all,endpoints -n easypj-dev ; kubectl logs deploy/easypj-web -n easypj-dev
kubectl -n easypj-dev port-forward svc/easypj-web 8000:80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — Deploy easypj from scratch in under 10 minutes</h5>
                <p>No peeking — this is the capstone of Part 2:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Create namespace <code>easypj-dev</code> and make it your default.</li>
                    <li>Create a ConfigMap (<code>DEBUG=False</code>, <code>ALLOWED_HOSTS=*</code>) and a Secret (<code>SECRET_KEY</code>).</li>
                    <li>Deploy <code>easypj:v1</code> (1 replica, port 8000, envFrom both, a readiness probe on <code>/jobs/</code>).</li>
                    <li>Expose it (80→8000), confirm endpoints, port-forward, and hit <code>/jobs/</code>.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev
kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DEBUG=False --from-literal=ALLOWED_HOSTS='*'
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret --from-literal=SECRET_KEY='change-me'
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; d.yaml
# edit d.yaml: ports 8000 · envFrom configMapRef+secretRef · readinessProbe httpGet /jobs/:8000
kubectl apply -f d.yaml
kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000
kubectl get endpoints easypj-web        <span class="comment"># pod IPs listed?</span>
kubectl port-forward svc/easypj-web 8000:80   <span class="comment"># → http://localhost:8000/jobs/</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Why:</strong> this exact ns→CM→Secret→Deployment→Service→verify loop is the template behind most Workloads/Services exam tasks.</p>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 8 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">The deploy workflow, recalled — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a namespace, Deployment, and Service for easypj imperatively.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate manifest</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl create namespace easypj-dev
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 -n easypj-dev
kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj-dev</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Imperative is fastest for simple objects; add <code>$do &gt; f.yaml</code> when you need to tweak fields the flags can't set.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Inject <code>DB_HOST</code> into the pod from a ConfigMap.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">configMap → env</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">env:
- name: DB_HOST
  valueFrom:
    configMapKeyRef:
      name: easypj-config
      key: DB_HOST</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>One key → one env var. For ALL keys use <code>envFrom: [configMapRef: {name: easypj-config}]</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Expose <code>SECRET_KEY</code> from a Secret as an env var.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">secret → env</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">env:
- name: SECRET_KEY
  valueFrom:
    secretKeyRef:
      name: easypj-secret
      key: SECRET_KEY</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Same shape as a ConfigMap, but <code>secretKeyRef</code>. <code>envFrom: [secretRef: …]</code> injects every key.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The pod is <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. Diagnose and fix.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">read pod events</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj-dev | grep -A6 Events   <span class="comment"># exact pull error</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>"not found" → wrong name/tag · "unauthorized" → add <code>imagePullSecrets</code> · timeout → registry/network. Build &amp; push <code>easypj:v1</code> if it doesn't exist.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Port-forward the Service and hit <code>/jobs/</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">access locally</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl -n easypj-dev port-forward svc/easypj-web 8000:80   <span class="comment"># → localhost:8000/jobs/</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>For testing only — production access uses NodePort/LoadBalancer/Ingress (Part 5).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Scale <code>easypj-web</code> to 3 replicas.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">scale</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl scale deploy easypj-web --replicas=3 -n easypj-dev</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The ReplicaSet reconciles to the new count; verify with <code>get deploy</code> (3/3) and <code>get endpoints</code> (3 IPs).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Add a readiness probe so traffic only flows when <code>/jobs/</code> responds.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">readiness probe</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">readinessProbe:
  httpGet: { path: /jobs/, port: 8000 }
  initialDelaySeconds: 5
  periodSeconds: 10</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A failing readiness probe removes the pod from endpoints (no restart) — clients never hit a not-yet-ready pod.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Confirm the Service has endpoints — and what if it doesn't?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check endpoints</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get endpoints easypj-web -n easypj-dev</code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Empty = selector doesn't match pod labels OR no pod is Ready. Compare selector vs labels and check the readiness probe.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Roll the image to <code>easypj:v2</code> and watch the rollout.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">rollout</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web web=easypj:v2 -n easypj-dev
kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># undo: kubectl rollout undo …</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Rolling update by default; the old ReplicaSet is kept at 0 for instant rollback (Ch 13).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is <code>Pending</code> — which command shows why, and where do you look?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
<div class="code-block-wrapper">
<div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">read pod events</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj-dev   <span class="comment"># read the Events at the bottom</span></code></pre>
</div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>"FailedScheduling" → no node fits (resources/taints/selector). Pending also covers image pulls — Events tells you which.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch8-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 8 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Real deploy "build it / fix it" tasks with the answers.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Walk through the complete workflow to deploy a Django application (anihpj) to Kubernetes. What resources do you need to create, and in what order?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create a namespace</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create a namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace anihpj-dev

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create ConfigMap for non-sensitive config</span>
kubectl create configmap anihpj-config \
  --from-literal=DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=anihpj.settings \
  --from-literal=ALLOWED_HOSTS="*" \
  -n anihpj-dev

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create Secret for sensitive data</span>
kubectl create secret generic anihpj-secret \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY='your-django-secret-key' \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD='dbpass123' \
  -n anihpj-dev

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create Deployment</span>
kubectl create deployment anihpj \
  --image=anihpj:latest --replicas=2 \
  -n anihpj-dev --dry-run=client -o yaml > deploy.yaml
<span class="comment"># Edit deploy.yaml to add envFrom, ports, probes</span>
kubectl apply -f deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Expose with a Service</span>
kubectl expose deployment anihpj \
  --port=80 --target-port=8000 \
  --type=NodePort -n anihpj-dev</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <p><strong>Order matters:</strong> Namespace → ConfigMap/Secrets → Deployment → Service</p>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The order is critical because each resource depends on the previous: the Deployment references ConfigMaps and Secrets (they must exist first or the pod will fail), and the Service targets pods created by the Deployment. On the CKA, questions often present the end state and ask you to recreate it. Always check: does the namespace exist? Are ConfigMaps/Secrets created? Are labels matching between Deployment and Service selectors?</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You deploy an application and the pod stays in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code>. The <code>kubectl logs</code> output shows: <em>"ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'django'"</em>. What is the problem and how do you fix it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify the logs</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Verify the logs</span>
kubectl logs pod/anihpj-7f8d6c9b4-x2k9p -n anihpj-dev

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the image — the issue is the container image</span>
<span class="comment"># Either wrong base image or dependencies not installed</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Debug inside the container</span>
kubectl run debug-pod --image=anihpj:latest --rm -it \
  --command -- pip list
<span class="comment"># If django is not listed, the Dockerfile is wrong</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Rebuild the Docker image with proper requirements</span>
<span class="comment"># In the Dockerfile:</span>
FROM python:3.11-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt  <span class="comment"># Must include django</span>
COPY . .
CMD ["python", "manage.py", "runserver", "0.0.0.0:8000"]</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> means the container starts, crashes, and Kubernetes keeps restarting it with exponential backoff. Common causes: missing dependencies (like here), incorrect CMD/ENTRYPOINT, missing environment variables, or permission issues. On the CKA, the debugging pattern is always the same: <code>kubectl describe pod</code> → check Events section → <code>kubectl logs</code> → check application error. If the container crashes too fast for logs, use <code>kubectl logs --previous</code> to see the previous container's logs.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you pass environment variables from a ConfigMap and a Secret to a Deployment? Show both <code>envFrom</code> and individual <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> approaches.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">envfrom — loads all keys from configmap/secret</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Method 1: envFrom — loads ALL keys from ConfigMap/Secret</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: easypj:1.0
    envFrom:
    - configMapRef:
        name: app-config      <span class="comment"># All keys become env vars</span>
    - secretRef:
        name: app-secrets     <span class="comment"># All keys become env vars</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: env with valueFrom — load specific keys</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: easypj:1.0
    env:
    - name: DB_HOST            <span class="comment"># Env var name in container</span>
      valueFrom:
        configMapKeyRef:
          name: app-config     <span class="comment"># ConfigMap name</span>
          key: database_host   <span class="comment"># Key in the ConfigMap</span>
    - name: DB_PASSWORD
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: app-secrets    <span class="comment"># Secret name</span>
          key: db_password     <span class="comment"># Key in the Secret</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>envFrom</code> is faster for bulk loading — every key in the ConfigMap/Secret becomes an environment variable with the same name. <code>env.valueFrom</code> gives you control: you can rename keys and pick only specific values. On the CKA, <code>envFrom</code> is faster to type but <code>valueFrom</code> is more common in exam questions. Key gotcha: if a ConfigMap key has a hyphen (like <code>db-host</code>), it becomes an invalid shell variable — use <code>env.valueFrom</code> to rename it (e.g., <code>DB_HOST</code>).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Your Deployment has <code>replicas: 3</code> but only 2 pods are running. The third pod is in <code>Pending</code> state. How do you diagnose and fix this?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check pod status</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pod status</span>
kubectl get pods -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME                     READY   STATUS    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># anihpj-xxx-abc           1/1     Running   5m</span>
<span class="comment"># anihpj-xxx-def           1/1     Running   5m</span>
<span class="comment"># anihpj-xxx-ghi           0/1     Pending   5m</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Describe the Pending pod — check Events</span>
kubectl describe pod anihpj-xxx-ghi -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  FailedScheduling  0/3 nodes are available:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1 node(s) had taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane},</span>
<span class="comment">#   2 node(s) had insufficient cpu</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Common causes and fixes:</span>
<span class="comment"># a) Insufficient resources → reduce requests or add nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># b) Node taints → add tolerations or untaint nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># c) NodeSelector/affinity mismatch → fix labels</span>
<span class="comment"># d) PVC not bound → check StorageClass/PV availability</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix for insufficient CPU:</span>
kubectl top nodes    <span class="comment"># Check resource usage</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit deployment to reduce CPU requests</span>
kubectl edit deployment anihpj -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># Or add a new node to the cluster</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>Pending</code> means the scheduler cannot find a suitable node. The Events section of <code>kubectl describe pod</code> tells you exactly why. The CKA frequently tests this scenario. The diagnosis tree: <strong>Pending → FailedScheduling → read the message</strong>. Messages include: "insufficient cpu/memory" (resource constraint), "didn't match node selector" (label mismatch), "had taint" (toleration needed), "unbound PVC" (storage issue). Always check <code>kubectl describe</code> Events first — the answer is almost always right there.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you update the container image of a running Deployment from <code>anihpj:v1</code> to <code>anihpj:v2</code> without editing YAML? What happens during the rollout?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">imperative image update (fastest on cka)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative image update (fastest on CKA)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/anihpj app=anihpj:v2 -n anihpj-dev

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/anihpj -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "anihpj" rollout to finish: 1 out of 3 new replicas updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment "anihpj" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens during the rollout:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. New ReplicaSet created with v2 image</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. New pods start coming up (controlled by maxSurge)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Old pods start terminating (controlled by maxUnavailable)</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Default: RollingUpdate with 25% maxSurge, 25% maxUnavailable</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify the update</span>
kubectl get replicasets -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME                DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># anihpj-7f8d6c9b4   0         0         0       10m   ← old (v1)</span>
<span class="comment"># anihpj-9a1b2c3d4   3         3         3       1m    ← new (v2)</span>

<span class="comment"># Undo if something goes wrong</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/anihpj -n anihpj-dev</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl set image</code> is the fastest way to update an image on the CKA. The Deployment controller creates a new ReplicaSet, gradually scales it up while scaling the old one down. This is a <strong>rolling update</strong> — the application has zero downtime because old pods serve traffic until new pods are ready. The old ReplicaSet is kept (scaled to 0) for rollback. <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> simply scales the old ReplicaSet back up and the new one down. You can also rollback to a specific revision: <code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/anihpj --to-revision=2</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a complete Service manifest that exposes the anihpj Deployment on NodePort 30080. What is the relationship between <code>port</code>, <code>targetPort</code>, and <code>nodePort</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yaml</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: anihpj-svc
  namespace: anihpj-dev
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: anihpj          <span class="comment"># Must match Deployment pod labels</span>
  ports:
  - port: 80             <span class="comment"># Service port (cluster-internal)</span>
    targetPort: 8000     <span class="comment"># Container port (where app listens)</span>
    nodePort: 30080      <span class="comment"># Node port (external access)</span>
    protocol: TCP</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Field</th>
                                            <th>What It Does</th>
                                            <th>Example</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>port</code></td>
                                            <td>Service's own port (other pods connect here)</td>
                                            <td>anihpj-svc:80</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>targetPort</code></td>
                                            <td>Container port (where the app listens)</td>
                                            <td>8000 (Django default)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>nodePort</code></td>
                                            <td>External port on every node (30000-32767)</td>
                                            <td>&lt;NodeIP&gt;:30080</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Traffic flow: External → <code>NodeIP:nodePort</code> → <code>ServiceIP:port</code> → <code>PodIP:targetPort</code>. The <code>selector</code> must match the pod labels from the Deployment. If <code>targetPort</code> is omitted, it defaults to the same value as <code>port</code>. If <code>nodePort</code> is omitted, Kubernetes assigns a random port in 30000-32767. On the CKA, the fastest approach: <code>kubectl expose deployment anihpj --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=NodePort --dry-run=client -o yaml > svc.yaml</code>, then add the nodePort value.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to deploy the anihpj app to a specific namespace <code>production</code> that doesn't exist yet. Write the complete sequence of commands.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create the namespace</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace production

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Set context to avoid -n flag on every command</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=production

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify context</span>
kubectl config view --minify | grep namespace

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create deployment (now in production namespace)</span>
kubectl create deployment anihpj --image=anihpj:v1 --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Expose it</span>
kubectl expose deployment anihpj --port=80 --target-port=8000

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify everything</span>
kubectl get all
<span class="comment"># Shows deployment, replicaset, pods, service all in production</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: Do it all without switching context</span>
kubectl create namespace production
kubectl create deployment anihpj --image=anihpj:v1 --replicas=3 -n production
kubectl expose deployment anihpj --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n production</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA exam, <strong>every question specifies a context and namespace</strong>. Always switch context first: <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code>. Then set the namespace: <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;</code>. This prevents the most common exam mistake — deploying to the wrong namespace. If a namespace doesn't exist, create it first. If you forget <code>-n</code> and deploy to default, you'll need to recreate in the correct namespace. Practice this workflow until it's automatic.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Your deployment is running but when you curl the Service, you get "Connection refused". How do you systematically debug this?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">debug chain: pod → service → endpoint → network</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Debug chain: Pod → Service → Endpoint → Network</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Is the pod actually running and ready?</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># Check READY column: 1/1 means healthy</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Can you reach the app directly in the pod?</span>
kubectl exec -it anihpj-xxx -- curl localhost:8000
<span class="comment"># If this fails → app issue, not K8s</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Does the Service have Endpoints?</span>
kubectl get endpoints anihpj-svc
<span class="comment"># If ENDPOINTS is empty → label mismatch!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check label match</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
kubectl describe svc anihpj-svc | grep Selector
<span class="comment"># Compare Selector with pod labels — must match</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify port mapping</span>
kubectl describe svc anihpj-svc
<span class="comment"># Check: TargetPort should match containerPort</span>

<span class="comment"># Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Labels don't match → fix selector or pod labels</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. targetPort ≠ containerPort → fix port mapping</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. App listening on 127.0.0.1 not 0.0.0.0 → fix app config</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Pod not Ready → check readinessProbe</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The debug chain for "can't reach service" is: <strong>Pod running? → App listening? → Endpoints exist? → Labels match? → Ports correct?</strong> The most common issue is empty Endpoints, caused by label mismatch between Service <code>selector</code> and pod labels. The second most common is port mismatch — the Service <code>targetPort</code> must match the container's actual listening port. On the CKA, always check Endpoints first: <code>kubectl get ep &lt;service-name&gt;</code>. No endpoints = no pods match the selector.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you scale a Deployment from 2 replicas to 5, and then verify that all pods are running and healthy?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl scale (imperative, fastest)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl scale (imperative, fastest)</span>
kubectl scale deployment anihpj --replicas=5 -n anihpj-dev

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl patch</span>
kubectl patch deployment anihpj -n anihpj-dev \
  -p '{"spec":{"replicas":5}}'

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl edit</span>
kubectl edit deployment anihpj -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># Change spec.replicas from 2 to 5</span>

<span class="comment"># Verification:</span>
kubectl get deployment anihpj -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># NAME    READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># anihpj  5/5     5            5           10m</span>

kubectl get pods -n anihpj-dev
<span class="comment"># All 5 pods should show STATUS: Running, READY: 1/1</span>

<span class="comment"># Watch in real-time</span>
kubectl get pods -n anihpj-dev -w
<span class="comment"># Shows pods being created live</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl scale</code> is the fastest method on the CKA. The Deployment controller adjusts the ReplicaSet's replica count, and the ReplicaSet controller creates new pods. To verify health: READY x/x means all containers are ready, UP-TO-DATE means all pods match the current template, AVAILABLE means pods have passed readinessProbe. If AVAILABLE < READY, some pods aren't passing their readiness checks. If pods stay Pending after scaling, it usually means insufficient node resources.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to generate the YAML for a complete application stack (Namespace, ConfigMap, Deployment, Service) from scratch using only imperative commands and redirect to a single file. How?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">generate all resources into one file using ---</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Generate all resources into one file using ---</span>

<span class="comment"># Namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace easypj-web --dry-run=client -o yaml > stack.yaml

<span class="comment"># ConfigMap (append with >>)</span>
echo "---" >> stack.yaml
kubectl create configmap easypj-web-config \
  --from-literal=APP_ENV=production \
  --from-literal=LOG_LEVEL=info \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml >> stack.yaml

<span class="comment"># Deployment</span>
echo "---" >> stack.yaml
kubectl create deployment easypj-web \
  --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml >> stack.yaml

<span class="comment"># Service</span>
echo "---" >> stack.yaml
kubectl create service clusterip easypj-web \
  --tcp=80:8000 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml >> stack.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify the file</span>
cat stack.yaml

<span class="comment"># Apply everything at once</span>
kubectl apply -f stack.yaml

<span class="comment"># Or validate first</span>
kubectl apply -f stack.yaml --dry-run=server</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is the <strong>CKA power technique</strong>: generate YAML imperatively, combine into one file, edit as needed, apply. The <code>> file.yaml</code> creates/overwrites, <code>>> file.yaml</code> appends. The <code>---</code> separator between documents is critical. This approach is faster than writing YAML from scratch. After generating, edit the file to add anything imperative commands don't support: <code>envFrom</code>, <code>volumeMounts</code>, <code>probes</code>, <code>resources</code>, <code>nodeSelector</code>, etc. On the CKA, speed matters — generate the boilerplate, then customize.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 8 Visual Summary — easypj live, end to end</div>
<pre>
   🗂️ Namespace easypj-dev
      ⚙️ ConfigMap ─┐                🔒 Secret ─┐
      (DEBUG,        │ envFrom                  │ envFrom
       ALLOWED_HOSTS)│                          │ (SECRET_KEY, DB_PASSWORD)
                     ▼                          ▼
      🚀 Deployment easypj-web ─▶ 🎛️ ReplicaSet ─▶ 📦 Pod(s)  image easypj:v1 :8000
                     │ readiness /jobs/ → Ready                 │
      🔌 Service easypj-web  80 ─▶ 8000  ◀──selector app=easypj,tier=web──┘
                     ▲
      💻 you ──port-forward 8000:80──▶ http://localhost:8000/jobs/  ✅
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ns → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service → port-forward → /jobs/. That's Part 2. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> you isolated easypj in a <strong>Namespace</strong>, put plain settings in a <strong>ConfigMap</strong> and credentials in a <strong>Secret</strong>, ran the app as a <strong>Deployment</strong> (pulling both via <code>envFrom</code>, gated by a readiness probe), fronted it with a <strong>Service</strong> (80→8000), and reached <code>/jobs/</code> through a port-forward.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> this exact order — <strong>ns → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service → verify</strong> — is the template behind most Workloads &amp; Services exam tasks. Generate with <code>$do</code>, wire labels/selectors carefully, and always confirm the Service has endpoints.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH8-INSERT:: -->
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART 2 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part2-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟢 Part 2 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Hands-On Basics — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You arrived with a mental model; you leave with a cluster you built, <code>kubectl</code> in your fingers, YAML you can read &amp; validate, and easypj running and reachable at <code>/jobs/</code>. Everything in Parts 3–14 operates on this foundation.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Part 2 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Environment (Ch 5):</strong> kubeconfig = clusters + users + contexts; <em>switch context &amp; set the namespace first</em>, every single task.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl (Ch 6):</strong> verb + resource + name + flags; <code>explain</code> is your manual; generate with <code>$do</code> (never hand-write); jsonpath/custom-columns extract anything; climb the debug ladder logs→exec→<code>debug</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>YAML (Ch 7):</strong> the 4 required fields; spaces not tabs; <em>selector == template labels</em>; validate with <code>--dry-run=server</code> + <code>diff</code> before applying.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deploy (Ch 8):</strong> Namespace → ConfigMap → Secret → Deployment → Service → port-forward → <code>/jobs/</code>; <code>envFrom</code> injects config; readiness gates traffic.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> kubectl speed + correct context/namespace + clean YAML = passing.</li>
                    <li>🔑 <code>config use-context · k get/desc/logs/exec/apply · explain · $do · port-forward</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 2 Visual Summary — the full deploy pipeline, easypj edition</div>
<pre>
   💻 laptop ─▶ kubeconfig / context   (use-context ▶ verify ▶ set -n)
        │ generate:  kubectl create … $do &gt; f.yaml ─▶ edit ─▶ apply -f
        ▼
   ☸️ cluster (Minikube now · kubeadm in Part 3)
        🗂️ ns easypj-dev
           ⚙️ ConfigMap + 🔒 Secret ──envFrom──▶ 🚀 Deployment ──▶ 📦 Pods (easypj:v1 :8000)
                                                  🔌 Service 80→8000 ◀──selector app=easypj
        ▼ port-forward 8000:80
   http://localhost:8000/jobs/  ✅
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   you now DRIVE the cluster: pick it, speak YAML to it, and ship easypj onto it. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 2 turned theory into hands. You stand up a cluster and point <code>kubectl</code> at it through a <strong>context</strong>; you generate and validate <strong>YAML</strong> instead of hand-writing it; and you assemble easypj from the five core objects in dependency order, then prove it works.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the loop is always the same — <strong>set context → generate with <code>$do</code> → validate (<code>--dry-run=server</code>/<code>diff</code>) → apply → verify (<code>get all</code> + endpoints)</strong>. Master that rhythm and the rest of the guide is variations on it.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END PART 2 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 3 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part3">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 3 · Cluster Installation &amp; Configuration</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Build &amp; Own the Cluster</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 9–12 · Intermediate → Advanced · <em>"Build it, configure it, protect it"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate → Advanced</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 4 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~10 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 CKA Cluster Architecture (25%)</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Part 2 drove a cluster someone else built. Part 3 is where you <strong>become the administrator</strong>: stand up a multi-node cluster from bare Linux nodes with <strong>kubeadm</strong>, configure the control plane and kubelet, back up and restore <strong>etcd</strong> (the one skill that can save a cluster), and manage the control plane's static pods. This is the heart of the CKA's <strong>Cluster Architecture (25%)</strong> domain.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you'll be able to do by the end:</strong> build a 3-node cluster from scratch, recover a lost join token, snapshot &amp; restore etcd, safely edit a control-plane component, and reason about every required port — the exact tasks the exam sets.</div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — Part 3</div>
<pre>
   Ch 9 — INSTALL          ─▶   Ch 10 — CONFIGURE        ─▶   Ch 11 — etcd            ─▶   Ch 12 — CONTROL PLANE
   kubeadm: build it            kubeconfig · apiserver        back up &amp; restore            static pods · health
   prep → init → CNI → join     kubelet · admission           the cluster database          modify · tune · CRI/CNI/CSI
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: from bare Linux nodes to a configured, backed-up, self-managed cluster you fully control. ☸️
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 9 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch9">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 9 · Installing Kubernetes with kubeadm</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">A cluster doesn't appear by magic — <strong>kubeadm</strong> builds one from ordinary Linux machines: it generates the certificates, launches the control plane, and hands you a command to join workers. The exam may give you a half-built or broken cluster and expect you to finish or fix it, so you must know <em>every</em> step cold.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~3 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Heavily Tested</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🔧 kubeadm</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ What kubeadm does for you — and what it deliberately leaves to you</li>
                    <li>✅ Prepare <em>every</em> node: swap, kernel modules, sysctl, containerd cgroup driver, the toolchain</li>
                    <li>✅ <code>kubeadm init</code> the control plane and wire up <code>kubeconfig</code></li>
                    <li>✅ Install a <strong>CNI</strong> plugin (and why nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until you do)</li>
                    <li>✅ <code>kubeadm join</code> workers — and recover a lost/expired join token</li>
                    <li>✅ Verify the cluster, open the right firewall ports, and reset cleanly</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">"create clusters with kubeadm" <span class="cka-weight">CORE TASK</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 Why this chapter is exam gold</h5>
                <p>"Prepare infra" and "create a cluster with kubeadm" are explicit CKA competencies — and a broken cluster is a favourite troubleshooting scenario. The five moves below are a script you should be able to run from memory; the exam rewards muscle memory here more than almost anywhere else.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Order is everything:</strong> prep <em>all</em> nodes → <code>init</code> the CP → install a CNI → <code>join</code> workers. Skip a step and nothing works.</li>
                    <li><strong>The #1 gotcha:</strong> all nodes <code>NotReady</code> after init = no CNI installed yet (not a kubelet bug).</li>
                    <li><strong>Save the join command</strong> — and know how to regenerate it when you didn't.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 9 Roadmap — bare nodes → a verified multi-node cluster</div>
<pre>
   9.1 WHAT is kubeadm ──▶ 9.2 PREP every node ──▶ 9.3 INIT control plane ──▶ 9.4 INSTALL CNI
   does/doesn't            swap·modules·sysctl       kubeadm init + kubeconfig    pods Ready only now
        │                  ·containerd·toolchain                                       │
        └──────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                        ▼
   9.5 JOIN workers ──▶ 9.6 VERIFY ──▶ 9.7 PRACTICE ──▶ 9.8 PORTS to open
   token + ca-hash       all Ready       build/recover     6443·2379-80·10250·…
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-1"><strong>9.1</strong> What is kubeadm?</a></td><td>What builds a cluster, and what's left to me?</td><td>kubeadm does certs + control plane + join; you bring infra + CNI.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-2"><strong>9.2</strong> Pre-requisites</a></td><td>What must be true on every node first?</td><td>swap off · modules · sysctl · containerd (SystemdCgroup) · toolchain + hold.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-3"><strong>9.3</strong> Init control plane</a></td><td>How do I create the cluster?</td><td><code>kubeadm init</code> → copy admin.conf → SAVE the join command.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-4"><strong>9.4</strong> Install a CNI</a></td><td>Why are my nodes NotReady?</td><td>No pod network until a CNI is applied — then nodes go Ready.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-5"><strong>9.5</strong> Join workers</a></td><td>How do nodes join — and if I lost the token?</td><td><code>kubeadm join</code> with token + ca-hash; regenerate with <code>token create</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-6"><strong>9.6</strong> Verify</a></td><td>Is the cluster actually healthy?</td><td>all nodes Ready · kube-system Running · deploy easypj as a smoke test.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-7"><strong>9.7</strong> Practice</a></td><td>How do I drill this to reflex?</td><td>build 3-node, recover token, add CP node, fix a NotReady worker.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s9-8"><strong>9.8</strong> Required ports</a></td><td>What firewall ports must be open?</td><td>6443 · 2379-2380 · 10250 · 10259/10257 · 30000-32767.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s9-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.1 What is kubeadm?</h3>
                <p><code>kubeadm</code> is the <strong>official cluster bootstrapper</strong>. Point it at a freshly-installed Linux machine and it does the genuinely hard, error-prone parts of standing up Kubernetes: it generates a whole <strong>PKI</strong> (a CA plus certs for every component), writes the control-plane <strong>static-pod manifests</strong>, brings up <strong>etcd</strong>, configures the <strong>kubelet</strong>, and prints a <code>join</code> command that lets workers enrol securely. What it deliberately does <em>not</em> do is provision machines or choose your networking — those stay your call.</p>
                <div class="analogy-box"><h5>🧠 In plain English</h5><p>Think of kubeadm as the <strong>contractor who builds the house</strong>, not the one who buys the land or picks the paint. You supply the plot (VMs, OS, runtime); kubeadm pours the foundation and frames the structure (certs, control plane, etcd); you still choose the "plumbing" (the CNI network plugin) and move in the furniture (your apps). That division is exactly why a fresh <code>kubeadm init</code> cluster sits <code>NotReady</code> until <em>you</em> install a CNI.</p></div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">kubeadm — what it does vs what it leaves to you</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ YOU provide (the infra)          🔧 kubeadm handles (the hard parts)        ❌ NOT kubeadm's job
   ─────────────────────────────      ──────────────────────────────────        ─────────────────────────
   VMs / bare metal                   ✅ bootstrap the control plane             VM provisioning
   OS installed (Ubuntu/etc.)         ✅ generate the TLS PKI (CA + certs)       (Terraform / cloud console)
   network connectivity               ✅ create the etcd member                  OS install &amp; patching
   swap disabled                      ✅ configure &amp; start the kubelet           CNI install (you choose it)
   container runtime (containerd)     ✅ render static-pod manifests             LoadBalancer for HA CP
   kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl installed  ✅ issue the bootstrap join token          app deployment (kubectl's job)
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5><code>kubeadm init</code></h5><p>Run <strong>once, on the control-plane node</strong>. Generates the PKI, brings up apiserver + etcd + scheduler + controller-manager as static pods, and prints the join command.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div><h5><code>kubeadm join</code></h5><p>Run <strong>on every worker</strong> (and extra control-plane nodes). Uses a <em>token</em> + <em>CA cert hash</em> to authenticate to the cluster and register the node.</p></div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the build pipeline — six moves, in order</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ provision    ⚙️ prerequisites   🔧 kubeadm init    🌐 install CNI     🔗 kubeadm join    ✅ cluster Ready
      VMs/OS    ─▶  swap·modules   ─▶  (control plane) ─▶ (pod network)  ─▶  (each worker)  ─▶  all nodes Ready
                    ·containerd                            nodes Ready                          → run easypj
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   skip a step and the next one fails — this exact order is what the exam tests.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 kubeadm vs the alternatives</h5><p><strong>kubeadm</strong> = the CKA-tested, do-it-yourself bootstrapper (you own upgrades, etcd, certs). <strong>Managed</strong> services (EKS/GKE/AKS) hide the control plane entirely. <strong>kops/kubespray</strong> wrap kubeadm with infra automation. The exam uses kubeadm precisely because it makes <em>you</em> the administrator of every layer.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.2 Pre-requisites for All Nodes</h3>
                <p>Before <code>kubeadm</code> will run, <strong>every node</strong> — control plane <em>and</em> workers — must be prepared identically. Each of these five steps fixes a specific way the kubelet or runtime would otherwise refuse to work, and each is a classic "broken node" the exam asks you to repair. Do all five on every machine.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🖥️</div><h5>Hardware</h5><p>2+ CPUs · 2 GB+ RAM · a <strong>unique</strong> hostname &amp; MAC per node · full network connectivity</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🚫</div><h5>Swap OFF</h5><p>the kubelet refuses to start with swap on — disable it now <em>and</em> in <code>/etc/fstab</code></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🐳</div><h5>Container runtime</h5><p>containerd (recommended) with the <strong>systemd</strong> cgroup driver</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Toolchain</h5><p><code>kubeadm</code> + <code>kubelet</code> + <code>kubectl</code>, same version, <strong>held</strong></p></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Step A — disable swap</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · swap</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo swapoff -a                              <span class="comment"># off right now (this boot)</span>
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab     <span class="comment"># and permanently (comment the fstab line)</span>
free -h | grep Swap                          <span class="comment"># verify</span>
<span class="comment"># Swap:            0B          0B          0B</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why swap must be off</h5><p>Kubernetes schedules by <em>memory requests/limits</em> and assumes a pod that hits its memory limit is OOM-killed deterministically. Swap breaks that contract (a "full" node could limp along on disk), so the kubelet refuses to start unless swap is off (or you explicitly opt into the newer, still-niche swap support). On the exam: a node <code>NotReady</code> with the kubelet crash-looping → check <code>swapoff</code> first.</p></div>

                <h4>Step B — load kernel modules</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf   <span class="comment"># load on every boot</span>
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF
sudo modprobe overlay        <span class="comment"># overlay = the overlayfs storage driver containers use</span>
sudo modprobe br_netfilter   <span class="comment"># br_netfilter = lets iptables see bridged pod traffic</span>
lsmod | grep -E "overlay|br_netfilter"   <span class="comment"># verify both are loaded</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step C — sysctl for bridged pod traffic</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  = 1   <span class="comment"># iptables sees IPv4 bridge traffic</span>
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1   <span class="comment"># …and IPv6</span>
net.ipv4.ip_forward                 = 1   <span class="comment"># route packets between pods/nodes</span>
EOF
sudo sysctl --system                       <span class="comment"># apply without a reboot</span>
sysctl net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  <span class="comment"># → ... = 1</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why these two settings exist</h5><p>Pods talk over a Linux <strong>bridge</strong>. Without <code>bridge-nf-call-iptables=1</code>, packets crossing that bridge bypass iptables — so kube-proxy's Service rules and NetworkPolicies silently don't apply. Without <code>ip_forward=1</code>, the node won't route packets between pods and other nodes at all. Symptom of missing these: pods schedule but can't reach Services or each other.</p></div>

                <h4>Step D — containerd with the systemd cgroup driver</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · containerd</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt-get update &amp;&amp; sudo apt-get install -y containerd
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml   <span class="comment"># write a default config</span>

<span class="comment"># CRITICAL — switch the cgroup driver to systemd (must match the kubelet)</span>
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' /etc/containerd/config.toml

sudo systemctl restart containerd &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl enable containerd
sudo systemctl status containerd      <span class="comment"># Active: active (running)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 The #1 hidden "broken node": <code>SystemdCgroup</code> mismatch</h5><p>Modern Linux uses <strong>systemd</strong> to manage cgroups. The kubelet and the runtime <em>must agree</em> on the driver — both systemd. If containerd is left on the default <code>SystemdCgroup = false</code> (cgroupfs) while the kubelet uses systemd, you get two competing cgroup managers: pods fail to start, the kubelet logs cgroup errors, and the node flaps. This is a favourite exam trap because the node looks "almost" healthy. Always verify this one line.</p></div>

                <h4>Step E — install &amp; pin the toolchain</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">all nodes · kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl gpg
<span class="comment"># add the Kubernetes apt repo (one minor-version stream, e.g. v1.34)</span>
curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.34/deb/Release.key \
  | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg
echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg] \
  https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.34/deb/ /' \
  | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl   <span class="comment"># ← pin: an apt upgrade must NOT break the node</span>
kubeadm version                              <span class="comment"># kubeadm version: ...Major:"1", Minor:"34"...</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Why <code>apt-mark hold</code> matters</h5><p>Without a hold, a routine <code>apt upgrade</code> could jump the kubelet a minor version ahead of the control plane — violating the version-skew policy and breaking the node. To upgrade <em>deliberately</em> (Ch 23) you <code>unhold</code> → upgrade → <code>hold</code> again. The apt-repo URL is also <strong>per-minor</strong> (<code>…/v1.34/…</code>), so upgrading minors means editing that URL too.</p></div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">A</div><h5>Swap off</h5><p><code>swapoff -a</code> + fstab</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">B</div><h5>Modules</h5><p><code>overlay</code> · <code>br_netfilter</code></p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">C</div><h5>Sysctl</h5><p>bridge-nf-call · ip_forward</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">D</div><h5>containerd</h5><p>SystemdCgroup = true</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">E</div><h5>Toolchain</h5><p>install + <code>hold</code></p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.3 Initialize the Control Plane</h3>
                <p>With every node prepared, run <code>kubeadm init</code> on the <strong>control-plane node only</strong>. This single command generates the cluster's entire PKI, writes the control-plane static-pod manifests, brings up etcd and the apiserver, and prints the command workers use to join. It's the most important command in cluster creation — and the exam expects you to know its key flags and what to do immediately after.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane only · kubeadm init</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo kubeadm init \
  --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 \        <span class="comment"># must match your CNI (Flannel uses this)</span>
  --apiserver-advertise-address=192.168.56.10 \  <span class="comment"># this node's IP</span>
  --kubernetes-version=v1.34.0

<span class="comment"># ── abbreviated output ──</span>
<span class="comment"># [init] Using Kubernetes version: v1.34.0</span>
<span class="comment"># [certs] Generating "ca", "apiserver", "etcd/ca", "etcd/server" …</span>
<span class="comment"># [kubeconfig] Writing "admin.conf", "kubelet.conf" …</span>
<span class="comment"># [control-plane] Creating static Pod manifests for apiserver/scheduler/controller-manager</span>
<span class="comment"># [etcd] Creating static Pod manifest for local etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># [mark-control-plane] Marking the node as control-plane</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Your Kubernetes control-plane has initialized successfully!</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># To start using your cluster:</span>
<span class="comment">#   mkdir -p $HOME/.kube</span>
<span class="comment">#   sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config</span>
<span class="comment">#   sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Then you can join any number of worker nodes by running:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm join 192.168.56.10:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1a2b3c…</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔧 <code>kubeadm init</code> flags — what each one does</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> --pod-network-cidr</div><div class="anatomy-value">the IP range pods get. <strong>Must match the CNI you'll install</strong> — Flannel expects <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>, Calico defaults to <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>. Mismatch = pods never get usable IPs.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📡</span> --apiserver-advertise-address</div><div class="anatomy-value">the IP the apiserver advertises to other nodes. Use the control-plane node's real (routable) IP, or workers can't reach <code>:6443</code>.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📌</span> --kubernetes-version</div><div class="anatomy-value">pin the exact version so all components match; otherwise kubeadm picks "latest stable," which may skew from your held packages.</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔒</span> --control-plane-endpoint</div><div class="anatomy-value">for <strong>HA</strong>: a shared DNS name / load-balancer fronting all control-plane nodes. Set it even for a single CP if you might add more later (it can't be added afterwards without pain).</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📤</span> --upload-certs</div><div class="anatomy-value">stores the control-plane certs in a Secret so extra CP nodes can join with <code>--control-plane</code> (HA). Covered in Ch 33.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Right after init — wire up <code>kubectl</code> (the 3 commands)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · as your normal user (not root)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME            STATUS     ROLES           AGE   VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># control-plane   NotReady   control-plane   30s   v1.34.0</span>
<span class="comment"># ↑ NotReady is EXPECTED here — there's no CNI yet (§9.4 fixes it)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 SAVE THE JOIN COMMAND — right now</h5><p>The <code>kubeadm join …</code> line printed at the end of <code>init</code> carries a <strong>bootstrap token that expires in 24 hours</strong>. Copy it somewhere safe immediately. Lost it (or it expired)? Regenerate any time with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> (§9.5) — but on the exam clock, saving it up front is one less thing to fix.</p></div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what <code>kubeadm init</code> actually creates on disk</div>
<pre>
   sudo kubeadm init
     │
     ├─ 🔐 PKI  → /etc/kubernetes/pki/
     │     ca.crt / ca.key ................ the cluster Certificate Authority (root of trust)
     │     apiserver.crt ................... apiserver's TLS serving cert
     │     apiserver-kubelet-client.crt .... apiserver → kubelet auth
     │     etcd/ca.crt · etcd/server.crt ... etcd's own TLS
     │     sa.key / sa.pub ................. signs ServiceAccount tokens
     │
     ├─ 📋 kubeconfigs → /etc/kubernetes/
     │     admin.conf (cluster-admin) · kubelet.conf · controller-manager.conf · scheduler.conf
     │
     ├─ 📦 static-pod manifests → /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
     │     etcd.yaml · kube-apiserver.yaml · kube-controller-manager.yaml · kube-scheduler.yaml
     │
     └─ 🚀 starts everything
           kubelet (systemd) watches that manifests dir → brings up etcd + apiserver +
           scheduler + controller-manager as STATIC PODS; kube-proxy runs as a DaemonSet
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 This is why Ch 11 &amp; 12 work the way they do</h5><p>Everything above lives on the control-plane node's <em>filesystem</em>, managed by the kubelet — not by the scheduler. That's why you fix a broken apiserver by editing a <strong>file</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (Ch 12), and why an <strong>etcd snapshot</strong> (Ch 11) plus <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code> is enough to rebuild a control plane.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.4 Install a CNI Plugin (Pod Networking)</h3>
                <p>A fresh <code>kubeadm</code> cluster has <strong>no pod network</strong> — so every node sits <code>NotReady</code> and CoreDNS stays <code>Pending</code>. The <strong>CNI</strong> plugin is the missing piece: it gives each pod an IP, wires up pod-to-pod routing across nodes, and (depending on the plugin) enforces NetworkPolicies. Install exactly one, on the control plane, and the nodes flip to <code>Ready</code> within a minute.</p>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 KEY TERM — CNI (Container Network Interface)</h5><p>A <strong>standard plug-point</strong>: the kubelet calls a CNI plugin each time a pod is created, and the plugin sets up that pod's network namespace, IP, and routes. Kubernetes ships <em>no</em> built-in pod network on purpose — you choose a CNI that fits your needs. This is one of the three big "interfaces" (CRI for runtime, CNI for network, CSI for storage — §12.5).</p></div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>CNI plugin</th><th>What it's known for</th><th>NetworkPolicy?</th><th>Complexity</th><th>CKA choice?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Calico</strong></td><td>BGP/IP-in-IP routing, mature policy engine</td><td>✅ full</td><td>🟡 medium</td><td>✅ the most common pick</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Flannel</strong></td><td>dead-simple VXLAN overlay</td><td>❌ none</td><td>🟢 low</td><td>✅ great for learning</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Cilium</strong></td><td>eBPF — high performance &amp; deep observability/security</td><td>✅ rich (L3-L7)</td><td>🔴 high</td><td>⚠️ advanced</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Weave Net</strong></td><td>simple encrypted mesh</td><td>✅ basic</td><td>🟢 low</td><td>⚠️ less common now</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · install ONE CNI</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Option A — Calico (default pod CIDR 192.168.0.0/16)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.28.0/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Option B — Flannel (expects --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 from init)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml

<span class="comment"># watch the nodes flip NotReady → Ready as the CNI pods come up</span>
kubectl get nodes -w
<span class="comment"># control-plane   NotReady → Ready   (≈30-60s after the CNI pods are Running)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">why nodes are NotReady until the CNI is up</div>
<pre>
   kubeadm init done            kubectl apply -f &lt;cni&gt;            CNI pods Running
   ┌──────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────┐            ┌──────────────────┐
   │ node: NotReady   │  ──▶    │ CNI DaemonSet     │   ──▶      │ node: Ready ✅    │
   │ CoreDNS: Pending │         │ lands on each node │            │ CoreDNS: Running │
   │ (no pod network) │         │ sets up CNI config │            │ pods get IPs     │
   └──────────────────┘         └──────────────────┘            └──────────────────┘
   the kubelet reports NotReady while its NetworkReady condition is false —
   i.e. until a CNI has written /etc/cni/net.d/ and its pods are healthy.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 The pod-CIDR must match</h5><p>The <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> you gave <code>kubeadm init</code> must match what the CNI expects (Flannel → <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>). Mismatch = pods get IPs the routing doesn't know about → cross-node pod traffic silently fails. With Calico's manifest you either use its default <code>192.168.0.0/16</code> or edit the manifest's CIDR to match your init value.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.5 Join Worker Nodes</h3>
                <p>With the control plane Ready, each prepared worker joins with the <code>kubeadm join</code> command <code>init</code> printed. The token authenticates the node to the apiserver; the <strong>CA cert hash</strong> lets the worker verify it's talking to the <em>real</em> control plane (not an impostor) before trusting it.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">each worker · kubeadm join</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo kubeadm join 192.168.56.10:6443 \
  --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1a2b3c…

<span class="comment"># ── output ──</span>
<span class="comment"># [preflight] Running pre-flight checks</span>
<span class="comment"># [kubelet-start] Starting the kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># This node has joined the cluster.</span>
<span class="comment"># → back on the control plane, the node appears and goes Ready once the CNI pod lands</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>🔑 Lost or expired the join command? Regenerate it</h4>
                <p>The bootstrap token expires after 24 hours. On the control plane, mint a fresh one — or rebuild the full command (token + correct CA hash) in a single shot:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · token recovery (memorise this)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># the one-liner that rebuilds the WHOLE join command (token + ca-cert-hash)</span>
kubeadm token create --print-join-command
<span class="comment"># kubeadm join 192.168.56.10:6443 --token 9z8y7x… --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:1a2b3c…</span>

<span class="comment"># …or do it by hand:</span>
kubeadm token create                         <span class="comment"># a fresh token</span>
kubeadm token list                           <span class="comment"># see tokens + their TTLs</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt -noout -pubkey \
  | openssl rsa -pubin -outform DER 2&gt;/dev/null | sha256sum | awk '{print $1}'   <span class="comment"># the ca-cert-hash</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the complete kubeadm build — one flow, the order that matters</div>
<pre>
   ALL NODES (prep)                          swap off · overlay+br_netfilter · sysctl bridge+ip_forward
   ─────────────────                         · containerd (SystemdCgroup=true) · install kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl + hold
         │
         ▼   CONTROL PLANE only
   kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16   ─►  generates PKI + static-pod manifests + etcd
         │   then: mkdir ~/.kube · cp admin.conf · chown      (+ SAVE the join command!)
         ▼
   kubectl apply -f &lt;CNI&gt;   (Calico / Flannel)     ─►  nodes go Ready (NOT before this!)
         │
         ▼   EACH WORKER
   kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;t&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;
         │   (lost it? kubeadm token create --print-join-command)
         ▼
   ✅ kubectl get nodes → all Ready   ─►  deploy easypj as the smoke test
   ⚠️ NotReady everywhere = no CNI · token expired = regenerate · swap on = init/join refuses
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div><h5>Workers show ROLES &lt;none&gt;</h5><p>Normal. Label one for cosmetics: <code>kubectl label node worker-1 node-role.kubernetes.io/worker=</code> — it changes nothing functionally.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🧹</div><h5>Remove a worker cleanly</h5><p>From the CP: <code>kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code> → <code>kubectl delete node worker-1</code>. On the worker: <code>sudo kubeadm reset</code> to wipe its state.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why a CA cert hash, not just a token?</h5><p>The token proves the <em>node</em> to the apiserver. The <code>--discovery-token-ca-cert-hash</code> works the other way — it lets the joining node verify the apiserver's CA before sending credentials, defeating a man-in-the-middle. Skipping verification (<code>--discovery-token-unsafe-skip-ca-verification</code>) exists but is exactly as dangerous as it sounds.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.6 Verifying the Cluster</h3>
                <p>Once every node has joined, four checks confirm the cluster is genuinely operational — not just "up." The last one (a real scheduled pod) is the proof that scheduling, networking, and the runtime all work end-to-end.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · verify the cluster</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. All nodes Ready?</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME            STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION   INTERNAL-IP</span>
<span class="comment"># control-plane   Ready    control-plane   10m   v1.34.0   192.168.56.10</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1        Ready    &lt;none&gt;          5m    v1.34.0   192.168.56.11</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2        Ready    &lt;none&gt;          3m    v1.34.0   192.168.56.12</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. All system pods Running? (CNI pods, CoreDNS, control plane)</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># calico-node-xxxxx (one per node)        1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-… (x2)                          1/1   Running   ← Running, not Pending</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-control-plane                      1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-control-plane            1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-control-plane   1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-xxxxx (one per node)         1/1   Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-control-plane            1/1   Running</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Where is the API server?</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://192.168.56.10:6443</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. The real test — deploy easypj and confirm it schedules to a WORKER</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=2
kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-…-abcde   1/1   Running   worker-1   ← scheduled off the control plane ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Healthy cluster checklist</h5><ul><li>all nodes <code>STATUS=Ready</code></li><li>every kube-system pod <code>Running</code></li><li>CoreDNS <code>Running</code> (not <code>Pending</code>)</li><li>a test pod schedules to a worker</li></ul></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div><h5>If something's off</h5><ul><li>node <code>NotReady</code> → kubelet down or CNI missing</li><li>CoreDNS <code>Pending</code> → CNI not installed</li><li>pods only on the CP → no workers joined</li><li><code>ImagePullBackOff</code> → registry/image (Ch 2)</li></ul></div>
                </div>
                <h4>🩺 kubeadm troubleshooting decision tree</h4>
                <p>When a freshly-built cluster misbehaves, follow the symptom down to the fix — this single tree resolves the vast majority of "my kubeadm cluster is broken" tasks:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">symptom → cause → fix</div>
<pre>
   kubectl get nodes
     │
     ├─ a node is NotReady ─────────────▶ on that node:
     │     ├─ CNI installed?        no ─▶ kubectl apply -f &lt;calico|flannel&gt;
     │     ├─ containerd running?   no ─▶ sudo systemctl restart containerd
     │     ├─ kubelet running?      no ─▶ journalctl -u kubelet  (swap? cgroup driver?)
     │     └─ swap off?             no ─▶ swapoff -a + fstab
     │
     ├─ kubectl can't connect ─────────▶
     │     ├─ kubeconfig set?       no ─▶ cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
     │     ├─ apiserver running?    no ─▶ crictl ps -a | grep apiserver ; crictl logs (Ch 12)
     │     └─ firewall blocking 6443? yes ─▶ open 6443 between nodes (§9.8)
     │
     └─ pods stuck Pending ────────────▶
           ├─ workers joined?       no ─▶ kubeadm join (or token create --print-join-command)
           ├─ enough cpu/memory?    no ─▶ lower requests / add a node
           └─ taints blocking?     yes ─▶ tolerate or remove the taint (Ch 14)
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.7 Practice Exercises</h3>
                <p>Building clusters is a <strong>muscle-memory</strong> skill — the exam gives you minutes, not hours. Drill these until Exercise 1 takes under 30 minutes and the recovery moves are reflex.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🏋️</div><h5>1 · Build a 3-node cluster</h5><p>3 VMs → prereqs on all → <code>kubeadm init</code> → install Calico → <code>join</code> 2 workers → deploy easypj and verify. <strong>⏱️ target: 30 min.</strong></p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div><h5>2 · Token recovery</h5><p>Assume the join token expired. Regenerate with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> and join a fresh worker with it.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div><h5>3 · Fix a broken node</h5><p>Deliberately break a worker (turn swap on, stop containerd, flip <code>SystemdCgroup=false</code>). Diagnose via <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> and restore it to <code>Ready</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>4 · Add a control-plane node</h5><p>Re-init with <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> + <code>--upload-certs</code>, then join a second CP with <code>--control-plane</code> (HA preview, Ch 33).</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>🧪 Capstone drill — rebuild from scratch in &lt; 30 minutes</h5><p>The single best Part-3 exercise: tear a cluster down with <code>kubeadm reset</code> on every node, then rebuild it end-to-end against the §9.5 flow without looking. When that's comfortable, the kubeadm exam questions become free marks.</p></div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div><h5>Vagrant quick setup for practice</h5><p>The fastest way to get throwaway multi-node VMs locally: a <code>Vagrantfile</code> defining <code>control-plane</code> + <code>worker-1/2</code> on a private network → <code>vagrant up</code> → SSH in and run the prereqs + kubeadm. Cheaper than cloud and resets in seconds with <code>vagrant destroy</code>.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🆘</div><h5>The 3-command debugging lifeline</h5><p>When <em>anything</em> in a kubeadm build is broken, these three answer 90% of it: <code>kubectl get nodes</code> (what's NotReady?) → <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> (which control-plane/CNI pod is unhealthy?) → <code>sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 50</code> (the node-level reason).</p></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 9.8 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s9-8">
                <h3 class="section-h">9.8 Required Ports (open these between nodes)</h3>
                <p>A cluster only works if its components can reach each other. On a real install (and in firewall-troubleshooting exam tasks) you must know which inbound ports each node type needs. A blocked port produces a <em>specific</em> failure — memorise the mapping and you can diagnose "joins fail" or "<code>kubectl logs</code> hangs" in seconds.</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Port(s)</th><th>Component</th><th>Who connects</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td rowspan="5"><strong>Control&nbsp;plane</strong> (inbound)</td><td><code>6443</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td>every client, kubelet &amp; node</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>2379-2380</code></td><td>etcd: client (2379) + peer/Raft (2380)</td><td>apiserver → etcd; etcd ↔ etcd</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10259</code></td><td>kube-scheduler</td><td>localhost (health/metrics)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10257</code></td><td>kube-controller-manager</td><td>localhost (health/metrics)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10250</code></td><td>kubelet API</td><td>control plane → kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td rowspan="3"><strong>Worker</strong> (inbound)</td><td><code>10250</code></td><td>kubelet API</td><td>control plane → kubelet (logs/exec/metrics)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>10256</code></td><td>kube-proxy health</td><td>load balancers / health checks</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>30000-32767</code></td><td>NodePort Service range</td><td>external clients → NodePort Services</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">who dials whom — the ports that must be open</div>
<pre>
        🧑 kubectl / clients ───────────────6443──────────────▶ 📡 apiserver ─┐
                                                                              │ 2379-2380
   🤖 worker kubelet ◀──10250── apiserver        🗄️ etcd ◀────────────────────┘
   🌐 external user ──30000-32767──▶ 🔀 NodePort (worker)      📋 scheduler :10259  🔄 ctrl-mgr :10257
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   control-plane inbound: 6443 · 2379-2380 · 10259 · 10257 · 10250
   worker inbound:        10250 · 10256 · 30000-32767
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Blocked port → exact symptom</h5><ul><li><strong>6443 blocked</strong> → <code>kubeadm join</code> fails &amp; <code>kubectl</code> can't reach the cluster ("connection refused" to <code>:6443</code>).</li><li><strong>10250 blocked</strong> → <code>kubectl logs</code>/<code>exec</code> hang and <code>kubectl top</code>/metrics fail (apiserver can't reach the kubelet).</li><li><strong>2379-2380 blocked</strong> → etcd can't form/keep quorum → apiserver errors, cluster effectively down.</li><li><strong>30000-32767 blocked</strong> → NodePort Services unreachable from outside.</li></ul></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — what <code>kubeadm init</code> actually does, phase by phase</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p><code>kubeadm init</code> isn't one action — it's an ordered pipeline of <strong>phases</strong>, each of which you can run (or skip) individually with <code>kubeadm init phase &lt;name&gt;</code>. Knowing the order explains every "init failed at step X" message and is the key to repairing a half-built control plane.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubeadm init
     ① preflight ............ check swap/cgroups/ports/CRI; pull images   (fails fast &amp; loud)
     ② certs ................ generate the PKI in /etc/kubernetes/pki  (CA, apiserver, etcd, sa keys)
     ③ kubeconfig .......... write admin/kubelet/controller-manager/scheduler .conf
     ④ etcd ................ render the local etcd static-pod manifest
     ⑤ control-plane ....... render apiserver + controller-manager + scheduler static-pod manifests
     ⑥ kubelet-start ....... write kubelet config + start the kubelet (it then runs the static pods)
     ⑦ upload-config ....... store kubeadm &amp; kubelet config in the kubeadm-config ConfigMap
     ⑧ upload-certs ........ (HA, optional) stash CP certs in a Secret for other CP nodes
     ⑨ mark-control-plane .. taint &amp; label this node as control-plane
     ⑩ bootstrap-token ..... create the join token + RBAC so nodes can bootstrap
     ⑪ kubelet-finalize .... point the kubelet at its rotated, cluster-issued cert
     ⑫ addons .............. deploy CoreDNS + kube-proxy
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why phases matter on the exam:</strong> a broken control plane rarely needs a full re-init. If certs expired you re-run just <code>kubeadm init phase certs</code> (or <code>kubeadm certs renew</code>); if the apiserver manifest is gone you regenerate just <code>kubeadm init phase control-plane apiserver</code>. And <code>kubeadm reset</code> undoes the lot. The phase list is also the troubleshooting order — an init that dies at "preflight" is swap/cgroup/port; one that dies later is usually certs or the kubelet.</div>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Zooming into phases ④–⑤ — etcd is the FIRST pod to start, and bootstraps the whole keyspace.</strong> The control plane has a strict start order because everything else needs a place to persist state. Watching it boot explains why a sick etcd makes the entire apiserver unavailable.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   ④/⑤  kubelet sees the static-pod manifests in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/ and starts them:
     1. kubelet reads etcd.yaml          → creates the etcd container FIRST
     2. etcd comes up as a SINGLE-NODE cluster (this CP node only)
     3. etcd reports healthy             → only NOW can kube-apiserver start
     4. apiserver connects to etcd (https://127.0.0.1:2379) and writes the INITIAL cluster state
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the first keys kubeadm writes into etcd (the skeleton every cluster starts with):
     /registry/namespaces/default
     /registry/namespaces/kube-system
     /registry/namespaces/kube-public
     /registry/namespaces/kube-node-lease
     /registry/serviceaccounts/kube-system/default
     /registry/configmaps/kube-public/cluster-info        ← how new nodes discover the CP
     /registry/secrets/kube-system/bootstrap-token-*       ← the join tokens (phase ⑩)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   later, when you `kubectl apply` the easypj Deployment, ITS objects land alongside these:
     /registry/deployments/easypj-prod/web
     /registry/pods/easypj-prod/web-<hash>
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why the order is non-negotiable:</strong> the apiserver is <em>stateless</em> — it keeps nothing locally; etcd is the single source of truth. So if etcd isn't healthy, the apiserver crash-loops on startup and <code>kubectl</code> returns <em>"connection refused"</em>. This is the deep reason "etcd down = cluster down," and why phase ④ (etcd) must succeed before phase ⑤ (the rest of the control plane). It also explains restore: you stop the apiserver, restore the etcd snapshot, and the apiserver comes back seeing the cluster exactly as of that snapshot.</div>

                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know (so you never need the docs)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>the kubeadm-config ConfigMap</strong></td><td>phase ⑦ stores your cluster's config in <code>kube-system/kubeadm-config</code>; upgrades &amp; new node joins read it. Inspect with <code>kubectl -n kube-system get cm kubeadm-config -o yaml</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>declarative <code>--config</code></strong></td><td>instead of flags, pass a <code>kubeadm init --config=cfg.yaml</code> (a <code>ClusterConfiguration</code> + <code>InitConfiguration</code>) — reproducible, version-controllable installs.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>TLS bootstrapping &amp; bootstrap tokens</strong></td><td>a joining kubelet uses the short-lived bootstrap token to submit a <strong>CSR</strong>; the cluster auto-approves it and issues the kubelet a real cert (which then auto-rotates). That's why a token, not a permanent credential, is enough to join.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong><code>--upload-certs</code> (HA)</strong></td><td>uploads CP certs (encrypted) so additional control-plane nodes join with <code>--control-plane --certificate-key …</code> instead of hand-copying <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>air-gapped installs</strong></td><td><code>kubeadm config images pull</code> pre-pulls every control-plane image; point it at a private registry with <code>--image-repository</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong><code>--ignore-preflight-errors</code> &amp; CRI socket</strong></td><td>bypass a specific preflight check when you know better; <code>--cri-socket unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code> when multiple runtimes are present.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Going deeper than the phase list — the PKI is one trust chain, and it explains everything.</strong> Phase ② doesn't just "make certs"; it builds a <strong>certificate hierarchy</strong> rooted in a self-signed cluster CA. Every component proves its identity with a cert that traces back to that root — which is why one CA can authenticate the whole cluster, and why an expired cert breaks things in predictable ways.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   /etc/kubernetes/pki/
   🔱 ca.crt + ca.key  ── the CLUSTER root CA (self-signed, valid 10 years) ── signs ▼
        ├─ apiserver.crt ................ apiserver's TLS serving cert (SANs: every cluster IP/DNS)
        ├─ apiserver-kubelet-client.crt . lets the apiserver authenticate TO each kubelet (logs/exec)
        └─ (kubelet client certs) ....... each kubelet's identity, auto-issued via the CSR flow
   🔱 etcd/ca.crt ───── a SEPARATE CA just for etcd ── signs ▼
        ├─ etcd/server.crt ............. etcd's serving + peer cert (:2379 client, :2380 peer)
        └─ apiserver-etcd-client.crt ... lets the apiserver authenticate to etcd
   🔑 sa.key / sa.pub ─ NOT a cert — the keypair that SIGNS &amp; verifies ServiceAccount tokens
   front-proxy-ca.crt ─ a third CA for the aggregation layer (extension apiservers, metrics-server)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this is the "to the core" view:</strong> there are <em>two</em> CAs on purpose — compromising the etcd CA shouldn't let you mint apiserver certs, and vice-versa. The certs default to a <strong>1-year</strong> life (the CA lasts 10) — that's why <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code>/<code>renew all</code> exist and why a <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (which auto-renews) keeps long-lived clusters healthy. And because identity is <em>entirely</em> these files, a working control plane can be rebuilt from just <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code> + an etcd snapshot — lose the PKI and even a perfect etcd backup can't re-establish trust. This is the deep reason Ch 11 (etcd) and the PKI together <em>are</em> the cluster.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Prep every node first:</strong> swap off · <code>overlay</code>+<code>br_netfilter</code> · sysctl bridge+ip_forward · containerd <code>SystemdCgroup=true</code> · install + <code>hold</code> the toolchain.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubeadm init</code> on the control plane</strong> → copy <code>admin.conf</code> to <code>~/.kube/config</code> → <strong>save the join command</strong>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Install a CNI</strong> — nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> and CoreDNS <code>Pending</code> until you do.</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubeadm join</code> the workers</strong> with token + ca-cert-hash; regenerate with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> CP needs 6443 · 2379-2380 · 10259/10257 · 10250; workers need 10250 · 10256 · 30000-32767.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Swap on.</strong> <code>kubeadm init/join</code> refuses outright — <code>swapoff -a</code> + comment fstab.</li>
                    <li><strong>No CNI.</strong> All nodes <code>NotReady</code> after init is <em>expected</em> until you apply a network plugin — not a kubelet bug.</li>
                    <li><strong>cgroup-driver mismatch.</strong> containerd <code>SystemdCgroup=false</code> vs systemd kubelet → pods won't start; the subtlest "broken node."</li>
                    <li><strong>Expired join token.</strong> Tokens last 24h — regenerate, don't panic.</li>
                    <li><strong>pod-CIDR mismatch.</strong> <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> must match the CNI's expectation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgot <code>apt-mark hold</code>.</strong> A later <code>apt upgrade</code> skews the kubelet and breaks the node.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> rebuilds a lost join in one line.</li>
                    <li><code>kubeadm config images pull</code> before <code>init</code> = faster init (and works air-gapped).</li>
                    <li><code>kubeadm reset -f</code> on a node to undo everything and start clean.</li>
                    <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> is your first stop for any "node won't come up."</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 9)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the kubeadm toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16 --apiserver-advertise-address=&lt;ip&gt;
kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;t&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;
kubeadm token create --print-join-command     <span class="comment"># regenerate a lost join</span>
kubeadm config images pull                    <span class="comment"># pre-pull control-plane images</span>
kubeadm reset -f                              <span class="comment"># undo init/join on a node</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration ; kubeadm certs renew all   <span class="comment"># cert lifecycle</span>
systemctl status kubelet ; journalctl -u kubelet -f        <span class="comment"># the node agent</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide ; kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — bootstrap a control plane &amp; join a worker (from memory)</h5>
                <p>No peeking — this is the core CKA build:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>On all nodes: disable swap, load modules, set sysctls, install containerd with <code>SystemdCgroup=true</code>, install &amp; hold the toolchain.</li>
                    <li>On the CP: <code>kubeadm init</code> with a pod-CIDR, then configure <code>~/.kube/config</code>.</li>
                    <li>Install a CNI and confirm the node goes <code>Ready</code>.</li>
                    <li>Regenerate the join command and join one worker; verify <code>get nodes</code> shows both Ready.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># every node</span>
sudo swapoff -a; sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab
sudo modprobe overlay br_netfilter
echo -e "net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables=1\nnet.ipv4.ip_forward=1" | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf; sudo sysctl --system
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/' /etc/containerd/config.toml; sudo systemctl restart containerd

<span class="comment"># control plane</span>
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16
mkdir -p ~/.kube; sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config; sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) ~/.kube/config
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml
kubeadm token create --print-join-command      <span class="comment"># copy this</span>

<span class="comment"># worker (paste the printed join command)</span>
sudo kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;t&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;
kubectl get nodes        <span class="comment"># both Ready ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 9 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">kubeadm build &amp; recovery recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Disable swap permanently on a node (commands).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">swap off</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo swapoff -a                            <span class="comment"># now</span>
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab   <span class="comment"># and across reboots</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>swapoff -a</code> only lasts until reboot; commenting the <code>swap</code> line in <code>/etc/fstab</code> makes it stick. The kubelet refuses to start with swap on (unless you explicitly enable the newer swap feature-gate).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the 3 commands you run right after <code>kubeadm init</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">configure kubectl</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>They copy the cluster-admin kubeconfig (<code>admin.conf</code>) into your home dir so <code>kubectl</code> works as your normal user. Run them as that user, not root. (And immediately save the printed join command.)</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">All nodes are <code>NotReady</code> right after init — most likely cause and fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>No CNI installed.</strong> The kubelet's <code>NetworkReady</code> condition is false until a pod-network plugin is applied. Fix: <code>kubectl apply -f &lt;calico|flannel&gt;</code>; nodes go Ready in ~30-60s.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>This is <em>expected</em> behaviour, not a bug — kubeadm intentionally ships no pod network. CoreDNS staying <code>Pending</code> is the corroborating symptom.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The join token expired — add a new worker anyway (commands).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane → then worker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubeadm token create --print-join-command   <span class="comment"># CP: prints a fresh full join command</span>
<span class="comment"># on the worker, paste it:</span>
sudo kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token &lt;new&gt; --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;h&gt;</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--print-join-command</code> bundles a new token with the correct CA hash in one step — far faster than minting a token and computing the hash by hand.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why must <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> match the CNI's expectation?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Because the CNI hands pods IPs from, and programs routes for, a specific CIDR. If kubeadm advertises one range and the CNI uses another, pods get IPs the routing fabric doesn't know about → cross-node pod traffic silently fails.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Flannel expects <code>10.244.0.0/16</code>; Calico defaults to <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>. Either pass the matching <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> at init or edit the CNI manifest's CIDR.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The kubelet is crash-looping on a fresh node — where do you look?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager   <span class="comment"># the actual error</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Top suspects in order: swap still on, containerd <code>SystemdCgroup</code> mismatch, container runtime down, or a bad <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>. The journal names which one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which kernel modules &amp; sysctls are required, and why?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Modules: <code>overlay</code> (overlayfs container storage) and <code>br_netfilter</code> (so iptables sees bridged traffic). Sysctls: <code>net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables=1</code> (kube-proxy/NetworkPolicy apply to bridged pod traffic) and <code>net.ipv4.ip_forward=1</code> (route packets between pods/nodes).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Without them, pods may schedule but can't reach Services or other nodes — a confusing "networking just doesn't work" failure.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Cleanly remove a worker (drain + delete + reset).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">CP, then the worker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data   <span class="comment"># evict workloads</span>
kubectl delete node worker-1                                        <span class="comment"># remove from the API</span>
<span class="comment"># on worker-1 itself:</span>
sudo kubeadm reset -f                                               <span class="comment"># wipe cluster state</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Drain first so pods reschedule elsewhere; delete removes the node object; <code>reset</code> cleans certs/manifests/CNI config off the machine so it could rejoin fresh.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Which ports must be open between the control plane and workers?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Control plane (inbound):</strong> <code>6443</code> (apiserver), <code>2379-2380</code> (etcd), <code>10259</code> (scheduler), <code>10257</code> (controller-manager), <code>10250</code> (kubelet). <strong>Workers (inbound):</strong> <code>10250</code> (kubelet), <code>10256</code> (kube-proxy), <code>30000-32767</code> (NodePort).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Memorise by symptom: 6443 down = joins/kubectl fail; 10250 down = logs/exec/metrics fail; 2379-2380 down = etcd loses quorum.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Pin the toolchain so an <code>apt upgrade</code> can't break the node.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">pin versions</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl
apt-mark showhold        <span class="comment"># verify they're held</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A hold freezes the package version so a routine upgrade can't skew the kubelet ahead of the control plane. To upgrade deliberately (Ch 23): <code>unhold</code> → upgrade → <code>hold</code> again.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch9-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 9 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style "build it / fix it" tasks with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You run <code>kubeadm init</code> and the initialization fails with: <em>"[ERROR Swap]: running with swap on is not supported."</em> What do you do?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">disable swap immediately</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Disable swap immediately</span>
sudo swapoff -a

<span class="comment"># Disable swap permanently (survives reboot)</span>
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab

<span class="comment"># Verify swap is off</span>
free -h
<span class="comment"># Swap line should show zeros: 0B  0B  0B</span>

<span class="comment"># Now retry kubeadm init</span>
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Kubernetes requires swap to be disabled because the scheduler's resource calculations assume physical memory only. If pods swap to disk, performance becomes unpredictable and memory limits lose meaning. <code>swapoff -a</code> disables it immediately, but editing <code>/etc/fstab</code> makes it permanent across reboots. This is a common CKA pre-requisite step. If you see any error during <code>kubeadm init</code>, always check the <code>[preflight]</code> section — it lists exactly what failed (swap, ports, container runtime, etc.).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">After running <code>kubeadm init</code>, the output prints three important commands. What are they and why is each critical?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">command 1: set up kubeconfig for the current user</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Command 1: Set up kubeconfig for the current user</span>
mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

<span class="comment"># Command 2: Install a CNI plugin (e.g., Calico)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Command 3: Join worker nodes (unique per init)</span>
kubeadm join 192.168.1.10:6443 --token abcdef.0123456789abcdef \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:abc123...</code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Step</th>
                                            <th>Why Critical</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>kubeconfig setup</td>
                                            <td>Without it, <code>kubectl</code> can't authenticate to the API server</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>CNI plugin</td>
                                            <td>Nodes stay <strong>NotReady</strong> until pod networking is installed</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Join command</td>
                                            <td>Workers need the token + CA hash to securely join the cluster</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>These three steps complete the cluster setup. The kubeconfig file at <code>~/.kube/config</code> contains the cluster endpoint, client certificate, and CA certificate — this is how kubectl authenticates. The CNI (Container Network Interface) plugin provides pod-to-pod networking — without it, pods can't communicate and nodes report NotReady. The join token is valid for 24 hours by default. If it expires: <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> generates a new one.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">All worker nodes show <code>NotReady</code> status. The control plane node is also <code>NotReady</code>. What is the most likely cause?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">most likely cause: no cni plugin installed</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Most likely cause: No CNI plugin installed</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify: check node conditions</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 Conditions
<span class="comment"># Look for: NetworkReady=false, "network plugin is not ready"</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Install a CNI plugin</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Or for Flannel:</span>
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flannel-io/flannel/master/Documentation/kube-flannel.yml

<span class="comment"># Verify nodes become Ready (may take 30-60 seconds)</span>
kubectl get nodes -w

<span class="comment"># Other causes of NotReady:</span>
<span class="comment"># - kubelet not running: systemctl status kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># - Container runtime down: systemctl status containerd</span>
<span class="comment"># - Node resource pressure: kubectl describe node | grep Conditions</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>If <strong>all</strong> nodes are NotReady, the cause is almost always a missing CNI plugin. The kubelet reports <code>Ready</code> only when the container runtime AND network plugin are both operational. A single node being NotReady suggests node-specific issues (kubelet crash, disk pressure, memory pressure). On the CKA, always check if CNI is deployed: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -i calico\|flannel\|weave</code>. No CNI pods = no networking = all NotReady.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">A worker node failed to join the cluster. The <code>kubeadm join</code> command returns: <em>"token has expired"</em>. How do you generate a new join command from the control plane node?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the control plane node</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># On the control plane node:</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 1: Create new token and print full join command</span>
kubeadm token create --print-join-command
<span class="comment"># Output: kubeadm join 10.0.0.10:6443 --token abc123.xyz789 \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:...</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: List existing tokens</span>
kubeadm token list
<span class="comment"># TOKEN                     TTL         EXPIRES</span>
<span class="comment"># abcdef.0123456789abcdef   23h         2024-01-02T10:00:00Z</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Create token with custom TTL</span>
kubeadm token create --ttl 2h --print-join-command

<span class="comment"># Then on the worker node, run the printed join command:</span>
sudo kubeadm join 10.0.0.10:6443 --token &lt;new-token&gt; \
  --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt;</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Bootstrap tokens expire after 24 hours by default. The <code>--print-join-command</code> flag is essential — it outputs the complete command including the token AND the CA cert hash. Without the hash, the worker can't verify it's connecting to the legitimate API server (preventing MITM attacks). On the CKA, if a question says "add a worker node," start with <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> on the control plane, then run the output on the worker.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> flag in <code>kubeadm init</code>, and what happens if you choose a CIDR that overlaps with your node network?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the pod-network-cidr defines the ip range for all…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The pod-network-cidr defines the IP range for ALL pods in the cluster</span>
kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16  <span class="comment"># Flannel default</span>
kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=192.168.0.0/16 <span class="comment"># Calico default</span>

<span class="comment"># Common CIDR allocations:</span>
<span class="comment"># Node network:    10.0.0.0/24  (physical/VM IPs)</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod network:     10.244.0.0/16 (pod IPs)</span>
<span class="comment"># Service network: 10.96.0.0/12  (ClusterIP range, default)</span>

<span class="comment"># ❌ OVERLAP PROBLEM:</span>
<span class="comment"># Node network: 10.244.1.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod CIDR:     10.244.0.0/16   ← OVERLAPS!</span>
<span class="comment"># Result: routing breaks — traffic to pods goes to nodes instead</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> tells the CNI plugin which IP range to assign to pods. Each node gets a subnet from this range (e.g., node1 gets 10.244.0.0/24, node2 gets 10.244.1.0/24). If the pod CIDR overlaps with your node IPs, the kernel can't distinguish between pod and node traffic — routing tables conflict, and networking breaks silently. On the CKA, the CIDR is usually provided in the question. Use exactly what they specify. Most CNI plugins also require the CIDR to match their default (Flannel = 10.244.0.0/16, Calico = 192.168.0.0/16).</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The kubelet on a worker node is not starting. <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> shows it's "activating (auto-restart)". How do you diagnose this?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check kubelet logs</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kubelet logs</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 50
<span class="comment"># or follow in real-time:</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Common errors and fixes:</span>

<span class="comment"># Error: "failed to load kubelet config file"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Check /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml exists and is valid</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml

<span class="comment"># Error: "container runtime is not running"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Start containerd</span>
sudo systemctl start containerd
sudo systemctl enable containerd

<span class="comment"># Error: "unable to load client CA file"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Node hasn't joined yet or certs are corrupted</span>
<span class="comment"># Re-join: kubeadm reset && kubeadm join ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify container runtime</span>
crictl info
sudo systemctl status containerd

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kubelet is enabled at boot</span>
sudo systemctl enable kubelet</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>kubelet crash-looping means it starts, encounters an error, exits, and systemd restarts it. <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> is your primary tool — the error message tells you exactly what's wrong. The three most common causes: (1) container runtime not running (containerd/CRI-O), (2) kubelet config file missing or corrupt, (3) node hasn't been joined to a cluster yet. On the CKA, always check in this order: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> → <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → <code>systemctl status containerd</code>. This pattern solves 90% of node failures.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are the kernel modules and system settings required before installing Kubernetes with kubeadm? List the prerequisites.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">load required kernel modules</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Load required kernel modules</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF
sudo modprobe overlay
sudo modprobe br_netfilter

<span class="comment"># 2. Set required sysctl parameters</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  = 1
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1
net.ipv4.ip_forward                 = 1
EOF
sudo sysctl --system

<span class="comment"># 3. Disable swap</span>
sudo swapoff -a
sudo sed -i '/ swap / s/^/#/' /etc/fstab

<span class="comment"># 4. Install container runtime (containerd)</span>
sudo apt-get install -y containerd
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml
<span class="comment"># Enable SystemdCgroup = true in config.toml</span>
sudo systemctl restart containerd

<span class="comment"># 5. Install kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl</span>
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm kubelet kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm kubelet kubectl</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>These prerequisites ensure the kernel can handle container networking. <code>br_netfilter</code> allows iptables to see bridged traffic (pod networking). <code>overlay</code> enables the overlay filesystem for container images. <code>ip_forward=1</code> allows packet forwarding between interfaces. <code>apt-mark hold</code> prevents accidental upgrades of kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl during <code>apt upgrade</code> — version skew between components breaks clusters. On the CKA, these are usually pre-configured, but understanding them helps troubleshoot: if pod networking breaks, check that <code>br_netfilter</code> is loaded and <code>bridge-nf-call-iptables = 1</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you completely tear down a Kubernetes cluster installed with kubeadm and start fresh?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on each node (workers first, then control plane)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># On EACH node (workers first, then control plane):</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Reset kubeadm (removes certs, configs, etcd data)</span>
sudo kubeadm reset -f

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Clean up networking</span>
sudo rm -rf /etc/cni/net.d
sudo iptables -F && sudo iptables -t nat -F && sudo iptables -t mangle -F
sudo iptables -X

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Remove kubeconfig</span>
rm -rf $HOME/.kube

<span class="comment"># Step 4: (Optional) Stop kubelet and containerd</span>
sudo systemctl stop kubelet
sudo systemctl stop containerd

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Clean up leftover data</span>
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/etcd
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/kubelet
sudo rm -rf /etc/kubernetes

<span class="comment"># Then re-initialize:</span>
sudo kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubeadm reset</code> undoes what <code>kubeadm init</code> or <code>kubeadm join</code> did — it removes TLS certificates, the local etcd data member, the kubelet config, and the admin kubeconfig. However, it does NOT clean up CNI config or iptables rules — those linger and can cause issues on re-init. On the CKA, you typically won't need to reset, but knowing this helps if you severely break something. Always reset workers BEFORE the control plane to avoid orphan node objects.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What ports must be open on control plane nodes vs worker nodes for a Kubernetes cluster to function? List the components and their ports.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Component</th>
                                            <th>Port</th>
                                            <th>Where</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>API Server</td>
                                            <td><code>6443</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>etcd</td>
                                            <td><code>2379-2380</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Scheduler</td>
                                            <td><code>10259</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Controller Manager</td>
                                            <td><code>10257</code></td>
                                            <td>Control Plane</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Kubelet</td>
                                            <td><code>10250</code></td>
                                            <td>All Nodes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>NodePort Services</td>
                                            <td><code>30000-32767</code></td>
                                            <td>All Nodes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Port conflicts are a common cause of cluster initialization failures. If a firewall blocks port 6443, workers can't reach the API server. If port 2379 is in use, etcd won't start. If 10250 is blocked, the API server can't reach kubelet (which means <code>kubectl exec</code> and <code>kubectl logs</code> fail). On the CKA exam, firewalls are usually pre-configured, but understanding ports helps with troubleshooting: <code>ss -tlnp | grep 6443</code> checks if the API server is listening, <code>nc -zv &lt;control-plane-ip&gt; 6443</code> tests connectivity from a worker.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to set up a cluster with a specific Kubernetes version (v1.29.0). How do you specify the version during installation and ensure all components match?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">install specific versions of kubeadm, kubelet</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Install specific versions of kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl</span>
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.29.0-1.1 kubelet=1.29.0-1.1 kubectl=1.29.0-1.1
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Initialize with version flag</span>
sudo kubeadm init --kubernetes-version=v1.29.0 \
  --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify versions match</span>
kubectl version --short
<span class="comment"># Client Version: v1.29.0</span>
<span class="comment"># Server Version: v1.29.0</span>

kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           STATUS   VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># control-plane  Ready    v1.29.0</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1       Ready    v1.29.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Check component versions</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pods -o custom-columns=\
'NAME:.metadata.name,IMAGE:.spec.containers[0].image' | grep kube
<span class="comment"># All images should show v1.29.0</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Version consistency is critical — Kubernetes supports a maximum skew of one minor version between components. kubelet can be one minor version behind the API server, but never ahead. The <code>apt-mark hold</code> command prevents <code>apt upgrade</code> from changing versions. On the CKA, the exam environment usually has specific versions pre-installed, but upgrade questions require you to install exact versions. Always verify with <code>kubectl version</code> and <code>kubeadm version</code> after installation.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 9 Visual Summary — bare nodes to a verified cluster, one flow</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ EVERY NODE (prep)   swapoff · overlay+br_netfilter · sysctl · containerd(SystemdCgroup=true) · kubeadm/kubelet/kubectl+hold
        │
        ▼  CONTROL PLANE
   🔧 kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=…  ─►  PKI + static-pod manifests + etcd + join token
        │   mkdir ~/.kube · cp admin.conf · chown      (SAVE the join command!)
        ▼
   🌐 kubectl apply -f &lt;CNI&gt;   ─►  nodes flip NotReady → Ready, CoreDNS Runs
        │
        ▼  EACH WORKER
   🔗 kubeadm join &lt;cp&gt;:6443 --token … --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash …   (lost? token create --print-join-command)
        ▼
   ✅ kubectl get nodes → all Ready  ─►  deploy easypj as the smoke test
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ports: CP 6443·2379-2380·10259·10257·10250 │ worker 10250·10256·30000-32767     ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> you prepare <em>every</em> node identically (swap off, kernel modules, sysctls, containerd on the systemd cgroup driver, the held toolchain). On the control plane, <code>kubeadm init</code> generates the PKI, writes the static-pod manifests, starts etcd, and prints a join command — then you wire up <code>kubectl</code> and <strong>save that join command</strong>. Nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until you apply a <strong>CNI</strong>; then workers <code>kubeadm join</code> with a token + CA hash.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the order is the exam — <strong>prep all → init CP → install CNI → join workers → verify</strong> — and the two recovery reflexes that save you are <code>kubeadm token create --print-join-command</code> (lost join) and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> (broken node). Get the right firewall ports open and the cluster simply works.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH9-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 9 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 10 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch10">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 10 · Cluster Configuration &amp; kubeconfig</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">A running cluster is a <em>configured</em> cluster. This chapter is where you learn to bend Kubernetes to requirements — read and merge <strong>kubeconfig</strong>, tune the <strong>apiserver</strong> and <strong>kubelet</strong> by editing their config, enable <strong>admission controllers</strong>, and navigate <strong>API groups</strong> — and, just as importantly, how to recover when an edit breaks the control plane.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Heavily Tested</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⚙️ Configuration</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Read a <strong>kubeconfig</strong> to the bottom — clusters, users, contexts, and how files merge</li>
                    <li>✅ Manage many clusters from one machine and switch safely between them</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure the <strong>kube-apiserver</strong> via its static-pod manifest (and recover a bad edit)</li>
                    <li>✅ Configure the <strong>kubelet</strong> via <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> + its systemd unit</li>
                    <li>✅ Understand &amp; enable <strong>admission controllers</strong> — where they run and why order matters</li>
                    <li>✅ Navigate <strong>API groups &amp; versioning</strong> — core vs named groups</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">apiserver/kubelet config <span class="cka-weight">CORE TASK</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 The one habit that saves your cluster</h5>
                <p>Configuring the control plane means <strong>editing files</strong> — and a single typo in <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> takes the apiserver (and your <code>kubectl</code>) offline. The exam loves this scenario.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Always back up first:</strong> <code>sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/</code> before any edit.</li>
                    <li><strong>The kubelet auto-restarts</strong> the component when you save the manifest — no <code>kubectl</code> needed (which is good, because it may be down).</li>
                    <li><strong>Recovery = fix the file.</strong> If the apiserver won't come back, your edit is wrong — restore from <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 10 Roadmap — read config, tune the control plane, recover safely</div>
<pre>
   10.1 kubeconfig ──▶ 10.2 multi-cluster ──▶ 10.3 apiserver config ──▶ 10.4 kubelet config
   clusters·users·         get/use-context        edit the static-pod        /var/lib/kubelet
   contexts·merge          KUBECONFIG merge        manifest (recover!)        config.yaml + unit
        │                                                                          │
        └──────────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────-┘
                                            ▼
   10.5 admission controllers ──▶ 10.6 API groups &amp; versioning
   authN→authZ→mutating→validating   core /api/v1 vs named /apis/grp/ver
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-1"><strong>10.1</strong> kubeconfig deep dive</a></td><td>How does kubectl know where/who/scope?</td><td>clusters (where) + users (who) + contexts (combo); files merge by <code>KUBECONFIG</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-2"><strong>10.2</strong> Multiple clusters</a></td><td>How do I juggle many clusters safely?</td><td><code>get/use/set-context</code>; merge precedence; verify before acting.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-3"><strong>10.3</strong> API server config</a></td><td>How do I change apiserver behaviour?</td><td>edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> — back up first!</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-4"><strong>10.4</strong> kubelet config</a></td><td>How do I configure the node agent?</td><td><code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> + systemd unit; <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-5"><strong>10.5</strong> Admission controllers</a></td><td>What gates a request after authZ?</td><td>mutating <em>then</em> validating; enable via <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s10-6"><strong>10.6</strong> API groups &amp; versioning</a></td><td>Where does each kind live in the API?</td><td>core <code>/api/v1</code> vs named <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;ver&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s10-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.1 kubeconfig — the Deep Dive</h3>
                <p>You met kubeconfig in Ch 5; now we read it to the bottom. A kubeconfig is three lists plus a pointer: <strong>clusters</strong> (where to connect), <strong>users</strong> (who you are), and <strong>contexts</strong> (a named pairing of cluster + user + default namespace), with <code>current-context</code> selecting the active one. Every field below is something the exam can ask you to read or change.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the three lists, joined by a context</div>
<pre>
   ~/.kube/config
   ├─ clusters[]   WHERE   { server: https://10.0.0.1:6443 , certificate-authority(-data): CA }
   ├─ users[]      WHO     { client-certificate(-data)+client-key(-data) | token | exec: &lt;plugin&gt; }
   └─ contexts[]   COMBO   { cluster: prod , user: admin , namespace: easypj-prod }
   current-context: prod   ◀── kubectl acts as THIS (cluster + user + namespace) until you switch
        🖥️ Cluster (WHERE)  +  👤 User (WHO)  +  📂 Namespace (SCOPE)  =  📋 Context
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">~/.kube/config — fully annotated</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
current-context: easypj-prod          <span class="comment"># the active context</span>
clusters:
- name: easypj-prod
  cluster:
    server: https://10.0.0.1:6443      <span class="comment"># WHERE — apiserver URL</span>
    certificate-authority-data: LS0t…  <span class="comment"># the cluster CA (base64) — verifies the server</span>
users:
- name: admin
  user:
    client-certificate-data: LS0t…     <span class="comment"># WHO — your client cert (base64)…</span>
    client-key-data: LS0t…             <span class="comment"># …and its private key</span>
    <span class="comment"># OR a token:        token: eyJhbGci…</span>
    <span class="comment"># OR an exec plugin: exec: { command: aws, args: [eks, get-token, …] }  (cloud auth)</span>
contexts:
- name: easypj-prod
  context:
    cluster: easypj-prod               <span class="comment"># ties a cluster…</span>
    user: admin                        <span class="comment"># …to a user…</span>
    namespace: easypj-prod             <span class="comment"># …and a default namespace</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>clusters</code></td><td>server URL + CA cert</td><td><strong>WHERE</strong> do I connect?</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>users</code></td><td>client cert/key, token, or exec plugin</td><td><strong>WHO</strong> am I?</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>contexts</code></td><td>cluster + user + namespace</td><td>the <strong>COMBO</strong> I switch to</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>📋 kubeconfig — field by field</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📍</span> clusters[].server</div><div class="anatomy-value">the apiserver URL — <code>https://&lt;ip&gt;:6443</code> (6443 is the default API port)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔐</span> clusters[].certificate-authority(-data)</div><div class="anatomy-value">the cluster CA — kubectl uses it to <em>verify the apiserver's</em> TLS cert (defeats a spoofed server)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> users[].client-certificate(-data) + client-key</div><div class="anatomy-value">your client cert + key — the apiserver verifies these to <em>authenticate you</em> (CN→username, O→group)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎟️</span> users[].token / exec</div><div class="anatomy-value">an alternative identity — a bearer token (ServiceAccounts, OIDC) or an exec plugin (cloud auth)</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> contexts[].cluster + user + namespace</div><div class="anatomy-value">a named triple binding a cluster to a user, with an optional default namespace</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">⭐</span> current-context</div><div class="anatomy-value">which context is active now — changed with <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Embedded vs file-path credentials</h5><p>The <code>-data</code> suffix means the cert/CA is <strong>base64-embedded</strong> in the file; without it (<code>certificate-authority: /path/ca.crt</code>) it's a <em>file reference</em>. kubeadm's <code>admin.conf</code> embeds everything (portable); Minikube uses file paths (so the file is short but not self-contained). Decode an embedded cert with <code>… | base64 -d | openssl x509 -noout -text</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.2 Managing Multiple Clusters</h3>
                <p>Admins juggle several clusters from one laptop — exactly like the exam's ~6 contexts. Four commands cover everything, and the discipline is always the same: <strong>switch → verify → act</strong>.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">context management</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config get-contexts                 <span class="comment"># list all (★ = current)</span>
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME          CLUSTER      AUTHINFO   NAMESPACE</span>
<span class="comment"># *         easypj-prod   easypj-prod  admin      easypj-prod</span>
<span class="comment">#           easypj-dev    easypj-dev   dev-user   easypj-dev</span>

kubectl config use-context easypj-dev       <span class="comment"># SWITCH</span>
kubectl config current-context             <span class="comment"># verify → easypj-dev</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj   <span class="comment"># change default ns</span>
kubectl config view --minify               <span class="comment"># show ONLY the active context (cluster/user/ns)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> merges files (precedence)</div>
<pre>
   export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/work/dev.yaml:~/work/prod.yaml
                         │              │             │
                         └── highest ──┘  (on a name clash, the FIRST file wins)
   kubectl config get-contexts   ─►  shows the UNION of contexts from all files
   kubectl config view --flatten &gt; merged && mv merged ~/.kube/config   ─►  bake the merge into one file
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ⚠️ a stray $KUBECONFIG in your shell silently overrides ~/.kube/config — the #1 "where's my cluster?" bug
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Precedence, top to bottom</h5><p><code>--kubeconfig=FILE</code> (flag) &gt; <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> (colon-separated list, first-wins on clashes) &gt; <code>~/.kube/config</code> (default). <code>--flatten</code> resolves a merged set into one self-contained file — handy after combining a teammate's config.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.3 API Server Configuration</h3>
                <p>The kube-apiserver is configured by its <strong>static-pod manifest</strong> at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. There is no "apiserver config UI" — you edit that file's <code>command:</code> flags, save, and the kubelet (which watches the directory) restarts the apiserver pod with the new flags. Powerful, and dangerous: a typo means the apiserver — and your <code>kubectl</code> — won't come back.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">control plane — who talks to whom (the apiserver is the hub)</div>
<pre>
                              CONTROL PLANE NODE
   ┌──────────────────┐                          ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ 📋 kube-scheduler │──────HTTPS :6443────────▶│ 📡 kube-apiserver         │
   │ "where should     │                          │  the CENTRAL hub —        │
   │  this pod run?"   │                          │  EVERY component talks    │
   └──────────────────┘                          │  through me               │
   ┌──────────────────┐                          │                           │
   │ 🔄 controller-mgr │──────HTTPS :6443────────▶│  :6443 HTTPS API          │
   │ "actual == desired?"                         └─────────────┬─────────────┘
   └──────────────────┘                                        │ HTTPS :2379
   ┌──────────────────┐                                        ▼
   │ 🤖 kubelet (nodes)│──────HTTPS :6443────────▶      ┌──────────────┐
   └──────────────────┘                                  │ 🗄️ etcd       │  ONLY the apiserver
   🧑 kubectl ─────────────HTTPS :6443──────────▶         │ :2379 client  │  talks to etcd
                                                          │ :2380 peer    │
                                                          └──────────────┘
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   it's a STAR: scheduler, controller-manager, kubelets &amp; kubectl all go through the apiserver;
   the apiserver alone reads/writes etcd. Configure the hub → you configure how the cluster behaves.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">how an apiserver flag change propagates</div>
<pre>
   you edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml   (add/change a --flag)
        │  save
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet (watching that dir) notices the change
        │  kills the old apiserver static pod, starts a new one with the new flags
        ▼
   📡 new kube-apiserver running   ──►  kubectl works again   (≈15-40s)
   ⚠️ bad flag / YAML typo → the new pod crash-loops → apiserver down → kubectl "connection refused"
      RECOVER: fix the file (or restore your /tmp backup); the kubelet keeps retrying until it's valid.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · the SAFE edit workflow</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># 1. BACK UP first, always</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml        <span class="comment"># 2. edit a --flag under command:</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. watch it restart (kubectl may briefly fail — that's expected)</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep apiserver        <span class="comment"># the container restarts with a new ID</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w | grep apiserver
<span class="comment"># broke it? restore and wait:</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Key flag</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>--authorization-mode</code></td><td>the authorizers, in order — typically <code>Node,RBAC</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code></td><td>which admission controllers run (e.g. <code>NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code>) — §10.5</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--etcd-servers</code></td><td>the etcd endpoint(s) the apiserver reads/writes (<code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code></td><td>the CIDR ClusterIPs are allocated from (e.g. <code>10.96.0.0/12</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--advertise-address</code></td><td>the IP the apiserver advertises to the cluster</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code></td><td>enables encryption-at-rest for Secrets (Ch 11 §11.5)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Recovering a dead apiserver</h5><p>If <code>kubectl</code> dies after your edit, the apiserver pod is crash-looping on your change. You can't use <code>kubectl</code> — work on the node: <code>sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code> and <code>sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> show <em>why</em> (e.g. "unknown flag," "no such file"). Fix the YAML (or restore the <code>/tmp</code> backup); the kubelet relaunches it within ~20s. This exact recovery is a classic exam task — the backup is what makes it a non-event.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.4 kubelet Configuration</h3>
                <p>The kubelet is a <strong>systemd service</strong>, not a pod, so it's configured differently from the control plane. Its settings live in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> (a <code>KubeletConfiguration</code> object), and the flags kubeadm set live in a systemd drop-in. After a change you reload systemd and restart the service.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node · /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml (excerpt)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: KubeletConfiguration
clusterDNS:                 <span class="comment"># the in-cluster DNS Service IP (CoreDNS)</span>
- 10.96.0.10
clusterDomain: cluster.local  <span class="comment"># the DNS suffix for Services/pods</span>
cgroupDriver: systemd       <span class="comment"># MUST match containerd (§9.2)</span>
evictionHard:               <span class="comment"># when the kubelet starts evicting pods</span>
  memory.available: "100Mi"
  nodefs.available: "10%"
authentication:             <span class="comment"># how the apiserver authenticates to this kubelet</span>
  x509: { clientCAFile: /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt }</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node · apply a kubelet change</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml          <span class="comment"># edit clusterDNS, evictionHard, etc.</span>
<span class="comment"># the kubelet's systemd flags live here:</span>
sudo cat /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf
sudo systemctl daemon-reload                   <span class="comment"># REQUIRED after a unit/drop-in change</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f                  <span class="comment"># watch it come back cleanly</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>daemon-reload</code> before restart</h5><p>If you change the systemd <em>unit</em> or a drop-in (<code>*.conf</code>), you must <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> before <code>restart</code> — otherwise systemd runs the <em>old</em> definition and your change appears to do nothing. (Editing only <code>config.yaml</code> needs just a <code>restart</code>.) When a node misbehaves, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> is always the first place to look.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.5 Admission Controllers</h3>
                <p>Admission controllers are gatekeepers the apiserver runs <strong>after authentication &amp; authorization, but before the object is persisted to etcd</strong>. They come in two waves: <strong>mutating</strong> ones can <em>change</em> the object (inject defaults, sidecars), then schema validation runs, then <strong>validating</strong> ones can only <em>accept or reject</em> it. This is where cluster policy lives.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the API request pipeline — every write travels this</div>
<pre>
   kubectl apply -f pod.yaml
        ▼
   🔐 AUTHENTICATION    who are you?     (client cert / token / OIDC)        ← reject → 401
        ▼
   🛂 AUTHORIZATION     are you allowed? (RBAC: verb + resource)             ← reject → 403
        ▼
   🧪 ADMISSION
        ├─ MUTATING controllers + webhooks   (ADD/CHANGE: LimitRanger defaults, sidecars)
        ├─ schema validation                 (OpenAPI: types, required fields)
        └─ VALIDATING controllers + webhooks (ACCEPT/REJECT only: PodSecurity, ResourceQuota)
        ▼
   🗄️ PERSIST to etcd   ─►  controllers + scheduler take over
   💡 mutating ALWAYS runs before validating — defaults are applied, THEN the final object is judged.
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>NamespaceLifecycle</code></td><td>validating</td><td>blocks creating objects in a terminating/absent namespace</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>LimitRanger</code></td><td>mutating</td><td>injects default CPU/memory requests/limits from a LimitRange</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ResourceQuota</code></td><td>validating</td><td>rejects a create that would exceed a namespace's quota</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>PodSecurity</code></td><td>validating</td><td>enforces Pod Security Standards (privileged/baseline/restricted) — Ch 26</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>NodeRestriction</code></td><td>validating</td><td>limits what a kubelet can modify (only its own node/pods) — key security default</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>DefaultStorageClass</code></td><td>mutating</td><td>stamps the default StorageClass onto a PVC that omits one</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Mutating/ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code></td><td>both</td><td>call <em>your</em> external webhooks (Kyverno, OPA, Istio injection) — Ch 38</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · enable a plugin</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># edit the apiserver manifest's command: flags (back up first!)</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,ResourceQuota</span>
<span class="comment"># (kubelet restarts the apiserver on save)</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-control-plane -o yaml \
  | grep enable-admission-plugins        <span class="comment"># verify it took</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why "mutating before validating" matters</h5><p>If validation ran first, a default injected by a mutating controller (say a LimitRanger memory request) could be rejected before it was ever added — or, worse, a sidecar webhook's container would dodge policy checks. Running all mutations first means the <em>final</em> object is the one that's validated. <code>--disable-admission-plugins</code> turns built-ins off; several (like <code>NamespaceLifecycle</code>, <code>NodeRestriction</code>) are on by default.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 10.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s10-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">10.6 API Groups &amp; Versioning</h3>
                <p>Every Kubernetes object lives in an <strong>API group</strong> at a <strong>version</strong>. There are two shapes: the original <strong>core</strong> group served at <code>/api/v1</code> (Pods, Services, ConfigMaps…), and <strong>named</strong> groups served at <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code> (apps, batch, networking…). The <code>apiVersion</code> field in every manifest is exactly this group/version string.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the API surface — core vs named groups</div>
<pre>
   kube-apiserver
   ├─ /api/v1                       ← the CORE group (no group name): apiVersion: v1
   │     Pod · Service · ConfigMap · Secret · Namespace · Node · PersistentVolumeClaim
   │
   └─ /apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;        ← NAMED groups: apiVersion: &lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;
         apps/v1 .................. Deployment · ReplicaSet · DaemonSet · StatefulSet
         batch/v1 ................. Job · CronJob
         networking.k8s.io/v1 ..... Ingress · NetworkPolicy
         rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1  Role · RoleBinding · ClusterRole…
         storage.k8s.io/v1 ........ StorageClass · CSIDriver
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   version maturity:  v1alpha1 (may vanish) → v1beta1 (usually safe) → v1 (stable, GA)
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">discover the API</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl api-resources                 <span class="comment"># every kind → short name, APIVERSION, namespaced?, KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME          SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION   NAMESPACED   KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># pods          po           v1           true         Pod</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy       apps/v1      true         Deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># nodes         no           v1           false        Node</span>

kubectl api-resources --api-group=apps   <span class="comment"># just one group's kinds</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments · replicasets · daemonsets · statefulsets   (all apps/v1)</span>

kubectl api-versions                  <span class="comment"># every served group/version</span>
<span class="comment"># apps/v1 · batch/v1 · networking.k8s.io/v1 · rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 · v1 (core)</span>

kubectl api-resources | grep -i ingress
<span class="comment"># ingresses   ing   networking.k8s.io/v1   true   Ingress</span>
kubectl explain ingress --api-version=networking.k8s.io/v1   <span class="comment"># pin the schema (shows KIND + VERSION)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why groups exist</h5><p>Splitting the API into versioned groups lets Kubernetes evolve features independently and deprecate cleanly — <code>batch</code> can ship a new <code>CronJob</code> version without touching <code>apps</code>. It's also the extension model: a <strong>CRD</strong> (Ch 38) adds your <em>own</em> named group (<code>easypj.example.com/v1</code>) that behaves exactly like a built-in. Full deprecation/conversion mechanics were covered in Ch 7's "Under the Hood."</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how a config change reaches a running control-plane component</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Editing a file and having the apiserver restart "by magic" is really the <strong>kubelet's static-pod reconcile loop</strong>. The kubelet watches <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and treats each YAML there as a pod it must keep running — so a file change <em>is</em> a redeploy.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml   (you save an edit)
        │
        ▼   the kubelet watches this directory (inotify + periodic resync)
   🤖 kubelet sees the manifest's spec changed
        │  → terminates the OLD apiserver static pod
        │  → creates a NEW one from the updated manifest (via the CRI)
        ▼
   📡 new kube-apiserver Running   (its pod is "mirror"-reflected into kube-system, read-only)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the kubelet (a systemd service) configures itself differently: edit /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml,
   then systemctl daemon-reload (if the unit changed) + restart kubelet — there's no manifest to watch.
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Two config models, one chapter:</strong> <em>control-plane components</em> are static pods → configure by editing a <strong>file the kubelet watches</strong> (auto-restart). The <em>kubelet itself</em> is a systemd service → configure via <code>config.yaml</code> + <code>systemctl</code>. Mixing them up ("why didn't my kubelet change apply?") is a common stumble — the kubelet has no one watching <em>it</em>.</div>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>The other "config change" — switching context does almost nothing, on purpose.</strong> <code>kubectl config use-context</code> feels like it connects somewhere, but kubectl is <strong>stateless</strong>: it holds no connection between commands. Switching context just rewrites one line, and the <em>next</em> command reads it fresh.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   $ kubectl config use-context easypj-prod
     • writes ONE line to ~/.kube/config →  current-context: easypj-prod
     • closes NO connection, starts NO process, touches NO other terminal session
   the NEXT kubectl command resolves that context from scratch:
     context easypj-prod → cluster easypj-cluster (server https://10.0.1.100:6443)
                         → user    easypj-admin   (client cert + key)
                         → namespace easypj-prod
     → kubectl builds ONE HTTPS request from those three and sends it
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   $ kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj-dev
     • edits the CURRENT context's namespace line in place (adds it if absent)
     • effect: every later command defaults to -n easypj-dev   (an explicit -n still wins)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why "stateless" matters:</strong> because nothing persists between invocations, two terminals can sit in two different contexts at once (each reads its own <code>$KUBECONFIG</code>/<code>~/.kube/config</code>), and a switch can never "half-apply." It also means a wrong <code>current-context</code> silently aims your next destructive command at the wrong cluster — which is why <code>kubectl config current-context</code> is the cheapest insurance on the exam.</div>

                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>API Priority &amp; Fairness (APF)</strong></td><td>the apiserver fair-queues concurrent requests by <code>FlowSchema</code>/<code>PriorityLevelConfiguration</code> so a noisy client can't starve others; a 429 with a <code>Retry-After</code> is APF shedding load.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>KMS v2 encryption providers</strong></td><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code> can wrap etcd Secrets with an external KMS (envelope encryption) instead of a local key — Ch 11 §11.5.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>audit logging</strong></td><td><code>--audit-policy-file</code> + <code>--audit-log-path</code> record who did what; a webhook backend ships audit events to an external SIEM.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>aggregation layer</strong></td><td>the front-proxy certs let extension apiservers (e.g. <code>metrics-server</code>, custom APIs) plug into the main API surface under <code>/apis</code>.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong><code>--feature-gates</code> &amp; OIDC</strong></td><td>toggle alpha/beta features per component; <code>--oidc-*</code> flags wire the apiserver to an identity provider for human SSO.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Where the cluster's own config lives — the kubeadm ConfigMaps.</strong> kubeadm doesn't keep its settings in a file on the node; it stores them as <strong>ConfigMaps in <code>kube-system</code></strong>, which <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> and <code>kubeadm join</code> read back. This is also where you look up a running cluster's service/pod CIDR on the exam:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>ConfigMap (kube-system)</th><th>Holds</th><th>Read by</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kubeadm-config</code></td><td><code>ClusterConfiguration</code> — the exact params from <code>kubeadm init</code> (service CIDR, pod CIDR, version…)</td><td><code>kubeadm upgrade</code>, <code>kubeadm join</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubelet-config</code></td><td>the cluster-wide <code>KubeletConfiguration</code></td><td>every kubelet (via <code>--config</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-proxy</code></td><td><code>KubeProxyConfiguration</code></td><td>the kube-proxy DaemonSet</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>coredns</code></td><td>the CoreDNS Corefile</td><td>the CoreDNS Deployment</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>cluster-info</code> (kube-public)</td><td>the cluster endpoint + public CA</td><td><code>kubectl cluster-info</code>, <code>kubeadm join</code> discovery</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Exam move:</strong> need a running cluster's service-CIDR or pod-CIDR (and you don't want to grep the apiserver manifest)? <code>kubectl -n kube-system describe cm kubeadm-config</code> shows the exact values used at <code>init</code> time. It's also where the <code>certificateKey</code> for joining extra control-plane nodes is recorded.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubeconfig</strong> = clusters (where) + users (who) + contexts (combo); files merge via <code>$KUBECONFIG</code> (first-wins), flatten with <code>--flatten</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Configure the control plane by editing its static-pod manifests</strong> in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — the kubelet auto-restarts the component on save. <em>Back up first.</em></li>
                    <li><strong>Configure the kubelet</strong> via <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> + systemd (<code>daemon-reload</code> → <code>restart</code>).</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission runs after authZ</strong>: mutating → schema → validating → persist; enable via <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>API groups:</strong> core <code>/api/v1</code> vs named <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;ver&gt;</code>; discover with <code>api-resources</code>/<code>api-versions</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Manifest typo kills the apiserver.</strong> <code>kubectl</code> dies too — recover by fixing the file on the node (<code>crictl logs</code> shows why).</li>
                    <li><strong>No backup.</strong> Always <code>cp … /tmp</code> before editing a control-plane manifest.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgot <code>daemon-reload</code>.</strong> A kubelet unit change won't apply until you reload systemd, then restart.</li>
                    <li><strong>Stray <code>$KUBECONFIG</code>.</strong> Silently overrides <code>~/.kube/config</code> — "where's my cluster?"</li>
                    <li><strong>Wrong admission order assumption.</strong> Mutating always precedes validating.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Back up, always:</strong> <code>sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/</code> before any edit.</li>
                    <li><strong>Watch it return:</strong> <code>crictl ps | grep apiserver</code> (the container ID changes) or <code>get pods -n kube-system -w</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl config view --minify</code> to confirm cluster/user/namespace fast.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; -o yaml | grep &lt;flag&gt;</code> to verify a flag took.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 10)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">config &amp; control-plane toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config view --minify ; kubectl config get-contexts ; kubectl config use-context &lt;c&gt;
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># back up before editing</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml       <span class="comment"># edit flags → kubelet restarts it</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep apiserver ; sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;       <span class="comment"># when kubectl is down</span>
sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml ; sudo systemctl daemon-reload &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl restart kubelet
kubectl api-resources ; kubectl api-versions ; journalctl -u kubelet -f</code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — enable an admission plugin, verify, then revert</h5>
                <p>No peeking — practise the back-up-edit-recover loop safely:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Back up <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> to <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
                    <li>Add <code>NamespaceAutoProvision</code> (or <code>NodeRestriction</code>) to <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> and save.</li>
                    <li>Wait for the apiserver to restart, then verify the flag is live.</li>
                    <li>Revert by restoring the backup; confirm <code>kubectl</code> still works.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal (on the control-plane node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># edit:  - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</span>
watch -n2 'sudo crictl ps | grep apiserver'      <span class="comment"># wait for a new container ID</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-$(hostname) -o yaml | grep enable-admission-plugins
<span class="comment"># revert</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
kubectl get nodes        <span class="comment"># kubectl works again ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 10 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Cluster-config recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Name the 3 kubeconfig sections and what each holds.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>clusters</code></td><td>server URL + CA cert (WHERE)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>users</code></td><td>client cert/key, token, or exec plugin (WHO)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>contexts</code></td><td>cluster + user + namespace (the COMBO)</td></tr></tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>current-context</code> selects the active context; <code>kubectl config view --minify</code> shows only it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Merge two kubeconfig files into one session.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">merge</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config:~/work/dev.yaml
kubectl config get-contexts                    <span class="comment"># union of both</span>
kubectl config view --flatten &gt; ~/.kube/merged  <span class="comment"># bake into one file</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Colon-separated (semicolon on Windows); on a name clash the first file wins. <code>--flatten</code> embeds all certs into one self-contained file.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable the <code>NodeRestriction</code> admission plugin — which file, which flag?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code> and add it to <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code>: <code>- --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code>. Save; the kubelet restarts the apiserver.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Back up first. Verify with <code>kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; -o yaml | grep enable-admission</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Find the current <code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code> of the apiserver.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find a flag</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">grep service-cluster-ip-range /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># - --service-cluster-ip-range=10.96.0.0/12</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>All apiserver flags live in its static-pod manifest's <code>command:</code> list — grep the file, or read the live pod's spec.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The apiserver won't start after an edit — how do you recover?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>kubectl</code> is down, so work on the node: <code>sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code> then <code>sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> to see the error. Fix the YAML (or restore the <code>/tmp</code> backup); the kubelet relaunches it within ~20s.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The component is a static pod the kubelet keeps retrying — you never "start" it manually; you just make the manifest valid.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Core API group vs a named group — give an example <code>apiVersion</code> of each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Core:</strong> <code>apiVersion: v1</code> (Pod, Service, ConfigMap) — served at <code>/api/v1</code>. <strong>Named:</strong> <code>apiVersion: apps/v1</code> (Deployment) — served at <code>/apis/apps/v1</code>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The core group has no group name (just <code>v1</code>); every other group is <code>&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Change the kubelet's <code>clusterDNS</code> and apply it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml      <span class="comment"># edit clusterDNS: [10.96.0.10]</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 20 --no-pager</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>It's a config-file change (not a unit change), so a plain <code>restart</code> suffices — no <code>daemon-reload</code> needed.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set a context's default namespace to <code>easypj</code>.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">set namespace</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>--current</code> edits the active context so every command defaults to <code>easypj</code> — no more <code>-n</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do mutating vs validating webhooks run in the request flow?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Both run in the <strong>admission</strong> phase, <em>after</em> authN+authZ and <em>before</em> etcd. Order: <strong>mutating</strong> (can change the object) → schema validation → <strong>validating</strong> (accept/reject only).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Mutating-first guarantees the <em>final</em>, defaulted object is the one judged by validation.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Discover which API version serves Ingress.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">discover</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl api-resources | grep -i ingress
<span class="comment"># ingresses   ing   networking.k8s.io/v1   true   Ingress</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Always look it up rather than guess — versions drift between releases. <code>api-resources</code> is the source of truth for your cluster.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch10-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 10 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style "configure it / fix it" tasks with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are the three main sections of a kubeconfig file? Draw the relationship between them.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the three sections</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The three sections:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. clusters    — WHERE to connect (API server endpoint + CA cert)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. users       — WHO you are (client cert + key, or token)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. contexts    — WHICH combo to use (cluster + user + namespace)</span>

apiVersion: v1
kind: Config
current-context: dev-context    # ← Active context

clusters:
- name: dev-cluster
  cluster:
    server: https://10.0.0.10:6443
    certificate-authority-data: &lt;base64-ca-cert&gt;

users:
- name: dev-admin
  user:
    client-certificate-data: &lt;base64-cert&gt;
    client-key-data: &lt;base64-key&gt;

contexts:
- name: dev-context              # ← Links cluster + user
  context:
    cluster: dev-cluster         # → References clusters[].name
    user: dev-admin              # → References users[].name
    namespace: default           <span class="comment"># Optional default namespace</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">relationship</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Relationship:</span>
<span class="comment">#  Context ──→ Cluster (server URL)</span>
<span class="comment">#     │</span>
<span class="comment">#     └──→ User (credentials)</span>
<span class="comment">#     │</span>
<span class="comment">#     └──→ Namespace (optional default)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>A kubeconfig is essentially a mapping: contexts bind a cluster to a user with an optional default namespace. You can have multiple clusters, users, and contexts in one file. The <code>current-context</code> field determines which context is active. On the CKA, understanding this structure is critical because exam questions give you different contexts per question. Each context points to a different cluster. If you use the wrong context, you'll work on the wrong cluster and fail the question.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You have two kubeconfig files: <code>/root/admin.conf</code> (production cluster) and <code>/root/dev.conf</code> (dev cluster). How do you merge them into one and switch between clusters?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">temporary merge (current session only)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Temporary merge (current session only)</span>
export KUBECONFIG=/root/admin.conf:/root/dev.conf
kubectl config view  <span class="comment"># Shows both clusters/contexts</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Permanent merge into one file</span>
KUBECONFIG=/root/admin.conf:/root/dev.conf \
  kubectl config view --flatten > ~/.kube/config

<span class="comment"># List all available contexts</span>
kubectl config get-contexts
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME         CLUSTER    AUTHINFO    NAMESPACE</span>
<span class="comment"># *         prod-ctx     prod       prod-admin  default</span>
<span class="comment">#           dev-ctx      dev        dev-admin   dev-ns</span>

<span class="comment"># Switch between clusters</span>
kubectl config use-context prod-ctx   # → production
kubectl config use-context dev-ctx    # → development

<span class="comment"># Verify current context</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># dev-ctx</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>KUBECONFIG</code> accepts a colon-separated list of files (semicolon on Windows). The <code>--flatten</code> flag resolves all external file references and outputs a self-contained config. On the CKA, the exam environment has pre-configured kubeconfig with multiple contexts — one per cluster/question. You MUST switch contexts at the start of each question. Always verify you're in the right context: <code>kubectl config current-context</code>. Working in the wrong context is the #1 reason candidates fail otherwise-correct answers.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are admission controllers? Name three important ones and explain how to enable/disable them.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">admission controllers intercept api requests after…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Admission controllers intercept API requests AFTER auth but BEFORE persistence</span>

<span class="comment"># View currently enabled admission controllers:</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-apiserver-controlplane \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep admission

<span class="comment"># Important admission controllers:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. NamespaceLifecycle — prevents creating objects in terminating namespaces</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. LimitRanger       — enforces LimitRange defaults in namespaces</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. ResourceQuota     — enforces ResourceQuota limits</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. NodeRestriction   — limits what kubelets can modify</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. PodSecurity       — enforces pod security standards</span>

<span class="comment"># Enable an admission controller:</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># Find or add:</span>
- --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,LimitRanger

<span class="comment"># Disable a specific one:</span>
- --disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass

<span class="comment"># After saving, the API server restarts automatically (static pod)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Admission controllers are plugins that govern what's allowed in the cluster. They run in order after authentication and authorization. There are two types: <strong>mutating</strong> (can modify the request, like adding default resource limits) and <strong>validating</strong> (can only accept/reject). On the CKA, you may be asked to enable a specific admission controller by editing the API server static pod manifest at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. After saving, kubelet restarts the API server — wait 30-60 seconds for it to come back up. Always verify with <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code>.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you find the API group and version for a specific Kubernetes resource? Show multiple methods.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl api-resources (most useful)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl api-resources (most useful)</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i deployment
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy   apps/v1   true   Deployment</span>
<span class="comment">#               ↑alias   ↑apiVersion ↑namespaced</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl api-versions (list all)</span>
kubectl api-versions
<span class="comment"># apps/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># networking.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># v1   ← core group (no prefix)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: kubectl explain (shows VERSION header)</span>
kubectl explain deployment
<span class="comment"># KIND:     Deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># VERSION:  apps/v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: API discovery endpoint</span>
kubectl get --raw /apis/apps/v1 | python3 -m json.tool

<span class="comment"># Quick reference — API groups:</span>
<span class="comment"># Core (v1):        Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Secret, PVC, SA, Node, Namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># apps/v1:          Deployment, ReplicaSet, DaemonSet, StatefulSet</span>
<span class="comment"># networking:       Ingress, NetworkPolicy</span>
<span class="comment"># rbac:             Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding</span>
<span class="comment"># storage:          StorageClass, VolumeAttachment</span>
<span class="comment"># batch/v1:         Job, CronJob</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl api-resources</code> is the definitive answer for the CKA. It shows the short name, API group/version, whether the resource is namespaced, and the kind. The core API group resources (Pod, Service, etc.) use <code>v1</code> with no group prefix in the <code>apiVersion</code>. All other resources use <code>&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code> format. The <code>kubectl explain</code> command is also invaluable — it shows the VERSION in its header output, plus detailed documentation of every field.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to create a new context called <code>staging-ctx</code> that connects to cluster <code>staging-cluster</code> as user <code>staging-admin</code> with default namespace <code>staging</code>. Then switch to it.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create the context</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the context</span>
kubectl config set-context staging-ctx \
  --cluster=staging-cluster \
  --user=staging-admin \
  --namespace=staging

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Switch to it</span>
kubectl config use-context staging-ctx
<span class="comment"># Switched to context "staging-ctx"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># staging-ctx</span>

kubectl config view --minify
<span class="comment"># Shows only staging-ctx details</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Test connectivity</span>
kubectl get pods
<span class="comment"># Should show pods in the staging namespace</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>kubectl config set-context</code> creates or updates a context entry in the kubeconfig. The <code>--cluster</code> and <code>--user</code> must reference entries that already exist in the kubeconfig's <code>clusters</code> and <code>users</code> sections. Setting <code>--namespace</code> makes every command in this context default to that namespace (no need for <code>-n staging</code> every time). On the CKA, the most common pattern is: <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code> then <code>kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=&lt;ns&gt;</code> — this sets the namespace for the current context without creating a new one.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you view and modify the API server configuration? What flags control authentication, authorization, and which admission controllers are active?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">api server is a static pod</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># API Server is a static pod — config is in the manifest</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml

<span class="comment"># Key flags to know:</span>
<span class="comment"># Authentication:</span>
--client-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
--authentication-token-webhook-config-file=...
--oidc-issuer-url=...

<span class="comment"># Authorization (order matters!):</span>
--authorization-mode=Node,RBAC
<span class="comment"># Options: AlwaysAllow, AlwaysDeny, Node, RBAC, Webhook, ABAC</span>

<span class="comment"># Admission Controllers:</span>
--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity
--disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass

<span class="comment"># To modify: edit the manifest directly</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects the change and restarts the API server</span>

<span class="comment"># Wait and verify:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver
<span class="comment"># Should show Running with RESTARTS incremented by 1</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The API server runs as a static pod, so its config is a YAML manifest at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Editing this file triggers kubelet to restart the pod automatically — no <code>kubectl apply</code> needed. On the CKA, you must be comfortable editing this file. The <code>--authorization-mode</code> flag determines the auth chain — requests are checked against each mode in order. <code>Node,RBAC</code> is the default: first check Node authorization (for kubelet), then RBAC for everything else. Be careful editing — a syntax error will take the API server down, and kubectl won't work until you fix it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl config view</code> and <code>kubectl config view --minify</code>? When should you use <code>--raw</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl config view — shows entire kubeconfig (all…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># kubectl config view — shows ENTIRE kubeconfig (all contexts)</span>
kubectl config view
<span class="comment"># Shows all clusters, users, contexts — can be very long</span>

<span class="comment"># kubectl config view --minify — current context ONLY</span>
kubectl config view --minify
<span class="comment"># Shows only the cluster, user, context for current-context</span>

<span class="comment"># kubectl config view --raw — shows actual certificate data</span>
kubectl config view --raw
<span class="comment"># Normally cert data is replaced with "DATA+OMITTED"</span>
<span class="comment"># --raw shows the full base64-encoded certificates</span>

<span class="comment"># Practical example: extract the CA cert</span>
kubectl config view --raw -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.certificate-authority-data}' | base64 -d > ca.crt

<span class="comment"># Extract the current context's server URL</span>
kubectl config view --minify -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.server}'
<span class="comment"># https://10.0.0.10:6443</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p><code>--minify</code> filters the output to only show what's relevant to the current context — essential on the CKA where kubeconfig has many contexts. <code>--raw</code> shows the actual certificate data instead of the <code>DATA+OMITTED</code> placeholder. You'd use <code>--raw</code> when you need to extract certificates for debugging or copying to another config. On the CKA, combine <code>--minify</code> with <code>-o jsonpath</code> to quickly extract specific values like the server URL, namespace, or user name.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The kube-apiserver won't start after you edited its manifest. How do you debug without <code>kubectl</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl is dead when api server is down</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># kubectl is DEAD when API server is down — use these alternatives:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Check container status via CRI</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver
<span class="comment"># Look for Exited containers with exit codes</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check API server container logs</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;
<span class="comment"># Shows the actual error message</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet logs (kubelet manages static pods)</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 30

<span class="comment"># 4. Check the manifest for syntax errors</span>
sudo cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for typos, wrong indentation, missing values</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Validate YAML syntax</span>
python3 -c "import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open('/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml'))"

<span class="comment"># 6. Common mistakes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Extra space in flag: --enable-admission -plugins (wrong!)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing value: --authorization-mode= (empty!)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong file path: --client-ca-file=/wrong/path</span>
<span class="comment"># - Typo: --enbale-admission-plugins (misspelled!)</span>

<span class="comment"># After fixing, kubelet auto-restarts the API server (30-60s)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>When you break the API server, you lose kubectl access — this is the most stressful CKA scenario. Your lifeline is <code>crictl</code> (CRI tool that talks directly to containerd) and <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>. The pattern: (1) <code>crictl ps -a</code> to see the crash-looping container, (2) <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> to read the error, (3) fix the manifest, (4) wait for auto-recovery. Don't panic — kubelet keeps trying to restart the static pod. As soon as the manifest is valid, the API server comes back within 30-60 seconds.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the Kubernetes API request flow? List every step from when you type <code>kubectl get pods</code> to when you see the output.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">complete api request flow</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Complete API request flow:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. kubectl reads ~/.kube/config</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Finds server URL, client cert, CA cert</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. kubectl sends HTTPS GET /api/v1/namespaces/default/pods</span>
<span class="comment">#    → TLS handshake with API server</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. API Server: Authentication</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Validates client certificate (or token/OIDC)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Identifies: "This is user kubernetes-admin"</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. API Server: Authorization (RBAC)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Checks: Can kubernetes-admin GET pods in default?</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Evaluates ClusterRoleBindings/RoleBindings</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. API Server: Admission Control</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Runs mutating admission controllers (modify request)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Runs validating admission controllers (accept/reject)</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. API Server: Read from etcd</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Retrieves pod objects from etcd datastore</span>

<span class="comment"># 7. Response serialization</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Converts internal objects to requested format (JSON/YAML/table)</span>

<span class="comment"># 8. kubectl: Output formatting</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Applies -o wide, -o yaml, --sort-by, JSONPath filters</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Understanding this flow helps with debugging: if authentication fails, check certificates. If authorization fails, check RBAC (Role/ClusterRole bindings). If admission fails, check admission controller configuration. For read operations (GET/LIST), admission controllers may still run (e.g., webhook admission). For write operations (CREATE/UPDATE), all three stages are critical. On the CKA, if you get "forbidden" errors, the problem is at step 4 (RBAC). If you get "denied by admission controller," the problem is at step 5.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you check what API groups and resources are available in your cluster? What is the difference between core API group and named API groups?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all api groups and versions</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all API groups and versions</span>
kubectl api-versions
<span class="comment"># apps/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># batch/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># networking.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># v1   ← This is the CORE group (no name prefix)</span>

<span class="comment"># List all resources with their API group</span>
kubectl api-resources -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME         SHORTNAMES  APIVERSION                    VERBS</span>
<span class="comment"># pods         po          v1                            [create delete get ...]</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments  deploy      apps/v1                       [create delete get ...]</span>
<span class="comment"># ingresses    ing         networking.k8s.io/v1          [create delete get ...]</span>

<span class="comment"># Core group (legacy, no prefix):</span>
<span class="comment"># API path: /api/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># Resources: Pod, Service, ConfigMap, Secret, PVC, Node, Namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># apiVersion in YAML: v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Named groups (newer, prefixed):</span>
<span class="comment"># API path: /apis/apps/v1, /apis/networking.k8s.io/v1</span>
<span class="comment"># Resources: Deployment, Ingress, NetworkPolicy, etc.</span>
<span class="comment"># apiVersion in YAML: apps/v1, networking.k8s.io/v1</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The core group (accessed at <code>/api/v1</code>) contains the original K8s resources and uses just <code>v1</code> as the apiVersion. Named groups (accessed at <code>/apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;version&gt;</code>) were added later for better organization. New features always go into named groups. On the CKA, knowing which group a resource belongs to determines the correct <code>apiVersion</code> in manifests. <code>kubectl api-resources</code> is the ultimate reference — it shows every installed resource, its API group, whether it's namespaced, and its shortname alias.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 10 Visual Summary — request pipeline + the two config models</div>
<pre>
   THE REQUEST PIPELINE (every write)
   kubectl ─▶ 🔐 authN ─▶ 🛂 authZ(RBAC) ─▶ 🧪 admission(mutating ▶ schema ▶ validating) ─▶ 🗄️ etcd ─▶ controllers/scheduler
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   CONFIGURING THE CLUSTER — two models
   control-plane components (apiserver/scheduler/etcd)   →  edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml  →  kubelet auto-restarts (BACK UP!)
   the kubelet itself (a systemd service)                →  edit /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml      →  daemon-reload + restart
   kubeconfig:  clusters(WHERE) + users(WHO) + contexts(COMBO);  merge via $KUBECONFIG (first-wins)
   API surface:  core /api/v1  vs  named /apis/&lt;group&gt;/&lt;ver&gt;   (api-resources · api-versions)            ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> every write runs the gauntlet — authenticate, authorize, then admission (mutating defaults first, then validation), then it's stored. You shape the cluster by configuring two different kinds of thing: <strong>control-plane components</strong>, which are static pods you reconfigure by editing files the kubelet watches; and the <strong>kubelet</strong> itself, a systemd service you configure with <code>config.yaml</code> + <code>systemctl</code>.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the single habit that protects you is <strong>back up before you edit</strong> a control-plane manifest — then recovery is just restoring a file. Know the admission order (mutating → validating), where each config lives, and how to read the API surface, and Chapter 10's tasks are routine.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH10-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 10 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 11 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch11">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 11 · etcd — Backup &amp; Restore</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">etcd is the cluster's entire memory — every object you've ever created lives there. Lose it without a backup and the cluster is <em>gone</em>. That makes <strong>etcd snapshot &amp; restore</strong> the single highest-value skill an administrator has, and an <strong>almost-guaranteed CKA task</strong>. This chapter makes the backup/restore dance automatic.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Almost-Guaranteed Task</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🗄️ etcd</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ What etcd is, what it stores (<code>/registry/*</code>), and why losing it loses the cluster</li>
                    <li>✅ etcd's <strong>Raft</strong> consensus — leader/follower, quorum math, why members are odd</li>
                    <li>✅ Take a <strong>verified snapshot</strong> with <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> + the 3 TLS flags</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Restore</strong> to a new data-dir and repoint the etcd static pod (both places!)</li>
                    <li>✅ Secure etcd: TLS (default) + <strong>encryption at rest</strong> for Secrets</li>
                    <li>✅ Automate nightly backups and check cluster health</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">etcd backup/restore <span class="cka-weight">ALMOST GUARANTEED</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 The task you will almost certainly get</h5>
                <p>"Back up etcd to <code>/opt/backup.db</code>" and "restore etcd from this snapshot" are among the most common CKA tasks — and they're free marks once the muscle memory is there.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Memorise the 4 inputs:</strong> <code>--endpoints</code> + <code>--cacert</code> + <code>--cert</code> + <code>--key</code>. The cert paths live in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Restore to a NEW data-dir</strong> — never the live one — and update <code>etcd.yaml</code> in <em>two</em> places (the <code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the hostPath volume).</li>
                    <li><strong><code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></strong> is required for the snapshot commands.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 11 Roadmap — the cluster's memory, backed up &amp; restorable</div>
<pre>
   11.1 WHAT is etcd ──▶ 11.2 Raft &amp; quorum ──▶ 11.3 BACKUP (snapshot save) ──▶ 11.4 RESTORE ──▶ 11.5 SECURE
   KV store · /registry   leader/follower         the 3 TLS flags + verify        new data-dir       TLS + encryption
   Secrets unencrypted    quorum = N/2+1                                          repoint etcd.yaml   at rest
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: snapshot save ▶ 💥 disaster ▶ snapshot restore ▶ repoint static pod ▶ cluster back.
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-1"><strong>11.1</strong> What is etcd?</a></td><td>What holds all cluster state?</td><td>a distributed KV store at <code>/registry/*</code>; Secrets unencrypted by default.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-2"><strong>11.2</strong> Architecture &amp; Raft</a></td><td>How does it stay consistent &amp; HA?</td><td>Raft leader/follower; quorum = N/2+1; odd members (3,5,7).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-3"><strong>11.3</strong> Backup</a></td><td>How do I snapshot it?</td><td><code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> + endpoint + 3 TLS flags; verify with <code>status</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-4"><strong>11.4</strong> Restore</a></td><td>How do I bring it back?</td><td>restore to a NEW data-dir; repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code> (flag + hostPath).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s11-5"><strong>11.5</strong> Security &amp; encryption</a></td><td>How do I protect it?</td><td>TLS (default) + EncryptionConfiguration for Secrets at rest.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s11-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.1 What is etcd?</h3>
                <p><strong>etcd</strong> is a distributed, strongly-consistent <strong>key-value store</strong> — and it is the <em>only</em> place Kubernetes keeps state. Every node, pod, Deployment, Secret, ConfigMap, and RBAC rule is a key under <code>/registry/</code>. The apiserver is the sole component that talks to it; everything else goes through the apiserver. That's the whole reason an etcd snapshot <em>is</em> a cluster backup.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">🗝️</div><h5>Key-Value Store</h5><p>data is keys → values under <code>/registry/…</code> — simple, fast, and easy to snapshot whole.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div><h5>Distributed</h5><p>runs as a cluster of members (Raft) so it survives a node loss — no single point of failure.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div><h5>Strongly Consistent</h5><p>every member agrees on every committed write — reads never return stale, contradictory state.</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">👁️</div><h5>Watch API</h5><p>clients <em>watch</em> key ranges and are pushed changes — the engine behind every K8s controller.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="analogy-box"><h5>🧠 In plain English</h5><p>etcd is the cluster's <strong>memory</strong>. The control plane is the brain that thinks, but every thought — every object's desired and current state — is written in etcd. Wipe etcd with no backup and the cluster has total amnesia: the nodes and containers may still be running, but Kubernetes no longer knows they exist. That's why this chapter is the most important in Part 3.</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">what's inside etcd — the <code>/registry</code> keyspace</div>
<pre>
   etcd  (key → value, value = the object as protobuf/JSON)
   /registry/
     ├─ namespaces/easypj-dev
     ├─ pods/easypj-dev/easypj-web-7d8f9-abcde
     ├─ deployments/easypj-dev/easypj-web
     ├─ replicasets/easypj-dev/easypj-web-7d8f9
     ├─ services/easypj-dev/easypj-web
     ├─ configmaps/easypj-dev/easypj-config
     ├─ secrets/easypj-dev/easypj-secret        ⚠️ stored UNENCRYPTED by default (§11.5)
     ├─ clusterroles/… · rolebindings/…
     └─ … every object in the cluster
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   only the kube-apiserver reads/writes etcd → one snapshot of etcd = the entire cluster's state
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 Secrets sit unencrypted in etcd by default</h5><p>A Secret's value is only <strong>base64-encoded</strong> in etcd — anyone who can read the etcd data files (or a snapshot) can read your Secrets in plaintext. That's why §11.5 (encryption at rest) and tight file permissions on snapshots matter, and why a leaked <code>etcd-backup.db</code> is a full credential breach.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 What etcd does NOT back up</h5><p>An etcd snapshot captures <em>Kubernetes objects</em> — not the data inside your <strong>PersistentVolumes</strong>. A restored cluster knows the PVC exists, but the actual disk contents are the storage backend's responsibility (Ch 22). Back up app data separately.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.2 etcd Architecture &amp; Raft</h3>
                <p>For high availability, etcd runs as a <strong>cluster of members</strong> that stay consistent using the <strong>Raft</strong> consensus algorithm. One member is elected <strong>leader</strong> and handles all writes; the rest are <strong>followers</strong>. A write is only committed once a <strong>majority</strong> (quorum) of members has acknowledged it — which is why etcd never loses a committed write but also why it needs an odd number of members.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">3-member etcd — Raft keeps the memory consistent</div>
<pre>
   ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────┐
   │ etcd-0   │◄────►│ etcd-1   │◄────►│ etcd-2   │     3 members
   │ LEADER   │      │ follower │      │ follower │     quorum = N/2+1 = 2
   │ :2379 cli│      │          │      │          │     tolerates 1 failure
   │ :2380 peer│     │          │      │          │
   └──────────┘      └──────────┘      └──────────┘
   WRITE: client ─► LEADER ─► replicate to followers ─► commit once a MAJORITY ack ─► ✅ ack client
   READ:  served from any member (linearizable reads go via the leader)
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Members</th><th>Quorum (N/2+1)</th><th>Failures tolerated</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>dev only — no HA</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>2</td><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>✅ the common HA choice</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td>3</td><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>✅ large/critical clusters</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2 or 4</td><td>2 / 3</td><td>0 / 1</td><td>❌ even = no extra tolerance, just more risk</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ Always odd, and beware quorum loss</h5><p>Even counts are pointless: a 4-member cluster tolerates the same 1 failure as a 3-member one but has more to go wrong. If a cluster <strong>loses quorum</strong> (e.g. 2 of 3 members down), etcd goes <strong>read-only / unavailable</strong> and the whole control plane stalls — recovery means restoring members or a snapshot. This is why production etcd is 3 or 5 members across failure domains.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Stacked vs external etcd</h5><p>kubeadm's default is <strong>stacked</strong>: etcd runs as a static pod <em>on each control-plane node</em>, alongside the apiserver. The alternative is <strong>external</strong> etcd on dedicated machines (better isolation, independent scaling). The backup/restore commands are identical; the operational difference is that with external etcd you stop the apiservers manually during a restore (Ch 33).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.3 etcd Backup — the Snapshot</h3>
                <p>A backup is a single <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> call. The catch is that etcd is TLS-protected, so <code>etcdctl</code> must authenticate with three certs plus the endpoint — <strong>four inputs you'll memorise</strong>. Get the cert paths from the etcd static-pod manifest, run the snapshot, then <em>always verify it</em>.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · snapshot save</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Snapshot saved at /opt/etcd-backup.db</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔑 The 4 inputs every <code>snapshot save</code> needs</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎯</span> --endpoints</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code> — etcd's client port on the local control-plane node</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔐</span> --cacert</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</code> — verifies etcd's server cert</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📄</span> --cert</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code> — etcdctl's client cert</div></div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row"><div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🗝️</span> --key</div><div class="anatomy-value"><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code> — its private key</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 Forgot the cert paths? Three ways to find them on the exam</h5><p>Any of these recovers the exact cert flags — use whichever you remember under pressure:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find the cert paths (3 methods)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1 — read them off the running etcd POD</span>
kubectl -n kube-system describe pod etcd-$(hostname) | grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca'

<span class="comment"># Method 2 — grep the etcd static-pod MANIFEST (works even when kubectl is down)</span>
grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file|listen-client' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># --cert-file → --cert · --key-file → --key · --trusted-ca-file → --cacert</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3 — the apiserver's etcd-client flags point at the same CA + a client cert</span>
grep etcd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code></pre></div></div>
                <h4>✅ Always verify the snapshot before you trust it</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | fe01c2ab |    95620 |       1480 |     4.6 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>⚡ Speed: alias the TLS flags</h5><p>Save typing all session: <code>alias e='sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key'</code> → then just <code>e snapshot save /opt/backup.db</code> and <code>e endpoint health</code>.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.4 etcd Restore</h3>
                <p>Restore reads a snapshot into a <strong>fresh data directory</strong>, then you repoint the etcd static pod at it. Two rules save you: restore to a <em>new</em> dir (never the live <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>), and update <code>etcd.yaml</code> in <strong>two</strong> places — the <code>--data-dir</code> flag <em>and</em> the hostPath volume that mounts it.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · restore</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># ── STEP 1: restore the snapshot into a NEW data directory ──</span>
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored
<span class="comment"># {"level":"info","msg":"restoring snapshot","path":"/opt/etcd-backup.db",</span>
<span class="comment">#  "data-dir":"/var/lib/etcd-restored","wal-dir":".../member/wal","snap-dir":".../member/snap"}</span>

<span class="comment"># ── STEP 2: repoint the etcd static pod — edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml ──</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment">#  (a) the flag:    BEFORE  - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment">#                   AFTER   - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment">#  (b) the volume:  BEFORE  hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment">#                   AFTER   hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd-restored   ← change BOTH, or the restore is invisible</span>

<span class="comment"># ── STEP 3: the kubelet auto-restarts etcd; watch it come back ──</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># etcd-control-plane   1/1   Running   0   10s</span>

<span class="comment"># ── STEP 4: verify — cluster is back at the snapshot's point-in-time ──</span>
kubectl get nodes ; kubectl get pods -A        <span class="comment"># everything from the snapshot is back ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">BACKUP ▶ 💥 DISASTER ▶ RESTORE — the #1 admin skill</div>
<pre>
   etcdctl snapshot save ──► 💾 /opt/etcd-backup.db        (needs --endpoints --cacert --cert --key)
        │  verify: snapshot status --write-table
        │
   ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ 💥 DISASTER (someone deleted prod / etcd corrupted) ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─
        ▼
   etcdctl snapshot restore ──► 📁 /var/lib/etcd-RESTORED   (a NEW dir — never the live one!)
        ▼
   edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
        change  --data-dir   AND   the hostPath volume    ──► BOTH, or etcd starts on old/empty data
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet auto-restarts etcd ──► ✅ cluster restored to the snapshot's moment
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 The three restore mistakes that cost marks</h5><ul><li><strong>Restoring into the live data-dir</strong> while etcd is running → corruption. Always a new dir.</li><li><strong>Updating only the flag, not the hostPath</strong> (or vice-versa) → etcd mounts the wrong directory and your restore "didn't work."</li><li><strong>Forgetting <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></strong> → the v2 API doesn't understand snapshots.</li></ul></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 What about HA (multiple etcd members)?</h5><p>On a multi-member cluster you restore the snapshot on <em>one</em> node, then either re-add the others as fresh members or restore the same snapshot to each with matching <code>--initial-cluster</code> settings. For the CKA's single stacked control plane, the steps above are all you need.</p></div>
                <h4>📋 The one-glance etcd backup &amp; restore cheat sheet</h4>
                <p>Everything you need for the exam task, in one place — copy the pattern, swap the paths:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CKA etcd — complete backup &amp; restore cheat sheet</div>
<pre>
   ┌─ BACKUP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   │ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db \                       │
   │   --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \                                     │
   │   --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \                               │
   │   --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \                             │
   │   --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key                                │
   │ VERIFY:  etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup.db -w table                   │
   ├─ RESTORE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db \                    │
   │   --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored        ← a NEW dir                     │
   │ THEN edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml — BOTH:                      │
   │   1) - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                                   │
   │   2) volumes → hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd-restored                       │
   │ WAIT: kubelet auto-restarts etcd · VERIFY: kubectl get pods -n kube-system │
   ├─ FORGOT THE CERT PATHS? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml │
   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
                <h4>🏥 Checking etcd health &amp; status</h4>
                <p>Before (and after) a backup/restore, confirm etcd is healthy — these show the leader, DB size, members, and write latency:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">etcd health &amp; status (alias the TLS flags as $CERTS)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># is it alive?</span>
etcdctl endpoint health $CERTS
<span class="comment"># https://127.0.0.1:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 2.3ms</span>

<span class="comment"># leader · version · DB size (the &lt;2 GB quota lives here)</span>
etcdctl endpoint status $CERTS --write-out=table
<span class="comment"># +------------------------+--------+---------+---------+-----------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |        ENDPOINT        |   ID   | VERSION | DB SIZE | IS LEADER |</span>
<span class="comment"># | https://127.0.0.1:2379 | abc123 | 3.5.x   | 4.2 MB  |   true    |</span>
<span class="comment"># +------------------------+--------+---------+---------+-----------+</span>

<span class="comment"># the members &amp; their roles (who's the leader, are all started?)</span>
etcdctl member list $CERTS --write-out=table

<span class="comment"># write-latency benchmark (etcd is fsync-bound — slow disks = a sick cluster)</span>
etcdctl check perf $CERTS</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div><h5>Healthy etcd indicators</h5><ul><li><code>endpoint health</code> says "is healthy"</li><li>DB size well under the quota (~2 GB)</li><li>Raft term stable (not incrementing)</li><li>all members show <code>started</code></li><li>write latency under ~10 ms</li><li>exactly one <code>IS LEADER = true</code></li></ul></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🚨</div><h5>Unhealthy red flags</h5><ul><li><code>endpoint health</code> timeouts/errors</li><li>DB size near/over quota → needs compaction</li><li><code>"no leader"</code> in member list</li><li>high/rising Raft term (frequent elections)</li><li><code>mvcc: database space exceeded</code></li><li>members showing <code>unstarted</code></li></ul></div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 11.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s11-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">11.5 etcd Security &amp; Encryption at Rest</h3>
                <p>etcd is protected on the wire by <strong>TLS</strong> already (that's why backups need certs). But the data <em>at rest</em> — including Secrets — is only base64-encoded on disk. <strong>Encryption at rest</strong> closes that gap: you give the apiserver an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> and it encrypts Secret values before writing them to etcd.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">etcd security — defense in depth (4 layers)</div>
<pre>
   ┌─────────────────────────── etcd security layers ───────────────────────────┐
   │ 🧱 Layer 1 — NETWORK ISOLATION                                              │
   │    etcd reachable ONLY from the control plane · firewall :2379 from the      │
   │    apiserver IP only · :2380 from other etcd peers only                      │
   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🔒 Layer 2 — TLS IN TRANSIT  (default in kubeadm)                            │
   │    apiserver ──TLS──▶ etcd (client certs) · etcd ──TLS──▶ peers (peer certs) │
   │    all verified against /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt                      │
   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🔐 Layer 3 — ENCRYPTION AT REST  (you enable — §11.5)                        │
   │    EncryptionConfiguration encrypts Secrets BEFORE they hit disk             │
   │    provider: aescbc / secretbox · without it: Secrets are base64 (readable!) │
   ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   │ 🛂 Layer 4 — RBAC + AUTH                                                     │
   │    only the apiserver holds etcd client creds · NO user talks to etcd direct │
   │    (everything goes through the apiserver, which RBAC then guards)           │
   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   threat model: anyone with the etcd data files or a snapshot can read every Secret in plaintext —
   layers 3 &amp; 4 are what stop a leaked backup or a curious user from becoming a breach.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
- resources:
  - secrets                       <span class="comment"># encrypt Secret objects…</span>
  providers:
  - aescbc:                       <span class="comment"># …with AES-CBC (a 32-byte key, base64)</span>
      keys:
      - name: key1
        secret: &lt;base64-32-byte-key&gt;
  - identity: {}                  <span class="comment"># fallback so EXISTING (unencrypted) reads still work</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · enable + re-encrypt</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. point the apiserver at the config (back up the manifest first!)</span>
<span class="comment"># in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml add:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#   + a hostPath volume mounting /etc/kubernetes/enc into the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. re-write every existing Secret so it gets encrypted with the new key</span>
kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>identity</code> first vs second matters</h5><p>The provider list is tried <strong>in order for writes</strong> (first provider encrypts), and <strong>all providers are tried for reads</strong>. Putting <code>aescbc</code> first means new writes are encrypted; keeping <code>identity</code> in the list lets the apiserver still <em>read</em> the old plaintext Secrets until you re-encrypt them. Step 2's <code>get … | replace</code> forces a re-write so everything ends up encrypted. Verify by reading the raw key directly from etcd — an encrypted value starts with <code>k8s:enc:aescbc:…</code>, not readable text.</p></div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Beyond aescbc — KMS v2</h5><p><code>aescbc</code> keeps the key in the config file on the node (better than nothing). Production uses a <strong>KMS provider</strong> (<code>kms:</code> v2): the apiserver asks an external key-management service (cloud KMS, Vault) to wrap a per-object data key — so the master key never touches the node. Same <code>--encryption-provider-config</code> flag, a different provider block.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — how etcd actually stores &amp; commits your data</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>etcd's durability and consistency come from three pieces working together: a <strong>WAL</strong> (write-ahead log) for crash safety, a <strong>BoltDB</strong> B+tree for the keyspace, and <strong>MVCC</strong> revisions so every change is versioned. Understanding them explains snapshots, "database space exceeded" alarms, and why defrag exists.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   A WRITE (e.g. kubectl scale → apiserver → etcd)
     ① leader appends the change to its WAL (write-ahead log) on disk      ← crash-safe
     ② leader replicates the log entry to followers (Raft)
     ③ once a MAJORITY have persisted it → the entry is COMMITTED
     ④ each member applies it to its BoltDB B+tree (the keyspace) under a NEW revision
     ⑤ leader acks the client
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   MVCC: every write bumps a global revision; old revisions are kept (history) until COMPACTION
   COMPACTION drops old revisions (frees logical space) · DEFRAG returns freed space to the disk
   a SNAPSHOT = a consistent copy of the BoltDB file at one revision (what `snapshot save` writes)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why "database space exceeded" happens:</strong> etcd has a <strong>quota</strong> (<code>--quota-backend-bytes</code>, default ~2 GB). MVCC history + a busy cluster fill it; etcd then raises a <strong>NOSPACE alarm</strong> and goes <em>read-only</em> — the control plane freezes. Recovery: compact to an old revision, <code>defrag</code> to reclaim disk, then <code>etcdctl alarm disarm</code>. This is the deep version of "etcd is full and my cluster won't take writes."</div>

                    <h5>The on-disk layout — what a snapshot actually copies</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   /var/lib/etcd/member/
     ├─ wal/   write-ahead log — sequential, fsync'd; EVERY write lands here FIRST (durability)
     │         0000000000000001-….wal
     └─ snap/  periodic compacted snapshots + the live database
        └─ db  the BoltDB B+tree — all keys in sorted order   ← this file IS what `snapshot save` copies
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   MVCC in practice — every change is a NEW revision; old ones are KEPT until compaction:
     PUT    /registry/pods/easypj-dev/web   → revision 100
     PUT    /registry/pods/easypj-dev/web   → revision 101   (an update — 100 still on disk)
     DELETE /registry/pods/easypj-dev/web   → revision 102   (a tombstone, not an erase)
</pre>

                    <h5>Raft leader election — what happens when the etcd leader dies</h5>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   followers stop hearing the leader's heartbeat
     1. a follower's election timeout fires (~1s, randomised so they don't all start at once)
     2. it becomes a CANDIDATE and increments the term
     3. it requests votes from the other members
     4. a MAJORITY votes yes → it's the new LEADER → starts sending heartbeats
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ⏱️ during an election (~1s) etcd is UNAVAILABLE FOR WRITES → the apiserver briefly can't persist.
      this is why etcd wants low-latency disks &amp; network, and an ODD member count for clean majorities.
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>compaction &amp; defrag</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl compact &lt;rev&gt;</code> drops old MVCC revisions; <code>etcdctl defrag</code> returns the freed pages to the filesystem. <code>--auto-compaction-mode=periodic --auto-compaction-retention=8h</code> automates it.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>NOSPACE alarm recovery</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl alarm list</code> → compact + defrag → <code>etcdctl alarm disarm</code> to take writes again.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>member &amp; learner management</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl member add/remove/list</code>; a <strong>learner</strong> joins read-only and catches up before voting — safer HA growth.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>quorum-loss recovery</strong></td><td>lost majority? restore a snapshot with <code>--force-new-cluster</code> on one survivor, then re-add members.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>performance tuning</strong></td><td><code>--heartbeat-interval</code>/<code>--election-timeout</code> for high-latency networks; etcd wants fast disks (it's fsync-bound).</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>

                    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>④ The deepest connection — how an etcd write becomes a cluster-wide event.</strong> MVCC revisions aren't just history; they're the engine behind <em>everything reactive</em> in Kubernetes. This is where Ch 11 (etcd), Ch 6 (<code>get -w</code>) and Ch 1 (controllers/informers) join up:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl scale deploy easypj-web --replicas=5
        ▼
   apiserver writes the new spec to etcd  →  etcd bumps the global REVISION (say 95620 → 95621)
        ▼
   etcd WATCH: every client watching that key range is pushed the change AT that revision
        ▼
   the apiserver is etcd's watcher → it fans the event out to ITS watchers:
        ReplicaSet controller (informer) sees replicas 3→5  →  creates 2 pods
        scheduler sees the new pods (nodeName="")           →  binds them
        kubelet on each node sees its bound pod             →  starts containers
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   resourceVersion (Ch 6) IS the etcd revision. A watch resumes from one; a stale watcher gets
   410 Gone and re-lists. The whole control loop is event-driven on top of etcd's MVCC watch stream.
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this is the "to the core" insight:</strong> nothing in Kubernetes polls. One etcd write at a new revision ripples — via the apiserver's watch — to every controller, the scheduler, and the kubelets, each reacting and writing <em>their</em> change back as a further revision. etcd isn't a passive database the cluster reads; it's the heartbeat whose every tick drives the next reconciliation. That's why losing it (this chapter) loses not just data but the cluster's ability to <em>act</em>.</div>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>etcd holds ALL cluster state</strong> at <code>/registry/*</code> — one snapshot = the whole cluster. Secrets are unencrypted by default.</li>
                    <li><strong>Backup:</strong> <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save</code> + <code>--endpoints</code> + the 3 TLS flags; verify with <code>snapshot status --write-table</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Restore</strong> into a <em>new</em> data-dir, then repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code> in <strong>two</strong> places (<code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the hostPath volume).</li>
                    <li><strong>Quorum = N/2+1</strong>; odd members (3 → tolerate 1, 5 → tolerate 2).</li>
                    <li><strong>Encryption at rest</strong> = <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> + <code>--encryption-provider-config</code>; re-encrypt with <code>get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Missing TLS flags.</strong> <code>snapshot save</code> without <code>--cacert/--cert/--key</code> = "context deadline exceeded."</li>
                    <li><strong>Restoring into the live data-dir.</strong> Always a NEW dir, or you corrupt running etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>Updating only one of the two places</strong> in <code>etcd.yaml</code> (flag vs hostPath) → restore appears to fail.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></strong> → snapshot commands don't exist in v2.</li>
                    <li><strong>Assuming etcd backs up PV data.</strong> It backs up <em>objects</em>, not volume contents.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Alias the TLS flags</strong> (see §11.3) so every etcdctl call is two words.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cert paths</strong> are always in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> — grep it instead of guessing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Always take a fresh snapshot before a restore</strong> — your current state is itself worth saving.</li>
                    <li><code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> / <code>endpoint status --write-table</code> for a one-line health check.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 11)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the etcdctl toolkit (ETCDCTL_API=3)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># with the TLS flags (alias them):  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=… --cert=… --key=…</span>
etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db        <span class="comment"># BACK UP</span>
etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table   <span class="comment"># verify</span>
etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored   <span class="comment"># RESTORE</span>
etcdctl member list -w table                     <span class="comment"># cluster members</span>
etcdctl endpoint health ; etcdctl endpoint status -w table   <span class="comment"># health</span>
etcdctl alarm list ; etcdctl defrag ; etcdctl alarm disarm   <span class="comment"># NOSPACE recovery</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — back up, "lose" a Deployment, restore it</h5>
                <p>The exact CKA disaster-recovery loop. No peeking:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Snapshot etcd to <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code> and verify it.</li>
                    <li>Delete the <code>easypj-web</code> Deployment (the "disaster").</li>
                    <li>Restore the snapshot into a new data-dir and repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code>.</li>
                    <li>Confirm the Deployment is back.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal (control-plane node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">alias e='sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key'
e snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db ; e snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table

kubectl delete deploy easypj-web -n easypj-dev          <span class="comment"># 💥 disaster</span>

sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml            <span class="comment"># --data-dir + hostPath → /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># wait for etcd + apiserver to restart…</span>
kubectl get deploy easypj-web -n easypj-dev             <span class="comment"># it's back ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 11 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">etcd backup/restore recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write the full <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> command with TLS flags.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">snapshot save</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Four inputs: endpoint + the three certs. Without them etcdctl can't authenticate to the TLS-protected etcd and times out.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Restore a snapshot and repoint the static pod — every step.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">restore</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># 1) - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># 2) hostPath volume etcd-data → path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet auto-restarts etcd; verify:</span>
kubectl get nodes</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>New data-dir, then update BOTH the flag and the hostPath — the most-failed half of this task is forgetting the volume.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do you find the 3 cert paths etcdctl needs?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find cert paths</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca-file' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># --cert-file → --cert · --key-file → --key · --trusted-ca-file → --cacert</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>They're under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code> by default, but reading them off the manifest is foolproof regardless of the cluster's layout.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Verify a snapshot file before trusting it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">verify</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table
<span class="comment"># shows HASH · REVISION · TOTAL KEYS · TOTAL SIZE</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>A non-zero key count and sane size confirm the file is a real, complete snapshot — not an empty/aborted one.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Quorum math: how many failures does a 5-member cluster tolerate?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>2.</strong> Quorum = N/2+1 = 5/2+1 = 3, so the cluster keeps working as long as 3 of 5 are up — i.e. it tolerates 2 failures.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>3 members tolerate 1; 5 tolerate 2; 7 tolerate 3. Even counts add risk without adding tolerance — always odd.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A restore failed with "data-dir not empty" — why, and the fix?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>You restored into an existing/used directory. Restore into a <strong>fresh</strong> path (or remove the target first): <code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code> where that dir doesn't already exist.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>snapshot restore</code> insists on creating a clean data-dir so it can't silently merge with live data — which is exactly the corruption it's protecting you from.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Enable encryption at rest for Secrets (outline).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Write an <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> (provider <code>aescbc</code> with a 32-byte key, then <code>identity</code>), add <code>--encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml</code> (+ a hostPath volume) to the apiserver manifest, then re-write existing Secrets:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">re-encrypt</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get secrets -A -o json | kubectl replace -f -</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>New writes encrypt immediately; the <code>replace</code> forces existing Secrets to be re-written (and thus encrypted). Verify by reading the raw etcd key — it should start <code>k8s:enc:aescbc:</code>.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">External vs stacked etcd — one operational difference.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Stacked:</strong> etcd is a static pod on each control-plane node (kubeadm default) — the kubelet manages it. <strong>External:</strong> etcd runs on dedicated machines; during a restore you stop the apiservers manually (no kubelet auto-restart of etcd).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>External isolates etcd failures from the control plane and scales independently, at the cost of more machines to run.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Check etcd cluster health from the command line.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">health</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=… --cert=… --key=…
sudo ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list -w table</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>endpoint health</code> confirms each member responds; <code>member list</code> shows roles and whether the cluster has a leader.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Automate a nightly etcd backup.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>A root <strong>cron</strong> entry on the control-plane node is simplest and most reliable (it doesn't depend on the cluster being healthy):</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/cron.d/etcd-backup</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">0 2 * * * root ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-$(date +\%F).db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Host cron beats a Kubernetes CronJob here: if the cluster is sick, a CronJob may not run — but the backup is exactly what you need then. Ship the files off-node and prune old ones.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch11-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 11 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style backup/restore/recovery tasks with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write the complete <code>etcdctl</code> command to take a snapshot backup of etcd to <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code>. Where do you find the required certificate paths?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find certificate paths from etcd static pod manifest</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find certificate paths from etcd static pod manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -E "\-\-cert|\-\-key|\-\-ca"
<span class="comment"># --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</span>
<span class="comment"># --trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Take the snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify the snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | abcdef12 |    45678 |       1234 |     5.2 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>This is a <strong>guaranteed CKA exam question</strong>. The three certificates are mandatory — etcd uses mutual TLS. The trick is knowing where to find them: <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> has the exact paths in its command arguments. Note the flag name differences: etcd uses <code>--cert-file</code> and <code>--trusted-ca-file</code>, but etcdctl uses <code>--cert</code> and <code>--cacert</code>. <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> must be set — API v2 uses different commands and snapshot formats. Always verify with <code>snapshot status</code> to confirm the backup is valid.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write the complete procedure to restore etcd from a snapshot at <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code>. What must you update after restoring?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">restore to a new data directory</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Restore to a NEW data directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Update etcd manifest to use the new data directory</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># Change TWO places:</span>
<span class="comment"># a) In container command args:</span>
<span class="comment">#    --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># b) In volumes/volumeMounts hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#    volumes:</span>
<span class="comment">#    - name: etcd-data</span>
<span class="comment">#      hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#        path: /var/lib/etcd-restored    ← Update this</span>
<span class="comment">#        type: DirectoryOrCreate</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Wait for the API server to recover (30-60 seconds)</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects manifest change → restarts etcd → API server reconnects</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl get pods -A
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The restore creates a <strong>brand new</strong> data directory — you must NOT restore to the original <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> because the member IDs won't match. After restoring, you update the etcd manifest in two places: (1) the <code>--data-dir</code> flag in the container command, and (2) the <code>hostPath</code> volume mount. kubelet detects the manifest change and restarts etcd with the restored data. The API server, scheduler, and controller-manager automatically reconnect. Give it 30-60 seconds — don't panic if kubectl is temporarily unavailable.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between etcd's <code>--cert-file</code>/<code>--key-file</code> and <code>--peer-cert-file</code>/<code>--peer-key-file</code>?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Flag</th>
                                            <th>Purpose</th>
                                            <th>Who Connects</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--cert-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Server cert for client connections</td>
                                            <td>API server → etcd</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--key-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Server key for client connections</td>
                                            <td>API server → etcd</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--peer-cert-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Cert for etcd-to-etcd communication</td>
                                            <td>etcd ↔ etcd (HA cluster)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>--peer-key-file</code></td>
                                            <td>Key for etcd-to-etcd communication</td>
                                            <td>etcd ↔ etcd (HA cluster)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">in a single-node etcd</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># In a single-node etcd, peer certs are still configured</span>
<span class="comment"># but only matter in multi-node (HA) etcd clusters</span>

<span class="comment"># Find all cert paths:</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -E "cert|key|ca"</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd uses two sets of TLS certificates: <strong>client certs</strong> (for API server connections) and <strong>peer certs</strong> (for etcd member replication). In a single control-plane cluster, peer certs exist but aren't actively used for multi-node communication. In HA clusters with 3 or 5 etcd members, peer certs are critical — they secure the Raft consensus protocol traffic between members. On the CKA, for backup/restore you use the <strong>client</strong> certs (<code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code> with etcdctl), not the peer certs.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you check the health of the etcd cluster? What commands show the member list and cluster status?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check etcd health</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check etcd health</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># 127.0.0.1:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal</span>

<span class="comment"># Check etcd member list (HA clusters)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key --write-table
<span class="comment"># Shows all etcd members, their status, and client URLs</span>

<span class="comment"># Check etcd endpoint status (detailed)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key --write-table
<span class="comment"># Shows DB size, leader, raft index, raft term</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick check via kubectl (simpler)</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pods | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane   1/1   Running   0   5d</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd healthchecks are essential before and after backup/restore operations. <code>endpoint health</code> verifies the etcd instance can accept writes. <code>member list</code> shows all members in the cluster (single or HA). <code>endpoint status</code> gives detailed info including DB size and who's the leader. All etcdctl commands need the three cert flags — without them, the connection is refused. On the CKA, always verify etcd health after restore: if the endpoint is unhealthy, check that the <code>--data-dir</code> path matches the restored directory.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the default endpoint for etcd? How do you find the actual endpoint if it's been changed? What's the difference between stacked and external etcd?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">default endpoint</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Default endpoint</span>
https://127.0.0.1:2379

<span class="comment"># Find actual endpoint from etcd manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep listen-client
<span class="comment"># --listen-client-urls=https://127.0.0.1:2379,https://10.0.0.10:2379</span>

<span class="comment"># Or from API server (how API server connects to etcd)</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># --etcd-servers=https://127.0.0.1:2379</span>

<span class="comment"># Stacked etcd: runs ON the control plane node (most common)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Static pod in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># - Data stored at /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># - Managed by kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># External etcd: runs on SEPARATE dedicated nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not a static pod — standalone systemd service</span>
<span class="comment"># - API server connects via --etcd-servers=https://&lt;external-ip&gt;:2379</span>
<span class="comment"># - Common in production HA setups</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>In CKA exam environments, etcd is almost always stacked (running on the control plane). But the exam may present external etcd scenarios for backup/restore questions. The key difference: for stacked etcd, use <code>127.0.0.1:2379</code> and certs from <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>. For external etcd, use the external node's IP and potentially different cert paths. Always check the API server manifest's <code>--etcd-servers</code> flag to confirm the endpoint, and the etcd manifest's <code>--listen-client-urls</code> for what etcd is actually listening on.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You restored etcd from a backup, but some pods that were created AFTER the backup was taken are now missing. Is this expected? What happened?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">yes — this is expected behavior</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># YES — this is expected behavior</span>

<span class="comment"># Timeline:</span>
<span class="comment"># T=0:   etcd backup taken (snapshot)</span>
<span class="comment"># T=1:   New deployment "webapp" created (pod exists)</span>
<span class="comment"># T=2:   New configmap "app-config" created</span>
<span class="comment"># T=3:   etcd restore from T=0 snapshot</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># After restore: cluster state = T=0</span>
<span class="comment"># "webapp" deployment is GONE — it didn't exist at T=0</span>
<span class="comment"># "app-config" is GONE — same reason</span>

<span class="comment"># What IS preserved:</span>
<span class="comment"># - All objects that existed at backup time</span>
<span class="comment"># - All RBAC rules from backup time</span>
<span class="comment"># - All namespaces from backup time</span>

<span class="comment"># What IS NOT preserved:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Any objects created AFTER the backup</span>
<span class="comment"># - Any modifications made AFTER the backup</span>
<span class="comment"># - Running pod state (pods restart from desired state)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd restore is a <strong>point-in-time recovery</strong> — it rolls the entire cluster state back to when the backup was taken. Everything after that point is lost. This is why regular backups are critical and why the CKA tests this scenario. In production, etcd snapshots are taken every few hours via CronJobs. The lesson: backups are only as good as their frequency. After a restore, you'll need to recreate any resources that were added between the backup and the restore. Running containers are killed and re-created based on the restored desired state.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">During an etcd restore, you get the error: <em>"Error: data-dir '/var/lib/etcd-restored' already exists"</em>. What should you do?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">the restore target directory must be empty or…</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The restore target directory must be EMPTY or non-existent</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 1: Use a different directory name</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored-v2

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Remove the existing directory first</span>
sudo rm -rf /var/lib/etcd-restored
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># IMPORTANT: Update etcd manifest to match the new path</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># Update --data-dir AND hostPath volume path</span>

<span class="comment"># Wait for etcd to restart with new data</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcdctl refuses to restore to an existing directory to prevent accidental data loss. Use a unique directory name each time. On the CKA, if you've already done one restore attempt and need to retry, either remove the directory or use a different name. Remember to update BOTH locations in the etcd manifest: the <code>--data-dir</code> command argument AND the <code>hostPath.path</code> in the volumes section. If they don't match, etcd starts with an empty data directory and the cluster appears empty.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you list all keys stored in etcd? How would you find a specific pod's data in etcd?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">list all keys in etcd (top-level prefixes)</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all keys in etcd (top-level prefixes)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get / --prefix --keys-only | head -20 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Kubernetes stores everything under /registry/</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/pods/default/nginx</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/deployments/kube-system/coredns</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/services/specs/default/kubernetes</span>
<span class="comment"># /registry/secrets/default/my-secret</span>

<span class="comment"># Find a specific pod</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/pods/default/nginx \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Count total keys</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get / --prefix --keys-only \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key | wc -l</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd stores all Kubernetes objects under <code>/registry/&lt;resource-type&gt;/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code>. The data is stored as protocol buffer (protobuf) format, not plain JSON — so the values look like binary. This is rarely needed on the CKA, but understanding the key structure helps debug issues like: "Why does my deleted namespace still show up?" (check if keys still exist). Secrets are stored base64-encoded by default in etcd. If <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> is set up, they're also encrypted at rest.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What happens to the Kubernetes cluster if etcd goes down? Which operations continue to work and which don't?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>✅ Still Works</th>
                                            <th>❌ Doesn't Work</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Running pods continue running</td>
                                            <td>Can't create new pods/resources</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Existing Services route traffic</td>
                                            <td>Can't update or delete anything</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Container processes keep running</td>
                                            <td>No scheduling of new pods</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Node-local operations (kubelet)</td>
                                            <td>No self-healing (crashed pods stay down)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>etcd is the cluster's <strong>source of truth</strong> but not the runtime engine. Running workloads continue because kubelet manages containers locally. However, the API server can't write to etcd, so all mutating operations fail. Controllers can't reconcile (no self-healing), the scheduler can't schedule (no new pods), and kubectl commands that modify state fail. Read operations may work briefly from API server cache, then fail. This is why etcd availability is critical in production — and why HA clusters run 3 or 5 etcd members with Raft consensus.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Write a complete etcd backup and restore script that could be used as a CronJob for automated backups. Include verification steps.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">bash</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
<span class="comment"># etcd-backup.sh — Automated etcd backup script</span>

<span class="comment"># Variables</span>
BACKUP_DIR="/opt/etcd-backups"
TIMESTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
BACKUP_FILE="${BACKUP_DIR}/etcd-snapshot-${TIMESTAMP}.db"
CACERT="/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt"
CERT="/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt"
KEY="/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key"

<span class="comment"># Create backup directory</span>
mkdir -p ${BACKUP_DIR}

<span class="comment"># Take snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save ${BACKUP_FILE} \
  --cacert=${CACERT} --cert=${CERT} --key=${KEY}

<span class="comment"># Verify snapshot</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status ${BACKUP_FILE} \
  --write-table

<span class="comment"># Keep only last 7 backups</span>
ls -tp ${BACKUP_DIR}/*.db | tail -n +8 | xargs -r rm --

echo "Backup completed: ${BACKUP_FILE}"

<span class="comment"># To restore (manual process):</span>
<span class="comment"># ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore ${BACKUP_FILE} \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># Then update /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Automated backups are essential in production. This script takes a timestamped snapshot, verifies it, and keeps only the last 7 backups (rotation). On the CKA, you won't need to write a full script, but understanding the flow is important. The exam tests individual commands — primarily backup (save) and restore. Remember: backup is non-disruptive (online snapshot), but restore requires etcd to restart, which briefly disrupts the cluster. Always take a fresh backup BEFORE performing cluster upgrades or major changes.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 11 Visual Summary — the cluster's memory, saved &amp; restorable</div>
<pre>
   🗄️ etcd = the cluster's memory   (/registry/* — every object; Secrets unencrypted by default)
        │  Raft: leader + followers · quorum N/2+1 · odd members (3→tolerate 1, 5→tolerate 2)
        ▼
   BACKUP   ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db  --endpoints --cacert --cert --key
        │   verify → snapshot status --write-table
        │
   ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─ 💥 DISASTER ─ ─ ─ ─
        ▼
   RESTORE  etcdctl snapshot restore … --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-RESTORED   (a NEW dir!)
        │   edit etcd.yaml:  --data-dir  AND  hostPath volume  → BOTH
        ▼
   🤖 kubelet restarts etcd → ✅ cluster back at the snapshot point
   SECURE   TLS in transit (default) · EncryptionConfiguration + --encryption-provider-config (at rest)   ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> etcd is the only place cluster state lives, kept consistent by Raft (writes commit on a majority; members are odd so quorum is meaningful). A backup is one <code>snapshot save</code> with the endpoint and three TLS certs — always verified with <code>snapshot status</code>. A restore reads that snapshot into a <em>new</em> data-dir, and you repoint the etcd static pod by editing <code>etcd.yaml</code> in two places.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> memorise the four backup inputs and the two restore edits, and disaster recovery is a 2-minute, free-marks task. Then secure what you backed up — TLS protects the wire, and <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> protects Secrets at rest so a leaked snapshot isn't a breach.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 11 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== CHAPTER 12 ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch12">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
            <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 12 · Managing the Control Plane</h2>
            <p class="chapter-lead">The control plane runs <em>as pods</em> — specifically <strong>static pods</strong> the kubelet manages from files on disk. That makes managing it a matter of reading logs and editing manifests, often with the apiserver (and <code>kubectl</code>) <strong>down</strong>. This chapter is where Cluster Architecture meets Troubleshooting: inspect health with and without kubectl, modify components safely, and recover from a bad edit.</p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 Arch 25% + Troubleshoot 30%</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎛️ Control Plane</span>
            </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The control plane <strong>as static pods</strong> — what's in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and who owns them</li>
                    <li>✅ Inspect control-plane health — with kubectl, and with <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl</code> when it's down</li>
                    <li>✅ Modify a component safely: back up → edit → wait → verify → recover</li>
                    <li>✅ Tune the scheduler &amp; controller-manager (profiles, PriorityClass, eviction timing)</li>
                    <li>✅ The three extension interfaces as a set: <strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">control-plane recovery <span class="cka-weight">CORE TASK</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 Debugging when <code>kubectl</code> itself is dead</h5>
                <p>The defining skill here is working <em>without</em> kubectl — because a control-plane problem often takes the apiserver offline. The exam loves "the control plane is broken, fix it."</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubectl works</strong> → <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> + component logs.</li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl is dead</strong> → on the node: <code>sudo crictl ps -a</code>, <code>sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code>, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Recovery is always:</strong> make the manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> valid — the kubelet does the rest.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <!-- ─────────── SECTION MAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">🧭 Chapter 12 Roadmap — read, fix &amp; tune the control plane</div>
<pre>
   12.1 static pods ──▶ 12.2 inspect health ──▶ 12.3 modify safely ──▶ 12.4 tune sched/ctrl-mgr ──▶ 12.5 CRI·CNI·CSI
   manifests dir          kubectl / crictl /        back up→edit→            profiles · PriorityClass    the 3 plug
   kubelet owns them      journalctl (when down)    verify→recover           eviction timing             points
</pre>
            </div>
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Takeaway</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-1"><strong>12.1</strong> Static pods</a></td><td>What IS the control plane, physically?</td><td>4 manifests in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>; the kubelet runs them.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-2"><strong>12.2</strong> Inspect health</a></td><td>How do I tell what's broken?</td><td>kubectl when up; <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl</code> when down.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-3"><strong>12.3</strong> Modify components</a></td><td>How do I change a flag without breaking it?</td><td>back up → edit → wait ~30-60s → verify → recover.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-4"><strong>12.4</strong> Sched/ctrl-mgr tuning</a></td><td>How do I influence placement &amp; timing?</td><td>scheduler profiles · PriorityClass · eviction/monitor timers.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><a href="#s12-5"><strong>12.5</strong> CRI · CNI · CSI</a></td><td>What makes Kubernetes pluggable?</td><td>three sockets: runtime · network · storage.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.1 ─────────── -->


            <section class="section-block" id="s12-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.1 The Control Plane as Static Pods</h3>
                <p>On a kubeadm cluster, the control plane isn't a set of system services — it's four <strong>static pods</strong>. The kubelet watches <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and keeps a pod running for each YAML there. No scheduler, no Deployment, no apiserver is involved in their lifecycle — which is exactly why they can bring the cluster up <em>before</em> the apiserver exists, and why you manage them by editing files.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/ — edit a file, the kubelet restarts the pod</div>
<pre>
   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
     ├─ etcd.yaml                     ┐
     ├─ kube-apiserver.yaml           ├─ 🤖 the kubelet WATCHES this directory
     ├─ kube-controller-manager.yaml  │     edit a file   → pod auto-restarts (~30-60s)
     └─ kube-scheduler.yaml           ┘     remove a file → pod is deleted
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   each running static pod is reflected into the API as a read-only "mirror pod"
   (that's why `kubectl get pods -n kube-system` shows kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt; etc.)
   ⚠️ a YAML typo here = the apiserver won't start = kubectl dies. Fix the file → kubelet recovers it.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Static pod vs mirror pod</h5><p>The kubelet runs the <strong>static pod</strong> directly from the file. It also creates a <strong>mirror pod</strong> in the apiserver so the pod is <em>visible</em> via <code>kubectl</code> — but that mirror is read-only: <code>kubectl delete pod kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt;</code> just makes the kubelet recreate it. To actually stop one, move its manifest out of the directory (a handy trick to force-restart a component: <code>mv … /tmp; sleep 5; mv … back</code>).</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">static pod vs regular pod — how each is managed</div>
<pre>
   REGULAR POD                                  STATIC POD
   ┌───────────────────────────┐               ┌───────────────────────────┐
   │ kubectl apply ─▶ apiserver │               │ YAML placed in            │
   │      ─▶ etcd               │               │ /etc/kubernetes/manifests │
   │           │                │               │        │                  │
   │           ▼                │               │        ▼                  │
   │ scheduler assigns a node   │               │ kubelet detects the file  │
   │           │                │               │ &amp; runs the pod directly    │
   │           ▼                │               │ on THIS node              │
   │ kubelet runs the pod       │               │                           │
   │ ✅ managed by the apiserver │               │ ✅ managed by the kubelet  │
   │ ✅ can run on any node      │               │ ❌ only the local node     │
   │ ✅ kubectl delete works     │               │ ❌ kubectl delete = no-op  │
   │ ✅ can be scaled            │               │ ❌ cannot be scaled        │
   └───────────────────────────┘               └───────────────────────────┘
   the apiserver creates a read-only "mirror pod" for each static pod so it shows in kubectl.
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active"><div class="step-number">1</div><h5>File placed</h5><p>YAML dropped in the manifests dir</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">2</div><h5>kubelet detects</h5><p>inotify + periodic resync</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">3</div><h5>Mirror created</h5><p>read-only copy POSTed to the API</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">4</div><h5>Auto-restart</h5><p>edit the file → pod recreated</p></div>
                    <div class="process-step-item"><div class="step-number">5</div><h5>File removed</h5><p>delete the file → pod deleted</p></div>
                </div>
                <h4>🔎 Telling a static pod from a regular one — the identification cheat sheet</h4>
                <p>On the exam you may need to prove a pod is static (or find which node owns it). Three tells distinguish them:</p>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Static pod</th><th>Regular pod</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Name pattern</td><td><code>&lt;component&gt;-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> (e.g. <code>etcd-controlplane</code>)</td><td><code>&lt;deploy&gt;-&lt;hash&gt;-&lt;hash&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>ownerReferences.kind</code></td><td><strong>Node</strong> (the kubelet owns it)</td><td>ReplicaSet / DaemonSet / Job</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/config.source</code> annotation</td><td><code>file</code></td><td><code>api</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deleted with <code>kubectl delete</code>?</td><td>❌ recreated from the file</td><td>✅ gone (or recreated by its controller)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">identify a static pod</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].kind}'
<span class="comment"># Node   ← it's static (owned by the kubelet, not a controller)</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system -o yaml | grep 'config.source'
<span class="comment"># kubernetes.io/config.source: file</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath   <span class="comment"># where the kubelet looks</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <h4>💥 What breaks when each component fails</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Component down</th><th>What still works</th><th>What breaks</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td>running pods keep running; <strong>Services keep routing</strong> (kube-proxy's iptables/IPVS rules are cached)</td><td>ALL <code>kubectl</code>, all controllers, all scheduling — the cluster is "frozen" (no changes possible)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>running pods run &amp; Services route (cached rules)</td><td>apiserver returns errors / can't read-write state → effectively a dead control plane</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>existing pods run; kubectl works</td><td>NEW pods stay <code>Pending</code> (nothing assigns them a node)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td>existing pods run; kubectl works</td><td>no self-healing — a deleted pod isn't recreated, nodes aren't marked NotReady, etc.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet (a node)</strong></td><td>other nodes fine</td><td>that node goes <code>NotReady</code>; its pods aren't managed</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box tip"><h5>💡 This matrix IS the diagnosis</h5><p>"New pods stuck Pending but kubectl works" → scheduler. "Deleted pods don't come back" → controller-manager. "kubectl itself is dead" → apiserver or etcd. Reading the symptom backwards to the component is half of every control-plane troubleshooting task.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.2 Inspecting Control-Plane Health</h3>
                <p>How you inspect the control plane depends on whether the apiserver is up. When it is, kubectl shows everything; when it isn't, you drop to the node and talk to the container runtime and the kubelet directly.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">when kubectl WORKS</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get pods -n kube-system            <span class="comment"># every control-plane + addon pod</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -E 'apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller'
kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-control-plane   <span class="comment"># a component's logs</span>
kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'        <span class="comment"># apiserver self-check, per gate</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses             <span class="comment"># legacy/deprecated, but still appears on the exam</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">when kubectl is DEAD (on the node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo crictl ps -a | grep -E 'apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller'   <span class="comment"># container states (incl. exited)</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;            <span class="comment"># WHY a component is crash-looping</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager  <span class="comment"># the kubelet's view (static-pod errors)</span>
ls -l /etc/kubernetes/manifests/          <span class="comment"># are the manifests present &amp; intact?</span>
sudo crictl ps                            <span class="comment"># is the apiserver container even running?</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Health endpoint (on the node)</th><th>Secure port</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-apiserver</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:6443/readyz?verbose</code></td><td>6443</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:10259/healthz</code></td><td>10259</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kube-controller-manager</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:10257/healthz</code></td><td>10257</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td><code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> (TLS) — or <code>https://localhost:2379/health</code></td><td>2379 / 2380</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td><code>curl -k https://localhost:10250/healthz</code></td><td>10250</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 The health endpoints</h5><p>Each component serves liveness/readiness over HTTP: <code>/healthz</code> (overall), <code>/livez</code> (alive), <code>/readyz</code> (ready to serve) — add <code>?verbose</code> to see each individual check. The apiserver's <code>readyz</code> is what a load balancer probes before sending traffic to a control-plane node; a single failing gate there explains an "intermittent apiserver."</p></div>
                <div class="info-box warning"><h5>⚠️ <code>crictl</code> needs its endpoint</h5><p>If <code>crictl</code> warns about the runtime endpoint, set it: <code>export CONTAINER_RUNTIME_ENDPOINT=unix:///run/containerd/containerd.sock</code> (or put it in <code>/etc/crictl.yaml</code>). <code>crictl</code> is your apiserver-independent window into what containers are actually doing on the node.</p></div>
                <h4>🩺 The kubectl-less troubleshooting decision tree</h4>
                <p>When <code>kubectl</code> itself is dead, follow this top-down on the control-plane node — it walks you from "is the kubelet even up?" to the exact failing manifest:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">control plane down → find &amp; fix it without kubectl</div>
<pre>
   kubectl get pods -n kube-system
     │
     ├─ ✅ works ─────────▶ normal debugging: check pod status, logs, events
     │
     └─ ❌ "connection refused" / timeout  ─▶  SSH to the control-plane node, then:
           │
           ├─ 1. systemctl status kubelet
           │        active (running) → kubelet OK, go to step 2
           │        inactive/failed  → sudo systemctl start kubelet (then journalctl -u kubelet)
           │
           ├─ 2. sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube
           │        all Running   → likely a network/cert issue, check logs
           │        some Exited   → note the exit codes → step 3
           │        none at all   → manifests missing? → step 4
           │
           ├─ 3. sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;        → read the error → fix the manifest
           │
           ├─ 4. ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
           │        all 4 .yaml present → check their content (bad flag/typo)
           │        a file is missing!  → restore from /tmp backup (or kubeadm init phase …)
           │
           └─ 5. sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50   → the root cause, in plain text
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.3 Modifying Components Safely</h3>
                <p>Changing any control-plane component means editing its manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. The kubelet restarts the pod on save — so a good edit is invisible and a bad one takes the component down. The same five-step ritual makes it safe every time.</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml (excerpt)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: kube-apiserver
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - command:                              <span class="comment"># ← ALL the apiserver flags live here</span>
    - kube-apiserver
    - --advertise-address=192.168.56.10
    - --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC
    - --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction
    - --etcd-servers=https://127.0.0.1:2379
    - --etcd-cafile=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt
    - --service-cluster-ip-range=10.96.0.0/12
    - --tls-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
    - --tls-private-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key
    image: registry.k8s.io/kube-apiserver:v1.34.0
    volumeMounts:
    - mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki      <span class="comment"># ← cert files mounted into the pod</span>
      name: k8s-certs
      readOnly: true
  hostNetwork: true                       <span class="comment"># ← uses the node's network directly</span>
  volumes:
  - name: k8s-certs
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/pki           <span class="comment"># ← from the node's filesystem</span>
      type: DirectoryOrCreate</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Reading this manifest</h5><p>Two things to internalise: <strong>(1)</strong> every apiserver setting is just a line in the <code>command:</code> list — adding a flag means adding a list item. <strong>(2)</strong> the cert files it references are <code>hostPath</code> volumes from the node's <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki</code> — so a flag pointing at a cert that isn't mounted will crash the pod. <code>hostNetwork: true</code> is why the apiserver binds the node's real <code>:6443</code>.</p></div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">the safe edit ritual — back up → edit → wait → verify → recover</div>
<pre>
   ① BACK UP    sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml /tmp/
        ▼
   ② EDIT       add/change a --flag under command:  (sudo vim …)
        ▼
   ③ WAIT       ~30-60s — the kubelet kills the old pod &amp; starts a new one
        ▼
   ④ VERIFY     crictl ps | grep &lt;comp&gt;  (new container ID) · get pods -n kube-system
        ▼
   ⑤ RECOVER    broke it? sudo cp /tmp/&lt;file&gt; /etc/kubernetes/manifests/  → kubelet relaunches
</pre>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control plane · modify a component</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># ① back up</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml       <span class="comment"># ② edit a flag</span>
watch -n2 'sudo crictl ps | grep controller'    <span class="comment"># ③/④ wait for a new container ID</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-$(hostname) | tail   <span class="comment"># verify it's healthy</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-controller-manager.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/  <span class="comment"># ⑤ recover if needed</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box danger"><h5>🚨 A typo in <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> is special</h5><p>It takes <code>kubectl</code> down with it, so you can't verify or recover <em>through</em> kubectl — work on the node with <code>crictl logs</code> and restore the backup. This is the same recovery you drilled in Ch 10 §10.3; the apiserver is the one component where the backup isn't optional.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.4 Scheduler &amp; Controller-Manager Tuning</h3>
                <p>Two control-plane components have behaviour you'll occasionally tune: the <strong>scheduler</strong> (how pods are placed) and the <strong>controller-manager</strong> (how fast the cluster reacts to node failures). Both are configured the same way — flags in their static-pod manifest — and both elect a single active leader.</p>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight"><div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div><h5>kube-scheduler</h5><p>Assigns pods to nodes via <strong>filter → score → bind</strong>. Tune with <strong>scheduler profiles</strong> (<code>--config</code>, custom plugin weights) and influence priority with <strong>PriorityClass</strong> objects (higher priority can preempt lower).</p></div>
                    <div class="info-card"><div class="card-icon-lg">🔄</div><h5>kube-controller-manager</h5><p>Runs the reconciliation loops. Key timing flags: <code>--node-monitor-grace-period</code> (how long before a silent node is marked NotReady) and the eviction timing that follows.</p></div>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Flag (component)</th><th>Default</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>--node-monitor-grace-period</code> (c-m)</td><td>~50s</td><td>how long without a kubelet heartbeat before a node → <code>NotReady</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--node-monitor-period</code> (c-m)</td><td>5s</td><td>how often node status is checked</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>taint-based eviction (<code>tolerationSeconds</code>)</td><td>300s</td><td>how long a pod tolerates a <code>NotReady</code>/<code>unreachable</code> node before eviction</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>--leader-elect</code> (sched &amp; c-m)</td><td>true</td><td>only one replica is active at a time (HA via a Lease)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">a PriorityClass (so critical pods win)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: scheduling.k8s.io/v1
kind: PriorityClass
metadata:
  name: easypj-critical
value: 1000000             <span class="comment"># higher = scheduled first, can preempt lower-priority pods</span>
globalDefault: false
description: "for easypj's must-run web tier"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 When you'd touch these</h5><p>Lowering <code>--node-monitor-grace-period</code> makes the cluster react to dead nodes faster (quicker pod rescheduling) at the cost of being twitchier on flaky networks. <strong>PriorityClass</strong> + preemption guarantees critical workloads get a node even when the cluster is full. Custom <strong>scheduler profiles</strong> let one scheduler run multiple policies (Ch 14 covers placement in depth).</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── 12.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s12-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">12.5 The Extension Interfaces — CRI · CNI · CSI</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes ships almost no opinion about <em>how</em> to run containers, network pods, or attach storage. Instead it defines three <strong>pluggable interfaces</strong> and lets you choose the implementation. Knowing the trio as a set explains how the whole system fits together — and why "the runtime/network/storage" is always someone's plugin, not built-in.</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">three sockets that make Kubernetes pluggable</div>
<pre>
   🤖 kubelet ───CRI──▶ 🐳 container runtime     "run this container"     (containerd · CRI-O)
   📦 pod     ───CNI──▶ 🌐 network plugin        "give this pod an IP &amp; routes"  (Calico · Cilium · Flannel)
   💽 volume  ───CSI──▶ 🗄️ storage driver        "attach/mount this volume"   (EBS · Ceph · local-path)
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   each is a stable API contract → swap the implementation without changing Kubernetes itself.
</pre>
                </div>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>Stands for</th><th>Who calls it</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>CRI</strong></td><td>Container Runtime Interface</td><td>the kubelet, to run containers</td><td>containerd, CRI-O</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CNI</strong></td><td>Container Network Interface</td><td>the kubelet, on pod create, to wire networking</td><td>Calico, Cilium, Flannel (Ch 9 §9.4)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CSI</strong></td><td>Container Storage Interface</td><td>kubelet + controllers, to provision/attach/mount volumes</td><td>AWS EBS, Ceph, local-path (Ch 22)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
                <div class="info-box note"><h5>📖 Why three separate interfaces?</h5><p>Decoupling lets each layer evolve and compete independently — you can run containerd with Cilium and the EBS CSI driver, or any other mix, and Kubernetes neither knows nor cares. It's the same extensibility philosophy as API groups and CRDs: a stable contract at the boundary, swappable implementations behind it. CRI failures show as pods stuck creating; CNI failures as <code>NotReady</code>/no pod IPs; CSI failures as volumes stuck attaching.</p></div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth">
                <summary>Under the Hood — leader election &amp; how static pods appear in the API</summary>
                <div class="uth-body">
                    <p>Two mechanics explain how the control plane stays both <em>highly available</em> and <em>visible</em>: the scheduler and controller-manager use <strong>leader election</strong> so only one is ever active, and the kubelet creates a <strong>mirror pod</strong> so each static pod shows up in <code>kubectl</code>.</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   LEADER ELECTION (scheduler &amp; controller-manager, when you run more than one)
     every replica races to update a Lease object:
       kube-system/kube-scheduler   ·   kube-system/kube-controller-manager
     the one that holds the Lease is ACTIVE; others stand by, watching renewTime
     leader dies → its Lease expires (~15s) → a standby grabs it → becomes active
       (this is why HA control planes run 3 of each, but only ONE does work at a time)

   STATIC POD → MIRROR POD
     kubelet runs /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml directly (no scheduler)
     kubelet also POSTs a read-only "mirror pod" to the apiserver → kubectl can SEE it
     deleting the mirror pod doesn't stop it — the kubelet just recreates it from the file
</pre>

                    <h5>① The component interconnection map — who finds whom, and how</h5>
                    <p>The control plane isn't a monolith — it's 5+ independent processes that discover each other through <strong>kubeconfig files, localhost ports, and certs</strong>. Every arrow below is a connection that can break (wrong endpoint, expired cert, bad kubeconfig), so knowing the wiring <em>is</em> the troubleshooting map:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
                         CONTROL PLANE NODE  ·  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
   ┌──────────────┐         ┌───────────────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ 🗄️ etcd.yaml  │◀────────│ 📡 kube-apiserver.yaml     │────────▶│ 🤖 kubelet (NOT static)   │
   │ listen :2379  │  etcd   │   --etcd-servers=          │ kubelet │   --kubeconfig=           │
   │       :2380   │  client │     https://127.0.0.1:2379 │ client  │     /etc/.../kubelet.conf │
   └──────────────┘  certs  │   listen :6443 (TLS)       │ cert    └──────────────────────────┘
   ┌──────────────┐         │   --kubelet-client-cert=…  │         ┌──────────────────────────┐
   │ 📋 scheduler  │────────▶│                            │◀────────│ 🔄 controller-manager     │
   │ --kubeconfig= │ schedu- └───────────────────────────┘ ctrl-mgr│   --kubeconfig=           │
   │  scheduler.conf│ ler.conf      ▲                       .conf    │     controller-manager.conf│
   └──────────────┘               │ ServiceAccount token            └──────────────────────────┘
                          🔀 kube-proxy (DaemonSet, every node) ─────┘
</pre>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Connects to</th><th>Auth method</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td>etcd (127.0.0.1:2379)</td><td>etcd client certs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-apiserver</code></td><td>kubelet (on each node)</td><td><code>apiserver-kubelet-client</code> cert</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-controller-manager</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td><code>controller-manager.conf</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-scheduler</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td><code>scheduler.conf</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kubelet</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td><code>kubelet.conf</code> (bootstrap → rotated cert)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>kube-proxy</code></td><td>kube-apiserver</td><td>ServiceAccount token</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Read this as a fault tree:</strong> "controller-manager can't reach the API" → its <code>--kubeconfig</code> (<code>controller-manager.conf</code>) or that cert. "apiserver can't reach etcd" → its <code>--etcd-servers</code> + etcd client certs. Every control-plane failure is a broken <em>edge</em> in this graph — find the edge, fix the file/cert it points at.</div>

                    <h5>② Leader election — how HA control planes avoid split-brain</h5>
                    <p>Run more than one control plane and you have multiple schedulers and controller-managers — but only <strong>one of each may act</strong>, or two would fight (double-scheduling, double-scaling). They coordinate through a <strong>Lease</strong> object in etcd:</p>
                    <pre class="uth-diagram">
   kubectl -n kube-system get leases
   NAME                      HOLDER                       AGE
   kube-controller-manager   controlplane-1_abc123        90d
   kube-scheduler            controlplane-1_ghi789        90d

   THE MECHANISM (per component)
     1. each instance tries to write a Lease:
          holderIdentity: controlplane-1_abc123 · leaseDurationSeconds: 15 · renewTime: <ts>
     2. only the one that writes its identity WINS → becomes the LEADER (others stand by)
     3. the leader RENEWS the lease every ~10s (a heartbeat into etcd)
     4. leader fails to renew within 15s → lease expires → a standby grabs it → new leader
     5. old leader comes back, sees a DIFFERENT holderIdentity → steps down  (NO split-brain)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   leader-elected: kube-controller-manager · kube-scheduler   (etcd uses Raft instead; the
   apiserver is stateless — every replica is active behind the load balancer)
</pre>
                    <div class="uth-callout"><strong>🔑 Why this matters:</strong> on an HA cluster, "the scheduler isn't scheduling" might mean the <em>active</em> leader is wedged while standbys wait — <code>kubectl -n kube-system get lease kube-scheduler -o yaml</code> shows <code>holderIdentity</code> + <code>renewTime</code> (a stale renewTime = the holder is dead but hasn't released). And knowing static pods are file-driven is the whole recovery model: you never <code>kubectl delete</code> a control-plane pod to fix it — you fix its <em>file</em>.</div>
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Beyond the basics</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>lease timing</strong></td><td><code>--leader-elect-lease-duration</code>/<code>-renew-deadline</code>/<code>-retry-period</code> tune failover speed vs stability; defaults give ~15s failover.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>secure ports &amp; bind-address</strong></td><td>components serve metrics/health on <code>10257</code>/<code>10259</code> bound to <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default; <code>--bind-address</code> exposes them (rarely wanted).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>health gates</strong></td><td><code>/livez?verbose</code> and <code>/readyz?verbose</code> list every individual check — invaluable for "apiserver is up but not ready."</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>profiling/metrics</strong></td><td><code>--profiling</code> (off in hardened clusters) and the <code>/metrics</code> endpoint feed Prometheus (Part 13).</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubelet staticPodPath</strong></td><td>the watched directory is set by <code>staticPodPath</code> in <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code> — point it elsewhere and that's where static pods come from.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The control plane runs AS static pods</strong> — 4 manifests in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>; the kubelet owns their lifecycle and auto-restarts on a file change (~30-60s).</li>
                    <li><strong>Diagnose by symptom:</strong> Pending pods → scheduler; no self-healing → controller-manager; kubectl dead → apiserver/etcd.</li>
                    <li><strong>When kubectl is down,</strong> debug on the node with <code>crictl ps -a</code> + <code>crictl logs</code> + <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Modify safely:</strong> back up → edit → wait → verify → recover. A typo in <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code> takes kubectl with it.</li>
                    <li><strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong> are the three plug points: runtime · network · storage.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Traps</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Editing the wrong manifest</strong> or forgetting to back up first.</li>
                    <li><strong>Not waiting ~30-60s</strong> for the kubelet to restart the pod before declaring it broken.</li>
                    <li><strong>apiserver typo</strong> kills <code>kubectl</code> — recover on the node, not through kubectl.</li>
                    <li><strong>Deleting a control-plane pod with kubectl</strong> — the kubelet just recreates the mirror; move the file instead.</li>
                    <li><strong>Confusing the symptoms</strong> — Pending (scheduler) vs no-recreate (controller-manager).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>apiserver down?</strong> <code>sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver</code> → <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> → fix the file.</li>
                    <li><strong>Force-restart a component:</strong> <code>sudo mv &lt;manifest&gt; /tmp; sleep 5; sudo mv /tmp/&lt;manifest&gt; back</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl -n kube-system get lease</code> to see which scheduler/c-m is the active leader.</li>
                    <li>Always <code>cp … /tmp</code> before any control-plane manifest edit.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 12)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">control-plane management toolkit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/             <span class="comment"># the 4 control-plane static pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system ; kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-scheduler-$(hostname)
sudo crictl ps -a ; sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;     <span class="comment"># when kubectl is down</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/&lt;comp&gt;.yaml /tmp/   <span class="comment"># back up before editing</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get lease          <span class="comment"># who holds leader election</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill — add a flag to the controller-manager, verify, then revert</h5>
                <p>No peeking — practise the safe-edit ritual on a non-fatal component:</p>
                <ol class="drill-steps">
                    <li>Back up <code>kube-controller-manager.yaml</code> to <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
                    <li>Add <code>--node-monitor-grace-period=20s</code> and save.</li>
                    <li>Wait for the pod to restart; verify the flag is live and the component is healthy.</li>
                    <li>Restore the backup; confirm it's back to normal.</li>
                </ol>
                <details class="drill">
                    <summary>Show solution</summary>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal (control-plane node)</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml /tmp/
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml
<span class="comment"># add under command:  - --node-monitor-grace-period=20s</span>
watch -n2 'sudo crictl ps | grep controller'        <span class="comment"># new container ID appears</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-$(hostname) \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep node-monitor
<span class="comment"># revert</span>
sudo cp /tmp/kube-controller-manager.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </details>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A · CONCEPT CHECK ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-lab"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions lab">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 12 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Control-plane management recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List the control-plane static pods and the directory they live in.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><code>etcd.yaml</code>, <code>kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, <code>kube-controller-manager.yaml</code>, <code>kube-scheduler.yaml</code> — all in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>The kubelet watches that directory and keeps one pod running per file; <code>ls</code> it to confirm what's present.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The scheduler is down — what's the symptom on new pods, and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>New pods stay <code>Pending</code> with no node assigned (existing pods keep running, kubectl works). Fix: check <code>kube-scheduler.yaml</code> and the pod's logs (<code>crictl logs</code> if needed) — usually a bad flag or a manifest that was moved/corrupted.</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Only the scheduler assigns <code>spec.nodeName</code>; without it, nothing binds pods to nodes.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Add a <code>--feature-gates=...</code> flag to a component safely.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">safe edit</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml /tmp/    <span class="comment"># back up</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml        <span class="comment"># add - --feature-gates=...</span>
sudo crictl ps | grep scheduler                              <span class="comment"># wait for new ID</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Every component is configured the same way: edit its manifest's <code>command:</code> list, save, let the kubelet restart it — always after a backup.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">ALL control-plane components appear down — what's your debugging order?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p>Work bottom-up on the node: <strong>(1)</strong> <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> + <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> (is the kubelet even running?), <strong>(2)</strong> <code>ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (are the manifests there?), <strong>(3)</strong> <code>crictl ps -a</code> + <code>crictl logs</code> per container (why each is failing).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>If the kubelet is down, nothing static-pod runs — fix that first. Otherwise it's per-manifest errors the kubelet's journal and crictl reveal.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Change the kubelet's <code>staticPodPath</code> (where it looks for static pods).</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">node</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo vim /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml       <span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet</code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p><code>staticPodPath</code> is a kubelet config setting; it defines the watched directory. Change it and the kubelet sources static pods from the new path after a restart.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Scheduler vs controller-manager — one job each.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <p><strong>Scheduler:</strong> assigns unscheduled pods to nodes (filter → score → bind). <strong>Controller-manager:</strong> runs the reconciliation loops that keep actual state matching desired (recreate pods, mark nodes NotReady, etc.).</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Scheduler decides <em>where</em>; controller-manager makes <em>desired = actual</em> everywhere else.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Check a component's health endpoint.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">health</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl get --raw='/readyz?verbose'         <span class="comment"># apiserver, per-gate</span>
curl -k https://127.0.0.1:10259/healthz     <span class="comment"># scheduler (on the node)</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Components serve <code>/healthz</code>, <code>/livez</code>, <code>/readyz</code>; <code>?verbose</code> lists each check so you see exactly which gate is failing.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The controller-manager is crash-looping — where do you look?</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">diagnose</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-$(hostname)   <span class="comment"># if kubectl works</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep controller ; sudo crictl logs &lt;id&gt;       <span class="comment"># if not</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Usual causes: a bad flag you added, a missing cert/file the manifest references, or a wrong <code>--kubeconfig</code> path. The logs name it.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a static pod on a worker node, then remove it.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper"><div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">on the worker</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">sudo kubectl run dbg --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml \
  | sudo tee /etc/kubernetes/manifests/dbg.yaml    <span class="comment"># drop a pod manifest in the dir</span>
<span class="comment"># it appears as dbg-&lt;nodename&gt; (a mirror pod). To remove:</span>
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/manifests/dbg.yaml         <span class="comment"># kubelet deletes the pod</span></code></pre></div>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Any manifest in the kubelet's <code>staticPodPath</code> becomes a static pod — not just control-plane ones. Removing the file is how you delete it (kubectl can't).</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
                <div class="exam-question-item"><span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Match CRI / CNI / CSI to runtime / network / storage.</div>
                    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                        <table><thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>Layer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>CRI</strong></td><td>container <strong>runtime</strong> (containerd, CRI-O)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CNI</strong></td><td>pod <strong>network</strong> (Calico, Cilium, Flannel)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>CSI</strong></td><td><strong>storage</strong> drivers (EBS, Ceph, local-path)</td></tr></tbody></table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation"><div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div><p>Three pluggable contracts: the kubelet uses CRI to run containers and CNI to network pods; CSI handles volume attach/mount.</p></div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch12-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 12 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Exam-style control-plane troubleshooting with the real commands.</p>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What are static pods? Where are their manifests stored, and how does the kubelet know where to look?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">static pods are managed directly by kubelet</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Static pods are managed directly by kubelet — NOT by the API server</span>
<span class="comment"># They exist only on the node where their manifest file lives</span>

<span class="comment"># Default manifest directory:</span>
/etc/kubernetes/manifests/

<span class="comment"># Control plane static pods:</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Finding the static pod path:</span>
<span class="comment"># Option 1: Check kubelet config</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Check kubelet process args</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep static
<span class="comment"># or: systemctl cat kubelet | grep config</span>

<span class="comment"># Create a static pod:</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF > /etc/kubernetes/manifests/my-static.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-static
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: nginx
EOF
<span class="comment"># kubelet automatically creates the pod</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods are pods managed by the kubelet daemon on a specific node, without the API server observing them. The kubelet watches the <code>staticPodPath</code> directory and creates/deletes pods when files appear/disappear. On the CKA, you may need to: (1) create a static pod by placing a YAML in the manifests directory, (2) find the manifest path if it's been changed from default, or (3) identify which pods are static pods. Static pods have the node name appended: <code>my-static-controlplane</code>. They can't be deleted with <code>kubectl delete</code> — you must remove the manifest file.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">Create a static pod named <code>test-web</code> running <code>nginx:1.25</code> on the control plane node. How do you verify it's running and how do you delete it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">create the manifest file</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the manifest file</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF > /etc/kubernetes/manifests/test-web.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-web
  labels:
    app: test-web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.25
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify (kubelet creates it within seconds)</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep test-web
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE   NAME                      READY   STATUS    RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># default     test-web-controlplane     1/1     Running   0</span>

<span class="comment"># Note the "-controlplane" suffix — that's the node name</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Delete the static pod</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ This does NOT work permanently:</span>
kubectl delete pod test-web-controlplane
<span class="comment"># kubelet will immediately recreate it!</span>

<span class="comment"># ✅ This works:</span>
rm /etc/kubernetes/manifests/test-web.yaml
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects file removal and deletes the pod</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods cannot be controlled through the API server — <code>kubectl delete</code> removes the mirror pod, but kubelet immediately recreates it from the manifest file. To truly delete a static pod, you must remove or move the YAML file from the <code>staticPodPath</code>. On the CKA, if asked to "create a static pod on node01," you must SSH to node01 first, find its <code>staticPodPath</code> (may differ from default), and place the manifest there. The pod name in <code>kubectl get pods</code> always has <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code> appended.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">The kube-scheduler is not working — new pods are stuck in <code>Pending</code> with "no nodes are available" even though nodes are <code>Ready</code>. How do you investigate?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check if scheduler pod is running</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check if scheduler pod is running</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pods | grep scheduler
<span class="comment"># If not listed or CrashLoopBackOff → scheduler is broke</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check scheduler logs</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-scheduler-controlplane

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check scheduler manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for: wrong image, typo in flags, wrong cert path</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Common scheduler issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># a) Manifest file deleted or corrupted</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml

<span class="comment"># b) Scheduler can't reach API server</span>
<span class="comment"># Check --kubeconfig flag points to valid config</span>

<span class="comment"># c) Wrong port or address</span>
<span class="comment"># Default: --bind-address=0.0.0.0 --secure-port=10259</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: If scheduler manifest is missing, recreate:</span>
<span class="comment"># Check another working cluster or use kubeadm defaults</span>
<span class="comment"># Or restore from etcd backup</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The scheduler's job is to assign pods to nodes. If it's down, pods get created (they exist in etcd) but stay <code>Pending</code> because no node is assigned. The CKA frequently tests this: they'll break the scheduler manifest and ask you to fix it. Diagnosis: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> to see if the scheduler pod exists. If it does exist but is crashing, check its logs. If it doesn't exist, check the manifest directory. Common gotcha: the scheduler manifest might be renamed (e.g., <code>kube-scheduler.yaml.bak</code>) — kubelet ignores non-.yaml files.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you identify which pods in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace are static pods vs regular pods?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">static pods have the node name as suffix</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: Static pods have the node name as suffix</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane                  ← static (has node name)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-controlplane        ← static</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-controlplane        ← static</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-controlplane ← static</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abc12           ← regular (has ReplicaSet hash)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-4x8n2                   ← regular (part of DaemonSet)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Check ownerReferences</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].kind}'
<span class="comment"># Node   ← Static pods are owned by the Node object</span>

kubectl get pod coredns-5d78c9869d-abc12 -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].kind}'
<span class="comment"># ReplicaSet   ← Regular pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check annotations</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system \
  -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations.kubernetes\.io/config\.source}'
<span class="comment"># file   ← Created from a file (static pod)</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Static pods have three distinguishing features: (1) their name ends with <code>-&lt;nodename&gt;</code>, (2) their <code>ownerReferences</code> shows kind <code>Node</code> instead of ReplicaSet/DaemonSet, (3) they have the annotation <code>kubernetes.io/config.source: file</code>. On the CKA, the quickest visual check is the name suffix — all four control plane components (apiserver, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager) are static pods with the node name appended. CoreDNS and kube-proxy are regular pods managed by Deployments/DaemonSets.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q15</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You need to add a custom flag <code>--profiling=false</code> to the kube-controller-manager. Walk through the complete process.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">find the manifest</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find the manifest</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Add the flag to the command section</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - command:
    - kube-controller-manager
    - --bind-address=127.0.0.1
    - --cluster-signing-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
    - --profiling=false              # ← Add this line
    <span class="comment"># ... existing flags ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Save and exit (:wq)</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects the change and restarts the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify the pod restarted</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane
<span class="comment"># RESTARTS count should increment</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify the flag is applied</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep profiling
<span class="comment"># --profiling=false</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>Modifying control plane components on the CKA always follows this pattern: edit the static pod manifest → save → wait for restart → verify. The YAML structure has the component flags as a list under <code>spec.containers[0].command</code>. Common mistakes: (1) adding the flag at the wrong indentation level, (2) using <code>=</code> vs space syntax incorrectly (use <code>--flag=value</code>), (3) forgetting that changes take 30-60 seconds to apply. If the pod doesn't restart, check <code>crictl ps -a</code> — a YAML error will prevent the pod from starting.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q16</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">All four control plane components suddenly stop working. You can SSH to the control plane node but <code>kubectl</code> returns "connection refused." What is your debugging checklist?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">without kubectl, use system-level tools</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Without kubectl, use system-level tools:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Check kubelet (manages all static pods)</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
sudo journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 30

<span class="comment"># 2. Check container runtime</span>
sudo systemctl status containerd
sudo crictl ps -a | grep kube

<span class="comment"># 3. Check if manifests exist</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># Should have: etcd.yaml, kube-apiserver.yaml,</span>
<span class="comment">#   kube-controller-manager.yaml, kube-scheduler.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check individual containers</span>
sudo crictl ps -a
<span class="comment"># Look for Exited containers and their exit codes</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Read container logs</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;apiserver-container-id&gt;
sudo crictl logs &lt;etcd-container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># 6. Check system resources</span>
df -h           <span class="comment"># Disk full?</span>
free -h         <span class="comment"># Memory exhaustion?</span>
top             <span class="comment"># CPU overload?</span>

<span class="comment"># 7. Common root causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Disk full: /var/lib/etcd grows indefinitely</span>
<span class="comment"># - Kubelet stopped: systemctl start kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># - Manifests deleted: restore from backup</span>
<span class="comment"># - Cert expired: check /etc/kubernetes/pki/</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>When all control plane components are down, the problem is usually kubelet, containerd, or a system-level issue (disk, memory). Start with kubelet — it manages all static pods. If kubelet is running but containers aren't, check <code>crictl ps -a</code> for container status and logs. If kubelet is down, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> shows why. Disk full (<code>/var/lib/etcd</code> growth) is a common cause in long-running clusters. On the CKA, this is the ultimate troubleshooting scenario — practice the kubectl-less debugging flow until it's second nature.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q17</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the kubelet's <code>staticPodPath</code>? How do you find it if it's been changed from the default, and how do you change it?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">default staticpodpath</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Default staticPodPath</span>
/etc/kubernetes/manifests

<span class="comment"># Method 1: Check kubelet config file</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Check kubelet process arguments</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -oP 'config=\S+'
<span class="comment"># Shows the kubelet config file path</span>
<span class="comment"># Then read that config file</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check systemd unit file</span>
systemctl cat kubelet
<span class="comment"># Shows ExecStart command and --config flag</span>

<span class="comment"># To change the static pod path:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Edit /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#    staticPodPath: /opt/my-static-pods</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># 3. Place manifests in the new path</span>
sudo mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml /opt/my-static-pods/</code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The CKA sometimes changes the <code>staticPodPath</code> to test if you know how to find it. Don't assume <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — always verify. The path is in the kubelet config file, typically at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>. The kubelet config location itself is passed via <code>--config</code> flag in the kubelet systemd unit. If asked to "create a static pod on node01," first SSH to node01, find its kubelet config, read the <code>staticPodPath</code>, then place your manifest there.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q18</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between the kube-controller-manager and the kube-scheduler? What happens if each one fails individually?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <table>
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Aspect</th>
                                            <th>kube-scheduler</th>
                                            <th>kube-controller-manager</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Job</strong></td>
                                            <td>Assign pods to nodes</td>
                                            <td>Run reconciliation loops</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>If down</strong></td>
                                            <td>New pods stay Pending</td>
                                            <td>No self-healing, no scaling</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Running pods</strong></td>
                                            <td>Unaffected</td>
                                            <td>Unaffected (until they crash)</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><strong>Controllers</strong></td>
                                            <td>N/A</td>
                                            <td>ReplicaSet, Namespace, Node, Job, SA, Endpoint, etc.</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>The scheduler only handles <strong>initial pod placement</strong> — after a pod is assigned to a node, the scheduler's job is done. The controller-manager runs dozens of control loops: the ReplicaSet controller ensures desired replica count, the Node controller marks unreachable nodes, the Job controller manages batch jobs, etc. If the scheduler fails, existing pods are fine but new pods wait forever. If the controller-manager fails, the cluster can't self-heal — crashed pods aren't replaced, scaling doesn't work, and terminated namespaces aren't cleaned up.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q19</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">How do you check the health of all control plane components? Show both <code>kubectl</code> and non-kubectl methods.</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">kubectl — check pods in kube-system</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl — check pods in kube-system</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># All should show Running, READY 1/1</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: componentstatuses (deprecated but quick)</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses  <span class="comment"># or: kubectl get cs</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                 STATUS    MESSAGE   ERROR</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler            Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager   Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-0               Healthy   ok</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Health check endpoints</span>
curl -k https://localhost:6443/healthz   <span class="comment"># API server</span>
curl -k https://localhost:10259/healthz  <span class="comment"># Scheduler</span>
curl -k https://localhost:10257/healthz  <span class="comment"># Controller Manager</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Without kubectl (when API server is down)</span>
crictl ps | grep kube
<span class="comment"># Shows running control plane containers</span>

systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># kubelet manages all static pods</span>

journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -n 20
<span class="comment"># kubelet logs show component status</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>On the CKA, <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> is the fastest health check. Each control plane component exposes a <code>/healthz</code> endpoint for liveness checks. <code>kubectl get cs</code> is deprecated since v1.19 but still works and gives a quick overview. Know the port numbers: API server (6443), scheduler (10259), controller-manager (10257), etcd (2379). When kubectl is unavailable, fall back to <code>crictl ps</code> and <code>journalctl</code>. A healthy cluster has all four static pods running with 0 recent restarts.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>

                    <div class="exam-question-item">
                        <span class="eq-number">Q20</span>
                        <div class="eq-question">You notice that the kube-controller-manager keeps restarting (high RESTARTS count). How do you investigate the cause?</div>
                        <details>
                            <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                                <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">check current status and restart count</span></div>
                                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check current status and restart count</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane
<span class="comment"># READY   STATUS             RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># 0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   15</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check events</span>
kubectl -n kube-system describe pod kube-controller-manager-controlplane
<span class="comment"># Look at Events section for error messages</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check logs (current attempt)</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-controlplane

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check logs from previous crash</span>
kubectl -n kube-system logs kube-controller-manager-controlplane --previous

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># a) Wrong certificate path in manifest</span>
<span class="comment">#    Error: "unable to load client certificate"</span>
<span class="comment">#    Fix: check --kubeconfig and cert file paths</span>

<span class="comment"># b) Can't reach API server</span>
<span class="comment">#    Error: "dial tcp 127.0.0.1:6443: connection refused"</span>
<span class="comment">#    Fix: check if API server is running first</span>

<span class="comment"># c) Invalid flag in manifest</span>
<span class="comment">#    Error: "unknown flag: --typo-flag"</span>
<span class="comment">#    Fix: remove or correct the flag</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Fix the manifest</span>
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml
<span class="comment"># Wait 30-60s for recovery</span></code></pre>
                                </div>
                            <div class="eq-explanation">
                                <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                                <p>CrashLoopBackOff on a control plane component means it starts, fails, and kubelet keeps restarting it. The <code>--previous</code> flag shows logs from the last crashed instance — crucial because the current instance might have started too recently to show useful logs. On the CKA, control plane troubleshooting follows a consistent pattern: <code>get pods</code> → <code>describe pod</code> → <code>logs --previous</code> → identify error → fix manifest → wait for recovery. The most common CKA trick: they modify a manifest with a typo or wrong path and ask you to fix it.</p>
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Chapter 12 Visual Summary — the control plane as static pods + the 3 plug points</div>
<pre>
   /etc/kubernetes/manifests/   ── 🤖 kubelet watches ──▶  edit a file → pod auto-restarts (~30-60s)
     etcd.yaml · kube-apiserver.yaml · kube-controller-manager.yaml · kube-scheduler.yaml
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   DIAGNOSE by symptom:   Pending pods → scheduler   ·   no self-healing → controller-manager
                          kubectl dead → apiserver/etcd  ·  node NotReady → kubelet
   WHEN kubectl IS DOWN:  sudo crictl ps -a · crictl logs &lt;id&gt; · journalctl -u kubelet  →  fix the FILE
   PLUG POINTS:   kubelet ─CRI─▶ runtime   ·   pod ─CNI─▶ network   ·   volume ─CSI─▶ storage          ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> the kubeadm control plane is four static pods the kubelet runs from files — so you manage it by editing those files, and a good edit just restarts the pod. You diagnose by mapping the symptom to the component (Pending → scheduler, no healing → controller-manager, dead kubectl → apiserver/etcd), and when kubectl is gone you drop to <code>crictl</code> + <code>journalctl</code> on the node. Recovery is always "make the manifest valid."</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> back up before every control-plane edit, wait for the restart, and read the runtime/kubelet directly when the apiserver is down. And remember the three interfaces — <strong>CRI · CNI · CSI</strong> — are what make every layer of Kubernetes swappable.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ::CH12-INSERT:: -->
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END CHAPTER 12 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 3 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part3-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 3 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Cluster Installation &amp; Configuration — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You arrived able to <em>drive</em> a cluster; you leave able to <strong>build, configure, protect, and repair</strong> one. From bare Linux nodes you can stand up a multi-node cluster with kubeadm, tune the control plane by editing static-pod manifests, snapshot &amp; restore etcd, and recover when the apiserver itself is down — the core of the CKA's 25% Cluster Architecture domain.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 Part 3 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubeadm (Ch 9):</strong> prep every node (swap off · modules · sysctl · containerd SystemdCgroup · toolchain+hold) → <code>kubeadm init</code> the CP → install a CNI (nodes stay <code>NotReady</code> until then) → <code>kubeadm join</code> workers. Save/regenerate the join command.</li>
                    <li><strong>Config (Ch 10):</strong> configure the control plane by editing static-pod manifests in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> (kubelet auto-restarts); kubelet via <code>config.yaml</code> + systemd; admission runs <em>mutating → validating</em>; API = core <code>/api/v1</code> vs named <code>/apis/grp/ver</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>etcd (Ch 11):</strong> it holds ALL state — <code>snapshot save</code> (endpoint + 3 TLS certs) + <code>restore</code> (NEW data-dir, repoint <code>etcd.yaml</code> twice) is the #1 admin skill; quorum = N/2+1, odd members; encrypt Secrets at rest.</li>
                    <li><strong>Control plane (Ch 12):</strong> it runs AS static pods — diagnose by symptom, debug with <code>crictl</code>+<code>journalctl</code> when kubectl is down, modify safely (back up→edit→verify→recover).</li>
                    <li><strong>Three plug points:</strong> <strong>CRI</strong> (runtime) · <strong>CNI</strong> (network) · <strong>CSI</strong> (storage).</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> 25% lives here; etcd &amp; kubeadm tasks are near-certain free marks once drilled.</li>
                    <li>🔑 <code>kubeadm init/join/token · etcdctl snapshot save/restore · crictl ps/logs · journalctl -u kubelet</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 3 Visual Summary — bare nodes → a configured, backed-up, self-managed cluster</div>
<pre>
   🖥️ bare Linux nodes
        │  Ch 9: prep all → kubeadm init → CNI → kubeadm join
        ▼
   ☸️ multi-node cluster (control plane = static pods in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/)
        │  Ch 10: edit apiserver/kubelet config · admission (mutating▶validating) · API groups
        │  Ch 12: diagnose by symptom · crictl+journalctl when kubectl down · CRI·CNI·CSI
        ▼
   🗄️ Ch 11: etcd snapshot save (+TLS) ──► 💥 disaster ──► snapshot restore (new data-dir) ──► ✅ recovered
   ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   you now OWN the cluster: build it, bend it to requirements, back it up, and bring it back. ☸️
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 3 turned you from operator into administrator. You build a cluster from nothing with kubeadm (prep → init → CNI → join), configure its control plane by editing the static-pod manifests the kubelet watches, and protect its memory with etcd snapshot/restore. When things break — even when <code>kubectl</code> itself is gone — you diagnose by symptom and fix the file on the node.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> the control plane is files + a watching kubelet + etcd's memory. Master the kubeadm build, the back-up-before-you-edit habit, and the etcd backup/restore dance, and you own the 25% Cluster Architecture domain — the densest scoring ground on the exam. Next: Part 4 makes the workloads on top robust and self-healing.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>
        <!-- ==================== END PART 3 ==================== -->

        <!-- ==================== PART 4 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part4">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 4 · Workloads &amp; Scheduling</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Run Apps the Right Way</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 13–16 · CKA 15% · <em>"Make it robust, place it well, budget it, specialize it"</em></p>
            <div class="chapter-meta">
                <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">📖 4 Chapters</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~6 hours</span>
                <span class="meta-tag">🎯 CKA Workloads &amp; Scheduling (15%)</span>
            </div>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A bare Pod is fragile: kill its node and it’s gone, bump its image and you hand-edit it, flood it with traffic and it has no way to grow. Part 4 is about the controllers and policies that make workloads <strong>robust and self-healing</strong> — Deployments &amp; rolling updates, autoscaling under load, scheduling so the right pod lands on the right node, resource governance so no pod starves another, and the specialized workload types (Job · CronJob · DaemonSet · StatefulSet). This is the bridge from “I can launch a container” to “I operate a fleet.”</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> turn easypj into a 3-replica, self-healing Deployment with zero-downtime rollouts and autoscaling; pin and spread pods with affinity, taints &amp; topology spread; drain a node safely; set requests/limits and namespace quotas; and pick the right controller for every job — web, database, batch, and per-node agent.</div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — the Part 4 pipeline</div>
<pre>
   Ch 13 RUN &amp; SCALE  ──▶  Ch 14 PLACE  ──▶  Ch 15 GOVERN
   Deploy · RS · HPA       affinity / taints     requests/limits/QoS
   rollouts · rollbacks    topology spread       LimitRange · Quota
            └─────────────────────┬─────────────────────┘
                                    ▼
        Ch 16 SPECIALIZE:  Job · CronJob · DaemonSet · StatefulSet
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: take easypj from “a fragile hand-launched Pod” to
   “a fleet that survives node loss, upgrades cleanly, and grows under load.”
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch13">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 13 · Deployments, ReplicaSets &amp; Scaling</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Deployments are the <strong>heart of Kubernetes workload management</strong>. In this chapter you'll master the complete lifecycle — creating, scaling, updating, and rolling back applications. We'll trace how a Deployment creates a ReplicaSet, which in turn creates Pods, and how the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) adds elastic scaling. Every example uses our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django app so you see real-world patterns, not toy demos.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🚀 Workloads</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Create Deployments imperatively and declaratively — know the full YAML anatomy</li>
                    <li>✅ Perform rolling updates and instant rollbacks using <code>kubectl rollout</code></li>
                    <li>✅ Understand how Deployments → ReplicaSets → Pods chain together under the hood</li>
                    <li>✅ Scale manually with <code>kubectl scale</code> and automatically with HPA</li>
                    <li>✅ Choose the right deployment strategy — RollingUpdate vs Recreate</li>
                    <li>✅ Control update pace with <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Deployments Are CKA Exam Bread &amp; Butter</h5>
                <p>Nearly every CKA exam has at least 2-3 tasks involving Deployments. You <strong>must</strong> be fast at:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl create deployment NAME --image=IMG --replicas=N</code> — imperative creation</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl set image deployment/NAME container=IMG:TAG</code> — trigger rolling update</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N</code> — rollback</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl scale deployment/NAME --replicas=N</code> — manual scaling</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/NAME</code> — verify update progress</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The Chapter 13 learning-arc roadmap (Deployments → ReplicaSets → HPA → Strategies → Probes) goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of §13.1–§13.5 goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <!-- ─────────── §13.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.1 Deployments — Complete Lifecycle</h3>
                <p>A Deployment is Kubernetes' <strong>recommended way to manage stateless applications</strong>. It provides declarative updates, self-healing, scaling, and version history — all in one resource.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Concept: Declarative vs Imperative</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes supports both styles. <strong>Imperative</strong>: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v1</code>. <strong>Declarative</strong>: <code>kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml</code>. On the CKA, use imperative commands to generate YAML fast, then edit if needed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="relationship-map">
                    <div class="rel-hub">Deployment</div>
                    <div class="rel-spokes">
                        <div class="rel-spoke">🎛️ Manages ReplicaSets</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">📦 Creates Pods via RS</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">🔄 Rolling Updates</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">⏪ Rollback History</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">📈 Scale In/Out</div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">✅ Self-Healing</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1a">a) Creating a Deployment</h4>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative — Fast Exam Creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create deployment (CKA exam speed)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML without creating (save to file, then edit)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml > easypj-deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get deployments
kubectl describe deployment easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-deployment.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj
  labels:
    app: easypj
    project: jobpost
spec:
  replicas: 3                    <span class="comment"># Desired number of pods</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                <span class="comment"># Must match template labels</span>
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                <span class="comment"># 1 extra pod during update</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0          <span class="comment"># Never fewer than 3 running</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj              <span class="comment"># Must match selector</span>
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "256Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "512Mi"
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 5
          periodSeconds: 10</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Deployment YAML Anatomy</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> replicas</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Desired pod count — the controller constantly ensures this number is running</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏷️</span> selector.matchLabels</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How the Deployment finds its pods — <strong>must match</strong> template labels exactly</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> strategy</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">RollingUpdate (default) or Recreate — controls how pods are replaced</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> template</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">The Pod spec — this is a "Pod blueprint" stamped out for each replica</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📈</span> maxSurge</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many extra pods beyond <code>replicas</code> are allowed during an update (number or %)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📉</span> maxUnavailable</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">How many pods can be unavailable during an update (number or %). 0 = zero downtime.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1b">b) Scaling</h4>
                <p>Scaling changes the <code>spec.replicas</code> field. The Deployment's ReplicaSet controller immediately creates or deletes pods to match.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Manual Scaling Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Scale up — handle more traffic</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=5

<span class="comment"># Scale down — save resources</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=2

<span class="comment"># Verify scaling</span>
kubectl get deployment easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME      READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj   5/5     5            5           10m</span>

<span class="comment"># Watch pods appear/disappear in real-time</span>
kubectl get pods -w -l app=easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Manual Scaling — Pods Appear Instantly</div>
<pre>
  kubectl scale deployment easypj --replicas=5

  BEFORE (3 replicas):             AFTER (5 replicas):
  ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐    ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐ ┌────────┐
  │easypj-1│ │easypj-2│ │easypj-3│    │easypj-1│ │easypj-2│ │easypj-3│ │easypj-4│ │easypj-5│
  │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │    │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │ │  📦 v1 │ │ 🆕 v1  │ │ 🆕 v1  │
  └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘    └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘ └────────┘
                                                                          ↑ NEW       ↑ NEW
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1c">c) Rolling Updates</h4>
                <p>Rolling updates replace pods <strong>one at a time</strong>, ensuring your application stays available throughout the upgrade. This is the default strategy and is <strong>heavily tested on the CKA</strong>.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Triggering a Rolling Update</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Update image — triggers rolling update</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj django=easypj:v2

<span class="comment"># Watch rollout progress (CKA exam: verify your work!)</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "easypj" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment">#   1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   2 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment">#   deployment "easypj" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># Check rollout history</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         &lt;none&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Add change cause for audit trail</span>
kubectl annotate deployment/easypj \
  kubernetes.io/change-cause="Update to v2 — new job search feature"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Rolling Update: easypj v1 → v2 (Zero Downtime)</div>
<pre>
  maxSurge: 1       (1 extra pod allowed during update)
  maxUnavailable: 0 (never fewer than 3 running)

  Time 0:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [📦v1]              ← All running v1
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 1:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [📦v1] [🆕v2]       ← v2 pod starting (surge=1)
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 2:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [🗑️  ] [✅v2]       ← v2 ready → old v1 terminating
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 3:  [📦v1] [📦v1] [✅v2] [🆕v2]       ← Next v2 pod starting
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 4:  [📦v1] [🗑️  ] [✅v2] [✅v2]       ← Second v1 terminating
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 5:  [📦v1] [✅v2] [✅v2] [🆕v2]       ← Last v2 starting
           ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Time 6:  [✅v2] [✅v2] [✅v2]              ← All v2 ✅ Zero downtime! 🎉

  Key insight: At NO point were there fewer than 3 healthy pods serving traffic!
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1d">d) Rollbacks</h4>
                <p>If v2 is broken, you can instantly rollback. Kubernetes keeps a revision history of ReplicaSets.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam Alert: Rollback Commands</h5>
                    <p>Rollback questions are extremely common. Know these cold:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME</code> — go back one revision</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N</code> — go to specific revision</li>
                        <li><code>kubectl rollout history deployment/NAME</code> — see all revisions</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Rollback Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Undo last update (go back to previous revision)</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Go to a specific revision</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj --to-revision=2

<span class="comment"># Check what happened</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj --revision=2

<span class="comment"># Verify rollback succeeded</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj
kubectl get pods -l app=easypj -o jsonpath='{.items[*].spec.containers[*].image}'
<span class="comment"># easypj:v1 easypj:v1 easypj:v1  ← back to v1!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Rollback Flow: v2 Broken → Instant Recovery to v1</div>
<pre>
  Deployment: easypj
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                          │
  │  Revision 1 (v1) ─── Revision 2 (v2) ─── Revision 3    │
  │  ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐        ┌──────────┐   │
  │  │ReplicaSet│        │ReplicaSet│        │ReplicaSet│   │
  │  │ (scaled  │  ──→   │ (scaled  │  ──→   │ = rev 1  │   │
  │  │  to 0)   │        │  to 0)   │  undo  │ (active) │   │
  │  └──────────┘        └──────────┘        └──────────┘   │
  │                        ↑ 💀 broken          ↑ ✅ fixed  │
  │                                                          │
  │  kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj                  │
  │  → K8s scales old RS back up, scales broken RS down     │
  │  → Pods running v1 again within seconds! 🎉             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Create</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create deployment</code> or <code>kubectl apply -f</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get deploy,rs,pods</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Scale</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl scale deploy --replicas=N</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Update</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl set image deploy/NAME</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Monitor</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl rollout status</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">6</div>
                        <h5>Rollback</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl rollout undo</code> if needed</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4 id="s13-1e">e) Pause &amp; Resume — Batch Multiple Changes</h4>
                <p>Sometimes you need to make multiple changes (image + resources + env vars) in one rollout instead of triggering separate updates for each. Pause the deployment, make all changes, then resume to trigger a single rollout.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Pause &amp; Resume Workflow</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pause — no changes will trigger a rollout</span>
kubectl rollout pause deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Make multiple changes (none trigger rollout yet)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj django=easypj:v3
kubectl set resources deployment/easypj -c django \
  --requests=cpu=500m,memory=512Mi
kubectl set env deployment/easypj DEBUG=false

<span class="comment"># Resume — all changes deploy in ONE rollout</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment/easypj

<span class="comment"># Verify single rollout applied all changes</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pause/Resume vs Normal — Rollout Count</div>
<pre>
  WITHOUT Pause/Resume:              WITH Pause/Resume:

  Change image → Rollout #1  🔄      kubectl rollout pause
  Change CPU   → Rollout #2  🔄      Change image  ┐
  Change env   → Rollout #3  🔄      Change CPU    ├─ no rollouts
                                      Change env    ┘
  3 separate rollouts!               kubectl rollout resume
  3 ReplicaSets created!              → Rollout #1 only  🔄
  ⚠️ Wasteful &amp; slower               ✅ Single clean rollout!
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.2 ReplicaSets — Under the Hood</h3>
                <p>A ReplicaSet ensures a specified number of pod replicas are running at any time. You <strong>rarely create ReplicaSets directly</strong> — Deployments create and manage them for you. But understanding them is essential for troubleshooting.</p>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why You Need to Know This</h5>
                    <p>When <code>kubectl get pods</code> shows a pod stuck in <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>, you need to trace: <strong>Pod → ReplicaSet → Deployment</strong> to find the root cause. The CKA tests this troubleshooting chain.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod Chain</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  DEPLOYMENT: easypj                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │ You manage this layer                                       │ │
  │  │ "I want 3 pods running easypj:v1"                          │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                             │ creates &amp; manages                 │
  │                             ▼                                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  REPLICASET: easypj-7d9f8b6c5d                           │   │
  │  │  Kubernetes manages this layer automatically              │   │
  │  │  selector: matchLabels: { app: easypj }                  │   │
  │  │  replicas: 3                                              │   │
  │  └───────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────┬────────────┘   │
  │          │ owns             │ owns             │ owns           │
  │          ▼                  ▼                  ▼                │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐          │
  │  │ POD          │  │ POD          │  │ POD          │          │
  │  │ easypj-..abc │  │ easypj-..def │  │ easypj-..ghi │          │
  │  │ 📦 easypj:v1│  │ 📦 easypj:v1│  │ 📦 easypj:v1│          │
  │  │ labels:      │  │ labels:      │  │ labels:      │          │
  │  │  app: easypj │  │  app: easypj │  │  app: easypj │          │
  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘          │
  │                                                                  │
  │  Delete a pod? → ReplicaSet auto-creates a replacement! 🔄     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">replicaset-example.yaml (for understanding — rarely created directly)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: ReplicaSet
metadata:
  name: easypj-replicaset
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:                 <span class="comment"># Which pods this RS owns</span>
      app: easypj
    <span class="comment"># Advanced: matchExpressions</span>
    <span class="comment"># matchExpressions:</span>
    <span class="comment">#   - key: app</span>
    <span class="comment">#     operator: In           # In, NotIn, Exists, DoesNotExist</span>
    <span class="comment">#     values: [easypj, easypj-staging]</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>matchLabels</h5>
                        <p>Simple equality: <code>app: easypj</code>. All labels must match. Most common approach.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔍</div>
                        <h5>matchExpressions</h5>
                        <p>Operators: <code>In</code>, <code>NotIn</code>, <code>Exists</code>, <code>DoesNotExist</code>. For complex multi-value selectors.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Inspecting the Chain</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># See all three layers at once</span>
kubectl get deploy,rs,pods -l app=easypj

<span class="comment"># NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj   3/3     3            3           5m</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                                DESIRED   CURRENT   READY   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># replicaset.apps/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d  3         3         3       5m</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME                          READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-def34  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-ghi56  1/1     Running   0          5m</span>

<span class="comment"># See owner references (pod → RS → Deployment)</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-7d9f8b6c5d-abc12 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.ownerReferences[0].name}'
<span class="comment"># easypj-7d9f8b6c5d  ← owned by the ReplicaSet</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Never Delete ReplicaSets Directly</h5>
                    <p>If you delete a ReplicaSet that belongs to a Deployment, the Deployment controller will immediately recreate it. To scale down, always modify the <strong>Deployment</strong>. Delete the Deployment to clean up all RS and Pods together.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 revisionHistoryLimit — Control Revision History</h5>
                    <p>By default, Kubernetes keeps <strong>10 old ReplicaSets</strong> (revision history) for rollback. Each old RS consumes etcd storage. You can control this with <code>spec.revisionHistoryLimit</code>:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 5</code> — keep last 5 revisions (good for production)</li>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 0</code> — keep none (⚠️ no rollback possible!)</li>
                        <li><code>revisionHistoryLimit: 10</code> — default</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.3 Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)</h3>
                <p>The HPA <strong>automatically scales</strong> your Deployment based on observed CPU, memory, or custom metrics. When traffic spikes, HPA adds pods. When traffic drops, it removes them. This is <strong>critical for production</strong> and tested on the CKA.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📋 Prerequisite: Metrics Server</h5>
                    <p>HPA requires the <strong>Metrics Server</strong> to be installed in the cluster. Without it, HPA has no data to make scaling decisions. Verify: <code>kubectl top nodes</code> — if this works, Metrics Server is running.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">HPA — Imperative (CKA Exam Speed)</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create HPA bound to deployment</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment easypj --min=3 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70

<span class="comment"># Check HPA status</span>
kubectl get hpa
<span class="comment"># NAME     REFERENCE           TARGETS   MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj  Deployment/easypj   45%/70%   3         10        3          2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Detailed view</span>
kubectl describe hpa easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-hpa.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-hpa
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj
  minReplicas: 3
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: cpu
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 70    <span class="comment"># Scale when avg CPU > 70%</span>
  - type: Resource
    resource:
      name: memory
      target:
        type: Utilization
        averageUtilization: 80    <span class="comment"># Also watch memory</span>
  behavior:
    scaleUp:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 30   <span class="comment"># React quickly to spikes</span>
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300  <span class="comment"># Wait 5 min before scaling down</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">HPA Scaling Flow — Automatic Response to Load</div>
<pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  HPA: Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for easypj                      │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 Metrics Server reports: CPU = 85%  (above target of 70%)   │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  🤖 HPA evaluates every 15 seconds:                             │
  │     desiredReplicas = ceil(currentReplicas × (85% / 70%))       │
  │     desiredReplicas = ceil(3 × 1.21) = 4                        │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  📈 HPA updates: deployment.spec.replicas = 4                   │
  │       │                                                          │
  │       ▼                                                          │
  │  📦📦📦📦  4 pods running → CPU drops to 60%                    │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ... traffic keeps climbing ...                                  │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 CPU = 90%  →  HPA: replicas = 6                             │
  │  📦📦📦📦📦📦  6 pods running → CPU stabilizes at 55%           │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ... traffic decreases (night time) ...                          │
  │                                                                  │
  │  📊 CPU = 25%  →  HPA waits 5 min (stabilization window)       │
  │               →  then scales down to minReplicas = 3            │
  │  📦📦📦  3 pods — cost efficient! 💰                             │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬆️</div>
                        <h5>Scale Up (Fast)</h5>
                        <p>Default stabilization: <strong>0 seconds</strong>. HPA reacts immediately to load spikes to protect users.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⬇️</div>
                        <h5>Scale Down (Slow)</h5>
                        <p>Default stabilization: <strong>300 seconds</strong>. Avoids flapping — waits 5 min to confirm load is truly gone.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Tip: HPA Requires Resource Requests</h5>
                    <p>HPA can only measure utilization as a percentage if <code>resources.requests</code> is set on the container. Without requests, HPA cannot calculate CPU percentage and will report <code>&lt;unknown&gt;/70%</code>. Always set resource requests on Deployments that use HPA.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §13.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s13-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">13.4 Deployment Strategies</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes supports two built-in deployment strategies. Choosing the right one depends on your application's ability to handle multiple versions running simultaneously.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Strategy</th>
                                <th>How It Works</th>
                                <th>Downtime</th>
                                <th>When to Use</th>
                                <th>easypj Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RollingUpdate</strong></td>
                                <td>Replace pods gradually, one (or few) at a time</td>
                                <td>❌ None</td>
                                <td>Default — use for most updates</td>
                                <td>New Django feature release</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Recreate</strong></td>
                                <td>Kill ALL old pods first, then start ALL new pods</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes — brief outage</td>
                                <td>Breaking changes, DB schema migrations, incompatible API versions</td>
                                <td>Major Django version upgrade with DB migration</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RollingUpdate vs Recreate — Visual Comparison</div>
<pre>
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ROLLINGUPDATE STRATEGY (default)          RECREATE STRATEGY
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  T0: [v1] [v1] [v1]                       [v1] [v1] [v1]
       │                                         │
  T1: [v1] [v1] [v1] [v2] ← surge              [💀] [💀] [💀] ← all killed
       │                                         │
  T2: [v1] [v1] [v2]      ← replace 1           [  ] [  ] [  ] ← downtime! ⚠️
       │                                         │
  T3: [v1] [v2] [v2]      ← replace 2           [v2] [v2] [v2] ← all new
       │
  T4: [v2] [v2] [v2]      ← complete ✅

  Availability:                             Availability:
  ██████████████████ 100%                   ████░░░░████ ~70%
  Never less than minAvailable              Brief outage window

  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
</pre>
                </div>

                <h4>RollingUpdate — Tuning Parameters</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">strategy-examples.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Conservative — zero downtime, slow rollout</span>
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: 1          <span class="comment"># Only 1 extra pod at a time</span>
    maxUnavailable: 0    <span class="comment"># Never fewer than desired replicas</span>

<span class="comment"># Aggressive — faster rollout, allows brief capacity dip</span>
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate
  rollingUpdate:
    maxSurge: "50%"      <span class="comment"># Up to 50% extra pods</span>
    maxUnavailable: "25%" <span class="comment"># Allow 25% to be unavailable</span>

<span class="comment"># Recreate — kill all, then create all</span>
strategy:
  type: Recreate         <span class="comment"># No rollingUpdate params needed</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="stat-bar-group">
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">RollingUpdate — Safety</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★★★</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width: 100%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">RollingUpdate — Speed</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★☆☆</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width: 60%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Recreate — Safety</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★☆☆☆</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill blue" style="width: 40%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="stat-bar-item">
                        <span class="stat-bar-label">Recreate — Speed</span>
                        <span class="stat-bar-value">★★★★★</span>
                        <div class="stat-bar"><div class="stat-bar-fill green" style="width: 100%"></div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Exam Strategy</h5>
                    <p>On the exam, the default RollingUpdate is almost always what you want. Only use <code>Recreate</code> if the question explicitly says "all pods must be running the same version" or mentions database migrations that can't handle mixed versions.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Beyond Built-in: Blue-Green &amp; Canary Deployments</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes only has <strong>RollingUpdate</strong> and <strong>Recreate</strong> built in. Two other common strategies require manual setup or tools like Argo Rollouts / Istio:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Blue-Green</strong> — Run two full Deployments (blue=current, green=new). Switch the Service selector from blue to green once verified. Instant rollback by switching back.</li>
                        <li><strong>Canary</strong> — Deploy new version alongside old with fewer replicas (e.g., 1 of 10). Route a small percentage of traffic to canary. If healthy, gradually increase. Popular with service meshes.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Blue-Green vs Canary — Not Built-in but Important to Know</div>
<pre>
  BLUE-GREEN:                           CANARY:

  ┌─────── Service ──────┐              ┌─────── Service ──────┐
  │  selector: ver=blue  │              │  selector: app=web   │
  └──────────┬───────────┘              └──────────┬───────────┘
             │                                     │
    ┌────────┴────────┐                   ┌────────┴────────┐
    ▼                 ▼                   ▼                 ▼
  Blue Deploy      Green Deploy        v1 Deploy (9)    v2 Deploy (1)
  (current ✅)     (new, testing)      (90% traffic)    (10% canary)
  [v1][v1][v1]     [v2][v2][v2]        [v1]×9           [v2]×1

  Switch: change   Promote: scale      Promote: scale v2 up,
  selector to      green, delete       scale v1 down gradually
  ver=green        blue
</pre>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Rolling Updates Actually Work: ReplicaSet Lifecycle, maxSurge/maxUnavailable Math, and Revision History</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Rolling Update — Step-by-Step with ReplicaSet Math</h5>
            <p>When you update a Deployment's image, Kubernetes doesn't modify the existing ReplicaSet. It creates a NEW ReplicaSet and orchestrates a gradual handover. Understanding the math behind maxSurge and maxUnavailable lets you tune updates for speed vs safety.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ROLLING UPDATE — Complete ReplicaSet Lifecycle                               │
│                                                                              │
│  INITIAL STATE: Deployment easypj-web, replicas=3, image=v2.0                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old):                                                         │    │
│  │   desired=3, ready=3                                                │    │
│  │   Pods: [web-abc, web-def, web-ghi] (all v2.0)                     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web easypj=easypj:v2.1                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  STEP 1: Deployment creates NEW ReplicaSet with updated pod template         │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new):                                                         │    │
│  │   desired=0, ready=0  <span class="comment">← Starts at 0                                 </span>│    │
│  │   Pods: []                                                           │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): unchanged (still 3/3)                                  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Scale up RS-v2 by maxSurge (default 25% = 1 pod)                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new): desired=1, ready=0→1 (pod starting...)                  │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=3, ready=3                                     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  TOTAL PODS: 4 (3 old + 1 new)  <span class="comment">← maxSurge allows extra pod         </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: New pod is Ready → Scale down RS-v1 by 1                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (new): desired=1, ready=1                                     │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=2, ready=2  <span class="comment">← One pod terminated               </span>│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  TOTAL PODS: 3 (2 old + 1 new)  <span class="comment">← Back to desired count             </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  Repeat STEP 2→3 until RS-v1=0, RS-v2=3 (full cutover):                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  RS-v2: 1→2→3 (scaling up)                                           │    │
│  │  RS-v1: 2→1→0 (scaling down)                                         │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  FINAL STATE:                                                                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ RS-v2 (current): desired=3, ready=3                                  │    │
│  │ RS-v1 (old): desired=0, ready=0  <span class="comment">← Kept for rollback!               </span>│    │
│  │   Pods: all terminated                                               │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  maxSurge & maxUnavailable MATH:                                             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ maxSurge:  EXTRA pods allowed ABOVE desired count                   │    │
│  │ maxUnavailable: pods allowed to be UNAVAILABLE during update        │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ Example: replicas=10, maxSurge=25%, maxUnavailable=25%               │    │
│  │   maxSurge pods:     ceil(10 × 0.25) = 3 extra pods allowed         │    │
│  │   maxUnavailable pods: floor(10 × 0.25) = 2 pods can be down        │    │
│  │   → During update: 10-13 pods running, at least 8 pods available    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ ZERO-DOWNTIME CONFIG (safest, slowest):                             │    │
│  │   maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0                                    │    │
│  │   → 1 new pod created, wait for Ready, then 1 old pod removed       │    │
│  │   → NEVER below desired replicas, 1 extra at a time                 │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ FAST UPDATE CONFIG (fastest, riskiest):                              │    │
│  │   maxSurge: 50%, maxUnavailable: 50%                                 │    │
│  │   → Half the pods replaced at once                                   │    │
│  │   → Application must handle 50% traffic loss gracefully             │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  REVISION HISTORY (stored in ReplicaSets):                                   │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Each image update creates a NEW ReplicaSet. Old ones are kept for    │    │
│  │ rollback (default: last 10 revisions).                                │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ $ kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web                          │    │
│  │ REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE                                                │    │
│  │ 1         Initial deploy v2.0                                         │    │
│  │ 2         Update to v2.1                                              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ $ kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web                              │    │
│  │ → Scales RS-v1 back to 3, scales RS-v2 to 0 (instant rollback!)     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, a stuck rollout nearly always means the new pods can't become Ready. Check: <code>kubectl rollout status deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code> (shows progress), <code>kubectl get rs</code> (see which RS is scaling), <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;new-pod&gt;</code> (find the failure). Common causes: wrong image tag, CrashLoopBackOff, insufficient cluster resources, or readiness probe failing. You can PAUSE a rollout: <code>kubectl rollout pause deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code> and RESUME: <code>kubectl rollout resume deploy/&lt;name&gt;</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways for Chapter 13 go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Rolling update is the default</strong> — If the question doesn't specify a strategy, it's <code>RollingUpdate</code>. Only use <code>Recreate</code> when explicitly asked (kills all pods first)</li>
                    <li><strong><code>maxUnavailable</code> + <code>maxSurge</code></strong> — Defaults are 25% each. The exam may ask you to set specific values. Remember: <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> + <code>maxSurge: 1</code> = zero-downtime</li>
                    <li><strong>Rollback requires history</strong> — <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> only works if there's a previous revision. Add <code>--record</code> (deprecated) or use <code>--to-revision=N</code> for precision</li>
                    <li><strong><code>kubectl set image</code> container name</strong> — You must specify the container name: <code>kubectl set image deployment/web nginx=nginx:1.25</code>. Get it from <code>kubectl get deploy web -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[*].name}'</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Deployment lifecycle in 60 seconds:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create deploy web --image=nginx:1.24 --replicas=3</code><br>
                <code>kubectl set image deploy/web nginx=nginx:1.25</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout status deploy/web</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout history deploy/web</code><br>
                <code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/web</code> — instant rollback</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 13)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Create deployment</td><td><code>kubectl create deployment NAME --image=IMG --replicas=N</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Scale</td><td><code>kubectl scale deployment NAME --replicas=N</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Update image</td><td><code>kubectl set image deployment/NAME container=IMG:TAG</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check rollout</td><td><code>kubectl rollout status deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View history</td><td><code>kubectl rollout history deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Undo last</td><td><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Go to revision</td><td><code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/NAME --to-revision=N</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Autoscale</td><td><code>kubectl autoscale deployment NAME --min=M --max=X --cpu-percent=P</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pause rollout</td><td><code>kubectl rollout pause deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Resume rollout</td><td><code>kubectl rollout resume deployment/NAME</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch13-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 4 minutes):</strong></p>
