            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 17 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch17-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 17 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the pod network model — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 4 fundamental Kubernetes networking rules? Why are they important for the CKA exam?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># The 4 Kubernetes Networking Rules:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Every pod gets its own unique IP address</span>
<span class="comment">#    → No port conflicts between pods</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Pods are treated like VMs from a networking perspective</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Pods on ANY node can communicate with ALL other pods</span>
<span class="comment">#    WITHOUT NAT (Network Address Translation)</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Flat network — no routing hacks needed</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Agents on a node (kubelet, kube-proxy) can communicate</span>
<span class="comment">#    with all pods on that node</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Node components can health-check and manage pods</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Pods in the host network of a node can communicate</span>
<span class="comment">#    with all pods on ALL nodes without NAT</span>
<span class="comment">#    → hostNetwork:true pods participate in the flat network</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify rule #1: Each pod has unique IP</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS   IP            NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-1   1/1    Running  10.244.1.5    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-2   1/1    Running  10.244.2.8    worker-2 (different subnet!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify rule #2: Cross-node pod communication</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- curl -s 10.244.2.8:8080
<span class="comment"># Response from webapp-2 (no NAT needed)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>These 4 rules define the Kubernetes network model. Every CNI plugin (Calico, Flannel, Weave, Cilium) MUST implement these rules. The flat network model means any pod can reach any other pod by IP — no NAT, no port mapping. This is fundamentally different from Docker's default bridge networking. On the CKA, understanding this model helps you: (1) debug connectivity issues (if pod-to-pod fails, the CNI is broken), (2) understand why Services exist (pods have dynamic IPs), (3) know that NetworkPolicies are the ONLY way to restrict traffic (everything is allowed by default).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A newly joined node shows <code>NotReady</code> status. <code>kubectl describe node</code> shows <em>"runtime network not ready: NetworkReady=false reason:NetworkPluginNotReady"</em>. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Diagnosis: CNI plugin is NOT installed on this node</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check if CNI is installed cluster-wide</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E "calico|flannel|weave|cilium"
<span class="comment"># (no output — CNI not installed!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Install the CNI plugin</span>
<span class="comment"># Calico:</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Flannel:</span>
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/flannel-io/flannel/releases/latest/download/kube-flannel.yml

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Wait and verify</span>
kubectl get nodes -w
<span class="comment"># NAME      STATUS   ROLES       AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1  Ready    worker      5m   ← Now Ready!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify CNI pods are running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=calico-node
<span class="comment"># calico-node-abc   1/1   Running   worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># calico-node-def   1/1   Running   worker-2</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The CNI (Container Network Interface) plugin is responsible for setting up pod networking. Without it, the kubelet can't configure network namespaces for pods, so the node reports <code>NotReady</code>. On the CKA, this is a CLASSIC troubleshooting question. After <code>kubeadm init</code> or <code>kubeadm join</code>, the CNI is NOT automatically installed — you must apply it manually. The exam question will tell you which CNI to use and may provide the URL. Check the <code>kubeadm init</code> output notes or the question instructions.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is kube-proxy and what modes can it operate in? How do you check which mode is being used?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># kube-proxy modes:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. iptables (default) — uses iptables rules for Service routing</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. IPVS — uses Linux IPVS for load balancing (better performance)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. userspace — legacy, rarely used (proxies in userspace)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current mode:</span>
kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode
<span class="comment"># mode: ""          ← empty string = iptables (default)</span>
<span class="comment"># mode: "ipvs"      ← IPVS mode</span>
<span class="comment"># mode: "iptables"  ← explicit iptables</span>

<span class="comment"># Or check kube-proxy logs:</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy | grep "Using"
<span class="comment"># "Using iptables proxier"</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify kube-proxy is running on all nodes:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy -o wide
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-abc   1/1   Running   controlplane</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-def   1/1   Running   worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-ghi   1/1   Running   worker-2</span>

<span class="comment"># kube-proxy runs as a DaemonSet:</span>
kubectl get ds -n kube-system kube-proxy</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kube-proxy runs on every node as a DaemonSet and maintains network rules that allow Service traffic to reach pods. In <code>iptables</code> mode, it creates nat table rules. In <code>IPVS</code> mode, it uses Linux Virtual Server for more efficient load balancing. On the CKA, know that: (1) kube-proxy handles Service → Pod routing (not pod-to-pod, which is CNI), (2) if kube-proxy is down, Services break but direct pod-to-pod communication still works, (3) the mode is configured in the <code>kube-proxy</code> ConfigMap in <code>kube-system</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Pod CIDR and Service CIDR? How do you find these ranges in an existing cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Pod CIDR: IP range for pod IPs</span>
<span class="comment"># Service CIDR: IP range for Service ClusterIPs</span>

<span class="comment"># Find Pod CIDR:</span>
kubectl cluster-info dump | grep -m1 cluster-cidr
<span class="comment"># --cluster-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</span>

<span class="comment"># Or from the controller manager:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep cluster-cidr
<span class="comment"># --cluster-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</span>

<span class="comment"># Find Service CIDR:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-apiserver \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].spec.containers[0].command}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep service-cluster
<span class="comment"># --service-cluster-ip-range=10.96.0.0/12</span>

<span class="comment"># Per-node Pod CIDR:</span>
kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.podCIDR}{"\n"}{end}'
<span class="comment"># controlplane    10.244.0.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1        10.244.1.0/24</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2        10.244.2.0/24</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pod CIDR is set during <code>kubeadm init --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16</code> and split into per-node subnets by the controller-manager. Service CIDR is set via <code>--service-cluster-ip-range</code> on the API server. These ranges MUST NOT overlap with each other or with the host network. On the CKA, knowing where to find these values is essential for: (1) troubleshooting IP conflicts, (2) configuring CNI plugins, (3) understanding why a pod IP is 10.244.x.x but a Service IP is 10.96.x.x.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you launch a network troubleshooting pod with all common tools? Show how to test connectivity between pods.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Launch a debug pod with network tools:</span>
kubectl run nettest --image=nicolaka/netshoot \
--rm -it --restart=Never -- bash

<span class="comment"># Inside the debug pod:</span>
<span class="comment"># Test DNS resolution</span>
nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Server: 10.96.0.10 (kube-dns)</span>
<span class="comment"># Name: kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.0.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Test connectivity to a service</span>
curl -s http://web-svc:80
<span class="comment"># (HTML response)</span>

<span class="comment"># Test pod-to-pod directly by IP</span>
curl -s http://10.244.1.5:8080

<span class="comment"># Check network path</span>
traceroute 10.244.2.8

<span class="comment"># Test TCP port connectivity</span>
nc -zv web-svc 80
<span class="comment"># Connection to web-svc 80 port [tcp/http] succeeded!</span>

<span class="comment"># Minimal alternative (no netshoot image):</span>
kubectl run test --image=busybox --rm -it -- wget -qO- http://web-svc:80
<span class="comment"># Or:</span>
kubectl exec existing-pod -- curl -s http://web-svc:80</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>nicolaka/netshoot</code> image includes curl, nslookup, dig, traceroute, nc (netcat), tcpdump, and more. On the CKA, you can also use <code>busybox</code> (has wget, nslookup) or the <code>curlimages/curl</code> image. The <code>--rm -it --restart=Never</code> flags ensure the pod is cleaned up after exit. Key debugging pattern: (1) Test DNS with <code>nslookup</code>, (2) Test HTTP with <code>curl</code>/<code>wget</code>, (3) Test TCP with <code>nc -zv</code>. If DNS fails, the issue is CoreDNS. If DNS works but curl fails, the issue is kube-proxy or NetworkPolicy.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to pod networking when kube-proxy crashes on a node? Can pods still communicate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># When kube-proxy is down on a node:</span>

<span class="comment"># STILL WORKS:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Pod-to-pod direct communication (by pod IP)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- curl http://10.244.2.8:8080  <span class="comment"># Works!</span>

<span class="comment"># BROKEN:</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Service → Pod routing (ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- curl http://web-svc:80  <span class="comment"># Times out!</span>
<span class="comment"># (iptables/IPVS rules aren't being updated)</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS MAY WORK:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ DNS resolution still works (CoreDNS is independent)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-1 -- nslookup web-svc
<span class="comment"># Returns the ClusterIP, but traffic to that IP won't route</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify kube-proxy status:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy -o wide
<span class="comment"># Check if the pod on the affected node is CrashLoopBackOff</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix:</span>
kubectl delete pod -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy \
--field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-1
<span class="comment"># DaemonSet recreates it</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kube-proxy only handles Service-to-Pod routing by maintaining iptables/IPVS rules. Pod-to-pod networking is handled by the CNI plugin. So when kube-proxy is down: Services break (ClusterIP doesn't route) but direct pod IP communication works fine. On the CKA, this distinction is critical for diagnosing network issues: "Service doesn't work but pods can ping each other" = kube-proxy problem. "No pod communication at all" = CNI problem. Deleting the kube-proxy pod lets the DaemonSet recreate it, which usually fixes the issue.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>hostNetwork: true</code> and a regular pod? When would you use it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Regular pod: gets its own network namespace with a pod IP</span>
<span class="comment"># hostNetwork pod: uses the node's network namespace directly</span>

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: host-network-pod
spec:
hostNetwork: true    # ← Uses node's IP and ports
containers:
- name: nginx
image: nginx
ports:
- containerPort: 80  <span class="comment"># Actually binds to the NODE's port 80!</span>

<span class="comment"># Regular pod:</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP: 10.244.1.5 (pod network)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Can bind any port without conflict with other pods</span>

<span class="comment"># hostNetwork pod:</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP: 192.168.1.100 (same as node!)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Port conflicts with other host-network pods</span>
<span class="comment">#   Can access node-level network interfaces</span>

<span class="comment"># Use cases:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Control plane components (kube-apiserver, etcd)</span>
<span class="comment"># - CNI plugins (need host network access)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Network monitoring tools</span>
<span class="comment"># - kube-proxy itself uses hostNetwork</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>hostNetwork: true</code> makes the pod share the node's network stack — it sees the node's interfaces, uses the node's IP, and can bind to the node's ports. This is how control-plane components work: the API server binds to port 6443 on the node's IP. On the CKA, <code>hostNetwork</code> appears in questions about: (1) static pods (control-plane components), (2) why certain system pods have node IPs instead of pod IPs, (3) networking troubleshooting where <code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> shows a node IP instead of the expected pod CIDR IP.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check if a CNI plugin is installed and which one is being used?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: Check running CNI pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -iE "calico|flannel|weave|cilium"
<span class="comment"># calico-node-abc           1/1   Running   (Calico is installed)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Check CNI configuration on the node</span>
ls /etc/cni/net.d/
<span class="comment"># 10-calico.conflist    ← Calico config file</span>

cat /etc/cni/net.d/10-calico.conflist | head -5
<span class="comment"># {"name": "k8s-pod-network", "cniVersion": "0.3.1", ...}</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check CNI binaries</span>
ls /opt/cni/bin/
<span class="comment"># calico  calico-ipam  bandwidth  bridge  flannel ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Check node annotations</span>
kubectl get node worker-1 -o jsonpath='{.metadata.annotations}' | python3 -m json.tool | grep cni

<span class="comment"># Method 5: Check kubelet config</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep cni
<span class="comment"># --cni-bin-dir=/opt/cni/bin --cni-conf-dir=/etc/cni/net.d</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CNI plugins install themselves as DaemonSets (one pod per node) and place their config in <code>/etc/cni/net.d/</code> and binaries in <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code>. The kubelet reads the CNI config to set up pod networking. On the CKA, the fastest way to identify the CNI is <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> — look for calico-node, flannel, weave-net, or cilium pods. If NO CNI pods exist and nodes are <code>NotReady</code>, you need to install one. The exam question will specify which CNI to use.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod can reach other pods on the SAME node but not pods on DIFFERENT nodes. What's likely wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Same-node works, cross-node fails → CNI overlay issue</span>

<span class="comment"># Diagnostic steps:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Verify the problem</span>
kubectl exec pod-on-node1 -- ping 10.244.1.5  <span class="comment"># Same node → works</span>
kubectl exec pod-on-node1 -- ping 10.244.2.8  <span class="comment"># Other node → fails</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check CNI pods on both nodes</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep calico
<span class="comment"># calico-node on node1: Running</span>
<span class="comment"># calico-node on node2: CrashLoopBackOff!  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check CNI pod logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system calico-node-xyz

<span class="comment"># 4. Check node routes</span>
<span class="comment"># On node1:</span>
ip route | grep 10.244
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.0/24 → local (works)</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.2.0/24 → via 192.168.1.102 (node2's IP)</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Check if nodes can reach each other</span>
<span class="comment"># SSH to node1:</span>
ping 192.168.1.102  <span class="comment"># Can node1 reach node2?</span>

<span class="comment"># Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - CNI pod crashed on one node</span>
<span class="comment"># - Firewall blocking VXLAN (port 4789) or IP-in-IP traffic</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing routes between node subnets</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Same-node pod communication uses the local bridge (handled by the CNI on that node). Cross-node communication requires the CNI's overlay network (VXLAN, IP-in-IP, or BGP routing). If cross-node fails: (1) Check if the CNI DaemonSet pod is running on both nodes, (2) Check for firewall rules blocking overlay protocols (VXLAN uses UDP 4789, Calico IP-in-IP uses protocol 4), (3) Verify node-to-node connectivity. On the CKA, this is an advanced troubleshooting scenario — check CNI pods first, then logs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that the Kubernetes networking model is working correctly? Show a systematic verification approach.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Systematic network verification (5 checks):</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Node status (CNI installed?)</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># All nodes should be Ready</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Pod-to-Pod same node</span>
kubectl run test1 --image=busybox --restart=Never -- sleep 3600
kubectl run test2 --image=busybox --restart=Never -- sleep 3600
<span class="comment"># Force same node with nodeName or check with -o wide</span>
kubectl exec test1 -- wget -qO- http://$(kubectl get pod test2 -o jsonpath='{.status.podIP}')

<span class="comment"># 3. Pod-to-Pod cross node</span>
<span class="comment"># Schedule pods on different nodes and test connectivity</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Pod-to-Service (kube-proxy working?)</span>
kubectl exec test1 -- wget -qO- http://kubernetes.default.svc:443 --no-check-certificate

<span class="comment"># 5. DNS resolution (CoreDNS working?)</span>
kubectl exec test1 -- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Should return 10.96.0.1</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick all-in-one test:</span>
kubectl run netcheck --image=nicolaka/netshoot --rm -it -- sh -c \
"echo '=== DNS ===' && nslookup kubernetes.default && \
echo '=== SVC ===' && curl -sk https://kubernetes.default:443/healthz && \
echo '=== PASS ==='"</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A systematic network check covers all layers: (1) Node-level (CNI installed, nodes Ready), (2) Pod-level (same-node communication), (3) Overlay (cross-node communication), (4) Service (kube-proxy routing), (5) DNS (CoreDNS resolution). On the CKA, if asked to "verify networking," start from the bottom up: nodes → pods → services → DNS. If step 1 fails, nothing else will work. If steps 1-3 work but step 4 fails, it's a kube-proxy issue. If steps 1-4 work but step 5 fails, it's CoreDNS.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 17 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch18">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 18 · Services Deep Dive</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Services are the <strong>stable networking abstraction</strong> that makes Kubernetes applications accessible. While pods come and go with dynamic IPs, a Service provides a <strong>fixed endpoint</strong> — a virtual IP and DNS name — that automatically routes traffic to healthy pods. In this chapter you'll master every Service type, understand what happens under the hood, and learn how pods discover each other using our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django stack throughout.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔗 Services</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ How Services work under the hood — Endpoints, selectors, and kube-proxy integration</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>ClusterIP</strong> — internal-only access (the default and most common type)</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>NodePort</strong> — expose a Service on every node's IP at a static port</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>LoadBalancer</strong> — cloud-provider external load balancers</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>ExternalName</strong> — CNAME-based redirection to external services</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Headless Services</strong> — direct pod DNS without proxy</li>
                    <li>✅ Service Discovery — DNS-based and environment-variable methods</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Services Are Heavily Tested on the CKA</h5>
                <p>Expect 2-3 questions creating/debugging Services. Must-know imperative commands:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl expose deployment easypj --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=ClusterIP</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl expose deployment easypj --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=NodePort</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get svc</code> and <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> — always check both</li>
                    <li>Debug: <code>kubectl describe svc easypj-svc</code> — verify selector, endpoints, ports</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <!-- ─────────── §18.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.1 How Services Work — Under the Hood</h3>
                <p>A Service is an API object that defines a <strong>logical set of pods</strong> (via a label selector) and a policy for accessing them. Here's what happens when you create a Service:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Service Creation — The Full Chain</div>
<pre>
  kubectl apply -f easypj-svc.yaml
           │
           ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │    API Server        │  Stores Service object in etcd
  └─────────┬───────────┘
            │
            ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │  Endpoint Controller │  Watches pods matching selector
  │                      │  Creates Endpoints object:
  │                      │  easypj-svc → [10.244.1.5:8000,
  │                      │                10.244.2.3:8000]
  └─────────┬───────────┘
            │
            ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │    kube-proxy        │  Watches Service + Endpoints
  │    (on every node)   │  Creates iptables/IPVS rules:
  │                      │  10.96.45.2:80 → DNAT → pod IPs
  └─────────┬───────────┘
            │
            ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐
  │      CoreDNS         │  Creates DNS record:
  │                      │  easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local
  │                      │  → 10.96.45.2
  └─────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Anatomy of a Service Object</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏷️</span> spec.selector</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Label selector — determines which pods receive traffic. <code>app: easypj</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📡</span> spec.ports</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>port</code> = Service port (what clients use), <code>targetPort</code> = pod port (where app listens)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> spec.clusterIP</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Virtual IP assigned from Service CIDR (10.96.0.0/12). Immutable once created.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> Endpoints</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Auto-populated list of pod IPs matching the selector. Updated on pod add/remove.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> spec.type</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">ClusterIP (default), NodePort, LoadBalancer, or ExternalName</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Key Concept: Port vs TargetPort</h5>
                    <p><code>port: 80</code> is what <strong>clients connect to</strong> on the Service IP. <code>targetPort: 8000</code> is the <strong>container port</strong> where your app actually listens. These can be different! Your easypj Django app listens on 8000, but clients access <code>http://easypj-svc:80</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.2 ClusterIP Services</h3>
                <p><strong>ClusterIP</strong> is the default and most common Service type. It assigns a virtual IP <strong>only reachable from within the cluster</strong> — pods and nodes can access it, but external clients cannot.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>When to Use</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Internal microservice communication</li>
                            <li>Database access (postgres-svc)</li>
                            <li>Cache layer (redis-svc)</li>
                            <li>Backend APIs not exposed externally</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div>
                        <h5>Limitations</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Not accessible from outside the cluster</li>
                            <li>Cannot be reached from your laptop</li>
                            <li>Need NodePort/LoadBalancer/Ingress for external access</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-clusterip-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-svc
  namespace: default
  labels:
    app: easypj
    tier: web
spec:
  type: ClusterIP          <span class="comment"># Default — can be omitted</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj             <span class="comment"># Must match pod labels!</span>
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80              <span class="comment"># Service port (clients connect here)</span>
      targetPort: 8000      <span class="comment"># Container port (Django listens here)</span>
    - name: admin
      protocol: TCP
      port: 8080            <span class="comment"># Second port — Django admin</span>
      targetPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Imperative ClusterIP Creation &amp; Verification</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ClusterIP service (imperative — fast for CKA)</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj --name=easypj-svc \
  --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=ClusterIP

<span class="comment"># Verify Service created</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME         TYPE        CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-svc  ClusterIP   10.96.45.2    &lt;none&gt;        80/TCP    5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Check endpoints — are pods registered?</span>
kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME         ENDPOINTS                        AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-svc  10.244.1.5:8000,10.244.2.3:8000  5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Test from another pod</span>
kubectl exec -it debug-pod -- curl http://easypj-svc:80
<span class="comment"># HTTP 200 — Django responds!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Default Behavior</th>
                                <th>Configuration</th>
                                <th>When to Use</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Session Affinity</strong></td>
                                <td>None — random pod each request</td>
                                <td><code>spec.sessionAffinity: ClientIP</code></td>
                                <td>Sticky sessions, WebSocket apps</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Session Timeout</strong></td>
                                <td>10800s (3 hours)</td>
                                <td><code>spec.sessionAffinityConfig.clientIP.timeoutSeconds: 3600</code></td>
                                <td>Adjust affinity window</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Multi-Port</strong></td>
                                <td>Single port</td>
                                <td>Multiple entries in <code>spec.ports[]</code> (each needs a <code>name</code>)</td>
                                <td>App + metrics, HTTP + HTTPS</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Session Affinity — Sticky Sessions</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># All requests from the same client IP go to the same pod</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-sticky
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  sessionAffinity: ClientIP      # ← Enable sticky sessions
  sessionAffinityConfig:
    clientIP:
      timeoutSeconds: 3600       <span class="comment"># 1 hour affinity window</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.3 NodePort Services</h3>
                <p><strong>NodePort</strong> exposes the Service on a static port on <strong>every node's IP</strong> in the cluster. External clients can access the Service via <code>&lt;NodeIP&gt;:&lt;NodePort&gt;</code>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">NodePort — How External Traffic Reaches Pods</div>
<pre>
  External client: http://192.168.1.10:30080

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                 │
  │  External Request → ANY node at port 30080                     │
  │                                                                 │
  │  ┌── Node 1 ──────────────┐    ┌── Node 2 ──────────────┐     │
  │  │  192.168.1.10:30080    │    │  192.168.1.11:30080    │     │
  │  │         │              │    │         │              │     │
  │  │         ▼ kube-proxy   │    │         ▼ kube-proxy   │     │
  │  │  ┌─────────────────┐   │    │  ┌─────────────────┐   │     │
  │  │  │ iptables/IPVS   │   │    │  │ iptables/IPVS   │   │     │
  │  │  │ DNAT to pod     │   │    │  │ DNAT to pod     │   │     │
  │  │  └────────┬────────┘   │    │  └────────┬────────┘   │     │
  │  │           │            │    │           │            │     │
  │  │  ┌────────▼─────────┐  │    │  ┌────────▼─────────┐  │     │
  │  │  │ easypj-pod-abc   │  │    │  │ easypj-pod-def   │  │     │
  │  │  │ 10.244.1.5:8000  │  │    │  │ 10.244.2.3:8000  │  │     │
  │  │  └──────────────────┘  │    │  └──────────────────┘  │     │
  │  └────────────────────────┘    └────────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                                 │
  │  Note: Request to Node 1:30080 can be routed to pod on Node 2  │
  │  NodePort range: 30000 - 32767 (configurable in API server)    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-nodeport-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-nodeport
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80              <span class="comment"># ClusterIP port (internal)</span>
      targetPort: 8000      <span class="comment"># Container port</span>
      nodePort: 30080       <span class="comment"># External port on every node</span>
                            <span class="comment"># Range: 30000-32767</span>
                            <span class="comment"># Omit to auto-assign</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Working with NodePort Services</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create NodePort (imperative)</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj --name=easypj-nodeport \
  --port=80 --target-port=8000 --type=NodePort

<span class="comment"># Service gets BOTH a ClusterIP AND a NodePort</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-nodeport
<span class="comment"># NAME              TYPE       CLUSTER-IP    PORT(S)        AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-nodeport  NodePort   10.96.80.12   80:31245/TCP   5s</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            ↑    ↑</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            |    └ NodePort (auto-assigned)</span>
<span class="comment">#                                            └ ClusterIP port</span>

<span class="comment"># Access from outside the cluster</span>
curl http://192.168.1.10:31245     <span class="comment"># via Node 1</span>
curl http://192.168.1.11:31245     <span class="comment"># via Node 2 — same result!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ NodePort Limitations</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Only 1 Service per port (limited range 30000-32767)</li>
                        <li>No TLS termination — clients use HTTP</li>
                        <li>Clients must know node IPs — no load balancing across nodes</li>
                        <li>Not suitable for production — use <strong>LoadBalancer</strong> or <strong>Ingress</strong> instead</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>externalTrafficPolicy</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Client Source IP</th>
                                <th>Trade-off</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Cluster</strong> (default)</td>
                                <td>Traffic can route to pods on <em>any</em> node — extra hop if pod is on a different node</td>
                                <td>❌ SNAT'd — original IP lost</td>
                                <td>Better load distribution, but loses client IP</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Local</strong></td>
                                <td>Traffic only routes to pods on the <em>same node</em> it arrived on</td>
                                <td>✅ Preserved — see real client IP</td>
                                <td>May have uneven load if pods aren't on every node</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Preserving Client Source IP</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Important for logging, rate limiting, geo-routing</span>
spec:
  type: NodePort
  externalTrafficPolicy: Local    # ← Preserves client IP
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8000
      nodePort: 30080</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.4 LoadBalancer Services</h3>
                <p><strong>LoadBalancer</strong> is the standard way to expose Services to the internet in <strong>cloud environments</strong> (AWS, GCP, Azure). It provisions an actual external load balancer that distributes traffic across your nodes.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">LoadBalancer Service — Cloud Integration</div>
<pre>
  Internet user: http://203.0.113.50:80

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐                    │
  │  │  Cloud Load Balancer (AWS ELB / GCP LB) │                    │
  │  │  External IP: 203.0.113.50              │                    │
  │  │  Provisioned automatically by K8s       │                    │
  │  └────────────┬────────────────┬───────────┘                    │
  │               │                │                                 │
  │     ┌─────────▼────┐    ┌─────▼────────┐                       │
  │     │ Node 1:30080 │    │ Node 2:30080 │   (NodePort used      │
  │     │ (kube-proxy) │    │ (kube-proxy) │    under the hood)    │
  │     └──────┬───────┘    └────┬─────────┘                       │
  │            │                 │                                   │
  │     ┌──────▼──────────┐  ┌──▼──────────────┐                   │
  │     │ easypj pod-abc  │  │ easypj pod-def  │                   │
  │     │ 10.244.1.5:8000 │  │ 10.244.2.3:8000 │                   │
  │     └─────────────────┘  └─────────────────┘                   │
  │                                                                  │
  │  LoadBalancer = ClusterIP + NodePort + External LB              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-loadbalancer-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-lb
  annotations:
    <span class="comment"># Cloud-specific annotations</span>
    service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type: nlb   <span class="comment"># AWS NLB</span>
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8000
  <span class="comment"># Optional: restrict external access by IP</span>
  loadBalancerSourceRanges:
    - 10.0.0.0/8
    - 203.0.113.0/24</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Working with LoadBalancer Services</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create LoadBalancer service</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-loadbalancer-svc.yaml

<span class="comment"># Check — EXTERNAL-IP may show &lt;pending&gt; initially</span>
kubectl get svc easypj-lb -w
<span class="comment"># NAME        TYPE           CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP     PORT(S)        AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-lb  LoadBalancer   10.96.90.1    &lt;pending&gt;       80:31890/TCP   5s</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-lb  LoadBalancer   10.96.90.1    203.0.113.50    80:31890/TCP   30s</span>

<span class="comment"># Access from the internet</span>
curl http://203.0.113.50:80
<span class="comment"># Django easypj responds!</span>

<span class="comment"># On bare metal without cloud LB — use MetalLB</span>
<span class="comment"># EXTERNAL-IP stays &lt;pending&gt; forever without a LB controller</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Bare Metal: Use MetalLB</h5>
                    <p>On-premises clusters have no cloud LB provider. <strong>MetalLB</strong> fills this gap by assigning real external IPs from a configured pool. Often used in CKA lab environments and homelab setups.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>EndpointSlices — The Modern Endpoints</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> Endpoints (legacy)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Single object listing ALL pod IPs for a Service. Becomes huge in large clusters — every pod change updates the entire object.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📦</span> EndpointSlices (v1)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Splits endpoints into chunks of ~100 pods each. Only the affected slice is updated — <strong>much more efficient</strong> at scale.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> View them</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=easypj-svc</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">✅</span> Default since</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Kubernetes 1.21+. Automatically created for every Service. <code>Endpoints</code> still exist for backward compatibility.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.5 ExternalName Services</h3>
                <p><strong>ExternalName</strong> is a special Service type that maps a Service name to an <strong>external DNS name</strong> via a CNAME record. No proxying, no ClusterIP — just DNS redirection.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔀</div>
                        <h5>How It Works</h5>
                        <p>When a pod resolves <code>external-db</code>, CoreDNS returns a CNAME pointing to the external hostname. Traffic goes directly to the external endpoint — kube-proxy is not involved.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Use Cases</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Point to an external database (RDS, Cloud SQL)</li>
                            <li>Migrate from external to internal service gradually</li>
                            <li>Abstract external dependencies behind K8s DNS</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">external-db-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: external-db
  namespace: default
spec:
  type: ExternalName
  externalName: mydb.example.com    <span class="comment"># External DNS name</span>
  <span class="comment"># No selector!</span>
  <span class="comment"># No ClusterIP!</span>
  <span class="comment"># No ports!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Using ExternalName Services</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From inside a pod:</span>
nslookup external-db
<span class="comment"># external-db.default.svc.cluster.local → CNAME → mydb.example.com</span>

<span class="comment"># Django settings.py can just use:</span>
<span class="comment"># DATABASES = { 'HOST': 'external-db' }</span>
<span class="comment"># If you later migrate to an internal postgres pod, just</span>
<span class="comment"># delete the ExternalName service and create a ClusterIP service</span>
<span class="comment"># with the same name — zero code changes!</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.6 Headless Services</h3>
                <p>A <strong>Headless Service</strong> is a ClusterIP Service with <code>clusterIP: None</code>. Instead of a single virtual IP, DNS resolution returns the <strong>individual pod IPs directly</strong>. This is essential for <strong>StatefulSets</strong> where clients need to reach specific pods.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Headless vs Normal Service — DNS Comparison</div>
<pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                    │
  │  NORMAL Service (ClusterIP: 10.96.45.2)                          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  nslookup easypj-svc                                     │    │
  │  │  → 10.96.45.2  (single ClusterIP — kube-proxy routes)   │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                    │
  │  HEADLESS Service (clusterIP: None)                               │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  nslookup postgres-headless                               │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.1.15  (postgres-0)                             │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.2.18  (postgres-1)                             │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.3.22  (postgres-2)                             │    │
  │  │                                                           │    │
  │  │  PLUS individual pod DNS:                                 │    │
  │  │  postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local  │    │
  │  │  → 10.244.1.15                                           │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
  │                                                                    │
  │  Use headless when you need to reach SPECIFIC pods               │
  │  (StatefulSets, database replicas, leader election)              │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">postgres-headless-svc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres-headless
spec:
  clusterIP: None            # ← This makes it headless!
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
    - name: postgres
      port: 5432
      targetPort: 5432
---
<span class="comment"># Used with StatefulSet</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: postgres
spec:
  serviceName: postgres-headless    # ← Must reference headless service
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: postgres
          image: postgres:15
          ports:
            - containerPort: 5432</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Using Headless Service DNS</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># DNS resolves to ALL pod IPs</span>
kubectl exec debug-pod -- nslookup postgres-headless
<span class="comment"># Name: postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.18</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.3.22</span>

<span class="comment"># Access specific pod by ordinal name</span>
kubectl exec debug-pod -- nslookup postgres-0.postgres-headless
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15    ← always the primary!</span>

<span class="comment"># Django connects to primary:</span>
<span class="comment"># DATABASES = { 'HOST': 'postgres-0.postgres-headless' }</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §18.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s18-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">18.7 Service Discovery — How Pods Find Services</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes provides <strong>two built-in methods</strong> for pods to discover Services. DNS is the primary method used in practice.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Method</th>
                                <th>How It Works</th>
                                <th>Pros</th>
                                <th>Cons</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>DNS (Primary)</strong></td>
                                <td>CoreDNS creates records: <code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>Always current, discoverable by name, works across namespaces</td>
                                <td>Requires CoreDNS running</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Environment Variables</strong></td>
                                <td>kubelet injects <code>SVC_NAME_SERVICE_HOST</code> and <code>SVC_NAME_SERVICE_PORT</code> into pods</td>
                                <td>Works without DNS, simple</td>
                                <td>Only services created BEFORE the pod; ordering-dependent</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Service Discovery in Action</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: DNS — the recommended way</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- nslookup easypj-svc
<span class="comment"># Name:   easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.45.2</span>

<span class="comment"># Short names work within the same namespace:</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- curl http://easypj-svc:80       # ✅ same namespace
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- curl http://easypj-svc.prod:80  # ✅ "prod" namespace
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- curl http://easypj-svc.prod.svc.cluster.local:80  # ✅ FQDN

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Environment variables</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- env | grep ANIHPJ_SVC
<span class="comment"># ANIHPJ_SVC_SERVICE_HOST=10.96.45.2</span>
<span class="comment"># ANIHPJ_SVC_SERVICE_PORT=80</span>
<span class="comment"># ANIHPJ_SVC_SERVICE_PORT_HTTP=80</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">DNS Name Resolution Hierarchy</div>
<pre>
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  DNS Resolution for Services                                     │
  │                                                                   │
  │  Short name:     easypj-svc                                      │
  │                  ↓ (search domain appended)                      │
  │  Within NS:      easypj-svc.default                              │
  │                  ↓                                                │
  │  Cluster FQDN:   easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local           │
  │                  ↓                                                │
  │  Resolves to:    10.96.45.2 (ClusterIP)                          │
  │                                                                   │
  │  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════          │
  │                                                                   │
  │  Cross-namespace: easypj-svc.prod                                │
  │                   ↓                                               │
  │  Cluster FQDN:    easypj-svc.prod.svc.cluster.local             │
  │                   ↓                                               │
  │  Resolves to:     10.96.88.5 (prod namespace Service)            │
  │                                                                   │
  │  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════          │
  │                                                                   │
  │  Pod DNS resolv.conf (auto-configured):                          │
  │  nameserver 10.96.0.10  (CoreDNS ClusterIP)                     │
  │  search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local│
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How kube-proxy Programs iptables/IPVS for Service Load Balancing: Complete Packet Flow from Client Pod to Backend Pod via ClusterIP</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Service Traffic Flow — From Client to Backend via ClusterIP</h5>
            <p>When a pod sends traffic to a Service ClusterIP (virtual IP), kube-proxy's iptables rules redirect it to a real pod IP. This is NOT a proxy — the kernel does it at wire speed with netfilter rules.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SERVICE TRAFFIC FLOW — Complete Packet Path (iptables mode)                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Client Pod (10.244.1.5) wants to reach easypj-web-svc (10.96.45.2:80)      │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: DNS Resolution                                                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Pod queries CoreDNS: easypj-web-svc.default.svc.cluster.local       │    │
│  │ CoreDNS returns: 10.96.45.2 (ClusterIP — VIRTUAL, not a real pod)  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Pod sends TCP SYN to 10.96.45.2:80                                 │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Source: 10.244.1.5:45678  →  Destination: 10.96.45.2:80             │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: Packet hits OUTPUT chain, then PREROUTING (node netns)              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ iptables NAT table rules (created by kube-proxy):                    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Match traffic to Service ClusterIP                                 │</span>    │
│  │ -A KUBE-SERVICES -d 10.96.45.2/32 -p tcp --dport 80 \              │    │
│  │   -j KUBE-SVC-XXXX                                                    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Load balance across 3 backend pods (random):                       │</span>    │
│  │ -A KUBE-SVC-XXXX -m statistic --mode random --probability 0.33333 \ │    │
│  │   -j KUBE-SEP-10.244.1.10                                            │    │
│  │ -A KUBE-SVC-XXXX -m statistic --mode random --probability 0.50000 \ │    │
│  │   -j KUBE-SEP-10.244.2.11                                            │    │
│  │ -A KUBE-SVC-XXXX -j KUBE-SEP-10.244.3.12                             │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ <span class="comment"># DNAT to selected backend pod:                                      │</span>    │
│  │ -A KUBE-SEP-10.244.1.10 -p tcp -j DNAT --to-destination 10.244.1.10:8000│   │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 4: Packet is DNATed → destination becomes 10.244.1.10:8000            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Source: 10.244.1.5:45678  →  Destination: 10.244.1.10:8000 (REAL pod)│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 5: Kernel routes to backend pod (CNI routing)                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Pod receives packet. Source is REAL client IP (10.244.1.5).          │    │
│  │ Pod responds: Source 10.244.1.10:8000 → Dest 10.244.1.5:45678      │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 6: Return packet hits conntrack (stateful NAT)                         │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ conntrack remembers: "10.244.1.5→10.96.45.2 was DNATed to 10.244.1.10"│    │
│  │ Automatically UN-DNATs the return packet:                            │    │
│  │ Source: 10.96.45.2:80 → Dest: 10.244.1.5:45678  (client sees ServiceIP)│   │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  KUBE-PROXY IS NOT A PROXY:                                                  │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ kube-proxy does NOT sit in the data path. It only WRITES iptables   │    │
│  │ rules. The kernel processes every packet at wire speed.              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ PROOF: Even if kube-proxy pod DIES, existing iptables rules remain  │    │
│  │ and traffic continues flowing. New Services won't get rules until   │    │
│  │ kube-proxy restarts, but existing Services are unaffected.          │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  SERVICE TYPES — Internal DNS vs External Access:                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ ClusterIP:    Virtual IP (10.96.x.x), internal only, DNS resolves   │    │
│  │               to ClusterIP. kube-proxy DNATs to pod IPs.            │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ NodePort:     ClusterIP + opens port 30000-32767 on EVERY node.     │    │
│  │               External traffic: &lt;any-node-ip&gt;:&lt;nodeport&gt; → ClusterIP│    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ LoadBalancer: NodePort + cloud provider creates external LB.        │    │
│  │               &lt;lb-ip&gt;:80 → &lt;node&gt;:&lt;nodeport&gt; → ClusterIP → Pod     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ ExternalName: DNS CNAME only. No proxy, no virtual IP.              │    │
│  │               easypj-db → rds.aws.com (external database)          │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> "Service not working" on the exam = check 3 things: (1) <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — if ENDPOINTS is empty, the selector doesn't match any pods. (2) <code>kubectl describe svc &lt;name&gt;</code> — check selector and ports match pod labels and container ports. (3) <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code> — do pod labels match the Service selector? Missing labels = no endpoints = no traffic.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>ClusterIP vs NodePort vs LoadBalancer</strong> — ClusterIP = internal only. NodePort = external via <code>&lt;NodeIP&gt;:30000-32767</code>. LoadBalancer = cloud only (won't work on kubeadm clusters without MetalLB)</li>
                    <li><strong>Empty endpoints = selector mismatch</strong> — If <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> shows no IPs, the Service selector doesn't match any pod labels. This is the #1 service debugging scenario on the exam</li>
                    <li><strong>targetPort vs port</strong> — <code>port</code> is what clients connect to (the Service port). <code>targetPort</code> is where traffic goes (the container port). If your app listens on 8000, <code>targetPort: 8000</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Headless Service</strong> — <code>clusterIP: None</code> — no virtual IP, DNS returns individual pod IPs. Required for StatefulSets. The exam tests this</li>
                    <li><strong>ExternalName Service</strong> — Returns a CNAME record. No proxying. Used for external database references. Rarely tested but know it exists</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Service troubleshooting flowchart:</strong><br>
                1. <code>kubectl get svc</code> — does the service exist?<br>
                2. <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — are there backend IPs?<br>
                3. If no endpoints: compare <code>kubectl get svc &lt;svc&gt; -o yaml | grep selector</code> vs <code>kubectl get pods --show-labels</code><br>
                4. If endpoints exist but no traffic: check kube-proxy logs and NetworkPolicies</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 18)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Service Type</th>
                            <th>ClusterIP</th>
                            <th>External Access</th>
                            <th>Use Case</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong></td><td>✅ Virtual IP</td><td>❌ Internal only</td><td>Pod-to-pod communication</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td>✅ Virtual IP</td><td>✅ via NodeIP:Port</td><td>Dev/test, simple external access</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td><td>✅ Virtual IP</td><td>✅ via cloud LB</td><td>Production external access</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ExternalName</strong></td><td>❌ None</td><td>N/A (CNAME only)</td><td>External service aliasing</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Headless</strong></td><td>❌ None</td><td>❌ Internal only</td><td>StatefulSets, direct pod access</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 18 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch18-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 18 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Service types &amp; routing — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ClusterIP Service that exposes a Deployment <code>webapp</code> on port 80, targeting container port 8080. Show the imperative and YAML approach.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Imperative (fastest on CKA):</span>
kubectl expose deployment webapp \
--port=80 --target-port=8080 \
--type=ClusterIP --name=webapp-svc

<span class="comment"># YAML equivalent:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: webapp-svc
spec:
type: ClusterIP        <span class="comment"># Default — internal only</span>
selector:
app: webapp           <span class="comment"># Must match Deployment pod labels!</span>
ports:
- port: 80             <span class="comment"># Service port (clients connect here)</span>
targetPort: 8080     <span class="comment"># Container port (traffic forwarded here)</span>
protocol: TCP

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME        TYPE       CLUSTER-IP    PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-svc  ClusterIP  10.96.45.23   80/TCP</span>

kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME        ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-svc  10.244.1.5:8080,10.244.2.8:8080,10.244.1.9:8080</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl expose</code> creates a Service using the Deployment's pod labels as the selector. <code>port</code> is what clients use (Service's listening port), <code>targetPort</code> is where traffic is forwarded (container's port). If <code>targetPort</code> is omitted, it defaults to the same value as <code>port</code>. On the CKA, always check <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> after creating a Service — if it shows no endpoints, the selector doesn't match any pod labels. This is the #1 Service debugging scenario.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the 4 Service types in Kubernetes? Explain each with a use case.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Type</th>
                                        <th>Access</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #22c55e;">ClusterIP</td>
                                        <td>Internal only (default)</td>
                                        <td>Backend services, databases</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #3b82f6;">NodePort</td>
                                        <td>External via &lt;NodeIP&gt;:30000-32767</td>
                                        <td>Dev/test, bare-metal access</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #a855f7;">LoadBalancer</td>
                                        <td>Cloud load balancer + external IP</td>
                                        <td>Production cloud deployments</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; color: #eab308;">ExternalName</td>
                                        <td>CNAME DNS redirect</td>
                                        <td>External service aliases</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># NodePort example:</span>
kubectl expose deployment webapp --type=NodePort \
--port=80 --target-port=8080
<span class="comment"># Access: curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:&lt;assigned-nodeport&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># LoadBalancer (cloud only):</span>
kubectl expose deployment webapp --type=LoadBalancer \
--port=80 --target-port=8080
<span class="comment"># Access: curl http://&lt;external-ip&gt;:80</span>

<span class="comment"># ExternalName:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: external-db
spec:
type: ExternalName
externalName: db.example.com  <span class="comment"># CNAME record</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Each type builds on the previous: <code>NodePort</code> includes a <code>ClusterIP</code>, <code>LoadBalancer</code> includes a <code>NodePort</code> + <code>ClusterIP</code>. <code>ExternalName</code> is different — it just returns a CNAME record (no proxying, no ClusterIP). On the CKA, <code>LoadBalancer</code> won't get an external IP on kubeadm clusters (stays <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code>) — only works on cloud providers or with MetalLB. NodePort range is always 30000-32767. Know these boundaries and which type to use in each scenario.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Service has no endpoints. <code>kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc</code> shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>. How do you diagnose and fix this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check the Service selector</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-svc -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector}'
<span class="comment"># {"app":"webapp"}</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check pod labels</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  LABELS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc    1/1    app=web         ← Mismatch! "web" ≠ "webapp"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Fix — either change the Service selector or pod labels</span>

<span class="comment"># Option A: Fix the Service selector</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"web"}}}'

<span class="comment"># Option B: Fix the pod labels</span>
kubectl label pod web-abc app=webapp --overwrite

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify endpoints appear</span>
kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME        ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># webapp-svc  10.244.1.5:8080</span>

<span class="comment"># Common causes of empty endpoints:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Selector doesn't match pod labels (most common!)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. No pods exist with those labels</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Pods exist but aren't Ready (failed readinessProbe)</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Pods are in a different namespace than the Service</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Empty endpoints = the Service selector doesn't match any pod labels. This is the #1 Service debugging scenario on the CKA exam. The fix is always: compare <code>service.spec.selector</code> with <code>pod.metadata.labels</code>. They must match exactly (key AND value). Also check: pods must be <code>Ready</code> (passing readinessProbe) AND in the same namespace. A pod that exists but fails its readiness probe won't appear in endpoints either. On the CKA, this 3-step pattern solves most Service issues: (1) get svc selector, (2) get pod labels, (3) compare.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a headless Service? How is it different from a regular ClusterIP Service and when do you need one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Headless Service: clusterIP: None</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: db-headless
spec:
clusterIP: None        # ← No virtual IP assigned
selector:
app: postgres
ports:
- port: 5432

<span class="comment"># Regular ClusterIP:</span>
<span class="comment"># DNS lookup → returns the Service's virtual IP (10.96.x.x)</span>
<span class="comment"># nslookup db-svc → 10.96.0.50</span>

<span class="comment"># Headless Service:</span>
<span class="comment"># DNS lookup → returns individual pod IPs directly</span>
<span class="comment"># nslookup db-headless → 10.244.1.5, 10.244.2.8</span>
<span class="comment"># (one IP per pod matching the selector)</span>

<span class="comment"># Use cases for headless Services:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. StatefulSets (each pod needs its own DNS name)</span>
<span class="comment">#    web-0.db-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment">#    web-1.db-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Client-side load balancing (app picks which pod)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Service discovery without proxying</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A headless Service has no ClusterIP — DNS returns pod IPs directly instead of a single virtual IP. This is required for StatefulSets because each pod needs its own stable DNS name (e.g., <code>postgres-0.db-headless.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>). Without a headless Service, StatefulSets can't provide stable network identities. On the CKA, the key signals for headless: (1) question mentions StatefulSet, (2) "individual pod DNS names needed," (3) "client-side load balancing." Set <code>clusterIP: None</code> — that's the only difference from a normal ClusterIP Service.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Expose a pod named <code>nginx-pod</code> as a NodePort Service on port 30080. Show the command and verify external access.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create NodePort with specific port</span>
kubectl expose pod nginx-pod --port=80 --type=NodePort \
--name=nginx-nodeport

<span class="comment"># The nodePort is randomly assigned (30000-32767)</span>
<span class="comment"># To set a specific nodePort, use YAML:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: nginx-nodeport
spec:
type: NodePort
selector:
run: nginx-pod    <span class="comment"># Match the pod's labels</span>
ports:
- port: 80
targetPort: 80
nodePort: 30080   # ← Specific port

kubectl apply -f nodeport-svc.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc nginx-nodeport
<span class="comment"># NAME             TYPE      CLUSTER-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># nginx-nodeport   NodePort  10.96.15.8   80:30080/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Test access from ANY node's IP:</span>
curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:30080
<span class="comment"># Welcome to nginx!</span>

<span class="comment"># Works on ALL nodes, even nodes where nginx-pod isn't running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy routes the traffic to the correct node</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>NodePort exposes the Service on a static port on every node. You can access it via <code>&lt;any-node-IP&gt;:&lt;nodePort&gt;</code>. kube-proxy ensures traffic reaches the correct pod regardless of which node you hit. On the CKA, use <code>kubectl expose --type=NodePort</code> for quick creation, but you'll need YAML to specify a custom <code>nodePort</code>. The <code>nodePort</code> field must be in the 30000-32767 range. Note: NodePort also creates a ClusterIP — so the Service is accessible both internally (via ClusterIP) and externally (via NodePort).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to Service endpoints when a pod fails its readiness probe? How is this different from a liveness probe failure?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Readiness probe failure:</span>
<span class="comment"># → Pod is REMOVED from Service endpoints</span>
<span class="comment"># → No traffic is routed to it</span>
<span class="comment"># → Pod keeps running (not restarted)</span>
<span class="comment"># → When probe passes again, pod is re-added to endpoints</span>

<span class="comment"># Liveness probe failure:</span>
<span class="comment"># → Pod is RESTARTED (killed and recreated)</span>
<span class="comment"># → During restart, it's also removed from endpoints</span>
<span class="comment"># → After restart, if readiness passes, re-added to endpoints</span>

<span class="comment"># Combined behavior:</span>
spec:
containers:
- name: app
readinessProbe:     <span class="comment"># Controls endpoint membership</span>
  httpGet:
    path: /ready
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 5
livenessProbe:      <span class="comment"># Controls pod lifecycle</span>
  httpGet:
    path: /healthz
    port: 8080
  periodSeconds: 10

<span class="comment"># Watch endpoints change in real-time:</span>
kubectl get endpoints webapp-svc -w
<span class="comment"># ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.5:8080,10.244.2.8:8080  (2 healthy pods)</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.5:8080                   (pod 2 failed readiness)</span>
<span class="comment"># 10.244.1.5:8080,10.244.2.8:8080  (pod 2 recovered)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Readiness probes control traffic routing: failing = removed from endpoints (no traffic). Liveness probes control pod lifecycle: failing = pod restarted. A pod can fail readiness but pass liveness — it stays running but receives no traffic (useful for warm-up periods). On the CKA, if a Service intermittently doesn't reach a pod, check its readiness probe. If a pod keeps restarting, check its liveness probe. Best practice: use BOTH — readiness for traffic gating, liveness for crash recovery.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you change an existing Service from ClusterIP to NodePort without deleting it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl edit</span>
kubectl edit svc webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># Change spec.type from ClusterIP to NodePort</span>
<span class="comment"># Save — a nodePort is auto-assigned</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: kubectl patch</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
-p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort"}}'

<span class="comment"># Method 3: With specific nodePort</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
-p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort","ports":[{"port":80,"nodePort":30080}]}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify the change:</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-svc
<span class="comment"># TYPE      CLUSTER-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># NodePort  10.96.45.23  80:30080/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Note: Going FROM NodePort TO ClusterIP requires removing nodePort:</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
--type='json' \
-p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/ports/0/nodePort"},
   {"op":"replace","path":"/spec/type","value":"ClusterIP"}]'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>You CAN change Service types in-place without deleting. Going ClusterIP → NodePort is straightforward. Going NodePort → ClusterIP requires removing the <code>nodePort</code> field first. Going to/from LoadBalancer depends on the cloud provider. On the CKA, <code>kubectl patch</code> is faster than <code>kubectl edit</code> for type changes. The ClusterIP is preserved during the change, so internal consumers aren't affected. Only the external access method changes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does Kubernetes discover Services? What are the two discovery mechanisms?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Mechanism 1: Environment Variables (legacy)</span>
<span class="comment"># Every pod gets env vars for each Service in the SAME namespace</span>
kubectl exec webapp-pod -- env | grep -i redis
<span class="comment"># REDIS_SVC_SERVICE_HOST=10.96.78.12</span>
<span class="comment"># REDIS_SVC_SERVICE_PORT=6379</span>
<span class="comment"># REDIS_SVC_PORT=tcp://10.96.78.12:6379</span>

<span class="comment"># Limitation: Service must exist BEFORE the pod starts!</span>
<span class="comment"># Pods don't get env vars for Services created after them</span>

<span class="comment"># Mechanism 2: DNS (preferred, always works)</span>
kubectl exec webapp-pod -- nslookup redis-svc
<span class="comment"># Server: 10.96.0.10 (CoreDNS)</span>
<span class="comment"># Name: redis-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.78.12</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS formats:</span>
<span class="comment"># Same namespace:     redis-svc</span>
<span class="comment"># Cross namespace:    redis-svc.other-ns</span>
<span class="comment"># Full FQDN:          redis-svc.other-ns.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># DNS always works regardless of creation order</span>
<span class="comment"># This is why DNS is the recommended discovery method</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes provides two discovery mechanisms: environment variables (injected at pod start) and DNS (via CoreDNS, always available). DNS is preferred because: (1) it works regardless of creation order, (2) it works cross-namespace, (3) it's dynamic (reflects Service changes). Environment variables only contain Services that existed when the pod started. On the CKA, always use DNS names (<code>redis-svc</code>) instead of ClusterIPs or environment variables. For cross-namespace access, use <code>svc-name.namespace</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Service that exposes multiple ports (HTTP 80 and HTTPS 443) for a single Deployment.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: webapp-multi-port
spec:
selector:
app: webapp
ports:
- name: http          <span class="comment"># Name is REQUIRED for multi-port</span>
port: 80
targetPort: 8080
protocol: TCP
- name: https         <span class="comment"># Name is REQUIRED for multi-port</span>
port: 443
targetPort: 8443
protocol: TCP

<span class="comment"># CRITICAL: When a Service has multiple ports,</span>
<span class="comment"># each port MUST have a unique "name" field!</span>
<span class="comment"># Without names, the API rejects the Service</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc webapp-multi-port
<span class="comment"># PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># 80/TCP,443/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Test each port:</span>
kubectl exec test -- curl http://webapp-multi-port:80
kubectl exec test -- curl -k https://webapp-multi-port:443</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Multi-port Services are common in production — web servers often serve both HTTP and HTTPS. The key CKA gotcha: when defining multiple ports, each MUST have a unique <code>name</code> field — otherwise the API server rejects the definition. Single-port Services don't require a name (it's optional). On the CKA, if you're asked to expose both ports, remember: (1) add <code>name</code> to each port, (2) ensure <code>targetPort</code> matches the container's actual listening ports, (3) names must be lowercase alphanumeric with hyphens.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>sessionAffinity</code> in a Service? How do you configure it so that all requests from the same client go to the same pod?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Default: No session affinity (round-robin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Requests from same client may hit different pods</span>

<span class="comment"># Enable session affinity (sticky sessions):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: webapp-svc
spec:
selector:
app: webapp
sessionAffinity: ClientIP      # ← Sticky by client IP
sessionAffinityConfig:
clientIP:
  timeoutSeconds: 10800      # ← Sticky for 3 hours (default)
ports:
- port: 80
targetPort: 8080

<span class="comment"># Or via kubectl patch:</span>
kubectl patch svc webapp-svc \
-p '{"spec":{"sessionAffinity":"ClientIP"}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl describe svc webapp-svc | grep -i session
<span class="comment"># Session Affinity: ClientIP</span>

<span class="comment"># Now all requests from the same source IP</span>
<span class="comment"># go to the same backend pod for 3 hours</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>sessionAffinity: ClientIP</code> makes the Service route all requests from the same client IP to the same backend pod. This is useful for applications with server-side sessions (though stateless apps are preferred). The only option Kubernetes supports is <code>ClientIP</code> (no cookie-based affinity at the Service level — use Ingress for that). On the CKA, session affinity is rarely tested directly, but understanding it helps debug "inconsistent responses" where different pods have different state.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create an <code>ExternalName</code> Service that maps <code>external-db</code> to <code>db.prod.example.com</code>. How does it differ from other Service types? When would you use it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create ExternalName Service</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: external-db
namespace: default
spec:
type: ExternalName
externalName: db.prod.example.com   # ← FQDN, not an IP!

<span class="comment"># Or imperatively:</span>
kubectl create service externalname external-db \
--external-name=db.prod.example.com

<span class="comment"># How it works:</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod does DNS lookup for "external-db" or</span>
<span class="comment">#   "external-db.default.svc.cluster.local"</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS returns a CNAME record → db.prod.example.com</span>
<span class="comment"># No proxy, no ClusterIP, no Endpoints — just DNS aliasing</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get svc external-db
<span class="comment"># NAME          TYPE           CLUSTER-IP   EXTERNAL-IP             PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># external-db   ExternalName   &lt;none&gt;       db.prod.example.com     &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Key differences from other Service types:</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ No ClusterIP assigned</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ No Endpoints object created</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ No label selector — doesn't match any pods</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Cannot use with IP addresses (must be DNS name)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Works purely at DNS level (CNAME redirect)</span>

<span class="comment"># Use case: point to external databases, APIs, or</span>
<span class="comment"># services in other namespaces without hardcoding URLs</span>

<span class="comment"># Cross-namespace alias example:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
name: postgres
namespace: dev
spec:
type: ExternalName
externalName: postgres-svc.production.svc.cluster.local</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>ExternalName</code> is the simplest Service type — it creates a DNS CNAME alias with no proxying. When a pod resolves <code>external-db</code>, CoreDNS returns a CNAME pointing to the external FQDN. No ClusterIP, no Endpoints, no kube-proxy rules. It's used to reference external services (databases, APIs) or services in other namespaces by a local name. Two key limitations: (1) it only works with DNS names, not IP addresses, (2) it doesn't support ports — the client must know the target port. On the CKA, know that ExternalName is the only Service type that creates no Endpoints and has no ClusterIP.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 18 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch19">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 19 · DNS &amp; CoreDNS</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">DNS is the <strong>service discovery backbone</strong> of Kubernetes. Every time your easypj pod connects to <code>postgres-svc</code>, a DNS query resolves that name to a ClusterIP. <strong>CoreDNS</strong> is the cluster DNS server that handles all of this — and knowing how to configure and troubleshoot it is a core CKA skill.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔍 DNS</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ How Kubernetes DNS works end-to-end — from pod query to CoreDNS response</li>
                    <li>✅ CoreDNS architecture, Corefile configuration, and plugins</li>
                    <li>✅ Every type of DNS record Kubernetes creates (A, AAAA, SRV, CNAME)</li>
                    <li>✅ Systematic DNS troubleshooting — the #1 exam debugging skill</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Services &amp; Networking <span class="cka-weight">20%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>DNS Questions Appear on Nearly Every CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>CoreDNS troubleshooting is one of the most tested topics. Memorize these:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>CoreDNS pods: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></li>
                    <li>CoreDNS ConfigMap: <code>kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml</code></li>
                    <li>Test DNS: <code>kubectl exec POD -- nslookup SERVICE_NAME</code></li>
                    <li>Full FQDN format: <code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <!-- ─────────── §19.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.1 How Kubernetes DNS Works</h3>
                <p>Every pod in Kubernetes is automatically configured to use the cluster DNS server (CoreDNS). Here's the complete resolution flow:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">DNS Resolution Flow — End to End</div>
<pre>
  easypj pod runs: curl http://postgres-svc:5432

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 1: Pod checks /etc/resolv.conf (auto-configured by kubelet)   │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  nameserver 10.96.0.10                                        │ │
  │  │  search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local           │ │
  │  │         cluster.local                                          │ │
  │  │  ndots:5                                                       │ │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 2: "postgres-svc" has fewer than 5 dots → search path used   │
  │          → Queries: postgres-svc.default.svc.cluster.local          │
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 3: Query sent to 10.96.0.10 (CoreDNS ClusterIP)             │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐                    │
  │  │  CoreDNS (kube-system namespace)           │                    │
  │  │  Pod: coredns-abc-123                      │                    │
  │  │  ClusterIP: 10.96.0.10 (kube-dns Service) │                    │
  │  │                                            │                    │
  │  │  Looks up Service "postgres-svc" in        │                    │
  │  │  namespace "default"                        │                    │
  │  │  → Found!  ClusterIP: 10.96.88.5          │                    │
  │  └──────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                    │
  │                     │                                                │
  │  Step 4: Returns A record: 10.96.88.5                              │
  │                                                                      │
  │  Step 5: Pod connects to 10.96.88.5:5432                           │
  │          → kube-proxy DNAT → actual postgres pod IP                │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>Pod's /etc/resolv.conf — Explained</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> nameserver</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>10.96.0.10</code> — The ClusterIP of the <code>kube-dns</code> Service in kube-system (points to CoreDNS pods)</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> search domains</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>default.svc.cluster.local</code> — Allows short names like <code>postgres-svc</code> to resolve within the namespace</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔢</span> ndots:5</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">If the name has fewer than 5 dots, append search domains first. <code>postgres-svc</code> (0 dots) → search path used. <code>api.example.com</code> (2 dots) → also search path first!</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 ndots:5 Performance Consideration</h5>
                    <p>The default <code>ndots:5</code> means external domains like <code>api.example.com</code> generate 4-5 DNS queries before the real one succeeds. For performance-sensitive apps, you can override this in the pod spec with <code>dnsConfig: { options: [{ name: ndots, value: "2" }] }</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>DNS Resolution Decision Path</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Pod Makes DNS Query</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>App calls <code>postgres-svc</code> → reads <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> → name has 0 dots (&lt; ndots:5)</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Search Path Applied</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Tries: <code>postgres-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → <code>postgres-svc.svc.cluster.local</code> → <code>postgres-svc.cluster.local</code></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>CoreDNS Lookup</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>CoreDNS checks its in-memory cache → watches K8s API for Services/Endpoints → finds match → returns ClusterIP</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Connection Made</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Pod connects to ClusterIP → kube-proxy DNAT rules → routed to actual pod IP → connection established</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>dnsPolicy</th>
                                <th>resolv.conf Source</th>
                                <th>Can Resolve Services?</th>
                                <th>When Used</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterFirst</strong> (default)</td>
                                <td>CoreDNS (10.96.0.10)<br>+ search domains</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>Standard pods — always use this</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Default</strong></td>
                                <td>Node's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code></td>
                                <td>❌ No — can't see Services</td>
                                <td>Rarely used; hostNetwork pods</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterFirstWithHostNet</strong></td>
                                <td>CoreDNS (10.96.0.10)</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>Pods with <code>hostNetwork: true</code> that need Service DNS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>None</strong></td>
                                <td>Must supply <code>dnsConfig</code> manually</td>
                                <td>Depends on config</td>
                                <td>Custom DNS (e.g., external resolver only)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Custom DNS Configuration in Pod Spec</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Override DNS settings for a specific pod</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-custom-dns
spec:
  dnsPolicy: None           # ← Take full control of DNS
  dnsConfig:
    nameservers:
      - 10.96.0.10           <span class="comment"># CoreDNS</span>
      - 8.8.8.8              <span class="comment"># Google DNS as fallback</span>
    searches:
      - default.svc.cluster.local
      - svc.cluster.local
    options:
      - name: ndots
        value: "2"           # ← Fewer search attempts for external domains
  containers:
    - name: easypj
      image: easypj:latest</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §19.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.2 CoreDNS Configuration</h3>
                <p>CoreDNS is deployed as a <strong>Deployment</strong> in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace, typically with 2 replicas for high availability. Its configuration lives in a <strong>ConfigMap</strong> called <code>coredns</code>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">CoreDNS Architecture in Kubernetes</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────── kube-system namespace ────────────────────┐
  │                                                                │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  Deployment: coredns (replicas: 2)                       │ │
  │  │  ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐              │ │
  │  │  │ coredns-abc-111 │   │ coredns-abc-222 │              │ │
  │  │  │ (Pod 1)         │   │ (Pod 2)         │              │ │
  │  │  └────────┬────────┘   └────────┬────────┘              │ │
  │  └───────────┼─────────────────────┼────────────────────────┘ │
  │              │                     │                           │
  │  ┌───────────▼─────────────────────▼────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  Service: kube-dns (ClusterIP: 10.96.0.10)              │ │
  │  │  Ports: 53/UDP, 53/TCP, 9153/TCP (metrics)              │ │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                                                                │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │  ConfigMap: coredns                                      │  │
  │  │  Contains: Corefile (plugin configuration)               │  │
  │  │  Mounted at: /etc/coredns/Corefile                       │  │
  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">The Default Corefile — Annotated</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: coredns
  namespace: kube-system
data:
  Corefile: |
    .:53 {                          <span class="comment"># Listen on port 53 for all zones</span>
        errors                       <span class="comment"># Log errors to stdout</span>
        health {                     <span class="comment"># Health check endpoint on :8080</span>
            lameduck 5s              <span class="comment"># Wait 5s before shutting down</span>
        }
        ready                        <span class="comment"># Readiness probe on :8181</span>
        kubernetes cluster.local in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa {
            pods insecure            <span class="comment"># Enable pod DNS records</span>
            fallthrough in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa
            ttl 30                   <span class="comment"># Cache TTL: 30 seconds</span>
        }
        prometheus :9153             <span class="comment"># Metrics endpoint for monitoring</span>
        forward . /etc/resolv.conf {  <span class="comment"># Forward external queries to</span>
            max_concurrent 1000       <span class="comment"># upstream DNS (node's resolv.conf)</span>
        }
        cache 30                     <span class="comment"># Cache all responses for 30s</span>
        loop                         <span class="comment"># Detect and stop DNS loops</span>
        reload                       <span class="comment"># Auto-reload Corefile on change</span>
        loadbalance                  <span class="comment"># Round-robin A/AAAA records</span>
    }</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Common CoreDNS Customizations</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Add a custom DNS entry (stub domain)</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the ConfigMap to forward *.example.com to a specific DNS server</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Add this block inside the Corefile:</span>
<span class="comment">#   example.com:53 {</span>
<span class="comment">#       forward . 10.0.0.1</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>

<span class="comment"># Add a static host entry (hosts plugin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Add inside the main server block, before kubernetes:</span>
<span class="comment">#   hosts {</span>
<span class="comment">#       10.10.10.50 mydb.internal</span>
<span class="comment">#       fallthrough</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>

<span class="comment"># After editing — CoreDNS auto-reloads (reload plugin)</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify: check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns --tail=20</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Exam: Never Delete the CoreDNS ConfigMap</h5>
                    <p>If you're asked to modify DNS configuration, use <code>kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system</code>. The <code>reload</code> plugin means changes take effect automatically within 30 seconds. Do NOT restart CoreDNS pods unless explicitly required — they auto-reload.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>CoreDNS Plugin</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                <th>Key Config</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>kubernetes</strong></td>
                                <td>Resolves Service and Pod DNS names from the K8s API</td>
                                <td><code>kubernetes cluster.local</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>forward</strong></td>
                                <td>Forwards non-cluster queries to upstream DNS servers</td>
                                <td><code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> or <code>forward . 8.8.8.8</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>cache</strong></td>
                                <td>Caches DNS responses to reduce API server load</td>
                                <td><code>cache 30</code> (seconds)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>loop</strong></td>
                                <td>Detects DNS forwarding loops and stops CoreDNS</td>
                                <td><code>loop</code> (no arguments)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>reload</strong></td>
                                <td>Auto-reloads Corefile when ConfigMap changes</td>
                                <td><code>reload</code> (default: every 30s)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>hosts</strong></td>
                                <td>Serves static DNS entries (like <code>/etc/hosts</code>)</td>
                                <td><code>hosts { 10.0.0.1 mydb.local }</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>errors</strong></td>
                                <td>Logs DNS errors to stdout (visible via <code>kubectl logs</code>)</td>
                                <td><code>errors</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>health</strong></td>
                                <td>HTTP health check endpoint on port 8080</td>
                                <td><code>health { lameduck 5s }</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ready</strong></td>
                                <td>Readiness probe endpoint on port 8181</td>
                                <td><code>ready</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>prometheus</strong></td>
                                <td>Exposes metrics for monitoring on port 9153</td>
                                <td><code>prometheus :9153</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §19.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.3 DNS Records in Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes automatically creates DNS records for Services and (optionally) Pods. Understanding these record formats is essential for cross-namespace communication.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Record Type</th>
                                <th>DNS Name Format</th>
                                <th>Resolves To</th>
                                <th>Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Service)</td>
                                <td><code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>ClusterIP</td>
                                <td><code>easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 10.96.45.2</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Headless)</td>
                                <td><code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>All Pod IPs</td>
                                <td><code>postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 10.244.1.15, 10.244.2.18</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Pod via Headless)</td>
                                <td><code>pod-name.svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>Specific Pod IP</td>
                                <td><code>postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 10.244.1.15</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>SRV Record</strong></td>
                                <td><code>_port-name._protocol.svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>Port + hostname</td>
                                <td><code>_http._tcp.easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → 0 10 80 ...</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>CNAME</strong> (ExternalName)</td>
                                <td><code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>External hostname</td>
                                <td><code>external-db.default.svc.cluster.local</code> → mydb.example.com</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>A Record</strong> (Pod direct)</td>
                                <td><code>pod-ip-dashed.namespace.pod.cluster.local</code></td>
                                <td>Pod IP</td>
                                <td><code>10-244-1-5.default.pod.cluster.local</code> → 10.244.1.5</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Exploring All DNS Record Types</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Launch a DNS debug pod</span>
kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it -- sh

<span class="comment"># A record — Service ClusterIP</span>
nslookup easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.45.2</span>

<span class="comment"># A records — Headless Service (returns all pod IPs)</span>
nslookup postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.2.18</span>

<span class="comment"># A record — specific pod via headless service</span>
nslookup postgres-0.postgres-headless.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.15</span>

<span class="comment"># SRV record — discover port and protocol</span>
nslookup -type=SRV _http._tcp.easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># service = 0 10 80 easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod DNS (IP dashes)</span>
nslookup 10-244-1-5.default.pod.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.244.1.5</span>

<span class="comment"># Cross-namespace resolution</span>
nslookup easypj-svc.prod.svc.cluster.local
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.88.5  (Service in "prod" namespace)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Short Names vs FQDN</h5>
                    <p>Within the <strong>same namespace</strong>, use short names: <code>easypj-svc</code> or <code>easypj-svc:80</code>. Across namespaces, include the namespace: <code>easypj-svc.prod</code>. Full FQDNs are rarely needed in practice but may appear on the CKA exam.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>📛 Short Names (Same Namespace)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>easypj-svc</code> → resolves using search path</li>
                            <li><code>easypj-svc:80</code> → with port</li>
                            <li>Queries: <code>easypj-svc.default.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Pro:</strong> Simple, clean code</li>
                            <li><strong>Con:</strong> Won't work cross-namespace</li>
                            <li><strong>Use for:</strong> Most app-to-service calls</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>🌐 FQDN (Cross-Namespace)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>easypj-svc.prod.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                            <li><code>db-svc.database.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                            <li>Bypasses search path → single DNS query</li>
                            <li><strong>Pro:</strong> Works from any namespace, slightly faster</li>
                            <li><strong>Con:</strong> Verbose, hardcoded namespace</li>
                            <li><strong>Use for:</strong> Cross-namespace calls, external DNS</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔤</div>
                        <h5>A Records (Address)</h5>
                        <p>Map service/pod names to IP addresses. ClusterIP services get one A record. Headless services return all pod IPs. Most common record type in K8s.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div>
                        <h5>SRV Records</h5>
                        <p>Include port number + hostname. Format: <code>_port._protocol.svc.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>. Used to discover both port and address of a service.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>CNAME Records</h5>
                        <p>Created by <code>ExternalName</code> services. Maps a K8s service name to an external hostname. No proxying — just DNS aliasing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏷️</div>
                        <h5>Pod DNS Records</h5>
                        <p>Pod IPs get dashed-IP format: <code>10-244-1-5.ns.pod.cluster.local</code>. Via headless services: <code>pod-name.svc-name.ns.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §19.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s19-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">19.4 Troubleshooting DNS Issues</h3>
                <p>DNS failures are one of the most common Kubernetes problems — and one of the most tested on the CKA. Here's a systematic approach to diagnose and fix DNS issues.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Is CoreDNS Running?</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — both pods should be Running</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Is the kube-dns Service Up?</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system</code> — should have ClusterIP 10.96.0.10</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Can Pods Reach CoreDNS?</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl exec POD -- nslookup kubernetes.default</code> — tests basic DNS connectivity</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Check CoreDNS Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — look for errors</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Check Corefile Config</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get cm coredns -n kube-system -o yaml</code> — verify configuration is correct</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>DNS Symptom</th>
                                <th>Likely Cause</th>
                                <th>Diagnostic</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>nslookup</code> times out</td>
                                <td>CoreDNS pods not running</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td>
                                <td>Check CoreDNS deployment, restart pods</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>SERVFAIL</code> response</td>
                                <td>Corefile misconfiguration</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td>
                                <td>Fix Corefile syntax in ConfigMap</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>NXDOMAIN</code> — name not found</td>
                                <td>Service doesn't exist or wrong namespace</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get svc -A | grep svc-name</code></td>
                                <td>Create the Service or use correct namespace prefix</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>External domains fail</td>
                                <td><code>forward</code> plugin pointing to wrong upstream</td>
                                <td>Check <code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> in Corefile</td>
                                <td>Fix upstream DNS (e.g., <code>forward . 8.8.8.8</code>)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>DNS works in some pods, not others</td>
                                <td>Pod's <code>dnsPolicy</code> is set to <code>Default</code></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pod POD -o yaml | grep dnsPolicy</code></td>
                                <td>Change to <code>dnsPolicy: ClusterFirst</code> (the default)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>DNS loop detected</td>
                                <td>Node's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to a local DNS that forwards back to CoreDNS</td>
                                <td>CoreDNS logs: <code>"Loop ... detected"</code></td>
                                <td>Change <code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> to <code>forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">DNS Troubleshooting Playbook</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># NAME                     READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-abc-111          1/1     Running   0          5d</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-abc-222          1/1     Running   0          5d</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check kube-dns Service</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># NAME       TYPE        CLUSTER-IP   PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-dns   ClusterIP   10.96.0.10   53/UDP,53/TCP,9153/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Test DNS from a debug pod</span>
kubectl run dnsdiag --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it -- sh
nslookup kubernetes.default     <span class="comment"># Should resolve to API server ClusterIP</span>
nslookup easypj-svc             <span class="comment"># Should resolve to Service ClusterIP</span>
nslookup google.com             <span class="comment"># Should resolve (tests external forwarding)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check CoreDNS logs for errors</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns --tail=50
<span class="comment"># Look for: "SERVFAIL", "i/o timeout", "Loop detected"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify pod resolv.conf</span>
kubectl exec easypj-pod -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver 10.96.0.10</span>
<span class="comment"># search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check the Corefile</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How CoreDNS Resolves Service Names: DNS Plugin Chain, Cache Mechanism, and the Cluster Domain Resolution Algorithm</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ CoreDNS — The Cluster's Internal DNS Server</h5>
            <p>CoreDNS replaces kube-dns as the cluster DNS server. It runs as a Deployment (2 replicas by default) and is exposed via the <code>kube-dns</code> Service. Every pod's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to it.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  COREDNS ARCHITECTURE — Plugin Chain                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Pod's /etc/resolv.conf (auto-configured by kubelet):                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ nameserver 10.96.0.10     <span class="comment">← kube-dns Service ClusterIP               </span>│   │
│  │ search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local     │   │
│  │ options ndots:5                                                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  DNS Query: easypj-web-svc                                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. Resolver prepends search domains:                                  │   │
│  │    easypj-web-svc.default.svc.cluster.local                           │   │
│  │    easypj-web-svc.svc.cluster.local                                   │   │
│  │    easypj-web-svc.cluster.local                                       │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ 2. CoreDNS receives first query → Plugin chain:                      │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │  ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐         │   │
│  │  │  errors  │──▶│   log    │──▶│  cache   │──▶│kubernetes│         │   │
│  │  │ (logging)│   │ (logging)│   │ (30s TTL)│   │ (RESOLVE)│         │   │
│  │  └──────────┘   └──────────┘   └──────────┘   └─────┬────┘         │   │
│  │                                                      │               │   │
│  │  kubernetes plugin RESOLVES:                         ▼               │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │   │
│  │  │ 1. Parse query: easypj-web-svc.default.svc.cluster.local     │  │   │
│  │  │ 2. Extract: service=easypj-web-svc, namespace=default        │  │   │
│  │  │ 3. Query K8s API: GET /api/v1/namespaces/default/services/   │  │   │
│  │  │    easypj-web-svc                                              │  │   │
│  │  │ 4. Service exists? → Return ClusterIP: 10.96.45.2            │  │   │
│  │  │ 5. Service NOT exists? → NXDOMAIN                             │  │   │
│  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  DNS RESOLUTION SHORTCUTS:                                                   │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Query                          Resolves To         Scope            │    │
│  │ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │    │
│  │ my-svc                         my-svc.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cl.local  Same NS   │    │
│  │ my-svc.other-ns                my-svc.other-ns.svc... Cross-NS     │    │
│  │ my-svc.other-ns.svc            my-svc.other-ns.svc... Cross-NS     │    │
│  │ my-svc.other-ns.svc.cluster.local      Full FQDN           Any NS  │    │
│  │ 10-244-1-5.default.pod.cluster.local   Pod DNS             Any NS  │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ STATEfulSet POD DNS (each pod gets unique DNS):                      │    │
│  │   easypj-db-0.easypj-db.default.svc.cluster.local → 10.244.1.5     │    │
│  │   easypj-db-1.easypj-db.default.svc.cluster.local → 10.244.2.3     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  COREDNS CONFIGURATION (Corefile):                                           │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ $ kubectl get cm coredns -n kube-system -o yaml                     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ Corefile: |                                                          │    │
│  │   .:53 {                                                             │    │
│  │       errors                                                         │    │
│  │       health { lameduck 5s }                                         │    │
│  │       ready                                                           │    │
│  │       kubernetes cluster.local in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa {               │    │
│  │         pods insecure                                                 │    │
│  │         fallthrough in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa                             │    │
│  │         ttl 30                                                        │    │
│  │       }                                                               │    │
│  │       prometheus :9153                                                │    │
│  │       forward . /etc/resolv.conf {  <span class="comment">← Upstream DNS for external      </span>│    │
│  │         max_concurrent 1000          │   names (e.g., github.com)     │    │
│  │       }                               │                               │    │
│  │       cache 30                                                       │    │
│  │       loop                                                            │    │
│  │       reload                <span class="comment">← Auto-reload on ConfigMap change!       </span>│    │
│  │       loadbalance                                                     │    │
│  │   }                                                                   │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, DNS is a common troubleshooting domain. If a pod can't resolve a Service name: (1) <code>kubectl exec pod -- nslookup &lt;svc&gt;</code> — test resolution, (2) <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — are CoreDNS pods running? (3) <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — any errors? (4) Check <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> inside the pod — is nameserver set to kube-dns Service IP? If DNS is completely broken, the cluster is effectively dead.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>DNS format matters</strong> — <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code> for services. <code>&lt;pod-ip-dashed&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.pod.cluster.local</code> for pods. The exam tests exact FQDN knowledge</li>
                    <li><strong>CoreDNS is a Deployment, not DaemonSet</strong> — Typically 2 replicas. Its ConfigMap is named <code>coredns</code>. The Service is <code>kube-dns</code> (not coredns!) in <code>kube-system</code></li>
                    <li><strong>DNS loop</strong> — If the node's <code>/etc/resolv.conf</code> points to <code>127.0.0.53</code> (systemd-resolved), CoreDNS's <code>forward . /etc/resolv.conf</code> creates a loop. Fix: use explicit upstream <code>8.8.8.8</code></li>
                    <li><strong><code>ndots: 5</code> default</strong> — Pods first try appending search domains. <code>nslookup postgres</code> actually tries 4-5 FQDNs before the bare name. This slows DNS. For external names, use trailing dot: <code>google.com.</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — DNS debugging in 30 seconds:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it -- nslookup kubernetes.default</code><br>
                If this fails, DNS is broken. Check:<br>
                <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — CoreDNS pods running?<br>
                <code>kubectl get svc -n kube-system kube-dns</code> — kube-dns service exists?<br>
                <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code> — error in CoreDNS?</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 19)</h3>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Action</th>
                            <th>Command</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Check CoreDNS pods</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check kube-dns Service</td><td><code>kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test DNS resolution</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- nslookup SERVICE</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View CoreDNS config</td><td><code>kubectl get cm coredns -n kube-system -o yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Edit CoreDNS config</td><td><code>kubectl edit cm coredns -n kube-system</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>CoreDNS logs</td><td><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pod DNS config</td><td><code>kubectl exec POD -- cat /etc/resolv.conf</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Service FQDN format</td><td><code>svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pod FQDN (headless)</td><td><code>pod-name.svc-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch19-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 5 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a service <code>web-svc</code> in namespace <code>prod</code></li>
                    <li>2. From a test pod, resolve: <code>nslookup web-svc.prod.svc.cluster.local</code></li>
                    <li>3. Verify short name works within same namespace: <code>nslookup web-svc</code></li>
                    <li>4. Edit CoreDNS Corefile to add a custom rewrite rule</li>
                    <li>5. Verify CoreDNS config reload: <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns | grep reload</code></li>
