            <div class="relationship-map">
                <h4>🗺️ Volume Types — When to Use What</h4>
                <div class="rel-hub">Pod Storage Need</div>
                <div class="rel-spokes">
                    <div class="rel-spoke">
                        <span class="spoke-icon">📂</span>
                        <strong>emptyDir</strong>
                        <small>Temp scratch space, sidecar sharing</small>
                    </div>
                    <div class="rel-spoke">
                        <span class="spoke-icon">🖥️</span>
                        <strong>hostPath</strong>
                        <small>Node logs, etcd certs, dev/testing</small>
                    </div>
                    <div class="rel-spoke">
                        <span class="spoke-icon">💾</span>
                        <strong>PVC</strong>
                        <small>Databases, uploads, persistent data</small>
                    </div>
                    <div class="rel-spoke">
                        <span class="spoke-icon">⚙️</span>
                        <strong>configMap</strong>
                        <small>Config files mounted into containers</small>
                    </div>
                    <div class="rel-spoke">
                        <span class="spoke-icon">🔐</span>
                        <strong>secret</strong>
                        <small>TLS certs, passwords as files</small>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.1 The Problem: Container Storage is Ephemeral</h3>
                <p>Every container starts with a <strong>fresh, writable filesystem layer</strong> provided by its image. When the container stops or crashes, that layer is destroyed. Any files created, modified, or downloaded during runtime are <strong>gone forever</strong>. This is by design — containers are disposable — but it creates a massive problem for stateful applications.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Data Loss Problem — Without Volumes</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  WITHOUT VOLUMES — DATA IS LOST ON EVERY POD RESTART                    │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Timeline:  t=0              t=5min             t=5min+1sec              │
  │             Pod starts       Pod crashes         New pod starts          │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐         │
  │  │ 📦 easypj Pod  │    │ 📦 easypj Pod  │    │ 📦 easypj Pod  │         │
  │  │                │    │                │    │   (new)         │         │
  │  │ 📄 db.sqlite3  │    │ 📄 db.sqlite3  │    │                │         │
  │  │ 📄 resume.pdf  │    │ 📄 resume.pdf  │    │ 📄 (empty!)    │  💀     │
  │  │ 📄 photo.jpg   │    │ 📄 photo.jpg   │    │                │         │
  │  │                │    │    💥 CRASH!    │    │ ALL DATA LOST! │         │
  │  └────────────────┘    └────────────────┘    └────────────────┘         │
  │                                                                          │
  │  50 job posts created    Everything inside     New container starts      │
  │  3 resumes uploaded      the container dies    with BLANK filesystem     │
  │  User profiles saved     with the container    Users see: "No jobs"     │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  WITH VOLUMES — DATA SURVIVES POD RESTARTS                              │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐         │
  │  │ 📦 easypj Pod  │    │ 📦 easypj Pod  │    │ 📦 easypj Pod  │         │
  │  │                │    │                │    │   (new)         │         │
  │  │ 📄 db.sqlite3 ─┼─┐  │    💥 CRASH!   │    │ 📄 db.sqlite3 ─┼─┐      │
  │  │ 📄 resume.pdf ─┼─┤  │                │    │ 📄 resume.pdf ─┼─┤      │
  │  └────────────────┘ │  └────────────────┘    └────────────────┘ │      │
  │                     │                                            │      │
  │                     │    ┌──────────────────┐                    │      │
  │                     └───▶│    💾 VOLUME      │◀───────────────────┘      │
  │                          │  db.sqlite3  ✅  │                           │
  │                          │  resume.pdf  ✅  │  Data survives!           │
  │                          │  photo.jpg   ✅  │  New pod reads same files │
  │                          └──────────────────┘                           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ This Happens More Often Than You Think</h5>
                    <p>Pods restart frequently in Kubernetes: OOMKilled (out of memory), node failures, rolling updates, scaling down. If your application stores ANY data inside the container filesystem, you <strong>will</strong> lose it. This includes:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Database files (SQLite, PostgreSQL data dir)</li>
                        <li>User uploads (resumes, images, documents)</li>
                        <li>Application logs written to local files</li>
                        <li>Cache files and session data</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Container Writable Layer</h5>
                        <p>Each container gets a thin writable layer on top of its read-only image layers. Writes go here. When the container stops, this layer is <strong>deleted</strong>. It's fast but temporary — perfect for stateless apps, fatal for stateful ones.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>Kubernetes Volumes</h5>
                        <p>A Volume is storage that lives <strong>outside the container</strong> but is mounted inside it at a specific path. The container reads and writes normally, but the data is stored externally. When the container restarts, the Volume is re-mounted with all data intact.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔑 Key Concept: volumes vs volumeMounts</h5>
                    <p>Every pod spec has two related fields. <code>spec.volumes[]</code> <strong>defines</strong> the storage (what type, where it comes from). <code>spec.containers[].volumeMounts[]</code> <strong>mounts</strong> that storage into a specific path inside the container. You always need both — a volume definition AND a mount point.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">volumes-vs-volumemounts.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># The two parts of Kubernetes volume configuration</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:latest
    volumeMounts:                    # ◀── WHERE to mount (inside container)
    - name: app-data                 <span class="comment">#     references volumes[] by name</span>
      mountPath: /app/data           <span class="comment">#     path inside the container</span>
    - name: static-files
      mountPath: /app/static
      readOnly: true                 <span class="comment">#     container can only READ</span>
  volumes:                           # ◀── WHAT to mount (storage source)
  - name: app-data                   <span class="comment">#     name must match volumeMounts</span>
    emptyDir: {}                     <span class="comment">#     type: temporary pod-scoped storage</span>
  - name: static-files
    configMap:                       <span class="comment">#     type: files from a ConfigMap</span>
      name: easypj-static-config</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Volume Lifecycle Rule</h5>
                    <p>A volume's lifetime depends on its type. An <code>emptyDir</code> lives as long as the <strong>pod</strong> — it survives container crashes but is deleted when the pod is removed. A <code>PersistentVolume</code> lives as long as the <strong>cluster</strong> — it survives pod deletions entirely. Understanding this lifecycle is critical for choosing the right volume type.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.2 Volume Types Overview</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes supports over 20 volume types, but for the CKA exam and daily work, you need to know <strong>7 key types</strong>. Each serves a different purpose, has a different lifecycle, and is suited to specific use cases.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Volume Type</th>
                                <th>Persists?</th>
                                <th>Scope</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                                <th>CKA Exam?</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>emptyDir</code></td>
                                <td>❌ Pod lifetime only</td>
                                <td>Pod</td>
                                <td>Temp files, cache, sidecar sharing</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>hostPath</code></td>
                                <td>⚠️ Node lifetime</td>
                                <td>Node</td>
                                <td>Dev/testing, node logs access</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>persistentVolumeClaim</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Cluster lifetime</td>
                                <td>Cluster</td>
                                <td>Databases, user uploads, any persistent data</td>
                                <td>✅ Heavily tested</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>configMap</code></td>
                                <td>N/A (config data)</td>
                                <td>Cluster</td>
                                <td>Config files mounted as volumes</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>secret</code></td>
                                <td>N/A (sensitive data)</td>
                                <td>Cluster</td>
                                <td>TLS certs, passwords as files</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>nfs</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Network storage</td>
                                <td>Network</td>
                                <td>Shared storage across pods/nodes</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Sometimes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>csi</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Provider-managed</td>
                                <td>Provider</td>
                                <td>Cloud storage (AWS EBS, GCE PD, Azure Disk)</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Conceptual</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Volume Types by Lifecycle and Scope</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  VOLUME LIFECYCLE SPECTRUM                                               │
  │                                                                          │
  │  Shortest ◀──────────────────────────────────────────▶ Longest          │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─────────────┐   ┌─────────────┐   ┌──────────────────────────────┐  │
  │  │  emptyDir    │   │  hostPath   │   │  persistentVolumeClaim (PVC) │  │
  │  │             │   │             │   │                              │  │
  │  │  Lives with  │   │  Lives on   │   │  Lives in cluster           │  │
  │  │  the POD     │   │  the NODE   │   │  Independent of pods        │  │
  │  │             │   │             │   │                              │  │
  │  │  Pod dies →  │   │  Node dies →│   │  Pod dies → data SAFE ✅   │  │
  │  │  data GONE 💀│   │  data GONE 💀│   │  Node dies → data SAFE ✅  │  │
  │  └─────────────┘   └─────────────┘   └──────────────────────────────┘  │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
  │  │  configMap / secret          │  nfs / csi                       │    │
  │  │                              │                                   │    │
  │  │  NOT storage — these inject  │  Network/cloud storage            │    │
  │  │  configuration and secrets   │  Fully independent of cluster     │    │
  │  │  as files into containers    │  Survives cluster deletion        │    │
  │  └──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────┘    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🎯</div>
                        <h5>CKA Exam Focus</h5>
                        <p>The exam heavily tests <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code> (creating PVs, PVCs, binding). You'll also see <code>emptyDir</code> in multi-container pod questions and <code>hostPath</code> for etcd backup paths. <code>configMap</code> and <code>secret</code> volumes appear in configuration questions.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div>
                        <h5>easypj Storage Plan</h5>
                        <p>Our Django app needs: <strong>PVC</strong> for PostgreSQL data (10Gi), <strong>PVC</strong> for user uploads (resumes, photos), <strong>ConfigMap</strong> volume for Django settings, <strong>Secret</strong> volume for database credentials, <strong>emptyDir</strong> for nginx ↔ Django static file sharing.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Quick Decision Guide</h5>
                    <p>Need temp scratch space shared between containers? → <code>emptyDir</code>. Need data to survive pod deletion? → <code>PVC</code>. Need to inject config files? → <code>configMap</code>. Need to inject secrets as files? → <code>secret</code>. Need shared storage across multiple nodes? → <code>nfs</code> or CSI with ReadWriteMany.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.3 emptyDir — Temporary Shared Storage</h3>
                <p>An <code>emptyDir</code> volume is the simplest volume type. It's created as an <strong>empty directory</strong> when a pod starts and is <strong>deleted when the pod is removed</strong>. It's perfect for sharing data between containers in the same pod — the classic sidecar pattern.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>When to Use emptyDir</h5>
                        <p>Sidecar containers sharing data (nginx + Django), temporary scratch space for computation, cache that can be regenerated, checkpoint data during multi-step processing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div>
                        <h5>When NOT to Use emptyDir</h5>
                        <p>Database storage, user uploads, anything that must survive a pod restart. If the data matters, use a PersistentVolumeClaim instead.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">emptyDir — Sidecar Pattern: Django + Nginx Sharing Static Files</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────── Pod: easypj-web ─────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                    │
  │  ┌────────────────────┐         ┌──────────────────────┐         │
  │  │  Container 1:      │         │  Container 2:        │         │
  │  │  django            │         │  nginx               │         │
  │  │                    │         │                      │         │
  │  │  1. collectstatic  │         │  3. Serves static    │         │
  │  │     writes CSS/JS  │         │     files from       │         │
  │  │     to /static/    │         │     /usr/share/nginx │         │
  │  │                    │         │                      │         │
  │  │  volumeMount:      │         │  volumeMount:        │         │
  │  │    /app/static     │         │    /usr/share/nginx  │         │
  │  └─────────┬──────────┘         └──────────┬───────────┘         │
  │            │                               │                      │
  │            │   2. SHARED emptyDir volume    │                      │
  │            └──────────┐  ┌─────────────────┘                      │
  │                       ▼  ▼                                         │
  │              ┌──────────────────┐                                  │
  │              │  emptyDir: {}    │                                  │
  │              │  /static-files   │                                  │
  │              │                  │                                  │
  │              │  style.css  ✅   │                                  │
  │              │  app.js     ✅   │                                  │
  │              │  logo.png   ✅   │                                  │
  │              └──────────────────┘                                  │
  │                                                                    │
  │  Both containers see the SAME files — writes by Django are        │
  │  immediately visible to Nginx. No network calls needed!           │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-emptydir-sidecar.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj Django + Nginx sidecar sharing static files via emptyDir</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  labels:
    app: easypj
    component: web
spec:
  initContainers:
  - name: collect-static
    image: easypj:latest
    command: ["python", "manage.py", "collectstatic", "--noinput"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: static-files              # ◀── writes static files here
      mountPath: /app/staticfiles
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000
    command: ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi:application", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: static-files              # ◀── Django can still access them
      mountPath: /app/staticfiles
    - name: tmp-scratch               # ◀── scratch space for processing
      mountPath: /tmp/processing
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:alpine
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
    volumeMounts:
    - name: static-files              # ◀── Nginx serves the same files
      mountPath: /usr/share/nginx/html/static
      readOnly: true                  <span class="comment">#     Nginx only READS, never writes</span>
  volumes:
  - name: static-files               # ◀── Shared between all containers
    emptyDir: {}
  - name: tmp-scratch                 # ◀── Only used by Django
    emptyDir:
      sizeLimit: 100Mi               <span class="comment">#     Optional: limit disk usage</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 emptyDir Medium Options</h5>
                    <p>By default, <code>emptyDir</code> uses the node's disk. You can also use RAM-backed storage with <code>emptyDir: { medium: Memory }</code>. This is faster but counts against the container's memory limit. Useful for high-speed temp files, but remember: RAM is more expensive than disk and the data is still lost when the pod dies.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">emptydir-memory-backed.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># RAM-backed emptyDir — fast but size-limited</span>
volumes:
- name: cache-volume
  emptyDir:
    medium: Memory          # ◀── Uses RAM instead of disk (tmpfs)
    sizeLimit: 256Mi        <span class="comment">#     Pod evicted if exceeds this limit</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔬 emptyDir Lifecycle — What Survives What?</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Container restart</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data SURVIVES ✅</strong> — emptyDir lives at the pod level, not the container level. If Django crashes and kubelet restarts it, the static files are still there.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💀</span> Pod deletion</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data LOST ❌</strong> — When the pod is deleted (scaling down, rescheduling, node failure), the emptyDir is permanently deleted. Cannot be recovered.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Rolling update</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data LOST ❌</strong> — A rolling update creates a NEW pod. The old pod (and its emptyDir) is deleted. The new pod gets a fresh empty emptyDir.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📍</span> Node failure</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><strong>Data LOST ❌</strong> — Pod is rescheduled to a different node. The emptyDir was on the old node's disk. New pod starts fresh.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §21.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s21-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">21.4 hostPath — Node-Level Storage</h3>
                <p>A <code>hostPath</code> volume mounts a <strong>file or directory from the node's filesystem</strong> directly into the pod. Unlike <code>emptyDir</code>, the data exists on the node's disk and persists even after the pod is deleted — but only on <strong>that specific node</strong>.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ hostPath Is Dangerous in Production</h5>
                    <p><code>hostPath</code> gives the container <strong>direct access to the node's filesystem</strong>. This is a security risk (containers can read/write sensitive host files) and a portability problem (pod must always run on the same node). Use it only for development, testing, or accessing node-level system files like logs.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">hostPath — Pod Reads Node's Log Directory</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────── Node: worker-1 ──────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                    │
  │  Node Filesystem:                                                  │
  │  /var/log/                                                         │
  │  ├── syslog                                                        │
  │  ├── kern.log                                                      │
  │  ├── containers/                                                   │
  │  │   ├── easypj-abc.log                                            │
  │  │   └── postgres-0.log                                            │
  │  └── pods/                                                         │
  │                                                                    │
  │  ┌────────────────────── Pod: log-collector ──────────────────┐   │
  │  │                                                              │   │
  │  │  Container: fluentd                                         │   │
  │  │  mountPath: /var/log/host  ◀──── hostPath: /var/log/        │   │
  │  │                                                              │   │
  │  │  $ ls /var/log/host/                                        │   │
  │  │  syslog  kern.log  containers/  pods/                       │   │
  │  │                                                              │   │
  │  │  ✅ Can read all node logs!                                 │   │
  │  │  ⚠️ Only works on THIS node                                │   │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │                                                                    │
  │  If pod moves to worker-2 → different /var/log/ directory!        │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">hostpath-log-collector.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Log collector pod using hostPath to access node logs</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: log-collector
spec:
  containers:
  - name: fluentd
    image: fluent/fluentd:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: node-logs
      mountPath: /var/log/host        # ◀── appears here inside container
      readOnly: true                  <span class="comment">#     ALWAYS use readOnly for safety</span>
    - name: container-logs
      mountPath: /var/lib/docker/containers
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: node-logs
    hostPath:
      path: /var/log                  # ◀── actual path on the NODE
      type: Directory                 <span class="comment">#     must exist, must be a directory</span>
  - name: container-logs
    hostPath:
      path: /var/lib/docker/containers
      type: DirectoryOrCreate        <span class="comment">#     creates if doesn't exist</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>hostPath Type</th>
                                <th>Behavior</th>
                                <th>Fails If...</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>""</code> (empty)</td>
                                <td>No checks before mounting</td>
                                <td>Never (unsafe)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>DirectoryOrCreate</code></td>
                                <td>Creates directory if missing</td>
                                <td>Never (auto-creates)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Directory</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a directory</td>
                                <td>Path doesn't exist or isn't a dir</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>FileOrCreate</code></td>
                                <td>Creates file if missing</td>
                                <td>Never (auto-creates)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>File</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a file</td>
                                <td>Path doesn't exist or isn't a file</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Socket</code></td>
                                <td>Must exist as a UNIX socket</td>
                                <td>Not a socket file</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Legitimate hostPath Uses</h5>
                        <p>Accessing node logs (<code>/var/log</code>), Docker socket (<code>/var/run/docker.sock</code>), etcd data dir for backups, <code>/etc/kubernetes</code> for kubelet config. Always with <code>readOnly: true</code> when possible.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⛔</div>
                        <h5>Never Use hostPath For</h5>
                        <p>Application databases, user uploads, any data that needs to move between nodes. If the pod gets rescheduled to a different node, it will see a different (or empty) directory. Use PVC instead.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">CKA Exam: etcd backup using hostPath</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># CKA Exam Classic: etcd backup pod needs hostPath for certificates</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: etcd-backup
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  containers:
  - name: etcd-backup
    image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.12-0
    command:
    - /bin/sh
    - -c
    - |
      etcdctl snapshot save /backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
        --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
        --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
        --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
        --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
    volumeMounts:
    - name: etcd-certs                # ◀── CKA: etcd certs on control plane
      mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
      readOnly: true
    - name: backup-dir                # ◀── where snapshot is saved
      mountPath: /backup
  volumes:
  - name: etcd-certs
    hostPath:
      path: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd  # ◀── actual certs on the node
      type: Directory
  - name: backup-dir
    hostPath:
      path: /opt/etcd-backup          # ◀── backup saved on node filesystem
      type: DirectoryOrCreate
  hostNetwork: true
  nodeName: controlplane              # ◀── must run on control plane node</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🏋️ CKA Practice Scenario: Volume Troubleshooting</h5>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> A developer reports that their easypj pod keeps losing uploaded resumes every time it restarts. They've configured the pod as shown:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">broken-pod.yaml (spot the problem)</span>
                            </div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># What's wrong with this pod?</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: uploads
      mountPath: /app/media/uploads
  volumes:
  - name: uploads
    emptyDir: {}        # ◀── 🤔 Is this the right volume type?</code></pre>
                        </div>
                        <p><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> <code>emptyDir</code> data is <strong>deleted when the pod is deleted</strong>. Every restart creates a new, empty directory. For data that must persist, they need a <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code>.</p>
                        <p><strong>Fix:</strong> Replace <code>emptyDir: {}</code> with a PVC reference:</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">fixed-pod.yaml</span>
                            </div>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Fixed: Use PVC instead of emptyDir for persistent data</span>
  volumes:
  - name: uploads
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: easypj-uploads-pvc   # ◀── data survives pod restarts!</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Chapter 21 Volume Troubleshooting Quick Reference -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>🔧 Volume Troubleshooting Quick Reference</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Likely Cause</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Pod won't start — <code>MountVolume.SetUp failed</code></td>
                                <td>hostPath directory doesn't exist on node</td>
                                <td>Use <code>type: DirectoryOrCreate</code> or pre-create the directory</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Data disappears after pod restart</td>
                                <td>Using <code>emptyDir</code> for persistent data</td>
                                <td>Switch to <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Container can't write to volume</td>
                                <td><code>readOnly: true</code> set on volumeMount</td>
                                <td>Remove <code>readOnly</code> or set to <code>false</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Permission denied inside container</td>
                                <td>Container runs as non-root but volume owned by root</td>
                                <td>Add <code>fsGroup</code> in <code>securityContext</code> to change group ownership</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>emptyDir exceeds size — pod evicted</td>
                                <td><code>sizeLimit</code> exceeded (or node disk pressure)</td>
                                <td>Increase <code>sizeLimit</code> or clean up data inside the volume</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Volume shows different data after reschedule</td>
                                <td><code>hostPath</code> is node-specific — different node = different data</td>
                                <td>Use PVC with network storage (NFS, cloud) for multi-node access</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- Chapter 21 Key Commands Card -->
                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>⌨️ Chapter 21 — Essential kubectl Commands</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔍</span> Inspect pod volumes</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; | grep -A10 "Volumes:"</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📂</span> List files in mounted volume</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- ls -la /path/to/mount</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">💾</span> Check disk usage in volume</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- df -h /path/to/mount</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> View pod events (mount errors)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; | grep -A5 "Events:"</code></div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔄</span> Watch pod status during restart</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -w</code></div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 21 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Volumes &amp; Persistent Storage Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong><code>emptyDir</code> is NOT persistent</strong> — It survives container restarts but is deleted when the pod is removed. Never use it for database files. The exam may present a scenario where data disappears after pod rescheduling — <code>emptyDir</code> is the culprit</li>
                        <li><strong><code>hostPath</code> pins pods to specific nodes</strong> — Data only exists on that one node. If the pod reschedules to another node, the data is gone. CKA traps: "Why can't my pod find its data after rescheduling?"</li>
                        <li><strong><code>volumeMounts</code> vs <code>volumes</code></strong> — <code>volumes:</code> declares the volume at pod level. <code>volumeMounts:</code> mounts it into a specific container. Forgetting either causes the pod to fail or the mount to not appear</li>
                        <li><strong><code>subPath</code> prevents directory overwrite</strong> — Without <code>subPath</code>, mounting a volume at <code>/etc/nginx</code> replaces ALL existing files in that directory. With <code>subPath: nginx.conf</code>, only that one file is mounted</li>
                        <li><strong><code>readOnly: true</code></strong> — Add this to volumeMounts for security when the container should only read data. The exam may ask you to mount a config volume as read-only</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Volume Mounts Are Actually Implemented: Bind Mounts, tmpfs, and the Container Filesystem Stack</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ The Container Filesystem Stack — Where Volumes Fit</h5>
            <p>When a container mounts a volume, the kubelet instructs the container runtime to create an additional mount point that overlays the container's root filesystem. Understanding where volumes sit in the filesystem stack helps you debug mount failures and data persistence issues.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CONTAINER FILESYSTEM STACK — Layers from Top to Bottom                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Container's view of /:                                                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  /app/                                                               │    │
│  │  ├── media/          <span class="comment">← VOLUME MOUNT (emptyDir/hostPath/PVC)          </span>│    │
│  │  │   └── uploads/    <span class="comment">← Survives container restart                   </span>│    │
│  │  ├── manage.py       <span class="comment">← From image layer 3 (COPY . .)               </span>│    │
│  │  ├── requirements/   <span class="comment">← From image layer 2 (pip install)            </span>│    │
│  │  └── usr/bin/python  <span class="comment">← From image layer 1 (FROM python:3.12)       </span>│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  UNDERLYING IMPLEMENTATION (overlay2 + volume mounts):                       │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────┐  <span class="comment">← VOLUME MOUNT (separate mount)      </span>│    │
│  │  │ /var/lib/kubelet/pods/   │    This sits ON TOP of the image       │    │
│  │  │   &lt;uid&gt;/volumes/kuber-   │    layers. Everything written here     │    │
│  │  │   netes.io~empty-dir/    │    stays on the node until pod is      │    │
│  │  │   media/                 │    deleted.                             │    │
│  │  └──────────────────────────┘                                         │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────┐  <span class="comment">← WRITABLE LAYER (ephemeral)         </span>│    │
│  │  │ /var/lib/containerd/...  │    All writes NOT in volume mounts     │    │
│  │  │   &lt;container-id&gt;/upper   │    go here. DELETED on container stop. │    │
│  │  └──────────────────────────┘                                         │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────┐  <span class="comment">← IMAGE LAYERS (read-only)           </span>│    │
│  │  │ easypj code (sha:jkl012) │    Never modified. Shared across      │    │
│  │  │ pip packages (sha:ghi789)│    all containers using same layers.  │    │
│  │  │ python:3.12 (sha:def456) │                                        │    │
│  │  └──────────────────────────┘                                         │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  VOLUME TYPE IMPLEMENTATIONS:                                                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  emptyDir:                                                           │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ • Created as a DIRECTORY on the node's filesystem           │     │    │
│  │  │ • Default: /var/lib/kubelet/pods/&lt;uid&gt;/volumes/...         │     │    │
│  │  │ • medium: "" → node's default disk (ext4/xfs)               │     │    │
│  │  │ • medium: "Memory" → tmpfs (RAM-backed, faster, ephemeral) │     │    │
│  │  │ • LIFETIME: Tied to POD. Survives container restarts.       │     │    │
│  │  │   DELETED when pod is deleted from the node.                │     │    │
│  │  │ • Use case: scratch space, cache, inter-container sharing   │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  hostPath:                                                           │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ • Direct bind-mount from NODE filesystem → container        │     │    │
│  │  │ • /var/log on node → /host-logs in container                │     │    │
│  │  │ • LIFETIME: Survives pod deletion (data stays on node)      │     │    │
│  │  │ • DANGER: Pod scheduled to different node → DIFFERENT data  │     │    │
│  │  │ • Use case: accessing node logs, Docker socket, /sys        │     │    │
│  │  │ • CKA: hostPath is the simplest PV type for testing         │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  PVC (PersistentVolumeClaim):                                        │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ • kubelet ATTACHES the PV's storage to the node             │     │    │
│  │  │ • For cloud: AWS EBS → AttachVolume → mount to container    │     │    │
│  │  │ • For NFS: mount -t nfs &lt;server&gt;:&lt;path&gt; &lt;mountpoint&gt;        │     │    │
│  │  │ • LIFETIME: Survives pod deletion (PV must be manually       │     │    │
│  │  │   deleted or reclaimed per reclaimPolicy)                   │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, emptyDir is your go-to for sharing data between containers in a pod. hostPath is for accessing node filesystem directly (but avoid in production except for specific use cases like log collectors). PVC is for persistent data across pod restarts. Remember: emptyDir lasts as long as the POD exists; hostPath lasts as long as the NODE exists; PVC lasts as long as the PV exists.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">emptyDir persists across pod restarts</span> — it persists across <strong>container</strong> restarts, NOT pod deletions</li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">emptyDir survives container crashes but dies with the pod</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">hostPath works the same on any node</span> — it reads <strong>that specific node's</strong> filesystem. Different node = different data</li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">hostPath is node-specific — pod rescheduling changes the data</span></li>
                    <li><code>volumes[]</code> and <code>volumeMounts[]</code> names <strong>must match exactly</strong> — typo = mount failure</li>
                    <li><code>emptyDir: {medium: Memory}</code> counts against the container's <strong>memory limit</strong>, not disk</li>
                    <li>Forgetting <code>readOnly: true</code> on hostPath mounts is a <strong>security risk</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Volume type decision tree:</strong><br>
                Need temp scratch space between containers? → <code>emptyDir</code><br>
                Need to access node files (logs, sockets)? → <code>hostPath</code> (use sparingly)<br>
                Need data to survive pod deletion? → PV/PVC (Chapter 22)<br>
                Need to inject config files? → ConfigMap volume (Chapter 23)<br>
                Need to inject credentials? → Secret volume (Chapter 23)</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 21)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 5 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a pod with two containers sharing an <code>emptyDir</code> volume at <code>/shared</code></li>
                    <li>2. Exec into container-1 and write a file: <code>echo hello > /shared/test.txt</code></li>
                    <li>3. Exec into container-2 and read it: <code>cat /shared/test.txt</code></li>
                    <li>4. Delete the pod and recreate it — verify <code>/shared/test.txt</code> is gone</li>
                    <li>5. Create a pod with <code>hostPath</code> mounting <code>/var/log</code> and list the node's logs</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 21 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch21-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 21 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of volume types &amp; mount behavior — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod with two containers that share an <code>emptyDir</code> volume. Container <code>writer</code> writes to <code>/data/output.log</code> and container <code>reader</code> reads from the same path.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: shared-volume-pod
spec:
volumes:                         # ← Pod-level: DECLARE the volume
- name: shared-data
emptyDir: {}                   # ← Ephemeral, deleted with pod

containers:
- name: writer
image: busybox
command: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
args: ["while true; do echo $(date) >> /data/output.log; sleep 5; done"]
volumeMounts:                  # ← Container-level: MOUNT the volume
- name: shared-data
  mountPath: /data

- name: reader
image: busybox
command: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
args: ["tail -f /data/output.log"]
volumeMounts:
- name: shared-data
  mountPath: /data             # ← Same volume, same path

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl exec shared-volume-pod -c reader -- cat /data/output.log</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>emptyDir</code> creates an empty directory on the node when a pod is assigned to it. Both containers see the same files because they mount the same volume. Key facts: (1) <code>volumes:</code> is pod-level, <code>volumeMounts:</code> is container-level, (2) the <code>name:</code> in volumeMount must match the volume name, (3) <code>emptyDir</code> data survives container restarts but NOT pod deletion. On the CKA, sidecar patterns (log collector + app) almost always use <code>emptyDir</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to data in an <code>emptyDir</code> volume when: (a) the container crashes, (b) the pod is deleted, (c) the pod is rescheduled to another node?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Scenario</th>
                                        <th>Data Preserved?</th>
                                        <th>Why?</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Container crashes/restarts</td>
                                        <td>✅ YES</td>
                                        <td>emptyDir is tied to the pod, not the container</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Pod is deleted</td>
                                        <td>❌ NO</td>
                                        <td>emptyDir lifecycle = pod lifecycle</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Pod rescheduled to new node</td>
                                        <td>❌ NO</td>
                                        <td>emptyDir is node-local storage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Common CKA trap scenario:</span>
<span class="comment"># "My database pod restarted and data is still there" → emptyDir, container restart</span>
<span class="comment"># "My database pod was rescheduled and data is GONE" → emptyDir, pod recreation</span>
<span class="comment"># Solution: Use PV/PVC for data that must survive pod deletion</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>emptyDir</code> is ephemeral — its lifetime matches the pod's. Container restarts don't affect it because the pod still exists on the same node. But pod deletion (or rescheduling by a Deployment) destroys the emptyDir. On the CKA, if you see a scenario where "data disappears after pod restart" — check if it's an <code>emptyDir</code> and the pod was DELETED (not just the container restarted). Use PersistentVolumes for durable storage.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that mounts the host's <code>/var/log</code> directory as read-only. What are the security concerns?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: log-reader
spec:
volumes:
- name: host-logs
hostPath:
  path: /var/log
  type: Directory          # ← Must already exist
containers:
- name: reader
image: busybox
command: ["sleep", "3600"]
volumeMounts:
- name: host-logs
  mountPath: /host-logs
  readOnly: true           # ← Security: read-only access

<span class="comment"># hostPath type options:</span>
<span class="comment"># ""                    - no checks (default)</span>
<span class="comment"># DirectoryOrCreate     - creates dir if missing</span>
<span class="comment"># Directory             - must exist, must be dir</span>
<span class="comment"># FileOrCreate          - creates file if missing</span>
<span class="comment"># File                  - must exist, must be file</span>
<span class="comment"># Socket                - must be Unix socket</span>
<span class="comment"># CharDevice            - must be char device</span>
<span class="comment"># BlockDevice           - must be block device</span>

<span class="comment"># Security concerns:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Access to host filesystem = container escape risk</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Pod pinned to specific node (data not portable)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. PodSecurityAdmission may block hostPath mounts</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. ALWAYS use readOnly: true when possible</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>hostPath</code> mounts a file or directory from the host node's filesystem. It's useful for DaemonSets that need node-level access (like log collectors or monitoring agents). On the CKA: (1) always specify the <code>type</code> field for validation, (2) use <code>readOnly: true</code> unless writing is required, (3) remember that <code>hostPath</code> ties pods to specific nodes. The CKA may ask you to mount logs for troubleshooting — know this pattern.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>subPath</code> in a volume mount and when do you use it? Show an example.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Problem: mounting a ConfigMap at /etc/nginx overwrites ALL files</span>
<span class="comment"># Solution: subPath mounts only ONE file</span>

<span class="comment"># Without subPath (BAD — replaces entire /etc/nginx):</span>
volumeMounts:
- name: config-vol
mountPath: /etc/nginx          # ← Entire dir replaced!

<span class="comment"># With subPath (GOOD — only replaces nginx.conf):</span>
volumeMounts:
- name: config-vol
mountPath: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
subPath: nginx.conf            # ← Only this file!

<span class="comment"># Full example:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: nginx-custom
spec:
volumes:
- name: config
configMap:
  name: nginx-config         # ← ConfigMap with key "nginx.conf"
containers:
- name: nginx
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: config
  mountPath: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf  # ← Target file
  subPath: nginx.conf               # ← Key from ConfigMap

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ subPath limitation: NO auto-update!</span>
<span class="comment"># ConfigMap volume without subPath → updates automatically</span>
<span class="comment"># ConfigMap with subPath → does NOT auto-update</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>subPath</code> mounts a single file from a volume instead of the entire volume. Without it, mounting at a directory path replaces everything in that directory. On the CKA, use <code>subPath</code> when mounting a ConfigMap key as a specific file without overwriting other files in the same directory. Critical caveat: <code>subPath</code> prevents auto-update — if you change the ConfigMap, the mounted file won't change until the pod restarts. This is a known CKA trick question.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you inspect volumes and volume mounts of an existing pod using kubectl?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: describe (human-readable)</span>
kubectl describe pod my-pod
<span class="comment"># Look for:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Volumes:</span>
<span class="comment">#     data-vol:</span>
<span class="comment">#       Type: PersistentVolumeClaim</span>
<span class="comment">#       ClaimName: my-pvc</span>
<span class="comment">#   Containers:</span>
<span class="comment">#     app:</span>
<span class="comment">#       Mounts:</span>
<span class="comment">#         /data from data-vol (rw)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: JSONPath (machine-parseable)</span>
<span class="comment"># List all volumes:</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.volumes[*].name}'
<span class="comment"># data-vol config-vol</span>

<span class="comment"># List volume mounts for first container:</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].volumeMounts}'

<span class="comment"># Pretty-print volumes:</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o jsonpath='{range .spec.volumes[*]}{.name}{"\t"}{end}'

<span class="comment"># Method 3: From inside the container</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- mount | grep /data
<span class="comment"># /dev/sda1 on /data type ext4 (rw,relatime)</span>

kubectl exec my-pod -- df -h /data
<span class="comment"># Filesystem  Size  Used  Avail  Use%  Mounted on</span>
<span class="comment"># /dev/sda1   5.0G  42M   4.9G   1%    /data</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three approaches for volume inspection: <code>describe</code> for quick overview, <code>jsonpath</code> for scripting, and <code>exec + mount/df</code> for verifying from inside the pod. On the CKA, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> is fastest — it shows volumes, mounts, and whether they're read-only. The <code>Mounts</code> section in describe output shows <code>(rw)</code> or <code>(ro)</code> — useful for debugging permission issues.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>emptyDir</code> with <code>medium: Memory</code>? When would you use it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: tmpfs-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: cache
emptyDir:
  medium: Memory          # ← Backed by tmpfs (RAM)
  sizeLimit: 256Mi        # ← Optional: cap memory usage
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: cache
  mountPath: /cache

<span class="comment"># Verify it's tmpfs:</span>
kubectl exec tmpfs-pod -- mount | grep cache
<span class="comment"># tmpfs on /cache type tmpfs (rw,relatime,size=262144k)</span>

kubectl exec tmpfs-pod -- df -h /cache
<span class="comment"># Filesystem  Size  Used  Avail  Use%  Mounted on</span>
<span class="comment"># tmpfs       256M  0     256M   0%    /cache</span>

<span class="comment"># Use cases:</span>
<span class="comment"># - High-speed temporary cache</span>
<span class="comment"># - Sensitive data that should never touch disk</span>
<span class="comment"># - Performance-critical scratch space</span>
<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Counts against container's memory limit!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>emptyDir</code> with <code>medium: Memory</code> creates a tmpfs (RAM-backed) volume. It's extremely fast but: (1) data is lost on pod deletion (like normal emptyDir), (2) uses node memory (counts against cgroup limits), (3) if no <code>sizeLimit</code> is set, can consume all memory and cause OOM. On the CKA, this is tested in scenarios like "store temporary encryption keys in memory only" or "high-performance cache." Always set <code>sizeLimit</code> to prevent memory exhaustion.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod spec has <code>volumeMounts</code> but is missing the <code>volumes</code> section. What happens?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Broken spec (volumes missing):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: broken-pod
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: data-vol            # ← References "data-vol"
  mountPath: /data
<span class="comment"># volumes: ← MISSING!</span>

<span class="comment"># Error when applying:</span>
kubectl apply -f broken.yaml
<span class="comment"># Error: pod "broken-pod" spec.containers[0].volumeMounts[0].name:</span>
<span class="comment"># Not found: "data-vol"</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod won't be created!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix — add the volumes section:</span>
spec:
volumes:                       # ← Pod level
- name: data-vol               # ← Must match volumeMount name
emptyDir: {}
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: data-vol             # ← References volumes[].name
  mountPath: /data</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The pod will fail validation — the API server rejects it because the volumeMount references a volume name that doesn't exist. This is a syntax error, not a runtime error. On the CKA, always verify both parts: (1) <code>spec.volumes[]</code> at the pod level to DECLARE the volume, (2) <code>spec.containers[].volumeMounts[]</code> at the container level to MOUNT it. The <code>name</code> field links the two. Forgetting either side is one of the most common YAML mistakes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">List ALL volume types available in Kubernetes with their primary use cases.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Volume Type</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                        <th>CKA Relevance</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>emptyDir</code></td>
                                        <td>Temp scratch space, sidecar sharing</td>
                                        <td>⭐⭐⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>hostPath</code></td>
                                        <td>Node files, DaemonSet logs</td>
                                        <td>⭐⭐⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>persistentVolumeClaim</code></td>
                                        <td>Durable storage via PVC</td>
                                        <td>⭐⭐⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>configMap</code></td>
                                        <td>Config files as volumes</td>
                                        <td>⭐⭐⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>secret</code></td>
                                        <td>Sensitive data as files</td>
                                        <td>⭐⭐⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>downwardAPI</code></td>
                                        <td>Pod metadata as files</td>
                                        <td>⭐⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>projected</code></td>
                                        <td>Combine multiple sources</td>
                                        <td>⭐⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>nfs</code>, <code>csi</code>, <code>iscsi</code></td>
                                        <td>Network/cloud storage</td>
                                        <td>⭐</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the CKA, focus on the 5 most common: <code>emptyDir</code>, <code>hostPath</code>, <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code>, <code>configMap</code>, and <code>secret</code>. Know when to use each. <code>projected</code> volumes combine multiple sources (secrets + configmaps + downwardAPI) into a single mount. CSI volume types are the modern standard for cloud storage but are rarely tested deeply on the CKA — you just need to know they exist.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you mount a single key from a ConfigMap as a specific file without using <code>subPath</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method: Use the ConfigMap volume's items field</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: selective-mount
spec:
volumes:
- name: config-vol
configMap:
  name: app-settings
  items:                      # ← Select specific keys
  - key: database.properties  # ← ConfigMap key
    path: db.conf             # ← Filename in the mount
  - key: app.properties
    path: app.conf
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: config-vol
  mountPath: /etc/config

<span class="comment"># Result:</span>
<span class="comment"># /etc/config/db.conf   ← content of database.properties</span>
<span class="comment"># /etc/config/app.conf  ← content of app.properties</span>
<span class="comment"># Other ConfigMap keys are NOT mounted</span>

<span class="comment"># vs subPath approach:</span>
<span class="comment"># items → selective keys, still replaces dir, auto-updates ✓</span>
<span class="comment"># subPath → single file, preserves other files, no auto-update ✗</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>items</code> field in a ConfigMap volume selects specific keys and maps them to custom filenames. Unlike <code>subPath</code>, this approach still auto-updates when the ConfigMap changes. The tradeoff: <code>items</code> replaces the entire mountPath directory (only selected keys appear). On the CKA, <code>items</code> is preferred when you need selective keys with auto-update. Use <code>subPath</code> only when you need to preserve existing files in the directory.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that uses a <code>downwardAPI</code> volume to expose its own labels and annotations as files.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: downward-pod
labels:
app: web
version: v2
annotations:
owner: team-alpha
spec:
volumes:
- name: pod-info
downwardAPI:
  items:
  - path: "labels"             # ← File name
    fieldRef:
      fieldPath: metadata.labels
  - path: "annotations"
    fieldRef:
      fieldPath: metadata.annotations
  - path: "name"
    fieldRef:
      fieldPath: metadata.name
  - path: "namespace"
    fieldRef:
      fieldPath: metadata.namespace
containers:
- name: app
image: busybox
command: ["sleep", "3600"]
volumeMounts:
- name: pod-info
  mountPath: /etc/podinfo

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl exec downward-pod -- cat /etc/podinfo/labels
<span class="comment"># app="web"</span>
<span class="comment"># version="v2"</span>

kubectl exec downward-pod -- cat /etc/podinfo/name
<span class="comment"># downward-pod</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The Downward API exposes pod metadata to containers via volumes or environment variables. As a volume, each field becomes a file. Available fields: <code>metadata.labels</code>, <code>metadata.annotations</code>, <code>metadata.name</code>, <code>metadata.namespace</code>, <code>metadata.uid</code>, and resource limits/requests via <code>resourceFieldRef</code>. On the CKA, this is less common than ConfigMap/Secret volumes, but knowing it exists helps for debugging scenarios where pods need self-awareness (e.g., registering themselves in a service discovery system).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 21 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch22">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 22 · PersistentVolumes, PVCs &amp; StorageClasses</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">This is the <strong>most heavily tested storage topic</strong> on the CKA exam. The PV/PVC model separates storage provisioning (admin responsibility) from storage consumption (developer responsibility). You'll create PersistentVolumes, bind them with PersistentVolumeClaims, mount them into pods, and set up StorageClasses for automatic dynamic provisioning — all demonstrated with our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> PostgreSQL database setup.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">💾 PV/PVC</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The PV/PVC model — how admin provisioning and developer consumption are separated</li>
                    <li>✅ How to create PersistentVolumes with correct access modes and reclaim policies</li>
                    <li>✅ How to create PersistentVolumeClaims and understand binding rules</li>
                    <li>✅ How to mount PVCs into Pods and Deployments for persistent storage</li>
                    <li>✅ StorageClasses for dynamic provisioning — eliminate manual PV creation</li>
                    <li>✅ Complete PostgreSQL + easypj storage setup using volumeClaimTemplates</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Storage <span class="cka-weight">10%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>PV/PVC Is the #1 Storage Topic on the CKA</h5>
                <p>Expect at least 1-2 questions on PV/PVC creation and binding. Master these commands:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pv,pvc</code> — check PV and PVC status</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;</code> — debug why a PVC is Pending</li>
                    <li>Know all three access modes: <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, <code>ReadOnlyMany</code>, <code>ReadWriteMany</code></li>
                    <li>Know reclaim policies: <code>Retain</code> (keep data), <code>Delete</code> (destroy data)</li>
                    <li>Write PV + PVC YAML from scratch — the exam doesn't always have templates</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>PV/PVC Imperative Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>List PVs (cluster-wide)</td><td><code>kubectl get pv</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List PVCs in namespace</td><td><code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;namespace&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List PVCs all namespaces</td><td><code>kubectl get pvc -A</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Debug Pending PVC</td><td><code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List StorageClasses</td><td><code>kubectl get storageclass</code> (or <code>kubectl get sc</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check PV details</td><td><code>kubectl describe pv &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Change reclaim policy</td><td><code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{"spec":{"persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy":"Retain"}}'</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Delete PV</td><td><code>kubectl delete pv &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PV + PVC + Pod — Complete Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">This 3-part pattern is the most tested storage question on the CKA. Write it from memory.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># 1. PersistentVolume (admin creates)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: my-pv                       <span class="comment"># cluster-scoped, no namespace</span>
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi                    # ◀── total capacity
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                    # ◀── RWO / ROX / RWX
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain  # ◀── Retain / Delete
  storageClassName: manual           # ◀── must match PVC
  hostPath:
    path: /mnt/data                  # ◀── node path (exam default)
---
<span class="comment"># 2. PersistentVolumeClaim (developer creates)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: my-pvc                      <span class="comment"># namespaced</span>
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                    # ◀── must match PV
  storageClassName: manual           # ◀── must match PV
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi                   # ◀── ≤ PV capacity
---
<span class="comment"># 3. Pod using PVC</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: my-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    volumeMounts:
    - name: data-vol
      mountPath: /data
  volumes:
  - name: data-vol
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: my-pvc              # ◀── reference PVC by name</code></pre>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find PV/PVC YAML Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain pv.spec                        <span class="comment"># PV spec fields</span>
kubectl explain pv.spec.capacity               <span class="comment"># storage size</span>
kubectl explain pv.spec.accessModes            <span class="comment"># RWO/ROX/RWX</span>
kubectl explain pv.spec.persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy  <span class="comment"># reclaim policy</span>
kubectl explain pvc.spec                       <span class="comment"># PVC spec fields</span>
kubectl explain pvc.spec.resources.requests    <span class="comment"># request size</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="flow-diagram">
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🔧</span><span>Admin creates PV</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">👨‍💻</span><span>Dev creates PVC</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🔗</span><span>K8s binds PVC→PV</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">📦</span><span>Pod mounts PVC</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">✅</span><span>Data persists!</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">3</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Access Modes (RWO/ROX/RWX)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Reclaim Policies (Retain/Delete)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-purple">
                    <span class="metric-value">4</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">PV Statuses</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">1:1</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">PV↔PVC Binding</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.1 The PV/PVC Model — Separation of Concerns</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes storage uses a <strong>two-layer model</strong> to separate the "how" from the "what". The cluster admin provisions storage (PersistentVolume), and the developer requests storage (PersistentVolumeClaim). Kubernetes automatically binds matching PVs to PVCs based on size, access mode, and StorageClass.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The PV/PVC Model — Admin vs Developer Responsibilities</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  THE PV / PVC MODEL — SEPARATION OF CONCERNS                            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  🔧 CLUSTER ADMIN                    👨‍💻 DEVELOPER                       │
  │  "I provision storage"               "I request storage"                 │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────┐           ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │  │  PersistentVolume    │           │  PersistentVolume    │            │
  │  │  (PV)                │           │  Claim (PVC)         │            │
  │  │                      │           │                      │            │
  │  │  capacity: 10Gi      │           │  request: 5Gi        │            │
  │  │  accessModes:        │           │  accessModes:        │            │
  │  │    - ReadWriteOnce   │◀─── BIND ─▶│    - ReadWriteOnce   │            │
  │  │  storageClass: fast  │           │  storageClass: fast  │            │
  │  │  hostPath:           │           │                      │            │
  │  │    /data/postgres    │           │                      │            │
  │  └──────────────────────┘           └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │                                                │                        │
  │  Admin doesn't need to know                    │ Developer doesn't      │
  │  WHICH pod will use it                         │ need to know WHERE     │
  │                                                │ the storage is         │
  │                                                ▼                        │
  │                                     ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │                                     │  Pod / Deployment    │            │
  │                                     │                      │            │
  │                                     │  volumes:            │            │
  │                                     │  - persistentVolume  │            │
  │                                     │    Claim:            │            │
  │                                     │    claimName: my-pvc │            │
  │                                     │                      │            │
  │                                     │  volumeMounts:       │            │
  │                                     │  - mountPath: /data  │            │
  │                                     └──────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
  │  BINDING RULES:                                                          │
  │  ✅ PVC.request ≤ PV.capacity        (size must fit)                    │
  │  ✅ PVC.accessModes ⊆ PV.accessModes (modes must match)                │
  │  ✅ PVC.storageClass = PV.storageClass (class must match)               │
  │  ❌ No match? → PVC stays "Pending" until a matching PV appears        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Admin Creates PV</h5>
                        <p>Defines capacity, access modes, reclaim policy, and points to actual storage (hostPath, NFS, cloud disk)</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Developer Creates PVC</h5>
                        <p>Requests storage size and access mode. Doesn't need to know where storage lives.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Kubernetes Binds</h5>
                        <p>Finds a PV that matches the PVC's requirements. PVC status changes from Pending → Bound.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Pod Mounts PVC</h5>
                        <p>Pod references the PVC by name. The bound PV's storage appears at the specified mountPath.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Data Persists ✅</h5>
                        <p>Pod dies, restarts, gets rescheduled — PVC binding survives. New pod mounts same data.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>🔬 PV/PVC Status Lifecycle</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📋</span> Available</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">PV exists but no PVC has claimed it yet. It's ready to be bound.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔗</span> Bound</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">PV is bound to a PVC. One-to-one relationship. The PV is now in use.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🔓</span> Released</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">PVC was deleted but PV still has data from the old claim. Admin must manually reclaim it (if Retain policy).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">❌</span> Failed</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Automatic reclamation failed. PV needs manual intervention.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.2 Creating PersistentVolumes</h3>
                <p>A PersistentVolume is a <strong>cluster-level resource</strong> — it doesn't belong to any namespace. The admin creates it with a specific capacity, access mode, reclaim policy, and storage backend.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pv-hostpath.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PersistentVolume with hostPath (for local testing / CKA exam)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: easypj-data-pv           # ◀── cluster-scoped, no namespace
  labels:
    type: local
    app: easypj
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi                 # ◀── total storage this PV provides
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                 # ◀── can be mounted read-write by ONE node
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain    # ◀── keep data when PVC deleted
  storageClassName: manual        # ◀── must match PVC's storageClassName
  hostPath:
    path: /data/easypj-postgres   # ◀── directory on the node
    type: DirectoryOrCreate</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Access Mode</th>
                                <th>Abbreviation</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>Typical Use</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadWriteOnce</code></td>
                                <td>RWO</td>
                                <td>Read-write by a <strong>single node</strong></td>
                                <td>Databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadOnlyMany</code></td>
                                <td>ROX</td>
                                <td>Read-only by <strong>multiple nodes</strong></td>
                                <td>Shared config, static assets</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadWriteMany</code></td>
                                <td>RWX</td>
                                <td>Read-write by <strong>multiple nodes</strong></td>
                                <td>Shared uploads, NFS-backed</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ReadWriteOncePod</code></td>
                                <td>RWOP</td>
                                <td>Read-write by a <strong>single pod</strong></td>
                                <td>Exclusive access (K8s 1.29+)</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Access Modes Are About NODES, Not Pods</h5>
                    <p><code>ReadWriteOnce</code> means only <strong>one node</strong> can mount the volume for writing. Multiple pods on that same node CAN all mount it. This is a common CKA misconception. For true single-pod exclusivity, use <code>ReadWriteOncePod</code> (v1.29+).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Reclaim Policy</th>
                                <th>What Happens When PVC Deleted</th>
                                <th>Use When</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Retain</code></td>
                                <td>PV keeps data, status → Released. Admin must manually clean up and re-provision.</td>
                                <td>Production databases, critical data you can't lose</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Delete</code></td>
                                <td>PV AND underlying storage deleted automatically. Data is gone.</td>
                                <td>Dynamic provisioning, temporary/recreatable data</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Recycle</code></td>
                                <td>Runs <code>rm -rf</code> on the volume and makes it Available again. <strong>Deprecated.</strong></td>
                                <td>Don't use — deprecated since K8s 1.15</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pv-nfs.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PersistentVolume backed by NFS (multi-node shared storage)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: easypj-uploads-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 20Gi
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteMany                 # ◀── NFS supports multi-node read-write
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: nfs
  nfs:
    server: 192.168.1.100         # ◀── NFS server IP
    path: /exports/easypj-uploads # ◀── shared directory on NFS server</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.3 Creating PersistentVolumeClaims</h3>
                <p>A PVC is the developer's side of the equation — a <strong>request</strong> for storage. Unlike PVs (cluster-scoped), PVCs are <strong>namespaced</strong>. When you create a PVC, Kubernetes searches for a PV that meets the requirements. If found, the PVC binds to it. If not, the PVC stays in <code>Pending</code> state.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pvc-postgres.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PVC requesting storage for easypj PostgreSQL database</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: easypj-postgres-pvc      # ◀── namespaced resource
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                 # ◀── must match PV's access modes
  storageClassName: manual        # ◀── must match PV's storageClassName
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi                # ◀── must be ≤ PV's capacity (10Gi)
<span class="comment"># Binding: K8s finds easypj-data-pv (10Gi, RWO, manual) → MATCH → Bound!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">PVC Binding Logic — How Kubernetes Matches PVCs to PVs</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  PVC BINDING DECISION TREE                                               │
  │                                                                          │
  │  New PVC created: requests 5Gi, RWO, storageClass: "manual"             │
  │       │                                                                  │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  Step 1: Filter by storageClassName       │                           │
  │  │  Looking for PVs with class = "manual"    │                           │
  │  └────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                           │
  │                   │                                                      │
  │       ┌───────────┴───────────┐                                         │
  │       ▼                       ▼                                         │
  │  ┌─────────┐            ┌─────────┐                                    │
  │  │ PV-A    │            │ PV-B    │                                    │
  │  │ 10Gi    │            │ 5Gi     │                                    │
  │  │ manual  │ ✅          │ fast    │ ❌ wrong class                     │
  │  └────┬────┘            └─────────┘                                    │
  │       │                                                                  │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  Step 2: Filter by accessModes            │                           │
  │  │  PVC needs RWO → PV-A has RWO?           │                           │
  │  │  YES ✅                                   │                           │
  │  └────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                           │
  │                   │                                                      │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  Step 3: Check capacity                   │                           │
  │  │  PVC requests 5Gi ≤ PV-A has 10Gi?       │                           │
  │  │  YES ✅                                   │                           │
  │  └────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘                           │
  │                   │                                                      │
  │       ▼                                                                  │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                           │
  │  │  ✅ BOUND!                                │                           │
  │  │  PVC → PV-A                               │                           │
  │  │  PVC gets full 10Gi (even though it       │                           │
  │  │  only asked for 5Gi — smallest match)     │                           │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────┘                           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Debugging: PVC Stuck in Pending</h5>
                    <p>If a PVC shows <code>Pending</code> status, run <code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;</code> and check the Events section. Common causes:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>No PV with matching <code>storageClassName</code></li>
                        <li>No PV with sufficient capacity</li>
                        <li>No PV with matching access modes</li>
                        <li>All matching PVs are already <code>Bound</code> to other PVCs</li>
                        <li>If using dynamic provisioning: the StorageClass provisioner is not installed</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Verify PV/PVC binding</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check PV and PVC status</span>
kubectl get pv
<span class="comment"># NAME              CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   RECLAIM POLICY   STATUS   CLAIM</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-data-pv    10Gi       RWO            Retain           Bound    easypj-production/easypj-postgres-pvc</span>

kubectl get pvc -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># NAME                   STATUS   VOLUME            CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-postgres-pvc    Bound    easypj-data-pv    10Gi       RWO</span>

<span class="comment"># Debug a Pending PVC</span>
kubectl describe pvc easypj-postgres-pvc -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  ProvisioningFailed  no persistent volumes available for this claim</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.4 Using PVCs in Pods</h3>
                <p>Once a PVC is Bound, you reference it in your Pod spec exactly like any other volume. The pod doesn't know or care whether the PVC is backed by a local disk, NFS, or cloud storage — it just mounts a directory.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deployment-with-pvc.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj Deployment using PVC for PostgreSQL data</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        volumeMounts:
        - name: user-uploads          # ◀── mount name (matches volumes[])
          mountPath: /app/media       # ◀── path INSIDE the container
        - name: static-files
          mountPath: /app/staticfiles
          readOnly: true
      volumes:
      - name: user-uploads            # ◀── references the PVC
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: easypj-uploads-pvc   # ◀── name of the PVC
      - name: static-files
        configMap:
          name: easypj-static-config</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete Flow: PV → PVC → Pod → Container Mount</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  COMPLETE MOUNT FLOW                                                     │
  │                                                                          │
  │  PersistentVolume          PVC                   Pod                     │
  │  ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐       │
  │  │ easypj-pv    │    │ easypj-pvc   │    │ easypj-web-abc       │       │
  │  │              │    │              │    │                      │       │
  │  │ /data/uploads│◀──▶│ BOUND        │◀───│ volumes:             │       │
  │  │ on node disk │    │              │    │ - pvc: easypj-pvc   │       │
  │  │              │    │              │    │                      │       │
  │  │ 10Gi, RWO    │    │ 5Gi request  │    │ containers:          │       │
  │  └──────────────┘    └──────────────┘    │ - volumeMounts:      │       │
  │                                          │   - /app/media ◀─┐  │       │
  │                                          └──────────────────┼──┘       │
  │                                                             │           │
  │  What the container sees:                                   │           │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐                       │           │
  │  │  / (container root)              │                       │           │
  │  │  ├── app/                        │                       │           │
  │  │  │   ├── manage.py               │                       │           │
  │  │  │   ├── easypj/                 │                       │           │
  │  │  │   └── media/ ◀───────────────────── PVC mounted here  │           │
  │  │  │       ├── resumes/            │                       │           │
  │  │  │       │   └── john_doe.pdf    │   Data on external    │           │
  │  │  │       └── photos/             │   storage, not in     │           │
  │  │  │           └── profile.jpg     │   the container!      │           │
  │  │  └── ...                         │                       │           │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────┘                       │           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 PVC Must Be in the Same Namespace as the Pod</h5>
                    <p>PVCs are namespaced resources. A pod in <code>easypj-production</code> can only mount PVCs that also exist in <code>easypj-production</code>. PVs themselves are cluster-scoped, but the PVC (the claim) must be co-located with the pod.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.5 StorageClasses — Dynamic Provisioning</h3>
                <p>StorageClasses eliminate the need for an admin to pre-create PVs. When a developer creates a PVC with a <code>storageClassName</code>, and that class has a <strong>provisioner</strong>, Kubernetes automatically creates a matching PV on the fly. This is how most production clusters work.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Static vs Dynamic Provisioning</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  STATIC PROVISIONING (manual)        DYNAMIC PROVISIONING (automatic)    │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐            ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │  │ 1. Admin creates PV │            │ 1. Admin creates     │            │
  │  │    (10Gi, RWO, host)│            │    StorageClass      │            │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘            │    (provisioner,     │            │
  │            │                        │     params)          │            │
  │            ▼                        └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐                       │  (one-time setup)      │
  │  │ 2. Dev creates PVC  │                       │                        │
  │  │    (5Gi, RWO)       │                       ▼                        │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘            ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │            │                        │ 2. Dev creates PVC   │            │
  │            ▼                        │    with storageClass │            │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐            └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │  │ 3. K8s binds        │                       │                        │
  │  │    PVC → PV         │                       ▼                        │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘            ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │            │                        │ 3. StorageClass auto │            │
  │            ▼                        │    creates PV! 🎉    │            │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐            └──────────┬───────────┘            │
  │  │ 4. Pod mounts PVC   │                       │                        │
  │  └─────────────────────┘                       ▼                        │
  │                                     ┌──────────────────────┐            │
  │  Every new PVC = admin must         │ 4. Auto-bind + mount │            │
  │  manually create a PV first 😰     └──────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                          │
  │                                     Admin sets up ONCE.                  │
  │                                     Every new PVC = auto PV! 🎉        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># StorageClass for dynamic provisioning</span>
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-ssd                      # ◀── the name PVCs reference
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner  # ◀── for local testing
#provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com         # ◀── AWS EBS

#provisioner: pd.csi.storage.gke.io   # ◀── GCE Persistent Disk

#provisioner: disk.csi.azure.com      # ◀── Azure Disk

reclaimPolicy: Delete                  # ◀── auto-delete PV when PVC deleted
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer  # ◀── delay binding until pod scheduled
allowVolumeExpansion: true             # ◀── allow PVC resize</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pvc-dynamic.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># PVC using dynamic provisioning — no PV needed, auto-created!</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: easypj-dynamic-pvc
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: fast-ssd          # ◀── references StorageClass above
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi
<span class="comment"># Result: StorageClass provisioner creates a 10Gi PV automatically</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC binds to the new PV → Ready to use in pods!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Static Provisioning</th>
                                <th>Dynamic Provisioning</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>PV creation</td>
                                <td>Manual by admin</td>
                                <td>Automatic by StorageClass</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Admin workload</td>
                                <td>High — create PV per request</td>
                                <td>Low — one-time StorageClass setup</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Storage waste</td>
                                <td>PVs may sit unused</td>
                                <td>PV created on-demand, no waste</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>CKA exam</td>
                                <td>✅ Tested (manual PV+PVC)</td>
                                <td>✅ Tested (StorageClass config)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Production use</td>
                                <td>Rare (legacy/special cases)</td>
                                <td>Standard practice in most clusters</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Default StorageClass</h5>
                    <p>Most clusters have a <strong>default StorageClass</strong>. If you create a PVC without specifying <code>storageClassName</code>, the default class is used automatically. Check with <code>kubectl get storageclass</code> — the default has <code>(default)</code> annotation. On the CKA exam, there's usually a default class already configured.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §22.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s22-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">22.6 PostgreSQL Storage for easypj</h3>
                <p>Let's put everything together: a <strong>complete production storage setup</strong> for PostgreSQL backing our easypj/jobpost Django application. This uses a StorageClass, PVC via <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> in a StatefulSet, and proper data directory mounting.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Complete easypj PostgreSQL Storage Architecture</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  easypj POSTGRESQL STORAGE — COMPLETE SETUP                              │
  │                                                                          │
  │  StorageClass: "postgres-storage"                                        │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────┐                                      │
  │  │  provisioner: local/hostPath   │                                      │
  │  │  reclaimPolicy: Retain         │ ◀── never delete DB data            │
  │  │  volumeBindingMode: WFFC       │                                      │
  │  └──────────────┬─────────────────┘                                      │
  │                 │ creates PV automatically                               │
  │                 ▼                                                         │
  │  PersistentVolumeClaim: "postgres-data-postgres-0"                       │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────┐                                      │
  │  │  10Gi, ReadWriteOnce           │                                      │
  │  │  storageClass: postgres-storage│                                      │
  │  │  STATUS: Bound ✅              │                                      │
  │  └──────────────┬─────────────────┘                                      │
  │                 │ mounted into                                           │
  │                 ▼                                                         │
  │  StatefulSet: "postgres"                                                 │
  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐             │
  │  │  Pod: postgres-0                                       │             │
  │  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │             │
  │  │  │  Container: postgres                              │  │             │
  │  │  │  image: postgres:15                               │  │             │
  │  │  │                                                    │  │             │
  │  │  │  /var/lib/postgresql/data  ◀── PVC mounted here   │  │             │
  │  │  │  ├── base/        (databases)                     │  │             │
  │  │  │  ├── global/      (cluster-wide tables)           │  │             │
  │  │  │  ├── pg_wal/      (write-ahead logs)              │  │             │
  │  │  │  └── postgresql.conf                              │  │             │
  │  │  │                                                    │  │             │
  │  │  │  Data survives:                                    │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ Container restart                              │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ Pod deletion                                   │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ StatefulSet scaling                            │  │             │
  │  │  │  ✅ Rolling updates                                │  │             │
  │  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │             │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘             │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-postgres-complete.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── 1. StorageClass ──
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: postgres-storage
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner
reclaimPolicy: Retain                 # ◀── NEVER auto-delete DB data
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
---
<span class="comment"># ── 2. PersistentVolume (for local/exam clusters) ──</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: postgres-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  storageClassName: postgres-storage
  hostPath:
    path: /data/postgres
    type: DirectoryOrCreate
---
<span class="comment"># ── 3. StatefulSet with volumeClaimTemplates ──</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: postgres
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  serviceName: postgres               # ◀── headless service name
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: postgres
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: postgres
        image: postgres:15
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5432
        env:
        - name: POSTGRES_DB
          value: easypj_db
        - name: POSTGRES_USER
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: postgres-credentials
              key: username
        - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: postgres-credentials
              key: password
        - name: PGDATA                 # ◀── PostgreSQL data subdirectory
          value: /var/lib/postgresql/data/pgdata
        volumeMounts:
        - name: postgres-data          # ◀── matches volumeClaimTemplates
          mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: 250m
            memory: 256Mi
          limits:
            cpu: "1"
            memory: 1Gi
  volumeClaimTemplates:                # ◀── auto-creates PVC per replica
  - metadata:
      name: postgres-data              # ◀── PVC name = postgres-data-postgres-0
    spec:
      accessModes:
      - ReadWriteOnce
      storageClassName: postgres-storage
      resources:
        requests:
          storage: 10Gi
---
<span class="comment"># ── 4. Headless Service for StatefulSet ──</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: postgres
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  clusterIP: None                      # ◀── headless = direct pod DNS
  selector:
    app: postgres
  ports:
  - port: 5432
<span class="comment"># DNS: postgres-0.postgres.easypj-production.svc.cluster.local</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Verify the complete setup</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply the complete storage stack</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-postgres-complete.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify StorageClass</span>
kubectl get storageclass postgres-storage
<span class="comment"># NAME               PROVISIONER                    RECLAIMPOLICY</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-storage   kubernetes.io/no-provisioner   Retain</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify PV is bound</span>
kubectl get pv postgres-pv
<span class="comment"># NAME          CAPACITY   ACCESS   STATUS   CLAIM</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-pv   10Gi       RWO      Bound    easypj-production/postgres-data-postgres-0</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify PVC is bound</span>
kubectl get pvc -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># NAME                       STATUS   VOLUME        CAPACITY</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-data-postgres-0   Bound    postgres-pv   10Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify pod is running with mounted volume</span>
kubectl describe pod postgres-0 -n easypj-production | grep -A5 "Mounts:"
<span class="comment">#     Mounts:</span>
<span class="comment">#       /var/lib/postgresql/data from postgres-data (rw)</span>

<span class="comment"># Test data persistence: kill the pod, check data survives</span>
kubectl delete pod postgres-0 -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># StatefulSet recreates postgres-0 → PVC re-mounts → data intact! ✅</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🏋️ CKA Practice Scenario: Create PV and PVC from Scratch</h5>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Task:</strong> Create a PersistentVolume named <code>exam-pv</code> with 2Gi capacity, <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, storageClass <code>manual</code>, backed by hostPath <code>/mnt/data</code>. Then create a PVC named <code>exam-pvc</code> requesting 1Gi from that PV. Verify binding.</p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">CKA Solution</span>
                            </div>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create PV</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: exam-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 2Gi
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: manual
  hostPath:
    path: /mnt/data
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create PVC</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: exam-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce
  storageClassName: manual
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 1Gi
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify binding</span>
kubectl get pv exam-pv
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Bound   CLAIM: default/exam-pvc ✅</span>

kubectl get pvc exam-pvc
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Bound   VOLUME: exam-pv ✅</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Chapter 22 PV Lifecycle State Machine -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔄 PersistentVolume State Machine — Complete Lifecycle</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  PV LIFECYCLE STATE MACHINE                                              │
  │                                                                          │
  │                   ┌──────────────┐                                       │
  │                   │  PV Created  │                                       │
  │                   │  by Admin    │                                       │
  │                   └──────┬───────┘                                       │
  │                          │                                               │
  │                          ▼                                               │
  │               ┌──────────────────┐                                       │
  │         ┌────▶│   AVAILABLE ☁️    │ No PVC has claimed this PV           │
  │         │     └────────┬─────────┘                                       │
  │         │              │ PVC created with matching criteria               │
  │         │              ▼                                                  │
  │         │     ┌──────────────────┐                                       │
  │         │     │    BOUND 🔗      │ PV bound to PVC (1:1 relationship)    │
  │         │     └────────┬─────────┘                                       │
  │         │              │ PVC deleted                                      │
  │         │              ▼                                                  │
  │         │     ┌──────────────────┐                                       │
  │         │     │   RELEASED 🔓   │ PV still has old data                  │
  │         │     └────────┬─────────┘                                       │
  │         │              │                                                  │
  │         │    ┌─────────┴─────────┐                                       │
  │         │    │                   │                                        │
  │         │    ▼                   ▼                                        │
  │         │  Retain policy      Delete policy                              │
  │         │  ┌────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐                           │
  │         │  │Admin must  │     │PV + storage  │                           │
  │         │  │clean up PV │     │deleted auto  │                           │
  │         │  │manually    │     │💀 Data gone! │                           │
  │         │  └─────┬──────┘     └──────────────┘                           │
  │         │        │ kubectl delete pv + recreate                           │
  │         └────────┘                                                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ❌ FAILED: Auto-reclaim failed → admin must fix manually               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Storage Capacity Planning Card -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>📊 Storage Capacity Planning — easypj</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>PostgreSQL data:</strong> 10Gi (grows ~1Gi/month with 10K job posts)</li>
                                <li><strong>User uploads (resumes):</strong> 20Gi (avg 200KB × 100K files)</li>
                                <li><strong>Static files (CSS/JS):</strong> emptyDir (regenerated, ~50MB)</li>
                                <li><strong>Redis cache:</strong> emptyDir with <code>medium: Memory</code> (256Mi)</li>
                                <li><strong>Logs:</strong> emptyDir (100Mi, forwarded to log aggregator)</li>
                                <li><strong>Total persistent storage needed:</strong> ~30Gi</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>⌨️ Chapter 22 — Must-Know kubectl Commands</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><code>kubectl get pv</code> — list all PVs (cluster-wide)</li>
                                <li><code>kubectl get pvc -A</code> — list PVCs across all namespaces</li>
                                <li><code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;</code> — debug binding issues</li>
                                <li><code>kubectl get sc</code> — list StorageClasses</li>
                                <li><code>kubectl patch pv &lt;name&gt; -p '{"spec":{"persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy":"Retain"}}'</code> — change reclaim policy</li>
                                <li><code>kubectl get events --field-selector reason=ProvisioningFailed</code> — find provisioning errors</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Time-Saver: PVC Expansion</h5>
                    <p>If a PVC needs more storage and the StorageClass has <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code>, you can simply <code>kubectl edit pvc &lt;name&gt;</code> and increase <code>requests.storage</code>. The PV resizes automatically (for cloud-backed storage). On the CKA exam, check if expansion is enabled: <code>kubectl get sc &lt;name&gt; -o yaml | grep allowVolumeExpansion</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 22 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: PV/PVC/StorageClass Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>PVC stuck in <code>Pending</code></strong> — Top 3 causes: (1) No PV matches the requested size/accessMode, (2) StorageClass name mismatch, (3) No default StorageClass and PVC doesn't specify one. Debug with <code>kubectl describe pvc</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Access modes must match</strong> — PV offers <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> but PVC requests <code>ReadWriteMany</code>? Won't bind. Know the modes: <code>RWO</code> (single node), <code>ROX</code> (many nodes read-only), <code>RWX</code> (many nodes read-write)</li>
                        <li><strong>Reclaim policies</strong> — <code>Retain</code>: PV data preserved after PVC deletion (manual cleanup). <code>Delete</code>: PV and backing storage removed. <code>Recycle</code>: deprecated. Default for dynamic PVs is <code>Delete</code> — dangerous for production!</li>
                        <li><strong><code>storageClassName: ""</code> vs omitting it</strong> — Empty string means "no StorageClass" (only matches PVs with no class). Omitting it uses the cluster's default StorageClass. This subtle difference is a classic CKA trap</li>
                        <li><strong>PV is cluster-scoped, PVC is namespace-scoped</strong> — <code>kubectl get pv</code> (no -n flag needed). <code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;ns&gt;</code>. A PVC in namespace <code>prod</code> can bind to any available PV</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How PVs and PVCs Bind: The Matching Algorithm, Access Modes, Dynamic Provisioning, and CSI Driver Architecture</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ PV-PVC Binding Algorithm — How Kubernetes Matches Claims to Volumes</h5>
            <p>The persistent volume controller runs a matching loop that pairs PVCs with PVs. Understanding the exact matching criteria helps you debug "PVC stuck in Pending" issues.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  PV-PVC BINDING ALGORITHM — Step by Step                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  PVC: easypj-media (requests: storage=10Gi, accessModes=[RWO])               │
│  Available PVs:                                                               │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: 100Gi, RWO, Retain    PV-2: 10Gi, RWO, Delete                │    │
│  │ PV-3: 10Gi, RWX, Retain     PV-4: 5Gi,  RWO, Retain                │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: FILTER by storage size (PVC requests 10Gi)                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: 100Gi >= 10Gi ✅     PV-2: 10Gi >= 10Gi ✅                    │    │
│  │ PV-3: 10Gi >= 10Gi ✅     PV-4: 5Gi >= 10Gi  ❌ (too small)        │    │
│  │ Remaining: PV-1, PV-2, PV-3                                          │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: FILTER by access mode (PVC requests RWO)                             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: RWO ✅              PV-2: RWO ✅                               │    │
│  │ PV-3: RWX ✅ (RWX includes RWO capability)                           │    │
│  │ Remaining: PV-1, PV-2, PV-3                                          │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ ACCESS MODE COMPATIBILITY:                                            │    │
│  │ ┌──────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │ │ RWO      │ ReadWriteOnce — single node read/write             │    │    │
│  │ │ ROX      │ ReadOnlyMany — many nodes read only               │    │    │
│  │ │ RWX      │ ReadWriteMany — many nodes read/write              │    │    │
│  │ │ RWOP     │ ReadWriteOncePod — single pod (new in 1.29)        │    │    │
│  │ └──────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: SELECT best match (smallest sufficient PV wins)                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PV-1: 100Gi → EXCESSIVE (wastes 90Gi)                               │    │
│  │ PV-2: 10Gi  → PERFECT MATCH 🏆 (exact size)                        │    │
│  │ PV-3: 10Gi  → Also matches, but PV-2 selected first                 │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ SELECTION RULES:                                                     │    │
│  │ 1. Smallest PV >= PVC request wins                                   │    │
│  │ 2. If same size, PV created earliest wins                            │    │
│  │ 3. Binding is EXCLUSIVE — one PV = one PVC                           │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  AFTER BINDING:                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ PVC status: Bound → PV-2                                              │    │
│  │ PV status:   Bound → Claim: default/easypj-media                     │    │
│  │ PV-2 is now UNAVAILABLE for any other PVC                             │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  STORAGECLASS — Dynamic Provisioning:                                        │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Without StorageClass: Admin creates PV manually → PVC binds          │    │
│  │ With StorageClass:     PVC specifies StorageClass → provisioner      │    │
│  │                        AUTO-CREATES PV → PVC binds automatically     │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  CSI (Container Storage Interface) Drivers:                          │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ 1. PVC created with storageClassName: aws-ebs               │     │    │
│  │  │ 2. StorageClass provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com                │     │    │
│  │  │ 3. CSI provisioner calls AWS API: CreateVolume(10Gi)        │     │    │
│  │  │ 4. AWS creates EBS volume → returns volumeID                 │     │    │
│  │  │ 5. CSI creates PV object with volumeHandle=&lt;volumeID&gt;       │     │    │
│  │  │ 6. PV binds to PVC                                             │     │    │
│  │  │ 7. Pod created → CSI node plugin ATTACHES volume to node    │     │    │
│  │  │ 8. CSI node plugin MOUNTS volume into container              │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  RECLAIM POLICIES — What Happens When PVC Is Deleted:                        │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Retain:  PV stays (Released). Admin must MANUALLY delete + clean    │    │
│  │          up underlying storage. Data persists.                       │    │
│  │ Delete:  PV + underlying storage DELETED automatically. Data gone.  │    │
│  │ Recycle: DEPRECATED. Was: scrub data (rm -rf) → make Available.    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> PVC stuck in Pending? Check: (1) <code>kubectl describe pvc</code> — what's the error? "no persistent volumes available" = create a PV or StorageClass. (2) <code>kubectl get pv</code> — any Available PVs with sufficient capacity and matching access mode? (3) StorageClass with <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays binding until a pod uses the PVC — this is NORMAL (pod must be scheduled first for topology-aware provisioning).
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">PVC size must exactly match PV size</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">PVC request must be ≤ PV capacity — K8s picks the smallest matching PV</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">ReadWriteOnce means only one pod can mount it</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">RWO means one NODE. Multiple pods on same node can all mount it</span></li>
                    <li><code>storageClassName</code> must match EXACTLY between PV and PVC — empty string ≠ omitted</li>
                    <li>PV is <strong>cluster-scoped</strong> but PVC is <strong>namespaced</strong> — pod and PVC must be in same namespace</li>
                    <li><code>Recycle</code> reclaim policy is <strong>deprecated</strong> — use <code>Retain</code> or <code>Delete</code></li>
                    <li>After PVC deletion with <code>Retain</code> policy, PV stays <code>Released</code> — must delete and recreate PV to reuse</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — PV/PVC binding checklist (memorize this):</strong><br>
                For a PVC to bind to a PV, ALL must match:<br>
                ✅ Sufficient capacity (PV size ≥ PVC request)<br>
                ✅ Compatible access mode (at least one in common)<br>
                ✅ Matching StorageClass (or both empty)<br>
                ✅ PV status must be <code>Available</code> (not <code>Released</code> or <code>Bound</code>)<br>
                ✅ Label selectors match (if PVC uses <code>selector</code>)</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 22)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 8 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a PV: 5Gi, <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, <code>hostPath: /mnt/data</code>, reclaim policy <code>Retain</code></li>
                    <li>2. Create a PVC requesting 3Gi with <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> — verify it binds</li>
                    <li>3. Create a pod that mounts the PVC at <code>/data</code></li>
                    <li>4. Write a file, delete the pod, recreate it — verify data persists</li>
                    <li>5. Delete the PVC — check PV status (should be <code>Released</code>, not <code>Available</code>)</li>
                    <li>6. Create a StorageClass with <code>provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner</code> and <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 22 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch22-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 22 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the PV/PVC binding lifecycle — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a PersistentVolume with 10Gi capacity, <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> access mode, <code>hostPath</code> at <code>/mnt/data</code>, and reclaim policy <code>Retain</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
name: pv-data
<span class="comment"># Note: PVs are cluster-scoped — no namespace!</span>
spec:
capacity:
storage: 10Gi
accessModes:
- ReadWriteOnce            # ← Single node read-write
persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain   # ← Keep data after PVC deletion
hostPath:
path: /mnt/data
type: DirectoryOrCreate

<span class="comment"># Apply and verify:</span>
kubectl apply -f pv.yaml
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS MODES  RECLAIM POLICY  STATUS     AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-data  10Gi      RWO           Retain          Available  5s</span>

<span class="comment"># Access mode abbreviations:</span>
<span class="comment"># RWO = ReadWriteOnce (single node)</span>
<span class="comment"># ROX = ReadOnlyMany  (many nodes, read-only)</span>
<span class="comment"># RWX = ReadWriteMany (many nodes, read-write)</span>
<span class="comment"># RWOP = ReadWriteOncePod (single pod, K8s 1.22+)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PersistentVolumes are cluster-scoped resources (no namespace). Key fields: <code>capacity</code> (must match/exceed PVC request), <code>accessModes</code> (must be compatible), <code>persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy</code> (what happens when PVC is deleted). <code>Retain</code> preserves data for manual cleanup, <code>Delete</code> removes backing storage. On the CKA, <code>hostPath</code> is the most common PV type (lab environments). Always use <code>Retain</code> for important data — the default for dynamically provisioned PVs is <code>Delete</code>!</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a PVC that binds to the PV from Q1. Then create a pod that mounts it at <code>/data</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># PVC:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: pvc-data
namespace: default         # ← PVCs are namespace-scoped!
spec:
accessModes:
- ReadWriteOnce            # ← Must match PV
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi           # ← Can be ≤ PV capacity (10Gi)
<span class="comment"># storageClassName: ""     # ← Omit for static binding</span>

---
<span class="comment"># Pod using the PVC:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: data-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: data-vol
persistentVolumeClaim:
  claimName: pvc-data    # ← Reference the PVC name
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: data-vol
  mountPath: /data

<span class="comment"># Verify binding:</span>
kubectl get pvc pvc-data
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS  VOLUME   CAPACITY  ACCESS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pvc-data   Bound   pv-data  10Gi      RWO     5s</span>

kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Bound  ← PV is now bound to pvc-data</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVC binding rules: PVC request ≤ PV capacity, access modes compatible, StorageClass matches. Once bound, the PVC gets the FULL PV capacity (5Gi requested but 10Gi available). The pod references the PVC by name in <code>persistentVolumeClaim.claimName</code>. On the CKA, the three-object chain is: PV (admin creates) → PVC (developer creates) → Pod (developer mounts PVC). If the PVC stays <code>Pending</code>, check: size, access mode, and StorageClass matching.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A PVC is stuck in <code>Pending</code> state. How do you diagnose the issue?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Describe the PVC for events</span>
kubectl describe pvc stuck-pvc
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   "no persistent volumes available for this claim"</span>
<span class="comment">#   OR "storageclass 'fast' not found"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check available PVs</span>
kubectl get pv
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS  RECLAIM  STATUS      CLASS    AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-1     5Gi       RWO     Retain   Available   slow     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-2     10Gi      ROX     Delete   Available   fast     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-3     5Gi       RWO     Retain   Bound       -        5m  ← Already taken!</span>

<span class="comment"># Common causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. No PV with enough capacity</span>
kubectl get pvc stuck-pvc -o yaml | grep storage
<span class="comment">#   requests: storage: 20Gi  ← But no PV ≥ 20Gi!</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Access mode mismatch</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC requests RWX but PV only offers RWO</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. StorageClass mismatch</span>
kubectl get pvc stuck-pvc -o yaml | grep storageClassName
<span class="comment"># storageClassName: "premium"  ← No PV with class "premium"!</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. No default StorageClass</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># No default → PVC with no class specified stays Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. WaitForFirstConsumer binding mode</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC stays Pending until a pod references it — this is NORMAL!</span>
kubectl get sc local-storage -o yaml | grep volumeBindingMode
<span class="comment"># volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVC <code>Pending</code> is the most common PV troubleshooting scenario on the CKA. Systematic approach: (1) <code>describe</code> the PVC for error events, (2) check available PVs for capacity/access/class, (3) verify StorageClass exists. Note that <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> is NOT a bug — the PVC intentionally stays Pending until a pod is created, so the PV can be provisioned on the same node as the pod. Always start with <code>kubectl describe pvc</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to a PV when its PVC is deleted? Explain all reclaim policies.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Policy</th>
                                        <th>PV Status After PVC Delete</th>
                                        <th>Data</th>
                                        <th>Next Steps</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Retain</code></td>
                                        <td><code>Released</code></td>
                                        <td>Preserved</td>
                                        <td>Manual cleanup, edit PV to remove claimRef</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Delete</code></td>
                                        <td>PV deleted</td>
                                        <td>Deleted</td>
                                        <td>Nothing — PV and data are gone</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>Recycle</code></td>
                                        <td><code>Available</code></td>
                                        <td>Scrubbed (rm -rf)</td>
                                        <td>Deprecated — don't use</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># To reuse a Retained PV after PVC deletion:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. PV shows "Released" — can't bind a new PVC yet</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Released</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Remove the old claimRef:</span>
kubectl patch pv pv-data -p '{"spec":{"claimRef":null}}'
<span class="comment"># OR:</span>
kubectl edit pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># Delete the entire claimRef section</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. PV returns to Available:</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Available ← Ready for new PVC</span>

<span class="comment"># Change reclaim policy on existing PV:</span>
kubectl patch pv pv-data -p \
'{"spec":{"persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy":"Retain"}}'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Reclaim policy controls PV fate after PVC deletion. <code>Retain</code> is safest — data preserved, PV goes to <code>Released</code> state (NOT <code>Available</code>). To reuse a <code>Released</code> PV, you must remove the <code>claimRef</code>. <code>Delete</code> removes both PV and backing storage (default for dynamic provisioning — dangerous!). On the CKA, changing policy from <code>Delete</code> to <code>Retain</code> with <code>kubectl patch</code> is a common task. Know the <code>Released</code> → <code>Available</code> transition requires manual intervention.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a StorageClass with <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code>. Why is this important?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
name: local-storage
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner  # ← Manual provisioning
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer    # ← KEY!
reclaimPolicy: Retain

<span class="comment"># What it does:</span>
<span class="comment"># Immediate (default):</span>
<span class="comment">#   PVC → PV binding happens immediately</span>
<span class="comment">#   PV might be on node-1</span>
<span class="comment">#   Pod gets scheduled to node-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Pod stuck Pending! PV is on wrong node!</span>

<span class="comment"># WaitForFirstConsumer:</span>
<span class="comment">#   PVC → Stays Pending (normal!)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Pod created → scheduler picks node-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   PV binding happens on node-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   → Pod starts successfully!</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get sc local-storage
<span class="comment"># NAME            PROVISIONER                    RECLAIMPOLICY  VOLUMEBINDINGMODE</span>
<span class="comment"># local-storage   kubernetes.io/no-provisioner   Retain         WaitForFirstConsumer</span>

<span class="comment"># PVC with this StorageClass:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: local-pvc
spec:
storageClassName: local-storage
accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi
<span class="comment"># This PVC stays Pending until a pod uses it!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays PV binding until a pod using the PVC is scheduled. This is critical for local storage (hostPath, local PVs) to ensure the PV is on the same node as the pod. Without it, the PV could bind to a node where no pod runs. On the CKA, if you see a PVC stuck in Pending with <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> and NO pod using it — that's expected behavior, not an error. Create the pod and it will bind. <code>kubernetes.io/no-provisioner</code> means admins must manually create PVs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>storageClassName: ""</code> and omitting <code>storageClassName</code> in a PVC?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Scenario: Cluster has a default StorageClass "standard"</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 1: Omit storageClassName</span>
spec:
accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi
<span class="comment"># storageClassName: ← not specified</span>
<span class="comment"># Result: Uses DEFAULT StorageClass ("standard")</span>
<span class="comment"># → Dynamic provisioning creates PV automatically</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 2: storageClassName: ""</span>
spec:
accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 5Gi
storageClassName: ""     # ← Explicitly empty
<span class="comment"># Result: NO StorageClass used</span>
<span class="comment"># → Only binds to PVs that also have NO class</span>
<span class="comment"># → No dynamic provisioning</span>

<span class="comment"># Option 3: storageClassName: "fast"</span>
spec:
storageClassName: "fast"
<span class="comment"># Result: Only binds to PVs with class "fast"</span>
<span class="comment"># → Or dynamic provisioning via "fast" StorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># Check default StorageClass:</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># NAME                  PROVISIONER          AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># standard (default)    rancher.io/local     5d</span>
<span class="comment">#          ↑ "(default)" annotation</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This is one of the most subtle CKA traps. Omitting <code>storageClassName</code> uses the cluster's default StorageClass (dynamic provisioning). Setting <code>storageClassName: ""</code> explicitly means "no class" — only matches PVs with no class. On the CKA, if you want static binding to a specific PV without dynamic provisioning, use <code>storageClassName: ""</code>. To check the default: <code>kubectl get sc</code> — look for <code>(default)</code> annotation.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Expand a PVC from 5Gi to 10Gi. What conditions must be met?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Requirements for PVC expansion:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. StorageClass must have allowVolumeExpansion: true</span>
kubectl get sc standard -o yaml | grep allowVolumeExpansion
<span class="comment"># allowVolumeExpansion: true  ← Required!</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Simply edit the PVC size:</span>
kubectl edit pvc my-pvc
<span class="comment"># Change: storage: 5Gi → storage: 10Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Or use patch:</span>
kubectl patch pvc my-pvc -p \
'{"spec":{"resources":{"requests":{"storage":"10Gi"}}}}'

<span class="comment"># 3. Check status:</span>
kubectl get pvc my-pvc
<span class="comment"># CAPACITY: 10Gi ← Expanded!</span>

<span class="comment"># For some volume types, expansion requires pod restart:</span>
kubectl describe pvc my-pvc
<span class="comment"># Conditions:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Type: FileSystemResizePending</span>
<span class="comment">#   Message: Waiting for user to restart the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart the pod:</span>
kubectl delete pod my-pod
<span class="comment"># Deployment recreates pod → filesystem expands</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Cannot SHRINK a PVC! Only expansion is supported.</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVC expansion requires <code>allowVolumeExpansion: true</code> on the StorageClass. Just edit the PVC spec to request more storage. Some provisioners expand online (no pod restart needed), others need pod restart (check for <code>FileSystemResizePending</code> condition). On the CKA, this is a straightforward task but watch for: (1) StorageClass doesn't allow expansion (verify first), (2) can never shrink — only grow, (3) may need to restart the pod for filesystem-level resize.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you sort PersistentVolumes by capacity and find all PVCs across all namespaces?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Sort PVs by capacity:</span>
kubectl get pv --sort-by=.spec.capacity.storage
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS  RECLAIM  STATUS     CLASS     AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-sm    1Gi       RWO     Retain   Available  local     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-md    5Gi       RWO     Delete   Bound      standard  10m</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-lg    20Gi      RWX     Retain   Available  fast      2m</span>

<span class="comment"># All PVCs in all namespaces:</span>
kubectl get pvc -A
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE  NAME       STATUS  VOLUME  CAPACITY  ACCESS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># default    data-pvc   Bound   pv-md   5Gi       RWO     10m</span>
<span class="comment"># prod       db-pvc     Bound   pv-lg   20Gi      RWX     5m</span>
<span class="comment"># dev        cache-pvc  Pending -       -         -       2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Custom columns for overview:</span>
kubectl get pv -o custom-columns=\
NAME:.metadata.name,\
SIZE:.spec.capacity.storage,\
STATUS:.status.phase,\
CLAIM:.spec.claimRef.name,\
CLASS:.spec.storageClassName

<span class="comment"># Find which PV is bound to which PVC:</span>
kubectl get pv -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\t"}{.spec.claimRef.namespace}/{.spec.claimRef.name}{"\n"}{end}'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PVs are cluster-scoped (no -n flag needed), PVCs are namespaced (<code>-A</code> to see all). <code>--sort-by</code> uses JSONPath for sorting. On the CKA, you may be asked to find a specific PV/PVC relationship — use the <code>claimRef</code> field on a PV to see which PVC it's bound to. Custom columns are faster than <code>-o wide</code> for specific information. Remember: <code>kubectl get pv</code> (no namespace), <code>kubectl get pvc -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> (needs namespace).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>ReadWriteOnce</code>, <code>ReadOnlyMany</code>, <code>ReadWriteMany</code>, and <code>ReadWriteOncePod</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Mode</th>
                                        <th>Short</th>
                                        <th>Description</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadWriteOnce</code></td>
                                        <td>RWO</td>
                                        <td>Read-write by ONE node</td>
                                        <td>Single database pod</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadOnlyMany</code></td>
                                        <td>ROX</td>
                                        <td>Read-only by many nodes</td>
                                        <td>Shared config/static assets</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadWriteMany</code></td>
                                        <td>RWX</td>
                                        <td>Read-write by many nodes</td>
                                        <td>Shared uploads (NFS,EFS)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ReadWriteOncePod</code></td>
                                        <td>RWOP</td>
                                        <td>Read-write by ONE pod</td>
                                        <td>Exclusive pod access (1.22+)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># CKA gotcha:</span>
<span class="comment"># RWO = "one NODE" not "one pod"!</span>
<span class="comment"># Multiple pods on the SAME node can share an RWO volume</span>
<span class="comment"># RWOP = "one POD" — exclusive access guarantee</span>

<span class="comment"># hostPath supports: RWO only</span>
<span class="comment"># NFS supports: RWO, ROX, RWX</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS EBS supports: RWO only</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS EFS supports: RWX</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The biggest trap: <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> means one NODE, not one pod. Multiple pods on the same node CAN mount the same RWO volume simultaneously. For true single-pod exclusivity, use <code>ReadWriteOncePod</code> (K8s 1.22+). On the CKA, access mode mismatches are a top reason for PVC binding failure. If a PVC requests <code>RWX</code> but only <code>RWO</code> PVs exist, it stays <code>Pending</code>. Not all storage backends support all modes — <code>hostPath</code> only supports <code>RWO</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A PV shows status <code>Released</code> after its PVC was deleted. How do you make it <code>Available</code> again for a new PVC?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check current state:</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># NAME     CAPACITY  ACCESS  RECLAIM  STATUS    CLAIM               AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># pv-data  10Gi      RWO     Retain   Released  default/old-pvc     1h</span>

<span class="comment"># The PV has a claimRef to the old PVC — must remove it:</span>
kubectl edit pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># Delete the entire claimRef section:</span>
<span class="comment"># spec:</span>
<span class="comment">#   claimRef:          ← DELETE these 4 lines</span>
<span class="comment">#     apiVersion: v1</span>
<span class="comment">#     kind: PersistentVolumeClaim</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: old-pvc</span>
<span class="comment">#     namespace: default</span>
<span class="comment">#     uid: abc-123</span>

<span class="comment"># Or use patch:</span>
kubectl patch pv pv-data --type json \
-p '[{"op": "remove", "path": "/spec/claimRef"}]'

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pv pv-data
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Available ← Ready for new PVC!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ The data from the old PVC is still on disk!</span>
<span class="comment"># You may want to clean it before reuse:</span>
<span class="comment"># SSH to node → rm -rf /mnt/data/*</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When a PVC is deleted and the PV has <code>Retain</code> policy, the PV goes to <code>Released</code> (not <code>Available</code>). The <code>claimRef</code> still references the old PVC, preventing new bindings. Remove it to return to <code>Available</code>. On the CKA, this is a multi-step task: (1) identify the Released PV, (2) remove claimRef, (3) verify Available status. Note that old data remains — the admin must decide whether to clean it. This is why <code>Retain</code> requires manual intervention by design.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a StatefulSet with <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> for a PostgreSQL database. How do <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> differ from regular PVCs? What happens to the volumes when pods are deleted?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
name: postgres
spec:
serviceName: postgres-headless
replicas: 3
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: postgres
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: postgres
spec:
  containers:
  - name: postgres
    image: postgres:15
    ports:
    - containerPort: 5432
    volumeMounts:
    - name: pgdata
      mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
volumeClaimTemplates:         # ← Not under template!
- metadata:
  name: pgdata               <span class="comment"># Matches volumeMount name</span>
spec:
  accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
  storageClassName: standard
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi

<span class="comment"># What gets created:</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-0 → PVC: pgdata-postgres-0 → PV (auto-provisioned)</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-1 → PVC: pgdata-postgres-1 → PV (auto-provisioned)</span>
<span class="comment"># postgres-2 → PVC: pgdata-postgres-2 → PV (auto-provisioned)</span>

<span class="comment"># Key differences from regular PVCs:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Each pod gets its OWN unique PVC (not shared)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. PVC name = &lt;template-name&gt;-&lt;statefulset-name&gt;-&lt;ordinal&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. PVCs are NOT deleted when pods or StatefulSet are deleted</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. When pod restarts, it reattaches to the SAME PVC</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># pgdata-postgres-0   Bound   pv-abc   10Gi   RWO    standard</span>
<span class="comment"># pgdata-postgres-1   Bound   pv-def   10Gi   RWO    standard</span>
<span class="comment"># pgdata-postgres-2   Bound   pv-ghi   10Gi   RWO    standard</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Deleting StatefulSet does NOT delete PVCs:</span>
kubectl delete sts postgres
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># All PVCs still exist! Data is preserved.</span>
<span class="comment"># Manual cleanup: kubectl delete pvc pgdata-postgres-{0,1,2}</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> are unique to StatefulSets and create a <strong>dedicated PVC per pod replica</strong>. Unlike Deployments where all pods share the same PVC (or have no persistent storage), StatefulSets guarantee that pod <code>postgres-0</code> always gets <code>pgdata-postgres-0</code>. PVCs survive pod restarts, rescheduling, and even StatefulSet deletion — this is by design to prevent accidental data loss. On the CKA, remember: <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> is a <strong>top-level field of StatefulSet spec</strong> (same level as <code>template</code>), NOT nested inside the pod template. The naming convention <code>&lt;vct-name&gt;-&lt;sts-name&gt;-&lt;ordinal&gt;</code> is deterministic.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 22 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch23">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 23 · ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Hard-coding configuration into container images is an anti-pattern. Every time you change a setting, you'd need to rebuild and redeploy the image. <strong>ConfigMaps</strong> and <strong>Secrets</strong> solve this by externalizing configuration — injecting settings into pods at runtime via environment variables or mounted files. For our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django app, this means database URLs, Django settings, and credentials all live outside the image, making it portable across dev, staging, and production environments.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔐 Config</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ How to create ConfigMaps from literals, files, and env-files</li>
                    <li>✅ How to inject ConfigMap data as environment variables and mounted files</li>
                    <li>✅ How Secrets differ from ConfigMaps — base64 encoding, types, and security caveats</li>
                    <li>✅ Best practices for managing configuration in production clusters</li>
                    <li>✅ Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets for performance and safety</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Storage <span class="cka-weight">10%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets Appear in Multiple CKA Domains</h5>
                <p>These topics cross storage and workload domains. You'll be asked to create them imperatively and mount them into pods. Master these commands:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-literal=key=value</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=password=abc123</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get configmap myconfig -o yaml</code> — inspect contents</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-file=config.properties</code></li>
                    <li>Know both injection methods: <code>envFrom</code> (env vars) and <code>volumeMounts</code> (files)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>ConfigMap &amp; Secret Imperative Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>CM from literal</td><td><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-literal=key=value</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>CM from file</td><td><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-file=app.conf</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>CM from env-file</td><td><code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-env-file=.env</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secret generic literal</td><td><code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=pass=abc123</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secret from file</td><td><code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-file=cert.pem</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Secret TLS</td><td><code>kubectl create secret tls tls-secret --cert=cert.crt --key=key.pem</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Inspect CM</td><td><code>kubectl get cm myconfig -o yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Decode Secret</td><td><code>kubectl get secret mysecret -o jsonpath='{.data.pass}' | base64 -d</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ConfigMap + Secret + Pod Injection — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">You MUST know both injection methods. Env-vars for single values, volume mounts for whole files.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># ConfigMap</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: app-config
data:
  APP_ENV: production              # ◀── key-value pair
  config.properties: |             # ◀── multi-line file content
    server.port=8080
    log.level=INFO
---
<span class="comment"># Secret</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: app-secret
type: Opaque
data:
  DB_PASS: cGFzc3dvcmQxMjM=       # ◀── base64 encoded
stringData:
  API_KEY: my-plain-text-key       # ◀── auto-encoded at creation
---
<span class="comment"># Pod — env var injection</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: app-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    envFrom:                       # ◀── inject ALL keys as env vars
    - configMapRef:
        name: app-config
    env:
    - name: DB_PASSWORD            # ◀── inject SINGLE key
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:
          name: app-secret
          key: DB_PASS
    volumeMounts:                  # ◀── inject as FILES
    - name: config-vol
      mountPath: /etc/config
      readOnly: true
    - name: secret-vol
      mountPath: /etc/secrets
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: config-vol
    configMap:
      name: app-config
  - name: secret-vol
    secret:
      secretName: app-secret</code></pre>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find ConfigMap &amp; Secret YAML Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain configmap.data                 <span class="comment"># key-value data</span>
kubectl explain secret.data                    <span class="comment"># base64-encoded data</span>
kubectl explain secret.stringData              <span class="comment"># plain text (auto-encoded)</span>
kubectl explain secret.type                    <span class="comment"># Opaque, tls, etc.</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.envFrom    <span class="comment"># bulk injection</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.env.valueFrom  <span class="comment"># single key injection</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.volumes.configMap     <span class="comment"># CM volume mount</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.volumes.secret        <span class="comment"># Secret volume mount</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>✅ DO</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Use <code>stringData</code> for plain-text Secret creation</li>
                        <li>Mount Secrets as <code>readOnly</code> volumes</li>
                        <li>Use <code>envFrom</code> to inject all CM keys at once</li>
                        <li>Use <code>--from-literal</code> for quick exam answers</li>
                        <li>Check Secret data with <code>base64 -d</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>❌ DON'T</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Store sensitive data in ConfigMaps</li>
                        <li>Assume Secrets are encrypted at rest</li>
                        <li>Forget <code>readOnly: true</code> on Secret mounts</li>
                        <li>Expect env var updates without Pod restart</li>
                        <li>Exceed the 1 MiB size limit</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Config Types (CM + Secret)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Injection Methods (env / vol)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">1 MiB</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Max Size Per Object</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-red">
                    <span class="metric-value">base64</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Secret Encoding (NOT encryption!)</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §23.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.1 ConfigMaps — Non-Sensitive Configuration</h3>
                <p>A ConfigMap stores <strong>key-value pairs</strong> of non-sensitive configuration data. It decouples application configuration from the container image, so the same image can run with different settings in different environments.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">ConfigMap — Same Image, Different Config Per Environment</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  ONE IMAGE — THREE ENVIRONMENTS — THREE CONFIGMAPS                       │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                                    │
  │  │  easypj:latest   │  ◀── same image everywhere                        │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                                    │
  │           │                                                              │
  │     ┌─────┼──────────────────┐                                          │
  │     │     │                  │                                          │
  │     ▼     ▼                  ▼                                          │
  │  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐                      │
  │  │ DEV      │  │ STAGING      │  │ PRODUCTION   │                      │
  │  │          │  │              │  │              │                      │
  │  │ ConfigMap│  │ ConfigMap    │  │ ConfigMap    │                      │
  │  │ DEBUG=   │  │ DEBUG=       │  │ DEBUG=       │                      │
  │  │  True    │  │  True        │  │  False       │                      │
  │  │ DB_HOST= │  │ DB_HOST=     │  │ DB_HOST=     │                      │
  │  │  localhost│  │  staging-db  │  │  prod-db     │                      │
  │  │ REPLICAS=│  │ REPLICAS=    │  │ REPLICAS=    │                      │
  │  │  1       │  │  2           │  │  5           │                      │
  │  │ LOG=     │  │ LOG=         │  │ LOG=         │                      │
  │  │  verbose │  │  info        │  │  warning     │                      │
  │  └──────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘                      │
  │                                                                          │
  │  No image rebuild needed! Just change the ConfigMap and restart pods.   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>Create from Literals</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DEBUG=False --from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres</code> — quick key-value pairs, great for simple settings.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📄</div>
                        <h5>Create from File</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap nginx-conf --from-file=nginx.conf</code> — entire file becomes a key (filename) with file contents as value. Perfect for config files.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Create from Env File</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create configmap app-env --from-env-file=.env</code> — reads a <code>.env</code> file and creates one key per line. Great for migrating from Docker Compose.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>Create from YAML</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml</code> — full control over keys and values using declarative YAML. Best for version-controlled configs.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ConfigMap for easypj Django settings</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj-production
data:
  <span class="comment"># Simple key-value pairs → injected as env vars</span>
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*.easypj.com,localhost"
  DB_HOST: "postgres.easypj-production.svc.cluster.local"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"
  STATIC_URL: "/static/"
  MEDIA_URL: "/media/"

  <span class="comment"># Multi-line value → best mounted as a file</span>
  settings.ini: |
    [database]
    engine = django.db.backends.postgresql
    host = postgres
    port = 5432
    name = easypj_db

    [cache]
    backend = django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache
    location = redis://redis:6379/0

    [email]
    backend = django.core.mail.backends.smtp.EmailBackend
    host = smtp.easypj.com
    port = 587</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Two Ways to Inject ConfigMap Data into Pods</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  METHOD 1: Environment Variables        METHOD 2: Mounted Files          │
  │  (envFrom / env)                        (volumeMounts)                   │
  │                                                                          │
  │  ConfigMap: easypj-config               ConfigMap: easypj-config         │
  │  ┌─────────────────────┐                ┌─────────────────────┐          │
  │  │ DEBUG=False          │                │ settings.ini: |     │          │
  │  │ DB_HOST=postgres     │                │   [database]        │          │
  │  │ DB_PORT=5432         │                │   engine=postgresql │          │
  │  └─────────┬───────────┘                └─────────┬───────────┘          │
  │            │                                      │                      │
  │            ▼                                      ▼                      │
  │  Pod sees env vars:                     Pod sees files:                  │
  │  $ echo $DEBUG                          $ ls /etc/config/                │
  │  False                                  settings.ini                     │
  │  $ echo $DB_HOST                        $ cat /etc/config/settings.ini   │
  │  postgres                               [database]                       │
  │                                         engine=postgresql                │
  │  ✅ Simple, fast access                 ✅ Complex configs, auto-update  │
  │  ❌ Requires pod restart to update      ✅ Updates without pod restart   │
  │  ❌ All keys become env vars            ✅ Selective file mounting       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deployment-with-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj Deployment using ConfigMap — both env vars AND mounted files</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj-production
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 1: All keys as env vars ──</span>
        envFrom:
        - configMapRef:
            name: easypj-config       # ◀── all keys become env vars
                                       <span class="comment">#     DEBUG, DB_HOST, DB_PORT, etc.</span>

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 1b: Selective env vars ──</span>
        env:
        - name: DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE
          value: easypj.settings       # ◀── hardcoded value
        - name: DATABASE_HOST           # ◀── rename the key
          valueFrom:
            configMapKeyRef:
              name: easypj-config
              key: DB_HOST              # ◀── specific key from ConfigMap

        <span class="comment"># ── METHOD 2: Mount as files ──</span>
        volumeMounts:
        - name: config-volume
          mountPath: /etc/easypj/config  # ◀── files appear here
          readOnly: true

      volumes:
      - name: config-volume
        configMap:
          name: easypj-config
          items:                         # ◀── selective: only mount these keys
          - key: settings.ini
            path: settings.ini           # ◀── /etc/easypj/config/settings.ini</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 ConfigMap Updates and Pod Behavior</h5>
                    <p>When you update a ConfigMap, <strong>volume-mounted files update automatically</strong> (within ~60 seconds, or on kubelet sync period). However, <strong>environment variables do NOT update</strong> — the pod must be restarted. This is a key distinction for the CKA exam. If you need live config reloads, use volume mounts.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 23.2: Secrets — Sensitive Configuration -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.2 Secrets — Sensitive Configuration</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-important">CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-warning">SECURITY</span>
                </div>
                <p>Secrets are the companion to ConfigMaps — they store <strong>sensitive</strong> data like passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates. While structurally similar to ConfigMaps, Secrets have additional protections and CKA exam-specific nuances you must understand.</p>

                <!-- ConfigMap vs Secret Comparison -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>📄 ConfigMap</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Non-sensitive config</li>
                                <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Plain text in etcd</li>
                                <li><strong>Data field:</strong> <code>data:</code> (plain text)</li>
                                <li><strong>Example:</strong> DB_HOST, LOG_LEVEL</li>
                                <li><strong>Safe to log:</strong> ✅ Yes</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>🔒 Secret</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Sensitive data</li>
                                <li><strong>Storage:</strong> Base64 in etcd (not encrypted by default!)</li>
                                <li><strong>Data field:</strong> <code>data:</code> (base64) or <code>stringData:</code> (plain)</li>
                                <li><strong>Example:</strong> DB_PASSWORD, SECRET_KEY</li>
                                <li><strong>Safe to log:</strong> ❌ Never</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Critical Warning: Base64 is NOT encryption -->
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Base64 is NOT Encryption!</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes Secrets are <strong>base64 encoded, NOT encrypted</strong> by default. Anyone with <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> access can decode them instantly. Base64 is just encoding — like translating English to pig Latin. It provides <strong>zero security</strong> on its own.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Base64 Encoding Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔍 Base64 Encoding — What Actually Happens</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  Creating a Secret:                         Reading a Secret:

  "postgres123"                              "cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM="
       │                                          │
       ▼                                          ▼
  ┌─────────────┐                          ┌─────────────┐
  │  base64     │                          │  base64     │
  │  encode     │                          │  decode     │
  └──────┬──────┘                          └──────┬──────┘
         ▼                                        ▼
  "cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM="                      "postgres123"
         │                                        │
         ▼                                        ▼
  ┌──────────────────┐                    ┌────────────────┐
  │  Stored in etcd  │                    │  Anyone with   │
  │  as base64       │                    │  RBAC access   │
  │                  │                    │  can read it!  │
  └──────────────────┘                    └────────────────┘

  $ echo -n "postgres123" | base64         $ echo "cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=" | base64 -d
  cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=                         postgres123
</pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Secret Types Table -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📋 Kubernetes Secret Types</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Type</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                <th>Required Keys</th>
                                <th>CKA Exam</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Opaque</code></td>
                                <td>Generic key-value (default)</td>
                                <td>Any</td>
                                <td>⭐ Most common</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/tls</code></td>
                                <td>TLS certificate + key</td>
                                <td><code>tls.crt</code>, <code>tls.key</code></td>
                                <td>⭐ Ingress TLS</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code></td>
                                <td>Private registry credentials</td>
                                <td><code>.dockerconfigjson</code></td>
                                <td>Sometimes tested</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/basic-auth</code></td>
                                <td>Basic authentication</td>
                                <td><code>username</code>, <code>password</code></td>
                                <td>Rare</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/ssh-auth</code></td>
                                <td>SSH private key</td>
                                <td><code>ssh-privatekey</code></td>
                                <td>Rare</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code></td>
                                <td>ServiceAccount token (legacy)</td>
                                <td>Auto-generated</td>
                                <td>Know it exists</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- Creating Secrets -->
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🛠️ Creating Secrets — Imperative Commands (CKA Speed)</h5>
                    <p>On the CKA exam, imperative commands save precious time. Master these:</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bash — imperative Secret creation</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. From literal values (most common on CKA)</span>
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secrets \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY=django-insecure-k8s-prod-key-2024 \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=postgres123 \
  --from-literal=DB_USER=easypj_admin

<span class="comment"># 2. From a file (e.g., TLS cert)</span>
kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \
  --cert=./tls.crt \
  --key=./tls.key

<span class="comment"># 3. From a docker registry (private images)</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
  --docker-server=registry.example.com \
  --docker-username=admin \
  --docker-password=secret123

<span class="comment"># 4. Generate YAML without applying (--dry-run trick)</span>
kubectl create secret generic my-secret \
  --from-literal=password=supersecret \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; secret.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify (notice values are hidden by default)</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secrets -o yaml
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   SECRET_KEY: ZGphbmdvLWluc2VjdXJlLWs4cy1wcm9kLWtleS0yMDI0</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_PASSWORD: cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Declarative Secret YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-secrets.yaml — declarative Secret</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secrets
  namespace: easypj
type: Opaque
<span class="comment"># Option A: base64-encoded (required for `data:`)</span>
data:
  SECRET_KEY: ZGphbmdvLWluc2VjdXJlLWs4cy1wcm9kLWtleS0yMDI0   # ◀── base64
  DB_PASSWORD: cG9zdGdyZXMxMjM=                                  # ◀── base64
  DB_USER: YW5paHBqX2FkbWlu                                      # ◀── base64

<span class="comment"># Option B: plain text (Kubernetes encodes it for you)</span>
<span class="comment"># stringData:                   # ◀── use this to avoid manual base64</span>
<span class="comment">#   SECRET_KEY: django-insecure-k8s-prod-key-2024</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_PASSWORD: postgres123</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_USER: easypj_admin</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 stringData vs data</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>stringData:</code> when writing YAML by hand — it accepts plain text and Kubernetes base64-encodes it automatically. Use <code>data:</code> when you already have base64-encoded values (e.g., from <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code>). On the CKA exam, <code>stringData:</code> saves time and prevents encoding mistakes.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Using Secrets in Pods -->
                <h4>📦 Injecting Secrets into Pods</h4>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔌 Two Injection Methods — Same as ConfigMaps</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                        easypj Pod                               │
  │                                                                 │
  │  Method 1: Environment Variables          Method 2: Volume      │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐   │
  │  │  env:                       │    │  /etc/easypj/secrets/│   │
  │  │    SECRET_KEY=django-ins... │    │  ├── SECRET_KEY      │   │
  │  │    DB_PASSWORD=postgres123  │    │  ├── DB_PASSWORD     │   │
  │  │    DB_USER=easypj_admin     │    │  └── DB_USER         │   │
  │  │                             │    │                      │   │
  │  │  ✅ Easy to use in code    │    │  ✅ Auto-updates     │   │
  │  │  ❌ Visible in env dump    │    │  ✅ One file per key │   │
  │  │  ❌ No auto-update         │    │  ❌ Read file in code│   │
  │  └─────────────────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘   │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-deployment-with-secrets.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: django
        image: easypj/web:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000

        <span class="comment"># ── Method 1: Inject ALL Secret keys as env vars ──</span>
        envFrom:
        - secretRef:
            name: easypj-secrets        # ◀── all keys become env vars

        <span class="comment"># ── Method 2: Inject specific keys as env vars ──</span>
        env:
        - name: DATABASE_URL
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: easypj-secrets
              key: DB_PASSWORD           # ◀── just one key

        <span class="comment"># ── Method 3: Mount as files ──</span>
        volumeMounts:
        - name: secret-volume
          mountPath: /etc/easypj/secrets
          readOnly: true                 # ◀── ALWAYS set readOnly for secrets

      volumes:
      - name: secret-volume
        secret:
          secretName: easypj-secrets
          defaultMode: 0400              # ◀── restrict file permissions (owner read only)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>🔐 Secret Volume Best Practices</h5>
                    <p>Always set <code>readOnly: true</code> on secret volume mounts and use restrictive <code>defaultMode: 0400</code> (owner read-only). This prevents containers from accidentally modifying secret files and follows the principle of least privilege. For the CKA exam, remember: <code>secret</code> volume type (not <code>configMap</code>), and <code>secretName</code> (not <code>name</code>) in the volume spec.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Verification Commands -->
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔍 Verifying Secrets (CKA Exam Commands)</h5>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bash — Secret verification commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List secrets in namespace</span>
kubectl get secrets -n easypj

<span class="comment"># View secret (values are hidden)</span>
kubectl describe secret easypj-secrets -n easypj

<span class="comment"># View base64-encoded values</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secrets -n easypj -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Decode a specific value</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secrets -n easypj \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.DB_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># Output: postgres123</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify secret is mounted in pod</span>
kubectl exec -it deploy/easypj-web -n easypj -- ls /etc/easypj/secrets/
<span class="comment"># Output: DB_PASSWORD  DB_USER  SECRET_KEY</span>

kubectl exec -it deploy/easypj-web -n easypj -- cat /etc/easypj/secrets/DB_PASSWORD
<span class="comment"># Output: postgres123  (automatically decoded when mounted!)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Secrets Are Decoded When Mounted</h5>
                    <p>When Secrets are injected as environment variables or mounted as volume files, Kubernetes <strong>automatically decodes the base64</strong>. Your application receives plain text — no decoding needed in your code. The base64 encoding only exists in etcd and the API response.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 23.3: ConfigMap and Secret Best Practices -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.3 ConfigMap and Secret Best Practices</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-concept">BEST PRACTICES</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
                <p>Real-world Kubernetes security depends on following strict practices around configuration management. These rules apply equally to the CKA exam and production clusters.</p>

                <!-- DO / DON'T Cards -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>✅ DO — Best Practices</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>ConfigMaps</strong> for non-sensitive config (DB_HOST, LOG_LEVEL, feature flags)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>Secrets</strong> for sensitive data (passwords, API keys, TLS certs)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>envFrom</code> for <strong>bulk injection</strong> — fewer lines, fewer mistakes</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>volume mounts</strong> when config is a complete file (nginx.conf, settings.ini)</li>
                                <li>✅ Enable <strong>encryption at rest</strong> for Secrets (<code>EncryptionConfiguration</code>)</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <strong>RBAC</strong> to restrict who can <code>get</code>/<code>list</code> Secrets</li>
                                <li>✅ Set <code>readOnly: true</code> on Secret volume mounts</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>stringData:</code> in YAML for readability</li>
                                <li>✅ Use <code>immutable: true</code> for configs that should never change</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>❌ DON'T — Common Mistakes</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never commit Secrets to Git</strong> — use sealed-secrets or external vaults</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never hardcode secrets in Dockerfiles</strong> — they persist in image layers</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never log secret values</strong> — use placeholder text in logs</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never use ConfigMaps for passwords</strong> — they have no access restrictions</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never share Secrets across namespaces</strong> without good reason</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never give broad Secret access</strong> — <code>get secrets *</code> is dangerous</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never skip RBAC</strong> for Secret access — default allows too much</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never assume base64 = encrypted</strong> — it's just encoding</li>
                                <li>❌ <strong>Never store large files in Secrets</strong> — 1MB limit per Secret</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Encryption at Rest Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔐 Secret Security Layers — Defense in Depth</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  Layer 1: RBAC (WHO can access)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Role: secret-reader                                         │
  │    rules:                                                    │
  │    - apiGroups: [""]                                         │
  │      resources: ["secrets"]                                  │
  │      verbs: ["get"]          ◀── only get, not list/watch   │
  │      resourceNames: ["easypj-secrets"]  ◀── specific secret │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Layer 2: Encryption at Rest (HOW it's stored in etcd)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Without encryption:  Secret → base64 → etcd (readable!)    │
  │  With encryption:     Secret → base64 → AES-CBC → etcd ✅  │
  │                                                              │
  │  Configure via: /etc/kubernetes/enc/enc.yaml                 │
  │  API server flag: --encryption-provider-config               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Layer 3: Network (HOW it's transmitted)
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  API server ←──TLS──→ kubelet ←──tmpfs──→ Pod              │
  │  Secrets mounted as tmpfs (memory) — never written to disk  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <!-- easypj Complete Config Architecture -->
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🏗️ easypj — Complete Configuration Architecture</h5>
                    <p>Putting it all together — how the easypj Django project separates configuration from code:</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>📐 easypj Configuration Flow</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    easypj Namespace                                 │
  │                                                                     │
  │  ConfigMap: easypj-config          Secret: easypj-secrets          │
  │  ┌──────────────────────┐          ┌──────────────────────┐        │
  │  │ DB_HOST=postgres-svc │          │ SECRET_KEY=django... │        │
  │  │ DB_NAME=easypj_db    │          │ DB_PASSWORD=post...  │        │
  │  │ DB_PORT=5432          │          │ DB_USER=easypj_admin│        │
  │  │ DEBUG=False            │          │                      │        │
  │  │ ALLOWED_HOSTS=*       │          │  (base64 encoded)    │        │
  │  │ settings.ini (file)   │          └──────────┬───────────┘        │
  │  └──────────┬───────────┘                      │                    │
  │             │                                  │                    │
  │             ▼                                  ▼                    │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │
  │  │               easypj-web Deployment                      │      │
  │  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │      │
  │  │  │  django container                                   │  │      │
  │  │  │                                                     │  │      │
  │  │  │  ENV:  DB_HOST, DB_NAME, DB_PORT  ◀── configMapRef │  │      │
  │  │  │        SECRET_KEY, DB_PASSWORD    ◀── secretRef    │  │      │
  │  │  │                                                     │  │      │
  │  │  │  FILES: /etc/easypj/config/settings.ini ◀── CM vol │  │      │
  │  │  │         /etc/easypj/secrets/       ◀── Secret vol  │  │      │
  │  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │      │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- Section 23.4: Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets -->
            <!-- ─────────── §23.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s23-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">23.4 Immutable ConfigMaps and Secrets</h3>
                <div class="chapter-meta" style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
                    <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                    <span class="badge badge-performance">PERFORMANCE</span>
                </div>
                <p>Kubernetes v1.21+ supports marking ConfigMaps and Secrets as <strong>immutable</strong> — once created, they cannot be modified. This provides safety and performance benefits for production clusters.</p>

                <!-- Why Immutable? Cards -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>🛡️ Safety Benefits</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Prevents accidental changes</strong> — can't <code>kubectl edit</code> or <code>kubectl apply</code> updates</li>
                                <li><strong>Protects production</strong> — config drift becomes impossible</li>
                                <li><strong>Audit trail</strong> — changes require delete + recreate (visible in audit logs)</li>
                                <li><strong>GitOps friendly</strong> — ensures deployed config matches Git source</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">                        <h5>⚡ Performance Benefits</h5>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Kubelet optimization</strong> — stops watching immutable objects for changes</li>
                                <li><strong>Reduced API server load</strong> — fewer watch connections per node</li>
                                <li><strong>Scales better</strong> — significant at 1000+ ConfigMaps/Secrets</li>
                                <li><strong>Faster sync</strong> — kubelet skips polling cycles for immutable objects</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Immutable ConfigMap YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">immutable-configmap.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config-v2          # ◀── version in name (required for updates)
  namespace: easypj
immutable: true                     # ◀── THE KEY FIELD — cannot be changed once set
data:
  DB_HOST: "postgres-svc"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DEBUG: "False"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Immutable Secret YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">immutable-secret.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secrets-v2         # ◀── version in name
  namespace: easypj
type: Opaque
immutable: true                    # ◀── same field works for Secrets too
stringData:
  SECRET_KEY: django-insecure-k8s-prod-key-2024
  DB_PASSWORD: postgres123
  DB_USER: easypj_admin</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- What happens when you try to modify -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🚫 What Happens When You Try to Modify an Immutable Object</h4>
<pre class="ascii-art">
  $ kubectl edit configmap easypj-config-v2
  error: configmaps "easypj-config-v2" is immutable

  $ kubectl apply -f updated-config.yaml
  The ConfigMap "easypj-config-v2" is invalid: data: Forbidden:
  field is immutable when `immutable` is set

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Update Strategy for Immutable Configs:                     │
  │                                                             │
  │  1. Create NEW ConfigMap with new version name              │
  │     easypj-config-v2 → easypj-config-v3                   │
  │                                                             │
  │  2. Update Deployment to reference new name                 │
  │     configMapRef:                                           │
  │       name: easypj-config-v3   ◀── point to new version   │
  │                                                             │
  │  3. Rolling update deploys pods with new config             │
  │                                                             │
  │  4. Delete old ConfigMap when no pods reference it          │
  │     kubectl delete configmap easypj-config-v2              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Immutability Rule of Thumb</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>immutable: true</code> for <strong>production configs that are versioned via CI/CD</strong>. Skip it for development environments where you frequently tweak settings. On the CKA exam, know that the <code>immutable</code> field exists and that the only way to "update" an immutable ConfigMap is to delete it and create a new one — which requires restarting pods that reference it.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Scenario Box -->
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🏋️ CKA Practice Scenario — ConfigMaps and Secrets</h5>
                    <div class="scenario-content">
                        <p><strong>Task:</strong> Create a namespace <code>webapp</code>. Create a ConfigMap named <code>app-config</code> with keys <code>APP_COLOR=blue</code> and <code>APP_MODE=prod</code>. Create a Secret named <code>app-secret</code> with key <code>DB_PASS=supersecret</code>. Create a Pod named <code>webapp-pod</code> using image <code>nginx</code> that loads the ConfigMap as environment variables and mounts the Secret at <code>/etc/secrets</code>. Verify the values are accessible.</p>
                        <p><strong>Solution:</strong></p>
                        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                            <div class="code-block-header">
                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                                <span class="code-lang">bash — CKA scenario solution</span>
                            </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace webapp

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create ConfigMap</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
  --from-literal=APP_COLOR=blue \
  --from-literal=APP_MODE=prod \
  -n webapp

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create Secret</span>
kubectl create secret generic app-secret \
  --from-literal=DB_PASS=supersecret \
  -n webapp

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create Pod with both ConfigMap and Secret</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: webapp-pod
  namespace: webapp
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx
    envFrom:
    - configMapRef:
        name: app-config         # ◀── all CM keys as env vars
    volumeMounts:
    - name: secret-vol
      mountPath: /etc/secrets
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: secret-vol
    secret:
      secretName: app-secret     # ◀── mount Secret as files
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl exec webapp-pod -n webapp -- printenv APP_COLOR
<span class="comment"># Output: blue</span>

kubectl exec webapp-pod -n webapp -- cat /etc/secrets/DB_PASS
<span class="comment"># Output: supersecret</span></code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: ConfigMap &amp; Secret Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Secrets are NOT encrypted by default</strong> — They are only base64-encoded (not encryption!). Anyone with API access can decode them. Enable encryption at rest via <code>EncryptionConfiguration</code> on the API server. The CKA tests this knowledge</li>
                        <li><strong><code>envFrom</code> vs <code>env</code></strong> — <code>envFrom</code> imports ALL keys from a ConfigMap/Secret as env vars. <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> imports one specific key. If the exam says "inject the key <code>DB_HOST</code> from configmap <code>app-config</code>", use <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Env vars do NOT auto-update</strong> — If you change a ConfigMap, pods using <code>envFrom</code> won't see the change until restarted. Volume-mounted ConfigMaps DO auto-update (within ~60s). This is a classic trick question</li>
                        <li><strong><code>immutable: true</code></strong> — Once set, the ConfigMap/Secret cannot be changed. This improves kubelet performance (stops watching for changes). You must delete and recreate to modify</li>
                        <li><strong>Secret types matter</strong> — <code>generic</code> (Opaque) for arbitrary data, <code>docker-registry</code> for image pull credentials, <code>tls</code> for TLS cert/key pairs. The exam may specify which type to create</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <strong>⚡ ConfigMap &amp; Secret commands:</strong>
                    <code>kubectl get configmaps</code> / <code>kubectl get secrets</code><br>
                    <code>kubectl describe configmap &lt;name&gt;</code> — see all key-value pairs<br>
                    <code>kubectl create configmap &lt;name&gt; --from-env-file=.env</code> — import from .env file<br>
                    <code>kubectl get secret &lt;name&gt; -o yaml</code> — see base64 values<br>
                    <code>kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred --docker-server=&lt;url&gt; --docker-username=&lt;user&gt; --docker-password=&lt;pw&gt;</code>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How ConfigMaps and Secrets Are Injected Into Pods: Volume Mounts vs Environment Variables, Atomic Updates, and Encryption at Rest</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ ConfigMap/Secret Injection — Two Mechanisms, Different Behaviors</h5>
            <p>ConfigMaps and Secrets can be consumed as environment variables OR volume mounts. These mechanisms behave differently — especially when config is updated at runtime.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  INJECTION METHOD 1: ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES                                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  apiVersion: v1                                                      │    │
│  │  spec:                                                                │    │
│  │    containers:                                                        │    │
│  │    - name: easypj                                                     │    │
│  │      env:                                                             │    │
│  │      - name: DB_HOST           <span class="comment">← Single key                          </span>│    │
│  │        valueFrom:                                                     │    │
│  │          configMapKeyRef:                                             │    │
│  │            name: easypj-config                                        │    │
│  │            key: DB_HOST                                               │    │
│  │      envFrom:                  <span class="comment">← ALL keys at once                    </span>│    │
│  │      - configMapRef:                                                  │    │
│  │          name: easypj-config                                          │    │
│  │      - secretRef:                                                     │    │
│  │          name: easypj-db-creds                                        │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  BEHAVIOR:                                                            │    │
│  │  ✅ Simple, well-understood by applications                          │    │
│  │  ❌ VALUES ARE FROZEN at pod start — updating ConfigMap does NOT    │    │
│  │     update env vars in running pods. Pod must be RESTARTED.         │    │
│  │  ❌ All Secret data visible in /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/environ (for root)      │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  INJECTION METHOD 2: VOLUME MOUNTS                                           │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  apiVersion: v1                                                      │    │
│  │  spec:                                                                │    │
│  │    containers:                                                        │    │
│  │    - name: easypj                                                     │    │
│  │      volumeMounts:                                                    │    │
│  │      - name: config                                                   │    │
│  │        mountPath: /etc/config   <span class="comment">← Each key becomes a FILE            </span>│    │
│  │        readOnly: true                                                   │    │
│  │  volumes:                                                             │    │
│  │  - name: config                                                       │    │
│  │    configMap:                                                         │    │
│  │      name: easypj-config                                              │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  RESULT: /etc/config/                                                │    │
│  │  ├── DB_HOST     (file containing "postgres")                        │    │
│  │  ├── DB_PORT     (file containing "5432")                            │    │
│  │  └── DJANGO_DEBUG (file containing "false")                          │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  BEHAVIOR:                                                            │    │
│  │  ✅ AUTO-UPDATES: When ConfigMap changes, kubelet updates mount      │    │
│  │     files within ~60 seconds (configurable via kubelet sync)         │    │
│  │  ✅ Atomic: Symlink swap — no partial updates visible               │    │
│  │  ✅ SubPath: Mount single key as file at specific path              │    │
│  │  ⚠️  App must watch files for changes (or use inotify)              │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  SECRET ENCRYPTION — Defense in Depth:                                       │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  LAYER 1: etcd at rest — Secrets are base64 encoded in etcd. This  │    │
│  │           is NOT encryption, just encoding. Anyone with etcd        │    │
│  │           access can decode them.                                    │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  LAYER 2: Encryption at rest — API Server encrypts Secret data      │    │
│  │           BEFORE writing to etcd. Requires EncryptionConfiguration. │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  LAYER 3: RBAC — Only authorized ServiceAccounts/pods/users can     │    │
│  │           GET the Secret.                                            │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  LAYER 4: External secrets management — Sealed Secrets, External    │    │
│  │           Secrets Operator (ESO), Vault injector. Secrets never     │    │
│  │           stored in K8s at all.                                      │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  ⚠️  CKA FACT: By default, Secrets in etcd are base64 ENCODED, not │    │
│  │     ENCRYPTED. `echo c3VwZXJzZWNyZXQ= | base64 -d` → supersecret!  │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  ATOMIC CONFIGMAP UPDATES — How Kubelet Swaps Config Without Downtime:      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  1. ConfigMap updated via kubectl                                    │    │
│  │  2. kubelet detects change (periodic sync, default ~60s)            │    │
│  │  3. kubelet creates NEW directory: ..data_tmp/&lt;timestamp&gt;/          │    │
│  │  4. kubelet writes updated files to new directory                   │    │
│  │  5. kubelet atomically swaps symlink:                                │    │
│  │     ..data → ..data_tmp/&lt;timestamp&gt;/  (atomic rename)              │    │
│  │  6. Container sees updated files INSTANTLY (no restart needed)      │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │  $ ls -la /etc/config/                                               │    │
│  │  ..data -> ..2026_06_02_10_05_23.123456789  <span class="comment">← Updated symlink!      </span>│    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, you'll frequently create ConfigMaps and Secrets. Key differences to remember: ConfigMap for non-sensitive config (DB_HOST, DEBUG), Secret for sensitive data (DB_PASSWORD, API_KEY). Use <code>--from-literal</code> for single values, <code>--from-file</code> for files. Secrets must be base64 encoded in YAML but NOT in imperative commands: <code>kubectl create secret generic --from-literal=key=value</code> auto-encodes. A pod referencing a non-existent ConfigMap will fail to start with <code>CreateContainerConfigError</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">Secrets are encrypted by default in etcd</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Secrets are only base64 encoded — enable EncryptionConfiguration for real encryption</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">ConfigMap changes auto-update environment variables</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Env vars are set at Pod start. Only volume-mounted CMs auto-update (with delay)</span></li>
                    <li>The ConfigMap/Secret must exist BEFORE the Pod — otherwise the Pod stays <code>Pending</code> (unless marked <code>optional: true</code>)</li>
                    <li><code>data:</code> values are base64 in Secrets, but <code>stringData:</code> accepts plain text (auto-encoded)</li>
                    <li>Secret type <code>Opaque</code> is the default — <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> requires both <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code></li>
                    <li>Max ConfigMap/Secret size: <strong>1 MiB</strong></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — ConfigMap/Secret creation cheat sheet:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-literal=key1=val1 --from-literal=key2=val2</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create configmap myconfig --from-file=config.properties</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create secret generic mysecret --from-literal=password=S3cret!</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create secret tls mytls --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key</code><br>
                <strong>Decode a secret:</strong> <code>kubectl get secret mysecret -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 23)</h3>
            <p>On the exam, you'll typically need to: (1) create a ConfigMap or Secret from literals, (2) mount it in a Pod/Deployment as env vars or volume, and (3) verify the values are accessible inside the container. Practice the full flow: <code>kubectl create → kubectl apply deployment → kubectl exec -- printenv</code>.</p>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 6 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a ConfigMap <code>app-config</code> with keys: <code>DB_HOST=postgres</code>, <code>DB_PORT=5432</code></li>
                    <li>2. Create a Secret <code>app-secret</code> with key: <code>DB_PASSWORD=MyP@ssw0rd</code></li>
                    <li>3. Create a pod that loads ALL ConfigMap keys via <code>envFrom</code></li>
                    <li>4. In the same pod, inject only <code>DB_PASSWORD</code> from the Secret via <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code></li>
                    <li>5. Exec in and verify: <code>echo $DB_HOST $DB_PORT $DB_PASSWORD</code></li>
                    <li>6. Mount the ConfigMap as a volume at <code>/etc/config</code> and verify files exist</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 23 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch23-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 23 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of config injection &amp; Secret handling — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ConfigMap named <code>app-config</code> with keys <code>DB_HOST=postgres</code> and <code>DB_PORT=5432</code> using an imperative command.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Imperative creation (fastest for CKA):</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres \
--from-literal=DB_PORT=5432

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get configmap app-config -o yaml
<span class="comment"># apiVersion: v1</span>
<span class="comment"># kind: ConfigMap</span>
<span class="comment"># metadata:</span>
<span class="comment">#   name: app-config</span>
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_HOST: postgres</span>
<span class="comment">#   DB_PORT: "5432"     ← Note: stored as STRING</span>

<span class="comment"># Other creation methods:</span>
<span class="comment"># From file:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=config.properties

<span class="comment"># From env file:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-env-file=.env

<span class="comment"># From directory (each file becomes a key):</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config --from-file=./configs/

<span class="comment"># Dry-run to generate YAML:</span>
kubectl create configmap app-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=postgres \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > configmap.yaml</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ConfigMaps store non-sensitive configuration data as key-value pairs. On the CKA, <code>--from-literal</code> is the fastest for simple key-value pairs. <code>--from-file</code> uses the filename as the key and file content as the value. <code>--from-env-file</code> reads KEY=VALUE lines from a .env file. Always use <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate YAML that you can modify. All values are stored as strings — even numbers like "5432".</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Secret named <code>db-creds</code> with <code>username=admin</code> and <code>password=S3cret!</code>. How do you decode the values?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create:</span>
kubectl create secret generic db-creds \
--from-literal=username=admin \
--from-literal=password='S3cret!'

<span class="comment"># View (values are base64-encoded):</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds -o yaml
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   password: UzNjcmV0IQ==</span>
<span class="comment">#   username: YWRtaW4=</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode a specific value:</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds \
-o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode all values:</span>
kubectl get secret db-creds -o go-template='
{{range $k, $v := .data}}
{{$k}}: {{$v | base64decode}}
{{end}}'
<span class="comment"># username: admin</span>
<span class="comment"># password: S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ base64 is NOT encryption!</span>
<span class="comment"># Anyone with API access can decode secrets</span>
<span class="comment"># Enable EncryptionConfiguration for at-rest encryption</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Secrets are base64-encoded, NOT encrypted. On the CKA, decoding secrets with <code>jsonpath + base64 -d</code> is a commonly tested skill. The imperative command auto-encodes values to base64. If creating via YAML, you must base64-encode values yourself (<code>echo -n "admin" | base64</code>) or use <code>stringData</code> (plain text, auto-encoded on apply). Secret types: <code>generic</code> (Opaque), <code>docker-registry</code> (image pull), <code>tls</code> (cert/key).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that injects ALL keys from a ConfigMap as environment variables using <code>envFrom</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: env-pod
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
envFrom:                       # ← Import ALL keys
- configMapRef:
    name: app-config           # ← All keys become env vars
- secretRef:
    name: db-creds             # ← All secret keys too

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl exec env-pod -- env | grep -E 'DB_|username|password'
<span class="comment"># DB_HOST=postgres</span>
<span class="comment"># DB_PORT=5432</span>
<span class="comment"># username=admin</span>
<span class="comment"># password=S3cret!</span>

<span class="comment"># vs. importing a SINGLE key:</span>
env:
- name: DATABASE_HOST              # ← Custom env var name
valueFrom:
configMapKeyRef:
  name: app-config
  key: DB_HOST                 # ← Specific key
- name: DB_PASS
valueFrom:
secretKeyRef:
  name: db-creds
  key: password</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>envFrom</code> imports ALL keys from a ConfigMap/Secret as environment variables. <code>env</code> with <code>valueFrom</code> imports ONE specific key with a custom name. On the CKA, know both patterns: (1) "inject all config" → use <code>envFrom</code>, (2) "inject specific key as <code>DATABASE_URL</code>" → use <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code>. Gotcha: if two sources have the same key name, the last one in the list wins. Invalid env var names (keys with dashes) are silently skipped with <code>envFrom</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Mount a ConfigMap as a volume. What happens when you update the ConfigMap?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Mount ConfigMap as volume:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: config-vol-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: config-vol
configMap:
  name: app-config
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: config-vol
  mountPath: /etc/config

<span class="comment"># Each ConfigMap key becomes a file:</span>
kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- ls /etc/config
<span class="comment"># DB_HOST  DB_PORT</span>

kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- cat /etc/config/DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># postgres</span>

<span class="comment"># Update the ConfigMap:</span>
kubectl edit configmap app-config
<span class="comment"># Change DB_HOST to "mysql"</span>

<span class="comment"># After ~60 seconds (kubelet sync period):</span>
kubectl exec config-vol-pod -- cat /etc/config/DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># mysql  ← Auto-updated!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ BUT env vars do NOT auto-update!</span>
<span class="comment"># envFrom values only change on pod restart</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ subPath mounts do NOT auto-update either!</span>
<span class="comment"># volumeMounts with subPath are frozen at pod creation</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Volume-mounted ConfigMaps auto-update within ~60 seconds (kubelet sync period). Environment variables do NOT auto-update. <code>subPath</code> mounts do NOT auto-update. On the CKA, this is a classic trick question: "How do you make your app pick up ConfigMap changes without restart?" Answer: mount as a volume (not env vars). Some apps also need to watch for file changes or receive SIGHUP to reload configuration. The auto-update uses symlinks internally — <code>/etc/config/DB_HOST</code> is actually a symlink to a timestamped directory.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>stringData</code> in a Secret YAML and how is it different from <code>data</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Using 'data' — values must be base64-encoded:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: my-secret
type: Opaque
data:
username: YWRtaW4=          <span class="comment"># echo -n "admin" | base64</span>
password: UzNjcmV0IQ==     <span class="comment"># echo -n "S3cret!" | base64</span>

<span class="comment"># Using 'stringData' — plain text (auto-encoded):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: my-secret
type: Opaque
stringData:                   # ← Plain text!
username: admin
password: S3cret!

<span class="comment"># Both produce the SAME Secret!</span>
<span class="comment"># stringData is write-only — after apply, API returns 'data' (base64)</span>

<span class="comment"># Mix both:</span>
data:
existing-key: YWJj
stringData:
new-key: plain text value   # ← Easier for new keys

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ stringData doesn't appear in kubectl get -o yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># After creation, everything is stored under 'data' as base64</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>stringData</code> is a convenience field that accepts plain text — Kubernetes auto-encodes to base64 on creation. <code>data</code> requires pre-encoded base64 values. On the CKA, <code>stringData</code> saves time when writing YAML manually (no need to run <code>echo -n | base64</code>). After creation, <code>kubectl get secret -o yaml</code> only shows <code>data</code> (base64). If both <code>data</code> and <code>stringData</code> have the same key, <code>stringData</code> wins. Use <code>stringData</code> to avoid base64 encoding mistakes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that mounts a Secret as a volume with specific file permissions (<code>0400</code>).</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: secret-vol-pod
spec:
volumes:
- name: secret-vol
secret:
  secretName: db-creds
  defaultMode: 0400           # ← Octal: owner read-only
  <span class="comment"># Per-key permissions:</span>
  items:
  - key: password
    path: db-password         # ← Custom filename
    mode: 0400                # ← Per-file mode
  - key: username
    path: db-username
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
volumeMounts:
- name: secret-vol
  mountPath: /etc/secrets
  readOnly: true              # ← Extra safety

<span class="comment"># Verify permissions:</span>
kubectl exec secret-vol-pod -- ls -la /etc/secrets/
<span class="comment"># -r--------  1 root root  7 Jan 1 00:00 db-password</span>
<span class="comment"># -r--------  1 root root  5 Jan 1 00:00 db-username</span>

kubectl exec secret-vol-pod -- cat /etc/secrets/db-password
<span class="comment"># S3cret!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>defaultMode</code> sets file permissions for all keys (octal format: 0644 = rw-r--r--, 0400 = r--------). Per-key <code>mode</code> overrides defaultMode for individual files. On the CKA, Secret volumes default to 0644 — which might be too permissive for passwords. Set <code>0400</code> for sensitive data. Adding <code>readOnly: true</code> on the volumeMount adds an extra layer. The <code>items</code> field controls which keys to mount and their filenames, similar to ConfigMap volumes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you make a ConfigMap immutable? What are the benefits?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
name: prod-config
data:
DB_HOST: prod-db.example.com
DB_PORT: "5432"
immutable: true                   # ← Cannot be modified after apply!

<span class="comment"># Trying to edit:</span>
kubectl edit configmap prod-config
<span class="comment"># error: configmaps "prod-config" is immutable</span>

<span class="comment"># To change: must delete and recreate</span>
kubectl delete configmap prod-config
kubectl create configmap prod-config \
--from-literal=DB_HOST=new-db.example.com

<span class="comment"># Benefits of immutable:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Protection against accidental changes</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Performance: kubelet stops watching for updates</span>
<span class="comment">#    (reduces API server load significantly)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Clear intent: "this config MUST NOT change"</span>

<span class="comment"># Works for Secrets too:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: prod-secret
immutable: true
stringData:
API_KEY: my-key-that-never-changes</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>immutable: true</code> prevents any modification. The main benefit is performance: kubelet stops watching for changes, reducing API server load (significant with many ConfigMaps). Once set, you CANNOT unset it — you must delete and recreate. On the CKA, this might appear as: "Ensure this ConfigMap cannot be accidentally modified." Apply <code>immutable: true</code>. Works for both ConfigMaps and Secrets. Note: pods mounting an immutable ConfigMap must be restarted after recreating the ConfigMap.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>docker-registry</code> secret type and how do you use it to pull images from a private registry?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create docker-registry secret:</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
--docker-server=registry.example.com \
--docker-username=myuser \
--docker-password=mypass \
--docker-email=user@example.com

<span class="comment"># Use in a pod:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: private-app
spec:
imagePullSecrets:               # ← Pod-level field
- name: regcred                 # ← Reference the secret
containers:
- name: app
image: registry.example.com/myapp:v1

<span class="comment"># Or attach to a ServiceAccount (all pods inherit):</span>
kubectl patch serviceaccount default \
-p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "regcred"}]}'

<span class="comment"># Verify the secret content:</span>
kubectl get secret regcred -o jsonpath='{.data.\.dockerconfigjson}' \
| base64 -d | python -m json.tool
<span class="comment"># {</span>
<span class="comment">#   "auths": {</span>
<span class="comment">#     "registry.example.com": {</span>
<span class="comment">#       "username": "myuser",</span>
<span class="comment">#       "password": "mypass",</span>
<span class="comment">#       "auth": "bXl1c2VyOm15cGFzcw=="</span>
<span class="comment">#     }</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>
<span class="comment"># }</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>docker-registry</code> secret type stores Docker credentials in <code>.dockerconfigjson</code> format. Use it with <code>imagePullSecrets</code> in the pod spec or attach to a ServiceAccount for automatic injection. On the CKA, if a pod fails with <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> from a private registry, create a <code>docker-registry</code> secret and reference it. Attaching to the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount affects all pods in the namespace that don't specify a different SA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod can't access its ConfigMap values. The ConfigMap exists and the pod is running. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check 1: Is the ConfigMap in the same namespace?</span>
kubectl get configmap app-config -n production
<span class="comment"># Error: not found ← ConfigMap is in wrong namespace!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 2: Pod spec references correct name?</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o yaml | grep -A5 configMapRef
<span class="comment">#   configMapRef:</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: app-configg  ← Typo! Extra 'g'</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 3: env vs envFrom?</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- env | grep DB_HOST
<span class="comment"># (empty) ← Env var not set!</span>

<span class="comment"># Possible causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - envFrom only imports keys that are valid env var names</span>
<span class="comment">#   Key "my-config.key" with dot → SKIPPED silently!</span>
<span class="comment"># - env with key that doesn't exist → pod won't start</span>
<span class="comment">#   (pod stays ContainerCreating with error event)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 4: Volume mount present?</span>
kubectl describe pod my-pod | grep -A5 Mounts
kubectl exec my-pod -- ls /etc/config/
<span class="comment"># ls: /etc/config/: No such file or directory ← Mount missing!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check 5: ConfigMap has the right keys?</span>
kubectl describe configmap app-config
<span class="comment"># Data</span>
<span class="comment"># ====</span>
<span class="comment"># db_host:   ← lowercase!</span>
<span class="comment"># Pod expects DB_HOST ← case mismatch!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ConfigMap troubleshooting checklist: (1) same namespace? (2) name spelled correctly? (3) key names valid for env vars? (4) volume mount correct? (5) key case match? On the CKA, the most common issues are namespace mismatch (ConfigMap in different ns than pod) and typos in names/keys. If using <code>envFrom</code>, keys with special characters are silently skipped. If using <code>env</code> + <code>valueFrom</code> with a non-existent key, the pod fails to start — check Events with <code>describe</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you create a Secret from a certificate file? Show creating a TLS Secret and verifying it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create TLS Secret from cert and key files:</span>
kubectl create secret tls app-tls \
--cert=server.crt \
--key=server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get secret app-tls -o yaml
<span class="comment"># type: kubernetes.io/tls</span>
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   tls.crt: LS0tLS1CRUd...   ← base64 of cert</span>
<span class="comment">#   tls.key: LS0tLS1CRUd...   ← base64 of key</span>

<span class="comment"># Decode and inspect the certificate:</span>
kubectl get secret app-tls \
-o jsonpath='{.data.tls\.crt}' | base64 -d | openssl x509 -text -noout
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN = myapp.example.com</span>
<span class="comment"># Issuer: ...</span>
<span class="comment"># Validity: ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Use in an Ingress:</span>
spec:
tls:
- hosts:
- myapp.example.com
secretName: app-tls      # ← Reference TLS secret

<span class="comment"># Create generic secret from file:</span>
kubectl create secret generic ssh-key \
--from-file=id_rsa=/path/to/private-key
<span class="comment"># Key = "id_rsa", value = file contents</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl create secret tls</code> validates that the cert and key are valid PEM format. The Secret type is <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> with required keys <code>tls.crt</code> and <code>tls.key</code>. On the CKA, TLS secrets are used with Ingress TLS termination and are covered in the security domain. Use <code>--from-file</code> on <code>generic</code> secrets for arbitrary files (SSH keys, config files). The filename becomes the key unless you use <code>--from-file=custom-key=/path</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

