                </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART6 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part6-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 6 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Storage — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You can now give easypj durable, dynamically-provisioned storage and inject its configuration cleanly. From the volume model and its lifecycle, through the PV/PVC binding and StorageClasses, to ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets — easypj’s Postgres data survives any pod, and its settings live outside the image. This is the CKA’s 10% Storage domain.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 🟡 Part 6 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Volumes (Ch 21):</strong> a volume is tied to the pod’s lifetime; emptyDir = scratch that survives container (not pod) restarts; hostPath = a specific node’s path; CSI is the storage plug-point.</li>
                    <li><strong>PV/PVC (Ch 22):</strong> a PVC requests storage, a PV supplies it; they bind by size + accessModes + StorageClass. StorageClasses enable <em>dynamic</em> provisioning; reclaimPolicy decides delete vs retain.</li>
                    <li><strong>ConfigMaps &amp; Secrets (Ch 23):</strong> inject config/credentials as env vars or mounted files; Secrets are base64 (not encrypted by default) — enable encryption-at-rest and RBAC.</li>
                    <li><strong>Access modes:</strong> RWO (one node) · ROX (many read) · RWX (many read-write) — must match between PVC and PV.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> 10% here — create a PVC and mount it, bind a static PV, and project a ConfigMap/Secret are fast points.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 6 Visual Summary — request → bind → mount, plus config injection</div>
<pre>
   📝 PVC (request: 10Gi, RWO)  ──bind──▶  💽 PV (supply)  ◀──dynamic── 🏷️ StorageClass
            │ mounted at /var/lib/postgresql/data
            ▼
   📦 easypj-db pod  ◀── ⚙️ ConfigMap (settings) + 🔒 Secret (password) as env/files
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   data survives pod deletion; config lives outside the image.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 6 made easypj stateful and configurable. A PVC asks for storage and binds to a PV (statically or dynamically via a StorageClass); the pod mounts that PVC so its data outlives any restart. ConfigMaps and Secrets feed settings and credentials in as env vars or files.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> remember request (PVC) → supply (PV) → mount, the access-mode match, and that Secrets aren’t encrypted by default, and you own the 10% Storage domain. Next: Part 7 locks the whole thing down with RBAC, identity, PKI, and Pod Security.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART7 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part7">
            <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 7 · Security</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Lock It Down</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 24–27 · RBAC · PKI · <em>"Least privilege, end to end"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A cluster anyone can do anything on is a breach waiting to happen. Part 7 is the access-control core: RBAC (Roles/Bindings) that grant least privilege, ServiceAccounts and projected tokens that identify workloads, the cluster PKI and certificate workflow that authenticates everyone, and Pod Security + admission control that constrain what pods may do.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> write Roles/ClusterRoles and bind them to users and ServiceAccounts; test access with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>; read the cluster PKI and sign a CertificateSigningRequest; and enforce Pod Security Standards via admission.</div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — who are you, and what may you do?</div>
<pre>
   Ch 24 RBAC  ──▶  Ch 25 SERVICE ACCOUNTS  ──▶  Ch 26 TLS / PKI
   Role · Binding     workload identity · tokens      certs · CSR · CA
   can-i              projected tokens                kubeconfig auth
            └────────────────────┬────────────────────┘
                                 ▼
            Ch 27 POD SECURITY &amp; ADMISSION CONTROL (constrain pods)
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: every request to easypj is authenticated, authorized,
   and admitted only if it meets the cluster’s security baseline.
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch24">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 24 · RBAC — Role-Based Access Control</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">In a production Kubernetes cluster, you don't want every developer deleting nodes or every CI pipeline creating ClusterRoleBindings. <strong>RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)</strong> is Kubernetes' built-in authorization system that controls <strong>WHO</strong> can perform <strong>WHAT actions</strong> on <strong>WHICH resources</strong>. For our <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django app, RBAC lets us give developers permission to deploy pods while preventing them from modifying secrets or disrupting the cluster. This chapter covers all four RBAC objects, how to create and test them, and real-world scenarios you'll face on the CKA exam.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔒 RBAC</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Why RBAC exists and what happens without it — the blast radius of unrestricted access</li>
                    <li>✅ The four RBAC objects: Role, ClusterRole, RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding</li>
                    <li>✅ How to create Roles with specific verbs, resources, and API groups</li>
                    <li>✅ How to bind Roles to users, groups, and ServiceAccounts</li>
                    <li>✅ Testing permissions with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> and the <code>--as</code> flag</li>
                    <li>✅ Building a complete RBAC setup for the easypj project with dev, QA, and ops roles</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>RBAC Is Heavily Tested on the CKA</h5>
                <p>Expect 2-3 questions creating Roles/Bindings and debugging access issues. Key exam skills:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Create Role and RoleBinding imperatively with <code>kubectl create role</code> and <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code></li>
                    <li>Know the difference between Role (namespaced) and ClusterRole (cluster-wide)</li>
                    <li>Use <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> with <code>--as</code> to verify permissions</li>
                    <li>Understand that a ClusterRole can be bound at namespace level with a RoleBinding</li>
                    <li>Write RBAC YAML from scratch — practice until it's muscle memory</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>RBAC Imperative Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Create Role</td><td><code>kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create ClusterRole</td><td><code>kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer --verb=get,list --resource=nodes</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create RoleBinding</td><td><code>kubectl create rolebinding dev-bind --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create ClusterRoleBinding</td><td><code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding admin-bind --clusterrole=cluster-admin --user=admin</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Bind to ServiceAccount</td><td><code>kubectl create rolebinding sa-bind --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:default -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test permissions</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List all permissions</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View roles</td><td><code>kubectl get roles,rolebindings -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Role + RoleBinding — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">This is the most common RBAC pattern on the CKA. Memorize it.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># 1. Role — WHAT permissions (namespaced)</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader                   # ◀── role name
  namespace: easypj                  # ◀── MUST specify namespace
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]                    # ◀── "" = core API group
  resources: ["pods"]                # ◀── resource type
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]    # ◀── allowed actions
- apiGroups: ["apps"]                # ◀── apps API group
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
<span class="comment"># 2. RoleBinding — WHO gets the permissions</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: read-pods-binding
  namespace: easypj                  # ◀── same namespace as Role
subjects:
- kind: User                        # ◀── User, Group, or ServiceAccount
  name: jane                        # ◀── the WHO
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role                        # ◀── Role or ClusterRole
  name: pod-reader                   # ◀── must match Role name
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find RBAC YAML Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain role.rules                     <span class="comment"># permission rules</span>
kubectl explain role.rules.apiGroups           <span class="comment"># API group filter</span>
kubectl explain role.rules.resources           <span class="comment"># resource types</span>
kubectl explain role.rules.verbs               <span class="comment"># allowed actions</span>
kubectl explain rolebinding.subjects           <span class="comment"># who gets access</span>
kubectl explain rolebinding.roleRef            <span class="comment"># which role to bind</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="flow-diagram">
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">👤</span><span>User / SA</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🔐</span><span>Authentication</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">✅</span><span>RBAC Check</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🛡️</span><span>Admission</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">💾</span><span>etcd</span></div>
            </div>

