            <!-- ─────────── §24.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.1 What is RBAC and Why It Matters</h3>
                <p>RBAC answers one question: <strong>"Is this user allowed to do this action on this resource?"</strong> Without RBAC, anyone who can reach the Kubernetes API server can create, delete, or modify anything — pods, secrets, nodes, the entire cluster.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Without RBAC vs With RBAC — The Security Gate</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ WITHOUT RBAC (or permissive RBAC)         ✅ WITH PROPER RBAC            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────┐                          ┌──────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 👨‍💻 Developer  │──┐                      │ 👨‍💻 Developer  │──┐              │
  │  └──────────────┘  │                      └──────────────┘  │              │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  │   ┌──────────┐       ┌──────────────┐  │  ┌────────┐ │
  │  │ 🤖 CI/CD Bot  │──┼──▶│ API      │       │ 🤖 CI/CD Bot  │──┼─▶│ RBAC   │ │
  │  └──────────────┘  │   │ Server   │       └──────────────┘  │  │ Gate   │ │
  │  ┌──────────────┐  │   │          │       ┌──────────────┐  │  │        │ │
  │  │ 🧪 Intern    │──┘   │ FULL     │       │ 🧪 Intern    │──┘  │ ✅ pods │ │
  │  └──────────────┘      │ ACCESS   │       └──────────────┘     │ ❌ nodes│ │
  │                         │ TO       │                            │ ❌ sec. │ │
  │  Everyone can:         │ EVERY-   │       Only allowed          │        │ │
  │  • Delete nodes 💀    │ THING    │       actions pass →       └───┬────┘ │
  │  • Read secrets 🔓    │          │                               │      │
  │  • Modify RBAC 😱     │          │                          ┌────▼─────┐ │
  │  • Break cluster 💣   └──────────┘                          │ API      │ │
  │                                                              │ Server   │ │
  │                                                              └──────────┘ │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Real-World Disaster Without RBAC</h5>
                    <p>In 2018, Tesla's Kubernetes dashboard was left open without RBAC. Attackers accessed the cluster, found AWS credentials in environment variables, and used Tesla's infrastructure for cryptocurrency mining. <strong>RBAC would have prevented this entirely.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔑 The Three Questions RBAC Answers</h5>
                    <p>Every API request is checked against these three dimensions:</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">👤</div>
                        <h5>WHO?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Subject</strong> — User, Group, or ServiceAccount making the request</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚡</div>
                        <h5>WHAT?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Verb</strong> — The action: get, list, create, update, delete, watch, patch</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>WHICH?</h5>
                        <p><strong>Resource</strong> — pods, services, secrets, nodes, deployments, etc.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">check-rbac-enabled.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify RBAC is enabled on your cluster</span>
kubectl api-versions | grep rbac
<span class="comment"># Output: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1</span>

<span class="comment"># Check API server authorization mode</span>
kubectl describe pod kube-apiserver-controlplane -n kube-system | grep authorization-mode
<span class="comment"># Output: --authorization-mode=Node,RBAC</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.2 The Four RBAC Objects</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes RBAC uses exactly <strong>four objects</strong> that work in pairs: two define permissions (Role, ClusterRole) and two assign permissions (RoleBinding, ClusterRoleBinding).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">The Four RBAC Objects — How They Connect</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                          RBAC OBJECT MAP                                     │
  │                                                                              │
  │         DEFINE PERMISSIONS              ASSIGN PERMISSIONS                   │
  │         (What can be done?)            (Who gets them?)                      │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────────────┐            │
  │  │   📋 Role               │     │   🔗 RoleBinding             │            │
  │  │   Scope: ONE namespace  │◀────│   Scope: ONE namespace      │            │
  │  │                         │     │                             │            │
  │  │   "pods: get,list"      │     │   "jane → pod-reader"      │            │
  │  │   in namespace: easypj  │     │   in namespace: easypj     │            │
  │  └─────────────────────────┘     └──────────────┬──────────────┘            │
  │                                                  │                          │
  │                                        subjects: │                          │
  │                                    ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐            │
  │                                    │             │             │            │
  │                                 ┌──▼──┐    ┌────▼───┐   ┌────▼────┐       │
  │                                 │User │    │ Group  │   │Service  │       │
  │                                 │jane │    │dev-team│   │Account  │       │
  │                                 └─────┘    └────────┘   └─────────┘       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────────────────┐            │
  │  │   📋 ClusterRole        │     │   🔗 ClusterRoleBinding      │            │
  │  │   Scope: ENTIRE cluster │◀────│   Scope: ENTIRE cluster     │            │
  │  │                         │     │                             │            │
  │  │   "nodes: get,list"     │     │   "ops-admin → node-viewer"│            │
  │  │   cluster-wide          │     │   cluster-wide              │            │
  │  └─────────────────────────┘     └─────────────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  💡 TRICK: A RoleBinding CAN reference a ClusterRole!                       │
  │     This gives ClusterRole permissions scoped to ONE namespace.              │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Object</th>
                                <th>Scope</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                <th>Can Reference</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Role</strong></td>
                                <td>Namespace</td>
                                <td>Define permissions within a single namespace</td>
                                <td>—</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterRole</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster</td>
                                <td>Define permissions cluster-wide (nodes, PVs, namespaces)</td>
                                <td>—</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>RoleBinding</strong></td>
                                <td>Namespace</td>
                                <td>Grant Role OR ClusterRole to subjects in a namespace</td>
                                <td>Role or ClusterRole</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>ClusterRoleBinding</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster</td>
                                <td>Grant ClusterRole to subjects cluster-wide</td>
                                <td>ClusterRole only</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="relationship-map">
                    <div class="rel-hub">RBAC Decision</div>
                    <div class="rel-spokes">
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">👤</span>
                            <strong>Subject</strong>
                            <small>User, Group, or ServiceAccount</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">⚡</span>
                            <strong>Verb</strong>
                            <small>get, list, create, delete...</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">📦</span>
                            <strong>Resource</strong>
                            <small>pods, services, secrets, nodes</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">🏷️</span>
                            <strong>API Group</strong>
                            <small>"" (core), apps, rbac, networking</small>
                        </div>
                        <div class="rel-spoke">
                            <span class="spoke-icon">🌐</span>
                            <strong>Namespace</strong>
                            <small>Scoped or cluster-wide</small>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 When to Use ClusterRole + RoleBinding</h5>
                    <p>Create ONE ClusterRole (e.g., "pod-reader") and bind it in multiple namespaces with separate RoleBindings. This avoids duplicating Role definitions in every namespace. The ClusterRole is reused, but access is scoped per namespace.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.3 Creating Roles and ClusterRoles</h3>
                <p>A Role is a set of <strong>rules</strong>. Each rule specifies an API group, a list of resources, and the verbs (actions) allowed on them.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 The Three Parts of Every RBAC Rule</h5>
                    <p><code>apiGroups</code> → <code>resources</code> → <code>verbs</code> — "In this API group, on these resources, allow these actions"</p>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>API Group</th>
                                <th>Value in YAML</th>
                                <th>Resources</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Core</td><td><code>""</code></td><td>pods, services, configmaps, secrets, PVCs, nodes, namespaces</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Apps</td><td><code>"apps"</code></td><td>deployments, replicasets, statefulsets, daemonsets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Batch</td><td><code>"batch"</code></td><td>jobs, cronjobs</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Networking</td><td><code>"networking.k8s.io"</code></td><td>networkpolicies, ingresses</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>RBAC</td><td><code>"rbac.authorization.k8s.io"</code></td><td>roles, rolebindings, clusterroles, clusterrolebindings</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Storage</td><td><code>"storage.k8s.io"</code></td><td>storageclasses, csinodes</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">role-pod-reader.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Role: Read-only access to pods in easypj namespace</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]              <span class="comment"># core API group</span>
  resources: ["pods", "pods/log"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["services"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">clusterrole-node-viewer.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ClusterRole: View nodes cluster-wide (no namespace)</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: node-viewer              <span class="comment"># no namespace field!</span>
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["persistentvolumes"]   <span class="comment"># PVs are cluster-scoped</span>
  verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">imperative-role-creation.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative — faster on the exam!</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=pods,pods/log \
  -n easypj

kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=nodes

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML to inspect before applying</span>
kubectl create role deploy-manager \
  --verb=get,list,create,update,delete \
  --resource=deployments.apps \
  -n easypj \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 Resource Sub-resources</h5>
                    <p>Some resources have sub-resources like <code>pods/log</code>, <code>pods/exec</code>, <code>pods/portforward</code>. These must be listed separately in the <code>resources</code> array. Getting pod logs requires both <code>pods</code> and <code>pods/log</code> permissions.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.4 Creating RoleBindings and ClusterRoleBindings</h3>
                <p>A Binding connects a <strong>subject</strong> (who) to a <strong>role</strong> (what permissions). The binding itself determines the scope.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Binding Combinations — All 4 Valid Configurations</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    ALL VALID BINDING COMBINATIONS                             │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 1️⃣ Role         │───▶│ RoleBinding       │───▶│ Access in ONE       │     │
  │  │   (namespaced)  │    │ (namespaced)      │    │ namespace           │     │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘    └─────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 2️⃣ ClusterRole  │───▶│ ClusterRoleBinding│───▶│ Access EVERYWHERE   │     │
  │  │   (cluster)     │    │ (cluster)         │    │ (all namespaces)    │     │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘    └─────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 3️⃣ ClusterRole  │───▶│ RoleBinding       │───▶│ ClusterRole scoped  │     │
  │  │   (cluster)     │    │ (namespaced)      │    │ to ONE namespace!   │     │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘    └─────────────────────┘     │
  │                                                  ☝️ Reuse ClusterRole       │
  │                                                     across namespaces       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐                                │
  │  │ 4️⃣ Role         │ ✘  │ ClusterRoleBinding│    ❌ INVALID! Never works    │
  │  │   (namespaced)  │    │ (cluster)         │                                │
  │  └─────────────────┘    └──────────────────┘                                │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">rolebinding-user.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Bind "pod-reader" role to user "jane" in easypj namespace</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: jane-pod-reader
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: jane
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">clusterrolebinding-admin.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Give "ops-admin" cluster-wide node viewing access</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: ops-node-viewer
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: ops-admin
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole          <span class="comment"># ClusterRoleBinding MUST ref ClusterRole</span>
  name: node-viewer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">imperative-bindings.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Imperative RoleBinding — FASTEST for exam</span>
kubectl create rolebinding jane-pod-reader \
  --role=pod-reader \
  --user=jane \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># ClusterRoleBinding</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding ops-node-viewer \
  --clusterrole=node-viewer \
  --user=ops-admin

<span class="comment"># Bind ClusterRole to namespace (combination #3)</span>
kubectl create rolebinding dev-pod-reader \
  --clusterrole=pod-reader-cluster \
  --group=dev-team \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Bind to ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create rolebinding sa-deployer \
  --role=deploy-manager \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ RoleBindings Are Immutable in roleRef</h5>
                    <p>You <strong>cannot change</strong> the <code>roleRef</code> field after creating a RoleBinding. To change which Role is referenced, you must delete and recreate the binding. You CAN add/remove subjects though.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.5 Testing RBAC</h3>
                <p>The <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> command is your best friend for RBAC verification. On the CKA exam, always verify after creating roles and bindings.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">testing-rbac.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Test specific permission</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (jane only has get,list,watch)</span>

kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌</span>

<span class="comment"># Test cluster-scoped resources</span>
kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (only get,list,watch)</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL permissions for a user in a namespace</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Resources       Non-Resource URLs   Resource Names   Verbs</span>
<span class="comment"># pods             []                  []               [get list watch]</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/log         []                  []               [get list watch]</span>
<span class="comment"># services         []                  []               [get list]</span>

<span class="comment"># Test as a ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># SelfSubjectAccessReview — check YOUR OWN permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes (checks your current kubeconfig identity)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">RBAC Testing Decision Tree</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                   RBAC TESTING WORKFLOW                          │
  │                                                                  │
  │     Step 1: Create Role/ClusterRole                             │
  │         │                                                        │
  │         ▼                                                        │
  │     Step 2: Create Binding                                      │
  │         │                                                        │
  │         ▼                                                        │
  │     Step 3: Test with can-i                                     │
  │         │                                                        │
  │    ┌────┴────┐                                                  │
  │    │         │                                                  │
  │  "yes" ✅  "no" ❌                                              │
  │    │         │                                                  │
  │    ▼         ▼                                                  │
  │  DONE    Debug:                                                 │
  │          ├── kubectl get rolebinding -n &lt;ns&gt; -o yaml           │
  │          ├── Check: namespace matches?                          │
  │          ├── Check: role name matches?                          │
  │          ├── Check: subject name correct?                       │
  │          ├── Check: apiGroup correct?                           │
  │          └── Check: verb and resource correct?                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Speed Tip: Use --list for Debugging</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n easypj</code> shows ALL permissions at once. Much faster than testing individual verbs when debugging why something is denied.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.6 RBAC for easypj — Practical Example</h3>
                <p>Let's build a complete RBAC setup for the <strong>easypj</strong> job board application with three team roles: <strong>developers</strong> (full deploy access), <strong>QA team</strong> (read-only), and <strong>monitoring</strong> (read across all namespaces).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj RBAC Architecture — Three Teams, Three Access Levels</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    easypj RBAC SETUP                                          │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                       │
  │  │ 👨‍💻 DEVELOPERS     │    Role: easypj-developer                            │
  │  │ (group: dev-team)│───▶ pods: get,list,watch,create,update,delete         │
  │  │                  │    deployments: get,list,create,update                 │
  │  │ Full deploy      │    services: get,list,create                          │
  │  │ access in easypj │    configmaps: get,list                               │
  │  └──────────────────┘    Scope: namespace "easypj" only                     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                       │
  │  │ 🧪 QA TEAM        │    Role: easypj-qa-viewer                             │
  │  │ (group: qa-team) │───▶ pods: get,list,watch                              │
  │  │                  │    pods/log: get                                       │
  │  │ Read-only in     │    deployments: get,list                              │
  │  │ easypj           │    services: get,list                                 │
  │  └──────────────────┘    Scope: namespace "easypj" only                     │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐                                                       │
  │  │ 📊 MONITORING     │    ClusterRole: monitoring-reader                     │
  │  │ (user: monitor)  │───▶ pods: get,list,watch (ALL namespaces)             │
  │  │                  │    nodes: get,list                                     │
  │  │ Read access      │    events: get,list                                   │
  │  │ across cluster   │    Scope: cluster-wide (ClusterRoleBinding)           │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-rbac-complete.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ── 1. Developer Role ──
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-developer
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "pods/log", "pods/exec", "services", "configmaps"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "create", "update", "delete"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "create", "update", "patch"]
---
<span class="comment"># ── 2. Developer RoleBinding ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: dev-team-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: Group
  name: dev-team
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-developer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># ── 3. QA Read-Only Role ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-qa-viewer
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "pods/log", "services", "events"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
<span class="comment"># ── 4. QA RoleBinding ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: qa-team-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: Group
  name: qa-team
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-qa-viewer
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># ── 5. Monitoring ClusterRole ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: monitoring-reader
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods", "nodes", "services", "events"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: ["apps"]
  resources: ["deployments", "replicasets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]
---
<span class="comment"># ── 6. Monitoring ClusterRoleBinding ──</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: monitoring-binding
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: monitor
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: monitoring-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">verify-easypj-rbac.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply all RBAC resources</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-rbac-complete.yaml

<span class="comment"># ── Test developer access ──</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods --as-group=dev-team --as=dev-user -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as-group=dev-team --as=dev-user
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (no cluster access)</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Test QA access ──</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as-group=qa-team --as=qa-user -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as-group=qa-team --as=qa-user -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (read-only)</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Test monitoring access ──</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=monitor -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># yes ✅ (all namespaces)</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=monitor -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (read-only)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ RBAC Best Practices</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Use <strong>Groups</strong> over individual Users</li>
                            <li>Apply <strong>least privilege</strong> — only verbs actually needed</li>
                            <li>Use ClusterRole + RoleBinding for reusable rules</li>
                            <li>Always <code>can-i</code> test after creating bindings</li>
                            <li>Name roles descriptively: <code>easypj-developer</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ RBAC Anti-Patterns</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Binding <code>cluster-admin</code> to regular users</li>
                            <li>Using <code>*</code> wildcard verbs and resources</li>
                            <li>Not testing after creating bindings</li>
                            <li>One huge Role instead of multiple specific ones</li>
                            <li>Forgetting namespace on RoleBindings</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─── RBAC Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>RBAC Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=USER -n NS</code> → verify access granted/denied</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get roles -n NS</code> → confirm role exists with correct name</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get rolebindings -n NS</code> → confirm binding exists</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe rolebinding NAME -n NS</code> → verify subject and roleRef match</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=USER -n NS</code> → list ALL permissions for user</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get clusterroles | grep NAME</code> → confirm ClusterRole exists</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe clusterrolebinding NAME</code> → verify cluster-wide binding</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §24.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s24-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">24.7 Practice Exercises</h3>
                <p>Practice these scenarios until you can complete each one in under 5 minutes — that's CKA exam speed.</p>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 4 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.1 — Create Role and RoleBinding</h5>
                        <p>Create RBAC resources so user <code>jane</code> can read pods in a specific namespace.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create namespace <code>exam-practice</code></li>
                            <li>Create Role <code>pod-reader</code> allowing <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code> on pods</li>
                            <li>Create RoleBinding binding <code>pod-reader</code> to user <code>jane</code></li>
                            <li>Test: <code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n exam-practice</code> → should return <strong>yes</strong></li>
                            <li>Test: <code>kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n exam-practice</code> → should return <strong>no</strong></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Use imperative commands — <code>kubectl create role</code> and <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code> are fastest.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Role</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=pods \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-binding \
  --role=pod-reader \
  --user=jane \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=jane -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># no</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 3 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.2 — ClusterRole for Node Viewer</h5>
                        <p>Create a ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding so user <code>ops-admin</code> can view nodes cluster-wide.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create ClusterRole <code>node-viewer</code> allowing <code>get</code>, <code>list</code> on nodes</li>
                            <li>Create ClusterRoleBinding for user <code>ops-admin</code></li>
                            <li>Verify: <code>kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=ops-admin</code> → <strong>yes</strong></li>
                            <li>Verify: <code>kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=ops-admin</code> → <strong>no</strong></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding = cluster-wide access (no namespace flag).</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create ClusterRole</span>
kubectl create clusterrole node-viewer \
  --verb=get,list \
  --resource=nodes

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create ClusterRoleBinding</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding node-viewer-binding \
  --clusterrole=node-viewer \
  --user=ops-admin

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=ops-admin
<span class="comment"># no</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.3 — ServiceAccount RBAC</h5>
                        <p>Grant the <code>easypj-deployer</code> ServiceAccount permissions to manage deployments and services in the <code>easypj</code> namespace.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create ServiceAccount <code>easypj-deployer</code> in namespace <code>easypj</code></li>
                            <li>Create Role allowing <code>create</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>update</code> on deployments and services</li>
                            <li>Bind ServiceAccount to the Role</li>
                            <li>Test: <code>kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer -n easypj</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: The <code>--as</code> impersonation for ServiceAccounts uses the format <code>system:serviceaccount:NAMESPACE:SA_NAME</code>.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-deployer -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Role with multiple resources</span>
kubectl create role easypj-deploy-role \
  --verb=create,get,list,update \
  --resource=deployments,services \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Bind SA to Role</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-deploy-binding \
  --role=easypj-deploy-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-deployer \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no — only deployments and services are allowed</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 24.4 — Debug RBAC Issues</h5>
                        <p>Intentionally create a broken Role, identify the issue, and fix it.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create a Role with the wrong verb (e.g., <code>getlist</code> instead of <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>)</li>
                            <li>Bind it and test — observe the failure</li>
                            <li>Use <code>kubectl auth can-i --list</code> to find what the user CAN do</li>
                            <li>Fix the Role and verify access is restored</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Use <code>kubectl edit role</code> to fix the verbs. Each verb must be a separate list item, not concatenated.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create broken Role (wrong verb)</span>
kubectl create role broken-role \
  --verb=getlist \
  --resource=pods \
  -n exam-practice

kubectl create rolebinding broken-binding \
  --role=broken-role \
  --user=testuser \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Test — fails because "getlist" isn't a valid verb</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=testuser -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># no</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Debug — see what user CAN do</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=testuser -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># Shows "getlist" verb — not "get" or "list"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix the Role</span>
kubectl edit role broken-role -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># Change verbs from ["getlist"] to ["get", "list"]</span>
<span class="comment"># Or delete and recreate:</span>
kubectl delete role broken-role -n exam-practice
kubectl create role broken-role \
  --verb=get,list \
  --resource=pods \
  -n exam-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify — now works</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=testuser -n exam-practice
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>⏱️ Target: 5 Minutes Per Exercise</h5>
                    <p>On the CKA exam, RBAC questions must be fast. Use imperative commands (<code>kubectl create role</code>, <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code>) not YAML files. Only fall back to YAML for complex multi-rule roles.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 24 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: RBAC Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Role vs ClusterRole</strong> — <code>Role</code> is namespace-scoped (pods, services). <code>ClusterRole</code> is cluster-wide (nodes, PVs, namespaces). If the exam says "allow listing nodes", you MUST use ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</li>
                        <li><strong>RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole</strong> — This is powerful and confusing. A RoleBinding in namespace <code>prod</code> pointing to a ClusterRole limits those permissions to <code>prod</code> only. The exam tests this</li>
                        <li><strong>Verbs are comma-separated strings, not arrays in CLI</strong> — <code>--verb=get,list,watch</code> NOT <code>--verb=[get,list,watch]</code>. In YAML they ARE arrays: <code>["get", "list"]</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> is your verification tool</strong> — Always verify after creating RBAC: <code>kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n dev</code>. The <code>--as</code> flag impersonates a user</li>
                        <li><strong>API groups matter</strong> — Core resources (pods, services) use <code>apiGroups: [""]</code>. Deployments use <code>apiGroups: ["apps"]</code>. Forgetting the API group = silent permission denial</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How RBAC Authorization Is Evaluated: Role/ClusterRole Resolution, Binding Matching, and the can-i Algorithm</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ RBAC Evaluation Algorithm — How the API Server Decides ALLOW/DENY</h5>
            <p>Every API request passes through the RBAC authorizer. Understanding the exact evaluation order helps you debug "why can't this user do X" and craft correct policies.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  RBAC AUTHORIZATION PIPELINE — Request Evaluation                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Request: User "jane" wants to GET /api/v1/namespaces/dev/pods               │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: Extract Subject Identity                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ From TLS cert: CN=jane, O=developers, O=viewers                      │   │
│  │ OR from JWT token: sub=jane, groups=[developers, viewers]            │   │
│  │ → Subject: User=jane, Groups=[developers, viewers]                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Build ALL Potential Roles (union of ALL bindings)                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Find RoleBindings matching jane OR [developers] OR [viewers]:         │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RoleBinding: pod-reader (namespace: dev)                               │   │
│  │   subjects: [{kind:User, name:jane}]  <span class="comment">← Direct match!                 </span>│   │
│  │   roleRef: {kind:Role, name:pod-reader}                               │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RoleBinding: view-ns (namespace: dev)                                  │   │
│  │   subjects: [{kind:Group, name:viewers}] <span class="comment">← Group match!               </span>│   │
│  │   roleRef: {kind:ClusterRole, name:view}                              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ → Collected roles: [pod-reader (Role), view (ClusterRole)]            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: Check EACH Role for Matching Rules (UNION of all rules)             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Role: pod-reader (namespace: dev):                                     │   │
│  │   rules:                                                               │   │
│  │   - apiGroups: [""]     <span class="comment">← core API group (v1)                        </span>│   │
│  │     resources: ["pods"]  <span class="comment">← Match! (request is for pods)              </span>│   │
│  │     verbs: ["get","list","watch"] <span class="comment">← Match! (request verb is "get")   </span>│   │
│  │   → RULE MATCHED! Pod reader allows get pods in dev.                  │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ ClusterRole: view:                                                     │   │
│  │   rules:                                                               │   │
│  │   - apiGroups: [""]                                                    │   │
│  │     resources: ["pods","services","deployments",...]                 │   │
│  │     verbs: ["get","list","watch"]                                    │   │
│  │   → RULE ALSO MATCHED (but redundant — pod-reader already covers it) │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 4: FINAL DECISION                                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ At least one matching rule found → ALLOW ✅                           │   │
│  │ No matching rules found → DENY ❌                                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RBAC IS ADDITIVE ONLY:                                                 │   │
│  │   ✅ Roles ADD permissions (union of all matching rules)              │   │
│  │   ❌ There is NO "deny" rule in RBAC                                 │   │
│  │   ❌ You cannot "subtract" a permission once granted                  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CLUSTERROLE vs ROLE — SCOPE MATTERS:                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Rule:     apiGroups: [""]                                             │   │
│  │           resources: ["nodes"]                                        │   │
│  │           verbs: ["get"]                                              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ In a ROLE (namespaced):        In a CLUSTERROLE (cluster-wide):       │   │
│  │ → Can get pods in dev          → Can get pods in ALL namespaces      │   │
│  │ → Can get services in dev      → Can get nodes (cluster-scoped)       │   │
│  │ → CANNOT get nodes!             → Can get namespaces (cluster-scoped) │   │
│  │   (nodes are cluster-scoped)                                           │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ A ClusterRole can be bound via:                                        │   │
│  │   RoleBinding → grants ClusterRole permissions in ONE namespace       │   │
│  │   ClusterRoleBinding → grants ClusterRole permissions CLUSTER-WIDE    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  IMPERSONATION — Testing RBAC (CKA Superpower):                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n dev             │   │
│  │ yes  /  no                                                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n dev                          │   │
│  │ Resources  Non-Resource URLs  Resource Names  Verbs                   │   │
│  │ pods       []                 []              [get list watch]        │   │
│  │ configmaps []                 []              [get]                   │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> RBAC is a guaranteed CKA topic. The exam tests creating Roles + RoleBindings for specific scenarios. Key tips: (1) RBAC is additive only — no deny rules, (2) ClusterRole + RoleBinding = namespaced permissions, (3) ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding = cluster-wide, (4) Always test with <code>kubectl auth can-i --as=&lt;user&gt;</code> before submitting your answer, (5) RoleBinding subjects support User, Group, and ServiceAccount.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">ClusterRoleBinding can reference a Role</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">ClusterRoleBinding can ONLY reference a ClusterRole — never a Role</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">A RoleBinding with a ClusterRole gives cluster-wide access</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">RoleBinding + ClusterRole = ClusterRole permissions scoped to THAT namespace only</span></li>
                    <li><code>apiGroups: [""]</code> means core API — pods, services, configmaps. Apps group needs <code>["apps"]</code></li>
                    <li>Verbs are: <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>deletecollection</code></li>
                    <li>ServiceAccount subject format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> returns "yes"/"no" — use <code>--list</code> to see ALL permissions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — RBAC in 4 imperative commands:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n dev</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-binding --role=pod-reader --user=jane -n dev</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create clusterrole node-reader --verb=get,list --resource=nodes</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding node-reader-binding --clusterrole=node-reader --user=jane</code><br>
                <strong>Never write RBAC YAML from scratch on the exam unless forced to.</strong></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 24)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 24 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch24-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 24 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Role/ClusterRole authorization — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Role named <code>pod-reader</code> in namespace <code>dev</code> that allows <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, and <code>watch</code> on pods. Then bind it to user <code>jane</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create Role:</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
--verb=get,list,watch \
--resource=pods \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Create RoleBinding:</span>
kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-binding \
--role=pod-reader \
--user=jane \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods --as=jane -n dev
<span class="comment"># no ← Only get, list, watch!</span>

kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n prod
<span class="comment"># no ← Role only in dev namespace!</span>

<span class="comment"># View created objects:</span>
kubectl describe role pod-reader -n dev
kubectl describe rolebinding pod-reader-binding -n dev</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Roles define WHAT actions on WHICH resources. RoleBindings connect WHO (user/group/SA) to a Role. Both are namespace-scoped. On the CKA, use imperative commands — never write RBAC YAML from scratch unless forced. The <code>--verb</code> flag accepts comma-separated verbs. Verify with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>. Common verbs: <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>deletecollection</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between Role/RoleBinding and ClusterRole/ClusterRoleBinding? When must you use ClusterRole?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Aspect</th>
                                        <th>Role + RoleBinding</th>
                                        <th>ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Scope</td>
                                        <td>Single namespace</td>
                                        <td>Cluster-wide</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Resources</td>
                                        <td>Namespaced (pods, svc, cm)</td>
                                        <td>All + non-namespaced (nodes, PVs, ns)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Use case</td>
                                        <td>Dev permissions in one ns</td>
                                        <td>Cluster admin, node ops</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Combo</td>
                                        <td>Role → RoleBinding only</td>
                                        <td>ClusterRole → RoleBinding OR ClusterRoleBinding</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># MUST use ClusterRole for:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Nodes (non-namespaced)</span>
<span class="comment"># - PersistentVolumes (non-namespaced)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Namespaces (non-namespaced)</span>
<span class="comment"># - CSRs, ClusterRoles, ClusterRoleBindings</span>

<span class="comment"># Trick: ClusterRole + RoleBinding = scoped power!</span>
<span class="comment"># Grant ClusterRole permissions in ONE namespace:</span>
kubectl create rolebinding scoped-admin \
--clusterrole=admin \
--user=jane \
-n dev    # ← jane is admin ONLY in dev</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ClusterRoles are needed for non-namespaced resources (nodes, PVs, namespaces) and cross-namespace access. The power combo: ClusterRole + RoleBinding limits ClusterRole permissions to a single namespace. On the CKA, if you see "allow user to list nodes" → ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding (nodes are cluster-scoped). If "allow user to list pods in namespace dev" → Role + RoleBinding (or ClusterRole + RoleBinding in dev).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check what permissions a user has? List all permissions for user <code>dev-user</code> in namespace <code>production</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check specific permission:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=dev-user -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete deployments --as=dev-user -n production
<span class="comment"># no</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL permissions:</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=dev-user -n production
<span class="comment"># Resources          Non-Resource URLs    Verbs</span>
<span class="comment"># pods               []                   [get list watch]</span>
<span class="comment"># services           []                   [get list]</span>
<span class="comment"># configmaps         []                   [get]</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments.apps   []                   [get list watch create update]</span>

<span class="comment"># For a ServiceAccount:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:production:deploy-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

<span class="comment"># Check if you (current user) can do something:</span>
kubectl auth can-i create clusterroles
<span class="comment"># no  (unless you're cluster-admin)</span>

<span class="comment"># Wildcard check:</span>
kubectl auth can-i '*' '*' --as=admin-user
<span class="comment"># yes ← Full admin!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> is the essential RBAC verification tool. Use <code>--as</code> to impersonate users, <code>--as=system:serviceaccount:NS:NAME</code> for ServiceAccounts. <code>--list</code> shows ALL permissions in a namespace. On the CKA, ALWAYS verify after creating RBAC objects — <code>auth can-i</code> confirms your configuration works. This is also how you debug "permission denied" errors: check what the user/SA can actually do.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What API group do you use for Deployments? For Pods? How does this affect RBAC rules?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Core API resources (apiGroups: [""]):</span>
<span class="comment"># pods, services, configmaps, secrets, PVCs, endpoints, nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># apps group (apiGroups: ["apps"]):</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments, replicasets, daemonsets, statefulsets</span>

<span class="comment"># networking.k8s.io:</span>
<span class="comment"># networkpolicies, ingresses</span>

<span class="comment"># rbac.authorization.k8s.io:</span>
<span class="comment"># roles, rolebindings, clusterroles, clusterrolebindings</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick lookup:</span>
kubectl api-resources | grep -i deployment
<span class="comment"># NAME          SHORTNAMES  APIVERSION  NAMESPACED  KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy      apps/v1     true        Deployment</span>
<span class="comment">#                           ↑ apps group!</span>

<span class="comment"># RBAC rule for Deployments:</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
name: deploy-manager
namespace: dev
rules:
- apiGroups: ["apps"]        # ← NOT empty string!
resources: ["deployments"]
verbs: ["get", "list", "create", "update", "delete"]
- apiGroups: [""]            # ← Core API (pods)
resources: ["pods"]
verbs: ["get", "list"]

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Forgetting apiGroups or using wrong group = silent denial!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>API groups are critical in RBAC rules. Core resources (pods, services) use empty string <code>""</code>. Deployments use <code>"apps"</code>. NetworkPolicies use <code>"networking.k8s.io"</code>. Use <code>kubectl api-resources</code> to find the right group. On the CKA, wrong API group = the role won't grant access (silently fails). Most common mistake: using <code>apiGroups: [""]</code> for Deployments when it should be <code>apiGroups: ["apps"]</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ClusterRole that allows reading nodes and PersistentVolumes. Bind it to user <code>infra-admin</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create ClusterRole:</span>
kubectl create clusterrole infra-reader \
--verb=get,list,watch \
--resource=nodes,persistentvolumes

<span class="comment"># Create ClusterRoleBinding:</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding infra-reader-binding \
--clusterrole=infra-reader \
--user=infra-admin

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl auth can-i list nodes --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i list pv --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete nodes --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># no ← Only read verbs!</span>

kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=infra-admin
<span class="comment"># no ← Only nodes and PVs!</span>

<span class="comment"># Multiple resources in one rule:</span>
<span class="comment"># The CLI creates:</span>
<span class="comment"># rules:</span>
<span class="comment"># - apiGroups: [""]</span>
<span class="comment">#   resources: ["nodes", "persistentvolumes"]</span>
<span class="comment">#   verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Nodes and PVs are cluster-scoped resources — they require ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding. A regular Role can't grant access to cluster-scoped resources. On the CKA, the imperative command handles multiple resources with comma separation. Both nodes and PVs are in the core API group (<code>""</code>). Verify with <code>auth can-i</code> — check both positive (what's allowed) and negative (what's denied) cases.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A user runs <code>kubectl get pods -n production</code> and gets <code>Forbidden</code>. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check what the user CAN do:</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># If empty → no permissions at all</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check RoleBindings in the namespace:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n production -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME           ROLE                 SUBJECTS</span>
<span class="comment"># dev-binding    Role/dev-role        User:jane  ← Found!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check the Role:</span>
kubectl describe role dev-role -n production
<span class="comment"># Rules:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Resources: [deployments]</span>
<span class="comment">#   Verbs: [get list]</span>
<span class="comment"># ← pods not included!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix — add pods to the role:</span>
kubectl edit role dev-role -n production
<span class="comment"># Add:</span>
<span class="comment"># - apiGroups: [""]</span>
<span class="comment">#   resources: ["pods"]</span>
<span class="comment">#   verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]</span>

<span class="comment"># Or create a new role and binding:</span>
kubectl create role pod-reader \
--verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n production
kubectl create rolebinding pod-reader-jane \
--role=pod-reader --user=jane -n production

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify fix:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>RBAC troubleshooting flow: (1) check current permissions with <code>auth can-i --list</code>, (2) find RoleBindings with <code>-o wide</code> to see subjects, (3) describe the Role to see rules, (4) fix the gap. On the CKA, common issues: wrong namespace, missing verb (has <code>get</code> but not <code>list</code>), wrong API group, or no binding at all. The <code>-o wide</code> flag on rolebindings shows the subject (user/group/SA) — essential for debugging.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you grant a ClusterRole's permissions in only ONE namespace? What is this pattern called?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Pattern: ClusterRole + RoleBinding (scoped power)</span>
<span class="comment"># Grant built-in "edit" ClusterRole in just "dev" namespace:</span>
kubectl create rolebinding dev-editor \
--clusterrole=edit \
--user=jane \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># jane now has edit permissions in dev ONLY:</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># no ← Not in other namespaces!</span>

<span class="comment"># Built-in ClusterRoles you can reuse:</span>
<span class="comment"># cluster-admin   → full access to everything</span>
<span class="comment"># admin           → full namespace access (except quotas/ns)</span>
<span class="comment"># edit            → read-write (no RBAC or quotas)</span>
<span class="comment"># view            → read-only</span>

<span class="comment"># This pattern is called "role aggregation" or "scoped ClusterRole"</span>
<span class="comment"># It avoids creating duplicate Roles in every namespace</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole — the ClusterRole's permissions are then scoped to the RoleBinding's namespace. This is efficient: define once as ClusterRole, bind in specific namespaces with RoleBindings. On the CKA, use the built-in ClusterRoles (<code>view</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>admin</code>) with namespace-scoped RoleBindings. Note: a RoleBinding can NEVER reference a regular Role from another namespace — it can only reference a ClusterRole or a Role in the same namespace.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an RBAC rule that allows accessing the <code>logs</code> subresource of pods. What is a subresource?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Subresources are actions on a resource's child endpoints:</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/log        → kubectl logs</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/exec       → kubectl exec</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/portforward → kubectl port-forward</span>
<span class="comment"># pods/status     → pod status updates</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments/scale → kubectl scale</span>

<span class="comment"># Role allowing logs and exec:</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
name: pod-debugger
namespace: dev
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods"]
verbs: ["get", "list"]         # ← Read pods
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods/log"]        # ← Log subresource
verbs: ["get"]
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods/exec"]       # ← Exec subresource
verbs: ["create"]              # ← exec uses create verb!

<span class="comment"># Imperative:</span>
kubectl create role pod-debugger \
--verb=get,list --resource=pods \
--verb=get --resource=pods/log \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ exec uses verb "create" not "get"!</span>
<span class="comment"># It creates an exec session</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Subresources extend resources with specific operations: <code>pods/log</code>, <code>pods/exec</code>, <code>pods/portforward</code>, <code>deployments/scale</code>. On the CKA, a common task: "allow a user to view logs but not exec into pods." Grant <code>pods/log</code> with <code>get</code> verb, but NOT <code>pods/exec</code>. Critical gotcha: <code>kubectl exec</code> uses the <code>create</code> verb (not <code>get</code>), because it creates a new exec session. Granting permission on <code>pods</code> does NOT automatically grant subresource access.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you grant permissions only on specific named resources (e.g., only pod <code>important-pod</code>)?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Use resourceNames to restrict to specific objects:</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
name: specific-pod-reader
namespace: production
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["pods"]
resourceNames: ["important-pod", "critical-pod"]  # ← Only these!
verbs: ["get", "watch", "delete"]

<span class="comment"># Result:</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pod/important-pod \
--as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i get pod/other-pod \
--as=jane -n production
<span class="comment"># no ← Not in resourceNames!</span>

<span class="comment"># ⚠️ resourceNames doesn't work with "list":</span>
<span class="comment"># You can't "list" specific resources</span>
<span class="comment"># "list" returns ALL resources — it's always all-or-nothing</span>
<span class="comment"># Use get for specific names, list for all</span>

<span class="comment"># Also works with secrets:</span>
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
resources: ["secrets"]
resourceNames: ["db-password"]
verbs: ["get"]</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>resourceNames</code> narrows access to specific named objects. Useful for secrets (only allow reading one specific secret). On the CKA, this is used in least-privilege scenarios: "grant access to secret <code>db-creds</code> only." Key limitation: <code>list</code> and <code>create</code> verbs cannot use <code>resourceNames</code> (list returns everything, create doesn't have a name yet). Only <code>get</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>patch</code>, <code>delete</code>, and <code>watch</code> work with resourceNames.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you quickly find which RoleBindings/ClusterRoleBindings apply to a specific user or ServiceAccount?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check namespace RoleBindings:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n dev -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME              ROLE                 SUBJECTS</span>
<span class="comment"># pod-reader-rb     Role/pod-reader      User:jane</span>
<span class="comment"># deploy-editor     ClusterRole/edit     Group:developers</span>

<span class="comment"># Check ClusterRoleBindings:</span>
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep jane
<span class="comment"># node-reader-crb   ClusterRole/node-reader   User:jane</span>

<span class="comment"># Search ALL namespaces:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -A -o wide | grep jane
<span class="comment"># dev        pod-reader-rb    Role/pod-reader    User:jane</span>
<span class="comment"># staging    deploy-rb        Role/deploy-mgr    User:jane</span>

<span class="comment"># For ServiceAccounts:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -A -o wide | grep deploy-sa
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep deploy-sa

<span class="comment"># Describe for full details:</span>
kubectl describe rolebinding pod-reader-rb -n dev
<span class="comment"># Role:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Kind: Role</span>
<span class="comment">#   Name: pod-reader</span>
<span class="comment"># Subjects:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Kind   Name   Namespace</span>
<span class="comment">#   ----   ----   ---------</span>
<span class="comment">#   User   jane</span>

<span class="comment"># All permissions summary:</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=jane -n dev</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Finding all RBAC bindings for a user: (1) <code>kubectl get rolebindings -A -o wide | grep &lt;user&gt;</code> for namespace bindings, (2) <code>kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep &lt;user&gt;</code> for cluster bindings. The <code>-o wide</code> flag shows the SUBJECTS column, making it greppable. On the CKA, when debugging permissions, check BOTH RoleBindings (per namespace) and ClusterRoleBindings (global). <code>auth can-i --list</code> gives the final computed permissions after all bindings are evaluated.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Set up a multi-role RBAC system: a <code>dev</code> user who can manage pods/deployments in the <code>dev</code> namespace, a <code>qa</code> user who can only view resources in all namespaces, and an <code>ops</code> user with full cluster admin. Show all the Roles, ClusterRoles, and Bindings.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># 1. DEV user — manage pods/deployments in "dev" namespace only</span>
kubectl create role dev-role -n dev \
--verb=get,list,watch,create,update,delete \
--resource=pods,deployments,services

kubectl create rolebinding dev-binding -n dev \
--role=dev-role --user=dev

<span class="comment"># 2. QA user — read-only across ALL namespaces</span>
<span class="comment"># Use built-in ClusterRole "view"</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding qa-binding \
--clusterrole=view --user=qa

<span class="comment"># 3. OPS user — full cluster admin</span>
<span class="comment"># Use built-in ClusterRole "cluster-admin"</span>
kubectl create clusterrolebinding ops-binding \
--clusterrole=cluster-admin --user=ops

<span class="comment"># Verify each user's permissions:</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n dev --as=dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments -n production --as=dev
<span class="comment"># no ← dev only has access to "dev" namespace</span>

kubectl auth can-i get pods -n kube-system --as=qa
<span class="comment"># yes ← "view" ClusterRole works in all namespaces</span>
kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n dev --as=qa
<span class="comment"># no ← view is read-only</span>

kubectl auth can-i '*' '*' --as=ops
<span class="comment"># yes ← cluster-admin can do everything</span>

<span class="comment"># List all bindings for verification:</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n dev -o wide
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o wide | grep -E 'qa|ops'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This pattern demonstrates the three common RBAC tiers: (1) <strong>Namespace-scoped</strong> via Role + RoleBinding — limits access to one namespace, (2) <strong>Cluster-wide read-only</strong> via built-in <code>view</code> ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding, (3) <strong>Full admin</strong> via built-in <code>cluster-admin</code> ClusterRole. Kubernetes ships with four default ClusterRoles: <code>view</code> (read-only), <code>edit</code> (read+write, no RBAC), <code>admin</code> (namespace admin including RBAC), <code>cluster-admin</code> (everything). On the CKA, always prefer imperative commands for speed: <code>kubectl create role</code>, <code>kubectl create rolebinding</code>, <code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding</code>. Use <code>--as=</code> to impersonate and verify.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 24 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch25">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 25 · Service Accounts &amp; API Access</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Humans use <code>kubectl</code> with certificates to talk to the API server, but what about pods? When your <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> Django application needs to read ConfigMaps, list pods, or interact with the Kubernetes API, it does so through a <strong>Service Account</strong>. Every pod in Kubernetes automatically gets a Service Account, but the default one has minimal permissions. In this chapter, you'll learn how to create dedicated Service Accounts, control their permissions with RBAC, manage token projection, and follow security best practices.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">👤 Service Accounts</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The difference between User Accounts (humans) and Service Accounts (pods)</li>
                    <li>✅ How the default Service Account works and why it's insufficient for production</li>
                    <li>✅ Creating and assigning Service Accounts to pods</li>
                    <li>✅ Token projection — how modern K8s handles SA tokens securely</li>
                    <li>✅ Binding RBAC roles to Service Accounts for precise pod-level permissions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Service Accounts on the CKA</h5>
                <p>Expect questions that combine SA creation with RBAC binding. Key skills:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl create serviceaccount &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> — know this by heart</li>
                    <li>Assign a SA to a Pod via <code>spec.serviceAccountName</code></li>
                    <li>Disable auto-mount with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></li>
                    <li>The SA subject format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Service Account Imperative Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Create SA</td><td><code>kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List SAs</td><td><code>kubectl get serviceaccounts -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Describe SA</td><td><code>kubectl describe sa easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create token</td><td><code>kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create token (1hr)</td><td><code>kubectl create token easypj-sa --duration=3600s -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Bind SA to Role</td><td><code>kubectl create rolebinding sa-bind --role=pod-reader --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Test SA permissions</td><td><code>kubectl auth can-i list pods --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ServiceAccount + Pod — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Know how to create a SA, assign it to a pod, and disable auto-mount.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
automountServiceAccountToken: true   # ◀── default; set false to disable
---
<span class="comment"># Pod using the ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa      # ◀── assign SA to pod
  automountServiceAccountToken: true  # ◀── pod-level override
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:latest
    <span class="comment"># Token is auto-mounted at:</span>
    <span class="comment"># /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</span>
    <span class="comment"># /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt</span>
    <span class="comment"># /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace</span></code></pre>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find Service Account Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain serviceaccount                         <span class="comment"># SA spec</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.serviceAccountName            <span class="comment"># assign SA to pod</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.automountServiceAccountToken  <span class="comment"># disable token mount</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">2</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Account Types (User vs SA)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">1</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default SA per Namespace</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">3</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Auto-Mounted Files</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-purple">
                    <span class="metric-value">1h</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default Token TTL</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.1 User Accounts vs Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes has two types of identities, but only one is managed by the API server. Understanding this distinction is critical for RBAC and the CKA exam.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>User Account (Human)</th>
                                <th>Service Account (Pod)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Used by</strong></td><td>Humans (kubectl, dashboard)</td><td>Pods / Applications</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Created by</strong></td><td>External system (LDAP, OIDC, certs)</td><td>Kubernetes API (<code>kubectl create sa</code>)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Cluster-wide (no namespace)</td><td>Namespaced</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Credentials</strong></td><td>X.509 certificates / OIDC tokens</td><td>Auto-mounted projected tokens</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Managed by K8s</strong></td><td>❌ No — external to cluster</td><td>✅ Yes — API objects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>kubectl create</strong></td><td>❌ Not possible</td><td>✅ <code>kubectl create sa</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>RBAC subject kind</strong></td><td><code>User</code> or <code>Group</code></td><td><code>ServiceAccount</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">User Account vs Service Account — Who Talks to the API Server?</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │    HUMAN (User Account)                    POD (Service Account)             │
  │                                                                              │
  │    ┌──────────────┐                       ┌──────────────────────┐           │
  │    │  👨‍💻 kubectl   │                       │  📦 easypj-web pod   │           │
  │    │              │                       │                      │           │
  │    │  ~/.kube/    │                       │  /var/run/secrets/   │           │
  │    │  config      │                       │  kubernetes.io/      │           │
  │    │              │                       │  serviceaccount/     │           │
  │    │  🔑 x509 cert│                       │  ├── token  🎫      │           │
  │    │  or OIDC     │                       │  ├── ca.crt 📜      │           │
  │    │  token       │                       │  └── namespace 📝   │           │
  │    └──────┬───────┘                       └──────────┬───────────┘           │
  │           │                                          │                       │
  │           │     HTTPS + Certificate/Token             │     HTTPS + SA Token │
  │           │                                          │                       │
  │           └──────────────┬───────────────────────────┘                       │
  │                          ▼                                                   │
  │                 ┌────────────────┐                                           │
  │                 │  🏛️ API Server  │                                           │
  │                 │                │                                           │
  │                 │  1. Auth-N ────│── Verify identity                         │
  │                 │  2. Auth-Z ────│── Check RBAC                              │
  │                 │  3. Admit  ────│── Apply policies                          │
  │                 │  4. Persist ───│── Write to etcd                           │
  │                 └────────────────┘                                           │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 Key Insight</h5>
                    <p>Kubernetes does NOT manage User Accounts — there's no <code>kubectl create user</code> command. Users are created externally (certificates, LDAP, OIDC). But Service Accounts ARE Kubernetes objects that you create, list, and delete with kubectl.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.2 Default Service Account</h3>
                <p>Every namespace automatically gets a <code>default</code> Service Account. If you don't specify a <code>serviceAccountName</code> in your pod spec, it uses this one automatically.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">inspect-default-sa.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Every namespace has a "default" SA</span>
kubectl get sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME      SECRETS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># default   0         30d</span>

<span class="comment"># See what the default SA can do (spoiler: almost nothing)</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:default \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Check which SA a running pod uses</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># default</span>

<span class="comment"># See the mounted token inside a pod</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -n easypj -- \
  cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Default SA is Not Safe for Production</h5>
                    <p>The default SA has a token auto-mounted into every pod. Even though it has minimal permissions, <strong>if your pod is compromised, an attacker gets that token</strong>. Best practice: create specific SAs per application and disable auto-mount where not needed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>3 Auto-Mounted Files in Every Pod</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🎫</span> token</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> — JWT token for authenticating to the API server. Auto-refreshed by kubelet before expiry (~1 hour).</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📜</span> ca.crt</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt</code> — Cluster CA certificate. Used to verify the API server's TLS certificate when making HTTPS calls.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📝</span> namespace</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace</code> — Plain text file containing the pod's namespace name. Used by SDK clients to discover their namespace.</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>When to Use Default SA</h5>
                        <p>Simple pods that never call the Kubernetes API. Disable the token with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔧</div>
                        <h5>When to Create Custom SA</h5>
                        <p>Any pod that needs API access: reading ConfigMaps, listing pods, managing deployments, or running as a controller.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.3 Creating and Using Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>Creating a dedicated SA for your easypj application is straightforward — create it, then reference it in the pod spec.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Create the SA</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Create RBAC Role</h5>
                        <p>Define what permissions the SA needs</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Bind SA to Role</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl create rolebinding sa-bind --role=... --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Assign to Pod</h5>
                        <p>Set <code>spec.serviceAccountName: easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-serviceaccount-setup.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># 1. ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
---
<span class="comment"># 2. Role — what easypj needs</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-app-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["configmaps", "secrets"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]          <span class="comment"># read config at startup</span>
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["pods"]
  verbs: ["get", "list"]                   <span class="comment"># health-check readiness</span>
---
<span class="comment"># 3. RoleBinding</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-app-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
---
<span class="comment"># 4. Pod using the ServiceAccount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa            # ◀── use our custom SA
  containers:
  - name: web
    image: easypj:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">disable-auto-mount.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod that does NOT need API access — disable token mount</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-worker
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
  automountServiceAccountToken: false      # ◀── no token mounted!
  containers:
  - name: worker
    image: easypj:latest
    command: ["python", "manage.py", "process_jobs"]</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.4 Token Projection and Token Request API</h3>
                <p>Before Kubernetes 1.22, SA tokens were stored as Secrets and never expired. Modern K8s uses <strong>projected tokens</strong> — they're time-limited, audience-bound, and automatically refreshed by the kubelet.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Legacy Tokens vs Projected Tokens — Security Evolution</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ LEGACY (pre-1.22)                  ✅ PROJECTED (1.22+)                  │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌────────────────────┐               ┌────────────────────────┐            │
  │  │  Secret object     │               │  TokenRequest API      │            │
  │  │  ├── token: xyz... │               │  ├── audience: api     │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  ├── expiry: 1 hour    │            │
  │  │  │  ⚠️ NEVER       │               │  ├── auto-refresh: ✅  │            │
  │  │  │    EXPIRES!     │               │  │                      │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  │  🔒 Time-limited    │            │
  │  │  │  ⚠️ NO audience│               │  │  🔒 Audience-bound  │            │
  │  │  │    binding      │               │  │  🔒 Auto-rotated    │            │
  │  │  │                 │               │  │                      │            │
  │  │  │  Stored in      │               │  │  Generated on       │            │
  │  │  │  etcd forever   │               │  │  demand by kubelet  │            │
  │  └────────────────────┘               └────────────────────────┘            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  If token is stolen →                 If token is stolen →                   │
  │  UNLIMITED access 💀                  Expires in ≤1 hour 🛡️                 │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">projected-token-volume.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Pod with explicit projected token volume (advanced scenario)</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-api-client
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
  containers:
  - name: api-client
    image: easypj:latest
    volumeMounts:
    - name: token-vol
      mountPath: /var/run/secrets/tokens
      readOnly: true
  volumes:
  - name: token-vol
    projected:
      sources:
      - serviceAccountToken:
          path: api-token               <span class="comment"># file name</span>
          expirationSeconds: 3600       <span class="comment"># 1 hour</span>
          audience: api                 <span class="comment"># intended audience</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">create-token-cli.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create a short-lived token from CLI (TokenRequest API)</span>
kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj --duration=3600s

<span class="comment"># Create a token for a specific audience</span>
kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj --audience=https://my-api.example.com

<span class="comment"># Decode and inspect a token (JWT)</span>
TOKEN=$(kubectl create token easypj-sa -n easypj)
echo $TOKEN | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq .
<span class="comment"># Shows: iss, sub, aud, exp, iat, kubernetes.io fields</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 CKA Exam Note</h5>
                    <p>The CKA rarely tests projected token details, but you must know that <code>kubectl create token</code> is the modern way to generate tokens, and that auto-mounted tokens now expire. Legacy Secret-based tokens are deprecated.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>Projected Token Lifecycle</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Pod Scheduled</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Kubelet calls TokenRequest API → gets a time-limited JWT token for the pod's ServiceAccount</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Token Mounted</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Token written to <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> as a projected volume</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>App Uses Token</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Application reads token → sends in <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header to API server</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Auto-Refresh</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Before token expires (~80% of TTL), kubelet automatically requests a new token and updates the mounted file</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §25.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s25-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">25.5 Binding RBAC to Service Accounts</h3>
                <p>The real power comes when you combine Service Accounts with RBAC — giving each application precisely the permissions it needs, nothing more.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🔑 SA Subject Format in RBAC</h5>
                    <p>When using <code>--as</code> flag or in RoleBinding subjects, the format is:<br><code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;sa-name&gt;</code></p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">easypj SA + RBAC — Least Privilege in Action</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │             easypj SERVICE ACCOUNT RBAC ARCHITECTURE                         │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 📦 easypj-web    │    │ 🔗 RoleBinding  │    │ 📋 Role              │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │ easypj-app-role      │     │
  │  │ SA: easypj-sa    │───▶│ easypj-sa ───▶│───▶│ configmaps: get,list │     │
  │  │                  │    │ Role           │    │ secrets: get         │     │
  │  │ Can read config  │    │                │    │ pods: get,list       │     │
  │  │ ✅ configmaps    │    └────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘     │
  │  │ ✅ secrets       │                                                       │
  │  │ ❌ delete pods   │                                                       │
  │  │ ❌ create deploy │                                                       │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────┐    ┌────────────────┐    ┌──────────────────────┐     │
  │  │ 📦 easypj-worker │    │ No binding     │    │ No permissions       │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │                      │     │
  │  │ SA: easypj-sa    │    │ automount:     │    │ Token not mounted    │     │
  │  │ automount: false │    │ false          │    │ Can't call API       │     │
  │  │                  │    │                │    │ 🔒 Maximum security  │     │
  │  │ ❌ everything    │    └────────────────┘    └──────────────────────┘     │
  │  └──────────────────┘                                                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">bind-sa-complete.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Complete SA + RBAC setup (imperative — exam speed!)</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Create ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 2. Create Role</span>
kubectl create role easypj-app-role \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=configmaps,secrets,pods \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 3. Bind SA to Role</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-sa-binding \
  --role=easypj-app-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i get configmaps \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes ✅</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no ❌ (least privilege working!)</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. List all SA permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ SA Best Practices</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>One SA per application/workload</li>
                            <li><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> if no API access needed</li>
                            <li>Bind with specific Role, not <code>cluster-admin</code></li>
                            <li>Use projected tokens (K8s 1.22+)</li>
                            <li>Always verify with <code>can-i</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ SA Anti-Patterns</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Using <code>default</code> SA for everything</li>
                            <li>Binding <code>cluster-admin</code> to a SA</li>
                            <li>Leaving auto-mount on when not needed</li>
                            <li>Sharing SAs across unrelated apps</li>
                            <li>Using legacy Secret-based tokens</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <!-- ─── Service Account Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>Service Account Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get sa -n NS</code> → confirm SA exists</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe sa SA_NAME -n NS</code> → check token and secrets</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=system:serviceaccount:NS:SA -n NS</code> → verify RBAC</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code> → check token mount</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> → inspect mounted token</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl create token SA_NAME -n NS</code> → verify TokenRequest API works</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🏋️ Chapter 25 Practice Exercises</h5>
                    <p>Complete each exercise in under 5 minutes to build CKA-level speed.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 25.1 — Create SA with RBAC</h5>
                        <p>Set up a ServiceAccount with read-only ConfigMap access in a dedicated namespace.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create namespace <code>sa-practice</code></li>
                            <li>Create SA <code>app-sa</code> in that namespace</li>
                            <li>Create Role <code>configmap-reader</code> allowing <code>get</code>, <code>list</code> on configmaps</li>
                            <li>Bind <code>app-sa</code> to the role</li>
                            <li>Verify: <code>kubectl auth can-i list configmaps --as=system:serviceaccount:sa-practice:app-sa -n sa-practice</code> → <strong>yes</strong></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Use <code>--serviceaccount=NS:SA</code> in rolebinding command, not <code>--user</code>.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create sa app-sa -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create Role</span>
kubectl create role configmap-reader \
  --verb=get,list \
  --resource=configmaps \
  -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Bind SA to Role</span>
kubectl create rolebinding app-sa-cm-binding \
  --role=configmap-reader \
  --serviceaccount=sa-practice:app-sa \
  -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list configmaps \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:sa-practice:app-sa \
  -n sa-practice
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 4 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 25.2 — Disable Token Auto-Mount</h5>
                        <p>Deploy a pod that does NOT mount a ServiceAccount token (for pods that don't need API access).</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create a pod <code>no-api-pod</code> using SA <code>app-sa</code></li>
                            <li>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></li>
                            <li>Exec into the pod and verify: <code>ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code> → should fail (directory doesn't exist)</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code> goes at <code>spec</code> level, not container level.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create the pod with no token mount</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: no-api-pod
  namespace: sa-practice
spec:
  serviceAccountName: app-sa
  automountServiceAccountToken: false
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: busybox
    command: ["sleep", "3600"]
EOF

<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to be ready</span>
kubectl wait --for=condition=ready pod/no-api-pod -n sa-practice

<span class="comment"># Verify no token is mounted</span>
kubectl exec no-api-pod -n sa-practice -- \
  ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ls: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/: No such file or directory</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 3 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 25.3 — Create Short-Lived Token</h5>
                        <p>Generate a time-limited token using the TokenRequest API and inspect its expiration.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create a token for <code>app-sa</code> valid for 10 minutes: <code>kubectl create token app-sa -n sa-practice --duration=600s</code></li>
                            <li>Decode the JWT payload: pipe through <code>cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d</code></li>
                            <li>Verify the <code>exp</code> field is ~10 minutes from <code>iat</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: JWT has 3 dot-separated parts (header.payload.signature). The payload (part 2) is base64-encoded JSON.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create a 10-minute token</span>
TOKEN=$(kubectl create token app-sa -n sa-practice --duration=600s)

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Decode the JWT payload (part 2)</span>
echo $TOKEN | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq .
<span class="comment"># Output includes:</span>
<span class="comment"># {</span>
<span class="comment">#   "iss": "https://kubernetes.default.svc",</span>
<span class="comment">#   "sub": "system:serviceaccount:sa-practice:app-sa",</span>
<span class="comment">#   "iat": 1700000000,      ← issued at</span>
<span class="comment">#   "exp": 1700000600,      ← expires (iat + 600s)</span>
<span class="comment">#   ...</span>
<span class="comment"># }</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify expiration (exp - iat should be 600)</span>
echo $TOKEN | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | \
  jq '{iat, exp, duration_seconds: (.exp - .iat)}'
<span class="comment"># duration_seconds: 600</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 25 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Service Account Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Default SA has no useful permissions</strong> — Every namespace has a <code>default</code> ServiceAccount, but it can't do much. The exam expects you to create dedicated SAs and bind RBAC roles to them</li>
                        <li><strong>K8s 1.24+ changed token behavior</strong> — Tokens are no longer auto-mounted as persistent secrets. Use <code>kubectl create token &lt;sa&gt;</code> for time-limited tokens. The exam uses 1.29+, so use the new approach</li>
                        <li><strong><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></strong> — Set this on pods that don't need API access (most app pods). This is a security hardening best practice the exam may ask about</li>
                        <li><strong>SA name in pod spec</strong> — Use <code>serviceAccountName</code> (NOT the deprecated <code>serviceAccount</code> field). The SA must exist before the pod is created or the pod stays Pending</li>
                        <li><strong>Token audience and expiry</strong> — <code>kubectl create token sa-name --duration=3600s --audience=https://myapi</code>. Projected tokens have expiry, bound to specific audiences. Know this for security questions</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How ServiceAccount Token Authentication Works: JWT Lifecycle, TokenRequest API, and the Token Projection Flow</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ ServiceAccount Token Lifecycle — From Creation to API Call</h5>
            <p>Every pod gets a ServiceAccount token mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code>. This JWT is the pod's identity and is used for ALL API calls from within the pod.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SERVICEACCOUNT — Complete Identity System                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  WHAT A SA GIVES A POD:                                               │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/                        │   │
│  │  ├── token           <span class="comment">← JWT (JSON Web Token) — pod's identity          </span>│   │
│  │  ├── ca.crt          <span class="comment">← Cluster CA certificate — validate API Server  </span>│   │
│  │  └── namespace       <span class="comment">← Namespace name — "easypj-dev"                  </span>│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  JWT PAYLOAD (decoded):                                                │   │
│  │  {                                                                     │   │
│  │    "iss": "kubernetes/serviceaccount",                                  │   │
│  │    "sub": "system:serviceaccount:easypj-dev:default",  <span class="comment">← IDENTITY    </span>│   │
│  │    "kubernetes.io": {                                                 │   │
│  │      "namespace": "easypj-dev",                                       │   │
│  │      "serviceaccount": {                                              │   │
│  │        "name": "default",                                             │   │
│  │        "uid": "abc-123-def"                                           │   │
│  │      }                                                                 │   │
│  │    },                                                                  │   │
│  │    "exp": 1717333899,  <span class="comment">← EXPIRATION (typically 1 hour)                </span>│   │
│  │    "iat": 1717330299   <span class="comment">← Issued at                                    </span>│   │
│  │  }                                                                     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  TOKEN LIFECYCLE (Automated TokenRequest API — K8s 1.24+):                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  OLD WAY (pre-1.24):                                                  │   │
│  │   1. SA created → Secret auto-created (non-expiring token)            │   │
│  │   2. Pod mounts Secret → permanent token                              │   │
│  │   3. ⚠️  Token NEVER expires — if stolen, valid forever              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  NEW WAY (1.24+, TokenRequest API):                                    │   │
│  │   1. SA created → NO automatic Secret                                 │   │
│  │   2. kubelet calls TokenRequest API for EACH pod start                │   │
│  │   3. API Server creates time-limited token (1h, bounded by SA)        │   │
│  │   4. kubelet projects token into pod's tmpfs volume                   │   │
│  │   5. kubelet REFRESHES token before expiry (every ~20 min)            │   │
│  │   6. ✅ Token auto-expires + auto-refreshes — secure by default       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  TO VERIFY CURRENT TOKEN EXPIRY:                                       │   │
│  │  $ kubectl exec pod -- cat /var/run/secrets/.../token | cut -d. -f2  │   │
│  │    | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | jq .exp                                  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  RBAC WITH SERVICEACCOUNTS — The Complete Chain:                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  ServiceAccount (identity)                                            │   │
│  │      │                                                                │   │
│  │      │  Bound via ClusterRoleBinding/RoleBinding                      │   │
│  │      ▼                                                                │   │
│  │  Role/ClusterRole (permissions)                                        │   │
│  │      │                                                                │   │
│  │      │  Defines: apiGroups, resources, verbs                         │   │
│  │      ▼                                                                │   │
│  │  Pod uses token → API Server validates → RBAC authorizes → ALLOW/DENY│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Example binding:                                                      │   │
│  │  kubectl create clusterrolebinding easypj-deployer \                  │   │
│  │    --clusterrole=edit \                                                │   │
│  │    --serviceaccount=easypj-dev:deploy-sa                               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, you'll create SAs and bind them to Roles. Remember: <code>kubectl create sa &lt;name&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> creates the SA. <code>kubectl create rolebinding &lt;name&gt; --role=&lt;role&gt; --serviceaccount=&lt;ns&gt;:&lt;sa&gt; -n &lt;ns&gt;</code> binds it. In pod YAML: <code>spec.serviceAccountName: &lt;sa&gt;</code>. SA subjects in RBAC use format: <code>system:serviceaccount:&lt;namespace&gt;:&lt;name&gt;</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">The default SA has full access to the namespace</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">The default SA has almost NO permissions — create specific SAs with explicit RBAC</span></li>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">SA tokens never expire</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Since K8s 1.22, projected tokens are time-limited and audience-bound. Legacy tokens are deprecated</span></li>
                    <li><code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> on the pod overrides the SA-level setting</li>
                    <li>SA is <strong>namespaced</strong> — you cannot use a SA from namespace A in namespace B</li>
                    <li>Deleting a SA doesn't delete its RoleBindings — orphaned bindings remain</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Service Account workflow:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl create sa my-sa -n prod</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create role my-role --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n prod</code><br>
                <code>kubectl create rolebinding my-rb --role=my-role --serviceaccount=prod:my-sa -n prod</code><br>
                <strong>Note the SA format in rolebinding:</strong> <code>--serviceaccount=NAMESPACE:SA-NAME</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 25)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 25 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch25-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 25 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of ServiceAccount auth &amp; token flow — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ServiceAccount named <code>deploy-sa</code> in namespace <code>production</code>. Then create a pod that uses this ServiceAccount.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create ServiceAccount:</span>
kubectl create sa deploy-sa -n production

<span class="comment"># Create pod using the SA:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: deploy-pod
namespace: production
spec:
serviceAccountName: deploy-sa    # ← Use custom SA
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx

<span class="comment"># Or imperatively with dry-run and edit:</span>
kubectl run deploy-pod --image=nginx -n production \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Add serviceAccountName: deploy-sa to spec</span>
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pod deploy-pod -n production \
-o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Check token is mounted:</span>
kubectl exec deploy-pod -n production -- \
ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  namespace  token</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ServiceAccounts provide pod identity for API access. Use <code>serviceAccountName</code> (not the deprecated <code>serviceAccount</code>). The SA must exist BEFORE the pod — otherwise the pod stays Pending. The token, CA cert, and namespace are auto-mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</code>. On the CKA, creating a SA, binding RBAC, and using it in a pod is a common multi-step task.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you prevent a pod from automatically mounting a ServiceAccount token?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: At the pod level (overrides SA setting):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: no-token-pod
spec:
automountServiceAccountToken: false  # ← Pod level
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx

<span class="comment"># Method 2: At the ServiceAccount level (affects all pods using it):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
name: no-api-sa
namespace: default
automountServiceAccountToken: false    # ← SA level

<span class="comment"># Verify no token mounted:</span>
kubectl exec no-token-pod -- \
ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ 2>&1
<span class="comment"># ls: No such file or directory ← Token not mounted!</span>

<span class="comment"># When to use this:</span>
<span class="comment"># - App pods that never call the Kubernetes API</span>
<span class="comment"># - Security hardening (principle of least privilege)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Reducing attack surface if pod is compromised</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod-level overrides SA-level:</span>
<span class="comment"># SA: automount=false + Pod: automount=true → token IS mounted</span>
<span class="comment"># SA: automount=true + Pod: automount=false → token NOT mounted</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> at pod or SA level. Pod-level overrides SA-level. On the CKA, this is a security hardening best practice — most application pods don't need API access. If a compromised pod has a token, an attacker could interact with the Kubernetes API. Disabling auto-mount reduces this risk. Set this on ALL pods that don't need API access.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ServiceAccount, bind a Role to it, and verify it can list pods but not delete them.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create SA</span>
kubectl create sa app-sa -n dev

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Role</span>
kubectl create role pod-lister \
--verb=get,list,watch \
--resource=pods \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Bind Role to SA</span>
<span class="comment"># Note: SA format is namespace:name</span>
kubectl create rolebinding app-sa-binding \
--role=pod-lister \
--serviceaccount=dev:app-sa \
-n dev

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:dev:app-sa -n dev
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:dev:app-sa -n dev
<span class="comment"># no</span>

kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:dev:app-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># no ← Only in dev!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Create pod with this SA</span>
kubectl run test-pod --image=nginx -n dev \
--overrides='{"spec":{"serviceAccountName":"app-sa"}}'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The full SA workflow: (1) create SA, (2) create Role, (3) bind with RoleBinding using <code>--serviceaccount=NAMESPACE:NAME</code>. For <code>auth can-i</code>, the SA format is <code>system:serviceaccount:NAMESPACE:NAME</code>. On the CKA, this three-step workflow is heavily tested. Common mistakes: forgetting the namespace prefix in <code>--serviceaccount</code>, or using the wrong format in <code>--as</code>. <code>--overrides</code> with <code>kubectl run</code> is the fastest way to set a SA imperatively.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you generate a short-lived token for a ServiceAccount? What changed in Kubernetes 1.24+?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Generate a time-limited token (1.24+ way):</span>
kubectl create token deploy-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...  ← JWT token (1 hour default)</span>

<span class="comment"># Custom duration:</span>
kubectl create token deploy-sa -n production --duration=3600s
<span class="comment"># Token valid for 1 hour</span>

<span class="comment"># Custom audience:</span>
kubectl create token deploy-sa -n production \
--audience=https://my-api.example.com

<span class="comment"># Pre-1.24 (old way — still works but deprecated):</span>
<span class="comment"># Secrets with type kubernetes.io/service-account-token</span>
<span class="comment"># were auto-created for each SA. No longer auto-created!</span>

<span class="comment"># If you need a long-lived token (NOT recommended):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
name: deploy-sa-token
namespace: production
annotations:
kubernetes.io/service-account.name: deploy-sa
type: kubernetes.io/service-account-token
<span class="comment"># K8s auto-populates the token data</span>

kubectl get secret deploy-sa-token -n production \
-o jsonpath='{.data.token}' | base64 -d</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubernetes 1.24+ removed auto-creation of Secret-based tokens. Now use <code>kubectl create token</code> for time-limited, projected tokens (more secure). On the CKA (K8s 1.29+), use <code>kubectl create token</code> — it generates a JWT with automatic expiration. For legacy compatibility, you can manually create a Secret with type <code>kubernetes.io/service-account-token</code> and annotate it with the SA name. But the exam prefers the new approach: short-lived, audience-bound tokens.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you assign a ServiceAccount to an existing Deployment without editing the YAML?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Use kubectl set:</span>
kubectl set sa deployment/web-app deploy-sa -n production
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/web-app serviceaccount updated</span>

<span class="comment"># This triggers a rolling update (new pods get the SA)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get deployment web-app -n production \
-o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: kubectl patch</span>
kubectl patch deployment web-app -n production \
-p '{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"serviceAccountName":"deploy-sa"}}}}'

<span class="comment"># Check the new pods:</span>
kubectl get pods -n production -l app=web-app
<span class="comment"># NAME                      READY  STATUS   RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># web-app-6f7b8c-new1       1/1    Running  0         10s ← New pod with SA</span>

kubectl exec web-app-6f7b8c-new1 -n production -- \
cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token | cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># {"iss":"https://kubernetes.default.svc","sub":"system:serviceaccount:production:deploy-sa"...}</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl set sa</code> is the fastest way to assign a SA to a Deployment. It triggers a rolling update — old pods are replaced with new ones that use the new SA. On the CKA, this is faster than editing YAML. The SA must exist before assignment, or new pods will fail to start. You can verify by checking the token's <code>sub</code> (subject) field inside the pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What files are automatically mounted in a pod from its ServiceAccount? Where are they?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Mounted at: /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- ls -la \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># lrwxrwxrwx 1  token     → projected token (JWT)</span>
<span class="comment"># lrwxrwxrwx 1  ca.crt    → cluster CA certificate</span>
<span class="comment"># lrwxrwxrwx 1  namespace → pod's namespace name</span>

<span class="comment"># token: JWT for API authentication</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token
<span class="comment"># eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...</span>

<span class="comment"># ca.crt: Used to verify API server's TLS certificate</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt
<span class="comment"># -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----</span>
<span class="comment"># MIICyDCCAbC...</span>

<span class="comment"># namespace: The pod's namespace</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/namespace
<span class="comment"># production</span>

<span class="comment"># Using these from inside a pod:</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- sh -c '
TOKEN=$(cat /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token)
curl -s --cacert /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/ca.crt \
-H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
https://kubernetes.default.svc/api/v1/namespaces/production/pods
'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three files are projected: <code>token</code> (JWT for authentication), <code>ca.crt</code> (CA to verify API server TLS), <code>namespace</code> (pod's namespace). In K8s 1.24+, the token is a *projected* volume with automatic rotation and expiry. On the CKA, knowing this path is important for: (1) debugging API access from pods, (2) security hardening (disable with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code>), (3) understanding how pods authenticate to the API server.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you attach <code>imagePullSecrets</code> to a ServiceAccount so all pods using it can pull from a private registry?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the docker-registry secret:</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
--docker-server=registry.example.com \
--docker-username=myuser \
--docker-password=mypass \
-n production

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Attach to ServiceAccount:</span>
kubectl patch sa deploy-sa -n production \
-p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "regcred"}]}'

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get sa deploy-sa -n production -o yaml
<span class="comment"># imagePullSecrets:</span>
<span class="comment"># - name: regcred</span>

<span class="comment"># Now ALL pods using deploy-sa automatically inherit imagePullSecrets!</span>
<span class="comment"># No need to add imagePullSecrets to each pod spec</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: attach to default SA (affects ALL pods in ns):</span>
kubectl patch sa default -n production \
-p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "regcred"}]}'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Attaching <code>imagePullSecrets</code> to a ServiceAccount means every pod using that SA inherits the credentials — no need to specify <code>imagePullSecrets</code> in each pod spec. On the CKA, patching the <code>default</code> SA in a namespace affects all pods in that namespace (quick fix for <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> on private images). This is more maintainable than adding secrets to every Deployment.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>serviceAccountName</code> and <code>serviceAccount</code> in a pod spec?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code># ✓ Correct (use this):
spec:
serviceAccountName: deploy-sa   # ← Current field

<span class="comment"># ✗ Deprecated (do NOT use):</span>
spec:
serviceAccount: deploy-sa       # ← Deprecated since 1.16

<span class="comment"># Both work, but:</span>
<span class="comment"># - serviceAccountName is the official field</span>
<span class="comment"># - serviceAccount is deprecated and may be removed</span>
<span class="comment"># - If both are set and different → error</span>
<span class="comment"># - CKA expects serviceAccountName</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick check which field a pod uses:</span>
kubectl get pod my-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Default SA:</span>
<span class="comment"># If no serviceAccountName is specified, pods use "default" SA</span>
kubectl get pod unset-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># default</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Always use <code>serviceAccountName</code> — <code>serviceAccount</code> is deprecated. On the CKA, using the deprecated field may not cause errors, but it's bad practice. If you see both in a YAML and they differ, the API server rejects the pod. When no SA is specified, pods use the <code>default</code> ServiceAccount in their namespace.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod using a custom ServiceAccount can't call the Kubernetes API. How do you debug?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check SA assignment</span>
kubectl get pod api-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># deploy-sa ← Correct SA</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check token is mounted</span>
kubectl exec api-pod -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  namespace  token  ← Token present</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check SA permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:default:deploy-sa
<span class="comment"># no ← Missing RBAC!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check for RoleBindings</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -o wide | grep deploy-sa
<span class="comment"># (empty) ← No binding!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix — create Role and RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create role api-reader \
--verb=get,list --resource=pods
kubectl create rolebinding api-rb \
--role=api-reader \
--serviceaccount=default:deploy-sa

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods \
--as=system:serviceaccount:default:deploy-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Check automountServiceAccountToken</span>
kubectl get pod api-pod -o yaml | grep automount
<span class="comment"># automountServiceAccountToken: false  ← Token not mounted!</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: set to true or remove the field</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>SA API access debugging: (1) verify SA is assigned, (2) check token is mounted, (3) test permissions with <code>auth can-i</code>, (4) find/create RBAC bindings, (5) check <code>automountServiceAccountToken</code>. On the CKA, the most common issue is: SA exists and is assigned, but no Role/RoleBinding grants it permissions. The default SA has no useful permissions — you must always create RBAC explicitly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a projected ServiceAccount token and how does it differ from legacy tokens?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Feature</th>
                                        <th>Legacy Token (pre-1.24)</th>
                                        <th>Projected Token (1.24+)</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Storage</td>
                                        <td>Stored in Secret object</td>
                                        <td>Projected volume (not in Secret)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Expiry</td>
                                        <td>Never expires</td>
                                        <td>Expires (default 1h, auto-rotated)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Audience</td>
                                        <td>Any API server</td>
                                        <td>Bound to specific audience</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Auto-created</td>
                                        <td>Yes (auto Secret per SA)</td>
                                        <td>No Secret created</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Security</td>
                                        <td>Risky (leaked = permanent)</td>
                                        <td>Better (expires, rotates)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Projected token is auto-rotated by kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># Check token expiry from inside pod:</span>
kubectl exec my-pod -- cat \
/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token \
| cut -d. -f2 | base64 -d 2>/dev/null | python3 -m json.tool
<span class="comment"># "exp": 1735689600  ← Expiry timestamp</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Projected tokens (K8s 1.24+) are more secure: they expire, are bound to a specific audience, and are auto-rotated by the kubelet. Legacy tokens were stored as Secrets and never expired — a leaked token gave permanent access. On the CKA (K8s 1.29+), projected tokens are the default. If a question asks about token security, mention: time-limited, audience-bound, auto-rotated. For legacy apps needing long-lived tokens, manually create a Secret with the SA annotation.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 25 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch26">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 26 · TLS Certificates &amp; PKI</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Every component in a Kubernetes cluster communicates over <strong>TLS-encrypted connections</strong>. The API server, etcd, kubelet, scheduler, and controller-manager all present certificates to prove their identity. For the CKA exam, you must understand where these certificates live, how to inspect them, how to approve Certificate Signing Requests (CSRs), check expiry, and renew certificates using <code>kubeadm</code>. This chapter covers the entire Kubernetes PKI (Public Key Infrastructure) from root CA to individual component certificates.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔐 TLS/PKI</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ The Kubernetes PKI architecture — what the CA signs and why</li>
                    <li>✅ Certificate file locations under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code></li>
                    <li>✅ How to create and approve Certificate Signing Requests (CSRs)</li>
                    <li>✅ Checking certificate expiry with <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                    <li>✅ Renewing certificates with <code>kubeadm certs renew</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>TLS/Certificates on the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>Certificate questions are frequently tested. You must be able to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Generate a private key, create a CSR, submit it to Kubernetes, approve it, and extract the certificate</li>
                    <li>Know certificate file locations: <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code></li>
                    <li>Check certificate expiry with <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                    <li>Renew certificates with <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></li>
                    <li>Use <code>openssl x509</code> to inspect certificate details</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>TLS Certificate Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Generate private key</td><td><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Create CSR</td><td><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team" -out user.csr</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Submit K8s CSR</td><td><code>kubectl apply -f csr.yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Approve CSR</td><td><code>kubectl certificate approve dev-user</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Deny CSR</td><td><code>kubectl certificate deny dev-user</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Get signed cert</td><td><code>kubectl get csr dev-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > user.crt</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check expiry</td><td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Renew all certs</td><td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Inspect cert</td><td><code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>CertificateSigningRequest — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">CSR creation is a common CKA task. Know this pattern cold.</p>
                <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate key + CSR (on command line)</span>
openssl genrsa -out dev-user.key 2048
openssl req -new -key dev-user.key \
  -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team" \
  -out dev-user.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create Kubernetes CSR object</span>
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: dev-user                         # ◀── CSR name
spec:
  request: $(cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')  # ◀── base64-encoded CSR
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client       # ◀── REQUIRED signer
  usages:
  - client auth                          # ◀── for user authentication
  expirationSeconds: 86400               # ◀── optional: 24 hours

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Approve and extract</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl certificate approve dev-user</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl get csr dev-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > dev-user.crt</span></code></pre>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find Certificate Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain csr.spec                       <span class="comment"># CSR spec</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.request               <span class="comment"># base64-encoded CSR</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.signerName            <span class="comment"># required signer</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.usages                <span class="comment"># client auth, server auth, etc.</span>
kubectl explain csr.spec.expirationSeconds     <span class="comment"># cert lifetime</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">6+</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Component Certificates</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">1 yr</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default Cert Lifetime</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">/etc/kubernetes/pki/</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Default Cert Location</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-red">
                    <span class="metric-value">1</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Root CA Signs Everything</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.1 Kubernetes PKI Overview</h3>
                <p><strong>PKI (Public Key Infrastructure)</strong> is the system of certificates that secure all communication in a Kubernetes cluster. At the top is a <strong>Root Certificate Authority (CA)</strong> that signs certificates for every cluster component.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Kubernetes PKI Architecture — The Certificate Tree</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                      KUBERNETES PKI HIERARCHY                                │
  │                                                                              │
  │                    ┌──────────────────────┐                                  │
  │                    │   🏛️ ROOT CA          │                                  │
  │                    │   /etc/kubernetes/    │                                  │
  │                    │   pki/ca.crt          │                                  │
  │                    │       ca.key          │                                  │
  │                    └──────────┬───────────┘                                  │
  │                               │ signs                                        │
  │          ┌────────────────────┼────────────────────┐                         │
  │          │                    │                    │                         │
  │          ▼                    ▼                    ▼                         │
  │  ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 🌐 API Server │   │ 💾 etcd       │   │ 📡 kubelet    │                 │
  │  │               │   │               │   │               │                 │
  │  │ apiserver.crt │   │ server.crt   │   │ kubelet.crt   │                 │
  │  │ apiserver.key │   │ server.key   │   │ kubelet.key   │                 │
  │  │               │   │               │   │               │                 │
  │  │ Also needs:   │   │ peer.crt     │   │ Authenticates │                 │
  │  │ kubelet-client│   │ peer.key     │   │ to API server │                 │
  │  │ .crt/.key     │   │               │   └───────────────┘                 │
  │  └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘                                      │
  │          │                                                                   │
  │          │ also serves as client to:                                         │
  │          ▼                                                                   │
  │  ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐   ┌───────────────┐                 │
  │  │ 📋 scheduler  │   │ 🔄 controller │   │ 🌐 front-proxy│                 │
  │  │               │   │   manager     │   │               │                 │
  │  │ Uses kubeconf │   │ Uses kubeconf │   │ front-proxy-  │                 │
  │  │ with embedded │   │ with embedded │   │ ca.crt/key    │                 │
  │  │ client cert   │   │ client cert   │   │ (aggregation) │                 │
  │  └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘   └───────────────┘                 │
  │                                                                              │
  │  📁 All certificates live under: /etc/kubernetes/pki/                        │
  │  📁 etcd certs specifically: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/                       │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔒</div>
                        <h5>Encryption</h5>
                        <p>All component communication uses TLS — no plaintext data ever crosses the network.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🪪</div>
                        <h5>Identity</h5>
                        <p>Certificates prove component identity. The API server verifies kubelet is who it claims to be.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🤝</div>
                        <h5>Mutual TLS</h5>
                        <p>Both sides present certificates. API server authenticates kubelet AND kubelet authenticates API server.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.2 Certificate Locations and Purpose</h3>
                <p>Every certificate in the Kubernetes cluster has a specific purpose. For the CKA, you must know where they live and what they do.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>File</th>
                                <th>Used By</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>ca.crt</code> / <code>ca.key</code></td><td>Cluster CA</td><td>Root CA — signs all other certificates</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>Serve HTTPS on port 6443</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver-kubelet-client.crt</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>API server authenticating to kubelets</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiserver-etcd-client.crt</code></td><td>API Server</td><td>API server authenticating to etcd</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcd/server.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>etcd</td><td>etcd serving TLS to API server</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>etcd/peer.crt</code> / <code>.key</code></td><td>etcd</td><td>etcd-to-etcd peer communication (HA)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>front-proxy-ca.crt</code></td><td>Aggregation</td><td>API aggregation layer (metrics server)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>sa.pub</code> / <code>sa.key</code></td><td>SA tokens</td><td>Signing/verifying ServiceAccount tokens</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">inspect-certificates.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all certificate files</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  ca.key  apiserver.crt  apiserver.key  ...</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  ca.key  server.crt  server.key  peer.crt  peer.key</span>

<span class="comment"># Inspect API server certificate details</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout

<span class="comment"># Key fields to check:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Issuer: kubernetes (the CA that signed it)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Subject: CN=kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not After: expiry date</span>
<span class="comment"># - Subject Alternative Names (SANs): all valid hostnames/IPs</span>

<span class="comment"># Check who signed a certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -issuer -noout
<span class="comment"># issuer=CN = kubernetes</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify cert is signed by the CA</span>
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
  /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
<span class="comment"># /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt: OK</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Certificate File Layout on Disk</div>
<pre>
  /etc/kubernetes/
  ├── pki/                                 ◀── Main PKI directory
  │   ├── ca.crt                           ◀── Root CA certificate
  │   ├── ca.key                           ◀── Root CA private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver.crt                    ◀── API server TLS cert
  │   ├── apiserver.key                    ◀── API server private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.crt     ◀── API→kubelet client cert
  │   ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.key     ◀── API→kubelet private key 🔒
  │   ├── apiserver-etcd-client.crt        ◀── API→etcd client cert
  │   ├── apiserver-etcd-client.key        ◀── API→etcd private key 🔒
  │   ├── front-proxy-ca.crt              ◀── Aggregation CA
  │   ├── front-proxy-ca.key              ◀── Aggregation CA key 🔒
  │   ├── front-proxy-client.crt          ◀── Aggregation client cert
  │   ├── front-proxy-client.key          ◀── Aggregation client key 🔒
  │   ├── sa.pub                           ◀── SA token verification key
  │   ├── sa.key                           ◀── SA token signing key 🔒
  │   └── etcd/                            ◀── etcd-specific certs
  │       ├── ca.crt                       ◀── etcd CA (may be separate)
  │       ├── ca.key
  │       ├── server.crt                   ◀── etcd server TLS
  │       ├── server.key
  │       ├── peer.crt                     ◀── etcd peer communication
  │       ├── peer.key
  │       ├── healthcheck-client.crt       ◀── etcd health checks
  │       └── healthcheck-client.key
  ├── admin.conf                           ◀── cluster-admin kubeconfig
  ├── controller-manager.conf              ◀── controller-manager kubeconfig
  ├── scheduler.conf                       ◀── scheduler kubeconfig
  └── kubelet.conf                         ◀── kubelet kubeconfig
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Private Keys Are Secrets</h5>
                    <p>The <code>.key</code> files are private keys — anyone with access can impersonate that component. In production, restrict access to <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> to root only. On the CKA exam, you'll SSH into the control plane node to read these files.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="anatomy-card">
                    <div class="anatomy-header"><h4>X.509 Certificate Fields — What to Read</h4></div>
                    <div class="anatomy-body">
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👤</span> Subject (CN)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>CN=kube-apiserver</code> — The Common Name identifies the component. For user certs, CN becomes the username in Kubernetes RBAC.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">👥</span> Subject (O)</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>O=system:masters</code> — The Organization becomes the group. <code>system:masters</code> maps to cluster-admin.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🏛️</span> Issuer</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value"><code>CN=kubernetes</code> — The CA that signed this certificate. All cluster certs should show the same issuer.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">📅</span> Not After</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Expiry date. Default 1 year for component certs. <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all expiry dates at once.</div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="anatomy-row">
                            <div class="anatomy-label"><span class="anat-icon">🌐</span> SANs</div>
                            <div class="anatomy-value">Subject Alternative Names — list of valid hostnames &amp; IPs (<code>kubernetes</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code>, <code>10.96.0.1</code>, control plane IP).</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.3 Certificate Signing Requests (CSR)</h3>
                <p>To add a new user to the cluster, you create a CSR and have the K8s CA sign it. This process is <strong>heavily tested on the CKA exam</strong>.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Generate Key</h5>
                        <p><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Create CSR</h5>
                        <p><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team"</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Submit to K8s</h5>
                        <p>Create CertificateSigningRequest YAML with base64-encoded CSR</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Approve</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl certificate approve dev-user</code></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Extract Cert</h5>
                        <p>Get the signed certificate from the CSR's <code>.status.certificate</code></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">csr-complete-workflow.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── COMPLETE CSR WORKFLOW — CKA EXAM PATTERN ──

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate a private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out dev-user.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create a Certificate Signing Request</span>
<span class="comment"># CN = username, O = group (for RBAC)</span>
openssl req -new -key dev-user.key \
  -subj "/CN=dev-user/O=dev-team" \
  -out dev-user.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Encode the CSR in base64 (single line, no wrapping)</span>
CSR_BASE64=$(cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create the K8s CSR YAML</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: dev-user
spec:
  request: ${CSR_BASE64}
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
  usages:
  - client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check CSR status</span>
kubectl get csr
<span class="comment"># NAME       AGE   SIGNERNAME                          CONDITION</span>
<span class="comment"># dev-user   5s    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client  Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve dev-user

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr dev-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' \
  | base64 -d > dev-user.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify the certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in dev-user.crt -text -noout
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN=dev-user, O=dev-team</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 9: Add user to kubeconfig</span>
kubectl config set-credentials dev-user \
  --client-certificate=dev-user.crt \
  --client-key=dev-user.key

kubectl config set-context dev-user@kubernetes \
  --cluster=kubernetes \
  --user=dev-user \
  --namespace=easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Shortcut: CSR Encoding</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>cat dev-user.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n'</code> to get the single-line base64 string for the CSR YAML. The <code>tr -d '\n'</code> removes line breaks that would break the YAML.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>CSR Approval Flow — From Key to Kubeconfig</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Generate Private Key</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>openssl genrsa -out user.key 2048</code> — creates RSA private key (stays with the user, never shared)</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Create CSR File</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>openssl req -new -key user.key -subj "/CN=user/O=group"</code> — CN becomes username, O becomes group in RBAC</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>Submit to Kubernetes</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Base64-encode the CSR → create CertificateSigningRequest YAML → <code>kubectl apply</code>. Status: <strong>Pending</strong></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Admin Approves</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>kubectl certificate approve user</code> — K8s CA signs the cert. Status: <strong>Approved, Issued</strong></p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">5</span>
                                <h5>Extract &amp; Use</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Extract signed cert from <code>.status.certificate</code> → add to kubeconfig with <code>kubectl config set-credentials</code></p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.4 Checking Certificate Expiry</h3>
                <p>Kubernetes certificates expire after <strong>1 year</strong> by default. If they expire, cluster components stop communicating and the cluster goes down. Monitoring expiry is critical.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">check-expiry.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── METHOD 1: kubeadm (EASIEST — use on CKA exam) ──
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Apr 01, 2027 00:00 UTC   364d</span>

<span class="comment"># ── METHOD 2: openssl (manual — for specific cert) ──</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
  -noout -enddate
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Apr  1 00:00:00 2027 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># ── METHOD 3: Check specific fields ──</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
  -noout -subject -issuer -dates
<span class="comment"># subject=CN = kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># issuer=CN = kubernetes</span>
<span class="comment"># notBefore=Apr  1 00:00:00 2026 GMT</span>
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Apr  1 00:00:00 2027 GMT</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box danger">
                    <h5>🚨 Expired Certificates = Cluster Down</h5>
                    <p>If the API server certificate expires, <code>kubectl</code> stops working entirely. If etcd certificates expire, the API server can't read/write state. <strong>Always check expiry at least monthly in production.</strong> On the CKA exam, <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> is the answer.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §26.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s26-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">26.5 Renewing Certificates</h3>
                <p>Certificate renewal is a critical cluster maintenance task and a common CKA exam topic.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">renew-certificates.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Renew ALL certificates at once ──
kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate embedded in admin.conf renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver-kubelet-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Renew a SPECIFIC certificate ──</span>
kubeadm certs renew apiserver
kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client
kubeadm certs renew etcd-server

<span class="comment"># ── CRITICAL: Restart control plane after renewal ──</span>
<span class="comment"># Control plane pods load certs at startup — they need restart!</span>

<span class="comment"># Option A: If using static pods (kubeadm default)</span>
<span class="comment"># Move and restore manifests to trigger restart:</span>
cd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
mv kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to stop...</span>
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml .
<span class="comment"># Repeat for controller-manager, scheduler, etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># Option B: Force restart via crictl</span>
crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs crictl stopp
crictl pods --name kube-controller-manager -q | xargs crictl stopp
crictl pods --name kube-scheduler -q | xargs crictl stopp

<span class="comment"># ── Verify renewal worked ──</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># All dates should now show ~1 year from today</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Certificate Renewal Workflow</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              CERTIFICATE RENEWAL PROCEDURE                       │
  │                                                                  │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 1. Check Expiry │─── kubeadm certs check-expiration          │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 2. Renew Certs  │─── kubeadm certs renew all                │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 3. Restart      │─── Move/restore manifests or crictl       │
  │  │    Components   │    (API server, controller, scheduler)     │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 4. Verify       │─── kubeadm certs check-expiration          │
  │  │    New Dates    │    (should show ~1 year from now)          │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                            │
  │           │                                                      │
  │           ▼                                                      │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                            │
  │  │ 5. Update       │─── Copy new admin.conf to ~/.kube/config  │
  │  │    kubeconfig   │    cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf           │
  │  └─────────────────┘    ~/.kube/config                          │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Don't Forget kubeconfig!</h5>
                    <p>After renewing certs, the kubeconfig files (<code>admin.conf</code>, etc.) also get updated. Copy the new admin.conf to <code>~/.kube/config</code> or <code>kubectl</code> will stop working with "certificate expired" errors!</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Automatic Renewal</h5>
                    <p><code>kubeadm upgrade</code> automatically renews all certificates during a cluster upgrade. If you upgrade regularly (recommended), you may never need manual renewal.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Certificate Troubleshooting — Common Errors &amp; Fixes</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │            CERTIFICATE TROUBLESHOOTING QUICK REFERENCE                       │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid"              │
  │  ✅ FIX:   kubeadm certs renew all → restart control plane pods            │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate             │
  │            signed by unknown authority"                                      │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Copy updated admin.conf: cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf           │
  │            ~/.kube/config                                                    │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "error: You must be logged in to the server (Unauthorized)"      │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Check certificate CN matches expected user. Verify CSR was       │
  │            approved. Check RBAC bindings.                                    │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: API server won't start after cert renewal                        │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Check /var/log/containers/ for kube-apiserver logs.              │
  │            Verify cert files exist in /etc/kubernetes/pki/.                  │
  │            Check file permissions (certs should be 644, keys 600).          │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ❌ ERROR: "tls: bad certificate" on etcd                                   │
  │  ✅ FIX:   Renew etcd-specific certs: kubeadm certs renew etcd-server      │
  │            kubeadm certs renew etcd-peer                                    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ Certificate DO</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Check expiry monthly: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                            <li>Always restart control plane after renewal</li>
                            <li>Update <code>~/.kube/config</code> after renewing admin.conf</li>
                            <li>Use <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> — it renews certs automatically</li>
                            <li>Set correct CN/O in CSRs (CN=username, O=group)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ Certificate DON'T</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Ignore certificate expiry warnings</li>
                            <li>Share private keys (<code>.key</code> files) across teams</li>
                            <li>Skip the <code>signerName</code> field on CSR objects (required since 1.22)</li>
                            <li>Forget to base64-encode the CSR <code>request</code> field</li>
                            <li>Assume certs renew themselves — only <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> auto-renews</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

            <!-- ─── TLS/PKI Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>TLS Certificate Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>openssl x509 -in CERT -text -noout | grep -A1 Subject:</code> → verify CN and O fields</li>
                    <li><code>openssl x509 -in CERT -text -noout | grep "Not After"</code> → check expiry date</li>
                    <li><code>openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt CERT</code> → confirm signed by cluster CA</li>
                    <li><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> → view all certificate expiry dates at once</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get csr</code> → check pending/approved CSR objects</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe csr NAME</code> → verify signerName, status, and conditions</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🏋️ Chapter 26 Practice Exercises</h5>
                    <p>These exercises simulate real CKA certificate questions.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 8 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 26.1 — Create User Certificate via CSR</h5>
                        <p>Generate a private key, create a CSR, submit it to Kubernetes, approve it, and extract the signed certificate.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Generate key: <code>openssl genrsa -out exam-user.key 2048</code></li>
                            <li>Create CSR: <code>openssl req -new -key exam-user.key -subj "/CN=exam-user/O=exam-team"</code></li>
                            <li>Submit K8s CSR object with <code>signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code></li>
                            <li>Approve: <code>kubectl certificate approve exam-user</code></li>
                            <li>Extract signed cert and verify CN with <code>openssl x509 -text</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: The CSR <code>request</code> field must be base64-encoded with <strong>no line wrapping</strong> — use <code>base64 -w 0</code>.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out exam-user.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create CSR (PEM format)</span>
openssl req -new -key exam-user.key \
  -subj "/CN=exam-user/O=exam-team" \
  -out exam-user.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Base64-encode the CSR (no line wrapping!)</span>
CSR_B64=$(cat exam-user.csr | base64 -w 0)

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create K8s CSR object</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
  name: exam-user
spec:
  request: ${CSR_B64}
  signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
  usages:
  - client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve exam-user

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr exam-user -o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' \
  | base64 -d > exam-user.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify the certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in exam-user.crt -text -noout | grep Subject
<span class="comment"># Subject: O = exam-team, CN = exam-user</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 3 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 26.2 — Inspect API Server Certificate</h5>
                        <p>Examine the API server's TLS certificate to identify key fields tested on the CKA exam.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>SSH to the control plane node</li>
                            <li>Run: <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout</code></li>
                            <li>Identify: Subject (CN), Issuer, expiry date, and Subject Alternative Names (SANs)</li>
                            <li>Verify it was signed by the cluster CA: <code>openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: SANs are in the <code>X509v3 Subject Alternative Name</code> section of the certificate output.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># SSH to control plane</span>
ssh controlplane

<span class="comment"># Inspect the API server certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout

<span class="comment"># Key fields to identify:</span>
<span class="comment"># Subject: CN = kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># Issuer:  CN = kubernetes   (signed by cluster CA)</span>
<span class="comment"># Not After: (expiry date — typically 1 year)</span>
<span class="comment"># X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes, DNS:kubernetes.default,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local,</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP Address:10.96.0.1, IP Address:192.168.x.x</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify CA signature</span>
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
  /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
<span class="comment"># apiserver.crt: OK</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 26.3 — Check and Renew Certificates</h5>
                        <p>Check certificate expiry dates and renew the API server certificate.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Check all certificate expiry dates: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                            <li>Renew the API server certificate only: <code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver</code></li>
                            <li>Restart the API server (move/restore static pod manifest)</li>
                            <li>Verify the new expiry date matches today + 1 year</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Moving the manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> stops the pod. Moving it back triggers a restart with the new cert.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check all certificate expiry dates</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Jan 01, 2026 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Jan 01, 2026 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Jan 01, 2026 00:00 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Renew API server certificate only</span>
kubeadm certs renew apiserver

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Restart API server (static pod)</span>
mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait for pod to stop (~10 seconds)</span>
sleep 10
mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># Wait for restart</span>
sleep 30

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify new expiry</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout \
  | grep "Not After"
<span class="comment"># Not After : (should be ~1 year from today)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify API server is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver</code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 26 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: TLS &amp; Certificate Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Certificate locations</strong> — All certs live under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code>. API server: <code>apiserver.crt</code>, <code>apiserver-key.crt</code>. CA: <code>ca.crt</code>, <code>ca.key</code>. etcd has its own directory: <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code></li>
                        <li><strong>CSR approval</strong> — <code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;csr-name&gt;</code>. The exam may ask you to create a CSR for a new user and approve it. Don't forget: the CSR object needs the right <code>signerName</code> (<code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code>)</li>
                        <li><strong>Certificate expiry</strong> — <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all cert expiry dates. Certs default to 1 year. CA cert is 10 years. If a cert expires, that component stops working</li>
                        <li><strong>After renewing certs, restart the component</strong> — For static pods (apiserver, controller-manager, scheduler): move the manifest out of <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>, wait, move it back. This is the tested pattern</li>
                        <li><strong><code>openssl x509 -in cert.crt -text -noout</code></strong> — Memorize this for inspecting any cert. Look at Subject, Issuer, Not After, and SANs (Subject Alternative Names)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Kubernetes PKI Works: Certificate Chains, kubeadm cert renewal, CSR API, and TLS Bootstrap</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Kubernetes PKI — The Certificate Trust Chain</h5>
            <p>Every component in Kubernetes authenticates using TLS certificates. Understanding the trust chain helps you debug "x509: certificate signed by unknown authority" errors and manage certificate rotation.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBERNETES PKI — Certificate Hierarchy                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │                     ROOT CA (ca.crt + ca.key)                         │   │
│  │                     /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.*                           │   │
│  │                     Self-signed, 10-year validity                     │   │
│  │                     Signs ALL cluster certificates                    │   │
│  └───────────────┬──────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                  │                      │                                     │
│         ┌────────▼────────┐    ┌────────▼────────┐                          │
│         │  API SERVER CERT │    │  ETCD CA        │                          │
│         │  apiserver.crt   │    │  etcd/ca.crt     │                          │
│         │  SANs:            │    │  Signs etcd certs│                          │
│         │   kubernetes      │    └────────┬────────┘                          │
│         │   kubernetes.     │             │                                    │
│         │   default         │    ┌────────▼────────┐                          │
│         │   127.0.0.1       │    │  ETCD SERVER     │                          │
│         │   10.96.0.1       │    │  etcd/server.crt │                          │
│         └────────┬──────────┘    │  CN=etcd-&lt;node&gt;  │                          │
│                  │               └──────────────────┘                          │
│                  │                                                            │
│         ┌────────▼──────────┐   ┌──────────────────────────┐                 │
│         │  KUBELET CLIENTS  │   │  FRONT-PROXY CA          │                 │
│         │  (signed by CA)   │   │  front-proxy-ca.crt      │                 │
│         │                    │   │  Signs aggregator certs   │                 │
│         │  apiserver-kubelet-│   └──────────────────────────┘                 │
│         │  client.crt       │                                                │
│         │  (API→kubelet auth)│                                                │
│         └───────────────────┘                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CERTIFICATE RENEWAL (CKA Exam Topic):                                       │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubeadm certs check-expiration                                      │   │
│  │ CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME     │   │
│  │ admin.conf                 Mar 10, 2025 09:00 UTC   364d              │   │
│  │ apiserver                  Mar 10, 2025 09:00 UTC   364d              │   │
│  │ apiserver-kubelet-client   Mar 10, 2025 09:00 UTC   364d              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubeadm certs renew all                                              │   │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Renews ALL certificates. Restart control plane pods after.          │</span>   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubeadm certs renew apiserver  <span class="comment"># Renew only API server cert         │   │</span>
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CSR API — Requesting Client Certificates Programmatically:                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. User generates private key + CSR                                   │   │
│  │ 2. User creates CertificateSigningRequest K8s object                  │   │
│  │ 3. Admin approves: kubectl certificate approve &lt;csr-name&gt;            │   │
│  │ 4. controller-manager signs the CSR with CA key                       │   │
│  │ 5. Signed certificate stored in CSR.status.certificate                │   │
│  │ 6. User extracts: kubectl get csr &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.      │   │
│  │    certificate}' | base64 -d > user.crt                               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Certificate management is directly tested on the CKA. Memorize: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> (view expiry), <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> (renew all), <code>kubeadm certs renew apiserver</code> (renew specific). After renewal, restart the affected control plane pod by moving its manifest temporarily. CSR API: <code>kubectl certificate approve</code> / <code>deny</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">CSR <code>request</code> field takes the raw CSR file content</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">The <code>request</code> field requires <strong>base64-encoded</strong> CSR content</span></li>
                    <li><code>signerName</code> is <strong>required</strong> since K8s 1.22 — use <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code> for user certs</li>
                    <li>Certificates expire after <strong>1 year</strong> by default — <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all dates</li>
                    <li>After renewing certs, you MUST <strong>restart control plane pods</strong> (they load certs at startup)</li>
                    <li>The CN (Common Name) in the CSR becomes the <strong>username</strong> in K8s. The O (Organization) becomes the <strong>group</strong></li>
                    <li><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code> is the default cert location — memorize this path</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Certificate inspection one-liners:</strong><br>
                <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A2 "Validity"</code> — check expiry<br>
                <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Alternative"</code> — check SANs<br>
                <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> — all certs at once<br>
                <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> — renew everything (then restart control plane)</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 26)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 8 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Check all certificate expiration dates: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></li>
                    <li>2. Inspect the API server cert's SANs: <code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Alternative"</code></li>
                    <li>3. Generate a private key and CSR for user "devuser": <code>openssl genrsa -out devuser.key 2048</code> then <code>openssl req -new -key devuser.key -subj "/CN=devuser/O=dev" -out devuser.csr</code></li>
                    <li>4. Create and approve a Kubernetes CSR object for devuser</li>
                    <li>5. Extract the signed certificate and create a kubeconfig for devuser</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 26 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch26-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 26 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the cluster's PKI &amp; certificate flow — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where are the Kubernetes PKI certificates stored on a kubeadm cluster? List the key files and their purpose.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Default PKI directory:</span>
/etc/kubernetes/pki/

<span class="comment"># Key files:</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt              ← Cluster CA certificate</span>
<span class="comment"># ca.key              ← Cluster CA private key (PROTECT THIS!)</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver.crt       ← API server TLS certificate</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver.key       ← API server TLS private key</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client.crt  ← API→kubelet auth</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client.key</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client.crt     ← API→etcd auth</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client.key</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-ca.crt  ← Front proxy CA</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-ca.key</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client.key</span>
<span class="comment"># sa.key              ← SA signing key</span>
<span class="comment"># sa.pub              ← SA verification key</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd certificates:</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt, ca.key, server.crt, server.key,</span>
<span class="comment"># peer.crt, peer.key, healthcheck-client.crt/key</span>

<span class="comment"># Kubeconfig files (contain embedded certs):</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/
<span class="comment"># admin.conf, controller-manager.conf,</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf, kubelet.conf</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>All kubeadm PKI files live in <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/</code>. The CA cert/key pair is the root of trust — <code>ca.key</code> must be protected. On the CKA, you need to know this path for: checking certificate expiry, renewing certificates, creating CSRs signed by the cluster CA, and troubleshooting TLS errors. The etcd subdirectory has separate CA and certificates for etcd mTLS.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check when Kubernetes certificates expire using <code>kubeadm</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check ALL certificate expirations:</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Dec 15, 2025 10:30 UTC   364d</span>

<span class="comment"># Check a specific cert with openssl:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -enddate
<span class="comment"># notAfter=Dec 15 10:30:00 2025 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># Check with days remaining:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -enddate -checkend 2592000
<span class="comment"># Certificate will not expire  ← (within 30 days)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code> shows all cert expiry dates at once — this is the fastest CKA method. Kubeadm certs are valid for 1 year by default. The CA cert is valid for 10 years. <code>openssl x509 -enddate</code> checks individual certs. <code>-checkend SECONDS</code> returns exit code 1 if cert expires within that time — useful in scripts. On the CKA, if you see API server TLS errors, cert expiry is the first thing to check.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you inspect the SANs (Subject Alternative Names) on the API server certificate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># View full certificate details:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -text | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative Name"
<span class="comment"># X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:controlplane, DNS:kubernetes, DNS:kubernetes.default,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc,</span>
<span class="comment">#   DNS:kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local,</span>
<span class="comment">#   IP Address:10.96.0.1, IP Address:192.168.1.10</span>

<span class="comment"># Just extract SANs cleanly:</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -ext subjectAltName

<span class="comment"># Check Subject (CN):</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -subject
<span class="comment"># subject= /CN=kube-apiserver</span>

<span class="comment"># Check Issuer (who signed it):</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -issuer
<span class="comment"># issuer= /CN=kubernetes</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>SANs define which hostnames/IPs the cert is valid for. The API server cert must include: <code>kubernetes</code>, <code>kubernetes.default</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc</code>, <code>kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local</code>, the ClusterIP of the kubernetes service (usually 10.96.0.1), and the node's IP. On the CKA, if you add a load balancer or new DNS name, you must regenerate the API server cert with updated SANs using <code>--apiserver-cert-extra-sans</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a CertificateSigningRequest (CSR) for a new user <code>devuser</code> in Kubernetes and approve it.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Generate private key</span>
openssl genrsa -out devuser.key 2048

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create CSR (CN=username, O=group)</span>
openssl req -new -key devuser.key \
-subj "/CN=devuser/O=developers" -out devuser.csr

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Base64 encode the CSR</span>
cat devuser.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n'

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create K8s CSR object</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: certificates.k8s.io/v1
kind: CertificateSigningRequest
metadata:
name: devuser-csr
spec:
request: $(cat devuser.csr | base64 | tr -d '\n')
signerName: kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client
expirationSeconds: 86400    <span class="comment"># 24 hours</span>
usages:
- client auth
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Approve the CSR</span>
kubectl certificate approve devuser-csr

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Extract the signed certificate</span>
kubectl get csr devuser-csr \
-o jsonpath='{.status.certificate}' | base64 -d > devuser.crt

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
openssl x509 -in devuser.crt -noout -subject
<span class="comment"># subject= /CN=devuser/O=developers</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The CSR workflow is a HIGH-WEIGHT CKA task. Key details: (1) <code>CN</code> becomes the username, <code>O</code> becomes the group, (2) <code>signerName</code> must be <code>kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</code> for user certs, (3) <code>usages</code> must include <code>client auth</code>, (4) the <code>request</code> field must be base64-encoded with no newlines (<code>tr -d '\n'</code>). After approval, extract the cert from <code>.status.certificate</code>. Common mistake: forgetting to base64-encode the CSR or using the wrong signerName.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you renew all kubeadm certificates? What happens to control plane components?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Renew ALL certificates at once:</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate embedded in admin.conf renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate front-proxy-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ... all renewed</span>

<span class="comment"># Renew specific certificate:</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client

<span class="comment"># CRITICAL: Restart control plane after renewal!</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm static pods auto-restart on cert change?</span>
<span class="comment"># NO — you must restart them:</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 1: Move and restore manifests</span>
cd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
sudo mv kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/
<span class="comment"># Wait ~20s for kubelet to stop the pod</span>
sudo mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml .
<span class="comment"># Repeat for controller-manager and scheduler</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Restart kubelet (easier)</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Method 3: If using crictl</span>
sudo crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs sudo crictl stopp

<span class="comment"># Verify new certs:</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># ← Should show dates ~1 year from now</span>

<span class="comment"># Also update admin kubeconfig:</span>
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) ~/.kube/config</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> renews every certificate. CRITICAL: after renewal, restart the control plane components so they pick up new certs. Static pods read certs from disk — if you restart kubelet, it detects the manifest changes and recreates the pods. Don't forget to update <code>~/.kube/config</code> (copy the new <code>admin.conf</code>). On the CKA, cert renewal + restart + kubeconfig update is a common multi-step question. Certs renewed via kubeadm inherit the same 1-year validity.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a kubeconfig file for user <code>devuser</code> using a signed certificate.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Assuming you have: devuser.key, devuser.crt, ca.crt</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Set cluster</span>
kubectl config set-cluster my-cluster \
--certificate-authority=/etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
--server=https://192.168.1.10:6443 \
--embed-certs=true \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Set credentials</span>
kubectl config set-credentials devuser \
--client-certificate=devuser.crt \
--client-key=devuser.key \
--embed-certs=true \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Set context</span>
kubectl config set-context devuser-context \
--cluster=my-cluster \
--user=devuser \
--namespace=dev \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Use context</span>
kubectl config use-context devuser-context \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Test</span>
kubectl get pods \
--kubeconfig=devuser.kubeconfig -n dev
<span class="comment"># Error (Forbidden) ← Need RBAC!</span>
<span class="comment"># This is expected — cert gives identity, RBAC gives permissions</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kubeconfig creation is a 4-step process: set-cluster, set-credentials, set-context, use-context. <code>--embed-certs=true</code> embeds certs directly in the kubeconfig (portable). On the CKA, the order matters: cluster → credentials → context → use-context. After creating the kubeconfig, the user still needs RBAC (Role+RoleBinding) to do anything. Without RBAC, they authenticate successfully but all requests return Forbidden.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The API server won't start and you see TLS certificate errors in the kubelet logs. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kubelet logs for API server errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -l --no-pager | grep -i "tls\|cert\|x509" | tail -20
<span class="comment"># "x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid"</span>
<span class="comment"># "tls: private key does not match public key"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check cert expiry</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -enddate -checkend 0
<span class="comment"># Certificate has expired ← Problem found!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check cert-key match</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -modulus | md5sum
openssl rsa -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key \
-noout -modulus | md5sum
<span class="comment"># If hashes differ → cert/key mismatch!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check static pod manifest references correct files</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep -E "cert|key"
<span class="comment">#   --tls-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt</span>
<span class="comment">#   --tls-private-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix expired certs</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver
sudo kubeadm certs renew apiserver-kubelet-client

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Restart</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify API is back</span>
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>TLS troubleshooting: check kubelet logs for x509 errors, verify cert expiry, confirm cert/key match (modulus comparison), verify manifest paths. Common causes: (1) expired certs — renew with kubeadm, (2) cert/key mismatch — regenerate, (3) wrong file paths in manifests — fix the static pod YAML. On the CKA, <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> is your first stop when the API server is down.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between the cluster CA certificate and component certificates?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Certificate</th>
                                        <th>Type</th>
                                        <th>Purpose</th>
                                        <th>Validity</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>ca.crt/key</code></td>
                                        <td>Root CA</td>
                                        <td>Signs all other certs</td>
                                        <td>10 years</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>apiserver.crt</code></td>
                                        <td>Server cert</td>
                                        <td>API server TLS</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>apiserver-kubelet-client</code></td>
                                        <td>Client cert</td>
                                        <td>API→kubelet auth</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>apiserver-etcd-client</code></td>
                                        <td>Client cert</td>
                                        <td>API→etcd auth</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>front-proxy-*</code></td>
                                        <td>Proxy CA+cert</td>
                                        <td>Aggregation layer</td>
                                        <td>1 year</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>etcd/ca.crt</code></td>
                                        <td>etcd CA</td>
                                        <td>etcd internal TLS</td>
                                        <td>10 years</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Verify cert is signed by cluster CA:</span>
openssl verify -CAfile /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt \
/etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
<span class="comment"># apiserver.crt: OK</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The cluster CA (<code>ca.crt/ca.key</code>) is the trust root — it signs all component certs. CAs last 10 years; component certs last 1 year. There are TWO CAs: the cluster CA (for API server, kubelets) and the etcd CA (for etcd peers/clients). On the CKA, <code>openssl verify</code> checks that a cert was signed by a specific CA. Client certs authenticate components to each other (mutual TLS). If any component cert doesn't chain to the correct CA, communication fails.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you deny a CertificateSigningRequest and list all CSRs?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># List all CSRs:</span>
kubectl get csr
<span class="comment"># NAME          AGE   SIGNERNAME                         REQUESTOR       CONDITION</span>
<span class="comment"># devuser-csr   2m    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client admin           Approved,Issued</span>
<span class="comment"># baduser-csr   1m    kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client admin           Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Deny a CSR:</span>
kubectl certificate deny baduser-csr
<span class="comment"># certificatesigningrequest.certificates.k8s.io/baduser-csr denied</span>

<span class="comment"># Delete a CSR:</span>
kubectl delete csr baduser-csr

<span class="comment"># View CSR details:</span>
kubectl get csr devuser-csr -o yaml
<span class="comment"># status:</span>
<span class="comment">#   conditions:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - type: Approved</span>
<span class="comment">#     reason: KubectlApprove</span>
<span class="comment">#     message: "This CSR was approved by kubectl certificate approve."</span>

<span class="comment"># Describe for more info:</span>
kubectl describe csr devuser-csr
<span class="comment"># Requesting User:  kubernetes-admin</span>
<span class="comment"># Signer:          kubernetes.io/kube-apiserver-client</span>
<span class="comment"># Status:          Approved,Issued</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CSR management: <code>kubectl certificate approve|deny</code> to handle requests. CSRs have three possible states: Pending, Approved, Denied. On the CKA, you'll see Pending CSRs (e.g., from node bootstrapping or user cert requests). <code>kubectl get csr</code> lists all with status. Key: CSRs don't auto-delete — clean up with <code>kubectl delete csr</code>. The CONDITION column shows the current state. Node bootstrap CSRs use <code>kubernetes.io/kubelet-serving</code> as signerName.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you add extra SANs to the API server certificate for a new load balancer IP <code>10.0.0.100</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Delete old API server cert</span>
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt
sudo rm /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.key

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Regenerate with extra SAN</span>
sudo kubeadm init phase certs apiserver \
--apiserver-cert-extra-sans=10.0.0.100,lb.example.com

<span class="comment"># OR: Edit kubeadm config first, then regenerate:</span>
kubectl -n kube-system get cm kubeadm-config -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Add to ClusterConfiguration:</span>
<span class="comment">#   apiServer:</span>
<span class="comment">#     certSANs:</span>
<span class="comment">#     - 10.0.0.100</span>
<span class="comment">#     - lb.example.com</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Restart API server</span>
sudo crictl pods --name kube-apiserver -q | xargs sudo crictl stopp
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify new SANs</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt \
-noout -text | grep -A2 "Subject Alternative"
<span class="comment"># DNS:kubernetes, DNS:lb.example.com, ...</span>
<span class="comment"># IP:10.96.0.1, IP:192.168.1.10, IP:10.0.0.100 ← New!</span>

<span class="comment"># CAUTION: Never delete ca.crt/ca.key!</span>
<span class="comment"># Only apiserver.crt/key are safe to regenerate</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>To add SANs: (1) delete old API server cert/key, (2) regenerate with <code>kubeadm init phase certs apiserver --apiserver-cert-extra-sans=...</code>, (3) restart API server. On the CKA, this is needed when adding a load balancer or additional access point. NEVER delete <code>ca.crt/ca.key</code> — only the API server cert is safe to regenerate. The kubeadm config in the <code>kube-system/kubeadm-config</code> ConfigMap stores the cluster configuration including extra SANs.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 26 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch27">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 27 · Pod Security &amp; Admission Control</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Kubernetes provides multiple layers of pod-level security. <strong>Pod Security Standards (PSS)</strong> define three security profiles (Privileged, Baseline, Restricted). <strong>Pod Security Admission (PSA)</strong> enforces these standards at the namespace level using labels. <strong>Security Contexts</strong> let you set user IDs, capabilities, and read-only filesystems per pod/container. <strong>Admission Controllers</strong> intercept API requests to validate and mutate objects before they hit etcd. Mastering these is essential for CKA and real-world cluster hardening.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🟡 Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🛡️ Pod Security</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Pod Security Standards — Privileged, Baseline, and Restricted profiles</li>
                    <li>✅ Pod Security Admission — enforce/audit/warn modes via namespace labels</li>
                    <li>✅ Security Contexts — runAsNonRoot, capabilities, readOnlyRootFilesystem</li>
                    <li>✅ Admission Controllers — built-in and webhook-based controllers</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Workloads &amp; Scheduling <span class="cka-weight">15%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Pod Security on the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>Expect questions that require you to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Apply PSA labels to namespaces to enforce security standards</li>
                    <li>Configure SecurityContext fields (runAsNonRoot, runAsUser, capabilities)</li>
                    <li>Know which admission controllers are enabled by default</li>
                    <li>Troubleshoot pods rejected by admission policies</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

