            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Pod Security Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Label namespace (enforce restricted)</td><td><code>kubectl label ns prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Label namespace (warn baseline)</td><td><code>kubectl label ns dev pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=baseline</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Label namespace (audit baseline)</td><td><code>kubectl label ns staging pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=baseline</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check namespace labels</td><td><code>kubectl get ns prod --show-labels</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Remove PSA label</td><td><code>kubectl label ns prod pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Dry-run pod against PSA</td><td><code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f pod.yaml</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List enabled admission controllers</td><td><code>kube-apiserver -h | grep enable-admission-plugins</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>SecurityContext — Exam Skeleton</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Know both pod-level and container-level SecurityContext fields.</p>
                <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-pod
spec:
  securityContext:                    # ◀── POD-level (applies to all containers)
    runAsUser: 1000                   # ◀── UID for all containers
    runAsGroup: 3000                  # ◀── GID for all containers
    fsGroup: 2000                     # ◀── supplemental group for volume mounts
    runAsNonRoot: true                # ◀── REJECT if image runs as root
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
    securityContext:                  # ◀── CONTAINER-level (overrides pod-level)
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false # ◀── block setuid binaries
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true    # ◀── immutable container filesystem
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]                 # ◀── drop ALL Linux capabilities
        add: ["NET_BIND_SERVICE"]     # ◀── add back only what's needed</code></pre>
            </div>


            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>Find Security Fields Fast</h4>
                <pre><code>kubectl explain pod.spec.securityContext             <span class="comment"># pod-level security</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.securityContext  <span class="comment"># container-level security</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.securityContext.runAsUser    <span class="comment"># UID field</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.securityContext.capabilities  <span class="comment"># Linux caps</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="metric-grid">
                <div class="metric-card metric-blue">
                    <span class="metric-value">3</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Security Levels (PSS)</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-green">
                    <span class="metric-value">3</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Enforcement Modes</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-orange">
                    <span class="metric-value">9</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">PSA Label Combinations</span>
                </div>
                <div class="metric-card metric-red">
                    <span class="metric-value">30+</span>
                    <span class="metric-label">Built-in Admission Controllers</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §27.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s27-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.1 Pod Security Standards (PSS)</h3>
                <p>Pod Security Standards define three progressively restrictive security profiles that cover the most common pod security needs.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Three Pod Security Standards — Increasing Restriction</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │          POD SECURITY STANDARDS (PSS) — THREE LEVELS                    │
  │                                                                         │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐       │
  │  │   🔓 PRIVILEGED │   │   🔒 BASELINE   │   │   🔐 RESTRICTED │       │
  │  │                 │   │                 │   │                 │       │
  │  │ • No restrict.  │   │ • Blocks known  │   │ • Strictest     │       │
  │  │ • Full access   │   │   escalations   │   │ • Hardened pods │       │
  │  │ • Host PID/Net  │   │ • No hostPID    │   │ • Non-root only │       │
  │  │ • Privileged    │   │ • No hostNet    │   │ • Drop ALL caps │       │
  │  │   containers OK │   │ • No privileged │   │ • No privilege  │       │
  │  │                 │   │   containers    │   │   escalation    │       │
  │  │ Use: system     │   │ • No hostPath   │   │ • Read-only FS  │       │
  │  │ components,     │   │                 │   │ • Seccomp       │       │
  │  │ kube-system     │   │ Use: general    │   │                 │       │
  │  │                 │   │ workloads       │   │ Use: sensitive  │       │
  │  │                 │   │                 │   │ workloads       │       │
  │  │  ◀─── OPEN ────│── ├── MODERATE ────│── ├── STRICT ──▶   │       │
  │  └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘       │
  │                                                                         │
  │  💡 Choose based on workload needs:                                     │
  │     kube-system → Privileged | apps → Baseline | PII/finance → Restric │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Check</th>
                                <th>Privileged</th>
                                <th>Baseline</th>
                                <th>Restricted</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Privileged containers</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Blocked</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Host namespaces (PID/IPC/Net)</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Blocked</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>hostPath volumes</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Blocked</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Host ports</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>Restricted</td><td>❌ Blocked</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>runAsNonRoot</td><td>Not required</td><td>Not required</td><td>✅ Required</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Privilege escalation</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>✅ Allowed</td><td>❌ Must be false</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Capabilities</td><td>Any</td><td>Restricted set</td><td>Drop ALL, add specific only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Seccomp profile</td><td>Not required</td><td>Not required</td><td>✅ Required (RuntimeDefault/Localhost)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 PSS vs PodSecurityPolicy</h5>
                    <p>PodSecurityPolicy (PSP) was deprecated in K8s 1.21 and <strong>removed in 1.25</strong>. Pod Security Standards + Pod Security Admission (PSA) replaced it. The CKA tests PSS/PSA, NOT PSP.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <h4>PSA Admission Decision Flow</h4>
                    <div class="flow-steps">
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">1</span>
                                <h5>Pod Created / Updated</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><code>kubectl apply -f pod.yaml</code> → API server receives the request</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">2</span>
                                <h5>Check Namespace Labels</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>PSA reads <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code>, <code>/warn</code>, <code>/audit</code> labels on the target namespace</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">3</span>
                                <h5>Evaluate Against Standard</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p>Compares pod spec fields (securityContext, capabilities, hostNetwork, etc.) against the configured PSS level</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="flow-connector">▼</div>
                        <div class="flow-step">
                            <div class="flow-step-header">
                                <span class="flow-step-number">4</span>
                                <h5>Apply Mode Action</h5>
                            </div>
                            <p><strong>enforce</strong> → REJECT pod | <strong>warn</strong> → allow + show warning | <strong>audit</strong> → allow + log to audit</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §27.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s27-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.2 Pod Security Admission (PSA)</h3>
                <p>Pod Security Admission is the <strong>built-in admission controller</strong> that enforces Pod Security Standards. It's configured entirely through <strong>namespace labels</strong> — no YAML files needed.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">PSA Enforcement via Namespace Labels</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │           POD SECURITY ADMISSION — HOW IT WORKS                         │
  │                                                                         │
  │   kubectl apply -f pod.yaml                                             │
  │          │                                                              │
  │          ▼                                                              │
  │   ┌──────────────┐                                                      │
  │   │  API Server   │                                                      │
  │   └──────┬───────┘                                                      │
  │          │ intercepts                                                    │
  │          ▼                                                              │
  │   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐                          │
  │   │  PSA Admission Controller                 │                          │
  │   │                                           │                          │
  │   │  Checks namespace labels:                 │                          │
  │   │  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=X      │                          │
  │   │  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=X         │                          │
  │   │  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=X        │                          │
  │   └──────┬───────────┬──────────┬────────────┘                          │
  │          │           │          │                                        │
  │      ENFORCE       WARN      AUDIT                                      │
  │          │           │          │                                        │
  │     ❌ REJECT   ⚠️ Warning   📝 Log                                    │
  │     the pod     but ALLOW    but ALLOW                                  │
  │                                                                         │
  │   💡 You can combine modes: enforce=baseline + warn=restricted          │
  │      → Blocks baseline violations, warns about restricted violations    │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">psa-namespace-labels.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Apply PSA labels to namespaces ──

<span class="comment"># Production: enforce restricted (reject non-compliant pods)</span>
kubectl label ns production \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest

<span class="comment"># Staging: enforce baseline, warn about restricted violations</span>
kubectl label ns staging \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted

<span class="comment"># Development: warn only (don't block anything)</span>
kubectl label ns development \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=baseline \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=baseline

<span class="comment"># Verify labels</span>
kubectl get ns production --show-labels
<span class="comment"># NAME         STATUS   AGE   LABELS</span>
<span class="comment"># production   Active   5d    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted,...</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Test: What happens when you deploy a non-compliant pod? ──</span>

<span class="comment"># This pod runs as root — violates "restricted"</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n production
<span class="comment"># Error: pods "test" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest"</span>

<span class="comment"># Same pod in dev namespace — just a warning</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n development
<span class="comment"># Warning: would violate PodSecurity "baseline:latest"</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/test created  ← allowed!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div>
                        <h5>enforce</h5>
                        <p><strong>Rejects</strong> pods that violate the standard. Use in production to prevent insecure workloads from deploying.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>warn</h5>
                        <p><strong>Warns</strong> the user but allows the pod. Perfect for migration — see what would break before enforcing.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📝</div>
                        <h5>audit</h5>
                        <p><strong>Logs</strong> the violation to the audit log silently. Pods still run. Use for monitoring compliance.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §27.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s27-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.3 Security Contexts</h3>
                <p>SecurityContext lets you set fine-grained security settings at the pod and container level. These are the fields that PSA actually checks against the Pod Security Standards.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Field</th>
                                <th>Level</th>
                                <th>What It Does</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>runAsUser</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>Set the UID the process runs as</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>runAsGroup</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>Set the primary GID</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>runAsNonRoot</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>Reject if image would run as root (UID 0)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>fsGroup</code></td><td>Pod only</td><td>Supplemental group for mounted volumes</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Make container filesystem read-only</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Block setuid/setgid binaries</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>capabilities.drop</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Remove Linux capabilities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>capabilities.add</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Add back specific capabilities</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>privileged</code></td><td>Container only</td><td>Full host access (avoid!)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>seccompProfile</code></td><td>Pod / Container</td><td>System call filtering profile</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">restricted-compliant-pod.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># This pod passes the "restricted" PSS — use this as template</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-app
  namespace: production     <span class="comment"># namespace with enforce=restricted</span>
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true      # ◀── Required by restricted
    runAsUser: 1000
    runAsGroup: 3000
    fsGroup: 2000
    seccompProfile:         # ◀── Required by restricted
      type: RuntimeDefault
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ◀── Required by restricted
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true     # ◀── Best practice
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]                  # ◀── Required by restricted
    resources:
      limits:
        memory: "128Mi"
        cpu: "250m"
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp
      mountPath: /tmp       # ◀── writable dir via emptyDir
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}            <span class="comment"># use emptyDir for writable paths</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Pod-Level vs Container-Level SecurityContext</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   POD-LEVEL securityContext          applies to ALL containers  │
  │   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
  │   │  runAsUser: 1000                                       │     │
  │   │  runAsGroup: 3000                                      │     │
  │   │  fsGroup: 2000                                         │     │
  │   │  runAsNonRoot: true                                    │     │
  │   │  seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault                        │     │
  │   └───────────┬───────────────────────┬────────────────────┘     │
  │               │                       │                          │
  │               ▼                       ▼                          │
  │   ┌───────────────────┐   ┌───────────────────┐                  │
  │   │ CONTAINER-LEVEL   │   │ CONTAINER-LEVEL   │                  │
  │   │ (overrides pod)   │   │ (inherits pod)    │                  │
  │   │                   │   │                   │                  │
  │   │ runAsUser: 2000   │   │ (uses 1000 from   │                  │
  │   │ ← overrides 1000  │   │  pod-level)       │                  │
  │   │                   │   │                   │                  │
  │   │ capabilities:     │   │ capabilities:     │                  │
  │   │   drop: ["ALL"]   │   │   drop: ["ALL"]   │                  │
  │   │                   │   │                   │                  │
  │   │ allowPrivilegeEsc │   │ readOnlyRootFS:   │                  │
  │   │ : false           │   │ true              │                  │
  │   └───────────────────┘   └───────────────────┘                  │
  │     container: app          container: sidecar                   │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Pattern: Restricted-Compliant Pod</h5>
                    <p>To make ANY pod pass the "restricted" standard, add these 4 things: <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code>, and <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code>. Missing any one ➔ rejected.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §27.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s27-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">27.4 Admission Controllers</h3>
                <p>Admission Controllers are plugins that intercept API requests <strong>after authentication and authorization</strong> but <strong>before the object is persisted to etcd</strong>. They can validate, mutate, or reject requests.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Request Flow Through Admission Controllers</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                API REQUEST LIFECYCLE                                          │
  │                                                                              │
  │   kubectl apply ──▶ API Server                                               │
  │                        │                                                     │
  │                   ┌────▼────┐                                                │
  │                   │  AuthN  │ ── Who are you? (certs, tokens)                │
  │                   └────┬────┘                                                │
  │                   ┌────▼────┐                                                │
  │                   │  AuthZ  │ ── Are you allowed? (RBAC)                     │
  │                   └────┬────┘                                                │
  │                   ┌────▼──────────────────────────────────┐                  │
  │                   │  ADMISSION CONTROLLERS                │                  │
  │                   │                                       │                  │
  │                   │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  │                  │
  │                   │  │  MUTATING    │  │  VALIDATING  │  │                  │
  │                   │  │              │  │              │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Defaults   │  │ • Policy     │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Inject     │  │ • Rules      │  │                  │
  │                   │  │   sidecars   │  │ • Quotas     │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Labels     │  │ • PSA        │  │                  │
  │                   │  │ • Webhooks   │  │ • Webhooks   │  │                  │
  │                   │  └──────┬───────┘  └──────┬───────┘  │                  │
  │                   │         │ modify           │ check    │                  │
  │                   └─────────┼──────────────────┼──────────┘                  │
  │                             │                  │                             │
  │                             └────────┬─────────┘                             │
  │                                      │ if ALL pass                           │
  │                                 ┌────▼────┐                                  │
  │                                 │  etcd   │ ── Object persisted              │
  │                                 └─────────┘                                  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Admission Controller</th>
                                <th>Type</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>NamespaceLifecycle</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Prevents creating resources in terminating/non-existent namespaces</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>LimitRanger</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Applies default resource limits from LimitRange objects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>ResourceQuota</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Enforces resource quotas per namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>ServiceAccount</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Auto-mounts SA tokens onto pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>PodSecurity</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Enforces Pod Security Standards (PSA)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>DefaultStorageClass</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Assigns default StorageClass to PVCs without one</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>MutatingAdmissionWebhook</code></td><td>Mutating</td><td>Calls external webhooks to modify objects</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code></td><td>Validating</td><td>Calls external webhooks to validate objects</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">admission-controllers.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ── Check which admission controllers are enabled ──
<span class="comment"># Look at API server manifest on control plane node</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml \
  | grep enable-admission-plugins
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,...</span>

<span class="comment"># ── Enable/disable admission controllers ──</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the API server static pod manifest:</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Add to the command args:</span>
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,ResourceQuota</span>
<span class="comment"># --disable-admission-plugins=DefaultStorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># ── After editing, API server auto-restarts (static pod) ──</span>

<span class="comment"># ── List default enabled controllers ──</span>
kube-apiserver -h | grep enable-admission-plugins
<span class="comment"># Default: CertificateApproval,CertificateSigning,</span>
<span class="comment"># CertificateSubjectRestriction,DefaultIngressClass,</span>
<span class="comment"># DefaultStorageClass,DefaultTolerationSeconds,LimitRanger,</span>
<span class="comment"># MutatingAdmissionWebhook,NamespaceLifecycle,PodSecurity,</span>
<span class="comment"># ResourceQuota,ServiceAccount,StorageObjectInUseProtection,</span>
<span class="comment"># ValidatingAdmissionPolicy,ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA: Editing API Server Manifest</h5>
                    <p>On the exam, you may need to enable an admission controller by editing <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. After saving, the kubelet auto-restarts the API server pod. Wait ~30 seconds and verify with <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code>. If the API server doesn't come back, check your YAML syntax.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ DO</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Use PSA labels for namespace-level enforcement</li>
                            <li>Start with <code>warn</code> mode before switching to <code>enforce</code></li>
                            <li>Set <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> for all application pods</li>
                            <li>Drop ALL capabilities, add only what's needed</li>
                            <li>Use <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> + emptyDir for writable paths</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>❌ DON'T</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Run containers as root without a compelling reason</li>
                            <li>Use <code>privileged: true</code> for application workloads</li>
                            <li>Skip seccomp profiles in restricted namespaces</li>
                            <li>Forget to restart API server after editing admission plugins</li>
                            <li>Confuse PodSecurityPolicy (removed) with Pod Security Standards</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">Security Context Quick Reference — CKA Cheat Sheet</div>
<pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │        SECURITY CONTEXT CHEAT SHEET — WHAT TO SET WHERE                      │
  │                                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐ │
  │  │  POD-LEVEL                       │  │  CONTAINER-LEVEL                 │ │
  │  │  spec.securityContext            │  │  spec.containers[].securityCtx   │ │
  │  │                                  │  │                                  │ │
  │  │  ✅ runAsUser: 1000              │  │  ✅ allowPrivilegeEscalation     │ │
  │  │  ✅ runAsGroup: 3000             │  │  ✅ readOnlyRootFilesystem       │ │
  │  │  ✅ runAsNonRoot: true           │  │  ✅ capabilities.drop/add       │ │
  │  │  ✅ fsGroup: 2000                │  │  ✅ privileged (avoid!)         │ │
  │  │  ✅ seccompProfile               │  │  ✅ runAsUser (overrides pod)   │ │
  │  │  ✅ supplementalGroups           │  │  ✅ runAsNonRoot (overrides)    │ │
  │  │                                  │  │  ✅ seccompProfile (overrides)  │ │
  │  │  ❌ NOT available:               │  │                                  │ │
  │  │     capabilities                 │  │  ❌ NOT available:               │ │
  │  │     readOnlyRootFilesystem       │  │     fsGroup                      │ │
  │  │     allowPrivilegeEscalation     │  │     supplementalGroups           │ │
  │  └──────────────────────────────────┘  └──────────────────────────────────┘ │
  │                                                                              │
  │  💡 Rule: Container-level OVERRIDES pod-level for shared fields             │
  │     Pod sets runAsUser: 1000, Container sets runAsUser: 2000                │
  │     → Container runs as UID 2000                                            │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>

            <!-- ─── Pod Security Verification Checklist ─── -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>Pod Security Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get ns NAME --show-labels</code> → verify PSA labels are applied</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl label ns NAME pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted --dry-run=server</code> → preview label effect</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f pod.yaml</code> → test pod against PSA before deploying</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- id</code> → confirm runAsUser/runAsGroup</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl exec POD -- touch /test</code> → verify readOnlyRootFilesystem (should fail)</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver</code> → verify apiserver restarted after admission controller changes</li>
                    <li><code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep enable-admission</code> → check enabled admission controllers</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>🏋️ Chapter 27 Practice Exercises</h5>
                    <p>These exercises cover the most common CKA pod security patterns.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 4 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 27.1 — Apply PSA to a Namespace</h5>
                        <p>Configure Pod Security Admission to enforce baseline and warn for restricted on a namespace.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create namespace <code>secure-ns</code></li>
                            <li>Label it to <strong>enforce</strong> the <code>baseline</code> standard and <strong>warn</strong> for <code>restricted</code></li>
                            <li>Try deploying a pod with <code>hostNetwork: true</code> → should be <strong>rejected</strong></li>
                            <li>Deploy a normal nginx pod → should succeed with a <strong>warning</strong> about restricted</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: PSA labels follow the format <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/MODE=LEVEL</code>. You can set multiple modes on one namespace.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create namespace</span>
kubectl create namespace secure-ns

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Apply PSA labels</span>
kubectl label ns secure-ns \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Try deploying a pod that violates baseline</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: bad-pod
  namespace: secure-ns
spec:
  hostNetwork: true    <span class="comment"># violates baseline!</span>
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: nginx
EOF
<span class="comment"># Error: pods "bad-pod" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "baseline:latest"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Deploy a normal pod (passes baseline, warns restricted)</span>
kubectl run nginx --image=nginx -n secure-ns
<span class="comment"># Warning: would violate PodSecurity "restricted:latest": ...</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/nginx created  ← allowed!</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 6 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 27.2 — Create Restricted-Compliant Pod</h5>
                        <p>Create a pod that passes the strictest PSS level — <strong>restricted</strong>.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Label namespace <code>secure-ns</code> to <strong>enforce restricted</strong></li>
                            <li>Create a pod with all required fields: <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code>, <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code></li>
                            <li>Use a non-root image (e.g., <code>gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot</code>)</li>
                            <li>Verify the pod runs successfully in the restricted namespace</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: The restricted standard requires ALL 4 of these: runAsNonRoot, no privilege escalation, drop ALL capabilities, and a seccomp profile. Missing any one causes rejection.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Upgrade PSA to enforce restricted</span>
kubectl label ns secure-ns \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  --overwrite

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create restricted-compliant pod</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-pod
  namespace: secure-ns
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsNonRoot: true
    runAsUser: 1000
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot
    securityContext:
      allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
      capabilities:
        drop: ["ALL"]
    resources:
      limits:
        memory: "64Mi"
        cpu: "100m"
EOF

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify pod is running</span>
kubectl get pod secure-pod -n secure-ns
<span class="comment"># NAME         READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># secure-pod   1/1     Running   0          5s</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 27.3 — SecurityContext Fields</h5>
                        <p>Practice setting security context fields and verifying their effects inside the container.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Create a pod with <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> and <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code></li>
                            <li>Exec into the pod: <code>kubectl exec -it pod-name -- id</code> → should show <code>uid=1000</code></li>
                            <li>Try writing a file: <code>touch /test</code> → should fail (read-only filesystem)</li>
                            <li>Mount an emptyDir at <code>/tmp</code> and verify writing works there</li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> is a container-level field. Use emptyDir volumes for any writable paths (logs, temp files, caches).</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create pod with security context + writable emptyDir</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: sec-ctx-pod
spec:
  securityContext:
    runAsUser: 1000
    runAsGroup: 3000
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: busybox
    command: ["sleep", "3600"]
    securityContext:
      readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
    volumeMounts:
    - name: tmp
      mountPath: /tmp
  volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}
EOF

<span class="comment"># Wait for pod</span>
kubectl wait --for=condition=ready pod/sec-ctx-pod

<span class="comment"># Verify UID</span>
kubectl exec sec-ctx-pod -- id
<span class="comment"># uid=1000 gid=3000</span>

<span class="comment"># Try writing to root filesystem (fails)</span>
kubectl exec sec-ctx-pod -- touch /test
<span class="comment"># touch: /test: Read-only file system</span>

<span class="comment"># Write to emptyDir (succeeds)</span>
kubectl exec sec-ctx-pod -- touch /tmp/test
kubectl exec sec-ctx-pod -- ls /tmp/test
<span class="comment"># /tmp/test  ← success!</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target: 5 minutes</div>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <h5>Exercise 27.4 — Enable Admission Controller</h5>
                        <p>Edit the API server manifest to enable an additional admission controller plugin.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>SSH to control plane and check current admission plugins: <code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | grep enable-admission</code></li>
                            <li>Add <code>NodeRestriction</code> to the list (if not present)</li>
                            <li>Save the file — kubelet auto-restarts the API server</li>
                            <li>Wait 30 seconds and verify: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></li>
                        </ol>
                    </div>
                    <div class="drill-hint">💡 Hint: Static pod manifests auto-restart when saved. If API server doesn't come back, you made a YAML syntax error — check with <code>crictl ps</code> and <code>crictl logs</code>.</div>
                    <div class="drill-solution">
                        <details>
                            <summary>📝 View Solution</summary>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: SSH to control plane</span>
ssh controlplane

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check current admission plugins</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml \
  | grep enable-admission-plugins
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Edit the manifest</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Find the --enable-admission-plugins line and add to the list:</span>
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,ResourceQuota</span>
<span class="comment"># IMPORTANT: comma-separated, no spaces!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Wait for API server to restart (auto by kubelet)</span>
sleep 30

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify API server is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep apiserver
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-controlplane   1/1   Running   0   25s</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify the admission plugin is active</span>
kubectl get pod kube-apiserver-controlplane \
  -n kube-system -o yaml | grep enable-admission
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,ResourceQuota</span></code></pre>
                        </details>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>⏱️ CKA Speed Tip: PSA Labels</h5>
                    <p>The fastest way to apply PSA is a single <code>kubectl label</code> command. No YAML needed!<br><code>kubectl label ns my-ns pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest</code></p>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 27 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Pod Security &amp; Admission Control Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>3 PSA modes</strong> — <code>enforce</code> (reject violations), <code>warn</code> (allow but show warning), <code>audit</code> (log only). The exam usually asks for <code>enforce</code>. Apply via namespace labels, not cluster config</li>
                        <li><strong>3 PSS levels</strong> — <code>privileged</code> (no restrictions), <code>baseline</code> (blocks known escalations), <code>restricted</code> (hardened). Restricted requires <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, drop ALL capabilities, and <code>seccompProfile</code></li>
                        <li><strong>SecurityContext at pod vs container level</strong> — <code>spec.securityContext</code> sets defaults for ALL containers. <code>spec.containers[].securityContext</code> overrides per container. Container-level wins</li>
                        <li><strong><code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> fails if image uses root</strong> — The container must have a non-root USER in Dockerfile, or set <code>runAsUser: 1000</code> explicitly. Otherwise the pod is rejected at startup</li>
                        <li><strong>Admission controllers are API server flags</strong> — <code>--enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code>. To add one, edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. The API server auto-restarts</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>
            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Pod Security Admission Enforces Standards: SecurityContext Resolution, Capability Dropping, and seccomp Profiles</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Pod Security Admission (PSA) — Replacing PSP</h5>
            <p>PSA is a built-in admission controller that enforces security standards at the namespace level. It replaces the deprecated PodSecurityPolicy (PSP). It's simpler, faster, and checked at pod creation time.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  POD SECURITY ADMISSION — Three Built-in Standards                           │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  STANDARD: PRIVILEGED (completely unrestricted)                       │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │   │
│  │  │ • Allows everything — no restrictions                          │  │   │
│  │  │ • Use case: system pods, CSI drivers, CNI plugins              │  │   │
│  │  │ • Equivalent to no PSA enforcement                             │  │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  STANDARD: BASELINE (common hardening, minimal disruption)             │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │   │
│  │  │ • Prevents known privilege escalations                          │  │   │
│  │  │ • Blocks: hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC, hostPath volumes       │  │   │
│  │  │ • Blocks: privileged containers, CAP_SYS_ADMIN                  │  │   │
│  │  │ • Allows: running as root, default capabilities                 │  │   │
│  │  │ • Use case: most application workloads                          │  │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  STANDARD: RESTRICTED (highest security, follows best practices)      │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │   │
│  │  │ • All Baseline restrictions                                     │  │   │
│  │  │ • MUST run as non-root (runAsNonRoot: true)                     │  │   │
│  │  │ • MUST drop ALL capabilities (then add back if needed)          │  │   │
│  │  │ • MUST set seccomp profile (RuntimeDefault or Localhost)         │  │   │
│  │  │ • Volume types limited to: configMap, csi, downwardAPI, etc.    │  │   │
│  │  │ • Use case: multi-tenant clusters, sensitive workloads          │  │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  NAMESPACE-LEVEL ENFORCEMENT:                                                │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Namespace label: pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  MODES (3 independent modes per namespace):                            │   │
│  │  ┌─────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │   │
│  │  │ enforce     │ REJECTS non-compliant pods (hard block)          │   │   │
│  │  │ audit       │ LOGS non-compliant pods (allowed, but recorded)  │   │   │
│  │  │ warn        │ WARNS user about non-compliance (allowed)        │   │   │
│  │  └─────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Example:                                                              │   │
│  │  kubectl label ns easypj-dev \                                         │   │
│  │    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \                     │   │
│  │    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \                        │   │
│  │    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted                         │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  SECURITY CONTEXT — Pod/Container-Level Hardening:                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  apiVersion: v1                                                       │   │
│  │  kind: Pod                                                             │   │
│  │  spec:                                                                 │   │
│  │    securityContext:          <span class="comment">← Pod-level (applies to ALL containers)  </span>│   │
│  │      runAsUser: 1000        <span class="comment">← Run as UID 1000 (non-root)             </span>│   │
│  │      runAsGroup: 3000       <span class="comment">← GID 3000                                </span>│   │
│  │      fsGroup: 2000          <span class="comment">← Volume filesystem group                  </span>│   │
│  │    containers:                                                         │   │
│  │    - name: easypj                                                      │   │
│  │      securityContext:       <span class="comment">← Container-level (OVERRIDES pod-level)  </span>│   │
│  │        allowPrivilegeEscalation: false                                 │   │
│  │        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true                                    │   │
│  │        capabilities:                                                   │   │
│  │          drop: ["ALL"]        <span class="comment">← Drop all Linux capabilities           </span>│   │
│  │          add: ["NET_BIND_SERVICE"] <span class="comment">← Add back only what's needed     </span>│   │
│  │        seccompProfile:                                                 │   │
│  │          type: RuntimeDefault <span class="comment">← Restrict syscalls                     </span>│   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  LINUX CAPABILITIES — Breaking Root Into 40+ Pieces:                         │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Traditional Linux: root = ALL power                                  │   │
│  │  Capabilities: root power split into 40+ individual permissions       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Common capabilities:                                                 │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────┐   │   │
│  │  │ CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE│ Bind to ports < 1024 (e.g., port 80)     │   │   │
│  │  │ CAP_SYS_TIME        │ Set system clock                         │   │   │
│  │  │ CAP_SYS_ADMIN       │ DANGEROUS — mount, swapon, many admin ops│   │   │
│  │  │ CAP_NET_RAW         │ Raw sockets (ping)                       │   │   │
│  │  │ CAP_SYS_PTRACE      │ Trace other processes                    │   │   │
│  │  │ CAP_CHOWN           │ Change file ownership                    │   │   │
│  │  └────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────┘   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Best practice: drop ALL, add back only what your app needs.          │   │
│  │  Most web apps need only: NET_BIND_SERVICE (port 80/443).            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Pod security is a CKA topic. Key commands: <code>kubectl label ns &lt;ns&gt; pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</code> to enable PSA. For exam pods: always use <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code>, and <code>seccompProfile.type: RuntimeDefault</code> for the restricted standard. If a pod is rejected by PSA, check the event: <code>kubectl describe rs &lt;name&gt;</code> or <code>kubectl get events</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <span class="gotcha-wrong">Container SecurityContext inherits all pod-level settings</span></li>
                    <li>✅ <span class="gotcha-correct">Container SecurityContext <strong>overrides</strong> pod-level for overlapping fields</span></li>
                    <li>PSA labels use the format <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/MODE=LEVEL</code> — three modes (enforce/warn/audit) × three levels (privileged/baseline/restricted)</li>
                    <li>PSA <code>enforce</code> mode <strong>rejects</strong> pods. <code>warn</code> shows a warning but allows. <code>audit</code> logs only</li>
                    <li><code>runAsNonRoot: true</code> checks the <strong>image USER directive</strong> — if the image has no USER set, the pod is rejected even if the process would run as non-root</li>
                    <li><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code> blocks writes to the container filesystem — use emptyDir volumes for writable directories</li>
                    <li><code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code> then <code>add</code> specific ones = principle of least privilege</li>
                    <li>The <code>restricted</code> PSS requires: runAsNonRoot, no privilege escalation, drop ALL capabilities, seccomp profile — missing ANY of these causes rejection</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — SecurityContext skeleton (memorize):</strong><br>
                <code>securityContext:</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>runAsUser: 1000</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>runAsNonRoot: true</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code><br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;<code>capabilities: { drop: ["ALL"] }</code><br>
                This covers 90% of SecurityContext questions on the exam.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-commands"></span>
