            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 27 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch27-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 27 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of Pod Security Admission &amp; SecurityContext — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a pod that runs as a non-root user with UID 1000, drops all capabilities, and has a read-only root filesystem.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: secure-pod
spec:
securityContext:
runAsNonRoot: true           # ← Reject if image runs as root
runAsUser: 1000              # ← Run all containers as UID 1000
runAsGroup: 3000             # ← Primary group
fsGroup: 2000                # ← Volume group
seccompProfile:
  type: RuntimeDefault       # ← Enable default seccomp
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ← No setuid
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true     # ← Immutable root fs
  capabilities:
    drop:
    - ALL                          # ← Drop ALL Linux caps
volumeMounts:
- name: tmp
  mountPath: /tmp                  # ← Writable tmp
- name: cache
  mountPath: /var/cache/nginx      # ← nginx needs this
- name: run
  mountPath: /var/run              # ← nginx PID file
volumes:
- name: tmp
emptyDir: {}
- name: cache
emptyDir: {}
- name: run
emptyDir: {}

<span class="comment"># Apply and verify:</span>
kubectl apply -f secure-pod.yaml
kubectl exec secure-pod -- id
<span class="comment"># uid=1000 gid=3000 groups=2000</span>
kubectl exec secure-pod -- touch /etc/test 2>&1
<span class="comment"># touch: /etc/test: Read-only file system ✓</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pod-level <code>securityContext</code> sets defaults for all containers; container-level overrides specific settings. <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem: true</code> makes the root filesystem immutable — you must provide emptyDir volumes for any writable paths (nginx needs /tmp, /var/cache/nginx, /var/run). <code>drop ALL</code> capabilities removes all Linux capabilities. <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> prevents setuid binaries from gaining elevated privileges. On the CKA, this is the "restricted" PSS-compliant pod pattern.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are the three Pod Security Standards (PSS) levels? Describe each.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Standard</th>
                                        <th>Purpose</th>
                                        <th>Key Restrictions</th>
                                        <th>Use Case</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Privileged</strong></td>
                                        <td>Unrestricted</td>
                                        <td>None — anything goes</td>
                                        <td>System-level (kube-system)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>
                                        <td>Prevent known escalations</td>
                                        <td>No privileged, no hostNetwork/PID/IPC, no hostPath, restricted ports</td>
                                        <td>Most workloads</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><strong>Restricted</strong></td>
                                        <td>Hardened best practices</td>
                                        <td>All baseline + must run as non-root, drop ALL caps, read-only root fs, seccomp required</td>
                                        <td>Security-sensitive apps</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Apply via namespace labels:</span>
kubectl label ns production \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=baseline \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted

<span class="comment"># enforce → REJECT violating pods</span>
<span class="comment"># warn    → ALLOW but warn the user</span>
<span class="comment"># audit   → ALLOW but log to audit log</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three levels: Privileged (no restrictions), Baseline (prevents known exploits), Restricted (hardened). Applied via namespace labels with three modes: enforce (reject), warn (allow + warning), audit (allow + log). On the CKA, the typical pattern is: enforce baseline + warn restricted. This catches most security issues while giving you visibility into what would break under restricted mode. <code>kube-system</code> usually stays at Privileged because system components need elevated access.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Label a namespace to enforce the <code>restricted</code> Pod Security Standard. Then try to create a default pod — what happens?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create and label namespace:</span>
kubectl create ns secure-ns
kubectl label ns secure-ns \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=latest

<span class="comment"># Try a default pod (no security context):</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n secure-ns
<span class="comment"># Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test" is forbidden:</span>
<span class="comment"># violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest":</span>
<span class="comment"># allowPrivilegeEscalation != false</span>
<span class="comment"># unrestricted capabilities</span>
<span class="comment"># runAsNonRoot != true</span>
<span class="comment"># seccompProfile not set</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix with compliant pod:</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n secure-ns \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > test.yaml
<span class="comment"># Edit test.yaml to add:</span>
<span class="comment"># spec:</span>
<span class="comment">#   securityContext:</span>
<span class="comment">#     runAsNonRoot: true</span>
<span class="comment">#     seccompProfile:</span>
<span class="comment">#       type: RuntimeDefault</span>
<span class="comment">#   containers:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - securityContext:</span>
<span class="comment">#       allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</span>
<span class="comment">#       capabilities:</span>
<span class="comment">#         drop: ["ALL"]</span>
kubectl apply -f test.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify labels:</span>
kubectl get ns secure-ns --show-labels
<span class="comment"># pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When <code>enforce=restricted</code> is set, the API server rejects any pod that doesn't meet ALL restricted requirements. The error message lists exactly which fields are missing — use it as a checklist. On the CKA, you'll need to: (1) label the namespace, (2) create a compliant pod with all required fields. The version label (<code>enforce-version=latest</code>) pins to the current K8s version's definition of "restricted."</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between pod-level and container-level <code>securityContext</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Field</th>
                                        <th>Pod Level</th>
                                        <th>Container Level</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>runAsUser</code></td>
                                        <td>✅ All containers</td>
                                        <td>✅ This container (overrides pod)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>runAsNonRoot</code></td>
                                        <td>✅</td>
                                        <td>✅ (overrides)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>fsGroup</code></td>
                                        <td>✅ Pod only</td>
                                        <td>❌ Not available</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>capabilities</code></td>
                                        <td>❌ Not available</td>
                                        <td>✅ Container only</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code></td>
                                        <td>❌ Not available</td>
                                        <td>✅ Container only</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code></td>
                                        <td>❌ Not available</td>
                                        <td>✅ Container only</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>seccompProfile</code></td>
                                        <td>✅ All containers</td>
                                        <td>✅ (overrides)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Container-level OVERRIDES pod-level:</span>
spec:
securityContext:
runAsUser: 1000      # ← Default for all containers
containers:
- name: special
securityContext:
  runAsUser: 2000    # ← This container = UID 2000
- name: normal
<span class="comment"># No override → Uses UID 1000 from pod level</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pod-level <code>securityContext</code> sets defaults; container-level overrides for that specific container. Some fields are ONLY pod-level (<code>fsGroup</code>) and some are ONLY container-level (<code>capabilities</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code>). On the CKA, a common gotcha: you set <code>runAsUser</code> at pod level but one container needs a different user — use container-level override. Also: <code>capabilities</code> can only be set per container, not pod-wide.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Add the <code>NET_ADMIN</code> capability to a container while dropping all others. When would you need this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: net-admin-pod
spec:
containers:
- name: network-tool
image: nicolaka/netshoot
securityContext:
  capabilities:
    drop:
    - ALL               # ← First drop ALL
    add:
    - NET_ADMIN          # ← Then add only what's needed
command: ["sleep", "3600"]

<span class="comment"># Verify capabilities:</span>
kubectl exec net-admin-pod -- cat /proc/1/status | grep -i cap
<span class="comment"># CapPrm: 00000000000010000  ← Only NET_ADMIN</span>

<span class="comment"># Common capabilities:</span>
<span class="comment"># NET_ADMIN    → Modify network config (iptables, routes)</span>
<span class="comment"># NET_RAW      → Use raw sockets (ping)</span>
<span class="comment"># SYS_TIME     → Set system clock</span>
<span class="comment"># SYS_PTRACE   → Trace processes (debugging)</span>
<span class="comment"># CHOWN        → Change file ownership</span>
<span class="comment"># DAC_OVERRIDE → Bypass file permission checks</span>

<span class="comment"># NOTE: In restricted PSS, you can ONLY add NET_BIND_SERVICE</span>
<span class="comment"># NET_ADMIN requires baseline or privileged PSS</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Best practice: <code>drop ALL</code> then <code>add</code> only what's needed. <code>NET_ADMIN</code> allows modifying network configuration (iptables rules, routing tables, interfaces) — needed by network plugins like Calico/Cilium. On the CKA, the pattern is always drop first, add selectively. Under the <code>restricted</code> PSS, only <code>NET_BIND_SERVICE</code> can be added. If you need <code>NET_ADMIN</code>, the namespace must use baseline or privileged enforcement.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a seccomp profile and how do you apply the default runtime seccomp profile to a pod?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># seccomp = Secure Computing mode</span>
<span class="comment"># Filters which syscalls a container can make</span>

<span class="comment"># Three types:</span>
<span class="comment"># RuntimeDefault → Container runtime's default profile</span>
<span class="comment"># Localhost      → Custom profile from node filesystem</span>
<span class="comment"># Unconfined     → No filtering (default if not set)</span>

<span class="comment"># Apply RuntimeDefault (REQUIRED for restricted PSS):</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: seccomp-pod
spec:
securityContext:
seccompProfile:
  type: RuntimeDefault    # ← Pod level (all containers)
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
securityContext:
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault  # ← Container level (overrides)

<span class="comment"># Custom profile (Localhost):</span>
spec:
securityContext:
seccompProfile:
  type: Localhost
  localhostProfile: profiles/my-profile.json
<span class="comment"># Profile must exist at:</span>
<span class="comment"># /var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/profiles/my-profile.json</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pod seccomp-pod -o jsonpath=\
'{.spec.securityContext.seccompProfile}'
<span class="comment"># {"type":"RuntimeDefault"}</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Seccomp filters syscalls — <code>RuntimeDefault</code> blocks dangerous ones like <code>reboot</code>, <code>mount</code>, <code>ptrace</code>. The restricted PSS REQUIRES <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code> (or Localhost). On the CKA, always set this in pods targeting restricted namespaces. Without it, seccomp is <code>Unconfined</code> (no filtering). Custom profiles live at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/seccomp/</code> on the node. Pod-level seccomp applies to all containers; container-level overrides.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod fails to deploy with <code>violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest"</code>. How do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Typical error message lists ALL violations:</span>
<span class="comment"># pods "my-pod" is forbidden: violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest":</span>
<span class="comment"># allowPrivilegeEscalation != false (container "app")</span>
<span class="comment"># unrestricted capabilities (container "app")</span>
<span class="comment"># runAsNonRoot != true</span>
<span class="comment"># seccompProfile (pod or containers[*] must set)</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix — add ALL required fields:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: my-pod
spec:
securityContext:
runAsNonRoot: true               # ← Fix 1
runAsUser: 1000                   # ← Ensure non-root UID
seccompProfile:
  type: RuntimeDefault           # ← Fix 4
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx
securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false # ← Fix 2
  capabilities:
    drop:
    - ALL                         # ← Fix 3

<span class="comment"># Quick checklist for restricted compliance:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ runAsNonRoot: true (pod or container)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault (pod or container)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ allowPrivilegeEscalation: false (each container)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ capabilities.drop: ["ALL"] (each container)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ No privileged: true</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ No hostNetwork/hostPID/hostIPC</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ No hostPath volumes</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The error message is your checklist — it lists EVERY violation. Fix them all. On the CKA, the four most common missing fields for restricted compliance are: (1) <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>, (2) <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>, (3) <code>capabilities.drop: ["ALL"]</code>, (4) <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code>. Apply these to every container. The restricted PSS also prohibits hostPath, hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC, and privileged mode.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What are Kubernetes admission controllers? How do you check which ones are enabled?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Admission controllers intercept API requests</span>
<span class="comment"># AFTER authentication & authorization, BEFORE persistence</span>

<span class="comment"># Two types:</span>
<span class="comment"># Mutating  → Can MODIFY the request (e.g., add defaults)</span>
<span class="comment"># Validating → Can only ACCEPT or REJECT</span>

<span class="comment"># Check enabled admission plugins:</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml | \
grep enable-admission
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</span>

<span class="comment"># OR: From running process</span>
ps aux | grep kube-apiserver | grep admission

<span class="comment"># Key admission controllers for CKA:</span>
<span class="comment"># NodeRestriction  → Limits what kubelets can modify</span>
<span class="comment"># PodSecurity      → Enforces PSS (replaced PSP)</span>
<span class="comment"># LimitRanger      → Applies default resource limits</span>
<span class="comment"># ResourceQuota    → Enforces namespace quotas</span>
<span class="comment"># NamespaceLifecycle → Prevents ops in terminating ns</span>
<span class="comment"># ServiceAccount   → Auto-mounts SA tokens</span>

<span class="comment"># Enable an additional controller:</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml:</span>
<span class="comment"># --enable-admission-plugins=NodeRestriction,PodSecurity,LimitRanger</span>
<span class="comment"># API server auto-restarts (static pod)</span>

<span class="comment"># Disable a controller:</span>
<span class="comment"># --disable-admission-plugins=DefaultTolerationSeconds</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Admission controllers are plugins in the API server pipeline: authn → authz → admission → etcd. They can mutate (add defaults, inject sidecars) or validate (reject invalid resources). On the CKA: (1) find enabled controllers in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>, (2) add new ones by editing the <code>--enable-admission-plugins</code> flag. The API server static pod auto-restarts when its manifest changes. Key controllers to know: NodeRestriction, PodSecurity, LimitRanger, ResourceQuota.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use the <code>warn</code> and <code>audit</code> PSA modes to test policies before enforcing?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Gradual rollout strategy:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Start with warn+audit (no enforcement):</span>
kubectl label ns production \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted
<span class="comment"># Existing pods keep running</span>
<span class="comment"># New non-compliant pods show warnings but ARE CREATED</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Deploy pods and watch for warnings:</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n production
<span class="comment"># Warning: would violate PodSecurity "restricted:latest":</span>
<span class="comment"># allowPrivilegeEscalation != false, ...</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/test created  ← Pod is STILL created!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check audit logs:</span>
<span class="comment"># /var/log/kubernetes/audit/audit.log</span>
<span class="comment"># Look for: "pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit-violations"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: After fixing all workloads, enforce:</span>
kubectl label ns production \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
--overwrite

<span class="comment"># Dry-run: Check existing pods against a policy:</span>
kubectl label ns production \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
--dry-run=server --overwrite
<span class="comment"># Warning: existing pods in namespace "production"</span>
<span class="comment"># that violate: pod1, pod2</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Use <code>warn</code> + <code>audit</code> to discover violations WITHOUT breaking workloads, then switch to <code>enforce</code> once compliant. <code>warn</code> shows messages to the user; <code>audit</code> logs to the audit log. On the CKA, you might be asked to: (1) label a namespace with warn mode, (2) identify violating pods, (3) fix them, (4) enable enforce. The <code>--dry-run=server</code> trick checks existing pods against a policy before applying it — very useful for CKA scenarios.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A container needs to run as root UID 0 but you want to prevent privilege escalation. Is this possible?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Yes — you can run as root but block escalation:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: root-limited
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: ubuntu
command: ["sleep", "3600"]
securityContext:
  runAsUser: 0                     # ← Root UID
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ← No setuid/setgid
  capabilities:
    drop:
    - ALL                          # ← No extra capabilities
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true     # ← Can't modify fs

<span class="comment"># This is ALLOWED under baseline PSS (not restricted!)</span>
<span class="comment"># Restricted requires runAsNonRoot: true</span>

<span class="comment"># The security context means:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Process runs as root</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Cannot gain additional privileges via setuid</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Has NO Linux capabilities</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Cannot write to filesystem</span>

<span class="comment"># When is this needed?</span>
<span class="comment"># Legacy apps that check UID=0 but don't need real root power</span>
<span class="comment"># Containers with ENTRYPOINT that checks whoami</span>

<span class="comment"># WARNING: This VIOLATES restricted PSS</span>
<span class="comment"># restricted requires: runAsNonRoot: true</span>
<span class="comment"># So this only works in baseline or privileged namespaces</span>

<span class="comment"># Better approach if possible:</span>
<span class="comment"># Use a non-root image: runAsUser: 1000</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Yes — <code>runAsUser: 0</code> + <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> + <code>capabilities.drop: ALL</code> gives a "root in name only" container. The process has UID 0 but no capabilities and can't escalate. This is allowed under baseline PSS but NOT restricted (which requires <code>runAsNonRoot: true</code>). On the CKA, understand the distinction: restricted PSS = must be non-root, baseline PSS = root is OK if you block escalation. The ideal solution is always to run as non-root, but legacy apps sometimes require UID 0.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 27 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART7 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part7-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🟡 Part 7 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Security — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You can now enforce least privilege end to end: RBAC that grants exactly the verbs a subject needs, ServiceAccounts and projected tokens that identify workloads, the cluster PKI and CSR workflow that authenticates everyone, and Pod Security + admission that constrain what pods may do. easypj now runs with the access it needs and nothing more.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 🟡 Part 7 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>RBAC (Ch 24):</strong> Role/ClusterRole = a set of verbs on resources; RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding ties them to a subject. Test with <code>kubectl auth can-i VERB RESOURCE --as=USER</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>ServiceAccounts (Ch 25):</strong> the identity a pod uses to call the API; modern tokens are short-lived, audience-bound projected tokens (TokenRequest), not legacy forever-secrets.</li>
                    <li><strong>TLS / PKI (Ch 26):</strong> the cluster CA signs every component’s cert; users authenticate via client certs in kubeconfig; create access with a CertificateSigningRequest, approve, then bind RBAC.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pod Security (Ch 27):</strong> Pod Security Standards (privileged/baseline/restricted) enforced by the built-in admission controller per namespace; securityContext drops capabilities and runs as non-root.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> create a Role + binding and verify with can-i, and read cert/kubeconfig fields — high-yield, fast tasks.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 7 Visual Summary — authenticate → authorize → admit</div>
<pre>
   👤 user / 🤖 ServiceAccount
        │ AUTHN: client cert (PKI/CA) or projected token
        ▼
   🔑 RBAC AUTHZ: Role/ClusterRole + Binding  ── can-i? ──▶ allow / deny
        │ (if allowed)
        ▼
   🛡️ ADMISSION: Pod Security Standards + securityContext gate the pod
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   least privilege, identity, and a security baseline — enforced.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 7 secured the cluster. Every request is authenticated (client cert from the PKI, or a ServiceAccount’s projected token), authorized by RBAC (Roles + Bindings, testable with can-i), and finally admitted only if the pod meets the namespace’s Pod Security Standard.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> internalize authenticate → authorize → admit, write a Role + RoleBinding and prove it with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>, and read the PKI/kubeconfig, and the security domain is yours. Next: Part 8 keeps the cluster itself healthy — upgrades, etcd DR, and node maintenance.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART8 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part8">
            <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 8 · Cluster Maintenance &amp; Lifecycle</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Keep It Alive</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 28–30 · Advanced · <em>"Day-2 operations without downtime"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Clusters are living systems that must be patched, backed up, and serviced while staying online. Part 8 is the operator’s toolkit: safe version-skew-respecting upgrades with kubeadm, the all-important etcd backup &amp; disaster-recovery drill, and node maintenance that drains and reschedules workloads without dropping traffic.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> upgrade a control plane and workers one minor version at a time; snapshot and restore etcd to recover a cluster; and cordon/drain/uncordon a node — protected by a PodDisruptionBudget — for OS and kernel patching.</div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — patch, protect, service</div>
<pre>
   Ch 28 UPGRADES  ──▶  Ch 29 etcd BACKUP &amp; DR  ──▶  Ch 30 NODE MAINTENANCE
   version skew         snapshot save · restore        cordon · drain
   kubeadm upgrade      the #1 admin safety net        uncordon · PDB
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: easypj stays available while you upgrade the cluster,
   and you can bring it back from a control-plane disaster.
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch28">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 28 · Cluster Upgrades with kubeadm</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Kubernetes releases a new minor version roughly every 4 months. As a cluster administrator, you must upgrade clusters regularly to stay within the supported version window, receive security patches, and access new features. For your <strong>easypj/jobpost</strong> production cluster, a botched upgrade means downtime for your job board. The CKA exam <strong>consistently tests</strong> the upgrade procedure — expect to perform a full control plane and worker node upgrade hands-on. This chapter gives you a battle-tested, step-by-step workflow.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⬆️ Upgrades</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Kubernetes release cycle — minor vs patch versions, support window</li>
                    <li>✅ Component version skew policy — what can differ, what can't</li>
                    <li>✅ Upgrade strategy — control plane first, then workers</li>
                    <li>✅ Hands-on control plane upgrade — kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl</li>
                    <li>✅ Hands-on worker node upgrade — drain, upgrade, uncordon</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>Cluster Upgrades on the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>Expect a question like: "Upgrade the control plane node from v1.30.0 to v1.31.0 using kubeadm." You must:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Know the exact sequence: upgrade kubeadm → plan → apply → drain → upgrade kubelet/kubectl → uncordon</li>
                    <li>Use <code>apt-mark unhold/hold</code> to manage package versions</li>
                    <li>Remember <code>systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl restart kubelet</code> after upgrading kubelet</li>
                    <li>Never skip minor versions — 1.30 → 1.31 is OK, 1.30 → 1.32 is NOT</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

