            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 28 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch28-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 28 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of the kubeadm upgrade sequence — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Upgrade the control plane node from Kubernetes 1.30.0 to 1.31.0. Show the exact commands.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Upgrade kubeadm</span>
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y --allow-change-held-packages \
kubeadm=1.31.0-1.1
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify kubeadm version</span>
kubeadm version
<span class="comment"># kubeadm version: v1.31.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check upgrade plan</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade plan
<span class="comment"># Components that must be upgraded:</span>
<span class="comment"># COMPONENT   CURRENT   TARGET</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver    v1.30.0   v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager ...</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler ...</span>
<span class="comment"># Available versions: v1.31.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Apply the upgrade</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0
<span class="comment"># [upgrade] Successfully upgraded cluster to v1.31.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Upgrade kubelet and kubectl</span>
sudo apt-get install -y --allow-change-held-packages \
kubelet=1.31.0-1.1 kubectl=1.31.0-1.1
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           STATUS   ROLES          VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   Ready    control-plane  v1.31.0</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Upgrade order is CRITICAL: kubeadm first → <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> → kubelet + kubectl → restart. Always <code>apt-mark hold</code> after installing to prevent accidental upgrades. <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> shows what will be upgraded and if there are prerequisites. On the CKA, this exact sequence is tested — you must know it by heart. Static pods (API server, etcd, etc.) restart automatically during <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Upgrade a worker node from 1.30.0 to 1.31.0. Include draining and uncordoning.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Drain the worker node</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 cordoned</span>
<span class="comment"># evicting pod default/web-app-xxxxx</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/web-app-xxxxx evicted</span>

<span class="comment"># ON WORKER NODE (ssh worker-1):</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 2: Upgrade kubeadm</span>
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y --allow-change-held-packages \
kubeadm=1.31.0-1.1
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Upgrade node config</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade node
<span class="comment"># [upgrade] Successfully upgraded node config</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Upgrade kubelet and kubectl</span>
sudo apt-get install -y --allow-change-held-packages \
kubelet=1.31.0-1.1 kubectl=1.31.0-1.1
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># BACK ON CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 6: Uncordon the worker</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 uncordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           STATUS   ROLES          VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   Ready    control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1       Ready    &lt;none&gt;         v1.31.0</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Worker upgrade: drain (from control plane) → SSH to worker → upgrade kubeadm → <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> (NOT <code>apply</code>) → upgrade kubelet/kubectl → restart → uncordon (from control plane). Key differences from control plane: workers use <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> (not <code>apply</code>), and you MUST drain before upgrading. Forgetting to uncordon is a common CKA mistake — the node stays <code>SchedulingDisabled</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Kubernetes version skew policy? Which component version relationships must be maintained?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Component</th>
                                        <th>Relative to API Server</th>
                                        <th>Example (API=1.31)</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>kubelet</td>
                                        <td>Up to 3 minor behind</td>
                                        <td>1.28 – 1.31</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>kube-proxy</td>
                                        <td>Same or 3 minor behind</td>
                                        <td>1.28 – 1.31</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>kubectl</td>
                                        <td>±1 minor version</td>
                                        <td>1.30 – 1.32</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>controller-manager</td>
                                        <td>Same minor version</td>
                                        <td>1.31 only</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>scheduler</td>
                                        <td>Same minor version</td>
                                        <td>1.31 only</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Key rules:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. NEVER skip minor versions (1.30→1.32 = INVALID)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Upgrade control plane BEFORE workers</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. API server is always the newest component</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current versions:</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
kubectl version --short</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The version skew policy ensures component compatibility. Key rules: kubelet can lag up to 3 minor versions behind the API server, kubectl is ±1, and control plane components must match. You cannot skip minor versions during upgrades (must upgrade sequentially). On the CKA, they may show you a cluster with mixed versions and ask if it's valid. The API server is always the "baseline" — everything is measured relative to it.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you find available Kubernetes package versions for upgrade?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># List all available kubeadm versions:</span>
apt-cache madison kubeadm
<span class="comment">#    kubeadm |  1.31.0-1.1 | https://pkgs.k8s.io ...</span>
<span class="comment">#    kubeadm |  1.30.2-1.1 | https://pkgs.k8s.io ...</span>
<span class="comment">#    kubeadm |  1.30.1-1.1 | https://pkgs.k8s.io ...</span>
<span class="comment">#    kubeadm |  1.30.0-1.1 | https://pkgs.k8s.io ...</span>

<span class="comment"># OR with apt-cache policy:</span>
apt-cache policy kubeadm
<span class="comment"># Shows installed version and available versions</span>

<span class="comment"># Check if kubeadm repo is configured:</span>
cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

<span class="comment"># Important: You may need to change the repo</span>
<span class="comment"># for a different minor version:</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list:</span>
<span class="comment"># deb https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.31/deb/ /</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current installed versions:</span>
kubeadm version
kubelet --version
kubectl version --client

<span class="comment"># Check held packages:</span>
apt-mark showhold
<span class="comment"># kubeadm kubelet kubectl</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>apt-cache madison kubeadm</code> lists all available versions — use this to find the exact version string for install commands. On the CKA, the version string format matters: <code>1.31.0-1.1</code> (not just <code>1.31.0</code>). If upgrading to a new minor version, you may need to update the apt repository URL first. Always check <code>apt-mark showhold</code> to see what's being held back.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">After running <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>, the API server doesn't come back up. How do you troubleshoot?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kubelet status</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># If inactive: sudo systemctl start kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check kubelet logs</span>
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -l --no-pager | tail -50
<span class="comment"># Look for: manifests error, cert error, port in use</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check static pod manifests</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># Should have: kube-apiserver.yaml, kube-controller-manager.yaml,</span>
<span class="comment">#              kube-scheduler.yaml, etcd.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check container runtime</span>
sudo crictl ps -a | grep apiserver
<span class="comment"># If exited: check logs</span>
sudo crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Common fix — daemon-reload</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check if kubelet was upgraded</span>
kubelet --version
<span class="comment"># If still old version → apt-get install kubelet=target</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Port conflict check</span>
sudo ss -tlnp | grep 6443
<span class="comment"># If something else is on 6443, kill it</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Wait and check</span>
<span class="comment"># Static pods take 30-60 seconds to restart</span>
sleep 60
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Post-upgrade API server failure: (1) check kubelet is running, (2) read kubelet logs, (3) verify manifests exist, (4) check container runtime with crictl, (5) ensure kubelet was also upgraded. Most common issue: forgetting <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> after upgrading kubelet. On the CKA, give the API server 60 seconds to come up before panicking — static pods restart can be slow.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> and <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Command</th>
                                        <th>Used On</th>
                                        <th>What It Does</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0</code></td>
                                        <td>First control plane node</td>
                                        <td>Upgrades cluster components, updates ConfigMaps, re-generates static pod manifests</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><code>kubeadm upgrade node</code></td>
                                        <td>Additional control planes + ALL workers</td>
                                        <td>Updates local kubelet config and static pods (if any), does NOT upgrade cluster-level components</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Summary:</span>
<span class="comment"># First control plane: kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># Second control plane: kubeadm upgrade node</span>
<span class="comment"># Third control plane: kubeadm upgrade node</span>
<span class="comment"># Worker-1: kubeadm upgrade node</span>
<span class="comment"># Worker-2: kubeadm upgrade node</span>
<span class="comment"># Worker-N: kubeadm upgrade node</span>

<span class="comment"># NEVER run "kubeadm upgrade apply" on workers!</span>
<span class="comment"># NEVER run "kubeadm upgrade apply" on non-first control planes!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code> is for the FIRST control plane only — it upgrades cluster-wide components (API server manifest, CoreDNS, kube-proxy, etc.). <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> is for everything else (additional control planes and all workers). On the CKA, using the wrong command is a common mistake. <code>apply</code> requires a version argument; <code>node</code> doesn't (it reads the target version from the cluster).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Why do you need <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> after upgrading kubelet?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># The kubelet package installs a systemd unit file:</span>
<span class="comment"># /usr/lib/systemd/system/kubelet.service</span>

<span class="comment"># The kubelet drop-in config:</span>
<span class="comment"># /usr/lib/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf</span>

<span class="comment"># When the package is upgraded, these files may change</span>
<span class="comment"># systemd CACHES the unit files in memory</span>
<span class="comment"># daemon-reload tells systemd to re-read them</span>

sudo systemctl daemon-reload    # ← Re-read unit files
sudo systemctl restart kubelet  # ← Restart with new binary

<span class="comment"># Without daemon-reload:</span>
<span class="comment"># systemd may use OLD unit configuration</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet may fail with config mismatch</span>
<span class="comment"># Or use deprecated flags from old service file</span>

<span class="comment"># Full upgrade sequence for kubelet:</span>
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-1.1
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet
sudo systemctl daemon-reload    # ← MUST do this
sudo systemctl restart kubelet  # ← MUST do this

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubelet --version
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes v1.31.0</span>
sudo systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: active (running)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>daemon-reload</code> tells systemd to re-read all unit files from disk. Without it, systemd uses cached (old) configuration, which can cause the kubelet to fail or use wrong settings. On the CKA, <code>daemon-reload</code> + <code>restart kubelet</code> is required after every kubelet binary upgrade. Forgetting this step is a classic exam mistake — the kubelet appears upgraded but behaves incorrectly.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that a cluster upgrade was successful?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check node versions</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           STATUS   ROLES          VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   Ready    control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1       Ready    &lt;none&gt;         v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># All nodes should show new version</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check all nodes are Ready</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># All should be Ready, not NotReady</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check system pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># All should be Running (not CrashLoopBackOff)</span>
<span class="comment"># Key pods: kube-apiserver, kube-controller-manager,</span>
<span class="comment">#           kube-scheduler, etcd, coredns, kube-proxy</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check component health</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses  # (deprecated but still works)
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
kubectl get --raw /healthz
<span class="comment"># ok</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify workloads</span>
kubectl get pods -A
<span class="comment"># Check no unexpected restarts or pending pods</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check no nodes are cordoned</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># Should NOT see SchedulingDisabled</span>
<span class="comment"># If seen: kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify version details</span>
kubectl version
<span class="comment"># Client Version: v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># Server Version: v1.31.0</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Post-upgrade verification checklist: (1) all nodes show new version, (2) all nodes are Ready, (3) system pods are Running, (4) cluster is healthy, (5) workloads are unaffected, (6) no nodes left cordoned. On the CKA, verifying after the upgrade is part of the task. The most common post-upgrade issue: worker nodes still cordoned (forgot <code>uncordon</code>) or kubelet not restarted.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to running workloads during a control plane upgrade?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># During kubeadm upgrade apply:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Running pods on worker nodes → UNAFFECTED</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Already-scheduled pods keep running</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ API server briefly unavailable (seconds)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Cannot create/delete/modify resources during restart</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ kubectl commands may timeout temporarily</span>

<span class="comment"># What restarts:</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver         → brief downtime</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager → auto-recovers</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler         → auto-recovers</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd                   → auto-recovers (if single node)</span>

<span class="comment"># What does NOT restart:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet (until you manually upgrade and restart it)</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy (DaemonSet, updated via rolling update)</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS (Deployment, updated via rolling update)</span>

<span class="comment"># Impact on workloads:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Existing pods: keep running (kubelet manages them)</span>
<span class="comment"># - New scheduling: paused during API server restart</span>
<span class="comment"># - Services/networking: unaffected (kube-proxy still runs)</span>
<span class="comment"># - DNS: unaffected (CoreDNS still runs)</span>

<span class="comment"># For zero-downtime upgrades:</span>
<span class="comment"># Use HA control plane (multiple API servers)</span>
<span class="comment"># Upgrade one control plane at a time</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Control plane upgrade causes brief API server downtime but does NOT affect running pods. Workers keep running independently. On the CKA, understand: existing pods survive, new scheduling pauses, kubectl commands fail briefly. For HA clusters, upgrade one control plane at a time for zero-downtime. The kubelet continues managing pods locally even without API server connectivity.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to upgrade a cluster but <code>apt-cache madison kubeadm</code> doesn't show the target version. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># The apt repository is versioned by minor release</span>
<span class="comment"># Each minor version has its own repo URL</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current repo:</span>
cat /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
<span class="comment"># deb https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.30/deb/ /</span>
<span class="comment">#                                        ^^^^ Current minor</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Update to target minor version repo:</span>
sudo vi /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
<span class="comment"># Change v1.30 → v1.31:</span>
<span class="comment"># deb https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.31/deb/ /</span>

<span class="comment"># OR use sed:</span>
sudo sed -i 's/v1.30/v1.31/' \
/etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list

<span class="comment"># Then update:</span>
sudo apt-get update

<span class="comment"># Now check again:</span>
apt-cache madison kubeadm
<span class="comment"># kubeadm | 1.31.0-1.1 | ... ← Now visible!</span>

<span class="comment"># Continue with normal upgrade:</span>
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.0-1.1</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Since Kubernetes 1.28+, packages are hosted in version-specific repositories. Each minor version (1.30, 1.31) has its own repo URL. If <code>madison</code> doesn't show the target version, the repo needs updating. On the CKA, the exam environment may already have the correct repo configured, but if not, you need to know how to update it. Always <code>apt-get update</code> after changing the repo.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 28 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch28 -->

        <article class="chapter" id="ch29">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 29 · etcd Backup &amp; Disaster Recovery</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">etcd is the <strong>single source of truth</strong> for your entire cluster. Every pod, service, secret, RBAC rule, and configuration lives in etcd. Losing etcd without a backup means losing your entire cluster state. This chapter covers the backup and restore procedures that are <strong>almost guaranteed</strong> to appear on the CKA exam.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">💾 etcd Backup</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Learning Objectives -->
            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Why etcd backup is critical</strong> — understand what's stored in etcd</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Perform etcd snapshot backup</strong> — memorize the 4 critical flags</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Restore from snapshot</strong> — full restore procedure with data-dir change</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Automate backups</strong> — create CronJob for scheduled etcd snapshots</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Find etcd certificates</strong> — locate cert paths from pod manifests</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- CKA Relevance -->
            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <h4>📋 CKA Exam Relevance</h4>
                <p><strong>Domain:</strong> Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration — <strong>25%</strong></p>
                <p><strong>Likelihood:</strong> Almost guaranteed to appear. etcd backup/restore is one of the most frequently tested topics on the CKA exam. You MUST practice this until you can do it in under 8 minutes.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h4>💡 CKA Exam Tip</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>The exam will tell you the backup path and may or may not give you cert paths</li>
                    <li>If cert paths aren't given, check the etcd pod: <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system</code></li>
                    <li>Always set <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> — version 2 uses different commands</li>
                    <li>After restore, you need to update the etcd manifest's <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <!-- CKA Exam Tip -->


            <!-- Imperative Reference -->
            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>⌨️ etcd Command Reference</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Action</th><th>Command</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Take snapshot</td><td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save &lt;path&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Verify snapshot</td><td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status &lt;path&gt; --write-table</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Restore snapshot</td><td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore &lt;path&gt; --data-dir=&lt;new-dir&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check etcd health</td><td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>List etcd members</td><td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Find cert paths</td><td><code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Check etcd pod</td><td><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=etcd</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>View etcd manifest</td><td><code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- Gotchas -->

            <!-- Quick Commands -->
            <div class="cka-kubectl-path">
                <h4>🚀 Quick Reference</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db --endpoints=... --cacert=... --cert=... --key=...</code></li>
                    <li><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup.db --write-table</code></li>
                    <li><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code></li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system | grep -A5 'Command\|--cert\|--key\|--cacert'</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

