            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 29.1 — Why etcd Backup is Critical                 -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §29.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s29-1">                    <h3 class="section-h">29.1 Why etcd Backup is Critical</h3>

                <p>etcd is a distributed key-value store that holds the <strong>entire state</strong> of your Kubernetes cluster. Without etcd, the API server has no data to serve — your cluster effectively doesn't exist.</p>

                <!-- What etcd stores diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">💾 What etcd Contains — Your Entire Cluster State</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                        etcd KEY-VALUE STORE                              │
│                   (Single Source of Truth)                                │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  📦 Workloads           🌐 Networking          🔐 Security              │
│  ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐       │
│  │ All Pods         │   │ All Services     │   │ All Secrets      │       │
│  │ All Deployments  │   │ All Ingresses    │   │ All RBAC Rules   │       │
│  │ All StatefulSets │   │ All NetworkPols  │   │ All ServiceAccts │       │
│  │ All DaemonSets   │   │ All Endpoints    │   │ All CertSignReqs │       │
│  │ All Jobs/CronJobs│   │ DNS Records      │   │ All ClusterRoles │       │
│  └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘       │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚙️ Configuration       📊 Storage            🏷️ Metadata              │
│  ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────┐       │
│  │ All ConfigMaps   │   │ PersistentVolumes│   │ Namespaces       │       │
│  │ All ResourceQuota│   │ PV Claims       │   │ Labels/Annotns   │       │
│  │ All LimitRanges  │   │ StorageClasses   │   │ Finalizers       │       │
│  │ Custom Resources │   │ VolumeAttach     │   │ OwnerReferences  │       │
│  │ Admission Config │   │ CSI Drivers      │   │ Resource Versions│       │
│  └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘   └─────────────────┘       │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️  LOSE etcd = LOSE EVERYTHING ABOVE                                  │
│  ✅  BACKUP etcd = CAN RESTORE ENTIRE CLUSTER TO THAT POINT IN TIME    │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Architecture Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🏗️ etcd in the Kubernetes Architecture</div>
                    <pre>
                    ┌─────────────────────────┐
                    │       kubectl            │
                    │   (User Commands)        │
                    └────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │ REST API
                                 ▼
                    ┌─────────────────────────┐
                    │      API Server          │
                    │  (kube-apiserver)         │
                    └────────────┬────────────┘
                                 │
                    ┌────────────┼────────────┐
                    │            │            │
                    ▼            ▼            ▼
              ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐
              │Controller│ │Scheduler │ │  etcd    │ ◀── THE BRAIN
              │ Manager  │ │          │ │          │     All state
              └──────────┘ └──────────┘ │  ┌────┐  │     lives here
                                        │  │ DB │  │
                                        │  └────┘  │
                                        └──────────┘
                                             │
                              ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
                              │              │              │
                        ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐
                        │  Node 1  │   │  Node 2  │   │  Node 3  │
                        │ kubelet  │   │ kubelet  │   │ kubelet  │
                        └──────────┘   └──────────┘   └──────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Real-World Disaster Scenarios</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Disk failure</strong> on the etcd node — all cluster state lost</li>
                        <li><strong>Accidental deletion</strong> — someone deletes all namespaces</li>
                        <li><strong>Botched upgrade</strong> — etcd data corrupted during cluster upgrade</li>
                        <li><strong>Security breach</strong> — attacker modifies RBAC rules and secrets</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>In every case, a recent etcd snapshot lets you restore the cluster to a known-good state.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 easypj — What's at Stake</h5>
                    <p>Your <strong>easypj</strong> job board has: 3 Deployment replicas, a Service, ConfigMaps with database URLs, Secrets with credentials, RBAC roles for the team, and NetworkPolicies. Without etcd backup, a disk failure means manually recreating ALL of this from scratch — assuming you even remember every configuration.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 29.2 — etcd Backup                                 -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §29.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s29-2">                    <h3 class="section-h">29.2 etcd Backup</h3>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>

                <p>The backup uses <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code> and requires <strong>4 critical flags</strong> for TLS authentication. Memorize these flags — you'll need them on the exam.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔗</div>
                        <h5>--endpoints</h5>
                        <p>Where etcd listens for connections.<br><code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code><br><em>From: --listen-client-urls in pod spec</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>--cacert</h5>
                        <p>CA certificate for TLS verification.<br><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</code><br><em>From: --trusted-ca-file in pod spec</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📜</div>
                        <h5>--cert</h5>
                        <p>Server TLS certificate.<br><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code><br><em>From: --cert-file in pod spec</em></p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔑</div>
                        <h5>--key</h5>
                        <p>Server private key.<br><code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code><br><em>From: --key-file in pod spec</em></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- The 4 Critical Flags Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🔑 The 4 Critical Flags — MEMORIZE THESE</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│              etcd BACKUP — THE 4 REQUIRED TLS FLAGS                      │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  Flag              │ Purpose                  │ Default Path             │
│  ──────────────────┼──────────────────────────┼────────────────────────  │
│  --endpoints       │ Where etcd listens       │ https://127.0.0.1:2379  │
│  --cacert          │ CA certificate           │ /etc/kubernetes/pki/     │
│                    │                          │   etcd/ca.crt            │
│  --cert            │ Server certificate       │ /etc/kubernetes/pki/     │
│                    │                          │   etcd/server.crt        │
│  --key             │ Server private key       │ /etc/kubernetes/pki/     │
│                    │                          │   etcd/server.key        │
│                                                                          │
│  💡 TIP: Check actual paths from etcd pod:                               │
│  $ kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system                │
│  Look under Command: for --cert-file, --key-file, --trusted-ca-file    │
│                                                                          │
│  Certificate Directory Structure:                                        │
│  /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/                                               │
│  ├── ca.crt          ← --cacert (--trusted-ca-file in pod)              │
│  ├── ca.key                                                              │
│  ├── server.crt      ← --cert   (--cert-file in pod)                    │
│  ├── server.key      ← --key    (--key-file in pod)                     │
│  ├── peer.crt                                                            │
│  ├── peer.key                                                            │
│  ├── healthcheck-client.crt                                              │
│  └── healthcheck-client.key                                              │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Finding Certificate Paths -->
                <h4>Step 1: Find Certificate Paths</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">find-etcd-certs.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Method 1: From the etcd pod spec</span>
kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Look for these lines in the Command section:</span>
<span class="comment"># --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</span>
<span class="comment"># --trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --listen-client-urls=https://127.0.0.1:2379,...</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: From the etcd static pod manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca|listen-client'

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Quick grep for just the values</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system -o yaml | grep -E 'cert|key|ca\.' </code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Backup Command -->
                <h4>Step 2: Take the Snapshot</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">etcd-backup.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Take etcd snapshot backup</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># ✅ Output: Snapshot saved at /opt/etcd-backup.db</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Verify Backup -->
                <h4>Step 3: Verify the Backup</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">verify-backup.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify the snapshot is valid</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db --write-table

<span class="comment"># Example output:</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | 5d16a713 |    38504 |       1234 |     2.1 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>

<span class="comment"># Also check file exists and has reasonable size</span>
ls -lh /opt/etcd-backup.db</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Mapping table: pod args to etcdctl flags -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 Mapping: etcd Pod Arguments → etcdctl Flags</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>etcd Pod Argument</th>
                                <th>etcdctl Flag</th>
                                <th>What It Is</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>--listen-client-urls</code></td>
                                <td><code>--endpoints</code></td>
                                <td>Where etcd accepts client connections</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>--trusted-ca-file</code></td>
                                <td><code>--cacert</code></td>
                                <td>CA certificate for verification</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>--cert-file</code></td>
                                <td><code>--cert</code></td>
                                <td>Server TLS certificate</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>--key-file</code></td>
                                <td><code>--key</code></td>
                                <td>Server TLS private key</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Exam Strategy: Build the Command Step by Step</h5>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Start with: <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /path/backup.db</code></li>
                        <li>Run: <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system</code></li>
                        <li>Find the 3 cert paths from the Command section</li>
                        <li>Find the endpoint from <code>--listen-client-urls</code></li>
                        <li>Append all 4 flags to your command</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>

                <h4>Backup Procedure — Quick Process</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Find Certs</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe pod etcd-* -n kube-system</code> — Locate <code>--trusted-ca-file</code>, <code>--cert-file</code>, <code>--key-file</code>, and <code>--listen-client-urls</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Take Snapshot</h5>
                        <p><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save &lt;path&gt;</code> with all 4 TLS flags. Snapshot is written to disk immediately.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Verify Backup</h5>
                        <p><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status &lt;path&gt; --write-table</code> — Confirm HASH, REVISION, TOTAL KEYS, and SIZE are valid.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 29.3 — etcd Restore                                -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §29.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s29-3">                    <h3 class="section-h">29.3 etcd Restore</h3>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>

                <p>Restoring from a snapshot is a <strong>multi-step process</strong>. You restore to a new data directory, then update the etcd static pod manifest to point to it. The kubelet detects the manifest change and restarts etcd with the restored data.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>💾 Backup Command</h5>
                        <p><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db</code></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Needs <strong>4 TLS flags</strong> (endpoints, cacert, cert, key)</li>
                            <li>Creates a single snapshot file</li>
                            <li>Non-disruptive (cluster keeps running)</li>
                            <li>No manifest changes needed</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>🔄 Restore Command</h5>
                        <p><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db --data-dir=&lt;new&gt;</code></p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>No TLS flags needed</strong> (works locally)</li>
                            <li>Must restore to a <strong>NEW</strong> directory</li>
                            <li><strong>Requires manifest changes</strong> (data-dir + hostPath)</li>
                            <li>Causes etcd pod restart (~30-60s downtime)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Restore Flow Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🔄 etcd Restore — Complete Flow</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    etcd SNAPSHOT RESTORE PROCEDURE                        │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  Step 1: RESTORE snapshot to NEW directory                               │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \   │   │
│  │     --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                             │   │
│  │                                                                    │   │
│  │  /var/lib/etcd           /var/lib/etcd-restored                   │   │
│  │  ├── member/    (OLD)    ├── member/    (RESTORED)                │   │
│  │  └── ...                 └── ...                                   │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 2: EDIT etcd static pod manifest                                   │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml                          │   │
│  │                                                                    │   │
│  │ Change TWO things:                                                 │   │
│  │                                                                    │   │
│  │ a) Command argument:                                               │   │
│  │    --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd                                        │   │
│  │              ↓                                                     │   │
│  │    --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                               │   │
│  │                                                                    │   │
│  │ b) Volume hostPath:                                                │   │
│  │    hostPath:                                                       │   │
│  │      path: /var/lib/etcd                                           │   │
│  │              ↓                                                     │   │
│  │    hostPath:                                                       │   │
│  │      path: /var/lib/etcd-restored                                  │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 3: WAIT for etcd pod to restart                                    │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ kubelet detects manifest change → restarts etcd pod               │   │
│  │ Wait 30-60 seconds, then verify:                                  │   │
│  │                                                                    │   │
│  │ $ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=etcd               │   │
│  │ $ kubectl get all --all-namespaces                                │   │
│  │                                                                    │   │
│  │ ✅ All previous resources should be back!                         │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Restore Commands -->
                <h4>Step 1: Restore the Snapshot</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">etcd-restore.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Restore snapshot to a NEW data directory</span>
<span class="comment"># ⚠️ Do NOT restore to /var/lib/etcd — use a NEW path!</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># Verify the restored directory exists</span>
ls -la /var/lib/etcd-restored/
<span class="comment"># Should show: member/ directory</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 2: Update the etcd Static Pod Manifest</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Edit the etcd manifest:</span>
<span class="comment"># $ vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># CHANGE 1: Update the --data-dir argument</span>
spec:
  containers:
  - command:
    - etcd
    - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored     # ← Changed from /var/lib/etcd
    - --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt
    <span class="comment"># ... other flags unchanged ...</span>

<span class="comment"># CHANGE 2: Update the hostPath volume mount</span>
  volumes:
  - hostPath:
      path: /var/lib/etcd-restored           # ← Changed from /var/lib/etcd
      type: DirectoryOrCreate
    name: etcd-data</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 3: Wait and Verify</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">verify-restore.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Wait for etcd pod to restart (kubelet watches manifest dir)</span>
<span class="comment"># This can take 30-60 seconds</span>

<span class="comment"># Watch etcd pod come back</span>
watch kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=etcd

<span class="comment"># Once etcd is running, verify cluster state is restored</span>
kubectl get all --all-namespaces
kubectl get nodes

<span class="comment"># Verify the easypj deployment is back</span>
kubectl get deployments -n default
<span class="comment"># Should show your easypj deployment with 3 replicas</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Exam Trap: The Two Changes You Must Make</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Change</th><th>Where</th><th>What to Change</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>1. Command arg</strong></td>
                                <td><code>spec.containers[0].command</code></td>
                                <td><code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</code> → <code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>2. Volume hostPath</strong></td>
                                <td><code>spec.volumes[0].hostPath.path</code></td>
                                <td><code>/var/lib/etcd</code> → <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p>If you only change the <code>--data-dir</code> but not the <code>hostPath</code>, etcd will mount the <strong>old</strong> data directory into the container and ignore your restored data!</p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Complete Restore — Copy-Paste Block</h5>
                    <p>Full restore procedure in one block:</p>
                </div>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">full-etcd-restore.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">#!/bin/bash
<span class="comment"># === COMPLETE etcd RESTORE PROCEDURE ===</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Restore snapshot to new directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># 2. Update etcd manifest (both --data-dir and hostPath)</span>
<span class="comment"># Using sed to make both changes:</span>
sed -i 's|path: /var/lib/etcd$|path: /var/lib/etcd-restored|' \
  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
sed -i 's|--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd$|--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored|' \
  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># 3. Wait for etcd to restart (kubelet detects manifest change)</span>
sleep 30

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=etcd
kubectl get all --all-namespaces</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 29.4 — etcd Backup Automation                      -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §29.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s29-4">                    <h3 class="section-h">29.4 etcd Backup Automation</h3>

                <p>In production, you should automate etcd backups using a CronJob or external tooling. Here's a Kubernetes CronJob that takes automatic etcd snapshots on a schedule.</p>

                <!-- CronJob YAML -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">etcd-backup-cronjob.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: etcd-backup
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  schedule: "0 */6 * * *"          <span class="comment"># Every 6 hours</span>
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          hostNetwork: true
          containers:
          - name: etcd-backup
            image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.12-0
            command:
            - /bin/sh
            - -c
            - |
              ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save \
                /backup/etcd-snapshot-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).db \
                --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
                --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
                --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
                --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
            volumeMounts:
            - name: etcd-certs
              mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
              readOnly: true
            - name: backup-dir
              mountPath: /backup
          volumes:
          - name: etcd-certs
            hostPath:
              path: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
          - name: backup-dir
            hostPath:
              path: /opt/etcd-backups
              type: DirectoryOrCreate
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          nodeSelector:
            node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: ""
          tolerations:
          - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
            effect: NoSchedule</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Backup Checklist -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>✅ etcd Backup Best Practices Checklist</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Practice</th><th>Why</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>✅ Backup before every cluster upgrade</td><td>Rollback point if upgrade fails</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Store backups externally (not on same node)</td><td>Node failure won't destroy backups too</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Test restore procedure regularly</td><td>Untested backups are unreliable backups</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Document backup/restore steps for team</td><td>Anyone on-call should be able to restore</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Include timestamp in backup filename</td><td>Identify which backup to restore from</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>✅ Verify backup with <code>snapshot status</code></td><td>Confirm backup is not corrupted</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- etcd Restore Troubleshooting -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>🛠️ etcd Restore Troubleshooting — Common Failures</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Problem</th>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>etcd pod won't start after restore</strong></td>
                                <td><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> for etcd pod</td>
                                <td>Check BOTH <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath.path</code> in manifest point to restored directory</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Forgot to update hostPath</strong></td>
                                <td>etcd starts but uses old data (restore appears to fail)</td>
                                <td>Edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> — change <code>volumes[].hostPath.path</code> too</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Permission denied on restored dir</strong></td>
                                <td><code>permission denied</code> in etcd logs</td>
                                <td><code>chown -R root:root /var/lib/etcd-restored</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>kubectl not responding after restore</strong></td>
                                <td>All <code>kubectl</code> commands hang or timeout</td>
                                <td>Wait 30-60s for kubelet to detect manifest change — use <code>crictl ps | grep etcd</code> instead</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Wrong backup file used</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster restored but resources are from wrong point in time</td>
                                <td>Verify backup with <code>etcdctl snapshot status</code> before restoring — check revision count</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>etcd restore command fails</strong></td>
                                <td><code>Error: data-dir already exists</code></td>
                                <td>Always restore to a NEW directory — never overwrite <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> directly</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Exam Quick Decision Flowchart -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🎯 CKA Exam: etcd Backup/Restore Decision Flowchart</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 etcd BACKUP/RESTORE — EXAM DECISION TREE                 │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  Question says "backup etcd"?                                            │
│  ┌──── YES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │
│  │ 1. Find cert paths:                                            │      │
│  │    kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system       │      │
│  │    Look for: --trusted-ca-file, --cert-file, --key-file        │      │
│  │    Also check: --listen-client-urls for endpoint               │      │
│  │                                                                │      │
│  │ 2. Take snapshot:                                              │      │
│  │    ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /path/backup.db \       │      │
│  │      --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \                     │      │
│  │      --cacert=&lt;ca-path&gt; --cert=&lt;cert-path&gt; --key=&lt;key-path&gt;   │      │
│  │                                                                │      │
│  │ 3. Verify:                                                     │      │
│  │    ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /path/backup.db       │      │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │
│                                                                          │
│  Question says "restore etcd"?                                           │
│  ┌──── YES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │
│  │ 1. Restore to NEW directory:                                   │      │
│  │    ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /path/backup.db \    │      │
│  │      --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                        │      │
│  │                                                                │      │
│  │ 2. Edit manifest (TWO changes!):                               │      │
│  │    vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml                      │      │
│  │    Change: --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                  │      │
│  │    Change: hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd-restored              │      │
│  │                                                                │      │
│  │ 3. Wait and verify:                                            │      │
│  │    Watch: crictl ps | grep etcd (don't rely on kubectl yet)   │      │
│  │    Then: kubectl get all --all-namespaces                      │      │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️ REMEMBER: etcdctl needs NO certs for restore — only for backup!    │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- etcd Data Contents Visual -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">📦 What's Inside an etcd Backup — Everything Kubernetes Knows</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  etcd BACKUP CONTAINS ALL CLUSTER STATE                  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  /registry/                                                              │
│  ├── deployments/                                                        │
│  │   ├── default/easypj          ← Your Deployment specs                │
│  │   └── kube-system/coredns     ← System Deployments                  │
│  ├── services/                                                           │
│  │   ├── default/easypj-svc      ← Your Service configs                │
│  │   └── default/kubernetes      ← Cluster API service                  │
│  ├── pods/                                                               │
│  │   └── (pod specs, not running state)                                 │
│  ├── configmaps/                                                         │
│  │   ├── default/easypj-config   ← Your ConfigMaps                     │
│  │   └── kube-system/kubeadm-config                                     │
│  ├── secrets/                                                            │
│  │   ├── default/easypj-secret   ← Your Secrets (encrypted)            │
│  │   └── (service account tokens, TLS certs)                            │
│  ├── namespaces/                  ← All namespace definitions           │
│  ├── roles/ &amp; rolebindings/     ← All RBAC policies                    │
│  ├── persistentvolumes/           ← PV definitions (not data!)          │
│  ├── nodes/                       ← Node registrations                  │
│  └── ...every API object          ← CRDs, NetworkPolicies, etc.        │
│                                                                          │
│  ❌ NOT in etcd backup:                                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ • Container images (stored in registry)                          │    │
│  │ • Persistent Volume DATA (stored on disk/cloud)                  │    │
│  │ • Container logs (stored on nodes)                               │    │
│  │ • Node OS state (packages, configs)                              │    │
│  │ • Running container state (only desired state is in etcd)        │    │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 29.6 — Practice Exercises                          -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill" id="s29-6">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 29.1 — Backup etcd</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Take a snapshot backup of etcd and save it to <code>/opt/etcd-backup-drill.db</code>. The etcd server is running on <code>https://127.0.0.1:2379</code> with certificates in the default location under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Determine the correct certificate file paths from the etcd pod</li>
                        <li>Take the snapshot using etcdctl with all 4 required flags</li>
                        <li>Verify the snapshot is valid</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Use <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system</code> to find cert paths. Remember to set <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> and include <code>--endpoints</code>, <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Find certificate paths</span>
kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system | grep -E 'cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca'

<span class="comment"># Take backup with all 4 flags</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup-drill.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup-drill.db --write-table</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill" id="s29-drill-2">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 29.2 — Restore etcd from Backup</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 6 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>A backup file exists at <code>/opt/etcd-backup-drill.db</code>. Some critical resources have been accidentally deleted. Restore the cluster from this backup.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Restore the snapshot to a new data directory <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code></li>
                        <li>Update the etcd static pod manifest to use the new data directory</li>
                        <li>Wait for the etcd pod to restart and verify the cluster is restored</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> You need to change TWO things in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>: the <code>--data-dir</code> command argument AND the <code>hostPath.path</code> volume. Both must point to the new directory.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Restore snapshot to new directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup-drill.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># 2. Update etcd manifest — change BOTH --data-dir and hostPath</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># Change: --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd → --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment"># Change: hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd → hostPath.path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Wait for etcd pod to restart (30-60 seconds)</span>
watch kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=etcd

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify cluster state is restored</span>
kubectl get all --all-namespaces
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill" id="s29-drill-3">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 29.3 — Create an Automated Backup CronJob</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 8 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Create a CronJob named <code>etcd-backup</code> in the <code>kube-system</code> namespace that takes an etcd snapshot every 6 hours. The backup should be saved to <code>/opt/etcd-backups/</code> on the control plane node with a timestamp in the filename.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Write a CronJob YAML with the correct schedule (<code>0 */6 * * *</code>)</li>
                        <li>Mount etcd certificates and the backup directory as volumes</li>
                        <li>Use the <code>registry.k8s.io/etcd</code> image with the snapshot save command</li>
                        <li>Add nodeSelector and tolerations for the control plane</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> The CronJob needs <code>hostNetwork: true</code> to reach etcd on localhost, <code>nodeSelector</code> for control-plane, and tolerations for the control-plane taint. Mount <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd</code> as read-only for certs.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: etcd-backup
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  schedule: "0 */6 * * *"
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      template:
        spec:
          hostNetwork: true
          containers:
          - name: backup
            image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.12-0
            command: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
            args:
            - |
              ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save \
                /backup/snapshot-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).db \
                --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
                --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
                --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
                --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
            volumeMounts:
            - name: certs
              mountPath: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
              readOnly: true
            - name: backup
              mountPath: /backup
          volumes:
          - name: certs
            hostPath:
              path: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
          - name: backup
            hostPath:
              path: /opt/etcd-backups
              type: DirectoryOrCreate
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          nodeSelector:
            node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: ""
          tolerations:
          - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
            effect: NoSchedule</code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Chapter 29 Verification Checklist -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>✅ Chapter 29 — Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup.db --write-table</code> — Backup is valid (shows hash, revision, keys, size)</li>
                    <li><code>ls -lh /var/lib/etcd-restored/member/</code> — Restored data directory exists after restore</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=etcd</code> — etcd pod is Running after restore</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get all --all-namespaces</code> — All resources restored to backup state</li>
                    <li><code>grep data-dir /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> — Manifest points to correct data directory</li>
                    <li><code>crictl ps | grep etcd</code> — etcd container is running (alternative to kubectl during restore)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 29 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: etcd Backup &amp; Restore Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>3 flags are REQUIRED for etcdctl snapshot save</strong> — <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code>. Without all three, the command fails. Find them from: <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system</code> or from <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> must be set</strong> — Either export it or prefix every command. Without it, etcdctl defaults to API v2 which uses different syntax and fails silently</li>
                        <li><strong>Restore creates a NEW data directory</strong> — <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> uses <code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>. You must then update the etcd manifest's <code>--data-dir</code> AND the <code>hostPath</code> volume to point to the new directory</li>
                        <li><strong>After restore, etcd restarts automatically</strong> — The kubelet watches <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. Changing <code>etcd.yaml</code> triggers a restart. Wait 30-60 seconds before verifying</li>
                        <li><strong>Backup path matters</strong> — The exam specifies WHERE to save the backup (e.g., <code>/opt/cluster-backup.db</code>). Use the exact path given or you lose marks</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>


                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <strong>⚡ etcd commands:</strong>
                    <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save &lt;path&gt;</code> — take backup<br>
                    <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status &lt;path&gt; --write-table</code> — verify backup integrity<br>
                    <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore &lt;path&gt; --data-dir=&lt;new-dir&gt;</code> — restore<br>
                    <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list</code> — list etcd cluster members<br>
                    <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system | grep -A5 Command</code> — find cert paths
                </div>


                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 29 — How etcd Backup and Restore Actually Works ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 29 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How etcd Backup and Restore Actually Works: Snapshot Internals, Restoration Mechanics, and Disaster Recovery Scenarios</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ etcd Snapshot Internals — What's Inside a Backup File</h5>
            <p>An etcd snapshot is NOT just a file copy. It's a point-in-time, consistent dump of the entire key space that resets cluster membership — allowing the backup to be restored to a completely different cluster.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ETCD SNAPSHOT INTERNALS                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  $ etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  SNAPSHOT FILE CONTENTS:                                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  /opt/backup.db (single file, SQLite/BoltDB format)                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │  HEADER:                                                     │      │   │
│  │  │  • etcd version (e.g., 3.5.x)                               │      │   │
│  │  │  • Cluster ID (unique to this cluster)                       │      │   │
│  │  │  • Member ID                                                │      │   │
│  │  │  • Snapshot revision (e.g., 105432)                          │      │   │
│  │  │  • Total key count                                          │      │   │
│  │  │  • Total size                                               │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │  KEY SPACE (ALL keys at snapshot revision):                  │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/namespaces/default            → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/namespaces/easypj-prod        → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/pods/default/nginx            → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/deployments/easypj-dev/web    → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/services/easypj-dev/web-svc   → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  /registry/secrets/easypj-dev/db-creds   → {json}            │      │   │
│  │  │  ... (ALL other K8s objects)                                  │      │   │
│  │  │  ... (ALL leases, endpoints, events, etc.)                    │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  RESTORE MECHANICS — What Actually Happens:                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  $ etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db \                          │   │
│  │      --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  RESTORE DOES:                                                         │   │
│  │  1. Creates NEW BoltDB from snapshot data                             │   │
│  │  2. Creates NEW WAL files                                             │   │
│  │  3. Creates NEW member directory                                       │   │
│  │  4. RESETS cluster membership (new Cluster ID, new Member ID)          │   │
│  │  5. Makes this member the SINGLE member of a new cluster               │   │
│  │  6. Does NOT touch existing /var/lib/etcd/data                         │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  AFTER RESTORE, YOU MUST:                                              │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │ 1. EDIT etcd.yaml static pod manifest:                      │      │   │
│  │  │       --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored  <span class="comment">← CHANGE PATH     </span>│      │   │
│  │  │                                                            │      │   │
│  │  │ 2. UPDATE volume mount in etcd.yaml:                       │      │   │
│  │  │       volumes:                                              │      │   │
│  │  │       - hostPath:                                           │      │   │
│  │  │           path: /var/lib/etcd-restored  <span class="comment">← CHANGE PATH     </span>│      │   │
│  │  │                                                            │      │   │
│  │  │ 3. WAIT for kubelet to detect manifest change               │      │   │
│  │  │    → kubelet restarts etcd with NEW data-dir               │      │   │
│  │  │    → etcd starts as single-node cluster                     │      │   │
│  │  │                                                            │      │   │
│  │  │ 4. VERIFY pods are back:                                    │      │   │
│  │  │    kubectl get pods -A (may take 30-60 seconds)            │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  DISASTER RECOVERY — When ALL etcd Is Lost:                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Scenario: All 3 etcd nodes corrupted, no quorum possible              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ Recovery (requires backup):                                            │   │
│  │  1. Stop all etcd instances                                            │   │
│  │  2. Pick ONE node to restore to                                        │   │
│  │  3. Restore snapshot to a fresh data-dir on that node                  │   │
│  │  4. Update etcd.yaml manifest on THAT node                             │   │
│  │  5. Start etcd → it runs as SINGLE-node cluster                       │   │
│  │  6. API Server connects → cluster is back (from snapshot point)       │   │
│  │  7. Rebuild remaining etcd nodes from this single member               │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ⚠️  ALL changes after the snapshot time are LOST!                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> etcd backup/restore is guaranteed on the CKA. The restore changes the cluster to a single-node etcd — all HA control plane nodes except the restore target must be removed from the etcd cluster. After restore, you MUST update --data-dir in BOTH the command AND the hostPath volume mount in etcd.yaml. Forgetting the volume mount is the #1 mistake — etcd starts but uses the old (empty) data directory.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>❌ Wrong</th><th>✅ Correct</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Forgetting <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></td><td>Always prefix with <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> or <code>export ETCDCTL_API=3</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Missing any of the 4 cert flags</td><td>Include <code>--endpoints</code>, <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Restoring to the same <code>--data-dir</code></td><td>Restore to a NEW directory, then update etcd manifest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Using wrong cert paths</td><td>Check <code>kubectl describe pod etcd-*</code> for actual paths</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Not waiting for etcd pod restart</td><td>After manifest change, wait for kubelet to restart etcd</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Forgetting to update hostPath volume</td><td>Both <code>--data-dir</code> arg AND <code>hostPath</code> must point to new dir</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — etcd backup one-liner (memorize):</strong><br>
                <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup.db \<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \<br>
                &nbsp;&nbsp;--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code><br>
                <strong>Restore:</strong><br>
                <code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup.db --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code><br>
                Then edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> → change <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath.path</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-commands"></span>
