            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 29 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch29-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 29 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (11)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of etcd backup/restore mechanics — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Take a snapshot of etcd and save it to <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code>. Show the complete command with all required flags.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Find cert paths from etcd pod:</span>
kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system | \
grep -E "cert|key|cacert"
<span class="comment"># --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</span>
<span class="comment"># --trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</span>

<span class="comment"># OR from the manifest:</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | \
grep -E "cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca"

<span class="comment"># Take the snapshot:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--write-table
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |  HASH  | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | abc123 |    14500 |       1200 |     4.1 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>

ls -la /opt/etcd-backup.db
<span class="comment"># -rw------- 1 root root 4194304 ...</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three flags are REQUIRED: <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, <code>--key</code>. Without all three, etcdctl fails with connection errors. Find the paths from the etcd pod manifest at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>. Always set <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> (v2 uses different syntax). On the CKA, use the EXACT path specified in the question for the backup file. <code>--write-table</code> gives a readable verification output.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Restore an etcd snapshot from <code>/opt/etcd-backup.db</code>. Show the full restore process including manifest updates.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Restore to NEW directory</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Update etcd manifest</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># Change 1: --data-dir flag</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM: --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># TO:   --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># Change 2: hostPath volume</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#       path: /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># TO:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#       path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Wait for etcd to restart (~30-60 seconds)</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects manifest change and recreates the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># etcd-controlplane  1/1  Running  0  30s</span>

kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A
<span class="comment"># Cluster should be in the state from backup time</span>

<span class="comment"># COMMON MISTAKES:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Forgetting to update BOTH --data-dir AND hostPath</span>
<span class="comment"># - Using the old /var/lib/etcd path (data is stale)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not waiting long enough for restart</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Restore creates a NEW data directory — never restore in-place to <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>. You MUST update TWO things in the etcd manifest: (1) the <code>--data-dir</code> command flag and (2) the <code>hostPath.path</code> volume. On the CKA, this is a HIGH-WEIGHT question. The kubelet auto-detects the manifest change and recreates the etcd pod. Wait 30-60 seconds before verifying. Resources created AFTER the backup are gone after restore.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Where do you find the etcd certificate file paths on a kubeadm cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: From etcd static pod manifest (FASTEST):</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | \
grep -E "cert-file|key-file|trusted-ca"
<span class="comment"># --cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</span>
<span class="comment"># --trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --peer-cert-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/peer.crt</span>
<span class="comment"># --peer-key-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/peer.key</span>
<span class="comment"># --peer-trusted-ca-file=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: From running pod:</span>
kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system | \
grep -A5 Command

<span class="comment"># Method 3: From process:</span>
ps aux | grep etcd | grep -oP 'cert-file=\S+'

<span class="comment"># Mapping to etcdctl flags:</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd flag:          → etcdctl flag:</span>
<span class="comment"># --cert-file         → --cert</span>
<span class="comment"># --key-file          → --key</span>
<span class="comment"># --trusted-ca-file   → --cacert</span>

<span class="comment"># Default paths (kubeadm):</span>
<span class="comment"># /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt      → --cacert</span>
<span class="comment"># /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt  → --cert</span>
<span class="comment"># /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key  → --key</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The etcd manifest at <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> is the fastest source. Note: etcd uses different flag names than etcdctl — <code>--cert-file</code> (etcd) maps to <code>--cert</code> (etcdctl). On the CKA, they may NOT give you the cert paths — you must find them yourself. The manifest method works even when kubectl is unavailable (API server down). Default kubeadm paths are always under <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify that an etcd backup is valid and not corrupted?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: snapshot status (recommended)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--write-table
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |  HASH  | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | 7c2...  |    15234 |       1456 |     5.2 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># ← Hash, revision, key count, size all shown</span>

<span class="comment"># If corrupted:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /bad-backup.db
<span class="comment"># Error: expected sha256 ...</span>
<span class="comment"># ← Hash mismatch = corrupted backup!</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: JSON output for scripting</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--write-table=false
<span class="comment"># Shows: hash, revision, totalKey, totalSize</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: File size check</span>
ls -la /opt/etcd-backup.db
<span class="comment"># Should be non-zero (typically several MB)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Dry-run restore</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup.db \
--data-dir=/tmp/etcd-test
ls /tmp/etcd-test/
<span class="comment"># member/  ← Restore succeeded</span>
rm -rf /tmp/etcd-test  <span class="comment"># Cleanup</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>etcdctl snapshot status</code> verifies the backup's integrity hash. If the hash doesn't match, the backup is corrupted. On the CKA, always verify after taking a backup — the exam may ask you to confirm the backup's validity. The <code>--write-table</code> flag gives human-readable output. A zero-size file or hash mismatch means you need to retake the backup.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The etcd pod is not starting after a restore. What should you check?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check if manifest was updated correctly</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep data-dir
<span class="comment"># --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored ← Correct?</span>

cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml | grep -A2 hostPath
<span class="comment"># hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#   path: /var/lib/etcd-restored ← Must match --data-dir!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the restored directory exists</span>
ls -la /var/lib/etcd-restored/
<span class="comment"># member/ ← Should exist</span>

<span class="comment"># Check ownership (must be root or etcd user):</span>
ls -la /var/lib/ | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># drwx------ 3 root root etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check kubelet logs</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -l | grep etcd | tail -20

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check container status</span>
crictl ps -a | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># If restarting: check crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Common issues after restore:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. hostPath not updated → etcd starts with old data</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. --data-dir not updated → path mismatch, crash</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Permission denied → chown the restored directory</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Both changes not saved → yaml syntax error</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. Wrong restore path → directory doesn't exist</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix example:</span>
sudo chown -R root:root /var/lib/etcd-restored
sudo chmod 700 /var/lib/etcd-restored</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Post-restore etcd failures: (1) <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath</code> mismatch — BOTH must point to the restored directory, (2) restored directory doesn't exist — re-run the restore, (3) permission issues — chown to root, (4) YAML syntax error in the manifest — validate the file. On the CKA, the most common mistake is updating only one of the two settings. Always verify both the command flag AND the volume path.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check etcd cluster health and list members?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># List etcd members:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
--write-out=table
<span class="comment"># +--------+---------+-----------+-------------------+-------------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   ID   | STATUS  |   NAME    |    PEER ADDRS     |   CLIENT ADDRS    |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+---------+-----------+-------------------+-------------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | abc123 | started | controlplane | https://10.0.0.1:2380 | https://10.0.0.1:2379 |</span>
<span class="comment"># +--------+---------+-----------+-------------------+-------------------+</span>

<span class="comment"># Check endpoint health:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># https://127.0.0.1:2379 is healthy: committed proposal: took = 2ms</span>

<span class="comment"># Endpoint status (detailed):</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
--write-out=table
<span class="comment"># Shows: endpoint, ID, version, db size, is leader, raft term/index</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>member list</code> shows cluster membership, <code>endpoint health</code> checks if etcd is responding, <code>endpoint status</code> shows detailed info including leader status. All require the same 3 cert flags. On the CKA, these commands help verify etcd is healthy after a restore or during troubleshooting. In multi-node etcd clusters, prefix with <code>--endpoints=</code> to check specific members.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>--endpoints</code> and <code>--advertise-client-urls</code> in etcd?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code># --endpoints (client-side, used with etcdctl):
<span class="comment"># Tells etcdctl WHERE to connect</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl \
--endpoints=https://10.0.0.1:2379 \
snapshot save /opt/backup.db \
--cacert=... --cert=... --key=...

<span class="comment"># Default endpoint: https://127.0.0.1:2379</span>
<span class="comment"># For multi-node etcd:</span>
<span class="comment"># --endpoints=https://10.0.0.1:2379,https://10.0.0.2:2379</span>

<span class="comment"># --advertise-client-urls (server-side, etcd config):</span>
<span class="comment"># Tells etcd what URL to advertise to clients</span>
<span class="comment"># Found in: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># --advertise-client-urls=https://10.0.0.1:2379</span>

<span class="comment"># --listen-client-urls (server-side):</span>
<span class="comment"># What addresses etcd actually listens on</span>
<span class="comment"># --listen-client-urls=https://127.0.0.1:2379,https://10.0.0.1:2379</span>

<span class="comment"># On the CKA exam:</span>
<span class="comment"># You usually DON'T need --endpoints (localhost works)</span>
<span class="comment"># The default https://127.0.0.1:2379 is fine when</span>
<span class="comment"># running etcdctl on the control plane node</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--endpoints</code> is a CLIENT-side flag (used with etcdctl) specifying where to connect. <code>--advertise-client-urls</code> is a SERVER-side config (in etcd.yaml) specifying what URL to advertise. On the CKA, you usually run etcdctl on the control plane, so the default localhost endpoint works. You only need <code>--endpoints</code> if connecting remotely or to a specific member in a multi-node etcd cluster.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you find the etcd <code>--data-dir</code> path on a running cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: From static pod manifest (FASTEST)</span>
grep data-dir /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: From running pod</span>
kubectl describe pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system | \
grep data-dir
<span class="comment"># --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: From process</span>
ps aux | grep etcd | grep -oP 'data-dir=\S+'
<span class="comment"># data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: From etcd pod volumes</span>
kubectl get pod etcd-controlplane -n kube-system \
-o jsonpath='{.spec.volumes[?(@.name=="etcd-data")].hostPath.path}'
<span class="comment"># /var/lib/etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># Check actual data directory size:</span>
sudo du -sh /var/lib/etcd
<span class="comment"># 48M /var/lib/etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># List contents:</span>
sudo ls -la /var/lib/etcd/
<span class="comment"># member/</span>
<span class="comment">#   snap/</span>
<span class="comment">#     db        ← The actual etcd database</span>
<span class="comment">#   wal/        ← Write-ahead log</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>--data-dir</code> tells etcd where to store its data. Default is <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>. On the CKA, you need this path for restore operations — the restored data goes to a NEW directory, then you update the manifest to point there. The fastest method is <code>grep data-dir /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>. The <code>member/snap/db</code> file is the actual BoltDB database.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What happens to the cluster state when you restore an older etcd backup?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Scenario:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Take backup at 10:00 AM</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. At 10:30 AM: create ns "test-ns", deploy app</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. At 11:00 AM: restore 10:00 AM backup</span>

<span class="comment"># After restore:</span>
kubectl get ns test-ns
<span class="comment"># Error: namespaces "test-ns" not found ← GONE!</span>

kubectl get deploy -n test-ns
<span class="comment"># Error: namespace not found ← GONE!</span>

<span class="comment"># EVERYTHING created AFTER the backup is lost:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Namespaces created after backup</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ Pods, Deployments, Services created after backup</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ ConfigMaps, Secrets created after backup</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ RBAC changes made after backup</span>
<span class="comment"># ✗ CRDs and custom resources created after backup</span>

<span class="comment"># EVERYTHING from BEFORE the backup is restored:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Namespaces that existed at backup time</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ All resources from backup time</span>
<span class="comment"># ✓ Cluster configuration from backup time</span>

<span class="comment"># Key insight:</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd backup = COMPLETE cluster state snapshot</span>
<span class="comment"># Restore = TIME TRAVEL to that exact moment</span>
<span class="comment"># This is why regular backups are critical</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Restoring an etcd backup reverts the ENTIRE cluster state to the backup moment. Everything created after the backup is lost — pods, namespaces, secrets, RBAC rules, everything. On the CKA, they verify this by asking you to create a resource after backup, restore, then confirm the resource no longer exists. This is why etcd backups are critical — losing etcd without a backup means rebuilding the entire cluster from scratch.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write a complete etcd backup and restore procedure as a scripted workflow.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>#!/bin/bash
<span class="comment"># === ETCD BACKUP AND RESTORE WORKFLOW ===</span>

<span class="comment"># --- Variables (adjust to your cluster) ---</span>
CACERT=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt
CERT=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt
KEY=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
BACKUP_PATH=/opt/etcd-backup.db
RESTORE_DIR=/var/lib/etcd-restored
MANIFEST=/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># --- BACKUP ---</span>
echo "Taking etcd backup..."
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save $BACKUP_PATH \
--cacert=$CACERT --cert=$CERT --key=$KEY

echo "Verifying backup..."
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status $BACKUP_PATH \
--write-table

<span class="comment"># --- RESTORE ---</span>
echo "Restoring from backup..."
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore $BACKUP_PATH \
--data-dir=$RESTORE_DIR

echo "Updating etcd manifest..."
<span class="comment"># Update --data-dir in command</span>
sudo sed -i "s|--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd|--data-dir=$RESTORE_DIR|" \
$MANIFEST

<span class="comment"># Update hostPath volume</span>
sudo sed -i "s|path: /var/lib/etcd$|path: $RESTORE_DIR|" \
$MANIFEST

echo "Waiting for etcd to restart (60s)..."
sleep 60

echo "Verifying cluster..."
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This workflow captures the complete CKA backup/restore procedure: set variables → save snapshot → verify → restore to new dir → update manifest (both <code>--data-dir</code> and <code>hostPath</code>) → wait → verify. On the CKA, you won't write a script, but knowing this flow by heart is essential. The <code>sed</code> commands show what needs changing in the manifest. Practice this workflow until you can do it in under 8 minutes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a CronJob that takes an automated etcd snapshot backup every 6 hours and stores it in <code>/opt/etcd-backups/</code>. Include the required certificates and retention of the last 7 backups.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
name: etcd-backup
namespace: kube-system
spec:
schedule: "0 */6 * * *"          <span class="comment"># Every 6 hours</span>
concurrencyPolicy: Forbid          <span class="comment"># Don't overlap backups</span>
successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 7
failedJobsHistoryLimit: 3
jobTemplate:
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      hostNetwork: true           <span class="comment"># Access etcd on localhost</span>
      restartPolicy: OnFailure
      containers:
      - name: etcd-backup
        image: registry.k8s.io/etcd:3.5.12-0
        command: ["/bin/sh", "-c"]
        args:
        - |
          BACKUP_FILE=/backups/etcd-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).db
          etcdctl snapshot save $BACKUP_FILE \
            --cacert=/certs/ca.crt \
            --cert=/certs/server.crt \
            --key=/certs/server.key
          etcdctl snapshot status $BACKUP_FILE --write-table
          <span class="comment"># Rotate: keep last 7 backups</span>
          ls -t /backups/etcd-*.db | tail -n +8 | xargs rm -f
          echo "Backup complete: $BACKUP_FILE"
        env:
        - name: ETCDCTL_API
          value: "3"
        volumeMounts:
        - name: etcd-certs
          mountPath: /certs
          readOnly: true
        - name: backup-dir
          mountPath: /backups
      volumes:
      - name: etcd-certs
        hostPath:
          path: /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd
          type: Directory
      - name: backup-dir
        hostPath:
          path: /opt/etcd-backups
          type: DirectoryOrCreate
      nodeSelector:
        node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane: ""
      tolerations:
      - key: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
        effect: NoSchedule

<span class="comment"># Verify the CronJob:</span>
kubectl get cronjob etcd-backup -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># NAME          SCHEDULE      SUSPEND  ACTIVE  LAST SCHEDULE</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-backup   0 */6 * * *   False    0       &lt;none&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># Trigger a manual run to test:</span>
kubectl create job test-backup -n kube-system \
--from=cronjob/etcd-backup

<span class="comment"># Check the backup:</span>
ls -la /opt/etcd-backups/</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Automated etcd backups require several pieces: (1) <code>hostNetwork: true</code> to reach etcd on localhost:2379, (2) etcd PKI certificates mounted from <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd</code>, (3) <code>nodeSelector</code> + <code>tolerations</code> to run on the control plane node (which has the taint <code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</code>), (4) backup directory mounted as hostPath. The rotation logic (<code>ls -t | tail -n +8 | xargs rm</code>) keeps the 7 newest files. On the CKA, you're more likely to do a manual backup, but understanding this CronJob pattern demonstrates mastery of Jobs, scheduling, hostPath, tolerations, and nodeSelector — all CKA-tested topics combined.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 29 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch29 -->

        <article class="chapter" id="ch30">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 30 · Node Maintenance &amp; OS Upgrades</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Safely removing nodes from service for maintenance, protecting applications with PodDisruptionBudgets, and performing OS upgrades without downtime. These are essential skills for any CKA candidate and day-to-day cluster operations.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔧 Maintenance</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Learning Objectives -->
            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Drain, cordon, uncordon</strong> — understand the difference and when to use each</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>PodDisruptionBudgets</strong> — protect application availability during voluntary disruptions</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>OS upgrades</strong> — safely perform kernel patches and OS updates on nodes</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Pod eviction timeout</strong> — understand what happens when nodes go down unexpectedly</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- CKA Relevance -->
            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <h4>📋 CKA Exam Relevance</h4>
                <p><strong>Domain:</strong> Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration — <strong>25%</strong></p>
                <p>Node drain/cordon operations appear frequently. You need to drain nodes before upgrades (Ch28), and PDBs control how drain interacts with your deployments.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h4>💡 CKA Exam Tip</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>The exam may ask you to drain a node — remember <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code></li>
                    <li>If pods with local storage exist, add <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code></li>
                    <li>Know how to create a PDB from a YAML manifest</li>
                    <li>Understand that drain = cordon + evict all pods</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
