            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 30.1 — Draining Nodes                              -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §30.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s30-1">                    <h3 class="section-h">30.1 Draining Nodes — Safe Pod Eviction</h3>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>

                <p>Before maintenance, you must safely move all pods off a node. Kubernetes provides three commands for this: <code>drain</code>, <code>cordon</code>, and <code>uncordon</code>. Understanding when to use each is critical.</p>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚿</div>
                        <h5>kubectl drain</h5>
                        <p>Cordons the node <strong>AND</strong> evicts all pods (except DaemonSets). Use before maintenance. Almost always needs <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🚧</div>
                        <h5>kubectl cordon</h5>
                        <p>Marks node as <strong>SchedulingDisabled</strong> — no new pods scheduled, but existing pods <strong>keep running</strong>. Use to gradually move workloads.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>kubectl uncordon</h5>
                        <p>Removes the SchedulingDisabled mark — node accepts new pods again. <strong>Always run after maintenance</strong> or pods won't come back.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛡️</div>
                        <h5>PodDisruptionBudget</h5>
                        <p>Controls <strong>how many pods can be evicted at once</strong>. Drain respects PDBs — if evicting would violate <code>minAvailable</code>, drain blocks until safe.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Drain vs Cordon vs Uncordon Comparison -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 drain vs cordon vs uncordon</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Command</th>
                                <th>Marks Unschedulable?</th>
                                <th>Evicts Existing Pods?</th>
                                <th>Use Case</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>❌ No — pods stay</td>
                                <td>Prevent new pods before planned maintenance</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Yes (implicit cordon)</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes — evicts all</td>
                                <td>Remove ALL workloads before maintenance</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>❌ Removes mark</td>
                                <td>N/A</td>
                                <td>Make node schedulable again after maintenance</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- Node Maintenance Flow Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🔄 Node Maintenance Flow — drain → maintain → uncordon</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     NODE MAINTENANCE LIFECYCLE                            │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│   NORMAL              CORDONED            DRAINED             READY      │
│  ┌──────────┐       ┌──────────┐       ┌──────────┐       ┌──────────┐  │
│  │  Node    │       │  Node    │       │  Node    │       │  Node    │  │
│  │  Ready   │──────▶│  Ready   │──────▶│  Ready   │──────▶│  Ready   │  │
│  │  Sched   │cordon │ SchedDis │ drain │ SchedDis │uncord │  Sched   │  │
│  │ 🟢🟢🟢🟢 │       │ 🟢🟢🟢🟢 │       │ ⬜⬜⬜⬜ │       │ 🟢🟢🟢🟢 │  │
│  └──────────┘       └──────────┘       └──────────┘       └──────────┘  │
│   Pods run           Pods STAY          Pods EVICTED       New pods      │
│   Accepts new        No new pods        to other nodes     can be        │
│   scheduling         scheduled          Empty node         scheduled     │
│                                          Do maintenance                  │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚡ drain = cordon (mark unschedulable) + evict all pods                │
│  ⚡ cordon = only mark unschedulable (pods keep running)                │
│  ⚡ uncordon = mark schedulable again                                   │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Drain Command Variations -->
                <h4>Drain Command — Common Flags</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">drain-commands.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Basic drain — will FAIL if DaemonSet pods exist</span>
kubectl drain worker01

<span class="comment"># With --ignore-daemonsets (almost always needed)</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets

<span class="comment"># With local storage pods (emptyDir volumes)</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># Force drain (delete pods not managed by a controller)</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --force

<span class="comment"># Drain with grace period</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --grace-period=60

<span class="comment"># Drain with timeout</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --timeout=120s</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- What happens during drain diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">📦 What Happens When You Drain a Node</div>
                    <pre>
  kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets
           │
           ▼
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ 1. Mark worker01 as SchedulingDisabled (cordon)         │
  │ 2. For each non-DaemonSet pod on worker01:              │
  │    ├── Check PodDisruptionBudgets                        │
  │    ├── If PDB would be violated → WAIT (don't evict)    │
  │    ├── Send SIGTERM to pod (graceful shutdown)           │
  │    └── Wait for pod termination (gracePeriod)            │
  │ 3. Controller detects missing replicas                   │
  │ 4. Scheduler places new pods on OTHER nodes              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
           │
           ▼
  ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐
  │  worker01    │    │  worker02    │
  │  (EMPTY)     │    │  🟢🟢🟢🟢🟢  │
  │  SchedDisabl │    │  All pods    │
  │  Ready for   │    │  rescheduled │
  │  maintenance │    │  here        │
  └──────────────┘    └──────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">cordon-uncordon.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Cordon — mark unschedulable without evicting</span>
kubectl cordon worker01
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS                     ROLES    VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># worker01   Ready,SchedulingDisabled   &lt;none&gt;   v1.31.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Uncordon — make schedulable again</span>
kubectl uncordon worker01
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS   ROLES    VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># worker01   Ready    &lt;none&gt;   v1.31.0</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📌 easypj Drain Scenario</h5>
                    <p>When you drain <code>worker01</code> where your easypj pods are running:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>easypj pods on worker01 receive SIGTERM</li>
                        <li>Django's graceful shutdown handler completes in-flight requests</li>
                        <li>The Deployment controller creates replacement pods</li>
                        <li>Scheduler places them on worker02</li>
                        <li>The Service endpoints update — traffic flows to new pods</li>
                        <li>Zero downtime if you have enough replicas and a PDB</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>

                <!-- Drain Troubleshooting Table -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>🛠️ Drain Troubleshooting — When Drain Gets Stuck</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Problem</th>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Solution</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>DaemonSet pods blocking</strong></td>
                                <td><code>cannot delete DaemonSet-managed Pods</code></td>
                                <td>Add <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> flag</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Pods with emptyDir</strong></td>
                                <td><code>cannot delete Pods with local storage</code></td>
                                <td>Add <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> flag</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Unreplicated pods</strong></td>
                                <td><code>cannot delete Pods not managed by...</code></td>
                                <td>Add <code>--force</code> (pod will be lost!)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>PDB blocking drain</strong></td>
                                <td>Drain hangs — waiting for eviction to be allowed</td>
                                <td>Wait for pods to reschedule, or use <code>--disable-eviction</code> (bypasses PDB!)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Drain taking too long</strong></td>
                                <td>Pods not terminating within timeout</td>
                                <td>Use <code>--timeout=300s</code> to set a deadline, or check <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Pod terminating but not dying</strong></td>
                                <td>Pod stuck in <code>Terminating</code> state</td>
                                <td>Check for finalizers: <code>kubectl get pod -o json | jq '.metadata.finalizers'</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- What Gets Evicted vs What Stays diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">📋 What Gets Evicted vs What Stays During Drain</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets                 │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  ❌ EVICTED (moved to other nodes):              ✅ STAYS (not touched): │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────┐            ┌────────────────────┐  │
│  │ Deployment-managed pods          │            │ DaemonSet pods     │  │
│  │ ReplicaSet-managed pods          │            │ (kube-proxy, etc.) │  │
│  │ StatefulSet-managed pods         │            │                    │  │
│  │ Job pods                         │            │ Static pods        │  │
│  │ Bare/naked pods (with --force)   │            │ (mirror pods)      │  │
│  └──────────────────────────────────┘            └────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️ SPECIAL CASES:                                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ emptyDir pods → need --delete-emptydir-data (data will be lost) │    │
│  │ Standalone pods → need --force (no controller to recreate them) │    │
│  │ PDB-protected → drain waits for eviction API to allow removal   │    │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 30.2 — PodDisruptionBudgets                        -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §30.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s30-2">                    <h3 class="section-h">30.2 PodDisruptionBudgets (PDB)</h3>
                    <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>

                <p>A PodDisruptionBudget controls how many pods can be unavailable during <strong>voluntary disruptions</strong> like drain, upgrades, and node scaling. It prevents drain from killing too many pods at once, ensuring your application stays available.</p>

                <!-- PDB concept diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🛡️ PDB — Protecting Applications During Disruptions</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│          PodDisruptionBudget — How It Works                               │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  Deployment: easypj (3 replicas)                                         │
│  PDB: minAvailable: 2                                                    │
│                                                                          │
│  WITHOUT PDB:                        WITH PDB:                           │
│  ┌────────────────────────┐         ┌────────────────────────┐           │
│  │ kubectl drain worker01 │         │ kubectl drain worker01 │           │
│  │                        │         │                        │           │
│  │ Pod A ─── EVICTED ❌   │         │ Pod A ─── EVICTED ❌   │           │
│  │ Pod B ─── EVICTED ❌   │         │ Pod B ─── WAIT ⏸️     │           │
│  │ Pod C ─── running ✅   │         │          (PDB blocks   │           │
│  │                        │         │           until Pod A   │           │
│  │ ⚠️ Only 1 replica!    │         │           reschedules)  │           │
│  │ Possible downtime!     │         │ Pod B ─── EVICTED ❌   │           │
│  │                        │         │ Pod C ─── running ✅   │           │
│  │                        │         │ Pod A' ── running ✅   │           │
│  │                        │         │ ✅ Always ≥2 replicas  │           │
│  └────────────────────────┘         └────────────────────────┘           │
│                                                                          │
│  PDB guarantees minimum availability during voluntary disruptions        │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Two types comparison -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 minAvailable vs maxUnavailable — Choose ONE</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Field</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>Example (3 replicas)</th>
                                <th>Max Disrupted</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>minAvailable: 2</code></td>
                                <td>At least N pods must stay running</td>
                                <td>2 must run → only 1 can be disrupted</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>minAvailable: "67%"</code></td>
                                <td>Percentage must stay running</td>
                                <td>67% of 3 = 2 must run → 1 disrupted</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>maxUnavailable: 1</code></td>
                                <td>At most N pods can be down</td>
                                <td>Max 1 down → 2 must run</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>maxUnavailable: "33%"</code></td>
                                <td>Max percentage can be down</td>
                                <td>33% of 3 = 1 can be down</td>
                                <td>1 pod</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- PDB YAML examples -->
                <h4>PDB with minAvailable (easypj Example)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-pdb-minavailable.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-pdb
  namespace: default
spec:
  minAvailable: 2              <span class="comment"># At least 2 pods must always be running</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj              <span class="comment"># Must match the Deployment's pod labels</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>PDB with maxUnavailable</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-pdb-maxunavailable.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-pdb
  namespace: default
spec:
  maxUnavailable: 1            <span class="comment"># At most 1 pod can be unavailable at a time</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- PDB commands -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">pdb-commands.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create PDB</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-pdb.yaml

<span class="comment"># View PDBs</span>
kubectl get pdb
<span class="comment"># NAME         MIN AVAILABLE   MAX UNAVAILABLE   ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-pdb   2               N/A               1                     5m</span>

<span class="comment"># Describe PDB for details</span>
kubectl describe pdb easypj-pdb

<span class="comment"># Now drain will respect the PDB</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets
<span class="comment"># If draining would violate PDB, it will WAIT until pods reschedule</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ PDB Only Protects Against Voluntary Disruptions</h5>
                    <p>PDBs protect against: <code>kubectl drain</code>, cluster autoscaler scale-down, rolling updates.</p>
                    <p>PDBs do <strong>NOT</strong> protect against: node crash, node out of memory (OOM), pod deletions with <code>kubectl delete pod</code>, hardware failure.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- PDB + Drain Eviction Sequence -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🔄 PDB + Drain — Eviction Sequence Timeline</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│     kubectl drain worker01 (Deployment: 3 replicas, PDB: minAvailable=2)│
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  TIME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────►     │
│                                                                          │
│  t=0s     Drain starts. Eviction API checks PDB:                        │
│           Running: [Pod-A, Pod-B, Pod-C] → 3 available                  │
│           PDB requires ≥2 → CAN evict 1                                 │
│           ┌───────────────────────────────┐                              │
│           │ Pod-A: EVICT ❌ → Terminating │                              │
│           │ Pod-B: running ✅             │                              │
│           │ Pod-C: running ✅             │                              │
│           └───────────────────────────────┘                              │
│                                                                          │
│  t=15s    Pod-A terminates. Deployment creates Pod-A'.                  │
│           Scheduler places Pod-A' on worker02.                          │
│           Running: [Pod-B, Pod-C] → only 2!                             │
│           PDB requires ≥2 → CANNOT evict more yet                       │
│           ┌───────────────────────────────┐                              │
│           │ Pod-A': Pending → Running     │                              │
│           │ Pod-B: running ✅ (blocked)   │                              │
│           │ Pod-C: running ✅             │                              │
│           └───────────────────────────────┘                              │
│                                                                          │
│  t=30s    Pod-A' is Running and Ready.                                  │
│           Running: [Pod-A', Pod-B, Pod-C] → 3 available                 │
│           PDB allows eviction again → evict Pod-B                       │
│           ┌───────────────────────────────┐                              │
│           │ Pod-A': running ✅            │                              │
│           │ Pod-B: EVICT ❌ → Terminating │                              │
│           │ Pod-C: running ✅             │                              │
│           └───────────────────────────────┘                              │
│                                                                          │
│  t=45s    Pod-B' scheduled on worker02. Drain complete.                 │
│           ✅ At NO point were there fewer than 2 running pods           │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Voluntary vs Involuntary Disruptions Detail -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 Voluntary vs Involuntary Disruptions — Complete Reference</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Disruption Type</th>
                                <th>Examples</th>
                                <th>PDB Honored?</th>
                                <th>Graceful?</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Voluntary</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl drain</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Voluntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Cluster autoscaler scale-down</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Voluntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Deployment rolling update</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Involuntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Node hardware failure / crash</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Involuntary</strong></td>
                                <td>Node kernel panic</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Involuntary</strong></td>
                                <td>OOM kill (out of memory)</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Bypass</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl delete pod</code></td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Bypass</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl drain --disable-eviction</code></td>
                                <td>❌ No</td>
                                <td>✅ Yes</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- PDB YAML Skeleton Quick Reference -->
                <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                    <h4>📋 PDB — YAML Skeleton (Exam Quick Reference)</h4>
                    <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                        <div class="code-block-header">                                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                            <span class="code-lang">pdb-skeleton.yaml</span>
                        </div>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1                    # ← Always policy/v1 (not policy/v1beta1)
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: &lt;pdb-name&gt;
  namespace: &lt;namespace&gt;
spec:
  <span class="comment"># Choose ONE of these (not both):</span>
  minAvailable: &lt;number-or-percent&gt;      <span class="comment"># At least N pods must be running</span>
  <span class="comment"># maxUnavailable: &lt;number-or-percent&gt;  # At most N pods can be down</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      &lt;label-key&gt;: &lt;label-value&gt;          <span class="comment"># Must match pod labels</span></code></pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Section 30.3 — OS Upgrades and Kernel Patches              -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §30.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s30-3">                    <h3 class="section-h">30.3 OS Upgrades and Kernel Patches</h3>

                <p>When a node needs an OS upgrade or kernel patch, you follow the drain → upgrade → uncordon pattern. The key concern is the <strong>pod-eviction-timeout</strong> — if a node goes down without being drained first, Kubernetes waits 5 minutes before rescheduling pods.</p>

                <!-- OS Upgrade Procedure Diagram -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🖥️ OS Upgrade Procedure — Step by Step</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    OS UPGRADE / KERNEL PATCH PROCEDURE                    │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  Step 1: DRAIN the node                                                  │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data│  │
│  │ Pods gracefully shut down and reschedule to other nodes           │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 2: PERFORM the OS upgrade / kernel patch                           │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ (SSH into the node)                                               │   │
│  │ $ apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y                            │   │
│  │ $ reboot     (if kernel patch requires reboot)                    │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 3: VERIFY the node is healthy                                      │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Wait for node to come back online after reboot                    │   │
│  │ $ systemctl status kubelet       (is kubelet running?)            │   │
│  │ $ kubectl get nodes              (is node Ready?)                 │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                               │                                          │
│                               ▼                                          │
│  Step 4: UNCORDON the node                                               │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl uncordon worker01                                       │   │
│  │ Node is now schedulable — new pods can be placed here             │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                          │
│  ⏱️ pod-eviction-timeout = 5 minutes (default)                          │
│  If a node goes down WITHOUT drain, the controller-manager waits 5      │
│  minutes before marking pods as "Unknown" and rescheduling them.        │
│  Always DRAIN first to avoid this delay!                                 │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Complete OS upgrade commands -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">os-upgrade-workflow.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># === COMPLETE OS UPGRADE WORKFLOW ===

<span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Drain the node</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># 2. SSH to worker and perform upgrade</span>
ssh worker01

<span class="comment"># ON WORKER NODE:</span>
apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y
<span class="comment"># If kernel upgrade:</span>
reboot

<span class="comment"># Wait for node to come back...</span>

<span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Verify node is healthy</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker01 should show Ready,SchedulingDisabled</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify kubelet is running</span>
ssh worker01 systemctl status kubelet

<span class="comment"># 5. Make node schedulable again</span>
kubectl uncordon worker01

<span class="comment"># 6. Final verification</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># All nodes should show Ready (no SchedulingDisabled)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Eviction Timeout comparison -->
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <h4>📊 Planned Drain vs Unplanned Node Failure</h4>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Scenario</th>
                                <th>Pod Rescheduling</th>
                                <th>Downtime</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Planned: <code>kubectl drain</code> first</strong></td>
                                <td>Immediate — pods evicted and rescheduled instantly</td>
                                <td>Minimal (graceful shutdown period only)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Unplanned: Node crashes</strong></td>
                                <td>After pod-eviction-timeout (default <strong>5 minutes</strong>)</td>
                                <td>5+ minutes — pods stuck in Unknown state</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Unplanned: Node power off</strong></td>
                                <td>After node becomes NotReady + eviction timeout</td>
                                <td>5+ minutes — no graceful shutdown</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key Takeaway</h5>
                    <p>Always <code>kubectl drain</code> before maintenance. It ensures pods are gracefully rescheduled immediately instead of waiting 5 minutes for the eviction timeout. Combined with a PDB, your application maintains availability throughout the process.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- Node Controller Timeline — What happens when a node goes offline -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">⏱️ Node Controller Timeline — Unplanned Node Failure</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   WHAT HAPPENS WHEN A NODE GOES DOWN                     │
│                   (without kubectl drain)                                │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  TIME ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────►     │
│                                                                          │
│  t=0s        Node stops sending heartbeats to API server                │
│              kubelet stops updating node lease object                    │
│              Status: Node still shows "Ready"                            │
│                                                                          │
│  t=40s       node-monitor-grace-period expires (default: 40s)           │
│              ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│              │ Node Controller marks node as "NotReady"     │            │
│              │ kubectl get nodes → STATUS: NotReady         │            │
│              │ Pods still show "Running" (not evicted yet)  │            │
│              └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                          │
│  t=5m 40s    pod-eviction-timeout expires (default: 5 minutes)          │
│              ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│              │ Node Controller adds NoExecute taint         │            │
│              │ Taint-based eviction kicks in                │            │
│              │ Pods get tolerationSeconds (default: 300s)   │            │
│              │ Pod status → "Unknown"                       │            │
│              └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                          │
│  t=10m 40s   tolerationSeconds expires for pods                         │
│              ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│              │ Pods finally evicted and rescheduled         │            │
│              │ Deployment controller creates replacements   │            │
│              │ ⚠️ Total downtime: ~10+ minutes!            │            │
│              └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                          │
│  Compare with kubectl drain: pods rescheduled in ~15-30 seconds! ✅     │
│                                                                          │
│  KEY PARAMETERS (kube-controller-manager):                               │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ --node-monitor-grace-period=40s    Time before marking NotReady  │   │
│  │ --pod-eviction-timeout=5m0s        Time before evicting pods     │   │
│  │ --node-monitor-period=5s           How often node status checked │   │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Multi-Node Rolling Maintenance Strategy -->
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <div class="diagram-title">🔄 Multi-Node Rolling Maintenance Strategy (easypj Production)</div>
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│          ROLLING MAINTENANCE — 3 Worker Nodes, Zero Downtime             │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                                          │
│  BEFORE: easypj (3 replicas), PDB: minAvailable=2                       │
│  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐                        │
│  │  worker01   │  │  worker02   │  │  worker03   │                      │
│  │  easypj-1 ✅│  │  easypj-2 ✅│  │  easypj-3 ✅│                     │
│  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘                        │
│                                                                          │
│  ROUND 1: Maintain worker01                                              │
│  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐                        │
│  │  worker01   │  │  worker02   │  │  worker03   │                      │
│  │  🔧 DRAIN  │  │  easypj-2 ✅│  │  easypj-3 ✅│                     │
│  │  upgrading  │  │  easypj-1'✅│  │             │                      │
│  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘                        │
│  ✅ 3 pods running across 2 nodes — PDB satisfied                       │
│                                                                          │
│  ROUND 2: Uncordon worker01, drain worker02                              │
│  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐                        │
│  │  worker01   │  │  worker02   │  │  worker03   │                      │
│  │  easypj-2'✅│  │  🔧 DRAIN  │  │  easypj-3 ✅│                     │
│  │             │  │  upgrading  │  │  easypj-1'✅│                      │
│  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘                        │
│  ✅ 3 pods running across 2 nodes — PDB satisfied                       │
│                                                                          │
│  ROUND 3: Uncordon worker02, drain worker03                              │
│  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐                        │
│  │  worker01   │  │  worker02   │  │  worker03   │                      │
│  │  easypj-1 ✅│  │  easypj-2 ✅│  │  🔧 DRAIN  │                     │
│  │             │  │  easypj-3'✅│  │  upgrading  │                      │
│  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘                        │
│  ✅ 3 pods running across 2 nodes — PDB satisfied                       │
│                                                                          │
│  AFTER: All nodes upgraded, zero downtime achieved! 🎉                   │
│  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐                        │
│  │  worker01 ✅│  │  worker02 ✅│  │  worker03 ✅│                      │
│  │  upgraded   │  │  upgraded   │  │  upgraded   │                      │
│  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘                        │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                </div>

                <!-- Complete multi-node rolling upgrade script -->
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">                            <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">rolling-maintenance.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># === MULTI-NODE ROLLING MAINTENANCE ===
<span class="comment"># Ensure PDB is in place BEFORE starting</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify PDB</span>
kubectl get pdb
<span class="comment"># ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS should be ≥ 1</span>

<span class="comment"># Node list</span>
NODES="worker01 worker02 worker03"

for NODE in $NODES; do
  echo "=== Maintaining $NODE ==="

  <span class="comment"># 1. Drain</span>
  kubectl drain $NODE --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
  echo "  Drained $NODE — pods rescheduled"

  <span class="comment"># 2. SSH and upgrade (customize per environment)</span>
  ssh $NODE "apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y && reboot"

  <span class="comment"># 3. Wait for node to come back</span>
  echo "  Waiting for $NODE to rejoin cluster..."
  until kubectl get node $NODE | grep -q " Ready"; do
    sleep 10
  done

  <span class="comment"># 4. Uncordon</span>
  kubectl uncordon $NODE
  echo "  $NODE back in service ✅"

  <span class="comment"># 5. Brief pause for pods to stabilize</span>
  sleep 30
done

echo "=== All nodes upgraded! ==="
kubectl get nodes</code></pre>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Exam Gotcha for Node Maintenance -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>🚨 CKA Exam Gotchas — Node Maintenance</h5>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>Trap</th><th>What Happens</th><th>Correct Approach</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Forgot to uncordon after maintenance</td>
                                <td>Node stays SchedulingDisabled — new pods won't land on it</td>
                                <td>Always <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code> as your LAST step</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Using <code>kubectl delete node</code> instead of drain</td>
                                <td>Node removed from cluster — must rejoin with kubeadm</td>
                                <td>Use <code>kubectl drain</code> for maintenance, never delete</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Confused cordon vs drain</td>
                                <td>Cordon only prevents NEW pods — existing pods stay running</td>
                                <td>Drain = cordon + evict all pods. Use drain for maintenance</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Draining control plane without thought</td>
                                <td>If single control plane, you lose API server access!</td>
                                <td>Control plane static pods can't be drained anyway — only user workloads</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

            </section>

            <!-- Node Maintenance Decision Flow -->
            <h4>Node Maintenance Decision Tree</h4>
            <div class="flow-diagram">
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🤔</span><span>Maintenance needed?</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🛡️</span><span>Create PDB first</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🚿</span><span>kubectl drain node</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">🔧</span><span>Perform work</span></div>
                <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="flow-step"><span class="flow-icon">✅</span><span>kubectl uncordon</span></div>
            </div>

            <div class="scenario-box">
                <h5>🛠️ Scenario: kubectl drain Gets Stuck</h5>
                <p>You run <code>kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets</code> and it hangs for minutes without completing. What's happening?</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>PDB violation:</strong> A PodDisruptionBudget prevents eviction — check <code>kubectl get pdb</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Pod with no controller:</strong> Standalone pods (not managed by Deployment/ReplicaSet) block drain — add <code>--force</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Local storage pods:</strong> Pods with emptyDir volumes block drain — add <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Long graceful shutdown:</strong> Pod has a high <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> — add <code>--grace-period=30</code></li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>Quick fix:</strong> <code>kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --force --grace-period=30</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <!-- Chapter 30 — Practice Drills                               -->
            <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
            <div class="cka-practice-drill" id="s30-drill-1">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 30.1 — Drain and Uncordon a Node</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 3 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Node <code>worker02</code> needs emergency maintenance. Safely remove all workloads from the node, then after the maintenance is complete, make it schedulable again.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Drain worker02, ignoring DaemonSet-managed pods</li>
                        <li>Verify no user pods are running on worker02</li>
                        <li>After maintenance, uncordon the node</li>
                        <li>Verify the node is Ready and schedulable</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> Use <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> with drain. DaemonSet pods (like kube-proxy) cannot be evicted — the flag tells drain to skip them. If pods use emptyDir, add <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Drain the node</span>
kubectl drain worker02 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># 2. Verify no user pods on worker02</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep worker02
<span class="comment"># Only DaemonSet pods should remain (kube-proxy, etc.)</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Perform maintenance (OS upgrade, kernel patch, etc.)</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Uncordon the node</span>
kubectl uncordon worker02

<span class="comment"># 5. Verify node is Ready and schedulable</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker02 should show Ready (no SchedulingDisabled)</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill" id="s30-drill-2">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 30.2 — Create a PodDisruptionBudget</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 4 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Your easypj deployment has 3 replicas with label <code>app: easypj</code>. Create a PodDisruptionBudget named <code>easypj-pdb</code> that ensures at least 2 pods are always available during voluntary disruptions.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Create a PDB YAML with <code>minAvailable: 2</code></li>
                        <li>Apply the PDB to the cluster</li>
                        <li>Verify the PDB shows correct allowed disruptions</li>
                        <li>Test by draining a node — observe the PDB in action</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> The PDB's <code>selector.matchLabels</code> must match the pod labels from your Deployment. Use <code>apiVersion: policy/v1</code> and choose either <code>minAvailable</code> or <code>maxUnavailable</code> — not both.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># easypj-pdb.yaml</span>
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-pdb
  namespace: default
spec:
  minAvailable: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj</code></pre>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply</span>
kubectl apply -f easypj-pdb.yaml

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get pdb
<span class="comment"># NAME         MIN AVAILABLE   MAX UNAVAILABLE   ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-pdb   2               N/A               1                     10s</span>

<span class="comment"># Test — drain will respect PDB</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets
<span class="comment"># Drain will evict pods ONE AT A TIME, waiting for each to reschedule</span>
<span class="comment"># before evicting the next, ensuring at least 2 are always running</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-practice-drill" id="s30-drill-3">
                <h4>🔬 Practice Drill 30.3 — Complete OS Upgrade Workflow</h4>
                <div class="drill-timer">⏱️ Target Time: 5 minutes</div>
                <div class="drill-scenario">
                    <h5>Scenario</h5>
                    <p>Worker node <code>worker01</code> needs a kernel upgrade. Perform the complete maintenance workflow: drain, upgrade, verify, and restore service.</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Drain worker01</li>
                        <li>SSH to worker01 and simulate an OS upgrade</li>
                        <li>Verify kubelet is running and the node is healthy</li>
                        <li>Uncordon and verify pods can be scheduled again</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="drill-hint">
                    <strong>Hint:</strong> After a reboot, kubelet should start automatically (it's a systemd service). If it doesn't, check with <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> and start it with <code>systemctl start kubelet</code>.
                </div>
                <div class="drill-solution">
                    <details>
                        <summary>View Solution</summary>
                        <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># From control plane:</span>
kubectl drain worker01 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># SSH to worker:</span>
ssh worker01
apt-get update && apt-get upgrade -y
<span class="comment"># If kernel upgrade: reboot</span>
<span class="comment"># Wait for node to come back...</span>

<span class="comment"># From control plane — verify:</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker01 should show Ready,SchedulingDisabled</span>

<span class="comment"># Check kubelet</span>
ssh worker01 systemctl status kubelet

<span class="comment"># Uncordon</span>
kubectl uncordon worker01

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># All nodes Ready, no SchedulingDisabled</span></code></pre>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Chapter 30 Verification Checklist -->
            <div class="cka-verify">
                <h4>✅ Chapter 30 — Verification Checklist</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get nodes</code> — All nodes Ready, no SchedulingDisabled after maintenance</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pods -A -o wide</code> — Pods distributed across available nodes</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get pdb</code> — PDBs show correct ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS count</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe pdb &lt;name&gt;</code> — PDB selector matches target pods</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --dry-run=client</code> — Preview what drain would do</li>
                    <li><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — kubelet running on nodes after reboot</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 30 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Node Maintenance Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong><code>drain</code> vs <code>cordon</code></strong> — <code>cordon</code> only marks the node as unschedulable (existing pods stay). <code>drain</code> cordons AND evicts all pods. The exam says "remove all workloads from node" = drain, not cordon</li>
                        <li><strong>Drain fails without flags</strong> — DaemonSet pods can't be evicted (use <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code>). Pods with emptyDir data (use <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>). Unmanaged pods without a controller (use <code>--force</code> — but data is lost)</li>
                        <li><strong>PDB blocks drain</strong> — If a PodDisruptionBudget would be violated, drain hangs. Check PDBs: <code>kubectl get pdb</code>. If ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS is 0, you must wait or scale up first</li>
                        <li><strong>Don't forget to uncordon</strong> — After maintenance, <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code>. Without this, the node stays SchedulingDisabled and no new pods land on it. The exam checks for this</li>
                        <li><strong>PDB spec: <code>minAvailable</code> vs <code>maxUnavailable</code></strong> — Use one OR the other, not both. <code>minAvailable: 2</code> means at least 2 pods must stay up. <code>maxUnavailable: 1</code> means at most 1 pod can be down</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>


                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <strong>⚡ Node maintenance commands:</strong>
                    <code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code> — mark unschedulable (no eviction)<br>
                    <code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code> — evict all pods<br>
                    <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code> — re-enable scheduling<br>
                    <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code> — list all PodDisruptionBudgets<br>
                    <code>kubectl describe pdb &lt;name&gt;</code> — check allowed disruptions and selector
                </div>


                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 30 — How Node Draining and PodDisruptionBudgets Work ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 30 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Node Draining and PodDisruptionBudgets Work: Graceful Eviction, PDB Enforcement, and the Cordon/Drain Distinction</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Node Maintenance — The Complete Drain Lifecycle</h5>
            <p>Draining a node is more than just deleting pods. It respects PDBs, honors graceful termination, and can stall indefinitely if not understood properly.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NODE DRAIN — Internal Sequence                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data        │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: CORDON the node (prevent new pods)                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ kubectl cordon worker-1                                               │   │
│  │ → Sets node.spec.unschedulable = true                                 │   │
│  │ → Node condition: SchedulingDisabled                                  │   │
│  │ → EXISTING pods continue running                                       │   │
│  │ → NEW pods CANNOT be scheduled to this node                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ CORDON ONLY: Pods stay, no new pods. Useful for quick isolation.      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: EVICT pods (one by one, respecting constraints)                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ For each pod on the node (except DaemonSets + mirror pods):            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  a) CHECK PodDisruptionBudget:                                         │   │
│  │     → Does evicting this pod violate any PDB?                          │   │
│  │     → PDB .status.disruptionsAllowed must be >= 1                      │   │
│  │     → If NOT allowed → DRAIN BLOCKS (waits, retries)                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  b) SEND SIGTERM to pod (graceful shutdown):                           │   │
│  │     → Pod receives SIGTERM                                             │   │
│  │     → App has terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s) to cleanup  │   │
│  │     → If pod doesn't exit → SIGKILL after grace period                 │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  c) REMOVE pod from node:                                              │   │
│  │     → API Server deletes the pod object                                │   │
│  │     → ReplicaSet controller creates replacement pod                    │   │
│  │     → Replacement scheduled to a DIFFERENT node (this one is cordoned) │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  d) REPEAT for next pod                                                 │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  CORDON vs DRAIN:                                                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ ┌──────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │   │
│  │ │ CORDON   │ Marks node unschedulable. Existing pods STAY.        │   │   │
│  │ │          │ Use: quick "do not use this node" flag.             │   │   │
│  │ ├──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤   │   │
│  │ │ DRAIN    │ Cordons + EVICTS all pods. Node becomes empty.       │   │   │
│  │ │          │ Use: maintenance, decommissioning, upgrades.         │   │   │
│  │ └──────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ DRAIN FLAGS:                                                           │   │
│  │  --ignore-daemonsets    <span class="comment">← Skip DaemonSet pods (they restart anyway)  </span>│   │
│  │  --delete-emptydir-data <span class="comment">← Delete emptyDir volumes (data loss warning)</span>│   │
│  │  --force                 <span class="comment">← Force even if pod not managed by controller</span>│   │
│  │  --timeout=5m           <span class="comment">← Max time to wait for drain to complete     </span>│   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  PODDISRUPTIONBUDGET — Protecting Application Availability:                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  apiVersion: policy/v1                                                │   │
│  │  kind: PodDisruptionBudget                                             │   │
│  │  metadata:                                                             │   │
│  │    name: easypj-pdb                                                    │   │
│  │  spec:                                                                 │   │
│  │    minAvailable: 2    <span class="comment">← At least 2 pods must be running               </span>│   │
│  │    selector:                                                           │   │
│  │      matchLabels: {app: easypj}                                       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  PDB MATH:                                                             │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │ Deployment: easypj-web (replicas=5)                         │      │   │
│  │  │ PDB: minAvailable=2                                          │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │ disruptionsAllowed = total pods - minAvailable               │      │   │
│  │  │                    = 5 - 2 = 3                                │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │ → Up to 3 pods can be disrupted simultaneously               │      │   │
│  │  │ → At least 2 pods ALWAYS running during maintenance          │      │   │
│  │  │                                                               │      │   │
│  │  │ DRAIN with PDB: Drains pods until disruptionsAllowed == 0,  │      │   │
│  │  │ then blocks until pods on other nodes are healthy again.     │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  GRACEFUL TERMINATION — The Full 30-Second Sequence:                         │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  1. Pod status set to "Terminating"                                  │   │
│  │  2. Pod removed from ALL Service Endpoints (stops receiving traffic)  │   │
│  │  3. SIGTERM sent to container (PID 1)                                 │   │
│  │  4. Container runs preStop hook (if defined)                          │   │
│  │  5. App has terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s) to exit       │   │
│  │  6. After grace period: SIGKILL sent (force kill)                     │   │
│  │  7. Pod deleted from API Server                                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Node maintenance is a CKA staple. Always drain before maintenance: <code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code>. If drain hangs: check PDBs (<code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>), check for pods not managed by controllers (<code>kubectl get pods -o wide | grep &lt;node&gt;</code> — bare pods block drain unless --force). After maintenance: <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code> to allow scheduling again.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>❌ Wrong</th><th>✅ Correct</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl drain node01</code> (no flags)</td><td><code>kubectl drain node01 --ignore-daemonsets</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Forgetting to uncordon after maintenance</td><td><code>kubectl uncordon node01</code> after work is done</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Draining node with local storage pods (fails)</td><td>Add <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> flag</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Thinking cordon evicts pods</td><td>Cordon only stops NEW scheduling; existing pods stay</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>PDB with both minAvailable AND maxUnavailable</td><td>Use only ONE — they are mutually exclusive</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch30-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Node maintenance workflow:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code><br>
                Perform maintenance (reboot, OS upgrade, etc.)<br>
                <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code><br>
                <code>kubectl get nodes</code> — verify Ready status<br>
                <strong>PDB creation:</strong> <code>kubectl create pdb my-pdb --selector=app=web --min-available=2</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
