            <!-- ─────────── §31.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.1 Pod Lifecycle and Status Codes</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Understanding pod phases and container states is the foundation of all Kubernetes troubleshooting. When something breaks, the status tells you <strong>where to look</strong>.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Pod Lifecycle — From Creation to Termination</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                        POD LIFECYCLE FLOW                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl apply ─▶ API Server ─▶ Scheduler ─▶ kubelet ─▶ Container Runtime   │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐     ┌─────────┐     ┌───────────┐             │
│  │ Pending │────▶│ Running │────▶│Succeeded│     │  Failed   │             │
│  │         │     │         │──┐  │ (exit 0)│     │(exit ≠ 0) │             │
│  └─────────┘     └─────────┘  │  └─────────┘     └───────────┘             │
│       │               │      │                        ▲                     │
│       │               │      └────────────────────────┘                     │
│       ▼               ▼                                                     │
│  ┌─────────┐     ┌──────────────┐                                          │
│  │ Unknown │     │CrashLoopBack │  ← Container keeps crashing              │
│  │(node    │     │    Off       │     and Kubernetes keeps restarting       │
│  │ lost)   │     │              │     it with exponential backoff           │
│  └─────────┘     └──────────────┘     (10s, 20s, 40s, ... 5min max)        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod Phases</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Phase</th>
                                <th>Meaning</th>
                                <th>What To Do</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Pending</code></td>
                                <td>Accepted by API server but not yet running — scheduling, image pull, or resource wait</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → check Events</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Running</code></td>
                                <td>At least one container is running, starting, or restarting</td>
                                <td>Check if pod is <strong>Ready</strong> (readiness probe passing)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Succeeded</code></td>
                                <td>All containers terminated with exit code 0</td>
                                <td>Normal for Jobs and CronJobs</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Failed</code></td>
                                <td>All containers terminated, at least one with non-zero exit</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Unknown</code></td>
                                <td>Cannot determine pod state — node communication lost</td>
                                <td>Check node status: <code>kubectl get nodes</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Container States — What Each Status Means</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Status</th>
                                <th>Common Cause</th>
                                <th>Debug Command</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: ContainerCreating</code></td>
                                <td>Image being pulled or volume mounting</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Events</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: ErrImagePull</code></td>
                                <td>Wrong image name, tag doesn't exist, or registry auth failed</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → check image field</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: ImagePullBackOff</code></td>
                                <td>Same as ErrImagePull but after repeated failures (with backoff)</td>
                                <td>Fix image reference or add <code>imagePullSecrets</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: CrashLoopBackOff</code></td>
                                <td>Container starts then crashes repeatedly</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Waiting: CreateContainerError</code></td>
                                <td>SecurityContext issue, ConfigMap/Secret missing</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Events</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Running</code> but not Ready</td>
                                <td>Readiness probe failing — app not serving traffic</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod</code> → Conditions section</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Terminated: OOMKilled</code></td>
                                <td>Container exceeded memory limit (exit code 137)</td>
                                <td>Increase <code>resources.limits.memory</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>Terminated: Error</code></td>
                                <td>Container exited with non-zero code (app crash)</td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §31.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.2 The Debugging Toolkit</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>When a pod isn't working, follow this decision tree systematically. <strong>Never guess — always gather evidence first.</strong></p>

                <h4>Systematic Debug Sequence</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Get Status</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code> — What phase is the pod in? How many restarts? Which node?</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Describe Pod</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> — Check Events at the bottom for scheduling/pulling/startup errors.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Check Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — For CrashLoopBackOff, <code>--previous</code> shows the last crash output.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Shell In</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code> — If pod is Running, interactively inspect files, network, processes.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Check Events</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code> — Cluster-wide timeline showing all recent events.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Exit Code Quick Reference</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">✅</div>
                        <h5>Exit 0 — Success</h5>
                        <p>Container completed normally. Expected for Jobs/CronJobs. For long-running pods, this means the process quit unexpectedly.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">❌</div>
                        <h5>Exit 1 — App Error</h5>
                        <p>Application crashed (unhandled exception, config error, missing dependency). Check <code>kubectl logs --previous</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💀</div>
                        <h5>Exit 137 — OOM Killed</h5>
                        <p>Container exceeded memory limit (128 + SIGKILL=9). Fix: increase <code>resources.limits.memory</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛑</div>
                        <h5>Exit 143 — SIGTERM</h5>
                        <p>Graceful termination (128 + SIGTERM=15). Normal during pod deletion/rollout. Not an error.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔍 Pod Debugging Decision Tree</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                 POD NOT WORKING? Follow this tree:                         │
│                                                                            │
│  $ kubectl get pods -o wide                                                │
│       │                                                                    │
│       ├── STATUS: Pending?                                                 │
│       │    └── $ kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;                               │
│       │         ├── "Insufficient cpu/memory"                              │
│       │         │    → Scale cluster or adjust resource requests           │
│       │         ├── "no nodes match pod's node selector"                   │
│       │         │    → Check nodeSelector/nodeAffinity labels              │
│       │         ├── "node(s) had taint... pod didn't tolerate"             │
│       │         │    → Add toleration to pod spec OR remove taint          │
│       │         └── "persistentvolumeclaim not found"                      │
│       │              → Create the missing PVC or fix PVC name              │
│       │                                                                    │
│       ├── STATUS: CrashLoopBackOff?                                        │
│       │    └── $ kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous                             │
│       │         ├── Application error / stack trace                        │
│       │         │    → Fix app code or configuration                       │
│       │         ├── "connection refused" / "no such host"                  │
│       │         │    → Dependent service not ready (DB, Redis)             │
│       │         ├── Missing config/secret reference                        │
│       │         │    → Check ConfigMap/Secret exists &amp; mounted correctly   │
│       │         └── Permission denied                                      │
│       │              → Check SecurityContext &amp; file permissions            │
│       │                                                                    │
│       ├── STATUS: ImagePullBackOff?                                        │
│       │    └── $ kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;                               │
│       │         ├── Wrong image name/tag                                   │
│       │         │    → Fix spec.containers[].image                         │
│       │         └── Private registry auth failed                           │
│       │              → Add imagePullSecrets to pod spec                    │
│       │                                                                    │
│       └── STATUS: Running but not working?                                 │
│            ├── $ kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;           ← application logs           │
│            ├── $ kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh  ← shell in            │
│            ├── $ kubectl port-forward &lt;pod&gt; 8080:80 ← test locally        │
│            └── Check readiness/liveness probes in describe output          │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §31.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.3 Essential Debug Commands</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>These are the commands you'll use on <strong>every single troubleshooting task</strong> in the CKA exam. Memorize them.</p>

                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <h4>🚀 The Debug Command Sequence</h4>
                    <p>Follow this order — each step narrows down the problem:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Step</th>
                                <th>Command</th>
                                <th>What You Learn</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>1. Overview</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -o wide</code></td>
                                <td>Pod status, restarts, node placement, IP</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>2. Details</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Config, conditions, events timeline</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>3. App logs</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Application stdout/stderr output</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>4. Prev logs</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                                <td>Output from last crashed container</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>5. Shell in</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></td>
                                <td>Interactive debugging inside container</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>6. Events</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code></td>
                                <td>Cluster-wide event timeline</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>7. Resources</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl top pod &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Current CPU/memory usage</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Essential Debug Commands — Complete Reference</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># 1. GET — Overview of pod status</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl get pods                           <span class="comment"># All pods in current namespace</span>
kubectl get pods -o wide                   # + Node, IP columns
kubectl get pods -A                        <span class="comment"># All namespaces</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels             <span class="comment"># See pod labels</span>
kubectl get pods -w                        <span class="comment"># Watch for changes in real-time</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. DESCRIBE — Detailed pod information + Events</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;                <span class="comment"># Full details + Events at bottom</span>
<span class="comment"># KEY SECTIONS TO CHECK:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Status / Conditions     → Is it ready? Why not?</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Containers → State      → Waiting/Running/Terminated + reason</span>
<span class="comment">#   - Events (bottom)         → Scheduling, pulling, startup timeline</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. LOGS — Application output</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;                         <span class="comment"># Current container stdout</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous              <span class="comment"># Previous crashed container ⭐</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -f                      <span class="comment"># Follow / stream live</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --tail=100              <span class="comment"># Last 100 lines only</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --since=1h              <span class="comment"># Last hour only</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;          <span class="comment"># Specific container (multi-container)</span>
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --all-containers        <span class="comment"># All containers in the pod</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. EXEC — Shell into a running container</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh          <span class="comment"># Interactive shell (Alpine/BusyBox)</span>
kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/bash        <span class="comment"># Bash shell (Ubuntu/Debian)</span>
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- cat /etc/resolv.conf <span class="comment"># Run single command</span>
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- env                  <span class="comment"># Check environment variables</span>
kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- wget -qO- http://svc <span class="comment"># Test connectivity from inside</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. EVENTS — Cluster-wide timeline</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl get events --sort-by='.metadata.creationTimestamp'
kubectl get events --field-selector reason=Failed
kubectl get events -n kube-system          <span class="comment"># Control plane events</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 6. RESOURCE USAGE — CPU and memory</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl top pods                           <span class="comment"># CPU/memory per pod</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu             <span class="comment"># Sort by CPU usage</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=memory          <span class="comment"># Sort by memory usage</span>
kubectl top nodes                          <span class="comment"># Node-level resource usage</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>✅ Verify Your Debug Skills</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Can you get previous container logs? → <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></li>
                        <li>Can you check events sorted by time? → <code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.metadata.creationTimestamp'</code></li>
                        <li>Can you shell into a specific container? → <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>Ephemeral Debug Containers — Debugging Distroless Images</h4>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 When <code>kubectl exec</code> Won't Work</h5>
                    <p>Many production images are <strong>distroless</strong> — they don't include a shell (<code>/bin/sh</code>), <code>curl</code>, or any debug tools. Ephemeral containers let you inject a debug container into a running pod without restarting it.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Ephemeral Debug Containers</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Attach a debug container to a running pod</span>
kubectl debug -it &lt;pod-name&gt; --image=busybox:1.36 --target=&lt;container&gt;

<span class="comment"># Debug with a full toolkit (nicolaka/netshoot has curl, dig, tcpdump, etc.)</span>
kubectl debug -it &lt;pod-name&gt; --image=nicolaka/netshoot --target=app

<span class="comment"># Create a copy of the pod with a debug container (doesn't affect original)</span>
kubectl debug &lt;pod-name&gt; -it --image=busybox --copy-to=debug-pod

<span class="comment"># Debug a node directly (creates a pod with host namespaces)</span>
kubectl debug node/&lt;node-name&gt; -it --image=busybox</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Ephemeral Containers vs kubectl exec</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                              │
│  REGULAR CONTAINER (distroless)        EPHEMERAL DEBUG CONTAINER             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐          ┌─────────────────────────┐           │
│  │  Your App (Go binary)   │          │  Debug Tools            │           │
│  │                         │          │  • /bin/sh              │           │
│  │  ❌ No /bin/sh          │  ◄──────▶│  • curl, wget           │           │
│  │  ❌ No curl, dig        │  shares  │  • dig, nslookup        │           │
│  │  ❌ No package manager  │  process │  • tcpdump, strace      │           │
│  │                         │  space   │  • ps, netstat          │           │
│  └─────────────────────────┘          └─────────────────────────┘           │
│                                                                              │
│  💡 The ephemeral container shares the same PID namespace, network,         │
│     and IPC namespace — you can see the app's processes, files, and         │
│     network connections without modifying the original container.            │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  Ephemeral containers CANNOT be removed once added — they stay          │
│     until the pod is deleted. They are never restarted.                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                <div class="scenario-box positive">
                    <h5>✅ kubectl exec — Standard Debugging</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Works when container has a shell</li>
                        <li>Direct access to running process</li>
                        <li>Can modify files, check network</li>
                        <li>Best for: standard images (nginx, python, node)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🔍 kubectl debug — Advanced Debugging</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox</code></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Works on <strong>distroless</strong> images (no shell)</li>
                        <li>Injects a sidecar with debug tools</li>
                        <li>Shares process/network namespace</li>
                        <li>Best for: Go binaries, scratch images, production pods</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §31.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s31-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">31.4 Debugging easypj — Common Scenarios</h3>
                <p>Let's apply the debugging toolkit to real-world scenarios using our easypj Django application. Each scenario follows the <strong>identify → gather → analyze → fix → verify</strong> flow.</p>

                <h4>Scenario 1: easypj Pod in CrashLoopBackOff</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔴 CrashLoopBackOff — Database Connection Refused</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP-BY-STEP WALKTHROUGH                                               │
│                                                                         │
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME                  READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE     │
│     easypj-web-7d4f8c5    0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5          10m     │
│     easypj-db-0           1/1     Running            0          10m     │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Check logs from the PREVIOUS crash:                        │
│     $ kubectl logs easypj-web-7d4f8c5 -n easypj --previous             │
│                                                                         │
│     django.db.utils.OperationalError:                                   │
│       could not connect to server: Connection refused               ◄── │
│       Is the server running on host "easypj-db" and                     │
│       accepting TCP/IP connections on port 5432?                        │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE:                                                            │
│     → The web pod starts before the DB pod is ready                     │
│     → Django tries to connect to PostgreSQL → fails → crashes           │
│     → Kubernetes restarts it → same thing → CrashLoopBackOff            │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Add an init container that waits for DB:                      │
│     initContainers:                                                     │
│     - name: wait-for-db                                                 │
│       image: busybox                                                    │
│       command: ['sh', '-c',                                             │
│         'until nc -z easypj-db 5432; do sleep 2; done']                 │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj -w                                     │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   0/1   Init:0/1   0   5s    ← init running        │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   1/1   Running    0   15s   ← ✅ success          │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Scenario 2: easypj Pod Stuck in Pending</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🟡 Pending — Insufficient Memory</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP-BY-STEP WALKTHROUGH                                               │
│                                                                         │
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME                  READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE              │
│     easypj-web-xxxx       0/1     Pending   0          5m               │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj                    │
│     ...                                                                 │
│     Events:                                                             │
│       Warning  FailedScheduling  0/2 nodes available:                   │
│         2 Insufficient memory.  ◄── The clue is always in Events!       │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE:                                                            │
│     → Pod requests 2Gi memory but no node has that much free            │
│     → Check current requests vs capacity:                               │
│       $ kubectl describe node worker01 | grep -A5 "Allocated"           │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Reduce the request to fit available resources:                │
│     resources:                                                          │
│       requests:                                                         │
│         memory: "512Mi"    ← was 2Gi, reduced to fit                    │
│         cpu: "250m"                                                     │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   1/1   Running   0   30s   ← ✅ scheduled         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Scenario 3: easypj Running but 502 Errors</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🟢 Running but Broken — Django Not Binding Correctly</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP-BY-STEP WALKTHROUGH                                               │
│                                                                         │
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   1/1   Running   0   5m   ← Looks fine!           │
│                                                                         │
│     $ kubectl get svc -n easypj                                         │
│     easypj-svc   ClusterIP   10.96.45.12   80/TCP   ← Service exists   │
│                                                                         │
│     $ kubectl exec test-pod -- wget -qO- http://easypj-svc.easypj      │
│     → 502 Bad Gateway                                                   │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Shell into the pod:                                        │
│     $ kubectl exec -it easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -- /bin/bash           │
│     root@easypj:/app$ python manage.py runserver                        │
│     → Listening on http://127.0.0.1:8000/   ◄── PROBLEM!               │
│     → Django binds to localhost only!                                    │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE:                                                            │
│     → Django listens on 127.0.0.1 (localhost)                           │
│     → Service sends traffic to the container's pod IP                   │
│     → Connection refused because Django isn't listening on 0.0.0.0      │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Bind to all interfaces:                                       │
│     command: ["python", "manage.py", "runserver", "0.0.0.0:8000"]       │
│     Also add to settings.py:                                            │
│       ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['*']    <span class="comment"># or specific hostnames                  │</span>
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl exec test-pod -- wget -qO- http://easypj-svc.easypj      │
│     → 200 OK ✅                                                         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🏋️ Practice Drill: Debug These Pods</h4>
                    <div class="drill-steps">
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">1</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Create a pod with a wrong image name:</strong>
                                <code>kubectl run broken --image=nginxxx:latest</code>
                                <p>Debug: What status? What does <code>describe</code> show?</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">2</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Create a pod that crashes immediately:</strong>
                                <code>kubectl run crasher --image=busybox -- /bin/sh -c "exit 1"</code>
                                <p>Debug: Use <code>--previous</code> flag. What exit code?</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">3</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Create a pod with impossible resource request:</strong>
                                <code>kubectl run toobig --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml | sed 's/resources: {}/resources:\n          requests:\n            memory: "100Gi"/' | kubectl apply -f -</code>
                                <p>Debug: Why is it Pending? Check <code>describe</code> Events.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 31 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Pod Debugging Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Pod status decode</strong> — <code>ImagePullBackOff</code> = wrong image name/tag or no registry access. <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> = container starts then exits (check logs + exit code). <code>Pending</code> = scheduling issue (resources, taints, nodeSelector). <code>Init:Error</code> = init container failed</li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl logs</code> shows CURRENT container</strong> — If the container crashed and restarted, use <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> to see the LAST crash output. Without <code>--previous</code> you see the new (maybe empty) logs</li>
                        <li><strong>Multi-container pods need <code>-c</code></strong> — <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code>. Without it, you get an error if there are multiple containers. Use <code>kubectl describe pod</code> to find container names</li>
                        <li><strong>Exit codes matter</strong> — Exit 0 = success (Completed). Exit 1 = application error. Exit 137 = OOMKilled (needs more memory). Exit 139 = segfault. The exam expects you to interpret these</li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl debug</code> for distroless images</strong> — <code>kubectl debug -it &lt;pod&gt; --image=busybox --target=&lt;container&gt;</code> attaches a debug container sharing the process namespace. Use this when the pod has no shell</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>


                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <strong>⚡ Pod debugging commands:</strong>
                    <code>kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running</code> — find non-running pods<br>
                    <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --all-containers=true</code> — logs from all containers<br>
                    <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --since=5m</code> — last 5 minutes only<br>
                    <code>kubectl top pod &lt;name&gt;</code> — CPU/memory usage (needs metrics-server)<br>
                    <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.status.containerStatuses[0].state}'</code> — detailed state
                </div>

                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 31 — How Pod Lifecycle States and Status Codes Work ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 31 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Pod Lifecycle States and Status Codes Actually Work: The Full Finita State Machine, Container Exit Code Meanings, and CrashLoopBackOff Internals</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Pod Lifecycle — The Complete State Machine</h5>
            <p>A pod goes through a precise sequence of phases. Understanding each phase and transition helps you diagnose exactly where things went wrong.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  POD LIFECYCLE STATE MACHINE                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐      │
│  │ Pending  │───▶│ ContainerCreating│───▶│ Running  │───▶│Succeeded │      │
│  │          │    │                  │    │          │    │  (exit 0)│      │
│  └────┬─────┘    └────────┬─────────┘    └────┬─────┘    └──────────┘      │
│       │                   │                  │                                │
│       │                   │                  │                                │
│       │         ┌─────────▼──────────┐       │                                │
│       │         │ ImagePullBackOff   │       │                                │
│       │         │ (image pull fails) │       │                                │
│       │         └─────────┬──────────┘       │                                │
│       │                   │                  │                                │
│       │         ┌─────────▼──────────┐       │                                │
│       │         │ CrashLoopBackOff   │◀──────┘                                │
│       │         │ (container exits   │                                         │
│       │         │  non-zero, repeats)│                                         │
│       │         └────────┬──────────┘                                         │
│       │                  │                                                     │
│       │         ┌────────▼──────────┐                                         │
│       │         │     Failed        │                                         │
│       │         │ (backoff limit    │                                         │
│       │         │  exceeded)         │                                         │
│       │         └───────────────────┘                                         │
│       │                                                                        │
│  ┌────▼──────────┐                                                            │
│  │   Unknown     │  <span class="comment">← Node lost contact with API Server                       </span>│
│  │ (node down)   │                                                            │
│  └───────────────┘                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CRASHLOOPBACKOFF INTERNALS — Exponential Backoff Algorithm:                 │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Container exits → kubelet restarts → fails again → repeat            │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │ Backoff timing (exponential with cap):                                │   │
│  │   Crash 1: wait 10s → restart                                        │   │
│  │   Crash 2: wait 20s → restart (10×2^1)                              │   │
│  │   Crash 3: wait 40s → restart (10×2^2)                              │   │
│  │   Crash 4: wait 80s → restart                                        │   │
│  │   Crash 5: wait 160s → restart                                       │   │
│  │   Crash 6+: wait 300s (5min cap) → restart                           │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │  Duration: backoff resets after pod runs successfully for 10min     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CONTAINER EXIT CODES — Quick Reference:                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ CODE  MEANING              CAUSE               DEBUG COMMAND          │   │
│  │ ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │   │
│  │  0    Success              Normal completion    No action needed      │   │
│  │  1    Application error    App crashed           kubectl logs         │   │
│  │  126  Command not exec     Wrong CMD/ENTRYPOINT  kubectl describe     │   │
│  │  127  Command not found    Missing binary         kubectl logs         │   │
│  │  137  SIGKILL (OOM)        Memory limit exceeded  Describe→OOMKilled  │   │
│  │  139  SIGSEGV              Segmentation fault     kubectl logs         │   │
│  │  143  SIGTERM              Graceful termination   Normal shutdown      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  DEBUGGING TOOLKIT PRIORITY (CKA Speed):                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. kubectl get pods -o wide           <span class="comment">← Which node? Status? Restarts?</span>│   │
│  │ 2. kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;        <span class="comment">← Events, state, exit codes   </span>│   │
│  │ 3. kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous       <span class="comment">← Previous crashed container   </span>│   │
│  │ 4. kubectl exec &lt;pod&gt; -- &lt;cmd&gt;         <span class="comment">← Run diagnostic commands     </span>│   │
│  │ 5. kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp <span class="comment">← Cluster perspective </span>│   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Troubleshooting is 30% of the CKA exam. The golden debugging sequence: <code>kubectl get pods</code> → <code>kubectl describe pod</code> → <code>kubectl logs</code> → <code>kubectl exec</code>. Exit code 137 ALWAYS means OOMKilled. Exit code 1 is app error (check logs). CrashLoopBackOff means kubelet is retrying with exponential backoff — check logs of the PREVIOUS container instance with <code>--previous</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Immediately editing the pod YAML without checking logs first</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Always run <code>describe</code> → <code>logs</code> → <code>events</code> before making changes</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Using <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> for a crashed container</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Using <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> to see the last crashed container's output</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Forgetting to check the Events section at the bottom of <code>describe</code></li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Scroll to the very bottom — Events show scheduling/pulling/startup timeline</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Pod debug flow (memorize):</strong><br>
                1. <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o wide</code> — status + node<br>
                2. <code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> — events at bottom tell the story<br>
                3. <code>kubectl logs &lt;name&gt; [--previous]</code> — app-level errors<br>
                4. <code>kubectl exec -it &lt;name&gt; -- sh</code> — get inside and investigate<br>
                5. <code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> — cluster-wide event timeline</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-commands"></span>
            <h3 class="section-h">🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 31)</h3>
            <p><strong>Exit 0</strong> = Success (normal completion). <strong>Exit 1</strong> = Application error. <strong>Exit 137</strong> = OOM Killed (128 + signal 9). <strong>Exit 139</strong> = Segmentation fault. <strong>Exit 143</strong> = Graceful termination (128 + signal 15 SIGTERM).</p>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 31 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch31-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 31 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Fast pod &amp; container debugging recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is in <code>CrashLoopBackOff</code> state. Walk through the debugging process.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pod status</span>
kubectl get pod crash-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS            RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># crash-pod  0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  5         3m</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check PREVIOUS container logs</span>
kubectl logs crash-pod --previous
<span class="comment"># Error: connection refused to database at db:5432</span>
<span class="comment"># ← App started but couldn't connect to DB</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check current logs (may be empty if crash is instant)</span>
kubectl logs crash-pod
<span class="comment"># (empty — container crashed before logging)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check describe for events and exit code</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-pod
<span class="comment"># Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#   Exit Code: 1          ← App error (non-zero exit)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Reason:    Error</span>
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Back-off restarting failed container</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Decode exit code</span>
<span class="comment"># 0   = Success (Completed)</span>
<span class="comment"># 1   = Generic app error</span>
<span class="comment"># 137 = OOMKilled (need more memory)</span>
<span class="comment"># 139 = Segfault</span>
<span class="comment"># 143 = SIGTERM (graceful termination)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Fix based on cause</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit 1 → Fix app config, env vars, dependencies</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit 137 → Increase memory limits</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit 139 → Fix application bug</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CrashLoopBackOff = container starts, crashes, Kubernetes restarts it with exponential backoff. Key: use <code>--previous</code> to see the LAST crash's logs (current container may not have logged anything yet). Exit codes tell the story: 1 = app error, 137 = OOM (out of memory), 139 = segfault. On the CKA, always check describe for events AND exit code, then logs with <code>--previous</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is stuck in <code>Pending</code> state. What are the possible causes and how do you diagnose?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Describe the pod — check Events</span>
kubectl describe pod pending-pod | tail -20
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment"># Warning  FailedScheduling  ...</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 1: Insufficient resources</span>
<span class="comment"># "0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient cpu"</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Lower resource requests OR add nodes</span>
kubectl get pod pending-pod -o yaml | grep -A5 resources

<span class="comment"># Cause 2: NodeSelector doesn't match</span>
<span class="comment"># "0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) didn't match Pod's node selector"</span>
kubectl get pod pending-pod -o yaml | grep -A3 nodeSelector
kubectl get nodes --show-labels | grep &lt;label&gt;
<span class="comment"># Fix: Add label to node OR fix nodeSelector</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 3: Taints with no toleration</span>
<span class="comment"># "0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) had taint {key: NoSchedule}"</span>
kubectl describe nodes | grep Taints
<span class="comment"># Fix: Add tolerations OR remove taint</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 4: PVC not bound</span>
<span class="comment"># "persistentvolumeclaim "data-pvc" not found" OR PVC Pending</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># Fix: Create PV or fix StorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># Cause 5: SA doesn't exist</span>
<span class="comment"># "serviceaccount "missing-sa" not found"</span>
kubectl get sa -n &lt;namespace&gt;
<span class="comment"># Fix: Create the ServiceAccount</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick diagnosis:</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector type=Warning \
--sort-by=.lastTimestamp | head -10</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pending = scheduler can't place the pod. Five main causes: insufficient resources, nodeSelector mismatch, taints without toleration, unbound PVC, missing ServiceAccount. On the CKA, <code>kubectl describe pod</code> Events section ALWAYS explains why. The FailedScheduling message lists: how many nodes exist, why each was rejected. Read this message carefully — it's your diagnosis.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod shows <code>ImagePullBackOff</code>. How do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Describe to find the exact error</span>
kubectl describe pod img-pod | grep -A5 "Events"
<span class="comment"># Warning  Failed  ...  Failed to pull image "ngnix:latest"</span>
<span class="comment"># Warning  Failed  ...  rpc error: pull access denied</span>

<span class="comment"># Diagnosis by error message:</span>
<span class="comment"># "manifest unknown"     → Wrong tag (e.g., ngnix instead of nginx)</span>
<span class="comment"># "pull access denied"   → Private registry, no credentials</span>
<span class="comment"># "no such host"         → Wrong registry URL</span>
<span class="comment"># "i/o timeout"          → Network issue</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 1: Wrong image name/tag</span>
kubectl set image pod/img-pod app=nginx:1.25
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
kubectl edit pod img-pod  <span class="comment"># Fix image name</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 2: Private registry credentials</span>
kubectl create secret docker-registry regcred \
--docker-server=registry.example.com \
--docker-username=user \
--docker-password=pass + then add imagePullSecrets

<span class="comment"># Fix 3: Check if image exists</span>
<span class="comment"># From a node with network:</span>
crictl pull nginx:1.25
<span class="comment"># If this works, the image name is correct</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick fix: delete and recreate with correct image</span>
kubectl delete pod img-pod
kubectl run img-pod --image=nginx:1.25</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ImagePullBackOff = can't download the container image. Three main causes: (1) typo in image name/tag (most common on CKA), (2) private registry without credentials, (3) network issues. On the CKA, the fix is usually a simple typo: <code>ngnix</code> → <code>nginx</code>, or wrong tag. <code>kubectl set image</code> is the fastest fix. Check the Events section for the exact error message — it tells you exactly what failed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>kubectl debug</code> to troubleshoot a pod with a distroless image (no shell)?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Problem: Pod has no shell (distroless/scratch image)</span>
kubectl exec -it app-pod -- sh
<span class="comment"># OCI runtime exec failed: exec failed: unable to start</span>
<span class="comment"># container process: exec: "sh": executable file not found</span>

<span class="comment"># Solution: Ephemeral debug container</span>
kubectl debug -it app-pod \
--image=busybox \
--target=app-container
<span class="comment"># Attaches a busybox container that shares:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Process namespace (can see app processes)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Network namespace (same IP, ports)</span>

<span class="comment"># Inside the debug container:</span>
ps aux  <span class="comment"># See app processes</span>
netstat -tlnp  <span class="comment"># See listening ports</span>
wget -O- localhost:8080  <span class="comment"># Test app endpoint</span>
cat /proc/1/environ | tr '\0' '\n'  <span class="comment"># See env vars</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: Copy pod for debugging</span>
kubectl debug app-pod -it \
--image=busybox \
--copy-to=debug-pod \
--share-processes
<span class="comment"># Creates a copy of the pod with debug container added</span>

<span class="comment"># Debug a node:</span>
kubectl debug node/worker-1 -it --image=ubuntu
<span class="comment"># Creates a privileged pod on the node</span>
<span class="comment"># Root filesystem at /host</span>
chroot /host  <span class="comment"># Full node access</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl debug</code> adds an ephemeral container to a running pod without restarting it. Use <code>--target=</code> to share the process namespace with a specific container. On the CKA, this is the way to debug distroless images. The debug container sees the app's processes and network. <code>kubectl debug node/</code> creates a privileged pod on a node for node-level debugging. The <code>--copy-to</code> option creates a copy, preserving the original pod.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check why a container was OOMKilled and fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Identify OOMKilled:</span>
kubectl describe pod oom-pod
<span class="comment"># Containers:</span>
<span class="comment">#   app:</span>
<span class="comment">#     Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#       Reason:    OOMKilled    ← Out of Memory!</span>
<span class="comment">#       Exit Code: 137          ← 128 + 9 (SIGKILL)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current memory usage:</span>
kubectl top pod oom-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME      CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># oom-pod   50m         490Mi  ← Close to limit!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check memory limits:</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o yaml | grep -A3 limits
<span class="comment"># limits:</span>
<span class="comment">#   memory: 512Mi  ← 490Mi usage / 512Mi limit = 95%!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Increase memory limit</span>
kubectl patch pod oom-pod -p \
'{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"app","resources":{"limits":{"memory":"1Gi"}}}]}}'
<span class="comment"># Error: Pod spec changes not allowed (immutable field)</span>

<span class="comment"># Must delete and recreate:</span>
kubectl get pod oom-pod -o yaml > oom-fix.yaml
<span class="comment"># Edit: Change memory limit from 512Mi to 1Gi</span>
vi oom-fix.yaml
kubectl delete pod oom-pod
kubectl apply -f oom-fix.yaml

<span class="comment"># For Deployments (easier):</span>
kubectl set resources deployment/web \
--limits=memory=1Gi --requests=memory=512Mi
<span class="comment"># Triggers rolling update with new limits</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>OOMKilled = container exceeded its memory limit. Exit code 137 = 128 + 9 (SIGKILL). On the CKA: (1) identify via <code>describe</code> (Reason: OOMKilled), (2) check current usage with <code>kubectl top</code>, (3) compare to limits, (4) increase memory limit. Pod resource specs are immutable — you must delete and recreate. For Deployments, use <code>kubectl set resources</code> which triggers a rolling update. Common CKA trap: confusing memory requests (scheduling) with limits (enforcement).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you get logs from all containers in a multi-container pod?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># List containers in the pod:</span>
kubectl get pod multi-pod -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'
<span class="comment"># app sidecar</span>

<span class="comment"># Logs from specific container:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod -c app
kubectl logs multi-pod -c sidecar

<span class="comment"># Logs from ALL containers:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod --all-containers=true

<span class="comment"># Stream ALL containers:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod --all-containers=true -f

<span class="comment"># Previous crash logs for specific container:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod -c app --previous

<span class="comment"># Last N lines from all containers:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod --all-containers --tail=50

<span class="comment"># Logs from all pods matching a label:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --all-containers
<span class="comment"># (max 5 pods by default)</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --all-containers --max-log-requests=20

<span class="comment"># Init container logs:</span>
kubectl logs multi-pod -c init-container-name

<span class="comment"># Check which container is failing:</span>
kubectl describe pod multi-pod | grep -A10 "Container.*:"</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Multi-container pods require <code>-c</code> to specify which container's logs to view. <code>--all-containers=true</code> shows logs from every container. On the CKA, if you see "a]]container name must be specified" error, the pod has multiple containers — use <code>-c</code>. Init container logs: same syntax with <code>-c init-name</code>. <code>-l</code> flag retrieves logs from multiple pods by label selector. Default limit is 5 concurrent pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod is running but the application inside isn't responding. How do you debug?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Pod shows Running but app doesn't work:</span>
kubectl get pod app-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME      READY  STATUS   RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># app-pod   1/1    Running  0         10m</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check app logs</span>
kubectl logs app-pod
<span class="comment"># [ERROR] Failed to bind to port 8080: address already in use</span>
<span class="comment"># ← App started but errored internally</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Exec into the pod</span>
kubectl exec -it app-pod -- sh
<span class="comment"># Inside:</span>
curl localhost:8080  <span class="comment"># Test from inside</span>
netstat -tlnp        <span class="comment"># Check listening ports</span>
ps aux               <span class="comment"># Verify process is running</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check readiness probe</span>
kubectl describe pod app-pod | grep -A5 Readiness
<span class="comment"># Readiness probe failed: connection refused</span>
<span class="comment"># READY shows 0/1 → Service won't send traffic</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check liveness probe</span>
kubectl describe pod app-pod | grep -A5 Liveness
<span class="comment"># If liveness fails → pod gets restarted</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check environment variables</span>
kubectl exec app-pod -- env | sort
<span class="comment"># Missing DB_HOST? Wrong PORT?</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check if service endpoints are populated</span>
kubectl get endpoints service-name
<span class="comment"># If empty → selector doesn't match pod labels</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix common issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># Wrong port in probe → kubectl edit pod</span>
<span class="comment"># Missing env var → fix ConfigMap/Secret</span>
<span class="comment"># App crash silent → check app-level config</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Running ≠ working. A pod can be Running but the app inside may be broken. On the CKA: check logs first (app-level errors), then exec in to test connectivity, then check probes (readiness/liveness), then verify environment variables. If READY shows 0/1, the readiness probe is failing — the Service won't send traffic to this pod. Common CKA scenario: probe targets wrong port or path.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you find all non-running pods across all namespaces?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: Field selector (fastest)</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE   NAME          READY  STATUS            RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># default     broken-pod    0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  8</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system missing-img   0/1    ImagePullBackOff  0</span>
<span class="comment"># prod        pending-pod   0/1    Pending           0</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Include Succeeded (completed jobs)</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running,status.phase!=Succeeded

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Custom columns for more detail</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase!=Running \
-o custom-columns=\
'NS:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,STATUS:.status.phase,NODE:.spec.nodeName'

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Sort by restart count (find crashers)</span>
kubectl get pods -A --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'

<span class="comment"># Method 5: Events for all failing pods</span>
kubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning \
--sort-by=.lastTimestamp | head -20

<span class="comment"># Quick health check:</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running | grep -v Completed</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--field-selector=status.phase!=Running</code> finds all non-running pods instantly. On the CKA, start troubleshooting by finding what's broken. <code>-A</code> for all namespaces. Sort by restart count to find pods that keep crashing. The events command with <code>type=Warning</code> shows what's going wrong cluster-wide. Quick grep: <code>| grep -v Running | grep -v Completed</code> filters to only problem pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check why a container exited with a specific exit code?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Exit Code</th>
                                        <th>Meaning</th>
                                        <th>Common Cause</th>
                                        <th>Fix</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>0</td>
                                        <td>Success</td>
                                        <td>Process completed normally</td>
                                        <td>Expected for Jobs/init containers</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>1</td>
                                        <td>Generic error</td>
                                        <td>App crash, missing config</td>
                                        <td>Check logs</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>126</td>
                                        <td>Cannot execute</td>
                                        <td>Permission denied</td>
                                        <td>Check command/entrypoint</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>127</td>
                                        <td>Command not found</td>
                                        <td>Wrong command in spec</td>
                                        <td>Fix command/args</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>137</td>
                                        <td>SIGKILL (128+9)</td>
                                        <td>OOMKilled or manual kill</td>
                                        <td>Increase memory limits</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>139</td>
                                        <td>SIGSEGV (128+11)</td>
                                        <td>Segmentation fault</td>
                                        <td>Fix app bug</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>143</td>
                                        <td>SIGTERM (128+15)</td>
                                        <td>Graceful termination</td>
                                        <td>Normal during rollouts</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Get exit code:</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-pod | grep "Exit Code"
<span class="comment">#   Exit Code:    137</span>

<span class="comment"># OR via jsonpath:</span>
kubectl get pod crash-pod -o jsonpath=\
'{.status.containerStatuses[0].lastState.terminated.exitCode}'
<span class="comment"># 137</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Exit codes above 128 mean the process was killed by a signal (exit code = 128 + signal number). On the CKA: 137 = OOMKilled (SIGKILL), 143 = graceful shutdown (SIGTERM), 1 = app error. Use <code>kubectl describe</code> or jsonpath to get the exit code. Combined with <code>--previous</code> logs, the exit code tells you exactly what happened and guides the fix.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod's init container is failing. How do you diagnose and fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Identify: Pod status shows Init:Error or Init:CrashLoopBackOff</span>
kubectl get pod init-pod
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS         RESTARTS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># init-pod   0/1    Init:Error     3         2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Find init container name</span>
kubectl describe pod init-pod | grep -A10 "Init Containers"
<span class="comment"># Init Containers:</span>
<span class="comment">#   wait-for-db:</span>
<span class="comment">#     State:    Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#     Reason:   Error</span>
<span class="comment">#     Exit Code: 1</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check init container logs</span>
kubectl logs init-pod -c wait-for-db
<span class="comment"># Connection refused: db-service:5432</span>
<span class="comment"># ← The database isn't ready yet</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Common init container issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Waiting for a service that doesn't exist</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong service name or port</span>
<span class="comment"># - DNS not resolving (service in different namespace)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing ConfigMap/Secret referenced by init container</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix</span>
<span class="comment"># Check if the dependency service exists:</span>
kubectl get svc db-service
<span class="comment"># If missing: create it</span>
<span class="comment"># If wrong namespace: use FQDN (db-service.other-ns.svc.cluster.local)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify init container succeeds</span>
kubectl get pod init-pod -w
<span class="comment"># Init:0/1 → PodInitializing → Running ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Remember: Init containers run SEQUENTIALLY</span>
<span class="comment"># If init-1 fails, init-2 never starts</span>
<span class="comment"># Main containers don't start until ALL inits succeed</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Init containers run before main containers and must complete successfully. Status shows <code>Init:Error</code> or <code>Init:CrashLoopBackOff</code>. Use <code>-c init-container-name</code> to get init container logs. On the CKA, init containers typically wait for a dependency (database, config). If the dependency doesn't exist, the init container loops. Fix the dependency → init succeeds → main containers start. Init containers run sequentially — if one fails, the rest don't execute.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 31 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch31 -->

        <article class="chapter" id="ch32">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 32 · Troubleshooting Nodes &amp; Cluster Components</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Node Not Ready diagnosis, kubelet troubleshooting, control plane component recovery, and networking issues. These skills are <strong>critical for the CKA exam</strong>.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔧 Troubleshoot</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Diagnosing and recovering Node NotReady conditions</li>
                    <li>✅ kubelet troubleshooting — service status, logs, configuration</li>
                    <li>✅ Control plane component recovery — API server, scheduler, controller-manager, etcd</li>
                    <li>✅ Network troubleshooting — DNS, Services, kube-proxy, CNI plugins</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Cluster Architecture <span class="cka-weight">25%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 Node &amp; Control-Plane Failures Are Classic CKA Tasks</h5>
                <p>A NotReady node or a dead control-plane component is a high-value exam scenario. Debug from the node up — the fix is almost always kubelet, the container runtime, or a broken static-pod manifest:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code> — spot the NotReady node first</li>
                    <li><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> / <code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> — the #1 source of NotReady</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code> — read the Conditions (MemoryPressure, DiskPressure)</li>
                    <li><code>crictl ps</code> / <code>crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code> — inspect containers when kubectl can't reach the API</li>
                    <li><code>ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> — a malformed static-pod YAML takes out apiserver/etcd</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <!-- ─────────── §32.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.1 Node Not Ready — Diagnosis</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>A node in <code>NotReady</code> state means the kubelet has stopped reporting to the API server. Your job is to find out <strong>why</strong> and fix it.</p>

                <h4>Common NotReady Causes</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🛑</div>
                        <h5>kubelet Stopped</h5>
                        <p>The most common cause. kubelet crashed or was stopped. Fix: <code>systemctl start kubelet</code> and <code>systemctl enable kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔌</div>
                        <h5>CNI Plugin Missing</h5>
                        <p>No network plugin installed or CNI binary missing. Pods can't get IPs. Fix: Install/reinstall the CNI plugin (Calico, Flannel, etc.).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💾</div>
                        <h5>Swap Enabled</h5>
                        <p>kubelet refuses to start if swap is on (default). Fix: <code>swapoff -a</code> and remove swap entry from <code>/etc/fstab</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>containerd Down</h5>
                        <p>Container runtime crashed. No containers can be created. Fix: <code>systemctl restart containerd</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Systematic Node NotReady Fix</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>SSH to Node</h5>
                        <p><code>ssh worker01</code> — You can't fix node issues remotely via kubectl. You must be on the node.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Check kubelet</h5>
                        <p><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — Is it active? If dead: <code>systemctl start kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Read Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50</code> — Error messages reveal the root cause.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Fix &amp; Restart</h5>
                        <p>Fix the underlying issue (config, certs, swap, containerd) then <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>Verify</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl get nodes</code> — Wait for node to transition from NotReady to Ready (may take 30-60s).</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔍 Node Troubleshooting Flowchart</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  $ kubectl get nodes                                                         │
│  NAME           STATUS     ROLES           AGE   VERSION                     │
│  controlplane   Ready      control-plane   30d   v1.31.0                    │
│  worker01       NotReady   &lt;none&gt;          30d   v1.31.0  ◄── PROBLEM      │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ▼                                                                      │
│  $ kubectl describe node worker01                                            │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ├── Conditions Section:                                                │
│       │   ┌──────────────────┬────────┬──────────────────────────────┐       │
│       │   │ Condition        │ Status │ What It Means                │       │
│       │   ├──────────────────┼────────┼──────────────────────────────┤       │
│       │   │ Ready            │ False  │ kubelet not reporting        │       │
│       │   │ MemoryPressure   │ True   │ Node running out of RAM     │       │
│       │   │ DiskPressure     │ True   │ Node running out of disk    │       │
│       │   │ PIDPressure      │ True   │ Too many processes          │       │
│       │   │ NetworkUnavail.  │ True   │ CNI plugin not working      │       │
│       │   └──────────────────┴────────┴──────────────────────────────┘       │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ▼                                                                      │
│  SSH into the node: $ ssh worker01                                           │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ├── $ systemctl status kubelet                                         │
│       │    ├── active (running) → kubelet is up, check network              │
│       │    └── inactive (dead)  → kubelet crashed or was stopped            │
│       │                                                                      │
│       ├── $ journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50                      │
│       │    └── Look for error messages about certificates, config, etc.     │
│       │                                                                      │
│       └── Common Fixes:                                                      │
│            ├── Kubelet stopped   → systemctl start kubelet                   │
│            ├── Kubelet disabled  → systemctl enable --now kubelet            │
│            ├── Container runtime → systemctl restart containerd              │
│            ├── Swap enabled      → swapoff -a                                │
│            └── Wrong config      → check /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml       │
│                                                                              │
│  VERIFY: $ kubectl get nodes   → worker01 should show Ready                 │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                    <h5>⚠️ Node Conditions vs Node Status</h5>
                    <p><strong>Status</strong> (Ready/NotReady) is the overall health. <strong>Conditions</strong> (MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, etc.) tell you <em>why</em> a node is unhealthy. A node is Ready only when ALL pressure conditions are False and kubelet is communicating with the API server.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Node Diagnosis Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Get node status overview</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Describe the problematic node</span>
kubectl describe node worker01

<span class="comment"># Key areas to check in the output:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Conditions     → Ready, MemoryPressure, DiskPressure</span>
<span class="comment"># - Capacity       → Total CPU, memory, pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - Allocatable    → Available for pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - Allocated      → Currently used by pods</span>
<span class="comment"># - Events         → Recent node events</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: SSH to node and check kubelet</span>
ssh worker01
systemctl status kubelet
journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -l | tail -50

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check container runtime</span>
systemctl status containerd
crictl ps -a                    <span class="comment"># List all containers on the node</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check disk and memory</span>
df -h                           <span class="comment"># Disk usage</span>
free -m                         <span class="comment"># Memory usage</span>
top                             <span class="comment"># Process and CPU overview</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §32.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.2 Troubleshooting kubelet</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>kubelet is the <strong>only component that runs as a systemd service</strong> (not a static pod). It's the node agent — if kubelet is down, the node goes NotReady.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>kubelet — The Node Agent Architecture</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────── Worker Node ─────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │                    kubelet (systemd service)                 │        │
│  │                                                             │        │
│  │  📡 Reports to API Server every 10s (node heartbeat)        │        │
│  │  📋 Watches for pod assignments from Scheduler              │        │
│  │  🔄 Manages pod lifecycle via Container Runtime             │        │
│  │  💉 Runs health probes (liveness, readiness, startup)       │        │
│  │  📊 Collects metrics for Metrics Server                     │        │
│  │                                                             │        │
│  │  Config: /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml                       │        │
│  │  Certs:  /var/lib/kubelet/pki/                              │        │
│  │  Logs:   journalctl -u kubelet                              │        │
│  └────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                           │                                              │
│                           ▼                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐                       │
│  │         Container Runtime (containerd)        │                       │
│  │                                              │                       │
│  │  ┌─────┐  ┌─────┐  ┌─────┐  ┌─────┐        │                       │
│  │  │Pod A│  │Pod B│  │Pod C│  │Pod D│        │                       │
│  │  └─────┘  └─────┘  └─────┘  └─────┘        │                       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘                       │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Common kubelet Issues and Fixes</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Problem</th>
                                <th>Symptom</th>
                                <th>Diagnosis</th>
                                <th>Fix</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>kubelet stopped</td>
                                <td>Node goes NotReady</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl status kubelet</code> → inactive</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl start kubelet</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>kubelet disabled</td>
                                <td>Doesn't start after reboot</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl is-enabled kubelet</code> → disabled</td>
                                <td><code>systemctl enable kubelet</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Wrong config</td>
                                <td>kubelet crashes repeatedly</td>
                                <td><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → config errors</td>
                                <td>Fix <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Certificate expired</td>
                                <td>"x509: certificate has expired"</td>
                                <td><code>openssl x509 -in cert -noout -dates</code></td>
                                <td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Cannot reach API server</td>
                                <td>"connection refused" in kubelet logs</td>
                                <td>Check network, firewall, API server IP</td>
                                <td>Fix network/firewall or API server address in kubelet config</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Swap enabled</td>
                                <td>kubelet refuses to start</td>
                                <td><code>free -m</code> shows swap</td>
                                <td><code>swapoff -a</code> and remove from <code>/etc/fstab</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kubelet Troubleshooting — Step by Step</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ On the problematic node (SSH in first) ═══

<span class="comment"># 1. Check kubelet status</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Look for: active (running) vs inactive (dead) vs failed</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check kubelet logs for errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -50
journalctl -u kubelet -f                    <span class="comment"># Follow live</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. If kubelet is stopped, start it</span>
systemctl start kubelet
systemctl enable kubelet                    <span class="comment"># Persist across reboots</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check kubelet configuration</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># Verify: clusterDNS, clusterDomain, authentication/authorization</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Check kubelet certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem \
  -noout -text | grep -A2 "Validity"

<span class="comment"># 6. Restart kubelet after any config change</span>
systemctl daemon-reload                     <span class="comment"># If service file changed</span>
systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># 7. Verify from control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes                           <span class="comment"># Should show Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §32.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.3 Troubleshooting Control Plane Components</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Control plane components run as <strong>static pods</strong> managed by kubelet. Their manifests live in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. If a component is broken, check its manifest and logs.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Control Plane Components — Where They Live</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────── Control Plane Node ──────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                          │
│  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/           ← kubelet watches this directory   │
│  ├── kube-apiserver.yaml              ← API Server static pod           │
│  ├── kube-controller-manager.yaml     ← Controller Manager static pod   │
│  ├── kube-scheduler.yaml              ← Scheduler static pod            │
│  └── etcd.yaml                        ← etcd static pod                 │
│                                                                          │
│  How static pods work:                                                   │
│  ┌──────────┐    watches    ┌────────────────────┐                      │
│  │ kubelet  │ ──────────▶  │ /etc/kubernetes/   │                      │
│  │          │    dir for    │    manifests/      │                      │
│  │          │    changes    │                    │                      │
│  └────┬─────┘               └────────────────────┘                      │
│       │ creates/restarts                                                 │
│       ▼                                                                  │
│  ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐                   │
│  │API Server│ │Controller│ │Scheduler │ │  etcd   │                   │
│  │   Pod    │ │Mgr Pod   │ │   Pod    │ │   Pod   │                   │
│  └──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────┘                   │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️ If kubelet is down, static pods don't restart!                      │
│  ⚠️ If manifest has syntax error, static pod won't start!              │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Control Plane Troubleshooting Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># 1. CHECK — Are control plane pods running?</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Expected: kube-apiserver, kube-scheduler, kube-controller-manager, etcd</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. CHECK — Control plane pod logs</span>
kubectl logs kube-apiserver-controlplane -n kube-system
kubectl logs kube-scheduler-controlplane -n kube-system
kubectl logs kube-controller-manager-controlplane -n kube-system
kubectl logs etcd-controlplane -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. IF API SERVER IS DOWN — kubectl won't work!</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Use container runtime directly:</span>
crictl ps -a                               <span class="comment"># List all containers</span>
crictl ps -a | grep kube-apiserver         <span class="comment"># Find API server container</span>
crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;                 <span class="comment"># Read its logs</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. CHECK — Static pod manifests</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-controller-manager.yaml

<span class="comment"># 5. FIX — Edit manifest, kubelet auto-restarts the pod</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml
<span class="comment"># kubelet detects change → restarts the static pod automatically</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. VERIFY</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
kubectl get componentstatuses             <span class="comment"># Quick cluster health check</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Pro Tip: When API Server Is Down</h5>
                    <p>If the API server won't start, <code>kubectl</code> doesn't work at all. You must use <code>crictl</code> (the container runtime CLI) to check container status and logs directly. This is a <strong>common CKA exam scenario</strong> — they break the API server manifest and you need to fix it.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>✅ When kubectl Works</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n kube-system</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>🚨 When kubectl Is Dead</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><code>crictl ps -a | grep kube</code></li>
                            <li><code>crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;</code></li>
                            <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager</code></li>
                            <li><code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/*.yaml</code></li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🏋️ Practice: Break and Fix the Control Plane</h4>
                    <div class="drill-steps">
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">1</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Break the scheduler:</strong>
                                <code>sudo sed -i 's/--leader-elect=true/--leader-elect=BROKEN/' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml</code>
                                <p>Watch: <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -w</code> — scheduler pod will restart/crash</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">2</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Diagnose:</strong>
                                <code>kubectl logs kube-scheduler-controlplane -n kube-system</code>
                                <p>Find the error message pointing to the invalid flag</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">3</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Fix:</strong> Edit the manifest back to <code>--leader-elect=true</code>
                                <p>kubelet detects the change and restarts the scheduler automatically</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §32.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s32-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">32.4 Troubleshooting Networking</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Network problems manifest as pods that can't reach services, DNS failures, or external traffic not arriving. Systematic diagnosis is key.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Network Troubleshooting — Layer by Layer</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NETWORKING TROUBLESHOOTING LAYERS                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 1: POD-TO-POD                                                         │
│  ┌──────────┐              ┌──────────┐                                     │
│  │  Pod A   │──── CNI ────▶│  Pod B   │                                     │
│  │ 10.244.1 │   network    │ 10.244.2 │                                     │
│  └──────────┘              └──────────┘                                     │
│  Debug: kubectl exec podA -- ping &lt;podB-IP&gt;                                 │
│  Fix:   Check CNI plugin pods (Calico/Weave/Flannel) running in kube-system │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 2: POD-TO-SERVICE                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐  ┌───────────────┐  ┌──────────┐                             │
│  │  Pod A   │─▶│  Service      │─▶│  Pod B   │                             │
│  │          │  │  (ClusterIP)  │  │(endpoint)│                             │
│  └──────────┘  └───────────────┘  └──────────┘                             │
│  Debug: kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;   ← Are there endpoints?               │
│  Fix:   Service selector must match pod labels                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 3: DNS RESOLUTION                                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────────┐                              │
│  │  Pod A   │─▶│ CoreDNS  │─▶│ Service IP   │                              │
│  │nslookup  │  │(kube-dns)│  │  resolved    │                              │
│  └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────────┘                              │
│  Debug: kubectl exec pod -- nslookup &lt;svc-name&gt;                             │
│  Fix:   Check CoreDNS pods, ConfigMap, and kube-dns Service                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Layer 4: EXTERNAL ACCESS                                                    │
│  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐                   │
│  │ External │─▶│ NodePort │─▶│ Service  │─▶│  Pod     │                   │
│  │  Client  │  │  :30080  │  │ ClusterIP│  │(backend) │                   │
│  └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────┘  └──────────┘                   │
│  Debug: curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:&lt;nodeport&gt;                                    │
│  Fix:   Check firewall rules, kube-proxy, service type                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Network Debugging Commands</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ SERVICE NOT RESPONDING ═══
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check endpoints — are pods registered?</span>
kubectl get endpoints &lt;service-name&gt;
<span class="comment"># If &lt;none&gt;: service selector doesn't match any pod labels!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Compare service selector with pod labels</span>
kubectl get svc &lt;service-name&gt; -o yaml | grep -A5 selector
kubectl get pods --show-labels

<span class="comment"># ═══ DNS NOT WORKING ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Test DNS from inside a pod</span>
kubectl run dnstest --image=busybox --rm -it --restart=Never -- \
  nslookup kubernetes.default

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS ConfigMap for typos</span>
kubectl get cm coredns -n kube-system -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kube-dns Service ClusterIP</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># This IP should match /etc/resolv.conf inside pods</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ CNI PLUGIN ISSUES ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Check CNI plugin pods (Calico, Weave, Flannel, etc.)</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -i "calico\|weave\|flannel\|cilium"
kubectl get ds -n kube-system            <span class="comment"># CNI typically runs as DaemonSet</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ KUBE-PROXY ISSUES ═══</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep kube-proxy
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy manages iptables rules for Service → Pod routing</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>✅ Verify Your Node &amp; Cluster Troubleshooting Skills</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Can you check a node's kubelet status? → <code>ssh node; systemctl status kubelet</code></li>
                        <li>Can you view static pod manifests? → <code>ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></li>
                        <li>Can you debug when API server is down? → <code>crictl ps -a; crictl logs &lt;id&gt;</code></li>
                        <li>Can you test DNS from a pod? → <code>kubectl exec pod -- nslookup svc-name</code></li>
                        <li>Can you check if service has endpoints? → <code>kubectl get endpoints svc-name</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>kube-proxy Modes — How Service Routing Works</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Mode</th>
                                <th>How It Works</th>
                                <th>Performance</th>
                                <th>Check Command</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>iptables</code> (default)</td>
                                <td>Creates iptables rules for each Service → Pod mapping. Kernel-level packet routing.</td>
                                <td>Good — random pod selection. Scales to ~5K services.</td>
                                <td><code>iptables -t nat -L -n | grep &lt;service-clusterip&gt;</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>ipvs</code></td>
                                <td>Uses IPVS kernel module with hash tables. More efficient routing algorithms.</td>
                                <td>Better — supports round-robin, least-conn, etc. Scales to 10K+ services.</td>
                                <td><code>ipvsadm -Ln</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>nftables</code> (v1.31+)</td>
                                <td>Successor to iptables using nftables kernel subsystem. More efficient rule processing.</td>
                                <td>Best — modern kernel-level routing with better rule optimization.</td>
                                <td><code>nft list ruleset</code></td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Diagnosing kube-proxy Issues</h5>
                    <p>If services exist but traffic doesn't reach pods (and endpoints ARE populated), the problem may be kube-proxy. Check: <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy</code>. If kube-proxy is down, iptables rules become stale and services stop routing to new pods.</p>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 32 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Node &amp; Cluster Component Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Node NotReady top causes</strong> — (1) kubelet not running: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>. (2) kubelet config error: <code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code>. (3) CNI plugin missing: <code>ls /etc/cni/net.d/</code>. (4) Container runtime down: <code>systemctl status containerd</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Static pod manifests location</strong> — <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. If kube-apiserver.yaml has a typo, the API server crashes. Fix the YAML, kubelet auto-restarts the pod. The exam loves this pattern</li>
                        <li><strong><code>crictl</code> not <code>docker</code></strong> — Modern clusters use containerd. Debug with <code>crictl ps</code>, <code>crictl logs</code>, <code>crictl inspect</code>. On the CKA exam, docker CLI is NOT available</li>
                        <li><strong>kubelet runs as systemd service</strong> — <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> to restart. <code>journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -l</code> for full logs. Config file at <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Control plane component logs</strong> — <code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-apiserver-&lt;node&gt;</code> for API server. Same pattern for controller-manager, scheduler. If API server is down, use <code>crictl logs &lt;container-id&gt;</code></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 32 — How Node and Control Plane Troubleshooting Works ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 32 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Node and Control Plane Troubleshooting Works: NotReady Diagnosis, Component Health Checks, and the Systemd/CRI Debug Chain</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Node NotReady — Systematic Diagnosis Flow</h5>
            <p>A node going NotReady is one of the most common CKA scenarios. The kubelet stops reporting heartbeats, and the node controller marks it NotReady after ~40 seconds.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  NODE NOTREADY — Root Cause Diagnosis Tree                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  $ kubectl get nodes → worker-1   NotReady                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  LEVEL 1: Check kubelet (most common cause)                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ ssh worker-1                                                        │   │
│  │ $ sudo systemctl status kubelet   <span class="comment">← Is kubelet running?               </span>│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ┌───────────────────┐                                                │   │
│  │  │ kubelet STOPPED    │ → sudo systemctl start kubelet                │   │
│  │  │ (inactive/dead)   │    Check why: journalctl -u kubelet -n 50     │   │
│  │  └───────────────────┘                                                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ┌───────────────────┐                                                │   │
│  │  │ kubelet RUNNING    │ → Check kubelet logs for errors:              │   │
│  │  │ but node NotReady │    sudo journalctl -u kubelet -f               │   │
│  │  └───────────────────┘                                                │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Common kubelet issues:                                               │   │
│  │  • Container runtime (containerd) stopped                              │   │
│  │    → sudo systemctl status containerd                                  │   │
│  │  • Kubelet cert expired                                                │   │
│  │    → Look for "certificate expired" in journalctl                     │   │
│  │  • API Server unreachable                                              │   │
│  │    → Check: curl -k https://&lt;api-server&gt;:6443/healthz                │   │
│  │  • Node disk full                                                      │   │
│  │    → df -h (check /var, /var/lib/kubelet)                             │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  LEVEL 2: Check control plane components                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ $ kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -v Running                   │   │
│  │ → Any control plane pods crashing?                                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ $ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20          │   │
│  │ → Recent cluster-wide issues                                           │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ NODE CONTROLLER:                                                       │   │
│  │ 1. kubelet sends heartbeat to API Server every node-status-update-    │   │
│  │    frequency (default 10s)                                             │   │
│  │ 2. Node controller checks: last heartbeat < node-monitor-grace-period │   │
│  │    (default 40s)                                                       │   │
│  │ 3. If grace period exceeded → marks node NotReady                     │   │
│  │ 4. After pod-eviction-timeout (default 5m) → evicts pods              │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CONTROL PLANE COMPONENT FAILURE DIAGNOSIS:                                  │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ SYMPTOM                      LIKELY CAUSE         CHECK              │   │
│  │ ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  │   │
│  │ kubectl: connection refused  API Server down       crictl ps | grep  │   │
│  │                                                     kube-apiserver    │   │
│  │ kubectl: etcd errors         etcd unavailable       crictl ps | grep  │   │
│  │                                                     etcd              │   │
│  │ Controller manager logs      kubeconfig invalid    Check mount paths  │   │
│  │ "0/3 nodes available"        Scheduler down        Get scheduler pod  │   │
│  │ All pods pending              Scheduler down        Check scheduler    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Node troubleshooting is CKA core. Diagnostic sequence: <code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code> (check Conditions section) → <code>ssh node</code> → <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> → <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> → <code>systemctl status containerd</code>. If kubelet is running but node NotReady: check kubelet logs for certificate/API server errors. The `Conditions` section in `describe node` shows exactly why the node is NotReady (MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, PIDPressure, NetworkUnavailable).
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Looking for kubelet in <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system</code> — kubelet is NOT a pod</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Check kubelet via <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> on the node itself</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Forgetting <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> after editing service files</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Always run <code>daemon-reload</code> before <code>restart</code> if you modified a systemd unit</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Restarting kubelet without checking WHY it crashed</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Read <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> first to understand the root cause</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Node NotReady systematic fix:</strong><br>
                1. SSH into the node<br>
                2. <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> — is it running?<br>
                3. <code>journalctl -u kubelet -f</code> — what's the error?<br>
                4. Common fixes: <code>systemctl enable --now kubelet</code>, fix <code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>, reinstall CNI<br>
                5. <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code> after any fix</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 32)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 6 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. SSH to a worker node and stop kubelet: <code>systemctl stop kubelet</code></li>
                    <li>2. From control plane: <code>kubectl get nodes</code> — observe NotReady status (may take ~40s)</li>
                    <li>3. Diagnose: <code>ssh worker journalctl -u kubelet</code></li>
                    <li>4. Fix: <code>ssh worker systemctl start kubelet</code></li>
                    <li>5. Verify: <code>kubectl get nodes</code> — node returns to Ready</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 32 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch32-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 32 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Fast node &amp; control-plane troubleshooting recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node shows <code>NotReady</code>. Walk through the troubleshooting process.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Identify which node is NotReady</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS     ROLES    AGE  VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2   NotReady   &lt;none&gt;   30d  v1.31.0</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Describe the node for conditions</span>
kubectl describe node worker-2 | grep -A20 Conditions
<span class="comment"># MemoryPressure    False</span>
<span class="comment"># DiskPressure      False</span>
<span class="comment"># PIDPressure       False</span>
<span class="comment"># Ready             False  ← kubelet is NOT reporting</span>
<span class="comment">#   Last Heartbeat: 5 minutes ago</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: SSH to the node</span>
ssh worker-2

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kubelet status</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: inactive (dead)  ← Kubelet is stopped!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check kubelet logs for errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager
<span class="comment"># "failed to load kubelet config file"</span>
<span class="comment"># OR "unable to connect to API server"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Common fixes:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet stopped → systemctl start kubelet</span>
<span class="comment"># kubelet crashing → check config: /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># cert expired → check /var/lib/kubelet/pki/</span>
<span class="comment"># wrong API server → check /etc/kubernetes/kubelet.conf</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Start and enable kubelet</span>
systemctl start kubelet
systemctl enable kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify from control plane</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-2   Ready   &lt;none&gt;   30d  v1.31.0 ✓</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>NotReady = kubelet stopped sending heartbeats. On the CKA: SSH to the node, check <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>, check <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> for errors, fix the issue, restart kubelet. The most common CKA scenario: kubelet is stopped or has a config error. Always check the config file path and API server address. After fixing, verify with <code>kubectl get nodes</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The kubelet is running but keeps restarting. How do you diagnose the config issue?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kubelet status</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: activating (auto-restart)</span>
<span class="comment"># ← kubelet keeps crashing and systemd restarts it</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check journal logs</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -n 100 --no-pager
<span class="comment"># Look for error messages:</span>

<span class="comment"># Error A: "failed to load kubelet config file"</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># Check YAML syntax, missing fields</span>

<span class="comment"># Error B: "unable to load client CA file"</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/pki/ca.crt
<span class="comment"># Check if cert file exists and is readable</span>

<span class="comment"># Error C: "server.key: no such file or directory"</span>
<span class="comment"># Wrong cert/key path in kubelet config</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check kubelet systemd unit</span>
cat /etc/systemd/system/kubelet.service.d/10-kubeadm.conf
<span class="comment"># Look for wrong flags or paths</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify kubelet config</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># Common issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong clusterDNS IP</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong clusterDomain</span>
<span class="comment"># - Invalid YAML syntax</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix and restart</span>
vi /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml  <span class="comment"># Fix the issue</span>
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify</span>
systemctl status kubelet  <span class="comment"># Should show "active (running)"</span>
kubectl get nodes          <span class="comment"># Node should be Ready</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When kubelet keeps restarting (activating/auto-restart), it's usually a configuration problem. On the CKA, check <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> for the specific error message. Common causes: broken kubelet config YAML, missing/wrong cert paths, wrong systemd flags. Fix the config file, <code>daemon-reload</code>, restart kubelet. Always do <code>daemon-reload</code> if you changed the systemd unit file.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A static pod isn't starting on a node. How do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find static pod manifest directory</span>
<span class="comment"># Check kubelet config:</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml | grep staticPodPath
<span class="comment"># staticPodPath: /etc/kubernetes/manifests</span>

<span class="comment"># OR check kubelet flags:</span>
ps aux | grep kubelet | grep -- --pod-manifest-path

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check the manifest exists</span>
ls -la /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
<span class="comment"># etcd.yaml  kube-apiserver.yaml  kube-controller-manager.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler.yaml  my-static-pod.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Validate the manifest YAML</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/my-static-pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check for syntax errors</span>
<span class="comment"># Common issues:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong indentation</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing required fields (apiVersion, kind, spec)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong image name</span>
<span class="comment"># - Invalid port numbers</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check kubelet logs for static pod errors</span>
journalctl -u kubelet | grep "my-static-pod"
<span class="comment"># "failed to parse static pod manifest: ..."</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Fix the manifest</span>
vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/my-static-pod.yaml
<span class="comment"># Kubelet auto-detects changes — no restart needed!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
kubectl get pods -A | grep my-static-pod
<span class="comment"># my-static-pod-worker-1   1/1  Running  0  10s</span>

<span class="comment"># Remember: Static pod names have node name appended</span>
<span class="comment"># Static pods are managed by kubelet, NOT the scheduler</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Static pods are managed directly by the kubelet (not the API server). They read manifests from <code>staticPodPath</code> (default: <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>). On the CKA: if a static pod isn't working, check the YAML in the manifest directory. Kubelet watches the directory and auto-restarts when manifests change — no need to restart kubelet. Static pod names have the node name appended (e.g., <code>etcd-master</code>). Control plane components (etcd, apiserver, scheduler, controller-manager) are static pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>crictl</code> to troubleshoot container runtime issues on a node?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># crictl = CRI Container Runtime Interface tool</span>
<span class="comment"># Works with containerd, CRI-O (not Docker)</span>

<span class="comment"># SSH to the node first</span>
ssh worker-1

<span class="comment"># List running containers (like docker ps)</span>
crictl ps
<span class="comment"># CONTAINER ID  IMAGE     CREATED    STATE    NAME     POD ID</span>
<span class="comment"># a1b2c3d4e5    nginx     5min ago   Running  nginx    f6g7h8</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL containers (including stopped)</span>
crictl ps -a

<span class="comment"># Get container logs</span>
crictl logs a1b2c3d4e5

<span class="comment"># Inspect container details</span>
crictl inspect a1b2c3d4e5

<span class="comment"># List pods on this node</span>
crictl pods
<span class="comment"># POD ID    STATE  NAME         NAMESPACE  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># f6g7h8    Ready  web-pod      default    30m</span>

<span class="comment"># List images</span>
crictl images

<span class="comment"># Check container runtime socket</span>
crictl info
<span class="comment"># If error: "connect: no such file or directory"</span>
<span class="comment"># → Container runtime is down!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix container runtime:</span>
systemctl status containerd  <span class="comment"># check status</span>
systemctl restart containerd  <span class="comment"># restart if needed</span>
systemctl restart kubelet     <span class="comment"># restart kubelet after</span>

<span class="comment"># Pull an image manually (for debugging)</span>
crictl pull nginx:1.25</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>crictl</code> interacts with the container runtime directly on the node. On the CKA, use it when <code>kubectl</code> commands timeout (API server issue) or to debug container-level problems. Key commands: <code>crictl ps</code> (list containers), <code>crictl logs</code> (container logs), <code>crictl pods</code> (list pods on node). If <code>crictl info</code> fails, the container runtime is down. Restart containerd first, then kubelet.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check control plane component health?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: Check control plane pods in kube-system</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># NAME                              READY  STATUS   RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-master                       1/1    Running  0</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-apiserver-master             1/1    Running  0</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager-master    1/1    Running  0</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler-master             1/1    Running  0</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abcde          1/1    Running  0</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-12345                  1/1    Running  0</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Component status (deprecated but still works)</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses
<span class="comment"># NAME                 STATUS    MESSAGE</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler            Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager   Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-0               Healthy   ok</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check API server directly</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://10.0.0.1:6443</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS is running at https://10.0.0.1:6443/api/v1/namespaces/...</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Check logs of control plane components</span>
kubectl logs kube-apiserver-master -n kube-system | tail -20
kubectl logs kube-scheduler-master -n kube-system | tail -20
kubectl logs kube-controller-manager-master -n kube-system | tail -20
kubectl logs etcd-master -n kube-system | tail -20

<span class="comment"># Method 5: On the master node directly</span>
crictl ps | grep kube
journalctl -u kubelet | grep -i error</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Control plane components run as static pods on the master node (in <code>kube-system</code> namespace). On the CKA: check if all 4 components are Running, check logs for errors, and use <code>crictl</code> on the master if kubectl doesn't work (API server down). Common issue: if API server is down, kubectl won't work — SSH to master and use <code>crictl</code> to check the container, then look at the static pod manifest in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you troubleshoot CNI (Container Network Interface) issues?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Symptom: Pods stuck in ContainerCreating</span>
kubectl describe pod stuck-pod
<span class="comment"># Warning  FailedCreatePodSandBox</span>
<span class="comment"># "network plugin is not ready: cni config uninitialized"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check CNI plugin is installed</span>
ls /etc/cni/net.d/
<span class="comment"># Should contain config files like:</span>
<span class="comment"># 10-calico.conflist  OR  10-weave.conflist</span>

<span class="comment"># If empty → CNI plugin not installed!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check CNI plugin pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E "calico|weave|flannel"
<span class="comment"># calico-node-abc12   0/1   CrashLoopBackOff  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CNI plugin logs</span>
kubectl logs calico-node-abc12 -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check CNI binary</span>
ls /opt/cni/bin/
<span class="comment"># Should contain: calico, bandwidth, bridge, etc.</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 1: Install CNI plugin (if missing)</span>
kubectl apply -f https://docs.projectcalico.org/manifests/calico.yaml

<span class="comment"># Fix 2: Restart CNI pods</span>
kubectl delete pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=calico-node
<span class="comment"># DaemonSet recreates them</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix 3: Check node network</span>
ssh worker-1
ip link show  <span class="comment"># Network interfaces</span>
ip route     <span class="comment"># Routing table</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify: Pods should move from ContainerCreating to Running</span>
kubectl get pods -w</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CNI provides pod networking. Without CNI, pods can't get IP addresses and stay in ContainerCreating. On the CKA: check <code>/etc/cni/net.d/</code> for config files and <code>/opt/cni/bin/</code> for binaries. If no CNI is installed, you need to apply the CNI plugin manifest. CNI plugins run as DaemonSets in <code>kube-system</code>. Common exam question: "pods stuck in ContainerCreating on a new node" = CNI not configured.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A node shows <code>MemoryPressure</code> or <code>DiskPressure</code>. What happens and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check node conditions:</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A20 Conditions
<span class="comment"># Type              Status</span>
<span class="comment"># MemoryPressure    True    ← Node running out of memory!</span>
<span class="comment"># DiskPressure      True    ← Node running out of disk!</span>
<span class="comment"># PIDPressure       False</span>
<span class="comment"># Ready             True    (can be True with pressure)</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens automatically:</span>
<span class="comment"># MemoryPressure → kubelet starts evicting pods (BestEffort first)</span>
<span class="comment"># DiskPressure   → kubelet reclaims dead containers, unused images</span>
<span class="comment"># Both           → Node gets tainted: node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># Check memory on the node:</span>
ssh worker-1
free -h
<span class="comment"># total: 4Gi  used: 3.8Gi  available: 200Mi ← Very low!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check disk:</span>
df -h /
<span class="comment"># /dev/sda1  50G  48G  2G  96% /  ← 96% full!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix MemoryPressure:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Find memory-hungry pods:</span>
kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=memory | head -10
<span class="comment"># 2. Delete or reduce limits on heavy consumers</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Add memory to the node (if possible)</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix DiskPressure:</span>
ssh worker-1
<span class="comment"># Clean up container logs:</span>
journalctl --vacuum-size=100M
<span class="comment"># Remove unused images:</span>
crictl rmi --prune
<span class="comment"># Clear unused containers:</span>
crictl rm $(crictl ps -a -q --state exited)

<span class="comment"># Eviction thresholds (defaults):</span>
<span class="comment"># memory.available &lt; 100Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># nodefs.available &lt; 10%</span>
<span class="comment"># imagefs.available &lt; 15%</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Node pressure conditions trigger automatic eviction. The kubelet evicts pods in order: BestEffort (no limits) first, then Burstable, then Guaranteed last. Pressure also adds a <code>NoSchedule</code> taint so new pods won't be scheduled. On the CKA: check with <code>describe node</code>, SSH to the node to check <code>free -h</code> and <code>df -h</code>, fix by removing unused resources or moving pods. The kubelet's eviction thresholds are configurable in <code>kubelet config.yaml</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">The <code>kube-proxy</code> DaemonSet isn't running on a node. How do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Symptom: Services don't work on one specific node</span>
<span class="comment"># Pods can ping other pods but Service ClusterIP fails</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check kube-proxy pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-proxy -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME              READY  STATUS   NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-abc12  1/1    Running  worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-proxy-def34  0/1    Error    worker-2  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check logs</span>
kubectl logs kube-proxy-def34 -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># "failed to load config: couldn't read file"</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check kube-proxy ConfigMap</span>
kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | head -30

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check kube-proxy DaemonSet</span>
kubectl get ds kube-proxy -n kube-system
kubectl describe ds kube-proxy -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check iptables/ipvs rules on the node</span>
ssh worker-2
iptables -L -t nat | grep -i kube
<span class="comment"># If empty → kube-proxy isn't creating rules</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix: Delete the erroring pod (DaemonSet recreates)</span>
kubectl delete pod kube-proxy-def34 -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># If still broken, check kube-proxy mode:</span>
kubectl get configmap kube-proxy -n kube-system -o yaml | grep mode
<span class="comment"># mode: "iptables" (default) or "ipvs"</span>

<span class="comment"># Restart ALL kube-proxy pods:</span>
kubectl rollout restart ds kube-proxy -n kube-system</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kube-proxy runs as a DaemonSet (one pod per node) and maintains network rules (iptables/ipvs) for Service routing. If kube-proxy is broken on a node, Services won't work on that node. On the CKA: check the kube-proxy pod logs, delete the broken pod (DaemonSet auto-recreates), or <code>rollout restart</code> the DaemonSet. Config comes from the <code>kube-proxy</code> ConfigMap in <code>kube-system</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you troubleshoot a kubelet that won't start after a cluster upgrade?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># After kubeadm upgrade, kubelet won't start:</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: activating (auto-restart)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check journal</span>
journalctl -u kubelet -n 50 --no-pager
<span class="comment"># Error messages to look for:</span>

<span class="comment"># A: "mismatched kubelet version"</span>
kubelet --version
<span class="comment"># v1.30.0 ← Not upgraded to v1.31!</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Upgrade kubelet package</span>
apt-get install kubelet=1.31.0-1.1

<span class="comment"># B: "failed to read kubelet config"</span>
cat /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml
<span class="comment"># Config may need updating</span>
kubeadm upgrade node  <span class="comment"># Updates kubelet config</span>

<span class="comment"># C: "certificate has expired"</span>
ls -la /var/lib/kubelet/pki/
openssl x509 -in /var/lib/kubelet/pki/kubelet-client-current.pem \
-noout -dates
<span class="comment"># Fix: kubeadm certs renew all (on master)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Full fix sequence</span>
apt-get update
apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-1.1 kubectl=1.31.0-1.1
kubeadm upgrade node  <span class="comment"># Only on worker nodes</span>
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify</span>
systemctl status kubelet  <span class="comment"># active (running)</span>
kubectl get nodes          <span class="comment"># Node Ready, correct version</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Post-upgrade kubelet issues are common on the CKA. Key checks: (1) kubelet binary version matches the cluster version, (2) <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> was run to update config, (3) <code>daemon-reload</code> was done before restart, (4) certificates are valid. The most common mistake is forgetting <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> on workers or not doing <code>daemon-reload</code>. Always check <code>journalctl</code> first — the error message tells you exactly what's wrong.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you find which pods are running on a specific node and what resources they're using?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: Field selector (recommended)</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-1
<span class="comment"># NAMESPACE     NAME              READY  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># default       web-abc12         1/1    Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system   kube-proxy-xyz    1/1    Running</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-system   calico-node-123   1/1    Running</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Using -o wide shows NODE column</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o wide | grep worker-1

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Resource usage on the node</span>
kubectl top node worker-1
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU(cores)  CPU%  MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   350m        17%   1200Mi         60%</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 4: Resource usage per pod on the node</span>
kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-1

<span class="comment"># Method 5: Node capacity vs allocatable vs used</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A10 "Allocated resources"
<span class="comment"># Allocated resources:</span>
<span class="comment">#   CPU Requests  CPU Limits  Memory Requests  Memory Limits</span>
<span class="comment">#   750m (37%)    2 (100%)    1Gi (50%)        2Gi (100%)</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 6: All resource details</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 "Capacity"
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 "Allocatable"

<span class="comment"># CKA shortcut: See what's running and how much room is left</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A30 "Non-terminated"</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--field-selector spec.nodeName=</code> filters pods by node. On the CKA, <code>kubectl describe node</code> shows Capacity, Allocatable, and Allocated Resources — this tells you total vs available vs used. <code>kubectl top node</code> shows real-time usage. Key CKA scenario: scheduling failure because node is at capacity — use <code>describe node</code> to see Allocated vs Allocatable resources to confirm.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 32 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch32 -->

        <article class="chapter" id="ch33">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 33 · Logging &amp; Monitoring</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Application logging, cluster-level logging architecture, Metrics Server, and monitoring resource usage. Essential for debugging and capacity planning.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~1 hour</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📊 Monitoring</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="learning-objectives">
                <h4>🎯 What You'll Learn</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>✅ Application logging with <code>kubectl logs</code> — all flags and patterns</li>
                    <li>✅ Cluster-level logging architecture — node agents, sidecar containers</li>
                    <li>✅ Metrics Server installation and resource monitoring</li>
                    <li>✅ Monitoring resource usage — <code>kubectl top</code>, capacity planning</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-chapter-relevance">
                <span class="relevance-label">🎓 CKA Relevance</span>
                <div class="relevance-domains">
                    <span class="cka-domain-badge">Troubleshooting <span class="cka-weight">30%</span></span>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                <h5>🎯 Logs &amp; Metrics Are Your First Debugging Signal</h5>
                <p>Before you change anything, read the logs and the resource numbers. The exam rewards candidates who reach for <code>logs</code> and <code>top</code> reflexively rather than guessing:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code> — app-level errors; add <code>-f</code> to stream</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — the crashed container's last output</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl top nodes</code> / <code>kubectl top pods</code> — needs metrics-server installed</li>
                    <li><code>kubectl get --raw /apis/metrics.k8s.io/v1beta1/nodes</code> — verify the metrics pipeline</li>
                    <li><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code> and <code>/var/log/pods/</code> — node-level container logs</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── CHAPTER ROADMAP ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧭 Chapter Roadmap</h4>
                <p>The learning-arc roadmap for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SECTION TOC ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🗂️ Section TOC</h4>
                <p>A jump-table of this chapter's sections goes here.</p>
            </div>


            <!-- ─────────── §33.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s33-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.1 Application Logging</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Containers write to <strong>stdout/stderr</strong>, and the container runtime captures these streams as log files on the node. <code>kubectl logs</code> retrieves this output.</p>

                <h4>kubectl logs — Debugging Flow</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>Basic Logs</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> — Start with current container output. If pod is running, this shows real-time stdout/stderr.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>Previous Crash</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — If CrashLoopBackOff, this shows output from the <strong>last crashed</strong> container instance.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>Specific Container</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code> — Multi-container pods require specifying which container. Use <code>--all-containers</code> for everything.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>Label Selector</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl logs -l app=name --tail=50</code> — Check logs across ALL replicas of a Deployment at once.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <h4>📋 kubectl logs — Complete Reference</h4>
                    <p>Master every flag for the CKA exam:</p>
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Command</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                <th>When to Use</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Current container stdout/stderr</td>
                                <td>First check for running pods</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                                <td>Previous crashed container output</td>
                                <td>CrashLoopBackOff debugging</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -f</code></td>
                                <td>Follow / stream live</td>
                                <td>Watch realtime during testing</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --tail=100</code></td>
                                <td>Last N lines only</td>
                                <td>Quick check without scrolling</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --since=1h</code></td>
                                <td>Logs from last hour</td>
                                <td>Recent errors only</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Specific container</td>
                                <td>Multi-container pods (sidecars)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --all-containers</code></td>
                                <td>All containers in a pod</td>
                                <td>Sidecar/init container logs</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs -l app=name</code></td>
                                <td>All pods matching label</td>
                                <td>Deployments with multiple replicas</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Application Logging Examples</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Basic logs</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Previous crashed container ⭐ (CKA critical)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj --previous

<span class="comment"># Follow live (useful during testing)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -f

<span class="comment"># Multi-container pod: specify the container</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -c django
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj -c nginx-sidecar

<span class="comment"># All containers at once</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj --all-containers

<span class="comment"># Logs from all pods with label app=easypj</span>
kubectl logs -l app=easypj -n easypj --tail=50

<span class="comment"># Combine flags: last 100 lines from last hour, follow live</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-xxxx --tail=100 --since=1h -f</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Where Do Container Logs Actually Live?</h5>
                    <p>On the <strong>node</strong>, container logs are stored at:<br>
                    <code>/var/log/containers/&lt;pod-name&gt;_&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;container&gt;-&lt;id&gt;.log</code><br>
                    <code>/var/log/pods/&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;pod-name&gt;_&lt;uid&gt;/&lt;container&gt;/</code><br>
                    These are symlinks managed by the container runtime. <code>kubectl logs</code> reads these files via kubelet.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §33.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s33-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.2 Cluster-Level Logging</h3>
                <p>Node-level logging only keeps logs on individual nodes. For production, you need a <strong>centralized logging architecture</strong> — a DaemonSet log agent that ships logs to a central store.</p>

                <h4>The 3 Logging Patterns</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📋</div>
                        <h5>Node-Level Agent (Recommended)</h5>
                        <p>DaemonSet runs 1 log agent per node (fluentd, filebeat). Agent reads <code>/var/log/containers/</code> and ships to central store. Lightweight, no pod modification needed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔀</div>
                        <h5>Sidecar Streaming</h5>
                        <p>Sidecar container reads app log files and streams to its own stdout. Node agent then picks up the sidecar's output. Use when app writes to files instead of stdout.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📡</div>
                        <h5>Sidecar with Agent</h5>
                        <p>Sidecar runs its own logging agent (fluentd) that ships directly to the backend. More flexible but resource-heavy — each pod gets its own agent.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">💡</div>
                        <h5>CKA Focus</h5>
                        <p>Know the <strong>architecture concepts</strong> and <code>kubectl logs</code> flags. The exam won't ask you to deploy EFK/Loki stacks — but may ask you to create a sidecar logging container.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Kubernetes Logging Architecture — From Container to Central Store</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  KUBERNETES LOGGING ARCHITECTURE                             │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────── Worker Node 1 ─────────────────────────────┐                 │
│  │                                                         │                 │
│  │  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌──────────┐             │                 │
│  │  │  Pod A   │  │  Pod B   │  │  Pod C   │             │                 │
│  │  │ stdout→  │  │ stdout→  │  │ stdout→  │             │                 │
│  │  └────┬─────┘  └────┬─────┘  └────┬─────┘             │                 │
│  │       │              │              │                   │                 │
│  │       ▼              ▼              ▼                   │                 │
│  │  /var/log/containers/*.log                              │                 │
│  │  /var/log/pods/&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;pod&gt;/                       │                 │
│  │       │                                                 │                 │
│  │       ▼                                                 │                 │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────┐                       │                 │
│  │  │ 📋 DaemonSet Log Agent     │   fluentd /            │                 │
│  │  │    (1 per node)             │   filebeat /           │                 │
│  │  │                             │   fluent-bit           │                 │
│  │  └──────────────┬──────────────┘                       │                 │
│  └─────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────┘                 │
│                    │                                                         │
│  ┌─────────────────┼── Worker Node 2 ──────────────────────┐                │
│  │  (same pattern) │                                        │                │
│  │  Pods → /var/log/ → DaemonSet Agent ───────────────┤    │                │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────┘                │
│                                                        │                     │
│              ┌─────────────────────────────────────────▼──────────┐          │
│              │  🗄️  CENTRAL LOG STORE                              │          │
│              │                                                     │          │
│              │  Elasticsearch / Loki / CloudWatch / Splunk        │          │
│              │         │                                          │          │
│              │         ▼                                          │          │
│              │  📊 Kibana / Grafana   (visualization &amp; alerts)   │          │
│              └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘          │
│                                                                              │
│  THREE LOGGING PATTERNS:                                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────┬──────────────────────┐             │
│  │ Node-level Agent │ Sidecar Streaming│ Sidecar with Agent   │             │
│  │ (DaemonSet)      │ (sidecar → stdout│ (sidecar ships logs) │             │
│  │ ✅ Recommended   │  → node agent)   │ ⚠️ Resource heavy   │             │
│  │ 1 agent per node │ Multi-format logs│ Direct to backend    │             │
│  └──────────────────┴──────────────────┴──────────────────────┘             │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Scope: Logging</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam tests <code>kubectl logs</code> commands, not deploying full logging stacks. Know the <strong>architecture concepts</strong> (DaemonSet agent, node-level logs, sidecar patterns) but focus your practice on the <code>kubectl logs</code> flags in section 33.1.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Sidecar Logging Pattern — When Apps Don't Log to stdout</h4>
                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📖 Why Sidecar Logging?</h5>
                    <p>Some applications write logs to <strong>files</strong> instead of stdout. Since <code>kubectl logs</code> only captures stdout/stderr, you need a sidecar container that reads the log file and streams it to its own stdout — making it visible via <code>kubectl logs</code>.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">sidecar-logging.yaml</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  <span class="comment"># Main application — writes logs to a file</span>
  - name: django
    image: easypj:v1
    volumeMounts:
    - name: log-volume
      mountPath: /var/log/django

  <span class="comment"># Sidecar — streams the log file to stdout</span>
  - name: log-streamer
    image: busybox:1.36
    args: ['sh', '-c', 'tail -F /var/log/django/app.log']
    volumeMounts:
    - name: log-volume
      mountPath: /var/log/django

  volumes:
  - name: log-volume
    emptyDir: {}

<span class="comment"># Now you can read the app logs via:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl logs easypj-web -c log-streamer</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Container Log Rotation</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Setting</th>
                                <th>Default</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>containerLogMaxSize</code></td>
                                <td>10Mi</td>
                                <td>Max size of a single log file before rotation</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><code>containerLogMaxFiles</code></td>
                                <td>5</td>
                                <td>Max number of rotated log files kept per container</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Log Rotation Config</h5>
                    <p>These are set in the <strong>kubelet configuration</strong> (<code>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml</code>). Default: each container can use up to <strong>50Mi of disk</strong> for logs (10Mi × 5 files). If a node has disk pressure, check if log rotation is properly configured.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §33.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s33-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.3 Monitoring with Metrics Server</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Metrics Server is a lightweight, in-cluster component that collects CPU and memory usage from kubelet on each node. It powers <code>kubectl top</code> and is <strong>required for HPA</strong> (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Metrics Server — How It Works</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                     METRICS SERVER ARCHITECTURE                          │
│                                                                          │
│  ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐                            │
│  │ kubelet  │   │ kubelet  │   │ kubelet  │                            │
│  │ (node 1) │   │ (node 2) │   │ (node 3) │                            │
│  │          │   │          │   │          │                            │
│  │ cAdvisor │   │ cAdvisor │   │ cAdvisor │   ← built into kubelet     │
│  │ collects │   │ collects │   │ collects │     collects container      │
│  │ metrics  │   │ metrics  │   │ metrics  │     CPU/memory metrics     │
│  └────┬─────┘   └────┬─────┘   └────┬─────┘                            │
│       │              │              │                                    │
│       └──────────────┼──────────────┘                                    │
│                      ▼                                                   │
│            ┌──────────────────┐                                         │
│            │  Metrics Server  │   ← Deployment in kube-system           │
│            │  (kube-system)   │     Scrapes /metrics/resource from      │
│            │                  │     each kubelet every 15 seconds       │
│            └────────┬─────────┘                                         │
│                     │ exposes                                            │
│                     ▼                                                    │
│            ┌──────────────────┐                                         │
│            │ metrics.k8s.io   │   ← Kubernetes API extension            │
│            │  API endpoint    │                                         │
│            └────────┬─────────┘                                         │
│                     │                                                    │
│           ┌─────────┼──────────────┐                                    │
│           ▼                        ▼                                    │
│  ┌──────────────┐       ┌──────────────────┐                           │
│  │ kubectl top  │       │ HPA Controller   │                           │
│  │ pods / nodes │       │ (auto-scaling)   │                           │
│  └──────────────┘       └──────────────────┘                           │
│                                                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Metrics Server Installation &amp; Usage</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ INSTALL METRICS SERVER ═══
<span class="comment"># Download and apply the official manifest</span>
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml

<span class="comment"># For local clusters (kubeadm, kind, minikube) — add insecure TLS flag:</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the Deployment and add to args:</span>
<span class="comment"># --kubelet-insecure-tls</span>
kubectl edit deployment metrics-server -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Verify metrics server is running</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep metrics-server
kubectl get apiservices | grep metrics     <span class="comment"># Should show v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ KUBECTL TOP — RESOURCE USAGE ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Node-level metrics</span>
kubectl top nodes                          <span class="comment"># CPU and memory per node</span>
kubectl top nodes --sort-by=cpu            <span class="comment"># Sort by CPU usage</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod-level metrics</span>
kubectl top pods                           <span class="comment"># CPU and memory per pod</span>
kubectl top pods -A                        <span class="comment"># All namespaces</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=memory          <span class="comment"># Sort by memory usage</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu             <span class="comment"># Sort by CPU usage</span>
kubectl top pods -n kube-system            <span class="comment"># Specific namespace</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod with containers breakdown</span>
kubectl top pods --containers              <span class="comment"># Per-container metrics</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Interpreting Resource Metrics</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Metric</th>
                                <th>Unit</th>
                                <th>Example</th>
                                <th>What It Means</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>CPU</td>
                                <td>millicores (m)</td>
                                <td><code>250m</code></td>
                                <td>25% of one CPU core</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>CPU</td>
                                <td>cores</td>
                                <td><code>1</code></td>
                                <td>100% of one CPU core</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Memory</td>
                                <td>Mi (mebibytes)</td>
                                <td><code>128Mi</code></td>
                                <td>~134 MB of RAM</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Memory</td>
                                <td>Gi (gibibytes)</td>
                                <td><code>2Gi</code></td>
                                <td>~2.1 GB of RAM</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §33.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s33-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">33.4 Monitoring easypj Resource Usage</h3>
                <p>Let's apply monitoring to our easypj project — find resource-hungry pods, identify OOM risks, and set appropriate limits.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Monitoring easypj — Finding Resource Issues</span>
                    </div>
<pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Which pods are using the most CPU?</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj --sort-by=cpu
<span class="comment"># NAME                          CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-7d4f8c5-abc12      250m         128Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-db-0                   100m         512Mi     ← DB uses most memory</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-worker-xxxx            50m          64Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># Which pods are using the most memory?</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj --sort-by=memory

<span class="comment"># Per-container breakdown (useful for sidecar pods)</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj --containers
<span class="comment"># NAME                  CONTAINER   CPU    MEMORY</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-xxx        django      200m   100Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-xxx        nginx       50m    28Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># Node-level — is any node under pressure?</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME           CPU(cores)  CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># controlplane   350m        17%    1200Mi         62%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker01       800m        40%    3200Mi         80%     ← ⚠️ High memory!</span>

<span class="comment"># Compare against resource requests/limits</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj | grep -A8 "Requests\|Limits"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Resource Monitoring — Requests vs Limits vs Actual Usage</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
<pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  easypj-web pod: CPU usage over time                                     │
│                                                                          │
│  CPU (millicores)                                                        │
│  500m ┤                                                                  │
│       │                                                                  │
│  400m ┤                              ╔═══════════════╗ ← LIMIT (400m)   │
│       │                        ┌─────╨───┐           ║                   │
│  300m ┤                   ┌────┘         └───┐       ║                   │
│       │              ┌────┘                  │       ║                   │
│  250m ┤─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─│─ ─ ─ ─║─ ← REQUEST (250m)│
│       │         ┌────┘                       └───┐   ║                   │
│  200m ┤    ┌────┘                                └───║──┐                │
│       │────┘                                         ║  └───             │
│  100m ┤                                              ║                   │
│       │                                              ║                   │
│    0m ┤──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶ Time  │
│       │                                                                  │
│  REQUEST = guaranteed minimum (scheduler uses this for placement)        │
│  LIMIT   = maximum allowed (container is throttled/killed above this)    │
│  ACTUAL  = the wavy line (what kubectl top shows)                        │
│                                                                          │
│  ⚠️ If ACTUAL exceeds MEMORY LIMIT → OOMKilled (exit 137)              │
│  ⚠️ If ACTUAL exceeds CPU LIMIT → Throttled (not killed)               │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h4>🏋️ Practice Drill: Monitoring Commands</h4>
                    <div class="drill-steps">
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">1</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Install Metrics Server</strong> and verify: <code>kubectl top nodes</code> returns data
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">2</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Find the pod using the most CPU:</strong> <code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -5</code>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="drill-step">
                            <span class="step-number">3</span>
                            <div class="step-content">
                                <strong>Find the node with highest memory usage:</strong> <code>kubectl top nodes --sort-by=memory</code>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 33 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Logging &amp; Monitoring Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Metrics Server must be installed</strong> — <code>kubectl top nodes</code> and <code>kubectl top pods</code> fail without it. The exam cluster usually has it pre-installed, but verify first: <code>kubectl get deployment metrics-server -n kube-system</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl top</code> shows CURRENT usage, not limits</strong> — A pod using 450Mi doesn't mean its limit is 450Mi. Compare with <code>kubectl describe pod</code> to see limits vs actual usage. Exam may ask "which pod is closest to its memory limit"</li>
                        <li><strong>Log rotation</strong> — Node-level logs at <code>/var/log/containers/</code> are rotated by the container runtime. If a pod restarted 5 times, only the current + previous logs are available via <code>kubectl logs</code></li>
                        <li><strong>Sidecar logging pattern</strong> — App writes to a file on a shared <code>emptyDir</code> volume. A sidecar container tails that file to stdout. This makes logs accessible via <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c sidecar</code></li>
                        <li><strong><code>--sort-by</code> flag</strong> — <code>kubectl top pods --sort-by=memory</code> or <code>--sort-by=cpu</code>. The exam may ask to find the pod consuming the most resources. Know these flags</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>


                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <strong>⚡ Logging &amp; monitoring commands:</strong>
                    <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --all-containers --since=10m</code> — all containers, last 10 min<br>
                    <code>kubectl logs -l app=web --max-log-requests=10</code> — logs from multiple pods by label<br>
                    <code>kubectl top pods -n &lt;ns&gt; --containers</code> — per-container resource usage<br>
                    <code>kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> — recent cluster events<br>
                    <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt; --previous</code> — previous crashed container's logs
                </div>

                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 33 — How Logging and Monitoring Architectures Work ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 33 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Logging and Monitoring Architectures Work: kubelet Log Management, Container Runtime Logs, and the Metrics Pipeline</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Kubernetes Logging Architecture — Where Logs Live</h5>
            <p>kubectl logs doesn't connect to a central log server. It reads directly from the node's filesystem where the container runtime writes stdout/stderr.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUBERNETES LOGGING — Data Flow                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Container stdout/stderr                                                      │
│       │                                                                       │
│       ▼                                                                       │
│  containerd captures output → writes to JSON file:                           │
│  /var/log/pods/&lt;namespace&gt;_&lt;pod-name&gt;_&lt;pod-uid&gt;/&lt;container&gt;/0.log          │
│                                                                              │
│  kubectl logs <span class="comment">→ kubelet reads from this file via CRI                        </span>│
│                                                                              │
│  NODE-LEVEL LOG LOCATIONS:                                                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ Application logs: /var/log/pods/                                       │   │
│  │ Container logs:   /var/log/containers/ (symlinks to pod logs)         │   │
│  │ kubelet logs:     journalctl -u kubelet                               │   │
│  │ containerd logs:  journalctl -u containerd                             │   │
│  │ API Server logs:  /var/log/kube-apiserver.log OR crictl logs &lt;id&gt;    │   │
│  │ etcd logs:        crictl logs &lt;id&gt;                                     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  LOG ROTATION (kubelet-managed):                                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ kubelet rotates logs when:                                            │   │
│  │   containerLogMaxSize: "10Mi"  <span class="comment">← Max size per log file               </span>│   │
│  │   containerLogMaxFiles: 5      <span class="comment">← Max rotated files to keep           </span>│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ After rotation: 0.log → 0.log.1 → 0.log.2 → ... → deleted            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ ⚠️  kubectl logs --previous reads from the PREVIOUS (rotated) file    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  METRICS PIPELINE — How kubectl top Works:                                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ cAdvisor (in kubelet) → collects CPU/memory per container            │   │
│  │       │                                                                │   │
│  │       ▼                                                                │   │
│  │ metrics-server → aggregates cAdvisor data from all nodes              │   │
│  │       │  (Deployment in kube-system, needs metrics-server installed)  │   │
│  │       ▼                                                                │   │
│  │ kubectl top pod/node → queries metrics-server API                     │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ WITHOUT metrics-server: kubectl top returns "error: Metrics API not    │   │
│  │   available". Install: kubectl apply -f metrics-server components.yaml │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  EVENTS — The Cluster's Timeline:                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp -A | tail -30            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ Events are kept for 1 hour by default. They tell you:                 │   │
│  │ • Why a pod was killed (OOMKilling)                                   │   │
│  │ • Why a pod can't start (FailedMount, FailedScheduling)               │   │
│  │ • Why a probe failed (Unhealthy)                                      │   │
│  │ • When nodes changed state (NodeReady→NodeNotReady)                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ Events are your FIRST stop for any cluster issue.                     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, <code>kubectl logs</code> is your primary diagnostic tool. Remember: <code>--previous</code> for crashed container logs, <code>--tail=50</code> for last N lines, <code>--since=5m</code> for time-windowed logs, <code>--all-containers</code> for multi-container pods, <code>-l app=web</code> for aggregate logs across pods. For control plane component logs, use <code>crictl logs</code> on the node since they run as static pods (not accessible via kubectl logs in kube-system).
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Expecting <code>kubectl top</code> to work without Metrics Server installed</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Install Metrics Server first — it's not included by default in kubeadm clusters</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Thinking Metrics Server stores historical data</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Metrics Server provides <strong>current snapshot only</strong> — for history use Prometheus</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-wrong">✗ Wrong</span> Forgetting <code>--kubelet-insecure-tls</code> in lab environments</li>
                    <li><span class="gotcha-correct">✓ Right</span> Add this flag in kubeadm/kind/minikube — otherwise Metrics Server can't scrape kubelet</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Resource usage commands:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl top nodes</code> — CPU/memory per node<br>
                <code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu</code> — highest CPU across all namespaces<br>
                <code>kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=memory | head -5</code> — top 5 memory consumers<br>
                <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -f --tail=50</code> — follow last 50 lines (live tail)</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 33)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 33 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch33-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 33 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of logging &amp; metrics tooling — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you get the last 100 lines of logs from a pod and stream new logs in real time?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Last 100 lines:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --tail=100

<span class="comment"># Stream (follow) logs in real time:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod -f

<span class="comment"># Combine: last 100 lines THEN stream:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --tail=100 -f

<span class="comment"># Logs since a specific time:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since=1h     <span class="comment"># Last 1 hour</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since=30m    <span class="comment"># Last 30 minutes</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since=5s     <span class="comment"># Last 5 seconds</span>

<span class="comment"># Logs since a timestamp:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --since-time="2024-01-15T10:00:00Z"

<span class="comment"># Add timestamps to log lines:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --timestamps=true
<span class="comment"># 2024-01-15T10:30:00Z  [INFO] Server started on port 8080</span>

<span class="comment"># Previous container logs (after crash/restart):</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --previous

<span class="comment"># Combine for exam speed:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --tail=50 --timestamps -f</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--tail=N</code> shows the last N lines (avoids flooding). <code>-f</code> follows/streams new logs. <code>--since</code> accepts durations (1h, 30m) or timestamps. <code>--timestamps</code> prepends timestamps to each line. On the CKA, use <code>--tail=50 -f</code> to quickly see recent logs and watch for new errors. <code>--previous</code> gets logs from the previous container instance (crucial after crashes).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a sidecar container that reads the main application's log file and streams it to stdout.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
name: app-with-sidecar
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: busybox
command: ['sh', '-c',
  'while true; do echo "$(date) - Processing request" >> /var/log/app.log; sleep 5; done']
volumeMounts:
- name: log-volume
  mountPath: /var/log

- name: log-sidecar
image: busybox
command: ['sh', '-c', 'tail -f /var/log/app.log']
volumeMounts:
- name: log-volume
  mountPath: /var/log

volumes:
- name: log-volume
emptyDir: {}

<span class="comment"># Now you can view app logs via kubectl:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl logs app-with-sidecar -c log-sidecar</span>
<span class="comment"># 2024-01-15 10:30:00 - Processing request</span>
<span class="comment"># 2024-01-15 10:30:05 - Processing request</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Sidecar logging pattern: the main app writes logs to a file, the sidecar reads that file with <code>tail -f</code> and outputs to stdout. Both containers share a volume (<code>emptyDir</code>). This makes file-based logs accessible via <code>kubectl logs</code>. On the CKA, you may need to add a sidecar container to stream logs from a file. Key: shared volume, <code>tail -f</code> in the sidecar, and matching <code>mountPath</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you install Metrics Server and get resource usage with <code>kubectl top</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check if metrics-server is installed:</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># Error: Metrics API not available</span>
<span class="comment"># ← Metrics Server is NOT installed</span>

<span class="comment"># Install Metrics Server:</span>
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml

<span class="comment"># For lab environments (self-signed certs), add --kubelet-insecure-tls:</span>
kubectl patch deployment metrics-server -n kube-system \
--type='json' -p='[{"op":"add","path":"/spec/template/spec/containers/0/args/-","value":"--kubelet-insecure-tls"}]'

<span class="comment"># Wait for it to be ready (~60 seconds):</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep metrics-server

<span class="comment"># Node resource usage:</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU(cores)  CPU%  MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># master     250m        12%   1500Mi         75%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   150m        7%    800Mi          40%</span>

<span class="comment"># Pod resource usage:</span>
kubectl top pods -A
kubectl top pods -n default

<span class="comment"># Sort by CPU or memory:</span>
kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu
kubectl top pods --sort-by=memory

<span class="comment"># Per-container metrics:</span>
kubectl top pods --containers

<span class="comment"># Specific namespace, sorted:</span>
kubectl top pods -n production --sort-by=memory</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Metrics Server collects CPU/memory metrics from kubelets. <code>kubectl top</code> requires Metrics Server to be installed. On the CKA, Metrics Server is usually pre-installed, but you should know how to verify. <code>--sort-by=cpu</code> or <code>--sort-by=memory</code> sorts the output — useful for finding resource-hungry pods. <code>--containers</code> shows per-container breakdown. In lab environments, <code>--kubelet-insecure-tls</code> is often needed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you find the pod consuming the most CPU in a specific namespace?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Sort by CPU usage (highest first):</span>
kubectl top pods -n production --sort-by=cpu
<span class="comment"># NAME          CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># data-proc     950m        2Gi          ← Highest CPU!</span>
<span class="comment"># web-server    200m        512Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># api-gateway   150m        256Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># cache         50m         128Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># Sort by memory:</span>
kubectl top pods -n production --sort-by=memory
<span class="comment"># NAME          CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># data-proc     950m        2Gi          ← Also highest memory!</span>
<span class="comment"># web-server    200m        512Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># All namespaces, top CPU consumers:</span>
kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu | head -10

<span class="comment"># Per-container breakdown:</span>
kubectl top pods -n production --containers --sort-by=cpu
<span class="comment"># POD          CONTAINER    CPU(cores)  MEMORY(bytes)</span>
<span class="comment"># data-proc    worker       800m        1.5Gi</span>
<span class="comment"># data-proc    sidecar      150m        512Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># Node-level: which node is busiest?</span>
kubectl top nodes --sort-by=cpu

<span class="comment"># CKA tip: Redirect output to a file</span>
kubectl top pods -A --sort-by=cpu > /opt/heavy-cpu.txt
<span class="comment"># Or extract just the pod name:</span>
kubectl top pods -n production --sort-by=cpu \
--no-headers | head -1 | awk '{print $1}' > /opt/answer.txt
<span class="comment"># data-proc</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On the CKA, you'll be asked to find the highest resource consumer and write the answer to a file. <code>kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu</code> sorts by CPU. Use <code>--no-headers | head -1 | awk '{print $1}'</code> to extract just the pod name. Common exam question: "find the pod using the most CPU in namespace X and write its name to /opt/answer.txt".</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you view and filter Kubernetes events?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># All events in current namespace:</span>
kubectl get events

<span class="comment"># All events, sorted by time (most recent first):</span>
kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

<span class="comment"># All events across all namespaces:</span>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

<span class="comment"># Only Warning events (errors):</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector type=Warning

<span class="comment"># Events for a specific object:</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=web-pod
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=my-deploy

<span class="comment"># Events by reason:</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector reason=FailedScheduling
kubectl get events --field-selector reason=BackOff
kubectl get events --field-selector reason=Killing

<span class="comment"># Watch events in real time:</span>
kubectl get events -w

<span class="comment"># Custom output:</span>
kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp \
-o custom-columns=\
'TIME:.lastTimestamp,TYPE:.type,REASON:.reason,OBJECT:.involvedObject.name,MESSAGE:.message'

<span class="comment"># Events in describe (per-object):</span>
kubectl describe pod web-pod | tail -20
<span class="comment"># Events section shows events specific to this pod</span>

<span class="comment"># Note: Events expire after ~1 hour by default</span>
<span class="comment"># (controlled by --event-ttl flag on kube-apiserver)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Events record what's happening in the cluster. On the CKA: <code>--sort-by=.lastTimestamp</code> shows newest first, <code>--field-selector type=Warning</code> filters to only errors. Events expire after ~1 hour, so check them early during troubleshooting. <code>kubectl describe</code> also shows events for a specific resource. Use <code>-w</code> to watch events in real time while reproducing an issue.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check resource requests and limits for all pods and compare with node capacity?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Pod resource requests/limits:</span>
kubectl get pods -o custom-columns=\
'NAME:.metadata.name,CPU_REQ:.spec.containers[0].resources.requests.cpu,CPU_LIM:.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.cpu,MEM_REQ:.spec.containers[0].resources.requests.memory,MEM_LIM:.spec.containers[0].resources.limits.memory'
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU_REQ  CPU_LIM  MEM_REQ  MEM_LIM</span>
<span class="comment"># web-pod    100m     500m     128Mi    512Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># api-pod    250m     1        256Mi    1Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Node capacity:</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 "Capacity"
<span class="comment"># Capacity:</span>
<span class="comment">#   cpu:     4</span>
<span class="comment">#   memory:  8Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Node allocatable (after system reservations):</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 "Allocatable"
<span class="comment"># Allocatable:</span>
<span class="comment">#   cpu:     3800m</span>
<span class="comment">#   memory:  7500Mi</span>

<span class="comment"># What's allocated on the node:</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A10 "Allocated"
<span class="comment"># Allocated resources:</span>
<span class="comment">#   CPU Requests  (Limits)    Memory Requests  (Limits)</span>
<span class="comment">#   1500m (39%)   3000m (78%) 2Gi (26%)        4Gi (53%)</span>

<span class="comment"># Available = Allocatable - Allocated</span>
<span class="comment"># CPU: 3800m - 1500m = 2300m free for scheduling</span>
<span class="comment"># Memory: 7500Mi - 2048Mi = 5452Mi free</span>

<span class="comment"># Actual usage vs requests (are pods using what they asked for?):</span>
kubectl top pods
<span class="comment"># Compare kubectl top (actual) vs requests (reserved)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Three levels: Capacity (total), Allocatable (after reservations), Allocated (sum of requests). On the CKA: scheduling uses requests (not limits or actual usage). If a pod requests 500m CPU and the node only has 300m allocatable, it won't schedule. <code>kubectl describe node</code> shows all three levels. <code>kubectl top</code> shows actual real-time usage. Common pitfall: pods requesting more than they use wastes resources.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you redirect pod logs to a file for a CKA exam question?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Basic: All logs to a file</span>
kubectl logs web-pod > /opt/web-pod-logs.txt

<span class="comment"># Specific container in multi-container pod:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod -c nginx > /opt/nginx-logs.txt

<span class="comment"># Only error lines:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod | grep -i error > /opt/errors.txt
kubectl logs web-pod | grep -i "ERROR\|WARN" > /opt/issues.txt

<span class="comment"># Last 100 lines to file:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --tail=100 > /opt/recent-logs.txt

<span class="comment"># With timestamps:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --timestamps > /opt/timestamped.txt

<span class="comment"># Previous crashed container:</span>
kubectl logs web-pod --previous > /opt/crash-logs.txt

<span class="comment"># Multiple pods by label:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web > /opt/all-web-logs.txt

<span class="comment"># CKA common question patterns:</span>
<span class="comment"># "Write the logs of pod X to /opt/logs.txt"</span>
kubectl logs web-pod > /opt/logs.txt

<span class="comment"># "Write only error logs of pod X to /opt/errors.txt"</span>
kubectl logs web-pod | grep -i error > /opt/errors.txt

<span class="comment"># "Write the name of the pod with most restarts to /opt/answer.txt"</span>
kubectl get pods --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount' \
--no-headers | tail -1 | awk '{print $1}' > /opt/answer.txt</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CKA exam frequently asks you to write output to specific files. Use <code>></code> to redirect. Key patterns: <code>kubectl logs pod > file</code>, <code>kubectl logs pod | grep error > file</code>, <code>kubectl top pods --sort-by=cpu --no-headers | head -1 | awk '{print $1}' > file</code>. Always verify: <code>cat /opt/answer.txt</code> to confirm the content is correct. Use pipe + grep + awk for extracting specific values.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A cluster has multiple pods with high restart counts. How do you investigate?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Find pods with high restart counts</span>
kubectl get pods -A --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'
<span class="comment"># Shows ascending — look at the bottom for highest</span>

<span class="comment"># Reverse sort trick (show highest first):</span>
kubectl get pods -A --sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount' \
-o custom-columns=\
'NS:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,RESTARTS:.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount,STATUS:.status.phase'
<span class="comment"># NS        NAME         RESTARTS  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># default   crash-app    45        Running  ← Lots of restarts!</span>
<span class="comment"># prod      buggy-api    23        Running</span>
<span class="comment"># default   web-app      0         Running</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check WHY it's restarting</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-app | grep -A5 "Last State"
<span class="comment"># Last State:  Terminated</span>
<span class="comment">#   Reason:    OOMKilled    ← Memory issue</span>
<span class="comment">#   Exit Code: 137</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check recent restarts with events</span>
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=crash-app \
--sort-by=.lastTimestamp

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check liveness probe</span>
kubectl describe pod crash-app | grep -A5 Liveness
<span class="comment"># If liveness probe fails → pod gets killed and restarted</span>

<span class="comment"># Common restart causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># OOMKilled (137) → Increase memory limit</span>
<span class="comment"># Exit Code 1     → App error, check logs --previous</span>
<span class="comment"># Liveness failed → Fix probe path/port/threshold</span>
<span class="comment"># Readiness fail  → Doesn't cause restart, but indicates issues</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Sort by restart count to find the most unstable pods. <code>--sort-by='.status.containerStatuses[0].restartCount'</code> sorts ascending (highest at bottom). Check <code>describe pod</code> for "Last State: Terminated" with the reason. Top causes of restarts: OOMKilled (exit 137), liveness probe failures, and app errors (exit 1). Note: high restart count + Running status = the pod keeps crashing and recovering.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you monitor cluster-wide resource usage and identify capacity issues?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Node-level overview:</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       CPU(cores)  CPU%  MEMORY(bytes)  MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># master     250m        12%   1500Mi         75%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   1800m       90%   6000Mi         80%  ← High!</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2   100m        5%    400Mi          20%</span>

<span class="comment"># Identify overloaded node:</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1 at 90% CPU and 80% memory → capacity issue!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check what's on the overloaded node:</span>
kubectl get pods -A --field-selector spec.nodeName=worker-1 \
-o custom-columns='NS:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,CPU:.spec.containers[0].resources.requests.cpu'

<span class="comment"># Check if pods are unevenly distributed:</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o wide | awk '{print $8}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
<span class="comment">#  15 worker-1    ← Too many pods!</span>
<span class="comment">#   3 worker-2</span>
<span class="comment">#   2 master</span>

<span class="comment"># Check node allocations:</span>
for node in $(kubectl get nodes -o name); do
echo "=== $node ==="
kubectl describe $node | grep -A8 "Allocated resources"
done

<span class="comment"># Check for resource quotas:</span>
kubectl get resourcequotas -A

<span class="comment"># Check for limit ranges:</span>
kubectl get limitranges -A

<span class="comment"># Quick cluster health:</span>
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -A | grep -v Running | grep -v Completed
kubectl top nodes
kubectl get events -A --field-selector type=Warning --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | head -10</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl top nodes</code> gives CPU% and Memory% per node — look for nodes above 80%. Check pod distribution with <code>-o wide</code> and count per node. If pods are unevenly distributed, it might indicate affinity rules or the scheduler choosing based on available resources. <code>describe node</code> shows allocated vs allocatable, which tells you what the scheduler sees. ResourceQuotas and LimitRanges control resource usage at the namespace level.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you get logs from all pods matching a label selector?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Logs from all pods with label app=web:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web
<span class="comment"># (shows logs from up to 5 pods by default)</span>

<span class="comment"># Increase the limit:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --max-log-requests=20

<span class="comment"># All containers in matching pods:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --all-containers=true

<span class="comment"># Stream logs from all matching pods:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web -f --max-log-requests=10

<span class="comment"># With prefix (shows pod name before each line):</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --prefix=true
<span class="comment"># [pod/web-abc12/nginx] 10.0.0.1 - GET /api/health 200</span>
<span class="comment"># [pod/web-def34/nginx] 10.0.0.2 - GET /api/users 500 ← Error!</span>

<span class="comment"># Only last 10 lines from each:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --tail=10

<span class="comment"># Filter specific namespace:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web -n production

<span class="comment"># Combine with grep:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web --all-containers | grep -i error

<span class="comment"># Labels with multiple selectors:</span>
kubectl logs -l app=web,tier=frontend

<span class="comment"># CKA tip: When you need to check multiple replicas</span>
<span class="comment"># Use -l with --prefix to identify which pod has the error</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>-l</code> (label selector) with <code>kubectl logs</code> fetches logs from all matching pods at once. Default limit is 5 concurrent pods — increase with <code>--max-log-requests</code>. <code>--prefix=true</code> prepends the pod/container name to each line, making it easy to identify which pod generated each log entry. On the CKA, this is faster than checking pods one by one when a Deployment has multiple replicas.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 33 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article><!-- end ch33 -->

