            <!-- ─────────── §34.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s34-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">34.1 Scenario Collection — 10 Must-Practice Scenarios</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Each scenario presents a broken cluster state and walks you through the exact commands and thinking process to diagnose and fix it. These mirror real CKA exam tasks.</p>

                <h4>The 5-Step Troubleshooting Method</h4>
                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number">1</div>
                        <h5>IDENTIFY</h5>
                        <p>What's broken? <code>kubectl get pods/nodes/svc</code> — find the failing resource and its status.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">2</div>
                        <h5>GATHER</h5>
                        <p><code>kubectl describe</code> + <code>kubectl logs</code> — collect Events, conditions, and error messages.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">3</div>
                        <h5>ANALYZE</h5>
                        <p>Read the error messages. Match symptoms to known causes. Narrow down root cause.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">4</div>
                        <h5>FIX</h5>
                        <p>Apply the fix — edit YAML, restart service, add missing resource. Make ONE change at a time.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number">5</div>
                        <h5>VERIFY</h5>
                        <p>Confirm the fix worked. <code>kubectl get</code> + <code>kubectl describe</code> — status should be healthy.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting Priority Layers</h4>
                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🏗️</div>
                        <h5>Infrastructure Layer</h5>
                        <p>Nodes NotReady, kubelet down, etcd unhealthy, certificates expired, kubeconfig issues. Fix infrastructure <strong>first</strong> — nothing else works if the cluster is broken.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">📦</div>
                        <h5>Workload Layer</h5>
                        <p>Pod Pending, CrashLoopBackOff, PVC not binding, resource limits. Most common exam tasks — fix pod/deployment configs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🌐</div>
                        <h5>Networking Layer</h5>
                        <p>Service not accessible, DNS failing, NetworkPolicy blocking. Hardest to debug — check selectors, endpoints, kube-proxy, CoreDNS.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⏱️</div>
                        <h5>CKA Time Strategy</h5>
                        <p>Easy tasks first (2-3 min each), hard tasks last. Skip if stuck &gt;8 minutes. Return to skipped tasks at the end. <strong>Never zero a question.</strong></p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Scenario Difficulty Map</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  10 MUST-PRACTICE CKA TROUBLESHOOTING SCENARIOS                             │
│                                                                              │
│  INFRASTRUCTURE LAYER                                                        │
│  ├── Scenario 1:  Worker Node NotReady        ████████░░  Difficulty: 8/10  │
│  ├── Scenario 6:  etcd Cluster Unhealthy      █████████░  Difficulty: 9/10  │
│  ├── Scenario 7:  Certificates Expired        ████████░░  Difficulty: 8/10  │
│  └── Scenario 10: kubeconfig Issues           ██████░░░░  Difficulty: 6/10  │
│                                                                              │
│  WORKLOAD LAYER                                                              │
│  ├── Scenario 2:  Pod Stuck in Pending        ██████░░░░  Difficulty: 6/10  │
│  ├── Scenario 4:  CrashLoopBackOff            ███████░░░  Difficulty: 7/10  │
│  └── Scenario 9:  PVC Not Binding             ██████░░░░  Difficulty: 6/10  │
│                                                                              │
│  NETWORKING LAYER                                                            │
│  ├── Scenario 3:  Service Not Accessible      ███████░░░  Difficulty: 7/10  │
│  ├── Scenario 5:  DNS Resolution Failing      ████████░░  Difficulty: 8/10  │
│  └── Scenario 8:  Network Policy Blocking     ███████░░░  Difficulty: 7/10  │
│                                                                              │
│  💡 Practice order: 2 → 4 → 3 → 10 → 9 → 1 → 8 → 5 → 7 → 6              │
│     (easiest first, build confidence before the hard ones)                   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ── Scenario 1: Worker Node NotReady ── -->
                <h4>Scenario 1: Worker Node NotReady</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Problem</h5>
                    <p><code>kubectl get nodes</code> shows <code>worker01 NotReady</code>. Pods on this node are being evicted.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔴 Worker Node NotReady — Step-by-Step Walkthrough</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get nodes                                                 │
│     NAME       STATUS     ROLES    AGE   VERSION                        │
│     master     Ready      master   30d   v1.31.0                        │
│     worker01   NotReady   &lt;none&gt;   30d   v1.31.0    ◄── PROBLEM        │
│     worker02   Ready      &lt;none&gt;   30d   v1.31.0                        │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Describe the node:                                         │
│     $ kubectl describe node worker01                                    │
│     Conditions:                                                         │
│       Ready   False   KubeletNotReady   container runtime not running   │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE — SSH to the node:                                          │
│     $ ssh worker01                                                      │
│     $ systemctl status kubelet                                          │
│     → Active: inactive (dead)    ◄── kubelet is not running!            │
│                                                                         │
│     $ systemctl status containerd                                       │
│     → Active: inactive (dead)    ◄── container runtime also down!       │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Restart services:                                             │
│     $ systemctl restart containerd                                      │
│     $ systemctl restart kubelet                                         │
│     $ systemctl enable kubelet    (ensure it starts on boot)            │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY — Back on the master:                                        │
│     $ kubectl get nodes                                                 │
│     worker01   Ready   &lt;none&gt;   30d   v1.31.0   ← ✅ Fixed!            │
│                                                                         │
│  ⚠️  OTHER COMMON CAUSES:                                               │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │ • kubelet config wrong → /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml   │            │
│  │ • swap enabled → swapoff -a                             │            │
│  │ • disk pressure → df -h (clear space)                   │            │
│  │ • CNI plugin missing → ls /etc/cni/net.d/               │            │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ── Scenario 2: Pod Stuck in Pending ── -->
                <h4>Scenario 2: Pod Stuck in Pending</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Problem</h5>
                    <p><code>easypj-web</code> pod in the <code>easypj</code> namespace is stuck in <code>Pending</code> state and won't schedule.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🟡 Pod Pending — Scheduling Failure Walkthrough</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME                 READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE               │
│     easypj-web-xxxx      0/1     Pending   0          5m                │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Describe the pod (Events are KEY):                         │
│     $ kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxxx -n easypj                    │
│     Events:                                                             │
│       Warning  FailedScheduling  0/3 nodes are available:               │
│         1 node(s) had taint {node-role.kubernetes.io/master: },    ◄──  │
│         that the pod didn't tolerate,                                   │
│         2 Insufficient cpu.                                        ◄──  │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE — Two issues revealed:                                      │
│     a) Master node has taint → pod can't schedule there                 │
│     b) Worker nodes don't have enough CPU for the request               │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Reduce resource requests:                                     │
│     $ kubectl edit deployment easypj-web -n easypj                      │
│     resources:                                                          │
│       requests:                                                         │
│         cpu: "100m"       ← was "2000m", reduced to fit                 │
│         memory: "256Mi"                                                 │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj -w                                     │
│     easypj-web-xxxx   1/1   Running   0   15s   ← ✅ Scheduled!        │
│                                                                         │
│  📋 COMMON PENDING CAUSES:                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐           │
│  │ Event Message            │ Fix                           │           │
│  ├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤           │
│  │ Insufficient cpu/memory  │ Reduce requests or add nodes  │           │
│  │ pod didn't tolerate      │ Add toleration or remove taint│           │
│  │ no nodes match selector  │ Fix nodeSelector or label node│           │
│  │ PVC not found            │ Create the missing PVC        │           │
│  └──────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘           │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ── Scenario 3: Service Not Accessible ── -->
                <h4>Scenario 3: Service Not Accessible</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Problem</h5>
                    <p>Can't reach <code>easypj</code> via the NodePort service. Requests to <code>http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:&lt;nodeport&gt;</code> time out.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔵 Service Not Accessible — Label Mismatch Walkthrough</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. IDENTIFY — Check the service:                                       │
│     $ kubectl get svc easypj-svc -n easypj                              │
│     NAME         TYPE       CLUSTER-IP    PORT(S)        AGE            │
│     easypj-svc   NodePort   10.96.45.12   80:30080/TCP   2h             │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Check if endpoints exist:                                  │
│     $ kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc -n easypj                        │
│     NAME         ENDPOINTS   AGE                                        │
│     easypj-svc   &lt;none&gt;      2h   ◄── NO ENDPOINTS! Service has        │
│                                         no matching pods!               │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE — Compare selector vs pod labels:                           │
│     $ kubectl get svc easypj-svc -n easypj -o yaml | grep -A3 selector │
│       selector:                                                         │
│         app: easypj-web       ◄── Service looks for this label          │
│                                                                         │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj --show-labels                          │
│       easypj-web-xxxx   Running   app=easypj   ◄── Pod has THIS label  │
│                                                                         │
│     → MISMATCH! Service: "easypj-web" vs Pod: "easypj"                 │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Correct the service selector:                                 │
│     $ kubectl edit svc easypj-svc -n easypj                             │
│     selector:                                                           │
│       app: easypj         ← changed from "easypj-web" to "easypj"      │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get endpoints easypj-svc -n easypj                        │
│     ENDPOINTS: 10.244.1.5:8000   ← ✅ Pod is now connected!            │
│                                                                         │
│     $ curl http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:30080                                       │
│     → ✅ easypj responds!                                               │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ── Scenario 4: CrashLoopBackOff ── -->
                <h4>Scenario 4: CrashLoopBackOff</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Problem</h5>
                    <p><code>easypj-db</code> pod keeps crashing — restart count is climbing and backoff interval increasing.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔴 CrashLoopBackOff — Missing Environment Variable</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. IDENTIFY:                                                           │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj                                        │
│     NAME         READY   STATUS             RESTARTS      AGE           │
│     easypj-db-0  0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   7 (3m ago)    15m           │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Logs from the PREVIOUS crash:                              │
│     $ kubectl logs easypj-db-0 -n easypj --previous                     │
│                                                                         │
│     Error: Database is uninitialized and superuser password              │
│     is not specified. You must specify POSTGRES_PASSWORD to a    ◄──    │
│     non-empty value for the superuser.                                  │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE — Check pod spec:                                           │
│     $ kubectl describe pod easypj-db-0 -n easypj                        │
│     Containers:                                                         │
│       postgres:                                                         │
│         Image: postgres:15                                              │
│         Environment: &lt;none&gt;    ◄── No env vars set! Missing            │
│                                    POSTGRES_PASSWORD                     │
│     Last State: Terminated (Exit Code 1)  ← App error                   │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Add the required env var via Secret:                          │
│     $ kubectl create secret generic db-creds \                          │
│         --from-literal=POSTGRES_PASSWORD=securepass123 -n easypj        │
│                                                                         │
│     Then patch the StatefulSet:                                          │
│     $ kubectl edit statefulset easypj-db -n easypj                       │
│     env:                                                                │
│     - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD                                           │
│       valueFrom:                                                        │
│         secretKeyRef:                                                   │
│           name: db-creds                                                │
│           key: POSTGRES_PASSWORD                                        │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n easypj -w                                     │
│     easypj-db-0   1/1   Running   0   20s   ← ✅ No more crashes!      │
│     $ kubectl logs easypj-db-0 -n easypj                                │
│     → "database system is ready to accept connections"                  │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ── Scenario 5: DNS Resolution Failing ── -->
                <h4>Scenario 5: DNS Resolution Failing</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Problem</h5>
                    <p>Pods can't resolve service names. <code>easypj-web</code> can't reach <code>easypj-db</code> by hostname.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔴 DNS Failure — CoreDNS Troubleshooting</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. IDENTIFY — Test DNS from inside a pod:                              │
│     $ kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it --restart=    │
│       Never -- nslookup kubernetes.default                              │
│     → ;; connection timed out; no servers could be reached     ◄──     │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Check CoreDNS pods:                                       │
│     $ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns              │
│     NAME                       READY   STATUS             RESTARTS     │
│     coredns-5644d7b6d9-abc12   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4            │
│     coredns-5644d7b6d9-def34   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   4            │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE — Check CoreDNS logs:                                      │
│     $ kubectl logs -n kube-system coredns-5644d7b6d9-abc12              │
│     → plugin/forward: no nameservers found                              │
│                                                                         │
│     $ kubectl get cm coredns -n kube-system -o yaml                     │
│     → Corefile has forward . /etc/resolv.conf                           │
│     → But /etc/resolv.conf in the pod is empty or wrong                 │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — Correct the CoreDNS ConfigMap:                               │
│     $ kubectl edit cm coredns -n kube-system                            │
│     forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4    ← use specific DNS servers            │
│                                                                         │
│     $ kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system         │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl run dns-test --image=busybox:1.36 --rm -it --restart=    │
│       Never -- nslookup kubernetes.default                              │
│     Name:    kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local                       │
│     Address: 10.96.0.1   ← ✅ DNS is working!                          │
│                                                                         │
│  🔍 ALSO CHECK:                                                        │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │ • CoreDNS service IP:  kubectl get svc kube-dns         │            │
│  │   -n kube-system (must match pod's /etc/resolv.conf)    │            │
│  │ • Network plugin: is the CNI functional?                │            │
│  │ • Firewall rules blocking UDP port 53?                  │            │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- ── Scenario 6: etcd Cluster Unhealthy ── -->
                <h4>Scenario 6: etcd Cluster Unhealthy</h4>
                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Problem</h5>
                    <p>API server is intermittently unavailable. <code>kubectl</code> commands sometimes time out. Suspected etcd issue.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔴 etcd Unhealthy — Diagnosis and Recovery</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. IDENTIFY — Check etcd health:                                       │
│     $ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \                           │
│       --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \                              │
│       --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \                        │
│       --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \                      │
│       --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key                         │
│                                                                         │
│     https://127.0.0.1:2379 is unhealthy: failed to commit    ◄──       │
│                                                                         │
│  2. GATHER — Check etcd member list and logs:                           │
│     $ etcdctl member list (with same cert flags above)                  │
│     $ kubectl logs etcd-master -n kube-system                           │
│     OR if API is down:                                                  │
│     $ crictl logs $(crictl ps -a | grep etcd | awk '{print $1}')       │
│                                                                         │
│  3. ANALYZE — Common causes:                                           │
│     → Disk too slow (WAL write latency)                                 │
│     → Member removed/added incorrectly                                  │
│     → Data corruption                                                   │
│                                                                         │
│  4. FIX — If data corruption, restore from backup:                     │
│     a) Move API server manifest out to stop it:                         │
│        $ mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/         │
│                                                                         │
│     b) Restore etcd from snapshot:                                      │
│        $ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /backup/snapshot.db \   │
│          --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                              │
│                                                                         │
│     c) Update etcd manifest with new data-dir:                          │
│        $ vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml                         │
│        → Change --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored                       │
│        → Update hostPath volume to /var/lib/etcd-restored               │
│                                                                         │
│     d) Move API server manifest back:                                   │
│        $ mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/         │
│                                                                         │
│  5. VERIFY:                                                             │
│     $ kubectl get nodes        ← API responds                          │
│     $ etcdctl endpoint health  ← etcd is healthy                       │
│     ✅ Cluster recovered!                                               │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §34.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s34-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">34.2 Troubleshooting Methodology Checklist</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Every troubleshooting task — on the exam or in production — follows the same 5-step systematic method. Internalize this flow and you'll never freeze under pressure.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>The 5-Step Troubleshooting Method</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   THE 5-STEP TROUBLESHOOTING METHOD                          │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────┐    ┌──────────┐  │
│  │    1.     │    │    2.    │    │    3.     │    │  4.  │    │    5.    │  │
│  │ IDENTIFY │───▶│ GATHER  │───▶│ ANALYZE  │───▶│ FIX  │───▶│ VERIFY  │  │
│  │          │    │         │    │          │    │      │    │         │  │
│  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────┘    └──────────┘  │
│       │               │              │              │             │         │
│       ▼               ▼              ▼              ▼             ▼         │
│  ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐  │
│  │ What is  │   │ kubectl  │   │ What     │   │ Apply    │ │ Did the  │  │
│  │ broken?  │   │ describe │   │ changed? │   │ smallest │ │ fix      │  │
│  │ What's   │   │ kubectl  │   │ What's   │   │ possible │ │ work?    │  │
│  │ the      │   │ logs     │   │ diff-    │   │ change   │ │ Are      │  │
│  │ symptom? │   │ kubectl  │   │ erent?   │   │          │ │ there    │  │
│  │          │   │ exec     │   │          │   │          │ │ side     │  │
│  │ get pods │   │ events   │   │ Compare  │   │ Don't    │ │ effects? │  │
│  │ get nodes│   │ journal  │   │ working  │   │ change   │ │          │  │
│  │ get svc  │   │ systemctl│   │ vs broken│   │ too much │ │ Re-run   │  │
│  │ get events│  │ crictl   │   │          │   │ at once  │ │ step 1   │  │
│  └──────────┘   └──────────┘   └──────────┘   └──────────┘ └──────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  NEVER SKIP STEP 5!  Many CKA points are lost because candidates        │
│     fix the issue but don't verify, and the fix was incomplete.             │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Essential Troubleshooting Commands — Quick Reference</h4>
                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Step</th>
                                <th>Command</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>1. IDENTIFY</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -A -o wide</code></td>
                                <td>See all pods across all namespaces with node placement</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code></td>
                                <td>Node status, IP, OS, container runtime</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code></td>
                                <td>Recent cluster-wide events timeline</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>2. GATHER</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Detailed pod info — Events section at bottom!</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                                <td>Logs from the last crashed container</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Logs from a specific container in multi-container pod</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></td>
                                <td>Shell into a running container to inspect</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>3. ANALYZE</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Node conditions, taints, allocated resources</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager -l</code></td>
                                <td>kubelet logs on a node (SSH first)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>crictl ps -a</code></td>
                                <td>Container runtime status (when API is down)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>4. FIX</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl edit &lt;resource&gt; &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                                <td>Quick in-place fix of a resource</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></td>
                                <td>Restart kubelet after fixing node issues</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>5. VERIFY</strong></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get pods -w</code></td>
                                <td>Watch pods transition to Running</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td><code>kubectl get events --field-selector reason=Started</code></td>
                                <td>Confirm containers started successfully</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Part 9 Key Takeaways</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Troubleshooting flow:</strong> <code>kubectl get → describe → logs → exec → events</code>. ALWAYS follow this order.</li>
                        <li><strong>Pod statuses:</strong> CrashLoopBackOff (app crashes), ImagePullBackOff (wrong image/tag), Pending (no node/resources), Error (exit non-zero)</li>
                        <li><strong>Node troubleshooting:</strong> Check kubelet (<code>systemctl status</code>), certificates (<code>kubeadm certs</code>), connectivity</li>
                        <li><strong>Control plane down?</strong> Check static pod manifests in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code> and kubelet logs</li>
                        <li><strong>5-step method:</strong> IDENTIFY → GATHER → ANALYZE → FIX → VERIFY — never skip VERIFY!</li>
                        <li><strong>Use <code>--previous</code></strong> to see logs from crashed containers. Use <code>-f</code> to follow logs in real-time.</li>
                        <li><strong>CKA weight: 30%</strong> — the heaviest domain! Practice every scenario until you can solve it in under 5 minutes.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h5>✅ Part 9 Competency Checklist</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Can you debug a CrashLoopBackOff pod in under 3 minutes?</li>
                        <li>Can you diagnose a NotReady node and bring it back?</li>
                        <li>Can you restore etcd from a snapshot backup?</li>
                        <li>Can you fix a broken service with no endpoints?</li>
                        <li>Can you troubleshoot DNS failure by inspecting CoreDNS?</li>
                        <li>Can you renew expired certificates and restore API access?</li>
                        <li>Can you identify and fix NetworkPolicy blocking traffic?</li>
                        <li>Do you follow IDENTIFY → GATHER → ANALYZE → FIX → VERIFY every time?</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <h4>CKA Exam Time Management — Troubleshooting Questions</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>CKA Exam Strategy — 2 Hours, ~17 Questions</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CKA EXAM TIME MANAGEMENT STRATEGY                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Total: 2 hours (120 minutes) │ ~17 questions │ Pass: 66%                   │
│  Troubleshooting: ~30% = approximately 5-6 questions                        │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐                │
│  │  RECOMMENDED APPROACH                                    │                │
│  │                                                          │                │
│  │  1. FIRST PASS (0-90 min):                               │                │
│  │     → Answer every question you can solve quickly         │                │
│  │     → Target: ~5 min per question                        │                │
│  │     → Skip any question that takes &gt; 8 min              │                │
│  │     → Flag skipped questions for second pass              │                │
│  │                                                          │                │
│  │  2. SECOND PASS (90-120 min):                             │                │
│  │     → Return to flagged/skipped questions                │                │
│  │     → Spend remaining time on highest-weight questions   │                │
│  │     → Partial answers still earn partial credit          │                │
│  │                                                          │                │
│  │  3. TROUBLESHOOTING SPEED TIPS:                           │                │
│  │     → Always check the NAMESPACE first (exam specifies)  │                │
│  │     → Use kubectl config set-context --current            │                │
│  │       --namespace=&lt;ns&gt; to avoid -n flag every time       │                │
│  │     → Have your debug sequence memorized:                 │                │
│  │       get → describe → logs → exec                       │                │
│  │     → Use kubectl explain &lt;resource&gt; for field reference │                │
│  │     → Copy-paste from kubernetes.io docs (allowed!)      │                │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘                │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  COMMON TIME WASTERS:                                                   │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ Trap                           │ Solution                        │       │
│  ├────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤       │
│  │ Typing long YAML from memory  │ Use --dry-run=client -o yaml    │       │
│  │ Searching docs endlessly      │ Bookmark key pages before exam  │       │
│  │ Forgetting to switch context  │ Read question header FIRST      │       │
│  │ Not verifying after fix       │ Budget 30s for verification     │       │
│  │ Perfecting a low-score Q      │ Move on, return if time permits │       │
│  └────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘       │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Troubleshooting Quick-Reference Cheat Sheet</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>🔥 One-Page Troubleshooting Cheat Sheet</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STATUS → FIRST COMMAND TO RUN                                               │
│  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════  │
│                                                                              │
│  Pod Pending          → kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;     (check Events)     │
│  CrashLoopBackOff     → kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous   (last crash log)   │
│  ImagePullBackOff     → kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;     (check image name) │
│  CreateContainerError → kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;     (missing CM/Secret)│
│  Running but unhealthy→ kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -f           (app errors)       │
│  OOMKilled (exit 137) → increase resources.limits.memory                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Node NotReady        → ssh node; systemctl status kubelet                  │
│  API Server down      → crictl ps -a; crictl logs &lt;id&gt;                     │
│  Scheduler broken     → cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml   │
│  etcd broken          → etcdctl endpoint health (with cert flags)           │
│                                                                              │
│  Service no endpoints → kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;   (selector mismatch) │
│  DNS not resolving    → kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns│
│  NetworkPolicy block  → kubectl describe netpol &lt;name&gt; (check selectors)  │
│  PVC Pending          → kubectl get storageclass       (class mismatch)    │
│                                                                              │
│  USEFUL SHORTCUTS:                                                           │
│  ─────────────────                                                           │
│  alias k=kubectl                          <span class="comment"># Already set on CKA exam         │</span>
│  export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"     <span class="comment"># Quick YAML generation          │</span>
│  export now="--force --grace-period 0"    <span class="comment"># Instant pod deletion            │</span>
│  k config set-context --current --namespace=X  <span class="comment"># Set default namespace     │</span>
│  k explain pod.spec.containers --recursive     <span class="comment"># Field reference            │</span>
│                                                                              │
│  FILE LOCATIONS TO REMEMBER:                                                 │
│  ─────────────────────────────                                               │
│  /etc/kubernetes/manifests/    → Static pod YAML files (control plane)      │
│  /etc/kubernetes/pki/          → Cluster certificates                       │
│  /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml  → kubelet configuration                      │
│  /var/log/containers/          → Container log files on node                │
│  /etc/cni/net.d/               → CNI plugin configuration                  │
│  /var/lib/etcd/                → etcd data directory                        │
│  ~/.kube/config                → kubectl configuration (kubeconfig)         │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 34 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: Troubleshooting Scenario Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Always VERIFY after fixing</strong> — The exam checks the end state. If you fix a broken service but don't verify with <code>curl</code> or <code>kubectl get endpoints</code>, you might have missed something. Follow the 5-step method: IDENTIFY → GATHER → ANALYZE → FIX → VERIFY</li>
                        <li><strong>Multiple issues per question</strong> — A single scenario may have 2-3 things broken (wrong label + wrong port + missing service). Fix one, test, then check for more. Don't assume one fix solves everything</li>
                        <li><strong>Read the question TWICE</strong> — "Ensure the pod runs on node01" could mean nodeSelector, nodeName, or taint toleration. "Fix the deployment" could mean image, resources, selectors, or mount issues. Misreading costs 5-10 minutes</li>
                        <li><strong>Use <code>kubectl explain</code> as your API reference</strong> — <code>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources --recursive</code>. Faster than searching docs. Works offline (exam environment)</li>
                        <li><strong>Time management</strong> — 17 questions in 2 hours = ~7 min per question. If stuck for 10 minutes, flag it and move on. Come back with fresh eyes. Easy questions first</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 34 — How Multi-Layer Troubleshooting Scenarios Work ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 34 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Multi-Layer Troubleshooting Scenarios Work: The Systematic Debugging Methodology, Dependency Chain Analysis, and the CKA Troubleshooting Mindset</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ The CKA Troubleshooting Methodology — Systematic Root Cause Analysis</h5>
            <p>Complex CKA scenarios involve multiple failing components. The key is a systematic top-down approach that isolates the root cause layer by layer.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SYSTEMATIC TROUBLESHOOTING METHODOLOGY                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  LAYER MODEL (debug from top down):                                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ LAYER 1: APPLICATION (logs, exit codes, probes)                       │   │
│  │   └── LAYER 2: POD (status, events, describe, resource usage)         │   │
│  │       └── LAYER 3: CONTROLLER (Deployment/RS/DaemonSet status)        │   │
│  │           └── LAYER 4: SERVICE/NETWORK (endpoints, DNS, NetworkPolicy)│   │
│  │               └── LAYER 5: NODE (kubelet, containerd, disk, memory)   │   │
│  │                   └── LAYER 6: CONTROL PLANE (API, etcd, scheduler)   │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  SCENARIO: "Deployment is running but unreachable"                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. APP: kubectl logs → app listening on correct port? ✅              │   │
│  │ 2. POD: kubectl describe → probes passing? ready? ✅                  │   │
│  │ 3. CONTROLLER: kubectl get deploy,rs → desired=ready? ✅              │   │
│  │ 4. SERVICE: kubectl get svc → selector matches pod labels?           │   │
│  │    kubectl get endpoints → ENDPOINTS is &lt;none&gt;? ❌                   │   │
│  │    → Service selector doesn't match pod labels!                      │   │
│  │    → Fix: update Service selector or pod labels                      │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ 5. VERIFY: curl &lt;svc-ip&gt;:&lt;port&gt; → works now ✅                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  DEPENDENCY CHAIN ANALYSIS — Finding the Root Cause:                         │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ When multiple things are broken, find the DEPENDENCY that broke first│   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Chain: DNS down → Services unreachable → Pods can't talk → app dead  │   │
│  │  ROOT CAUSE: CoreDNS pods crashed (check kube-system)                 │   │
│  │  FIX: Restart CoreDNS → everything recovers                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Chain: Node disk full → kubelet can't write → node NotReady           │   │
│  │  → pods evicted → Deployment shows fewer replicas                      │   │
│  │  ROOT CAUSE: DiskPressure on node (check df -h)                       │   │
│  │  FIX: Clean disk → node recovers                                       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CKA TROUBLESHOOTING MINDSET — Rules for the Exam:                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ 1. ALWAYS check namespace first: -n &lt;ns&gt; (most common mistake)       │   │
│  │ 2. ALWAYS check context: kubectl config current-context               │   │
│  │ 3. DESCRIBE before LOGS: describe gives events + state; logs gives    │   │
│  │    application-level details                                          │   │
│  │ 4. CHECK EVENTS: kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp          │   │
│  │ 5. WHEN STUCK: Move up one layer. Pod broken? Check node. Node        │   │
│  │    broken? Check kubelet. Kubelet broken? Check disk/containerd.      │   │
│  │ 6. VERIFY AFTER FIXING: Don't assume. Run a test command.             │   │
│  │ 7. ONE FIX AT A TIME: Fix one thing, verify, then fix the next.       │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> Troubleshooting is 30% of the CKA — the largest single domain. Exam questions often have 2-3 cascading failures. Fix the ROOT CAUSE first (not the symptoms). Common root causes: wrong namespace, wrong context, label mismatch, missing ConfigMap/Secret, insufficient resources, expired certs. After every fix, verify with a test command before moving on. Don't fix-and-forget.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <p>When restoring etcd, you must use a <strong>new data-dir path</strong> (<code>--data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>). Don't restore in-place to <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> — it will fail if the existing data conflicts. Also update <strong>both</strong> the <code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the <code>hostPath</code> volume in the etcd static pod manifest.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Troubleshooting decision tree:</strong><br>
                <strong>Pod not running?</strong> → <code>kubectl describe pod</code> → check Events<br>
                <strong>Service not reachable?</strong> → <code>kubectl get endpoints</code> → if empty, fix selector<br>
                <strong>Node NotReady?</strong> → SSH + <code>systemctl status kubelet</code> + <code>journalctl -u kubelet</code><br>
                <strong>API server down?</strong> → Check <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code><br>
                <strong>DNS broken?</strong> → <code>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns</code></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 34)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-drill"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧪 Drill</h4>
                <p>A hands-on practice drill goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 34 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch34-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 34 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (10)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Multi-layer troubleshooting recall — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment's pods are running but the Service returns no response. Walk through the troubleshooting methodology.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Systematic 5-step approach:</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check pods are running and ready</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=web
<span class="comment"># NAME        READY  STATUS   RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc12   1/1    Running  0        ← Looks good</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check Service exists and has correct selector</span>
kubectl get svc web-svc
kubectl describe svc web-svc
<span class="comment"># Selector: app=web</span>
<span class="comment"># Port:     80/TCP</span>
<span class="comment"># TargetPort: 8080</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check Endpoints (MOST CRITICAL STEP)</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># NAME      ENDPOINTS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-svc   &lt;none&gt;     ← NO ENDPOINTS! Selector mismatch!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Compare selector with pod labels</span>
kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment"># NAME        LABELS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc12   app=webapp    ← "webapp" not "web"!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix the selector</span>
kubectl edit svc web-svc
<span class="comment"># Change selector from app: web → app: webapp</span>

<span class="comment"># OR fix the pod labels:</span>
kubectl label pod web-abc12 app=web --overwrite

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># web-svc   10.244.1.5:8080   ← Endpoints populated!</span>

<span class="comment"># Test:</span>
kubectl run test --rm -it --image=busybox -- wget -qO- web-svc
<span class="comment"># &lt;html&gt;...&lt/html&gt; ✓</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The #1 CKA troubleshooting pattern: Service → Endpoints → Pods. If Endpoints shows <code>&lt;none&gt;</code>, the Service selector doesn't match any pod labels. Always: (1) check pods are Running+Ready, (2) check Service selector, (3) check Endpoints, (4) compare labels, (5) fix. This scenario appears in almost every CKA exam. The <code>targetPort</code> (container port) must match what the app listens on.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A Deployment isn't rolling out new pods after an image update. What's wrong?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check rollout status:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment web
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "web" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># ← Stuck! New pods aren't becoming ready</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the new ReplicaSet:</span>
kubectl get rs
<span class="comment"># NAME          DESIRED  CURRENT  READY</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc123    3        3        3     ← OLD (still running)</span>
<span class="comment"># web-def456    1        1        0     ← NEW (0 ready!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Check the new pod:</span>
kubectl get pods | grep web-def456
<span class="comment"># web-def456-xyz   0/1   ImagePullBackOff   0   2m</span>

<span class="comment"># Root cause: Wrong image name</span>
kubectl describe pod web-def456-xyz | grep Image
<span class="comment"># Image: nginxx:latest  ← Typo! "nginxx"</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix:</span>
kubectl set image deployment/web web=nginx:latest
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment web

<span class="comment"># If rollout was paused:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment web
<span class="comment"># "deployment is paused" ← Someone paused it!</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment web

<span class="comment"># If maxUnavailable=0 and cluster is at capacity:</span>
<span class="comment"># New pod can't schedule → old pod can't terminate → deadlock</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Increase maxSurge or reduce replicas temporarily</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment web
<span class="comment"># deployment "web" successfully rolled out ✓</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Stuck rollouts happen when new pods can't become Ready. Common causes: (1) wrong image name/tag (ImagePullBackOff), (2) readiness probe fails, (3) resource constraints, (4) rollout paused. On the CKA: check `kubectl rollout status`, then check the new ReplicaSet's pods. <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> is the fastest way to revert. Deadlock scenario: maxUnavailable=0 + maxSurge=0 = can't add new or remove old pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">DNS resolution isn't working inside pods. How do you debug?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Test DNS from inside a pod</span>
kubectl run dns-test --rm -it --image=busybox -- nslookup kubernetes
<span class="comment"># Server:    10.96.0.10</span>
<span class="comment"># nslookup: can't resolve 'kubernetes'  ← DNS broken!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check CoreDNS pods</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns
<span class="comment"># NAME                      READY  STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># coredns-5d78c9869d-abc12  0/1    CrashLoopBackOff  ← Problem!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS logs</span>
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check CoreDNS ConfigMap</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for syntax errors in Corefile</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check kube-dns Service</span>
kubectl get svc kube-dns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Should be ClusterIP 10.96.0.10</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check pod DNS config</span>
kubectl exec dns-test -- cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver 10.96.0.10     ← Should point to kube-dns</span>
<span class="comment"># search default.svc.cluster.local svc.cluster.local cluster.local</span>

<span class="comment"># Common fixes:</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS crash → Check ConfigMap for syntax errors</span>
<span class="comment"># Wrong nameserver → Check kubelet --cluster-dns flag</span>
<span class="comment"># Loop detected → Remove "loop" plugin from Corefile</span>
<span class="comment"># Upstream DNS → Check forward . directive in Corefile</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix CoreDNS ConfigMap:</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Then restart:</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>DNS is CoreDNS running in <code>kube-system</code>. Pods get the DNS IP (10.96.0.10) from kubelet config. On the CKA: test with <code>nslookup kubernetes</code>, check CoreDNS pods, check the Corefile ConfigMap. Common issue: loop detection plugin causes crashes on some setups — fix by editing the ConfigMap. The <code>kube-dns</code> Service name is kept for backward compatibility, but it routes to CoreDNS pods.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A user reports they can't create pods in a namespace. How do you check RBAC permissions?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check what the user CAN do</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as=user1
<span class="comment"># no  ← User can't create pods!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check all permissions for the user</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=user1 -n dev
<span class="comment"># Resources  Verbs</span>
<span class="comment"># (nothing relevant)  ← No roles bound!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check existing RoleBindings</span>
kubectl get rolebindings -n dev
<span class="comment"># NAME       ROLE              SUBJECTS</span>
<span class="comment"># (empty)    ← No bindings!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check ClusterRoleBindings</span>
kubectl get clusterrolebindings | grep user1
<span class="comment"># (nothing)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Create the necessary RBAC</span>
<span class="comment"># Option A: Create Role + RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create role pod-manager -n dev \
--verb=create,get,list,delete \
--resource=pods

kubectl create rolebinding user1-pods -n dev \
--role=pod-manager \
--user=user1

<span class="comment"># Option B: Use existing ClusterRole</span>
kubectl create rolebinding user1-edit -n dev \
--clusterrole=edit \
--user=user1

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as=user1
<span class="comment"># yes ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Debug: Check which role is blocking</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as=user1 -v=5
<span class="comment"># Shows API calls and authorization decisions</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl auth can-i</code> tests permissions. <code>--as=user1</code> impersonates the user. <code>--list</code> shows all allowed actions. On the CKA: if a user can't do something, check RoleBindings in the namespace and ClusterRoleBindings. The fix is creating a Role + RoleBinding. Built-in ClusterRoles (view, edit, admin, cluster-admin) save time. <code>-v=5</code> shows verbose authorization debugging.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A NetworkPolicy is blocking expected traffic. How do you debug it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Symptom: Pod A can't reach Pod B, but it worked before</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check NetworkPolicies in the namespace</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies -n prod
<span class="comment"># NAME           POD-SELECTOR  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># deny-all       app=api       5m   ← Recently created!</span>
<span class="comment"># allow-web      app=web       30d</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Examine the blocking policy</span>
kubectl describe networkpolicy deny-all -n prod
<span class="comment"># PodSelector: app=api</span>
<span class="comment"># Allowing ingress traffic: &lt;none&gt;   ← Blocks ALL ingress!</span>
<span class="comment"># Allowing egress traffic:  &lt;none&gt;   ← Blocks ALL egress!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Verify traffic is actually blocked</span>
kubectl exec -n prod web-pod -- curl -s --max-time 3 api-svc:8080
<span class="comment"># curl: (28) Connection timed out  ← Blocked!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Fix — Add allow rule for needed traffic</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
name: allow-web-to-api
namespace: prod
spec:
podSelector:
matchLabels:
  app: api
policyTypes: ["Ingress"]
ingress:
- from:
- podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
ports:
- port: 8080

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl exec -n prod web-pod -- curl -s api-svc:8080
<span class="comment"># {"status": "ok"} ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Key rules:</span>
<span class="comment"># - If ANY NetworkPolicy selects a pod, only allowed traffic works</span>
<span class="comment"># - No NetworkPolicy = all traffic allowed</span>
<span class="comment"># - Multiple policies are ADDITIVE (union of all allow rules)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>NetworkPolicies are default-deny once applied. If a pod has ANY NetworkPolicy selecting it, only explicitly allowed traffic passes. On the CKA: check which policies select the target pod (via <code>podSelector</code>), verify with <code>curl</code>/<code>wget</code> from source pod, then add an allow rule if needed. Multiple NetworkPolicies are additive — the union of all rules determines what's allowed. Common trap: creating a deny-all without corresponding allow rules.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You have 5 minutes left on the CKA exam and 3 questions remaining. What's your strategy?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Strategy</th>
                                        <th>Action</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; font-weight: bold;">Triage</td>
                                        <td>Check weights — do highest % question first</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; font-weight: bold;">Imperative First</td>
                                        <td>Use <code>kubectl run/create/expose</code> not YAML</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; font-weight: bold;">Skip Stuck</td>
                                        <td>If stuck &gt;60s, flag and move on</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; font-weight: bold;">Partial Credit</td>
                                        <td>Create the resource even if incomplete — partial credit exists</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="padding: 8px; font-weight: bold;">Context!</td>
                                        <td>Always check <code>kubectl config use-context</code> — wrong context = 0 points</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Speed aliases (should already be set):</span>
export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"
export now="--force --grace-period 0"

<span class="comment"># Fast pod creation:</span>
k run web --image=nginx --port=80 $do > pod.yaml
vi pod.yaml  <span class="comment"># Quick edit</span>
k apply -f pod.yaml

<span class="comment"># Fast service:</span>
k expose pod web --port=80 --target-port=80

<span class="comment"># Fast delete and recreate:</span>
k delete pod broken $now
k run broken --image=nginx</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Time management is critical on the CKA. With 5 minutes left: (1) do the highest-weight question, (2) use imperative commands exclusively, (3) don't debug — create from scratch if faster, (4) always verify the context with <code>kubectl config use-context</code>. Partial credit exists, so creating an incomplete resource is better than skipping entirely. The <code>$do</code> and <code>$now</code> variables save significant time.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A pod can't resolve an external domain (e.g., <code>google.com</code>) but can resolve cluster services. What's the issue?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Verify internal DNS works</span>
kubectl exec test-pod -- nslookup kubernetes.default
<span class="comment"># Name:   kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 10.96.0.1  ← Internal DNS works!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Test external DNS</span>
kubectl exec test-pod -- nslookup google.com
<span class="comment"># ** server can't find google.com: SERVFAIL ← External fails!</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check CoreDNS forward config</span>
kubectl get configmap coredns -n kube-system -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Corefile:</span>
<span class="comment">#   .:53 {</span>
<span class="comment">#     forward . /etc/resolv.conf    ← Should forward to upstream DNS</span>
<span class="comment">#   }</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check the node's /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS forwards external queries to the node's DNS</span>
ssh worker-1
cat /etc/resolv.conf
<span class="comment"># nameserver 127.0.0.53  ← systemd-resolved (might cause loops!)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Fix — Point CoreDNS to a real DNS server</span>
kubectl edit configmap coredns -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Change: forward . /etc/resolv.conf</span>
<span class="comment"># To:     forward . 8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Restart CoreDNS</span>
kubectl rollout restart deployment coredns -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify</span>
kubectl exec test-pod -- nslookup google.com
<span class="comment"># Name:   google.com</span>
<span class="comment"># Address: 142.250.80.46 ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative: Check NetworkPolicy blocking DNS egress</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies -n &lt;namespace&gt;
<span class="comment"># If egress restricted, must allow UDP port 53 to kube-dns</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Internal DNS works (CoreDNS resolves <code>.cluster.local</code>) but external fails = upstream DNS forwarding issue. CoreDNS forwards external queries using the <code>forward</code> directive. If the node uses <code>systemd-resolved</code> (127.0.0.53), CoreDNS may loop. Fix by pointing to real upstream DNS (8.8.8.8). Also check NetworkPolicies — if egress is restricted, UDP port 53 to kube-dns must be explicitly allowed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">You need to fix a broken cluster where <code>kubectl</code> commands timeout. Where do you start?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># kubectl timeout = API server unreachable</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: SSH to the master node</span>
ssh master

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Check kubelet (manages static pods)</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># If stopped → systemctl start kubelet</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Check API server container</span>
crictl ps | grep kube-apiserver
<span class="comment"># If not listed → Check static pod manifest</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Check API server manifest</span>
cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># Look for:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong image</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong port</span>
<span class="comment"># - Missing/wrong cert paths</span>
<span class="comment"># - Syntax errors</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check API server logs</span>
crictl logs $(crictl ps -a | grep apiserver | awk '{print $1}')
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
journalctl -u kubelet | grep apiserver

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Check etcd (API server depends on it)</span>
crictl ps | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># If etcd is down → API server can't work</span>
<span class="comment"># Check: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Check certificates</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -noout -dates
<span class="comment"># notAfter=... ← Expired?</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix sequence:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Fix manifests in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. kubelet auto-restarts static pods</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Wait 30-60 seconds</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Test: kubectl get nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># Common CKA causes:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Wrong port in kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># - Cert file path typo</span>
<span class="comment"># - etcd endpoint wrong</span>
<span class="comment"># - Image tag wrong</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>kubectl timeout = API server down. On the CKA: SSH to master, use <code>crictl</code> (not kubectl!), check kubelet, check static pod manifests. API server is a static pod managed by kubelet. Common exam scenario: a deliberate error is placed in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</code>. Fix the manifest → kubelet detects the change → API server restarts. Check etcd too — if etcd is down, API server won't work even if its config is correct.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you use <code>kubectl explain</code> to quickly find the correct YAML field paths during the exam?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Basic: What fields does a Pod spec have?</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec
<span class="comment"># Lists all fields with descriptions</span>

<span class="comment"># Recursive: ALL nested fields</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive
<span class="comment"># Shows the full tree without descriptions</span>
<span class="comment"># Perfect for finding the exact path</span>

<span class="comment"># Specific field:</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources
<span class="comment"># Shows: limits, requests sub-fields</span>

<span class="comment"># Common exam lookups:</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec.volumes --recursive
<span class="comment"># Shows all volume types and their fields</span>

kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.volumeMounts
<span class="comment"># mountPath, name, subPath, readOnly</span>

kubectl explain pod.spec.tolerations
<span class="comment"># effect, key, operator, tolerationSeconds, value</span>

kubectl explain pod.spec.affinity --recursive
<span class="comment"># nodeAffinity, podAffinity, podAntiAffinity</span>

kubectl explain deployment.spec.strategy
<span class="comment"># type, rollingUpdate (maxSurge, maxUnavailable)</span>

kubectl explain pv.spec --recursive
<span class="comment"># All PV fields: capacity, accessModes, hostPath, nfs...</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA shortcut: Use with aliases</span>
alias kx="kubectl explain"
kx pod.spec.securityContext --recursive
kx pod.spec.initContainers

<span class="comment"># Can't remember the field name? Use recursive + grep:</span>
kubectl explain pod.spec --recursive | grep -i "toleration"
<span class="comment">#   tolerations  &lt;[]Toleration&gt;</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl explain</code> is your built-in documentation during the CKA — it's faster than searching docs. <code>--recursive</code> shows the full field tree. Use it when you can't remember the exact YAML path (e.g., "is it <code>spec.containers.resources.limits</code> or <code>spec.resources.limits</code>?"). <code>grep</code> with recursive output helps find field names. On the CKA, alias it as <code>kx</code> for speed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A multi-issue scenario: pods are Pending, a Service has no endpoints, and a node is NotReady. Solve it systematically.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Multi-issue diagnosis order: Infrastructure → Workloads → Networking</span>

<span class="comment"># PHASE 1: FIX INFRASTRUCTURE (Node)</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS     ROLES          AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># master     Ready      control-plane  30d</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   NotReady   &lt;none&gt;         30d  ← Fix this first!</span>

ssh worker-1
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: inactive (dead)</span>
systemctl start kubelet
systemctl enable kubelet
exit
<span class="comment"># Wait: kubectl get nodes → worker-1 Ready ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># PHASE 2: FIX WORKLOADS (Pending Pods)</span>
kubectl get pods
<span class="comment"># NAME       READY  STATUS   RESTARTS</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc    0/1    Pending  0</span>
kubectl describe pod web-abc | tail -10
<span class="comment"># "0/1 nodes available: 1 node had taint {NoSchedule}"</span>
<span class="comment"># (Before we fixed the node, no node was available)</span>
<span class="comment"># After node fix → pod auto-schedules!</span>
kubectl get pods  <span class="comment"># web-abc → Running ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># PHASE 3: FIX NETWORKING (Service)</span>
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># web-svc   &lt;none&gt;  ← Still no endpoints!</span>

kubectl get svc web-svc -o yaml | grep -A2 selector
<span class="comment"># selector:</span>
<span class="comment">#   app: website      ← Wrong label!</span>

kubectl get pods --show-labels
<span class="comment"># web-abc   app=web   ← Label is "web" not "website"</span>

kubectl patch svc web-svc -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"app":"web"}}}'
kubectl get endpoints web-svc
<span class="comment"># web-svc   10.244.1.5:80 ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># VERIFY ALL:</span>
kubectl get nodes        <span class="comment"># All Ready ✓</span>
kubectl get pods         <span class="comment"># All Running ✓</span>
kubectl get endpoints    <span class="comment"># All populated ✓</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Multi-issue scenarios test your ability to prioritize. Fix order: Infrastructure (nodes) → Workloads (pods) → Networking (services). Fixing the node first often auto-resolves the Pending pods (they were waiting for a schedulable node). Then fix the Service selector to populate endpoints. On the CKA, issues are often cascading — one root cause creates multiple symptoms. Fix the root cause first and check if downstream issues self-resolve before spending time on them.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 34 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART9 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part9-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 9 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Troubleshooting — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You can now run a disciplined fault-finding loop across the whole stack: pods (status → events → logs), nodes and the control plane (NotReady, crictl/journalctl, static-pod manifests), logs and metrics, and the Service/DNS/NetworkPolicy playbook. This is the CKA’s single biggest domain — 30%.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 🔴 Part 9 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Pods (Ch 31):</strong> read STATUS (Pending/ImagePullBackOff/CrashLoopBackOff/OOMKilled) → <code>describe</code> Events → <code>logs</code> (+<code>--previous</code>) → <code>exec</code>/<code>debug</code>. Status names the cause.</li>
                    <li><strong>Nodes &amp; control plane (Ch 32):</strong> NotReady → check kubelet (<code>journalctl -u kubelet</code>) and the CNI; when kubectl is down, use <code>crictl ps/logs</code> and inspect <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong>Logs &amp; metrics (Ch 33):</strong> <code>kubectl logs</code>, node/pod <code>kubectl top</code> (needs metrics-server), and Events are your evidence; there’s no built-in log aggregation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Scenario playbook (Ch 34):</strong> Service has no endpoints (selector mismatch), DNS fails (CoreDNS), traffic blocked (NetworkPolicy/kube-proxy) — a fixed symptom → command → cause → fix routine.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam:</strong> 30% lives here — it’s the largest domain; speed and a methodical loop win the most marks.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 9 Visual Summary — the symptom → command → cause → fix loop</div>
<pre>
   🔎 SYMPTOM ──▶ 🧪 COMMAND ──▶ 🧠 CAUSE ──▶ 🔧 FIX ──▶ ✅ VERIFY
   pod:  get → describe(Events) → logs --previous → exec/debug
   node: get nodes → journalctl -u kubelet → crictl ps/logs → manifests
   net:  endpoints? → CoreDNS? → NetworkPolicy/kube-proxy?
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   a repeatable loop turns "broken easypj" into a fixed, verified task.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 9 gave you a method. Every failure follows symptom → command → cause → fix → verify: a pod’s status and events name the problem, a node’s kubelet/crictl/journalctl reveal a NotReady cause, and the networking playbook (endpoints → DNS → policy) finds why traffic doesn’t flow.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> practise the loop until it’s automatic and you own the 30% Troubleshooting domain — the difference between passing and failing the CKA. easypj can now be broken and fixed at every layer. Next: Parts 10+ take it to production HA, packaging, hardening, and observability.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

                <!-- ==================== PART10 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part10">
            <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 10 · Production and Beyond</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Run It in Production</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 35–37 · Advanced · <em>"No single point of failure, anywhere"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">A single-control-plane cluster is a demo, not production. Part 10 takes easypj to a real operating posture: highly-available control planes with etcd quorum, the managed cloud Kubernetes services (EKS/GKE/AKS) and how your kubeadm skills map onto them, and the CI/CD + GitOps pipelines that ship changes automatically and safely.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> design an HA control plane (stacked vs external etcd, quorum math, leader election); map self-managed concepts onto EKS/GKE/AKS; and build a CI/CD + GitOps flow that deploys easypj from a git commit with rollback.</div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — from one cluster to a production platform</div>
<pre>
   Ch 35 HIGH AVAILABILITY ──▶ Ch 36 CLOUD K8s ──▶ Ch 37 CI/CD &amp; GITOPS
   stacked/external etcd        EKS · GKE · AKS        pipelines · ArgoCD/Flux
   quorum · leader election     managed control plane  git as source of truth
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: easypj runs on a fault-tolerant platform and ships
   itself from git, with no single point of failure and no manual deploys.
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <article class="chapter" id="ch35">
