            </div>


            <!-- ─────────── §35.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.1 HA Architecture</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>A single control plane node is a <strong>single point of failure</strong>. If it goes down, the API server becomes unreachable, no new pods can be scheduled, and the cluster is essentially frozen. High Availability (HA) eliminates this by running multiple control plane nodes.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Single vs HA Control Plane — Why HA Matters</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                              │
│  ❌ SINGLE CONTROL PLANE                ✅ HIGH AVAILABILITY (HA)            │
│  ════════════════════════               ═══════════════════════════           │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────┐                       ┌────────────────────┐              │
│  │ Control Plane│ ← Single point        │   LOAD BALANCER    │              │
│  │              │    of failure!         │  (HAProxy/nginx)   │              │
│  │ API Server   │                       └───────┬────────────┘              │
│  │ etcd         │                      ┌────────┼────────┐                  │
│  │ Scheduler    │                      ▼        ▼        ▼                  │
│  │ Ctrl-Manager │               ┌──────────┐┌──────────┐┌──────────┐       │
│  └──────┬───────┘               │  CP-1    ││  CP-2    ││  CP-3    │       │
│         │                       │ API ✅    ││ API ✅    ││ API ✅    │       │
│    ┌────┼────┐                  │ etcd ✅   ││ etcd ✅   ││ etcd ✅   │       │
│    ▼    ▼    ▼                  │ sched ✅  ││ sched ⏸  ││ sched ⏸  │       │
│  W-1  W-2  W-3                  │ c-mgr ✅  ││ c-mgr ⏸  ││ c-mgr ⏸  │       │
│                                 └──────────┘└──────────┘└──────────┘       │
│  If CP goes down:                     (leader)   (standby)   (standby)      │
│  ❌ No new scheduling                                                        │
│  ❌ No API access                ┌────┼────┐                                │
│  ❌ No self-healing              ▼    ▼    ▼                                │
│  ❌ Cluster is FROZEN           W-1  W-2  W-3                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Existing pods keep running     If CP-1 goes down:                          │
│  but nothing new can happen     ✅ LB routes to CP-2/CP-3                   │
│                                 ✅ New leader elected in seconds             │
│                                 ✅ Full cluster operations continue          │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Key HA Principle</h5>
                    <p><strong>Only ONE</strong> scheduler and controller-manager are active at any time (the "leader"). The others are on <strong>hot standby</strong> — they watch and are ready to take over in seconds. All API servers, however, handle requests simultaneously (active-active behind the load balancer).</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>HA Cluster Architecture — Complete View</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                       HA CLUSTER ARCHITECTURE                                │
│                                                                              │
│                    ┌──────────────────────────┐                              │
│                    │     LOAD BALANCER         │                              │
│                    │   (HAProxy / nginx)       │                              │
│                    │   VIP: 10.0.0.100:6443    │                              │
│                    └────────────┬─────────────┘                              │
│                     ┌──────────┼──────────┐                                  │
│                     │          │          │                                   │
│                     ▼          ▼          ▼                                   │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
│  │                  CONTROL PLANE TIER                                  │     │
│  │                                                                     │     │
│  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐             │     │
│  │  │ cp-1 (10.0.1)│  │ cp-2 (10.0.2)│  │ cp-3 (10.0.3)│             │     │
│  │  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤             │     │
│  │  │ kube-apiserver│  │ kube-apiserver│  │ kube-apiserver│             │     │
│  │  │ (active)     │  │ (active)     │  │ (active)     │             │     │
│  │  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤             │     │
│  │  │ kube-scheduler│  │ kube-scheduler│  │ kube-scheduler│             │     │
│  │  │ ★ LEADER     │  │ ⏸ standby    │  │ ⏸ standby    │             │     │
│  │  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤             │     │
│  │  │ ctrl-manager │  │ ctrl-manager │  │ ctrl-manager │             │     │
│  │  │ ★ LEADER     │  │ ⏸ standby    │  │ ⏸ standby    │             │     │
│  │  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤  ├──────────────┤             │     │
│  │  │ etcd         │  │ etcd         │  │ etcd         │             │     │
│  │  │ (peer)       │  │ (peer)       │  │ (peer)       │             │     │
│  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘             │     │
│  │                                                                     │     │
│  │  etcd replication: cp-1 ◀──▶ cp-2 ◀──▶ cp-3 (Raft consensus)      │     │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
│  │                   WORKER NODE TIER                                   │     │
│  │                                                                     │     │
│  │  ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐       │     │
│  │  │ worker-1  │  │ worker-2  │  │ worker-3  │  │ worker-N  │       │     │
│  │  │┌─────────┐│  │┌─────────┐│  │┌─────────┐│  │┌─────────┐│       │     │
│  │  ││ easypj  ││  ││ easypj  ││  ││ easypj  ││  ││ jobpost ││       │     │
│  │  ││ web-1   ││  ││ web-2   ││  ││ db-0    ││  ││ worker  ││       │     │
│  │  │└─────────┘│  │└─────────┘│  │└─────────┘│  │└─────────┘│       │     │
│  │  │ kubelet   │  │ kubelet   │  │ kubelet   │  │ kubelet   │       │     │
│  │  │ kube-proxy│  │ kube-proxy│  │ kube-proxy│  │ kube-proxy│       │     │
│  │  └───────────┘  └───────────┘  └───────────┘  └───────────┘       │     │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Exam: Know the Components</h5>
                    <p>You must know what runs on control plane nodes vs worker nodes. <strong>API server</strong> is active-active (all serve requests). <strong>Scheduler</strong> and <strong>controller-manager</strong> are active-passive (only the leader acts). <strong>etcd</strong> uses Raft consensus (all peers participate but one is the Raft leader).</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.2 Stacked vs External etcd</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>There are two ways to deploy etcd in an HA cluster. The choice affects fault tolerance, complexity, and cost. You must understand both for the CKA exam.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Stacked etcd Topology — etcd on Control Plane Nodes</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STACKED etcd TOPOLOGY (Default with kubeadm)                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Each control plane node runs BOTH Kubernetes components AND etcd            │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐                │
│  │  CP Node 1    │    │  CP Node 2    │    │  CP Node 3    │                │
│  │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │                │
│  │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │                │
│  │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │                │
│  │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │                │
│  │ ├───────────┤ │    │ ├───────────┤ │    │ ├───────────┤ │                │
│  │ │  etcd  📀 │ │    │ │  etcd  📀 │ │    │ │  etcd  📀 │ │                │
│  │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │                │
│  └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘                │
│          │                    │                    │                          │
│          └─────── Raft Replication ───────────────┘                          │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ Simpler setup — fewer nodes to manage                                   │
│  ✅ Default kubeadm topology                                                │
│  ⚠️ Risk: If a node dies, you lose both CP and an etcd member              │
│  📊 Minimum: 3 nodes (for etcd quorum)                                     │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>External etcd Topology — etcd on Dedicated Servers</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  EXTERNAL etcd TOPOLOGY                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  etcd runs on SEPARATE dedicated nodes — decoupled from control plane        │
│                                                                              │
│  Control Plane Tier:                                                         │
│  ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐    ┌───────────────┐                │
│  │  CP Node 1    │    │  CP Node 2    │    │  CP Node 3    │                │
│  │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │    │ ┌───────────┐ │                │
│  │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │    │ │API Server │ │                │
│  │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │    │ │Scheduler  │ │                │
│  │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │    │ │Ctrl-Mgr   │ │                │
│  │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │    │ └───────────┘ │                │
│  └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘    └───────┬───────┘                │
│          └────────────┬───────┘                    │                          │
│                       │  Connect via TLS            │                          │
│          ┌────────────┼────────────────────────────┘                          │
│          ▼            ▼            ▼                                          │
│  ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐  ┌───────────┐                                │
│  │ etcd-1 📀 │  │ etcd-2 📀 │  │ etcd-3 📀 │  ← Dedicated etcd cluster    │
│  └─────┬─────┘  └─────┬─────┘  └─────┬─────┘                                │
│        └───── Raft Replication ───────┘                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ etcd survives control plane failures                                    │
│  ✅ Can scale etcd independently                                            │
│  ⚠️ More complex — requires 6+ nodes minimum (3 CP + 3 etcd)               │
│  ⚠️ More certs to manage (API→etcd TLS)                                    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Topology Comparison</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>Stacked (default)</th>
                                <th>External etcd</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>etcd location</strong></td>
                                <td>On control plane node</td>
                                <td>Separate dedicated servers</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Setup complexity</strong></td>
                                <td>Easier — <code>kubeadm init</code> handles it</td>
                                <td>Harder — manual etcd cluster setup</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Fault isolation</strong></td>
                                <td>⚠️ CP failure = etcd member lost too</td>
                                <td>✅ etcd survives CP node failure</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Minimum nodes</strong></td>
                                <td>3 (combined CP + etcd)</td>
                                <td>6 (3 CP + 3 etcd)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Cost</strong></td>
                                <td>Lower — fewer servers</td>
                                <td>Higher — 2× the infrastructure</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>CKA Exam</strong></td>
                                <td>Most tested topology ✅</td>
                                <td>Conceptual understanding needed</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Best for</strong></td>
                                <td>Most production clusters</td>
                                <td>Critical workloads with strict SLAs</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.3 Setting Up HA with kubeadm</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>Setting up an HA cluster with kubeadm follows a specific sequence: configure the load balancer first, initialize the first control plane, then join additional control planes and workers.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>HA Setup Sequence — Step by Step</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    HA CLUSTER SETUP SEQUENCE                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 1          Step 2           Step 3           Step 4                    │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐              │
│  │ Setup    │    │ Init 1st │    │ Join     │    │ Join     │              │
│  │ Load     │───▶│ Control  │───▶│ More CP  │───▶│ Worker   │              │
│  │ Balancer │    │ Plane    │    │ Nodes    │    │ Nodes    │              │
│  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘              │
│  HAProxy/nginx    kubeadm init    kubeadm join    kubeadm join              │
│  on :6443         --control-      --control-      (no flag)                 │
│                   plane-endpoint  plane                                      │
│                   =LB_IP:6443                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 5          Step 6           Step 7                                     │
│  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐                               │
│  │ Install  │    │ Verify   │    │ Test     │                               │
│  │ CNI      │───▶│ etcd     │───▶│ Failover │                               │
│  │ Plugin   │    │ Health   │    │          │                               │
│  └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────┘                               │
│  Calico/Cilium    etcdctl         Kill a CP node                             │
│                   endpoint        → verify LB                                │
│                   health          reroutes                                    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 1: HAProxy Configuration</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># HAProxy configuration for Kubernetes API Server HA</span>
frontend kubernetes-api
    bind *:6443
    mode tcp
    option tcplog
    default_backend kubernetes-api-backend

backend kubernetes-api-backend
    mode tcp
    option tcp-check
    balance roundrobin
    <span class="comment"># Health check: API server /healthz endpoint</span>
    server cp-1 10.0.0.1:6443 check fall 3 rise 2
    server cp-2 10.0.0.2:6443 check fall 3 rise 2
    server cp-3 10.0.0.3:6443 check fall 3 rise 2</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 2: Initialize First Control Plane</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — cp-1 (first control plane)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Initialize first control plane — point to the load balancer!</span>
$ sudo kubeadm init \
    --control-plane-endpoint "10.0.0.100:6443" \
    --upload-certs \
    --pod-network-cidr=10.244.0.0/16

<span class="comment"># Output includes TWO join commands:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. For additional control plane nodes (has --control-plane flag)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. For worker nodes (no --control-plane flag)</span>

<span class="comment"># Copy kubeconfig</span>
$ mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
$ sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
$ sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Steps 3 &amp; 4: Join Additional Nodes</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — cp-2 and cp-3</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Join as additional control plane (note --control-plane flag!)</span>
$ sudo kubeadm join 10.0.0.100:6443 \
    --token &lt;token&gt; \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt; \
    --control-plane \
    --certificate-key &lt;cert-key&gt;</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — worker nodes</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Join as worker node (NO --control-plane flag)</span>
$ sudo kubeadm join 10.0.0.100:6443 \
    --token &lt;token&gt; \
    --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:&lt;hash&gt;</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 6: Verify Cluster Health</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — verify HA cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify all nodes are Ready</span>
$ kubectl get nodes
NAME   STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION
cp-1   Ready    control-plane   10m   v1.31.0
cp-2   Ready    control-plane   8m    v1.31.0
cp-3   Ready    control-plane   6m    v1.31.0
w-1    Ready    &lt;none&gt;          4m    v1.31.0
w-2    Ready    &lt;none&gt;          3m    v1.31.0
w-3    Ready    &lt;none&gt;          2m    v1.31.0

<span class="comment"># Verify etcd cluster health</span>
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
    --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
    --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># Should show 3 members: cp-1, cp-2, cp-3</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>✅ Verify: HA Setup Checklist</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li><code>kubectl get nodes</code> — all control plane nodes show <code>Ready</code></li>
                        <li><code>etcdctl member list</code> — shows 3 etcd members</li>
                        <li><code>etcdctl endpoint health</code> — all endpoints healthy</li>
                        <li>Load balancer health check — all backends UP</li>
                        <li>Kill one CP node → API still responds via LB</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.4 etcd Quorum and Leader Election</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>etcd uses the <strong>Raft consensus algorithm</strong>. A majority of etcd members must agree before any write is committed. This majority is called the <strong>quorum</strong>. Understanding quorum math is essential for CKA.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>etcd Quorum Formula: (N/2) + 1</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    ETCD QUORUM — THE MATH                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Formula: Quorum = floor(N/2) + 1                                            │
│  Tolerated Failures = N - Quorum                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────┬────────┬─────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ Members │ Quorum │ Tolerated Failures  │ Recommendation               │  │
│  ├─────────┼────────┼─────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤  │
│  │    1    │   1    │         0           │ ❌ Dev/test only              │  │
│  │    2    │   2    │         0           │ ❌ NEVER — worse than 1!     │  │
│  │    3    │   2    │         1           │ ✅ Standard production       │  │
│  │    4    │   3    │         1           │ ❌ Same tolerance as 3!      │  │
│  │    5    │   3    │         2           │ ✅ Large-scale production    │  │
│  │    6    │   4    │         2           │ ❌ Same tolerance as 5!      │  │
│  │    7    │   4    │         3           │ ⚠️ Rare — more latency       │  │
│  └─────────┴────────┴─────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
│  🔑 KEY INSIGHT: Even numbers are WASTEFUL!                                 │
│  ─────────────────────────────────────────                                   │
│  3 members → tolerate 1 failure                                              │
│  4 members → STILL only tolerate 1 failure (need 3 for quorum)              │
│  → You added a node but got ZERO extra fault tolerance!                      │
│                                                                              │
│  ALWAYS USE ODD NUMBERS: 1, 3, 5, 7                                        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Quorum in Action — What Happens During Failures</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  3-MEMBER CLUSTER FAILURE SCENARIOS                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Normal (3/3 alive):         1 Node Down (2/3 alive):                        │
│  ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐     ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐                        │
│  │ E1 │  │ E2 │  │ E3 │     │ E1 │  │ E2 │  │ ╳╳ │  ← Node 3 down       │
│  │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │     │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │  │ ❌ │                        │
│  └────┘  └────┘  └────┘     └────┘  └────┘  └────┘                        │
│  Quorum: 2 of 3 = ✅         Quorum: 2 of 3 = ✅ (still works!)            │
│  Writes: ✅                  Writes: ✅                                      │
│  Reads:  ✅                  Reads:  ✅                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  2 Nodes Down (1/3 alive):   Split Brain (network partition):                │
│  ┌────┐  ┌────┐  ┌────┐     ┌────┐  │  ┌────┐  ┌────┐                     │
│  │ E1 │  │ ╳╳ │  │ ╳╳ │     │ E1 │  │  │ E2 │  │ E3 │                     │
│  │ ✅ │  │ ❌ │  │ ❌ │     │ ✅ │  │  │ ✅ │  │ ✅ │                     │
│  └────┘  └────┘  └────┘     └────┘  │  └────┘  └────┘                     │
│  Quorum: 1 of 3 = ❌         Side A: 1 (no quorum ❌)                       │
│  Writes: ❌ CLUSTER DOWN!    Side B: 2 (has quorum ✅)                       │
│  Reads:  ❌ (stale reads     → Side B continues serving                      │
│             may be possible)  → Side A becomes read-only                     │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Exam Quick Recall</h5>
                    <p>If asked "how many etcd node failures can a 5-member cluster tolerate?" — the answer is <strong>2</strong>. Formula: <code>floor(5/2) + 1 = 3</code> needed for quorum, so <code>5 - 3 = 2</code> failures tolerated. <strong>Always use odd numbers for etcd cluster size.</strong></p>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why Even Numbers Are Dangerous</h5>
                    <p>A 4-member etcd cluster tolerates only <strong>1 failure</strong> — the same as a 3-member cluster — but uses more resources. Worse, with 4 members a network partition can create two equal groups (2 vs 2), and <strong>neither has quorum</strong>, causing a total outage. Odd numbers eliminate this symmetric split risk.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>etcd Backup &amp; Restore in HA — Critical CKA Skill</h4>
                <p>Backing up and restoring etcd is a <strong>guaranteed CKA exam topic</strong>. In an HA cluster, you must back up from <strong>one member</strong> and restore to <strong>all members</strong>.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — etcd backup &amp; restore (CKA exam critical)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># STEP 1: BACKUP — Take snapshot from any healthy member</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /tmp/etcd-backup.db \
    --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
    --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
    --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
    --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify the backup</span>
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /tmp/etcd-backup.db --write-table
+----------+----------+------------+------------+
|   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |
+----------+----------+------------+------------+
| abc12def |   150234 |       1847 |     4.2 MB |
+----------+----------+------------+------------+

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># STEP 2: RESTORE — Stop API server, restore, restart</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># On EACH control plane node:</span>
$ mv /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml /tmp/

<span class="comment"># Restore with a NEW data directory (don't overwrite!)</span>
$ ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /tmp/etcd-backup.db \
    --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored \
    --name=cp-1 \
    --initial-cluster="cp-1=https://10.0.0.1:2380,cp-2=https://10.0.0.2:2380,cp-3=https://10.0.0.3:2380" \
    --initial-advertise-peer-urls=https://10.0.0.1:2380

<span class="comment"># Update etcd manifest to point to new data dir</span>
$ sed -i 's|/var/lib/etcd|/var/lib/etcd-restored|g' \
    /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># Restart API server</span>
$ mv /tmp/kube-apiserver.yaml /etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>etcd Backup Strategy — When &amp; Where</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  etcd BACKUP BEST PRACTICES                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐               │
│  │  BACKUP SCHEDULE:                                         │               │
│  │                                                           │               │
│  │  CronJob every 30 min ──▶ /backups/etcd-{timestamp}.db   │               │
│  │  Keep last 48 backups (24 hours of 30-min snapshots)      │               │
│  │  Store offsite: S3 / Azure Blob / GCS bucket ✅           │               │
│  │  NEVER store backups only on the etcd nodes! ❌            │               │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘               │
│                                                                              │
│  RESTORE SCENARIOS:                                                          │
│  ┌───────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┐               │
│  │ Scenario          │ Action                               │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ 1 etcd member     │ Remove &amp; re-add member (simpler)   │               │
│  │ lost              │ etcdctl member add                   │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ Quorum lost       │ Full restore from snapshot on ALL    │               │
│  │ (majority down)   │ remaining members                    │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ Data corruption   │ Full restore from known-good         │               │
│  │                   │ snapshot + verify cluster health      │               │
│  ├───────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┤               │
│  │ Disaster recovery │ Restore snapshot to new cluster +    │               │
│  │                   │ update certificates if needed         │               │
│  └───────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┘               │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  REMEMBER: etcd restore creates a NEW cluster ID — all members          │
│     must restore from the SAME snapshot to form a consistent cluster!        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Common Mistake: Forgetting TLS Flags</h5>
                    <p>The #1 reason <code>etcdctl</code> commands fail on the CKA exam: missing the <code>--cacert</code>, <code>--cert</code>, and <code>--key</code> flags. These certs are always at <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>. Also remember to set <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> — version 2 has different commands!</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.5 Leader Election Deep Dive</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>In an HA cluster, only <strong>one instance</strong> of the kube-scheduler and kube-controller-manager is actively making decisions at any time. This is managed through <strong>leader election</strong> using the Kubernetes Lease API.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Leader Election — How It Works</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  LEADER ELECTION MECHANISM                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Component        │ Active Instances │ Election Method                       │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  kube-apiserver   │ ALL (active-     │ No election needed —                  │
│                   │ active)          │ stateless, LB distributes             │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  kube-scheduler   │ ONE (leader)     │ Lease object in kube-system          │
│                   │                  │ --leader-elect=true                   │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  kube-controller  │ ONE (leader)     │ Lease object in kube-system          │
│  -manager         │                  │ --leader-elect=true                   │
│  ─────────────────┼──────────────────┼────────────────────────               │
│  etcd             │ ALL (Raft        │ Raft protocol — internal             │
│                   │ consensus)       │ leader for write ordering            │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │  LEASE-BASED LEADER ELECTION FLOW:                          │            │
│  │                                                              │            │
│  │  1. All instances try to acquire a Lease object              │            │
│  │  2. Winner writes its identity + timestamp to Lease          │            │
│  │  3. Leader renews Lease every ~2 seconds (leaseDuration)     │            │
│  │  4. Other instances watch — if Lease expires, they compete   │            │
│  │  5. Fastest to write wins → new leader in ~15 seconds        │            │
│  │                                                              │            │
│  │  Timeline:                                                   │            │
│  │  ─────────── Leader CP-1 ──────────── ╳ (dies)              │            │
│  │  ───── standby CP-2 ────────────────── ★ new leader ──────  │            │
│  │  ───── standby CP-3 ────────────────── standby ───────────  │            │
│  │                               ~15s gap                       │            │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Inspecting Leader Election</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — check current leaders</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all leases in kube-system</span>
$ kubectl get lease -n kube-system
NAME                      HOLDER       AGE
kube-scheduler            cp-1_xxx     3d
kube-controller-manager   cp-1_xxx     3d

<span class="comment"># Describe scheduler lease — see who holds it</span>
$ kubectl describe lease kube-scheduler -n kube-system
Name:         kube-scheduler
Namespace:    kube-system
Spec:
  Holder Identity:    cp-1_xxxxxxxx-xxxx  ← THIS is the active leader
  Lease Duration:     15s
  Renew Time:         2024-03-15T10:45:32Z

<span class="comment"># Check leader-elect flags in scheduler manifest</span>
$ cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml | grep leader
    - --leader-elect=true    ← Enables leader election (default)</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 What Happens During Leader Failover</h5>
                    <p>When the leader dies, there's a brief gap (~15 seconds) before a new leader is elected. During this time, <strong>no new pods are scheduled</strong> and <strong>no controller actions happen</strong> (like scaling Deployments). Existing pods continue running — they don't depend on the scheduler or controller-manager once scheduled.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h5>🏋️ Practice Drill — Leader Election Investigation</h5>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Your team reports that new pods aren't being scheduled. You suspect the scheduler leader may have failed over. Investigate and verify.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Check which node currently holds the scheduler lease:<br><code>kubectl get lease kube-scheduler -n kube-system -o yaml</code></li>
                            <li>Check the controller-manager lease as well:<br><code>kubectl get lease kube-controller-manager -n kube-system -o jsonpath='{.spec.holderIdentity}'</code></li>
                            <li>Verify the lease <code>renewTime</code> is recent (within last ~15 seconds). If stale → the leader may have crashed</li>
                            <li>Check scheduler pod logs for election events:<br><code>kubectl logs -n kube-system kube-scheduler-cp-1 | grep -i "leader\|elected\|lost"</code></li>
                            <li>Verify all control plane nodes are healthy:<br><code>kubectl get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane</code></li>
                        </ol>
                        <p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> You can identify which control plane node is the active leader and confirm the lease is being renewed.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.6 Load Balancer Configuration for HA</h3>
                <p>The load balancer is the <strong>front door</strong> to your HA cluster. All kubectl commands, worker node registrations, and internal API calls go through it. If the LB fails, the entire cluster is unreachable — making the LB itself another critical HA component.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Load Balancer Health Check Flow</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  LOAD BALANCER HEALTH CHECK &amp; ROUTING                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐                                        │
│  │        kubectl / kubelet        │                                        │
│  │        All API requests         │                                        │
│  └───────────────┬─────────────────┘                                        │
│                  │                                                            │
│                  ▼ :6443                                                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐                                        │
│  │        LOAD BALANCER            │      Health Check:                      │
│  │      (HAProxy / nginx)          │      GET /healthz on :6443              │
│  │                                 │      Every 5 seconds                    │
│  │   Rules:                        │                                        │
│  │   - TCP mode (Layer 4)          │      Response 200 → Backend UP ✅      │
│  │   - Round-robin distribution    │      No response  → Remove from pool ❌│
│  │   - 3 failed checks → remove   │                                        │
│  │   - 2 passed checks → re-add   │                                        │
│  └──┬──────────┬──────────┬───────┘                                        │
│     │          │          │                                                  │
│     ▼          ▼          ▼                                                  │
│  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐                                              │
│  │CP-1  │  │CP-2  │  │CP-3  │                                              │
│  │:6443 │  │:6443 │  │:6443 │                                              │
│  │ ✅   │  │ ✅   │  │ ❌   │  ← CP-3 failed health check                 │
│  └──────┘  └──────┘  └──────┘    → removed from pool                       │
│  ▲ 50%     ▲ 50%     (0%)       → traffic split between CP-1 &amp; CP-2       │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>nginx Stream Proxy Configuration (Alternative to HAProxy)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">/etc/nginx/nginx.conf — stream proxy for K8s API</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-nginx">stream {
    upstream kubernetes_api {
        <span class="comment"># Round-robin between control plane nodes</span>
        server 10.0.0.1:6443 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
        server 10.0.0.2:6443 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
        server 10.0.0.3:6443 max_fails=3 fail_timeout=30s;
    }

    server {
        listen 6443;
        proxy_pass kubernetes_api;
        proxy_timeout 10m;
        proxy_connect_timeout 1s;
    }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ LB Single Point of Failure?</h5>
                    <p>Yes — a single load balancer is itself a SPOF. In production, use <strong>keepalived + VRRP</strong> for LB failover (two LB hosts sharing a virtual IP), or use a cloud-managed load balancer (AWS NLB, Azure LB, GCP TCP LB) which is inherently HA.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>keepalived + VRRP — Making the Load Balancer HA</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  LOAD BALANCER HIGH AVAILABILITY WITH keepalived                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Problem: Single LB = Single Point of Failure                                │
│  Solution: Two LBs sharing a Virtual IP (VIP) via VRRP protocol             │
│                                                                              │
│                     ┌──────────────┐                                        │
│   kubectl ────▶     │  VIP:        │     ← Clients connect to VIP           │
│   kubelet           │  10.0.0.100  │        NOT individual LB IPs            │
│                     └──────┬───────┘                                        │
│                            │                                                 │
│              ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐                                  │
│              │                           │                                   │
│     ┌────────▼────────┐        ┌────────▼────────┐                          │
│     │  LB-1 (MASTER)  │  VRRP  │  LB-2 (BACKUP)  │                          │
│     │  10.0.0.10      │◄──────▶│  10.0.0.11       │                          │
│     │  Owns VIP ✅    │ health  │  Standby ⏳      │                          │
│     │  Priority: 101  │ checks  │  Priority: 100   │                          │
│     └────────┬────────┘        └────────┬─────────┘                          │
│              │                           │                                   │
│              └─────────┬─┬───────────────┘                                  │
│                        │ │ │                                                 │
│                   CP-1 CP-2 CP-3                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Failover Flow:                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │  1. LB-1 sends VRRP advertisements every 1 second          │            │
│  │  2. LB-2 monitors — if no VRRP from LB-1 for 3 seconds    │            │
│  │  3. LB-2 claims the VIP → sends gratuitous ARP             │            │
│  │  4. Traffic redirects to LB-2 in &lt;5 seconds ✅            │            │
│  │  5. When LB-1 recovers → reclaims VIP (preempt mode)       │            │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘            │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §35.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s35-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">35.7 Production HA Checklist for easypj</h3>
                <p>Here's everything needed to deploy the <strong>easypj</strong> Django application on a production HA Kubernetes cluster. Use this as your go-live checklist.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>easypj Production HA Architecture</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  easypj PRODUCTION HA DEPLOYMENT                                             │
│                                                                              │
│                        ┌───────────────┐                                    │
│    Users ──────▶       │  Ingress /    │                                    │
│                        │  Cloud LB     │                                    │
│                        └───────┬───────┘                                    │
│                                │                                             │
│               ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐                            │
│               ▼                ▼                ▼                            │
│  ┌────────────────┐ ┌────────────────┐ ┌────────────────┐                   │
│  │  easypj-web    │ │  easypj-web    │ │  easypj-web    │                   │
│  │  replica 1     │ │  replica 2     │ │  replica 3     │                   │
│  │  (worker-1)    │ │  (worker-2)    │ │  (worker-3)    │                   │
│  └───────┬────────┘ └───────┬────────┘ └───────┬────────┘                   │
│          └──────────┬───────┘                   │                            │
│                     ▼                           ▼                            │
│          ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐                      │
│          │  easypj-db (PostgreSQL StatefulSet)        │                      │
│          │  Primary + Read Replica                     │                      │
│          │  PVC: 50Gi on fast-ssd StorageClass         │                      │
│          └────────────────────────────────────────────┘                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Infrastructure:                                                             │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐          │
│  │  3× Control Plane (HA)  │  3× Workers  │  HAProxy LB (HA)    │          │
│  │  etcd backup: 6h cycle  │  autoscaler  │  keepalived + VIP   │          │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘          │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Production Readiness Checklist</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Category</th>
                                <th>Requirement</th>
                                <th>Status</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Control Plane</strong></td>
                                <td>3+ CP nodes across availability zones</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>etcd backup CronJob every 6 hours</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Load balancer with health checks on <code>/healthz</code></td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Workloads</strong></td>
                                <td>PodDisruptionBudget on easypj (<code>minAvailable: 2</code>)</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Anti-affinity: spread easypj pods across nodes</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Resource requests/limits on all containers</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Liveness and readiness probes configured</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Monitoring</strong></td>
                                <td>Alerts for node NotReady and pod crash loops</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Central logging (Fluentd/Fluent Bit → ELK/Loki)</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Security</strong></td>
                                <td>RBAC: least-privilege ServiceAccounts</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>NetworkPolicies: restrict inter-namespace traffic</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>Secrets encrypted at rest in etcd</td>
                                <td>✅ Required</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Anti-Patterns</strong></td>
                                <td>❌ Never run single-replica Deployments in prod</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Avoid</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>❌ Never run without resource limits</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Avoid</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td></td>
                                <td>❌ Never skip PodDisruptionBudgets</td>
                                <td>⚠️ Avoid</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-pdb.yaml — PodDisruptionBudget</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-pdb
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  minAvailable: 2          <span class="comment"># At least 2 pods must always be running</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
---
<span class="comment"># Anti-affinity in Deployment to spread pods across nodes</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-web
    spec:
      affinity:
        podAntiAffinity:
          preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
          - weight: 100
            podAffinityTerm:
              labelSelector:
                matchExpressions:
                - key: app
                  operator: In
                  values:
                  - easypj-web
              topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: easypj/web:v2.0
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "200m"
            memory: "256Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "512Mi"
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health/
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 10
          periodSeconds: 15
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /ready/
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 5
          periodSeconds: 10</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>✅ Chapter 35 Competency Checklist</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Can you explain the difference between stacked and external etcd topologies?</li>
                        <li>Can you initialize a multi-master cluster with <code>kubeadm init --control-plane-endpoint</code>?</li>
                        <li>Can you calculate etcd quorum for any cluster size?</li>
                        <li>Can you find the current scheduler leader using <code>kubectl get lease</code>?</li>
                        <li>Can you configure HAProxy/nginx to load-balance API servers?</li>
                        <li>Can you set up PodDisruptionBudgets and anti-affinity for production workloads?</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <!-- CKA Augmentation: Chapter 35 -->
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: High Availability Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Stacked vs External etcd</strong> — Stacked: etcd runs on control plane nodes (simpler, fewer machines). External: etcd on dedicated nodes (better isolation, harder to set up). The CKA uses stacked topology. Know both for conceptual questions</li>
                        <li><strong>etcd quorum formula</strong> — <code>(N/2) + 1</code> members must be alive. 3 nodes = tolerate 1 failure. 5 nodes = tolerate 2. Always use ODD numbers. Even numbers waste a node without improving fault tolerance</li>
                        <li><strong>Leader election</strong> — Only ONE controller-manager and ONE scheduler are active at a time (the leader). Others are standby. Check leader: <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code>. If the leader dies, a new one is elected in ~15s</li>
                        <li><strong><code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> is critical</strong> — Must point to a load balancer, not a single node IP. Without this, adding control plane nodes later fails. Set during <code>kubeadm init</code> and it CANNOT be changed after</li>
                        <li><strong>Pod anti-affinity for HA workloads</strong> — Without <code>podAntiAffinity</code>, all replicas could land on the same node. If that node dies, 100% downtime. Always spread critical workloads across nodes</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 35 — How High Availability Clusters Work ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 35 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How High Availability Clusters Actually Work: Stacked vs External etcd, Quorum Math, and Leader Election Across Components</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ HA Topologies — Stacked vs External etcd</h5>
            <p>Kubernetes HA comes in two architectures. Understanding the trade-offs helps you choose the right topology and debug HA failures on the CKA.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HA TOPOLOGIES — Stacked vs External etcd                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  STACKED ETCD (default, simpler):                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  CP Node 1          CP Node 2          CP Node 3                     │   │
│  │  ┌─────────────┐    ┌─────────────┐    ┌─────────────┐              │   │
│  │  │ API Server  │    │ API Server  │    │ API Server  │              │   │
│  │  │ etcd        │◀──▶│ etcd        │◀──▶│ etcd        │              │   │
│  │  └─────────────┘    └─────────────┘    └─────────────┘              │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │  ✅ Simpler to set up (1 command: kubeadm join --control-plane)      │   │
│  │  ✅ Fewer nodes needed (3 CP nodes = 3 etcd nodes)                   │   │
│  │  ⚠️  etcd competes for resources with API Server                    │   │
│  │  ⚠️  If CP node fails, lose BOTH API Server AND etcd member         │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  EXTERNAL ETCD (separate etcd cluster):                                      │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  CP Node 1          CP Node 2          CP Node 3                     │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────┐   ┌──────────────┐            │   │
│  │  │  API Server  │   │  API Server  │   │  API Server  │            │   │
│  │  └──────┬───────┘   └──────┬───────┘   └──────┬───────┘            │   │
│  │         │                 │                 │                        │   │
│  │         └─────────────────┼─────────────────┘                        │   │
│  │                           ▼                                           │   │
│  │  etcd Node 1        etcd Node 2        etcd Node 3                   │   │
│  │  ┌────────────┐     ┌────────────┐     ┌────────────┐               │   │
│  │  │   etcd     │◀───▶│   etcd     │◀───▶│   etcd     │               │   │
│  │  └────────────┘     └────────────┘     └────────────┘               │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │  ✅ API Server and etcd failures are independent                      │   │
│  │  ✅ etcd on dedicated nodes → better performance                     │   │
│  │  ⚠️  More nodes (3 CP + 3 etcd = 6 minimum)                         │   │
│  │  ⚠️  More complex setup                                               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  QUORUM MATH — How Many Failures Can Your Cluster Survive:                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  etcd uses RAFT — needs MAJORITY (N/2 + 1) to commit writes          │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  ┌───────┬──────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────────────────┐   │   │
│  │  │ Nodes │ Majority │ Max Failures │ Notes                        │   │   │
│  │  ├───────┼──────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────────────────┤   │   │
│  │  │   1   │    1     │      0       │ No HA — single point of      │   │   │
│  │  │       │          │              │ failure                       │   │   │
│  │  │   2   │    2     │      0       │ Worse than 1 — needs both!    │   │   │
│  │  │   3   │    2     │      1       │ Minimum for production HA     │   │   │
│  │  │   4   │    3     │      1       │ Still only 1 failure allowed  │   │   │
│  │  │   5   │    3     │      2       │ Production recommendation     │   │   │
│  │  │   7   │    4     │      3       │ Maximum recommended            │   │   │
│  │  └───────┴──────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────────────────┘   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  RULE: ALWAYS use ODD number of etcd nodes (3, 5, 7).                 │   │
│  │  Even numbers waste resources with NO reliability gain.               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  API SERVER LOAD BALANCING:                                                   │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  ┌──────────┐                                                         │   │
│  │  │  kubectl │────▶ haproxy/nginx LB (10.0.1.200:6443)                │   │
│  │  └──────────┘        │                                                 │   │
│  │              ┌───────┼───────┐                                         │   │
│  │              ▼       ▼       ▼                                         │   │
│  │         CP-Node1  CP-Node2  CP-Node3                                  │   │
│  │         :6443     :6443     :6443                                      │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  All API Servers are ACTIVE (no leader election for API Server).      │   │
│  │  Any API Server can serve read requests.                              │   │
│  │  Writes go through ANY API Server → etcd (Raft leader handles).      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> On the CKA, you may be asked about etcd quorum. Formula: <code>quorum = floor(N/2) + 1</code>, <code>max failures = floor((N-1)/2)</code>. Always use odd numbers (3, 5, 7). 3 etcd nodes tolerate 1 failure. API Servers have NO leader election — ALL are active. Controller manager and scheduler DO have leader election (only one active at a time). Check with: <code>kubectl get leases -n kube-system</code>.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <p>The CKA exam uses <strong>stacked etcd</strong> topology (kubeadm default). When asked to backup/restore etcd, the etcd data dir is on the control plane node at <code>/var/lib/etcd</code>. The API server connects to etcd on <code>localhost:2379</code>. Know both topologies conceptually, but practice stacked for the exam.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — HA concepts to know:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code> — see leader election leases<br>
                <code>kubectl get endpoints -n kube-system kube-scheduler -o yaml</code> — current leader annotation<br>
                Quorum table: 1 node=no HA, 3=tolerate 1, 5=tolerate 2, 7=tolerate 3<br>
                <strong>Rule:</strong> Never run production with fewer than 3 control plane nodes</p>
            </div>

