            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch35-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 35 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (12)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of HA control-plane architecture — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How many etcd nodes are needed to tolerate 2 node failures? Explain the quorum formula.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Total Nodes</th>
                                        <th>Quorum Needed</th>
                                        <th>Failures Tolerated</th>
                                        <th>Useful?</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>1</td>
                                        <td>1</td>
                                        <td>0</td>
                                        <td>❌ No HA</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>2</td>
                                        <td>2</td>
                                        <td>0</td>
                                        <td>❌ Worse than 1</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>3</td>
                                        <td>2</td>
                                        <td>1</td>
                                        <td>✅ Minimum HA</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>4</td>
                                        <td>3</td>
                                        <td>1</td>
                                        <td>⚠️ Wastes a node</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td style="color:#34d399;font-weight:bold;">5</td>
                                        <td style="color:#34d399;font-weight:bold;">3</td>
                                        <td style="color:#34d399;font-weight:bold;">2</td>
                                        <td>✅ Answer!</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>7</td>
                                        <td>4</td>
                                        <td>3</td>
                                        <td>✅ Large clusters</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Formula: Quorum = (N / 2) + 1 (integer division)</span>
<span class="comment"># Failures tolerated = N - Quorum</span>

<span class="comment"># 5 nodes: Quorum = (5/2) + 1 = 3</span>
<span class="comment"># Failures = 5 - 3 = 2 ← Answer!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check etcd members:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>etcd uses Raft consensus: a majority (quorum) must agree on every write. Formula: quorum = ⌊N/2⌋ + 1. To tolerate 2 failures, you need 5 nodes (quorum of 3, so 2 can fail). Always use odd numbers — 4 nodes tolerates the same failures as 3 but costs more. On the CKA, know this table cold. Two nodes is WORSE than one because you need both alive for quorum.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between stacked etcd and external etcd topologies?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Aspect</th>
                                        <th>Stacked etcd</th>
                                        <th>External etcd</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Where etcd runs</td>
                                        <td>On control plane nodes</td>
                                        <td>On dedicated separate nodes</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Min machines (HA)</td>
                                        <td>3 (total)</td>
                                        <td>6 (3 CP + 3 etcd)</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Failure coupling</td>
                                        <td>Coupled: node failure loses both CP + etcd</td>
                                        <td>Decoupled: CP failure ≠ etcd failure</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Complexity</td>
                                        <td>Simpler setup</td>
                                        <td>More complex</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>CKA exam</td>
                                        <td>✅ Default (kubeadm)</td>
                                        <td>Know conceptually</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>kubeadm init flag</td>
                                        <td>(default)</td>
                                        <td><code>--external-etcd-endpoints</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Stacked: etcd is a local static pod</span>
ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml  <span class="comment"># Exists on CP nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># External: API server points to remote etcd</span>
grep etcd /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml
<span class="comment"># --etcd-servers=https://etcd1:2379,https://etcd2:2379,https://etcd3:2379</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Stacked = etcd runs on the same nodes as the control plane (simpler, fewer machines). External = etcd on dedicated nodes (better isolation, requires more machines). The CKA uses stacked topology (kubeadm default). In stacked, losing a control plane node also loses an etcd member. In external, control plane and etcd fail independently, providing better resilience at the cost of complexity.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check which node is the current leader for <code>kube-controller-manager</code> and <code>kube-scheduler</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: Check Lease objects (modern way)</span>
kubectl get lease -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># NAME                          HOLDER            AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-controller-manager       master-1_abc123   30d</span>
<span class="comment"># kube-scheduler                master-2_def456   30d</span>
<span class="comment"># ← HOLDER shows which node is the current leader</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Detailed lease info</span>
kubectl describe lease kube-controller-manager -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Spec:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Holder Identity:   master-1_abc123</span>
<span class="comment">#   Lease Duration:    15s</span>
<span class="comment">#   Renew Time:        2024-01-15 10:30:00</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Check endpoint annotations (legacy)</span>
kubectl get endpoints kube-controller-manager -n kube-system -o yaml
<span class="comment"># metadata:</span>
<span class="comment">#   annotations:</span>
<span class="comment">#     control-plane.alpha.kubernetes.io/leader:</span>
<span class="comment">#       holderIdentity: master-1_abc123</span>

<span class="comment"># How leader election works:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Multiple instances compete for a Lease object</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Winner writes its identity + holds the lease</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Winner renews every ~10s (leaseDuration: 15s)</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. If leader stops renewing, another takes over (~15s)</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. Only the leader actively processes work</span>

<span class="comment"># On each control plane node, check:</span>
kubectl logs kube-scheduler-master-2 -n kube-system | head -5
<span class="comment"># "successfully acquired lease kube-system/kube-scheduler"</span>
<span class="comment"># ← This node is the scheduler leader</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>In HA clusters, only ONE controller-manager and ONE scheduler are active (the leader). Others are standby. Leaders use Lease objects (in kube-system) for election. The HOLDER field shows which node is leading. If the leader dies, a new one is elected within the lease duration (~15s). On the CKA, <code>kubectl get lease -n kube-system</code> is the fastest way to check.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> flag and why is it critical for HA?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># kubeadm init with HA setup:</span>
kubeadm init \
--control-plane-endpoint="lb.example.com:6443" \
--upload-certs \
--pod-network-cidr=192.168.0.0/16

<span class="comment"># --control-plane-endpoint sets the API server address for ALL nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># Must be a load balancer or DNS name that fronts all CP nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># What it configures:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. All kubeconfigs point to lb.example.com:6443</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. All kubelet configs use this address</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Workers join via this address (not a single master IP)</span>

<span class="comment"># WITHOUT --control-plane-endpoint:</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm uses the first master's IP (e.g., 10.0.0.1:6443)</span>
<span class="comment"># If that master dies → ALL kubeconfigs point to dead IP</span>
<span class="comment"># Workers can't reach API server → cluster is down</span>
<span class="comment"># CANNOT be changed after kubeadm init!</span>

<span class="comment"># Load balancer options:</span>
<span class="comment"># - HAProxy (common for bare-metal)</span>
<span class="comment"># - nginx (also common)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Cloud LB (ELB, ALB, Azure LB)</span>
<span class="comment"># - keepalived + virtual IP</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify current endpoint:</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://lb.example.com:6443</span>

<span class="comment"># Check kubeconfig:</span>
cat ~/.kube/config | grep server
<span class="comment"># server: https://lb.example.com:6443</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> must be a load balancer address that distributes traffic across all control plane nodes. It's set during <code>kubeadm init</code> and is IMMUTABLE — changing it later requires rebuilding the cluster. Without it, all configs point to a single master IP, making HA impossible. On the CKA, know this conceptually: it ensures API access survives individual master node failures.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you ensure critical workloads are spread across nodes for high availability?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Problem: All 3 replicas land on the same node</span>
kubectl get pods -l app=web -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># web-def    worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># web-ghi    worker-1  ← All on one node! Not HA!</span>

<span class="comment"># Solution: Pod Anti-Affinity</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
name: web
spec:
replicas: 3
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: web
spec:
  affinity:
    podAntiAffinity:
      requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:
      - labelSelector:
          matchExpressions:
          - key: app
            operator: In
            values:
            - web
        topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx

<span class="comment"># Result: 1 pod per node</span>
<span class="comment"># web-abc  worker-1</span>
<span class="comment"># web-def  worker-2</span>
<span class="comment"># web-ghi  worker-3  ← Spread across nodes!</span>

<span class="comment"># Softer version (preferred, not required):</span>
<span class="comment"># preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution</span>
<span class="comment"># → Best effort spread, won't block scheduling</span>

<span class="comment"># Topology Spread Constraints (newer API):</span>
spec:
topologySpreadConstraints:
- maxSkew: 1
topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
labelSelector:
  matchLabels:
    app: web</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pod anti-affinity prevents pods with the same label from running on the same node. <code>requiredDuringScheduling</code> = hard rule (pod stays Pending if no unique node). <code>preferredDuringScheduling</code> = soft rule (best effort). <code>topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname</code> means one pod per node. On the CKA, you might need to add anti-affinity to ensure HA. <code>topologySpreadConstraints</code> is the newer, more flexible alternative.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you add a new control plane node to an existing HA cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Prerequisites:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Initial cluster created with --control-plane-endpoint</span>
<span class="comment"># - Load balancer configured for existing + new CP node</span>
<span class="comment"># - New node meets prerequisites (OS, container runtime, kubeadm)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: On existing control plane, generate join command</span>
kubeadm token create --print-join-command
<span class="comment"># kubeadm join lb.example.com:6443 --token abc123 \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:xyz789</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Upload certificates (need key for new CP node)</span>
kubeadm init phase upload-certs --upload-certs
<span class="comment"># [upload-certs] Using certificate key: abc123def456...</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: On new node, join as control plane</span>
kubeadm join lb.example.com:6443 \
--token abc123 \
--discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:xyz789 \
--control-plane \
--certificate-key abc123def456

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Update load balancer</span>
<span class="comment"># Add new node's IP to the LB backend pool</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS  ROLES          VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># master-1   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># master-2   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># master-3   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0  ← New!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check etcd is healthy:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd
<span class="comment"># etcd-master-1  1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-master-2  1/1  Running</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-master-3  1/1  Running  ← New etcd member!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Adding a control plane node uses <code>kubeadm join</code> with the <code>--control-plane</code> flag and <code>--certificate-key</code> to download shared certificates. The cluster must have been initialized with <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code>. On the CKA, know this workflow conceptually. The key steps: generate token, upload certs, join with control-plane flag, update load balancer. Stacked etcd automatically adds a new member when a control plane node joins.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is a PodDisruptionBudget and how does it protect HA during maintenance?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Create PDB imperatively:</span>
kubectl create pdb web-pdb --selector=app=web --min-available=2

<span class="comment"># OR declaratively:</span>
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
name: web-pdb
spec:
minAvailable: 2          <span class="comment"># At least 2 pods must be running</span>
<span class="comment"># OR: maxUnavailable: 1  # At most 1 pod can be down</span>
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web

<span class="comment"># What PDB protects against:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ kubectl drain (node maintenance)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Cluster autoscaler removing nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ kubeadm upgrade</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Does NOT protect against: node crash, OOMKill, pod deletion</span>

<span class="comment"># Without PDB (3 replicas, draining 2 nodes):</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --delete-emptydir-data --ignore-daemonsets
kubectl drain worker-2 --delete-emptydir-data --ignore-daemonsets
<span class="comment"># Both succeed → 0 pods running → DOWNTIME!</span>

<span class="comment"># With PDB (minAvailable: 2):</span>
kubectl drain worker-1  <span class="comment"># Succeeds (2 pods remain on worker-2,3)</span>
kubectl drain worker-2  <span class="comment"># BLOCKS! "Cannot evict pod: would violate PDB"</span>
<span class="comment"># Service stays up with 2 pods ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Check PDB status:</span>
kubectl get pdb
<span class="comment"># NAME     MIN-AVAILABLE  MAX-UNAVAILABLE  ALLOWED-DISRUPTIONS  AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># web-pdb  2              N/A              1                    5m</span>
<span class="comment"># ALLOWED-DISRUPTIONS: 1 = can safely evict 1 more pod</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>PDB sets limits on voluntary disruptions (drain, upgrades, autoscaler). <code>minAvailable</code> = minimum pods that must stay running. <code>maxUnavailable</code> = maximum pods that can be down. PDB blocks drain commands that would violate the budget. On the CKA, create PDBs to protect critical workloads during node maintenance. <code>kubectl create pdb</code> is the fastest imperative method. PDB does NOT prevent involuntary disruptions (node crashes, OOMKill).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">An HA cluster has 3 control plane nodes and one goes down. What happens?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># 3 CP nodes → Quorum = 2 → Can tolerate 1 failure ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># What happens when master-2 goes down:</span>

<span class="comment"># API Server:</span>
<span class="comment"># master-1 API server: ✅ Still serving requests</span>
<span class="comment"># master-3 API server: ✅ Still serving requests</span>
<span class="comment"># Load balancer routes to remaining 2 nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl commands still work (via LB)</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd:</span>
<span class="comment"># 3 members, 1 down → 2 alive → Quorum (2) met ✅</span>
<span class="comment"># Reads work, writes work</span>
<span class="comment"># "etcdctl member list" shows member as unreachable</span>

<span class="comment"># Controller Manager:</span>
<span class="comment"># If master-2 was the leader → new leader elected (~15s)</span>
<span class="comment"># If master-2 was standby → no impact</span>
kubectl get lease kube-controller-manager -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># HOLDER changes to master-1 or master-3</span>

<span class="comment"># Scheduler:</span>
<span class="comment"># Same as controller manager — leader election kicks in</span>
kubectl get lease kube-scheduler -n kube-system

<span class="comment"># Impact on workloads:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Existing pods keep running (kubelet is independent)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ New pods can be created (scheduler on remaining CP)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Self-healing works (controller-manager re-elected)</span>
<span class="comment"># ⚠️ But now: ONE MORE failure = total cluster loss!</span>

<span class="comment"># Action: Fix/replace master-2 ASAP</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Fix the node if recoverable</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. OR remove and re-add: kubeadm reset + re-join</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>With 3 CP nodes and 1 down: quorum is still met (2/3 alive), so the cluster functions normally. API server (via LB), etcd, controller-manager, and scheduler all continue. Leader election replaces the lost leader in ~15 seconds. Critical: you now have ZERO fault tolerance. If another node fails, you lose quorum and the cluster becomes read-only (no writes to etcd). Fix the failed node immediately.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you verify an HA cluster is properly configured?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># 1. Check all control plane nodes:</span>
kubectl get nodes -l node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS  ROLES          VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># master-1   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># master-2   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># master-3   Ready   control-plane  v1.31.0  ✓ 3 nodes</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check all control plane pods running:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep -E "apiserver|etcd|scheduler|controller"
<span class="comment"># All should show 1/1 Running for each master node</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check etcd cluster health:</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health \
--endpoints=https://master-1:2379,https://master-2:2379,https://master-3:2379 \
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/healthcheck-client.crt \
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/healthcheck-client.key
<span class="comment"># master-1:2379 is healthy</span>
<span class="comment"># master-2:2379 is healthy</span>
<span class="comment"># master-3:2379 is healthy  ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check leader election:</span>
kubectl get lease -n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Controller-manager and scheduler should have HOLDERs ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Check API server endpoint:</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Should show LB address, not single node IP</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. Check PDBs for critical workloads:</span>
kubectl get pdb -A
<span class="comment"># Critical apps should have PDBs ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># 7. Test failover (carefully!):</span>
<span class="comment"># Simulate by stopping kubelet on one master</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify cluster still works after ~30s</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>HA verification checklist: (1) 3+ control plane nodes all Ready, (2) all CP pods running (3 of each), (3) etcd cluster healthy, (4) leader election active, (5) API endpoint is a load balancer, (6) PDBs protect workloads. On the CKA, these verification steps help you understand what a working HA cluster looks like, which helps you diagnose when something is broken.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the role of the load balancer in an HA Kubernetes cluster?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Load Balancer sits in front of ALL API servers:</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment">#   kubectl → Load Balancer → API Server 1 (master-1)</span>
<span class="comment">#                           → API Server 2 (master-2)</span>
<span class="comment">#                           → API Server 3 (master-3)</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Workers also connect through the LB:</span>
<span class="comment">#   kubelet → Load Balancer → API Server (any)</span>

<span class="comment"># Configuration (HAProxy example):</span>
<span class="comment"># /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg</span>
<span class="comment"># frontend k8s-api</span>
<span class="comment">#   bind *:6443</span>
<span class="comment">#   default_backend k8s-api-backend</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># backend k8s-api-backend</span>
<span class="comment">#   balance roundrobin</span>
<span class="comment">#   option httpchk GET /healthz</span>
<span class="comment">#   server master-1 10.0.0.1:6443 check</span>
<span class="comment">#   server master-2 10.0.0.2:6443 check</span>
<span class="comment">#   server master-3 10.0.0.3:6443 check</span>

<span class="comment"># What the LB does:</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Distributes API requests across CP nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Health checks — removes dead nodes from rotation</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Single endpoint for all clients (kubectl, kubelet, etc.)</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ Survives individual CP node failures</span>

<span class="comment"># What the LB does NOT do:</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Doesn't handle etcd traffic (etcd peers talk directly)</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Doesn't load balance pod-to-pod traffic (that's kube-proxy)</span>
<span class="comment"># ❌ Doesn't replace Ingress controllers</span>

<span class="comment"># LB is a SPOF — must also be HA in production:</span>
<span class="comment"># keepalived + virtual IP (active/passive)</span>
<span class="comment"># Cloud LB (inherently HA)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The load balancer provides a single stable endpoint for the Kubernetes API, distributing requests across all control plane nodes. It's what <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> points to. Health checks automatically remove unresponsive masters from the pool. On the CKA, understand that the LB only handles API server traffic (port 6443). etcd uses separate peer-to-peer communication. The LB itself must be HA in production (keepalived, cloud LB).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Write an HAProxy configuration for load balancing across 3 API servers. What health checks ensure traffic only goes to healthy masters?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code># /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg

frontend kubernetes-api
bind *:6443
mode tcp
option tcplog
default_backend api-servers

backend api-servers
mode tcp
option tcp-check
balance roundrobin
<span class="comment"># Health check: TCP connect to port 6443</span>
server master1 192.168.1.10:6443 check fall 3 rise 2 inter 5s
server master2 192.168.1.11:6443 check fall 3 rise 2 inter 5s
server master3 192.168.1.12:6443 check fall 3 rise 2 inter 5s

<span class="comment"># Alternative: HTTP health check against /healthz</span>
backend api-servers-http-check
mode tcp
option httpchk GET /healthz
http-check expect status 200
server master1 192.168.1.10:6443 check check-ssl verify none
server master2 192.168.1.11:6443 check check-ssl verify none
server master3 192.168.1.12:6443 check check-ssl verify none

<span class="comment"># Health check explanation:</span>
<span class="comment"># check        → enable health checking</span>
<span class="comment"># fall 3       → mark down after 3 consecutive failures</span>
<span class="comment"># rise 2       → mark up after 2 consecutive successes</span>
<span class="comment"># inter 5s     → check every 5 seconds</span>

<span class="comment"># Start HAProxy:</span>
sudo systemctl enable --now haproxy

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
curl -k https://&lt;haproxy-ip&gt;:6443/healthz
<span class="comment"># ok</span>

<span class="comment"># kubeadm init with LB:</span>
kubeadm init --control-plane-endpoint="&lt;haproxy-ip&gt;:6443" \
--upload-certs</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The load balancer sits in front of all API servers and is the address all clients (kubectl, kubelet, kube-proxy) connect to. HAProxy in TCP mode passes through TLS without terminating it <code>mode tcp</code>. Health checks (<code>fall 3 rise 2</code>) automatically remove a crashed master from the rotation and re-add it when it recovers. This is what <code>--control-plane-endpoint</code> points to during <code>kubeadm init</code>. On the CKA, you won't configure HAProxy, but understanding that the LB is needed for HA and what port it forwards (6443 only) is essential for HA cluster questions.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the production readiness checklist for an HA Kubernetes cluster? List the key items you should verify before considering a cluster production-ready.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Production HA Readiness Checklist:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. Control Plane HA</span>
kubectl get nodes | grep control-plane
<span class="comment"># At least 3 control plane nodes (odd number for quorum)</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. etcd Cluster Health</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint health --cluster \
--cacert=... --cert=... --key=...
<span class="comment"># All members healthy, leader elected</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Load Balancer</span>
curl -k https://&lt;lb-endpoint&gt;:6443/healthz
<span class="comment"># ok — LB routing to healthy API servers</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Automated etcd Backups</span>
kubectl get cronjob -n kube-system | grep etcd-backup
<span class="comment"># Backup CronJob running on schedule</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Pod Disruption Budgets</span>
kubectl get pdb -A
<span class="comment"># Critical workloads have PDBs preventing total downtime</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. Resource Requests &amp; Limits</span>
kubectl get pods -A -o json | jq '.items[] |
select(.spec.containers[].resources.requests == null) |
.metadata.name'
<span class="comment"># No pods without resource requests (prevents scheduling chaos)</span>

<span class="comment"># 7. Node Redundancy</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># Multiple worker nodes across failure domains</span>

<span class="comment"># 8. Monitoring &amp; Alerting</span>
kubectl get pods -n monitoring
<span class="comment"># Prometheus/Grafana or equivalent running</span>

<span class="comment"># 9. RBAC Configured</span>
kubectl get clusterrolebindings | grep -v system
<span class="comment"># Custom roles set up, no default service account abuse</span>

<span class="comment"># 10. Certificate Expiration</span>
kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># All certs valid, auto-renewal configured</span>

<span class="comment"># 11. Network Policies</span>
kubectl get networkpolicies -A
<span class="comment"># Default deny + explicit allow rules</span>

<span class="comment"># 12. Storage</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># StorageClass with appropriate reclaim policy (Retain for prod)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>A production HA cluster needs more than just multiple masters. The key areas: (1) <strong>etcd quorum</strong> — odd number of members (3 or 5), (2) <strong>load balancer</strong> with health checks, (3) <strong>automated backups</strong>, (4) <strong>PDBs</strong> for critical workloads, (5) <strong>resource requests</strong> on all pods for proper scheduling, (6) <strong>certificate management</strong>, (7) <strong>RBAC</strong> and network policies. On the CKA, you're tested on individual pieces (etcd backup, PDBs, RBAC), but understanding how they fit together as a production readiness checklist shows mastery of the full stack.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 35 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

