            <!-- ─────────── §36.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.1 Managed vs Self-Managed Kubernetes</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <p>All three major cloud providers offer <strong>managed Kubernetes</strong> — they handle the control plane while you manage the worker nodes and workloads. Here's how they compare.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>EKS (AWS)</th>
                                <th>GKE (Google)</th>
                                <th>AKS (Azure)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Control Plane</strong></td>
                                <td>Managed ($0.10/hr)</td>
                                <td>Managed (free tier avail.)</td>
                                <td>Managed (free!)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Worker Nodes</strong></td>
                                <td>Self-managed / Fargate</td>
                                <td>Standard / Autopilot</td>
                                <td>VMSS node pools</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Networking</strong></td>
                                <td>VPC CNI (AWS native)</td>
                                <td>VPC-native / Calico</td>
                                <td>Azure CNI / kubenet</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Load Balancer</strong></td>
                                <td>ALB / NLB</td>
                                <td>GCP TCP/HTTP LB</td>
                                <td>Azure LB / App GW</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Storage</strong></td>
                                <td>EBS CSI driver</td>
                                <td>PD CSI driver</td>
                                <td>Azure Disk CSI</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Identity</strong></td>
                                <td>IAM Roles (IRSA)</td>
                                <td>Workload Identity</td>
                                <td>Managed Identity</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>CKA Relevance</strong></td>
                                <td>Concepts apply ✅</td>
                                <td>Concepts apply ✅</td>
                                <td>Concepts apply ✅</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Managed vs Self-Managed — Responsibility Model</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SELF-MANAGED (kubeadm)          │  CLOUD-MANAGED (EKS/GKE/AKS)           │
│                                   │                                         │
│  YOU manage:                      │  CLOUD manages:                         │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐      │  ┌─────────────────────────┐           │
│  │  Control Plane           │      │  │  Control Plane    ☁️    │           │
│  │  ├── API Server          │      │  │  ├── API Server (HA)    │           │
│  │  ├── Scheduler           │      │  │  ├── Scheduler          │           │
│  │  ├── Controller Mgr      │      │  │  ├── Controller Mgr     │           │
│  │  ├── etcd (backup/HA)    │      │  │  └── etcd (auto-backup) │           │
│  │  └── Certificates        │      │  └─────────────────────────┘           │
│  ├─────────────────────────┤      │                                         │
│  │  Worker Nodes            │      │  YOU manage:                           │
│  │  ├── kubelet             │      │  ┌─────────────────────────┐           │
│  │  ├── kube-proxy          │      │  │  Worker Nodes            │           │
│  │  └── Container runtime   │      │  │  ├── Node pool config    │           │
│  ├─────────────────────────┤      │  │  └── Autoscaling rules   │           │
│  │  Networking              │      │  ├─────────────────────────┤           │
│  │  ├── CNI plugin          │      │  │  Workloads              │           │
│  │  └── Load Balancer       │      │  │  ├── Deployments         │           │
│  ├─────────────────────────┤      │  │  ├── Services             │           │
│  │  Upgrades                │      │  │  └── Config/Secrets      │           │
│  │  └── kubeadm upgrade     │      │  └─────────────────────────┘           │
│  └─────────────────────────┘      │                                         │
│                                   │  Upgrade: Click button / az aks upgrade │
│  Effort: ████████████ HIGH       │  Effort: ████░░░░░░░░ LOW               │
│                                   │                                         │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-3">
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🟠</div>
                        <h5>AWS EKS</h5>
                        <p><strong>Control plane:</strong> $0.10/hr. <strong>Nodes:</strong> EC2 or Fargate (serverless). <strong>Identity:</strong> IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts). <strong>Storage:</strong> EBS CSI. <strong>Best for:</strong> AWS-heavy orgs.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔵</div>
                        <h5>Google GKE</h5>
                        <p><strong>Control plane:</strong> Free (standard). <strong>Nodes:</strong> Standard or Autopilot. <strong>Identity:</strong> Workload Identity. <strong>Storage:</strong> PD CSI. <strong>Best for:</strong> K8s-native features first.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🔷</div>
                        <h5>Azure AKS</h5>
                        <p><strong>Control plane:</strong> Free! <strong>Nodes:</strong> VMSS pools. <strong>Identity:</strong> Managed Identity. <strong>Storage:</strong> Azure Disk CSI. <strong>Best for:</strong> Azure/enterprise shops.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>🔧 Self-Managed (kubeadm)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Full control over every component</li>
                            <li>✅ Deep understanding of K8s internals</li>
                            <li>✅ No vendor lock-in</li>
                            <li>❌ You manage etcd, certs, upgrades, HA</li>
                            <li>❌ Responsible for node provisioning</li>
                            <li>❌ Must run your own monitoring/alerting</li>
                            <li><strong>CKA:</strong> This is what the exam tests</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card">
                        <h5>☁️ Cloud-Managed (EKS/GKE/AKS)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li>✅ Control plane fully managed &amp; HA</li>
                            <li>✅ Auto-rotating certs, auto-backup etcd</li>
                            <li>✅ 1-click upgrades, cluster autoscaler</li>
                            <li>✅ Integrated identity, storage, networking</li>
                            <li>❌ Less visibility into control plane</li>
                            <li>❌ Vendor-specific tooling and patterns</li>
                            <li><strong>CKA:</strong> Where you’ll apply exam skills</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.2 CKA Concepts in Cloud Kubernetes</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">🎯 CKA EXAM TOPIC</span>
                <p>The good news: <strong>most CKA skills transfer directly</strong> to cloud Kubernetes. Your kubectl commands, YAML manifests, and troubleshooting skills work everywhere. Here's exactly what changes and what stays the same.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Self-Managed vs Cloud-Managed — What Changes?</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CKA SKILL                SELF-MANAGED     CLOUD-MANAGED                    │
│  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────          │
│  kubeadm init/join         You do it ✋      Not needed ☁️                   │
│  etcd backup/restore       You do it ✋      Auto-managed ☁️                 │
│  Certificate rotation      You do it ✋      Auto-rotated ☁️                 │
│  Cluster upgrades          kubeadm ✋       Cloud console / CLI ☁️          │
│  Node scaling              Manual ✋         Cluster Autoscaler ☁️           │
│  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────          │
│  kubectl commands          Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  YAML manifests            Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  RBAC                      Same ✅           Same ✅ + Cloud IAM             │
│  NetworkPolicies           Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  PV / PVC                  Same ✅           Same ✅ + Cloud Disks           │
│  Troubleshooting           Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  Pod scheduling            Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  Services / Ingress        Same ✅           Same ✅ + Cloud LB              │
│  Resource limits           Same ✅           Same ✅                          │
│  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────          │
│                                                                              │
│  🔑  KEY INSIGHT: ~80% of CKA skills are IDENTICAL on cloud K8s.           │
│      The 20% that differ are infrastructure setup tasks that cloud          │
│      providers handle FOR you. That's WHY companies use managed K8s.        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA to Cloud Translation</h5>
                    <p>When you see a CKA task like "backup etcd" — on EKS/GKE/AKS, this is handled automatically. But the <strong>concept</strong> of "etcd holds cluster state, and losing it means losing the cluster" still applies. The CKA teaches you <strong>why</strong> things matter; cloud just automates the <strong>how</strong>.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.3 Cloud-Native Storage and Networking</h3>
                <p>Cloud providers use <strong>CSI (Container Storage Interface)</strong> drivers to connect Kubernetes PersistentVolumes to cloud block storage. You write the same PVC YAML — only the <code>StorageClass</code> provisioner changes.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>CSI Architecture — How Cloud Storage Plugs In</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CSI (Container Storage Interface)                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────┐      ┌──────────────┐      ┌──────────────────┐              │
│  │  Pod      │      │  Kubernetes  │      │  Cloud Provider  │              │
│  │  (easypj)│─────▶│  PVC → PV    │─────▶│  CSI Driver      │              │
│  └──────────┘      └──────────────┘      └────────┬─────────┘              │
│                                                    │                         │
│                                           ┌────────▼─────────┐              │
│                                           │  Cloud Disk API  │              │
│                                           │  (EBS / PD / AD) │              │
│                                           └──────────────────┘              │
│                                                                              │
│  Your YAML (same pattern):       StorageClass (cloud-specific):             │
│  ┌──────────────────────┐       ┌────────────────────────────────┐          │
│  │ apiVersion: v1        │       │ provisioner:                   │          │
│  │ kind: PVC             │       │   AWS:   ebs.csi.aws.com      │          │
│  │ spec:                 │       │   GCP:   pd.csi.storage.gke.io│          │
│  │   storageClass: fast  │       │   Azure: disk.csi.azure.com   │          │
│  │   resources:          │       │ parameters:                    │          │
│  │     requests:         │       │   type: gp3 / pd-ssd / Premium│          │
│  │       storage: 20Gi   │       └────────────────────────────────┘          │
│  └──────────────────────┘                                                   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>StorageClass Configurations by Cloud Provider</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass-aws.yaml — AWS EBS StorageClass</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-ebs
provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com      <span class="comment"># AWS EBS CSI driver</span>
parameters:
  type: gp3                        <span class="comment"># General Purpose SSD (gp3)</span>
  encrypted: "true"                <span class="comment"># Encrypt at rest</span>
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer  <span class="comment"># Schedule-aware binding</span>
allowVolumeExpansion: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass-azure.yaml — Azure Disk StorageClass</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-azure
provisioner: disk.csi.azure.com    <span class="comment"># Azure Disk CSI driver</span>
parameters:
  skuName: Premium_LRS             <span class="comment"># Premium SSD</span>
  cachingmode: ReadOnly
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
allowVolumeExpansion: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">storageclass-gcp.yaml — GCE Persistent Disk StorageClass</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-gce
provisioner: pd.csi.storage.gke.io  <span class="comment"># GCE PD CSI driver</span>
parameters:
  type: pd-ssd                       <span class="comment"># SSD persistent disk</span>
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
allowVolumeExpansion: true</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Exam Relevance</h5>
                    <p>The CKA tests you on <code>PersistentVolume</code>, <code>PersistentVolumeClaim</code>, and <code>StorageClass</code> concepts — the YAML structure is identical across all providers. Know the <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code> pattern — it ensures the volume is created in the same zone as the pod.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.4 Cloud Identity Integration</h3>
                <p>In cloud Kubernetes, pods can <strong>assume cloud IAM roles</strong> without storing credentials. This is the production-grade way to give your application access to cloud resources like databases, storage buckets, and message queues.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Pod → ServiceAccount → Cloud IAM Role → Cloud Resource</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLOUD IDENTITY INTEGRATION — How Pods Access Cloud Resources               │
│                                                                              │
│  ❌ BAD: Hardcoded credentials (Secret with access keys)                    │
│  ┌──────┐   Secret: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID      ┌──────────┐                    │
│  │ Pod  │──────────────────────────────────▶│ S3 Bucket│  ← Key leaked!    │
│  └──────┘                                   └──────────┘                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ✅ GOOD: IAM Role via ServiceAccount (no credentials stored)              │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌─────────────┐    ┌──────────┐          │
│  │ Pod  │───▶│ K8s Service  │───▶│ Cloud IAM   │───▶│ Cloud    │          │
│  │      │    │ Account      │    │ Role         │    │ Resource │          │
│  └──────┘    └──────────────┘    └─────────────┘    └──────────┘          │
│                                                                              │
│  How Each Cloud Does It:                                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │  AWS (IRSA):                                                     │       │
│  │  Pod → ServiceAccount → IAM Role via OIDC → AWS Resources       │       │
│  │  (annotation: eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn)                        │       │
│  │                                                                  │       │
│  │  Azure (Workload Identity):                                      │       │
│  │  Pod → ServiceAccount → Managed Identity → Azure Resources      │       │
│  │  (annotation: azure.workload.identity/client-id)                │       │
│  │                                                                  │       │
│  │  GCP (Workload Identity):                                        │       │
│  │  Pod → KSA → GSA binding → GCP Resources                        │       │
│  │  (annotation: iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account)                   │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                                                              │
│  easypj Example:                                                             │
│  Pod (easypj-web) → SA (easypj-sa) → IAM Role → Cloud Database Access     │
│  No passwords in YAML! No secrets to rotate! ✅                              │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why This Matters for easypj</h5>
                    <p>Instead of storing database passwords in Kubernetes Secrets, easypj pods can authenticate to a cloud-managed PostgreSQL database using IAM roles. The ServiceAccount annotation is the only config needed — no credentials ever appear in your YAML manifests or container environment.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>ServiceAccount Annotations — Per Cloud Provider</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">serviceaccount.yaml — Cloud IAM integration (all 3 providers)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># AWS EKS — IRSA (IAM Roles for Service Accounts)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    <span class="comment"># This links the K8s SA to an AWS IAM Role</span>
    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/easypj-db-role
---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Azure AKS — Workload Identity</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    azure.workload.identity/client-id: "abc-def-123-456"
  labels:
    azure.workload.identity/use: "true"    <span class="comment"># Required label!</span>
---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># GCP GKE — Workload Identity Federation</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account: easypj-app@project-id.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">deployment.yaml — Using the annotated ServiceAccount</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Reference the SA in your Deployment — that's it!</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: easypj-sa    # ← Pod inherits cloud IAM role
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: easypj/web:v2.1
        env:
        - name: DATABASE_URL
          <span class="comment"># No password needed — IAM auth handles it!</span>
          value: "postgresql://easypj-db.cluster-xyz.us-east-1.rds.amazonaws.com/easypj"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Focus — ServiceAccounts</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam tests <code>serviceAccountName</code> in pod specs. While cloud IAM annotations are provider-specific and not directly tested, understanding <strong>how ServiceAccounts bind to pods</strong> and that <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> restricts API access is core CKA knowledge.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.5 Deploying easypj to Cloud Kubernetes</h3>
                <p>The same Kubernetes manifests you use locally work on cloud. The differences are <strong>how you create the cluster</strong> and <strong>how you connect kubectl</strong>. Here's the complete walkthrough for all three clouds.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Cloud Deployment Flow — 4 Steps on Any Cloud</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DEPLOYMENT FLOW (same for all clouds)                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Step 1               Step 2               Step 3              Step 4       │
│  ┌─────────────┐     ┌─────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐   ┌───────────┐ │
│  │ Create      │     │ Configure   │     │ Apply same   │   │ Expose    │ │
│  │ Managed     │────▶│ kubectl     │────▶│ YAML as      │──▶│ via Cloud │ │
│  │ Cluster     │     │ Context     │     │ local env    │   │ LB/Ingress│ │
│  └─────────────┘     └─────────────┘     └──────────────┘   └───────────┘ │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 1 &amp; 2: Create Cluster + Configure kubectl</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — create cluster and connect (all 3 clouds)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># AWS EKS</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ eksctl create cluster --name easypj-prod --region us-east-1 --nodes 3
$ aws eks update-kubeconfig --name easypj-prod --region us-east-1

<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Azure AKS</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ az aks create --resource-group easypj-rg --name easypj-prod \
    --node-count 3 --generate-ssh-keys
$ az aks get-credentials --resource-group easypj-rg --name easypj-prod

<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Google GKE</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ gcloud container clusters create easypj-prod --zone us-central1-a --num-nodes 3
$ gcloud container clusters get-credentials easypj-prod --zone us-central1-a

<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify connection (same for ALL clouds!)</span>
<span class="comment"># ════════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ kubectl get nodes
NAME                  STATUS   ROLES   AGE   VERSION
node-pool-xxxx-001    Ready    &lt;none&gt;  2m    v1.29.0
node-pool-xxxx-002    Ready    &lt;none&gt;  2m    v1.29.0
node-pool-xxxx-003    Ready    &lt;none&gt;  2m    v1.29.0</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 3 &amp; 4: Deploy easypj (Same YAML, Any Cloud)</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — deploy easypj to cloud cluster</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Same commands whether on EKS, AKS, or GKE!</span>
$ kubectl create namespace easypj
$ kubectl apply -f easypj-deployment.yaml -n easypj
$ kubectl apply -f easypj-service.yaml -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Expose via cloud LoadBalancer (auto-provisions cloud LB)</span>
$ kubectl expose deployment easypj-web --type=LoadBalancer \
    --port=80 --target-port=8000 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Wait for external IP</span>
$ kubectl get svc -n easypj -w
NAME          TYPE           CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP      PORT(S)
easypj-web    LoadBalancer   10.100.0.52   52.186.xxx.xxx   80:31234/TCP

<span class="comment"># Test it! 🎉</span>
$ curl http://52.186.xxx.xxx/
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;&lt;html&gt;...easypj is running!...</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 Focus on CKA Core Concepts</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam won't ask you to create an EKS/AKS/GKE cluster. But it <strong>will</strong> test Deployments, Services, PVCs, and troubleshooting — which are <strong>identical</strong> on cloud and on-prem. Master the core concepts, and they transfer everywhere.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §36.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s36-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">36.6 Cluster Autoscaler</h3>
                <p>The Cluster Autoscaler <strong>automatically adjusts the number of worker nodes</strong> in your cluster. When pods can't be scheduled (no node has enough resources), it adds nodes. When nodes are underutilized for 10+ minutes, it removes them.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Cluster Autoscaler Decision Flow</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CLUSTER AUTOSCALER — HOW IT WORKS                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  SCALE UP:                                                                  │
│  ┌────────────────────┐                                                     │
│  │ New pod created    │                                                     │
│  │ (e.g. HPA scales  │                                                     │
│  │  easypj to 10)    │                                                     │
│  └────────┬───────────┘                                                     │
│           │                                                                  │
│           ▼                                                                  │
│  ┌────────────────────┐     ┌────────────────────┐                          │
│  │ Scheduler tries to │ NO  │ Pod stays PENDING   │                          │
│  │ place pod on node  │────▶│ (Insufficient CPU/  │                          │
│  └────────────────────┘     │  Memory)             │                          │
│                              └────────┬───────────┘                          │
│                                       │                                      │
│                                       ▼                                      │
│                              ┌────────────────────┐                          │
│                              │ Cluster Autoscaler  │                          │
│                              │ detects pending pod  │                          │
│                              └────────┬───────────┘                          │
│                                       │                                      │
│                                       ▼                                      │
│                              ┌────────────────────┐                          │
│                              │ Cloud API: Create   │                          │
│                              │ new node instance   │                          │
│                              └────────┬───────────┘                          │
│                                       │ (~2-5 min)                           │
│                                       ▼                                      │
│                              ┌────────────────────┐                          │
│                              │ Node Ready → Pod    │                          │
│                              │ scheduled ✅         │                          │
│                              └────────────────────┘                          │
│                                                                              │
│  SCALE DOWN:                                                                │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐             │
│  │  Node utilization &lt; 50% for 10 minutes                     │             │
│  │       │                                                     │             │
│  │       ▼                                                     │             │
│  │  Check: Can all pods be rescheduled to other nodes?        │             │
│  │       │                                                     │             │
│  │       ▼  YES                                                │             │
│  │  Cordon → Drain → Terminate node instance                  │             │
│  │  (Respects PDB! Won't violate minAvailable)                │             │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘             │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 36 — How Managed Kubernetes Services Work ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 36 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How Managed Kubernetes Services Actually Work: Control Plane Hosting, Cloud Controller Manager, and the Shared Responsibility Model</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ Managed Kubernetes — What the Cloud Provider Runs vs What You Run</h5>
            <p>EKS, GKE, and AKS don't just "host Kubernetes." They run the control plane for you while you manage worker nodes and workloads. Understanding the boundary is critical for CKA scenarios involving cloud clusters.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  MANAGED KUBERNETES — Shared Responsibility Model                             │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │                       CLOUD PROVIDER MANAGES                          │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │  kube-apiserver     etcd     kube-controller-manager        │      │   │
│  │  │  kube-scheduler     cloud-controller-manager                │      │   │
│  │  │  (YOU CANNOT SSH INTO CONTROL PLANE NODES!)                 │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │                          YOU MANAGE                                   │   │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │  │  Worker Nodes (EC2/VM instances)                            │      │   │
│  │  │  kubelet + container runtime (managed node groups possible) │      │   │
│  │  │  Pods, Deployments, Services, Ingress, etc.                 │      │   │
│  │  │  RBAC, NetworkPolicies, PodSecurity                         │      │   │
│  │  │  Storage (PV/PVC/StorageClass)                               │      │   │
│  │  │  Monitoring, Logging, Backup                                 │      │   │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  CLOUD CONTROLLER MANAGER — The Bridge to Cloud APIs:                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Node Controller:                                                     │   │
│  │    Detects unhealthy nodes → removes from cloud LB → terminates VM  │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Route Controller:                                                     │   │
│  │    Creates cloud routes for pod CIDRs (pod-to-pod across nodes)      │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Service Controller:                                                     │   │
│  │    Creates cloud LoadBalancers for type: LoadBalancer Services       │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  Volume Controller:                                                     │   │
│  │    Creates cloud volumes for PVCs (via CSI driver)                    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  PROVIDER DIFFERENCES:                                                       │
│  ┌────────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬───────────────────────┐   │
│  │ FEATURE        │ EKS (AWS)    │ GKE (GCP)    │ AKS (Azure)           │   │
│  ├────────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┼───────────────────────┤   │
│  │ Auth           │ aws-iam-     │ GKE Workload │ Azure AD Pod Identity  │   │
│  │                │ authenticator│ Identity     │                        │   │
│  │ Networking     │ VPC CNI      │ Native CNI   │ Azure CNI              │   │
│  │ Storage        │ EBS CSI      │ Persistent   │ Azure Disk/File CSI    │   │
│  │                │              │ Disk CSI     │                        │   │
│  │ Ingress        │ AWS LB       │ GKE Ingress  │ Application Gateway    │   │
│  │                │ Controller   │ Controller   │ Ingress Controller     │   │
│  │ Autoscaling    │ Cluster      │ Cluster      │ Cluster Autoscaler     │   │
│  │                │ Autoscaler   │ Autoscaler   │                        │   │
│  │ Upgrade        │ eksctl       │ Auto-upgrade │ az aks upgrade         │   │
│  │                │ upgrade      │ channels     │                        │   │
│  └────────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┴───────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> The CKA is distribution-neutral but may reference cloud concepts. Key differences from self-managed: you CANNOT SSH into control plane nodes, etcd is fully managed (no manual backup needed), upgrades are handled by the provider, and you must use cloud-specific CSI drivers and CNI plugins. For the exam, focus on the kubeadm-based cluster management — cloud specifics are supplementary knowledge.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <p>The Cluster Autoscaler <strong>respects PDBs</strong> during scale-down. If removing a node would violate a PodDisruptionBudget (e.g., easypj's <code>minAvailable: 2</code>), the node won't be removed. This is why PDBs are important even with autoscaling!</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — CKA skills that transfer to cloud:</strong><br>
                ✅ RBAC, NetworkPolicies, PV/PVC, ConfigMaps/Secrets — identical across all platforms<br>
                ✅ Deployments, Services, Ingress, DaemonSets — same YAML everywhere<br>
                ✅ Troubleshooting (logs, describe, exec) — universal toolkit<br>
                ❌ kubeadm, etcd backup, static pods, certificate management — cloud providers handle these<br>
                <strong>The CKA tests everything including what cloud hides from you.</strong></p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-commands"></span>
