            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch36-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 36 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (12)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of managed-Kubernetes trade-offs — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What Kubernetes components does a managed service (EKS/GKE/AKS) handle vs what you manage?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Component</th>
                                        <th>Managed (EKS/GKE/AKS)</th>
                                        <th>Self-Managed (kubeadm)</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>API Server</td>
                                        <td>☁️ Cloud manages</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>etcd</td>
                                        <td>☁️ Cloud manages</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Controller Manager</td>
                                        <td>☁️ Cloud manages</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Scheduler</td>
                                        <td>☁️ Cloud manages</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Certificates</td>
                                        <td>☁️ Cloud manages</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Worker Nodes</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage*</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Pods/Deployments</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>RBAC/NetworkPolicy</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Upgrades</td>
                                        <td>☁️ CP auto / 🔧 workers you</td>
                                        <td>🔧 You manage everything</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <p style="color: #94a3b8; font-size: 0.85em;">* Fargate/Autopilot = Cloud also manages workers</p>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Managed services handle the control plane (API server, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager, certificates). You handle workloads, RBAC, networking, and typically worker nodes. The CKA tests everything including what cloud hides: etcd backup, kubeadm upgrades, certificate management, static pod manifests. Understanding this split helps you know exactly why the CKA matters.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A PVC stays in <code>Pending</code> state on a kubeadm cluster. How does this differ from cloud and how do you fix it?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check PVC status:</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># NAME     STATUS   VOLUME  CAPACITY  ACCESS  SC       AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># data     Pending                            (none)   5m</span>

<span class="comment"># Why it's Pending:</span>
kubectl describe pvc data | tail -10
<span class="comment"># "no persistent volumes available for this claim"</span>

<span class="comment"># Cloud vs kubeadm differences:</span>
<span class="comment"># Cloud: Default StorageClass auto-provisions PVs</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm: NO default StorageClass → PVCs without SC stay Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Check StorageClasses</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># (empty) ← No StorageClass!</span>

<span class="comment"># On cloud, you'd see:</span>
<span class="comment"># gp2 (default)  OR  standard (default)</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 1: Create a PV manually</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
name: data-pv
spec:
capacity:
storage: 1Gi
accessModes:
- ReadWriteOnce
hostPath:
path: /data/pv
persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 2: Specify storageClassName in PVC</span>
<span class="comment"># and create a matching StorageClass</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix Option 3: Create PV + PVC with matching labels</span>
<span class="comment"># PV: storageClassName: manual</span>
<span class="comment"># PVC: storageClassName: manual</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pvc
<span class="comment"># NAME  STATUS  VOLUME   CAPACITY  SC      AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># data  Bound   data-pv  1Gi       manual  1m  ✓</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>On cloud, StorageClasses with dynamic provisioning auto-create PVs. On kubeadm/bare-metal, you usually need to create PVs manually. CKA uses kubeadm, so expect to create PVs with <code>hostPath</code>. Common trap: PVC with no <code>storageClassName</code> looks for the default SC — if none exists, it stays Pending forever. Always check <code>kubectl get sc</code> first.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you create a HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA) and what prerequisites does it need?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Prerequisite 1: Metrics Server must be installed</span>
kubectl top pods  <span class="comment"># If this works, metrics-server is ready</span>

<span class="comment"># Prerequisite 2: Pods must have resource REQUESTS defined</span>
<span class="comment"># HPA compares actual usage vs requested</span>

<span class="comment"># Create HPA imperatively:</span>
kubectl autoscale deployment web \
--min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70
<span class="comment"># Scales up when avg CPU > 70% of requests</span>
<span class="comment"># Scales down when avg CPU < 70%</span>

<span class="comment"># Check HPA:</span>
kubectl get hpa
<span class="comment"># NAME  REFERENCE        TARGETS        MINPODS  MAXPODS  REPLICAS</span>
<span class="comment"># web   Deployment/web   45%/70%        2        10       3</span>
<span class="comment">#                        ↑ current/target</span>

<span class="comment"># Declarative:</span>
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
name: web
spec:
scaleTargetRef:
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
name: web
minReplicas: 2
maxReplicas: 10
metrics:
- type: Resource
resource:
  name: cpu
  target:
    type: Utilization
    averageUtilization: 70

<span class="comment"># If TARGETS shows &lt;unknown&gt;/70%:</span>
<span class="comment"># → Metrics Server not installed</span>
<span class="comment"># OR → Pod has no resource requests defined!</span>
<span class="comment"># Fix: Add requests to pod spec</span>
resources:
requests:
cpu: 100m
memory: 128Mi</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>HPA requires: (1) Metrics Server installed, (2) pods have CPU/memory requests defined. Without requests, HPA can't calculate percentage. <code>kubectl autoscale</code> is the imperative shortcut. If TARGETS shows <code>&lt;unknown&gt;</code>, check Metrics Server and resource requests. On the CKA, HPA is a practical topic — know how to create it imperatively and what the prerequisites are.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service shows <code>Pending</code> EXTERNAL-IP on a kubeadm cluster. Why and what's the alternative?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check Service:</span>
kubectl get svc web-lb
<span class="comment"># NAME     TYPE          CLUSTER-IP    EXTERNAL-IP  PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># web-lb   LoadBalancer  10.96.50.100  &lt;pending&gt;    80:31234/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Why Pending?</span>
<span class="comment"># LoadBalancer type requires a cloud provider's LB controller</span>
<span class="comment"># kubeadm has NO cloud LB controller → stays Pending forever</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative 1: Use NodePort</span>
kubectl expose deployment web --type=NodePort --port=80
kubectl get svc web
<span class="comment"># NAME  TYPE      CLUSTER-IP   EXTERNAL-IP  PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># web   NodePort  10.96.1.100  &lt;none&gt;       80:30080/TCP</span>
<span class="comment"># Access: http://&lt;any-node-ip&gt;:30080</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative 2: Convert existing LB to NodePort</span>
kubectl patch svc web-lb -p '{"spec":{"type":"NodePort"}}'

<span class="comment"># Alternative 3: Install MetalLB (bare-metal LB)</span>
<span class="comment"># MetalLB assigns real IPs to LoadBalancer services</span>
<span class="comment"># Not CKA scope but useful to know</span>

<span class="comment"># Alternative 4: Use Ingress (for HTTP/HTTPS)</span>
<span class="comment"># Ingress controller (nginx) with NodePort</span>
<span class="comment"># Routes traffic by hostname/path</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA exam: NodePort is the expected answer</span>
<span class="comment"># Port range: 30000-32767 (default)</span>
kubectl create svc nodeport web --tcp=80:80 --node-port=30080</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>type: LoadBalancer</code> needs a cloud provider's load balancer integration. On kubeadm/bare-metal, it stays Pending permanently. The CKA expects you to use <code>NodePort</code> instead (port range 30000-32767). NodePort makes the service accessible on every node's IP at the allocated port. MetalLB can provide LoadBalancer functionality on bare-metal but is not CKA scope.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What CKA skills transfer directly to cloud Kubernetes (EKS/GKE/AKS)?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Skill Category</th>
                                        <th>Transfers to Cloud?</th>
                                        <th>Notes</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Pods, Deployments, Services</td>
                                        <td>✅ 100%</td>
                                        <td>Identical YAML everywhere</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>RBAC, NetworkPolicy</td>
                                        <td>✅ 100%</td>
                                        <td>Core Kubernetes API</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>ConfigMaps, Secrets</td>
                                        <td>✅ 100%</td>
                                        <td>Same everywhere</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>PV/PVC, StorageClass</td>
                                        <td>✅ 95%</td>
                                        <td>SC names differ per cloud</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Troubleshooting (logs, describe)</td>
                                        <td>✅ 100%</td>
                                        <td>Universal debugging toolkit</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>kubeadm, etcd backup</td>
                                        <td>❌ No</td>
                                        <td>Cloud handles these</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Certificate management</td>
                                        <td>❌ No</td>
                                        <td>Cloud handles these</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Static pod manifests</td>
                                        <td>❌ No</td>
                                        <td>Cloud abstracts CP</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Most CKA skills (workloads, RBAC, networking, troubleshooting, storage) transfer directly to cloud. What doesn't transfer: kubeadm operations, etcd management, certificate handling, and static pod manifests — cloud providers handle these. The CKA tests BOTH: the universal Kubernetes skills you'll use daily AND the infrastructure skills that cloud hides but are essential for understanding how Kubernetes works.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does cloud IAM interact with Kubernetes RBAC?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Two authentication layers:</span>

<span class="comment"># Layer 1: Cloud IAM (WHO can access the cluster)</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS:   IAM Users/Roles → aws-auth ConfigMap → K8s users</span>
<span class="comment"># GCP:   GCP IAM → RBAC bindings</span>
<span class="comment"># Azure: Azure AD → AKS integration</span>

<span class="comment"># Layer 2: Kubernetes RBAC (WHAT they can do inside)</span>
<span class="comment"># Roles/ClusterRoles → RoleBindings/ClusterRoleBindings</span>

<span class="comment"># Flow:</span>
<span class="comment"># Developer → Cloud IAM (authenticated) → API Server → RBAC (authorized)</span>
<span class="comment">#                  ↓                                        ↓</span>
<span class="comment">#          "Yes, this is user1"              "user1 can list pods in dev ns"</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA focuses on Layer 2 (RBAC):</span>
<span class="comment"># Create Role:</span>
kubectl create role pod-viewer -n dev \
--verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods

<span class="comment"># Create RoleBinding:</span>
kubectl create rolebinding user1-view -n dev \
--role=pod-viewer --user=user1

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods -n dev --as=user1
<span class="comment"># yes ✓</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n dev --as=user1
<span class="comment"># no ← Only get,list,watch allowed</span>

<span class="comment"># On kubeadm (CKA exam):</span>
<span class="comment"># Authentication via X.509 certificates</span>
<span class="comment"># Certificate CN = username, O = group</span>
<span class="comment"># Then RBAC authorizes the authenticated user</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Cloud IAM handles authentication (WHO are you?), Kubernetes RBAC handles authorization (WHAT can you do?). Both must pass. On cloud: cloud IAM authenticates, then K8s RBAC authorizes. On kubeadm (CKA): X.509 certificates authenticate (CN=username, O=group), then RBAC authorizes. The CKA tests RBAC extensively — creating Roles, ClusterRoles, RoleBindings, ClusterRoleBindings, and verifying with <code>kubectl auth can-i</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How does the Cluster Autoscaler differ from HPA and when do they work together?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># HPA: Scales PODS (horizontal pod autoscaler)</span>
<span class="comment"># CA:  Scales NODES (cluster autoscaler)</span>

<span class="comment"># HPA alone:</span>
<span class="comment"># Traffic increases → HPA adds replicas → BUT...</span>
<span class="comment"># If no node capacity → pods stay Pending → HPA can't help!</span>

<span class="comment"># CA alone:</span>
<span class="comment"># Detects Pending pods (due to insufficient resources)</span>
<span class="comment"># Adds a new node → Pending pods get scheduled</span>

<span class="comment"># Together (the magic):</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Load increases</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. HPA: "CPU > 70%, need 10 replicas (currently 5)"</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. HPA creates pods 6-10</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Pods 8-10: "Pending" — no room on existing nodes</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. CA: "Pending pods detected, adding a node"</span>
<span class="comment"># 6. New node Ready → pods 8-10 scheduled</span>
<span class="comment"># 7. Load decreases</span>
<span class="comment"># 8. HPA: "CPU < 70%, scale down to 5 replicas"</span>
<span class="comment"># 9. HPA removes pods 6-10</span>
<span class="comment"># 10. CA: "Node is underutilized, removing it" (after cooldown)</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA exam:</span>
<span class="comment"># HPA: kubectl autoscale deployment web \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70</span>
<span class="comment"># CA: NOT on CKA (it's a cloud add-on)</span>

<span class="comment"># But know conceptually:</span>
<span class="comment"># HPA = pod-level scaling (CKA ✅)</span>
<span class="comment"># CA = node-level scaling (cloud only)</span>
<span class="comment"># VPA = Vertical Pod Autoscaler (adjusts requests/limits)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>HPA scales pods based on metrics (CPU, memory, custom). CA scales nodes when pods are Pending due to resource shortage. Together: HPA creates demand, CA adds capacity. On the CKA, you'll work with HPA (imperative: <code>kubectl autoscale</code>). CA is a cloud add-on. Key: HPA needs Metrics Server + resource requests defined on pods. Without requests, HPA can't calculate percentages.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do StorageClasses differ between cloud and bare-metal clusters?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Cloud: Dynamic provisioning with cloud storage</span>
kubectl get sc  <span class="comment"># On GKE:</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME              PROVISIONER          DEFAULT</span>
<span class="comment"># standard (def)    kubernetes.io/gce-pd  Yes</span>
<span class="comment"># premium-rwo       pd.csi.storage...     No</span>

<span class="comment"># PVC automatically creates a cloud disk:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: data-pvc
spec:
accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 10Gi
<span class="comment"># storageClassName: standard  ← Optional, uses default</span>

<span class="comment"># kubectl get pv → Auto-created PV bound to cloud disk!</span>

<span class="comment"># Bare-metal/kubeadm (CKA):</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># (empty or local-storage only)</span>

<span class="comment"># Must create PV manually:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
name: data-pv
spec:
capacity:
storage: 10Gi
accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]
hostPath:
path: /mnt/data
storageClassName: manual

<span class="comment"># PVC must specify matching storageClassName:</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: data-pvc
spec:
accessModes: [ReadWriteOnce]
resources:
requests:
  storage: 10Gi
storageClassName: manual

<span class="comment"># CKA exam: Expect hostPath PVs with manual provisioning</span>
<span class="comment"># Check default SC: kubectl get sc</span>
<span class="comment"># Set default: kubectl patch sc &lt;name&gt; \</span>
<span class="comment">#   -p '{"metadata":{"annotations":{"storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class":"true"}}}'</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Cloud clusters have dynamic provisioning: create PVC → StorageClass auto-creates a PV backed by cloud storage (EBS, GCE PD, Azure Disk). On kubeadm (CKA), there's typically no default StorageClass, so you must create PVs manually using <code>hostPath</code> or <code>local</code> volumes. Key CKA skill: creating both PV and PVC with matching <code>storageClassName</code>, capacity, and access modes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you check if a cluster has a default StorageClass and set one?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Check existing StorageClasses:</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># NAME              PROVISIONER        RECLAIMPOLICY  DEFAULT</span>
<span class="comment"># local-storage     local              Delete</span>
<span class="comment"># ← No "(default)" annotation!</span>

<span class="comment"># PVCs without storageClassName look for the default SC</span>
<span class="comment"># If no default → PVC stays Pending</span>

<span class="comment"># Set a StorageClass as default:</span>
kubectl patch sc local-storage -p \
'{"metadata":{"annotations":{"storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class":"true"}}}'

<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get sc
<span class="comment"># NAME                    PROVISIONER  RECLAIMPOLICY  DEFAULT</span>
<span class="comment"># local-storage (default) local        Delete         Yes ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Remove default from a StorageClass:</span>
kubectl patch sc local-storage -p \
'{"metadata":{"annotations":{"storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class":"false"}}}'

<span class="comment"># Important: Only ONE SC should be default</span>
<span class="comment"># Multiple defaults → PVCs fail with "multiple default SCs"</span>

<span class="comment"># Create a new StorageClass:</span>
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
name: fast-storage
annotations:
storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
reclaimPolicy: Retain</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The default StorageClass is marked by the annotation <code>storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"</code>. PVCs without <code>storageClassName</code> use the default SC. On the CKA, check with <code>kubectl get sc</code> and look for <code>(default)</code>. If multiple SCs are marked default, PVC creation fails. <code>WaitForFirstConsumer</code> delays provisioning until a pod actually uses the PVC.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What's the difference between Ingress on cloud vs bare-metal?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Cloud Ingress:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Install Ingress controller (nginx, ALB, etc.)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Controller creates: Service type=LoadBalancer → Cloud LB</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. External IP assigned automatically</span>
kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># NAME                  TYPE          EXTERNAL-IP</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress-controller    LoadBalancer  35.200.1.1  ← Cloud LB</span>

<span class="comment"># Bare-metal/kubeadm Ingress:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Install Ingress controller (nginx)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Controller creates: Service type=NodePort (or hostNetwork)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Access via node IP + NodePort</span>
kubectl get svc -n ingress-nginx
<span class="comment"># NAME                  TYPE      EXTERNAL-IP  PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># ingress-controller    NodePort  &lt;none&gt;       80:30080/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Access: http://&lt;node-ip&gt;:30080</span>

<span class="comment"># Create Ingress resource (same on both):</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
name: web-ingress
annotations:
nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
ingressClassName: nginx
rules:
- host: web.example.com
http:
  paths:
  - path: /
    pathType: Prefix
    backend:
      service:
        name: web-svc
        port:
          number: 80

<span class="comment"># CKA exam: Know Ingress resource YAML (same everywhere)</span>
<span class="comment"># The controller setup differs, but the Ingress object is identical</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The Ingress resource YAML is identical on cloud and bare-metal. The difference is how the Ingress controller is exposed: cloud uses <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service (gets external IP), bare-metal uses <code>NodePort</code> or <code>hostNetwork</code>. On the CKA, you create Ingress resources (the YAML) — know <code>ingressClassName</code>, rules, paths, backends. The controller is typically pre-installed on the exam cluster.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Walk through deploying the easypj Django application to a managed Kubernetes cluster (EKS, GKE, or AKS). What cloud-specific resources are automatically provisioned when you create a <code>LoadBalancer</code> Service and a PVC?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create cluster (example: AKS)</span>
az aks create -g myRG -n easypj-cluster --node-count 3
az aks get-credentials -g myRG -n easypj-cluster

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Deploy easypj (same YAML as bare-metal!)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Expose with LoadBalancer</span>
kubectl expose deployment easypj --type=LoadBalancer \
--port=80 --target-port=8000

<span class="comment"># Cloud auto-provisions:</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS:   Elastic Load Balancer (ELB/ALB) + public IP</span>
<span class="comment"># GCP:   Google Cloud Load Balancer + external IP</span>
<span class="comment"># Azure: Azure Load Balancer + public IP</span>

<span class="comment"># Wait for external IP:</span>
kubectl get svc easypj -w
<span class="comment"># NAME     TYPE           EXTERNAL-IP      PORT(S)</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj   LoadBalancer   40.112.73.215    80:31234/TCP</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Create PVC for PostgreSQL</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
name: postgres-data
spec:
accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
storageClassName: managed-premium   <span class="comment"># Cloud StorageClass</span>
resources:
requests:
  storage: 50Gi

<span class="comment"># Cloud auto-provisions:</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS:   EBS volume (gp3)</span>
<span class="comment"># GCP:   Persistent Disk (pd-ssd)</span>
<span class="comment"># Azure: Azure Managed Disk (Premium_LRS)</span>

<span class="comment"># Key difference from bare-metal:</span>
<span class="comment"># No manual PV creation! Cloud CSI driver handles it</span>
kubectl get pv
<span class="comment"># pvc-abc123   50Gi   RWO   Bound   default/postgres-data</span>
<span class="comment"># (PV auto-created by cloud provisioner)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify application:</span>
curl http://40.112.73.215/api/jobs/</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The beauty of Kubernetes is that the <strong>same YAML works everywhere</strong>. The difference is what happens behind the scenes: on bare-metal, <code>type: LoadBalancer</code> stays Pending forever (no cloud controller). On managed clusters, the cloud controller automatically provisions a real load balancer with a public IP. Similarly, PVCs with a cloud StorageClass trigger automatic disk provisioning via CSI drivers — no manual PV creation needed. On the CKA (which uses bare-metal/kubeadm), you create PVs manually. But understanding that cloud clusters automate this helps you grasp why StorageClasses and the CSI architecture exist.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the Cluster Autoscaler and how does it differ from HPA? Configure a node pool with autoscaling enabled (min 2, max 10 nodes). What triggers a scale-up vs scale-down?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># HPA vs Cluster Autoscaler:</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler):</span>
<span class="comment">#   Scales PODS within existing nodes</span>
<span class="comment">#   Based on CPU/memory metrics</span>
<span class="comment">#   kubectl autoscale deployment easypj --min=2 --max=20 --cpu-percent=70</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Cluster Autoscaler (CA):</span>
<span class="comment">#   Scales NODES (adds/removes VMs)</span>
<span class="comment">#   Based on pod scheduling failures</span>
<span class="comment">#   Configured per node pool via cloud CLI</span>

<span class="comment"># Enable Cluster Autoscaler:</span>
<span class="comment"># AWS EKS:</span>
eksctl create nodegroup --cluster=easypj-cluster \
--name=workers --nodes-min=2 --nodes-max=10 \
--asg-access

<span class="comment"># GCP GKE:</span>
gcloud container clusters update easypj-cluster \
--enable-autoscaling --min-nodes=2 --max-nodes=10

<span class="comment"># Azure AKS:</span>
az aks update -g myRG -n easypj-cluster \
--enable-cluster-autoscaler \
--min-count 2 --max-count 10

<span class="comment"># SCALE-UP triggers:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Pod is Pending due to "Insufficient cpu/memory"</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. No existing node can fit the pod's resource requests</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. CA adds a new node to the pool</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Scheduler places the Pending pod on the new node</span>

<span class="comment"># SCALE-DOWN triggers:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Node utilization drops below threshold (~50%)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. All pods on the node can be rescheduled elsewhere</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. No pods with PDBs that would be violated</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. CA drains the node and removes the VM</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. Default cooldown: 10 minutes after scale-up</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify autoscaler status:</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># Shows current node count</span>

kubectl describe configmap cluster-autoscaler-status \
-n kube-system
<span class="comment"># Shows scaling decisions and events</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>HPA and Cluster Autoscaler work together: HPA creates more pods when demand increases → eventually nodes fill up → new pods go Pending → Cluster Autoscaler detects Pending pods → adds nodes → pods get scheduled. For scale-down, CA checks if a node's pods can be moved elsewhere and respects PDBs before removing nodes. The CKA tests HPA directly (create with <code>kubectl autoscale</code>), while Cluster Autoscaler is conceptual knowledge. Key insight: <code>resource requests</code> on pods are what drive both systems — without requests, neither HPA nor CA can make informed decisions.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

