            </div>


            <!-- ─────────── §37.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.1 CI/CD Pipeline for Kubernetes</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <p>CI/CD automates the path from <strong>code commit</strong> to <strong>running pods</strong>. Every push to Git triggers a pipeline: build, test, package as container image, push to registry, and deploy to Kubernetes.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>CI/CD Pipeline — End to End</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CI/CD PIPELINE FOR KUBERNETES                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐  ┌──────┐    │
│  │  📝  │  │  🔨  │  │  ✅  │  │  🏷️  │  │  📦  │  │  ☸️  │  │  🔍  │    │
│  │ CODE │→│BUILD │→│ TEST │→│ TAG  │→│ PUSH │→│DEPLOY│→│VERIFY│    │
│  └──────┘  └──────┘  └──────┘  └──────┘  └──────┘  └──────┘  └──────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  Git       Docker    Unit      Docker    Container  kubectl    Smoke        │
│  Push      Build     Tests     Tag       Registry   apply     Tests         │
│                      + Lint    v1.2.3    (ECR/ACR/  -f deploy  curl /health │
│                                          GCR/GHCR)                          │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│                                                                              │
│  EXAMPLE: easypj CI/CD with GitHub Actions                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │  on: push to main                                               │        │
│  │                                                                  │        │
│  │  jobs:                                                           │        │
│  │    build:                                                        │        │
│  │      ├── Checkout code                                           │        │
│  │      ├── Run Django tests (python manage.py test)               │        │
│  │      ├── Build Docker image                                      │        │
│  │      ├── Tag: ghcr.io/user/easypj:${{ github.sha }}            │        │
│  │      └── Push to GitHub Container Registry                       │        │
│  │                                                                  │        │
│  │    deploy:                                                       │        │
│  │      ├── Update image tag in deployment YAML                    │        │
│  │      ├── kubectl apply -f k8s/                                   │        │
│  │      └── kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web           │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CI vs CD vs CD</h5>
                    <p><strong>CI (Continuous Integration)</strong> = build + test on every commit. <strong>CD (Continuous Delivery)</strong> = automatically prepare a deployable artifact. <strong>CD (Continuous Deployment)</strong> = automatically deploy to production. Most teams do CI + Continuous Delivery, with manual approval before production.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Complete GitHub Actions Workflow for easypj</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">.github/workflows/ci-cd.yaml — Full pipeline</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">name: easypj CI/CD Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:
    branches: [main]

env:
  REGISTRY: ghcr.io
  IMAGE_NAME: ${{ github.repository }}/easypj-web

jobs:
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># JOB 1: Test (CI)</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4

    - name: Set up Python
      uses: actions/setup-python@v5
      with:
        python-version: "3.12"

    - name: Install dependencies
      run: pip install -r requirements.txt

    - name: Run Django tests
      run: python manage.py test --verbosity=2

    - name: Run linter
      run: flake8 . --max-line-length=120

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># JOB 2: Build &amp; Push Image (CD prep)</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  build:
    needs: test           <span class="comment"># Only build if tests pass</span>
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'   <span class="comment"># Only on main</span>
    permissions:
      packages: write     <span class="comment"># Push to GHCR</span>

    steps:
    - uses: actions/checkout@v4

    - name: Log in to Container Registry
      uses: docker/login-action@v3
      with:
        registry: ${{ env.REGISTRY }}
        username: ${{ github.actor }}
        password: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}

    - name: Build and push Docker image
      uses: docker/build-push-action@v5
      with:
        context: .
        push: true
        tags: |
          ${{ env.REGISTRY }}/${{ env.IMAGE_NAME }}:${{ github.sha }}
          ${{ env.REGISTRY }}/${{ env.IMAGE_NAME }}:latest

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># JOB 3: Update manifest repo (GitOps trigger)</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  update-manifests:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
    - name: Checkout manifest repo
      uses: actions/checkout@v4
      with:
        repository: youruser/easypj-k8s-manifests
        token: ${{ secrets.MANIFEST_REPO_TOKEN }}

    - name: Update image tag
      run: |
        cd overlays/production
        kustomize edit set image \
          easypj-web=${{ env.REGISTRY }}/${{ env.IMAGE_NAME }}:${{ github.sha }}

    - name: Commit and push
      run: |
        git config user.name "GitHub Actions"
        git config user.email "actions@github.com"
        git commit -am "Deploy easypj ${{ github.sha }}"
        git push    <span class="comment"># ArgoCD picks this up automatically!</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 Key Takeaway for CKA</h5>
                    <p>The CKA won't ask you to write CI/CD YAML, but understanding this pipeline helps you grasp <strong>why image tags matter</strong> (they trigger rolling updates), <strong>why container registries matter</strong> (image pull policies, imagePullSecrets), and <strong>how deployments update</strong> — all directly tested on the CKA.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Multi-Stage Dockerfile — Security + Efficiency for easypj</h4>
                <p>A multi-stage build separates the <strong>build environment</strong> (with compilers, dev dependencies) from the <strong>runtime environment</strong> (minimal, secure). This shrinks the final image by 60-80% and eliminates build tools that attackers could exploit:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Dockerfile — Multi-stage build for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-dockerfile"># ═══════════════ STAGE 1: BUILDER (compilers, dev deps) ═══════════════
FROM python:3.12-slim AS builder
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --user --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════ STAGE 2: RUNTIME (minimal, no build tools) ═══════════════</span>
FROM python:3.12-slim
WORKDIR /app

<span class="comment"># Copy ONLY installed packages from builder (no pip, gcc, headers)</span>
COPY --from=builder /root/.local /root/.local
COPY . .

<span class="comment"># Run as non-root user</span>
RUN useradd -m -u 1000 easypj && chown -R easypj:easypj /app
USER easypj

ENV PATH=/root/.local/bin:$PATH
EXPOSE 8000
CMD ["gunicorn", "easypj.wsgi:application", "--bind", "0.0.0.0:8000"]</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Why Multi-Stage Matters for the CKA Exam</h5>
                    <p>The CKA won't ask you to write a Dockerfile, but <strong>image size directly affects pod startup time</strong>. A 1GB image takes 60+ seconds to pull; a 50MB image takes 3 seconds. When you <code>kubectl describe pod</code> and see "Pulling image" for 2 minutes during the exam — that's a real-world consequence of bloated images.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Tagging Strategy — What Every Tag Means in Production</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Image Tag Strategy — easypj Examples</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  IMAGE TAGGING STRATEGY                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Tag Pattern        Meaning              When to Use        Example          │
│  ─────────────────  ───────────────────  ─────────────────  ───────────────  │
│  :latest            Latest build         NEVER in prod      ⚠️ Avoid         │
│  :v1.2.3            Semantic version     Production release  easypj:v2.1.0  │
│  :&lt;git-sha&gt;         Commit-specific      CI builds           easypj:abc1234  │
│  :v2.1-stable       Named stable track   Promotion pipeline  easypj:stable   │
│  :dev / :staging    Environment-specific  Dev pipelines       easypj:dev      │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│                                                                              │
│  HOW TAGS DRIVE ROLLING UPDATES:                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │
│  │ 1. CI builds image → easypj:abc1234 (git SHA tag)                │      │
│  │ 2. CI updates deployment YAML → image: easypj:abc1234            │      │
│  │ 3. kubectl apply → Deployment sees NEW image → triggers rollout  │      │
│  │ 4. ReplicaSet creates pods with easypj:abc1234 image             │      │
│  │ 5. Old ReplicaSet scales down → new one scales up               │      │
│  │                                                                   │      │
│  │ ⚠️  If image tag didn't change → NO rollout (kubectl is a no-op) │      │
│  │ ⚠️  :latest with imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent → NEVER updates   │      │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Container Image Internals — What Actually Is an "Image"?</h4>
                <p>Understanding what a container image really is helps you debug image pull issues, optimize builds, and secure your supply chain. An image is NOT a single file — it's a <strong>collection of layers + a manifest</strong> stored in a registry:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>OCI Image Anatomy — Inside ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CONTAINER IMAGE INTERNALS — OCI SPECIFICATION                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Image Reference: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │                     IMAGE MANIFEST (JSON)                            │    │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │  │ {                                                            │    │    │
│  │  │   "schemaVersion": 2,                                        │    │    │
│  │  │   "mediaType": "application/vnd.oci.image.manifest.v1+json", │    │    │
│  │  │   "config": {                                                │    │    │
│  │  │     "mediaType": "application/vnd.oci.image.config.v1+json", │    │    │
│  │  │     "digest": "sha256:abc123...",  ← Config blob digest     │    │    │
│  │  │     "size": 1204                                             │    │    │
│  │  │   },                                                         │    │    │
│  │  │   "layers": [                                                │    │    │
│  │  │     {                                                        │    │    │
│  │  │       "mediaType": "application/vnd.oci.image.layer.v1.tar+gzip",│    │    │
│  │  │       "digest": "sha256:def456...",  ← Layer 1 digest        │    │    │
│  │  │       "size": 28123456                                       │    │    │
│  │  │     },                                                       │    │    │
│  │  │     { digest: "sha256:ghi789...", size: 12345 },  ← Layer 2 │    │    │
│  │  │     { digest: "sha256:jkl012...", size: 567890 }   ← Layer 3│    │    │
│  │  │   ]                                                          │    │    │
│  │  │ }                                                            │    │    │
│  │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  IMAGE LAYERS (content-addressable, immutable):                               │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │    │
│  │  │  Layer 3: COPY . .                     (easypj source code)   │    │    │
│  │  │  sha256:jkl012...   567KB                                      │    │    │
│  │  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │    │    │
│  │  │  │  Layer 2: RUN pip install -r requirements.txt            │ │    │    │
│  │  │  │  sha256:ghi789...   12MB   (Django, gunicorn, etc.)      │ │    │    │
│  │  │  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │    │    │
│  │  │  │  │  Layer 1: FROM python:3.12-slim                      │ │ │    │    │
│  │  │  │  │  sha256:def456...   28MB   (OS + Python runtime)     │ │ │    │    │
│  │  │  │  │                                                       │ │ │    │    │
│  │  │  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ │    │    │
│  │  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │    │    │
│  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  KEY CONCEPTS:                                                               │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ CONTENT-ADDRESSABLE: Each layer is named by its SHA256 hash.        │    │
│  │   Same content = same hash = same layer. This is how caching works. │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ LAYER SHARING: If two images use the same base (python:3.12-slim),  │    │
│  │   the 28MB base layer is downloaded ONCE and shared.                │    │
│  │   → 10 microservices using python:3.12-slim = 28MB total, not 280MB│    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ IMMUTABLE: Layers are never modified. Changing one line of code    │    │
│  │   creates a NEW layer (new SHA256 hash). Old layers persist in      │    │
│  │   registry until garbage collected.                                 │    │
│  │                                                                      │    │
│  │ UNION FILESYSTEM: At runtime, container runtime (containerd) uses   │    │
│  │   overlay2 to stack layers + adds a writable top layer (ephemeral). │    │
│  │   All writes go to the top layer; base layers are read-only.        │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Pull Lifecycle — What Happens When kubelet Pulls an Image</h4>
                <p>When you create a pod with image <code>ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1</code>, here's the exact sequence kubelet follows:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Image Pull Sequence (kubelet + CRI + containerd)</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  IMAGE PULL LIFECYCLE (chronological)                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  T=0: Pod scheduled to node                                                  │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ kubelet receives Pod spec from API server                            │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  T=0.01s: Check imagePullPolicy                                             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ ┌─────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │    │
│  │ │ Always          │ Pull EVERY time pod starts (even if cached)  │  │    │
│  │ │ IfNotPresent    │ Pull only if not cached locally              │  │    │
│  │ │ Never            │ Use ONLY local image (fail if not present)   │  │    │
│  │ └─────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │ ⚠️  Default: IfNotPresent for tagged images (:v2.1)                 │    │
│  │ ⚠️  Default: Always for :latest tag                                 │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  T=0.02s: Resolve registry authentication                                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ 1. Check Pod's imagePullSecrets for registry credentials            │    │
│  │ 2. Check ServiceAccount's imagePullSecrets (pod-level overrides)    │    │
│  │ 3. Check node's configured credential providers (ECR, ACR, GCR)    │    │
│  │ 4. For ghcr.io: check ~/.docker/config.json equivalent              │    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │ If no credentials found → ANONYMOUS pull (public repos only)        │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  T=0.05s: Fetch image manifest (HTTP GET)                                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ GET /v2/user/easypj/manifests/v2.1                                  │    │
│  │ Host: ghcr.io                                                        │    │
│  │ Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;                                       │    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │ Response: Image Manifest JSON (list of layer digests)                │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  T=0.1s: Download layers (parallel, content-addressable)                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ For each layer in manifest:                                         │    │
│  │   1. Check local cache: sha256:def456... exists?                    │    │
│  │   2. If YES → SKIP (already cached!)                                │    │
│  │   3. If NO → GET /v2/user/easypj/blobs/sha256:def456...            │    │
│  │   4. Verify: downloaded blob's SHA == expected SHA                  │    │
│  │   5. Extract: gunzip + untar to content store                       │    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │ Layers are downloaded in PARALLEL (up to 3 concurrent by default)   │    │
│  │                                                                       │    │
│  │ ⏱️  Time: depends on layer sizes + network bandwidth               │    │
│  │    • Base layer (python:3.12-slim): 28MB → ~3s on good connection  │    │
│  │    • App layer (easypj code): 567KB → ~0.1s                        │    │
│  │    • Total: ~3-5 seconds for cached base, ~20-30s for full pull    │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  T=5s: Unpack layers + prepare rootfs                                        │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ containerd uses snapshotter (overlay2) to:                          │    │
│  │   1. Stack layers in order (Layer 1 → Layer 2 → Layer 3)           │    │
│  │   2. Create writable snapshot (empty top layer)                     │    │
│  │   3. Apply image config (ENV, CMD, WORKDIR, USER)                   │    │
│  │   4. Result: runnable container root filesystem                     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  T=6s: Container starts                                                      │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ kubectl describe pod → Events:                                      │    │
│  │   Normal  Pulling     6s    Pulling image "ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1"│    │
│  │   Normal  Pulled      5s    Successfully pulled image               │    │
│  │   Normal  Created     4s    Created container easypj                │    │
│  │   Normal  Started     3s    Started container easypj                │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  COMMON IMAGE PULL ERRORS:                                                   │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ ErrImagePull                    │ Network timeout, registry down,      │  │
│  │                                 │ authentication failed                │  │
│  │ ImagePullBackOff                │ Retrying after previous failure      │  │
│  │                                 │ (exponential backoff: 10s→20s→40s)  │  │
│  │ ErrImageNeverPull               │ imagePullPolicy: Never, not cached  │  │
│  │ InvalidImageName                │ Malformed image reference            │  │
│  │ manifest unknown                 │ Tag doesn't exist in registry        │  │
│  │ unauthorized                    │ Authentication failed (check secret) │  │
│  └────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>imagePullSecrets — Registry Authentication in Kubernetes</h4>
                <p>Private registries (like ghcr.io private repos, ECR, ACR) require authentication. Here's the end-to-end setup:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — Setting up private registry access for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># CREATE DOCKER REGISTRY SECRET</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ kubectl create secret docker-registry ghcr-secret \
    --docker-server=ghcr.io \
    --docker-username=youruser \
    --docker-password=$GITHUB_TOKEN \
    --docker-email=you@example.com \
    -n easypj

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># OPTION 1: Reference in Pod spec</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
spec:
  imagePullSecrets:
  - name: ghcr-secret        <span class="comment"># Used for ALL containers in this pod</span>
  containers:
  - name: easypj
    image: ghcr.io/youruser/easypj:v2.1

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># OPTION 2: Attach to ServiceAccount (recommended)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ kubectl patch serviceaccount default \
    -n easypj \
    -p '{"imagePullSecrets": [{"name": "ghcr-secret"}]}'
<span class="comment"># Now EVERY pod using this ServiceAccount auto-gets ghcr-secret</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># VERIFY: Check if kubelet can pull using the secret</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ kubectl describe pod easypj-web-xxx -n easypj | grep -A5 "Pulling"
<span class="comment"># Normal  Pulling   Pulling image "ghcr.io/youruser/easypj:v2.1"</span>
<span class="comment"># Normal  Pulled    Successfully pulled image  ← Secret works!</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 CKA Exam Tip: imagePullSecrets</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam frequently tests imagePullSecrets. Remember: if you create a secret in namespace X and the pod is in namespace Y, the pod CANNOT use the secret. Secrets are namespace-scoped. Also, patching a ServiceAccount with imagePullSecrets only affects NEW pods — existing pods must be restarted.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.2 GitOps with ArgoCD</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <p><strong>GitOps</strong> means Git is the single source of truth for your cluster state. Instead of running <code>kubectl apply</code> from a CI pipeline (push-based), a GitOps tool like ArgoCD <strong>watches your Git repo and syncs changes automatically</strong> (pull-based).</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Traditional CI/CD (Push) vs GitOps (Pull)</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  TRADITIONAL CI/CD (Push-Based):       GITOPS (Pull-Based):                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Developer pushes code                 Developer pushes code                 │
│       │                                     │                                │
│       ▼                                     ▼                                │
│  CI builds &amp; tests                    CI builds &amp; tests                    │
│       │                                     │                                │
│       ▼                                     ▼                                │
│  CD runs kubectl apply ⚠️              CD updates manifest                   │
│  (push to cluster directly)            in Git repo                           │
│       │                                     │                                │
│       ▼                                     ▼                                │
│  Deployed. But...                      ArgoCD detects Git change             │
│  - Who ran it? 🤷                            │                                │
│  - What changed? 🤷                          ▼                                │
│  - How to rollback? 😬                 ArgoCD syncs to cluster ✅            │
│  - Drift undetected 😱                                                       │
│                                         Benefits:                            │
│                                         ✅ Full audit trail (Git log)       │
│                                         ✅ Rollback = git revert             │
│                                         ✅ Drift detection &amp; auto-fix      │
│                                         ✅ PR-based deployments              │
│                                         ✅ No cluster creds in CI           │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>ArgoCD Architecture</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ARGOCD SYNC LOOP                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────┐      Poll every 3min     ┌──────────────────┐            │
│  │              │   ◀─────────────────────  │                  │            │
│  │  Git Repo    │                           │    ArgoCD        │            │
│  │  (manifests) │   Detect diff?            │    Controller    │            │
│  │              │   ─────────────────────▶  │                  │            │
│  └──────────────┘                           └────────┬─────────┘            │
│                                                       │                      │
│         Git state ≠ Cluster state?                   │ Sync!                │
│         → OutOfSync status                           │                      │
│         → Auto-sync or manual approval               ▼                      │
│                                              ┌──────────────────┐           │
│                                              │   K8s Cluster    │           │
│                                              │   ┌──────────┐   │           │
│                                              │   │ easypj   │   │           │
│                                              │   │ pods     │   │           │
│                                              │   └──────────┘   │           │
│                                              │   Status: Synced │           │
│                                              │   Health: Healthy │           │
│                                              └──────────────────┘           │
│                                                                              │
│  ArgoCD UI Dashboard:                                                        │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐             │
│  │ App: easypj     │ Sync: ✅ Synced │ Health: 💚 Healthy    │             │
│  │ Repo: github.com/user/easypj-k8s   │ Revision: abc1234   │             │
│  │ Target: easypj namespace            │ Auto-Sync: ON       │             │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘             │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Webhooks vs Polling — Instant Sync</h4>
                <p>ArgoCD polls Git every 3 minutes by default. For instant deployment, configure a <strong>webhook</strong> in your Git provider (GitHub, GitLab, BitBucket) so ArgoCD syncs within seconds of a push:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — Webhook setup for ArgoCD</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># GITHUB WEBHOOK CONFIGURATION</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Expose argocd-server (production uses Ingress/LB, not port-forward)</span>
$ kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443 &

<span class="comment"># 2. Get ArgoCD admin password</span>
$ ARGO_PASS=$(kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
    -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d)

<span class="comment"># 3. Login via CLI</span>
$ argocd login localhost:8080 --username admin --password $ARGO_PASS --insecure

<span class="comment"># 4. Set webhook on GitHub repo:</span>
<span class="comment">#    GitHub Repo → Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook</span>
<span class="comment">#    Payload URL: https://argocd.example.com/api/webhook</span>
<span class="comment">#    Content type: application/json</span>
<span class="comment">#    Events: Just the push event</span>
<span class="comment">#    Secret: &lt;generate-a-random-string&gt;</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Configure webhook secret in ArgoCD</span>
$ argocd admin settings rbac \
    --set argocd-secret.github-webhook-secret=&lt;random-string&gt;

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># TEST: Push to Git → ArgoCD syncs in &lt;5 seconds!</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>ArgoCD RBAC — Who Can Sync What?</h4>
                <p>ArgoCD has its own RBAC layer — separate from Kubernetes RBAC. It controls who can access the ArgoCD UI/CLI and what actions they can perform on specific apps:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">argocd-rbac-cm.yaml — ArgoCD RBAC Configuration</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: argocd-rbac-cm
  namespace: argocd
data:
  <span class="comment"># Default policy — deny all unless explicitly allowed</span>
  policy.default: role:none

  <span class="comment"># Scopes define permission granularity</span>
  scopes: '[groups, email]'

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># RBAC MATRIX — who can do what to which apps</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  policy.csv: |
    <span class="comment"># Syntax: p, &lt;subject&gt;, &lt;resource&gt;, &lt;action&gt;, &lt;object&gt;</span>
    <span class="comment"># ────────────────────────────────────────────────</span>
    <span class="comment"># TEAM BACKEND — full control over easypj</span>
    p, role:backend-team, applications, *, easypj/*, allow
    p, role:backend-team, logs, get, easypj/*, allow

    <span class="comment"># TEAM PLATFORM — read-only for all apps (monitoring)</span>
    p, role:platform-team, applications, get, *, allow

    <span class="comment"># CI BOT — sync-only (no manual access)</span>
    p, role:ci-bot, applications, sync, easypj/*, allow

    <span class="comment"># ADMIN — full control (use sparingly)</span>
    g, admin@company.com, role:admin

    <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════</span>
    <span class="comment"># SUBJECT RESOLUTION — SSO groups → roles</span>
    <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════</span>
    g, backend@yourorg.com, role:backend-team
    g, platform@yourorg.com, role:platform-team</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Sync Strategies — Automated, Manual, and Hybrid</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>ArgoCD Sync Strategy Comparison</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SYNC STRATEGIES                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  STRATEGY A: FULL AUTO-SYNC (dev / staging)                                  │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ syncPolicy:                                                     │        │
│  │   automated:                                                    │        │
│  │     prune: true        <span class="comment"># Remove resources deleted from Git      │        │</span>
│  │     selfHeal: true     <span class="comment"># Auto-fix manual kubectl changes       │        │</span>
│  │                                                                 │        │
│  │ Git push ──▶ Auto-sync ──▶ Cluster updated  (seconds)          │        │
│  │ Drift detected ──▶ Auto-heal ──▶ Back to Git state (seconds)   │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  STRATEGY B: MANUAL SYNC (production)                                        │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ syncPolicy: {}   <span class="comment"># Empty — no automated sync                    │        │</span>
│  │                                                                 │        │
│  │ Git push ──▶ ArgoCD shows OutOfSync ──▶ Human clicks Sync      │        │
│  │ Why? Release windows, change control, pre-sync approvals        │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  STRATEGY C: HYBRID (recommended for production)                             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ syncPolicy:                                                     │        │
│  │   automated:                                                    │        │
│  │     prune: false       <span class="comment"># NEVER auto-delete in prod              │        │</span>
│  │     selfHeal: true     <span class="comment"># Fix drift (someone ran kubectl)        │        │</span>
│  │                                                                 │        │
│  │ Result: Drift auto-fixed. Resource deletions require manual     │        │
│  │         approval (PR + Sync button). Safe for production!       │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  CRITICAL: selfHeal in production — if an incident response team        │
│      manually scales up a deployment (kubectl scale), selfHeal will         │
│      scale it BACK DOWN. In emergencies, pause ArgoCD first!               │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>ArgoCD Reconciliation Loop — Under the Hood</h4>
                <p>The <strong>application-controller</strong> is the brain of ArgoCD. It runs an infinite reconciliation loop that ensures cluster state matches Git state. Understanding this loop is key to debugging sync issues:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>ArgoCD Controller Reconciliation Loop (Deep Dive)</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ARGOCD APPLICATION CONTROLLER — RECONCILIATION LOOP                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Every 3 minutes (or on webhook):                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  PHASE 1: FETCH DESIRED STATE (from Git/Helm repo)                  │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ 1. Clone/pull Git repo (shallow clone, --depth=1)          │     │    │
│  │  │ 2. Checkout targetRevision (main branch, tag, or commit)   │     │    │
│  │  │ 3. If Helm: helm template → render YAML                     │     │    │
│  │  │ 4. If Kustomize: kustomize build → render YAML              │     │    │
│  │  │ 5. If plain YAML: read directory as-is                      │     │    │
│  │  │ 6. Result: list of K8s resource objects (desired state)    │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  PHASE 2: FETCH LIVE STATE (from cluster via K8s API)               │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ 1. kubectl get &lt;resource&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt; -o yaml           │     │    │
│  │  │ 2. For each desired resource, fetch live equivalent         │     │    │
│  │  │ 3. Cache live state in Redis for 3 min (reduces API load)  │     │    │
│  │  │ 4. Result: list of K8s resource objects (live state)       │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  PHASE 3: COMPARE (three-way diff)                                   │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ 1. Diff desired vs live YAML                                │     │    │
│  │  │ 2. Normalize both sides (strip runtime fields)              │     │    │
│  │  │ 3. Fields normalized out:                                   │     │    │
│  │  │    - metadata.generation, metadata.resourceVersion          │     │    │
│  │  │    - status.* (all status subfields)                        │     │    │
│  │  │    - spec.clusterIP (assigned by cluster)                   │     │    │
│  │  │ 4. Result: Synced (no diff) or OutOfSync (diff found)      │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │  PHASE 4: SYNC (if auto-sync or manual trigger)                     │    │
│  │  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │    │
│  │  │ 1. PreSync hooks execute (kubectl apply hook resources)   │     │    │
│  │  │ 2. Apply resources in wave order (0, 1, 2, ...)           │     │    │
│  │  │ 3. Each resource: kubectl apply -f &lt;resource&gt;              │     │    │
│  │  │ 4. If prune=true: delete resources in live but not in git │     │    │
│  │  │ 5. PostSync hooks execute                                   │     │    │
│  │  │ 6. Health assessment (check pod status, etc.)              │     │    │
│  │  │ 7. Result: Synced + Healthy/Degraded                       │     │    │
│  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │    │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  KEY INSIGHT: ArgoCD uses kubectl apply (declarative), not kubectl create    │
│  (imperative). This means:                                                   │
│  - apply is idempotent — running it twice = same result                     │
│  - apply uses last-applied-configuration annotation to track changes         │
│  - apply can update AND create resources                                     │
│  - Removing a field from Git → apply removes it from cluster               │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>ArgoCD Health Assessment — How Healthy/Degraded Is Determined</h4>
                <p>ArgoCD doesn't just check if a resource exists — it evaluates <strong>resource-specific health rules</strong> written in Lua. You can customize these rules for CRDs:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">argocd-cm.yaml — Custom health check for Django app CRD</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: argocd-cm
  namespace: argocd
data:
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># BUILT-IN HEALTH CHECKS (what ArgoCD does internally):</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># Deployment:  Healthy if .status.availableReplicas >= .spec.replicas</span>
  <span class="comment"># StatefulSet: Healthy if .status.readyReplicas == .spec.replicas</span>
  <span class="comment"># DaemonSet:   Healthy if .status.numberReady == .status.desiredNumberScheduled</span>
  <span class="comment"># Service:     Healthy if .spec.type != LoadBalancer OR .status.loadBalancer.ingress exists</span>
  <span class="comment"># Ingress:     Healthy if .status.loadBalancer.ingress exists</span>
  <span class="comment"># Pod:         Healthy if .status.phase == "Running"</span>
  <span class="comment"># Job:         Healthy if .status.succeeded >= 1</span>
  <span class="comment"># PersistentVolumeClaim: Healthy if .status.phase == "Bound"</span>

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># CUSTOM HEALTH CHECK for DjangoApp CRD (Lua script)</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  resource.customizations: |
    easypj.io/DjangoApp:
      health.lua: |
        hs = {}
        hs.status = "Progressing"
        hs.message = "Waiting for DjangoApp to be ready"
        
        if obj.status ~= nil then
          if obj.status.phase == "Running" then
            hs.status = "Healthy"
            hs.message = "DjangoApp is running"
            return hs
          elseif obj.status.phase == "Failed" then
            hs.status = "Degraded"
            hs.message = "DjangoApp failed: " .. (obj.status.reason or "unknown")
            return hs
          end
        end
        return hs</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Multi-Cluster ArgoCD — Hub-Spoke Pattern</h4>
                <p>In production, you run ArgoCD in a <strong>management cluster</strong> and have it deploy to <strong>multiple workload clusters</strong>. This is the hub-spoke pattern:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Hub-Spoke Architecture</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  MULTI-CLUSTER GITOPS — HUB-SPOKE PATTERN                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │                    MANAGEMENT CLUSTER (Hub)                           │   │
│  │                                                                       │   │
│  │  ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐   ┌──────────┐         │   │
│  │  │ ArgoCD   │   │ ArgoCD   │   │ Git Repo │   │   SSO    │         │   │
│  │  │ Server   │   │ App Ctlr │   │  Mirror  │   │ (OIDC)   │         │   │
│  │  └──────────┘   └─────┬────┘   └──────────┘   └──────────┘         │   │
│  │                       │                                               │   │
│  └───────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                          │                                                    │
│              ┌───────────┼───────────┐                                       │
│              │           │           │                                        │
│              ▼           ▼           ▼                                        │
│  ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐                         │
│  │  PROD-US-EAST │ │  PROD-EU-WEST│ │   STAGING    │   (Spoke Clusters)     │
│  │               │ │               │ │               │                         │
│  │ easypj v2.1   │ │ easypj v2.1   │ │ easypj v2.2   │                         │
│  │ 5 replicas    │ │ 5 replicas    │ │ 2 replicas    │                         │
│  └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘                         │
│                                                                              │
│  CONFIGURATION (on hub cluster):                                             │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Add spoke clusters to ArgoCD                                     │</span>     │
│  │ $ argocd cluster add prod-us-east-context   <span class="comment"># Adds kubeconfig       │     │</span>
│  │ $ argocd cluster add prod-eu-west-context                          │     │
│  │                                                                     │     │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Application YAML targets specific cluster:                       │</span>     │
│  │ spec:                                                               │     │
│  │   destination:                                                      │     │
│  │     server: https://prod-us-east-cluster.example.com               │     │
│  │     namespace: easypj                                               │     │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
│                                                                              │
│  BENEFITS:                                                                   │
│  ✅ One ArgoCD instance manages ALL clusters                                 │
│  ✅ Single pane of glass for multi-cluster deployments                       │
│  ✅ Spoke clusters have NO ArgoCD running (less resource usage)              │
│  ✅ If spoke cluster is down, hub still tracks desired state                 │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Diff Customization — Ignoring Irrelevant Differences</h4>
                <p>Sometimes ArgoCD reports OutOfSync for differences you don't care about (like HPA setting replicas at runtime). Configure diff customization to ignore these:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">argocd-cm.yaml — Ignore HPA replicas, mutating webhook annotations</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: argocd-cm
  namespace: argocd
data:
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># IGNORE FIELDS that change at runtime</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  resource.customizations.ignoreDifferences.all: |
    <span class="comment"># Ignore HPA-controlled replica count (HPA changes it dynamically)</span>
    jsonPointers:
    - /spec/replicas
    
  resource.customizations.ignoreDifferences.admissionregistration.k8s.io_MutatingWebhookConfiguration: |
    jsonPointers:
    - /webhooks/0/clientConfig/caBundle    <span class="comment"># Rotated by cert-manager</span>
    
  <span class="comment"># App-level overrides (in Application YAML):</span>
  <span class="comment"># spec:</span>
  <span class="comment">#   ignoreDifferences:</span>
  <span class="comment">#   - group: apps</span>
  <span class="comment">#     kind: Deployment</span>
  <span class="comment">#     jsonPointers:</span>
  <span class="comment">#     - /spec/replicas                    # Ignore HPA changes</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>ArgoCD Notifications — Alerting on Sync Failures</h4>
                <p>ArgoCD can send notifications to Slack, email, or webhooks when syncs succeed, fail, or health degrades:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">argocd-notifications-cm.yaml — Slack alerts for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: argocd-notifications-cm
  namespace: argocd
data:
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># SLACK INTEGRATION</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  service.slack: |
    token: $slack-token

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># TRIGGER: When to send notification</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  trigger.on-sync-failed: |
    - when: app.status.operationState.phase == 'Failed'
      send: [on-sync-failed-slack]
  trigger.on-sync-succeeded: |
    - when: app.status.operationState.phase == 'Succeeded'
      send: [on-sync-succeeded-slack]
  trigger.on-health-degraded: |
    - when: app.status.health.status == 'Degraded'
      send: [on-health-degraded-slack]

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># TEMPLATE: What the message looks like</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  template.on-sync-failed-slack: |
    message: |
      🚨 *Sync FAILED*: {{.app.metadata.name}}
      • Environment: {{.app.spec.destination.namespace}}
      • Revision: {{.app.status.sync.revision}}
      • Error: {{.app.status.operationState.message}}
      • Operator: {{.app.status.operationState.operation.initiatedBy.username}}
      • <{{.context.argocdUrl}}/applications/{{.app.metadata.name}}|View in ArgoCD>

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># SUBSCRIPTION: Which apps get notified</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  subscriptions: |
    - recipients:
      - slack:easypj-alerts
      triggers:
      - on-sync-failed
      - on-health-degraded
      selector: team=backend    <span class="comment"># Only apps with label team=backend</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.3 ArgoCD for easypj — Practical Setup</h3>
                <p>Here's a complete walkthrough of setting up ArgoCD to deploy easypj using GitOps.</p>

                <h4>Step 1: Install ArgoCD</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — install ArgoCD</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create namespace and install ArgoCD</span>
$ kubectl create namespace argocd
$ kubectl apply -n argocd \
    -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml

<span class="comment"># Wait for all pods to be ready</span>
$ kubectl -n argocd get pods -w
NAME                                  READY   STATUS    RESTARTS
argocd-server-xxxxx                   1/1     Running   0
argocd-repo-server-xxxxx              1/1     Running   0
argocd-application-controller-xxxxx   1/1     Running   0
argocd-redis-xxxxx                    1/1     Running   0

<span class="comment"># Get the initial admin password</span>
$ kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
    -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d

<span class="comment"># Access the UI (port-forward for local access)</span>
$ kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Step 2: Create ArgoCD Application for easypj</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-argocd-app.yaml — ArgoCD Application</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: easypj
  namespace: argocd
spec:
  project: default
  source:
    repoURL: https://github.com/youruser/easypj-k8s-manifests
    targetRevision: main          <span class="comment"># Track main branch</span>
    path: overlays/production     <span class="comment"># Kustomize overlay path</span>
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: easypj
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true                 <span class="comment"># Delete resources removed from Git</span>
      selfHeal: true              <span class="comment"># Fix manual changes (drift)</span>
    syncOptions:
    - CreateNamespace=true        <span class="comment"># Auto-create namespace</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>easypj GitOps Workflow — Day-to-Day</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DAILY GITOPS WORKFLOW FOR easypj                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  1. Developer updates easypj code                                            │
│     └── CI builds new image: easypj/web:v2.1                                │
│                                                                              │
│  2. Developer updates manifest repo (PR)                                     │
│     └── Change: image: easypj/web:v2.0 → image: easypj/web:v2.1           │
│                                                                              │
│  3. PR reviewed &amp; merged to main                                           │
│     └── Git log: "Bump easypj to v2.1 — fix login bug"                     │
│                                                                              │
│  4. ArgoCD detects change (3 min polling or webhook)                        │
│     └── Status: OutOfSync → Syncing → Synced ✅                              │
│                                                                              │
│  5. ArgoCD applies changes to cluster                                        │
│     └── Rolling update: v2.0 pods → v2.1 pods                              │
│                                                                              │
│  6. Something wrong? Rollback in seconds!                                    │
│     └── $ git revert HEAD &amp;&amp; git push                                     │
│     └── ArgoCD syncs back to v2.0 ✅                                         │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>ArgoCD Troubleshooting — Common Issues</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — ArgoCD debugging commands</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># CHECK APPLICATION STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ argocd app get easypj
Name:               easypj
Project:            default
Server:             https://kubernetes.default.svc
Namespace:          easypj
Sync Status:        OutOfSync     ← Drift detected!
Health Status:      Degraded       ← Something's wrong

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># SEE WHAT'S DIFFERENT (drift detection)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ argocd app diff easypj
===== apps/Deployment easypj/easypj-web ======
  replicas: 3               <span class="comment"># Git says 3</span>
- replicas: 5               <span class="comment"># Cluster has 5 (someone used kubectl scale!)</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># FORCE SYNC (override manual changes)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ argocd app sync easypj --force --prune

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># CHECK SYNC HISTORY</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ argocd app history easypj
ID  DATE                  REVISION
1   2024-03-10 09:00:00   abc1234 (Deploy v2.0)
2   2024-03-12 14:30:00   def5678 (Fix login bug)
3   2024-03-15 11:00:00   ghi9012 (Scale to 3 replicas)

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ROLLBACK TO PREVIOUS SYNC</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ argocd app rollback easypj 2    <span class="comment"># Go back to revision 2</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>ArgoCD Status Cheat Sheet</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  ARGOCD STATUS REFERENCE                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  SYNC STATUS:                                                                │
│  ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Synced     ✅    │ Cluster matches Git — everything is good         │    │
│  │ OutOfSync  ⚠️    │ Cluster differs from Git — needs sync            │    │
│  │ Unknown    ❓    │ ArgoCD can't determine state                      │    │
│  └──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  HEALTH STATUS:                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ Healthy    💚    │ All resources are running correctly               │    │
│  │ Progressing ⏳   │ Resource is updating (rolling update in progress) │    │
│  │ Degraded   🟡    │ Resource is unhealthy (CrashLoop, pending)       │    │
│  │ Suspended  ⏸️    │ Resource is suspended (scaled to 0)              │    │
│  │ Missing    ❌    │ Resource exists in Git but not in cluster         │    │
│  └──────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
│  COMMON TROUBLESHOOTING:                                                     │
│  ┌────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ OutOfSync + Healthy        │ Someone used kubectl directly —    │        │
│  │                            │ sync to restore Git state          │        │
│  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤        │
│  │ Synced + Degraded          │ Git state applied but pods         │        │
│  │                            │ crashing — check image/config      │        │
│  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤        │
│  │ OutOfSync + Missing        │ New resource in Git not yet        │        │
│  │                            │ synced — run sync                  │        │
│  ├────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤        │
│  │ Unknown + Unknown          │ ArgoCD can't reach repo or         │        │
│  │                            │ cluster — check credentials        │        │
│  └────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Sync Waves &amp; Hooks — Controlling Deployment Order</h4>
                <p>When deploying easypj, order matters: ConfigMap before Deployment, Deployment before HPA, migrate DB before new pods start. Sync waves and hooks let you enforce this:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">YAML — Sync waves and hooks for ordered deployment</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># WAVE 0: Namespace first</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: easypj
  annotations:
    argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: "0"    <span class="comment"># Created FIRST</span>

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># WAVE 1: ConfigMaps and Secrets (before pods)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: "1"    <span class="comment"># Created SECOND</span>
data:
  DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE: "easypj.settings.production"

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># HOOK: PreSync — Run DB migrations before deploy</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-migrate
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    argocd.argoproj.io/hook: PreSync      <span class="comment"># Runs BEFORE sync</span>
    argocd.argoproj.io/hook-delete-policy: HookSucceeded  <span class="comment"># Clean up after</span>
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: migrate
        image: ghcr.io/youruser/easypj-web:v2.1
        command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate", "--noinput"]

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># WAVE 2: Deployments (after configs)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: "2"    <span class="comment"># Created THIRD</span>

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># HOOK: PostSync — Run health check (smoke test)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-smoke-test
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    argocd.argoproj.io/hook: PostSync     <span class="comment"># Runs AFTER sync</span>
    argocd.argoproj.io/hook-delete-policy: HookSucceeded
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: test
        image: curlimages/curl:latest
        command: ["sh", "-c"]
        args:
        - |
          <span class="comment"># Wait for service, then check health</span>
          sleep 5
          curl -f http://easypj-web.easypj:8000/health/ || exit 1
          echo "✅ easypj is healthy!"</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Sync Wave Execution Order</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  SYNC ORDER (single sync operation):                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Phase 1: PreSync hooks execute                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────┐                                                        │
│  │ DB migration Job  │  → python manage.py migrate                          │
│  └──────┬───────────┘                                                        │
│         │                                                                     │
│  Phase 2: Apply resources in wave order (lowest to highest)                  │
│         ▼                                                                     │
│  Wave 0  Wave 1       Wave 2        Wave 3                                   │
│  ┌────┐  ┌─────────┐  ┌──────────┐  ┌───────┐                               │
│  │ NS │→│ConfigMap│→│Deployment│→│  HPA  │                               │
│  │    │  │ Secrets │  │ Service  │  │  PDB  │                               │
│  └────┘  └─────────┘  └──────────┘  └───────┘                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Phase 3: PostSync hooks execute                                             │
│  ┌──────────────────┐                                                        │
│  │ Smoke test Job    │  → curl /health/ → ✅ or ❌                           │
│  └──────────────────┘                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  If PostSync hook FAILS → Sync marked as Failed (but resources stay)        │
│  If PostSync hook SUCCEEDS → Sync = Synced &amp; Healthy                        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>AppProject — Organizing Multiple Apps with Guardrails</h4>
                <p>The <code>Application</code> resources live in <code>argocd</code> namespace, but the <strong>AppProject</strong> defines what destinations and sources are allowed — a crucial security boundary:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-appproject.yaml — Scoped deployment boundaries</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: AppProject
metadata:
  name: easypj-team
  namespace: argocd
spec:
  description: "easypj application project — backend team"

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># ALLOWED SOURCES — which Git repos / chart repos</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  sourceRepos:
  - https://github.com/youruser/easypj-k8s-manifests
  - https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami   <span class="comment"># Allowed Helm charts</span>

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># ALLOWED DESTINATIONS — which clusters / namespaces</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  destinations:
  - namespace: easypj
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
  - namespace: easypj-staging
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># ALLOWED RESOURCE KINDS — deny dangerous types</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  clusterResourceWhitelist:
  - group: ""
    kind: Namespace
  - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
    kind: ClusterRole

  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  <span class="comment"># DENY SPECIFIC RESOURCES (blacklist)</span>
  <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
  namespaceResourceBlacklist:
  - group: ""
    kind: ResourceQuota    <span class="comment"># Don't let apps set cluster quotas</span>
  - group: "policy"
    kind: PodSecurityPolicy</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.4 Helm — Kubernetes Package Manager</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <p>Helm packages Kubernetes manifests into <strong>charts</strong> — reusable, versioned, configurable bundles. Think of it as <code>apt</code> or <code>pip</code> for Kubernetes. Values files let you customize the same chart for dev, staging, and production.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Helm Chart Structure — easypj Example</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HELM CHART DIRECTORY STRUCTURE                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  easypj-chart/                                                               │
│  ├── Chart.yaml              <span class="comment"># Chart metadata (name, version, description)  │</span>
│  ├── values.yaml             <span class="comment"># Default configuration values                  │</span>
│  ├── values-prod.yaml        <span class="comment"># Production overrides                          │</span>
│  ├── templates/                                                              │
│  │   ├── _helpers.tpl        <span class="comment"># Template helper functions                     │</span>
│  │   ├── deployment.yaml     # {{ .Values.replicas }}                       │
│  │   ├── service.yaml        # {{ .Values.serviceType }}                    │
│  │   ├── ingress.yaml        # {{ .Values.host }}                           │
│  │   ├── configmap.yaml      # {{ .Values.dbHost }}                         │
│  │   ├── secret.yaml         # {{ .Values.dbPassword }}                     │
│  │   └── hpa.yaml            # {{ .Values.autoscaling.enabled }}            │
│  └── charts/                 <span class="comment"># Sub-chart dependencies                        │</span>
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│                                                                              │
│  values.yaml (defaults):       values-prod.yaml (overrides):                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐   ┌─────────────────────────┐                  │
│  │ replicas: 1              │   │ replicas: 3              │                  │
│  │ image:                   │   │ image:                   │                  │
│  │   tag: latest            │   │   tag: v2.1-stable       │                  │
│  │ serviceType: ClusterIP   │   │ serviceType: LoadBalancer│                  │
│  │ resources:               │   │ resources:               │                  │
│  │   requests:              │   │   requests:              │                  │
│  │     cpu: 100m            │   │     cpu: 500m            │                  │
│  │     memory: 128Mi        │   │     memory: 512Mi        │                  │
│  │ ingress:                 │   │ ingress:                 │                  │
│  │   enabled: false         │   │   enabled: true          │                  │
│  │   host: ""               │   │   host: easypj.com      │                  │
│  └─────────────────────────┘   └─────────────────────────┘                  │
│                                                                              │
│  Same chart, different values → different environments! 🎯                  │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Helm Essential Commands</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — Helm workflow</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># CHART REPOSITORIES</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
$ helm repo update
$ helm search repo nginx

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># INSTALL / UPGRADE / ROLLBACK</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Install easypj chart with production values</span>
$ helm install easypj ./easypj-chart --values values-prod.yaml -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Upgrade (change image tag to v2.2)</span>
$ helm upgrade easypj ./easypj-chart --values values-prod.yaml \
    --set image.tag=v2.2 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Oops! Rollback to previous release</span>
$ helm rollback easypj 1 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># INSPECT / DEBUG</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
$ helm list -A                    <span class="comment"># List all releases</span>
$ helm history easypj -n easypj   <span class="comment"># Release history</span>
$ helm get values easypj -n easypj  <span class="comment"># Current values</span>
$ helm template ./easypj-chart    <span class="comment"># Render without installing (dry-run)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Helm Go Template Syntax — Crash Course</h4>
                <p>Helm templates use Go's <code>text/template</code> language. Here's how a <strong>real Helm deployment template</strong> looks for easypj:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">templates/deployment.yaml — Helm Go template syntax</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  <span class="comment"># {{ .Release.Name }} = helm install NAME → "easypj"</span>
  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-web
  labels:
    {{- include "easypj.labels" . | nindent 4 }}
spec:
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicas | default 1 }}
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: {{ .Release.Name }}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: {{ .Release.Name }}
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: {{ .Chart.Name }}
        image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"
        ports:
        - containerPort: {{ .Values.containerPort | default 8000 }}
        {{- if .Values.resources }}
        resources:
          {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 10 }}
        {{- end }}
        {{- if .Values.env }}
        env:
        {{- range .Values.env }}
        - name: {{ .name }}
          value: {{ .value | quote }}
        {{- end }}
        {{- end }}
      {{- if .Values.nodeSelector }}
      nodeSelector:
        {{- toYaml .Values.nodeSelector | nindent 8 }}
      {{- end }}

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># GO TEMPLATE CHEAT SHEET:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ .Values.xxx }}           → Read from values.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ .Release.Name }}         → Helm release name</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ .Chart.Name }}           → From Chart.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ .Release.Namespace }}    → Target namespace</span>
<span class="comment"># {{- ... }}                  → Trim left whitespace</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ ... -}}                  → Trim right whitespace</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ if .Values.x }}...{{ end }}  → Conditional</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ range .Values.list }}        → Loop</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ include "tpl" . }}           → Include named template</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ toYaml .Values.x | nindent 4 }} → Render as YAML</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ .Values.x | default "val" }}     → Default value</span>
<span class="comment"># {{ .Values.x | quote }}        → Wrap in quotes</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Helm Debugging Tips</h5>
                    <p>Template not rendering correctly? Use <code>helm template ./chart --debug</code> to see the rendered YAML. Common errors: missing <code>{{- }}</code> whitespace control causes extra blank lines, and forgetting <code>| nindent N</code> causes YAML indentation errors. Always validate with <code>helm lint ./chart</code> before installing.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Helm Chart Dependencies — Composing the Stack</h4>
                <p>Helm charts can depend on other charts. For easypj to run, it needs PostgreSQL and Redis. Instead of creating them manually, declare them as dependencies:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Chart.yaml — easypj with sub-chart dependencies</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v2
name: easypj
description: A Helm chart for easypj Django application
type: application
version: 1.0.0
appVersion: "2.1.0"

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># CHART DEPENDENCIES (installed automatically)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
dependencies:
- name: postgresql
  version: "15.x.x"
  repository: https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
  condition: postgresql.enabled      <span class="comment"># Can toggle via --set postgresql.enabled=false</span>
  alias: easypj-db                   <span class="comment"># Referenced as .Values.easypj-db in templates</span>

- name: redis
  version: "19.x.x"
  repository: https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
  condition: redis.enabled
  alias: easypj-cache

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># DOWNLOAD DEPENDENCIES</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># $ helm dependency update    # Downloads charts/ directory</span>
<span class="comment"># $ helm dependency build     # Builds from Chart.lock</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Dependency Benefits for CKA Context</h5>
                    <p>When you <code>helm install easypj ./chart</code>, Helm <strong>automatically installs PostgreSQL and Redis first</strong>, then easypj. This mirrors what Kubernetes operators do — one command stands up an entire multi-tier application. The CKA may test your ability to <strong>troubleshoot multi-tier deployments</strong>, where knowing dependency order (DB before app) saves time diagnosing CrashLoopBackOff.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Helm Hooks — Controlling Install/Upgrade Lifecycle</h4>
                <p>Just like ArgoCD hooks, Helm hooks run Jobs at specific lifecycle stages. Common use: run Django database migrations <strong>before</strong> the new deployment starts:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">templates/hook-db-migrate.yaml — Helm pre-upgrade hook</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-db-migrate
  annotations:
    <span class="comment"># Run BEFORE upgrade (migrate DB, then start new pods)</span>
    "helm.sh/hook": pre-upgrade
    <span class="comment"># Delete job after it succeeds (no clutter)</span>
    "helm.sh/hook-delete-policy": hook-succeeded
    <span class="comment"># Job must complete before deployment rolls out</span>
    "helm.sh/hook-weight": "0"
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
      - name: migrate
        image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"
        command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate", "--noinput"]

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># HELM HOOK REFERENCE:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># pre-install      → Before chart is first installed</span>
<span class="comment"># post-install     → After chart is first installed</span>
<span class="comment"># pre-delete       → Before resources are deleted</span>
<span class="comment"># post-delete      → After resources are deleted</span>
<span class="comment"># pre-upgrade      → Before an upgrade (release exists)</span>
<span class="comment"># post-upgrade     → After an upgrade</span>
<span class="comment"># pre-rollback     → Before a rollback</span>
<span class="comment"># post-rollback    → After a rollback</span>
<span class="comment"># test             → When `helm test` is run</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># HOOK DELETE POLICIES:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># hook-succeeded   → Delete after success (cleanup)</span>
<span class="comment"># hook-failed      → Delete after failure (leave for debugging)</span>
<span class="comment"># before-hook-creation → Delete previous hook before running again</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Helm Test — Verifying a Release</h4>
                <p>Helm supports built-in test pods that validate a deployment is working. The test runs in its own pod and reports success/failure without affecting production traffic:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">templates/tests/test-health.yaml — Automated smoke test</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: "{{ .Release.Name }}-health-test"
  annotations:
    "helm.sh/hook": test        <span class="comment"># Only runs during `helm test`</span>
    "helm.sh/hook-delete-policy": hook-succeeded
spec:
  restartPolicy: Never
  containers:
  - name: test
    image: curlimages/curl:latest
    command: ["sh", "-c"]
    args:
    - |
      STATUS=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" \
        http://{{ .Release.Name }}-web.{{ .Release.Namespace }}:8000/health/)
      if [ "$STATUS" != "200" ]; then
        echo "❌ Health check failed: HTTP $STATUS"
        exit 1
      fi
      echo "✅ easypj is healthy (HTTP $STATUS)"

<span class="comment"># Usage:</span>
<span class="comment"># $ helm install easypj ./chart</span>
<span class="comment"># $ helm test easypj              # Run the test pod</span>
<span class="comment"># NAME: easypj-health-test</span>
<span class="comment"># STATUS: Succeeded</span>
<span class="comment"># ✅ easypj is healthy (HTTP 200)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Helm Release Storage — Where Helm Keeps State</h4>
                <p>Helm doesn't have a database. It stores release history as <strong>Kubernetes Secrets</strong> (or ConfigMaps in Helm 2). Every install, upgrade, or rollback creates a new versioned Secret:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Helm Release Storage Internals</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HELM RELEASE STORAGE — VERSIONED SECRETS                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  $ helm install easypj ./chart -n easypj                                     │
│  → Creates Secret: sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v1                              │
│                                                                              │
│  $ helm upgrade easypj ./chart --set replicas=3 -n easypj                    │
│  → Creates Secret: sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v2                              │
│                                                                              │
│  $ helm upgrade easypj ./chart --set image.tag=v2.2 -n easypj                │
│  → Creates Secret: sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v3                              │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ $ kubectl get secrets -n easypj -l owner=helm                   │        │
│  │ NAME                               TYPE     DATA   AGE            │        │
│  │ sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v1       helm.sh/release.v1   1   2d   │        │
│  │ sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v2       helm.sh/release.v1   1   1d   │        │
│  │ sh.helm.release.v1.easypj.v3       helm.sh/release.v1   1   2h   │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  WHAT'S INSIDE EACH SECRET:                                                  │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ $ helm get manifest easypj --revision 2                         │        │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Shows the EXACT YAML that was applied at revision 2           │</span>        │
│  │                                                                  │        │
│  │ $ helm get values easypj --revision 2                            │        │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Shows the values used at revision 2                            │</span>        │
│  │                                                                  │        │
│  │ $ helm get hooks easypj --revision 2                             │        │
│  │ <span class="comment"># Shows any hooks that ran at revision 2                         │</span>        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│                                                                              │
│  RELEASE STORAGE LIMITS:                                                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ • Default: keeps last 256 revisions (configurable with          │        │
│  │   --history-max flag)                                            │        │
│  │ • Each secret is ~10KB (base64+gzip encoded manifest)           │        │
│  │ • 256 revisions × ~10KB = ~2.5MB per release                    │        │
│  │ • 100 releases × 2.5MB = 250MB of cluster storage               │        │
│  │ • Tip: $ helm upgrade --history-max 10  <span class="comment"># Keep only 10 revs     │        │</span>
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  ⚠️  CRITICAL: Helm 3 uses Secrets (base64, not encrypted). Anyone with     │
│      kubectl get secrets access can read ALL your Helm values including     │
│      passwords! Use sealed-secrets or external-secrets for sensitive data.  │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Helm Rendering Pipeline — From Chart to YAML Step-by-Step</h4>
                <p>Understanding the exact sequence Helm follows when rendering templates is essential for debugging. Here's the complete pipeline:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>The Helm Template Rendering Pipeline</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HELM RENDERING PIPELINE (what `helm template` does internally)              │
│                                                                              │
│  INPUT:                                                                       │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ 1. values.yaml (default values)                                   │       │
│  │ 2. --values values-prod.yaml (user overrides)                     │       │
│  │ 3. --set replicas=5 (command-line overrides, HIGHEST priority)    │       │
│  │ 4. Chart.yaml (metadata about this chart)                         │       │
│  │ 5. templates/*.yaml (Go template files)                           │       │
│  │ 6. charts/* (dependency charts, if any)                           │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 1: VALUE MERGING (from lowest to highest priority)                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ Priority (lowest → highest):                                      │       │
│  │   values.yaml  →  parent chart values  →  --values file          │       │
│  │   → --set key=val  →  --set-string key=val                       │       │
│  │                                                                    │       │
│  │ Example:                                                          │       │
│  │   values.yaml:        replicas: 1                                 │       │
│  │   values-prod.yaml:   replicas: 3                                 │       │
│  │   --set replicas=5                                                │       │
│  │   → Final: replicas = 5 (--set wins)                             │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 2: BUILD RENDER CONTEXT (the "." object)                                │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ The "." passed to templates contains:                             │       │
│  │                                                                    │       │
│  │ .Values          → merged values from step 1                      │       │
│  │ .Release          → release metadata:                             │       │
│  │   .Name           → "easypj" (helm install NAME)                  │       │
│  │   .Namespace      → "easypj" (-n flag)                            │       │
│  │   .Revision       → 1, 2, 3, ... (auto-incrementing)             │       │
│  │   .IsInstall      → true on first install                         │       │
│  │   .IsUpgrade       → true on upgrades                             │       │
│  │ .Chart            → Chart.yaml contents                           │       │
│  │   .Name            → "easypj"                                     │       │
│  │   .Version         → "1.0.0"                                      │       │
│  │   .AppVersion      → "2.1.0"                                      │       │
│  │ .Capabilities      → K8s cluster capabilities:                   │       │
│  │   .KubeVersion     → "1.30" (server version)                      │       │
│  │   .APIVersions     → all available API versions                   │       │
│  │ .Template          → current template info                        │       │
│  │   .Name            → "easypj/templates/deployment.yaml"           │       │
│  │   .BasePath        → path to templates/ directory                 │       │
│  │ .Files             → access to non-template files in chart/       │       │
│  │ .Library           → library chart utilities                      │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 3: TEMPLATE EXECUTION (for each file in templates/)                    │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ For each file in templates/ (NOT in templates/NOTES.txt):         │       │
│  │                                                                    │       │
│  │ a. Parse file as Go text/template                                 │       │
│  │ b. Execute template with merged context "."                        │       │
│  │ c. Apply Sprig template functions (60+ functions)                  │       │
│  │ d. Collect output (rendered YAML string)                           │       │
│  │                                                                    │       │
│  │ Special files:                                                    │       │
│  │   templates/NOTES.txt → rendered AFTER install (helm status)      │       │
│  │   templates/tests/    → only executed during `helm test`          │       │
│  │   templates/_*.tpl    → partials, NOT rendered directly           │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 4: YAML PARSING & VALIDATION                                           │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ a. Split multi-document YAML (--- separator)                      │       │
│  │ b. Parse each document as K8s resource                            │       │
│  │ c. Validate required fields: apiVersion, kind, metadata.name      │       │
│  │ d. If validation fails → ERROR (with template file + line)        │       │
│  │ e. helm lint does extra: checks for deprecated APIs               │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 5: RESOURCE ORDERING (for apply)                                       │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
│  │ Helm applies resources in this order (Helm's built-in sort):      │       │
│  │                                                                    │       │
│  │ 1. Namespace        6. Deployment                                 │       │
│  │ 2. NetworkPolicy     7. StatefulSet                               │       │
│  │ 3. ResourceQuota     8. DaemonSet                                 │       │
│  │ 4. LimitRange        9. Job                                       │       │
│  │ 5. PodSecurityPolicy 10. CronJob                                  │       │
│  │ 11. Secret/ConfigMap 12. Service                                  │       │
│  │ 13. Ingress          14. PVC                                      │       │
│  │ 15. ServiceAccount   16. Role/RoleBinding                         │       │
│  │ 17. ClusterRole/CRB  18. Hooks run at specific lifecycle points   │       │
│  │                                                                    │       │
│  │ ⚠️  Helm's built-in ordering is NOT always correct for your app. │       │
│  │     Use hook weights (helm.sh/hook-weight: "0") to control order. │       │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Chart.lock — Reproducible Deployments</h4>
                <p>Just like <code>package-lock.json</code> or <code>go.sum</code>, Helm's <code>Chart.lock</code> pins exact dependency versions for reproducible builds:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Chart.lock — Pinned dependency versions</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">dependencies:
- name: postgresql
  repository: https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
  version: 15.5.20          <span class="comment"># EXACT version (from Chart.yaml: "15.x.x")</span>
- name: redis
  repository: https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
  version: 19.6.2            <span class="comment"># EXACT version (from Chart.yaml: "19.x.x")</span>
digest: sha256:8f9e7d6c5b4a3e2d1f0e9d8c7b6a5f4e3d2c1b0a9e8f7d6c5b4a3e2d1f0e9

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Why Chart.lock matters:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Without lock: helm dependency update may fetch 15.5.21 (patch bump)</span>
<span class="comment">#                → Could introduce bugs from dependency change</span>
<span class="comment"># With lock:    helm dependency build uses EXACT versions from lock</span>
<span class="comment">#                → Reproducible across CI, dev laptops, production</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Rule: COMMIT Chart.lock to Git. It's generated code but critical</span>
<span class="comment">#       for reproducibility. CI should fail if lock is out of date.</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.5 Kustomize — Built-in Manifest Customization</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <p>Kustomize is <strong>built into kubectl</strong> — no separate binary needed. It uses a <strong>base + overlays</strong> pattern: write your YAML once (base), and patch it per environment (overlays). No templates, no Go syntax — just strategic merge patches on plain YAML.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Kustomize Directory Structure — easypj</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUSTOMIZE BASE + OVERLAYS PATTERN                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  easypj-kustomize/                                                           │
│  ├── base/                          <span class="comment"># Shared across ALL environments         │</span>
│  │   ├── kustomization.yaml         <span class="comment"># Lists resources to include             │</span>
│  │   ├── deployment.yaml            <span class="comment"># easypj base: replicas: 1              │</span>
│  │   ├── service.yaml               <span class="comment"># ClusterIP service                     │</span>
│  │   └── configmap.yaml             <span class="comment"># Shared config values                  │</span>
│  │                                                                           │
│  └── overlays/                      <span class="comment"># Per-environment patches                │</span>
│      ├── dev/                                                                │
│      │   ├── kustomization.yaml     <span class="comment"># bases: [../../base]                   │</span>
│      │   └── patch-replicas.yaml    <span class="comment"># replicas: 1, cpu: 100m               │</span>
│      │                                                                       │
│      ├── staging/                                                            │
│      │   ├── kustomization.yaml     <span class="comment"># bases: [../../base]                   │</span>
│      │   └── patch-resources.yaml   <span class="comment"># replicas: 2, cpu: 250m               │</span>
│      │                                                                       │
│      └── production/                                                         │
│          ├── kustomization.yaml     <span class="comment"># bases: [../../base]                   │</span>
│          ├── patch-replicas.yaml    <span class="comment"># replicas: 5                           │</span>
│          └── patch-resources.yaml   <span class="comment"># cpu: 500m, memory: 512Mi             │</span>
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│                                                                              │
│  Deploy:   $ kubectl apply -k overlays/production/                           │
│  Preview:  $ kubectl kustomize overlays/production/                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Result: base/deployment.yaml MERGED with production patches                 │
│  → replicas: 5, cpu: 500m, memory: 512Mi                                    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Kustomize Files for easypj</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">base/kustomization.yaml &amp; overlays/production/kustomization.yaml</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># base/kustomization.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
- deployment.yaml
- service.yaml
- configmap.yaml
commonLabels:
  app: easypj

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># overlays/production/kustomization.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
- ../../base
namePrefix: prod-              <span class="comment"># All resources get prod- prefix</span>
namespace: easypj-production
patches:
- path: patch-replicas.yaml
  target:
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web
images:
- name: easypj/web
  newTag: v2.1-stable          <span class="comment"># Override image tag for prod</span>

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># overlays/production/patch-replicas.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  replicas: 5                  <span class="comment"># Production: 5 replicas</span>
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "500m"
            memory: "512Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "1000m"
            memory: "1Gi"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kustomize Power Features — Generators &amp; Transformers</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                                                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                            <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kustomization.yaml — Generators, labels, and config management</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
- ../../base

namespace: easypj-production

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># CONFIGMAP GENERATOR — auto-creates ConfigMaps from files/literals</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
configMapGenerator:
- name: easypj-config
  literals:
  - DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=easypj.settings.production
  - ALLOWED_HOSTS=easypj.com
  - DEBUG=false

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># SECRET GENERATOR — auto-creates Secrets</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
secretGenerator:
- name: easypj-db-creds
  literals:
  - DB_PASSWORD=supersecret   <span class="comment"># In real life: use sealed-secrets or external-secrets</span>
  type: Opaque

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># COMMON LABELS — applied to ALL resources</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
commonLabels:
  app: easypj
  env: production
  team: backend

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># COMMON ANNOTATIONS</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
commonAnnotations:
  managed-by: kustomize

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># IMAGE TRANSFORMER — override image tags</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
images:
- name: easypj/web
  newName: ghcr.io/youruser/easypj-web
  newTag: v2.1-stable

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># PATCHES — strategic merge or JSON patches</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
patches:
- path: patch-replicas.yaml
  target:
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-practice-drill">
                    <h5>🏋️ Practice Drill — Kustomize Multi-Environment Deploy</h5>
                    <div class="drill-scenario">
                        <p><strong>Scenario:</strong> Deploy easypj to staging and production using Kustomize overlays with different configurations.</p>
                        <ol>
                            <li>Preview what Kustomize will generate for production:<br><code>kubectl kustomize overlays/production/</code></li>
                            <li>Apply the production overlay:<br><code>kubectl apply -k overlays/production/</code></li>
                            <li>Verify resources were created in the correct namespace:<br><code>kubectl get all -n easypj-production</code></li>
                            <li>Check that labels were applied correctly:<br><code>kubectl get deploy -n easypj-production --show-labels</code></li>
                            <li>Verify the image tag matches the overlay:<br><code>kubectl get deploy easypj-web -n easypj-production -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'</code></li>
                            <li>Compare with staging config:<br><code>diff &lt;(kubectl kustomize overlays/staging/) &lt;(kubectl kustomize overlays/production/)</code></li>
                        </ol>
                        <p><strong>Key point:</strong> Same base YAML, but <code>-k</code> flag applies environment-specific patches. This is DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) for Kubernetes!</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-exam-tip">
                    <h5>🎯 CKA Exam — Kustomize</h5>
                    <p>The CKA exam may reference Kustomize in the exam environment. Know these two commands cold: <code>kubectl apply -k &lt;directory&gt;</code> applies Kustomize configuration, and <code>kubectl kustomize &lt;directory&gt;</code> previews without applying. The <code>-k</code> flag is your friend!</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Replacements — Cross-Resource Value Injection (Substitutions)</h4>
                <p>Kustomize <strong>replacements</strong> (replacing the deprecated <code>vars</code>) let you inject values from one resource into another — like taking a Service's ClusterIP and putting it into a ConfigMap:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kustomization.yaml — Replacements for cross-resource references</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># USE CASE: Inject Service name into Django ALLOWED_HOSTS</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization

resources:
- ../../base

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># REPLACEMENT: Take Service name → inject into ConfigMap</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
replacements:
- source:
    kind: Service
    name: easypj-web
    fieldPath: metadata.name        <span class="comment"># Source: "prod-easypj-web"</span>
  targets:
  - select:
      kind: ConfigMap
      name: easypj-config
    fieldPaths:
    - data.ANIHU_SERVICE_NAME       <span class="comment"># Target: ANIHU_SERVICE_NAME=prod-easypj-web</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># RESULT (rendered output):</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># apiVersion: v1</span>
<span class="comment"># kind: ConfigMap</span>
<span class="comment"># metadata:</span>
<span class="comment">#   name: easypj-config</span>
<span class="comment"># data:</span>
<span class="comment">#   ANIHU_SERVICE_NAME: prod-easypj-web    ← Injected from Service!</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ANOTHER EXAMPLE: Inject namespace into deployment env</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
replacements:
- source:
    kind: Namespace
    name: easypj-production
    fieldPath: metadata.name
  targets:
  - select:
      kind: Deployment
      name: easypj-web
    fieldPaths:
    - spec.template.spec.containers.[name=easypj].env.[name=K8S_NAMESPACE].value</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Patch Types — Strategic Merge vs JSON Patch (6902)</h4>
                <p>Kustomize supports two patch strategies. Know when to use each:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Strategic Merge vs JSON Patch — Comparison</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  PATCH TYPE COMPARISON                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  STRATEGIC MERGE PATCH (default for patches:):                               │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ File: patch-replicas.yaml                                       │        │
│  │ apiVersion: apps/v1                                              │        │
│  │ kind: Deployment                                                 │        │
│  │ metadata:                                                        │        │
│  │   name: easypj-web                                              │        │
│  │ spec:                                                            │        │
│  │   replicas: 5         ← Merged: ONLY this field changes         │        │
│  │                                                                  │        │
│  │ ✅ K8s-aware: knows list merge strategy for containers, ports   │        │
│  │ ✅ Simple: just write the YAML you want to override             │        │
│  │ ⚠️  Can't remove fields (set to null → ignored)                 │        │
│  │ ⚠️  Can't rename keys                                           │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  JSON PATCH (RFC 6902) — patchesJson6902:                                     │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ - target:                                                       │        │
│  │     kind: Deployment                                             │        │
│  │     name: easypj-web                                           │        │
│  │   patch: |-                                                     │        │
│  │     - op: replace                                               │        │
│  │       path: /spec/replicas                                      │        │
│  │       value: 5                                                  │        │
│  │     - op: add                                                   │        │
│  │       path: /spec/template/metadata/labels/new-label            │        │
│  │       value: "true"                                            │        │
│  │     - op: remove                                                │        │
│  │       path: /spec/template/spec/containers/0/resources/limits   │        │
│  │                                                                  │        │
│  │ ✅ Precise: add, replace, remove, copy, move, test operations   │        │
│  │ ✅ Can REMOVE fields (strategic merge can't)                    │        │
│  │ ⚠️  Verbose: path syntax is tedious                            │        │
│  │ ⚠️  Not K8s-aware: need exact JSON paths                        │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  RULE OF THUMB:                                                              │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐         │
│  │ Use strategic merge 90% of the time (simpler, K8s-aware)      │         │
│  │ Use JSON patch when you need to REMOVE a field or rename keys │         │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘         │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Kustomize Components — Reusable, Composable Modules</h4>
                <p>Components are like mini-Kustomizations you can mix into any overlay — ideal for shared concerns like TLS certificates, monitoring sidecars, or common labels:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">components/ — Reusable building blocks</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># components/tls/kustomization.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1alpha1
kind: Component
patches:
- path: add-tls-volume.yaml
  target:
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># components/monitoring/kustomization.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1alpha1
kind: Component
patches:
- path: add-prometheus-annotations.yaml
  target:
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># USAGE IN OVERLAY — mix in components as needed</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># overlays/production/kustomization.yaml</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
- ../../base
components:                      <span class="comment"># Mix in reusable parts!</span>
- ../../components/tls            <span class="comment"># TLS for production</span>
- ../../components/monitoring     <span class="comment"># Prometheus annotations</span>
<span class="comment"># Dev overlay: only uses ../../base (no TLS, no monitoring)</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># DIFFERENCE: resources vs components</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># resources:  Add NEW resources to the output</span>
<span class="comment"># components: Apply PATCHES/CONFIG to existing resources</span>
<span class="comment"># Think: resources = new things, components = modify existing things</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kustomize Merge Algorithm — How Bases + Overlays Combine</h4>
                <p>Understanding <strong>how</strong> Kustomize merges YAML is essential for debugging when patches don't apply as expected. Here's the exact algorithm:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Strategic Merge Patch Algorithm — Under the Hood</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  KUSTOMIZE BUILD PROCESS (what `kubectl kustomize` does)                     │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: RESOURCE COLLECTION                                                 │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ • Read kustomization.yaml                                            │   │
│  │ • Load all resources: listed in resources:, from generators, etc.    │   │
│  │ • Recursively load bases (each base is a kustomization directory)    │   │
│  │ • Result: flat list of ALL K8s resource objects                     │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 2: LABEL/ANNOTATION APPLICATION                                        │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ • Apply commonLabels to ALL resources                                 │   │
│  │ • Apply commonAnnotations to ALL resources                            │   │
│  │ • Apply namePrefix/nameSuffix to all resource names                   │   │
│  │ • Set namespace on all namespaced resources                           │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 3: PATCH APPLICATION (the critical step)                                │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ For each patch in patches:                                            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ a. MATCH target resource:                                             │   │
│  │    • Match by kind + name (from target: selector)                     │   │
│  │    • If no match → patch silently skipped (WARNING!)                  │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ b. APPLY strategic merge:                                              │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │    EXAMPLE:                                                            │   │
│  │    Base:                          Patch:                               │   │
│  │    ┌──────────────────────┐       ┌──────────────────────┐            │   │
│  │    │ replicas: 1          │       │ replicas: 5          │            │   │
│  │    │ containers:          │  +    │ containers:          │  =         │   │
│  │    │ - name: web          │       │ - name: web          │            │   │
│  │    │   image: easypj:v1   │       │   image: easypj:v2   │            │   │
│  │    │   ports:             │       │   ports:             │            │   │
│  │    │   - containerPort:80 │       │   - containerPort:80 │            │   │
│  │    └──────────────────────┘       └──────────────────────┘            │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │    Result:                                                            │   │
│  │    ┌──────────────────────┐                                           │   │
│  │    │ replicas: 5          │  ← Scalar: patch value wins               │   │
│  │    │ containers:          │                                           │   │
│  │    │ - name: web          │                                           │   │
│  │    │   image: easypj:v2   │  ← List merged by "name" key              │   │
│  │    │   ports:             │                                           │   │
│  │    │   - containerPort:80 │  ← Sub-list merged by "containerPort"     │   │
│  │    └──────────────────────┘                                           │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │ RULES OF STRATEGIC MERGE:                                              │   │
│  │ ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐      │   │
│  │ │ Scalar fields (replicas, image tag):    patch value WINS    │      │   │
│  │ │ Lists with merge key (containers[name]): MERGED by key      │      │   │
│  │ │ Lists without merge key (command args):  patch REPLACES     │      │   │
│  │ │ Maps (labels, annotations):              MERGED (union)     │      │   │
│  │ │ Null values in patch:                    IGNORED (can't     │      │   │
│  │ │                                          delete fields)     │      │   │
│  │ └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘      │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 4: IMAGE TRANSFORMATION                                                │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ • Replace image names and tags per images: config                     │   │
│  │ • images: [{name: easypj/web, newTag: v2.1}]                         │   │
│  │   → Any container with image "easypj/web" gets tag "v2.1"            │   │
│  │ • This runs AFTER patches, so it overrides patch-level image changes │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                    │                                          │
│                                    ▼                                          │
│  STEP 5: GENERATOR HASH SUFFIXES                                             │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │ • ConfigMapGenerator creates ConfigMap with content hash suffix       │   │
│  │ • Name: easypj-config-7g8h9j0k                                       │   │
│  │ • Deployment automatically updated to reference new ConfigMap name   │   │
│  │ • WHY: If ConfigMap content changes → hash changes → new name        │   │
│  │   → Deployment rolls out new pods (picks up new config)              │   │
│  │ • This is how Kustomize triggers rolling updates on config changes!  │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  COMMON PITFALL:                                                             │
│  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     │
│  │ Patch doesn't match → silently ignored                             │     │
│  │                                                                     │     │
│  │ Example:                                                            │     │
│  │   target:                                                           │     │
│  │     kind: Deployment                                                │     │
│  │     name: easypj-web          ← Base has name "easypj-web"          │     │
│  │                                                                     │     │
│  │ BUT overlay has namePrefix: prod-                                   │     │
│  │ → Resource name is now "prod-easypj-web"                            │     │
│  │ → Patch target "easypj-web" DOESN'T MATCH "prod-easypj-web"        │     │
│  │ → Patch silently dropped!                                           │     │
│  │                                                                     │     │
│  │ FIX: Use annotation-based targeting instead:                        │     │
│  │   target:                                                           │     │
│  │     annotationSelector: app=easypj                                  │     │
│  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘     │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Kustomize Name Reference Tracking — How ConfigMap Hashes Work</h4>
                <p>Kustomize's killer feature: when a ConfigMap changes, the Deployment automatically rolls. Here's exactly how:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Name reference tracking — before and after kustomize build</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># BEFORE kustomize build:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># base/deployment.yaml references ConfigMap by literal name:</span>
<span class="comment">#   configMapRef:</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: easypj-config</span>

<span class="comment"># kustomization.yaml uses configMapGenerator:</span>
<span class="comment">#   configMapGenerator:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - name: easypj-config</span>
<span class="comment">#     literals:</span>
<span class="comment">#     - DJANGO_DEBUG=false</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># AFTER kubectl kustomize:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ConfigMap gets content hash suffix:</span>
<span class="comment">#   name: easypj-config-7g8h9j0k  # hash of all literals/files</span>

<span class="comment"># Deployment's configMapRef is automatically updated:</span>
<span class="comment">#   configMapRef:</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: easypj-config-7g8h9j0k  # ← Auto-updated to match!</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># WHAT HAPPENS ON CONFIG CHANGE:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. You change DJANGO_DEBUG=true in configMapGenerator</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. kustomize build → new ConfigMap: easypj-config-a1b2c3d4</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Deployment YAML updated: configMapRef → easypj-config-a1b2c3d4</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. kubectl apply → Deployment spec changed → triggers rolling update</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. New pods mount new ConfigMap → pick up DJANGO_DEBUG=true</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># This is Kustomize's secret weapon: config changes trigger deployments</span>
<span class="comment"># automatically, with NO templating required!</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §37.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s37-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">37.6 Helm vs Kustomize — When to Use Each</h3>
                <p>This is one of the most common questions in Kubernetes. The short answer: <strong>they serve different purposes and complement each other.</strong></p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Helm</th>
                                <th>Kustomize</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Approach</strong></td>
                                <td>Templates + values</td>
                                <td>Patches on base YAML</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Learning Curve</strong></td>
                                <td>Moderate (Go templates)</td>
                                <td>Low (plain YAML patches)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Package Sharing</strong></td>
                                <td>✅ Chart repos (Artifact Hub)</td>
                                <td>❌ Not designed for sharing</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Complexity</strong></td>
                                <td>Go template syntax</td>
                                <td>Strategic merge patches</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Built into kubectl</strong></td>
                                <td>No (separate binary)</td>
                                <td>Yes (<code>kubectl apply -k</code>)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Versioning</strong></td>
                                <td>Chart versions + release history</td>
                                <td>Git-based versioning</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Rollback</strong></td>
                                <td><code>helm rollback</code></td>
                                <td><code>git revert</code></td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Best For</strong></td>
                                <td>Shared / public apps (nginx, Redis)</td>
                                <td>Internal config management</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>easypj Use Case</strong></td>
                                <td>Package for distribution</td>
                                <td>Multi-env deployments</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Decision Tree — Helm, Kustomize, or Both?</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  DECISION TREE                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Need to share charts publicly?                                              │
│  ├── YES → Use Helm (chart repos)                                           │
│  └── NO                                                                      │
│      │                                                                       │
│      Need complex templating (conditionals, loops)?                         │
│      ├── YES → Use Helm (Go templates are powerful)                         │
│      └── NO                                                                  │
│          │                                                                   │
│          Just need different configs per environment?                        │
│          ├── YES → Use Kustomize (simplest solution)                        │
│          └── BOTH                                                            │
│              │                                                               │
│              Use Helm to render, Kustomize to patch!                        │
│              $ helm template ./chart | kubectl apply -k -                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│                                                                              │
│  🔑 PRO TIP: Many teams use BOTH together:                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐     ┌──────────┐          │
│  │  Helm    │────▶│ Rendered │────▶│Kustomize │────▶│ Applied  │          │
│  │  Chart   │     │  YAML    │     │  Patches │     │ to K8s   │          │
│  └──────────┘     └──────────┘     └──────────┘     └──────────┘          │
│                                                                              │
│  ArgoCD natively supports both Helm and Kustomize as sources!               │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                    </pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="cka-verify">
                    <h4>✅ Chapter 37 Competency Checklist</h4>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Can you explain the stages of a CI/CD pipeline for Kubernetes?</li>
                        <li>Can you write a GitHub Actions workflow that builds, tests, and pushes a container image?</li>
                        <li>Can you articulate why GitOps (pull-based) is preferred over push-based CD?</li>
                        <li>Can you set up an ArgoCD Application pointing at a Git repo?</li>
                        <li>Can you troubleshoot ArgoCD sync/health status issues?</li>
                        <li>Can you create a Helm chart with Go templates and values files?</li>
                        <li>Can you read and write Helm Go template syntax (conditionals, loops, defaults)?</li>
                        <li>Can you use <code>kubectl apply -k</code> with a base + overlay Kustomize structure?</li>
                        <li>Can you use Kustomize generators (configMapGenerator, secretGenerator)?</li>
                        <li>Can you explain when to choose Helm vs Kustomize vs both?</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Real-World Stack for easypj — Putting It All Together</h5>
                    <p>Here's how a production team would combine everything from this chapter:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>GitHub Actions</strong> — CI: run Django tests, build Docker image, push to GHCR</li>
                        <li><strong>Kustomize</strong> — Manage dev/staging/prod overlays for easypj manifests</li>
                        <li><strong>ArgoCD</strong> — Watch the manifest repo, auto-sync to cluster, detect drift</li>
                        <li><strong>Helm</strong> — Install third-party tools (nginx-ingress, cert-manager, Prometheus)</li>
                        <li><strong>HPA + Cluster Autoscaler</strong> — Scale pods and nodes based on traffic</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>This is the <strong>modern Kubernetes deployment stack</strong> used by most teams. You now understand every layer.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Complete Production Deployment Architecture for easypj</h4>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>End-to-End: Code → Production in One Diagram</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  COMPLETE GITOPS ARCHITECTURE — easypj Production                            │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────────┐            │
│  │  GIT REPO: easypj-code  │        │ GIT REPO: easypj-k8s    │            │
│  │                         │        │                         │            │
│  │  src/                   │        │  base/                  │            │
│  │  Dockerfile             │        │  overlays/              │            │
│  │  requirements.txt       │        │    dev/                 │            │
│  │  .github/workflows/     │        │    staging/             │            │
│  └────────┬────────────────┘        │    production/          │            │
│           │                         └────────┬────────────────┘            │
│           │ Push to main                     │ Git tag: easypj-v2.1        │
│           ▼                                  │                              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐                │                              │
│  │  GITHUB ACTIONS (CI)    │                │                              │
│  │  ┌───────────────────┐  │                │                              │
│  │  │ 1. Python tests   │  │                │                              │
│  │  │ 2. Docker build   │  │                │                              │
│  │  │ 3. Push to GHCR   │──┼───▶ ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1                 │
│  │  │ 4. Update k8s repo│──┼───▶ ─────────────────────────────┐            │
│  │  └───────────────────┘  │                                   │            │
│  └─────────────────────────┘                                   │            │
│                                                                 ▼            │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │                          ARGOCD (GitOps Engine)                        │  │
│  │                                                                        │  │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │  │
│  │  │ App: easypj-prod                                                  │ │  │
│  │  │ Source: easypj-k8s/overlays/production (Kustomize)                │ │  │
│  │  │ Destination: easypj-production namespace                          │ │  │
│  │  │ Sync: Automated (selfHeal=true, prune=false)                      │ │  │
│  │  │                                                                    │ │  │
│  │  │ Sync Phases:                                                      │ │  │
│  │  │ ┌────────────┐   ┌───────────┐   ┌────────────┐   ┌──────────┐  │ │  │
│  │  │ │PreSync Hook│──▶│Wave 0: NS │──▶│Wave 1: CM  │──▶│Wave 2:   │  │ │  │
│  │  │ │DB Migrate  │   │           │   │+ Secrets   │   │Deployment │  │ │  │
│  │  │ └────────────┘   └───────────┘   └────────────┘   └──────────┘  │ │  │
│  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │  │
│  └─────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                    │                                           │
│                                    ▼                                           │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │                       KUBERNETES CLUSTER                               │  │
│  │                                                                        │  │
│  │  Namespace: easypj-production                                          │  │
│  │  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐      │  │
│  │  │ easypj-web │  │ easypj-web │  │ easypj-web │  │ easypj     │      │  │
│  │  │  Pod v2.1  │  │  Pod v2.1  │  │  Pod v2.1  │  │  Service   │      │  │
│  │  │  Ready ✅  │  │  Ready ✅  │  │  Ready ✅  │  │  ClusterIP │      │  │
│  │  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └─────┬──────┘      │  │
│  │                                                        │              │  │
│  │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘              │  │
│  │  │  Namespace: ingress-nginx (installed via Helm)                     │  │
│  │  │  ┌────────────────┐                                                │  │
│  │  │  │ nginx-ingress  │────▶ easypj.com → easypj-web:8000            │  │
│  │  │  │ LoadBalancer   │                                                │  │
│  │  │  └────────────────┘                                                │  │
│  │                                                                        │  │
│  │  Monitoring (installed via Helm):                                      │  │
│  │  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐                │  │
│  │  │  Prometheus  │  │   Grafana    │  │ AlertManager │                │  │
│  │  │  (metrics)   │  │  (dashboards)│  │  (alerts)    │                │  │
│  │  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘  └──────────────┘                │  │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  TOOL USAGE IN THIS ARCHITECTURE:                                            │
│  ┌────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
│  │ GitHub Actions │ CI: build, test, push image, update manifest repo  │    │
│  │ ArgoCD         │ CD: detect Git change → sync to cluster            │    │
│  │ Kustomize      │ Manages easypj's OWN manifests (dev/staging/prod)  │    │
│  │ Helm           │ Manages THIRD-PARTY tools (nginx, Prometheus)      │    │
│  │ Docker/OCI     │ container images in GHCR (or ECR/ACR/GCR)          │    │
│  └────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Image Promotion Pipeline — Dev → Staging → Production</h4>
                <p>In a real team workflow, images don't go directly to production. They get <strong>promoted</strong> through environments:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Terminal — Image promotion workflow</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># STEP 1: PR merges to main → CI builds &amp; pushes</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># CI outputs: ghcr.io/user/easypj:abc1234 (git SHA tag)</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># STEP 2: Deploy to DEV (auto-sync via ArgoCD)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ArgoCD watches overlays/dev/ — auto-deploys :abc1234</span>
<span class="comment"># Dev team tests new features here</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># STEP 3: Promote to STAGING (manual PR)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Developer creates PR in easypj-k8s repo:</span>
<span class="comment">#   overlays/staging/kustomization.yaml:</span>
<span class="comment">#     images:</span>
<span class="comment">#     - name: easypj/web</span>
<span class="comment">#       newTag: abc1234    # ← Promote this SHA to staging</span>
<span class="comment"># PR merged → ArgoCD syncs staging environment</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># STEP 4: Promote to PRODUCTION (release tag + PR)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># After QA signs off on staging:</span>
$ docker tag ghcr.io/user/easypj:abc1234 ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1
$ docker push ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1

<span class="comment"># PR in easypj-k8s repo:</span>
<span class="comment">#   overlays/production/kustomization.yaml:</span>
<span class="comment">#     images:</span>
<span class="comment">#     - name: easypj/web</span>
<span class="comment">#       newTag: v2.1       # ← Promote release tag to production</span>
<span class="comment"># PR merged → ArgoCD syncs production</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># STEP 5: EMERGENCY ROLLBACK (seconds!)</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># Option A: Git revert the PR → ArgoCD syncs back</span>
$ git revert HEAD &amp;&amp; git push

<span class="comment"># Option B: ArgoCD CLI rollback (faster in emergency)</span>
$ argocd app rollback easypj-prod @prev

<span class="comment"># Option C: kubectl rollout (fastest, but Git drifts)</span>
$ kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web -n easypj-production
<span class="comment"># ⚠️  ArgoCD will detect drift and (with selfHeal) revert it back!</span>
<span class="comment">#     In emergencies, pause ArgoCD first:</span>
$ argocd app set easypj-prod --sync-policy none</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Canary Deployments — Progressive Rollout with Traffic Splitting</h4>
                <p>Instead of replacing all pods at once (risky!), a <strong>canary deployment</strong> sends a small percentage of traffic to the new version first. If it's healthy, gradually increase. If not, rollback instantly:</p>
                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Canary Deployment — Step by Step</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  CANARY DEPLOYMENT PATTERN                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 1: Both versions running, 100% traffic to v2.0 (stable)                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │                                                                    │        │
│  │  ┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐      │        │
│  │  │ easypj v2.0  │     │ easypj v2.0  │     │ easypj v2.0  │      │        │
│  │  │   Pod 1      │     │   Pod 2      │     │   Pod 3      │  100%│        │
│  │  └──────────────┘     └──────────────┘     └──────────────┘  ◀─── │        │
│  │                                                                    │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 2: Deploy 1 canary pod (v2.1), send 10% traffic                       │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │                                                                    │        │
│  │  ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐ ┌─────────┐ │        │
│  │  │ easypj v2.0  │ │ easypj v2.0  │ │ easypj v2.0  │ │ v2.1 🆕 │ │        │
│  │  │   Pod 1      │ │   Pod 2      │ │   Pod 3      │ │ canary  │ │        │
│  │  └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘ └─────────┘ │        │
│  │       ◀──── 90% traffic ────▶                       ◀ 10% ──    │        │
│  │                                                                   │        │
│  │ Monitor: error rate, latency, CPU for 5-10 minutes                │        │
│  │ ✅ v2.1 looks good!                                                │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 3: Increase canary → 50% traffic                                       │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │                                                                    │        │
│  │  ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐ ┌─────────┐ │        │
│  │  │ easypj v2.0  │ │ easypj v2.0  │ │ easypj v2.1  │ │ v2.1 🆕 │ │        │
│  │  │   Pod 1      │ │   Pod 2      │ │   Pod 3      │ │ canary  │ │        │
│  │  └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘ └─────────┘ │        │
│  │       ◀──── 50% traffic ────▶       ◀──── 50% traffic ────▶     │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  STEP 4: Full promotion → 100% v2.1                                          │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │  ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐              │        │
│  │  │ easypj v2.1  │ │ easypj v2.1  │ │ easypj v2.1  │  100%        │        │
│  │  │   Pod 1      │ │   Pod 2      │ │   Pod 3      │  ◀──────     │        │
│  │  └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘              │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
│  IF CANARY FAILS:                                                            │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐        │
│  │ 1. Detect: error rate spikes, latency increases                  │        │
│  │ 2. Rollback: scale canary to 0, OR                               │        │
│  │ 3. Git revert + ArgoCD sync (returns to v2.0)                    │        │
│  │ 4. Only 10% of users affected (vs 100% with rolling update!)     │        │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘        │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
                </div>

                <h4>Blue-Green Deployments — Instant Rollback by Switching Services</h4>
                <p>Blue-Green runs TWO complete environments side by side and switches traffic instantly by updating a Service selector:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">Blue-Green Deployment — easypj example</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ═══════════════════════════════════════
<span class="comment"># BLUE (current production) — labeled version: blue</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-blue
  labels:
    app: easypj
    version: blue
spec:
  replicas: 3
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
        version: blue        <span class="comment"># Service currently points to "blue"</span>
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.0

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># GREEN (new version) — labeled version: green</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-green
  labels:
    app: easypj
    version: green
spec:
  replicas: 3
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj
        version: green       <span class="comment"># Not receiving traffic yet</span>
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1  <span class="comment"># NEW version</span>

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># SERVICE — switch between blue and green</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  selector:
    app: easypj
    version: blue            # ← Change to "green" to switch!

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># SWITCHING TRAFFIC (zero downtime):</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Deploy green alongside blue (both running)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Test green: kubectl port-forward deploy/easypj-green 8080:8000</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Smoke test green internally</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Switch Service: kubectl patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"version":"green"}}}'</span>
<span class="comment">#    → All traffic now goes to green INSTANTLY</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. PROBLEM? Switch back:</span>
<span class="comment">#    kubectl patch svc easypj-web -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"version":"blue"}}}'</span>
<span class="comment">#    → Instant rollback! (sub-second, no pod restarts)</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># 6. Green stable for 24h → delete blue deployment</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ROLLING UPDATE vs BLUE-GREEN vs CANARY:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ┌──────────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┬──────────────┐</span>
<span class="comment"># │                  │ Rolling      │ Blue-Green   │ Canary       │</span>
<span class="comment"># ├──────────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┼──────────────┤</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Rollback speed   │ Minutes      │ Seconds      │ Seconds      │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Resource usage   │ Same         │ 2× (both env)│ 1.1×         │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Risk             │ Medium       │ Low          │ Lowest       │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Complexity        │ Low          │ Medium       │ High         │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ K8s native?      │ ✅ Built-in  │ ⚙️ Manual    │ ⚙️ Manual    │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Best for          │ Most apps    │ Critical     │ High-traffic │</span>
<span class="comment"># └──────────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┴──────────────┘</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Argo Rollouts — Progressive Delivery with Automated Analysis</h4>
                <p>Argo Rollouts is a Kubernetes controller that makes canary and blue-green deployments <strong>first-class resources</strong>, with automated metric analysis:</p>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-rollout.yaml — Argo Rollouts with automated canary</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Rollout                          <span class="comment"># Replaces Deployment</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
spec:
  replicas: 5
  strategy:
    canary:                            <span class="comment"># Canary strategy</span>
      steps:
      - setWeight: 10                  <span class="comment"># 10% traffic to canary</span>
        pause: {duration: 5m}          <span class="comment"># Wait 5 minutes</span>
      - setWeight: 30                  <span class="comment"># 30% traffic</span>
        pause: {duration: 10m}         <span class="comment"># Wait 10 minutes</span>
      - setWeight: 50                  <span class="comment"># 50% traffic</span>
        pause: {duration: 10m}
      - setWeight: 100                 <span class="comment"># Full promotion</span>
      
      <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════</span>
      <span class="comment"># AUTOMATED ANALYSIS (Prometheus metrics)</span>
      <span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════</span>
      analysis:
        templates:
        - templateName: canary-health-check
        startingStep: 1                <span class="comment"># Start analysis at 10% step</span>
        
        args:
        - name: service-name
          value: easypj-web

  template:
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: easypj
        image: ghcr.io/user/easypj:v2.1

---
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># ANALYSIS TEMPLATE — Automated pass/fail criteria</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: AnalysisTemplate
metadata:
  name: canary-health-check
spec:
  metrics:
  - name: error-rate
    interval: 30s
    failureLimit: 3                   <span class="comment"># Fail if 3 consecutive bad checks</span>
    successCondition: result[0] < 0.01 <span class="comment"># Error rate must be < 1%</span>
    provider:
      prometheus:
        address: http://prometheus.monitoring:9090
        query: |
          rate(http_requests_total{
            service="{{args.service-name}}",
            status=~"5.."
          }[1m]) / rate(http_requests_total{
            service="{{args.service-name}}"
          }[1m])
  
  - name: latency-p99
    interval: 30s
    failureLimit: 2
    successCondition: result[0] < 0.5  <span class="comment"># p99 < 500ms</span>
    provider:
      prometheus:
        query: |
          histogram_quantile(0.99,
            rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{
              service="{{args.service-name}}"
            }[1m]))

<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># WHAT HAPPENS:</span>
<span class="comment"># ═══════════════════════════════════════</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Rollout starts → 10% pods get v2.1</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. AnalysisTemplate runs every 30s</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. If error-rate stays below 1% AND p99 < 500ms → continue</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. If either metric fails → AUTOMATIC ROLLBACK to v2.0</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. No human intervention needed!</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># This is the gold standard of production deployments.</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Gotcha: CI/CD &amp; GitOps Traps</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>CKA doesn't test CI/CD directly</strong> — But understanding the deployment pipeline helps with troubleshooting questions. If a deployment is broken, knowing whether the issue is in the image (CI) vs the manifest (CD) vs the cluster saves time</li>
                        <li><strong>Helm is NOT on the CKA exam</strong> — But <code>Kustomize</code> IS available via <code>kubectl apply -k</code>. Know that <code>-k</code> applies a kustomization directory. You may see it in exam documentation</li>
                        <li><strong><code>kubectl apply</code> vs <code>kubectl create</code></strong> — <code>apply</code> is declarative (idempotent, can update). <code>create</code> is imperative (fails if resource exists). GitOps uses <code>apply</code>. The exam expects you to know both — use <code>create</code> for speed, <code>apply</code> for YAML files</li>
                        <li><strong>Image pull policies</strong> — <code>Always</code> (default for <code>:latest</code>), <code>IfNotPresent</code> (default for tagged), <code>Never</code>. If a CI pipeline pushes a new image with the same tag, pods with <code>IfNotPresent</code> won't pull the update. This is a real-world trap</li>
                        <li><strong>Rolling update strategy</strong> — <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code> control deployment speed. Default is 25% each. For zero-downtime: <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code>, <code>maxSurge: 1</code>. Know how to set these on the exam</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>


                <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                    <strong>⚡ Deployment &amp; rollout commands:</strong>
                    <code>kubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod/</code> — apply Kustomize overlay<br>
                    <code>kubectl diff -f manifest.yaml</code> — preview changes before applying<br>
                    <code>kubectl annotate deployment &lt;name&gt; kubernetes.io/change-cause="reason"</code> — record change reason<br>
                    <code>kubectl rollout pause deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code> — pause mid-rollout<br>
                    <code>kubectl rollout resume deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code> — resume paused rollout
                </div>


                <!-- ═══ UTH Chapter 37 — How GitOps Reconciliation and Controller Patterns Work ═══ -->

                <!-- Chapter 37 Exam Questions -->

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-uth"></span>
            <details class="uth"><summary>Under the Hood — How GitOps Reconciliation and Controller Patterns Actually Work: ArgoCD Sync Loop Internals, Helm Storage Secrets, and Kustomize Strategic Merge Algorithm</summary>
            <div class="uth-body">
            <h5>🗺️ GitOps Reconciliation — The Pull-Based Deployment Model</h5>
            <p>Unlike traditional CI/CD that pushes to clusters, GitOps tools like ArgoCD continuously reconcile cluster state with Git. This provides drift detection, auto-healing, and a complete audit trail.</p>
            <div class="uth-diagram"><pre>
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  GITOPS RECONCILIATION LOOP — ArgoCD Internals                               │
│                                                                              │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │                         GIT REPO (source of truth)                     │   │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │   │
│  │  │  easypj-k8s-manifests/                                        │    │   │
│  │  │  ├── overlays/dev/                                             │    │   │
│  │  │  ├── overlays/staging/                                         │    │   │
│  │  │  └── overlays/production/  <span class="comment">← ArgoCD watches this              </span>│    │   │
│  │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                 │                                            │
│                    Every 3 min (or webhook):                                  │
│                                 │                                            │
│                                 ▼                                            │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  ARGOCD APPLICATION CONTROLLER                                        │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  1. FETCH DESIRED STATE:                                              │   │
│  │     Git clone → kustomize build overlays/production → YAML resources │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  2. FETCH LIVE STATE:                                                 │   │
│  │     kubectl get &lt;resource&gt; -n easypj-prod → actual resources        │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  3. COMPARE (three-way diff):                                         │   │
│  │     desired YAML vs live YAML vs last-applied-configuration           │   │
│  │     → Synced (matches) or OutOfSync (diff detected)                   │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  4. ACT (if auto-sync enabled):                                       │   │
│  │     kubectl apply desired resources → drive cluster to Git state      │   │
│  │     kubectl delete resources not in Git (if prune=true)               │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  PUSH vs PULL DEPLOYMENT:                                                     │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  PUSH (CI/CD):          CI runs kubectl apply → cluster              │   │
│  │  Issues: Who ran it? What changed? How to rollback? Drift?           │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  PULL (GitOps):         CI updates Git → ArgoCD syncs → cluster       │   │
│  │  Benefits: Full audit (git log), rollback (git revert), drift fix     │   │
│  │  (selfHeal), no cluster creds in CI, PR-based deployments            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
│  ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════     │
│  HELM + KUSTOMIZE — Complementary, Not Competitive:                          │
│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│  │  Helm: Templates + values → great for SHARING (chart repos)          │   │
│  │  Kustomize: Base + patches → great for INTERNAL config management    │   │
│  │                                                                        │   │
│  │  BEST PRACTICE: Use Helm for third-party tools (nginx, Prometheus)   │   │
│  │  and Kustomize for your OWN apps (easypj dev/staging/prod).          │   │
│  │  ArgoCD supports BOTH natively as sources.                            │   │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │
│                                                                              │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre></div>
            <div class="uth-callout">
            <strong>🔑 CKA Insight:</strong> CI/CD and GitOps are not directly tested on the CKA, but understanding deployment patterns helps with exam scenarios. Key concepts: (1) Deployment rolling updates use maxSurge/maxUnavailable, (2) Rollback = <code>kubectl rollout undo</code>, (3) kubectl apply is declarative and idempotent, (4) Image pull policies determine when new images are fetched. These ARE tested on the CKA.
            </div>
            </div>
            </details>

            <!-- ─────────── KEY TAKEAWAYS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-takeaways"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Key Takeaways</h4>
                <p>Bulleted key takeaways go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── GOTCHAS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-gotchas"></span>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Gotchas &amp; Misconceptions</h5>
                <p>Every Application <strong>must</strong> belong to a project (default is <code>project: default</code>). The <code>default</code> project allows ALL sources and ALL destinations — fine for learning, but in production, create scoped AppProjects. If an Application references a repo/destination not whitelisted in its project, ArgoCD shows <code>Unknown</code> status with no helpful error — check the <code>argocd-application-controller</code> logs.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SPEED TIPS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-speed"></span>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <h5>⚡ Speed Tips</h5>
                <p><strong>CKA Speed Tip — Deployment update commands:</strong><br>
                <code>kubectl set image deployment/&lt;name&gt; &lt;container&gt;=&lt;image&gt;:&lt;tag&gt;</code> — update image<br>
                <code>kubectl rollout status deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code> — watch rollout progress<br>
                <code>kubectl rollout history deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code> — see revision history<br>
                <code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/&lt;name&gt;</code> — rollback to previous version<br>
                <code>kubectl rollout undo deployment/&lt;name&gt; --to-revision=2</code> — rollback to specific revision</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── COMMANDS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-commands"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔑 Commands to Know (Chapter 37)</h4>
                <p>The command reference for this chapter goes here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── PRACTICE DRILL ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-drill"></span>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>🧪 Drill</h5>
                <p><strong>Timed challenge (target: 5 minutes):</strong></p>
                <ul>
                    <li>1. Create a deployment: <code>kubectl create deploy webapp --image=nginx:1.24 --replicas=3</code></li>
                    <li>2. Update the image: <code>kubectl set image deploy/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25</code></li>
                    <li>3. Watch the rollout: <code>kubectl rollout status deploy/webapp</code></li>
                    <li>4. Check history: <code>kubectl rollout history deploy/webapp</code></li>
                    <li>5. Rollback: <code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/webapp</code></li>
                    <li>6. Verify: <code>kubectl describe deploy webapp | grep Image</code> — should show nginx:1.24</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 37 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch37-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 37 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (14)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of GitOps reconciliation &amp; CI/CD flow — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q1</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you update a Deployment's image and track the rollout?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Method 1: kubectl set image (fastest)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25
<span class="comment"># Records the change automatically</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 2: Edit directly</span>
kubectl edit deployment webapp
<span class="comment"># Change image: nginx:1.24 → nginx:1.25</span>

<span class="comment"># Method 3: Patch</span>
kubectl patch deployment webapp -p \
'{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"nginx","image":"nginx:1.25"}]}}}}'

<span class="comment"># Track the rollout:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "webapp" rollout to finish:</span>
<span class="comment"># 2 out of 3 new replicas have been updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment "webapp" successfully rolled out ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Record the reason for the change:</span>
kubectl annotate deployment webapp \
kubernetes.io/change-cause="Updated nginx to 1.25 for security patch"

<span class="comment"># Check rollout history:</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         &lt;none&gt;</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         Updated nginx to 1.25 for security patch</span>

<span class="comment"># View specific revision details:</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp --revision=2</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl set image</code> is the fastest way to update a Deployment's image on the CKA. <code>rollout status</code> watches the rollout in real time. <code>annotate</code> with <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> records why the change was made (shows in <code>rollout history</code>). On the CKA, <code>set image</code> + <code>rollout status</code> is the combo you'll use most. Each image update creates a new revision for rollback.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q2</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you rollback a Deployment to a previous version or a specific revision?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Rollback to previous version:</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/webapp rolled back</span>

<span class="comment"># Rollback to specific revision:</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         Initial deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         Updated to nginx:1.25</span>
<span class="comment"># 3         Updated to nginx:1.26-broken  ← Current (broken!)</span>

kubectl rollout undo deployment/webapp --to-revision=2
<span class="comment"># Rolls back to revision 2 (nginx:1.25)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify the rollback:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># deployment "webapp" successfully rolled out ✓</span>

kubectl describe deployment webapp | grep Image
<span class="comment"># Image: nginx:1.25  ← Back to working version!</span>

<span class="comment"># After rollback, revision numbers change:</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         Initial deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># 3         Updated to nginx:1.26-broken</span>
<span class="comment"># 4         Updated to nginx:1.25  ← Undo created rev 4</span>

<span class="comment"># How it works:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Each undo creates a NEW revision</span>
<span class="comment"># - Old ReplicaSet is scaled up, new one scaled down</span>
<span class="comment"># - Rolling update strategy still applies</span>
<span class="comment"># - Pods are NOT recreated all at once (gradual)</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl rollout undo</code> reverts to the previous revision. <code>--to-revision=N</code> targets a specific revision. On the CKA, this is the fastest way to fix a broken deployment. Key: undo creates a NEW revision (it doesn't delete history). The rollback uses the same rolling update strategy as a normal update. Always verify with <code>describe deployment | grep Image</code> after rollback.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q3</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the difference between <code>kubectl apply</code> and <code>kubectl create</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <table>
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th>Feature</th>
                                        <th><code>kubectl create</code></th>
                                        <th><code>kubectl apply</code></th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Style</td>
                                        <td>Imperative</td>
                                        <td>Declarative</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>If resource exists</td>
                                        <td>❌ Error (AlreadyExists)</td>
                                        <td>✅ Updates it</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>If resource doesn't exist</td>
                                        <td>✅ Creates it</td>
                                        <td>✅ Creates it</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>Idempotent</td>
                                        <td>❌ No</td>
                                        <td>✅ Yes</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>GitOps/CI/CD</td>
                                        <td>❌ Not suitable</td>
                                        <td>✅ Standard</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td>CKA usage</td>
                                        <td>Generate YAML quickly</td>
                                        <td>Apply YAML files</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># CKA workflow:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Generate YAML with create + dry-run:</span>
kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx \
--dry-run=client -o yaml > web.yaml

<span class="comment"># 2. Edit the YAML:</span>
vi web.yaml  <span class="comment"># Add resources, probes, etc.</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Apply it:</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml

<span class="comment"># 4. Need to change? Edit and re-apply:</span>
vi web.yaml  <span class="comment"># Change replicas</span>
kubectl apply -f web.yaml  <span class="comment"># Updates existing resource ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Using create for the same file twice:</span>
kubectl create -f web.yaml
<span class="comment"># Error: deployments.apps "web" already exists ✗</span>

<span class="comment"># Kustomize with apply:</span>
kubectl apply -k ./overlays/prod/  <span class="comment"># Apply kustomization</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>create</code> = imperative, fails if resource exists. <code>apply</code> = declarative, creates OR updates. On the CKA, use <code>create --dry-run=client -o yaml</code> to generate YAML, then <code>apply -f</code> to create/update. GitOps uses <code>apply</code> because it's idempotent (safe to run multiple times). <code>apply</code> stores the "last applied configuration" annotation for three-way merge on subsequent applies.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q4</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you configure a rolling update strategy with zero downtime?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Zero-downtime rolling update:</span>
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
name: web
spec:
replicas: 3
strategy:
type: RollingUpdate
rollingUpdate:
  maxSurge: 1          <span class="comment"># Add 1 extra pod during update</span>
  maxUnavailable: 0    <span class="comment"># Never reduce below desired count</span>
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: web
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: web
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:1.25
    readinessProbe:      <span class="comment"># CRITICAL for zero downtime!</span>
      httpGet:
        path: /
        port: 80
      initialDelaySeconds: 5
      periodSeconds: 5

<span class="comment"># maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0 means:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Create 1 new pod (4 total)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Wait for new pod to be READY</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Terminate 1 old pod (3 total)</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Create 1 new pod (4 total)</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. Wait for READY, terminate old...</span>
<span class="comment"># Always at least 3 healthy pods!</span>

<span class="comment"># Without readinessProbe:</span>
<span class="comment"># New pod is "Ready" immediately (before app starts)</span>
<span class="comment"># Old pod terminated → brief downtime before new pod serves traffic</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify strategy:</span>
kubectl describe deployment web | grep -A3 Strategy
<span class="comment"># RollingUpdateStrategy: 1 max unavailable, 1 max surge</span>

<span class="comment"># Set imperatively:</span>
kubectl patch deployment web -p \
'{"spec":{"strategy":{"rollingUpdate":{"maxSurge":1,"maxUnavailable":0}}}}'</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Zero downtime requires: (1) <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> — never reduce pod count below desired, (2) <code>maxSurge: 1+</code> — allow extra pods during update, (3) readinessProbe — only send traffic when pod is actually ready. Without a readiness probe, Kubernetes considers pods Ready immediately, causing brief traffic failures. On the CKA, know how to set maxSurge/maxUnavailable and always add readiness probes.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q5</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>kubectl apply -k</code> (Kustomize) and how does it work?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Kustomize: built into kubectl, no extra tools needed</span>
<span class="comment"># Customizes YAML without modifying original files</span>

<span class="comment"># Directory structure:</span>
<span class="comment"># base/</span>
<span class="comment">#   deployment.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#   service.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#   kustomization.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># overlays/</span>
<span class="comment">#   prod/</span>
<span class="comment">#     kustomization.yaml  ← References base + adds overrides</span>

<span class="comment"># base/kustomization.yaml:</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
- deployment.yaml
- service.yaml

<span class="comment"># overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml:</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
- ../../base
namePrefix: prod-
namespace: production
patches:
- patch: |-
- op: replace
  path: /spec/replicas
  value: 5
target:
kind: Deployment
name: web

<span class="comment"># Preview what will be applied:</span>
kubectl kustomize overlays/prod/

<span class="comment"># Apply:</span>
kubectl apply -k overlays/prod/

<span class="comment"># Diff before applying:</span>
kubectl diff -k overlays/prod/

<span class="comment"># CKA relevance:</span>
<span class="comment"># - You may see -k in exam documentation</span>
<span class="comment"># - Know that -k applies kustomization directories</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not heavily tested but good to recognize</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Kustomize is built into kubectl via <code>-k</code> flag. It lets you customize YAML manifests without editing originals — using overlays for different environments. On the CKA, you likely won't need to create kustomization files, but know that <code>kubectl apply -k</code> applies a kustomization directory. <code>kubectl kustomize</code> previews the output. Kustomize can add prefixes, namespaces, labels, and patches on top of base resources.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q6</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you preview changes before applying a manifest with <code>kubectl diff</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># See what will change before applying:</span>
kubectl diff -f deployment.yaml
<span class="comment"># --- /tmp/LIVE-123</span>
<span class="comment"># +++ /tmp/MERGED-456</span>
<span class="comment"># @@ -15,7 +15,7 @@</span>
<span class="comment">#      containers:</span>
<span class="comment">#      - name: nginx</span>
<span class="comment"># -      image: nginx:1.24</span>
<span class="comment"># +      image: nginx:1.25    ← Will change this!</span>
<span class="comment">#        resources:</span>
<span class="comment"># -        limits:</span>
<span class="comment"># -          memory: 256Mi</span>
<span class="comment"># +        limits:</span>
<span class="comment"># +          memory: 512Mi    ← And this!</span>

<span class="comment"># Exit codes:</span>
<span class="comment"># 0 = No differences (already in desired state)</span>
<span class="comment"># 1 = Differences found (changes will be made)</span>
<span class="comment"># >1 = Error</span>

<span class="comment"># Diff with kustomize:</span>
kubectl diff -k overlays/prod/

<span class="comment"># Diff a specific resource:</span>
kubectl diff -f service.yaml

<span class="comment"># CKA usage: Verify before applying</span>
kubectl diff -f fix.yaml    <span class="comment"># Review changes</span>
kubectl apply -f fix.yaml   <span class="comment"># Apply if correct</span>

<span class="comment"># dry-run alternatives:</span>
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --dry-run=server
<span class="comment"># Validates against the API server without persisting</span>
<span class="comment"># Shows what WOULD happen</span>

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml --dry-run=client -o yaml
<span class="comment"># Client-side validation + outputs the result YAML</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>kubectl diff</code> shows a diff between the live state and what would be applied — like a preview before <code>apply</code>. On the CKA, use diff to verify your changes before applying (especially when editing manifests). Exit code 0 = no changes needed, 1 = changes will be made. <code>--dry-run=server</code> validates against the API server without persisting. Use these to avoid mistakes under time pressure.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q7</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is <code>imagePullPolicy</code> and how does it affect deployments?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Three policies:</span>
<span class="comment"># Always         → Pull image every time (default for :latest)</span>
<span class="comment"># IfNotPresent   → Pull only if not cached (default for tagged)</span>
<span class="comment"># Never          → Only use cached images (error if not cached)</span>

<span class="comment"># Example:</span>
spec:
containers:
- name: app
image: nginx:1.25
imagePullPolicy: Always  <span class="comment"># Forces fresh pull every time</span>

<span class="comment"># Defaults:</span>
<span class="comment"># image: nginx          → imagePullPolicy: Always (implicit :latest)</span>
<span class="comment"># image: nginx:latest   → imagePullPolicy: Always</span>
<span class="comment"># image: nginx:1.25     → imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent</span>

<span class="comment"># Problem scenario:</span>
<span class="comment"># CI pushes new image with same tag "nginx:1.25"</span>
<span class="comment"># Existing pods have the OLD image cached</span>
<span class="comment"># imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent → won't pull the new version!</span>

<span class="comment"># Fix:</span>
<span class="comment"># Option 1: Use unique tags (nginx:1.25-build-456)</span>
<span class="comment"># Option 2: Set imagePullPolicy: Always</span>
<span class="comment"># Option 3: Delete pods to force re-pull</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA gotcha:</span>
<span class="comment"># Using :latest is BAD practice:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Not reproducible (you don't know which version is running)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Always pulls (slower restarts)</span>
<span class="comment"># - Can't rollback reliably</span>

<span class="comment"># Best practice:</span>
image: nginx:1.25.3   <span class="comment"># Specific tag</span>
imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent  <span class="comment"># Cache-friendly</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>imagePullPolicy</code> controls when the container runtime downloads the image. <code>Always</code> = pull every time (ensures latest), <code>IfNotPresent</code> = use cache if available (faster), <code>Never</code> = cache only. On the CKA, know the defaults: <code>:latest</code> tag → Always, specific tag → IfNotPresent. Using <code>:latest</code> is an anti-pattern because you can't reliably track or rollback versions. Use specific tags in production.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q8</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">How do you pause and resume a Deployment rollout?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Pause a rollout (stop mid-update):</span>
kubectl rollout pause deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/webapp paused</span>

<span class="comment"># While paused:</span>
<span class="comment"># - Current state is frozen</span>
<span class="comment"># - You can make MULTIPLE changes without triggering rollouts</span>
kubectl set image deployment/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25
kubectl set resources deployment/webapp \
--limits=cpu=500m,memory=512Mi
kubectl set env deployment/webapp APP_ENV=production
<span class="comment"># None of these trigger a rollout yet!</span>

<span class="comment"># Resume to apply ALL changes at once:</span>
kubectl rollout resume deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/webapp resumed</span>
<span class="comment"># Now all 3 changes roll out in a SINGLE rollout! ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Without pause, each change triggers a separate rollout:</span>
<span class="comment"># Change 1 → rollout starts → Change 2 → rollout restarts → slow!</span>

<span class="comment"># Check if paused:</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/webapp
<span class="comment"># "deployment is paused"</span>

<span class="comment"># Use case on CKA:</span>
<span class="comment"># "Make multiple changes to a deployment and apply them together"</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. kubectl rollout pause deployment/webapp</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Make all changes</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. kubectl rollout resume deployment/webapp</span>

<span class="comment"># Cancel a rollout (if something goes wrong):</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/webapp  <span class="comment"># Reverts changes</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Pausing batches multiple changes into a single rollout. Without pause, each <code>set image</code>, <code>set env</code>, or <code>set resources</code> triggers a separate rolling update (wasteful). Pause → make all changes → resume = one clean rollout. On the CKA, this saves time when you need to update image + env vars + resources simultaneously. <code>rollout undo</code> works even on paused deployments.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q9</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is the <code>Recreate</code> strategy and when would you use it instead of <code>RollingUpdate</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># RollingUpdate (default):</span>
<span class="comment"># Gradually replaces pods: old ↓ new ↑</span>
<span class="comment"># Zero downtime (with proper probes)</span>
<span class="comment"># Two versions run simultaneously during transition</span>

<span class="comment"># Recreate:</span>
<span class="comment"># Kills ALL old pods first, then creates new ones</span>
<span class="comment"># Brief downtime between old and new</span>
<span class="comment"># Only ONE version runs at a time</span>

spec:
strategy:
type: Recreate  <span class="comment"># No rollingUpdate sub-fields needed</span>

<span class="comment"># When to use Recreate:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Database migrations — can't have two schema versions</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. Persistent volume — RWO volume can't mount on two pods</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Application can't handle two versions concurrently</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Port conflicts — new and old both need the same port</span>

<span class="comment"># Timeline:</span>
<span class="comment"># RollingUpdate:  old-1 old-2 old-3</span>
<span class="comment">#                 old-1 old-2 new-1</span>
<span class="comment">#                 old-1 new-1 new-2</span>
<span class="comment">#                 new-1 new-2 new-3  ← Zero downtime</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># Recreate:       old-1 old-2 old-3</span>
<span class="comment">#                 (none)              ← DOWNTIME</span>
<span class="comment">#                 new-1 new-2 new-3</span>

<span class="comment"># CKA: Know both strategies exist</span>
<span class="comment"># Default is RollingUpdate (most common)</span>
<span class="comment"># Set Recreate when asked "ensure only one version runs"</span>

<span class="comment"># Check current strategy:</span>
kubectl describe deployment webapp | grep Strategy
<span class="comment"># StrategyType: RollingUpdate</span>
<span class="comment"># OR</span>
<span class="comment"># StrategyType: Recreate</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>Recreate</code> = terminate all old pods before creating new ones. Use when: two versions can't coexist (DB schema changes, RWO volumes, port conflicts). <code>RollingUpdate</code> = gradually replace (zero downtime, two versions briefly coexist). On the CKA, default is <code>RollingUpdate</code>. Use <code>Recreate</code> only when specifically asked for single-version deployment. Key: <code>Recreate</code> has no <code>maxSurge</code>/<code>maxUnavailable</code> — it's all-or-nothing.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q10</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Complete this timed challenge: create a deployment, update it, check history, and rollback — all in under 2 minutes.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Timer starts! ⏱️</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 1: Create deployment (15 sec)</span>
kubectl create deploy webapp --image=nginx:1.24 --replicas=3
kubectl annotate deploy webapp kubernetes.io/change-cause="initial v1.24"

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify running (10 sec)</span>
kubectl rollout status deploy/webapp
<span class="comment"># deployment "webapp" successfully rolled out ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Update image (10 sec)</span>
kubectl set image deploy/webapp nginx=nginx:1.25
kubectl annotate deploy webapp kubernetes.io/change-cause="upgrade to v1.25" --overwrite

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Watch rollout (15 sec)</span>
kubectl rollout status deploy/webapp
<span class="comment"># deployment "webapp" successfully rolled out ✓</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Check history (10 sec)</span>
kubectl rollout history deploy/webapp
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         initial v1.24</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         upgrade to v1.25</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Rollback to revision 1 (10 sec)</span>
kubectl rollout undo deploy/webapp --to-revision=1

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Verify rollback (10 sec)</span>
kubectl describe deploy webapp | grep Image
<span class="comment"># Image: nginx:1.24  ← Back to original ✓</span>

kubectl rollout history deploy/webapp
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         upgrade to v1.25</span>
<span class="comment"># 3         initial v1.24  ← Rollback created rev 3</span>

<span class="comment"># Total: ~80 seconds ✓ Under 2 minutes!</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>This drill tests the complete deployment lifecycle. On the CKA: <code>create deploy</code> (imperative, fast), <code>set image</code> (update), <code>rollout status</code> (verify), <code>rollout history</code> (check revisions), <code>rollout undo</code> (rollback). Use <code>annotate</code> with <code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code> to label each revision. Practice this workflow until it's muscle memory — 2 minutes or less. These commands appear in almost every CKA exam.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q11</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What is GitOps? How does ArgoCD implement the GitOps pattern? Write an ArgoCD <code>Application</code> manifest that watches a Git repo and auto-syncs deployments to the cluster.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># GitOps Principles:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Git is the SINGLE source of truth for desired state</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. All changes via Git commits (no kubectl apply manually)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. Automated agent reconciles cluster state with Git</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. Drift detection: agent alerts/fixes when cluster ≠ Git</span>

<span class="comment"># ArgoCD Application CRD:</span>
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
name: easypj
namespace: argocd
spec:
project: default
source:
repoURL: https://github.com/org/easypj-k8s-manifests.git
targetRevision: main
path: k8s/production       <span class="comment"># Directory with YAML manifests</span>
destination:
server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
namespace: production
syncPolicy:
automated:
  prune: true              <span class="comment"># Delete resources removed from Git</span>
  selfHeal: true           <span class="comment"># Fix manual changes (drift)</span>
syncOptions:
  - CreateNamespace=true

<span class="comment"># How it works:</span>
<span class="comment"># 1. Developer pushes change to Git (e.g., update image tag)</span>
<span class="comment"># 2. ArgoCD detects the change (polls every 3 min or webhook)</span>
<span class="comment"># 3. ArgoCD compares Git state vs cluster state</span>
<span class="comment"># 4. If different: auto-applies the changes (sync)</span>
<span class="comment"># 5. If someone does kubectl edit: selfHeal reverts it</span>

<span class="comment"># Install ArgoCD:</span>
kubectl create namespace argocd
kubectl apply -n argocd -f \
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml

<span class="comment"># Access ArgoCD UI:</span>
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443

<span class="comment"># CLI: sync manually</span>
argocd app sync easypj
argocd app get easypj</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>GitOps treats Git as the single source of truth — the desired cluster state is defined in a Git repo, and an operator (ArgoCD) continuously reconciles the actual cluster state to match. <code>automated.selfHeal: true</code> means any manual <code>kubectl edit</code> or <code>kubectl delete</code> gets reverted automatically. <code>prune: true</code> means deleting a file from Git also deletes the resource from the cluster. The CKA doesn't test ArgoCD directly, but understanding GitOps principles helps you appreciate why declarative YAML and <code>kubectl apply</code> (vs imperative commands) are the recommended workflow in production.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q12</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Install a Helm chart, customize it with values, and show how to manage releases. What is the Helm chart structure, and how does <code>helm template</code> differ from <code>helm install</code>?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># Helm chart structure:</span>
<span class="comment"># mychart/</span>
<span class="comment">#   Chart.yaml          ← Metadata (name, version)</span>
<span class="comment">#   values.yaml         ← Default configuration values</span>
<span class="comment">#   templates/           ← Kubernetes manifests with Go templates</span>
<span class="comment">#     deployment.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#     service.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#     ingress.yaml</span>
<span class="comment">#   charts/              ← Dependencies (sub-charts)</span>

<span class="comment"># Add a repo and install:</span>
helm repo add bitnami https://charts.bitnami.com/bitnami
helm repo update

<span class="comment"># Install with default values:</span>
helm install my-postgres bitnami/postgresql -n database
<span class="comment">#  NAME: my-postgres</span>
<span class="comment">#  STATUS: deployed</span>
<span class="comment">#  REVISION: 1</span>

<span class="comment"># Install with custom values:</span>
helm install my-postgres bitnami/postgresql -n database \
--set auth.postgresPassword=secretpass \
--set primary.resources.requests.memory=256Mi \
--set primary.persistence.size=50Gi

<span class="comment"># Or use a values file:</span>
helm install my-postgres bitnami/postgresql -n database \
-f custom-values.yaml

<span class="comment"># helm template vs helm install:</span>
helm template my-postgres bitnami/postgresql \
--set auth.postgresPassword=secretpass
<span class="comment"># ↑ Renders YAML locally (stdout) — does NOT apply to cluster</span>
<span class="comment"># Use this to preview or pipe into kubectl apply</span>

helm install my-postgres bitnami/postgresql \
--set auth.postgresPassword=secretpass
<span class="comment"># ↑ Renders AND applies to cluster. Tracks as a "release"</span>

<span class="comment"># Manage releases:</span>
helm list -A                    <span class="comment"># List all releases</span>
helm status my-postgres -n database  <span class="comment"># Release details</span>
helm upgrade my-postgres bitnami/postgresql \
--set auth.postgresPassword=newpass  <span class="comment"># Upgrade</span>
helm rollback my-postgres 1     <span class="comment"># Rollback to revision 1</span>
helm uninstall my-postgres -n database  <span class="comment"># Remove</span>

<span class="comment"># Show what Helm would render:</span>
helm get manifest my-postgres -n database</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Helm is the Kubernetes package manager — charts bundle multiple YAML manifests with templated values. <code>helm template</code> renders the YAML locally without applying (useful for review, CI/CD pipelines, or piping into <code>kubectl apply</code>). <code>helm install</code> renders AND applies, tracking the deployment as a "release" with revision history (enabling <code>helm rollback</code>). Key distinction: Helm manages releases server-side (stored as Secrets in the namespace), while <code>helm template</code> is stateless. The CKA may ask you to install something with Helm or use <code>helm template</code> to generate manifests for review. Know: <code>--set</code> for inline values, <code>-f</code> for values files, and <code>helm list</code> to see what's installed.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q13</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare Helm and Kustomize. When would you use each? Show how to deploy the same easypj application using both approaches.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># HELM approach — templating with values:</span>
<span class="comment"># Chart has templates with {{ .Values.image }} placeholders</span>
helm install easypj ./easypj-chart \
--set image.tag=v2 \
--set replicas=5 \
--set database.password=secret

<span class="comment"># KUSTOMIZE approach — patching base manifests:</span>
<span class="comment"># base/deployment.yaml (plain YAML, no templates)</span>
<span class="comment"># overlays/production/kustomization.yaml:</span>
apiVersion: kustomize.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: Kustomization
resources:
- ../../base
namePrefix: prod-
namespace: production
images:
- name: easypj
newTag: v2
replicas:
- name: easypj
count: 5
patchesStrategicMerge:
- increase-resources.yaml

<span class="comment"># Apply with kubectl (built-in!):</span>
kubectl apply -k overlays/production/

<span class="comment"># Comparison:</span>
<span class="comment"># ┌──────────────────┬────────────────────┬───────────────────┐</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Aspect           │ Helm                 │ Kustomize           │</span>
<span class="comment"># ├──────────────────┼────────────────────┼───────────────────┤</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Approach         │ Templating (Go tmpl) │ Patching (overlays) │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Install needed?  │ Yes (helm binary)    │ No (built into      │</span>
<span class="comment"># │                  │                      │ kubectl)            │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Base manifests   │ Templates (not valid │ Plain YAML (valid   │</span>
<span class="comment"># │                  │ YAML until rendered) │ as-is)              │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Release tracking │ Yes (revisions)      │ No                  │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Rollback         │ helm rollback        │ Git revert + apply  │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ Dependencies     │ Sub-charts           │ Remote bases        │</span>
<span class="comment"># │ CKA relevance    │ Know helm commands   │ kubectl apply -k    │</span>
<span class="comment"># └──────────────────┴────────────────────┴───────────────────┘</span>

<span class="comment"># When to use which:</span>
<span class="comment"># Helm:      3rd-party apps (PostgreSQL, Prometheus, nginx-ingress)</span>
<span class="comment"># Kustomize: Your own apps with per-environment overrides</span>
<span class="comment"># Both:      Helm for install, then Kustomize to patch Helm output</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>Helm uses <strong>templating</strong> — base files have <code>{{ .Values.x }}</code> placeholders that get replaced. Kustomize uses <strong>patching</strong> — base manifests are valid YAML, and overlays modify them without touching the originals. Kustomize is built into kubectl (<code>kubectl apply -k</code>), while Helm needs a separate binary. Use Helm for installing third-party charts (databases, monitoring). Use Kustomize for your own apps with dev/staging/prod overlays. On the CKA, <code>kubectl apply -k</code> (Kustomize) is more likely to appear since it's built-in. Know both approaches conceptually.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <span class="eq-number">Q14</span>
                    <div class="eq-question">What would an ArgoCD-managed deployment workflow look like for the easypj application? Describe how a code change flows from commit to running in the cluster, and how drift is handled.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <div class="eq-answer-label">Answer</div>
                            <pre><code><span class="comment"># ArgoCD GitOps Deployment Flow for easypj:</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. COMMIT: Developer updates image tag in Git</span>
<span class="comment">#    k8s/production/deployment.yaml:</span>
<span class="comment">#    image: easypj:v2    (was v1)</span>
<span class="comment">#    git commit -m "Upgrade easypj to v2"</span>
<span class="comment">#    git push origin main</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. DETECT: ArgoCD detects change</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Polls Git repo every 3 minutes (or webhook triggers)</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Compares Git manifests vs live cluster state</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Status changes: Synced → OutOfSync</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. SYNC: ArgoCD applies the change</span>
<span class="comment">#    - With automated sync: applies immediately</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Without: shows OutOfSync in UI, waits for manual sync</span>
<span class="comment">#    argocd app sync easypj</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. VERIFY: ArgoCD monitors rollout</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Watches Deployment rollout progress</span>
<span class="comment">#    - Status: Progressing → Healthy (or Degraded if fails)</span>
<span class="comment">#    argocd app get easypj</span>
<span class="comment">#    Health Status: Healthy</span>
<span class="comment">#    Sync Status:   Synced</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. DRIFT DETECTION AND SELF-HEAL:</span>
<span class="comment">#    Someone does: kubectl scale deploy easypj --replicas=1</span>
<span class="comment">#    ArgoCD detects: cluster state ≠ Git state</span>
<span class="comment">#    With selfHeal: true → ArgoCD reverts to Git's replica count</span>
<span class="comment">#    Without: Shows OutOfSync, alerts admin</span>

<span class="comment"># 6. ROLLBACK (if v2 is broken):</span>
<span class="comment">#    Option A: Git revert + push → ArgoCD syncs to v1</span>
<span class="comment">#    Option B: argocd app rollback easypj</span>
<span class="comment">#    Git revert is preferred (maintains Git as source of truth)</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify the full pipeline:</span>
argocd app list
<span class="comment"># NAME     SYNC     HEALTH    PROJECT</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj  Synced   Healthy   default</span>

argocd app history easypj
<span class="comment"># ID  DATE                 REVISION</span>
<span class="comment"># 1   2024-01-15 10:00:00  abc1234</span>
<span class="comment"># 2   2024-01-15 14:30:00  def5678  ← current</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The ArgoCD workflow enforces a strict Git → Cluster pipeline: no manual <code>kubectl apply</code> changes survive because <code>selfHeal</code> reverts them. This is the core GitOps benefit — you always know what's running by looking at Git, and there's a full audit trail of every change. Rollbacks are just <code>git revert</code>. The CKA doesn't test ArgoCD directly, but understanding this CI/CD pattern explains why Kubernetes emphasizes declarative manifests, <code>kubectl apply</code> (vs <code>create</code>), and version control. In production, ArgoCD or Flux is the standard way to deploy to Kubernetes clusters.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>📌 Chapter 37 Visual Summary</h4>
                <p>A visual-summary diagram + .vs-prose recap go here.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART10 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part10-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🔴 Part 10 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Production &amp; Beyond — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You can now run easypj like a production service: a highly-available control plane with etcd quorum that survives a node loss, an understanding of how your skills carry to managed cloud Kubernetes, and an automated CI/CD + GitOps pipeline that ships changes from git with safe rollback.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 🔴 Part 10 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>High availability (Ch 35):</strong> ≥3 control-plane nodes behind a load balancer; etcd quorum = N/2+1 (use odd counts); stacked vs external etcd; leader election keeps one active controller-manager/scheduler.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cloud Kubernetes (Ch 36):</strong> EKS/GKE/AKS manage the control plane for you; your kubeadm-era concepts (nodes, networking, storage, IAM-to-RBAC) map directly — you manage workloads + node pools, not the masters.</li>
                    <li><strong>CI/CD &amp; GitOps (Ch 37):</strong> a pipeline builds/tests/pushes an image and updates manifests; GitOps (ArgoCD/Flux) makes the cluster continuously reconcile to git — git is the source of truth, with auditable rollback.</li>
                    <li><strong>Quorum math:</strong> 3 etcd members tolerate 1 failure, 5 tolerate 2 — even counts add no fault tolerance.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Exam + beyond:</strong> HA architecture and etcd quorum are exam-relevant; cloud + GitOps are Day-2 production skills.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 10 Visual Summary — a fault-tolerant, self-shipping platform</div>
<pre>
   🔁 git push ──▶ 🏗️ CI builds/pushes image ──▶ 🤖 GitOps reconciles to cluster
                                                     │
        ┌────────────── HA control plane ───────────┘
        ▼            (LB ▶ apiserver ×3 · etcd quorum N/2+1)
   ☸️ easypj runs on EKS/GKE/AKS or self-managed HA — survives a node loss
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   no single point of failure; deploys come from git, not from a human.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 10 made easypj production-grade. An HA control plane (≥3 apiservers behind a load balancer, etcd at quorum) removes the single point of failure; managed cloud K8s offloads the control plane while your workload skills transfer unchanged; and CI/CD + GitOps turn a git commit into a safe, reconciled deploy.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> remember quorum = N/2+1 with odd member counts, that cloud K8s changes who runs the masters but not how you run workloads, and that GitOps makes git the source of truth — and you can stand easypj up as a resilient, automated service. Next: Part 11 extends and hardens the platform.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART11 COVER ==================== -->
        <section class="part-cover" id="part11">
            <span class="part-kicker">🔧 Part 11 · Extending and Hardening</span>
            <h2 class="part-cover-title">Extend It &amp; Lock It Down</h2>
            <p class="part-cover-sub">Chapters 38–40 · Advanced · <em>"Make Kubernetes your own — safely"</em></p>
            <p class="part-cover-lead">Kubernetes is a platform you can extend and must secure. Part 11 covers the extension surface — CRDs, the operator pattern, and API aggregation that add your own resources and controllers — then the hardening playbook and the policy engines (Pod Security, OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno, ValidatingAdmissionPolicy) that enforce a secure, compliant baseline across the cluster.</p>
            <div class="part-goal">🎯 <strong>What you’ll be able to do by the end:</strong> define a CRD and reason about the operator pattern + API aggregation; apply a security-hardening checklist (RBAC, secrets, audit, runtime, supply chain); and enforce guardrails with Pod Security Standards and a policy engine.</div>
            <div class="diagram-container">
                <div class="diagram-title">How the chapters fit — extend the API, then govern it</div>
<pre>
   Ch 38 EXTENDING K8s ──▶ Ch 39 HARDENING ──▶ Ch 40 POLICIES DEEP DIVE
   CRDs · operators        RBAC · secrets · audit   Pod Security · CEL
   API aggregation         runtime · supply chain   OPA/Gatekeeper · Kyverno
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   the through-line: add your own resources and controllers, then fence the
   whole cluster behind a hardened, policy-enforced baseline.
</pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <!--            CHAPTER 38: EXTENDING KUBERNETES                -->
        <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <article class="chapter" id="ch38">
            <div class="chapter-intro">
                <h2 class="chapter-title">Chapter 38 · Extending Kubernetes — CRDs, Operators &amp; API Extensions</h2>
                <p class="chapter-lead">Learn how to extend the Kubernetes API with Custom Resource Definitions, build automation with Operators, use kubectl plugins, and implement Admission Webhooks — all demonstrated with the <strong>easypj</strong> Django project.</p>
                <div class="chapter-meta">
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔴 Advanced</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">⏱️ ~2.5 hours</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">🔧 Extensions</span>
                    <span class="meta-tag">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
                </div>
