            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch38-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 38 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (5)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of CRDs, Operators &amp; API extension — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q38.1</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a CRD called <code>backups.data.easypj.io</code> with a <code>Backup</code> kind. It should be namespaced, have a <code>v1</code> version, and require <code>spec.source</code> (string) and <code>spec.schedule</code> (string) fields. Then create a Backup resource in the easypj namespace.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># CRD definition</span>
apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
name: backups.data.easypj.io
spec:
group: data.easypj.io
versions:
- name: v1
  served: true
  storage: true
  schema:
    openAPIV3Schema:
      type: object
      properties:
        spec:
          type: object
          required: ["source", "schedule"]
          properties:
            source:
              type: string
            schedule:
              type: string
scope: Namespaced
names:
plural: backups
singular: backup
kind: Backup
---
<span class="comment"># Backup instance</span>
apiVersion: data.easypj.io/v1
kind: Backup
metadata:
name: db-daily
namespace: easypj
spec:
source: postgresql
schedule: "0 2 * * *"</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CRD <code>metadata.name</code> MUST be <code>plural.group</code> — so <code>backups.data.easypj.io</code>. The schema is required and validates that <code>source</code> and <code>schedule</code> are strings. Once the CRD is registered, you create instances using the CRD's <code>apiVersion</code> (group/version) and <code>kind</code>. On the CKA, always apply the CRD first, verify with <code>kubectl get crd</code>, then create the custom resource.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q38.2</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">List all CRDs in the cluster. Find which CRDs belong to cert-manager. Then check who can create <code>certificates</code> (cert-manager CRD) in the <code>easypj</code> namespace.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List all CRDs</span>
kubectl get crd

<span class="comment"># Filter for cert-manager CRDs</span>
kubectl get crd | grep cert-manager
<span class="comment"># certificates.cert-manager.io</span>
<span class="comment"># certificaterequests.cert-manager.io</span>
<span class="comment"># clusterissuers.cert-manager.io</span>
<span class="comment"># issuers.cert-manager.io</span>
<span class="comment"># orders.acme.cert-manager.io</span>
<span class="comment"># challenges.acme.cert-manager.io</span>

<span class="comment"># Check who can create certificates</span>
kubectl auth can-i create certificates.cert-manager.io \
--as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:default -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Use who-can (if available) for comprehensive RBAC analysis</span>
kubectl who-can create certificates.cert-manager.io -n easypj</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>CRDs are cluster-scoped resources — <code>kubectl get crd</code> lists all of them regardless of namespace. To filter by a specific operator, grep for the group name (e.g., <code>cert-manager</code>). When checking RBAC for custom resources, use the full resource name including the API group: <code>certificates.cert-manager.io</code>, not just <code>certificates</code>. The <code>kubectl auth can-i</code> command also works with <code>--as=</code> to test permissions for other users/service accounts.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q38.3</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a <code>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</code> that prevents creating Pods with <code>privileged: true</code> security context. Bind it to the <code>easypj</code> namespace only.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
name: deny-privileged-pods
spec:
failurePolicy: Fail
matchConstraints:
resourceRules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    apiVersions: ["v1"]
    operations: ["CREATE"]
    resources: ["pods"]
validations:
- expression: >-
    object.spec.containers.all(c,
      !has(c.securityContext) ||
      !has(c.securityContext.privileged) ||
      c.securityContext.privileged == false)
  message: "Privileged containers are not allowed"
---
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicyBinding
metadata:
name: deny-privileged-pods-binding
spec:
policyName: deny-privileged-pods
validationActions: [Deny]
matchResources:
namespaceSelector:
  matchLabels:
    kubernetes.io/metadata.name: easypj</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy uses CEL expressions for in-cluster policy enforcement without external webhooks. The expression checks every container in the pod spec — if none has <code>privileged: true</code>, the pod is allowed. The binding uses <code>namespaceSelector</code> to limit enforcement to the <code>easypj</code> namespace (using the automatic label <code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code>). This replaces the need for OPA Gatekeeper or Kyverno for simple policies. Available since K8s v1.30 GA.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q38.4</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Explain the difference between CRDs and API Aggregation. Give an example of when you'd use each approach. How does metrics-server use API aggregation?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check if metrics-server uses API aggregation</span>
kubectl get apiservice v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io -o yaml
<span class="comment"># spec:</span>
<span class="comment">#   group: metrics.k8s.io</span>
<span class="comment">#   service:</span>
<span class="comment">#     name: metrics-server</span>
<span class="comment">#     namespace: kube-system</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify the aggregated API works</span>
kubectl top pods -n easypj
kubectl top nodes</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><strong>CRDs</strong>: Store data in etcd, no custom server needed, simple to create. Use for: app configuration, operator-managed resources (Certificates, Applications). <strong>API Aggregation</strong>: Runs a separate API server, can have custom storage backend, complex to build. Use for: real-time data that shouldn't be in etcd (metrics), protocol translation. <strong>metrics-server</strong> uses API aggregation because it serves real-time CPU/memory metrics collected from kubelets — storing this in etcd would be wasteful and slow. It registers at <code>metrics.k8s.io</code> and proxies <code>kubectl top</code> requests to its own server.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q38.5</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">A <code>ValidatingWebhookConfiguration</code> is blocking pod creation with the error "webhook denied the request". The webhook service is down. How do you temporarily fix this?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># List validating webhooks</span>
kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations

<span class="comment"># Check which webhook is failing</span>
kubectl get validatingwebhookconfiguration &lt;name&gt; -o yaml

<span class="comment"># Option 1: Change failurePolicy to Ignore (temporary!)</span>
kubectl patch validatingwebhookconfiguration &lt;name&gt; \
--type='json' -p='[{"op": "replace", "path": "/webhooks/0/failurePolicy", "value": "Ignore"}]'

<span class="comment"># Option 2: Delete the webhook configuration entirely (if safe)</span>
kubectl delete validatingwebhookconfiguration &lt;name&gt;

<span class="comment"># Option 3: Fix the webhook service (proper fix)</span>
kubectl get svc -n webhook-system
kubectl get pods -n webhook-system
kubectl logs -n webhook-system &lt;webhook-pod&gt;</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>When a webhook with <code>failurePolicy: Fail</code> is unreachable, ALL matching API requests are rejected — this can lock you out of creating/updating resources. Quick fix: patch the <code>failurePolicy</code> to <code>Ignore</code> so requests pass through while you fix the webhook server. The proper fix is to ensure the webhook service and pods are running. On the CKA, if you see "webhook denied the request" errors, check <code>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations</code> and <code>kubectl get mutatingwebhookconfigurations</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

