            <!-- ─────────── §39.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.1 The Kubernetes Security Hardening Checklist</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Use this as a reference checklist when securing a cluster. Each item links to the detailed section below.</p>

                <div class="process-steps">
                    <div class="process-step-item active">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #ef4444;">1</div>
                        <h5>Infrastructure Layer</h5>
                        <p>API server flags, etcd encryption at rest, audit logging, component TLS — secure the foundation</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #f59e0b;">2</div>
                        <h5>Auth & RBAC</h5>
                        <p>Least-privilege roles, disable default SA token mounting, no wildcard permissions, audit bindings</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #eab308;">3</div>
                        <h5>Admission Control</h5>
                        <p>Pod Security Standards (restricted), ValidatingAdmissionPolicy, image registry allowlisting</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #22c55e;">4</div>
                        <h5>Workload Hardening</h5>
                        <p>SecurityContext (runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem), drop ALL capabilities, seccomp profiles</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #60a5fa;">5</div>
                        <h5>Network Isolation</h5>
                        <p>Default-deny NetworkPolicy per namespace, egress filtering, DNS rules, mTLS with service mesh</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="process-step-item">
                        <div class="step-number" style="background: #8b5cf6;">6</div>
                        <h5>Data Protection</h5>
                        <p>Secrets encrypted at rest, mount as files (not env), external secret stores, credential rotation</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Security Layers — Defense in Depth</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │                  DEFENSE IN DEPTH                            │
 ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 1: CLUSTER INFRASTRUCTURE                            │
 │  ├── API server flags (--anonymous-auth=false)              │
 │  ├── etcd encryption at rest                                │
 │  ├── Audit logging enabled                                  │
 │  └── Component TLS (kubelet, etcd)                          │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 2: AUTHENTICATION & AUTHORIZATION                    │
 │  ├── RBAC with least privilege                              │
 │  ├── Disable default SA token auto-mount                    │
 │  ├── No cluster-admin for workloads                         │
 │  └── Audit who has wildcard permissions                     │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 3: ADMISSION CONTROL                                 │
 │  ├── Pod Security Admission (restricted)                    │
 │  ├── ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)                        │
 │  ├── Image allowlisting                                     │
 │  └── Deny privileged containers                              │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 4: WORKLOAD SECURITY                                 │
 │  ├── SecurityContext (runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem) │
 │  ├── Resource limits (CPU/memory)                           │
 │  ├── seccomp / AppArmor profiles                            │
 │  └── No hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC                       │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 5: NETWORK SECURITY                                  │
 │  ├── Default-deny NetworkPolicy                             │
 │  ├── Egress filtering                                       │
 │  ├── Namespace isolation                                    │
 │  └── mTLS (service mesh)                                    │
 │                                                              │
 │  Layer 6: DATA SECURITY                                     │
 │  ├── Secrets encrypted at rest                              │
 │  ├── External secret management (Vault, etc.)               │
 │  ├── No secrets in environment variables                    │
 │  └── Rotate credentials regularly                           │
 │                                                              │
 └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.2 RBAC Hardening Deep Dive</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <h4>Dangerous Permissions to Avoid</h4>
                <p>These RBAC rules grant far more access than they appear to. Treat them as red flags during security reviews.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Dangerous Permission</th><th>Why It's Dangerous</th><th>What to Do Instead</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>verbs: ["*"]</code> on any resource</td><td>Grants ALL operations including delete</td><td>List only the verbs you need: <code>["get", "list", "watch"]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>resources: ["*"]</code></td><td>Access to ALL resource types in the API group</td><td>List specific resources: <code>["pods", "services"]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>apiGroups: ["*"]</code></td><td>Access across ALL API groups</td><td>Specify: <code>["", "apps", "networking.k8s.io"]</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>create</code> on <code>pods</code></td><td>Can exec into nodes, mount secrets, escape</td><td>Grant <code>create</code> on <code>deployments</code> instead</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>escalate</code> on <code>roles</code></td><td>Can grant themselves cluster-admin</td><td>Never grant to workload service accounts</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>bind</code> on <code>clusterroles</code></td><td>Can bind any role to any subject</td><td>Limit to specific roles with resourceNames</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>impersonate</code> on <code>users/groups</code></td><td>Can act as any user including cluster-admin</td><td>Never grant unless absolutely required</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>RBAC Best Practices — easypj Example</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">rbac-hardened.yaml — least-privilege for easypj app</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ❌ BAD — overly permissive
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: bad-example-do-not-use
rules:
  - apiGroups: ["*"]
    resources: ["*"]
    verbs: ["*"]           <span class="comment"># This is basically cluster-admin!</span>
---
<span class="comment"># ✅ GOOD — least privilege for easypj web app</span>
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
  <span class="comment"># Read-only access to ConfigMaps (for Django settings)</span>
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps"]
    resourceNames: ["easypj-config"]  <span class="comment"># Only THIS specific ConfigMap</span>
    verbs: ["get", "watch"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Read-only access to Secrets (for DB credentials)</span>
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["secrets"]
    resourceNames: ["easypj-db-secret"]
    verbs: ["get"]
  
  <span class="comment"># No access to pods, services, etc. — the app doesn't need it</span>
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: easypj-web-sa
    namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-web-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit Existing RBAC Permissions</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — RBAC audit commands</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Find all ClusterRoleBindings that reference cluster-admin</span>
kubectl get clusterrolebindings -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.roleRef.name=="cluster-admin") |
  .metadata.name + " → " + (.subjects[]? | .kind + "/" + .name)'

<span class="comment"># Check what a specific ServiceAccount can do</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Find roles with wildcard permissions</span>
kubectl get roles,clusterroles -A -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.rules[]? | .verbs[]? == "*") |
  .kind + "/" + .metadata.name'

<span class="comment"># Verify no default SA has extra bindings</span>
kubectl get rolebindings,clusterrolebindings -A -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.subjects[]? | .name == "default") |
  .metadata.namespace + "/" + .metadata.name'</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.3 Secrets Management Best Practices</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Kubernetes Secrets are Base64-encoded, <strong>not encrypted</strong> by default. Anyone with RBAC access to Secrets or etcd can read them. Here's how to harden Secrets properly.</p>

                <div class="info-box warning">
                    <h5>⚠️ Critical Security Truth</h5>
                    <p><code>echo "cGFzc3dvcmQ=" | base64 -d</code> → <code>password</code>. Base64 is encoding, NOT encryption. Without additional measures, Secrets in etcd are plaintext-equivalent. NEVER assume Secrets are safe by default.</p>
                </div>

                <h4>Enable Encryption at Rest</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">encryption-config.yaml — encrypt secrets in etcd</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># /etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml
apiVersion: apiserver.config.k8s.io/v1
kind: EncryptionConfiguration
resources:
  - resources:
      - secrets
    providers:
      - aescbc:                      <span class="comment"># AES-CBC encryption</span>
          keys:
            - name: key1
              secret: &lt;base64-encoded-32-byte-key&gt;
      - identity: {}                 <span class="comment"># Fallback: read unencrypted (for migration)</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — enable encryption at rest</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Generate a 32-byte encryption key</span>
head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64

<span class="comment"># 2. Add --encryption-provider-config flag to kube-apiserver</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-apiserver.yaml</span>
<span class="comment"># Add to spec.containers[0].command:</span>
<span class="comment">#   --encryption-provider-config=/etc/kubernetes/encryption-config.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Mount the encryption config into the API server pod</span>
<span class="comment"># Add volume and volumeMount to kube-apiserver.yaml</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Re-encrypt existing Secrets (they're still unencrypted!)</span>
kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces -o json | kubectl replace -f -

<span class="comment"># 5. Verify encryption is working</span>
<span class="comment"># Read a Secret directly from etcd — should be encrypted</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl get /registry/secrets/easypj/easypj-db-secret \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt | hexdump -C | head</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Secrets Anti-Patterns vs Best Practices</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Anti-Pattern ❌</th><th>Best Practice ✅</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Secrets in env vars</td><td>Mount as files via <code>volumeMount</code></td><td>Env vars appear in logs, process listings, and crash dumps</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Secrets in Git repos</td><td>Use SealedSecrets or external secret stores</td><td>Git history is permanent — secrets can never be truly removed</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Using the <code>default</code> SA</td><td>Create dedicated ServiceAccounts</td><td>Default SA is shared by all pods in a namespace</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Auto-mounting SA tokens</td><td>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code></td><td>Most pods don't need API server access</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Hardcoding secrets in YAML</td><td>Use <code>kubectl create secret</code> or external tool</td><td>Keeps secret values out of manifest files</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">How should I provide secrets to my pod?</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-branch-label">Need K8s-native?</div>
                            <div class="decision-node yes">✅ Use K8s Secret mounted as volume file</div>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-branch-label">Production/enterprise?</div>
                            <div class="decision-node yes">✅ External secret store (Vault, AWS SM)</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node question">Never use these approaches:</div>
                    <div class="decision-branches">
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-node no">❌ Environment variables — leak in logs/crashes</div>
                        </div>
                        <div>
                            <div class="decision-node no">❌ Hardcoded in YAML — committed to Git history</div>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-secure-pod.yaml — proper secret handling</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  serviceAccountName: easypj-web-sa
  automountServiceAccountToken: false   # ✅ Don't mount SA token
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      volumeMounts:
        - name: db-creds
          mountPath: /etc/secrets/db     # ✅ Mount as files
          readOnly: true                 # ✅ Read-only mount
  volumes:
    - name: db-creds
      secret:
        secretName: easypj-db-secret
        defaultMode: 0400                # ✅ Restrictive permissions</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.4 API Server & Control Plane Security</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>The API server is the front door to your cluster. Hardening it is your highest priority.</p>

                <h4>Critical API Server Flags</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Recommended Value</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>--anonymous-auth</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Disable unauthenticated access to the API</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--enable-admission-plugins</code></td><td>Include <code>NodeRestriction,PodSecurity</code></td><td>Restrict node self-modification, enforce pod security</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-log-path</code></td><td><code>/var/log/kubernetes/audit.log</code></td><td>Enable audit logging for all API requests</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--audit-policy-file</code></td><td>Point to policy YAML</td><td>Define what events to record and at what level</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--profiling</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Disable profiling endpoints (information disclosure)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--insecure-port</code></td><td><code>0</code> (disabled)</td><td>No HTTP access — HTTPS only (deprecated, removed in 1.24)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>--encryption-provider-config</code></td><td>Point to config file</td><td>Encrypt secrets at rest in etcd</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h4>Audit Logging — Tracking Who Did What</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">audit-policy.yaml — sample audit policy</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: audit.k8s.io/v1
kind: Policy
rules:
  <span class="comment"># Log Secret access at Metadata level (don't log Secret content!)</span>
  - level: Metadata
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["secrets"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Log all write operations at RequestResponse level</span>
  - level: RequestResponse
    verbs: ["create", "update", "patch", "delete"]
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["pods", "services", "configmaps"]
      - group: "apps"
        resources: ["deployments", "statefulsets", "daemonsets"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Log RBAC changes at RequestResponse level</span>
  - level: RequestResponse
    resources:
      - group: "rbac.authorization.k8s.io"
        resources: ["roles", "rolebindings", "clusterroles", "clusterrolebindings"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Don't log read-only requests to known non-sensitive endpoints</span>
  - level: None
    resources:
      - group: ""
        resources: ["events"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  
  <span class="comment"># Catch-all: log everything else at Metadata level</span>
  - level: Metadata</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.5 Runtime Security — seccomp & AppArmor</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <h4>seccomp Profiles</h4>
                <p><strong>seccomp</strong> (Secure Computing Mode) restricts which <strong>Linux system calls</strong> a container can make. By blocking dangerous syscalls, you reduce the attack surface if a container is compromised.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">easypj-seccomp.yaml — seccomp profile for Django app</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-hardened
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  securityContext:
    seccompProfile:
      type: RuntimeDefault          # ✅ Use container runtime's default profile
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true           # ✅ Never run as root
        runAsUser: 1000
        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true # ✅ Prevent filesystem writes
        allowPrivilegeEscalation: false  # ✅ Block setuid/setgid
        capabilities:
          drop: ["ALL"]              # ✅ Drop ALL Linux capabilities
      volumeMounts:
        - name: tmp
          mountPath: /tmp            <span class="comment"># Django needs a writable temp dir</span>
  volumes:
    - name: tmp
      emptyDir: {}                   # ✅ Provide writable tmp via emptyDir</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>AppArmor Profiles</h4>
                <p><strong>AppArmor</strong> is a Linux Security Module that restricts a program's capabilities — file access, network access, and raw socket creation.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">apparmor-pod.yaml — apply AppArmor profile</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-apparmor
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      securityContext:
        appArmorProfile:
          type: RuntimeDefault       <span class="comment"># Use runtime default AppArmor profile</span>
          <span class="comment"># type: Localhost           # Or use a custom profile:</span>
          <span class="comment"># localhostProfile: easypj-web-profile</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 seccomp vs AppArmor</h5>
                    <p><strong>seccomp</strong> restricts <em>system calls</em> (kernel-level operations). <strong>AppArmor</strong> restricts <em>file and network access</em> (path-based). They are complementary — use both for defense in depth. The <code>RuntimeDefault</code> profile for both is a good starting point that blocks obviously dangerous operations without breaking most applications.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-2">
                    <div class="scenario-box positive">
                        <h5>🔒 seccomp (System Call Filter)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Layer:</strong> Kernel syscalls</li>
                            <li><strong>Blocks:</strong> mount, reboot, ptrace, raw sockets</li>
                            <li><strong>Config:</strong> <code>securityContext.seccompProfile</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Types:</strong> RuntimeDefault, Localhost, Unconfined</li>
                            <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Preventing kernel exploits</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="scenario-box">
                        <h5>🛡️ AppArmor (Application Armor)</h5>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Layer:</strong> File/network access paths</li>
                            <li><strong>Blocks:</strong> /etc/shadow reads, raw network, specific file writes</li>
                            <li><strong>Config:</strong> <code>securityContext.appArmorProfile</code></li>
                            <li><strong>Types:</strong> RuntimeDefault, Localhost, Unconfined</li>
                            <li><strong>Best for:</strong> Preventing file-based attacks</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.6 Supply Chain Security</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p>Securing the container image supply chain prevents compromised or vulnerable images from running in your cluster.</p>

                <div class="icon-feature-list">
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">📌</span><div><h5>Pin Image Versions</h5><p>Use specific tags (<code>v2.1</code>) or SHA digests — never <code>:latest</code></p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">🔍</span><div><h5>Scan Images</h5><p>Run vulnerability scanners (Trivy, Grype) in CI/CD before deployment</p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">✍️</span><div><h5>Sign Images</h5><p>Use cosign/Notation to verify image provenance and integrity</p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">🏢</span><div><h5>Allowlist Registries</h5><p>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy to restrict image sources to trusted repos only</p></div></div>
                    <div class="icon-feature"><span class="if-icon">📦</span><div><h5>Minimal Base Images</h5><p>Use <code>-slim</code> or distroless images — fewer packages = fewer vulnerabilities</p></div></div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">image-security-best-practices</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"># ✅ Best Practice 1: Use specific image tags (NEVER use :latest)
containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1        # ✅ Pinned version
    <span class="comment"># image: easypj/jobpost:latest     # ❌ Mutable, unpredictable</span>
    <span class="comment"># image: easypj/jobpost             # ❌ Defaults to :latest</span>

<span class="comment"># ✅ Best Practice 2: Use image digests for maximum immutability</span>
containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj/jobpost@sha256:abc123...  # ✅ Content-addressable

<span class="comment"># ✅ Best Practice 3: Set imagePullPolicy</span>
containers:
  - name: django
    image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
    imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent     <span class="comment"># or Always for security-critical apps</span>

<span class="comment"># ✅ Best Practice 4: Use minimal base images</span>
<span class="comment"># Dockerfile:</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM python:3.12-slim              # ✅ Minimal — fewer vulnerabilities</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM python:3.12                    # ❌ Full — larger attack surface</span>
<span class="comment"># FROM ubuntu:24.04                   # ❌ General purpose — bloated</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Restrict Image Sources with ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">restrict-registries.yaml — only allow trusted registries</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingAdmissionPolicy
metadata:
  name: restrict-image-registries
spec:
  failurePolicy: Fail
  matchConstraints:
    resourceRules:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: ["v1"]
        operations: ["CREATE", "UPDATE"]
        resources: ["pods"]
  validations:
    - expression: >-
        object.spec.containers.all(c,
          c.image.startsWith('easypj/') ||
          c.image.startsWith('registry.company.com/') ||
          c.image.startsWith('gcr.io/distroless/'))
      message: "Images must come from approved registries: easypj/, registry.company.com/, gcr.io/distroless/"</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.7 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-7">
                <h3 class="section-h">39.7 Multi-Tenancy & Namespace Isolation</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>In multi-tenant clusters, namespaces are the primary isolation boundary. But namespaces alone aren't enough — you need RBAC, NetworkPolicy, ResourceQuota, and Pod Security working together.</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Multi-Tenancy Isolation Stack</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │              MULTI-TENANCY ISOLATION STACK                 │
 ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │                                                            │
 │  Namespace: team-a        │  Namespace: team-b             │
 │  ┌──────────────────┐    │  ┌──────────────────┐          │
 │  │ RBAC: team-a-role │    │  │ RBAC: team-b-role │          │
 │  │ (only team-a      │    │  │ (only team-b      │          │
 │  │  resources)       │    │  │  resources)       │          │
 │  ├──────────────────┤    │  ├──────────────────┤          │
 │  │ ResourceQuota:    │    │  │ ResourceQuota:    │          │
 │  │  CPU: 4 cores     │    │  │  CPU: 8 cores     │          │
 │  │  Memory: 8Gi      │    │  │  Memory: 16Gi     │          │
 │  ├──────────────────┤    │  ├──────────────────┤          │
 │  │ NetworkPolicy:    │    │  │ NetworkPolicy:    │          │
 │  │  deny-all +       │    │  │  deny-all +       │          │
 │  │  allow internal   │    │  │  allow internal   │          │
 │  ├──────────────────┤    │  ├──────────────────┤          │
 │  │ Pod Security:     │    │  │ Pod Security:     │          │
 │  │  restricted       │    │  │  restricted       │          │
 │  └──────────────────┘    │  └──────────────────┘          │
 │                           │                                │
 └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">tenant-namespace.yaml — complete tenant isolation</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># 1. Namespace with Pod Security Standard</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: easypj
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn: restricted
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit: restricted
---
<span class="comment"># 2. Default deny-all NetworkPolicy</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-all
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]
---
<span class="comment"># 3. Allow internal communication within namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-internal
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: ["Ingress", "Egress"]
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector: {}            <span class="comment"># Allow from same namespace</span>
  egress:
    - to:
        - podSelector: {}            <span class="comment"># Allow to same namespace</span>
    - to:                            <span class="comment"># Allow DNS resolution</span>
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system
          podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              k8s-app: kube-dns
      ports:
        - protocol: UDP
          port: 53
---
<span class="comment"># 4. ResourceQuota to limit resource consumption</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "4"
    requests.memory: 8Gi
    limits.cpu: "8"
    limits.memory: 16Gi
    pods: "20"
    services: "10"
    secrets: "20"
    configmaps: "20"</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §39.exam ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s39-exam">
                <h3 class="section-h">📝 Chapter 39 — CKA Practice Questions</h3>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch39-uth"></span>
