            <!-- ─────────── §40.1 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-1">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.1 Pod Security Standards — The Three Levels</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>Pod Security Standards define three progressive security levels. They replaced the deprecated PodSecurityPolicy in Kubernetes v1.25.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Description</th><th>What It Blocks</th><th>Use Case</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Privileged</strong></td>
                                <td>No restrictions</td>
                                <td>Nothing — everything allowed</td>
                                <td>System/infrastructure namespaces (<code>kube-system</code>)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>
                                <td>Minimally restrictive</td>
                                <td>Privileged containers, hostNetwork, hostPID, hostIPC, known dangerous capabilities</td>
                                <td>General workloads with basic security</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Restricted</strong></td>
                                <td>Heavily restricted</td>
                                <td>Everything in baseline + must run as non-root, read-only filesystem encouraged, drop all capabilities, seccomp required</td>
                                <td>Security-critical and multi-tenant workloads</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 3px solid #ef4444;">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">⚠️</div>
                        <h5>Privileged</h5>
                        <p>No restrictions. Use for <code>kube-system</code> and infrastructure components only.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 3px solid #f59e0b;">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🟡</div>
                        <h5>Baseline</h5>
                        <p>Blocks known escalations. Good starting point for general workloads.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card highlight" style="border-left: 3px solid #22c55e;">
                        <div class="card-icon-lg">🟢</div>
                        <h5>Restricted</h5>
                        <p>Maximum security. Required for production and multi-tenant clusters.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>Pod Security Admission — Enforcement Modes</h4>
                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Label Key</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>enforce</strong></td><td>Block pods that violate the policy</td><td><code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>warn</strong></td><td>Allow but show warning to user</td><td><code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>audit</strong></td><td>Allow but record in audit log</td><td><code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit</code></td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — applying Pod Security Standards</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Apply restricted level with all three modes</span>
kubectl label namespace easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/audit=restricted

<span class="comment"># Verify labels</span>
kubectl get namespace easypj --show-labels

<span class="comment"># Test — this pod violates restricted (runs as root)</span>
kubectl run test --image=nginx -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Error from server (Forbidden): pods "test" is forbidden:</span>
<span class="comment">#   violates PodSecurity "restricted:latest":</span>
<span class="comment">#   allowPrivilegeEscalation != false</span>
<span class="comment">#   unrestricted capabilities</span>
<span class="comment">#   runAsNonRoot != true</span>

<span class="comment"># Pin to a specific K8s version for consistent behavior</span>
kubectl label namespace easypj \
  pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Pod Security Standard Decision Tree</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 Is it a system namespace (kube-system, etc.)?
 ├── YES → Use "privileged" (system components need elevated privileges)
 └── NO
     ├── Does it run trusted, well-known images only?
     │   ├── YES → Use "baseline" (prevents obvious attacks)
     │   └── NO  → Use "restricted" (maximum security)
     └── Multi-tenant cluster?
         └── ALWAYS → Use "restricted" + "warn" on next level
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.2 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-2">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.2 LimitRange vs ResourceQuota — Complete Comparison</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>These two resources work together but solve different problems. Understanding their differences is critical for the CKA.</p>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📏 LimitRange + ResourceQuota = Complete Resource Governance</h5>
                    <p>LimitRange sets guardrails for <em>individual</em> pods (defaults, min, max). ResourceQuota caps the <em>total</em> namespace consumption. Use both together — LimitRange provides defaults so pods satisfy ResourceQuota's requirement that all pods have limits.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="lifecycle-chain">
                    <div class="lc-pill active">Pod Created</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill">LimitRange Injects Defaults</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill">LimitRange Validates Min/Max</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill">ResourceQuota Checks Totals</div>
                    <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="lc-pill active">Pod Scheduled</div>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>LimitRange</th><th>ResourceQuota</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Per-container / per-pod defaults</td><td>Namespace-wide totals</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>What it controls</strong></td><td>Default, min, max for individual pods</td><td>Total aggregate consumption across all pods</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Applies when</strong></td><td>Pod is created (sets defaults)</td><td>Namespace hits capacity limits</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>If not set</strong></td><td>Pods get no default limits</td><td>No cap on total namespace usage</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Primary purpose</strong></td><td>Prevent one pod from being too large/small</td><td>Prevent one team from consuming all cluster resources</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">limitrange-vs-quota.yaml — both working together</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># LimitRange — defaults and bounds for INDIVIDUAL pods/containers</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
  name: easypj-limits
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  limits:
    - type: Container
      default:              <span class="comment"># Applied if container doesn't specify limits</span>
        cpu: 200m
        memory: 256Mi
      defaultRequest:       <span class="comment"># Applied if container doesn't specify requests</span>
        cpu: 100m
        memory: 128Mi
      max:                  <span class="comment"># No container can exceed these</span>
        cpu: "2"
        memory: 2Gi
      min:                  <span class="comment"># No container can request less than these</span>
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 64Mi
    - type: Pod
      max:                  <span class="comment"># Total pod limits (sum of all containers)</span>
        cpu: "4"
        memory: 4Gi
---
<span class="comment"># ResourceQuota — aggregate limits for the ENTIRE namespace</span>
apiVersion: v1
kind: ResourceQuota
metadata:
  name: easypj-quota
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  hard:
    requests.cpu: "8"         <span class="comment"># Total CPU requests across all pods</span>
    requests.memory: 16Gi
    limits.cpu: "16"
    limits.memory: 32Gi
    pods: "30"                <span class="comment"># Max 30 pods in namespace</span>
    services: "10"
    persistentvolumeclaims: "10"
    secrets: "20"
    configmaps: "20"
    replicationcontrollers: "10"
    services.loadbalancers: "2"
    services.nodeports: "5"</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — checking limits and quotas</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View LimitRange settings</span>
kubectl describe limitrange easypj-limits -n easypj

<span class="comment"># View ResourceQuota usage</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Name:            easypj-quota</span>
<span class="comment"># Resource         Used   Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># --------         ---    ----</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.cpu       600m   16</span>
<span class="comment"># limits.memory    768Mi  32Gi</span>
<span class="comment"># pods             3      30</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu     300m   8</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.memory  384Mi  16Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Test: try to create a pod that exceeds LimitRange max</span>
kubectl run big --image=nginx -n easypj \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"containers":[{"name":"big","image":"nginx","resources":{"limits":{"cpu":"5"}}}]}}'
<span class="comment"># Error: cpu max constraint (2) exceeded</span></code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.3 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-3">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.3 PID Limits — Preventing Fork Bombs</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p><strong>PID limits</strong> restrict the number of process IDs a pod can use, preventing fork bombs and runaway process creation from destabilizing the entire node.</p>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kubelet-config.yaml — PID limits</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Kubelet configuration — set per node</span>
apiVersion: kubelet.config.k8s.io/v1beta1
kind: KubeletConfiguration
podPidsLimit: 1024           <span class="comment"># Max PIDs per pod</span>
<span class="comment"># System reserved PIDs are configured separately:</span>
<span class="comment"># --system-reserved=pid=1000</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">terminal — checking PID usage</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check current PID allocation on a node</span>
kubectl get --raw /api/v1/nodes/&lt;node-name&gt;/proxy/stats/summary | \
  jq '.pods[] | select(.podRef.name | startswith("easypj")) |
  {name: .podRef.name, pids: .process_stats.process_count}'

<span class="comment"># Check kubelet PID limit setting</span>
kubectl get --raw /api/v1/nodes/&lt;node-name&gt;/proxy/configz | \
  jq '.kubeletconfig.podPidsLimit'</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box note">
                    <h5>📝 PID Limits and the CKA</h5>
                    <p>PID limits are configured at the kubelet level, not via YAML manifests. On the CKA, you may see this in troubleshooting scenarios where pods are evicted due to PID pressure. Check <code>kubectl describe node</code> for the <code>PIDPressure</code> condition.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.4 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-4">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.4 Topology Spread Constraints</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p><strong>Topology Spread Constraints</strong> ensure pods are evenly distributed across failure domains (nodes, zones, regions). This prevents all replicas landing on one node or in one availability zone.</p>

                <!-- Anatomy card: 4 key fields -->
                <div class="card-grid cols-4">
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #e74c3c;">
                        <h4>🎯 maxSkew</h4>
                        <p>Maximum allowed pod count difference between topology domains. Lower = more even. <code>1</code> means perfect balance.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #3498db;">
                        <h4>🗺️ topologyKey</h4>
                        <p>Node label that defines domains: <code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code> (per-node) or <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code> (per-zone).</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #f39c12;">
                        <h4>⚖️ whenUnsatisfiable</h4>
                        <p><code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard — block) or <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft — best-effort). Choose per constraint.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="info-card" style="border-left: 4px solid #2ecc71;">
                        <h4>🏷️ labelSelector</h4>
                        <p>Selects which pods to count for skew calculation. Must match the pod template's own labels.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">topology-spread.yaml — even distribution for easypj</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 6
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-web
    spec:
      topologySpreadConstraints:
        <span class="comment"># Spread evenly across zones</span>
        - maxSkew: 1
          topologyKey: topology.kubernetes.io/zone
          whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
          labelSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj-web
        <span class="comment"># Spread evenly across nodes within each zone</span>
        - maxSkew: 1
          topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
          whenUnsatisfiable: ScheduleAnyway
          labelSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj-web
      containers:
        - name: django
          image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1</code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th><th>Values</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>maxSkew</code></td><td>Maximum difference between zone/node pod counts</td><td>Integer ≥ 1 (lower = more even)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>topologyKey</code></td><td>Node label to group topology domains</td><td><code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code>, <code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code></td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>whenUnsatisfiable</code></td><td>What to do if constraint can't be met</td><td><code>DoNotSchedule</code> (hard), <code>ScheduleAnyway</code> (soft)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>labelSelector</code></td><td>Which pods to count in the topology calculation</td><td>Must match the pod template labels</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Topology Spread Visualization</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 Without Topology Spread          With maxSkew=1, by zone
 ┌────────────────────┐          ┌────────────────────┐
 │ Zone A             │          │ Zone A             │
 │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ │          │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ │
 │ │Node1 │ │Node2 │ │          │ │Node1 │ │Node2 │ │
 │ │██████│ │      │ │          │ │██    │ │██    │ │
 │ │██████│ │      │ │          │ └──────┘ └──────┘ │
 │ └──────┘ └──────┘ │          │ 2 pods             │
 │ 6 pods             │          ├────────────────────┤
 ├────────────────────┤          │ Zone B             │
 │ Zone B             │          │ ┌──────┐ ┌──────┐ │
 │ ┌──────┐          │          │ │Node3 │ │Node4 │ │
 │ │Node3 │          │          │ │██    │ │██    │ │
 │ │      │          │          │ └──────┘ └──────┘ │
 │ └──────┘          │          │ 2 pods             │
 │ 0 pods             │          ├────────────────────┤
 ├────────────────────┤          │ Zone C             │
 │ Zone C             │          │ ┌──────┐          │
 │ ┌──────┐          │          │ │Node5 │          │
 │ │Node5 │          │          │ │██    │          │
 │ │      │          │          │ └──────┘          │
 │ └──────┘          │          │ 2 pods             │
 │ 0 pods             │          └────────────────────┘
 └────────────────────┘          Evenly distributed!
  All eggs in one basket!
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.5 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-5">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.5 External Policy Engines — OPA Gatekeeper & Kyverno</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>

                <p>For complex policy requirements beyond what ValidatingAdmissionPolicy and Pod Security Standards provide, organizations use external policy engines. These are <strong>not on the CKA exam</strong> but are critical for production environments.</p>

                <div class="comparison-table">
                    <table>
                        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>OPA Gatekeeper</th><th>Kyverno</th><th>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>Policy language</strong></td><td>Rego (custom DSL)</td><td>YAML (native K8s)</td><td>CEL (simple expressions)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Learning curve</strong></td><td>Steep (learn Rego)</td><td>Low (YAML-based)</td><td>Low (CEL expressions)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Mutation support</strong></td><td>Yes (v3.7+)</td><td>Yes (native)</td><td>No (validation only)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Generation support</strong></td><td>No</td><td>Yes (generate resources)</td><td>No</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Report/audit mode</strong></td><td>Yes (audit constraint)</td><td>Yes (audit mode)</td><td>Yes (Audit action)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>Requires webhook?</strong></td><td>Yes (runs as webhook)</td><td>Yes (runs as webhook)</td><td>No (built into API server)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>CKA exam?</strong></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (may appear)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <!-- Decision tree: which policy engine? -->
                <div class="decision-tree">
                    <div class="decision-node question">Do you need only validation (no mutation/generation)?</div>
                    <div class="decision-node yes">YES → Is it a simple per-resource rule (e.g., require labels, enforce limits)?
                        <div class="decision-node yes">YES → Use <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (built-in, no extra components)</div>
                        <div class="decision-node no">NO → Need cross-resource or complex logic? → <strong>OPA Gatekeeper</strong></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="decision-node no">NO → Do you need to mutate resources or auto-generate defaults?
                        <div class="decision-node yes">YES → Use <strong>Kyverno</strong> (YAML-native, supports validate + mutate + generate)</div>
                        <div class="decision-node no">NO → Combine <strong>ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (simple rules) + external engine (complex rules)</div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <h4>OPA Gatekeeper Example — ConstraintTemplate</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">gatekeeper-example.yaml — require labels (Rego)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># ConstraintTemplate defines the policy logic in Rego</span>
apiVersion: templates.gatekeeper.sh/v1
kind: ConstraintTemplate
metadata:
  name: k8srequiredlabels
spec:
  crd:
    spec:
      names:
        kind: K8sRequiredLabels
      validation:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          properties:
            labels:
              type: array
              items:
                type: string
  targets:
    - target: admission.k8s.gatekeeper.sh
      rego: |
        package k8srequiredlabels
        violation[{"msg": msg}] {
          provided := {label | input.review.object.metadata.labels[label]}
          required := {label | label := input.parameters.labels[_]}
          missing := required - provided
          count(missing) > 0
          msg := sprintf("Missing required labels: %v", [missing])
        }
---
<span class="comment"># Constraint applies the template with specific parameters</span>
apiVersion: constraints.gatekeeper.sh/v1beta1
kind: K8sRequiredLabels
metadata:
  name: require-team-label
spec:
  match:
    kinds:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        kinds: ["Namespace"]
  parameters:
    labels: ["team", "environment"]</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h4>Kyverno Example — ClusterPolicy</h4>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">kyverno-example.yaml — require labels (YAML)</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: kyverno.io/v1
kind: ClusterPolicy
metadata:
  name: require-team-label
spec:
  validationFailureAction: Enforce
  rules:
    - name: check-team-label
      match:
        any:
          - resources:
              kinds:
                - Pod
      validate:
        message: "All pods must have a 'team' label"
        pattern:
          metadata:
            labels:
              team: "?*"         <span class="comment"># Must exist and be non-empty</span></code></pre>
                </div>

                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>💡 Which Policy Engine Should You Choose?</h5>
                    <p><strong>Start with ValidatingAdmissionPolicy</strong> (built-in, no extra components). If you need mutation or resource generation, add <strong>Kyverno</strong> (easiest to learn). If your organization already uses OPA or needs complex cross-resource policies, use <strong>Gatekeeper</strong>. In practice, many teams combine ValidatingAdmissionPolicy for simple rules with an external engine for complex ones.</p>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.6 ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-6">
                <h3 class="section-h">40.6 Combining Policies — The Complete Stack</h3>
                <span class="badge badge-concept">CONCEPT</span>
                <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>

                <p>In production, you use multiple policy types together. Here's the complete policy stack for the easypj namespace:</p>

                <div class="diagram-container">
                    <h4>Complete Policy Stack for easypj Namespace</h4>
                    <div class="diagram-box">
                        <pre>
 ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
 │              easypj NAMESPACE — POLICY STACK                │
 ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
 │                                                              │
 │  1. Pod Security Admission (enforce=restricted)             │
 │     └─ Blocks: privileged, hostNetwork, root, no seccomp    │
 │                                                              │
 │  2. ValidatingAdmissionPolicy (CEL)                         │
 │     ├─ Require minimum 2 replicas                           │
 │     ├─ Only approved image registries                       │
 │     └─ All containers need resource limits                  │
 │                                                              │
 │  3. LimitRange                                              │
 │     ├─ Default: 200m CPU / 256Mi memory                     │
 │     ├─ Max per container: 2 CPU / 2Gi memory                │
 │     └─ Min per container: 50m CPU / 64Mi memory             │
 │                                                              │
 │  4. ResourceQuota                                           │
 │     ├─ Total: 8 CPU / 16Gi memory                           │
 │     ├─ Max 30 pods                                          │
 │     └─ Max 2 LoadBalancers                                  │
 │                                                              │
 │  5. NetworkPolicy                                           │
 │     ├─ Default deny-all                                     │
 │     ├─ Allow internal namespace traffic                     │
 │     └─ Allow DNS egress to kube-system                      │
 │                                                              │
 │  6. PodDisruptionBudget                                     │
 │     └─ minAvailable: 2 (at least 2 pods during disruptions) │
 │                                                              │
 └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Scenario box: policy stack enforcement order -->
                <div class="scenario-box">
                    <h5>🎬 Scenario: What Happens When easypj Deploys a Pod?</h5>
                    <div class="lifecycle-chain">
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #e74c3c; color: white;">1. PSA Gate</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #e67e22; color: white;">2. Admission Policy (CEL)</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #f1c40f; color: #333;">3. LimitRange Defaults</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #2ecc71; color: white;">4. ResourceQuota Check</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #3498db; color: white;">5. Schedule + NetPolicy</div>
                        <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                        <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #9b59b6; color: white;">6. PDB Protected</div>
                    </div>
                    <p style="margin-top:12px;"><strong>If the pod runs <code>privileged: true</code></strong> → blocked at step 1 (PSA). <strong>If it has no resource limits</strong> → LimitRange injects defaults at step 3. <strong>If the namespace is over quota</strong> → rejected at step 4. Each layer adds defense-in-depth.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header"><span class="code-dot red"></span><span class="code-dot yellow"></span><span class="code-dot green"></span><span class="code-lang">terminal — verify the policy stack</span></div>
<pre><code class="language-bash">echo "=== Pod Security Standards ==="
kubectl get namespace easypj -o jsonpath='{.metadata.labels}' | jq .

echo "=== LimitRange ==="
kubectl get limitrange -n easypj

echo "=== ResourceQuota ==="
kubectl describe resourcequota -n easypj

echo "=== NetworkPolicies ==="
kubectl get networkpolicy -n easypj

echo "=== PodDisruptionBudgets ==="
kubectl get pdb -n easypj

echo "=== ValidatingAdmissionPolicies ==="
kubectl get validatingadmissionpolicybindings -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.spec.matchResources.namespaceSelector.matchLabels | 
  to_entries[]? | .value == "easypj" or .key == "kubernetes.io/metadata.name") |
  .metadata.name'</code></pre>
                </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §40.exam ─────────── -->
            <section class="section-block" id="s40-exam">
                <h3 class="section-h">📝 Chapter 40 — CKA Practice Questions</h3>
            </section>

            <!-- ─────────── UNDER THE HOOD ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-uth"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🔧 Under the Hood</h4>
                <p>Internals / deeper-dive for this chapter go here.</p>
            </div>

