            <!-- ─────────── LAB Q&A ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-lab"></span>
            <div class="chapter-placeholder">
                <h4>🧠 Chapter 40 — Lab Q&amp;A · Concept Check</h4>
                <p>Concept-check questions go here.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── SCENARIO & COMMAND DRILLS ─────────── -->
            <span class="ch-jump" id="ch40-scenario"></span>
            <div class="cka-exam-questions drills">
                <h4>⚙️ Chapter 40 — Scenario &amp; Command Drills (5)</h4>
                <p class="section-lead">Quick recall of policy enforcement mechanics — each with a full answer &amp; explanation.</p>
                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q40.1</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Apply the <code>baseline</code> Pod Security Standard to the <code>easypj</code> namespace in <code>warn</code> mode, and the <code>restricted</code> standard in <code>enforce</code> mode. Pin both to version <code>v1.30</code>.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash">kubectl label namespace easypj \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce-version=v1.30 \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=baseline \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn-version=v1.30 \
--overwrite

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get namespace easypj --show-labels</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>You can mix enforcement levels and modes. Here, <code>restricted</code> is enforced (blocks violations), while <code>baseline</code> in <code>warn</code> mode shows warnings for baseline violations that pass restricted enforcement. Pinning to a version (e.g., <code>v1.30</code>) ensures consistent behavior even after cluster upgrades. Use <code>--overwrite</code> if labels already exist.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q40.2</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a LimitRange in the <code>easypj</code> namespace that sets default container limits of 300m CPU and 512Mi memory, with a maximum of 1 CPU and 1Gi. Then create a pod WITHOUT specifying resources and verify the defaults were applied.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create LimitRange</span>
cat &lt;&lt;EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: LimitRange
metadata:
name: easypj-defaults
namespace: easypj
spec:
limits:
- type: Container
  default:
    cpu: 300m
    memory: 512Mi
  defaultRequest:
    cpu: 100m
    memory: 256Mi
  max:
    cpu: "1"
    memory: 1Gi
EOF

<span class="comment"># Create pod without resources</span>
kubectl run test-defaults --image=nginx -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Verify defaults were injected</span>
kubectl get pod test-defaults -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}' | jq .
<span class="comment"># {</span>
<span class="comment">#   "limits": { "cpu": "300m", "memory": "512Mi" },</span>
<span class="comment">#   "requests": { "cpu": "100m", "memory": "256Mi" }</span>
<span class="comment"># }</span>

<span class="comment"># Cleanup</span>
kubectl delete pod test-defaults -n easypj</code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>LimitRange injects default resource requests and limits into containers that don't specify them. This is done by the LimitRanger admission controller. The <code>default</code> field sets limits, <code>defaultRequest</code> sets requests. If you only set <code>default</code> without <code>defaultRequest</code>, the request defaults to the limit value. The <code>max</code> field rejects pods that exceed the maximum even if they explicitly set higher values.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q40.3</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a ResourceQuota in the <code>easypj</code> namespace limiting: 10 pods, 4 CPU requests, 8Gi memory requests, and 2 PVCs. Then check the current usage.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ResourceQuota</span>
kubectl create quota easypj-quota -n easypj \
--hard=pods=10,requests.cpu=4,requests.memory=8Gi,persistentvolumeclaims=2

<span class="comment"># Check usage</span>
kubectl describe resourcequota easypj-quota -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Name:                   easypj-quota</span>
<span class="comment"># Namespace:              easypj</span>
<span class="comment"># Resource                Used  Hard</span>
<span class="comment"># --------                ----  ----</span>
<span class="comment"># persistentvolumeclaims  0     2</span>
<span class="comment"># pods                    3     10</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.cpu            300m  4</span>
<span class="comment"># requests.memory         768Mi 8Gi</span>

<span class="comment"># Try to exceed (if 10 pods exist)</span>
kubectl run overflow --image=nginx -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Error: forbidden: exceeded quota: easypj-quota,</span>
<span class="comment"># requested: pods=1, used: pods=10, limited: pods=10</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p>The <code>kubectl create quota</code> imperative command is fast for the CKA. The <code>--hard</code> flag sets limits. <code>kubectl describe resourcequota</code> shows both <code>Used</code> and <code>Hard</code> columns so you can see current consumption. When a quota is exceeded, new resource creation is denied with a clear error message. Remember: when ResourceQuota exists, all pods MUST have resource requests/limits (or a LimitRange must provide defaults).</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q40.4</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Create a Deployment with topology spread constraints that distributes pods evenly across nodes (maxSkew=1, DoNotSchedule). Deploy 4 replicas of the easypj app.</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
name: easypj-spread
namespace: easypj
spec:
replicas: 4
selector:
matchLabels:
  app: easypj-spread
template:
metadata:
  labels:
    app: easypj-spread
spec:
  topologySpreadConstraints:
    - maxSkew: 1
      topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
      whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
      labelSelector:
        matchLabels:
          app: easypj-spread
  containers:
    - name: django
      image: easypj/jobpost:v2.1
      resources:
        requests:
          cpu: 100m
          memory: 128Mi
        limits:
          cpu: 200m
          memory: 256Mi</code></pre>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Verify distribution</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj -l app=easypj-spread -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME                     NODE      STATUS</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-spread-abc12      node-1    Running</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-spread-def34      node-2    Running</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-spread-ghi56      node-3    Running</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-spread-jkl78      node-1    Running  ← only if ≤3 nodes</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><code>maxSkew: 1</code> means the difference in pod count between any two nodes is at most 1. With 4 pods and 3 nodes, distribution would be 2-1-1, not 4-0-0. <code>DoNotSchedule</code> makes it a hard constraint — if the skew can't be maintained, the pod stays Pending. The <code>labelSelector</code> MUST match your pod labels so the scheduler counts existing pods correctly. Always include <code>resources</code> in production deployments.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>

                <div class="exam-question-item">
                    <div class="eq-number">Q40.5</div>
                    <div class="eq-question">Compare the three Pod Security Standard levels: privileged, baseline, and restricted. Which level should you use for the <code>kube-system</code> namespace? Which for a production application namespace? Why?</div>
                    <details>
                        <summary>Show Answer</summary>
                            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># kube-system — Use privileged (system components need elevated access)</span>
kubectl label namespace kube-system \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=privileged

<span class="comment"># Production app namespace — Use restricted (maximum security)</span>
kubectl label namespace easypj \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce=restricted \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted

<span class="comment"># Check pods that would violate restricted in kube-system</span>
kubectl label namespace kube-system \
pod-security.kubernetes.io/warn=restricted --overwrite --dry-run=server
<span class="comment"># Warning: would violate "restricted": kube-proxy, kube-apiserver, etc.</span></code></pre>
                        <div class="eq-explanation">
                            <div class="eq-exp-label">Explanation</div>
                            <p><strong>Privileged</strong>: No restrictions — for <code>kube-system</code> because system components (kube-proxy, CNI plugins, etc.) need host networking, privileged mode, and special capabilities. <strong>Baseline</strong>: Prevents known privilege escalations — good for general workloads that haven't been hardened yet. <strong>Restricted</strong>: Maximum security — for production application namespaces. Requires non-root, drops all capabilities, requires seccomp, etc. On the CKA, remember: <code>kube-system</code> = privileged, app namespaces = restricted, staging/dev = baseline as a stepping stone.</p>
                        </div>
                    </details>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- ─────────── VISUAL SUMMARY ─────────── -->
