            </div>
        </article>

        <!-- ==================== PART11 RECAP ==================== -->
        <article class="chapter" id="part11-recap">
            <section class="part-cover recap">
                <span class="part-kicker">🔧 Part 11 · Recap · Complete</span>
                <h2 class="part-cover-title">Extending &amp; Hardening — What You Now Know</h2>
                <p class="part-cover-lead">You can now make Kubernetes your own and lock it down: extend the API with CRDs, operators, and aggregation; apply a concrete hardening checklist across RBAC, secrets, audit, runtime, and supply chain; and enforce a secure baseline with Pod Security Standards and a policy engine (OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno, or CEL-based ValidatingAdmissionPolicy).</p>
            </section>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
                <h4>🔑 🔧 Part 11 — Key Takeaways</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Extending K8s (Ch 38):</strong> a CRD adds a new resource type; an operator is a custom controller that reconciles it; API aggregation adds whole new API servers — this is how the ecosystem (cert-manager, Prometheus, etc.) plugs in.</li>
                    <li><strong>Hardening (Ch 39):</strong> least-privilege RBAC, encrypt secrets at rest, enable audit logging, restrict the API server, secure the runtime (drop capabilities, non-root, read-only fs), and verify supply-chain provenance.</li>
                    <li><strong>Policies (Ch 40):</strong> Pod Security Standards (privileged/baseline/restricted) via admission; richer rules via OPA/Gatekeeper, Kyverno, or built-in CEL ValidatingAdmissionPolicy; layer them as defense-in-depth.</li>
                    <li><strong>Admission is the enforcement point:</strong> mutating then validating webhooks (and built-in policy controllers) gate every create/update before it persists.</li>
                    <li>🎯 <strong>Beyond the exam:</strong> CRDs/operators and policy engines are core to real platform engineering and multi-tenant safety.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="visual-summary">
                <div class="diagram-title">📌 Part 11 Visual Summary — extend the API, then govern every request</div>
<pre>
   ➕ EXTEND:  CRD (new kind) + Operator (controller) + API aggregation
        │
        ▼
   🚪 EVERY request ──▶ AuthN ──▶ AuthZ (RBAC) ──▶ 🛡️ ADMISSION
                                                   mutating ▶ validating
                                                   Pod Security · OPA/Kyverno · CEL
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   add your own resources/controllers, then fence the cluster behind policy.
</pre>
                <div class="vs-prose">
                    <p><strong>In words:</strong> Part 11 extended and secured the platform. CRDs + operators + aggregation let you add first-class resources and automation; the hardening checklist closes the obvious attack surface (RBAC, secrets, audit, runtime, supply chain); and Pod Security plus a policy engine enforce a compliant baseline at admission time.</p>
                    <p class="mb-0"><strong>The takeaway:</strong> think of admission (mutating ▶ validating) as the single chokepoint where Pod Security Standards and policy engines enforce your rules, and treat CRDs/operators as the way to make Kubernetes speak your domain — and you can both extend and harden a cluster for production. Next: the remaining production, observability, and exam-mastery material.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </article>

