        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-a">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">⌨️ Appendix A · kubectl Admin Command Reference</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Complete kubectl admin command reference with CKA speed aliases — sections A.1 through A.7. Every command uses the <strong>easypj</strong> project context. Bookmark this page for the exam.</p>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>Exam Day Setup</strong> — Run these aliases <strong>first</strong> when your exam terminal opens:</p>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">~/.bashrc — exam aliases</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash">alias k=kubectl
alias kgp='kubectl get pods'
alias kgs='kubectl get svc'
alias kgn='kubectl get nodes'
alias kd='kubectl describe'
alias kaf='kubectl apply -f'
alias kdel='kubectl delete'
export do='--dry-run=client -o yaml'
export now='--force --grace-period=0'

<span class="comment"># Apply immediately:</span>
source ~/.bashrc</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="info-box warning">
            <h5>⚠️ Context Switching — #1 Exam Killer</h5>
            <p>The CKA exam uses <strong>multiple clusters</strong>. Each question tells you which cluster to use. If you forget to switch, you'll modify the <strong>wrong cluster</strong> and lose points with no way to undo. Run the context command <strong>first</strong> for every question:</p>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — context switching (EVERY question!)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># The exam tells you: "Use context: k8s-cluster-1"</span>
kubectl config use-context k8s-cluster-1

<span class="comment"># Verify you're on the right cluster</span>
kubectl config current-context
<span class="comment"># k8s-cluster-1</span>

<span class="comment"># List all available contexts</span>
kubectl config get-contexts
<span class="comment"># CURRENT   NAME              CLUSTER           AUTHINFO</span>
<span class="comment"># *         k8s-cluster-1     k8s-cluster-1     admin</span>
<span class="comment">#           k8s-cluster-2     k8s-cluster-2     admin</span>

<span class="comment"># Set default namespace (saves typing -n every time)</span>
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=easypj</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>kubectl Command Flow — What Happens When You Run a Command</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
 ┌─────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────────┐
 │  YOU type:   │    │  ~/.kube/    │    │  API Server  │    │    etcd      │
 │  kubectl ... │───▶│  config      │───▶│  :6443       │───▶│  (store)     │
 │              │    │  (auth+ctx)  │    │  (AuthN/Z)   │    │              │
 └─────────────┘    └──────────────┘    └──────┬───────┘    └──────────────┘
                                               │
                    ┌──────────────────────────┬┴──────────────────────┐
                    ▼                          ▼                      ▼
             ┌─────────────┐         ┌──────────────┐       ┌──────────────┐
             │  Scheduler  │         │  Controller  │       │   kubelet    │
             │  (assigns   │         │  Manager     │       │  (runs pods  │
             │   nodes)    │         │  (reconcile) │       │   on nodes)  │
             └─────────────┘         └──────────────┘       └──────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Section A.1: Cluster Management Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.1 Cluster Management Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>These commands help you inspect the overall health and capabilities of your cluster. Use them <strong>first</strong> when troubleshooting any cluster issue.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Cluster Health Check Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
                        🏥 Cluster Health Check
                        ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
                                │
                    ┌───────────┼───────────┐
                    ▼           ▼           ▼
              kubectl       kubectl     kubectl
             cluster-info  get nodes   get cs
                    │           │           │
                    ▼           ▼           ▼
              ✅ API server  ✅ Nodes    ⚠️ Deprecated
              reachable?     Ready?      in v1.19+
                    │           │           │
                    ▼           ▼           ▼
               If ❌:       If NotReady:  Use instead:
              Check         SSH to node,  kubectl get
              kubeconfig    check kubelet componentstatuses
              + API pod     + containerd  (still works)
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Cluster Inspection Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Shows</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl cluster-info</code></td>
                            <td>API server URL + CoreDNS URL</td>
                            <td>First check — is the API reachable?</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get componentstatuses</code></td>
                            <td>scheduler, controller-manager, etcd health</td>
                            <td>Control plane component health check</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl top nodes</code></td>
                            <td>CPU/memory usage per node</td>
                            <td>Resource pressure diagnosis</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl top pods --all-namespaces</code></td>
                            <td>CPU/memory usage per pod</td>
                            <td>Find resource-hungry pods</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp</code></td>
                            <td>Recent cluster events (newest last)</td>
                            <td>Troubleshooting — what just happened?</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl api-resources</code></td>
                            <td>All resource types + short names + API group</td>
                            <td>Discover resource types and their short names</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl api-versions</code></td>
                            <td>All API group/versions available</td>
                            <td>Check if an API version exists</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — cluster inspection</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check cluster is reachable</span>
kubectl cluster-info
<span class="comment"># Kubernetes control plane is running at https://10.0.0.10:6443</span>
<span class="comment"># CoreDNS is running at https://10.0.0.10:6443/api/v1/namespaces/kube-system/...</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick health check</span>
kubectl get componentstatuses
<span class="comment"># NAME                 STATUS    MESSAGE</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler            Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager   Healthy   ok</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-0               Healthy   ok</span>

<span class="comment"># Resource usage (requires metrics-server)</span>
kubectl top nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME     CPU(cores)   CPU%   MEMORY(bytes)   MEMORY%</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1     213m         10%    1024Mi          25%</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1 156m         7%     768Mi           19%</span>

<span class="comment"># Find the most recent events</span>
kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | tail -20

<span class="comment"># Discover all resource types with short names</span>
kubectl api-resources --sort-by=name | head -20
<span class="comment"># NAME          SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION   NAMESPACED   KIND</span>
<span class="comment"># configmaps    cm           v1           true         ConfigMap</span>
<span class="comment"># deployments   deploy       apps/v1      true         Deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># pods          po           v1           true         Pod</span>
<span class="comment"># services      svc          v1           true         Service</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Short Names Save Time</h5>
                <p>Use short names on the exam: <code>po</code> (pods), <code>svc</code> (services), <code>deploy</code> (deployments), <code>cm</code> (configmaps), <code>ns</code> (namespaces), <code>no</code> (nodes), <code>pv</code> (persistentvolumes), <code>pvc</code> (persistentvolumeclaims), <code>sa</code> (serviceaccounts), <code>netpol</code> (networkpolicies), <code>ing</code> (ingresses).</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.2: Node Management ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.2 Node Management</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Node management is a core CKA skill — you'll cordon, drain, and uncordon nodes during upgrades and maintenance.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Node Maintenance Lifecycle</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    NODE MAINTENANCE WORKFLOW                            │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                         │
  │   ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐    ┌──────────┐     │
  │   │  NORMAL  │    │ CORDON   │    │    DRAIN     │    │ UNCORDON │     │
  │   │          │───▶│          │───▶│              │───▶│          │     │
  │   │ Schedul- │    │ No new   │    │ Evict all    │    │ Allow    │     │
  │   │ able ✅  │    │ pods ⛔  │    │ pods 🚀      │    │ pods ✅  │     │
  │   └──────────┘    └──────────┘    └──────────────┘    └──────────┘     │
  │                                          │                              │
  │                                   ┌──────┴──────┐                      │
  │                                   │  PERFORM    │                      │
  │                                   │  MAINTENANCE│                      │
  │                                   │  (upgrade,  │                      │
  │                                   │   reboot)   │                      │
  │                                   └─────────────┘                      │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Node Management Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA Context</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get nodes -o wide</code></td>
                            <td>List nodes with IP, OS, kernel, container runtime</td>
                            <td>First check during troubleshooting</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl describe node &lt;name&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Full node details: conditions, capacity, pods, taints</td>
                            <td>Diagnose NotReady, resource pressure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Mark unschedulable (existing pods stay)</td>
                            <td>Pre-maintenance — stop new pods</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data</code></td>
                            <td>Cordon + evict all pods (except DaemonSets)</td>
                            <td>Safely evacuate before maintenance</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Mark schedulable again</td>
                            <td>After maintenance — resume scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl taint nodes &lt;node&gt; key=value:NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Add taint — only pods with matching toleration can schedule</td>
                            <td>Isolate nodes for specific workloads</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl label nodes &lt;node&gt; disk=ssd</code></td>
                            <td>Add label — used with nodeSelector for scheduling</td>
                            <td>Target pods to specific nodes</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — node maintenance for easypj</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Check current node status</span>
kubectl get nodes -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS   ROLES           VERSION   INTERNAL-IP   OS-IMAGE</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1       Ready    control-plane   v1.31.0   10.0.0.10     Ubuntu 22.04</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   Ready    &lt;none&gt;          v1.31.0   10.0.0.11     Ubuntu 22.04</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-2   Ready    &lt;none&gt;          v1.31.0   10.0.0.12     Ubuntu 22.04</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Prepare worker-1 for maintenance</span>
kubectl cordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 cordoned</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Drain it (move easypj pods to worker-2)</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
<span class="comment"># evicting pod easypj/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-xk2vn</span>
<span class="comment"># pod/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-xk2vn evicted</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify pods moved</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj -o wide
<span class="comment"># NAME                          READY   NODE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q   1/1     worker-2  ← moved here</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Perform maintenance (upgrade kubelet, etc.)</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Bring node back</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1
<span class="comment"># node/worker-1 uncordoned</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Drain Fails? Common Causes</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Unmanaged pods</strong> — Pods not managed by a controller (bare pods). Fix: add <code>--force</code> flag (pods will be deleted, not rescheduled!)</li>
                    <li><strong>PodDisruptionBudget violated</strong> — PDB prevents eviction. Fix: check PDB with <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>, adjust <code>minAvailable</code></li>
                    <li><strong>emptyDir data loss</strong> — Need <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code> flag to acknowledge data loss</li>
                    <li><strong>DaemonSet pods</strong> — Always need <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> (they're present on every node)</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.3: Workload Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.3 Workload Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Deployment lifecycle commands — create, scale, update, rollback. These are your bread and butter on the CKA exam.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Deployment Rollout Lifecycle</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  CREATE ──▶ SCALE ──▶ UPDATE ──▶ ROLLBACK
    │          │         │          │
    ▼          ▼         ▼          ▼
  kubectl    kubectl   kubectl    kubectl
  create     scale     set image  rollout undo
  deploy     deploy    deploy/    deploy/
  --image    --rep=5   name       name
    │          │         │          │
    ▼          ▼         ▼          ▼
  1 replica   5 reps   Rolling    Previous
  created     running  update     revision
                       started    restored

  📊 MONITOR: kubectl rollout status deploy/name
  📜 HISTORY: kubectl rollout history deploy/name
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Deployment &amp; Workload Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Effect</th><th>CKA Speed Tip</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3</code></td>
                            <td>Create deployment with 3 replicas</td>
                            <td>Fastest way — no YAML needed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5</code></td>
                            <td>Scale to 5 replicas</td>
                            <td>Instant — no rolling update</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl set image deploy/easypj-web web=easypj:v2</code></td>
                            <td>Update container image (triggers rolling update)</td>
                            <td>Container name must match spec</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout status deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Watch rollout progress</td>
                            <td>Blocks until complete or failed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout history deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Show revision history</td>
                            <td>See all past versions</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web</code></td>
                            <td>Rollback to previous revision</td>
                            <td>Instant rollback — saves exam time</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl rollout undo deploy/easypj-web --to-revision=2</code></td>
                            <td>Rollback to specific revision</td>
                            <td>When previous isn't enough</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — easypj deployment lifecycle</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create deployment imperatively</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Generate YAML without creating (exam trick!)</span>
kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --replicas=3 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; deploy.yaml

<span class="comment"># Scale up for traffic</span>
kubectl scale deployment easypj-web --replicas=5 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Update to new version (triggers rolling update)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web web=easypj:v2 -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Watch the rollout</span>
kubectl rollout status deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Waiting for deployment "easypj-web" rollout to finish: 2 of 5 updated...</span>
<span class="comment"># deployment "easypj-web" successfully rolled out</span>

<span class="comment"># Something wrong? Rollback!</span>
kubectl rollout undo deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># deployment.apps/easypj-web rolled back</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p>The <code>--dry-run=client -o yaml</code> combo is your best friend. Use it to generate YAML for any resource, then edit as needed. With the <code>$do</code> alias: <code>k create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 $do &gt; deploy.yaml</code></p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.4: Debugging Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.4 Debugging Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Debugging is <strong>30% of the CKA exam</strong>. Master these commands — they're your diagnostic toolkit.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Pod Debugging Decision Tree</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
                        🔍 Pod Not Working?
                        ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
                                │
               ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
               ▼                ▼                ▼
          Pod Status?      Container        Can't reach
               │           crashing?        the service?
               │                │                │
    ┌──────────┼──────┐        ▼                ▼
    ▼          ▼      ▼   kubectl logs     kubectl get
  Pending  CrashLoop  Image  pod -c ctr    endpoints svc
    │      BackOff    Pull     │                │
    ▼        ▼       BackOff   ▼                ▼
  describe  logs     describe  Fix app      Empty? →
  pod →     pod →    pod →     crash or     Labels don't
  check     check    check     missing      match between
  events    app      image/    config       pod &amp; service
            error    secret
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Debug &amp; Inspect Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Shows</th><th>Debug Scenario</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Full pod details, events, conditions</td>
                            <td>First stop for ANY pod issue</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Container stdout/stderr</td>
                            <td>App crash, startup failure</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code></td>
                            <td>Logs from previous (crashed) container</td>
                            <td>CrashLoopBackOff — see WHY it crashed</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Logs from specific container in multi-container pod</td>
                            <td>Sidecar / init container issues</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- /bin/sh</code></td>
                            <td>Open shell inside container</td>
                            <td>Check files, env vars, network from inside</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl port-forward pod/&lt;pod&gt; 8080:8000</code></td>
                            <td>Forward local 8080 → pod 8000</td>
                            <td>Test pod directly without Service</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=&lt;pod&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Events for specific pod only</td>
                            <td>Targeted event search</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; --as=&lt;user&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Check RBAC permissions</td>
                            <td>Forbidden errors — who can do what?</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — debugging easypj pods</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Pod stuck in CrashLoopBackOff — investigate</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  BackOff  kubelet  Back-off restarting failed container</span>

<span class="comment"># Get logs from the crashed container</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-crash -n easypj --previous
<span class="comment"># ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'easypj.settings'</span>

<span class="comment"># Shell into a running pod to investigate</span>
kubectl exec -it easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q -n easypj -- /bin/sh
<span class="comment"># $ env | grep DB          ← check environment variables</span>
<span class="comment"># $ cat /app/manage.py     ← check files exist</span>
<span class="comment"># $ python manage.py check ← test Django</span>
<span class="comment"># $ exit</span>

<span class="comment"># Test pod connectivity directly</span>
kubectl port-forward pod/easypj-web-7d4b8c6f9-m8p3q 8080:8000 -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Then: curl http://localhost:8080/jobs/</span>

<span class="comment"># Check who can create pods in easypj namespace</span>
kubectl auth can-i create pods -n easypj --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Log Gotcha: Multi-Container Pods</h5>
                <p>If a pod has multiple containers (sidecar pattern), <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt;</code> will fail with <strong>"a container name must be specified"</strong>. Always use <code>-c &lt;container-name&gt;</code>. List containers with: <code>kubectl get pod &lt;name&gt; -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].name}'</code></p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.5: RBAC Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.5 RBAC Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) commands for managing who can do what in your cluster. These are frequently tested on the CKA exam.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>RBAC Components — The Permission Chain</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                    RBAC PERMISSION MODEL                           │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                    │
  │   WHO (Subject)         BINDING              WHAT (Role)           │
  │   ──────────────    ─────────────────    ──────────────────        │
  │                                                                    │
  │   👤 User            RoleBinding           Role                    │
  │   👥 Group           (namespace-scoped)    (namespace-scoped)      │
  │   🤖 ServiceAccount  ─────────────────    ──────────────────       │
  │                      ClusterRoleBinding    ClusterRole              │
  │                      (cluster-wide)        (cluster-wide)          │
  │                                                                    │
  │   Example for easypj:                                              │
  │   ┌────────────┐  ┌─────────────────┐  ┌──────────────────┐       │
  │   │ easypj-sa  │──│ easypj-binding  │──│ easypj-role      │       │
  │   │ (SA)       │  │ (RoleBinding)   │  │ get,list pods    │       │
  │   └────────────┘  └─────────────────┘  │ in ns: easypj   │       │
  │                                         └──────────────────┘       │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>RBAC Imperative Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Creates</th><th>Scope</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>Namespace-scoped Role</td>
                            <td>easypj namespace only</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create clusterrole node-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=nodes</code></td>
                            <td>Cluster-wide ClusterRole</td>
                            <td>All namespaces</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding --role=easypj-role --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></td>
                            <td>RoleBinding (links SA → Role)</td>
                            <td>easypj namespace</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl create clusterrolebinding admin-binding --clusterrole=cluster-admin --user=admin</code></td>
                            <td>ClusterRoleBinding</td>
                            <td>Cluster-wide</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa</code></td>
                            <td>Lists all permissions for that SA</td>
                            <td>Debug RBAC issues</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — RBAC setup for easypj</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ServiceAccount for easypj app</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Create Role: can read pods and services</span>
kubectl create role easypj-role \
  --verb=get,list,watch \
  --resource=pods,services \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Bind the role to the ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding \
  --role=easypj-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Verify permissions</span>
kubectl auth can-i list pods -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n easypj \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa
<span class="comment"># no  ← correct! We only granted get,list,watch</span>

<span class="comment"># List ALL permissions for the SA</span>
kubectl auth can-i --list \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa \
  -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.6: Certificate Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.6 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-6">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.6 Certificate Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Kubernetes uses TLS certificates extensively. Certificate management — checking expiry, renewing, and inspecting certs — is a core CKA topic.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Kubernetes Certificate Architecture</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              KUBERNETES PKI — /etc/kubernetes/pki/                  │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                     │
  │  📁 /etc/kubernetes/pki/                                            │
  │  ├── ca.crt + ca.key               ← Cluster CA (root of trust)    │
  │  ├── apiserver.crt + apiserver.key  ← API Server cert               │
  │  ├── apiserver-kubelet-client.crt   ← API → kubelet auth           │
  │  ├── front-proxy-ca.crt            ← Aggregation layer CA          │
  │  ├── sa.key + sa.pub               ← ServiceAccount signing        │
  │  └── etcd/                                                          │
  │      ├── ca.crt + ca.key           ← etcd CA (separate!)           │
  │      ├── server.crt + server.key   ← etcd server cert              │
  │      ├── peer.crt + peer.key       ← etcd peer-to-peer             │
  │      └── healthcheck-client.crt    ← etcd health check             │
  │                                                                     │
  │  ⏰ Default validity: 1 year (except CA: 10 years)                  │
  │  🔄 kubeadm certs renew all ← renews all 1-year certs              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>Certificate Management Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What It Does</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code></td>
                            <td>Show expiry dates for all certs</td>
                            <td>Regular health check / before renewal</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></td>
                            <td>Renew all kubeadm-managed certificates</td>
                            <td>Before expiry or after upgrade</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>openssl x509 -in &lt;cert&gt; -text -noout</code></td>
                            <td>Inspect certificate details (CN, SANs, dates)</td>
                            <td>Verify specific cert properties</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — certificate management</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Check expiration dates for ALL certs</span>
sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># admin.conf                 Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-etcd-client      Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># controller-manager.conf    Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-healthcheck-client    Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-peer                  Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># etcd-server                Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># front-proxy-client         Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># scheduler.conf             Dec 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   364d</span>
<span class="comment"># CA                         Dec 12, 2035 10:30 UTC   10y  ← CA lasts 10 years</span>

<span class="comment"># Renew all certificates</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># [renew] Reading configuration from the cluster...</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver-kubelet-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>

<span class="comment"># IMPORTANT: Restart control plane after renewal!</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Inspect a specific certificate</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A2 "Validity"
<span class="comment">#     Validity</span>
<span class="comment">#         Not Before: Dec 15 10:30:00 2025 GMT</span>
<span class="comment">#         Not After : Dec 15 10:30:00 2026 GMT</span>

<span class="comment"># Check Subject Alternative Names (SANs)</span>
openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -text -noout | grep -A1 "Subject Alternative"
<span class="comment">#     X509v3 Subject Alternative Name:</span>
<span class="comment">#         DNS:cp-1, DNS:kubernetes, DNS:kubernetes.default, IP:10.96.0.1, IP:10.0.0.10</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ After Renewing Certs</h5>
                <p>You <strong>must restart</strong> the control plane components after renewing certificates. With kubeadm, simply restart kubelet: <code>sudo systemctl restart kubelet</code>. The static pods (API server, controller-manager, scheduler) will be recreated automatically. If you forget this step, the old (expired) certs remain loaded in memory!</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section A.7: etcd Commands ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §A.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sa-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">A.7 etcd Commands</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-command">COMMANDS</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>etcd backup and restore is <strong>guaranteed on the CKA exam</strong>. Every command requires TLS flags — memorize the cert paths.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>etcd Backup &amp; Restore Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                 etcd BACKUP &amp; RESTORE WORKFLOW                     │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                     │
  │  📸 BACKUP                          🔄 RESTORE                      │
  │  ──────────                         ─────────                       │
  │                                                                     │
  │  etcdctl snapshot save              etcdctl snapshot restore        │
  │       │                                   │                         │
  │       ▼                                   ▼                         │
  │  /opt/backup/                        /var/lib/etcd-restored/        │
  │  etcd-snapshot.db                    (new data dir)                 │
  │       │                                   │                         │
  │       ▼                                   ▼                         │
  │  etcdctl snapshot                    Update etcd pod manifest:      │
  │  status (verify)                     --data-dir=/var/lib/           │
  │                                      etcd-restored                  │
  │                                           │                         │
  │                                           ▼                         │
  │  ⚠️ ALL COMMANDS REQUIRE:            Restart: etcd auto-restarts    │
  │  --cacert, --cert, --key             from static pod manifest       │
  │  + ETCDCTL_API=3                                                    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <h4>etcd Admin Commands</h4>
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Purpose</th><th>CKA Exam Note</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save &lt;file&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Create backup snapshot</td>
                            <td>Always specify full path: <code>/opt/backup/snapshot.db</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore &lt;file&gt;</code></td>
                            <td>Restore from snapshot</td>
                            <td>Use <code>--data-dir</code> to set new directory</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status &lt;file&gt; --write-table</code></td>
                            <td>Verify backup integrity</td>
                            <td>Shows hash, revision, total keys</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list</code></td>
                            <td>List etcd cluster members</td>
                            <td>Verify HA etcd cluster health</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — etcd backup &amp; restore (CKA exam)</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"># ═══ STEP 1: BACKUP ═══
<span class="comment"># The TLS flags are MANDATORY — memorize these paths!</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379
<span class="comment"># Snapshot saved at /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 2: VERIFY BACKUP ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot status /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --write-table
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># |   HASH   | REVISION | TOTAL KEYS | TOTAL SIZE |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>
<span class="comment"># | 3c5d8a2f |    48291 |       1043 |     4.1 MB |</span>
<span class="comment"># +----------+----------+------------+------------+</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 3: RESTORE ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/backup/etcd-snapshot.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># ═══ STEP 4: UPDATE ETCD MANIFEST ═══</span>
<span class="comment"># Edit the static pod manifest:</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml
<span class="comment"># Change: --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># To:     --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>
<span class="comment">#</span>
<span class="comment"># ALSO update the hostPath volume:</span>
<span class="comment"># path: /var/lib/etcd → path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># etcd will auto-restart with restored data</span>

<span class="comment"># ═══ VERIFY ═══</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl member list \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key
<span class="comment"># Shows: member ID, name, peer URLs, client URLs</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ The 3 Cert Flags — Don't Forget Any!</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt</code> — etcd CA certificate</li>
                    <li><code>--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt</code> — etcd server certificate</li>
                    <li><code>--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code> — etcd server private key</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Also: <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> is required. Without it, etcdctl defaults to API v2 which uses completely different commands. If your command returns an error about unknown flags, check this first!</p>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Restore Creates a NEW Data Directory</h5>
                <p>Never restore into the existing <code>/var/lib/etcd</code> directory. Always use a new path like <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>, then update the etcd static pod manifest to point to the new directory. This prevents data corruption and allows you to keep the old data as a safety net.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Appendix A Summary ─── -->
        <div class="summary-box">
            <h4>📋 Appendix A — Quick Reference Card</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.1</strong> — Cluster health: <code>cluster-info</code>, <code>top nodes</code>, <code>get events</code>, <code>api-resources</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.2</strong> — Node lifecycle: <code>cordon</code> → <code>drain</code> → maintain → <code>uncordon</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.3</strong> — Workloads: <code>create deploy</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>set image</code>, <code>rollout undo</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.4</strong> — Debug: <code>describe</code>, <code>logs --previous</code>, <code>exec -it</code>, <code>port-forward</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.5</strong> — RBAC: <code>create role</code>, <code>create rolebinding</code>, <code>auth can-i</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.6</strong> — Certs: <code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code>, <code>renew all</code>, <code>openssl x509</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>A.7</strong> — etcd: <code>snapshot save</code>, <code>snapshot restore</code>, always use TLS flags + <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </article>

    
        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-b">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">📝 Appendix B · CKA YAML Templates</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">12 fully annotated YAML templates covering all CKA exam resources — sections B.1 through B.12. Every template uses the <strong>easypj</strong> project with the <strong>jobpost</strong> app. Copy, adapt, deploy.</p>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>YAML Template Map — Which Template for Which Task?</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │            CKA YAML TEMPLATE QUICK-FIND MAP                            │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                                                         │
  │  🏗️ WORKLOADS               🌐 NETWORKING          🔒 SECURITY         │
  │  ─────────────              ─────────────          ─────────────        │
  │  B.1  Pod                   B.3  Service (3 types) B.6  Role+Binding    │
  │  B.2  Deployment            B.8  NetworkPolicy     B.7  ClusterRole     │
  │  B.10 PDB                   B.11 Ingress           B.9  ServiceAccount  │
  │                                                                         │
  │  💾 STORAGE                 ⚙️ CONFIGURATION                            │
  │  ─────────────              ─────────────                               │
  │  B.4  PV + PVC             B.12 ConfigMap                               │
  │  B.5  StorageClass         B.12 Secret                                  │
  │                                                                         │
  │  💡 Exam tip: Generate base YAML with --dry-run=client -o yaml          │
  │     then ADD fields like resources, probes, volumes from memory         │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>YAML Generation Shortcut:</strong> Never write YAML from scratch on the exam. Generate it first, then add what's missing:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><code>kubectl run easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; pod.yaml</code></li>
                <li><code>kubectl create deploy easypj-web --image=easypj:v1 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; deploy.yaml</code></li>
                <li><code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000 --dry-run=client -o yaml &gt; svc.yaml</code></li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Section B.1: Pod Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.1 Pod Template (with all common fields)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>The Pod is the atomic unit in Kubernetes. This template shows every field you'll encounter on the CKA exam.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Pod — Complete Exam Template</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Fields marked with ← are most commonly tested on the CKA exam</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1                          <span class="comment"># Always v1 for pods</span>
kind: Pod                               <span class="comment"># Object type</span>
metadata:
  name: easypj-web                      # ← Required: unique within namespace
  namespace: easypj                     # ← Omit for default namespace
  labels:                               # ← Key-value pairs for selection
    app: easypj
    tier: frontend
    version: v1
spec:
  containers:                           # ← List of containers (at least 1)
  - name: web                           <span class="comment"># Container name</span>
    image: easypj:v1                    # ← Image[:tag]
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000               <span class="comment"># Informational only — does NOT expose</span>
    env:                                # ← Environment variables
    - name: DEBUG
      value: "False"
    - name: DB_HOST
      valueFrom:
        configMapKeyRef:                # ← From ConfigMap
          name: easypj-config
          key: DB_HOST
    - name: SECRET_KEY
      valueFrom:
        secretKeyRef:                   # ← From Secret
          name: easypj-secret
          key: SECRET_KEY
    resources:                          # ← ALWAYS set in production
      requests:                         <span class="comment"># Scheduling guarantee</span>
        memory: "128Mi"
        cpu: "250m"
      limits:                           <span class="comment"># Hard ceiling</span>
        memory: "256Mi"
        cpu: "500m"
    readinessProbe:                     # ← When pod is ready for traffic
      httpGet:
        path: /health
        port: 8000
      initialDelaySeconds: 5
      periodSeconds: 10
    livenessProbe:                      # ← When to restart container
      httpGet:
        path: /health
        port: 8000
      initialDelaySeconds: 15
      periodSeconds: 20
    volumeMounts:                       # ← Mount volumes into container
    - name: app-data
      mountPath: /app/data
  volumes:                              # ← Define volumes at pod level
  - name: app-data
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: easypj-pvc
  restartPolicy: Always                 <span class="comment"># Always | OnFailure | Never</span>
  serviceAccountName: easypj-sa         # ← Explicit SA (security best practice)</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Pod Spec Structure — Visual Map</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Pod
   ├── metadata
   │    ├── name: easypj-web
   │    ├── namespace: easypj
   │    └── labels: {app: easypj}
   │
   └── spec
        ├── serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
        ├── restartPolicy: Always
        │
        ├── containers[]
        │    └── [0] web
        │         ├── image: easypj:v1
        │         ├── ports: [{containerPort: 8000}]
        │         ├── env: [{name, value/valueFrom}]
        │         ├── resources: {requests, limits}
        │         ├── readinessProbe: {httpGet}
        │         ├── livenessProbe: {httpGet}
        │         └── volumeMounts: [{name, mountPath}]
        │
        └── volumes[]
             └── [0] {name: app-data, pvc: easypj-pvc}
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Multi-Container Pod (Sidecar Pattern) — Exam Favourite</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">The CKA frequently asks you to add a sidecar container to an existing pod</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-with-logger
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  containers:
  - name: web                           <span class="comment"># Main application container</span>
    image: easypj:v1
    ports:
    - containerPort: 8000
    volumeMounts:
    - name: shared-logs
      mountPath: /var/log/app
  - name: log-sidecar                   # ← Sidecar: ships logs
    image: busybox:1.36
    command: ["sh", "-c", "tail -f /var/log/app/access.log"]
    volumeMounts:
    - name: shared-logs                 # ← Same volume = shared filesystem
      mountPath: /var/log/app
  initContainers:                       # ← Runs BEFORE main containers
  - name: init-db-check
    image: busybox:1.36
    command: ["sh", "-c", "until nslookup easypj-db-svc; do echo waiting; sleep 2; done"]
  volumes:
  - name: shared-logs
    emptyDir: {}                        # ← Temporary shared storage</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Multi-Container Pod Patterns</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  Pod: easypj-web-with-logger                                 │
  │                                                              │
  │  ┌─ INIT CONTAINER ──────────┐  Runs FIRST, must complete   │
  │  │  init-db-check            │  before main containers start │
  │  │  (waits for DB DNS)       │                               │
  │  └───────────────────────────┘                               │
  │           │ ✅ done                                           │
  │           ▼                                                  │
  │  ┌─ MAIN CONTAINERS (run in parallel) ───────────────────┐  │
  │  │                                                        │  │
  │  │  ┌─────────────┐    shared     ┌─────────────────┐    │  │
  │  │  │ web         │    volume     │ log-sidecar     │    │  │
  │  │  │ easypj:v1   │◄────────────▶│ busybox:1.36    │    │  │
  │  │  │ :8000       │  /var/log/app │ tail -f logs    │    │  │
  │  │  └─────────────┘               └─────────────────┘    │  │
  │  │                                                        │  │
  │  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.2: Deployment Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.2 Deployment Template (with strategy)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Deployments manage ReplicaSets which manage Pods. The <code>strategy</code> field controls how updates are rolled out.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Deployment — Rolling Update Strategy</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app: easypj
spec:
  replicas: 3                           # ← Desired pod count
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate                 # ← RollingUpdate | Recreate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1                       <span class="comment"># Extra pods during update (can be % or int)</span>
      maxUnavailable: 0                 # ← Zero-downtime: never go below replicas
  selector:
    matchLabels:                        # ← MUST match template.metadata.labels
      app: easypj
  template:                             # ← Pod template (same fields as B.1)
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj                     # ← MUST match selector.matchLabels
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: easypj-sa
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: easypj:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8000
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: "128Mi"
            cpu: "250m"
          limits:
            memory: "256Mi"
            cpu: "500m"
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 5</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Behavior</th><th>When to Use</th><th>Downtime?</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>RollingUpdate</strong></td>
                            <td>Gradually replaces pods one by one</td>
                            <td>Production — zero-downtime deploys</td>
                            <td>❌ No</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Recreate</strong></td>
                            <td>Kills ALL old pods, then creates new ones</td>
                            <td>Dev/test, or when old+new can't coexist</td>
                            <td>✅ Yes</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Label Mismatch = Deployment Stuck</h5>
                <p>If <code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code> doesn't match <code>spec.template.metadata.labels</code>, the Deployment can't find its pods. Kubernetes will reject the manifest outright. This is the #1 YAML mistake — always ensure these labels are identical.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.3: Service Templates ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.3 Service Templates (ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer)</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Services expose pods to the network. Three types, each for different access patterns.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Service Types — Access Levels</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                     │
  │  ☁️ INTERNET                                                        │
  │     │                                                               │
  │     ▼                                                               │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐                                                │
  │  │  LoadBalancer    │ ← Cloud provider provisions external LB       │
  │  │  (external IP)  │   Port: 80 → targetPort: 8000                 │
  │  └────────┬────────┘                                                │
  │           │                                                         │
  │     ┌─────┴─────┐                                                   │
  │     ▼           ▼                                                   │
  │  ┌────────┐  ┌────────┐                                             │
  │  │NodePort│  │NodePort│ ← Every node opens port 30000-32767        │
  │  │ :30080 │  │ :30080 │   Access: &lt;nodeIP&gt;:30080                 │
  │  └───┬────┘  └───┬────┘                                             │
  │      │           │                                                  │
  │      └─────┬─────┘                                                  │
  │            ▼                                                        │
  │     ┌─────────────┐                                                 │
  │     │  ClusterIP   │ ← Internal only (default)                     │
  │     │  10.96.0.100 │   Access: easypj-web-svc.easypj.svc:80       │
  │     └──────┬──────┘                                                 │
  │            │                                                        │
  │     ┌──────┴──────┐                                                 │
  │     ▼             ▼                                                 │
  │  ┌──────┐     ┌──────┐                                              │
  │  │ Pod  │     │ Pod  │ ← selector: app=easypj matches these        │
  │  │:8000 │     │:8000 │                                              │
  │  └──────┘     └──────┘                                              │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ClusterIP Service (internal only — default)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-svc
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  type: ClusterIP                       <span class="comment"># Default — can omit</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj                         # ← MUST match pod labels
  ports:
  - port: 80                            <span class="comment"># Service port (what clients connect to)</span>
    targetPort: 8000                    <span class="comment"># Container port (where app listens)</span>
    protocol: TCP</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>NodePort Service (external via node IP:port)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-nodeport
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  type: NodePort
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
  - port: 80                            <span class="comment"># Service port</span>
    targetPort: 8000                    <span class="comment"># Container port</span>
    nodePort: 30080                     # ← 30000-32767 range (omit for auto)</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>LoadBalancer Service (cloud provider LB)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-lb
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  selector:
    app: easypj
  ports:
  - port: 80                            <span class="comment"># External port</span>
    targetPort: 8000                    <span class="comment"># Container port</span>
  <span class="comment"># Cloud provider assigns EXTERNAL-IP automatically</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Access</th><th>Use Case</th><th>Imperative</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>ClusterIP</strong></td>
                            <td>Internal only</td>
                            <td>DB, internal APIs</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --port=80 --target-port=8000</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>NodePort</strong></td>
                            <td>External via nodeIP:port</td>
                            <td>Dev/test, on-prem</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --type=NodePort --port=80</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>LoadBalancer</strong></td>
                            <td>External via cloud LB</td>
                            <td>Production, cloud</td>
                            <td><code>kubectl expose deploy easypj-web --type=LoadBalancer --port=80</code></td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.4: PV + PVC Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.4 PersistentVolume + PVC Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>PVs are cluster-level storage resources; PVCs are namespace-scoped claims that bind to PVs. Admin creates PV, developer creates PVC.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>PV ↔ PVC Binding Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ADMIN creates:                  DEVELOPER creates:          Pod uses:
  ┌──────────────┐               ┌────────────────┐        ┌──────────┐
  │ PersistentVol│               │ PersistentVol  │        │   Pod    │
  │              │    BINDING    │ Claim          │ MOUNT  │          │
  │ capacity:10Gi│◄────────────▶│ request: 10Gi  │───────▶│ volumes: │
  │ accessMode:  │  (automatic) │ accessMode:    │        │  - pvc:  │
  │  RWO         │               │  RWO           │        │  easypj- │
  │ storageClass:│               │ storageClass:  │        │  pvc     │
  │  manual      │               │  manual        │        │          │
  └──────────────┘               └────────────────┘        └──────────┘
     ↑                              ↑
     Must match:                    Must match:
     - accessModes                  - storageClassName
     - storageClassName             - capacity ≤ PV capacity
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PersistentVolume (admin creates)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: easypj-pv                       <span class="comment"># PVs are cluster-scoped (no namespace)</span>
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi                       # ← Storage amount
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                       # ← RWO | ROX | RWX
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain # ← Retain | Delete | Recycle
  storageClassName: manual              # ← MUST match PVC
  hostPath:                             # ← Dev only! Use cloud storage in prod
    path: /data/easypj-db</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PersistentVolumeClaim (developer creates)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: easypj-pvc
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  accessModes:
  - ReadWriteOnce                       # ← Must match PV
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi                     # ← Must be ≤ PV capacity
  storageClassName: manual              # ← Must match PV</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Access Mode</th><th>Abbreviation</th><th>Description</th><th>Use Case</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>ReadWriteOnce</strong></td><td>RWO</td><td>Read-write by a single node</td><td>Database (PostgreSQL)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ReadOnlyMany</strong></td><td>ROX</td><td>Read-only by many nodes</td><td>Shared config/assets</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>ReadWriteMany</strong></td><td>RWX</td><td>Read-write by many nodes</td><td>Shared uploads (NFS)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.5: StorageClass Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.5 StorageClass Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>StorageClasses enable <strong>dynamic provisioning</strong> — no pre-created PVs needed. When a PVC references a StorageClass, Kubernetes automatically creates the PV.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Static vs Dynamic Provisioning</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  STATIC Provisioning (B.4)              DYNAMIC Provisioning (B.5)
  ──────────────────────────             ──────────────────────────

  1. Admin creates PV manually           1. Admin creates StorageClass
     apiVersion: v1                          apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
     kind: PersistentVolume                  kind: StorageClass
     capacity: 10Gi                          provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com
           │                                       │
  2. Dev creates PVC                     2. Dev creates PVC (same)
     storageClassName: manual                storageClassName: fast-storage
           │                                       │
  3. K8s binds PVC → PV                  3. K8s AUTO-CREATES PV + binds!
     (must find matching PV)                 (provisioner handles it)
           │                                       │
           ▼                                       ▼
  ⚠️ If no PV matches → Pending          ✅ PV created on-demand = no Pending

  💡 Exam: Dynamic is preferred in cloud. Static for hostPath/local dev.
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>StorageClass — Dynamic Provisioning</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: fast-storage
provisioner: kubernetes.io/no-provisioner  # ← Or: ebs.csi.aws.com, pd.csi.storage.gke.io
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer    # ← Delay until pod schedules (topology-aware)
reclaimPolicy: Delete                      # ← Delete PV when PVC is deleted
allowVolumeExpansion: true                 # ← Allow PVC resize</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Binding Mode</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Best For</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Immediate</strong></td><td>PV created as soon as PVC is created</td><td>Single-zone clusters</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>WaitForFirstConsumer</strong></td><td>PV created when first pod using PVC is scheduled</td><td>Multi-zone clusters (prevents zone mismatch)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.6: Role + RoleBinding Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.6 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-6">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.6 Role + RoleBinding Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Namespace-scoped RBAC — grants permissions within a specific namespace.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Role (namespace-scoped permissions)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  namespace: easypj                     # ← Scoped to this namespace
  name: easypj-role
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]                       # ← "" = core API group (pods, services, etc.)
  resources: ["pods", "services"]       # ← What resources
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]       # ← What actions allowed
- apiGroups: ["apps"]                   # ← apps group (deployments, replicasets)
  resources: ["deployments"]
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch", "update"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>RoleBinding (assigns role to subject)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  namespace: easypj                     # ← Same namespace as the Role
  name: easypj-binding
subjects:                               # ← WHO gets the permissions
- kind: ServiceAccount
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
roleRef:                                # ← WHICH role to assign
  kind: Role                            <span class="comment"># Role or ClusterRole</span>
  name: easypj-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Common API Groups</h5>
                <p><code>""</code> (core): pods, services, secrets, configmaps, PVCs, namespaces, nodes, events. <code>"apps"</code>: deployments, replicasets, statefulsets, daemonsets. <code>"rbac.authorization.k8s.io"</code>: roles, rolebindings. <code>"networking.k8s.io"</code>: networkpolicies, ingresses.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.7: ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.7 ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Cluster-wide RBAC — grants permissions across <strong>all namespaces</strong> or on cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs).</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ClusterRole (cluster-wide permissions)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: node-reader                     # ← No namespace (cluster-scoped)
rules:
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["nodes"]                  # ← Cluster-scoped resource
  verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
- apiGroups: [""]
  resources: ["persistentvolumes"]      # ← Also cluster-scoped
  verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ClusterRoleBinding</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding
metadata:
  name: node-reader-binding             # ← No namespace
subjects:
- kind: User
  name: jane
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: node-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Role + RoleBinding</th><th>ClusterRole + ClusterRoleBinding</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>Single namespace</td><td>All namespaces / cluster resources</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Use for</strong></td><td>App-level access (easypj pods)</td><td>Admin access (nodes, PVs)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Has namespace?</strong></td><td>Yes (in metadata)</td><td>No</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Example</strong></td><td>easypj-sa can read pods in easypj ns</td><td>jane can read nodes cluster-wide</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.8: NetworkPolicy Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.8 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-8">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.8 NetworkPolicy Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>NetworkPolicies control pod-to-pod traffic. By default, all pods can talk to all pods. A NetworkPolicy restricts that — <strong>deny by default, allow explicitly</strong>.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>NetworkPolicy — easypj Traffic Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  namespace: easypj                                              │
  │                                                                 │
  │  ┌──────────────┐     ✅ port 5432    ┌──────────────────┐     │
  │  │  easypj-web  │ ──────────────────▶ │  easypj-db       │     │
  │  │  app: easypj │                     │  app: easypj-db  │     │
  │  └──────────────┘                     └──────────────────┘     │
  │                                               ▲                 │
  │  ┌──────────────┐     ❌ BLOCKED              │                 │
  │  │  other-pod   │ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ┘                 │
  │  │  app: hacker │    NetworkPolicy denies                       │
  │  └──────────────┘    non-matching pods                          │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>NetworkPolicy — Allow Only easypj-web → easypj-db</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: db-restrict
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:                          # ← Apply to these pods
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-db
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress                             # ← Control incoming traffic
  ingress:
  - from:
    - podSelector:                      # ← Only allow FROM these pods
        matchLabels:
          app: easypj
    ports:
    - port: 5432                        # ← Only on PostgreSQL port
      protocol: TCP</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ NetworkPolicy Requires a CNI That Supports It</h5>
                <p>NetworkPolicies only work if your CNI plugin enforces them. <strong>Calico</strong>, <strong>Cilium</strong>, <strong>Weave Net</strong> support them. <strong>Flannel does NOT</strong>. On the CKA exam, the cluster uses a supporting CNI. If you apply a NetworkPolicy and traffic still flows unrestricted, check your CNI plugin.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Deny-All Default Policy (Common Exam Question!)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">Apply this FIRST, then allow specific traffic with additional policies</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: deny-all-ingress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}                       # ← Empty = applies to ALL pods in namespace
  policyTypes:
  - Ingress                             # ← Block all incoming traffic
  <span class="comment"># No ingress rules = nothing allowed</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 NetworkPolicy Logic</h5>
                <p><strong>No policy</strong> = all traffic allowed. <strong>Policy with empty podSelector</strong> = applies to all pods. <strong>Policy with no ingress rules</strong> = deny all ingress. Combine deny-all + specific allow policies for a "zero trust" network model. The CKA exam loves this pattern.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.9: ServiceAccount Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.9 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-9">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.9 ServiceAccount Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>ServiceAccounts provide identity for pods. Each pod runs as a ServiceAccount — always create a dedicated one instead of using <code>default</code>.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ServiceAccount</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-sa
  namespace: easypj
automountServiceAccountToken: false     # ← Security: don't auto-mount token
                                        <span class="comment">#   Only set true if pod needs API access</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Security Best Practice</h5>
                <p>Set <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> unless your pod actually needs to talk to the Kubernetes API. The default SA token mounted at <code>/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token</code> gives the pod API access. If compromised, attackers could query the API. The CKA exam may ask you to configure this.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — ServiceAccount lifecycle</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create SA imperatively</span>
kubectl create serviceaccount easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Assign to a pod (in pod spec)</span>
<span class="comment"># spec.serviceAccountName: easypj-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Verify which SA a pod uses</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.spec.serviceAccountName}'
<span class="comment"># easypj-sa</span>

<span class="comment"># Check if token is mounted</span>
kubectl exec easypj-web -n easypj -- ls /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/
<span class="comment"># ca.crt  namespace  token</span>

<span class="comment"># Full RBAC chain: create SA → create Role → bind them</span>
kubectl create sa easypj-sa -n easypj
kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n easypj
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding --role=easypj-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.10: PodDisruptionBudget Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.10 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-10">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.10 PodDisruptionBudget Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>PDBs protect your app during voluntary disruptions (node drain, cluster upgrade). They guarantee minimum availability.</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>PodDisruptionBudget</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-pdb
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  minAvailable: 2                       # ← At least 2 pods must stay running
  <span class="comment"># OR: maxUnavailable: 1              # ← At most 1 pod can be down</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj                       # ← Protects pods with this label</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example (3 replicas)</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>minAvailable: 2</code></td><td>Keep at least 2 running</td><td>Can evict max 1 at a time</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>maxUnavailable: 1</code></td><td>At most 1 can be down</td><td>Same effect for 3 replicas</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>minAvailable: "50%"</code></td><td>Keep at least 50% running</td><td>Can evict 1 of 3</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ PDB Blocks kubectl drain!</h5>
                <p>If a PDB prevents eviction, <code>kubectl drain</code> will hang indefinitely. Common CKA trap: you try to drain a node but it just waits. Check PDBs: <code>kubectl get pdb -A</code>. If <code>minAvailable</code> equals the current replica count and there's only one node, the drain can never succeed. You must either scale up first or temporarily lower the PDB's <code>minAvailable</code>.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.11: Ingress Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.11 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-11">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.11 Ingress Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>Ingress provides HTTP/HTTPS routing from external traffic to internal Services. Requires an Ingress Controller (e.g., nginx) to be installed in the cluster.</p>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Ingress Routing — easypj Example</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Internet
     │
     ▼
  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              Ingress Controller (nginx)                   │
  │                                                          │
  │  Rule 1: easypj.example.com/                             │
  │           └──▶ Service: easypj-web-svc:80                │
  │                                                          │
  │  Rule 2: easypj.example.com/api/                         │
  │           └──▶ Service: easypj-api-svc:80                │
  │                                                          │
  │  Rule 3: admin.example.com/                              │
  │           └──▶ Service: admin-svc:80                     │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
     │                    │                    │
     ▼                    ▼                    ▼
  ┌────────┐         ┌────────┐         ┌────────┐
  │easypj  │         │easypj  │         │ admin  │
  │web pods│         │api pods│         │ pods   │
  └────────┘         └────────┘         └────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Ingress — Host &amp; Path-Based Routing</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress
  namespace: easypj
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx               # ← Which Ingress Controller to use
  rules:
  - host: easypj.example.com            # ← Domain name routing
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /                         # ← URL path
        pathType: Prefix                # ← Prefix | Exact | ImplementationSpecific
        backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-web-svc        # ← Target Service name
            port:
              number: 80                # ← Target Service port
      - path: /api
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-api-svc
            port:
              number: 80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>pathType</th><th>Matches</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Prefix</strong></td><td>URL prefix match</td><td><code>/jobs</code> matches <code>/jobs</code>, <code>/jobs/123</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Exact</strong></td><td>Exact URL match only</td><td><code>/jobs</code> matches only <code>/jobs</code></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Ingress with TLS (HTTPS)</h4>
                <p class="skeleton-note">The CKA may ask you to configure TLS termination — create the Secret first</p>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create TLS secret</span>
<span class="comment"># kubectl create secret tls easypj-tls \</span>
<span class="comment">#   --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key -n easypj</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Reference in Ingress</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress-tls
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  tls:                                  # ← TLS termination
  - hosts:
    - easypj.example.com
    secretName: easypj-tls              # ← Secret with tls.crt + tls.key
  rules:
  - host: easypj.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: easypj-web-svc
            port:
              number: 80</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Section B.12: ConfigMap + Secret Template ─── -->
            <!-- ─────────── §B.12 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sb-12">
            <h3 class="section-h">B.12 ConfigMap + Secret Template</h3>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 CKA EXAM</span>
            <p>ConfigMaps store non-sensitive config; Secrets store sensitive data (base64-encoded, not encrypted by default).</p>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>ConfigMap</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj
data:                                   # ← Key-value pairs (plain text)
  DEBUG: "False"
  DB_HOST: "easypj-db-svc"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-yaml-skeleton">
                <h4>Secret (base64-encoded values)</h4>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secret
  namespace: easypj
type: Opaque                            # ← Most common type
data:                                   # ← Values MUST be base64-encoded
  SECRET_KEY: c2VjcmV0MTIz              <span class="comment"># echo -n 'secret123' | base64</span>
  DB_PASSWORD: cGFzc3dvcmQ=             <span class="comment"># echo -n 'password' | base64</span>
  DB_USER: YW5paHBq                     <span class="comment"># echo -n 'easypj' | base64</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — imperative ConfigMap/Secret creation</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Create ConfigMap imperatively</span>
kubectl create configmap easypj-config \
  --from-literal=DEBUG=False \
  --from-literal=DB_HOST=easypj-db-svc \
  --from-literal=ALLOWED_HOSTS='*' \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Create Secret imperatively (auto-encodes to base64!)</span>
kubectl create secret generic easypj-secret \
  --from-literal=SECRET_KEY=secret123 \
  --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=password \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Decode a secret value</span>
kubectl get secret easypj-secret -n easypj -o jsonpath='{.data.DB_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d
<span class="comment"># password</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>ConfigMap/Secret → Pod — Three Injection Methods</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  METHOD 1: Environment Variables (most common)                  │
  │  ──────────────────────────────────────────────                 │
  │  env:                                                           │
  │  - name: DB_HOST                                                │
  │    valueFrom:                                                   │
  │      configMapKeyRef:                                           │
  │        name: easypj-config                                      │
  │        key: DB_HOST                                             │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  METHOD 2: envFrom (all keys at once)                           │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────────                          │
  │  envFrom:                                                       │
  │  - configMapRef:                                                │
  │      name: easypj-config                                        │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  METHOD 3: Volume Mount (as files)                              │
  │  ─────────────────────────────────                              │
  │  volumes:                                                       │
  │  - name: config-vol                                             │
  │    configMap:                                                    │
  │      name: easypj-config                                        │
  │  volumeMounts:                                                  │
  │  - name: config-vol                                             │
  │    mountPath: /etc/config     ← Each key becomes a file         │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Secrets Aren't Encrypted by Default!</h5>
                <p>Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded, not encrypted. Anyone with API access can decode them. For real security: enable <strong>encryption at rest</strong> (<code>EncryptionConfiguration</code>), use <strong>RBAC</strong> to restrict Secret access, and consider external tools like <strong>HashiCorp Vault</strong> or <strong>Sealed Secrets</strong>.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Appendix B Summary ─── -->
        <div class="summary-box">
            <h4>📋 Appendix B — Template Quick Reference</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.1</strong> Pod — containers, env, resources, probes, volumes</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.2</strong> Deployment — replicas, strategy (RollingUpdate/Recreate), selector must match labels</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.3</strong> Service — ClusterIP (internal), NodePort (external:30000-32767), LoadBalancer (cloud)</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.4</strong> PV+PVC — capacity, accessModes, storageClassName must match</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.5</strong> StorageClass — dynamic provisioning, WaitForFirstConsumer</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.6</strong> Role+RoleBinding — namespace-scoped permissions</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.7</strong> ClusterRole+ClusterRoleBinding — cluster-wide permissions</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.8</strong> NetworkPolicy — pod-level firewall, requires supporting CNI</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.9</strong> ServiceAccount — pod identity, disable auto-mount token</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.10</strong> PDB — minAvailable or maxUnavailable during disruptions</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.11</strong> Ingress — HTTP routing by host/path, needs Ingress Controller</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>B.12</strong> ConfigMap+Secret — env vars, envFrom, volume mount</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </article>

    
        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-c">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">🚨 Appendix C · Common Errors and Solutions</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Quick reference table — Error → Cause → Fix for the most common Kubernetes issues you'll encounter on the CKA exam and in production.</p>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>Troubleshooting Decision Tree — Where to Start</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  Something is wrong!
        │
        ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Pod running?       ├───────────▶│ Check: logs, exec, describe       │
  │ kubectl get pods   │            │ → CrashLoopBackOff (app crash)    │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → OOMKilled (memory limit)        │
           │ NO                     └──────────────────────────────────┘
           ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Pod Pending?       ├───────────▶│ Check: describe pod events        │
  │                    │            │ → Insufficient resources          │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → No matching PV                  │
           │ NO                     │ → Taints/tolerations mismatch     │
           ▼                        └──────────────────────────────────┘
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ ImagePullBackOff?  ├───────────▶│ Check: image name, tag, registry  │
  │                    │            │ → Wrong image name/tag            │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → Missing imagePullSecrets        │
           │ NO                     └──────────────────────────────────┘
           ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐     YES     ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Node NotReady?     ├───────────▶│ SSH to node, check kubelet        │
  │ kubectl get nodes  │            │ → systemctl status kubelet        │
  └────────┬──────────┘            │ → journalctl -u kubelet           │
           │ NO                     └──────────────────────────────────┘
           ▼
  ┌───────────────────┐
  │ API/Network issue? │─────▶ Check kubeconfig, API server, certs
  └───────────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>Exam Debugging Shortcuts:</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt;</code> — Always start here; the <strong>Events</strong> section at the bottom shows why things failed</li>
                <li><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> — See logs from the LAST crashed container</li>
                <li><code>kubectl get events --sort-by='.lastTimestamp'</code> — Cluster-wide timeline of issues</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Error Reference Table ─── -->
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Error / Status</th>
                        <th>Category</th>
                        <th>Common Cause</th>
                        <th>How to Diagnose</th>
                        <th>Fix</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong></td>
                        <td>🏗️ Workload</td>
                        <td>App crashes on startup — missing env var, wrong command, config error</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code><br><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code></td>
                        <td>Fix app config/code. Check <code>command</code>, <code>args</code>, env vars. Ensure ConfigMap/Secret exists.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong></td>
                        <td>🏗️ Workload</td>
                        <td>Wrong image name/tag, private registry without credentials</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> → Events section</td>
                        <td>Fix image name. For private registries: create <code>imagePullSecrets</code> and reference in pod spec.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Pending</strong> (Pod)</td>
                        <td>🏗️ Workload</td>
                        <td>Insufficient CPU/memory, no node matches nodeSelector/affinity, all nodes tainted</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> → Events<br><code>kubectl describe node &lt;node&gt;</code></td>
                        <td>Reduce resource requests, add nodes, remove taints, or add tolerations.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Pending</strong> (PVC)</td>
                        <td>💾 Storage</td>
                        <td>No PV matches accessMode/capacity/storageClass</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;</code><br><code>kubectl get pv</code></td>
                        <td>Create matching PV or fix StorageClass name. Ensure <code>accessModes</code> and <code>storageClassName</code> match.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>NodeNotReady</strong></td>
                        <td>🖥️ Node</td>
                        <td>kubelet stopped, resource pressure (disk/memory), network partition</td>
                        <td><code>ssh &lt;node&gt;</code><br><code>systemctl status kubelet</code><br><code>journalctl -u kubelet</code></td>
                        <td>Restart kubelet: <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code>. Free disk/memory. Check network connectivity.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>FailedScheduling</strong></td>
                        <td>🖥️ Node</td>
                        <td>All nodes tainted (no matching tolerations), nodeAffinity mismatch</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> → Events<br><code>kubectl describe node &lt;node&gt;</code> → Taints</td>
                        <td>Add <code>tolerations</code> to pod spec, or remove taint: <code>kubectl taint node &lt;n&gt; key:NoSchedule-</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>Forbidden</strong></td>
                        <td>🔒 RBAC</td>
                        <td>ServiceAccount/User lacks permissions</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;resource&gt; --as=system:serviceaccount:ns:sa</code></td>
                        <td>Create or update Role/ClusterRole + Binding to grant required permissions.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>connection refused</strong></td>
                        <td>🌐 Network</td>
                        <td>Target pod/service not running, wrong port, NetworkPolicy blocking</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl get svc</code><br><code>kubectl get endpoints</code><br><code>kubectl get netpol</code></td>
                        <td>Ensure target pod is running and healthy. Verify Service selector matches pod labels. Check NetworkPolicy rules.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>certificate has expired</strong></td>
                        <td>🔐 Certs</td>
                        <td>Kubernetes PKI cert past its validity period (typically 1 year)</td>
                        <td><code>kubeadm certs check-expiration</code><br><code>openssl x509 -in /etc/kubernetes/pki/apiserver.crt -noout -dates</code></td>
                        <td><code>kubeadm certs renew all</code> then restart control plane:<br><code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>etcd cluster is unavailable</strong></td>
                        <td>🗄️ etcd</td>
                        <td>etcd pod down, cert expired, disk full</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl -n kube-system get pods -l component=etcd</code><br><code>crictl ps | grep etcd</code></td>
                        <td>Check etcd logs. Verify certs in <code>/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/</code>. Restore from backup if data corrupt.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>unable to connect to server</strong></td>
                        <td>⚙️ API</td>
                        <td>Wrong kubeconfig, API server pod down, firewall blocking port 6443</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl cluster-info</code><br><code>cat ~/.kube/config</code> — check server URL<br><code>crictl ps | grep kube-api</code></td>
                        <td>Fix kubeconfig server URL. Restart API server. Check firewall rules for port 6443.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>OOMKilled</strong></td>
                        <td>🏗️ Workload</td>
                        <td>Container exceeded its memory limit</td>
                        <td><code>kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;</code> → Last State: OOMKilled</td>
                        <td>Increase <code>resources.limits.memory</code>, or fix memory leak in application.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Detailed Fixes for Top 5 Errors ─── -->
        <!-- ─────────── §C.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block">
            <h3 class="section-h">Top 5 CKA Exam Errors — Detailed Fix Walkthroughs</h3>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 CKA Debug Pattern (memorize this!)</h5>
                <p><code>describe</code> → <code>logs</code> → <code>exec</code> → <code>events</code>. Start with <code>describe</code> for events, check <code>logs</code> for app errors, <code>exec</code> into the pod to test connectivity, and <code>get events</code> for cluster-wide issues.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — CrashLoopBackOff deep debug</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check pod status and restart count</span>
kubectl get pod easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># NAME         READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE</span>
<span class="comment"># easypj-web   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5          2m</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. See why it crashed (logs from PREVIOUS container)</span>
kubectl logs easypj-web -n easypj --previous
<span class="comment"># ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'django'</span>
<span class="comment"># → Fix: wrong image or missing dependency</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Check events for scheduling/pull issues</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web -n easypj | tail -20
<span class="comment"># Events:</span>
<span class="comment">#   Warning  BackOff  kubelet  Back-off restarting failed container</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. If env var issue — check ConfigMap exists</span>
kubectl get configmap easypj-config -n easypj
kubectl get secret easypj-secret -n easypj</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — Pending Pod / FailedScheduling debug</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check WHY it's pending</span>
kubectl describe pod easypj-web -n easypj | grep -A5 Events
<span class="comment"># 0/3 nodes are available: 3 Insufficient memory</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check node resources</span>
kubectl top nodes
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep -A5 "Allocated resources"

<span class="comment"># 3. If taint issue — check node taints</span>
kubectl describe node worker-1 | grep Taints
<span class="comment"># Taints: node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Fix: add toleration to pod OR remove taint</span>
kubectl taint node worker-1 node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane:NoSchedule-</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — RBAC Forbidden debug</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Test what the SA can do</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># no</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. Check existing roles/bindings</span>
kubectl get roles,rolebindings -n easypj
kubectl get clusterroles,clusterrolebindings | grep easypj

<span class="comment"># 3. Fix: create the missing Role + RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n easypj
kubectl create rolebinding easypj-binding --role=easypj-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># yes</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — NodeNotReady recovery</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Identify the problem node</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME       STATUS     ROLES           AGE    VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1       Ready      control-plane   30d    v1.31.0</span>
<span class="comment"># worker-1   NotReady   &lt;none&gt;          30d    v1.31.0  ← PROBLEM</span>

<span class="comment"># 2. SSH to the affected node</span>
ssh worker-1

<span class="comment"># 3. Check kubelet status</span>
systemctl status kubelet
<span class="comment"># Active: inactive (dead)  ← kubelet stopped!</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Check kubelet logs for the root cause</span>
journalctl -u kubelet --no-pager | tail -30
<span class="comment"># "failed to run Kubelet: unable to load bootstrap kubeconfig"</span>
<span class="comment"># → OR: "eviction threshold met: memory.available"</span>

<span class="comment"># 5. Fix and restart</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet
sudo systemctl enable kubelet

<span class="comment"># 6. Back on control plane — verify recovery</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-1   Ready   &lt;none&gt;   30d   v1.31.0  ← RECOVERED</span>

<span class="comment"># 7. If disk pressure — free space on the node</span>
ssh worker-1
df -h                                   <span class="comment"># Check disk usage</span>
sudo crictl rmi --prune                 <span class="comment"># Remove unused images</span>
sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=100M      <span class="comment"># Trim system logs</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — certificate expired recovery</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Symptom: kubectl commands return "certificate has expired"</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># Unable to connect to the server: x509: certificate has expired</span>

<span class="comment"># 1. SSH to the control plane node (kubectl won't work!)</span>
ssh cp-1

<span class="comment"># 2. Check which certs are expired</span>
sudo kubeadm certs check-expiration
<span class="comment"># CERTIFICATE                EXPIRES                  RESIDUAL TIME</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver                  Jan 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   EXPIRED  ← !</span>
<span class="comment"># apiserver-kubelet-client   Jan 15, 2026 10:30 UTC   EXPIRED</span>

<span class="comment"># 3. Renew all certificates</span>
sudo kubeadm certs renew all
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># certificate apiserver-kubelet-client renewed</span>
<span class="comment"># ...</span>
<span class="comment"># Done renewing certificates.</span>

<span class="comment"># 4. Restart kubelet (restarts static pods with new certs)</span>
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># 5. Update admin kubeconfig (your kubectl config)</span>
sudo cp /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf ~/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) ~/.kube/config

<span class="comment"># 6. Verify everything works</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># NAME   STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION</span>
<span class="comment"># cp-1   Ready    control-plane   30d   v1.31.0  ← WORKING!</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Debug Command Quick Reference Card</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │              WHICH COMMAND FOR WHICH ERROR?                          │
  ├───────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  ERROR                │  FIRST COMMAND TO RUN                       │
  ├───────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │  CrashLoopBackOff     │  kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous             │
  │  ImagePullBackOff     │  kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;                │
  │  Pending (pod)        │  kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;                │
  │  Pending (PVC)        │  kubectl describe pvc &lt;name&gt;               │
  │  NodeNotReady         │  ssh node → systemctl status kubelet       │
  │  FailedScheduling     │  kubectl describe pod → check taints       │
  │  Forbidden            │  kubectl auth can-i &lt;verb&gt; &lt;res&gt; --as=SA  │
  │  connection refused   │  kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;               │
  │  cert expired         │  ssh cp → kubeadm certs check-expiration   │
  │  etcd unavailable     │  crictl ps | grep etcd                     │
  │  can't connect to API │  cat ~/.kube/config (check server URL)     │
  │  OOMKilled            │  kubectl describe pod → Last State          │
  └───────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Appendix C Summary ─── -->
        <div class="summary-box">
            <h4>🚨 Appendix C — Quick Debug Cheat Sheet</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ <strong>CrashLoopBackOff</strong> → <code>logs --previous</code> → fix config/code</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>ImagePullBackOff</strong> → check image name, add imagePullSecrets</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Pending Pod</strong> → <code>describe pod</code> → resources, taints, affinity</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Pending PVC</strong> → match PV accessMode + storageClassName + capacity</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>NodeNotReady</strong> → SSH, <code>systemctl restart kubelet</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Forbidden</strong> → <code>auth can-i</code> → create Role + RoleBinding</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>connection refused</strong> → verify pod running + Service selector + NetworkPolicy</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>cert expired</strong> → <code>kubeadm certs renew all</code></li>
                <li>✅ <strong>etcd unavailable</strong> → check etcd pod/certs, restore from backup</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>unable to connect</strong> → fix kubeconfig, check API server</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </article>

    
        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-d">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">📖 Appendix D · Kubernetes Terminology Glossary</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">110+ Kubernetes terms organized alphabetically across 22 letter sections (A–W) with CKA-relevant definitions, object hierarchy diagrams, and resource scope reference. Bookmark this glossary — if you see an unfamiliar term on the CKA exam, this is your quick-reference.</p>

        <!-- Quick Navigation -->
        <div class="info-box note" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(99,102,241,0.1), rgba(139,92,246,0.1)); border-left: 4px solid #8b5cf6; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h4>🔤 Jump to Letter</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 2.2;">
