        <!-- Kubernetes Architecture Context Diagram -->
        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>Where These Terms Fit — Kubernetes Architecture Map</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  ┌──────────────────────────── CONTROL PLANE ────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                       │
  │  ┌────────────┐  ┌──────────────┐  ┌─────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  │
  │  │ API Server │  │  Scheduler   │  │ Controller  │  │    etcd    │  │
  │  │            │  │              │  │  Manager    │  │  (key-val  │  │
  │  │ AuthN/AuthZ│  │ NodeSelector │  │ ReplicaSet  │  │   store)   │  │
  │  │ Admission  │  │ Affinity     │  │ Deployment  │  │            │  │
  │  │ RBAC       │  │ Taints       │  │ DaemonSet   │  │ Backup/    │  │
  │  │            │  │ Tolerations  │  │ StatefulSet │  │ Restore    │  │
  │  └─────┬──────┘  └──────────────┘  └─────────────┘  └────────────┘  │
  └────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
           │
  ┌────────┼──────────────── WORKER NODES ────────────────────────────────┐
  │        ▼                                                              │
  │  ┌──────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌─────────────────────────────────┐   │
  │  │ kubelet  │  │ kube-proxy │  │           PODS                  │   │
  │  │ (agent)  │  │ (network   │  │  ┌──────────┐  ┌─────────────┐ │   │
  │  │          │  │  rules)    │  │  │Container │  │  Sidecar    │ │   │
  │  │ Probes   │  │ Services   │  │  │ Image    │  │  Init Ctr   │ │   │
  │  │ cgroups  │  │ iptables   │  │  │ Volumes  │  │  emptyDir   │ │   │
  │  └──────────┘  └────────────┘  │  │ Env/CM/  │  │             │ │   │
  │                                │  │  Secret  │  │             │ │   │
  │  ┌──────────────────────────┐  │  └──────────┘  └─────────────┘ │   │
  │  │ Container Runtime        │  └─────────────────────────────────┘   │
  │  │ (containerd / CRI-O)     │                                        │
  │  │ OCI images, CNI plugins  │                                        │
  │  └──────────────────────────┘                                        │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>Exam Tip:</strong> You won't be asked definitions directly, but understanding these terms helps you parse question wording correctly. The CKA uses precise terminology — knowing the difference between <code>cordon</code> vs <code>drain</code>, or <code>Role</code> vs <code>ClusterRole</code> saves critical seconds.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Kubernetes Object Hierarchy Diagram -->
        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>Kubernetes Object Ownership Hierarchy</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                        DEPLOYMENT                                │
  │          (Desired state: replicas, image, strategy)              │
  └───────────┬─────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┘
              │ creates &amp; manages            │ creates on update
              ▼                              ▼
  ┌─────────────────────┐      ┌─────────────────────────┐
  │   ReplicaSet (v2)   │      │   ReplicaSet (v1)       │
  │   replicas: 3        │      │   replicas: 0 (scaled   │
  │   ✅ current         │      │   down after rollout)   │
  └───────┬──┬──┬────────┘      └─────────────────────────┘
          │  │  │ creates &amp; maintains desired count
          ▼  ▼  ▼
  ┌─────┐ ┌─────┐ ┌─────┐
  │Pod-0│ │Pod-1│ │Pod-2│    Each pod gets:
  └──┬──┘ └──┬──┘ └──┬──┘    • Unique IP
     │       │       │        • Shared network namespace
     ▼       ▼       ▼        • ownerReference → ReplicaSet
  ┌──────┐┌──────┐┌──────┐
  │ Ctr  ││ Ctr  ││ Ctr  │   Container = running image
  │nginx ││nginx ││nginx │   (the actual process)
  └──────┘└──────┘└──────┘

  🔑 Ownership chain: Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod → Container
  🗑️ Delete Deployment = cascading delete of ALL children
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Resource Scope Quick Reference -->
        <div class="info-box tip">
            <h5>🔍 Resource Scope Quick Reference</h5>
            <p>Every K8s resource is either <strong>cluster-scoped</strong> or <strong>namespace-scoped</strong>. This determines where you can create it and how RBAC applies.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th style="width:50%;">🌐 Cluster-Scoped (no namespace)</th>
                        <th style="width:50%;">📁 Namespace-Scoped</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Node, PersistentVolume, StorageClass</td><td>Pod, Deployment, ReplicaSet, StatefulSet</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Namespace (yes, it's cluster-scoped!)</td><td>Service, Endpoints, Ingress</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>ClusterRole, ClusterRoleBinding</td><td>Role, RoleBinding, ServiceAccount</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CustomResourceDefinition (CRD)</td><td>ConfigMap, Secret, PVC</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>PriorityClass, RuntimeClass</td><td>Job, CronJob, DaemonSet, HPA</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>CSIDriver, CSINode</td><td>NetworkPolicy, PodDisruptionBudget</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>IngressClass</td><td>LimitRange, ResourceQuota</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="cka-gotcha">
            <h5>⚠️ CKA Trap: Forgetting -n namespace</h5>
            <p>If you create a Role but forget <code>-n &lt;namespace&gt;</code>, it goes to <code>default</code>. Always specify namespace explicitly on the exam: <code>kubectl create role myrole --verb=get --resource=pods <strong>-n target-ns</strong></code></p>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── A ─── -->
        <div id="gd-a" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">A</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Admission Controller</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Plugin that intercepts API requests <em>after</em> authentication/authorization but <em>before</em> persistence. Examples: <code>LimitRanger</code>, <code>PodSecurity</code>, <code>MutatingWebhook</code>. Can mutate or validate objects.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Affinity</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Rules controlling pod placement. <code>nodeAffinity</code> targets specific nodes; <code>podAffinity</code>/<code>podAntiAffinity</code> attracts/repels pods relative to other pods. Softer than nodeSelector.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Annotation</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Key-value metadata attached to objects. Unlike labels, annotations are <strong>NOT</strong> used for selection. Used for: build info, config hints (<code>nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target</code>), tool metadata.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>API Group</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Logical grouping of API resources. Core group: <code>""</code> (pods, services). Named groups: <code>apps</code> (deployments), <code>batch</code> (jobs), <code>networking.k8s.io</code> (networkpolicies), <code>rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>API Server</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Central management hub of Kubernetes (<code>kube-apiserver</code>). All <code>kubectl</code> commands and internal components communicate through it. Runs on port <strong>6443</strong>. Handles AuthN → AuthZ → Admission → etcd persistence.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── B ─── -->
        <div id="gd-b" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">B</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Base64</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Encoding (NOT encryption) used by Secrets. <code>echo -n "password" | base64</code> to encode; <code>echo "cGFzc3dvcmQ=" | base64 -d</code> to decode. Anyone with RBAC access to the Secret can decode it.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Binding</strong></td><td></td><td>API object that ties a pod to a node. The Scheduler creates a Binding when it assigns a pod. Rarely used directly — understanding helps when debugging <code>Pending</code> pods.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Blue-Green Deployment</strong></td><td></td><td>Deployment strategy: run two identical environments (blue=current, green=new), switch traffic instantly via Service selector change. Not built-in — implemented using label swaps on Services.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── C ─── -->
        <div id="gd-c" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">C</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Calico</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>CNI plugin providing pod networking <strong>and</strong> NetworkPolicy enforcement. Most popular choice for CKA-style clusters. Supports BGP routing and IPIP tunneling.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CertificateSigningRequest</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>API object to request a certificate signed by the cluster CA. Used for user authentication: create CSR → approve → extract cert. <code>kubectl certificate approve &lt;name&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>cgroup</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Linux kernel feature that limits, accounts for, and isolates resource usage (CPU, memory, I/O) of process groups. Kubernetes uses cgroups to enforce <code>resources.limits</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CIDR</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Classless Inter-Domain Routing — notation for IP address ranges. Example: <code>10.244.0.0/16</code> = pod network, <code>10.96.0.0/12</code> = service network. Used in <code>--pod-network-cidr</code> and NetworkPolicy.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ClusterIP</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Default Service type — accessible only <strong>within</strong> the cluster. Gets a virtual IP from the service CIDR. Access via <code>&lt;svc-name&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ClusterRole</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Cluster-wide permission set (no namespace). Used for cluster-scoped resources (nodes, PVs) or cross-namespace access. Bound via <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CNI</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Container Network Interface — plugin standard for pod networking. Provides IP allocation and inter-pod connectivity. Examples: Calico, Flannel, Cilium, Weave Net.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ConfigMap</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Stores non-sensitive configuration as key-value pairs. Injected into pods via: environment variables (<code>envFrom</code>), individual env vars (<code>configMapKeyRef</code>), or volume mounts (files).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Container Runtime</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Software that actually runs containers. Kubernetes supports any CRI-compatible runtime. Standard: <code>containerd</code>. Alternative: <code>CRI-O</code>. Docker was removed in v1.24.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>containerd</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Industry-standard container runtime. Manages the complete container lifecycle: pull images, create containers, manage storage, networking. Default runtime for most K8s distributions.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Control Plane</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>The "brain" of the cluster running on master/control-plane nodes. Components: API Server, etcd, Scheduler, Controller Manager. Manages cluster state and makes scheduling decisions.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CoreDNS</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>DNS server built into Kubernetes (runs as a Deployment in <code>kube-system</code>). Resolves service names: <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>. Debug: <code>kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox -- nslookup &lt;svc&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Cordon</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Mark a node as <strong>unschedulable</strong> — no new pods will be placed, but existing pods remain. <code>kubectl cordon &lt;node&gt;</code>. First step before maintenance. Reverse: <code>uncordon</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CRI</strong></td><td></td><td>Container Runtime Interface — protocol between kubelet and container runtime. Replaced Docker-specific Dockershim in K8s v1.24.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CronJob</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Runs Jobs on a schedule (cron syntax). Example: <code>schedule: "0 2 * * *"</code> = daily at 2 AM. Creates Job objects which create Pods. Good for backups, report generation.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CSI</strong></td><td></td><td>Container Storage Interface — standard for exposing storage systems to Kubernetes. Drivers: <code>ebs.csi.aws.com</code>, <code>pd.csi.storage.gke.io</code>, <code>disk.csi.azure.com</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>CRD (CustomResourceDefinition)</strong></td><td></td><td>Extend the Kubernetes API with your own resource types. Combined with a custom controller creates an <strong>Operator</strong>. Example: <code>kind: Certificate</code> (cert-manager).</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── D ─── -->
        <div id="gd-d" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">D</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>DaemonSet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Ensures exactly <strong>one pod per node</strong> (or per matching node). Used for: log collectors (Fluentd), monitoring agents (node-exporter), CNI plugins, kube-proxy. Pods survive <code>kubectl drain --ignore-daemonsets</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Deployment</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Manages ReplicaSets for <strong>declarative updates</strong>. Supports rolling updates, rollbacks, and scaling. The most common workload controller: <code>kubectl create deployment easypj-web --image=easypj:v1</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Drain</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Evict <strong>all pods</strong> from a node for maintenance. Cordons the node first, then evicts pods. Flags: <code>--ignore-daemonsets</code> (required), <code>--delete-emptydir-data</code>. Managed pods are rescheduled elsewhere.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── E ─── -->
        <div id="gd-e" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">E</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>emptyDir</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Temporary volume created when a pod starts, <strong>deleted when pod is removed</strong>. Shared between containers in the same pod (sidecar pattern). <code>volumes: [{name: shared, emptyDir: {}}]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Endpoint</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>IP:port combination backing a Service. When a Service selector matches pods, Kubernetes auto-creates Endpoints. Debug: <code>kubectl get endpoints &lt;svc&gt;</code> — if empty, selector/labels mismatch.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>etcd</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Distributed key-value store holding <strong>all cluster state</strong>. Runs on control plane (port 2379). Backup: <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>. Restore: <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code>. Always requires TLS flags.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── F ─── -->
        <div id="gd-f" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">F</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Finalizer</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Metadata key that prevents object deletion until cleanup is done. When you delete an object with finalizers, it enters <code>Terminating</code> state. The controller removes the finalizer after cleanup, then K8s completes deletion.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Flannel</strong></td><td></td><td>Simple CNI plugin using overlay networking (VXLAN). Easy to set up but does <strong>NOT</strong> support NetworkPolicies. Use Calico or Cilium instead for production.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>FQDN</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Fully Qualified Domain Name for Services: <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>. Same-namespace pods can use just <code>&lt;svc&gt;</code>. Cross-namespace requires at least <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;ns&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── G ─── -->
        <div id="gd-g" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">G</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Garbage Collection</strong></td><td></td><td>Automatic cleanup of orphaned objects. When a Deployment is deleted, its ReplicaSets and Pods are garbage collected via <strong>ownerReferences</strong>. Cascade modes: <code>Foreground</code>, <code>Background</code>, <code>Orphan</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Gateway API</strong></td><td></td><td>Next-gen routing API (successor to Ingress). Resources: <code>GatewayClass</code>, <code>Gateway</code>, <code>HTTPRoute</code>. More expressive than Ingress — supports header-based routing, traffic splitting, and cross-namespace references.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>GracePeriod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Time Kubernetes waits for a pod to shut down gracefully (default: <strong>30 seconds</strong>). Override: <code>kubectl delete pod &lt;name&gt; --grace-period=0 --force</code>. During this time, SIGTERM is sent, then SIGKILL after timeout.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── H ─── -->
        <div id="gd-h" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">H</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Helm</strong></td><td></td><td>Kubernetes package manager. Bundles YAML manifests into <strong>Charts</strong>. Install: <code>helm install my-release chart-name</code>. Not on CKA exam directly, but widely used in practice.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>hostPath</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Volume that mounts a file/directory from the <strong>host node's filesystem</strong> into a pod. Used for: dev/test, static pods reading node configs. <strong>Not safe for production</strong> — data only on one node.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Automatically scales pod replicas based on CPU/memory metrics (requires metrics-server). <code>kubectl autoscale deploy easypj-web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── I ─── -->
        <div id="gd-i" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">I</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Ingress</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>API object defining HTTP/HTTPS routing rules. Routes external traffic to Services based on host/path. Requires an <strong>Ingress Controller</strong> (nginx, traefik). Supports TLS termination.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Ingress Controller</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Component that <em>implements</em> Ingress rules. It's a pod running a reverse proxy (nginx, HAProxy). Without it, Ingress resources have no effect. Install: <code>kubectl apply -f nginx-ingress.yaml</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Init Container</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Runs <strong>before</strong> main containers. Must complete successfully before app containers start. Used for: waiting for dependencies, database migrations, config generation. Defined in <code>spec.initContainers[]</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── J ─── -->
        <div id="gd-j" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">J</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Job</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Run-to-completion workload. Pod runs once (or N times), then stops. <code>restartPolicy: Never</code> or <code>OnFailure</code>. Used for: backups, data processing, one-time migrations.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>JSONPath</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Query language for extracting data from JSON output. <code>kubectl get pods -o jsonpath='{.items[*].metadata.name}'</code>. Powerful for scripting and CKA exam output formatting.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── K ─── -->
        <div id="gd-k" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">K</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>kubeadm</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Official tool to bootstrap Kubernetes clusters. <code>kubeadm init</code> (control plane), <code>kubeadm join</code> (workers), <code>kubeadm upgrade</code> (version upgrades), <code>kubeadm certs</code> (certificate management). Core CKA tool.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kubectl</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>CLI for communicating with the Kubernetes API server. Every CKA task uses it. Config: <code>~/.kube/config</code>. Structure: <code>kubectl [verb] [resource] [name] [flags]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kubelet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Agent running on <strong>every node</strong>. Receives pod specs from API server, ensures containers are running and healthy. Manages probes, reports node status. Not a pod — it's a systemd service: <code>systemctl status kubelet</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kube-proxy</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Network proxy on each node maintaining network rules (iptables/IPVS). Implements Service abstraction — routes traffic to pod endpoints. Runs as a DaemonSet in <code>kube-system</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>kube-scheduler</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Control plane component that assigns pods to nodes. Considers: resource requests, nodeSelector, affinity, taints/tolerations, PV topology. If it can't find a node → pod stays <code>Pending</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Kustomize</strong></td><td></td><td>Kubernetes-native config management (built into <code>kubectl apply -k</code>). Overlays patches on base YAML without modifying originals. Alternative to Helm for simpler use cases.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── L ─── -->
        <div id="gd-l" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">L</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Label</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Key-value pair attached to objects for <strong>identification and selection</strong>. Used by selectors, services, deployments: <code>app: easypj</code>. Filter: <code>kubectl get pods -l app=easypj</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Lease</strong></td><td></td><td>Lightweight object for leader election and node heartbeats. kubelet creates a Lease in <code>kube-node-lease</code> namespace — if it stops updating, node is marked <code>NotReady</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>LimitRange</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Sets default/min/max resource constraints <strong>per pod/container</strong> in a namespace. If a pod doesn't specify resources, LimitRange can inject defaults. Prevents runaway resource consumption.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Liveness Probe</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Health check: "is the container alive?" If it fails, kubelet <strong>restarts</strong> the container. Types: <code>httpGet</code>, <code>tcpSocket</code>, <code>exec</code>. Configure <code>initialDelaySeconds</code> to avoid false kills on startup.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── M ─── -->
        <div id="gd-m" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">M</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Manifest</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>YAML or JSON file declaring the desired state of Kubernetes objects. Applied with <code>kubectl apply -f manifest.yaml</code>. The declarative approach — Kubernetes reconciles actual state to match.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Metrics Server</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Cluster add-on that collects CPU/memory usage from kubelets. Required for <code>kubectl top nodes</code>, <code>kubectl top pods</code>, and HPA autoscaling. Install: <code>kubectl apply -f components.yaml</code> from GitHub.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Multi-container Pod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Pod with multiple containers sharing network and volumes. Patterns: <strong>Sidecar</strong> (helper alongside main), <strong>Ambassador</strong> (proxy), <strong>Adapter</strong> (transform output). Init containers run before app containers.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── N ─── -->
        <div id="gd-n" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">N</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Namespace</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Virtual cluster within a cluster. Provides resource isolation, RBAC boundaries, and resource quotas. Default namespaces: <code>default</code>, <code>kube-system</code>, <code>kube-public</code>, <code>kube-node-lease</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>NetworkPolicy</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Firewall rules for pod traffic. Controls ingress (incoming) and egress (outgoing) at L3/L4. <strong>Requires supporting CNI</strong> (Calico, Cilium). No policy = all traffic allowed. Empty podSelector = all pods.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Node</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>A worker machine (VM or physical) in the cluster. Runs kubelet, kube-proxy, and container runtime. Control plane nodes also run API server, etcd, scheduler, controller-manager.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>NodePort</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Service type that opens a port (30000-32767) on <strong>every node's IP</strong>. External access via <code>&lt;nodeIP&gt;:&lt;nodePort&gt;</code>. Automatically creates a ClusterIP too. Good for dev/on-prem.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── O ─── -->
        <div id="gd-o" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">O</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>OCI</strong></td><td></td><td>Open Container Initiative — industry standard for container image format and runtime specification. All K8s container images follow OCI spec.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Operator</strong></td><td></td><td>Pattern: custom controller + CRD that automates complex application lifecycle management. Examples: Prometheus Operator, cert-manager, PostgreSQL Operator. Extends Kubernetes capabilities.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── P ─── -->
        <div id="gd-p" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">P</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>PersistentVolume (PV)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Cluster-level storage resource provisioned by admin (or dynamically by StorageClass). <strong>Cluster-scoped</strong> — no namespace. Bound to PVCs. Reclaim policies: <code>Retain</code>, <code>Delete</code>, <code>Recycle</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>User's <strong>request</strong> for storage. Namespace-scoped. Binds to a PV matching accessMode, capacity, and storageClass. Pod references PVC in <code>volumes[].persistentVolumeClaim.claimName</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Pod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Smallest deployable unit — one or more containers sharing network namespace (same IP) and volumes. Usually one container per pod. Created by Deployments, not directly. <code>kubectl run nginx --image=nginx</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PodDisruptionBudget (PDB)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Guarantees minimum availability during <strong>voluntary disruptions</strong> (node drain, upgrades). <code>minAvailable: 2</code> or <code>maxUnavailable: 1</code>. Can block <code>kubectl drain</code> if violated.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PodSecurityAdmission</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Built-in admission controller enforcing Pod Security Standards at namespace level. Modes: <code>enforce</code>, <code>audit</code>, <code>warn</code>. Label: <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>PodSecurityStandard</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Three profiles: <strong>Privileged</strong> (unrestricted), <strong>Baseline</strong> (sensible defaults), <strong>Restricted</strong> (hardened). Applied via PodSecurityAdmission labels on namespaces.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Probe</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Health check mechanism. Three types: <strong>Liveness</strong> (restart if fail), <strong>Readiness</strong> (remove from service if fail), <strong>Startup</strong> (disable other probes until success). Methods: httpGet, tcpSocket, exec.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── R ─── -->
        <div id="gd-r" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">R</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Role-Based Access Control — who can do what. Four objects: <code>Role</code> (ns-scoped permissions), <code>ClusterRole</code> (cluster-wide), <code>RoleBinding</code> (links subject→Role), <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Readiness Probe</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Health check: "is the pod ready for traffic?" If it fails, pod is <strong>removed from Service endpoints</strong> (no restart). Critical for zero-downtime deployments during rolling updates.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ReplicaSet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Ensures a specified number of pod replicas are running. Managed by Deployments — rarely created directly. <code>kubectl get rs</code> shows ReplicaSets behind a Deployment.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ResourceQuota</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Limits <strong>total</strong> resource consumption per namespace (cpu, memory, pods count, PVCs). Prevents one team from consuming all cluster resources. Applied at namespace level.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>RoleBinding</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Binds a Role (or ClusterRole) to subjects (users, groups, ServiceAccounts) within a <strong>single namespace</strong>. <code>kubectl create rolebinding name --role=role --serviceaccount=ns:sa -n ns</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Rolling Update</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Default Deployment strategy: gradually replace old pods with new ones. Controlled by <code>maxSurge</code> and <code>maxUnavailable</code>. Zero-downtime when combined with readiness probes.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── S ─── -->
        <div id="gd-s" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">S</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Secret</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Stores sensitive data (base64-encoded, <strong>not encrypted by default</strong>). Types: <code>Opaque</code> (generic), <code>kubernetes.io/tls</code> (certs), <code>kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson</code> (registry auth). Protect with RBAC + encryption at rest.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>SecurityContext</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Security settings at pod or container level. Controls: <code>runAsUser</code>, <code>runAsNonRoot</code>, <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>, <code>capabilities</code>, <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation</code>. CKA tests pod-level and container-level contexts.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Selector</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Label-based mechanism to identify a set of objects. Used by Services (<code>spec.selector</code>), Deployments (<code>spec.selector.matchLabels</code>), and NetworkPolicies (<code>spec.podSelector</code>).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Service</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Stable network endpoint for a set of pods. Types: <code>ClusterIP</code> (internal), <code>NodePort</code> (external:30000+), <code>LoadBalancer</code> (cloud LB), <code>ExternalName</code> (DNS alias). Selector matches pod labels.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>ServiceAccount</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Identity for pods to authenticate to the API server. Every namespace has a <code>default</code> SA. Best practice: create dedicated SA with <code>automountServiceAccountToken: false</code> unless needed.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Sidecar</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Additional container in a pod running alongside the main container. Pattern: log shippers, proxies (Envoy/Istio), config reloaders. Shares volumes and network with main container.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Static Pod</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Pod managed directly by <strong>kubelet</strong> (not API server). Defined in <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/</code>. Used for control plane components: API server, scheduler, controller-manager, etcd. Mirror pod appears in API.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>StatefulSet</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Manages stateful applications with <strong>stable identity</strong>: ordered pod names (db-0, db-1), persistent storage per pod, ordered deployment/scaling. Used for: databases, ZooKeeper, Kafka.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>StorageClass</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Defines types of storage for <strong>dynamic provisioning</strong>. When a PVC references a StorageClass, K8s auto-creates the PV. Key fields: <code>provisioner</code>, <code>volumeBindingMode</code>, <code>reclaimPolicy</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── T ─── -->
        <div id="gd-t" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">T</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Taint</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Mark on a node that <strong>repels pods</strong> unless they have a matching toleration. Effects: <code>NoSchedule</code>, <code>PreferNoSchedule</code>, <code>NoExecute</code>. Control plane has default taint. <code>kubectl taint nodes node key=val:NoSchedule</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Toleration</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Pod spec field allowing scheduling on <strong>tainted nodes</strong>. Must match taint key, value, and effect. <code>spec.tolerations: [{key: "key", operator: "Equal", value: "val", effect: "NoSchedule"}]</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── U ─── -->
        <div id="gd-u" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">U</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Uncordon</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Make a cordoned node <strong>schedulable again</strong>. <code>kubectl uncordon &lt;node&gt;</code>. Final step after maintenance. Existing pods won't move back automatically — new pods may be scheduled there.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Upgrade (kubeadm)</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>CKA-critical procedure: <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code> → <code>kubeadm upgrade apply vX.Y.Z</code> on control plane, then upgrade kubelet/kubectl on each node. Always upgrade <strong>one minor version at a time</strong> (e.g., 1.29→1.30).</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── V ─── -->
        <div id="gd-v" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">V</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Volume</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Storage attached to a pod. Types: <code>emptyDir</code> (temp), <code>hostPath</code> (node fs), <code>persistentVolumeClaim</code> (PVC), <code>configMap</code>, <code>secret</code>, <code>projected</code>. Defined in <code>spec.volumes[]</code>, mounted via <code>volumeMounts[]</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>VolumeMount</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>How a volume is mounted inside a container. Specifies: <code>name</code> (matches volume name), <code>mountPath</code> (path in container), optional <code>readOnly</code> and <code>subPath</code>.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── W ─── -->
        <div id="gd-w" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <h3 class="gloss-letter">W</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="comparison-table">
            <table>
                <thead><tr><th style="width:25%;">Term</th><th style="width:10%;">CKA?</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><strong>Webhook</strong></td><td></td><td>HTTP callback for admission control. <code>MutatingAdmissionWebhook</code> can modify objects; <code>ValidatingAdmissionWebhook</code> can accept/reject. Used by tools like cert-manager, OPA/Gatekeeper.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><strong>Worker Node</strong></td><td>📝</td><td>Node that runs application workloads (pods). Contains kubelet, kube-proxy, and container runtime. Joined to cluster via <code>kubeadm join</code>. Managed from control plane.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- ─── Commonly Confused Terms ─── -->
        <div class="cka-gotcha">
            <h5>⚠️ Frequently Confused Term Pairs</h5>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Label vs Annotation</strong> — Labels are for <em>selection</em> (selectors use them); Annotations are <em>metadata only</em></li>
                <li><strong>Cordon vs Drain</strong> — Cordon stops <em>new</em> pods; Drain evicts <em>existing</em> pods too</li>
                <li><strong>Role vs ClusterRole</strong> — Role is namespace-scoped; ClusterRole is cluster-wide</li>
                <li><strong>PV vs PVC</strong> — PV is the <em>actual storage</em> (admin); PVC is the <em>request</em> (developer)</li>
                <li><strong>Liveness vs Readiness</strong> — Liveness <em>restarts</em> container; Readiness <em>removes from service</em></li>
                <li><strong>DaemonSet vs Deployment</strong> — DaemonSet: one pod per node; Deployment: specified replica count</li>
                <li><strong>ConfigMap vs Secret</strong> — ConfigMap: plain text config; Secret: base64 sensitive data</li>
                <li><strong>Taint vs Toleration</strong> — Taint is on <em>nodes</em> (repels); Toleration is on <em>pods</em> (allows)</li>
                <li><strong>NodePort vs LoadBalancer</strong> — NodePort: nodeIP:port; LoadBalancer: cloud-provisioned external IP</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Appendix D Summary -->
        <div class="summary-box">
            <h4>📖 Appendix D — Glossary Quick Stats</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>✅ <strong>110+ terms</strong> across <strong>22 letter sections</strong> (A–W) covering all CKA exam domains</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>📝 = CKA-relevant</strong> — terms you may encounter on the exam</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Object hierarchy diagram</strong> — Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod → Container ownership chain</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Resource scope reference</strong> — cluster-scoped vs namespace-scoped at a glance</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Definitions include</strong> commands, examples, and cross-references</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>Confused pairs</strong> — 9 most commonly mixed-up term pairs clarified</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </article>

    
        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-e">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">🐍 Appendix E · Complete easypj Kubernetes Manifests</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Production-ready YAML manifests for the complete easypj Django/jobpost application — E.1 through E.12. Each manifest follows CKA best practices: resource limits, security contexts, labels, and health checks.</p>

        <!-- Full Architecture Diagram -->
        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>easypj Application — Complete Kubernetes Architecture</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
                          ┌──────────────────┐
                INTERNET ─│   Ingress (NGINX) │
                          │  easypj.local     │
                          └────────┬─────────┘
                                   │  :80/:443
                          ┌────────▼─────────┐
                          │  Django Service   │─────── NetworkPolicy ──────┐
                          │  (ClusterIP)      │       (restricts traffic)  │
                          └────────┬─────────┘                             │
                  ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐                      │
                  ▼                ▼                 ▼                      │
          ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐ ┌──────────────┐               │
          │ Django Pod-0 │ │ Django Pod-1 │ │ Django Pod-N │  ◄── HPA      │
          │ ┌──────────┐ │ │              │ │              │  (2-10 pods)  │
          │ │Container │ │ │  Deployment  │ │  Deployment  │               │
          │ │easypj:v1 │ │ │  replicas=3  │ │  replicas=3  │               │
          │ ├──────────┤ │ └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘               │
          │ │ConfigMap │ │                                                  │
          │ │ DB_HOST  │ │    ServiceAccount: easypj-web-sa                │
          │ │ DB_NAME  │ │    PDB: minAvailable=2                          │
          │ ├──────────┤ │                                                  │
          │ │ Secret   │ │                                                  │
          │ │ DB_PASS  │ │                                                  │
          │ └──────────┘ │                                                  │
          └──────┬───────┘                                                  │
                 │                                                          │
          ┌──────▼──────────┐                                               │
          │  PostgreSQL Svc │  ◄────────────────────────────────────────────┘
          │  (ClusterIP)    │
          └──────┬──────────┘
                 │
          ┌──────▼──────────┐
          │ PostgreSQL       │   StatefulSet (replicas=1)
          │ StatefulSet-0    │   volumeClaimTemplates → PVC
          │ ┌──────────────┐ │
          │ │ pgdata volume│ │   StorageClass: easypj-storage
          │ │ (PVC 5Gi)    │ │   PersistentVolume: 10Gi
          │ └──────────────┘ │
          └──────────────────┘

  ┌─────────── NAMESPACE: easypj ─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  RBAC: Role (easypj-role) + RoleBinding (easypj-web-sa)          │
  │  CronJob: db-backup (daily at 2 AM)                              │
  │  Job: db-migrate (one-time schema migration)                     │
  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>How to Use These Manifests:</strong> Apply in order (E.1→E.12). Each manifest depends on the previous ones. Or apply all at once: <code>kubectl apply -f easypj/</code> (place all YAML in one directory).</p>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.1 Namespace ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.1 Namespace</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Why First</h5>
                <p>Every other manifest references <code>namespace: easypj</code>. Create this before anything else.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">01-namespace.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: easypj-platform
    environment: production
  annotations:
    description: "easypj Django/jobpost application namespace"</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalent:</strong>
                <code>kubectl create namespace easypj</code>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.2 ConfigMap ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.2 ConfigMap</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Purpose</h5>
                <p>Non-sensitive configuration — database host, port, app settings. Referenced by Django Deployment pods via <code>envFrom</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">02-configmap.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: easypj-config
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: config
data:
  <span class="comment"># Database connection (non-sensitive)</span>
  DB_HOST: "easypj-postgres"
  DB_PORT: "5432"
  DB_NAME: "easypj_db"

  <span class="comment"># Django settings</span>
  DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE: "easypj.settings"
  DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS: "*"
  DJANGO_DEBUG: "False"

  <span class="comment"># Application</span>
  APP_NAME: "easypj-jobpost"
  LOG_LEVEL: "INFO"</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalent:</strong>
                <code>kubectl create configmap easypj-config --from-literal=DB_HOST=easypj-postgres --from-literal=DB_PORT=5432 --from-literal=DB_NAME=easypj_db -n easypj</code>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.3 Secret ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.3 Secret</h3>
            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Secrets are base64-encoded, NOT encrypted</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Anyone with RBAC access to Secrets can decode them: <code>echo &lt;value&gt; | base64 -d</code></li>
                    <li>Enable <strong>encryption at rest</strong> in production (<code>EncryptionConfiguration</code>)</li>
                    <li>Use <code>stringData</code> (plain text, auto-encoded) instead of <code>data</code> (pre-encoded) for readability</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">03-secret.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: easypj-secrets
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: secrets
type: Opaque
stringData:
  <span class="comment"># Using stringData — Kubernetes auto-encodes to base64</span>
  DB_PASSWORD: "your-strong-password-here"
  DJANGO_SECRET_KEY: "your-django-secret-key-here"
  POSTGRES_PASSWORD: "your-strong-password-here"</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalent:</strong>
                <code>kubectl create secret generic easypj-secrets --from-literal=DB_PASSWORD=your-strong-password-here --from-literal=DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=your-django-secret-key-here -n easypj</code>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.4 StorageClass + PVC ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.4 StorageClass &amp; PersistentVolumeClaim</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Storage Flow</h5>
                <p>StorageClass defines <em>how</em> storage is provisioned → PVC requests storage → StatefulSet (E.5) uses the PVC via <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Storage Binding Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  StorageClass                PVC                     StatefulSet
  ┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────────┐     ┌────────────────────┐
  │ easypj-      │────▶│ pgdata-claim │────▶│ postgres-0 Pod     │
  │ storage      │     │ 5Gi RWO      │     │ mountPath: /data   │
  │              │     │              │     │                    │
  │ provisioner: │     │ storageClass │     │ volumeClaimTemplates│
  │  hostPath    │     │  =easypj-    │     │  uses this class   │
  │ (dev/test)   │     │   storage    │     │                    │
  └──────────────┘     └──────────────┘     └────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">04a-storageclass.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: easypj-storage
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: storage
provisioner: docker.io/hostpath       <span class="comment"># Dev/test only</span>
reclaimPolicy: Retain                 <span class="comment"># Keep data after PVC deletion</span>
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer  <span class="comment"># Bind when pod is scheduled</span>
allowVolumeExpansion: true</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">04b-pvc.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: pgdata-claim
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: database
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce              <span class="comment"># Single node read-write</span>
  storageClassName: easypj-storage
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 5Gi</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>CKA Tip:</strong> For the exam, remember the three access modes: <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> (RWO), <code>ReadOnlyMany</code> (ROX), <code>ReadWriteMany</code> (RWX). Databases almost always use <strong>RWO</strong>.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.5 PostgreSQL StatefulSet + Service ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.5 PostgreSQL StatefulSet &amp; Service</h3>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>Why StatefulSet?</h5>
                <p>Databases need: stable network identity (<code>easypj-postgres-0</code>), persistent storage per pod, ordered deployment. A Deployment would lose data on pod reschedule.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">05a-postgres-statefulset.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: StatefulSet
metadata:
  name: easypj-postgres
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: database
spec:
  serviceName: easypj-postgres    <span class="comment"># Headless service name</span>
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-postgres
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-postgres
        app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
        app.kubernetes.io/component: database
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: postgres
          image: postgres:15-alpine
          ports:
            - containerPort: 5432
              name: postgres
          env:
            - name: POSTGRES_DB
              valueFrom:
                configMapKeyRef:
                  name: easypj-config
                  key: DB_NAME
            - name: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
              valueFrom:
                secretKeyRef:
                  name: easypj-secrets
                  key: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
            - name: PGDATA
              value: /var/lib/postgresql/data/pgdata
          volumeMounts:
            - name: pgdata
              mountPath: /var/lib/postgresql/data
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 250m
              memory: 256Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 500m
              memory: 512Mi
          livenessProbe:
            exec:
              command: ["pg_isready", "-U", "postgres"]
            initialDelaySeconds: 30
            periodSeconds: 10
          readinessProbe:
            exec:
              command: ["pg_isready", "-U", "postgres"]
            initialDelaySeconds: 5
            periodSeconds: 5
  volumeClaimTemplates:
    - metadata:
        name: pgdata
      spec:
        accessModes: ["ReadWriteOnce"]
        storageClassName: easypj-storage
        resources:
          requests:
            storage: 5Gi</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">05b-postgres-service.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-postgres
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: database
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  clusterIP: None                 <span class="comment"># Headless service for StatefulSet</span>
  selector:
    app: easypj-postgres
  ports:
    - port: 5432
      targetPort: 5432
      name: postgres</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ StatefulSet Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>clusterIP: None</code> makes a <strong>headless</strong> service — required for StatefulSet DNS (<code>easypj-postgres-0.easypj-postgres.easypj.svc.cluster.local</code>)</li>
                    <li><code>serviceName</code> in StatefulSet <strong>must match</strong> the headless service name</li>
                    <li>PVCs from <code>volumeClaimTemplates</code> are <strong>NOT deleted</strong> on StatefulSet deletion — delete manually if needed</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.6 Django Deployment + Service ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.6 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-6">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.6 Django Deployment &amp; Service</h3>
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>Core Workload</h5>
                <p>This is the main easypj application. Uses Deployment (stateless) with ConfigMap/Secret injection, resource limits, probes, and security context.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">06a-django-deployment.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: web
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxSurge: 1
      maxUnavailable: 0           <span class="comment"># Zero-downtime deployments</span>
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: easypj-web
        app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
        app.kubernetes.io/component: web
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: easypj-web-sa
      securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true
        runAsUser: 1000
        fsGroup: 1000
      containers:
        - name: django
          image: easypj/jobpost:v1.0.0
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8000
              name: http
          envFrom:
            - configMapRef:
                name: easypj-config
            - secretRef:
                name: easypj-secrets
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 100m
              memory: 128Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 500m
              memory: 512Mi
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /health/
              port: 8000
            initialDelaySeconds: 15
            periodSeconds: 20
            failureThreshold: 3
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /ready/
              port: 8000
            initialDelaySeconds: 5
            periodSeconds: 10
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            capabilities:
              drop: ["ALL"]
          volumeMounts:
            - name: tmp
              mountPath: /tmp
      volumes:
        - name: tmp
          emptyDir: {}            <span class="comment"># Writable /tmp for Django</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">06b-django-service.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: web
spec:
  type: ClusterIP
  selector:
    app: easypj-web
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8000
      protocol: TCP
      name: http</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>CKA Pattern:</strong> <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code> + readiness probe = zero-downtime rolling update. Old pods stay in the Service until new pods pass readiness. This is a common exam scenario.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.7 Ingress ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.7 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-7">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.7 Ingress</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">07-ingress.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: easypj-ingress
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: networking
  annotations:
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/rewrite-target: /
    nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "false"
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx
  rules:
    - host: easypj.local
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: easypj-web
                port:
                  number: 80
          - path: /api
            pathType: Prefix
            backend:
              service:
                name: easypj-web
                port:
                  number: 80</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Ingress Traffic Flow</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Client Request                 Ingress Controller          Backend Pods
  ────────────                   ──────────────────          ────────────
  GET easypj.local/      ──▶   nginx pod (DaemonSet)  ──▶  easypj-web-xxx
  GET easypj.local/api   ──▶   matches path rules     ──▶  easypj-web-yyy
  GET other.local/       ──▶   404 (no matching rule)
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Ingress Gotchas</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>Ingress resource alone does nothing — you need an <strong>Ingress Controller</strong> installed (nginx, traefik)</li>
                    <li><code>ingressClassName</code> replaced the deprecated <code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class</code> annotation in v1.19+</li>
                    <li><code>pathType: Prefix</code> matches <code>/api</code> and <code>/api/jobs</code>; <code>Exact</code> matches only <code>/api</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.8 NetworkPolicy ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.8 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-8">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.8 NetworkPolicy</h3>
            <div class="info-box warning">
                <h5>⚠️ Prerequisite</h5>
                <p>NetworkPolicies require a CNI that supports them (Calico, Cilium). Flannel does <strong>NOT</strong> enforce network policies.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">08-networkpolicy.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml"><span class="comment"># Default deny all ingress in namespace</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: default-deny-ingress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector: {}                 <span class="comment"># All pods in namespace</span>
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
---
<span class="comment"># Allow Django to receive traffic from Ingress</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-web-ingress
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress-nginx
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 8000
---
<span class="comment"># Allow Django → PostgreSQL traffic only</span>
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: allow-postgres-from-web
  namespace: easypj
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-postgres
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: easypj-web
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 5432</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Network Policy Effect — What's Allowed &amp; What's Blocked</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌── ingress-nginx namespace ───┐     ┌────── easypj namespace ────────────┐
  │                               │     │                                    │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐         │     │  ┌──────────────┐                  │
  │  │ Ingress         │─── ✅ ──┼────▶│  │ easypj-web   │                  │
  │  │ Controller      │  :8000  │     │  │ (Django)     │                  │
  │  └─────────────────┘         │     │  └──────┬───────┘                  │
  │                               │     │         │ ✅ :5432                 │
  └───────────────────────────────┘     │         ▼                          │
                                        │  ┌──────────────┐                  │
  ┌── any other namespace ────┐         │  │ easypj-      │                  │
  │                            │        │  │ postgres     │                  │
  │  ┌─────────────────┐      │        │  └──────────────┘                  │
  │  │ Random Pod      │── ❌ ─┼───X   │                                    │
  │  └─────────────────┘      │        │  ❌ All other ingress denied       │
  └────────────────────────────┘        └────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.9 RBAC ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.9 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-9">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.9 RBAC (Role &amp; RoleBinding)</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">09a-serviceaccount.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-sa
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: rbac
automountServiceAccountToken: false   <span class="comment"># Best practice: opt-in only</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">09b-role.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: easypj-role
  namespace: easypj
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["configmaps", "secrets"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list"]</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">09c-rolebinding.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-binding
  namespace: easypj
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: easypj-web-sa
    namespace: easypj
roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: easypj-role
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalents:</strong>
                <code>kubectl create sa easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code>
                <code>kubectl create role easypj-role --verb=get,list,watch --resource=configmaps,secrets -n easypj</code>
                <code>kubectl create rolebinding easypj-web-binding --role=easypj-role --serviceaccount=easypj:easypj-web-sa -n easypj</code>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.10 PDB ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.10 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-10">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.10 PodDisruptionBudget</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Purpose</h5>
                <p>Guarantees at least 2 Django pods are always running during voluntary disruptions (node drain, cluster upgrades).</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">10-pdb.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-pdb
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: availability
spec:
  minAvailable: 2                 <span class="comment"># Always keep 2+ pods running</span>
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: easypj-web</code></pre>
            </div>
            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>CKA Tip:</strong> Use <code>minAvailable</code> when you know the minimum OR <code>maxUnavailable</code> when you want flexibility. With 3 replicas and <code>minAvailable: 2</code>, only 1 pod can be disrupted at a time.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.11 HPA ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.11 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-11">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.11 Horizontal Pod Autoscaler</h3>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">11-hpa.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: easypj-web-hpa
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: autoscaling
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: easypj-web
  minReplicas: 2
  maxReplicas: 10
  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: memory
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 80
  behavior:
    scaleDown:
      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300   <span class="comment"># Wait 5 min before scaling down</span>
      policies:
        - type: Pods
          value: 1
          periodSeconds: 60             <span class="comment"># Remove max 1 pod per minute</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>HPA Scaling Behavior</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  CPU Load     Replicas     Action
  ────────     ────────     ──────
    20%          2          Minimum (idle)
    50%          2          Still within threshold
    70%          3          Scale up triggered (target: 70%)
    90%          5          Aggressive scale up
   100%         10          Maximum reached
    60%         10          Stabilization window (5 min wait)
    40%          9          Scale down: -1 pod/min
    20%          2          Back to minimum
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-imperative-ref">
                <strong>⚡ Imperative equivalent:</strong>
                <code>kubectl autoscale deployment easypj-web --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70 -n easypj</code>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ E.12 Jobs (Migration + CronJob Backup) ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §E.12 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="se-12">
            <h3 class="section-h">E.12 Jobs &amp; CronJobs</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>Two Job Types</h5>
                <p>A one-time <strong>Job</strong> for database migration, and a <strong>CronJob</strong> for daily database backups.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">12a-db-migrate-job.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-migrate
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: migration
spec:
  backoffLimit: 3                 <span class="comment"># Retry up to 3 times</span>
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 300      <span class="comment"># Timeout after 5 minutes</span>
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: migrate
          image: easypj/jobpost:v1.0.0
          command: ["python", "manage.py", "migrate", "--noinput"]
          envFrom:
            - configMapRef:
                name: easypj-config
            - secretRef:
                name: easypj-secrets
          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: 100m
              memory: 128Mi
            limits:
              cpu: 250m
              memory: 256Mi</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">12b-db-backup-cronjob.yaml</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: easypj-db-backup
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/component: backup
spec:
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"          <span class="comment"># Daily at 2:00 AM</span>
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid       <span class="comment"># Don't overlap backup runs</span>
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 1
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      backoffLimit: 2
      template:
        spec:
          restartPolicy: OnFailure
          containers:
            - name: backup
              image: postgres:15-alpine
              command:
                - /bin/sh
                - -c
                - |
                  pg_dump -h $DB_HOST -U postgres -d $DB_NAME \
                    -Fc -f /backup/easypj-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).dump
              envFrom:
                - configMapRef:
                    name: easypj-config
              env:
                - name: PGPASSWORD
                  valueFrom:
                    secretKeyRef:
                      name: easypj-secrets
                      key: POSTGRES_PASSWORD
              resources:
                requests:
                  cpu: 100m
                  memory: 128Mi
                limits:
                  cpu: 250m
                  memory: 256Mi</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Appendix E Summary -->
        <div class="summary-box">
            <h4>🐍 Appendix E — Manifest Deployment Order</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f 01-namespace.yaml</code> — Create namespace first</li>
                <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f 02-configmap.yaml -f 03-secret.yaml</code> — Config &amp; secrets</li>
                <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f 04a-storageclass.yaml -f 04b-pvc.yaml</code> — Storage</li>
                <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f 05a-postgres-statefulset.yaml -f 05b-postgres-service.yaml</code> — Database</li>
                <li><strong>Step 5:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f 12a-db-migrate-job.yaml</code> — Run migrations</li>
                <li><strong>Step 6:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f 06a-django-deployment.yaml -f 06b-django-service.yaml</code> — App</li>
                <li><strong>Step 7:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f 07-ingress.yaml</code> — External access</li>
                <li><strong>Step 8:</strong> Apply remaining: NetworkPolicy, RBAC, PDB, HPA, CronJob</li>
                <li>✅ <strong>All at once:</strong> <code>kubectl apply -f easypj/ --recursive</code></li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Verification Commands Quick Reference -->
        <div class="info-box tip">
            <h5>✅ Post-Deployment Verification Checklist</h5>
            <p>After applying all manifests, run these commands to verify everything is healthy:</p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-block-wrapper">
            <div class="code-block-header">
                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                <span class="code-lang">verify-deployment.sh</span>
            </div>
            <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># 1. Check all pods are Running</span>
kubectl get pods -n easypj -o wide

<span class="comment"># 2. Verify Services have endpoints</span>
kubectl get svc -n easypj
kubectl get endpoints -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 3. Check PVC is Bound</span>
kubectl get pvc -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 4. Verify Ingress has an address</span>
kubectl get ingress -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 5. Check HPA is reading metrics</span>
kubectl get hpa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 6. Verify NetworkPolicy is applied</span>
kubectl get networkpolicy -n easypj

<span class="comment"># 7. Test DNS resolution</span>
kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=busybox -n easypj --restart=Never \
  -- nslookup easypj-django-service

<span class="comment"># 8. Test application endpoint</span>
kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=curlimages/curl -n easypj --restart=Never \
  -- curl -s http://easypj-django-service:8000/health/

<span class="comment"># 9. Check RBAC is working</span>
kubectl auth can-i get pods -n easypj --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:easypj-web-sa

<span class="comment"># 10. View recent events for issues</span>
kubectl get events -n easypj --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -20</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- Troubleshooting Flow Diagram -->
        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>Pod Not Running? — Troubleshooting Decision Flow</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  Pod Status?
  │
  ├── Pending ──────────► kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; -n easypj
  │   │                   Look at Events section:
  │   ├── "Insufficient cpu/memory" ──► Increase node resources or reduce requests
  │   ├── "no nodes match selector"  ──► Check nodeSelector/affinity/taints
  │   └── "PVC not bound"           ──► Check StorageClass exists, PV available
  │
  ├── CrashLoopBackOff ─► kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj --previous
  │   │                   (--previous shows logs from crashed container)
  │   ├── "Connection refused DB" ──► PostgreSQL not ready yet (check ordering)
  │   ├── "Permission denied"     ──► SecurityContext too restrictive
  │   └── "Module not found"      ──► Wrong image or missing dependencies
  │
  ├── ImagePullBackOff ──► kubectl describe pod &lt;name&gt; -n easypj
  │   ├── "repository not found"  ──► Wrong image name/tag
  │   └── "unauthorized"          ──► Missing imagePullSecrets
  │
  └── Running but ──────► kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj -f
      not working         kubectl exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -n easypj -- /bin/sh
                          Check: env vars, mounted configs, network connectivity
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cka-gotcha">
            <h5>⚠️ Common Manifest Mistakes on CKA</h5>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Wrong namespace</strong> — Forgetting <code>namespace: easypj</code> in metadata, so object lands in <code>default</code></li>
                <li><strong>Label mismatch</strong> — Service <code>selector</code> doesn't match Deployment's <code>template.metadata.labels</code> → 0 endpoints</li>
                <li><strong>Port mismatch</strong> — Service <code>targetPort</code> doesn't match container <code>containerPort</code> → connection refused</li>
                <li><strong>Secret key typo</strong> — <code>secretKeyRef.key</code> doesn't match actual Secret data key → pod fails to start</li>
                <li><strong>Missing resources</strong> — No <code>resources.requests</code> means scheduler can't account for pod needs → OOM kills</li>
                <li><strong>PVC accessMode</strong> — Using <code>ReadWriteMany</code> with a StorageClass that only supports <code>ReadWriteOnce</code> → PVC stays Pending</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

    </article>

    
        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-f">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">🎓 Appendix F · CKA Exam Preparation Guide</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Exam format, domain weights, must-know tips, time management, practice labs, exam day checklist. Everything you need to pass the CKA on your first attempt.</p>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ F.1 Exam Format ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §F.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.1 Exam Format and Rules</h3>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>CKA Exam Environment Overview</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌─────────────────────────── CKA EXAM ───────────────────────────────┐
  │                                                                     │
  │   Duration:    2 hours                                              │
  │   Questions:   15–20 performance-based tasks                        │
  │   Pass Score:  66%                                                  │
  │   Format:      Hands-on (real clusters, real kubectl)               │
  │   Retake:      1 free retake included                               │
  │   Validity:    2 years (was 3 years before 2023)                    │
  │   Proctored:   Online via PSI Secure Browser                        │
  │                                                                     │
  │   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐       │
  │   │  YOUR SCREEN DURING EXAM                                │       │
  │   │  ┌──────────────────┐  ┌────────────────────────────┐   │       │
  │   │  │ Question Panel   │  │ Terminal                    │   │       │
  │   │  │                  │  │ $ kubectl ...               │   │       │
  │   │  │ Task description │  │                             │   │       │
  │   │  │ Requirements     │  │ One or more pre-configured  │   │       │
  │   │  │ Context switch   │  │ cluster contexts available  │   │       │
  │   │  │                  │  │                             │   │       │
  │   │  └──────────────────┘  └────────────────────────────┘   │       │
  │   │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │       │
  │   │  │ Built-in Browser Tab (allowed docs only)         │   │       │
  │   │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │       │
  │   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘       │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:30%;">Aspect</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes Version</strong></td><td>Latest stable (currently v1.31 — always check CNCF site before exam)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Number of Clusters</strong></td><td>Multiple pre-configured clusters. Each question specifies which context to use: <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Root Access</strong></td><td>You get <code>sudo</code> on nodes when needed (e.g., for static pods, kubelet troubleshooting)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Clipboard</strong></td><td>Copy/paste works within the exam terminal. Use <code>Ctrl+Shift+C/V</code> in the terminal</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Notepad</strong></td><td>Built-in notepad provided — use it to track flagged questions</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>IDE/Editor</strong></td><td><code>vim</code>, <code>nano</code>, and <code>emacs</code> available. Set <code>export EDITOR=vim</code> if needed</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box note" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(16,185,129,0.1), rgba(34,197,94,0.1)); border-left: 4px solid #10b981;">
                <h4>✅ Allowed Documentation During Exam</h4>
                <p>You may open <strong>ONE extra browser tab</strong> with these domains only:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>https://kubernetes.io/docs/</code> — Official documentation</li>
                    <li><code>https://kubernetes.io/blog/</code> — Official blog</li>
                    <li><code>https://helm.sh/docs/</code> — Helm documentation (since CKA 2024)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin-top: 0.5rem; color: #f87171;"><strong>NOT allowed:</strong> GitHub, Stack Overflow, any other website, personal notes, second monitor.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ F.2 Domain Weights ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §F.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.2 CKA Exam Domain Weights</h3>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>CKA Domain Weight Distribution (2024/2025)</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  Domain                               Weight    Visual
  ─────────────────────────────────    ──────    ──────────────────────────────
  🔧 Troubleshooting                    30%     ████████████████████████████████
  🏗️ Cluster Architecture &amp; Setup       25%     ██████████████████████████
  🌐 Services &amp; Networking              20%     █████████████████████
  📦 Workloads &amp; Scheduling             15%     ████████████████
  💾 Storage                             10%     ███████████

                              Pass threshold: 66% ─────────▶ ┃
  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┃──────────┐
  0%                   33%                  66%   ┃      100%
  ■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■■□□□□□□□□□□□□
  ◀── FAIL zone ──▶                   ◀── PASS ─▶
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:5%;">%</th><th style="width:25%;">Domain</th><th>Key Topics to Master</th><th style="width:15%;">Chapters</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>30%</strong></td>
                            <td>🔧 Troubleshooting</td>
                            <td>Application failure, pod debugging, cluster component failure, node issues, networking issues. <strong>Highest weight</strong> — practice logs, events, describe.</td>
                            <td>Ch 27–31</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>25%</strong></td>
                            <td>🏗️ Cluster Architecture</td>
                            <td>RBAC, kubeadm upgrades, etcd backup/restore, HA setup, node management (cordon/drain/uncordon).</td>
                            <td>Ch 1–6, 22–26</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>20%</strong></td>
                            <td>🌐 Services &amp; Networking</td>
                            <td>Service types (ClusterIP, NodePort, LB), Ingress, NetworkPolicy, DNS resolution, CNI.</td>
                            <td>Ch 16–21</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>15%</strong></td>
                            <td>📦 Workloads &amp; Scheduling</td>
                            <td>Deployments, DaemonSets, resource limits, scheduling (nodeSelector, affinity, taints), scaling.</td>
                            <td>Ch 7–12</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>10%</strong></td>
                            <td>💾 Storage</td>
                            <td>PV/PVC, StorageClass, volume types, accessModes, dynamic provisioning.</td>
                            <td>Ch 13–15</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Strategy:</strong> Troubleshooting (30%) + Cluster Architecture (25%) = <strong>55%</strong> of the exam. Master these two domains first — they alone can nearly pass you.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ F.3 Tips & Tricks ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §F.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.3 Tips, Tricks &amp; Time Management</h3>

            <div class="info-box note" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(251,191,36,0.1), rgba(245,158,11,0.1)); border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b;">
                <h4>⏱️ Time Management Strategy (120 Minutes)</h4>
                <p>With 15–20 questions in 2 hours, you have <strong>~6–8 minutes per question</strong>. Not all questions are equal weight.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Recommended Time Allocation</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ┌────── FIRST PASS (90 min) ──────┐  ┌──── SECOND PASS (25 min) ────┐  ┌─ REVIEW ─┐
  │                                  │  │                               │  │  (5 min)  │
  │  Answer ALL questions you can    │  │  Return to flagged/skipped    │  │  Verify   │
  │  do quickly (&lt;5 min each)       │  │  questions                    │  │  contexts │
  │                                  │  │                               │  │  Check    │
  │  FLAG and SKIP hard ones         │  │  Spend remaining time on      │  │  answers  │
  │  (come back in pass 2)           │  │  highest-weight questions     │  │           │
  │                                  │  │                               │  │           │
  └──────────────────────────────────┘  └───────────────────────────────┘  └───────────┘
   0 min                         90 min                             115 min          120 min
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>⚡ First Thing — Set Up Your Aliases</h5>
                <p>Paste these at the exam terminal start. They save <strong>hundreds of keystrokes</strong>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">~/.bashrc exam setup</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Paste at start of exam (takes 30 seconds, saves 10+ minutes)</span>
alias k=kubectl
alias kgp='kubectl get pods'
alias kgs='kubectl get svc'
alias kgn='kubectl get nodes'
alias kaf='kubectl apply -f'
alias kdel='kubectl delete'
alias kd='kubectl describe'
alias kl='kubectl logs'
alias kx='kubectl exec -it'

<span class="comment"># Auto-complete</span>
source &lt;(kubectl completion bash)
complete -o default -F __start_kubectl k

<span class="comment"># Default editor</span>
export EDITOR=vim
export KUBE_EDITOR=vim

<span class="comment"># Quick dry-run shortcut</span>
export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"

<span class="comment"># Usage: k run nginx --image=nginx $do > pod.yaml</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:5%;">#</th><th style="width:25%;">Tip</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Read context switch</strong></td><td>Every question starts with <code>kubectl config use-context &lt;name&gt;</code>. <strong>DO NOT SKIP THIS</strong> — wrong context = wrong cluster = zero points.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Use imperative commands</strong></td><td><code>kubectl create deploy</code>, <code>kubectl run</code>, <code>kubectl expose</code> are 5x faster than writing YAML from scratch.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Generate YAML skeletons</strong></td><td><code>kubectl run nginx --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > pod.yaml</code> — then edit the YAML.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>4</td><td><strong>Use <code>kubectl explain</code></strong></td><td><code>kubectl explain pod.spec.containers.resources</code> — built-in docs when you forget field names.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>5</td><td><strong>Bookmark docs pages</strong></td><td>Before exam, bookmark: <em>cheat sheet</em>, <em>kubectl reference</em>, <em>API reference</em>, <em>tasks page</em>.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>6</td><td><strong>Don't get stuck</strong></td><td>If a 4% question takes &gt;10 min, <strong>flag it and move on</strong>. An 8% question waiting is worth more.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>7</td><td><strong>Verify your work</strong></td><td>After creating a resource: <code>kubectl get</code>, <code>kubectl describe</code>, check events. Confirm it's in the right namespace.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>8</td><td><strong>Know vim basics</strong></td><td><code>i</code> (insert), <code>Esc</code> (normal), <code>:wq</code> (save+quit), <code>dd</code> (delete line), <code>/search</code> (find), <code>:set number</code> (line numbers).</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Common Exam Mistakes</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Wrong namespace:</strong> Always check <code>-n &lt;namespace&gt;</code> — many questions use non-default namespaces</li>
                    <li><strong>Wrong context:</strong> Run the context switch command at the start of EVERY question</li>
                    <li><strong>YAML indentation:</strong> Spaces only (no tabs). A single wrong indent breaks everything</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgetting to save:</strong> In vim, <code>:wq</code> — many have lost work by exiting without saving</li>
                    <li><strong>Over-engineering:</strong> The exam wants minimal correct answers, not production-grade YAML</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- Must-Bookmark URLs -->
            <div class="info-box note" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(99,102,241,0.1), rgba(139,92,246,0.1)); border-left: 4px solid #8b5cf6;">
                <h4>🔖 Must-Bookmark kubernetes.io Pages (Before Exam Day)</h4>
                <p style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Open these before the exam starts and bookmark them in your allowed browser tab:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>kubectl Cheat Sheet</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/cheatsheet/</code></li>
                    <li><strong>kubectl Reference</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/reference/generated/kubectl/kubectl-commands</code></li>
                    <li><strong>API Reference (v1.31)</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubernetes-api/</code></li>
                    <li><strong>Tasks Index</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/</code> (copy-paste YAML snippets)</li>
                    <li><strong>kubeadm upgrade</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/kubeadm/kubeadm-upgrade/</code></li>
                    <li><strong>etcd backup/restore</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/</code></li>
                    <li><strong>NetworkPolicy</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/</code></li>
                    <li><strong>RBAC</strong> — <code>kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- Vim Survival Guide -->
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📝 Vim Survival Guide for the CKA Exam</h5>
                <p>You <strong>will</strong> use vim on the exam. Master these 15 commands:</p>
            </div>
            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:15%;">Key</th><th style="width:25%;">Action</th><th>When to Use</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>i</code></td><td>Enter insert mode</td><td>Start typing/editing text</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>Return to normal mode</td><td>Stop editing, run commands</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>:wq</code></td><td>Save and quit</td><td>Done editing — save file</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>:q!</code></td><td>Quit without saving</td><td>Messed up — discard all changes</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>dd</code></td><td>Delete entire line</td><td>Remove unwanted YAML lines</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>5dd</code></td><td>Delete 5 lines</td><td>Remove a block of YAML</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>yy</code> / <code>p</code></td><td>Copy line / Paste below</td><td>Duplicate a YAML block</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>u</code></td><td>Undo last change</td><td>Revert a mistake</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>/text</code></td><td>Search forward</td><td>Find a field in large YAML</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>n</code></td><td>Next search match</td><td>Jump to next occurrence</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>G</code></td><td>Go to end of file</td><td>Jump to bottom of file</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>gg</code></td><td>Go to start of file</td><td>Jump to top of file</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>:set number</code></td><td>Show line numbers</td><td>Debug YAML indentation errors</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>:set paste</code></td><td>Paste mode (no auto-indent)</td><td>Before pasting YAML from docs</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>Shift+V</code> → select → <code>d</code></td><td>Visual line delete</td><td>Select and delete multiple lines</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <!-- Essential kubectl Patterns for Exam -->
            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>⚡ Essential kubectl Patterns You MUST Know</h5>
                <p>These patterns cover ~80% of CKA tasks. Practice until they're muscle memory.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">essential-exam-patterns.sh</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Generate YAML skeleton (most-used pattern)</span>
k run nginx --image=nginx $do > pod.yaml
k create deploy web --image=nginx --replicas=3 $do > deploy.yaml
k expose deploy web --port=80 --target-port=8080 $do > svc.yaml

<span class="comment"># Context switching (FIRST thing every question)</span>
k config use-context &lt;context-name&gt;
k config get-contexts    <span class="comment"># verify current</span>

<span class="comment"># Quick troubleshooting chain</span>
k get pods -A            <span class="comment"># all namespaces overview</span>
k describe pod &lt;name&gt;   <span class="comment"># events + conditions</span>
k logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;ctr&gt;   <span class="comment"># container logs</span>
k logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous  <span class="comment"># crashed container logs</span>
k exec -it &lt;pod&gt; -- sh  <span class="comment"># shell into container</span>

<span class="comment"># RBAC creation chain</span>
k create sa mysa -n &lt;ns&gt;
k create role myrole --verb=get,list --resource=pods -n &lt;ns&gt;
k create rolebinding myrb --role=myrole --serviceaccount=&lt;ns&gt;:mysa -n &lt;ns&gt;
k auth can-i get pods --as=system:serviceaccount:&lt;ns&gt;:mysa -n &lt;ns&gt;

<span class="comment"># Node maintenance</span>
k cordon &lt;node&gt;
k drain &lt;node&gt; --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data
k uncordon &lt;node&gt;

<span class="comment"># etcd backup (know this exact command)</span>
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /tmp/backup.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ F.4 Practice Labs ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §F.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.4 Practice Lab Recommendations</h3>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th style="width:20%;">Platform</th><th style="width:12%;">Cost</th><th style="width:12%;">CKA Sim?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><strong>killer.sh</strong></td><td>Free (2 sessions with exam purchase)</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐</td><td><strong>Most realistic.</strong> Included with CKA exam purchase. Harder than real exam. Use both sessions in final week.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>KodeKloud CKA</strong></td><td>~$15-25/mo</td><td>⭐⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Structured course with hands-on labs. Great for beginners. Mock exams included. Lightning labs for speed practice.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Killercoda</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Free browser-based K8s scenarios. Good for daily 15-min practice. By the makers of killer.sh.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Kubernetes the Hard Way</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Kelsey Hightower's manual cluster setup. Deepens understanding but NOT exam format. Do once for learning.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Play with Kubernetes</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐</td><td>4-hour free browser clusters. Good for quick experiments when you don't have local setup.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><strong>Local (kubeadm/kind/minikube)</strong></td><td>Free</td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td><td>Practice kubeadm installs, upgrades, etcd backup locally. Closest to exam for cluster-admin tasks.</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-speed-tip">
                <p><strong>Recommended Study Path:</strong> KodeKloud (learn concepts + labs) → Killercoda (daily practice) → killer.sh (final 2 sessions before exam). Practice until you can complete killer.sh with 20+ minutes to spare.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══════════════════════ F.5 Exam Day Checklist ═══════════════════════ -->
            <!-- ─────────── §F.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sf-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">F.5 Exam Day Checklist</h3>

            <div class="diagram-container">
                <h4>Exam Day Timeline</h4>
                <div class="diagram-box">
                    <pre>
  ─── BEFORE EXAM (Day Before) ───────────────────────────────────────────

  ☐  Test PSI Secure Browser (download + system check)
  ☐  Verify webcam + microphone work
  ☐  Prepare government-issued photo ID
  ☐  Clear desk — nothing except computer, monitor, keyboard, mouse
  ☐  Close all applications, disconnect extra monitors
  ☐  Review your bookmarked kubernetes.io pages

  ─── EXAM DAY (30 min before) ──────────────────────────────────────────

  ☐  Close ALL apps except PSI Secure Browser
  ☐  Clear browser cache
  ☐  Ensure stable internet (wired recommended)
  ☐  Check-in opens 15 min before scheduled time
  ☐  Show room to proctor via webcam (360° sweep)
  ☐  Show desk surface and under desk

  ─── DURING EXAM (first 2 minutes) ────────────────────────────────────

  ☐  Set up aliases (paste your prepared .bashrc snippet)
  ☐  Test: k get nodes (verify cluster access)
  ☐  Skim all questions — identify easy wins
  ☐  Start with highest-confidence questions first

  ─── DURING EXAM (ongoing) ────────────────────────────────────────────

  ☐  READ context switch command for EVERY question
  ☐  Flag difficult questions, move on after 8 min
  ☐  Verify work: kubectl get / describe after each task
  ☐  Use notepad to track flagged question numbers

  ─── LAST 10 MINUTES ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

  ☐  Return to flagged questions (highest weight first)
  ☐  Verify all resources are in correct namespaces
  ☐  Don't make last-minute changes without checking
</pre>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>⚠️ Environment Requirements</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Clean room:</strong> No papers, books, phones, smartwatches, or second screens</li>
                    <li><strong>Alone:</strong> No other people in the room during the exam</li>
                    <li><strong>No headphones/earbuds</strong> — even noise-canceling</li>
                    <li><strong>Browser:</strong> Only PSI Secure Browser + one kubernetes.io tab allowed</li>
                    <li><strong>Talking:</strong> Do not read questions aloud or mumble — proctor may flag it</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>Post-Exam</h5>
                <p>Results arrive within <strong>24 hours</strong> via email. If you don't pass, you get <strong>one free retake</strong> (must use within 12 months). Review weak areas using the domain breakdown in your score report.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

