        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-h">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">🎯 Appendix H · CKA Exam Simulator — 20 Performance-Based Tasks</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-exam">📝 EXAM SIMULATOR</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Simulate the real CKA exam: 20 performance-based tasks, weighted by domain, using the easypj project. Each task matches the difficulty, format, and time pressure of the actual exam. <strong>Target: complete all 20 in under 120 minutes.</strong> Answers use <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> — try each task before revealing the solution.</p>

        <div class="diagram-container">
            <h4>CKA Exam Simulator — Domain Distribution</h4>
            <div class="diagram-box">
                <pre>
  Domain                                    Weight    Tasks    Questions
  ──────────────────────────────────────    ──────    ─────    ─────────
  🔧 Cluster Architecture, Install &amp; Config  25%      5       H.1-H.5
  📦 Workloads &amp; Scheduling                  15%      3       H.6-H.8
  🌐 Services &amp; Networking                   20%      4       H.9-H.12
  💾 Storage                                  10%      2       H.13-H.14
  🔍 Troubleshooting                          30%      6       H.15-H.20

  Total: 20 tasks | Time: 120 minutes | Pass: 66% (≥14 correct)

  ⏱️ Suggested Pace:
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Easy (2-3 min each)  │ Medium (5-7 min)  │ Hard (8-10 min)    │
  │ H.1, H.6, H.9, H.13 │ H.2-H.5, H.7-H.8 │ H.15-H.20          │
  │        ~12 min       │ H.10-H.12, H.14   │ (troubleshooting)  │
  │                      │     ~56 min        │     ~52 min        │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="cka-speed-tip">
            <p><strong>Before You Start:</strong> Set up your aliases first (just like the real exam): <code>alias k=kubectl</code>, <code>export do="--dry-run=client -o yaml"</code>, <code>export now="--force --grace-period 0"</code>. Then set your context: <code>kubectl config use-context k8s</code>.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══════════ DOMAIN 1: CLUSTER ARCHITECTURE (25%) — Tasks H.1-H.5 ═══════════ -->
        <div class="info-box note">
            <h5>🔧 Domain 1: Cluster Architecture, Installation &amp; Configuration (25%)</h5>
            <p>Tasks H.1 through H.5 — RBAC, kubeadm upgrades, etcd backup/restore, cluster components.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- H.1 -->
            <!-- ─────────── §H.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sh-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.1 RBAC — Create a Scoped ServiceAccount [5 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>Create a ServiceAccount <code>deploy-sa</code> in namespace <code>easypj</code>. Create a Role named <code>deploy-role</code> that allows <code>get</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>watch</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>update</code> on <code>deployments</code> in the <code>apps</code> API group. Bind the Role to the ServiceAccount using a RoleBinding named <code>deploy-binding</code>.</p>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>kubectl create</code> imperatively for speed. Remember: <code>--resource=deployments</code> needs <code>--verb=</code> and the API group is <code>apps</code> (not empty string).</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h1.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Create the ServiceAccount</span>
kubectl create sa deploy-sa -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Create the Role (note --resource and API group)</span>
kubectl create role deploy-role \
  --verb=get,list,watch,create,update \
  --resource=deployments.apps \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Create the RoleBinding</span>
kubectl create rolebinding deploy-binding \
  --role=deploy-role \
  --serviceaccount=easypj:deploy-sa \
  -n easypj

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Verify</span>
kubectl auth can-i create deployments --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:deploy-sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Expected: yes</span>

kubectl auth can-i delete deployments --as=system:serviceaccount:easypj:deploy-sa -n easypj
<span class="comment"># Expected: no (delete was not granted)</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Common Mistakes</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Forgetting the <code>.apps</code> API group suffix — <code>--resource=deployments</code> alone targets the wrong API group</li>
                        <li>Using <code>--serviceaccount=deploy-sa</code> without the namespace prefix — must be <code>easypj:deploy-sa</code></li>
                        <li>Creating a ClusterRole instead of Role — the task says <strong>namespace-scoped</strong></li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

        <!-- H.2 -->
            <!-- ─────────── §H.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sh-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.2 etcd Backup [7 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>Create a snapshot of the etcd database and save it to <code>/opt/etcd-backup/snapshot.db</code>. The etcd server is running on the control plane node with the following endpoints and certificates:</p>
            </div>
            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">etcd connection details</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Endpoint</span>
--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379

<span class="comment"># Certificates (find these in /etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/)</span>
--cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt
--cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt
--key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key</code></pre>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>Use <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>. Set <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> first. If you forget the cert paths, check the etcd static pod manifest: <code>cat /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h2.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Ensure etcdctl API version 3</span>
export ETCDCTL_API=3

<span class="comment"># Create backup directory</span>
mkdir -p /opt/etcd-backup

<span class="comment"># Take the snapshot</span>
etcdctl snapshot save /opt/etcd-backup/snapshot.db \
  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \
  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \
  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \
  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key

<span class="comment"># Verify the snapshot</span>
etcdctl snapshot status /opt/etcd-backup/snapshot.db --write-table</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ CKA Trap</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Forgetting <code>ETCDCTL_API=3</code> — version 2 uses different commands</li>
                        <li>Using wrong cert files — <code>server.crt</code>/<code>server.key</code> for etcdctl, NOT <code>peer.crt</code></li>
                        <li>Not creating the output directory first — the command will fail silently</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

        <!-- H.3 -->
            <!-- ─────────── §H.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sh-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.3 etcd Restore [8 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>A previous etcd snapshot exists at <code>/opt/etcd-backup/snapshot.db</code>. Restore etcd from this snapshot to a new data directory <code>/var/lib/etcd-restored</code>. Then update the etcd static pod to use the restored data directory.</p>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>Two steps: (1) <code>etcdctl snapshot restore</code> to a new directory, (2) edit <code>/etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml</code> to point <code>--data-dir</code> and the hostPath volume to the new path. The kubelet will auto-restart etcd.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h3.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Restore snapshot to new directory</span>
export ETCDCTL_API=3
etcdctl snapshot restore /opt/etcd-backup/snapshot.db \
  --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Edit the etcd static pod manifest</span>
<span class="comment"># Change --data-dir flag AND the hostPath volume</span>
sudo vi /etc/kubernetes/manifests/etcd.yaml

<span class="comment"># In the container spec, change:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># To:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - --data-dir=/var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># In the volumes section, change:</span>
<span class="comment">#   hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#     path: /var/lib/etcd</span>
<span class="comment"># To:</span>
<span class="comment">#   hostPath:</span>
<span class="comment">#     path: /var/lib/etcd-restored</span>

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Wait for etcd to restart (kubelet detects manifest change)</span>
<span class="comment"># Verify:</span>
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep etcd
kubectl get nodes  <span class="comment"># Should show Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Critical: TWO Changes Required</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>You must change BOTH the <code>--data-dir</code> flag AND the <code>hostPath.path</code> in the volume mount</li>
                        <li>If you only change one, etcd will fail to start and the entire cluster goes down</li>
                        <li>Don't panic if <code>kubectl</code> stops working temporarily — the API server depends on etcd</li>
                        <li>Wait 1-2 minutes for the kubelet to detect the manifest change and restart the pod</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

        <!-- H.4 -->
            <!-- ─────────── §H.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sh-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.4 Cluster Upgrade — Control Plane [10 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>Upgrade the control plane node <code>controlplane</code> from Kubernetes v1.30.0 to v1.31.0. Upgrade kubeadm first, then the control plane components, then kubelet and kubectl. Ensure the node is schedulable after the upgrade.</p>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>Order matters: (1) upgrade kubeadm, (2) <code>kubeadm upgrade plan</code>, (3) <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code>, (4) drain node, (5) upgrade kubelet+kubectl, (6) restart kubelet, (7) uncordon. On Debian/Ubuntu, use <code>apt-mark unhold</code> → install → <code>apt-mark hold</code>.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h4.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Step 1: Upgrade kubeadm</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Verify upgrade plan</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade plan

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Apply the upgrade</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade apply v1.31.0

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Drain the control plane node</span>
kubectl drain controlplane --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Upgrade kubelet and kubectl</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-* kubectl=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 7: Uncordon the node</span>
kubectl uncordon controlplane

<span class="comment"># Step 8: Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># controlplane should show v1.31.0 and Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Upgrade Order is Critical</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Always upgrade kubeadm <strong>before</strong> running <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code></li>
                        <li>Always upgrade kubelet <strong>after</strong> the control plane components</li>
                        <li>Never skip minor versions — upgrade one version at a time (1.30→1.31, not 1.29→1.31)</li>
                        <li>Don't forget <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> before restarting kubelet</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

        <!-- H.5 -->
            <!-- ─────────── §H.5 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sh-5">
            <h3 class="section-h">H.5 Cluster Upgrade — Worker Node [7 min]</h3>
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>📋 Task</h5>
                <p>Upgrade worker node <code>worker-1</code> to Kubernetes v1.31.0. The control plane has already been upgraded. Ensure workloads are safely evicted during the upgrade and the node is schedulable afterwards.</p>
            </div>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>💡 Hints</strong></summary>
                <div class="info-box tip">
                    <h5>Approach</h5>
                    <p>From the control plane: drain the worker. SSH to the worker: upgrade kubeadm, run <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> (NOT <code>apply</code>), upgrade kubelet+kubectl, restart. Back on control plane: uncordon.</p>
                </div>
            </details>
            <details>
                <summary><strong>✅ Solution</strong></summary>
                <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                    <div class="code-block-header">
                        <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                        <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                        <span class="code-lang">solution-h5.sh</span>
                    </div>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 1: Drain the worker node</span>
kubectl drain worker-1 --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data

<span class="comment"># SSH TO WORKER NODE:</span>
ssh worker-1

<span class="comment"># Step 2: Upgrade kubeadm</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubeadm
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubeadm=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubeadm

<span class="comment"># Step 3: Upgrade node config (NOT "apply" — that's for control plane only)</span>
sudo kubeadm upgrade node

<span class="comment"># Step 4: Upgrade kubelet and kubectl</span>
sudo apt-mark unhold kubelet kubectl
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet=1.31.0-* kubectl=1.31.0-*
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubectl

<span class="comment"># Step 5: Restart kubelet</span>
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart kubelet

<span class="comment"># Step 6: Exit back to control plane</span>
exit

<span class="comment"># FROM CONTROL PLANE:</span>
<span class="comment"># Step 7: Uncordon the worker</span>
kubectl uncordon worker-1

<span class="comment"># Verify</span>
kubectl get nodes
<span class="comment"># worker-1 should show v1.31.0 and Ready</span></code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="cka-gotcha">
                    <h5>⚠️ Worker vs Control Plane Difference</h5>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Workers use <code>kubeadm upgrade node</code> — NOT <code>kubeadm upgrade apply</code></li>
                        <li>You must SSH to the worker to run upgrade commands — kubectl runs on control plane</li>
                        <li>Drain happens from control plane, upgrade happens on the worker node itself</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </details>
        </section>

    </article>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!--           APPENDIX I: WELL-KNOWN LABELS & ANNOTATIONS          -->
    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    
        <article class="chapter" id="appendix-i">
        <h2 class="chapter-title">🏷️ Appendix I · Well-Known Labels, Annotations & Taints</h2>
        <div class="chapter-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-appendix">APPENDIX</span>
            <span class="badge badge-reference">📋 REFERENCE</span>
        </div>
        <p class="chapter-lead">Complete reference of Kubernetes well-known labels, annotations, and taints that are automatically applied by the system or recognized by core components. Essential for scheduling, topology, and node management — all referenced with the <strong>easypj</strong> project context.</p>

        <!-- Card grid: 4 metadata categories -->
        <div class="card-grid cols-4">
            <div class="info-card highlight">
                <h4>🏷️ Node Labels</h4>
                <p>Auto-populated by kubelet and cloud providers. Used for affinity, topology spread, and zone-aware scheduling.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card highlight">
                <h4>📝 Annotations</h4>
                <p>Non-identifying metadata recognized by K8s components. Record change causes, last-applied configs, and tool hints.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card highlight">
                <h4>🚫 Taints</h4>
                <p>Applied by node lifecycle controller or kubelet. Control scheduling and eviction — critical for troubleshooting Pending pods.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="info-card highlight">
                <h4>📋 App Labels</h4>
                <p>Recommended <code>app.kubernetes.io/*</code> convention used by Helm, ArgoCD, and kubectl for identifying resources.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

            <!-- ─────────── §I.1 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-1">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.1 Well-Known Node Labels</h3>
            <p>These labels are automatically populated by the kubelet or cloud provider. They are used by the scheduler for node affinity, topology spread constraints, and zone-aware scheduling.</p>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Example Value</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Set By</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/hostname</code></td><td><code>node-1</code></td><td>Node hostname — used for per-node affinity/anti-affinity</td><td>kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/os</code></td><td><code>linux</code></td><td>Operating system (linux/windows)</td><td>kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/arch</code></td><td><code>amd64</code></td><td>CPU architecture (amd64/arm64)</td><td>kubelet</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>topology.kubernetes.io/zone</code></td><td><code>us-east-1a</code></td><td>Cloud availability zone — zone-aware scheduling</td><td>cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>topology.kubernetes.io/region</code></td><td><code>us-east-1</code></td><td>Cloud region — region-aware scheduling</td><td>cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/instance-type</code></td><td><code>m5.xlarge</code></td><td>Cloud instance type — schedule by machine size</td><td>cloud provider</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/metadata.name</code></td><td><code>easypj</code></td><td>Auto-set to namespace/node name — used in selectors</td><td>API server</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — using well-known labels</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View all labels on a node</span>
kubectl get node node-1 --show-labels

<span class="comment"># Schedule easypj pods only on Linux amd64 nodes</span>
kubectl get nodes -l kubernetes.io/os=linux,kubernetes.io/arch=amd64

<span class="comment"># Find nodes in a specific availability zone</span>
kubectl get nodes -l topology.kubernetes.io/zone=us-east-1a

<span class="comment"># Use in node affinity for easypj deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># spec.affinity.nodeAffinity.requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution:</span>
<span class="comment">#   nodeSelectorTerms:</span>
<span class="comment">#     - matchExpressions:</span>
<span class="comment">#         - key: topology.kubernetes.io/zone</span>
<span class="comment">#           operator: In</span>
<span class="comment">#           values: ["us-east-1a", "us-east-1b"]</span></code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §I.2 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-2">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.2 Well-Known Annotations</h3>
            <p>Annotations hold non-identifying metadata. These well-known annotations are recognized by Kubernetes components and common tools.</p>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Annotation</th><th>Used On</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/change-cause</code></td><td>Deployments</td><td>Records why a rollout happened — shown in <code>kubectl rollout history</code></td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/description</code></td><td>Any resource</td><td>Human-readable description of the resource</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration</code></td><td>Any resource</td><td>Stores the last <code>kubectl apply</code> manifest — used for 3-way merge</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>scheduler.alpha.kubernetes.io/defaultTolerations</code></td><td>Namespaces</td><td>Default tolerations applied to all pods in namespace</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class</code></td><td>StorageClass</td><td>Marks the default StorageClass (<code>"true"</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>kubernetes.io/ingress.class</code></td><td>Ingress</td><td>Legacy ingress controller selector (replaced by <code>ingressClassName</code>)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>node.kubernetes.io/exclude-from-external-load-balancers</code></td><td>Nodes</td><td>Exclude node from cloud load balancer backends</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — working with annotations</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># Record change cause for rollout history</span>
kubectl annotate deployment easypj-web -n easypj \
  kubernetes.io/change-cause="Updated to v2.1 with new job filters"

<span class="comment"># View rollout history (shows change-cause)</span>
kubectl rollout history deployment/easypj-web -n easypj
<span class="comment"># REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE</span>
<span class="comment"># 1         Initial deployment</span>
<span class="comment"># 2         Updated to v2.1 with new job filters</span>

<span class="comment"># Or use --record flag (deprecated but still works)</span>
kubectl set image deployment/easypj-web django=easypj/jobpost:v2.2 -n easypj --record

<span class="comment"># Check the default StorageClass</span>
kubectl get storageclass -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | select(.metadata.annotations["storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class"]=="true") | .metadata.name'</code></pre>
            </div>
        </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §I.3 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-3">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.3 Well-Known Taints</h3>
            <p>Taints are automatically applied by the node lifecycle controller or kubelet. Understanding them is critical for troubleshooting scheduling issues.</p>

                <!-- Flow diagram: taint effect decision -->
                <div class="flow-diagram">
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #2c3e50; color: white;">Pod Submitted</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #e67e22; color: white;">Node Has Taint?</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #27ae60; color: white;">Pod Tolerates It?</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #3498db; color: white;">✅ Scheduled</div>
                </div>
                <div class="flow-diagram" style="margin-top: 8px; justify-content: center;">
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #e74c3c; color: white;">NoSchedule → Blocked</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">|</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #c0392b; color: white;">NoExecute → Evicted</div>
                    <div class="flow-arrow">|</div>
                    <div class="flow-step" style="background: #f39c12; color: #333;">PreferNoSchedule → Soft Avoid</div>
                </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Taint Key</th><th>Effect</th><th>When Applied</th><th>Impact</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>By kubeadm on control plane nodes</td>
                            <td>Prevents workloads on control plane (add toleration to override)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/not-ready</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code> + <code>NoExecute</code></td>
                            <td>Node condition NotReady</td>
                            <td>Stops scheduling; evicts pods after <code>tolerationSeconds</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/unreachable</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code> + <code>NoExecute</code></td>
                            <td>Node condition Unknown (network issue)</td>
                            <td>Same as not-ready but for network partition</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/disk-pressure</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Disk usage exceeds eviction threshold</td>
                            <td>Stops new pods from scheduling; does NOT evict existing</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/memory-pressure</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Memory usage exceeds eviction threshold</td>
                            <td>Stops new pods from scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/pid-pressure</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>PID usage exceeds eviction threshold</td>
                            <td>Stops new pods from scheduling</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/network-unavailable</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td>Node's network is not configured (pre-CNI)</td>
                            <td>Removed once CNI plugin sets up networking</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><code>node.kubernetes.io/unschedulable</code></td>
                            <td><code>NoSchedule</code></td>
                            <td><code>kubectl cordon</code> or <code>kubectl drain</code></td>
                            <td>Prevents new pods; existing pods continue running</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">terminal — working with taints</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-bash"><span class="comment"># View taints on a node</span>
kubectl describe node node-1 | grep -A5 Taints

<span class="comment"># Check all taints across all nodes</span>
kubectl get nodes -o json | \
  jq -r '.items[] | .metadata.name + ": " + 
  ([.spec.taints[]? | .key + "=" + (.value // "") + ":" + .effect] | join(", "))'

<span class="comment"># Tolerate control-plane taint (e.g., for single-node clusters)</span>
<span class="comment"># In pod spec:</span>
<span class="comment"># tolerations:</span>
<span class="comment">#   - key: "node-role.kubernetes.io/control-plane"</span>
<span class="comment">#     operator: "Exists"</span>
<span class="comment">#     effect: "NoSchedule"</span>

<span class="comment"># Schedule easypj pods with custom tolerations</span>
kubectl run easypj-debug --image=easypj/jobpost:v2.1 -n easypj \
  --overrides='{"spec":{"tolerations":[{"key":"dedicated","operator":"Equal","value":"easypj","effect":"NoSchedule"}]}}'

<span class="comment"># Remove a taint</span>
kubectl taint nodes node-1 dedicated=easypj:NoSchedule-

<span class="comment"># Common troubleshooting: pod stuck Pending due to taints</span>
kubectl describe pod &lt;pending-pod&gt; | grep -A3 Events
<span class="comment"># 0/3 nodes are available: 3 node(s) had untolerated taint {key: value}</span></code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="cka-gotcha">
                <h5>Taint Troubleshooting on the CKA</h5>
                <ul>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Pod stuck Pending</strong> — check <code>kubectl describe pod</code> Events for "untolerated taint" messages</li>
                    <li>❌ <strong>Pods evicted after drain</strong> — <code>NoExecute</code> taints evict pods that don't tolerate them. DaemonSet pods tolerate everything by default</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Toleration matching</strong> — <code>operator: Exists</code> matches any value; <code>operator: Equal</code> requires exact value match</li>
                    <li>✅ <strong>Empty key + Exists</strong> — tolerates ALL taints (used by DaemonSets): <code>{"operator": "Exists"}</code></li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </section>

            <!-- ─────────── §I.4 ─────────── -->
        <section class="section-block" id="sI-4">
            <h3 class="section-h">I.4 Recommended Labels for Applications</h3>
            <p>These labels follow the Kubernetes recommended labeling convention (<code>app.kubernetes.io/*</code>) and are used by tools like Helm, ArgoCD, and kubectl.</p>

            <!-- Anatomy card: how labels fit together -->
            <div class="info-box note">
                <h5>🧬 Anatomy of easypj Labels</h5>
                <p>Think of these labels as a hierarchy: <strong>part-of</strong> (project) → <strong>name</strong> (app) → <strong>component</strong> (role) → <strong>instance</strong> (deployment) → <strong>version</strong> (release)</p>
            </div>
            <div class="lifecycle-chain">
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #8e44ad; color: white;">part-of: easypj</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #2980b9; color: white;">name: jobpost</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #27ae60; color: white;">component: web</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #e67e22; color: white;">instance: easypj-web</div>
                <div class="lc-arrow">→</div>
                <div class="lc-pill" style="background: #c0392b; color: white;">version: v2.1</div>
            </div>

            <div class="comparison-table">
                <table>
                    <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>Example Value</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/name</code></td><td><code>jobpost</code></td><td>The name of the application</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/instance</code></td><td><code>easypj-web</code></td><td>A unique instance name (for multiple deployments)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/version</code></td><td><code>v2.1</code></td><td>Current application version</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/component</code></td><td><code>frontend</code></td><td>Component within the architecture (frontend, backend, db)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/part-of</code></td><td><code>easypj</code></td><td>The larger application this is part of</td></tr>
                        <tr><td><code>app.kubernetes.io/managed-by</code></td><td><code>helm</code></td><td>Tool managing this resource (helm, argocd, kubectl)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block-wrapper">
                <div class="code-block-header">
                    <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                    <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                    <span class="code-lang">easypj-labels.yaml — recommended labels in practice</span>
                </div>
                <pre><code class="language-yaml">apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: easypj-web
  namespace: easypj
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: jobpost
    app.kubernetes.io/instance: easypj-web
    app.kubernetes.io/version: "v2.1"
    app.kubernetes.io/component: web
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: easypj
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: kubectl
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app.kubernetes.io/name: jobpost
      app.kubernetes.io/instance: easypj-web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app.kubernetes.io/name: jobpost
        app.kubernetes.io/instance: easypj-web
        app.kubernetes.io/version: "v2.1"
        app.kubernetes.io/component: web
        app.kubernetes.io/part-of: easypj</code></pre>
            </div>

            <div class="info-box tip">
                <h5>💡 Label Quick Reference</h5>
                <p><code>app.kubernetes.io/name</code> + <code>app.kubernetes.io/instance</code> together uniquely identify an application deployment. Use <code>part-of</code> to group related components (web, db, cache) under one project name. Helm automatically sets <code>managed-by: Helm</code> and <code>helm.sh/chart: chartname-version</code>.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

    </article>
    </main>

    <button type="button" class="to-top" id="toTop" aria-label="Back to top" title="Back to top">↑</button>

    <script>
        const body = document.body;
        const toggleBtn = document.getElementById('tocToggle');
        const backdrop = document.getElementById('navBackdrop');
        const isDesktop = () => window.matchMedia('(min-width:1000px)').matches;
        const navIsOpen = () => isDesktop() ? !body.classList.contains('nav-closed') : body.classList.contains('nav-open');

        function syncToggle(){ if (toggleBtn) toggleBtn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(navIsOpen())); }
        function toggleNav(){
            if (isDesktop()) body.classList.toggle('nav-closed');
            else body.classList.toggle('nav-open');
            syncToggle();
        }
        if (toggleBtn) toggleBtn.addEventListener('click', toggleNav);
        if (backdrop) backdrop.addEventListener('click', () => { body.classList.remove('nav-open'); syncToggle(); });

        /* Collapsible sidebar tree (called from inline onclick) */
        function togglePart(header){ const list = header.nextElementSibling; if (list) list.classList.toggle('visible'); }
        function toggleSections(btn){
            const sub = btn.closest('.chapter-item').querySelector('.sub-toc');
            if (sub){ sub.classList.toggle('visible'); btn.textContent = sub.classList.contains('visible') ? '▼' : '▶'; }
        }
        function toggleSubSub(btn){
            const ss = btn.parentElement.querySelector('.sub-sub-toc');
            if (ss){ ss.classList.toggle('visible'); btn.textContent = ss.classList.contains('visible') ? '▾' : '▸'; }
        }
        function expandAllParts(){ document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-list').forEach(l => l.classList.add('visible')); }
        function collapseAllParts(){
            document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-list').forEach(l => l.classList.remove('visible'));
            document.querySelectorAll('.sub-toc').forEach(s => s.classList.remove('visible'));
            document.querySelectorAll('.sub-sub-toc').forEach(s => s.classList.remove('visible'));
            document.querySelectorAll('.section-toggle-btn').forEach(b => b.textContent = '▶');
            document.querySelectorAll('.sub-sub-toggle').forEach(b => b.textContent = '▸');
        }

        /* Open the first part by default so the tree isn't blank */
        document.querySelector('.chapter-list')?.classList.add('visible');

        /* Close the overlay nav after tapping a link (mobile/tablet) */
        document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-link, .sub-toc a, .sub-sub-toc a').forEach(link => {
            link.addEventListener('click', () => { if (!isDesktop()){ body.classList.remove('nav-open'); syncToggle(); } });
        });

        /* which chapter's top has passed the 150px line = the one you're reading */
        function currentChapterId(){
            let current = '';
            document.querySelectorAll('main article[id^="ch"]').forEach(sec => { if (sec.getBoundingClientRect().top <= 150) current = sec.id; });
            return current;
        }
        /* Scrollspy — highlights the active chapter link (runs every scroll) */
        function highlightActive(){
            const current = currentChapterId();
            document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-link[href^="#ch"]').forEach(l => l.classList.toggle('active', l.getAttribute('href') === '#' + current));
        }
        window.addEventListener('scroll', highlightActive, { passive: true });

        /* Auto-open the submenu of the chapter you SETTLE on. Debounced so that
           scrolling past intermediate chapters (e.g. 8 → 3) opens only the one you
           land on, not every chapter in between. */
        function openCurrentSubmenu(){
            const current = currentChapterId();
            if (!current) return;
            const item = document.querySelector('.chapter-link[href="#' + current + '"]')?.closest('.chapter-item');
            const sub = item?.querySelector('.sub-toc');
            if (sub && !sub.classList.contains('visible')){
                sub.classList.add('visible');
                const btn = item.querySelector('.section-toggle-btn');
                if (btn) btn.textContent = '▼';
            }
        }
        let settleTimer = null;
        window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
            if (settleTimer) clearTimeout(settleTimer);
            settleTimer = setTimeout(openCurrentSubmenu, 260);   // fires only once scrolling stops
        }, { passive: true });
        window.addEventListener('hashchange', openCurrentSubmenu);  // clicking a side-menu link opens immediately
        highlightActive();
        openCurrentSubmenu();

        /* Smooth-scroll for TOC + appendix cards */
        document.querySelectorAll('.toc-card a, .appendix-card a').forEach(a => {
            a.addEventListener('click', e => {
                const target = document.querySelector(a.getAttribute('href'));
                if (target){ e.preventDefault(); target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' }); }
            });
        });

        /* Keyboard accessibility for collapsible part headers (a11y — better than cka_1) */
        document.querySelectorAll('.part-header').forEach(h => {
            h.setAttribute('role', 'button');
            h.setAttribute('tabindex', '0');
            h.addEventListener('keydown', e => { if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') { e.preventDefault(); togglePart(h); } });
        });

        /* Sidebar live filter — type to find any chapter/section */
        const filterInput = document.getElementById('tocFilter');
        const filterEmpty = document.getElementById('filterEmpty');
        function applyFilter(raw){
            const q = raw.trim().toLowerCase();
            let anyVisible = false;
            document.querySelectorAll('.toc-list > li').forEach(part => {
                const chList = part.querySelector('.chapter-list');
                if (!chList) return;
                let partHasMatch = false;
                part.querySelectorAll('.chapter-item').forEach(item => {
                    const link = item.querySelector('.chapter-link');
                    const chText = link ? link.textContent.toLowerCase() : '';
                    let subAnyMatch = false;
                    item.querySelectorAll('.sub-toc a').forEach(sl => {
                        const m = !q || sl.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(q);
                        sl.parentElement.classList.toggle('filtered-out', !!q && !m && !chText.includes(q));
                        if (q && m) subAnyMatch = true;
                    });
                    const show = !q || chText.includes(q) || subAnyMatch;
                    item.classList.toggle('filtered-out', !show);
                    if (show) partHasMatch = true;
                });
                part.classList.toggle('part-hidden', !!q && !partHasMatch);
                if (!q || partHasMatch) anyVisible = true;
                if (q && partHasMatch) chList.classList.add('visible');
            });
            if (filterEmpty) filterEmpty.classList.toggle('show', !!q && !anyVisible);
        }
        if (filterInput) filterInput.addEventListener('input', e => applyFilter(e.target.value));

        /* Reading-progress bar + back-to-top */
        const progress = document.getElementById('readProgress');
        const toTop = document.getElementById('toTop');
        function onScrollUI(){
            const h = document.documentElement;
            const max = h.scrollHeight - h.clientHeight;
            const pct = max > 0 ? (h.scrollTop / max) * 100 : 0;
            if (progress) progress.style.width = pct + '%';
            if (toTop) toTop.classList.toggle('show', h.scrollTop > 600);
        }
        if (toTop) toTop.addEventListener('click', () => window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' }));
        window.addEventListener('scroll', onScrollUI, { passive: true });
        onScrollUI();

        /* Deeper scrollspy — highlight the active sub-section link too */
        function highlightSub(){
            const subLinks = document.querySelectorAll('.sub-toc a[href^="#s"]');
            let current = '';
            document.querySelectorAll('main [id^="s"]').forEach(sec => { if (sec.getBoundingClientRect().top <= 160) current = sec.id; });
            subLinks.forEach(l => l.classList.toggle('active', l.getAttribute('href') === '#' + current));
        }
        window.addEventListener('scroll', highlightSub, { passive: true });

        window.addEventListener('resize', syncToggle);
        syncToggle();

        /* UTH ascii-diagram annotations: gray out an inline "-> explains this" or
           "<- explains this" aside and everything after it, up to the box-drawing border.
           A compact value-transition like "3->5" (no space before the arrow) is left alone -
           only whitespace-preceded arrows are treated as annotation separators.
           Scoped to Parts 1-3 (Ch 1-12) only; Parts 4+ are untouched. */
        function grayUthArrows(chapterIds){
            chapterIds.forEach(id => {
                const article = document.getElementById(id);
                if (!article) return;
                article.querySelectorAll('.uth-diagram').forEach(el => {
                    const pre = el.tagName === 'PRE' ? el : el.querySelector('pre');
                    if (!pre || pre.dataset.arrowsGrayed) return;
                    const lines = pre.innerHTML.split('\n').map(line => {
                        if (line.indexOf('<span') !== -1) return line;
                        const m = /(^|\s)([←→][^│┌┐└┘]*)/.exec(line);
                        if (!m) return line;
                        const prefixEnd = m.index + m[1].length;
                        const arrowText = m[2];
                        return line.slice(0, prefixEnd) + '<span class="comment">' + arrowText + '</span>' + line.slice(prefixEnd + arrowText.length);
                    });
                    pre.innerHTML = lines.join('\n');
                    pre.dataset.arrowsGrayed = '1';
                });
            });
        }
        grayUthArrows(['ch1','ch2','ch3','ch4','ch5','ch6','ch7','ch8','ch9','ch10','ch11','ch12']);
    </script>

    <!-- Prism.js syntax highlighting -->
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1/prism.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1/components/prism-yaml.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1/components/prism-bash.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1/components/prism-python.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1/components/prism-docker.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1/components/prism-json.min.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
